                <h4>The Kubernetes Release Cycle — What an Admin Must Track</h4>
                <p>Kubernetes ships <strong>three minor versions a year</strong> (~every 4 months: v1.31, v1.32, v1.33…). As the admin, keeping the cluster inside the supported window — and upgrading it safely — is <em>your</em> recurring job, and a guaranteed exam task. Each release has a life:</p>
                <div class="evolution-strip">
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">🆕</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">Month 0</div>
                        <h5>New Release</h5>
                        <p>e.g. v1.33 ships — new features, deprecations, and bug fixes land.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status good">✅ Latest</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item active">
                        <div class="evo-icon">🛡️</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">~0–14 months</div>
                        <h5>Supported</h5>
                        <p>Gets patch releases (security &amp; bug fixes). The CKA tests within this window.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status good">✅ Patched</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">⚠️</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">14+ months</div>
                        <h5>End of Life</h5>
                        <p>No more patches. Running EOL = a growing security risk → you must upgrade.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status bad">❌ Unsupported</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Golden Upgrade Rule: one minor version at a time</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes only supports upgrading <strong>one minor version at a step</strong> — <code>1.31 → 1.32 → 1.33</code>. You <strong>cannot skip</strong> (no 1.31 → 1.33 jump). There's also a <strong>version-skew</strong> rule: the kubelet may lag the API server by up to 3 minors but never run ahead of it. The CKA puts you in front of a real cluster and asks you to upgrade it with <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> (control plane first, then workers) — you'll do exactly this in Chapter 28.</p>
                </div>

