                <h4>Real Companies Running Kubernetes at Scale</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎵</div><h5>Spotify</h5><p>Migrated 150+ services to K8s; deployments dropped from hours to minutes.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛒</div><h5>Shopify</h5><p>Survives Black Friday by auto-scaling clusters to ~10× normal load.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎮</div><h5>Pokémon GO</h5><p>Launched on GKE; scaled from 1× to ~50× expected traffic in days.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏦</div><h5>Capital One</h5><p>Runs regulated banking workloads on K8s — proof it works under compliance.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Why Organizations Actually Adopt Kubernetes (and What It Costs)</h4>
                <p>Spotify and Capital One didn't adopt Kubernetes for the logo — they adopted it because it pays back three concrete dividends. Understanding these tells you <em>when</em> K8s is the right call (and, honestly, when it isn't):</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚀</div>
                        <h5>1 · Velocity</h5>
                        <p>Ship more often, safely. Rolling updates + instant rollback + a declarative pipeline turn risky, hours-long deploys into routine, minutes-long ones — so teams release many times a day instead of monthly.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>2 · Resilience</h5>
                        <p>Self-healing + rescheduling + HA mean a crashed pod or a dead node is a non-event, not a 3 AM page. Uptime stops depending on a human being awake.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💰</div>
                        <h5>3 · Efficiency</h5>
                        <p>Bin-packing many containers onto shared nodes (and autoscaling them) drives up utilization and drives down the cloud bill — versus one fat VM per app sitting mostly idle.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>There's a fourth, quieter dividend: <strong>portability</strong>. Because workloads target the Kubernetes API (not a specific cloud), the same easypj manifests run on your laptop, on bare metal, and on any cloud — which is real leverage against lock-in.</p>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚖️ The honest trade-off — it isn't free</h5>
                    <p>Those dividends are bought with <strong>operational complexity</strong>: a control plane to run (or pay a cloud to run), certificates that expire, upgrades to perform, networking and storage to wire up, and a real learning curve. That complexity <em>is the administrator's job</em> — which is precisely why the CKA exists and why this skill is valuable. The rule of thumb: Kubernetes pays off when you have <em>multiple services, real scale/uptime needs, and frequent releases</em>; for a single low-traffic app, it's usually more machine than the job requires (see the "when NOT to use it" box below).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Managed vs Self-Managed — Who Runs the Control Plane?</h4>
                <p>There are two ways to get a cluster, and the difference decides what's <em>your</em> job. The CKA deliberately tests the <strong>self-managed</strong> path (you run everything), because once you understand that, managed clusters are easy.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>🛠️ Self-managed (kubeadm) — what the CKA tests</th><th>☁️ Managed (EKS / GKE / AKS)</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Control plane</strong></td><td>You install &amp; run it (API server, etcd, scheduler…)</td><td>The cloud runs &amp; upgrades it for you</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd backups</strong></td><td>Your responsibility (Chapter 29)</td><td>Handled by the provider</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Upgrades</strong></td><td>You run <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> (Chapter 28)</td><td>Click/CLI; you still upgrade node pools</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Nodes</strong></td><td>You join &amp; maintain them</td><td>Managed node pools / autoscaling</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>You still own</strong></td><td colspan="2" class="tac">Workloads · RBAC · NetworkPolicy · storage · ingress — <strong>everything in this guide transfers to both</strong></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why learn the hard (self-managed) way?</h5>
                    <p>Because it makes you <em>unbreakable</em>. When a managed cluster misbehaves, the engineers who understand etcd, certificates, and the kubelet are the ones who fix it. Chapter 36 maps every skill here onto EKS/GKE/AKS — same concepts, the provider just runs the control plane.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚫 Honest Take: When NOT to Use Kubernetes</h5>
                    <p>Maturity means knowing the limits. Kubernetes is <em>overkill</em> when:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>A single small app, low traffic</strong> — a managed PaaS (Heroku, Render, Fly.io) or one VM is simpler and cheaper.</li>
                        <li><strong>No one to operate it</strong> — K8s adds real operational complexity; without an admin (you!) it becomes a liability.</li>
                        <li><strong>Static workloads that never scale</strong> — if you'll never need scaling, healing, or rolling updates, you're paying complexity for nothing.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="mb-0">Use it when you have <strong>multiple services</strong>, need <strong>resilience &amp; scale</strong>, ship <strong>frequently</strong>, or want a <strong>portable</strong> platform across clouds — which is exactly where easypj is headed.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What "Operating" a Cluster Actually Means — the Day-2 Task Matrix</h4>
                <p>Installing a cluster is <em>Day 1</em>; keeping it healthy for years is <em>Day 2</em> — and Day-2 work is the bulk of both the job and the exam. Here is the recurring task set: how often each comes up, how risky it is when botched, and where this guide drills it — so "administration" becomes a concrete checklist rather than a vague word:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Day-2 task</th><th>How often</th><th>Risk if botched</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Watch health — nodes, pods, events, <code>kubectl top</code></td><td>daily</td><td>low (read-only)</td><td>Ch 32–33</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Deploy / roll out / roll back workloads</td><td>daily–weekly</td><td>medium (user-facing)</td><td>Ch 13</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Manage access — RBAC, ServiceAccounts</td><td>weekly</td><td>high (security)</td><td>Ch 24–25</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Drain → patch → uncordon a node (OS/kernel)</td><td>monthly</td><td>medium (needs PodDisruptionBudgets)</td><td>Ch 30</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Back up etcd &amp; <em>test</em> the restore</td><td>daily backup / periodic test</td><td>🔴 critical (the whole cluster)</td><td>Ch 29</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Upgrade Kubernetes, one minor at a time</td><td>~every few months</td><td>high (control plane)</td><td>Ch 28</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Rotate / renew certificates before expiry</td><td>yearly (or on alert)</td><td>🔴 critical (API lockout)</td><td>Ch 26</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Capacity plan — add nodes, right-size requests/limits</td><td>ongoing</td><td>medium (cost / evictions)</td><td>Ch 15</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Incident response — diagnose &amp; fix breakage</td><td>whenever it breaks</td><td>varies</td><td>Ch 31–34</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The two tasks that can lose the entire cluster</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Notice the two 🔴 rows — <strong>etcd backups</strong> and <strong>certificate renewal</strong>. An un-tested backup and a silently-expired cert are the two classic ways admins lose (or lock themselves out of) a cluster, which is exactly why the CKA weights them heavily and this guide gives each a full chapter. Most other mistakes you can recover from; these two you must plan <em>ahead</em> of.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Where you fit in — the easypj journey</h5>
                    <p>This guide follows <strong>easypj</strong> — our Django <code>jobpost</code> board — from a single-server app to a production-grade cluster. You'll containerize it, deploy it, scale it, secure it, break it, fix it, and ship it. By the end you'll pass the CKA <em>and</em> be able to run real clusters.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 1.7 OBJECT MODEL & API — official-docs enrichment ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.7 The Object Model &amp; the Kubernetes API</h3>
                <p>Everything you will ever create is a <strong>Kubernetes object</strong> — a persisted record of intent the cluster works to make real. Four ideas make the whole API click: the <em>shape</em> of every object, how you <em>name</em> it, how the API is <em>organised &amp; versioned</em>, and the <em>ways you apply</em> it. Master these and manifests stop being magic.</p>

                <h4>① The four required fields — in every manifest, no exceptions</h4>
                <p>Every object you write carries the same four top-level fields. Omit one — or mis-case <code>kind</code> — and the apiserver rejects the manifest before it ever reaches etcd.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it is</th><th>easypj example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>apiVersion</code></td><td>Which API group + version defines this object (so the server knows its schema)</td><td><code>apps/v1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kind</code></td><td>The object type — what you're creating</td><td><code>Deployment</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>metadata</code></td><td>Identity: <code>name</code> (required), <code>namespace</code>, <code>labels</code>, <code>annotations</code>, and the server-set <code>uid</code></td><td><code>name: easypj-web</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>spec</code></td><td>Your <strong>desired state</strong> — the shape varies per <code>kind</code></td><td><code>replicas: 3</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>② <code>spec</code> vs <code>status</code> — desired vs actual, inside the object itself</h4>
                <p>Almost every object has two halves: a <code>spec</code> you write (the goal) and a <code>status</code> the cluster writes (reality). The reconciliation loop from §1.4 is just "keep <code>status</code> matching <code>spec</code>." You never edit <code>status</code> — <em>reading</em> it is how you troubleshoot.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl get deploy easypj-web -o yaml (trimmed)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:                 <span class="comment"># 👈 YOU declare this — desired state</span>
  replicas: 3
status:               <span class="comment"># 👈 KUBERNETES writes this — actual state (read-only)</span>
  replicas: 3
  readyReplicas: 2    <span class="comment"># only 2 are Ready right now → a controller is closing the gap</span>
  conditions:
  - type: Available
    status: "False"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>③ Naming rules — why <code>easypj_web</code> is rejected</h4>
                <p>Object names aren't free-form. <strong>Most</strong> objects — Pods, Deployments, ConfigMaps, and even Nodes — follow the <strong>DNS Subdomain</strong> rule; only a few — <strong>namespaces</strong> (and <strong>Services</strong>, under the related RFC 1035) — must be a stricter <strong>DNS Label</strong>. Break the rule and you get a validation error, not a running object.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Used by</th><th>Max</th><th>Allowed</th><th>✅ valid · ❌ invalid</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>DNS Subdomain</strong></td><td>most objects: Deployments, ConfigMaps, Secrets…</td><td>253</td><td>lowercase <code>a–z 0–9 - .</code>; start/end alphanumeric (may start with a digit)</td><td>✅ <code>easypj-web</code>, <code>app.prod</code> · ❌ <code>easypj_web</code>, <code>EasyPJ</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>DNS Label</strong></td><td>namespaces (RFC 1123 label)</td><td>63</td><td>lowercase <code>a–z 0–9 -</code> (<strong>no dots</strong>); <strong>start with a letter</strong>, end alphanumeric</td><td>✅ <code>easypj-prod</code> · ❌ <code>my.ns</code>, <code>NS-1</code>, <code>9ns</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>DNS Label (RFC 1035)</strong></td><td>Service names</td><td>63</td><td>same, but <strong>must start with a letter</strong> (a feature gate relaxes this)</td><td>✅ <code>easypj-web</code> · ❌ <code>9web</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>UID</strong></td><td>every object (set by the server)</td><td>—</td><td>cluster-unique, auto-generated, never user-supplied</td><td>tells apart two objects that reused a name over time</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>④ API groups, versions &amp; stability levels</h4>
                <p>The API is split into <strong>groups</strong>, each <strong>versioned independently</strong>. The <code>apiVersion</code> you write picks the group + version; the stability ladder tells you what's safe to depend on.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">how apiVersion resolves to an API path</div>
<pre>
   apiVersion: v1          → CORE group    → /api/v1           Pod · Service · ConfigMap · Namespace · Node
   apiVersion: apps/v1     → "apps" group  → /apis/apps/v1     Deployment · ReplicaSet · StatefulSet · DaemonSet
   apiVersion: batch/v1    → "batch" group → /apis/batch/v1    Job · CronJob
   apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1                    Role · RoleBinding · ClusterRole
   └─ kubectl api-resources   lists every kind → its group, version &amp; whether it's namespaced
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Means</th><th>On by default?</th><th>In a name</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Alpha</strong></td><td>experimental — may change or vanish, may have bugs</td><td>no (needs a feature gate)</td><td><code>v1alpha1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Beta</strong></td><td>well-tested; details may still change before GA</td><td>varies (newer betas off)</td><td><code>v1beta1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Stable / GA</strong></td><td>production-ready, backward-compatible</td><td>yes</td><td><code>v1</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><p class="mb-0"><strong>Deprecation policy:</strong> a <strong>GA</strong> (stable) API version is <strong>never removed within a major version</strong> — a <code>v1</code> API lasts for all of Kubernetes 1.x; a deprecated <strong>beta</strong> version is dropped after <strong>9 months or 3 releases</strong> (whichever is longer); <strong>alpha</strong> can change or vanish in any release. There's <strong>no LTS</strong>: each minor release is maintained ~14 months (≈12 months support + a ~2-month maintenance window) — read the release notes and upgrade before EOL.</p></div>

                <h4>⑤ Three ways to manage objects — and why the exam loves <code>apply</code></h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Command</th><th>Idempotent?</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Imperative command</td><td><code>kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v2</code></td><td>no — errors if it exists</td><td>exam speed &amp; quick fixes; keeps no file</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Imperative object config</td><td><code>kubectl create -f f.yaml</code> / <code>replace -f</code></td><td>no</td><td>rarely — forces delete-then-recreate</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Declarative</strong></td><td><code>kubectl apply -f f.yaml</code></td><td><strong>yes</strong> — run 100×, same result</td><td>production &amp; GitOps; the default to reach for</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip"><p class="mb-0">💡 <strong>Server-Side Apply</strong> (<code>kubectl apply --server-side</code>) moves the merge into the apiserver and tracks <em>field ownership</em>, so several controllers can co-own one object without clobbering each other. ⚡ <strong>Exam speed:</strong> <code>kubectl create deploy … --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; d.yaml</code> scaffolds a manifest you then edit &amp; <code>apply</code>.</p></div>

                <h4>⑥ Add-ons — what a fresh cluster still needs</h4>
                <p>A brand-new cluster can schedule pods but is missing pieces you'll assume are there. These ship as <strong>add-ons</strong> (themselves pods), not part of the core binaries.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>🧭 CoreDNS</h5><p>In-cluster DNS — every pod resolves <code>name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code> here. Normally installed; no DNS → no name-based service discovery.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>📊 metrics-server</h5><p>Feeds CPU/memory to the API. <strong>Required</strong> for <code>kubectl top</code> and the HorizontalPodAutoscaler. Not installed by default.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🌍 Ingress controller</h5><p>Terminates &amp; routes external HTTP(S) to Services (e.g. ingress-nginx). You must pick &amp; install one — none ships by default.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🖥️ Dashboard</h5><p>Optional web UI. Handy, never required for admin work — and one more thing to secure if you deploy it.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>⑦ Labels, selectors, annotations &amp; ownership — the glue that wires objects together</h4>
                <p>The four required fields give an object its identity; three more <code>metadata</code> ideas are how objects <em>find and own each other</em>. These aren't cosmetic — they're the wiring behind Services, controllers, and cascading deletes, and confusing them is the single most common cause of "my Service has no endpoints."</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it is</th><th>Kubernetes uses it to…</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>labels</strong></td><td>key/value tags <em>meant for selection</em> (<code>app: easypj</code>, <code>tier: web</code>)</td><td>let selectors find groups of objects — the basis of all wiring</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>selectors</strong></td><td>a query over labels (<code>app=easypj</code>)</td><td>connect a Service to its Pods, a Deployment to its Pods, a NetworkPolicy to its targets</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>annotations</strong></td><td>key/value metadata <em>not</em> for selection (URLs, checksums, tool config)</td><td>carry non-identifying info tools read, e.g. <code>kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ownerReferences</strong></td><td>a pointer from child → parent (Pod → ReplicaSet → Deployment)</td><td>drive <strong>cascading deletion</strong> &amp; adoption of orphans</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Labels wire a Service to Pods; ownerReferences chain the delete</div>
<pre>
   WIRING (by label selector)                      OWNERSHIP (by ownerReferences)
   ┌────────────────────────────┐                   Deployment easypj-web
   │ Service easypj-web           │                        │ owns
   │   selector:  app=easypj      │                        ▼
   └──────────────┬─────────────┘                    ReplicaSet easypj-web-6d8f9
                  │ matches the label                       │ owns
        ┌─────────┼─────────┐                               ▼
        ▼         ▼         ▼                        📦 Pod   📦 Pod   📦 Pod
   📦 app=easypj 📦 app=easypj 📦 app=easypj          delete the Deployment ⇒ the whole
   (Ready pods → EndpointSlice → traffic)            chain is garbage-collected ⬇ (cascade)

   ⚠️ if the pod labels ≠ the Service selector → 0 endpoints → the Service "works" but nothing answers
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Finalizers — why a namespace sometimes hangs in "Terminating"</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">A <strong>finalizer</strong> is a key in <code>metadata.finalizers</code> that tells the API server "run cleanup <em>before</em> you actually delete me." A delete sets <code>deletionTimestamp</code> and then <em>waits</em> until the responsible controller removes its finalizer. It's the mechanism behind orderly teardown — and the reason a stuck controller can leave a namespace in <code>Terminating</code> indefinitely (you'll diagnose exactly that in Ch 32).</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <h5>📖 The takeaway that saves you hours later</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>Labels are load-bearing.</strong> Services, Deployments, NetworkPolicies, and DaemonSets all target pods <em>by label selector</em>, so a one-character label typo silently breaks the wiring with <em>no error</em> — the object looks healthy, it just selects nothing. Whenever something is mysteriously unreachable, an early check is <code>kubectl get pods --show-labels</code> against the selector.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Label &amp; selector rules worth knowing (they bite on the exam)</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>A label <strong>value</strong> is ≤63 chars; the <strong>key</strong> may carry an optional DNS-subdomain <strong>prefix</strong> (≤253 chars) before a <code>/</code>.</li>
                        <li>The <code>kubernetes.io/</code> and <code>k8s.io/</code> prefixes are <strong>reserved</strong> for Kubernetes core components — don't invent your own keys under them.</li>
                        <li>Selectors support equality (<code>=</code>, <code>!=</code>) and set-based (<code>in</code>, <code>notin</code>, <code>exists</code>) conditions; multiple conditions are AND-ed by commas. There is <strong>no OR</strong> operator.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Where objects live — the four built-in namespaces</h4>
                <p>Every namespaced object lives in exactly one <strong>namespace</strong> (a virtual cluster for isolation &amp; quotas). A fresh cluster ships with four you must recognize:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Namespace</th><th>What's in it</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>default</code></td><td>where your objects land if you don't specify one (easypj should get its <em>own</em> namespace instead)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kube-system</code></td><td>the control plane &amp; add-ons — CoreDNS, kube-proxy, the static-pod mirrors, Leases</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kube-public</code></td><td>world-readable (even to unauthenticated clients) cluster info; rarely used directly</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kube-node-lease</code></td><td>one <code>Lease</code> per node for the fast node heartbeats (see Under the Hood ⑥)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <p class="mb-0">📖 Namespaces can't be nested, and a resource lives in only one. The control plane stamps every namespace with an immutable <code>kubernetes.io/metadata.name</code> label (equal to its own name), so you can select namespaces by name. Set your working namespace once with <code>kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj</code> — full depth in Chapter 4.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — the reconciliation loop that powers everything</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Self-healing isn't magic — it's a <strong>closed control loop</strong> running forever inside the controller-manager. Each controller does three things, over and over:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ┌──────────── CONTROL LOOP (runs continuously) ────────────┐
   │                                                          │
   │   👁️ OBSERVE  ──▶  🧮 DIFF  ──▶  🛠️ ACT  ──┐             │
   │   read actual     desired vs     make a change           │
   │   state from      actual          (create/delete/        │
   │   the API          (a gap?)        update an object)     │
   │        ▲                                    │            │
   │        └────────────────────────────────────┘            │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   easypj example: desired replicas=3, actual=2  →  create 1 pod  →  repeat
                    </pre>

                    <h5>🗺️ The Full Trace — Deploying easypj with 3 Replicas, Step by Step</h5>
                    <p>Here is the loop playing out for real. Notice that <strong>no single component drives the whole thing</strong> — each controller independently reconciles its own slice, and the result emerges:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
 1. USER declares desired state (deploy.yaml):
    ┌───────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │ apiVersion: apps/v1                        │
    │ kind: Deployment                           │
    │ spec:                                      │
    │   replicas: 3                              │
    │   template:                                │
    │     spec:                                  │
    │       containers:                          │
    │       - name: web                          │
    │         image: easypj:v2                   │
    └───────────────────────────────────────────┘

 2. kubectl apply → API server → persisted in etcd
    /registry/deployments/easypj/easypj-web = {replicas:3, image:v2}

 3. Deployment controller OBSERVES: desired replicas=3, image=v2
                            ACTUAL: no ReplicaSet, no Pods   → DIFF: everything missing
 4. Deployment controller ACTS:  creates ReplicaSet easypj-web-7d8f9

 5. ReplicaSet controller OBSERVES: want 3 pods (app=easypj-web)
                             ACTUAL: 0 pods                  → DIFF: 3 needed
 6. ReplicaSet controller ACTS:  creates 3 Pod objects (nodeName empty → Pending)

 7. Scheduler OBSERVES: 3 Pods with no node
                ACTS:  filters + scores nodes, binds each Pod → a Node

 8. kubelet (on each chosen node) OBSERVES: a Pod is assigned to me
                                     ACTS:  CRI pull image → create → start container
                                            CNI assigns the pod an IP

 9. easypj pods RUNNING :8000  → kubelet reports status up to the API server → etcd

10. Controllers keep watching forever: actual=3 = desired=3 ✅
    ── crash a pod? ReplicaSet makes a new one.  kill a node? Scheduler re-places.
    ── At NO step did anyone say "start these pods on these nodes." It EMERGED.
                    </pre>

                    <h5>🔬 Level-Triggered vs Edge-Triggered — Why Controllers Never "Miss" an Event</h5>
                    <p>The deep reason self-healing is reliable: Kubernetes is <strong>level-triggered</strong>. Controllers don't depend on catching every event — they periodically re-read the <em>whole</em> state. A dropped watch event (network blip) is simply corrected on the next resync.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Edge-triggered (imperative systems)</th><th>Level-triggered (Kubernetes)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Relies on receiving every event</td><td>Periodically re-reads the full state</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Missed event → permanently inconsistent</td><td>Missed event → caught on next resync</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"Start Apache" — if it crashes, nobody restarts it</td><td>"3 replicas should exist" — a crash is auto-replaced</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Needs complex error handling</td><td>Self-healing by design</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <div class="uth-callout">
                        <strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> When you <code>kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml</code> you are NOT telling Kubernetes "do these steps" — you're declaring "I want 3 easypj:v2 replicas." The controllers figure out the steps. That's <em>why</em> <code>apply</code> is idempotent: run it 100 times, same result as once. No state machine, no step sequence — just a declared end state and loops that converge on it.
                    </div>
                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> almost <em>everything</em> in Kubernetes is an API object stored in etcd and reconciled by a controller — Deployments, Services, Nodes, even certificates and namespaces. Master this one pattern and the whole system becomes predictable: to understand any feature, ask "what object, and which controller reconciles it?" This is what makes Kubernetes a universal <em>control plane</em>, not just a container runner.</p>

                    <h5>① How a controller <em>really</em> OBSERVES — list-watch, informers &amp; the work queue</h5>
                    <p>The loop above says "observe actual state," but <em>how</em>? Naively you'd poll — "is it 3 yet? is it 3 yet?" — which would hammer the API server with thousands of controllers asking constantly. Real Kubernetes never polls. It uses the <strong>list-watch</strong> pattern behind an <strong>informer</strong>: LIST the full state once, then WATCH a live stream of changes, keeping a local cache in sync.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   🗄️ etcd ──change──▶ 📡 apiserver (watch cache)
                            │  streams events:  ADDED · MODIFIED · DELETED
                            ▼
                 ┌────────────────────────────┐
                 │ 🧠 INFORMER (in the controller)│
                 │  • LIST once → fill a local  │   the cache = a live, in-memory
                 │    cache (the "store")       │   mirror of cluster state, so the
                 │  • WATCH → apply deltas      │   controller reads it WITHOUT
                 │  • fire event handlers       │   touching etcd every time
                 └───────────────┬────────────┘
                                 │ enqueue the changed object's KEY  (namespace/name)
                                 ▼
                 ┌────────────────────────────┐
                 │ 📥 WORK QUEUE                │   dedups duplicates · rate-limits ·
                 │                              │   retries failures with backoff
                 └───────────────┬────────────┘
                                 │ a worker pops one key
                                 ▼
   🔄 Reconcile(key):  read DESIRED + ACTUAL from the cache  →  diff  →  act
                       (this is the OBSERVE → DIFF → ACT loop, in code)</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>❌ Naive polling</th><th>✅ list-watch + informer (real K8s)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>API-server load</td><td>constant, heavy (every controller, every second)</td><td>one LIST + a single long-lived WATCH stream</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Latency to react</td><td>up to the poll interval</td><td>near-instant — changes are pushed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Reads of "actual state"</td><td>hit the API/etcd each time</td><td>served from the informer's in-memory cache</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Missed events</td><td>silently lost</td><td>a periodic <strong>resync</strong> re-LISTs and re-reconciles everything (this <em>is</em> level-triggering in practice)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Failures</td><td>DIY retry logic</td><td>work queue dedups, rate-limits &amp; retries with backoff</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 So "observe actual state" is cheap</strong> because the controller reads its own <em>informer cache</em>, not etcd — and the cache is kept fresh by one shared WATCH. The periodic resync is the safety net that makes missed events impossible to matter.</div>

                    <h5>② Inside the API server — what <code>kubectl apply</code> passes through before it's saved</h5>
                    <p>"The API server validates and persists" hides a precise 6-stage pipeline. Knowing it tells you <em>exactly</em> which stage rejected a request from the error message alone:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml ─▶ 📡 kube-apiserver
        │
        ├─ 1. 🔐 Authentication      WHO are you?        cert / token / OIDC
        ├─ 2. 🛂 Authorization       MAY you do this?    RBAC (verb + resource)
        ├─ 3. 🧬 Mutating admission   change the object   webhooks: inject sidecars, set defaults
        ├─ 4. 📋 Schema validation    is it well-formed?  OpenAPI schema check
        ├─ 5. ✅ Validating admission policy gate         PodSecurity, ValidatingWebhooks, quota
        └─ 6. 💾 persist to etcd      write + bump resourceVersion  ──▶ controllers WATCH it</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Typical rejection you'd see</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Authentication</td><td>Who is calling?</td><td><code>Unauthorized</code> (401) — bad/expired cert or token</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Authorization (RBAC)</td><td>Are they allowed?</td><td><code>Forbidden</code> (403) — "User cannot create deployments…"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Mutating admission</td><td>—</td><td>(usually silent — it <em>edits</em> the object, e.g. adds a sidecar)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Schema validation</td><td>Is the YAML valid?</td><td><code>error validating data: unknown field…</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Validating admission</td><td>Does it meet policy?</td><td><code>admission webhook denied the request</code> / PodSecurity violation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Persist to etcd</td><td>—</td><td><code>Conflict</code> (409) if resourceVersion is stale (see ③)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Read the error, name the stage.</strong> "Forbidden" = stage 2 (fix RBAC). "unknown field" = stage 4 (fix the YAML). "admission webhook denied" = stage 5 (a policy). This single mapping shortcuts a huge share of troubleshooting.</div>

                    <h5>③ etcd under the hood — Raft consensus, MVCC &amp; optimistic concurrency</h5>
                    <p>Stage 6 above ("persist to etcd") is where state actually lives. etcd is a distributed key-value store that stays <em>consistent across replicas</em> via the <strong>Raft</strong> algorithm and versions <em>every</em> change via <strong>MVCC</strong>. Every Kubernetes object is just a key:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Every object is ONE key in etcd (the "/registry" tree):
     /registry/deployments/easypj/easypj-web        = { …spec…, resourceVersion: 4821 }
     /registry/replicasets/easypj/easypj-web-6d8f9  = { …spec…, …status… }
     /registry/pods/easypj/easypj-web-6d8f9-x7k2    = { …spec…, …status… }
     /registry/services/easypj/easypj-web           = { … }

   Writes go through RAFT — a MAJORITY (quorum) must agree before a commit:
     ┌─────────┐   append entry   ┌─────────┐   replicate   ┌─────────┐
     │ etcd-1  │ ───────────────▶ │ etcd-2  │ ────────────▶ │ etcd-3  │
     │(leader) │ ◀─────ack─────── │(follower)│ ◀────ack───── │(follower)│
     └─────────┘                  └─────────┘               └─────────┘
        committed only once a MAJORITY acks  →  3 members tolerate 1 down (quorum = 2)
        ⚠️ lose quorum (2 of 3 down) → etcd goes READ-ONLY → the cluster "freezes"</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it is</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>MVCC + revision</td><td>every write bumps a global <em>revision</em>; old versions are kept until compaction</td><td>lets a WATCH replay changes from a point in time → reliable informers</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resourceVersion</code></td><td>the per-object version stamped on every read/write</td><td>the basis of optimistic concurrency (below)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Optimistic concurrency</td><td>an update carries the rv you read; if it changed meanwhile → <code>409 Conflict</code></td><td>two controllers can't clobber each other — <strong>no locks needed</strong></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Raft quorum</td><td>a majority of members must agree to commit</td><td>use ODD counts; losing quorum makes the API read-only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Compaction / defrag</td><td>prune old revisions &amp; reclaim disk</td><td>keeps etcd healthy — a real Day-2 admin task (Ch 29)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>Optimistic concurrency is the quiet hero that lets the level-triggered loop stay correct even when many controllers touch the same object at once — <em>no locks, just retry-on-conflict</em>:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   controller A reads easypj-web   (resourceVersion 4821)
   controller B reads easypj-web   (resourceVersion 4821)
   A writes its change            → accepted → object is now rv 4822
   B writes with rv 4821          → ❌ 409 CONFLICT  (someone moved first)
   B re-reads (gets rv 4822), recomputes the diff, writes again → ✅ accepted
                   └──────────────── this is just the loop running again ────────────────┘</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 It's all one machine.</strong> The reconcile loop (OBSERVE→DIFF→ACT), the informer/watch that feeds OBSERVE, the API-server pipeline that guards every write, and etcd's revisions + optimistic concurrency are not separate features — they're one coherent design. That's why the right question for <em>any</em> Kubernetes behaviour is always: "which object, which controller, what's the gap?"</div>

                    <h5>🔬 Deeper still — why the loop scales &amp; survives failures</h5>
                    <p>Four production mechanisms keep that loop fast, cheap and reliable across thousands of objects and flaky networks:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>What it does</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Shared informer</strong></td><td>one <code>LIST</code>+<code>WATCH</code> per <em>resource type</em>, cached in memory and shared by every controller that needs it</td><td>API-server load is <strong>O(resource types)</strong>, not O(controllers) — a 50th controller adds no new watch on etcd</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Work queue + rate limiter</strong></td><td>a failed reconcile is re-queued with <strong>exponential back-off</strong> (~5ms, doubling, capped ~1000s), deduped, and reset on success</td><td>a broken resource is retried <em>calmly</em>, not hammered thousands of times a second</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>WATCH bookmarks</strong></td><td>the apiserver streams periodic <code>BOOKMARK</code> events ("still here, at <code>resourceVersion</code> X") even when nothing changed</td><td>a client can tell "quiet" from "stalled" and resume from the last rv without a full re-LIST</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>410 Gone</strong></td><td>watching from a <code>resourceVersion</code> older than the apiserver's history window returns <strong>HTTP 410 Gone</strong></td><td>forces a fresh <code>LIST</code> + resync so caches can't silently drift — and is why aggressive etcd compaction (Ch 29) shrinks that window</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The pattern, named:</strong> read → compute → write <em>with</em> the <code>resourceVersion</code> you read → on <code>409 Conflict</code>, re-read &amp; retry. That's <strong>lock-free optimistic concurrency</strong>: two controllers touching one object never deadlock — the loser just retries and still makes progress.</div>

                    <h5>④ The bootstrap paradox — how the control plane starts <em>itself</em></h5>
                    <p>Here's a puzzle the diagrams above quietly skip: controllers and the scheduler all run <em>through</em> the API server, and the API server is itself just a program something has to start. On a kubeadm cluster there's no "outer" Kubernetes to launch it — so how does the control plane come up at all? The answer is <strong>static pods</strong>, and it's a favourite exam trap.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   The kubelet can run pods in TWO ways:
     (a) normal pods  → assigned by the scheduler THROUGH the API server   ← needs the CP up
     (b) STATIC pods  → read straight off local disk, NO API server needed ← breaks the cycle

   On a control-plane node the kubelet watches this folder every few seconds:
     📁 /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
         ├── etcd.yaml                      ┐
         ├── kube-apiserver.yaml            │  the kubelet reads these files and runs them
         ├── kube-controller-manager.yaml   │  as pods DIRECTLY — that's how the control
         └── kube-scheduler.yaml            ┘  plane boots when no control plane exists yet

   Boot order:  kubelet starts → sees the manifests → runs etcd + apiserver as static pods
                → the apiserver comes up → NOW scheduler &amp; controllers (also static) can use it
                → the cluster is alive and schedules everything else the normal way.</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Two admin superpowers fall out of this.</strong> (1) To change a control-plane component you just <em>edit its file</em> in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — the kubelet notices and recreates the pod automatically, no <code>kubectl apply</code>. (2) When the API server is down and <code>kubectl</code> is dead, you debug these pods on the node with <code>crictl ps</code>, because they don't need the API server to exist. The apiserver also surfaces them as read-only <em>mirror pods</em> so you can <em>see</em> them via <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> — but you can't manage them that way. This is the backbone of Chapters 12 and 32.</div>

                    <h5>⑤ Leader election — how an HA control plane avoids two brains</h5>
                    <p>A production cluster runs <strong>several</strong> control-plane nodes for availability, so multiple <code>kube-controller-manager</code> and <code>kube-scheduler</code> processes are alive at once. If they <em>all</em> acted, two schedulers could place the same pod twice and two controllers could fight. Kubernetes prevents this with <strong>leader election</strong>: exactly one instance is active; the rest stand by.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   3 controller-managers alive, but only ONE reconciles at a time:
     ┌──────────────┐   ┌──────────────┐   ┌──────────────┐
     │ ctrl-mgr A   │   │ ctrl-mgr B   │   │ ctrl-mgr C   │
     │ 👑 LEADER    │   │   standby    │   │   standby    │
     └──────┬───────┘   └──────────────┘   └──────────────┘
            │ renews a Lease object every few seconds:
            ▼
     🗄️ Lease  kube-system/kube-controller-manager
        holderIdentity: A   ·   renewTime: 10:00:15   ·   leaseDurationSeconds: 15
            │
            └─ A stops renewing (crash) → the Lease expires → B or C grabs it → new leader
               (failover in ~15s; there is never more than one active leader)</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 It's the same primitive, reused.</strong> Leader election is just optimistic writes to a <code>Lease</code> object (<code>coordination.k8s.io/v1</code>) — the leader is whoever atomically updates it first; watch it live with <code>kubectl -n kube-system get lease</code>. Contrast the API server: it's <em>active-active</em> (every instance serves traffic behind a load balancer, because it's stateless and etcd is the shared truth). Only the singleton <em>deciders</em> — scheduler and controller-manager — need one leader. HA is drilled in Chapter 35.</div>

                    <h5>⑥ How the control plane knows a node died — heartbeats, conditions &amp; eviction timing</h5>
                    <p>"The node goes <code>NotReady</code> and its pods are rescheduled" hides precise, exam-tested machinery. Every kubelet sends <strong>two</strong> heartbeats, and specific timers decide when a silent node's pods actually move:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  TWO heartbeats per node:
    ① a lightweight Lease  →  a Lease object in the  kube-node-lease  namespace whose
                              .spec.renewTime the kubelet updates ~every 10s  (cheap, frequent)
    ② the Node .status     →  full status (conditions, capacity) — pushed on change / ~every 5m

  WHEN HEARTBEATS STOP:
    the node-controller checks nodes every ~5s → Ready flips to  Unknown  after
        NodeMonitorGracePeriod (default ~50s)
        │  the control plane then TAINTS the node:
        │     node.kubernetes.io/unreachable  (Ready=Unknown)  or  .../not-ready  (Ready=False)
        ▼
    most pods tolerate that taint for  tolerationSeconds: 300  (5 min)  →  then they're
    evicted and rescheduled onto healthy nodes
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Node condition</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>Ready</code></td><td>healthy &amp; accepting pods (<code>Unknown</code> = the control plane lost contact)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>MemoryPressure</code> · <code>DiskPressure</code> · <code>PIDPressure</code></td><td>the node is low on memory / disk / process-IDs → it begins evicting pods</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>NetworkUnavailable</code></td><td>the node's network is not correctly configured</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why the numbers matter.</strong> This is <em>why</em> a crashed node's pods take ~<strong>40–60s to be flagged</strong> and ~<strong>5 more minutes to move</strong> — never instant. A planned <code>kubectl drain</code> (Ch 30) skips all of it by evicting gracefully up front. The <code>kube-node-lease</code> heartbeat, the node conditions, and these defaults are drilled in Chapters 3 &amp; 32.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Orchestration automates operations</strong> — placement, scaling, healing, networking, and rollout, done by software instead of a human at 3 AM.</li>
                    <li><strong>Declarative, not imperative</strong> — you declare desired state; control loops drive actual state toward it, forever.</li>
                    <li><strong>The administrator owns everything below the app</strong> — install, certs/PKI, etcd, upgrades, nodes, networking, storage, and troubleshooting.</li>
                    <li><strong>The API server is the only path to etcd</strong> — every component and every <code>kubectl</code> goes through it, via authN → authZ → admission → validate → persist.</li>
                    <li><strong>The 5 core concepts frame the whole guide</strong> — cluster, control plane, worker nodes, pod, and desired-vs-actual state; every later topic is a deeper look at one of these.</li>
                    <li><strong>Every object carries the 4 required fields</strong> — <code>apiVersion</code>, <code>kind</code>, <code>metadata</code>, <code>spec</code>; <code>spec</code> is your intent, <code>status</code> is the cluster's observed reality (read-only).</li>
                    <li><strong>Level-triggered reconciliation</strong> — controllers react to the <em>current state</em>, not to events, which is why K8s self-heals reliably even after a controller itself restarts.</li>
                    <li><strong>Troubleshooting is 30% of the exam</strong> — the biggest single domain; method beats memorization.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>"Kubernetes replaces Docker."</strong> No — Kubernetes <em>orchestrates</em> containers; a runtime (containerd/CRI-O) still runs them. Only the Docker <em>shim</em> was removed (v1.24), not container images.</li>
                    <li><strong>Thinking imperatively</strong> ("start a container on node 2"). Wrong mental model — you declare <em>what</em> (3 replicas) and let K8s decide <em>where/how</em>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Confusing CKA with CKAD.</strong> CKAD builds apps; CKA builds and operates the <em>cluster</em>. etcd, kubeadm, certs, and node management are CKA-only.</li>
                    <li><strong>"Control plane" = "master node."</strong> Modern Kubernetes dropped the word <em>master</em> — the exam says <strong>control plane node</strong>. Know its four components: API server, scheduler, controller-manager, etcd.</li>
                    <li><strong>"A Pod is a container."</strong> A Pod can hold several containers sharing one network namespace &amp; storage (sidecar / init-container patterns). The Pod, not the container, is the unit K8s schedules.</li>
                    <li><strong>Desired ≠ current state.</strong> The exam constantly probes whether you grasp the reconciliation loop — controllers continuously compare the two and self-heal the gap.</li>
                    <li><strong>Editing <code>status</code> in a manifest does nothing.</strong> <code>status</code> is read-only, written by the cluster. Only <code>spec</code> is yours — you change reality by changing <em>desired</em> state, then reading <code>status</code> to see what happened.</li>
                    <li><strong>Names must be valid, not pretty.</strong> Most objects follow the DNS-Subdomain rule (lowercase <code>a–z 0–9 - .</code>); only a few — <strong>namespaces</strong> and <strong>Services</strong> (RFC 1035) — use the stricter DNS-Label rule (no dots, must <em>start with a letter</em>). So <code>easypj_web</code> (underscore) and <code>EasyPJ</code> (uppercase) are rejected <em>everywhere</em>, while <code>9web</code> is fine as a Deployment but rejected as a namespace/Service name.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Think in desired state.</strong> When something's wrong, ask "what's declared vs what's actually running?" — that's the whole troubleshooting model.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl explain</code> is your in-exam manual.</strong> <code>kubectl explain pod.spec --recursive</code> beats hunting the docs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Read events first.</strong> <code>kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp</code> and <code>describe</code> usually name the cause before you guess.</li>
                    <li><strong>Architecture quick-recall mnemonic:</strong> <code>kube-apiserver</code> → front door · <code>etcd</code> → brain · <code>kube-scheduler</code> → which-node · <code>kube-controller-manager</code> → reconciliation. All four run as <strong>static pods</strong> in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — edit a file there and the kubelet recreates the pod.</li>
                    <li><strong>Prefer <code>kubectl apply -f</code> over <code>create</code>/<code>replace</code>.</strong> It's idempotent and re-runnable — the safe default under exam time pressure, and it won't error if the object already exists.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

