                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Sort "admin-owns vs dev-owns": etcd backup · a Deployment · RBAC · a ConfigMap · a node upgrade.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>🔧 CKA admin owns</th><th>👩‍💻 CKAD dev owns</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>etcd backup</td><td>a Deployment</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>RBAC</td><td>a ConfigMap</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>a node upgrade</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>RBAC is borderline — devs may <em>request</em> roles, but defining and granting cluster access is an admin responsibility. The rule of thumb: if it's <em>below the app</em>, it's CKA.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the four required top-level fields of every manifest, and explain the difference between <code>spec</code> and <code>status</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>apiVersion</code>, <code>kind</code>, <code>metadata</code>, and <code>spec</code>. <strong><code>spec</code></strong> is <em>your</em> desired state (what you declare); <strong><code>status</code></strong> is the cluster's observed reality, written by controllers and read-only to you.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the 4 required fields + spec vs status</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1     <span class="comment"># ① group + version</span>
kind: Deployment        <span class="comment"># ② object type</span>
metadata:               <span class="comment"># ③ identity (name, namespace, labels)</span>
  name: easypj-web
spec:                   <span class="comment"># ④ YOUR desired state</span>
  replicas: 3
status:                 <span class="comment"># ← the cluster writes this (read-only to you)</span>
  readyReplicas: 3</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Omit a field (or mis-case <code>kind</code>) and the apiserver rejects the manifest before it reaches etcd. The reconciliation loop is simply "make <code>status</code> match <code>spec</code>" — which is why editing <code>status</code> does nothing, and why <em>reading</em> <code>status</code> is how you troubleshoot.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why are <code>easypj_web</code>, <code>EasyPJ</code>, and <code>9web</code> all rejected as object names? Give the two naming rules.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Names must be valid DNS. <strong>DNS Subdomain</strong> (most objects — Pods, Deployments, Nodes… — ≤253 chars): lowercase <code>a–z 0–9 - .</code>, start/end alphanumeric. <strong>DNS Label</strong> (namespaces; Services use RFC 1035, ≤63 chars): lowercase <code>a–z 0–9 -</code>, <strong>no dots</strong>, must <strong>start with a letter</strong>.</p>
                        <p>So <code>easypj_web</code> ❌ (underscore), <code>EasyPJ</code> ❌ (uppercase), <code>9web</code> ❌ (starts with a digit, for Label-rule objects).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Used by</th><th>✅ valid</th><th>❌ invalid</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>DNS Subdomain</td><td>most objects (Deployments, ConfigMaps…)</td><td><code>easypj-web</code>, <code>app.prod</code></td><td><code>easypj_web</code>, <code>EasyPJ</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>DNS Label</td><td>namespaces, Services (RFC 1035)</td><td><code>easypj-prod</code></td><td><code>my.ns</code>, <code>9ns</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Names become DNS names — a Service <code>easypj</code> resolves at <code>easypj.&lt;ns&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>. It's a <em>validation</em> error, not a style choice: the apiserver refuses the object outright.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between the <em>core</em> API group and a <em>named</em> group, and what do <code>v1alpha1</code>, <code>v1beta1</code>, and <code>v1</code> signal?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>apiVersion</th><th>Group → path</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>v1</code></td><td>core → <code>/api/v1</code></td><td>Pod, Service, ConfigMap, Node</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>apps/v1</code></td><td>named → <code>/apis/apps/v1</code></td><td>Deployment, ReplicaSet, DaemonSet</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>batch/v1</code></td><td>named → <code>/apis/batch/v1</code></td><td>Job, CronJob</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p>Stability ladder: <strong>alpha</strong> (<code>v1alpha1</code>, off by default, may vanish) → <strong>beta</strong> (<code>v1beta1</code>, may still change) → <strong>GA/stable</strong> (<code>v1</code>, backward-compatible).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl api-resources</code> maps every kind → its group, version, and namespaced-ness. A GA <code>v1</code> API is never removed within a major version; a deprecated beta drops after ~9 months / 3 releases. Depend on stable versions.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Distinguish <strong>labels</strong>, <strong>selectors</strong>, and <strong>annotations</strong>. Why is a label typo the #1 cause of a Service with no endpoints, and what do <code>ownerReferences</code> do?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Labels</strong> = key/value tags <em>for selection</em>. <strong>Selectors</strong> = a query over labels (how a Service/Deployment/NetworkPolicy finds its pods). <strong>Annotations</strong> = metadata <em>not</em> used for selection (URLs, checksums, tool config).</p>
                        <p>A Service matches pods <em>only</em> by label selector, so if the pod labels ≠ the selector, the Service gets <strong>zero endpoints</strong> — no error, it just selects nothing. <strong><code>ownerReferences</code></strong> point child → parent (Pod → ReplicaSet → Deployment) and drive <em>cascading deletion</em>.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Labels wire the Service — a one-char typo = 0 endpoints</div>
<pre>
  ✅ selector app=easypj  MATCHES          ❌ selector app=easypj  vs pods app=easypj-web
        │                                        │ matches nothing
        ▼                                        ▼
  📦 app=easypj  📦 app=easypj             📦 app=easypj-web  📦 app=easypj-web
  → EndpointSlice has the pod IPs          → 0 endpoints → the Service answers nobody
</pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Labels are load-bearing wiring, not decoration. When something is mysteriously unreachable, compare <code>kubectl get pods --show-labels</code> against the selector. Deleting a Deployment garbage-collects its ReplicaSet and Pods <em>because</em> of the ownerReference chain.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does Kubernetes need a single source of truth (etcd) plus consensus, and how does the <em>control plane vs data plane</em> split speed up troubleshooting?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Because it's a <strong>distributed system</strong>: separate machines share no clock or memory and fail independently, so state must live in <em>one</em> authoritative store (etcd), and <strong>consensus</strong> (Raft — a majority must agree) prevents split-brain. It's also <strong>level-triggered</strong>, so a dropped message is corrected on the next pass.</p>
                        <p>The <strong>control plane</strong> (apiserver, etcd, scheduler, controllers) <em>decides</em>; the <strong>data plane</strong> (your pods + kube-proxy/CNI) <em>does the work</em>.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>🟥 Control plane (decides)</th><th>🟦 Data plane (does the work)</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>apiserver · etcd · scheduler · controllers</td><td>your pods · kube-proxy · CNI</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>symptom: "can't create / nothing self-heals"</td><td>symptom: "app errors / pods unreachable"</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>if it's down, running apps keep serving</td><td>this <em>is</em> the app — user-visible</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Triage by plane first: "the app errors / pods can't reach each other" = data plane; "I can't create anything / nothing self-heals" = control plane. Running pods survive a control-plane outage (they run on reflex), which is why an etcd/apiserver failure is invisible to users but paralyzing to admins.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 1 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (16)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — 16 drills that mirror how the CKA actually asks: a broken state, and you produce the fix with real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A freshly built cluster has its control plane up, but worker nodes show <code>NotReady</code>. You SSH to a worker and find the node agent isn't running. Which component is it, and how do you recover?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>kubelet</strong> — the per-node agent that (1) registers the node with the API server and (2) starts/monitors the pods assigned to it. No kubelet ⇒ the node can't report in ⇒ <code>NotReady</code>.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the worker node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Why is it down? Read the logs first.</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -30

<span class="comment"># Start it and make it survive reboots</span>
sudo systemctl enable --now kubelet

<span class="comment"># Verify from the control plane</span>
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>On the CKA, <strong>NotReady → always check the kubelet first</strong>: <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> and <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>. Common root causes are a stopped service, a bad <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>, swap enabled, a missing CNI, or a down container runtime (Chapter 32 drills all of these).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the four control-plane components and each one's primary job. Where do they run on a kubeadm cluster?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>kube-apiserver</code></td><td>Front door — REST API, authn/authz/admission; the <em>only</em> component that talks to etcd</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>etcd</code></td><td>Distributed key-value store — the single source of truth for all cluster state</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kube-scheduler</code></td><td>Assigns unscheduled pods to nodes (resources, affinity, taints/tolerations)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kube-controller-manager</code></td><td>Runs reconciliation loops (Deployment, ReplicaSet, Node… controllers)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>On a kubeadm cluster all four run as <strong>static pods</strong> defined in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> on the control-plane node — edit a manifest there and the kubelet recreates the pod. The topology is a <strong>star</strong>: every other component (scheduler, controller-manager, kubelet) talks <em>only</em> through the API server, never directly to etcd.</p>
                            <p class="mb-0">There's an optional <strong>fifth</strong> component — the <code>cloud-controller-manager</code> — on managed/cloud clusters; it runs the cloud-specific loops (provision LoadBalancers, manage node lifecycle &amp; routes) so the core four stay cloud-agnostic.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Deployment specifies <code>replicas: 3</code>. You manually delete one of its pods. What happens, and which component is responsible?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>ReplicaSet controller</strong> (inside kube-controller-manager) sees current=2 ≠ desired=3 and immediately creates a replacement — count returns to 3.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">watch self-healing happen</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v2 --replicas=3
kubectl get pods                 <span class="comment"># 3 running</span>
kubectl delete pod easypj-xxxxx-yyyyy
kubectl get pods                 <span class="comment"># still 3 — a new one was created!</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The declarative model in action: you declared "3," and the control loop (Observe → Diff → Act) continuously enforces it. Controllers never stop watching — this self-healing is heavily tested. (To remove the pod permanently you must change the <em>desired</em> state — scale down or delete the Deployment.)</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between a control-plane node and a worker node? Which components run on each — and why does the kubelet run on <em>both</em>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Control-plane node:</strong> the management components — <code>kube-apiserver</code>, <code>etcd</code>, <code>kube-scheduler</code>, <code>kube-controller-manager</code> (as static pods).</p>
                        <p><strong>Worker node:</strong> runs your workloads — needs <code>kubelet</code>, <code>kube-proxy</code>, and a <strong>container runtime</strong> (containerd/CRI-O).</p>
                        <p><strong>Both:</strong> kubelet and kube-proxy run on control-plane nodes too — because the control-plane components <em>are</em> static pods, and only the kubelet can run static pods.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>🧠 control-plane node</th><th>💪 worker node</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>apiserver · etcd · scheduler · controller-manager</td><td>✅ (as static pods)</td><td>—</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>kubelet</td><td>✅ (it <em>runs</em> those static pods)</td><td>✅ (runs your workloads)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>kube-proxy · container runtime</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>your app pods (easypj)</td><td>❌ blocked by the <code>NoSchedule</code> taint</td><td>✅</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Say "control-plane node," not "master." By default control-plane nodes carry the taint <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</code> so ordinary workloads (like easypj) don't land there. That taint — and how to tolerate/remove it — is exactly the kind of thing the scheduling chapter (14) covers.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You run <code>kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml</code>. Trace the request through the architecture — which components act, and in what order?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <ol>
                            <li><strong>kubectl</strong> → HTTPS request to the API server</li>
                            <li><strong>kube-apiserver</strong> → authenticate → authorize (RBAC) → admission → validate</li>
                            <li><strong>etcd</strong> → API server persists the Deployment object</li>
                            <li><strong>Deployment controller</strong> → creates a ReplicaSet</li>
                            <li><strong>ReplicaSet controller</strong> → creates Pod objects (<code>nodeName</code> empty)</li>
                            <li><strong>kube-scheduler</strong> → binds each pod to a node</li>
                            <li><strong>kubelet</strong> (on the node) → pulls image via the runtime, starts the container; CNI assigns an IP</li>
                        </ol>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>It's <strong>asynchronous and event-driven</strong> — no single orchestrator. Each component watches the API server for changes relevant to it and acts independently. That loose coupling is what makes Kubernetes resilient: if the scheduler is briefly down, pods just queue as <code>Pending</code> and get placed when it returns. This flow is the backbone of every troubleshooting scenario (Part 9).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>etcd</code>, why is backing it up critical, and what command takes a snapshot?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>etcd</code> is a distributed, consistent key-value store holding <strong>all</strong> cluster state — every Pod, Service, Secret, ConfigMap, RBAC rule, and CRD. Lose it without a backup and the whole cluster is gone (an etcd snapshot effectively <em>is</em> a cluster backup).</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">guaranteed exam task</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Snapshot (flags come from the etcd static-pod manifest)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /tmp/etcd-backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Verify it's readable</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /tmp/etcd-backup.db --write-table</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Three things the exam checks: (1) use <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code>; (2) supply the three TLS flags — read the exact paths from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code>; (3) the endpoint is usually <code>https://127.0.0.1:2379</code>. Full backup <em>and restore</em> is drilled end-to-end in Chapter 29.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does <code>kube-proxy</code> do? If it stops on a node, what breaks — and what still works?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>kube-proxy</code> programs each node's dataplane (iptables / IPVS) so traffic to a <strong>Service</strong> VIP is load-balanced to the backing pods. It watches the API server for Service &amp; EndpointSlice objects.</p>
                        <p><strong>If it's down on a node:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>❌ Service (ClusterIP / NodePort) traffic stops reaching pods</li>
                            <li>❌ DNS via CoreDNS may fail (CoreDNS is reached <em>through</em> a Service)</li>
                            <li>✅ Pod-to-pod by pod IP still works (that's the CNI, not kube-proxy)</li>
                            <li>✅ <code>kubectl exec</code> / <code>logs</code> still work (API server → kubelet directly)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">two separate dataplanes — which one broke?</div>
<pre>
  📦 pod ──by pod IP──▶ 📦 pod          🌐 CNI's job        ✅ still works
  📦 pod ──▶ 🔌 Service VIP ──▶ 📦 pod   🔀 kube-proxy's job  ❌ broken (rules not programmed)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  triage: pods ping each other but Services time out  ⇒  suspect kube-proxy
          pods can't even reach other pods by IP      ⇒  suspect the CNI
</pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Key distinction for troubleshooting: <strong>kube-proxy = Service networking</strong>; the <strong>CNI plugin = pod-to-pod networking</strong>. So if pods can ping each other by IP but Services are unreachable, suspect kube-proxy (Chapter 34 makes this a 4-layer method).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the Container Runtime Interface (CRI)? Name two CRI runtimes. Why was Docker's direct support removed in v1.24 — and do my images still work?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>CRI</strong> is the standard interface the kubelet uses to talk to <em>any</em> container runtime without runtime-specific code. Two runtimes: <strong>containerd</strong> (most common, extracted from Docker) and <strong>CRI-O</strong> (purpose-built, common on OpenShift).</p>
                        <p>Docker didn't implement CRI directly, so K8s shipped a <code>dockershim</code> translation layer — extra complexity for no gain (Docker uses containerd internally anyway). v1.24 removed the shim. <strong>Your images still work</strong> — they're OCI images, a universal format; only the <em>runtime plumbing</em> changed.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node-level: use crictl, not docker</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">crictl ps                 <span class="comment"># list running containers</span>
crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;
crictl inspect &lt;container-id&gt;</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>After v1.24 you debug containers on a node with <code>crictl</code> (talks to containerd via CRI), not <code>docker</code>. This matters in Chapter 32 when the API server is down and you must diagnose from the node itself.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Imperative vs declarative management — what's the difference, and what's the recommended CKA workflow?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Imperative</strong> = tell K8s the steps (<code>kubectl create/run/expose</code>) — fast, one-off, no YAML. <strong>Declarative</strong> = describe the end state in YAML + <code>kubectl apply -f</code> — idempotent, version-controllable, repeatable.</p>
                        <p><strong>CKA gold standard:</strong> generate YAML imperatively, then apply it declaratively:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative generation → declarative apply</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Scaffold YAML instantly — don't write it from scratch</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v2 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; easypj.yaml

<span class="comment"># Edit in vim (add probes, resources…), then apply</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj.yaml</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Speed wins the exam. <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> scaffolds correct YAML in seconds; you tweak the bits a one-liner can't express (multi-container, volumes, probes). Pure <code>kubectl create</code> is fine for trivial objects, but the hybrid is the habit to build.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're asked to verify overall cluster health under exam time pressure. Write the command sequence and say what each reveals.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">top-down health check</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get nodes                       <span class="comment"># 1. all nodes Ready?</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system         <span class="comment"># 2. control-plane + CNI + CoreDNS Running?</span>
kubectl cluster-info                    <span class="comment"># 3. API + CoreDNS endpoints reachable</span>
kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -20   <span class="comment"># 4. recent failures</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep -v Running   <span class="comment"># 5. anything CrashLoop/Error/Pending?</span>
kubectl run dns-test --image=busybox:1.28 --rm -it --restart=Never \
  -- nslookup kubernetes               <span class="comment"># 6. DNS resolving?</span>
<span class="comment"># 7. if a node is NotReady: ssh in → systemctl status kubelet → journalctl -u kubelet</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A systematic <strong>top-down diagnosis</strong>: nodes → control-plane pods → events → DNS → node logs. Look for all nodes <code>Ready</code>, all kube-system pods <code>Running</code>, no <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>, and DNS resolving <code>kubernetes</code>. If a static-pod component is down, check its manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and that node's kubelet logs — the exact method Part 9 turns into reflex.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate claims "the control plane is scheduled by Kubernetes like any other pod." Disprove it on a kubeadm control-plane node — where are the API server, scheduler, controller-manager and etcd actually defined?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>They are <strong>static pods</strong>, run by the kubelet straight from files on disk — <em>not</em> scheduled through the API server. That's how the control plane can boot before any control plane exists (the bootstrap paradox).</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the control-plane node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The manifests the kubelet runs directly</span>
ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># etcd.yaml  kube-apiserver.yaml  kube-controller-manager.yaml  kube-scheduler.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># They surface as read-only "mirror pods" (note the -&lt;nodename&gt; suffix)</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep -E 'apiserver|scheduler|controller|etcd'

<span class="comment"># Edit a manifest → the kubelet auto-recreates that pod (no apply needed)</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>You manage these by editing their <em>files</em>, not with <code>kubectl edit</code> (the mirror pod is read-only). This is why "change an apiserver flag" on the exam means "edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code> and wait for the kubelet to restart it" — drilled in Chapter 12.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>kubectl</code> commands hang with a connection timeout — the API server looks down. Since <code>kubectl</code> is useless, how do you diagnose from the control-plane node itself?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Drop to the <strong>node</strong> and use the container runtime directly via <code>crictl</code> (it talks to containerd, no API server involved), then read the apiserver pod's logs and its static-pod manifest.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">API server down — diagnose on the node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Is the apiserver container even running?</span>
sudo crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver

<span class="comment"># Read its logs directly from the runtime</span>
sudo crictl logs $(sudo crictl ps -aq --name kube-apiserver | head -1)

<span class="comment"># Classic cause: a typo in the static-pod manifest (bad flag / invalid YAML)</span>
sudo less /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

<span class="comment"># Is the kubelet healthy? It's what runs the static pods</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet; sudo journalctl -u kubelet | tail -30</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The reflex for the whole "control plane is down" family: <strong>no API server ⇒ no <code>kubectl</code> ⇒ debug at the node with <code>crictl</code> + <code>journalctl</code></strong>. A malformed edit to the apiserver manifest is the classic self-inflicted outage — Chapter 32 makes recovering from it routine.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Classify each symptom as a <strong>control-plane</strong> or <strong>data-plane</strong> problem, and say why the split speeds up your fix: (a) easypj returns HTTP 500s; (b) <code>kubectl apply</code> is refused; (c) a deleted pod isn't recreated; (d) two pods can't reach each other by IP.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Plane</th><th>Where to look</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>(a) easypj returns 500s</td><td>🟦 Data plane</td><td>the app/container — <code>logs</code>, DB connectivity, config</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>(b) <code>kubectl apply</code> refused</td><td>🟥 Control plane</td><td>apiserver auth / RBAC / admission (or it's down)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>(c) deleted pod not recreated</td><td>🟥 Control plane</td><td>controller-manager (the reconcile loops)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>(d) pod-to-pod by IP fails</td><td>🟦 Data plane</td><td>the CNI plugin / node networking</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The split halves your search space instantly: control-plane = "I can't <em>manage</em> or <em>reconcile</em>," data-plane = "the workload itself or its packets." Running pods survive a control-plane outage (they run on reflex), which is why (a) and (d) keep failing even when the API server is healthy — and why (b)/(c) can break while the app keeps serving traffic.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You run <code>kubectl delete ns easypj-test</code> and it sits in <code>Terminating</code> for minutes. What mechanism is holding it, how do you investigate, and what is the <em>safe</em> fix?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A <strong>finalizer</strong> is blocking deletion: the namespace has <code>deletionTimestamp</code> set, but the API server won't remove it until the responsible controller finishes cleanup and drops the finalizer. Usually a leftover resource whose controller is gone or broken.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">investigate first, force only as last resort</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. What's still inside, and which finalizers are set?</span>
kubectl get all -n easypj-test
kubectl get ns easypj-test -o yaml | grep -A5 -E 'finalizers|conditions'   <span class="comment"># names the stuck resource</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. SAFE fix: delete/repair the offending resource so its finalizer clears itself.</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. LAST RESORT (only if its data is already gone): force-remove the finalizer</span>
kubectl get ns easypj-test -o json | jq 'del(.spec.finalizers)' \
  | kubectl replace --raw /api/v1/namespaces/easypj-test/finalize -f -</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Finalizers exist so deletion is <em>orderly</em>, not instant — so the right instinct is to fix the blocked cleanup, not to rip the finalizer out (which can orphan real resources). Force-clearing is a genuine last resort. This exact scenario recurs in Chapter 32.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You expose easypj with a Service, but <code>curl</code> to its ClusterIP hangs and <code>kubectl get endpoints easypj</code> shows <code>&lt;none&gt;</code> — yet the pods are <code>Running</code>. What's the most likely cause, and how do you confirm &amp; fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A <strong>label-selector mismatch</strong>: the Service's <code>selector</code> doesn't match the pods' <code>labels</code>, so no pod IPs land in its EndpointSlice. The Service looks healthy but points at nothing — the single most common Service bug.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">confirm the mismatch, then fix it</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Confirm: no endpoints even though pods are Running</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj              <span class="comment"># ENDPOINTS: &lt;none&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Compare what the Service SELECTS vs how the pods are LABELLED</span>
kubectl get svc easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}{"\n"}'   <span class="comment"># app=easypj-web</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels                                <span class="comment"># app=easypj  ← mismatch!</span>

<span class="comment"># 3a. Fix by correcting the Service selector to match the pods…</span>
kubectl patch svc easypj -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"app":"easypj"}}}'
<span class="comment"># 3b. …or relabel the pods to match the selector</span>
kubectl label pod -l app=easypj app=easypj-web --overwrite

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify the endpoints now populate</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj              <span class="comment"># now lists the pod IPs:port</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Services find pods <em>only</em> by label selector (§1.7). A one-character difference silently yields zero endpoints — no error, because nothing is technically broken, the selector just matches nothing. <code>get endpoints</code> (or <code>get endpointslices</code>) is the fastest confirmation, and <code>--show-labels</code> reveals the mismatch instantly. This exact bug recurs in Chapter 18.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Mid-exam you can't recall whether a Deployment's field is <code>spec.strategy</code> or <code>spec.updateStrategy</code>, nor whether CronJob is namespaced. You may open <code>kubernetes.io/docs</code> — but a faster tool is already on the cluster. What do you run?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use <code>kubectl explain</code> (the live schema) and <code>kubectl api-resources</code> — no browser needed, and far quicker than hunting the docs.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">discover the API from the terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Field-level docs — Deployment uses spec.strategy (StatefulSet/DaemonSet use updateStrategy)</span>
kubectl explain deployment.spec.strategy
kubectl explain deployment.spec --recursive | less   <span class="comment"># the whole field tree</span>

<span class="comment"># Is CronJob namespaced? What's its short name &amp; apiVersion?</span>
kubectl api-resources | grep -i cronjob
<span class="comment"># cronjobs   cj   batch/v1   true   CronJob     ← NAMESPACED = true</span>

<span class="comment"># All served group/versions if you're unsure of an apiVersion</span>
kubectl api-versions | grep batch</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl explain</code> reads the cluster's own OpenAPI schema, so it's always correct for that version — the fastest way to settle a field name or check nesting without leaving the terminal. <code>api-resources</code> answers "namespaced?", "short name?", and "which apiVersion?" in one line. Building this reflex saves minutes per task on the real exam.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>


