            <section class="section-block" id="s3-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.1 Cluster Architecture Overview</h3>
                <p>A Kubernetes cluster is two layers: a <strong>control plane</strong> (the brain — makes every decision) and one or more <strong>worker nodes</strong> (the muscle — actually run the workloads). As a CKA admin you install, configure, and repair <em>both</em>. Everything else in this chapter is a zoom-in on one of these two halves.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The complete cluster — brain + muscle</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────────────────── ☸️ CLUSTER ───────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  🧠 CONTROL PLANE  (decides)                                                            │
   │   ┌──────────────┐ ┌────────────┐ ┌────────────────┐ ┌────────────────────────────┐    │
   │   │ 📡 apiserver  │ │ 🗄️ etcd     │ │ 📋 scheduler    │ │ 🔄 controller-manager       │    │
   │   │ the ONLY door │ │ ALL state  │ │ places pods    │ │ reconcile loops             │    │
   │   │ :6443         │ │ :2379/2380 │ │ filter→score→  │ │ (node · ReplicaSet ·        │    │
   │   │ authN/Z/admit │ │ (Raft)     │ │ bind           │ │  endpoint · serviceaccount) │    │
   │   └──────┬───────┘ └────────────┘ └────────────────┘ └────────────────────────────┘    │
   │          │   ⚠️ ONLY the apiserver talks to etcd; every component &amp; kubectl goes        │
   │          │      THROUGH the apiserver (the single choke point).                          │
   │  ────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │
   │  💪 WORKER NODES  (run)                                                                  │
