            <!-- ─────────── §3.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.2 Control-Plane Components — Deep Dive</h3>
                <p>These are the components you maintain, troubleshoot, and restore. For each, learn three things: its <strong>job</strong>, its <strong>config location</strong>, and — most exam-relevant — <strong>what breaks when it's gone</strong>.</p>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🍽️ In plain English — the cluster as a restaurant</h5>
                    <p>If the components feel abstract, map them to a busy restaurant — it makes every role (and every failure) intuitive:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Restaurant</th><th>Kubernetes</th><th>So if it's gone…</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>🛎️ the <strong>front desk</strong> — every order/request goes through it</td><td>📡 <strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td>nobody can place or change orders (no kubectl)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>📖 the <strong>order ledger</strong> — the single written record of every order</td><td>🗄️ <strong>etcd</strong></td><td>lose the ledger = lose the whole restaurant's state</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🪑 the <strong>host</strong> who seats each party at the best table</td><td>📋 <strong>scheduler</strong></td><td>new parties wait at the door (pods Pending)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🧑‍💼 the <strong>floor manager</strong> watching everything &amp; fixing gaps</td><td>🔄 <strong>controller-manager</strong></td><td>no one refills empty tables (no self-healing)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>👨‍🍳 the <strong>line cook</strong> at each station who actually makes the food</td><td>🤖 <strong>kubelet</strong> (on each node)</td><td>that station stops cooking (node NotReady)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🏃 the <strong>runner</strong> delivering plates to the right table</td><td>🌐 <strong>kube-proxy</strong></td><td>food's made but never reaches diners (Services break)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="mb-0">Notice the front desk (apiserver) is the only one who reads/writes the ledger (etcd) — exactly like the real cluster. Keep this picture; the rest of §3.2 just adds detail to each role.</p>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2a">a) 📡 kube-apiserver — the front door</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📡 kube-apiserver</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>only</strong> entry point. Every <code>kubectl</code> call, every kubelet, every controller talks to it — and it's the only component that reads/writes etcd.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔐</span> Pipeline</div><div class="anatomy-value">Each request: <strong>authentication</strong> (certs/tokens) → <strong>authorization</strong> (RBAC) → <strong>admission</strong> (validate/mutate) → persist to etcd.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📂</span> Config</div><div class="anatomy-value">Static-pod manifest: <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code></div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔌</span> Port</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>6443</code> (secure HTTPS)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💀</span> If it fails</div><div class="anatomy-value">No <code>kubectl</code>, no new pods, nodes can't report status. <strong>Existing pods keep running</strong> — the kubelet is independent.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2b">b) 🗄️ etcd — the cluster database</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🗄️ etcd — the brain's memory</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">A distributed key-value store holding <strong>ALL</strong> cluster state under <code>/registry/…</code> — pods, services, secrets, RBAC, everything.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> Consensus</div><div class="anatomy-value">Uses <strong>Raft</strong> — a quorum (majority) must agree on every write. That's why HA clusters run 3 or 5 etcd members.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔌</span> Ports</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>2379</code> (client) · <code>2380</code> (peer)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📝</span> CKA</div><div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Backup &amp; restore is directly tested</strong> — know <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> / <code>restore</code> cold.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 etcd lost = cluster lost</h5>
                    <p>Lose etcd without a backup and your <em>entire</em> cluster — every pod, service, secret, and RBAC rule — is gone. This is precisely why "back up etcd" is the #1 admin task and a guaranteed exam item (Ch 29).</p>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — etcd backup (CKA skill)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># snapshot (cert paths come from /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># verify it's readable</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2c">c) 📋 kube-scheduler — the matchmaker</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📋 kube-scheduler</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">Watches for Pods with no node assigned, evaluates every node, and binds the pod to the best fit.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔍</span> Process</div><div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Filter</strong> (which nodes <em>can</em> run it?) → <strong>Score</strong> (which is best?) → <strong>Bind</strong> (assign the winner).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📊</span> Considers</div><div class="anatomy-value">CPU/mem requests, nodeSelector, affinity/anti-affinity, taints/tolerations, PV topology, spread constraints.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💀</span> If it fails</div><div class="anatomy-value">New pods stay <code>Pending</code> (existing pods unaffected). Spot it: <code>kubectl get pods -A | grep Pending</code>.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Watch</h5><p>See a Pod with empty <code>nodeName</code></p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>Filter</h5><p>Drop nodes that can't fit (resources, taints, affinity)</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>Score</h5><p>Rank the survivors (spread, balance, preferences)</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Bind</h5><p>Assign the top node → write to etcd → kubelet takes over</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2d">d) 🔄 kube-controller-manager — the watchdog</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🔄 kube-controller-manager (many controllers, one process)</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🖥️</span> Node ctrl</div><div class="anatomy-value">Watches node heartbeats; marks a node <code>NotReady</code> after ~40s of silence, evicts its pods after ~5 min.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> ReplicaSet ctrl</div><div class="anatomy-value">Keeps the pod count right — spec says 3, only 2 running → creates 1 more.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔗</span> Endpoint ctrl</div><div class="anatomy-value">Wires Services to Pods by maintaining the EndpointSlice (the list of ready pod IPs).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👤</span> ServiceAccount ctrl</div><div class="anatomy-value">Creates the default ServiceAccount for each new namespace.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💀</span> If it fails</div><div class="anatomy-value">No self-healing: crashed pods aren't replaced, scaling/endpoints stall, dead nodes aren't acted on.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">A controller loop — the heart of Kubernetes</div>
<pre>
   ┌─────────── CONTROLLER LOOP (runs forever) ───────────┐
   │  👁️ OBSERVE  ──▶  🧮 COMPARE  ──▶  🛠️ ACT  ──┐         │
   │  actual state    desired vs       reconcile          │
   │  "2 pods"        actual "want 3"  "create 1 pod"      │
   │      ▲                                    │           │
   │      └────────────────────────────────────┘           │
   └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   easypj ReplicaSet: spec replicas=3, see 2 → create 1 → repeat
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2e">e) ☁️ cloud-controller-manager — the cloud glue (optional)</h4>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Only on cloud clusters</h5>
                    <p>The <strong>cloud-controller-manager</strong> integrates a cloud provider: provisioning LoadBalancers, managing routes, and handling node lifecycle (detecting deleted VMs). Bare-metal/kubeadm clusters don't run it. It exists so the core controllers stay cloud-agnostic — another example of Kubernetes keeping vendor specifics behind a plug.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.3 Worker-Node Components — Deep Dive</h3>
                <p>Every node — worker <em>and</em> control-plane — runs three things that actually make containers happen: the <strong>kubelet</strong> (agent), <strong>kube-proxy</strong> (service routing), and a <strong>container runtime</strong>. These are where "run" problems live.</p>

                <h4>a) 🤖 kubelet — the node agent</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🤖 kubelet</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">Receives PodSpecs from the API server, drives the runtime to start/stop containers, runs probes, and reports status back.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⚡</span> Key fact</div><div class="anatomy-value"><strong>The kubelet is NOT a pod</strong> — it's a <code>systemd</code> service on the node. Check it with <code>systemctl status kubelet</code>.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📂</span> Config</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔌</span> Port</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>10250</code> (HTTPS API)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔍</span> Troubleshoot</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code> — most common causes: expired cert, runtime down, swap on, or cgroup-driver mismatch.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>b) 🌐 kube-proxy — the network plumber</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🌐 kube-proxy</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">Programs each node's dataplane so a <strong>Service VIP</strong> load-balances to the right backing Pod IPs.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⚙️</span> Modes</div><div class="anatomy-value"><strong>iptables</strong> (still the upstream default), <strong>nftables</strong> (GA since v1.33, needs kernel ≥5.13 — the recommended target for new clusters), <strong>IPVS</strong> (was the faster-at-scale option, but <strong>deprecated as of v1.35</strong> in favor of nftables — don't build new deployments on it).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> Runs as</div><div class="anatomy-value">A <strong>DaemonSet</strong> (one pod per node) in <code>kube-system</code> — unlike the kubelet, it <em>is</em> a pod.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💡</span> Remember</div><div class="anatomy-value">kube-proxy handles <em>Service</em> networking; the <strong>CNI</strong> handles <em>pod-to-pod</em> networking. Pods ping by IP but Services fail → suspect kube-proxy.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>c) 🐋 container runtime</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐋</div><h5>containerd</h5><p>The default for kubeadm, EKS, AKS, GKE. CNCF-graduated. Drives the actual container lifecycle via the CRI.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔴</div><h5>CRI-O</h5><p>Kubernetes-native, minimal. Used by OpenShift; versioned in lockstep with K8s.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA triage: node shows NotReady? Check in this order</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>systemctl status kubelet</code> — is the agent running?</li>
                        <li><code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> — what's the error? (cert / swap / CNI / cgroup)</li>
                        <li><code>systemctl status containerd</code> — is the runtime up?</li>
                        <li><code>crictl ps</code> — can the runtime even list containers?</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Everything the kubelet Actually Does</h4>
                <p>"The kubelet runs containers" undersells it — it's the busiest agent in the cluster and the prime suspect in most node problems. Knowing its full job list tells you exactly what can break:</p>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🤖 the kubelet's responsibilities</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📝</span> Register</div><div class="anatomy-value">Registers the node with the apiserver on startup (creates the Node object, advertises capacity/labels).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💓</span> Heartbeat</div><div class="anatomy-value">Renews a <strong>node Lease</strong> (in <code>kube-node-lease</code>) every few seconds so the control plane knows it's alive — stop renewing → NotReady.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👁️</span> Watch</div><div class="anatomy-value">Watches the apiserver for Pods bound to <em>its</em> node (and the static-pod dir on disk).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🐋</span> Run</div><div class="anatomy-value">Drives the CRI to pull images and start/stop containers; sets up the pod sandbox.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💾</span> Mount</div><div class="anatomy-value">Attaches/mounts volumes via the CSI before starting containers (emptyDir, PVC, configMap/secret).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🩺</span> Probe</div><div class="anatomy-value">Runs liveness/readiness/startup probes → restarts dead containers, gates traffic on readiness.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📊</span> Report</div><div class="anatomy-value">Reports pod &amp; node status back to the apiserver; exposes stats (cAdvisor) for <code>kubectl top</code>.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🧹</span> Evict</div><div class="anatomy-value">Under node pressure (disk/memory) it evicts pods and applies the matching taint (e.g. DiskPressure).</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why this matters: the kubelet is "trusted" but standalone</h5>
                    <p>Because the kubelet reads its work from the apiserver but acts <em>independently</em>, two important things follow: (1) if the <strong>apiserver dies, running pods keep running</strong> — the kubelet doesn't need it to keep containers alive; and (2) the kubelet can run <strong>static pods</strong> with no control plane at all, which is exactly how kubeadm bootstraps the cluster (§3.6). It's the one component that bridges "declared in the API" and "actually running on Linux."</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.4 How a Pod Is Born — The Full Journey</h3>
                <p>This is the single most important sequence in Kubernetes — every troubleshooting task assumes it. Watch what happens after <code>kubectl apply -f easypj-deploy.yaml</code>, and notice that <strong>no single component runs the show</strong>: each one reconciles its own slice and the pod emerges.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>kubectl → apiserver</h5><p>HTTPS request to <code>:6443</code> with a client cert.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>authN → authZ → admit</h5><p>Who are you? (cert) · Allowed? (RBAC) · Valid? (admission).</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>apiserver → etcd</h5><p>The Deployment object is persisted — desired state recorded.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>controllers</h5><p>Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod objects (still nodeless).</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>scheduler</h5><p>Sees a nodeless Pod → filter → score → binds to a node.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><h5>kubelet</h5><p>On that node: pulls <code>easypj:v2</code>, starts the container, CNI gives it an IP.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">7</div><h5>kube-proxy</h5><p>Programs Service routing to the new Pod IP.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">8</div><h5>Running ✅</h5><p>kubelet reports status → apiserver → etcd; the loops keep watching.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubectl apply -f easypj-deploy.yaml → a Running pod</div>
<pre>
   👩‍💻 kubectl apply
        │ ① HTTPS :6443 + client cert
        ▼
   📡 apiserver ──② authN → authZ(RBAC) → admission──┐
        │ ③ persist Deployment                       │
        ▼                                            │
   🗄️ etcd  (desired state)  ◀───────────────────────┘
        │ ④ controller-manager: Deployment → ReplicaSet → 3 Pod objects (Pending)
        ▼
   📋 scheduler ── ⑤ filter → score → BIND each Pod → a node
        ▼
   🤖 kubelet (on the node) ── ⑥ 🐋 pull easypj:v2 · start container · 🌐 CNI assigns IP
        ▼
   🌐 kube-proxy ── ⑦ program Service routing to the new Pod IP
        ▼
   📦 easypj Running :8000 ── ⑧ status ─▶ apiserver ─▶ etcd     (loops keep reconciling)
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The troubleshooting map — match the symptom to the broken step</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Connection refused / kubectl hangs</strong> → step 1–3 (apiserver/etcd down)</li>
                        <li><strong>Forbidden / Unauthorized</strong> → step 2 (RBAC or certs)</li>
                        <li><strong>Pod stuck Pending</strong> → step 5 (scheduler can't place it)</li>
                        <li><strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong> → step 6 (kubelet can't pull the image)</li>
                        <li><strong>CrashLoopBackOff</strong> → step 6 (container starts, then dies)</li>
                        <li><strong>Service unreachable</strong> → step 7 (kube-proxy / endpoints)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🍽️ In plain English — the same journey, as a restaurant order</h5>
                    <p>Tie it back to §3.2's restaurant: <em>"You hand your order to the <strong>front desk</strong> (apiserver), which checks you're allowed and writes it in the <strong>ledger</strong> (etcd). The <strong>floor manager</strong> (controllers) turns 'a table of 3' into three seat requests; the <strong>host</strong> (scheduler) seats each at a table (node); the <strong>line cook</strong> there (kubelet) actually makes the dish (starts the container); and the <strong>runner</strong> (kube-proxy) makes sure diners can find it (Service routing)."</em></p>
                    <p class="mb-0">Same eight steps, zero jargon — and the failure map still works: nobody seated = the host (scheduler) is out; food never delivered = the runner (kube-proxy) is out.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.5 Cluster Networking Overview</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes networking has one founding rule: <strong>every Pod gets its own unique IP</strong>, and all Pods can reach all Pods <strong>without NAT</strong> — one flat network. A CNI plugin makes that real. Keep the layers straight and networking stops being scary (full depth in Part 5).</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The four network layers — from the wire up to the user</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 🌍 EXTERNAL traffic (users, internet)                          │
   │    enters via: NodePort · LoadBalancer · Ingress · Gateway     │
   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 🔗 SERVICE network  (stable virtual IPs, e.g. 10.96.0.0/12)    │
   │    kube-proxy DNATs a Service VIP → a ready Pod IP             │
   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 📦 POD network  (real pod IPs, e.g. 10.244.0.0/16)            │
   │    assigned by the CNI · flat · every pod reaches every pod     │
   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 🖧 NODE network  (the machines/VMs, e.g. 192.168.1.0/24)       │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   easypj-web pod 10.244.1.5  ──reaches──▶  easypj-db 10.244.2.4 (no NAT)
   a client hits the Service VIP → kube-proxy picks a ready easypj-web pod
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>CNI Plugins — Your Choice as Admin</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐯</div><h5>Calico</h5><p>Most popular. Full NetworkPolicy, BGP routing. Common in CKA environments.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐝</div><h5>Cilium</h5><p>eBPF-based, deep observability (Hubble), high performance. NetworkPolicy +.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🟦</div><h5>Flannel</h5><p>Simple VXLAN overlay, easy setup — but <strong>no NetworkPolicy</strong>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🕸️</div><h5>Weave</h5><p>Encrypted mesh, auto-discovery, NetworkPolicy support.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 CKA task: install a CNI right after <code>kubeadm init</code></h5>
                    <p>A fresh cluster's pods (including CoreDNS) sit <code>Pending</code> and nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> until a CNI is installed — e.g. <code>kubectl apply -f calico.yaml</code>. "No CNI" is a classic NotReady cause: the fix is install a CNI, not restart the kubelet.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Why it's all pluggable — the three "sockets" (CRI · CNI · CSI)</div>
<pre>
   🤖 kubelet   ──CRI──▶  🐋 containerd / CRI-O          RUN containers
   pod network ──CNI──▶  🌐 Calico / Cilium / Flannel   WIRE pods (give IPs)
   volumes     ──CSI──▶  🧱 EBS / PD / Ceph / NFS driver ATTACH storage
   📖 CRI = runtime · CNI = network · CSI = storage — swap the plug, K8s is unchanged.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The Rules of the Kubernetes Network Model</h4>
                <p>Every CNI must honour the same contract — four rules that make the "flat network" promise concrete. Memorize these; they're the foundation of all Part 5 troubleshooting:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Rule</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1</td><td>Every Pod gets its <strong>own unique IP</strong></td><td>No port-mapping juggling — each pod is a "real host" on the network</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2</td><td>All Pods reach all Pods <strong>without NAT</strong></td><td>The destination sees the <em>real</em> source pod IP (flat L3 network)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>3</td><td>Nodes reach all Pods <strong>without NAT</strong></td><td>The kubelet &amp; health checks talk to pods directly</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>4</td><td>A Pod sees <strong>its own IP</strong> as others see it</td><td>No surprising address translation inside the pod</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 CoreDNS — names instead of IPs</h5>
                    <p>Pods come and go with changing IPs, so apps address each other by <em>name</em>. <strong>CoreDNS</strong> (a Deployment in <code>kube-system</code>, fronted by the <code>kube-dns</code> Service, usually <code>10.96.0.10</code>) resolves Service names to ClusterIPs — e.g. <code>easypj-db.easypj.svc.cluster.local</code>. Every pod's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points at it. If name resolution breaks cluster-wide, suspect CoreDNS first (full chapter: Ch 19).</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ kube-proxy vs CNI — keep them separate when debugging</h5>
                    <p>The <strong>CNI</strong> implements rules 1–4 (pod-to-pod). <strong>kube-proxy</strong> implements the <em>Service</em> abstraction on top (VIP → pod). So: pods can ping each other by IP but a Service is unreachable → <strong>kube-proxy/endpoints</strong> problem; pods can't reach each other by IP at all → <strong>CNI</strong> problem. That one split routes most networking tickets.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.6 Static Pods — an Admin-Only Concept</h3>
                <p>Static pods are managed <strong>directly by the kubelet</strong> on one node — not by the scheduler or API server. They're how kubeadm runs the control plane itself, and they're tested directly on the CKA.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Regular pods</h5><p>Created via the API server (kubectl) → stored in etcd → scheduled by the scheduler → managed by controllers.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📌</div><h5>Static pods</h5><p>Created by the kubelet from YAML in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. The kubelet watches that dir and owns the lifecycle. Drop a file → pod appears; remove it → pod gone.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The control plane IS static pods</h5>
                    <p>On a kubeadm cluster, the apiserver, scheduler, controller-manager, and etcd all run as <strong>static pods</strong> — which is why you configure them by editing YAML in the manifests dir (the kubelet reloads automatically):</p>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — the control-plane static pods</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># etcd.yaml  kube-apiserver.yaml  kube-controller-manager.yaml  kube-scheduler.yaml</span>

kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep controlplane
<span class="comment"># etcd-controlplane                       1/1  Running   ← note the -&lt;nodename&gt; suffix</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver-controlplane             1/1  Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager-controlplane    1/1  Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler-controlplane             1/1  Running</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Creating a Custom Static Pod</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">/etc/kubernetes/manifests/static-easypj.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: static-easypj
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    image: easypj:v1
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Static-pod facts the exam loves</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>You <strong>cannot</strong> <code>kubectl delete</code> a static pod — the kubelet instantly recreates it. Remove the <em>manifest file</em> instead.</li>
                        <li>The kubelet creates a read-only <strong>"mirror pod"</strong> in the API so you can see it; that's why the name carries a <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code> suffix.</li>
                        <li>The watched directory is set by <code>staticPodPath</code> in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>.</li>
                        <li>Static pods (kubelet-managed) ≠ DaemonSets (API-managed) — both put one pod per node, but only DaemonSets are API objects.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>etcd Restore — the Companion Procedure</h4>
                <p>You saw <em>backup</em> in §3.2; restore is the other half (and a classic exam task). Always restore to a <strong>new</strong> data dir, then repoint the etcd static pod at it:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — etcd restore</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1) restore the snapshot into a NEW directory</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># 2) edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml → point the data hostPath</span>
<span class="comment">#    volume from /var/lib/etcd  →  /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># 3) the kubelet sees the manifest change &amp; recreates etcd automatically</span>
<span class="comment"># 4) verify</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Restore gotcha</h5>
                    <p><code>--data-dir</code> must be a <strong>new</strong> path (e.g. <code>/var/lib/etcd-restored</code>), then repoint the manifest. Restoring over the live dir while etcd runs can corrupt it. Full DR drill in Chapter 29.</p>
                </div>

