kubectl get pods                        <span class="comment"># gone ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the pod's name gained a <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code> suffix — that's the read-only <em>mirror pod</em> the kubelet publishes so you can <em>see</em> it via the API. Deleting the mirror did nothing because the source of truth is the <em>file</em>. This is exactly how the control plane itself runs — and a recurring exam task.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 3.3 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 3.3 — Restart the API Server Without kubectl</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>You need to force-restart the <code>kube-apiserver</code> on the control-plane node (say, to pick up a config change that isn't applying). <code>kubectl delete</code> is useless against a static pod — use the manifest-move trick, and watch the whole lifecycle with <code>crictl</code> since <code>kubectl</code> dies the moment the apiserver stops.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> The kubelet stops a static pod when its manifest <em>leaves</em> the folder and starts it again when it <em>returns</em>. Move it out to <code>/tmp</code>, wait, move it back — and observe via <code>crictl ps</code>, not kubectl.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the control-plane node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Move the manifest OUT — the kubelet stops the apiserver (~seconds)</span>
sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/

<span class="comment"># 2. kubectl is now DEAD (the front door is gone) — watch via the runtime</span>
kubectl get pods                        <span class="comment"># connection refused ❌ (expected!)</span>
sudo crictl ps -a | grep apiserver      <span class="comment"># container Exited</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Move it BACK — the kubelet recreates the pod</span>
sudo mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/

<span class="comment"># 4. Watch it come up, then confirm the front door is open again</span>
sudo crictl ps | grep apiserver         <span class="comment"># Running again</span>
kubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose' | head   <span class="comment"># per-check health ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> two deep truths just became muscle memory — (1) the control plane is <em>files run by the kubelet</em>, so its lifecycle is controlled from the filesystem; (2) when the apiserver is down you observe the node through <code>crictl</code>, because <code>kubectl</code>'s only door is closed. The brief outage didn't touch running workloads — the data plane kept serving.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 3.4 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 3.4 — Node Health Forensics: Conditions, Allocatable &amp; Heartbeats</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Answer three questions about a live node using only <code>kubectl</code>: (1) what are its five conditions right now? (2) how much CPU/memory can the scheduler <em>actually</em> place on it (vs the hardware total)? (3) when did its last heartbeat land — and where do heartbeats live?</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Everything is in <code>kubectl describe node</code> (Conditions, Capacity, Allocatable) — except the fast heartbeat, which is a <code>Lease</code> in the <code>kube-node-lease</code> namespace.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node forensics in three reads</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. CONDITIONS — Ready + the four pressure flags</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A8 "Conditions:"
<span class="comment">#   MemoryPressure  False   DiskPressure  False</span>
<span class="comment">#   PIDPressure     False   Ready         True   (Unknown = lost contact)</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. CAPACITY vs ALLOCATABLE — what the scheduler may actually use</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A6 -E "^Capacity|^Allocatable"
<span class="comment">#   Capacity:    cpu: 4    memory: 8Gi     ← the hardware</span>
<span class="comment">#   Allocatable: cpu: 3800m memory: 7.2Gi  ← Capacity − kube/system-reserved − eviction</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. HEARTBEATS — the kubelet renews this Lease ~every 10s</span>
kubectl get lease worker-1 -n kube-node-lease -o yaml | grep -E "holderIdentity|renewTime"</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Question on the exam</th><th>Where the answer lives</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>"Why is this pod Pending though the node looks free?"</td><td><strong>Allocatable</strong>, not Capacity — the scheduler only fills the reserved-out slice</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>"Why did the node flip NotReady?"</td><td>Conditions + the kubelet's stopped Lease renewals (→ <code>Unknown</code> after ~50s)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>"Is the node under pressure?"</td><td><code>MemoryPressure</code>/<code>DiskPressure</code>/<code>PIDPressure</code> conditions → the kubelet is evicting</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> you can now read a node like a chart — <em>conditions</em> for health, <em>Allocatable</em> for schedulable room, the <em>Lease</em> for liveness. Those three views solve most "what's wrong with this node?" tasks before you ever SSH in.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 3.5 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 3.5 — The Scheduler Vanishes: Diagnose Pending Pods</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Simulate a scheduler outage on a test cluster: stop the <code>kube-scheduler</code> (manifest-move trick from Drill 3.3), create a new easypj pod, and observe <em>exactly</em> what breaks — and what doesn't. Then restore the scheduler and watch the queued pod get placed. Explain the symptom chain.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> The scheduler's only job is writing the <em>Binding</em> (setting <code>spec.nodeName</code>). No scheduler → new pods exist in etcd but have no node, forever <code>Pending</code>. Existing pods don't care.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">break → observe → restore → verify</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. BREAK — stop the scheduler (control-plane node)</span>
sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml /tmp/

<span class="comment"># 2. OBSERVE — create a pod; it lodges as Pending with NO node</span>
kubectl run easypj-test --image=nginx
kubectl get pod easypj-test -o wide            <span class="comment"># STATUS Pending · NODE &lt;none&gt;</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-test -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}{"\n"}'   <span class="comment"># (empty!)</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-test | grep -A3 Events
<span class="comment">#   (no scheduling events at all — nobody is even TRYING to place it)</span>

<span class="comment"># existing pods? completely unaffected — they were already bound</span>
kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase=Running | head

<span class="comment"># 3. RESTORE — bring the scheduler back</span>
sudo mv /tmp/kube-scheduler.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/

<span class="comment"># 4. VERIFY — the queued pod is bound within seconds</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-test -o wide            <span class="comment"># Running · NODE worker-2 ✅</span>
kubectl delete pod easypj-test                 <span class="comment"># cleanup</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the tell for "scheduler down" is precise — <code>Pending</code> pods with an <em>empty</em> <code>nodeName</code> and <em>no scheduling events whatsoever</em> (vs. a resource problem, where Events actively list the filter that rejected each node). And nothing already running was touched: the control plane decides, the data plane runs. That distinction — plus the instant catch-up when the scheduler returned (level-triggered!) — is Chapter 3 in one experiment.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 3 Visual Summary — the whole cluster, annotated</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────────────── ☸️ CLUSTER ───────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 🧠 CONTROL PLANE (decides)        every request &amp; watch flows through apiserver │
   │   📡 apiserver :6443  ── the ONLY door, the ONLY etcd client                    │
   │        │  authN → authZ(RBAC) → admission                                       │
   │        ├── 🗄️ etcd :2379/2380   ALL state (Raft quorum)                          │
   │        ├── 📋 scheduler          filter → score → BIND (sets nodeName)           │
   │        └── 🔄 controller-mgr     reconcile loops (node·RS·endpoint·SA)           │
   │  ───────────────────────────── apiserver ⇅ nodes (watch) ──────────────────────│
   │ 💪 WORKER NODE                                                                   │
   │   🤖 kubelet (systemd, not a pod) · 🌐 kube-proxy (Service routing) · 🐋 runtime  │
   │   🌐 CNI gives each 📦 pod a flat IP   ·   💾 CSI attaches volumes                │
   │   ┌────────────┐ ┌────────────┐                                                 │
   │   │📦 easypj-web│ │🐘 postgres  │   the control plane itself = static pods in     │
   │   │  :8000      │ │  :5432      │   /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ (kubelet-run)      │
   │   └────────────┘ └────────────┘                                                 │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   Decide (control plane) vs Run (nodes) · only apiserver→etcd · symptom→component = the exam.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> A cluster splits cleanly into two halves. The <strong>control plane decides</strong>; the <strong>worker nodes run</strong>. The <strong>apiserver</strong> (:6443) is the only way in and the <em>only</em> component that talks to <strong>etcd</strong> — every request first passes authentication → authorization (RBAC) → admission. etcd (:2379) holds <em>all</em> cluster state, kept consistent by Raft quorum.</p>
                    <p>Behind the apiserver, the <strong>scheduler</strong> filters and scores nodes then <em>binds</em> each pod (sets <code>nodeName</code>), and the <strong>controller-manager</strong> runs the reconcile loops (node, ReplicaSet, endpoint, service-account…). On each worker node, the <strong>kubelet</strong> runs the containers (it's a systemd service, not a pod), <strong>kube-proxy</strong> programs Service routing, the <strong>runtime</strong> executes containers, <strong>CNI</strong> gives each pod a flat IP, and <strong>CSI</strong> attaches storage. The control plane itself runs as <em>static pods</em> the kubelet starts from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> hold the "decide vs run" split and "only the apiserver touches etcd" in your head, and troubleshooting becomes a lookup — match the <em>symptom</em> to the responsible <em>component</em>. That mapping is the heart of the exam's Architecture and Troubleshooting domains.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
