                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the relationship between a <strong>Deployment</strong>, a <strong>ReplicaSet</strong>, and a <strong>Pod</strong>? If you create a Deployment with 3 replicas, how many of each object exist?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The hierarchy is: <strong>Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods</strong></p>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">Deployment (nginx-deploy)
  └── ReplicaSet (nginx-deploy-7d4f6b9c8)     ← 1 ReplicaSet
        ├── Pod (nginx-deploy-7d4f6b9c8-abc12)  ← Pod 1
        ├── Pod (nginx-deploy-7d4f6b9c8-def34)  ← Pod 2
        └── Pod (nginx-deploy-7d4f6b9c8-ghi56)  ← Pod 3

Total: 1 Deployment + 1 ReplicaSet + 3 Pods = 5 objects</code></pre>
</div>
                                <p><strong>Ownership chain:</strong> Each object has an <code>ownerReference</code> pointing to its parent. Deleting the Deployment cascades down — deletes the ReplicaSet, which deletes the pods.</p>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Understanding this hierarchy is critical for CKA troubleshooting. Key points: (1) You should <strong>never</strong> create ReplicaSets directly — always use Deployments. (2) During rolling updates, the Deployment creates a <em>new</em> ReplicaSet and scales it up while scaling the old one down. (3) Old ReplicaSets are kept (with 0 replicas) for rollback — controlled by <code>spec.revisionHistoryLimit</code> (default: 10). (4) If you see orphaned pods not managed by a Deployment, check for missing ownerReferences.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Explain the three Service types: <code>ClusterIP</code>, <code>NodePort</code>, and <code>LoadBalancer</code>. For each type, describe who can access the service and provide an example command to create one.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Type</th>
                                            <th>Accessible From</th>
                                            <th>Port Range</th>
                                            <th>Use Case</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>ClusterIP</code></td>
                                            <td>Inside cluster only</td>
                                            <td>Any</td>
                                            <td>Internal services (DB, cache)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>NodePort</code></td>
                                            <td>External via node IP</td>
                                            <td>30000-32767</td>
                                            <td>Dev/test external access</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>LoadBalancer</code></td>
                                            <td>External via cloud LB</td>
                                            <td>Any</td>
                                            <td>Production external access</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">clusterip (default)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># ClusterIP (default)</span>
kubectl expose deployment nginx --port=80 --target-port=80

<span class="comment"># NodePort</span>
kubectl expose deployment nginx --type=NodePort --port=80 --target-port=80

<span class="comment"># LoadBalancer</span>
kubectl expose deployment nginx --type=LoadBalancer --port=80 --target-port=80</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Services provide stable networking for ephemeral pods. Each Service gets a permanent <strong>ClusterIP</strong> and DNS name. Key CKA concepts: (1) <code>NodePort</code> automatically creates a <code>ClusterIP</code> underneath — it's an extension. (2) <code>LoadBalancer</code> creates both <code>ClusterIP</code> and <code>NodePort</code> underneath. (3) The <code>selector</code> in the Service spec must match labels on the pods — if they don't match, the service has no endpoints. (4) <code>kubectl expose</code> is the fastest way to create a service on the CKA exam.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You create a Deployment with <code>kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3</code>. Now you need to update the image to <code>easypj:v2</code>. What command do you use, and what happens internally during the rolling update?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">update the image</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Update the image</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web easypj-web=easypj:v2

<span class="comment"># Watch the rollout</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj-web

<span class="comment"># What happens internally:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Deployment controller creates a NEW ReplicaSet (easypj-web-v2-xxxxx)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Scales new RS up: 0 → 1</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Scales old RS down: 3 → 2</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Repeats until new RS has 3 and old RS has 0</span>
<span class="comment"># (controlled by maxSurge and maxUnavailable)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check rollout history</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check rollout history</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj-web

<span class="comment"># Rollback if v2 is broken</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web

<span class="comment"># Rollback to a specific revision</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web --to-revision=1</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Rolling updates are a core CKA task. The Deployment's <code>strategy.rollingUpdate</code> settings control the pace: <code>maxSurge</code> (how many extra pods above desired count) and <code>maxUnavailable</code> (how many pods can be down during the update). Default is <code>25%</code> for both. The old ReplicaSet is kept with 0 replicas so you can <code>rollout undo</code>. Pro tip: to record the change cause, add <code>--record</code> (deprecated but still works) or annotate the deployment with <code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are <strong>Labels</strong> and <strong>Selectors</strong> in Kubernetes? How do they connect Services to Pods? Give an example where a Service fails to route traffic because of a label mismatch.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p><strong>Labels</strong> are key-value pairs attached to objects. <strong>Selectors</strong> are queries that match labels to connect objects together.</p>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pod has label: app=web</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Pod has label: app=web</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: web-pod
  labels:
    app: web        # ← Label
    version: v1

<span class="comment"># Service selects pods with app=web</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: web-svc
spec:
  selector:
    app: web        # ← Must EXACTLY match pod label
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 8080

<span class="comment"># ❌ BROKEN: Service selector doesn't match pod labels</span>
spec:
  selector:
    app: frontend   # ← "frontend" ≠ "web" → NO endpoints!</code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">diagnose: check if service has endpoints</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Diagnose: check if service has endpoints</span>
kubectl get endpoints web-svc
<span class="comment"># If ENDPOINTS shows &lt;none&gt;, selector doesn't match any pods</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Label/selector mismatches are one of the <strong>most common CKA troubleshooting scenarios</strong>. If traffic doesn't reach pods: (1) Check <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> — empty means no matching pods. (2) Compare <code>kubectl get svc &lt;svc&gt; -o yaml | grep -A 5 selector</code> with <code>kubectl get pods --show-labels</code>. (3) Labels must match <strong>exactly</strong> — <code>app: web</code> ≠ <code>App: web</code> (case sensitive). ReplicaSets and Deployments also use selectors to find their pods — same matching rules apply.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Create a Namespace called <code>exam-prep</code>, then create a Deployment named <code>api-server</code> in that namespace with 2 replicas of <code>httpd:2.4</code>. Finally, expose it as a NodePort service on port 80. Write all the imperative commands.</div>
                        <details>
