            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s1-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.1 Why Kubernetes Security Is Different</h3>
                <p>Imagine your <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> Django application is running on Kubernetes. Employers post jobs, candidates browse listings, and user data flows through your cluster. Now imagine an attacker finds a way in. <em>How many layers do they have to breach before they reach your database?</em></p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Concept: The K8s Attack Surface</h5>
                    <p><strong>Attack surface</strong> is the sum of all points where an attacker can try to enter or extract data. Kubernetes has a uniquely large attack surface because it manages networking, storage, secrets, identities, and application lifecycle — all through a single API. As a CKS specialist, your job is to <strong>reduce, monitor, and defend</strong> every inch of that surface.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0">Critically, <strong>security is not an add-on you bolt on at the end</strong> — it has to be built into every layer, from the Dockerfile's first line to the API server's startup flags. A cluster that's hardened everywhere except one forgotten layer is not "mostly secure"; per the shared-kernel and defense-in-depth reasoning later in this chapter, the one gap you didn't close is the one an attacker will find.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🧟 The "Assume Breach" Mindset — Kubernetes Security's Other Foundational Shift</h5>
                    <p>Traditional IT security is largely <strong>prevention-first</strong>: build a strong enough perimeter and trust what's inside it. CKS-grade Kubernetes security starts from the opposite assumption — <strong>assume an attacker is already inside</strong>, right now, in some pod, in some namespace, and ask "what can they reach from there, and how fast do I find out?"</p>
                    <p class="mb-0">This isn't paranoia for its own sake — it's a direct consequence of the flat networking and shared kernel covered later in this section: given how easily lateral movement happens by default, betting everything on "attackers won't get in" is a losing bet. Every control in this guide answers the assume-breach question for one specific blast radius: NetworkPolicy limits <em>where</em> a breached pod can reach, SecurityContext limits <em>what</em> it can do once there, and Falco + audit logging limit <em>how long</em> it goes unnoticed. You'll see this exact framing return explicitly in Chapter 2's threat modeling and Chapter 29's Falco detection rules.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>5 Inconvenient Truths About Default Kubernetes Security</h4>
                <p>Out of the box, a freshly installed Kubernetes cluster is <strong>insecure by default</strong>. Knowing these defaults is essential — because every CKS exam question is essentially asking you to fix one of them:</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-1">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔴</div>
                        <h5>Truth 1: All Pods Can Talk to Each Other</h5>
                        <p>No NetworkPolicy exists by default. Any pod in any namespace can reach any other pod on any port. This means a compromised jobpost container could directly connect to your PostgreSQL database — no firewall, no barrier, nothing. <strong>CKS Fix: Chapter 6 — Network Policies.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔴</div>
                        <h5>Truth 2: Containers Run as Root (UID 0)</h5>
                        <p>Unless you explicitly set <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> and <code>runAsUser</code>, every container runs with full root privileges. If an attacker exploits a CVE in your app, they get root on the container — and potentially the node. <strong>CKS Fix: Chapter 18 — Security Contexts.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔴</div>
                        <h5>Truth 3: An Unaudited Default Capability Set — Plus One Copy-Paste Away From ALL 40+</h5>
                        <p>Precision matters here — a common myth is that a default container already has <code>SYS_ADMIN</code> and <code>SYS_PTRACE</code>. It does not: the container runtime's default bounding set is a smaller ~14 capabilities (<code>CHOWN</code>, <code>NET_RAW</code>, <code>SETUID</code>, <code>SETGID</code>, and others), and none of the genuinely dangerous ones ship by default. But that default set is still unaudited noise for most apps — <code>NET_RAW</code> alone lets a process craft raw/spoofed packets, something a Django app never needs — and the moment anyone adds <code>privileged: true</code> or <code>capabilities: {add: [SYS_ADMIN]}</code> to "just get it working," the container jumps to the FULL Linux capability set (41 named capabilities on a modern kernel, historically cited as "38+"). You must explicitly <code>drop: [ALL]</code> and add back only what's truly needed — never inherit the default set unexamined, and never reach for <code>privileged: true</code> as a shortcut. <strong>CKS Fix: Chapter 18 — capabilities drop ALL.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔴</div>
                        <h5>Truth 4: Secrets Are Stored as Plain Base64 in etcd</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create secret</code> stores your database passwords, API keys, and TLS certificates as <strong>base64-encoded text</strong> — not encrypted. Anyone with etcd access can decode them. Encryption at rest must be explicitly configured. <strong>CKS Fix: Chapter 19 — EncryptionConfiguration.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔴</div>
                        <h5>Truth 5: No Audit Logging by Default</h5>
                        <p>By default, Kubernetes records ZERO information about who did what. If an attacker deletes your production namespace at 3 AM, you'll have no record of who, how, or when. Audit logging must be explicitly enabled and configured. <strong>CKS Fix: Chapter 28 — Audit Logging.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>A Day in the Life — What CKS Controls Protect, 24/7, for easypj</h4>
                <p>The five truths above aren't abstract — here's what one real day looks like once every CKS control is switched on. Notice that <strong>every single event is caught or blocked automatically</strong>, with zero manual intervention:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>🕐 00:00 — Automatic Trivy Scan</h5>
                        <p>A scheduled Trivy scan detects a new CVE in the <code>python:3.12</code> base image. The Grafana dashboard turns red and a PagerDuty alert fires. <em>Ch 23, 28, 32.</em></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>🕖 07:30 — Developer Pushes Code</h5>
                        <p>A PR opens. GitHub Actions runs kube-linter + kubesec + Trivy. The build <strong>FAILS</strong> — the container runs as root! The developer adds <code>USER 1000</code> to the Dockerfile, the gates pass, and cosign signs the image. <em>Ch 25, 26.</em></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>🕘 09:15 — Deployed to Staging</h5>
                        <p>Kyverno admission verifies the cosign signature ✅. PSS Restricted confirms non-root + drop ALL caps ✅. OPA confirms the image comes from the approved registry ✅. The pod starts. <em>Ch 17, 18, 24.</em></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>🕚 11:45 — Traffic Spikes on Job Listings</h5>
                        <p>The HPA scales easypj from 3 to 8 replicas. Every new pod inherits the same SecurityContext and the same NetworkPolicy — auto-applied by label selector. Zero manual intervention. <em>Ch 6, 35.</em></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>🕑 14:20 — Attacker Probes the Pod</h5>
                        <p>Attempt 1: SSH to port 22 — blocked by the firewall. Attempt 2: direct HTTP to <code>easypj:8000</code> — blocked by NetworkPolicy. Attempt 3: a CVE exploit via a crafted POST — Seccomp blocks the syscall. Falco logs all three attempts for the security team. <em>Ch 6, 15, 29.</em></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">6</div>
                        <h5>🕔 17:00 — Audit Review Catches a Mistake</h5>
                        <p>An intern accidentally runs <code>kubectl exec easypj -- bash</code>. A Falco alert fires: "Shell spawned in easypj container." The audit log shows exactly who (<code>user=intern</code>) did what (<code>verb=pods/exec</code>). No damage — just a policy reminder. <em>Ch 28, 29.</em></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">7</div>
                        <h5>🕙 22:00 — Nightly kube-bench CronJob</h5>
                        <p>All CIS Benchmark checks pass, except one (a new audit policy not yet applied). An alert is sent; DevOps will fix it in the morning. <em>Ch 4, 32.</em></p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🛡️ 24 Hours. Zero Breaches. Every Layer Held.</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">That's what CKS-hardened Kubernetes looks like in production. Every event above is caught by a control you will build with your own hands in this guide — not a theoretical scenario.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🏙️ Think of Your K8s Cluster as a City — and Security as Infrastructure</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>🏛️ <strong>Control Plane</strong> = City Hall — If someone breaks in, they control everything. <em>Lock every door and window (API server hardening).</em></li>
                        <li>🏘️ <strong>Worker Nodes</strong> = Residential blocks — Your apps live here. <em>Each home needs its own lock (SecurityContext, seccomp).</em></li>
                        <li>🌐 <strong>Network</strong> = Roads — By default, every building has a door to every other building. <em>Install gates between districts (NetworkPolicy).</em></li>
                        <li>📦 <strong>Containers</strong> = Delivery packages — Anyone can send one. <em>Scan every package before accepting it (image scanning, admission control).</em></li>
                        <li>🦅 <strong>Falco</strong> = CCTV cameras on every street — <em>You'll know the moment something suspicious happens.</em></li>
                        <li>📋 <strong>Audit Logs</strong> = Visitor registry — <em>Every entry and exit is recorded forever.</em></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The city, zoomed in — four districts, four distinct attack surfaces</div>
<pre>
  🏙️ KUBERNETES CLUSTER (your city)

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  🧠 Control Plane — the mayor's office                    │
  │  API Server exposed on the network → attackers target it │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  🏘️ Worker Nodes — the city blocks                        │
  │  Pods run as root by default → privilege escalation       │
  │  Containers share one OS kernel → container-escape risk   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  🌐 Networking — the city roads                           │
  │  All pods can talk to each other by default                │
  │  East-west traffic is unrestricted → lateral movement      │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  📦 Images — delivery packages                             │
  │  Images pulled from the internet → malicious code risk    │
  │  No scanning = you don't know what's actually inside       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
                <p>Each district above is a <strong>separate CKS domain</strong> you'll master in this guide: the Control Plane maps to Cluster Setup &amp; Hardening (Ch 3–12), Worker Nodes to System Hardening (Ch 13–18), Networking to Cluster Setup's NetworkPolicy work (Ch 6), and Images to Supply Chain Security (Ch 23–27). A CKS specialist doesn't secure "the cluster" as one blob — they walk through each district in turn and ask "what's the specific attack surface here, and what specifically closes it?"</p>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Real-World Breaches That Teach Kubernetes Security Lessons</h5>
                    <p>These are not hypothetical. Only one of the three below is a Kubernetes-specific incident — the other two are cloud/IAM and insider-access incidents. All three are worth knowing precisely, because CKS controls exist to close the exact failure mode each one demonstrates:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Tesla (2018)</strong> — A genuine Kubernetes incident: crypto-mining malware deployed via an exposed Kubernetes dashboard with no authentication enabled. Attackers used Tesla's own cloud compute to mine cryptocurrency, discovered by security researchers (RedLock) scanning for exactly this kind of open dashboard.</li>
                        <li><strong>Capital One (2019)</strong> — <em>Not</em> a Kubernetes incident, but a directly relevant cloud/IAM one: a misconfigured WAF running on an AWS EC2 instance let an attacker use SSRF to reach the instance metadata service (<code>169.254.169.254</code>) and steal the credentials of an over-permissioned IAM role, exposing 100+ million customer records. The exact same metadata-endpoint risk applies to Kubernetes nodes — it's a named line item on the official Security Checklist you'll meet in §1.4.</li>
                        <li><strong>Shopify (2020)</strong> — <em>Not</em> a technical exploit at all, Shopify said so explicitly: two "rogue" support-team employees abused their own legitimate, standing access to an internal support tool to pull transactional data on roughly 200 merchants. No RBAC misconfiguration was ever confirmed — the lesson is that standing, over-broad access is itself the vulnerability, regardless of which system enforces it.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="margin-top:8px"><strong>Common thread across all three:</strong> excessive standing privilege plus no runtime visibility — whether the excess privilege lived in a cloud IAM role, an internal support tool, or (as you'll see starting in Chapter 9) a Kubernetes RBAC binding. <em>Everything you will learn in this guide directly hardens against that same pattern inside a Kubernetes cluster.</em></p>
