                <h4>Traditional IT Security vs Kubernetes Security</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Traditional IT Security</th><th>Kubernetes Security</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>One server = one firewall rule</td><td>1000 pods = 1000 potential targets</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Static IPs and hostnames</td><td>Ephemeral pods, rotating IPs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Update OS once a month</td><td>Images rebuilt &amp; deployed daily</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Users log in via SSH</td><td>Pods authenticate with ServiceAccount tokens</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Audit logs in <code>/var/log/auth.log</code></td><td>Audit logs via the Kubernetes API audit policy</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Firewall blocks at edge</td><td>NetworkPolicy <em>inside</em> the cluster</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Manual security reviews</td><td>Policy-as-code enforced at admission</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>App runs as its own Linux user</td><td>🔴 <strong>By default: runs as root!</strong></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Secret in /etc/app.conf</td><td>🔴 <strong>Secret in etcd (unencrypted!)</strong></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Patch = update one binary</td><td>Patch = rebuild the image + redeploy</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p>Notice the pattern across all ten rows: traditional security assumes <strong>long-lived, identifiable things</strong> — a server with a name, a user with an SSH key, a binary you patch in place. Kubernetes gives you <strong>short-lived, anonymous, and endlessly regenerated things</strong> instead. Every tool built for the old model (a firewall rule keyed to an IP, a login audit keyed to a username) has to be rethought for a world where the "thing" being secured might not exist five minutes from now.</p>

                <h4>The Kubernetes Security Evolution Timeline — How We Got Here</h4>
                <p>Kubernetes security wasn't designed in one sitting — it was <em>built reactively</em>, one incident and one CVE at a time. Knowing this history explains why the tooling looks the way it does today, and why the CKS exam covers what it covers:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">2014 → 2024+ — a decade of incident-driven hardening</div>
<pre>
  2014   K8s released. Security barely considered. No RBAC.
   │     Anyone with API access = full cluster access.
   ▼
  2017   RBAC reaches beta (v1.6, GA in v1.8). PodSecurityPolicy (alpha
   │     since v1.3) matures alongside it — the first admission-control attempt.
   ▼
  2018   Tesla breach. Crypto-mining via an exposed dashboard.
   │     Industry wakes up to K8s security risks.
   ▼
  2019   Kubernetes security starts being treated as its own specialty in the
   │     wake of Tesla — NetworkPolicy sees wider production adoption.
   ▼
  2020   CNCF/Linux Foundation announce the CKS certification (Jul 2020) and
   │     GA it four months later (Nov 17, 2020) — security becomes a
   │     dedicated, examinable career path for the first time.
   ▼
  2021   PodSecurityPolicy DEPRECATED (v1.21). Cosign reaches v1.0 (Jul 2021) —
   │     image signing goes production-ready. Supply-chain attacks surge
   │     (the SolarWinds effect reaches K8s). Audit logging becomes standard.
   ▼
  2022   PodSecurityPolicy REMOVED; Pod Security Admission reaches GA (v1.25)
   │     as its replacement — Pod Security Standards (Privileged/Baseline/
