</pre>
                </div>
                <p>Two things jump out from this timeline. First, <strong>every major security feature exists because something broke</strong> — RBAC after "anyone with API access owns the cluster," PSS after PodSecurityPolicy proved too rigid to maintain, audit logging after operators kept asking "who did this?" with no answer. Second, the <strong>pace is accelerating</strong>: it took three years to go from "no RBAC" to "RBAC exists," but only one year to go from "cosign v1.0" to "PSS replaces PSP" to "eBPF becomes standard." As a CKS specialist, you're not learning a finished product — you're learning the current state of a field that is still actively hardening itself against new attack classes every year.</p>

                <h4>The Shared Kernel Architecture — Why K8s Security Is Fundamentally Harder</h4>
                <p>At the heart of Kubernetes security lies an uncomfortable truth: <strong>every container on a node shares the exact same Linux kernel.</strong> This is not a bug — it is the defining architectural property of containerization.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">VM Isolation vs Container Isolation — The Kernel Is the Difference</div>
<pre>
  VM MODEL: VM1(Kernel A) VM2(Kernel B) VM3(Kernel C) → Hypervisor (hw isolation) → Hardware
    A kernel exploit in VM1 affects ONLY VM1 — escape needs a kernel exploit AND a hypervisor escape.

  CONTAINER MODEL: PodA(UID1000) PodB(UID101) PodC(UID999) PodD(UID70) PodE-attacker(UID0)
    → Container Runtime (namespace isolation, no hw boundary) → ONE SHARED Linux kernel → Hardware
    A SINGLE kernel exploit affects ALL containers — Pod E escapes the namespace and reaches A-D + the host.
</pre>
                </div>
                <p><strong>The implication for CKS:</strong> every control in this guide exists because the shared kernel model creates a single point of failure. NetworkPolicies (Ch6) prevent lateral reach. Non-root (Ch18) needs TWO vulnerabilities to escalate. Seccomp (Ch15) blocks the syscalls an exploit needs. AppArmor (Ch14) blocks the files it needs to read.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s1-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.2 The 4Cs of Cloud Native Security (The Layered Model)</h3>
                <p>The Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF) defines a <strong>four-layer security model</strong> for cloud-native applications. Each layer builds on the security of the layer below it. If the Cloud layer is insecure, nothing above it matters. This model — <strong>Cloud → Cluster → Container → Code</strong> — is the foundation of every CKS exam question.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The 4Cs</h5>
                    <p><strong>Cloud → Cluster → Container → Code:</strong> nested Russian dolls. Cloud is outermost (infrastructure); Cluster is Kubernetes itself; Container is how your app runs; Code is your actual application. A breach at any outer layer compromises ALL inner layers.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Cloud (outermost) → Cluster → Container → Code (innermost) — the full nested picture</div>
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                                                          │
│    ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│    │  ☁️  CLOUD                                     │    │
│    │  IAM policies, VPC security groups, firewall  │    │
│    │   ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐    │    │
│    │   │  ☸️  CLUSTER                          │    │    │
│    │   │  RBAC, NetworkPolicy, API server sec │    │    │
│    │   │   ┌───────────────────────────────┐  │    │    │
│    │   │   │  📦 CONTAINER                 │  │    │    │
│    │   │   │  Image scanning, Seccomp,     │  │    │    │
│    │   │   │  AppArmor, non-root           │  │    │    │
│    │   │   │   ┌──────────────────────┐   │  │    │    │
│    │   │   │   │  💻 CODE              │   │  │    │    │
│    │   │   │   │  SAST, dep. scanning, │   │  │    │    │
│    │   │   │   │  secrets in code, TLS │   │  │    │    │
│    │   │   │   └──────────────────────┘   │  │    │    │
│    │   │   └───────────────────────────────┘  │    │    │
│    │   └──────────────────────────────────────┘    │    │
│    └───────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                          │
│  ⚠️  A breach in an outer layer compromises ALL inner   │
│      layers — security is only as strong as its weakest │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
                <p>This is why the ordering in §1.2's decision flowchart is outermost-first, not arbitrary: CLOUD is drawn as the outermost ring because a breach there — a leaked cloud IAM key, an open security group — hands the attacker a straight walk into every ring nested inside it, no matter how tightly CLUSTER, CONTAINER, and CODE are individually locked down. The reverse is not true: a hardened CLOUD layer does nothing to stop a root container or an unsigned image, because those rings sit entirely inside it and are governed by their own, independent controls.</p>

                <h4>CKS Domain Coverage Per Layer</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>4C Layer</th><th>CKS Topics Covered</th><th>CKS Domains Mapped</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>☁️ Cloud</td><td>IAM, VPC/firewall config</td><td>❌ Not CKS-tested (outside K8s' control)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>☸️ Cluster</td><td>API server, RBAC, ServiceAccount, NetworkPolicy, <strong>node OS hardening</strong></td><td>Cluster Setup (10%) + Cluster Hardening (15%) + System Hardening (15%)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>📦 Container</td><td>Seccomp, AppArmor, SecurityContext, PSS</td><td>System Hardening (15%) + Microservice Vulns (20%)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>💻 Code</td><td>Image scanning, signing, supply chain</td><td>Supply Chain Security (20%)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Add Up the Percentages — Where's the Missing 20%?</h5>
                    <p>Tally the table above and you get 80%, not 100%: Cluster Setup (10%) + Cluster Hardening (15%) + System Hardening (15%, counted once even though it appears in two rows) + Microservice Vulns (20%) + Supply Chain Security (20%). The missing 20% is the CKS's sixth domain — <strong>Monitoring, Logging &amp; Runtime Security</strong> — and it's missing from this table <em>on purpose</em>, not by omission: Falco (a Container-layer runtime agent) and Kubernetes audit logging (a Cluster-layer API server feature) are graded under this ONE domain even though the 4Cs model would place them in two different rings. Monitoring doesn't live in any single ring — it watches all of them at once.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0">This is precisely the WHERE-vs-WHEN gap that §1.4 exists to close: the official docs' <strong>Runtime</strong> lifecycle phase (its Access and Compute areas specifically) is where this domain's controls actually live, cutting across Cluster and Container rather than being owned by either. Every Falco rule and audit-policy control you build in Ch 28–29 counts toward this 20% no matter which 4C ring its target pod happens to sit in — don't go looking for a "Monitoring" row above, because there isn't one to find.</p>
                </div>

