                <h4>Security Decision Flowchart — Which 4C Layer Does This Belong To?</h4>
                <p>When you hit an unfamiliar security concern — on the exam or in production — don't guess at a fix before you've placed it. Walk this flowchart top to bottom; the first "YES" tells you exactly which chapter's toolbox to reach for:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Map any security concern to the correct 4C layer</div>
<pre>
  You identify a security concern. Ask, in order:

  Is it about cloud infra (VPC, IAM, firewall)?
    YES → ☁️  CLOUD layer → fix at the cloud-provider level
    NO  ↓

  Is it about the K8s API, RBAC, nodes, networking, or policies?
    YES → ☸️  CLUSTER layer → fix with RBAC, NetworkPolicy, etc.
    NO  ↓

  Is it about how a container runs (user, capabilities, filesystem)?
    YES → 📦 CONTAINER layer → fix with SecurityContext, Seccomp, AppArmor
    NO  ↓

  Is it about the software, libraries, or secrets IN the image?
    YES → 💻 CODE layer → fix with scanning, signing, dependency updates
</pre>
                </div>
                <p>Notice the flowchart is <strong>ordered outermost-first</strong> — you check Cloud before Cluster, Cluster before Container, Container before Code. That ordering matters: if you "fix" a Cluster-layer symptom (say, tightening RBAC) when the real cause is a Cloud-layer misconfiguration (an over-permissive node IAM role), the attacker just routes around your fix through the layer you never checked.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧩</div>
                        <h5>Worked Example 1 — "easypj pods can reach the payments namespace"</h5>
                        <p>Not cloud infra. Not container internals. Not image contents. It <em>is</em> about pod-to-pod traffic inside the cluster → <strong>CLUSTER layer</strong> → the fix is a NetworkPolicy (Ch 6), not a firewall rule or a Dockerfile change.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧩</div>
                        <h5>Worked Example 2 — "A CVE was found in our base image's OpenSSL"</h5>
                        <p>Not cloud, not cluster API, not runtime pod behavior — it's a vulnerability shipped <em>inside</em> the image itself → <strong>CODE layer</strong> → the fix is rebuilding from a patched base image and re-scanning with Trivy (Ch 23), not an RBAC or NetworkPolicy change.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">☁️</div>
                        <h5>When the CLOUD Layer Fails...</h5>
                        <p>An overly permissive node IAM role lets an attacker delete every S3 bucket after compromising just one node. Kubernetes cannot protect you from cloud IAM — NodeRestriction limits what a compromised kubelet can do via the API server, but the root fix is at the cloud level.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">☸️</div>
                        <h5>When the CLUSTER Layer Fails...</h5>
                        <p>Anonymous auth enabled means anyone reaching port 6443 has full cluster access — the #1 most common K8s breach vector (Tesla 2018). Fix: <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> plus least-privilege RBAC.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>When the CONTAINER Layer Fails...</h5>
                        <p>A root container with a library CVE gives an attacker immediate root inside the container — backdoors, secret reads, container escape attempts. Non-root + drop ALL + readOnlyRootFS is mandatory for every production pod.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💻</div>
                        <h5>When the CODE Layer Fails...</h5>
                        <p>A bloated base image (python:3.11, 1GB+) ships every CVE in every package it contains as YOUR CVE — even if your application code is perfect. Fix: multi-stage builds, distroless images, Trivy scanning at every CI/CD stage.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Most Dangerous Assumption in Kubernetes Security</h5>
                    <p>"I deployed on a managed K8s service (EKS/GKE/AKS), so the cloud provider handles security" is dangerously wrong. Managed K8s secures the CONTROL PLANE. It does NOT secure your pod configs, NetworkPolicies, RBAC, images, node OS, audit logging, or runtime detection. <strong>Everything from the container level down is YOUR responsibility.</strong></p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Shared Responsibility Model — What EKS/GKE/AKS Actually Secures</h4>
                <p>The danger box above names the assumption; here's the exact line, control by control, so there's never a guess about which side of it you're on:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Layer / Control</th><th>Who Owns It</th><th>The Detail That Trips People Up</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Control plane (API server, etcd, scheduler)</td><td>☁️ Cloud Provider</td><td>Patched, backed up, and made highly-available for you — you never SSH into it</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Control plane's host OS &amp; hardware</td><td>☁️ Cloud Provider</td><td>Entirely invisible to you; not a CKS-tested layer (this section's Cloud ring)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Worker node OS &amp; kubelet version</td><td>🧑‍💻 You (mostly)</td><td>Managed node groups auto-patch the OS image, but YOU still choose kubelet flags, node taints, and the upgrade cadence (Ch 12, 13)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>CNI plugin &amp; NetworkPolicy rules</td><td>🧑‍💻 You</td><td>The provider ships a default CNI so pods CAN talk — it ships ZERO NetworkPolicies restricting that traffic (Ch 6)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>RBAC, ServiceAccounts, admission policy</td><td>🧑‍💻 You</td><td>100% your responsibility on every managed offering, with no exceptions (Ch 9, 10, 24)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pod SecurityContext / Pod Security Standards</td><td>🧑‍💻 You</td><td>A fresh managed-cluster namespace has NO PSS label — every pod runs unrestricted until you set one yourself (Ch 17, 18)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Secrets encryption at rest</td><td>⚠️ Shared</td><td>Most providers offer a KMS integration for envelope encryption — but it's opt-in; the untouched default is still plain base64 in etcd (Ch 19)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Image content, CVEs, signing</td><td>🧑‍💻 You</td><td>No managed K8s service scans, signs, or verifies what you push to your own registry (Ch 23, 26)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Audit logging</td><td>⚠️ Shared</td><td>The provider CAN stream control-plane audit logs to a log sink — but it's disabled by default and the policy detail is still yours to author (Ch 28)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Runtime threat detection</td><td>🧑‍💻 You</td><td>Falco, audit alerting, and incident response are not bundled with ANY managed K8s offering (Ch 29, 30)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p>Notice the pattern: every row marked <strong>☁️ Cloud Provider</strong> lives entirely in the Cloud ring from this section's own 4Cs diagram — the one ring the CKS exam never tests, precisely because it's not yours to configure. Every row marked <strong>🧑‍💻 You</strong> or <strong>⚠️ Shared</strong> falls inside Cluster, Container, or Code — which is exactly why the exam CAN test 100% of what it tests: everything gradable is, by construction, something you and only you control.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s1-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.3 Security Principles You Will Apply Throughout This Guide</h3>
                <p>Every single CKS exam task — and every real-world security decision — flows from <strong>five core security principles</strong>. These are the mindset you will apply to every kubectl command, every YAML file, and every security audit you perform on easypj/jobpost.</p>

                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div>
                        <h5>Least Privilege</h5>
                        <p>Grant ONLY what is needed. Every RBAC role, every ServiceAccount — start from zero and add only what's required.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧅</div>
                        <h5>Defense in Depth</h5>
                        <p>Multiple layers; no single point of failure. If NetworkPolicy fails, SecurityContext still blocks. Every layer is a backup.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5>Zero Trust</h5>
                        <p>Trust no pod, user, or service by default. Default-deny is the starting position for every NetworkPolicy and RBAC rule.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧊</div>
                        <h5>Immutability</h5>
                        <p>Containers should not change once running. readOnlyRootFilesystem: true. Changes go through Git → CI/CD → rebuild → new pod.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Audit Everything</h5>
                        <p>Log every API call, every access, every anomaly. If you don't know it happened, you can't respond to it.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The easypj Application Security Context — What Are We Actually Protecting?</h4>
                <p>Before mapping requirements to controls, get concrete about what <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> actually exposes. Every principle in this guide gets applied against these five specific surfaces, over and over, for the next 37 chapters:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>The jobpost App — Public Job Listings</h5>
                        <p>Employers post jobs, candidates browse them at <code>/jobs/</code>. It's public-facing by design — but it must never leak the data <em>behind</em> that public surface (user accounts, internal notes, unpublished postings).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔐</div>
                        <h5>The Django Admin Interface</h5>
                        <p><code>/admin/</code> is the single highest-value target in the whole app — full CRUD over every model. It must be restricted by both RBAC (who can reach the cluster) and NetworkPolicy (what can reach the pod).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🐘</div>
                        <h5>The PostgreSQL Database</h5>
                        <p>Holds every user record, every job posting, every credential hash. It must be completely unreachable from the public internet — the <em>only</em> path in is from the easypj-web pods, over an explicitly allowed NetworkPolicy rule.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📎</div>
                        <h5>Media Uploads</h5>
                        <p>Résumés, company logos, profile photos — any user-uploaded file is a vector for malware or a path-traversal attack. Every upload must be scanned before it's trusted.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔑</div>
                        <h5>API Keys in <code>settings.py</code></h5>
                        <p>Payment provider keys, email service credentials, the Django <code>SECRET_KEY</code> itself — none of these may ever live in code or a plain environment variable. They belong in a Kubernetes Secret, encrypted at rest.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>easypj Security Requirements → CKS Controls Mapping</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>easypj Requirement</th><th>CKS Control That Satisfies It</th></tr></thead>
