                <h4>The 5 Principles, Applied to easypj Every Single Day</h4>
                <p>Principles are easy to nod along to in the abstract. Here is exactly what each one looks like as a <strong>recurring daily practice</strong> on this specific project — not a one-time setup step, but something you do or verify constantly:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-1">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div>
                        <h5>Least Privilege — Daily</h5>
                        <p>The easypj pod's ServiceAccount has zero API permissions (<code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>). The CI/CD ServiceAccount can only update Deployments — nothing else, not even read Secrets it doesn't own.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧅</div>
                        <h5>Defense in Depth — Daily</h5>
                        <p>easypj runs behind NetworkPolicy + PSS + SecurityContext + Falco + TLS, simultaneously. If NetworkPolicy is ever misconfigured, SecurityContext still blocks root execution. If an image somehow ships a vulnerability, Seccomp still blocks the dangerous syscalls it needs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5>Zero Trust — Daily</h5>
                        <p>The default-deny NetworkPolicy in <code>easypj-prod</code> means nothing gets in and nothing gets out unless it's explicitly allowed. Every connection — ingress → easypj, easypj → postgres — is its own named, reviewed rule.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧊</div>
                        <h5>Immutability — Daily</h5>
                        <p><code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> on every easypj container, every deploy. Changes only ever flow Git → CI/CD → image rebuild → new pod — an attacker who lands inside a running container cannot make any change persist.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Audit Everything — Daily</h5>
                        <p>The audit policy logs every secret read, every <code>pods/exec</code>, every Deployment change. A standing Falco rule alerts on any shell spawned in an easypj container. Trivy Operator continuously re-scans the running images, not just at build time.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Principle Anti-Patterns — What NOT to Do</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="split-side split-bad">
                        <h5>❌ ANTI-PATTERN: Over-Permissive RBAC</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create clusterrole easypj-admin --verb=* --resource=*</code></p>
                        <p>Wildcard permissions defeat RBAC entirely.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="split-side split-good">
                        <h5>✅ CORRECT: Least Privilege RBAC</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create role easypj-reader --verb=get,list --resource=pods,deployments -n easypj-prod</code></p>
                        <p>Grant ONLY specific verbs/resources. Verify with <code>kubectl auth can-i --as=sa</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="split-side split-bad">
                        <h5>❌ ANTI-PATTERN: Running as Root</h5>
                        <p><code>containers: - name: easypj</code><br><em>(no securityContext — runs as root by default!)</em></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="split-side split-good">
                        <h5>✅ CORRECT: Non-Root with Drop ALL</h5>
                        <p><code>securityContext: runAsNonRoot: true, runAsUser: 1000, capabilities: {drop: [ALL]}, readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code></p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Defense in Depth — The Onion Model Applied to easypj Security</h4>
                <p>The 4Cs (§1.2) organize security by <em>where</em> a control lives. The Onion Model below is the same idea turned into a single, concrete attack path — eight literal layers, each one nested inside the last, each one an independent holdout an attacker must individually defeat before reaching easypj's data:</p>
