            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s1-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.4 The Official CNCF Security Lifecycle — Develop, Distribute, Deploy, Runtime</h3>
                <p>Everything in §1.2 taught the <strong>4Cs</strong> — Cloud, Cluster, Container, Code — which organizes security by <em>WHERE</em> a control lives. The current official Kubernetes documentation (<code>kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/cloud-native-security/</code>) teaches a complementary model built on the CNCF's own security whitepaper: four <strong>lifecycle phases</strong> — <strong>Develop → Distribute → Deploy → Runtime</strong> — which organize security by <em>WHEN</em> in your software's life a control applies. Knowing both is what separates someone who memorized "4Cs" from someone who actually understands how the industry frames this problem.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Two Models, Same Reality — WHERE vs WHEN</h5>
                    <p>The 4Cs and the Lifecycle Phases aren't competing theories — they're two different cuts through the exact same set of controls. A NetworkPolicy is a <strong>Cluster-layer</strong> control (WHERE) that you apply during the <strong>Deploy phase</strong> and enforce continuously through <strong>Runtime</strong> (WHEN). Image scanning is a <strong>Code-layer</strong> control (WHERE) that happens during <strong>Distribute</strong> (WHEN). Neither model is "more correct" — the 4Cs is the framing most CKS prep material (including most of this guide) uses for organizing chapters; the Lifecycle Phases is the framing the official docs currently use. This section teaches the second one directly from the source, so nothing in the official documentation ever surprises you.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The CNCF Security Lifecycle — four phases, one continuous pipeline</div>
<pre>
  💻 DEVELOP              📦 DISTRIBUTE            🚀 DEPLOY               ⚙️ RUNTIME
  Design & write code  →  Package & ship images →  Admit & place pods  →  Operate, forever
  threat model,           scan, sign, restrict      namespaces, admission   access · compute · storage
  zero-trust design       registry access           control, verification  networking · observability
        │                       │                        │                       │
        └───────────────────────┴────────────────────────┴───────────────────────┘
                     Security is enforced at EVERY phase, not bolted on at the end.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Phase 1 — Develop: Security Starts Before the First Line of Code Ships</h4>
                <p>Per the official docs, this phase is about ensuring <strong>development environment integrity</strong> and designing applications with security in mind from the start — before anything is even built into an image.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div>
                        <h5>Zero-Trust Architecture</h5>
                        <p>Adopt an architecture that minimizes the attack surface, <em>even against internal threats</em> — don't assume a request from inside the cluster is safe just because it's internal. This is §1.3's Zero Trust principle, applied at design time.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔍</div>
                        <h5>Security-Aware Code Review</h5>
                        <p>A code review process that explicitly considers security concerns — not just "does it work," but "can this be abused." For easypj, this means the Django admin, auth flows, and file-upload handlers get extra scrutiny every PR.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗺️</div>
                        <h5>Threat Modeling</h5>
                        <p>Build a <em>threat model</em> that identifies trust boundaries in your system, then use it to find risks and decide how to treat them. For easypj: where does untrusted input enter (job listing forms, file uploads, the API), and what's on the other side of each boundary?</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧪</div>
                        <h5>Security Automation</h5>
                        <p>Advanced automation like <em>fuzzing</em> and <em>security chaos engineering</em> — deliberately injecting failure/attack conditions to see if your defenses actually hold, not just assuming they do.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Where this guide covers it:</strong> the mindset threads through every chapter, but Chapter 25 (Static Analysis) is where "security-aware code review" becomes an automated, repeatable gate rather than a hope.</p>

                <h4>Phase 2 — Distribute: Securing the Supply Chain Before It Ever Reaches Your Cluster</h4>
                <p>This phase is about the integrity of everything you pull <em>into</em> your cluster — container images, and any other component (like an external database) your application depends on.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Official Control</th><th>What It Means for easypj</th><th>Built in</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Scan images &amp; artifacts for known vulnerabilities</td><td>Trivy scans the easypj image on every build</td><td>Ch 23</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Encryption in transit + chain of trust for the source</td><td>TLS registry pulls, verified image provenance</td><td>Ch 26, 27</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Process to update dependencies on security announcements</td><td>Dependabot/Renovate + a patch-Tuesday-style review cadence</td><td>Ch 23</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Digital certificates for supply-chain assurance</td><td>cosign signatures verified before deploy</td><td>Ch 26</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Subscribe to security-risk alert feeds</td><td>CVE feeds for the Python/Django ecosystem</td><td>Ch 23</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Restrict access to artifacts — private registries only</td><td>easypj's image lives in a private registry, no public pulls</td><td>Ch 23</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>Phase 3 — Deploy: Restricting What, Who, and Where</h4>
                <p>The official docs frame Deploy as ensuring <strong>appropriate restrictions on what can be deployed, who can deploy it, and where it can be deployed</strong> — this is also where controls from the Distribute phase (like verifying a signed image) actually get <em>enforced</em>, not just produced.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Deploy-time enforcement — verifying what Distribute produced</div>
<pre>
  📦 DISTRIBUTE produced:          🚀 DEPLOY verifies + enforces:
  a scanned, signed image     →    admission control checks the cosign signature
  a private-registry artifact →    OPA/Kyverno confirms the registry is on the allow-list
  a patched dependency set    →    (nothing further to check — already baked into the image)

  Namespaces + containers both provide isolation that matters here — separate namespaces
  for easypj-prod / easypj-staging keep a compromise in one from reaching the other.
</pre>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Where this guide covers it:</strong> Chapter 33 (namespace-based isolation — separate PSS-labeled <code>easypj-prod</code>/<code>easypj-dev</code> namespaces, §33.4), Chapter 24 (Admission Controllers — the mechanical gate that enforces "what/who/where"), Chapter 26 (verifying the cryptographic identity of an image at exactly this stage).</p>

                <h4>Phase 4 — Runtime: Three Areas, Protected Forever</h4>
                <p>Runtime is where a pod actually executes — and it's the largest phase in the official model, explicitly broken into <strong>three critical areas</strong>: access, compute, and storage. This one phase alone maps to roughly half of this entire guide's chapters, because "runtime" is where a cluster lives for the vast majority of its life.</p>

                <h5>🔐 Runtime — Access</h5>
                <p>"The Kubernetes API is what makes your cluster work. Protecting this API is key to providing effective cluster security" — official docs. Every one of these is a named, specific control:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔑</div>
                        <h5>Authentication + Authorization</h5>
                        <p>Effective AuthN (who are you) and AuthZ (what are you allowed to do) for every API call. Ch 8 (API access), Ch 9 (RBAC).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🤖</div>
                        <h5>ServiceAccounts</h5>
                        <p>Provide and manage security identities for workloads and cluster components — not just for humans. Ch 10.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5>TLS Everywhere</h5>
                        <p>Deploy the cluster using TLS — including node-to-control-plane traffic — and protect the encryption keys. Ch 8, 22.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📜</div>
                        <h5>CertificateSigningRequests</h5>
                        <p>If you use Kubernetes' own CSR API, pay special attention to restricting its misuse — a compromised CSR approval flow can mint valid cluster credentials. Ch 24.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h5>📦 Runtime — Compute</h5>
                <p>The official docs frame this precisely: "containers provide two things: isolation between applications and a mechanism to combine those isolated applications... isolation and aggregation mean that runtime security involves identifying trade-offs." This is the shared-kernel tension from §1.1, restated by the source docs themselves.</p>
