                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h5>💾 Runtime — Storage</h5>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔐</div>
                        <h5>Encryption at Rest</h5>
                        <p>Both for external storage plugins (volumes) and for the Kubernetes API objects themselves (etcd). Ch 19.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div>
                        <h5>Backups You've Actually Tested</h5>
                        <p>"Protect data durability using backups, and verify that you can restore them whenever needed" — the official docs explicitly call out testing the restore, not just taking the backup.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div>
                        <h5>Authenticated Storage Connections</h5>
                        <p>Authenticate connections between cluster nodes and any network storage they depend on — an unauthenticated NFS mount is a silent hole.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>Application-Level Encryption</h5>
                        <p>Implement data encryption inside your own application too — don't rely solely on the infrastructure layer for your most sensitive fields.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 A Precise Detail Worth Remembering</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">The official docs specifically call out <strong>hardware security modules (HSMs)</strong> for encryption keys: "generating these within specialized hardware provides the best protection against disclosure risks... a hardware security module can let you perform cryptographic operations without allowing the security key to be copied elsewhere." This is beyond what most CKS prep covers, but it's exactly the kind of real-world-administration detail that separates "passed the exam" from "actually runs production clusters."</p>
                </div>

                <h5>🌐 Networking and 📊 Observability — the Two Cross-Cutting Concerns</h5>
                <p>The official docs also name two concerns that cut across all three Runtime areas rather than belonging to just one:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div>
                        <h5>Networking</h5>
                        <p>NetworkPolicy (Ch 6) or a service mesh; some CNI plugins add network-level encryption via a VPN overlay. Your choice of CNI plugin directly shapes how strong "in-transit" protection actually is.</p>
