                    <h5>① The Kernel Primitives That Make (and Unmake) Container Isolation</h5>
                    <p>Section 1.1 said "every container shares one kernel." <em>Under the hood</em>, what actually separates PodA from PodB on the same node is a set of Linux kernel features — and knowing them precisely is what separates "I configured SecurityContext" from "I understand why SecurityContext works":</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Kernel Primitive</th><th>What It Isolates</th><th>What It Does NOT Isolate</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>PID namespace</strong></td><td>Process trees — PID 1 inside a container isn't PID 1 on the host</td><td>CPU scheduling — processes still compete for the same CPU cores</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>NET namespace</strong></td><td>Network interfaces, routing tables, ports (this IS a pod's private network)</td><td>The physical NIC and kernel network stack itself</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>MNT namespace</strong></td><td>The filesystem mount tree — a container's <code>/</code> isn't the host's <code>/</code></td><td>The underlying block device, or a bind-mounted hostPath</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>UTS namespace</strong></td><td>Hostname &amp; domain name</td><td>Nothing security-relevant — mostly cosmetic isolation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>IPC namespace</strong></td><td>Shared memory, semaphores, message queues</td><td>Kernel-level resources like the dentry cache</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>USER namespace</strong></td><td>UID/GID mapping — "root" inside can map to an unprivileged UID outside (rootless)</td><td>Not enabled by default in most K8s setups — a real hardening opportunity (Ch 13)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>cgroups v2</strong></td><td>CPU, memory, I/O <em>limits</em> — prevents one container from starving the node</td><td>NOT a security boundary — it caps resource use, it doesn't block syscalls</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Seccomp / capabilities</strong></td><td>WHICH syscalls and privileged operations a process may even attempt</td><td>Anything already permitted — a dangerous syscall you didn't block still works</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The critical realization:</strong> namespaces isolate <em>what a process can see</em>; cgroups limit <em>how much a process can use</em>; Seccomp/capabilities restrict <em>what a process is even allowed to attempt</em>. None of the three is a hardware boundary like a VM's hypervisor — <strong>all three run inside one shared kernel</strong>, so a single kernel vulnerability (a CVE in a namespace's implementation, for instance) can potentially defeat all of them at once. This is precisely why CKS spends five full chapters (14–18) making these primitives airtight instead of trusting them by default.</div>

                    <h5>🩹 A Named Example — CVE-2019-5736, the runc Container-Breakout</h5>
                    <p>The table above is not theoretical — here is a real, patched CVE that defeated exactly the MNT-namespace boundary just described. <strong>CVE-2019-5736</strong> let a malicious (or already-compromised) container overwrite the HOST's own <code>runc</code> binary, by abusing how <code>runc exec</code>/<code>runc run</code> opens <code>/proc/self/exe</code>:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
 1. Attacker controls a container's ENTRYPOINT, or gets it `exec`'d into by an admin/CI job.
 2. The container replaces its own target executable with a symlink pointing at
    /proc/self/exe — a self-referential handle to the very runc PROCESS ON THE HOST
    that is about to exec into this container.
 3. When the host's runc process execs the "container's" binary, it actually re-opens
    and OVERWRITES ITS OWN on-disk binary — because /proc/self/exe resolves to runc
    itself (a host process), not to anything inside the container's mount namespace.
 4. Every FUTURE `runc exec` / `runc run` on that HOST now runs attacker-controlled
    code, as root, entirely OUTSIDE any container. The MNT namespace never contained
    this at all — the compromise happened one layer BELOW it, in the runtime itself.</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Which layer/primitive was implicated?</td><td>The container runtime's own privileged exec path — NOT a hole in the MNT namespace mechanism itself</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Did SecurityContext (non-root, drop ALL) stop it?</td><td>❌ No. A non-root, capability-dropped container could still trigger it — the bug lived in the runtime, not the pod spec</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>What DOES stop it?</td><td>✅ Patching the container runtime (runc ≥ 1.0-rc7 / a patched containerd); a sandboxed runtime (gVisor/Kata) removes the shared-runtime assumption entirely</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Which CKS chapter owns this?</td><td>Ch 12/13 (version management &amp; node/runtime patching cadence), Ch 21 (sandboxed runtimes)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The lesson under the lesson:</strong> SecurityContext, Seccomp, and AppArmor all assume the <em>runtime that enforces them is itself trustworthy</em>. CVE-2019-5736 is a reminder that "harden the pod spec" and "patch the underlying container runtime" are two <strong>separate</strong> CKS obligations — Ch 18 covers the first, Ch 12–13's patching cadence covers the second, and skipping either one leaves a real, previously-exploited class of gap open.</div>

