                    <h5>🕸️ Admission Webhooks Under the Hood — Ordering, Timeouts &amp; Fail-Open vs Fail-Closed</h5>
                    <p>Stages 3 and 5 above ("mutating admission" and "validating admission") are not one monolithic check — each is an ordered CHAIN of independent HTTP callouts to webhook servers (Kyverno, OPA/Gatekeeper, or your own custom webhook). Each callout can succeed, time out, or error — and <strong>what happens on failure is a policy decision you control</strong>, one that quietly decides whether your policy engine is fail-closed or fail-open:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Webhook Setting</th><th>What It Controls</th><th>CKS-Relevant Default/Gotcha</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>failurePolicy</code></td><td><strong>Fail</strong> = reject the request if the webhook is unreachable or errors. <strong>Ignore</strong> = let the request through as if the webhook never existed.</td><td>A misconfigured <code>Ignore</code> means "policy engine is down" silently becomes "no policy enforced" — a fail-<strong>OPEN</strong> hole, not a fail-closed safety net.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>timeoutSeconds</code></td><td>How long the API server waits for the webhook to respond before treating the call as failed.</td><td>Too short + a slow policy engine = spurious failures under load; combined with <code>failurePolicy: Ignore</code>, a slow engine can silently stop enforcing precisely when the cluster is busiest.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Webhook ordering</td><td>Multiple mutating webhooks run in a defined sequence and can each further mutate the object; ALL validating webhooks see the FINAL, fully-mutated object.</td><td>A mutating webhook can never "undo" what validation already approved — validation always runs last, against the finished result.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>sideEffects</code></td><td>Declares whether the webhook has effects outside the admission review itself (e.g. writing an external audit log).</td><td>Must be declared accurately so <code>kubectl --dry-run</code> can safely skip webhooks that would otherwise trigger real, external side effects.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The single most dangerous misconfiguration in this whole pipeline:</strong> a validating webhook (e.g. Kyverno enforcing "no root containers") set to <code>failurePolicy: Ignore</code>. The instant that webhook's pod crashes or its endpoint becomes unreachable, EVERY policy it enforces silently stops applying — with zero error surfaced to whoever just submitted a now-unchecked manifest. Chapter 24 covers auditing a cluster's webhook configurations for exactly this trap before an attacker (or an outage) finds it first.</div>

                    <h5>③ The Reliability Math of Defense in Depth — Why Layering Isn't Just a Slogan</h5>
                    <p>"Defense in depth" can sound like a comforting platitude. It isn't — it's a statement about probability, and the math is worth seeing once so the principle stops being an article of faith:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Layers Deployed</th><th>If Each Layer Independently Blocks ~70% of Attacks</th><th>Chance an Attack Defeats ALL Layers</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>1 layer (e.g. RBAC only)</td><td>70% blocked</td><td>30% get through</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>3 layers (RBAC + NetworkPolicy + SecurityContext)</td><td>~97.3% blocked</td><td>0.3³ = 2.7% get through</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>5 layers (+ PSS + Seccomp)</td><td>~99.76% blocked</td><td>0.3⁵ ≈ 0.24% get through</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>8 layers (the full onion model, §1.3)</td><td>~99.9934% blocked</td><td>0.3⁸ ≈ 0.0066% get through</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>The exact percentages are illustrative, not measured — but the <strong>shape</strong> of the math is real and is the entire justification for layering: independent controls' failure probabilities <em>multiply</em>, not add. A control that's individually imperfect (and every control is) becomes part of a system that's extraordinarily hard to defeat completely, <em>as long as the layers are genuinely independent</em> — which is precisely why Chapter 13's "why one misconfiguration shouldn't cascade" mindset matters as much as any single control.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The one assumption that can break this math:</strong> the layers must fail <em>independently</em>. If one root cause (say, a compromised CI/CD pipeline) can simultaneously plant a bad RBAC binding, a permissive NetworkPolicy, AND a root container, the layers are correlated, not independent — and the real-world protection is far weaker than the naive multiplication suggests. This is why supply-chain integrity (Ch 23–27) is treated as its own domain, not folded into "just another layer": a single compromised pipeline can invalidate several layers at once.</div>

                    <h5>Worked Example — When "8 Independent Layers" Quietly Becomes 5</h5>
                    <p>Apply that assumption-check to one concrete, plausible incident: a compromised CI/CD pipeline pushes a poisoned Helm chart into easypj's cluster via a single <code>helm upgrade</code>.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Layer (from the Onion Model, §1.3)</th><th>Independently Defeated?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Layer 2: Network Segmentation</td><td>✅ Compromised</td><td>The poisoned chart ships its own permissive NetworkPolicy, silently replacing the safe default-deny one</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Layer 3: Pod Admission</td><td>✅ Compromised</td><td>The chart's own ServiceAccount already has the RBAC needed to relabel the namespace, disabling PSS enforcement before the pod is even created</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Layer 4: Container Hardening</td><td>✅ Compromised</td><td>The chart's pod spec simply omits SecurityContext entirely — nothing catches it, because Layer 3 no longer enforces</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Layers 5-7: Kernel, Detection, Response</td><td>❌ Still independent</td><td>Seccomp/AppArmor profiles, Falco rules, and AlertManager routing are not controlled by the Helm release at all — these 3 layers still hold</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="mb-0">One compromised root cause collapsed 3 of 8 layers <em>simultaneously</em> — the real, effective layer count for THIS incident dropped from 8 to 5, not 7. This is exactly why supply-chain integrity (image + chart signing, admission-time verification, Ch 23–26) is treated as protecting <em>the gate that decides what's allowed to run in the first place</em>, not as merely "one more layer alongside the rest": a gate failure is the one kind of failure that can cascade into several other layers at once.</p>

                    <h5>④ Real, Disclosed Container-Escape CVEs — Which Primitive Failed, and What Actually Closes the Gap</h5>
                    <p>Everything above has been architecture and probability theory. Here it is validated against five real, publicly disclosed vulnerabilities that broke exactly the boundaries this chapter describes — because the CKS exam, and real incident response, rewards recognizing <em>which layer</em> a real-world CVE defeats, not just reciting the layer names:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>CVE</th><th>Root Cause (Summary)</th><th>Primitive/Layer Defeated</th><th>What Actually Closes It</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>CVE-2019-5736</strong> (runc)</td><td>Malicious container tricks the host <code>runc</code> process into overwriting its own on-disk binary via <code>/proc/self/exe</code> (full trace above)</td><td>Container-runtime trust boundary, one layer below the MNT namespace</td><td>Patch runc/containerd promptly (Ch 12–13); a sandboxed runtime like gVisor/Kata removes the shared-runtime assumption entirely (Ch 21)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CVE-2020-8558</strong> (kube-proxy)</td><td>Traffic to a service bound only to a node's <code>localhost</code> was reachable from OTHER nodes and pods on the cluster network, not just that one node</td><td>NET namespace / network-segmentation assumption</td><td>Patch kube-proxy to a fixed version — NetworkPolicy alone does not fully cover this class of node-level localhost exposure (Ch 6, 13)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CVE-2021-25741</strong> (kubelet <code>subPath</code>)</td><td>A symlink-exchange race condition in <code>subPath</code> volume mounts let a pod read/write paths OUTSIDE its declared volume, including host paths</td><td>MNT namespace / volume-mount isolation</td><td>Patch the kubelet; avoid <code>subPath</code> with untrusted images where possible; <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code> limits the blast radius even if triggered (Ch 18)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CVE-2022-0185</strong> (Linux kernel)</td><td>A heap buffer overflow in the kernel's filesystem-context parsing, reachable from inside a container holding <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code> (e.g. via a user namespace), publicly demonstrated as a container-escape technique</td><td>The shared kernel itself — every namespace assumes the kernel underneath has no bugs</td><td>Drop <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code> as part of "drop ALL" (Ch 18); Seccomp blocking the syscalls involved (Ch 15); prompt kernel/node patching (Ch 13)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CVE-2024-21626</strong> ("Leaky Vessels", runc)</td><td>An internal file-descriptor leak let a malicious image's working-directory setup gain a file handle into the HOST filesystem</td><td>MNT namespace, via a runtime-internal implementation bug rather than a K8s config gap</td><td>Patch runc/containerd; image-provenance verification (Ch 26) reduces the odds of ever running the malicious image that triggers it</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The pattern across all five:</strong> notice that <strong>none</strong> were fixed by a NetworkPolicy, an RBAC change, or a smarter Kyverno policy alone — three (runc ×2, kernel) required patching the runtime or kernel itself, one required patching kube-proxy, and one required patching the kubelet. <strong>SecurityContext, Seccomp, and admission policies reduce the BLAST RADIUS of bugs like these — they do not substitute for keeping the runtime, kubelet, and kernel on a current, patched version.</strong> Chapter 13 (node hardening) and Chapter 12 (version management) exist precisely because "we have great pod-level policies" is never a complete answer to "are we patched against known, public container escapes."</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch1-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Kubernetes is insecure by default</strong> — flat networking, root containers, plaintext secrets in etcd, zero audit logging. Every CKS control exists to fix one of these five defaults.</li>
                    <li><strong>The shared kernel is the root architectural risk</strong> — every container on a node shares one Linux kernel; a single kernel exploit can reach every pod on that node.</li>
                    <li><strong>The 4Cs model organizes everything</strong> — Cloud → Cluster → Container → Code, nested, each independently secured; a breach at an outer layer compromises all inner layers.</li>
                    <li><strong>Five principles drive every task</strong> — Least Privilege, Defense in Depth, Zero Trust, Immutability, Audit Everything are the mindset behind every kubectl command in this guide.</li>
                    <li><strong>Detection speed is the difference that matters</strong> — 204 days to detect without CKS controls vs minutes-to-hours with Falco + audit logs.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch1-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>"My cluster is behind a firewall, so it's secure."</strong> Firewalls protect north-south traffic only. They do nothing for east-west (pod-to-pod) traffic — a compromised pod can attack every other pod without NetworkPolicies (Ch 6).</li>
                    <li><strong>"I use RBAC, so my cluster is locked down."</strong> RBAC controls API access only. It does NOT control what a container does at runtime — you need SecurityContext + Seccomp + AppArmor for that (Ch 14-18).</li>
                    <li><strong>"My images are scanned, so my supply chain is secure."</strong> Scanning finds KNOWN vulnerabilities; it does not prevent a malicious image from being pushed. You need signing + admission control to verify provenance (Ch 23-26).</li>
                    <li><strong>"Security is a one-time setup."</strong> New CVEs appear daily. Security is continuous — scan, monitor, and update continuously (Ch 28-35).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch1-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Ask "which 4C layer?" first.</strong> Before touching a task, classify it — Cloud/Cluster/Container/Code — and you'll immediately know which chapter's tool to reach for.</li>
                    <li><strong>Precision over speed.</strong> A wildcard in RBAC, a missing <code>runAsUser</code>, or skipping verification all cost points — the exam rewards correctness, not raw speed.</li>
                    <li><strong>Verify after every configuration.</strong> The exam grades on verification — always run a check command (<code>kubectl auth can-i</code>, a connectivity test, etc.) after every change.</li>
