            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch1-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 1 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're auditing a cluster and discover pods in "default" can talk freely to "kube-system". Which 4C layer, and which resource fixes it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Layer:</strong> Cluster. <strong>Fix:</strong> a NetworkPolicy in "default" restricting egress to kube-system.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">before → after, one NetworkPolicy</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># BEFORE: nothing exists — every pod in "default" reaches kube-system freely</span>

<span class="comment"># AFTER: apply this, and only DNS to kube-system survives</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: restrict-to-dns-only
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: ["Egress"]
  egress:
  - to: [{namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system}}}]
    ports: [{protocol: UDP, port: 53}]</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>NetworkPolicy operates at the Cluster layer — it controls pod-to-pod communication via the CNI. Without one, Kubernetes allows all traffic between any pods in any namespace by default. The policy above keeps DNS resolution alive (UDP/53) while closing every other path into kube-system.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Difference between a 401 and a 403 error — which system rejected you, and what do you check?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>401</strong> = Authentication failure (bad cert/token — check kubeconfig). <strong>403</strong> = Authorization failure (known but not allowed — check with <code>kubectl auth can-i</code>).</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Stage That Rejected You</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What to Check</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>401</strong> Unauthorized</td><td>Authentication (Stage 1)</td><td>The server doesn't know who you are</td><td>Cert expiry, <code>~/.kube/config</code>, OIDC token</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>403</strong> Forbidden</td><td>Authorization (Stage 2)</td><td>You're known, but not allowed to do this</td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i &lt;verb&gt; &lt;resource&gt;</code>, RBAC bindings</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The API server chain is AuthN → AuthZ → Admission. 401 means identity was rejected; 403 means identity is known but lacks permission. Never confuse the two on the exam — fixing a 401 means renewing credentials, fixing a 403 means changing RBAC, and applying the wrong fix wastes time you don't have.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod runs as root with all capabilities. Which mechanism auto-rejects it, and what label enables it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Pod Security Admission (PSA) with the Restricted profile: <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code> on the namespace.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">turn on the gate, then watch it reject</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl label ns easypj-prod pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted

<span class="comment"># Any pod with runAsNonRoot missing/false, or capabilities not dropped, is now rejected AT CREATION:</span>
kubectl run bad-pod --image=nginx -n easypj-prod
<span class="comment"># Error: pods "bad-pod" is forbidden: violates PodSecurity "restricted:latest": ...</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>PSA replaced PodSecurityPolicy in 1.25. The <code>enforce</code> label REJECTS violating pods before creation — a preventive control tested extensively on the CKS. Chapter 17 covers the full three-profile ladder (Privileged/Baseline/Restricted) and the difference between <code>enforce</code>, <code>audit</code>, and <code>warn</code> modes.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Least Privilege via ServiceAccounts: what's the default risk, and the fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Every pod auto-mounts the namespace's "default" SA token unless overridden. Fix: dedicated SA with <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">stop the silent auto-mount</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-web-sa   <span class="comment"># a dedicated, purpose-built SA — never "default"</span>
  automountServiceAccountToken: false  <span class="comment"># no token mounted unless the app truly calls the API</span>
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: easypj:v2</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A compromised pod can use its mounted token to make authenticated API calls with whatever RBAC the SA has — the default SA should never carry bound permissions. If the app genuinely needs API access, bind the dedicated SA to a tightly-scoped Role instead of relying on the namespace's shared default identity.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Map the 4Cs to one CKS control each, and name the layer entirely outside Kubernetes' control.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>4C Layer</th><th>Example Control</th><th>K8s-Managed?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>☁️ Cloud</td><td>IAM role, VPC firewall</td><td>❌ Not K8s-managed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>☸️ Cluster</td><td>RBAC, NetworkPolicy</td><td>✅ K8s-managed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>📦 Container</td><td>SecurityContext, Seccomp</td><td>✅ K8s-managed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>💻 Code</td><td>Trivy scan, cosign signature</td><td>✅ K8s-managed (via CI/CD + admission)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The Cloud layer is critical precisely because K8s cannot fix cloud misconfigurations — an over-permissive IAM role can expose etcd even with perfect K8s RBAC. This is also why the CKS exam never asks you to configure a VPC or IAM policy: it's the one layer entirely outside the API server's reach.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code> and save. What happens, and what kind of pod is this?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>A <strong>Static Pod</strong> — managed directly by the kubelet, not the API server. The kubelet detects the change and restarts the container; kubectl will drop for 30-60s.</p>
                    <div class="diagram-container">
                        <div class="diagram-title">No API server needed — the kubelet watches the directory directly</div>
<pre>
  👤 you edit kube-apiserver.yaml on disk
        │
        ▼
  🤖 kubelet (polling /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ every ~20s) sees the file changed
        │
        ▼
  🔄 kubelet kills the old static pod's container, starts a new one from the new spec
        │
        ▼
  📡 API server is briefly unreachable (kubectl hangs/errors for ~30-60s) — then it's back
</pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Static pods bootstrap the control plane so it doesn't depend on an already-running API server. Editing a manifest = automatic restart, no <code>kubectl apply</code> needed — the kubelet's own filesystem watch is the entire mechanism, with no scheduler or controller involved at all.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Defense in Depth: an easypj pod is compromised. Name four layers that limit blast radius.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>NetworkPolicy (blocks lateral movement) · SecurityContext (non-root, readOnlyRootFS) · Seccomp/AppArmor (blocks dangerous syscalls/files) · Falco + audit logs (detects and alerts).</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   💥 easypj pod compromised — attacker has a shell inside
        │
        ├─ 🌐 NetworkPolicy    → can't reach postgres or other pods laterally
        ├─ 👤 SecurityContext  → non-root + readOnlyRootFS: can't escalate or persist changes
        ├─ 🔒 Seccomp/AppArmor → the exploit's next syscall/file-read is blocked outright
        └─ 🦅 Falco + audit    → the shell itself triggers an alert within minutes
   Each layer is a SEPARATE holdout — the attacker must defeat all four, not just one.</pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>No single control is the only protection — if one fails, the next still holds. This is the "Swiss cheese" model tested throughout the CKS, and it's the exact same reasoning behind §1.1's Under-the-Hood reliability-math table: independent layers multiply their combined effectiveness rather than adding it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A cluster has <code>--anonymous-auth=true</code>. What can an attacker do, and what's the fix + verification command?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Reach any endpoint not requiring auth as <code>system:anonymous</code>. Fix: <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>. Verify: <code>kubectl get pods --as=system:anonymous</code> should return Forbidden.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the exact CKS fix-and-verify sequence</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Prove the exposure (this should NOT succeed on a hardened cluster)</span>
kubectl get pods --as=system:anonymous -A

<span class="comment"># 2. Fix — edit the static pod manifest (kubelet auto-restarts)</span>
sudo sed -i 's/--anonymous-auth=true/--anonymous-auth=false/' \
  /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

<span class="comment"># 3. Re-verify — this must now return Forbidden</span>
kubectl get pods --as=system:anonymous -A</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--anonymous-auth=true</code> is the DEFAULT. If any Role/ClusterRole is bound to <code>system:anonymous</code> or <code>system:unauthenticated</code>, anonymous callers gain those permissions. This is the exact root cause of the Tesla 2018 breach (§1.1) — an exposed, unauthenticated dashboard.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is Immutability, and how do you enforce it at both the image and pod level?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Pod:</strong> <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code>, <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>. <strong>Image:</strong> pin by digest (<code>image@sha256:...</code>) instead of a mutable tag.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">immutability at both levels, in one manifest</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    image: easypj@sha256:8f3e1c9b2a...  <span class="comment"># pinned by digest, NOT :latest</span>
    securityContext:
      runAsNonRoot: true
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Once deployed, nothing changes — fixes go through Git → CI/CD → rebuild → redeploy, never a live edit. This eliminates configuration drift and attacker persistence: even a successful exec into the container can't write a backdoor to disk that survives a restart, and a digest-pinned image can never silently change underneath you the way a mutable <code>:latest</code> tag can.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List one audit command per 4C layer to discover a live cluster's complete attack surface.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">one audit command per 4C layer</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># ☁️  Cloud — inventory the nodes (their cloud IAM roles are out-of-cluster)</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide

<span class="comment"># ☸️  Cluster — what can everyone actually do?</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list -A

<span class="comment"># 📦 Container — which pods are running as root?</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq -r '.items[] | select(.spec.containers[].securityContext.runAsNonRoot != true) | .metadata.name'

<span class="comment"># 💻 Code — what images (and therefore what CVEs) are actually deployed?</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq -r '.items[].spec.containers[].image' | sort -u</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the exact audit approach for a "find all security issues" exam task — use JSON output + jq to query across all namespaces at once rather than inspecting pods manually. Run all four in sequence and you have a complete, layer-by-layer attack-surface snapshot in under a minute.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch1-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 1 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (8)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">The CKS is performance-based — you don't just know a concept, you produce the fix on a live terminal. These 8 drills mirror that: a broken or unaudited state, and you write the real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A cluster you've inherited has <code>--anonymous-auth=true</code> on the API server. Prove the exposure exists, then fix it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">prove it, fix it, verify it</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Prove the exposure — this should be Forbidden, not a real response</span>
kubectl get pods --as=system:anonymous -A

<span class="comment"># 2. Fix: edit the static-pod manifest, the kubelet auto-restarts the apiserver</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment">#   change: --anonymous-auth=false</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Verify the fix took effect (allow ~30-60s for the restart)</span>
kubectl get pods --as=system:anonymous -A   <span class="comment"># now: Forbidden</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the single most common CKS "prove and fix" pattern: reproduce the bad behavior with <code>--as</code>, edit the static-pod manifest, then re-run the exact same command to prove the fix. Never skip the verification step — the exam grades on it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Namespace <code>easypj-prod</code> has zero NetworkPolicies. Apply a default-deny baseline and prove it blocks traffic.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">default-deny, then prove it</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-all
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: ["Ingress", "Egress"]</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">prove it blocks traffic</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f default-deny-all.yaml
kubectl run probe --image=busybox:1.28 -n easypj-prod --rm -it --restart=Never \
  -- wget -T 3 easypj-web:8000   <span class="comment"># should now time out</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>An empty <code>podSelector: {}</code> matches ALL pods in the namespace; empty ingress/egress rules with both policyTypes listed means "deny everything by default." This is Chapter 6's foundation pattern — you will type this exact YAML from memory dozens of times in this guide.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Audit every pod in the cluster for root containers in one command — no manual per-pod inspection allowed.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cluster-wide root-container audit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq -r '
  .items[] |
  select(.spec.containers[].securityContext.runAsNonRoot != true) |
  "\(.metadata.namespace)/\(.metadata.name)"'</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The <code>-o json | jq</code> pattern is the single highest-leverage exam technique for "find every X across the cluster" tasks — it's the difference between checking 200 pods by hand and one query. Every domain has its own version of this same pattern (Ch 18 covers the SecurityContext-specific queries in depth).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Prove that a Kubernetes Secret is "encoded, not encrypted" — then check whether this cluster has encryption at rest configured.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">decode it yourself, then check the config</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Anyone who can read the Secret object can trivially decode it</span>
kubectl get secret easypj-db-creds -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d

<span class="comment"># 2. Is encryption-at-rest even configured on this cluster?</span>
sudo cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml | grep encryption-provider-config
sudo cat /etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml 2>/dev/null   <span class="comment"># if absent: NOT encrypted</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Base64 is an <em>encoding</em>, not encryption — it's reversible with zero secret material required. Without an <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> wired into the API server's <code>--encryption-provider-config</code> flag, everything you just decoded is stored in etcd exactly as plainly. Chapter 19 builds this configuration end to end.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Check whether this cluster has audit logging enabled at all, and if not, name the two flags required to turn it on.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check, then know the minimum flags</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Is it already on?</span>
grep -E 'audit-log-path|audit-policy-file' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

<span class="comment"># Minimum two flags to enable it (plus a policy file and a hostPath mount):</span>
<span class="comment">#   --audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</span>
<span class="comment">#   --audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>By default, neither flag is set — a fresh kubeadm cluster logs nothing. Chapter 28 builds a real audit policy from scratch and wires both flags plus the required volume mounts into the static pod manifest.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Reproduce the "Day in the Life" 17:00 incident: an intern ran <code>kubectl exec easypj -- bash</code>. Without Falco installed yet, how do you find this in the audit log alone?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find the exec in the audit trail</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">cat /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log | \
  jq 'select(.objectRef.subresource == "exec")' | \
  jq '{user: .user.username, pod: .objectRef.name, ns: .objectRef.namespace, time: .requestReceivedTimestamp}'</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Every <code>pods/exec</code> call is a distinct audit event with <code>objectRef.subresource == "exec"</code> — this is the exact forensic query for "who exec'd into what, and when." Falco (Ch 29) gives you the same fact in real time instead of after the fact; audit logs are your ground truth either way.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Map out exactly what the <code>easypj-ci</code> ServiceAccount can do across the whole cluster — don't read RBAC YAML, ask the API directly.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">ask the API, don't parse YAML by hand</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:easypj-ci -A</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>kubectl auth can-i --list</code> is the authoritative source of truth for what an identity can do — it resolves every Role/ClusterRole binding for you, which is far more reliable than manually tracing YAML across possibly a dozen RoleBindings. This is a guaranteed exam technique for any "verify least privilege" task.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj's Dockerfile starts <code>FROM python:3.11</code> and the resulting image is 1.2GB. Rewrite it as a multi-stage build to shrink the attack surface.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">multi-stage build — build tools never ship</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-dockerfile"><span class="comment"># Stage 1: build — has compilers, pip cache, dev headers</span>
FROM python:3.11 AS builder
WORKDIR /app
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --user --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

<span class="comment"># Stage 2: final — only the runtime + installed packages ship</span>
FROM python:3.11-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=builder /root/.local /root/.local
COPY . .
USER 1000
ENV PATH=/root/.local/bin:$PATH
CMD ["gunicorn", "jobpost.wsgi:application", "--bind", "0.0.0.0:8000"]</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The build stage's compilers, headers, and pip cache never reach the final image — only the compiled packages and your application code do. Combined with a slim base and a non-root <code>USER</code>, this is the exact pattern that took easypj's before/after image size from 1.2GB to ~180MB (§1.1). Chapter 23 goes further with distroless bases.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

