            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s2-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.1 How Kubernetes Gets Attacked — The Attack Surface</h3>
                <p>The Kubernetes attack surface is <strong>massive</strong>. Unlike a traditional server with one entry point (SSH), a K8s cluster has the API server, the kubelet on every node, the etcd database, the container runtime, the network overlay, and hundreds of pods — each a potential entry point. Here are the seven most common attack vectors, ranked by real-world frequency:</p>

                <h4>The 7 Most Common Kubernetes Attack Vectors</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">1️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Exposed API Server (No Authentication)</h5>
                        <p>The API server listens on port 6443. If <code>--anonymous-auth=true</code> (the default!), anyone who can reach that port has full cluster access. <strong>This is how Tesla was breached in 2018.</strong> Attackers scan the internet for open K8s API ports using Shodan and Masscan. Fix: Chapter 8.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">2️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Misconfigured RBAC — Over-Permission</h5>
                        <p>Developers get <code>cluster-admin</code> "to make things easier." ServiceAccounts default to full namespace access. A single compromised credential gives attackers the keys to the kingdom. Fix: Chapter 9.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">3️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Vulnerable Container Images — Known CVEs</h5>
                        <p>A container image left unrebuilt accumulates dozens of known vulnerabilities within months. If you deploy <code>python:3.9</code> without scanning, you're shipping CVEs directly into production. Attackers exploit known CVEs in frameworks like Django, Flask, or even the base OS packages. Fix: Chapter 23.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">4️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Secrets in Plaintext — Environment Variables &amp; Git</h5>
                        <p>Database passwords in <code>configmap.yaml</code>. API keys in <code>.env</code> files committed to Git. ServiceAccount tokens mounted in every pod by default. A single <code>kubectl describe pod</code> or a Git history search reveals everything. Fix: Chapters 10, 19, 20.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">5️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Privilege Escalation — Root Containers</h5>
                        <p>Containers run as root by default. If an attacker exploits any application vulnerability, they immediately have root inside the container. From there: container escape, node compromise, cluster takeover. Fix: Chapters 14, 15, 18.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">6️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Lateral Movement — Flat Network</h5>
                        <p>No NetworkPolicies = every pod can reach every other pod on every port. One compromised pod gives attackers access to your database, your cache, your message queue, and every other service in the cluster. Fix: Chapter 6.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">7️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Supply Chain — Malicious Images</h5>
                        <p>Pulling images from <code>docker.io/random-user/app</code> without verification. No image signing. No registry allowlist. Attackers publish typosquatted images with backdoors. Fix: Chapters 24, 26, 27.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

