                <h4>Who's Actually Attacking Your Cluster? — Threat Actor Profiles</h4>
                <p>The 7 vectors and the four eras above describe <em>how</em> and <em>when</em> attacks evolved — but the CKS mindset sharpens further once you also know <em>who</em> is on the other end of the connection, because different actors reach for different vectors for entirely different reasons. Every profile below maps directly onto a breach or an ongoing campaign already in this chapter:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-5">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🪙</div>
                        <h5>Opportunistic Cybercriminal</h5>
                        <p class="text-sm">Wants fast, low-effort money — stolen compute or a ransom payment. Tools: Shodan/Masscan sweeps, off-the-shelf exploit kits, public miner images. No custom malware required. <strong>Tesla (2018)</strong> fits here — the attacker didn't invent anything, they found an unlocked door and dropped a miner.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🤖</div>
                        <h5>Automated Worm / Botnet</h5>
                        <p class="text-sm">No human decides per-target — self-propagating malware families like <strong>Kinsing</strong>, <strong>TeamTNT</strong>, and the Kubernetes-specific <strong>Hildegard</strong> variant continuously scan the entire internet for exposed Docker daemon APIs and anonymous kubelet endpoints (§2.1's kubelet callout above), then auto-deploy XMRig-style miners at machine speed. This is the single MOST common real-world attack against Kubernetes today — far more frequent than any named breach, because it never sleeps.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🕵️</div>
                        <h5>Lone Skilled Individual</h5>
                        <p class="text-sm">Motivated by notoriety, thrill, or a grudge more than money. <strong>Capital One (2019)</strong> fits here — a former cloud-provider engineer with deep infrastructure knowledge chained the SSRF/metadata exploit, and was caught largely because she posted about the haul publicly rather than selling it quietly.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏢</div>
                        <h5>Malicious / Compromised Insider</h5>
                        <p class="text-sm">Wants money or leverage, obtained by abusing legitimate access instead of breaking in. <strong>Shopify (2020)</strong> fits here — two members of Shopify's own support team accessed merchant data on behalf of an outside party; no exploit needed, because RBAC already trusted them. Against the REAL mechanism (an already-trusted identity abusing legitimate access), NetworkPolicy alone never stops this — least-privilege RBAC + audit logging + anomaly detection (Ch 9, 28, 29) are what actually do. §2.2's breach card teaches the K8s-native stand-in for this pattern instead — a compromised pod moving laterally across namespaces — which default-deny NetworkPolicy (Ch 6) DOES stop, because that's the exploitable technical mechanism the CKS exam tests; just don't confuse the teaching simplification with the real 2020 incident's true root cause of trusted human access, a layer no NetworkPolicy can reach.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚩</div>
                        <h5>Nation-State APT</h5>
                        <p class="text-sm">Wants long-term strategic access, espionage, or disruption — money is rarely the goal. <strong>SolarWinds (2020)</strong> fits here — publicly attributed by the US government to Russia's SVR (tracked as APT29 / Cozy Bear), this actor had the patience to compromise a BUILD PIPELINE rather than a running cluster, and stayed hidden for 9-14 months (see Under the Hood §④).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Actor Type</th><th>Real-World Frequency</th><th>Typical Sophistication</th><th>Typical Dwell Time</th><th>This Chapter's Example</th></tr></thead>
