                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why This Matters for CKS: The Fix Doesn't Change, But the Priority Does</h5>
                    <p>Notice that every actor type above is stopped by controls this guide already teaches — RBAC, NetworkPolicy, encryption, Seccomp/AppArmor, Falco, audit logging. Knowing WHO you're likely defending against doesn't change WHICH tool you reach for; it changes what you harden <em>first</em>. A cluster facing mostly automated worms (the overwhelming majority of real-world Kubernetes compromises) gets the fastest ROI from closing Vector 1 and Vector 5 — the two defaults worms scan for first. A cluster handling regulated data should never treat insider risk or nation-state patience as "unlikely," because RBAC and audit logging are exactly what those two actor types bypass or exploit.</p>
                </div>

