                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Root Cause of EVERY Default K8s Attack</h5>
                    <p>Notice the pattern: every single attack above succeeds because <strong>Kubernetes ships with security features OFF by default.</strong> Anonymous auth: ON. Root containers: YES. Flat network: YES. Plaintext secrets: YES. No audit: YES. The CKS exam is not testing advanced security concepts — it's testing whether you know which defaults to turn OFF and how to configure the secure alternatives. <strong>Every question is essentially: "The default is insecure. Fix it."</strong></p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Precision Check — "Anonymous Auth Is On" Doesn't ALONE Mean "Anyone Has Full Access"</h5>
                    <p>This chapter (and most CKS prep material) teaches Vector 1 as "<code>--anonymous-auth=true</code> is the default → anyone who reaches port 6443 has full cluster access." That's the right exam-level mental model, but the current official docs (<code>kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/authentication/#anonymous-requests</code>) describe a more precise two-step mechanism worth knowing exactly, because it changes what you verify:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Teaching Simplification (this chapter, most CKS material)</th><th>Precise Official Mechanism</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>What "anonymous-auth=true" does</strong></td><td>Grants unauthenticated callers cluster access</td><td>Only <em>authenticates</em> the caller as identity <code>system:anonymous</code> / group <code>system:unauthenticated</code> — nothing more</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>What actually grants access</strong></td><td>(treated as automatic)</td><td>The <strong>authorizer</strong> (RBAC/ABAC) must separately have a Role/ClusterRole bound to <code>system:anonymous</code> or <code>system:unauthenticated</code> — the docs are explicit: "The built-in ABAC and RBAC authorizers require explicit authorization of the <code>system:anonymous</code> user or the <code>system:unauthenticated</code> group"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Why the simplification still holds in practice</strong></td><td>—</td><td>Legacy clusters upgraded from ABAC-era policies, over-broad <code>system:discovery</code>/<code>system:basic-user</code> bindings, or a Dashboard/tool whose OWN ServiceAccount (not <code>system:anonymous</code> itself) carries a <code>cluster-admin</code> ClusterRoleBinding (exactly Tesla's 2018 case, §2.2) reliably reproduce "anonymous auth ⇒ full access" as an end-to-end outcome, even though two separate misconfigurations chained to get there</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Is "the default" even unconditional?</strong></td><td>Treated as always-on out of the box</td><td>Not quite — the docs' precise wording is: "Anonymous access is enabled by default if an <strong>authorization mode</strong> other than <code>AlwaysAllow</code> is used." Anonymous auth's default is <em>coupled</em> to your authorization mode, not a standalone default. In practice this distinction rarely saves you: every kubeadm/managed cluster ships RBAC (not the legacy <code>AlwaysAllow</code> mode) as its authorizer, so the "if RBAC is in use" condition is met almost universally — which is exactly why treating it as "the default" is a safe exam simplification, just not a technically unconditional one</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">verify BOTH halves of the chain — auth is on is not the whole story</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Half 1 — is anonymous auth even enabled? (almost always yes, it's the default)
kubectl get pods --as=system:anonymous -A   # Forbidden = authz is doing its job

# Half 2 — the half most people forget to check: does ANY binding touch the anonymous identity?
kubectl get clusterrolebindings,rolebindings -A -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.subjects[]?.name=="system:anonymous" or .subjects[]?.name=="system:unauthenticated") |
  "\(.metadata.namespace // "cluster-wide")/\(.metadata.name) -> \(.roleRef.name)"'
# ANY output here is a critical finding — Vector 1, confirmed end-to-end, not just "auth is on"</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>Exam takeaway:</strong> don't stop at "I set <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>." A hardened answer also proves no RoleBinding/ClusterRoleBinding ever targeted <code>system:anonymous</code> or <code>system:unauthenticated</code> — the second half of the chain is exactly what the Q1 drill in this chapter's Scenario &amp; Command Drills exercises.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🔭 Where This Is Heading — the <code>--anonymous-auth</code> Flag Is Being Superseded, Not Replaced Overnight</h5>
                    <p>This chapter (like most CKS material) teaches anonymous access as a single on/off <code>--anonymous-auth</code> flag — and for exam purposes that framing is still correct and still the fastest way to reason about Vector 1. But the current official docs describe where the API server is actually heading: a structured <strong>AuthenticationConfiguration</strong> file, whose anonymous-auth support graduated to stable in Kubernetes v1.34. The file-based approach doesn't change WHAT anonymous access is (still <code>system:anonymous</code> / <code>system:unauthenticated</code>) — it changes the GRANULARITY of control, letting you enable anonymous access for specific safe endpoints only (like <code>/livez</code>, <code>/readyz</code>, <code>/healthz</code> — so load balancers and monitoring can health-check the API server) while keeping every other endpoint fully authenticated. The docs are explicit that the two mechanisms are mutually exclusive: "If you set the anonymous field in the <code>AuthenticationConfiguration</code> file then you cannot set the <code>--anonymous-auth</code> command line option." <strong>Practical takeaway:</strong> don't be surprised on a newer cluster if <code>--anonymous-auth</code> is absent from the API server's flags entirely — check for an <code>AuthenticationConfiguration</code> file before concluding anonymous access is misconfigured or unset.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>🔍 The Attack Surface This List Doesn't Call Out By Name: Every Node's Own Kubelet API</h5>
                    <p>Vector 1 above covers the <strong>control-plane</strong> API server on port 6443 — but every single <strong>node</strong> also runs its own separate HTTPS API, the <strong>kubelet API</strong>, typically on port <strong>10250</strong> (with an older, even more dangerous unauthenticated read-only variant on port <strong>10255</strong> in pre-hardened clusters). The official docs are blunt about the default here: <em>"Kubelets expose HTTPS endpoints which grant powerful control over the node and containers... by default kubelets allow unauthenticated access."</em> This is a genuinely distinct attack surface from Vector 1 — a locked-down API server does nothing to protect a node whose kubelet itself has no authentication.</p>
                    <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                        <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔓</div><h5>What an Open Kubelet Grants</h5><p class="text-sm">Direct <code>exec</code>/<code>run</code>/<code>logs</code> against every pod on that node, and a <code>/pods</code> or <code>/runningpods</code> listing — bypassing the API server's RBAC entirely, because the kubelet has its own independent authorization mode.</p></div>
                        <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div><h5>Real-World Pattern</h5><p class="text-sm">The same Shodan/Masscan sweeps that found Tesla's Dashboard (§2.2) have historically also turned up thousands of internet-reachable kubelets — same reconnaissance technique, a completely different door.</p></div>
                        <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div><h5>The CKS Fix</h5><p class="text-sm">Webhook authn/authz on the kubelet (not <code>AlwaysAllow</code>), <code>anonymous-auth: false</code> and <code>readOnlyPort: 0</code> in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>, plus the <strong>NodeRestriction</strong> admission plugin so even an authenticated kubelet can only modify objects tied to its own node. <span class="badge">Ch 4 §4.4</span> <span class="badge">Ch 11</span></p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Is Your Cluster Vulnerable? — CKS Decision Tree</h4>
                <p>Walk through each question honestly. Every "Yes" is a concrete, exploitable vulnerability the CKS exam expects you to know how to fix. The tree is ordered by attack criticality — fix the top item first.</p>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="decision-node question">🧠 Is anonymous-auth enabled on the API server?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ YES — Anyone can access your cluster! Fix: Chapter 8 (disable anonymous auth) → RBAC (Ch 9)</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">✅ NO — Good. Move to next check.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">📦 Do your containers run as root (uid 0)?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ YES — Root escape risk! Fix: SecurityContext runAsNonRoot (Ch 18) + Seccomp (Ch 15) + AppArmor (Ch 14)</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">✅ NO — Good. Move to next check.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">🌐 Is there no default-deny NetworkPolicy?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ YES — Flat network = lateral movement risk! Fix: NetworkPolicy (Ch 6)</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">✅ NO — Good. Move to next check.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">🔐 Are etcd secrets stored without encryption?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ YES — Secrets are plaintext! Fix: EncryptionConfiguration (Ch 19)</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">✅ NO — Good. Move to next check.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">📝 Is audit logging disabled (the default)?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ YES — No traceability! Fix: Audit Policy (Ch 28) + Falco (Ch 29)</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">🎉 NO — Your cluster has basic hardening. Now add Falco (Ch 29), image scanning (Ch 23), and supply chain controls (Ch 25-27).</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p><strong>How to interpret your results:</strong> Count your "Yes" answers. Anonymous auth → Domain 2 (Cluster Hardening — Ch 8, where <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> lives). Root containers → Domain 3 (System Hardening). Flat network / plaintext secrets → Domain 4 (Minimize Microservice Vulnerabilities). No audit logging → Domain 6 (Monitoring &amp; Runtime Security). Three or more "Yes" answers means your cluster would fail a basic penetration test. The rest of this guide (Chapters 4-32) walks you through fixing every single "Yes" systematically.</p>
            </section>

