            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s2-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.3 Kubernetes Security Threat Model (STRIDE)</h3>
                <p>STRIDE is a threat modeling framework developed by Microsoft that categorizes security threats into six types. When applied to Kubernetes, it provides a systematic way to identify and mitigate every category of risk.</p>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 What is STRIDE?</h5>
                    <p><strong>S</strong>poofing · <strong>T</strong>ampering · <strong>R</strong>epudiation · <strong>I</strong>nformation Disclosure · <strong>D</strong>enial of Service · <strong>E</strong>levation of Privilege. Every security threat falls into one of these six categories. For each, we identify the K8s-specific attack, the impact on easypj, and the CKS control that mitigates it.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>STRIDE × CKS Coverage Matrix</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Threat</th><th>What It Means</th><th>K8s Attack Example</th><th>Impact on easypj</th><th>CKS Mitigation</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>S</strong>poofing</td><td>Impersonating user/service</td><td>Fake ServiceAccount token, stolen kubeconfig</td><td>Attacker acts as easypj pod, queries DB</td><td>RBAC (Ch 9), Bound SA tokens (Ch 10), mTLS (Ch 22)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>T</strong>ampering</td><td>Modifying data/configs</td><td>Modify etcd, alter pod specs, inject ConfigMap</td><td>Attacker changes easypj Deployment to malicious image</td><td>etcd encryption (Ch 19), RBAC (Ch 9), image signing (Ch 26)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>R</strong>epudiation</td><td>Actions without audit trails</td><td>No audit logging → attacker denies actions</td><td>Attacker deletes jobpost data; no proof of who or when</td><td>Audit logging (Ch 28), Falco (Ch 29)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>I</strong>nfo Disclosure</td><td>Secrets or data leaked</td><td>Secrets in plaintext env vars, unencrypted etcd</td><td>User emails, DB passwords, SECRET_KEY exposed</td><td>Encryption at rest (Ch 19), Vault (Ch 20)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>D</strong>enial of Service</td><td>Resource exhaustion</td><td>Unlimited CPU/memory, fork bombs, disk exhaustion</td><td>Attacker crashes easypj pods → listings unavailable</td><td>ResourceQuota, LimitRange, PSS (Ch 17), admission control (Ch 24)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>E</strong>levation of Priv</td><td>Gaining more permissions</td><td>Root container → node escape, hostPath mount, privileged pod</td><td>Attacker escapes easypj container → compromises node</td><td>Non-root (Ch 18), Seccomp (Ch 15), AppArmor (Ch 14)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>STRIDE Applied to easypj: Code-Level Threat Walkthrough</h4>
                <p>Let's walk through each STRIDE category with REAL code examples from the easypj/jobpost Django app. This is what the attacker sees and exploits:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Spoofing: How an Attacker Impersonates the easypj ServiceAccount</div>
<pre>
  # DEFAULT easypj pod (no security hardening):
  $ kubectl run easypj --image=easypj:latest

  # The pod gets a ServiceAccount token mounted automatically:
  $ kubectl exec -it easypj -- cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
  eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsImtpZCI6IiJ9...  ← THIS IS A JWT WITH CLUSTER PERMISSIONS

  # Decode it:
  $ echo "eyJhbGciOi..." | cut -d'.' -f2 | base64 -d | python -m json.tool
  { "iss": "kubernetes/serviceaccount", "kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace": "default",
    "kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/service-account.name": "default",
    "sub": "system:serviceaccount:default:default" }

  # If 'default' SA has cluster-admin:
  $ kubectl --token="eyJhbGciOi..." get secrets --all-namespaces
  → ALL secrets in the cluster. Database passwords. TLS keys. Everything.

  # THE FIX (Ch 10):
  # 1. automountServiceAccountToken: false (if pod doesn't need API access)
  # 2. Create dedicated SA with minimal RBAC
  # 3. Use projected tokens with expiration
</pre>
