                            <tr><td><strong>Typical Base Score</strong></td><td>Combined severity</td><td><strong>9.8 CRITICAL</strong></td><td>7.8 HIGH (until chained into an escape — then 9.8+)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>Three real, publicly disclosed Kubernetes CVEs make this concrete — notice how every one of them lives at "Network or low-Privileges-Required," which is exactly why they scored so high and why patching cadence (Ch 12–13) is a graded CKS topic, not a footnote:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>CVE</th><th>CVSS Score</th><th>What It Actually Did</th><th>§2.1 Vector</th><th>The Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>CVE-2018-1002105</strong> (widely called "the worst Kubernetes vulnerability found to date" at the time)</td><td>9.8 CRITICAL</td><td>A crafted request through the API server's proxy/upgrade-request handling could smuggle privileged requests directly to the kubelet (or to any aggregated API server) over an already-established connection — turning a low-privilege user with basic <code>exec</code>/<code>attach</code>/<code>port-forward</code> rights into a full cluster-admin, with no exploit code beyond a malformed HTTP request</td><td>Vector 1/2 — API server request handling</td><td>Upgrade past the patched versions (1.10.11 / 1.11.5 / 1.12.3+); this is precisely why Ch 12's version-currency discipline exists</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CVE-2019-11247</strong></td><td>8.1 HIGH</td><td>A routing bug let a user authorized only for NAMESPACED resources also read/modify CLUSTER-SCOPED custom resources of a similarly-named type — RBAC intent and RBAC enforcement silently diverged</td><td>Vector 2 — RBAC / API server routing</td><td>Patch the API server; separately, always verify RBAC with <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> rather than trusting the YAML's intent (Ch 9)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CVE-2020-8555</strong></td><td>6.3 MEDIUM</td><td>A "half-blind" SSRF in kube-controller-manager during certain volume-provisioning flows (GlusterFS, Quobyte, StorageOS, ScaleIO) let an already-authorized user make the control plane issue HTTP/GET/POST requests to internal-only endpoints (link-local/loopback) on the attacker's behalf, leaking up to 500 bytes of the response</td><td>Vector 1/6 — network reachability from inside the trust boundary</td><td>Patch (fixed in 1.15.12+/1.16.9+/1.17.5+/1.18.1+); restrict which storage provisioners are enabled; this is the exact SSRF pattern behind Capital One (§2.2) replayed inside the cluster itself</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The lesson:</strong> CVSS's "Attack Vector: Network" + "Privileges Required: None" combination is the formal name for exactly what §2.1 calls Vector 1 — that's why unauthenticated, network-reachable flaws (API server, Dashboard) score near 10.0, while a root container alone tops out around 7-8 <em>until</em> it's chained into an escape. The CKS exam prioritizes fixes the same way CVSS does: fix what's reachable with zero prior access, first.</div>

                    <h5>② STRIDE vs MITRE ATT&amp;CK — Two Coordinate Systems for the Same Attack, Not Competing Frameworks</h5>
                    <p>Students often ask "do I use STRIDE or MITRE ATT&amp;CK on the exam?" — the honest answer is <strong>neither is optional, because they answer different questions about the identical event</strong>. STRIDE is a <em>design-time</em> tool: given a system, what PROPERTIES could be violated? MITRE ATT&amp;CK is an <em>empirical, detection-time</em> catalog: given observed attacker behavior in the wild, what TECHNIQUE was just used? Watch one event — <code>kubectl exec</code> into a compromised pod — pass through both coordinate systems at once:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
 EVENT:  attacker runs `kubectl exec -it easypj-pod -- /bin/bash`

 STRIDE LENS (what PROPERTY is violated?)                MITRE LENS (what TECHNIQUE, empirically?)
 ─────────────────────────────────────────               ──────────────────────────────────────────
 Elevation of Privilege                                   T1609 — Container Administration Command
   → attacker gains an interactive shell they                → the specific, catalogued technique ID
     were never authorized to have                            security tools (Falco rules, SIEMs) key off

 Repudiation (if unlogged)                                 Execution (TA0002)
   → without audit logging, there's no record                → the broader TACTIC this technique serves
     this shell session ever happened                          within the attacker's overall campaign

 ⚙️  STRIDE asks: "which security PROPERTY just broke?"
 ⚙️  MITRE asks: "which known, named TECHNIQUE just fired — so which detection rule should catch it?"
 🔑 Falco (Ch 29) ships MITRE-tagged rules; you still explain the IMPACT in STRIDE terms.</pre>
                    <p>The table below is the complete crosswalk — use it to translate instantly between "the exam described a property violation" and "the exam described an observed technique":</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>STRIDE Category</th><th>Nearest MITRE ATT&amp;CK Tactic(s)</th><th>Why They Connect</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Spoofing</td><td>Initial Access, Credential Access</td><td>Impersonation requires either getting in as someone else, or stealing the credential that lets you</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Tampering</td><td>Execution, Persistence, Defense Evasion</td><td>Modifying specs/images is HOW an attacker runs code, stays, and hides their tracks</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Repudiation</td><td>Defense Evasion</td><td>Deleting/avoiding logs is itself a cataloged evasion technique (clearing artifacts)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Information Disclosure</td><td>Credential Access, Collection, Discovery</td><td>Reading secrets/data IS the collection and discovery phase, empirically observed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Denial of Service</td><td>Impact</td><td>MITRE's "Impact" tactic is explicitly the end-state category DoS/resource-hijacking falls into</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Elevation of Privilege</td><td>Privilege Escalation, Lateral Movement</td><td>Gaining more power and using it to reach further are two sides of one escalation</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Practical exam rule:</strong> if a question describes a SYSTEM WEAKNESS ("Secrets are stored in plaintext") — answer in STRIDE (Information Disclosure). If it describes an OBSERVED ACTION ("we saw <code>kubectl get secrets -A</code> from an unfamiliar identity at 3 AM") — answer in MITRE (Credential Access / Discovery) AND name the STRIDE property it violated. The strongest CKS answers do both in one sentence, exactly like Q1 and Q7 in this chapter's Lab Q&amp;A.</div>

                    <h5>③ The Economics of a Breach — Why Attackers Choose Crypto-Mining, Data Theft, Ransomware, or a Silent Implant</h5>
                    <p>§2.2's four breaches monetized in four completely different ways. That's not random — attackers make a rational, almost businesslike choice among monetization strategies based on how fast they get paid versus how loud the attack is. Understanding this economic model tells you WHICH defense matters most for a given target profile:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Monetization Speed</th><th>Detection Risk</th><th>Skill/Resources Needed</th><th>§2.2 Example</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>🪙 Crypto-mining</td><td><strong>Fast</strong> — compute converts to coin immediately, no buyer needed</td><td><strong>High</strong> — 100% CPU pods and a cloud-bill spike are hard to hide</td><td>Low — pull a public miner image, done</td><td>Tesla (2018) — caught via unusual outbound traffic to a mining pool</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>📦 Data exfiltration</td><td>Slow — must find/negotiate a buyer, or extort the victim directly</td><td>Medium — quiet until the data resurfaces for sale or in a leak</td><td>Medium — needs a valuable, sellable dataset (PII, financial records)</td><td>Capital One (2019) — 106M records, discovered via an external tip</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🔒 Ransomware</td><td>Direct but requires victim cooperation — payment is the whole point</td><td><strong>Very High</strong> — deliberately loud; the attacker WANTS you to notice</td><td>Medium — encryption tooling + a negotiation/leak-site operation</td><td>Rising against exposed databases/Redis reachable through flat K8s networks</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🕵️ Silent supply-chain implant</td><td><strong>Slowest</strong> — patient, long-horizon access is the payoff itself</td><td><strong>Lowest</strong> — signed, trusted software; no anomalous traffic at install time</td><td><strong>Highest</strong> — requires compromising a build pipeline, not a running system</td><td>SolarWinds (2020) — undetected for roughly 14 months</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>The strategic implication: an attacker who wants to stay hidden the LONGEST will never choose crypto-mining, because it is the loudest option by design (100% CPU is trivially alarmed on). Conversely, an attacker willing to invest months in a supply-chain compromise is playing for the highest-value, most patient payoff — which is exactly why Supply Chain Security earns a full 20% of the CKS exam despite being the rarest attack in practice.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Match the defense to the economics, not just the technique:</strong> ResourceQuota/LimitRange (Ch 17) stops crypto-mining fast because it attacks the ECONOMICS (no compute to steal, not just "blocking a technique"). Egress NetworkPolicy + DLP-style monitoring (Ch 6, 29) stops exfiltration by cutting off the "sell it later" payoff. Immutable backups (Ch 19/29 territory) blunt ransomware by making the extortion pointless. SBOM + signing + admission control (Ch 24-27) is the only category of defense aimed at the SLOWEST, most patient attacker — which is why it can't be skipped just because it "feels unlikely."</div>

                    <h5>④ Kill-Chain Timing Under the Hood — Real MTTD/MTTC Numbers, and Why "Isolate → Capture → Delete" Is Non-Negotiable</h5>
                    <p>Security teams measure breach severity with two timing metrics: <strong>MTTD</strong> (Mean Time To Detect — how long the attacker was inside before anyone noticed) and <strong>MTTC</strong> (Mean Time To Contain — how long it took to fully evict them once found). §2.2's four breaches sit at wildly different points on this scale, and the gap explains why the Gotchas panel's "never panic-delete a pod" rule exists:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Breach</th><th>Approx. Time to Detect (MTTD)</th><th>How It Was Found</th><th>What That Implies</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Tesla (2018)</td><td>Days</td><td>External researchers (RedLock) spotted anomalous mining-pool traffic</td><td>Loud attacks (crypto-mining) get found fast — EVEN without internal tooling</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Capital One (2019)</td><td>~4 months from initial access to disclosure</td><td>An external tip to Capital One's responsible-disclosure program, not internal monitoring</td><td>Quiet, credential-based attacks can run for months with zero internal signal</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>SolarWinds (2020)</td><td>~9–14 months</td><td>FireEye discovered it <em>while investigating a breach of their own systems</em> — not proactively</td><td>The most patient, highest-value attacks can go undetected for over a year</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Industry average, all breach types (IBM Cost of a Data Breach, 2024 report)</td><td>~194 days to identify + ~64 days to contain (~258 total — a 7-year low, down from ~277 the year before)</td><td>Composite of thousands of real incidents across industries</td><td>Kubernetes clusters without Falco/audit logging default to this industry-average blind spot</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
 🕐 DWELL TIME (unknown to you until AFTER detection) ─────────────────▶ 🔍 DETECTION MOMENT

 Every minute of dwell time is a minute the attacker had to:
   • move laterally (§2.4 kill-chain stage 5)      • plant additional persistence (stage 4)
   • exfiltrate more data (stage 6)                • cover their tracks (Repudiation, §2.3)

 ⚠️  YOU NEVER KNOW, AT THE MOMENT YOU FIND A COMPROMISED POD, WHETHER DWELL TIME WAS
     5 MINUTES (Tesla-like) OR 14 MONTHS (SolarWinds-like) — the evidence you'd need to
     tell the difference is exactly what a panic-delete destroys.

 THE ONLY SAFE PROCEDURE, REGARDLESS OF ASSUMED DWELL TIME:
   1. ISOLATE   → NetworkPolicy deny-all on the pod's labels, RIGHT NOW (stops the bleeding)
   2. CAPTURE   → forensic copy: `kubectl cp`, `kubectl logs --previous`, describe, audit log query
   3. DELETE    → only now — the pod is gone, but the evidence already exists elsewhere</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The exam rewards this ordering because it's the only one that's correct at EVERY possible dwell time.</strong> If you assume "it's probably nothing" and delete first, you've destroyed the only proof of a SolarWinds-scale incident. If you assume "this must be huge" and spend hours investigating before isolating, you've let a Tesla-scale crypto-miner keep mining the whole time. Isolate → Capture → Delete is correct whether the true dwell time is 5 minutes or 14 months — which is exactly why it's the answer the CKS exam always wants, not a judgment call you make per-incident.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch2-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Every attack in this chapter exploited a default, not a zero-day</strong> — Tesla (no auth), Capital One (metadata exposed), Shopify (flat network), SolarWinds (unsigned build). The CKS exam tests whether you know which defaults to turn off.</li>
                    <li><strong>STRIDE gives you a lookup table, not a guess</strong> — Spoofing→RBAC/tokens, Tampering→signing/encryption, Repudiation→audit logs, Info Disclosure→encryption/Vault, DoS→resource limits, Elevation→non-root/Seccomp/AppArmor.</li>
                    <li><strong>The kill chain (Scan→Access→Map→Steal→Move→Exfiltrate) is universal</strong> — real breaches and the easypj walkthrough follow the identical six steps, and a different CKS control stops each one.</li>
                    <li><strong>Lateral movement and root containers are the two most-tested exam scenarios</strong> — any "pod-to-pod" question means NetworkPolicy; any "shell/exec" question means SecurityContext + Seccomp + AppArmor.</li>
                    <li><strong>Supply chain security is now 20% of the CKS exam</strong> — SolarWinds proved that signed software is not automatically safe software; SBOM + static analysis + signing + admission control work together, not individually.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch2-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 2 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>STRIDE ≠ MITRE:</strong> STRIDE is a threat modeling framework (Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Info Disclosure, DoS, Elevation). MITRE ATT&amp;CK is a taxonomy of observed attacker techniques. The exam won't ask you to recite either — it asks you to CONFIGURE defenses against specific attack patterns.</li>
                    <li><strong>Lateral movement is the #1 exam scenario:</strong> if the exam mentions a compromised pod connecting to other pods, the answer always involves NetworkPolicy (Ch 6) + RBAC (Ch 9) + audit logging (Ch 28).</li>
                    <li><strong>Supply chain attacks are now 20% of the exam:</strong> know the difference between compromising an image (Ch 23/26/27) vs. compromising a running pod (Ch 29/30) — different tools, different response.</li>
                    <li><strong>Panic-deleting a compromised pod destroys evidence:</strong> the exam expects isolate → capture → THEN delete. Order matters for points.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

