                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An attacker has obtained a valid ServiceAccount token from a compromised pod. Using STRIDE, classify this threat and list the THREE CKS controls that would limit the damage.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Classification:</strong> Spoofing (attacker impersonates the SA) + Information Disclosure (token leaked). <strong>Three controls:</strong> (1) <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> — no token mounted in the first place. (2) Minimal RBAC — even with a token, the SA can do very little. (3) NetworkPolicy default-deny — limits which pods the attacker can reach via the API.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the three controls, concretely — layered so each one alone still blocks the attack</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># Control 1 — nothing to steal in the first place
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-api
spec:
  automountServiceAccountToken: false   # token never mounted into the container
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa
---
# Control 2 — RoleBinding — if a token DOES leak, keep the blast radius tiny
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: easypj-minimal
  namespace: prod
rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["configmaps"]
    verbs: ["get"]                      # nowhere near cluster-admin
---
# Control 3 — NetworkPolicy — even with a valid token, limit what it can REACH
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-egress
  namespace: prod
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress: []                            # no destination reachable unless explicitly allowed</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A stolen SA token is a textbook Spoofing attack; the token's exposure is also Information Disclosure. The defense is layered: prevent the token from existing, limit what it can do, and limit what it can reach. With all three in place, even a full pod compromise yields the attacker almost nothing.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You discover that Kubernetes Secrets are stored as plaintext base64 in etcd and anyone with etcd access can read them. Map this to a STRIDE category and describe the exact CKS control to fix it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>STRIDE:</strong> Information Disclosure + Tampering. <strong>Fix:</strong> Enable EncryptionConfiguration with the aescbc provider — Secrets are encrypted BEFORE writing to etcd. Verify: <code>etcdctl get /registry/secrets/ns/name | hexdump -C</code> should show binary with a <code>k8s:enc:aescbc:v1:key1:</code> prefix.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the exact before / after proof, straight from etcd</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># BEFORE encryption — anyone with etcd access reads plaintext INSTANTLY
$ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/default/easypj-secrets | hexdump -C | head -2
00000000  2f 72 65 67 69 73 74 72  79 2f 73 65 63 72 65 74  |/registry/secret|
00000010  73 2f 64 65 66 61 75 6c  74 2f 65 61 73 79 70 6a  |s/default/easypj|
→ DJANGO_SECRET_KEY visible as plain UTF-8 text a few lines further down

# Apply this EncryptionConfiguration and restart the API server:
apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
  - resources: ["secrets"]
    providers:
      - aescbc:
          keys:
            - name: key1
              secret: &lt;base64-encoded-32-byte-key&gt;
      - identity: {}          # lets OLD, unencrypted secrets still be read during migration

# --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/enc/encryption-config.yaml   (kube-apiserver flag)

# AFTER encryption — same command, now binary + prefixed, not readable
$ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/default/easypj-secrets | hexdump -C | head -2
00000000  6b 38 73 3a 65 6e 63 3a  61 65 73 63 62 63 3a 76  |k8s:enc:aescbc:v|
00000010  31 3a 6b 65 79 31 3a e4  9a 2c ...                |1:key1:.,.......|
→ k8s:enc:aescbc:v1:key1: prefix + binary ciphertext. No plaintext leaks even with raw etcd access.</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Base64 is encoding, not encryption — anyone with etcd access can trivially decode secrets. EncryptionConfiguration encrypts at rest using AES-CBC; the <code>identity: {}</code> provider at the end allows decrypting old, pre-encryption secrets. On the exam: create the YAML, mount it to the API server, add <code>--encryption-provider-config</code>, and verify with a raw etcd read.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is running with <code>privileged: true</code> and has mounted the host's /etc/kubernetes directory via hostPath. What STRIDE categories apply and what's the worst-case outcome?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>STRIDE:</strong> Elevation of Privilege (container→host escape), Tampering (modify API server manifests), Information Disclosure (read all PKI certificates). <strong>Worst case:</strong> attacker rewrites <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code> to disable authentication, restarts the API server, and gains full cluster-admin. <strong>Prevention:</strong> PSS Restricted blocks privileged containers and hostPath mounts.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the exact pod spec this question describes</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: dangerous-pod
spec:
  containers:
    - name: app
      image: easypj:latest
      securityContext:
        privileged: true              # full host device + capability access
      volumeMounts:
        - name: host-k8s-config
          mountPath: /host-etc-k8s
  volumes:
    - name: host-k8s-config
      hostPath:
        path: /etc/kubernetes         # the node's control-plane manifests, live</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>STRIDE Category</th><th>Why This Pod Triggers It</th><th>Worst-Case Action Inside the Pod</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Elevation of Privilege</td><td><code>privileged: true</code> disables nearly every container isolation boundary</td><td><code>nsenter --target 1 --mount --net --pid -- bash</code> → root on the node</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Tampering</td><td>hostPath exposes <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests</code> as writable</td><td>Edit <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code> to add <code>--anonymous-auth=true</code>, kubelet restarts it automatically</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Information Disclosure</td><td>hostPath exposes <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki</code></td><td>Copy <code>ca.key</code> + <code>etcd/server.key</code> — forge any client cert, read all of etcd directly</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>privileged+hostPath is one of the most dangerous combinations in Kubernetes — it maps to three STRIDE categories simultaneously. The CKS exam expects you to recognize this combination as critical and immediately recommend PSS Restricted + NetworkPolicy.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An attacker has deleted all audit log files from /var/log/kubernetes/ to cover their tracks. What STRIDE category is this and what defense prevents it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>STRIDE:</strong> Repudiation — denying an action occurred by destroying evidence. <strong>Defense:</strong> ship audit logs off-node via webhook backend to a SIEM in real time — even if local files are deleted, the SIEM retains the complete record. Add Falco rules detecting <code>rm -rf /var/log/</code> patterns.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">ship logs off-node BEFORE an attacker can ever reach the local file</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># kube-apiserver.yaml — add the webhook flag pointing at the kubeconfig below
--audit-webhook-config-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit/webhook-kubeconfig.yaml
--audit-webhook-batch-max-wait=5s

# webhook-kubeconfig.yaml — where events get shipped, off this node entirely
apiVersion: v1
kind: Config
clusters:
  - cluster: {server: "https://siem.easypj-security.internal:8443/audit"}
    name: siem-backend
contexts:
  - context: {cluster: siem-backend, user: audit-shipper}
    name: siem-context
current-context: siem-context</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Falco rule — detect the cover-up attempt itself, in real time</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">- rule: Audit Log Tampering Attempt
  desc: Detect deletion or truncation of Kubernetes audit log files
  condition: >
    open_write and fd.name startswith /var/log/kubernetes/audit
    and (evt.arg.flags contains O_TRUNC or proc.name in (rm, shred))
  output: >
    Audit log tampering detected (user=%user.name command=%proc.cmdline file=%fd.name)
  priority: CRITICAL
  tags: [repudiation, mitre_defense_evasion]</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Real-time log shipping is the only reliable defense against Repudiation. On the exam, configuring an <code>--audit-webhook-config-file</code> pointing to a kubeconfig that sends events to an external service is a common task.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Map each stage of the Kubernetes Attack Kill Chain (Recon → Initial Access → Execution → Persistence → Privilege Escalation → Lateral Movement → Exfiltration) to at least ONE CKS chapter/control. Which two stages are most commonly tested?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Recon → <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>, firewall (Ch8). Initial Access → RBAC/OIDC/mTLS (Ch5,9). Execution → Seccomp/AppArmor/readOnlyRootFS (Ch14,15). Persistence → RBAC audit, Falco, admission control (Ch24,29). Privilege Escalation → PSS Restricted, runAsNonRoot (Ch17,18). Lateral Movement → <strong>NetworkPolicy default-deny (Ch6)</strong>. Exfiltration → egress NetworkPolicy, DNS monitoring (Ch6,14). <strong>Most tested: Privilege Escalation and Lateral Movement.</strong></p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Kill-Chain Stage</th><th>CKS Control</th><th>Chapter</th><th>Exam Frequency</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>1</td><td>Reconnaissance</td><td><code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>, firewall/security group</td><td>Ch 8</td><td>Low</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>2</td><td>Initial Access</td><td>RBAC, OIDC auth, mTLS</td><td>Ch 5, 9</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>3</td><td>Execution</td><td>Seccomp, AppArmor, readOnlyRootFS</td><td>Ch 14, 15</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>4</td><td>Persistence</td><td>RBAC restricting object creation, Falco, admission control</td><td>Ch 24, 29</td><td>Low-Medium</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>5</td><td>Privilege Escalation</td><td>PSS Restricted, runAsNonRoot</td><td>Ch 17, 18</td><td><strong>Highest</strong></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>6</td><td>Lateral Movement</td><td>NetworkPolicy default-deny</td><td>Ch 6</td><td><strong>Highest</strong></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>7</td><td>Exfiltration</td><td>Egress NetworkPolicy, DNS monitoring</td><td>Ch 6, 14</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>CKS scenario questions typically describe a breach at one stage and ask you to configure controls stopping the NEXT stage — e.g. "a pod is compromised (Execution). Prevent Lateral Movement" always answers NetworkPolicy. Memorize this mapping; it's the fastest way to answer scenario questions.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Analyze the Tesla 2018 breach: attackers found an exposed Kubernetes Dashboard and deployed crypto-mining pods. Name FOUR CKS controls that would have prevented this at each stage.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(1) <strong>Firewall/Security Group</strong> — never expose the dashboard via LoadBalancer to 0.0.0.0/0. (2) <strong>OIDC + RBAC</strong> — require real authentication, bind minimal roles, never cluster-admin. (3) <strong>Admission control (PSS/OPA/Kyverno)</strong> — block crypto-miner image patterns and enforce allowed registries. (4) <strong>Falco + Audit Logging</strong> — alert on unexpected pod creation and provide forensic attribution.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Attack Stage It Blocks</th><th>Control</th><th>Without It (what Tesla experienced)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>1</td><td>Reconnaissance → Initial Access</td><td>Firewall/Security Group — no public LoadBalancer for the Dashboard</td><td>Shodan-visible dashboard, reachable from anywhere on the internet</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>2</td><td>Initial Access</td><td>OIDC authentication + minimal RBAC binding</td><td>Dashboard granted <em>admin</em> role with zero login required</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>3</td><td>Execution</td><td>Admission control (PSS/OPA/Kyverno) blocking miner images &amp; unapproved registries</td><td>Attacker deployed a crypto-mining pod with no gatekeeper to stop it</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>4</td><td>Detection (after the fact)</td><td>Falco + audit logging — alert + attribution</td><td>Compute hijacked for weeks before anomalous billing was noticed</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Tesla is the textbook "exposed dashboard" CKS case study, touching every domain: Cluster Setup, Cluster Hardening, System Hardening, Supply Chain, Runtime Security. <strong>Authentication is the most critical fix</strong> — an unauthenticated dashboard bypasses every other control.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Using MITRE ATT&amp;CK, map three techniques to their tactics and CKS controls: (a) <code>kubectl get secrets -A</code>, (b) deploying a DaemonSet with hostPID:true, (c) modifying a ConfigMap to redirect app traffic.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(a) <strong>Credential Access</strong> (TA0006) → RBAC deny list/get secrets, EncryptionConfiguration, Vault (Ch20). (b) <strong>Privilege Escalation</strong> (TA0004) → PSS Restricted + Kyverno block hostPID/hostIPC/hostNetwork, Seccomp blocks process listing (Ch15). (c) <strong>Collection/Exfiltration</strong> (TA0009) → RBAC deny update configmaps, admission webhook validation, audit log alerts.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>MITRE Tactic</th><th>What It Actually Looks Like</th><th>CKS Control</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>(a) <code>kubectl get secrets -A</code></td><td>Credential Access (TA0006)</td><td>Dumps every Secret's base64 value cluster-wide in one call</td><td>RBAC denies <code>get/list</code> on secrets, EncryptionConfiguration, Vault (Ch 20)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>(b) DaemonSet with <code>hostPID: true</code></td><td>Privilege Escalation (TA0004)</td><td>Pod's processes share the HOST's PID namespace — sees every process on every node</td><td>PSS Restricted + Kyverno policy blocking hostPID/hostIPC/hostNetwork, Seccomp (Ch 15)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>(c) Edit ConfigMap's DB connection string</td><td>Collection / Exfiltration (TA0009)</td><td>App silently starts sending queries/data to an attacker-controlled endpoint</td><td>RBAC denies <code>update</code> on configmaps, admission webhook validation, audit alerts</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">the hostPID pod that lets the attacker see every process on the node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: recon-daemon
spec:
  hostPID: true              # this container's `ps aux` now shows EVERY process, on every namespace
  containers:
    - name: watcher
      image: busybox
      command: ["sh", "-c", "ps aux; sleep 3600"]</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The exam doesn't test MITRE IDs but DOES test the security concepts behind them. hostPID gives a pod visibility into ALL host processes; a poisoned ConfigMap can redirect DB connection strings to an attacker's server — always treat ConfigMaps carrying connection info as sensitive as Secrets.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the SolarWinds 2020 supply chain attack and which CKS chapters specifically teach the controls that detect or prevent it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Attackers compromised SolarWinds' build pipeline and injected malware (SUNBURST) into a signed update; 18,000 organizations ran the trojanized software. <strong>Chapters:</strong> Ch23 SBOM (know what's in your software), Ch25 static analysis (would catch beaconing logic), Ch27 cosign signing (unsigned images rejected even if build is compromised), Ch6 egress NetworkPolicy (blocks C2 beaconing), Ch29 Falco (detects anomalous outbound connections).</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Control</th><th>Would It Have Caught SUNBURST?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Ch 23 — SBOM</td><td>Generate a Software Bill of Materials for every build</td><td>Would reveal the exact injected component/version once suspicion arose — speeds up the "what's affected" phase</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Ch 25 — Static Analysis</td><td>Scan source/build artifacts before signing (kubesec, conftest, OPA)</td><td>Could flag the anomalous beaconing/DGA logic hidden inside the build, IF the scan rules covered it</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Ch 27 — cosign Signing</td><td>Require every deployed image to carry a verified signature</td><td>No — SUNBURST was signed with SolarWinds' <em>legitimate</em> certificate. Signing alone does not prove "safe," only "came from this vendor"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Ch 6 — Egress NetworkPolicy</td><td>Block outbound traffic except to explicitly allowed destinations</td><td>Yes — would block or expose the C2 beaconing traffic SUNBURST relied on to phone home</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Ch 29 — Falco</td><td>Runtime anomaly detection on process/network behavior</td><td>Yes — anomalous outbound connections and unexpected process spawns are exactly Falco's detection surface</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>SolarWinds is why Supply Chain Security is 20% of the CKS exam. Key lesson: <strong>signed software is not automatically safe software</strong> — SUNBURST was signed with SolarWinds' legitimate certificate. No single control would have stopped it, but SBOM + SAST + signing + NetworkPolicy + Falco together would have detected it within hours, not months.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod in namespace "web" was compromised and is attempting lateral movement to "database". Which STRIDE category, and what's the single most effective control? Provide the YAML.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>STRIDE:</strong> Information Disclosure + Elevation of Privilege. <strong>Control:</strong> default-deny NetworkPolicy in the "database" namespace.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: db-ingress-only-from-web
  namespace: database
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels: {app: postgres}
  policyTypes: [Ingress]
  ingress:
  - from:
    - namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {name: web}}
    - podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: easypj-api}}
    ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 5432}]</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Lateral movement is the #1 exam scenario. Key design: <code>policyTypes: [Ingress]</code> only controls inbound to the database; <code>namespaceSelector</code> AND <code>podSelector</code> must BOTH match; only port 5432 is allowed — even a compromised web pod can't use that port for anything but postgres protocol.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Build a Likelihood × Impact risk matrix for: Container Escape, Secret Exfiltration, Supply Chain Poisoning, DoS via Resource Exhaustion. Which should you fix FIRST?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Container Escape: Medium likelihood / Critical impact → fix #2 (PSS Restricted). <strong>Secret Exfiltration: High likelihood / Critical impact → FIX FIRST</strong> (EncryptionConfiguration + disable automount + external secrets). Supply Chain Poisoning: Medium / Critical → fix #3 (SBOM + signing). DoS: Low / Medium → fix #4 (ResourceQuota + LimitRange).</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Threat</th><th>Likelihood</th><th>Impact</th><th>Why This Rank</th><th>First Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>#1</strong></td><td>Secret Exfiltration</td><td>High</td><td>Critical</td><td>Unencrypted by default + SA tokens auto-mounted → attacker always has SOME credential</td><td>EncryptionConfiguration + disable automount (Ch 19, 10)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>#2</td><td>Container Escape</td><td>Medium</td><td>Critical</td><td>Requires an initial foothold first, but escape = full node compromise</td><td>PSS Restricted (Ch 17, 18)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>#3</td><td>Supply Chain Poisoning</td><td>Medium</td><td>Critical</td><td>Rarer in practice, but blast radius spans every cluster running the image</td><td>SBOM + cosign signing (Ch 23, 26)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>#4</td><td>DoS via Resource Exhaustion</td><td>Low</td><td>Medium</td><td>Loud and easily detected/contained; rarely catastrophic alone</td><td>ResourceQuota + LimitRange (Ch 17)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="diagram-container">
                        <div class="diagram-title">Likelihood × Impact — the same 4 threats, plotted</div>
<pre>
                  MED IMPACT ←──────────────────→ CRITICAL IMPACT
  HIGH        │                                  │ 🥇 Secret Exfiltration    │
  LIKELIHOOD  │                                  │    FIX FIRST              │
              │──────────────────────────────────│───────────────────────────│
  MEDIUM      │                                  │ 🥈 Container Escape       │
  LIKELIHOOD  │                                  │ 🥉 Supply Chain Poisoning │
              │──────────────────────────────────│───────────────────────────│
  LOW         │ 4️⃣ DoS (Resource Exhaustion)      │                           │
  LIKELIHOOD  │                                  │                           │
</pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Secret exfiltration ranks #1 because secrets are unencrypted by default (every cluster is vulnerable out of the box), SA tokens are auto-mounted (attackers always have a credential), and the impact is complete cluster-wide credential compromise. When asked "what first?", choose the threat that is BOTH likely (due to defaults) AND high-impact.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch2-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 2 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (8)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">The CKS is performance-based — you don't just name a threat, you produce the commands that surface or contain it on a live terminal. These 8 drills mirror that: a broken or unaudited state, and you write the real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You've inherited a cluster and need to check it for Vector 1 (§2.1) — an API server reachable without authentication. Prove whether it's exposed.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">test as an anonymous, unauthenticated caller</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># From inside the cluster context, impersonate the anonymous user</span>
kubectl get pods --as=system:anonymous -A
<span class="comment"># If this returns pods instead of "Forbidden", anonymous-auth is effectively open</span>

<span class="comment"># From OUTSIDE — the attacker's actual view, no kubeconfig at all</span>
curl -k https://&lt;API_SERVER_IP&gt;:6443/api/v1/namespaces</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The <code>--as=system:anonymous</code> trick tests it safely from a working kubeconfig; the raw <code>curl</code> reproduces exactly what an internet-scanning attacker (Shodan/Masscan, §2.1) would see with zero credentials. Both should return <code>403 Forbidden</code> on a hardened cluster — any 200 response here is the Tesla 2018 vulnerability, reproduced live.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Determine the blast radius of the <code>easypj-api</code> ServiceAccount as if the pod using it had just been compromised — without reading a single line of RBAC YAML.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">ask the API directly, don't trace bindings by hand</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl auth can-i --list -n prod --as=system:serviceaccount:prod:easypj-api
<span class="comment"># Also check cluster-scoped permissions — a namespaced SA can still hold ClusterRoleBindings</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:prod:easypj-api</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the exact command from the chapter's Speed Tips, applied as a drill: it resolves every Role/ClusterRole binding for you and answers "what can the attacker do RIGHT NOW with this stolen token" in one call — the same question STRIDE's Spoofing category asks (§2.3).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Audit the ENTIRE cluster for pods with no CPU/memory limits set (the Denial-of-Service vector from STRIDE, §2.3) — one command, no per-pod inspection.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cluster-wide resource-limit audit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq -r '
  .items[] |
  select(.spec.containers[].resources.limits == null) |
  "\(.metadata.namespace)/\(.metadata.name)"'</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Every pod this returns is a potential crypto-mining or resource-exhaustion target — exactly the STRIDE "Denial of Service" row, and exactly the Tesla-style economics from this chapter's Under the Hood §③ (crypto-mining is the loudest, fastest-monetizing attack, and unlimited CPU is what makes it possible).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Reproduce the Tesla 2018 exposure check: determine whether ANY Service in the cluster exposes a Dashboard-like admin UI via a public-facing type.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find every internet-facing Service, cluster-wide</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get svc -A -o json | jq -r '
  .items[] | select(.spec.type=="LoadBalancer" or .spec.type=="NodePort") |
  "\(.metadata.namespace)/\(.metadata.name)  type=\(.spec.type)"'</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Anything named <code>dashboard</code>, <code>*-admin</code>, or <code>*-ui</code> in this list is a candidate for the exact Tesla vector — a management interface reachable from outside the cluster with no additional authentication layer in front of it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Confirm (or rule out) the Information Disclosure vector from STRIDE: is this cluster's etcd encrypting Secrets at rest, or just base64-encoding them?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check the API server flag, then decode a real secret</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Is EncryptionConfiguration even wired in?</span>
sudo grep encryption-provider-config /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

<span class="comment"># 2. If that flag is ABSENT, prove the exposure — decode any Secret in one line</span>
kubectl get secret easypj-secrets -o jsonpath='{.data.DJANGO_SECRET_KEY}' | base64 -d</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>If step 1 shows no flag, step 2 will ALWAYS succeed instantly — that's the entire Capital One/easypj credential-theft pattern from §2.4. This is a "prove and defer" drill: proving the gap is this chapter's job, closing it with an <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> is Chapter 19's.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>kubectl get events -A</code> shows an unfamiliar CronJob was just created in <code>kube-system</code>. Classify it by STRIDE and MITRE tactic, then write the command to inspect exactly what it does.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>STRIDE:</strong> Tampering (an unauthorized object was created) + potential Elevation of Privilege if it's bound to a powerful ServiceAccount. <strong>MITRE:</strong> Persistence (TA0003) — a CronJob is a classic backdoor-persistence primitive, surviving pod restarts.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">inspect it immediately, don't delete yet</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get cronjob -n kube-system -o yaml | grep -A5 'image:\|serviceAccountName:\|command:'
kubectl get events -n kube-system --sort-by='.lastTimestamp' | tail -20</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Follow this chapter's Under the Hood §④ discipline: <strong>isolate → capture → delete</strong>. Reading the CronJob's image and ServiceAccount FIRST tells you the attacker's persistence mechanism and blast radius — deleting it immediately would destroy that evidence with no gain, since a well-designed backdoor already has other footholds.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Simulate Kill-Chain Stage 5 (Lateral Movement, §2.4): from a throwaway debug pod, prove the flat-network problem exists, then prove a NetworkPolicy fixes it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">prove the flat network, then close it, then re-prove</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Prove lateral reach BEFORE any policy exists</span>
kubectl run attacker-sim --image=nicolaka/netshoot --rm -it --restart=Never -n prod -- \
  nc -zv postgres.prod.svc.cluster.local 5432   <span class="comment"># succeeds — flat network</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Apply default-deny + explicit allow (Ch 6's pattern)</span>
kubectl apply -f db-ingress-only-from-web.yaml

<span class="comment"># 3. Re-run step 1's exact command from a DIFFERENT namespace — should now time out</span>
kubectl run attacker-sim --image=nicolaka/netshoot --rm -it --restart=Never -n other-ns -- \
  nc -zv -w3 postgres.prod.svc.cluster.local 5432   <span class="comment"># times out — blocked</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is §2.4's kill-chain Stage 5 fix, reproduced as a hands-on drill: prove the vulnerability exists, apply the control, then re-run the IDENTICAL attacker command to prove it now fails. The exam grades the verification step as heavily as the fix itself.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Someone reports "a shell got spawned in the easypj pod around 2 PM, but we don't have Falco yet." Reconstruct what happened using only the audit log.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">forensic reconstruction from audit logs alone</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">cat /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log | \
  jq 'select(.objectRef.subresource == "exec" and .objectRef.name == "easypj-pod")' | \
  jq '{user: .user.username, sourceIP: .sourceIPs[0], time: .requestReceivedTimestamp}'</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This defeats the Repudiation category from STRIDE (§2.3) after the fact — the audit log is the ground truth Falco (Ch 29) would otherwise have surfaced in real time. Cross-reference the <code>sourceIP</code> against your known admin IP ranges to distinguish a legitimate on-call action from an actual compromise.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

