            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 2.2 — Classify 5 Real Techniques by STRIDE Category</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 4 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Classify each of these 5 real attacker actions into exactly one STRIDE category (§2.3), then name the CKS chapter that fixes it: (1) a stolen kubeconfig is used to authenticate; (2) a ConfigMap holding a DB connection string is silently edited to point at an attacker's server; (3) audit logging is disabled before an attack begins; (4) <code>kubectl get secrets -A</code> is run by an unfamiliar identity; (5) a crypto-mining DaemonSet consumes every node's CPU.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Ask "which of the six properties — impersonation, modification, deniability, exposure, availability, or excess power — did this violate?"
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Action</th><th>STRIDE Category</th><th>Fixed in</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1</td><td>Stolen kubeconfig used to authenticate</td><td>Spoofing</td><td>Ch 8/10 (short-lived certs, bound tokens)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2</td><td>ConfigMap silently redirected</td><td>Tampering</td><td>Ch 24/26 (admission control, signing)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>3</td><td>Audit logging disabled pre-attack</td><td>Repudiation</td><td>Ch 28 (audit policy)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>4</td><td>Unfamiliar identity reads all secrets</td><td>Information Disclosure</td><td>Ch 19 (encryption) + Ch 9 (RBAC)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>5</td><td>Crypto-mining DaemonSet</td><td>Denial of Service</td><td>Ch 17 (ResourceQuota/LimitRange) + Ch 24</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> #2 is the one most people miscategorize as "Information Disclosure" because a secret-like value is involved — but the ATTACK itself is a silent modification of trusted config, which is Tampering. STRIDE classifies by which property the ACTION violates, not by which data happens to be nearby.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
