            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 2.3 — Extend the easypj Kill-Chain Map with a New Attack Path</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>§2.4's kill-chain map covered 6 stages against easypj. Invent a 7th variant path an attacker could take at Stage 2 (Initial Access) that ISN'T "exposed dashboard," "stolen kubeconfig," or "anonymous API" — be specific — then trace it through to Exfiltration, naming a CKS control at each stage it passes through.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Think about paths that don't require the API server at all — a compromised CI/CD runner, a leaked container-registry credential, or a vulnerable Ingress controller.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution (one worked example — yours will differ)</summary>
                        <p><strong>Example — a compromised CI/CD runner has a long-lived deploy token.</strong> Stage 2 (Initial Access): attacker steals the CI/CD system's Kubernetes deploy token from a misconfigured pipeline secret store — no API server exposure needed at all. Stage 3 (Execution): the token has <code>create</code> rights on Deployments, so the attacker pushes a backdoored image through the NORMAL deploy path. Stage 4 (Persistence): the backdoored image redeploys automatically on every pipeline run, surviving manual pod deletions. Stage 5 (Lateral Movement): from inside the newly-deployed pod, same flat-network problem as the chapter's main walkthrough. Stage 6 (Exfiltration): same egress path. <strong>Controls:</strong> scope the CI/CD token to a single namespace and image repository (Ch 9), require signed images so an unsigned backdoored build is rejected at admission (Ch 26), rotate CI/CD credentials on a short expiry (Ch 10).</p>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Check yourself:</strong> did your path skip the API server's front door entirely, the way a supply-chain or CI/CD compromise always does? That's the point of this drill — not every kill chain starts with §2.1's Vector 1; recognizing the OTHER entry points is exactly what Chapters 23-27 (Supply Chain Security, 20% of the exam) exist to cover.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
