                <h4>Deep Dive: What Happens When Each Component Is Compromised</h4>
                <p>Understanding the architecture is not enough. You must understand the <strong>blast radius</strong> of each component's failure. When the CKS exam asks you to "harden a cluster," it is asking you to reduce the blast radius of each component to the minimum possible. Here is what happens when each component falls — and what the CKS controls do to contain it:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Component Compromise Scenarios — Blast Radius Analysis</div>
<pre>
  COMPONENT: kube-apiserver
  ─────────────────────────
  IF COMPROMISED (e.g., anonymous auth enabled + exposed):
    Attacker can: create/delete any resource, read ALL Secrets, deploy
    malicious pods as cluster-admin, disable audit logging, modify RBAC
    rules to create permanent backdoors, delete all namespaces.
    BLAST RADIUS: ENTIRE CLUSTER. Every namespace. Every secret. Every pod.

  CKS CONTROLS THAT CONTAIN THIS:
    ✅ --anonymous-auth=false (Ch 8) — first line of defense
    ✅ RBAC with least privilege (Ch 9) — even if authenticated, limited
    ✅ OIDC with MFA (Ch 5) — strong identity verification
    ✅ API audit logging (Ch 28) — detect anomalous API calls
    ✅ NetworkPolicy on port 6443 — restrict which IPs can reach API

  COMPONENT: etcd
  ────────────────
  IF COMPROMISED (e.g., direct network access to port 2379):
    Attacker can: read ALL cluster state (every pod, secret, config),
    modify any resource by writing directly to etcd, delete the entire
    cluster state, create backdoor ServiceAccounts that bypass RBAC.
    BLAST RADIUS: ENTIRE CLUSTER. Worse than API server — etcd access
    bypasses ALL authentication, authorization, and admission control.

  CKS CONTROLS THAT CONTAIN THIS:
    ✅ Firewall: only API server IP can reach port 2379 (Ch 8)
    ✅ etcd peer-to-peer TLS with client certificate verification (Ch 8)
    ✅ Encryption at rest (Ch 19) — even with direct etcd access, Secrets
       are encrypted and unreadable without the encryption key
    ✅ etcd backup (Ch 12) — recover from catastrophic etcd loss

  COMPONENT: kubelet (port 10250)
  ───────────────────────────────
  IF COMPROMISED (e.g., anonymous auth enabled on kubelet):
    Attacker can: run arbitrary commands inside any container on that
    node via /exec, read pod logs, read Secret values mounted as volumes,
    access the node filesystem via hostPath, potentially escape to node.
    BLAST RADIUS: ALL PODS ON THAT NODE. Not the whole cluster — but
    all applications and all Secrets on that specific worker.

  CKS CONTROLS THAT CONTAIN THIS:
    ✅ --anonymous-auth=false (Ch 8)
    ✅ --authorization-mode=Webhook (Ch 11) — every kubelet request
       is authorized by the API server, not locally
    ✅ NodeRestriction admission plugin (Ch 11) — kubelet can only
       modify its own node's resources
    ✅ --read-only-port=0 (Ch 8) — disable the insecure port entirely

  COMPONENT: Container Runtime
  ────────────────────────────
  IF COMPROMISED (container escape, e.g., root + privileged + no Seccomp):
    Attacker can: break out of container → gain root on the node,
    access all other containers on that node, read all Secrets mounted
    on that node, access cloud metadata (169.254.169.254), deploy
    crypto-miners, establish persistent node access.
    BLAST RADIUS: THE NODE AND ALL PODS ON IT. From there, lateral
    movement to other nodes is possible.

  CKS CONTROLS THAT CONTAIN THIS:
    ✅ runAsNonRoot: true (Ch 18) — even if app is exploited, no root
    ✅ Seccomp profile (Ch 15) — blocks dangerous syscalls like nsenter
    ✅ AppArmor profile (Ch 14) — blocks file/network paths
    ✅ readOnlyRootFilesystem (Ch 18) — attacker cannot write tools
    ✅ drop ALL capabilities (Ch 18) — no CAP_SYS_ADMIN, no escape
    ✅ PodSecurity: Restricted (Ch 17) — enforces all of the above

  COMPONENT: CNI / kube-proxy
  ───────────────────────────
  IF COMPROMISED (no NetworkPolicy — flat network):
    Attacker can: from one compromised pod, reach every other pod,
    every database, every cache, every message queue, pods in kube-system
    that have cluster-admin ServiceAccounts.
    BLAST RADIUS: EVERY POD IN THE CLUSTER reachable from the compromised
    pod. With no NetworkPolicies, this is ALL pods.

  CKS CONTROLS THAT CONTAIN THIS:
    ✅ Default-deny NetworkPolicy (Ch 6) — blocks all pod-to-pod traffic
    ✅ Explicit allow rules — only whitelisted pod-to-pod communication
    ✅ Egress NetworkPolicy (Ch 6) — block data exfiltration
    ✅ mTLS via Istio/Linkerd (Ch 22) — even if network allows it,
       unauthorized pods cannot authenticate to services
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Real easypj Example: Applying the 5-Layer Defense Model</h4>
                <p>Let's trace what happens to a single <code>kubectl apply -f easypj-deployment.yaml</code> through all 5 layers. This is not theoretical — this is exactly what you will configure in later chapters, and it is exactly what the CKS exam tests:</p>

                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="split-side split-bad">
                        <h5>❌ easypj WITHOUT Defense-in-Depth</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Layer 1 (Edge):</strong> Dashboard on public internet, no TLS on Ingress, anyone with the URL can access</li>
                            <li><strong>Layer 2 (API):</strong> Default ServiceAccount has cluster-admin, no RBAC restrictions, anonymous auth enabled</li>
                            <li><strong>Layer 3 (Network):</strong> No NetworkPolicies. Every pod can reach every other pod on every port. easypj pod can connect directly to postgres, redis, and kube-system pods.</li>
                            <li><strong>Layer 4 (Container):</strong> easypj runs as root, no Seccomp, no AppArmor, writable filesystem, all capabilities. One RCE in Django = full container + node escape.</li>
                            <li><strong>Layer 5 (Data):</strong> SECRET_KEY and DATABASE_URL in plaintext Secret (base64 only). etcd stores them unencrypted. Anyone with etcd access reads everything.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-weight:700;">RESULT: One vulnerability in any layer = total cluster compromise.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="split-side split-good">
                        <h5>✅ easypj WITH Defense-in-Depth (CKS)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Layer 1 (Edge):</strong> Ingress with TLS termination, WAF in front, Dashboard requires OIDC authentication (Ch 5, 7)</li>
                            <li><strong>Layer 2 (API):</strong> RBAC: easypj SA can only create pods in "easypj" namespace. Anonymous auth disabled. Audit logging records all API calls. (Ch 8, 9, 28)</li>
                            <li><strong>Layer 3 (Network):</strong> Default-deny NetworkPolicy. Explicit allow: easypj→postgres:5432, ingress→easypj:8000. Nothing else. (Ch 6)</li>
                            <li><strong>Layer 4 (Container):</strong> Non-root user (uid 1000), Seccomp profile (50 syscalls, not 300+), AppArmor profile (only /app writable), readOnlyRootFS, drop ALL capabilities. (Ch 14, 15, 18)</li>
                            <li><strong>Layer 5 (Data):</strong> Secrets encrypted at rest in etcd (AES-256). Vault injects DB credentials at runtime — they never touch etcd at all. (Ch 19, 20)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="color:#4ade80;font-weight:700;">RESULT: Attacker must breach 5 independent layers. Failure at any layer is contained. 5 failures needed for total compromise.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKS Exam Insight: The Component Question</h5>
                    <p>When you face a CKS task, silently ask: <strong>"Which component enforces this?"</strong> If the task says "encrypt secrets," the component is etcd. If the task says "restrict syscalls," the component is the container runtime. If the task says "block pods from a namespace," the component is the API server (via admission control). This one question — <strong>"Which component?"</strong> — guides you to the right configuration file, the right flags, and the right YAML. Master this component map and you master the exam.</p>
                </div>
