            <!-- ─────────── §3.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s3-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.2 Authentication vs Authorization vs Admission Control</h3>
                <p>Every API request to Kubernetes passes through <strong>three sequential security gates</strong> before it is persisted to etcd. Understanding the difference between these three stages — and the order in which they execute — is essential for the CKS exam. If you confuse authorization with admission control (a common mistake), you will configure the wrong component and the exam task will fail.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Three Gates, Three Questions, Three Answers</h5>
                    <p><strong>Authentication (AuthN):</strong> "Who are you?" — Verifies identity via certificates, tokens, or OIDC. <strong>Authorization (AuthZ):</strong> "What can you do?" — Checks RBAC policies against the authenticated identity. <strong>Admission Control:</strong> "Is this request allowed?" — Validates and mutates the request before it is persisted. <strong>All three must pass. If any one fails, the request is rejected with an HTTP error code.</strong></p>
                    <p style="margin-top:8px;"><strong>A numbering note before you memorize anything:</strong> this section labels these three checks "Gate 1/2/3" to teach the concept in isolation. Section 3.4 below adds the TLS handshake that happens <em>before</em> authentication and splits Admission Control into two independent sub-phases — which renumbers these same three checks as Gates 2, 3, and 4a/4b in this guide's canonical 5-gate model. From 3.4 onward (and on the exam), use THOSE numbers — "Gate 1" always means TLS from here on, never AuthN.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Kubernetes Request Pipeline — Complete Flow</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">From kubectl to etcd — every gate, every check</div>
<pre>
  kubectl apply -f easypj-pod.yaml
       │
       ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  GATE 1: AUTHENTICATION — "Who are you?"                 │
  │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
  │  Methods (checked in order):                              │
  │    ① X.509 Client Certificate (from kubeconfig)          │
  │    ② Bearer Token (ServiceAccount JWT)                   │
  │    ③ OIDC ID Token (Google, GitHub, Azure AD)            │
  │    ④ Bootstrap Token (node join)                         │
  │    ⑤ Static Token File (legacy — avoid)                  │
  │    ⑥ Anonymous (disabled by default → Ch 8)              │
  │                                                           │
  │  ❌ FAIL → HTTP 401 Unauthorized                          │
  │  ✅ PASS → Identity established: user or SA name          │
  └──────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┘
                                 │ ✅ "I am user 'admin'" or
                                 │    "I am SA 'default/any'"
                                 ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  GATE 2: AUTHORIZATION — "What can you do?"              │
  │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
  │  Modes (checked in order):                                │
  │    ① Node authorizer (kubelet-only requests)             │
  │    ② ABAC (legacy — avoid)                               │
  │    ③ RBAC (Role + RoleBinding) ← THE CKS MODE            │
  │    ④ Webhook (external authorization service)            │
  │                                                           │
  │  Process:                                                 │
  │    • API server looks up RoleBindings for this user/SA   │
  │    • Checks if Role allows the requested verb+resource   │
  │    • ClusterRoles apply cluster-wide                     │
  │    • Roles are namespace-scoped                           │
  │                                                           │
  │  ❌ FAIL → HTTP 403 Forbidden                             │
  │  ✅ PASS → User has permission for this action            │
  └──────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┘
                                 │ ✅ "User 'admin' CAN create
                                 │    pods in namespace 'easypj'"
                                 ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  GATE 3: ADMISSION CONTROL — "Is this request allowed?"  │
  │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
  │  Two phases:                                              │
  │                                                           │
  │  Phase 1: MUTATING Admission (run first)                 │
  │    • Can MODIFY the request (add sidecars, inject env)   │
  │    • Example: Istio adds sidecar proxy automatically      │
  │    • Example: inject imagePullSecrets                     │
  │                                                           │
  │  Phase 2: VALIDATING Admission (run second)               │
  │    • Can only ALLOW or DENY — cannot modify              │
  │    • Example: Pod Security Standards (PSS) — Ch 17        │
  │    • Example: OPA/Gatekeeper policies — Ch 24             │
  │    • Example: ImagePolicyWebhook — Ch 23, 24              │
  │                                                           │
  │  ❌ FAIL → HTTP 403 with policy reason                    │
  │  ✅ PASS → Request is valid and compliant                 │
  └──────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┘
                                 │ ✅ Pod spec is valid,
                                 │    secure, and compliant
                                 ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  ✅ STORED IN etcd + Scheduled to worker node             │
  │  • kubectl receives "pod/easypj created"                  │
  │  • Scheduler assigns pod to a healthy node               │
  │  • kubelet on that node starts the container             │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
