                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why ABAC Is Labeled "Legacy — Avoid"</h5>
                    <p><strong>ABAC (Attribute-Based Access Control)</strong> was the authorization mode Kubernetes shipped with before RBAC existed. Instead of Role/RoleBinding objects living in the API, ABAC reads a static JSON policy file from local disk on the API server — one JSON object per line, each describing which user/group can do which verb on which resource:</p>
<pre>{"apiVersion":"abac.authorization.kubernetes.io/v1beta1","kind":"Policy","spec":{"user":"sarah","namespace":"easypj","resource":"pods","apiGroup":"*","readonly":true}}</pre>
                    <p>Three reasons the CKS exam (and every production cluster) treats it as "legacy — avoid": <strong>(1) No API objects.</strong> RBAC's Role/RoleBinding are ordinary Kubernetes resources — you can <code>kubectl get</code>, <code>diff</code>, GitOps, and audit them. ABAC's policy file is invisible to the API entirely; there is no <code>kubectl get abacpolicies</code>. <strong>(2) Static, not dynamic.</strong> Changing an ABAC policy means editing a file on every control-plane node's disk and restarting (or SIGHUP-ing) the API server — RBAC changes take effect the instant you <code>kubectl apply</code> them. <strong>(3) No audit trail for the policy itself.</strong> Since the file lives outside etcd, there's no history of who changed an ABAC rule or when. RBAC's Kubernetes-native design directly fixes all three gaps, which is exactly why it replaced ABAC as the default and why the exam expects you to recognize <code>--authorization-mode=ABAC</code> on a cluster as a finding to remediate, not a mode to configure.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>AuthN vs AuthZ vs Admission — Side-by-Side Comparison</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Authentication (AuthN)</th><th>Authorization (AuthZ)</th><th>Admission Control</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Question</strong></td><td>Who are you?</td><td>What can you do?</td><td>Is this allowed?</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Failure Code</strong></td><td>401 Unauthorized</td><td>403 Forbidden</td><td>403 with policy reason</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Can Modify Request?</strong></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (mutating phase only)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CKS Chapters</strong></td><td>Ch 5 (OIDC), Ch 8 (API)</td><td>Ch 9 (RBAC), Ch 11 (Node)</td><td>Ch 17 (PSS), Ch 24 (OPA), Ch 23 (Image)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Example Config</strong></td><td><code>--oidc-issuer-url</code></td><td><code>--authorization-mode=RBAC</code></td><td><code>--enable-admission-plugins</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>easypj Example</strong></td><td>User "dev@easypj.com" logs in via OIDC</td><td>Can create pods in "easypj" namespace only</td><td>Pod must be non-root, no hostPath, restricted PSS</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Order</strong></td><td>1st</td><td>2nd</td><td>3rd (last gate before etcd)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Why the Order Matters — A CKS Exam Scenario</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>😰 Scenario: What Happens When You Get the Order Wrong</h5>
                    <p>Imagine you set up an OPA policy that blocks pods with <code>hostPath</code> mounts. But you also configure RBAC to deny all users the ability to create pods. A user submits a pod YAML with a <code>hostPath</code> mount. What happens?</p>
                    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The request fails at Gate 2 (Authorization) with 403 Forbidden — the OPA policy at Gate 3 is never even checked. The user sees "Forbidden" — not "Policy violation: hostPath not allowed." Without understanding the order, you might waste hours debugging why your admission policy "isn't working" when in fact RBAC is blocking the request first.</p>
                    <p><strong>CKS Lesson:</strong> Always check Gate 1 first, then Gate 2, then Gate 3. If a security control isn't working, ask "which gate am I at, and is an earlier gate blocking the request?"</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Order Is Fixed — You Cannot Change It</h5>
                    <p>The Authentication → Authorization → Admission Control pipeline is <strong>hardcoded into the API server</strong>. You cannot reorder it. You cannot skip a gate (well, you can disable admission plugins, but you cannot skip AuthN or AuthZ — though you can set them to "AlwaysAllow" which is effectively skipping them, and that's exactly what the CKS exam tests you on fixing). <strong>The exam will give you a cluster where one gate is misconfigured and ask you to fix it. Identifying WHICH gate is broken is the first step.</strong></p>
                </div>

                <h4>Decision Tree: Which Gate Is Failing?</h4>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="decision-node question">🔍 You run kubectl and get an error. Which gate failed?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">401 Unauthorized → Gate 1 (AuthN). Fix: Check certs, tokens, OIDC config. Chapter 8.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">Not 401. Move to next check.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">📋 Is the error 403 Forbidden with NO policy reason?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">Yes → Gate 2 (AuthZ). Fix: Check RBAC roles and bindings. Chapter 9.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">Not a plain 403. Move to next check.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">📝 Is the error 403 Forbidden WITH a policy reason (e.g., "DENIED by PodSecurity")?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">Yes → Gate 3 (Admission Control). Fix: Check PSS labels, OPA policies, ImagePolicyWebhook. Chapters 17, 23, 24.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">None of the above? Check API server logs with <code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt;</code>. Look for the exact error message and matching gate.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Deep Dive: Authentication Methods — How Each One Works</h4>
                <p>The CKS exam expects you to understand not just that authentication exists, but <strong>how each method works at the protocol level</strong> — because the exam tasks require you to configure them. Here is each method, how it works, and when the exam expects you to use it:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">X.509 Client Certificates — The Default kubectl Auth Method</div>
<pre>
  HOW IT WORKS:
  Every kubeconfig file contains a client certificate + private key embedded
  as base64. When kubectl connects, it presents the certificate. The API
  server verifies it against the cluster's CA certificate. The certificate's
  Subject CN (Common Name) becomes the username. The O (Organization) fields
  become the user's groups.

  Example kubeconfig (simplified):
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ users:                                                       │
  │ - name: easypj-admin                                         │
  │   user:                                                      │
  │     client-certificate-data: LS0tLS1...  ← YOUR identity     │
  │     client-key-data: LS0tLS1...         ← YOUR private key   │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Why it matters for CKS:
  • This is the DEFAULT authentication method for kubectl
  • The CN= field maps to the username in RBAC RoleBindings
  • The O= field maps to the group — ClusterRoleBindings can target O=
  • Certificate rotation (Ch 12) is about renewing these certs before expiry
  • If someone steals your kubeconfig, they ARE you — no password needed
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Bearer Tokens (JWT) — The ServiceAccount Auth Method</div>
<pre>
  HOW IT WORKS:
  Every pod has a ServiceAccount token (JWT) mounted at:
  /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token

  This token is sent as an HTTP header: Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;

  The JWT payload identifies the ServiceAccount:
  {
    "iss": "kubernetes/serviceaccount",
    "kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace": "default",
    "kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/service-account.name": "default",
    "sub": "system:serviceaccount:default:default"
  }

  Why it matters for CKS:
  • The token is mounted BY DEFAULT — even if your pod never talks to the API
  • automountServiceAccountToken: false (Ch 10) disables this
  • Bound tokens (--service-account-token-volume-projection) have expiration
  • Legacy tokens are NON-expiring — they are permanent backdoors
  • The CKS exam WILL test you on disabling automount and enabling bound tokens
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">OIDC (OpenID Connect) — Enterprise Authentication</div>
<pre>
  HOW IT WORKS:
  1. User authenticates with external IdP (Google, GitHub, Azure AD, Okta)
  2. IdP returns an ID Token (JWT) to the user
  3. User includes ID Token in kubectl requests: --token=&lt;id_token&gt;
  4. API server validates the token against the IdP's public keys
  5. The JWT's "sub" claim becomes the username; "groups" claim → RBAC groups

  API server configuration (Ch 5):
  --oidc-issuer-url=https://accounts.google.com
  --oidc-client-id=easypj-cluster.apps.googleusercontent.com
  --oidc-username-claim=email
  --oidc-groups-claim=groups

  Why it matters for CKS:
  • This is the ENTERPRISE auth method — used with Dashboards and CI/CD
  • API server flags above are CKS exam material (Ch 5, 8)
  • OIDC enables MFA — user must pass IdP's MFA before getting kubectl access
  • Groups from the IdP (e.g., "k8s-admins", "k8s-developers") map to RBAC
  • Without OIDC, you manage individual certs/tokens — does not scale
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>RBAC in Practice — The easypj Example</h4>
                <p>Let's trace exactly what happens when a developer from the easypj team tries to deploy a new version. This is the mental model you need for every RBAC question on the exam:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">RBAC Walkthrough: kubectl apply easypj-deploy.yaml</div>
<pre>
  STEP 1: Authentication (Gate 1)
  ───────────────────────────────
  User "sarah" (developer on easypj team)
  Authentication: OIDC via Google Workspace
  Her JWT ID Token contains:
    sub: "sarah@easypj.com"
    groups: ["k8s-developers", "easypj-team"]

  API server identifies her as: user "sarah@easypj.com", groups ["k8s-developers","easypj-team"]


  STEP 2: Authorization — RBAC Lookup (Gate 2)
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  She runs: kubectl apply -f easypj-deployment.yaml

  The API server checks:
  ① Does Sarah have "create deployments" permission in namespace "easypj"?
  ② RBAC lookup algorithm:
     a. Find all RoleBindings in "easypj" namespace that reference Sarah
        OR reference a group she belongs to
     b. Find all ClusterRoleBindings that reference Sarah or her groups
     c. Combine all Roles/ClusterRoles found
     d. Check if any of them allow "create" on "deployments" in "easypj"

  Example RBAC that ALLOWS her:
  ──────────────────────────────
  apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
  kind: RoleBinding
  metadata:
    name: easypj-developers
    namespace: easypj               ← Only in easypj namespace!
  subjects:
  - kind: Group
    name: easypj-team               ← Sarah's OIDC group
    apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  roleRef:
    kind: ClusterRole
    name: edit                      ← Built-in edit role
    apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

  RESULT: Sarah CAN create deployments in easypj namespace.
  She CANNOT create deployments in "kube-system" or any other namespace.
  She CANNOT read Secrets even in easypj (edit role does not include secrets).


  STEP 3: Admission Control (Gate 3)
  ──────────────────────────────────
  Her deployment YAML passes RBAC. Now admission controllers check it:
  • PodSecurity: namespace "easypj" labeled "pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted"
  • The deployment's pod spec must meet restricted PSS:
    - Must be non-root
    - No hostPath volumes
    - No privileged containers
    - Must have Seccomp profile
  • OPA/Gatekeeper: custom policy checks image is from easypj-registry.io/*
  • ImagePolicyWebhook: verifies image signature (cosign)

  If all pass → Deployment created. Pod scheduled. Container starts.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Debugging Gate Failures — Real kubectl Examples</h4>
                <p>On the CKS exam, you WILL encounter broken authentication or authorization. Here are the exact error messages, what they mean, and how to fix them. Memorize these patterns:</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Error Message</th><th>Which Gate</th><th>Root Cause</th><th>Fix Command</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>error: You must be logged in to the server (Unauthorized)</code></td><td>Gate 1 (AuthN)</td><td>No valid credentials in kubeconfig. Cert expired or missing.</td><td><code>kubectl config view</code> → check cert expiration. Renew with <code>kubeadm certs renew</code> (Ch 12)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>Error from server (Forbidden): pods is forbidden: User "system:serviceaccount:default:default" cannot create resource "pods" in the namespace "default"</code></td><td>Gate 2 (AuthZ)</td><td>SA has no RoleBinding allowing pod creation. Classic default SA problem.</td><td>Create RoleBinding: <code>kubectl create rolebinding create-pods --role=edit --sa=default:easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>Error from server (Forbidden): ...admission webhook "validation.gatekeeper.sh" denied the request: Container is running as root. Set runAsNonRoot: true.</code></td><td>Gate 3 (Admission)</td><td>OPA/Gatekeeper or PSS policy rejected the pod. Policy reason is included in error.</td><td>Fix the pod spec: add <code>securityContext.runAsNonRoot: true</code> and <code>runAsUser: 1000</code> (Ch 17, 18)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>Error from server (Forbidden): secrets is forbidden: User "sarah@easypj.com" cannot list resource "secrets" in the namespace "easypj"</code></td><td>Gate 2 (AuthZ)</td><td>RBAC allows deployments but NOT secrets. Built-in "edit" role does not include secrets.</td><td>RBAC is working correctly — least privilege by design. Create custom Role to allow secrets access if needed (Ch 9).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>error: unable to recognize "pod.yaml": no matches for kind "Pod"</code></td><td>Gate 2 (AuthZ)</td><td>You are authenticated but RBAC denies LIST on pods. API server hides resources you cannot see.</td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i list pods</code>. Check RoleBindings for your user/group (Ch 9).</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

