            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s3-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.3 Your Security Toolkit — Tools You Will Use Throughout This Guide</h3>
                <p>The CKS curriculum is built around a core set of open-source security tools. You will use these tools in nearly every chapter from this point forward. This section introduces each tool: what it does, when to use it, whether it appears on the CKS exam, and the key command you must memorize. Treat this as your toolbox reference — come back here whenever you encounter an unfamiliar tool in later chapters.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 8 Tools, 8 Purposes</h5>
                    <p>Each tool addresses a specific stage of the Kubernetes security lifecycle. Together they form a complete pipeline: <strong>Assess → Scan → Enforce → Sign → Sandbox → Detect → Manage.</strong> (AppArmor and Seccomp both live in the Sandbox stage — see the pipeline diagram below for the full tool-by-tool breakdown.) The CKS exam tests your ability to configure and use each tool in the right context. Some are mandatory (Falco, kube-bench), others are good-to-know (cosign, Vault).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>CKS Security Toolkit — 8 Tools You Must Master</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div>
                        <h5>kube-bench — CIS Benchmark Checker</h5>
                        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Checks your cluster against the CIS Kubernetes Benchmark — hundreds of security configuration tests covering API server, etcd, kubelet, and worker nodes. <strong>When to use:</strong> After cluster setup, after upgrades, and during regular security audits. <strong>CKS Exam:</strong> ✅ Yes — you must know how to run <code>kube-bench</code> and interpret its output. <strong>Key command:</strong> <code>kube-bench run --targets master,node --check all</code>. <strong>Output:</strong> PASS/WARN/FAIL for each CIS check, with remediation steps for each FAIL. Used extensively in Chapter 4.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔍</div>
                        <h5>Trivy — Image Vulnerability Scanner</h5>
                        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Scans container images for known CVEs (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures), misconfigurations, and embedded secrets. <strong>When to use:</strong> In CI/CD pipelines before pushing images, and periodically against running clusters. <strong>CKS Exam:</strong> ✅ Yes — you must know how to scan an image and interpret CVE severity levels. <strong>Key command:</strong> <code>trivy image easypj:v1 --severity HIGH,CRITICAL</code>. <strong>Output:</strong> CVE ID, severity, affected package, fixed version, and reference URLs. Used extensively in Chapter 23.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🦅</div>
                        <h5>Falco — Runtime Threat Detection</h5>
                        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Monitors system calls in real-time to detect anomalous behavior — shell spawns in containers, unexpected network connections, file writes to sensitive paths. <strong>When to use:</strong> Continuously, as a DaemonSet on every node. <strong>CKS Exam:</strong> ✅ MUST know — this is one of the most heavily tested tools. You must know how to deploy Falco, read its rules, and write custom rules. <strong>Key command:</strong> <code>falco -r /etc/falco/falco_rules.yaml</code>. <strong>Alert example:</strong> "A shell was spawned in a container (user=root container_id=abc123)." Used extensively in Chapter 29.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>OPA/Gatekeeper — Policy-as-Code</h5>
                        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Enforces custom security policies at admission time using Rego, a declarative policy language. Gatekeeper wraps OPA with Kubernetes-native CRDs. <strong>When to use:</strong> As a ValidatingWebhook — every pod creation request is checked against your policies. <strong>CKS Exam:</strong> ✅ Yes — you must know how to write and deploy OPA policies. <strong>Key command:</strong> <code>kubectl apply -f constraint-template.yaml && kubectl apply -f constraint.yaml</code>. <strong>Example policy:</strong> "Reject any pod that runs as root or uses hostPath." Used extensively in Chapter 24.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔏</div>
                        <h5>cosign — Image Signing & Verification</h5>
                        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Cryptographically signs container images and verifies signatures before deployment. Part of the Sigstore project. <strong>When to use:</strong> In CI/CD after build (sign), and at admission time via Policy Controller (verify). <strong>CKS Exam:</strong> ✅ Know it — you must understand the concept and basic usage. <strong>Key command:</strong> <code>cosign sign --key cosign.key myregistry/easypj:v1</code> and <code>cosign verify --key cosign.pub myregistry/easypj:v1</code>. Used extensively in Chapter 26.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>AppArmor — Application Sandboxing</h5>
                        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Linux kernel module that restricts what files, capabilities, and network resources a process (container) can access. <strong>When to use:</strong> Applied as an annotation on every pod to restrict its filesystem and network access. <strong>CKS Exam:</strong> ✅ Yes — you must know how to load profiles and apply them via annotations. <strong>Key command:</strong> <code>apparmor_parser -a /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-profile</code>. <strong>Pod annotation:</strong> <code>container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/easypj: localhost/easypj-profile</code>. Used extensively in Chapter 14.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5>Seccomp — Syscall Filtering</h5>
                        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Filters which Linux system calls a container is allowed to make. A Seccomp profile can reduce the attack surface from 300+ syscalls to just the 50-60 your application actually needs. <strong>When to use:</strong> Applied via SecurityContext on every pod. <strong>CKS Exam:</strong> ✅ Yes — you must know how to create and apply custom Seccomp profiles. <strong>Key command:</strong> The profile is a JSON file stored in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code>. <strong>Pod field:</strong> <code>securityContext.seccompProfile.type: Localhost</code> + <code>localhostProfile: easypj-seccomp.json</code>. Used extensively in Chapter 15.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏦</div>
                        <h5>Vault — External Secret Management</h5>
                        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> HashiCorp Vault stores, rotates, and dynamically generates secrets outside Kubernetes. Pods authenticate to Vault and receive time-limited credentials — secrets never touch etcd. <strong>When to use:</strong> For production secrets that must never be stored in Kubernetes Secrets at all. <strong>CKS Exam:</strong> ✅ Know it — you must understand the concept and basic integration pattern. <strong>Key command:</strong> <code>vault kv get secret/easypj/database</code>. <strong>K8s integration:</strong> Vault Sidecar Injector or CSI driver mounts secrets as tmpfs volumes. Used extensively in Chapter 20.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

