                <h4>Extended Component → Security Responsibility Matrix</h4>
                <p>The earlier component map covered the 5 core components. But a real cluster has more moving parts. Here is every component the CKS exam may reference — and exactly what security responsibility each one owns:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>K8s Component</th><th>Security Responsibility</th><th>Key Configuration</th><th>CKS Chapter</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td>AuthN, AuthZ, Admission, Audit, TLS termination</td><td><code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>, <code>--authorization-mode=RBAC</code>, <code>--audit-policy-file</code></td><td>Ch 8, 9, 28</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td>Encryption at rest, mTLS, network isolation</td><td><code>--encryption-provider-config</code>, <code>--peer-cert-file</code>, firewall on port 2379</td><td>Ch 8, 19</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubelet</strong></td><td>Pod security enforcement, anonymous auth off, cert rotation</td><td><code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>, <code>--authorization-mode=Webhook</code>, <code>--rotate-certificates</code></td><td>Ch 8, 11, 12</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td>TLS-only communication, least-privilege RBAC</td><td><code>--tls-cert-file</code>, <code>--tls-private-key-file</code>. Only needs "bind" and "update" on pods.</td><td>Ch 8, 9</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kube-controller-manager</strong></td><td>TLS, ServiceAccount signing key rotation</td><td><code>--service-account-private-key-file</code>, <code>--use-service-account-credentials</code></td><td>Ch 8, 10</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Container runtime</strong></td><td>Seccomp, AppArmor, namespace isolation, cgroup limits</td><td><code>/etc/containerd/config.toml</code>, <code>/etc/crio/crio.conf</code> for runtime-level Seccomp defaults</td><td>Ch 14, 15, 21</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CNI plugin</strong></td><td>NetworkPolicy enforcement, pod-to-pod traffic filtering</td><td>CNI-specific config. Must support NetworkPolicy (Calico, Cilium, Weave; NOT Flannel)</td><td>Ch 6</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CoreDNS</strong></td><td>DNS security, prevent cache poisoning, restrict external queries</td><td><code>prometheus</code> plugin for monitoring, <code>errors</code> plugin for logging. Restrict to cluster domain.</td><td>Ch 8</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Ingress controller</strong></td><td>TLS termination, WAF integration, rate limiting, request filtering</td><td>TLS Secret in Ingress spec, <code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/whitelist-source-range</code>, ModSecurity WAF rules</td><td>Ch 5, 7</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Image registry</strong></td><td>Private, authenticated, scanned images — the source of truth for everything the cluster runs</td><td>Private registry with mandatory auth (no anonymous pulls), Trivy scanning on push, <code>imagePullSecrets</code>, cosign signature verification before pull</td><td>Ch 23, 26</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key Takeaway for the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p>Every CKS exam task is a combination of <strong>Component + Tool</strong>. "Scan the image for CVEs" → Component: CI/CD pipeline, Tool: Trivy. "Block privileged pods" → Component: API server (admission), Tool: OPA/Gatekeeper. "Detect shell spawn in containers" → Component: worker node (runtime), Tool: Falco. <strong>When you see a task, identify the component first, then the tool. This chapter's component map + tool reference is your answer key for the entire exam.</strong></p>
                </div>

