            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s3-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.4 The Complete API Request Lifecycle — The Five Security Gates</h3>
                <p>When you type <code>kubectl get pods -n easypj</code>, that simple command triggers a chain of security checks across four Kubernetes components. Understanding this chain is the <strong>single most valuable mental model for the CKS exam</strong> — because every exam task involves configuring one of these gates. Here is the exact sequence, with the component, the security check, and the CKS chapter that controls it:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The kubectl request lifecycle — Authentication → Authorization → Admission → Auditing</div>
<pre>
  $ kubectl get pods -n easypj
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  GATE 1: TLS MUTUAL AUTHENTICATION (kube-apiserver, port 6443)          │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────                    │
  │  • kubectl presents its client certificate to the API server.           │
  │  • API server verifies the certificate against its --client-ca-file.    │
  │  • If the cert is NOT signed by the cluster's CA → connection REFUSED. │
  │  ⚡ CKS: Chapter 7 (TLS certificates), Chapter 8 (API server hardening) │
  │  ⚡ SECURITY: Stops attackers at the network layer — they can't even     │
  │     reach the API if they don't have a valid cluster-signed cert.        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │  (TLS handshake succeeded — encrypted channel established)
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  GATE 2: AUTHENTICATION (kube-apiserver)                                 │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────                                │
  │  • API server extracts the Common Name (CN) from the client certificate. │
  │  • CN = "k8s-admin" → this is the username.                              │
  │  • Group "O" (Organization) field = "system:masters" → group membership. │
  │  • Other auth methods: bearer tokens (ServiceAccount JWTs), OIDC,       │
  │    webhook token authentication.                                          │
  │  • If anonymous-auth is enabled (the dangerous default!) and no cert is  │
  │    provided → request is authenticated as "system:anonymous".            │
  │  ⚡ CKS: Chapter 8 (disable anonymous auth), Chapter 10 (SA tokens)      │
  │  ⚡ SECURITY: Identifies WHO is making the request. Without this,         │
  │     authorization is impossible — everything is "anonymous."              │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │  (Authenticated as: k8s-admin, groups: [system:masters])
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  GATE 3: AUTHORIZATION (kube-apiserver → RBAC)                           │
  │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────                           │
  │  • API server asks the authorizer: "Can user 'k8s-admin' [get]           │
  │    [pods] in namespace [easypj]?"                                         │
  │  • RBAC authorizer checks ALL ClusterRoleBindings + RoleBindings.        │
  │  • Finds: RoleBinding "easypj-reader" binds user "k8s-admin" to Role     │
  │    "pod-reader" which has: verbs: [get, list], resources: [pods].         │
  │  • MATCH → ALLOWED. kube-apiserver proceeds to the next gate.            │
  │  • If NO matching binding → DENIED. 403 Forbidden returned.              │
  │  ⚡ CKS: Chapter 9 (RBAC), Chapter 11 (Node authorization)               │
  │  ⚡ SECURITY: Determines WHAT the authenticated user can do. The most    │
  │     heavily tested CKS domain — every exam has at least 2 RBAC tasks.    │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │  (Authorized: get pods in easypj → ALLOWED)
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  GATE 4: ADMISSION CONTROL (kube-apiserver → webhooks)                   │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────                    │
  │  • NOTE: Admission control runs for MUTATING requests (create, update,   │
  │    delete). For READ requests (get, list, watch), this gate is SKIPPED.  │
  │  • For a CREATE pod request:                                              │
  │    a) MUTATING admission webhooks run first: Kyverno adds a default      │
  │       runAsNonRoot: true if not specified. Istio injects the sidecar.    │
  │    b) VALIDATING admission webhooks run second: OPA checks that the      │
  │       pod has runAsNonRoot=true. Kyverno verifies the image signature.   │
  │    c) If ANY validating webhook returns DENY → request is REJECTED.      │
  │  ⚡ CKS: Chapter 24 (Admission Controllers), Chapter 26 (cosign/Kyverno) │
  │  ⚡ SECURITY: Policy enforcement BEFORE the object is persisted to etcd. │
  │     Stops bad configurations BEFORE they become running pods.            │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │  (For this GET request: admission is SKIPPED — GET doesn't mutate state)
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  GATE 5: AUDIT LOGGING (kube-apiserver records the event)                │
  │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────                   │
  │  • Audit policy determines what gets logged and at what level.           │
  │  • For this GET request (if audit policy includes pods/get):             │
  │    {"kind":"Event","level":"RequestResponse","verb":"get",                │
  │     "user":{"username":"k8s-admin","groups":["system:masters"]},          │
  │     "objectRef":{"resource":"pods","namespace":"easypj"},                 │
  │     "responseStatus":{"code":200},                                        │
  │     "requestReceivedTimestamp":"2026-06-14T11:23:45Z"}                    │
  │  ⚡ CKS: Chapter 28 (Audit Logging)                                       │
  │  ⚡ SECURITY: Non-repudiation. Every action is recorded with WHO,         │
  │     WHAT, WHEN, and RESULT. In a breach investigation, this is gold.      │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  kubectl receives the list of pods in easypj namespace. Total gates passed: 4 of 5.
  (Admission skipped for GET. All other gates: passed.)
</pre>
                </div>
                <p><strong>The CKS exam maps to these gates:</strong> Gate 1 (TLS) → Ch7-8. Gate 2 (AuthN) → Ch8, 10. Gate 3 (AuthZ) → Ch9, 11. Gate 4 (Admission) → Ch24, 26. Gate 5 (Audit) → Ch28. When you see an exam question, identify which gate it's asking about, and you'll know exactly which chapter's tools to reach for.</p>

                <h4>Gate Reference — Quick Lookup Table</h4>
                <p>Use this table as your fast lookup during the exam: read the error message (or the lack of one), find the matching row, jump straight to the chapter. Note that Gate 4 (Admission Control) is really <strong>two sub-gates run back to back</strong> — <strong>4a Mutating</strong> runs first and can silently rewrite the object with no error at all, <strong>4b Validating</strong> runs second and can only accept or reject what 4a produced. A "policy denied" message and a "mystery field appeared in my pod" symptom both trace back to Gate 4, but they are two different sub-gates doing two different jobs.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Question</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Failure Signal</th><th>Chapter</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>1 — TLS</strong></td><td>Is the channel encrypted and trusted?</td><td>Client/server certs, mutual TLS to the API endpoint</td><td>TLS handshake error (wrong CA / expired cert)</td><td>Ch 7, 8</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>2 — AuthN</strong></td><td>Who are you?</td><td>Client certs, bearer/SA tokens, OIDC, webhook</td><td>401 Unauthorized</td><td>Ch 8, 10</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>3 — AuthZ</strong></td><td>Are you allowed to do this?</td><td>Node authorizer → RBAC → Webhook (evaluated in order)</td><td>403 Forbidden — plain, no policy reason in the message</td><td>Ch 9, 11</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>4a — Mutating Admission</strong></td><td>Should the spec be rewritten before it's judged?</td><td>MutatingWebhookConfigurations (Kyverno mutate rules, Istio sidecar injector)</td><td>No error — but fields you never wrote appear in the stored object</td><td>Ch 24</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>4b — Validating Admission</strong></td><td>Is the (possibly mutated) spec acceptable?</td><td>Pod Security Standards, OPA/Gatekeeper, Kyverno validate rules, ImagePolicyWebhook, ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL, in-process)</td><td>403 Forbidden — WITH a policy/webhook reason in the message</td><td>Ch 17, 23, 24</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>5 — Audit</strong></td><td>What happened, for the permanent record?</td><td>Audit Policy → <code>audit.log</code> or webhook backend</td><td>Never blocks the request — it only records the outcome</td><td>Ch 28</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>— Persist (etcd)</strong></td><td>Is the data itself safe once it's written down?</td><td>Not a gate — the final landing spot. <code>--encryption-provider-config</code> encrypts the object before it touches disk</td><td>N/A — nothing to fail here; a plaintext Secret here means an earlier control was never configured</td><td>Ch 19</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align:center;color:#93c5fd;font-size:0.9em;"><strong>Why this row isn't "Gate 6":</strong> the 5 gates all <em>decide</em> whether a request proceeds; persistence to etcd is what happens <em>after</em> every gate has already said yes. It earns a row in this lookup table because "is my data safe" is a distinct question from "was my request allowed" — and the answer lives in Chapter 19, not in any authorizer or webhook.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 This Guide's "5 Gates" vs. the Official Docs' Numbered Steps — Same Chain, Different Bookkeeping</h5>
                    <p>The official Kubernetes documentation (<code>kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/controlling-access/</code>) walks through this exact same chain with its own numbering: <strong>Transport Security</strong> (TLS — described as happening before any numbered step) → <strong>Step 1: Authentication</strong> → <strong>Step 2: Authorization</strong> → <strong>Step 3: Admission Control</strong> → <strong>Step 4: the request is validated against the target object's schema, then written to the store</strong>. Two differences are worth reconciling explicitly, because both are easy to misread going into the exam:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Audit isn't one of the docs' numbered steps at all.</strong> The official page treats auditing as a parallel, chronological record of the whole chain — not a link in it. This guide numbers it "Gate 5" anyway, because from a debugging point of view it IS a fifth place to look ("did this even happen, and who did it?") even though it architecturally never blocks anything (see the Gotchas section below). Same reality, different bookkeeping choice — use whichever vocabulary the question in front of you is using.</li>
                        <li><strong>The docs' "Step 4" is NOT the same thing as this guide's Gate 4b (Validating Admission).</strong> Step 4 is built-in, non-negotiable object-schema validation (does this even look like a structurally valid Pod?) that runs <em>after</em> every admission plugin has already said yes, immediately before the object is written to etcd. It has no <code>failurePolicy</code>, no webhook, nothing to enable or disable — you cannot "fix" it on the exam because it isn't a security policy, it's basic type-safety the API server always performs. Gate 4b's PSS / OPA / Kyverno / ValidatingAdmissionPolicy checks are security <em>policy</em> decisions, and they all run <em>before</em> this schema check, as part of Admission Control (the docs' Step 3).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="margin-top:8px;">Neither framing is "more correct" — the docs' 4-step-plus-audit view is the canonical source-of-truth vocabulary; this guide's 5-gate view exists purely to give every distinct exam symptom (TLS error, 401, plain 403, 403-with-reason, "nothing was rejected but I need proof of what happened") its own number. Cite the docs' words in an incident writeup; use this guide's gate numbers when you're triaging a broken cluster against the clock.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Worked Example — One easypj Deployment, All 5 Gates, Start to Finish</h4>
                <p>The diagram above traced a read-only <code>GET</code> that skips Gate 4 entirely. Here is the case the exam loves to test instead — a <code>CREATE</code> request that fires every gate, including both admission sub-phases, back to back:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubectl apply -f easypj-deploy.yaml — submitted by sarah@easypj.com</div>
<pre>
  GATE 1  TLS             kubectl's client cert is signed by the cluster CA           ✅ PASS
  GATE 2  AuthN           Cert/OIDC identity maps to user "sarah@easypj.com"          ✅ PASS
  GATE 3  AuthZ           RoleBinding "easypj-developers" (group easypj-team) grants
                          the built-in "edit" ClusterRole in namespace "easypj" →
                          create/update on deployments is allowed here                 ✅ PASS
  GATE 4a Mutating Adm.   Kyverno's mutate rule rewrites the pod template:
                          + runAsNonRoot: true                    (default injected)
                          + capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]             (default injected)
                          + seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault    (default injected)
                                                                        (OBJECT REWRITTEN)
  GATE 4b Validating Adm. PSS "restricted" + an OPA Gatekeeper constraint re-check
                          the NOW-MUTATED pod: non-root ✅, no hostPath ✅, image
                          from easypj-registry.io and cosign-verified ✅               ✅ PASS
  GATE 5  Audit           JSON event recorded: user=sarah@easypj.com verb=create
                          resource=deployments namespace=easypj decision=allow         recorded

  ✅ RESULT: Deployment persisted to etcd (any Secrets it mounts are encrypted at
     rest, Ch 19) → scheduler assigns a healthy node → kubelet pulls the cosign-
     verified image → container starts with the mutated, hardened securityContext.
</pre>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align:center;color:#93c5fd;font-size:0.9em;"><strong>Compare the two worked examples:</strong> the read-only GET stops mattering at Gate 3 and never touches Gate 4; this CREATE fires every gate — and Gate 4 alone quietly does two different jobs (rewrite, then judge) before the object is ever allowed anywhere near etcd.</p>
