                    <h5>① The RBAC Authorizer Chain — Why "Explicit Deny" Doesn't Exist in RBAC</h5>
                    <p>Section 3.2 said "RBAC checks if a Role allows the verb" — but the mechanism the exam actually tests is <strong>how the authorizer chain decides</strong>. With <code>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</code>, the API server calls each configured authorizer <em>in order</em>, and each one returns exactly one of three verdicts: <code>Allow</code>, <code>Deny</code>, or <code>NoOpinion</code>. The chain short-circuits the instant any authorizer gives a decisive answer — and RBAC's own verdict set has a surprising gap.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  AUTHORIZER CHAIN — evaluated strictly in the order configured
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC

  Request: can "system:node:worker-1" GET secrets mounted on its own node?
       │
       ▼
  ① Node authorizer  ── recognizes group system:nodes + user system:node:*
       │                  → Allow      (kubelet reading its own node's objects)
       │                  → NoOpinion  (not a kubelet-shaped request) ────┐
       ▼ Allow                                                            │
  ✅ SHORT-CIRCUIT — chain stops here. RBAC is NEVER EVEN CONSULTED.       │
                                                                           ▼
                                                             ② RBAC authorizer
                                                             → Allow      (a Role/RoleBinding
                                                                           grants this verb+resource)
                                                             → NoOpinion  (NO rule matches — RBAC
                                                               has NO "explicit deny" verdict, only
                                                               "I found nothing that allows this")
                                                                           │
                                                                           ▼
                                                 no more authorizers configured
                                                 → implicit DENY → HTTP 403 Forbidden
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Verdict</th><th>What it means</th><th>Effect on the chain</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>Allow</code></td><td>This authorizer explicitly permits the request</td><td>Chain stops immediately — request proceeds to Admission (Gate 4)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>Deny</code></td><td>This authorizer explicitly forbids the request</td><td>Chain stops immediately — 403 Forbidden returned</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>NoOpinion</code></td><td>This authorizer has no rule about this request</td><td>Chain continues to the next authorizer</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>(end of chain)</td><td>Every configured authorizer returned NoOpinion</td><td>Implicit <strong>Deny</strong> — RBAC's "default-deny" behavior comes from this fallthrough, not from any rule you wrote</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The #1 RBAC misconception the exam exploits:</strong> RBAC has <strong>no "deny" rule type at all</strong> — you cannot write a Role that says "deny delete on secrets." RBAC only ever grants. If a ClusterRoleBinding anywhere else in the cluster grants a broader permission to the same user or group, that <code>Allow</code> wins regardless of how narrow a Role you wrote for them — because the very first <code>Allow</code> found anywhere in the chain short-circuits everything else, including any other RBAC rule. To restrict access you must remove or narrow the grant; there is no "add a deny rule" escape hatch.</div>

                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 <code>system:masters</code> is not "another group with a wide RoleBinding" — it is hardcoded into the RBAC authorizer itself, bypassing the Allow/Deny/NoOpinion lookup entirely.</strong> Any identity whose certificate carries the Organization (O) field <code>system:masters</code> — exactly how <code>kubeadm</code>'s <code>admin.conf</code> super-admin credential and every control-plane component's client cert are built — is treated as an automatic <code>Allow</code> before RBAC ever reads a single Role or RoleBinding object. Two consequences the exam loves to test: <strong>(1) Deleting the <code>cluster-admin</code> ClusterRoleBinding does NOTHING to a <code>system:masters</code> identity.</strong> There was never an RBAC object granting that access — it's a hardcoded authorizer shortcut, not a binding you can find with <code>kubectl get clusterrolebindings</code> or remove with <code>kubectl delete</code>. The only way to revoke it is to stop the API server trusting the certificate at all (reissue it under a different O field, or rotate the CA and every cert signed by it). <strong>(2) Never hand a <code>system:masters</code> cert to anything short-lived or automatable</strong> — a CI pipeline, a controller, a developer laptop — because it is full, permanent, unauditable-by-RBAC superuser access for as long as that certificate remains valid. kubeadm-issued leaf certificates (including <code>admin.conf</code>) default to a 1-year validity and must be explicitly rotated with <code>kubeadm certs renew</code> (Ch 12); only the cluster's root CA itself is issued for 10 years. On a CKS exam finding of "who has cluster-admin," always check for <code>system:masters</code> group membership in certificates FIRST — RBAC bindings alone will not show it to you.</div>

                    <div class="info-box note">
                        <h5>📖 Beyond <code>--authorization-mode</code>: the structured <code>AuthorizationConfiguration</code> file</h5>
                        <p>Everything above describes the classic <code>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC,Webhook</code> comma-separated flag — still the CKS baseline and still exactly how the authorizer chain behaves. But modern clusters (the <code>AuthorizationConfiguration</code> structured config file reached GA/stable in Kubernetes v1.32) can replace that flag with a YAML file passed via <code>--authorization-config</code>. The structured file solves one specific gap the flag can't: <strong>the old flag allows only ONE Webhook authorizer</strong> in the chain, while the structured file lets you configure <strong>multiple independent Webhook authorizers</strong>, each with its own <code>failurePolicy</code> (<code>Deny</code> vs <code>NoOpinion</code> on error) and CEL <code>matchConditions</code> to control exactly which requests reach it. You cannot mix the two approaches — setting both <code>--authorization-config</code> and <code>--authorization-mode</code>/<code>--authorization-webhook-*</code> flags makes the API server refuse to start. Same underlying Node → RBAC → Webhook chain-of-authorizers model you just learned; just a richer way to configure the Webhook links in that chain.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">/etc/kubernetes/authorization-config.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: AuthorizationConfiguration
authorizers:
- type: Node
  name: node-authorizer
- type: RBAC
  name: rbac-authorizer
- type: Webhook
  name: easypj-audit-gate           <span class="comment"># 1st webhook: soft, informational</span>
  webhook:
    timeout: 3s
    subjectAccessReviewVersion: v1
    failurePolicy: NoOpinion         <span class="comment"># unreachable → defer, don't block</span>
    matchConditions:
    - expression: "request.resourceAttributes.namespace == 'easypj-prod'"
    connectionInfo:
      type: KubeConfigFile
      kubeConfigFile: /etc/kubernetes/easypj-audit-webhook.kubeconfig
- type: Webhook
  name: easypj-policy-gate          <span class="comment"># 2nd webhook: hard, security-critical</span>
  webhook:
    timeout: 3s
    subjectAccessReviewVersion: v1
    failurePolicy: Deny              <span class="comment"># unreachable → fail closed, block the request</span>
    matchConditions:
    - expression: "request.resourceAttributes.resource == 'secrets'"
    connectionInfo:
      type: KubeConfigFile
      kubeConfigFile: /etc/kubernetes/easypj-policy-webhook.kubeconfig

<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver.yaml:
#   --authorization-config=/etc/kubernetes/authorization-config.yaml
#   (mutually exclusive with --authorization-mode / --authorization-webhook-*)</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p style="margin-top:8px;">Two Webhook authorizers, two different failure modes, controlled by <strong>which requests they even see</strong>: the flag-based chain can never express this — it has exactly one <code>failurePolicy</code> for its one Webhook, applied to every request that reaches it. Here, a non-critical audit webhook can fail open (<code>NoOpinion</code>) on any namespace, while a Secrets-specific policy webhook fails closed (<code>Deny</code>) — and CEL <code>matchConditions</code> route requests to the right one before either fires.</p>
                    </div>

                    <h5>② Admission Webhooks Under the Hood — AdmissionReview, JSONPatch &amp; the Fields That Decide Fail-Open vs Fail-Closed</h5>
                    <p>OPA/Gatekeeper, Kyverno, and every custom admission controller mentioned in this guide are really just <strong>HTTPS endpoints</strong> the API server calls with a JSON body. Knowing the exact request/response shape — and the handful of webhook configuration fields that control its failure behavior — is what lets you debug "my policy isn't running" instead of guessing.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  MutatingWebhookConfiguration / ValidatingWebhookConfiguration — the wire format

  API server ──POST https://gatekeeper-webhook.gatekeeper-system.svc/v1/admit──▶ webhook
              AdmissionReview{ request: { uid, kind, namespace, operation: CREATE,
                                           object: {...the pod being created...} } }

  webhook responds ◀── AdmissionReview{ response: {
                            uid,
                            allowed: true|false,
                            status: { message: "why, if denied" },
                            patch: "&lt;base64 JSONPatch&gt;",        ← MUTATING ONLY
                            patchType: "JSONPatch"               ← MUTATING ONLY
                        }}

  Example JSONPatch a mutating webhook returns (base64-decoded):
  [
    {"op":"add","path":"/spec/securityContext/runAsNonRoot","value":true},
    {"op":"add","path":"/spec/containers/0/securityContext/capabilities",
     "value":{"drop":["ALL"]}}
  ]
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Values</th><th>What it controls</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>failurePolicy</code></td><td><code>Fail</code> / <code>Ignore</code></td><td><code>Fail</code> = webhook unreachable → request REJECTED (fail-closed: secure, but risks an outage). <code>Ignore</code> = webhook unreachable → request ALLOWED anyway (fail-open: available, but risks a silent policy bypass).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>matchPolicy</code></td><td><code>Exact</code> / <code>Equivalent</code></td><td><code>Equivalent</code> (default) also matches requests sent via an older, equivalent API version — prevents silently skipping callers still using <code>v1beta1</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>sideEffects</code></td><td><code>None</code> / <code>NoneOnDryRun</code></td><td>Must be set correctly or <code>kubectl apply --dry-run=server</code> and <code>kubectl diff</code> break for every user in the cluster.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>timeoutSeconds</code></td><td>1–30 (default 10)</td><td>How long the API server waits before treating the webhook as unreachable and applying <code>failurePolicy</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>namespaceSelector</code></td><td>label selector</td><td>Exempts matching namespaces (almost always <code>kube-system</code>) from the webhook entirely — without this, a strict policy can deadlock the cluster's own control-plane pods.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>reinvocationPolicy</code></td><td><code>Never</code> (default) / <code>IfNeeded</code></td><td>MUTATING webhooks only. <code>Never</code> = this webhook runs at most once per request. <code>IfNeeded</code> = if a LATER mutating webhook changes the object after this one already ran, the API server calls this webhook again on the newly-mutated object — which is exactly why every mutating webhook must be idempotent.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Never assume mutating webhook call order — the official Kubernetes documentation is explicit: "don't rely on mutating webhook invocation order."</strong> When more than one <code>MutatingWebhookConfiguration</code> matches the same request, the order they run in is <strong>not guaranteed</strong> to be alphabetical, registration order, or anything else you can predict — and with <code>reinvocationPolicy: IfNeeded</code> a webhook can even be called a second time mid-chain. The only safe design is to make every mutating webhook <strong>idempotent</strong>: applying it once or five times, in any order, produces the same final object. On the exam and in production, never write a mutating webhook that assumes "I run before/after webhook X."</div>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The bootstrap-deadlock trap:</strong> if a <code>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</code> with <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> has no <code>namespaceSelector</code> excluding <code>kube-system</code>, and the webhook's own pod ever needs to be recreated there (e.g. after a node reboot) while the webhook Service is briefly unreachable, the cluster can deadlock — the webhook can't come back up because creating its own replacement pod requires calling itself first. This is a real CKS exam scenario: always exempt <code>kube-system</code> (and the namespace the webhook itself lives in) unless you have a specific reason not to.</div>

                    <h5>③ Audit Logging Internals — the 4 Stages, Rule-Matching Order &amp; Backend Mechanics</h5>
                    <p>Gate 5 looks simple from the outside ("it just logs stuff"), but two internal mechanics are exam-tested: the audit pipeline actually fires <strong>up to four times per request</strong> — once per stage — and your Audit Policy's rules are evaluated <strong>top-to-bottom with first-match-wins</strong>, so reordering two rules can silently change what gets logged.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  THE 4 AUDIT STAGES — a single request can generate up to 4 audit events

  RequestReceived  ── the instant the API server receives the request
                       (only logged if the policy level is ≥ Metadata AND
                        omitStages doesn't exclude it — usually excluded, it's noisy)
       │
       ▼
  ResponseStarted  ── response headers sent, body still streaming
                       (relevant mainly for watch / long-running requests)
       │
       ▼
  ResponseComplete ── the FULL response has been sent — 95% of what you query
                       {"responseStatus":{"code":200 or 403 or 500}, ...}
       │
       ▼
  Panic            ── the API server panicked while handling the request
                       (rare — always worth alerting on if it ever appears)

  RULE-MATCHING ORDER — the FIRST rule that matches wins, no fallthrough:
  rules:
    - level: RequestResponse              ← put SPECIFIC / narrow rules FIRST
      resources: [{group:"", resources:["secrets"]}]
    - level: Metadata                     ← generic catch-all rules go LAST —
      resources: []                          if this were first, the secrets
                                              rule above would never be reached
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-log-maxage</code></td><td>Days to retain old audit log files before deletion</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-log-maxbackup</code></td><td>Number of rotated log files to keep on disk</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-log-maxsize</code></td><td>Megabytes a log file may reach before it's rotated</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-webhook-batch-max-size</code></td><td>Events batched into one webhook POST — the webhook backend is asynchronous, so a burst of events never blocks the API server from serving other requests</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Order your rules narrow-to-broad.</strong> Because the FIRST matching rule wins with no fallthrough, a catch-all <code>level: Metadata</code> rule placed <em>before</em> your specific <code>secrets</code> rule silently swallows it — the secrets rule becomes dead code that never fires. Always put your most specific resource rules first and the generic catch-all (or <code>level: None</code> for noise you don't want logged at all) last.</div>

                    <h5>④ ValidatingAdmissionPolicy — the CEL-Based, No-Webhook Way to Do Gate 4b</h5>
                    <p>Every Gate 4b example so far (PSS, OPA/Gatekeeper, Kyverno, ImagePolicyWebhook) works the same way under the hood: the API server makes an HTTPS call to an external service and waits for an <code>AdmissionReview</code> response. <strong><code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> (stable/GA since Kubernetes v1.30)</strong> is a fundamentally different mechanism for the exact same job — it evaluates a rule written in <strong>CEL (Common Expression Language)</strong> <em>in-process, inside the API server itself</em>. No Service, no Deployment, no network hop, no separate thing that can crash or become unreachable.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">ValidatingAdmissionPolicy + Binding — no webhook Service required</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicy
metadata:
  name: easypj-require-nonroot
spec:
  failurePolicy: Fail
  matchConstraints:
    resourceRules:
    - apiGroups: [""]
      apiVersions: ["v1"]
      operations: ["CREATE", "UPDATE"]
      resources: ["pods"]
  validations:
  - expression: "object.spec.securityContext.runAsNonRoot == true"
    message: "Pods in easypj namespaces must set runAsNonRoot: true"
---
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding
metadata:
  name: easypj-require-nonroot-binding
spec:
  policyName: easypj-require-nonroot
  validationActions: ["Deny"]        <span class="comment"># or ["Warn"], or ["Audit"] — never Deny+Warn together</span>
  matchResources:
    namespaceSelector:
      matchLabels: { environment: easypj-prod }</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration (OPA/Gatekeeper, Kyverno)</th><th>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Where the rule runs</strong></td><td>External process — API server calls out over HTTPS</td><td>In-process — compiled CEL, evaluated inside the API server</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Rule language</strong></td><td>Whatever the webhook's own code implements (Rego for OPA, YAML for Kyverno)</td><td>CEL expressions directly in the policy object</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Can go down independently?</strong></td><td>Yes — if the webhook Service/pod is unreachable, <code>failurePolicy</code> decides Fail vs Ignore</td><td>No separate backend to lose — it fails only if the API server itself is down</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Two resources needed</strong></td><td>1 (the <code>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</code>)</td><td>2 — <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> (the rule) + <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding</code> (what it applies to + <code>validationActions</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Evaluation order (same request)</strong></td><td>Runs <em>after</em> ValidatingAdmissionPolicy, if both match</td><td>Runs <em>before</em> any ValidatingWebhookConfiguration, within Gate 4b</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Still Gate 4b — just a second way to implement it.</strong> <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> does not add a new gate or change this chapter's 5-gate numbering — it is one more mechanism living inside Gate 4b, alongside PSS and webhooks. The one ordering fact worth memorizing: when a request matches both a <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> and an external <code>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</code>, the CEL policy is evaluated first, in-process, before the API server ever makes the network call to the webhook. For simple, self-contained checks (a required label, a forbidden field, a numeric limit) it is usually the lower-risk choice precisely because the bootstrap-deadlock trap two callouts up — a fail-closed webhook that can't come back up because its own replacement pod needs to call it first — cannot happen to a <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> at all; there is no separate pod to deadlock.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch3-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Every CKS control maps to exactly one Kubernetes component</strong> — apiserver (AuthN/AuthZ/Admission/Audit), etcd (encryption at rest), kubelet (node security), container runtime (Seccomp/AppArmor), CNI/kube-proxy (NetworkPolicy). Configuring the wrong component fixes nothing.</li>
                    <li><strong>Every API request passes through three sequential gates</strong> — Authentication (401 on failure) → Authorization (403) → Admission Control (403 + policy reason) — in a fixed, hardcoded order you cannot skip or reorder.</li>
                    <li><strong>Admission Control has two phases</strong> — Mutating webhooks run first and can modify the request (inject sidecars, add defaults); Validating webhooks run second and can only allow or deny.</li>
                    <li><strong>The CKS toolkit is 8 tools in one continuous pipeline</strong> — Assess (kube-bench) → Scan (Trivy) → Enforce (OPA/Kyverno) → Sign (cosign) → Sandbox (AppArmor/Seccomp) → Detect (Falco) → Manage (Vault).</li>
                    <li><strong>The full request lifecycle is actually 5 gates</strong> — TLS mutual auth → AuthN → AuthZ → Admission (mutating + validating) → Audit — but read-only GET/LIST requests skip Gate 4 (Admission) entirely.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch3-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 3 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Node Authorizer ≠ RBAC:</strong> The Node authorizer is a special-purpose authorizer that auto-allows kubelets to access their own pods, secrets, and PVs. RBAC is general-purpose. Both run in the authorizer chain but serve different purposes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Mutating before Validating:</strong> MutatingWebhooks run FIRST and can CHANGE the object. ValidatingWebhooks run SECOND on the mutated object. If you're debugging why your OPA policy is evaluating something unexpected, check if a mutating webhook modified the request first.</li>
                    <li><strong>AlwaysPullImage + ImagePolicyWebhook:</strong> These are separate admission plugins. AlwaysPullImage ensures the image is re-pulled before each pod start. ImagePolicyWebhook calls an external service to approve/reject images. Both should be enabled together for defense-in-depth.</li>
                    <li><strong>Audit logging never blocks anything:</strong> Gate 5 is a recorder, not a gatekeeper — a "deny" you see documented in an audit event already happened at Gate 2, 3, or 4; audit logging only writes down what the earlier gates decided. If a request is getting through that shouldn't be, don't waste exam time staring at the audit policy — the fix is always at an earlier gate.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>system:masters</code> group membership bypasses RBAC entirely — it is not a RoleBinding you can find or delete.</strong> Deleting the <code>cluster-admin</code> ClusterRoleBinding does nothing to a certificate whose Organization field is <code>system:masters</code>; that identity short-circuits the authorizer before any Role or RoleBinding is even consulted (full mechanics in this chapter's Under the Hood). Always check certificate O= fields first when auditing "who has admin," not just <code>kubectl get clusterrolebindings</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>A tool being "essential to CKS" doesn't mean it ships with Kubernetes.</strong> Falco, Trivy, kube-bench, OPA/Gatekeeper, cosign, and Vault are all third-party software you install and run yourself — there is no built-in equivalent. Only AuthN/AuthZ/Admission/Audit (the API server's own gates) and Seccomp/AppArmor (Linux kernel features the runtime calls into) are truly native. If an exam task says "detect" or "scan" or "sign," assume you must deploy or invoke a tool — it will not already be silently running.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

