            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl get --raw /healthz?verbose</code> — shows which API server health checks pass/fail, including poststarthook status for admission plugins.</li>
                    <li><strong>Remember the 5-gate pipeline in order:</strong> TLS → AuthN → AuthZ → Admission (4a Mutating, then 4b Validating) → Audit. If you know which gate rejected the request, you know exactly what to fix.</li>
                    <li><strong>Error-message triage in one glance:</strong> no response at all / TLS text → Gate 1. <code>401</code> → Gate 2. Plain <code>403</code> with no extra wording → Gate 3. <code>403</code> with a named policy/webhook in the message → Gate 4b. An unexplained field you never wrote → Gate 4a. Nothing rejected but you need to know who did it → Gate 5.</li>
                    <li><strong>Component-first, tool-second.</strong> Under exam time pressure, silently answer "which component enforces this?" before reaching for a command — it collapses most tasks to one obvious fix (etcd → encryption config; runtime → Seccomp/AppArmor; apiserver → RBAC/admission flag) instead of guessing across all 8 tools.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=&lt;identity&gt;</code> is the fastest possible RBAC audit — it queries the live authorizer chain directly, so it always beats manually reading Role/RoleBinding YAML and never misses a ClusterRoleBinding you forgot existed.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

