            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch3-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 3 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A developer reports: "kubectl apply works, but kubectl get pods returns Forbidden." Which security gate is failing, and what specific RBAC resource do you need to fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Gate 3 — Authorization.</strong> The user passed Authentication (Gate 2) but lacks the "get pods" permission. Fix: create a Role with <code>verbs: ["get", "list"]</code> on <code>resources: ["pods"]</code> and bind it to the user's ServiceAccount via a RoleBinding.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list --resource=pods -n easypj-prod
kubectl create rolebinding rb-pod-reader --role=pod-reader --serviceaccount=easypj-prod:dev-sa -n easypj-prod
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:dev-sa -n easypj-prod</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The API request flow is: TLS handshake → AuthN → AuthZ → Admission → Audit. "Forbidden" (HTTP 403) means AuthZ failed — the user is known but not authorized. "Unauthorized" (HTTP 401) means AuthN failed — the user's identity couldn't be verified. This distinction is critical on the CKS exam. Always use <code>kubectl auth can-i --as=&lt;user&gt;</code> to test RBAC before applying changes to production.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You deploy a pod and it's rejected with: "violates PodSecurity restricted:v1.30: runAsNonRoot != true." Which gate blocked this pod, and what TWO changes do you need to make?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Gate 4 — Admission Control.</strong> The Pod Security Admission controller rejected the pod. Fix: (1) Add <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> to the container securityContext. (2) Ensure your Dockerfile uses a non-root USER (e.g., <code>USER 1000</code>). The pod-level seccompProfile may also need to be RuntimeDefault.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000
  seccompProfile:
    type: RuntimeDefault</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Admission controllers are the <strong>last gate before etcd</strong>. Even if AuthN and AuthZ pass, an admission controller can reject the request. PSA (Pod Security Admission) is a built-in admission controller that enforces pod security standards. The error message tells you exactly what to fix: <code>runAsNonRoot != true</code> means your pod is trying to run as root. Add the securityContext above and redeploy. If your Dockerfile uses USER root, you must change it to a non-root user (e.g., USER 1000) for this to work.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to prove that a specific user accessed Kubernetes Secrets at 3:14 AM. Which gate provides this information, and what command would you run to find the evidence?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Gate 5 — Audit Logging.</strong> If audit logging is configured, every API request is recorded as a JSON event. Command:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">cat /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log | jq 'select(.objectRef.resource=="secrets" and .stageTimestamp >= "2026-06-14T03:00:00Z" and .stageTimestamp <= "2026-06-14T04:00:00Z") | {user: .user.username, verb, resource: .objectRef.resource, name: .objectRef.name, time: .stageTimestamp, code: .responseStatus.code}'</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Audit logging is the <strong>forensic record</strong> of everything that happens in your cluster. It captures: who, what, when, from which IP, and whether it succeeded or failed. The CKS exam tests your ability to (1) enable audit logging via API server flags, (2) create an Audit Policy to filter what's logged, and (3) query audit logs with jq to find specific events. The four audit stages are: RequestReceived → ResponseStarted → ResponseComplete → Panic. Most queries target ResponseComplete events because they include the HTTP status code.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between a MutatingWebhookConfiguration and a ValidatingWebhookConfiguration? At which security gate does each operate, and can a single request trigger both?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Both operate at <strong>Gate 4 — Admission Control</strong>. <strong>MutatingWebhooks</strong> run FIRST and can MODIFY the request (e.g., inject sidecars, add securityContext). <strong>ValidatingWebhooks</strong> run SECOND and can only ACCEPT or REJECT (e.g., OPA Gatekeeper checks Rego policies, Kyverno validates patterns). A single request triggers ALL matching mutating webhooks first, then ALL matching validating webhooks. If any validating webhook rejects, the request is denied.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>MutatingWebhookConfiguration</th><th>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Runs</strong></td><td>1st (Gate 4a)</td><td>2nd (Gate 4b) — sees the mutated object</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Can change the object?</strong></td><td>Yes — returns a base64 JSONPatch</td><td>No — accept or reject only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Call order (same phase)</strong></td><td><strong>NOT guaranteed</strong> — must be idempotent</td><td>Also not guaranteed to be ordered</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Typical CKS example</strong></td><td>Kyverno mutate rule injects <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>; Istio injects sidecar</td><td>PSS / OPA Gatekeeper checks the (now-mutated) pod against policy</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Symptom if this is the culprit</strong></td><td>Unexplained extra field appears — nothing was rejected</td><td>403 Forbidden with an explicit policy reason in the message</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The order is critical: <strong>Mutating → Validating</strong>. If you're debugging why a Kyverno policy is rejecting a pod, check if a mutating webhook (like Istio sidecar injection) modified the pod spec BEFORE Kyverno evaluated it. But do NOT assume you know which mutating webhook ran first if more than one is registered — the official Kubernetes documentation explicitly warns "don't rely on mutating webhook invocation order" and instead requires every mutating webhook to be idempotent (safe to apply more than once, in any order) precisely because the order across separate webhook configurations is not guaranteed. On the CKS exam, you may need to configure a webhook's failurePolicy: <code>Fail</code> (reject if webhook is down) vs <code>Ignore</code> (allow if webhook is down). For security-critical checks, always use <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The API server has <code>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</code>. A kubelet on worker-1 needs to report its pod status. Which authorizer handles this request, and why is Node listed BEFORE RBAC in the chain?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The <strong>Node authorizer</strong> handles kubelet requests. It's a special-purpose authorizer that auto-allows kubelets to access resources associated with their node (pods scheduled on that node, secrets mounted by those pods, PVs attached, etc.).</p>
                    <p><strong>Why Node before RBAC:</strong> Authorizers are evaluated in ORDER. Node comes first for efficiency — kubelet requests are high-volume and the Node authorizer can quickly approve them without checking RBAC rules. If Node denies (request not kubelet-related), the chain continues to RBAC. If Node allows, RBAC is never consulted.</p>
<pre>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC
                  request: worker-1's kubelet reporting its own pod status
                       │
                       ▼
   ① Node authorizer  ── is this system:node:worker-1, and is it about
                          worker-1's OWN pods/secrets/PVs?
                       │
              ┌────────┴────────┐
              ▼ yes              ▼ no (NoOpinion)
      ✅ ALLOW — STOP.      ② RBAC authorizer  ── any Role/ClusterRole
      RBAC never runs.         granting this verb+resource?
                              ┌────────┴────────┐
                              ▼ yes              ▼ no (NoOpinion, end of chain)
                          ✅ ALLOW           ❌ implicit DENY → 403 Forbidden</pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The authorization chain is short-circuiting: the first authorizer that says ALLOW stops the chain. The first that says DENY also stops the chain. Only NOOPINION continues to the next authorizer. Node authorizer recognizes kubelet traffic by the <code>system:node:&lt;nodename&gt;</code> username and the <code>system:nodes</code> group. On the CKS exam, you might need to add Node to the authorization chain if it's missing — without it, kubelets can't report pod/node status and your cluster breaks.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You run <code>kubectl get pods --as=system:serviceaccount:default:easypj-sa</code> and get a 403 Forbidden. Walk through the EXACT path this request takes through all 5 security gates. At which gate was it rejected?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="process-steps vertical">
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><div class="step-content"><h5>TLS Handshake</h5><p>Your kubectl presents its client certificate and the API server presents its server certificate. Mutual TLS is verified. ✅ PASS</p></div></div>
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Authentication</h5><p>You used <code>--as=...</code> which performs <strong>impersonation</strong>. Your original identity (from kubeconfig) must have impersonate permissions. If you can impersonate, the API server accepts <code>system:serviceaccount:default:easypj-sa</code> as the request user. ✅ PASS</p></div></div>
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Authorization ❌ REJECTED HERE</h5><p>RBAC evaluates: does <code>system:serviceaccount:default:easypj-sa</code> have <code>get pods</code> in the requested namespace? If no Role/ClusterRole grants this, RBAC returns DENY. HTTP 403 Forbidden.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Admission Control</h5><p>NEVER REACHED — the request was denied at Gate 3.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Audit Logging</h5><p>Even though the request was denied, if audit policy logs "forbidden" requests, this 403 is recorded. The audit log shows who tried to do what and that it was denied.</p></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This walkthrough exercise is one of the most valuable mental models for CKS. Every API request goes through ALL gates in order, but <strong>rejection at any gate stops the chain</strong>. Impersonation (<code>--as</code>) is itself an RBAC-checked operation — you need <code>impersonate</code> verb on the target user/group/ServiceAccount. On the exam, use <code>kubectl auth can-i --as=...</code> to test what a specific identity can do without actually switching credentials.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An attacker floods the API server with 10,000 requests per second from a compromised pod. Which Kubernetes mechanism limits the impact of this DoS attack on other legitimate users? What API server flags control this?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>API Priority and Fairness (APF)</strong> limits the impact. APF categorizes requests into "flow schemas" and assigns each flow a "priority level." If a flow exceeds its concurrency or queue limit, excess requests are rejected with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests).</p>
                    <p><strong>API server flags:</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Enable APF (GA since 1.29, enabled by default)
--enable-priority-and-fairness=true
--max-requests-inflight=400          # Total inflight non-mutating requests
--max-mutating-requests-inflight=200 # Total inflight mutating requests

# View current APF configuration
kubectl get flowschemas
kubectl get prioritylevelconfigurations</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>APF is the <strong>anti-DoS mechanism</strong> built into the API server. Without it, one noisy client can starve all others. APF pre-dates Kubernetes 1.29 as GA. FlowSchemas match requests based on user, group, or ServiceAccount and assign them a priority level. Each priority level has limits on concurrency and queuing. On the CKS exam, you may need to verify APF is enabled and check that critical flows (like system:nodes for kubelet) have guaranteed capacity.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to add <strong>EncryptionConfiguration</strong> for Secrets. What API server flag must you add, where do you add it, and what happens during the API server restart? What's the verification command?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Flag:</strong> <code>--encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml</code></p>
                    <p><strong>Location:</strong> Add to <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code> in the <code>spec.containers[0].command</code> arguments list.</p>
                    <p><strong>During restart:</strong> The API server stops, kubelet detects the manifest change, recreates the container (30-60s downtime). kubectl connections drop.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Verify encryption is working
kubectl create secret generic test-secret --from-literal=key=value -n default

# Check raw etcd (should show k8s:enc:aescbc prefix)
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
    --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
    --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
    get /registry/secrets/default/test-secret | hexdump -C | head</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is one of the most common CKS exam tasks. The key gotchas: (1) The EncryptionConfiguration YAML must be placed on the control plane node and mounted as a hostPath volume in the API server manifest. (2) The <code>--encryption-provider-config</code> flag must point to the mounted path inside the container. (3) After adding the flag, you must <strong>rewrite all existing secrets</strong> to encrypt them: <code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code>. Secrets created before the config was added remain in plaintext until rewritten.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You discover that the API server has <code>--anonymous-auth=true</code> and <code>--insecure-port=8080</code> enabled. What's the security impact of EACH, and how do you fix both?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Impact</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>--anonymous-auth=true</code></td><td>Anyone can access API endpoints that don't explicitly require auth. The <code>system:anonymous</code> user may have RBAC permissions if mistakenly bound.</td><td>Change to <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> in kube-apiserver.yaml</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--insecure-port=8080</code></td><td>API server listens on port 8080 WITHOUT TLS. Anyone who can reach this port bypasses authentication and authorization entirely — they ARE cluster-admin.</td><td>Remove the flag entirely. Insecure port was removed in Kubernetes 1.24+. If present, it's a critical finding.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--insecure-port</code> was <strong>deprecated in 1.10 and removed in 1.24</strong>. If you find it on a CKS exam cluster, it means the cluster is running an ancient, insecure version — flag it as critical. <code>--anonymous-auth=true</code> is still the DEFAULT today. The defense is RBAC hygiene: ensure <code>system:anonymous</code> and <code>system:unauthenticated</code> have ZERO RoleBindings or ClusterRoleBindings. Use <code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:anonymous</code> to verify.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're configuring audit logging and need to log: (a) ALL Secret reads/writes at the Metadata level, (b) ALL pod exec attempts at the RequestResponse level, (c) ALL ConfigMap changes at the Request level. Write the Audit Policy YAML for these three rules. What's the difference between the audit levels?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1
kind: Policy
rules:
# (a) Log all Secret reads/writes — Metadata only (who, what, when)
- level: Metadata
  resources:
  - group: ""
    resources: ["secrets"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch", "create", "update", "patch", "delete"]

# (b) Log all pod exec — full request + response body
- level: RequestResponse
  resources:
  - group: ""
    resources: ["pods/exec"]
  verbs: ["create", "get"]

# (c) Log all ConfigMap changes — request body only
- level: Request
  resources:
  - group: ""
    resources: ["configmaps"]
  verbs: ["create", "update", "patch"]

# Catch-all: don't log anything else
- level: None</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>Audit levels:</strong> <code>None</code> (don't log), <code>Metadata</code> (who/what/when/response code — no body), <code>Request</code> (metadata + request body), <code>RequestResponse</code> (metadata + request body + response body).</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Audit levels control the <strong>verbosity vs. storage tradeoff</strong>. Metadata is lightweight — use it for high-volume resources like Secrets reads. RequestResponse captures everything but generates massive logs — use it sparingly for security-critical operations like pod exec. The CKS exam typically asks you to write an audit policy that logs specific resources at specific levels. Key gotcha: <code>pods/exec</code> is a subresource — you must include the slash. The rules are evaluated in order, and the first matching rule applies.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch3-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 3 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (8)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — a broken cluster state, and you produce the fix with real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A new worker node joins the cluster but stays <code>NotReady</code> forever, and its kubelet logs show <code>Unauthorized</code> when it tries to report node status. You check the API server manifest and find <code>--authorization-mode=RBAC</code> — no <code>Node</code>. Fix it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Add <code>Node</code> to the authorization-mode chain, ahead of RBAC, then let the static pod restart itself.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Edit the API server manifest on EVERY control-plane node
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
# change:  --authorization-mode=RBAC
# to:      --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC

# No systemctl restart needed — kubelet watches this file and
# recreates the static pod automatically within ~20-30s
watch crictl ps --name kube-apiserver

# Confirm the node registers
kubectl get nodes -w</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Without the Node authorizer, kubelet requests fall straight through to RBAC — and there is no default RBAC grant for <code>system:node:*</code> identities to report their own status, so every kubelet call comes back <code>Unauthorized</code>/<code>Forbidden</code>. This is a from-scratch cluster misconfiguration the exam loves: one missing word in one flag breaks every future node join.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A security audit finds a <code>ClusterRoleBinding</code> that grants <code>cluster-admin</code> to <code>system:anonymous</code> — left over from a "quick test" months ago. Find it and remove it, then prove no anonymous access remains.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Find every binding touching anonymous/unauthenticated identities
kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.subjects[]?.name=="system:anonymous" or .subjects[]?.name=="system:unauthenticated") | .metadata.name'

# Remove the dangerous one
kubectl delete clusterrolebinding anonymous-test-access

# Prove it: anonymous should now be able to do NOTHING
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:anonymous
kubectl auth can-i create pods --as=system:anonymous -A   # should print "no"</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Gate 2 (AuthN) being correctly configured (<code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>) doesn't help if Gate 3 (AuthZ) still has a stale grant bound to <code>system:anonymous</code> or <code>system:unauthenticated</code> from before hardening. Always audit bindings to these two identities directly — they're the highest-value target for an attacker probing an unauthenticated endpoint.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Every <code>kubectl apply</code> in the cluster — including in <code>kube-system</code> — is suddenly failing with <code>Error from server: failed calling webhook "vpolicy.example.com": Post ".../validate": dial tcp: connection refused</code>. The policy engine's own pod crashed and its <code>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</code> uses <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> with no <code>kube-system</code> exemption. You have an outage. Recover it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># EMERGENCY: temporarily fail-open so the policy engine's own pod can be recreated
kubectl patch validatingwebhookconfiguration vpolicy \
  --type='json' -p='[{"op":"replace","path":"/webhooks/0/failurePolicy","value":"Ignore"}]'

# Now the crashed pod can actually be recreated
kubectl -n policy-system rollout restart deployment policy-engine
kubectl -n policy-system rollout status deployment policy-engine

# Once healthy again, restore the strict setting
kubectl patch validatingwebhookconfiguration vpolicy \
  --type='json' -p='[{"op":"replace","path":"/webhooks/0/failurePolicy","value":"Fail"}]'

# Permanent fix: add the namespaceSelector exemption so this can't happen again
kubectl label namespace kube-system policy-exempt=true</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the bootstrap-deadlock trap from the Under the Hood section made real: a fail-closed webhook with no exemption for its own namespace can prevent itself from ever coming back once it crashes. The immediate fix is a temporary <code>failurePolicy: Ignore</code> patch to break the deadlock; the permanent fix is a <code>namespaceSelector</code> that exempts <code>kube-system</code> (and the webhook's own namespace) so this specific failure mode can never recur.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your CI pipeline should <strong>fail the build</strong> only on CRITICAL/HIGH CVEs that actually have a fix available (ignore ones with no patch yet, and ignore MEDIUM/LOW noise). Write the exact Trivy command.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">trivy image \
  --severity HIGH,CRITICAL \
  --ignore-unfixed \
  --exit-code 1 \
  easypj-registry.io/easypj:v2

# exit code 0 = pipeline continues; exit code 1 = pipeline FAILS the build</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--severity</code> filters which CVEs are reported at all. <code>--ignore-unfixed</code> drops CVEs where no patched package version exists yet — failing the build on those just blocks shipping with no way to resolve them. <code>--exit-code 1</code> is what actually makes Trivy's process exit non-zero so your CI system treats it as a failed step; without it, Trivy prints the report but the pipeline sails on regardless.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Falco is supposed to be running on every node as a DaemonSet, but a developer reports "no alerts, ever, even when I <code>kubectl exec</code> into a pod and spawn a shell." Diagnose and fix.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Is it even scheduled on every node?
kubectl get daemonset falco -n falco -o wide
kubectl get pods -n falco -o wide   # compare pod count to `kubectl get nodes` count

# 2. Is it actually detecting syscalls (driver loaded)?
kubectl -n falco logs ds/falco --tail=50 | grep -i "driver\|error"

# 3. Reproduce and watch live
kubectl exec -it easypj-web-abc123 -- /bin/sh &
kubectl -n falco logs -f ds/falco | grep -i "shell"

# If the DaemonSet is missing nodes: check for a taint it's not tolerating
kubectl describe node worker-3 | grep Taints
# Fix: add the missing toleration to the Falco DaemonSet's pod spec</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>"No alerts ever" has two very different root causes with two very different fixes: either Falco isn't running on the node where the activity happened (a scheduling problem — check DaemonSet pod count vs node count, and taints/tolerations), or it's running but its kernel driver/eBPF probe failed to load (a runtime problem — visible in the pod's own logs). Always check "is it even there" before "is its rule set right."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>cosign verify --key cosign.pub easypj-registry.io/easypj:v2</code> fails with <code>Error: no matching signatures</code> — but you're certain the image WAS signed in CI. What are the two most likely causes, and what commands do you run to tell them apart?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Cause A: wrong public key (mismatched keypair)
cosign verify --key wrong-cosign.pub easypj-registry.io/easypj:v2   # fails
cosign verify --key correct-cosign.pub easypj-registry.io/easypj:v2 # passes → it was Cause A

# Cause B: tag was overwritten/re-pushed AFTER signing (digest changed)
crane digest easypj-registry.io/easypj:v2      # get the CURRENT digest
# Compare to the digest recorded in your CI logs at sign time — if they
# differ, the tag was mutated after signing and the old signature no
# longer applies to what "v2" currently points at.

# Verify by DIGEST instead of by mutable tag to make this impossible going forward:
cosign verify --key correct-cosign.pub easypj-registry.io/easypj@sha256:abc123...</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A signature is bound to an image's content digest, not its tag. If someone rebuilds and re-pushes the same tag without re-signing, <code>cosign verify</code> against the tag will correctly report "no matching signatures" — the bytes behind the tag changed. The fix going forward for admission-time verification is to pin policies to digests, or re-sign on every push as part of the same CI step that pushes.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A "least privilege" audit needs to answer: "what can the ServiceAccount <code>easypj-prod:ci-deployer</code> actually do, across every namespace?" One command, no guessing from reading YAML.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:ci-deployer

# Narrow to one namespace
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:ci-deployer -n easypj-prod

# Check one specific, high-risk permission directly
kubectl auth can-i create clusterrolebindings --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:ci-deployer</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=...</code> queries the LIVE authorizer chain (Node → RBAC → any Webhook authorizer) the exact same way a real request would be evaluated — it's authoritative in a way that manually reading Role/RoleBinding YAML never is, because it also picks up ClusterRoleBindings you might have forgotten existed. This is the single fastest RBAC audit tool on the exam.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>kube-bench</code> flags: <em>"1.2.24 Ensure that the --audit-log-path argument is set (Scored)"</em> as FAIL. Fix it, then re-run only that one check to confirm.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Add the missing flags to the API server manifest
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
# add under spec.containers[0].command:
#   - --audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit/audit.log
#   - --audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml
#   - --audit-log-maxage=30
#   - --audit-log-maxbackup=10
#   - --audit-log-maxsize=100
# also add the matching hostPath volume + volumeMount for the log directory

# Re-run just this one CIS check instead of the full suite
kube-bench run --targets master --check 1.2.24</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Every audit-logging flag must be added together — <code>--audit-log-path</code> alone does nothing without <code>--audit-policy-file</code> telling the API server what to log, and the log directory must be a hostPath volume mounted into the static pod or the path won't exist inside the container. <code>kube-bench run --check &lt;id&gt;</code> re-verifies one specific control instead of re-running the entire multi-minute suite — much faster during iterative remediation.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

