            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 3.2 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 3.2 — Read the Error, Name the Gate</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 4 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>For each real error message below, name the <strong>exact gate</strong> that rejected the request (including 4a vs 4b where relevant) and one command you'd run next to confirm your diagnosis:</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li><code>Unable to connect to the server: x509: certificate signed by unknown authority</code></li>
                        <li><code>Error from server (Forbidden): pods is forbidden: User "dev" cannot list resource "pods" in API group ""</code></li>
                        <li><code>Error from server: admission webhook "check-image-signature.sigstore.dev" denied the request: image is not signed</code></li>
                        <li>A pod you created has a <code>sidecar</code> container you never wrote in its spec.</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> an x509 error never reaches AuthN at all; a plain "Forbidden" with no policy wording is AuthZ; a "webhook denied" message with a policy reason is 4b; an unexplained EXTRA field is always 4a, never 4b (validating webhooks can only accept/reject, never add).
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Gate</th><th>Confirm with</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Gate 1 — TLS</strong> (fails before AuthN is ever reached)</td><td><code>openssl x509 -in ~/.kube/client.crt -noout -issuer</code>; compare to the cluster CA</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Gate 3 — AuthZ</strong></td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=dev</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Gate 4b — Validating Admission</strong></td><td><code>kubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations</code>; check the sigstore policy's requirements</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Gate 4a — Mutating Admission</strong></td><td><code>kubectl get mutatingwebhookconfigurations</code>; find which one injects sidecars (often a service mesh)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the giveaway on #4 is that nothing was REJECTED — the pod was created successfully, just not exactly as you wrote it. That's the signature of mutation, and only mutating webhooks (Gate 4a) can do it.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 3.3 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 3.3 — Prove RBAC Has No "Deny," Hands-On</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>On any test cluster (Minikube/kind is fine): create a narrow Role that grants a ServiceAccount only <code>get</code> on <code>pods</code>. Then add a SECOND, broader ClusterRoleBinding granting the same ServiceAccount <code>cluster-admin</code>. Prove which one "wins" and explain why — using the mental model from this chapter's Under the Hood section, not guesswork.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> the authorizer chain stops at the FIRST <code>Allow</code> it finds anywhere — it does not keep looking for a more specific rule, and it does not average/merge permissions. Broader always wins if it's found before or after the narrow one; there's no ordering between RBAC bindings themselves.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">narrow Role + broad ClusterRoleBinding, same identity</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Narrow: only 'get pods' in namespace easypj
kubectl create serviceaccount narrow-sa -n easypj
kubectl create role pod-getter --verb=get --resource=pods -n easypj
kubectl create rolebinding narrow-binding --role=pod-getter \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:narrow-sa -n easypj

# Confirm the narrow grant, and confirm secrets are (correctly) denied
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:narrow-sa -n easypj      # yes
kubectl auth can-i get secrets --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:narrow-sa -n easypj   # no

# 2. Now ALSO grant cluster-admin to the SAME identity
kubectl create clusterrolebinding narrow-sa-is-admin \
  --clusterrole=cluster-admin \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:narrow-sa

# Re-check: the narrow Role is now irrelevant
kubectl auth can-i get secrets --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:narrow-sa -n easypj   # YES now
kubectl auth can-i delete namespaces --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:narrow-sa       # YES too

# Cleanup
kubectl delete clusterrolebinding narrow-sa-is-admin
kubectl delete rolebinding narrow-binding -n easypj
kubectl delete role pod-getter -n easypj
kubectl delete serviceaccount narrow-sa -n easypj</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the broad <code>cluster-admin</code> grant won completely — the narrow Role you carefully wrote first didn't "limit" anything once a second, wider grant existed anywhere in the cluster. This is exactly the Under the Hood lesson: RBAC only ever accumulates <code>Allow</code>s from every binding that matches; the very first one the authorizer finds ends the search. There is no such thing as a Role "overriding" or "restricting" a broader one — least privilege only works if you never create the broad grant in the first place.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

