            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s4-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.1 What Are CIS Benchmarks?</h3>
                <p>The <strong>Center for Internet Security (CIS)</strong> is a non-profit organization that publishes security configuration guidelines — called CIS Benchmarks — for operating systems, cloud providers, and software platforms. The <strong>CIS Kubernetes Benchmark</strong> is a document containing over 200 specific, testable security configuration checks for every component in a Kubernetes cluster.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 What Makes a CIS Check "Testable"?</h5>
                    <p>Every CIS check is written as a <strong>binary assertion</strong>: it either passes or it fails. There are no subjective "best practices" in the CIS Benchmark — only verifiable configuration states. For example: "Ensure that the <code>--anonymous-auth</code> argument is set to <code>false</code>" (Check 1.2.1). kube-bench reads the actual API server configuration and answers PASS or FAIL. This binary nature is why kube-bench can automate the entire benchmark — and why the CKS exam relies so heavily on it.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>CIS Benchmark Check Areas — What Gets Audited</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The 5 major categories of CIS Kubernetes Benchmark checks</div>
<pre>
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  CIS KUBERNETES BENCHMARK — 5 CHECK AREAS                 │
  │                                                            │
  │  1. CONTROL PLANE COMPONENTS                               │
  │     → kube-apiserver flags (auth, admission, TLS, audit)  │
  │     → kube-controller-manager flags                        │
  │     → kube-scheduler flags                                 │
  │     Covers: 50+ checks on API server alone                 │
  │                                                            │
  │  2. etcd                                                   │
  │     → TLS configuration (peer and client certs)            │
  │     → File permissions on certs and data directory         │
  │     → Client certificate authentication enabled            │
  │     Covers: 10+ checks                                     │
  │                                                            │
  │  3. CONTROL PLANE CONFIGURATION                            │
  │     → Audit logging enabled and configured                 │
  │     → Encryption at rest for Secrets                       │
  │     → Admission plugins enabled (NodeRestriction, etc.)    │
  │     → RBAC enabled, ABAC disabled                          │
  │     Covers: 30+ checks                                     │
  │                                                            │
  │  4. WORKER NODES                                           │
  │     → kubelet configuration (auth, read-only port)         │
  │     → Node OS hardening (kernel params, packages)          │
  │     → kube-proxy configuration                             │
  │     Covers: 40+ checks                                     │
  │                                                            │
  │  5. KUBERNETES POLICIES                                    │
  │     → NetworkPolicy enforcement                            │
  │     → RBAC minimal permissions                             │
  │     → Secrets management                                   │
  │     → Pod Security Standards                               │
  │     Covers: 30+ checks                                     │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Why CIS Benchmarks Matter for the CKS Exam</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📏</div>
                        <h5>Industry Standard</h5>
                        <p>CIS Benchmarks are used by auditors, compliance officers, and security teams worldwide. PCI-DSS, HIPAA, and SOC 2 audits often reference CIS checks. Passing them is not optional for regulated industries.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div>
                        <h5>Exam Blueprint</h5>
                        <p>The CKS exam's "Cluster Setup" and "Cluster Hardening" domains are essentially a practical test of your ability to pass CIS checks. If you can run kube-bench and fix every FAIL, you can pass those exam sections.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div>
                        <h5>Continuous Compliance</h5>
                        <p>In production, kube-bench runs as a CronJob or is integrated into CI/CD. Every configuration change is checked against CIS. Drift is detected immediately — not during the next annual audit.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>How CIS Benchmarks Are Created — And Why They Are Trusted</h4>
                <p>CIS Benchmarks are not written by a single company or vendor. They are developed through a <strong>consensus-based process</strong> involving hundreds of security professionals from different organizations — cloud providers, security vendors, government agencies, and independent researchers. The Kubernetes Benchmark is maintained by the CIS Kubernetes Community, which includes engineers from Google, Red Hat, VMware, and Aqua Security (the creators of kube-bench). This multi-stakeholder approach means the benchmark is vendor-neutral — it does not favor any specific tool or platform. Every check in the benchmark must survive peer review and demonstrate a clear security benefit before being included.</p>

                <h4>CIS Benchmarks and Regulatory Compliance</h4>
                <p>Passing CIS checks is not just about security — it is increasingly about <strong>legal and contractual obligation</strong>. Many regulatory frameworks and industry standards explicitly reference or align with CIS Benchmarks. Understanding these connections helps you justify the hardening work to management and auditors:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Framework</th><th>How CIS Maps to It</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>PCI-DSS</strong> (Payment Card Industry)</td><td>Requires secure system configurations. CIS checks satisfy Requirement 2 (secure configurations) and Requirement 7 (access control).</td><td>If easypj processes payments, CIS 1.2.1 (anonymous-auth=false) satisfies PCI-DSS Req 7.2.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>SOC 2</strong> (Service Organization Control)</td><td>Requires documented security controls and evidence of their effectiveness. kube-bench reports serve as audit evidence.</td><td>A kube-bench JSON report showing 95% PASS rate is auditable evidence for SOC 2 control testing.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>HIPAA</strong> (Healthcare)</td><td>Requires access controls and audit trails. CIS checks for RBAC (1.2.6) and audit logging (1.2.14) directly satisfy these.</td><td>If easypj stores health-related job data, CIS 1.2.22 (audit policy) is a HIPAA requirement.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ISO 27001</strong></td><td>Requires a formal Information Security Management System. CIS benchmarks provide the technical controls annex.</td><td>A.9.2.1 (access control) → CIS 1.2.6 (RBAC). A.12.4.1 (event logging) → CIS 1.2.14 (audit logs).</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>CIS Benchmark Versions — Which One Should You Use?</h4>
                <p>The CIS Kubernetes Benchmark is versioned alongside Kubernetes itself. Always use the benchmark version that matches your cluster's Kubernetes version. Running kube-bench against the wrong benchmark version produces false positives (checks for features that don't exist in your version) and false negatives (missing checks for new security features). kube-bench auto-detects your K8s version and selects the appropriate benchmark, but on the exam, be aware that the exam environment will have the correct version pre-configured.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">CIS Benchmark Version → Kubernetes Version Mapping (per kube-bench's own cfg/config.yaml)</div>
<pre>
  KUBERNETES VERSION        kube-bench's CIS BENCHMARK FOLDER
  ─────────────────         ───────────────────────────────────
  v1.25                     cis-1.7
  v1.26                     cis-1.8
  v1.27                     cis-1.9
  v1.28                     cis-1.10
  v1.29 – v1.31             cis-1.11
  v1.32 – v1.34 (latest)    cis-1.12

  Key changes across recent versions:
  • CIS 1.6 → 1.7: Removed PodSecurityPolicy checks (PSP was deprecated in K8s 1.21,
    removed entirely in K8s 1.25 — the benchmark tracks the same deprecation timeline)
  • CIS 1.7 → 1.8: Added Pod Security Standards (PSS) checks
  • CIS 1.8 → 1.12: Continued, version-by-version alignment with each new K8s minor
    release — admission-config checks, audit-policy refinements, updated kubelet
    and API server flag defaults as upstream Kubernetes itself changes them

  ⚠️  NEVER run a benchmark written for v1.34 against a v1.24 cluster.
      It will check for features that do not exist — guaranteed false positives.

  📌 This exact table WILL be stale by the time you read it — Kubernetes ships
     roughly 3 minor versions a year, and the CIS Benchmark tracks it closely.
     Don't memorize these numbers. What matters for the exam is the CONCEPT
     (wrong benchmark version → false positives/negatives) plus knowing where
     the authoritative, always-current mapping actually lives: kube-bench's own
     cfg/config.yaml (github.com/aquasecurity/kube-bench) — and that kube-bench
     auto-detects your cluster's version and picks the right folder for you,
     which is exactly why you almost never need to pass --benchmark manually.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>CIS Profile Levels — Level 1 vs Level 2 (A Different Axis Than PASS/FAIL)</h4>
                <p>Every check in the CIS Kubernetes Benchmark PDF also carries a <strong>Profile Level</strong> — either 1 or 2. This is completely independent of PASS/FAIL/WARN: it is a judgment about how <em>aggressively</em> a recommendation should be applied, not whether your cluster currently meets it. Understanding this distinction matters in the real world because blindly fixing every FAIL with equal urgency can break things a Level 2 check would have warned you about.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Definition</th><th>Typical Example</th><th>Rollout Guidance</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Level 1</strong></td><td>A practical, low-risk recommendation. Reduces the attack surface significantly with minimal chance of breaking legitimate functionality.</td><td><code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>, <code>readOnlyPort: 0</code> — nothing legitimate depends on anonymous or unauthenticated access.</td><td>Apply directly to production. No compensating testing usually required.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Level 2</strong></td><td>A "defense-in-depth" recommendation. More restrictive; may reduce functionality, require additional operational tooling, or need workload-specific validation before it is safe to roll out.</td><td>Some admission-plugin combinations or stricter Pod Security Standards levels can reject workloads that were previously running fine.</td><td>Test in a staging cluster first. Have a rollback plan. Communicate to workload owners before enforcing cluster-wide.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 kube-bench Does Not Filter by Level — You Must Consult the PDF</h5>
                    <p>kube-bench's own output has no concept of "Level 1 vs Level 2" — it runs every <code>scored: true</code> check in its bundled YAML regardless of level and reports PASS/FAIL/WARN the same way for both. The level classification lives only in the official CIS Benchmark PDF, alongside each check's number. On the exam this rarely matters (you're told exactly what to fix), but in <strong>real-world administration</strong> it is the difference between "fix this today" (Level 1 FAILs) and "schedule a change window and notify teams first" (Level 2 FAILs) — treating every FAIL as equally urgent is how well-intentioned hardening work causes a self-inflicted outage.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The CIS Benchmark Is Free</h5>
                    <p>Download it at <strong>cisecurity.org/benchmark/kubernetes</strong>. The PDF includes every check with rationale, audit steps, and remediation — including the Level 1/Level 2 classification above. The CKS exam aligns closely with the CIS Benchmark — studying it is studying for the exam.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s4-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.2 Running kube-bench — Your Security Audit Tool</h3>
                <p><strong>kube-bench</strong> is an open-source Go application from Aqua Security that automates the CIS Kubernetes Benchmark. It connects to your cluster, inspects the configuration of every component, and produces a report showing exactly which checks passed and which failed — with specific remediation steps for every failure.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 kube-bench at a Glance</h5>
                    <p><strong>Repository:</strong> github.com/aquasecurity/kube-bench · <strong>Language:</strong> Go · <strong>License:</strong> Apache 2.0 · <strong>CKS Exam:</strong> ✅ MUST KNOW — pre-installed in exam environment. <strong>How it works:</strong> kube-bench reads component configuration files directly from the node filesystem. It does not use the Kubernetes API — it reads <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>, <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>, and systemd service files directly.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Installing and Running kube-bench</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Installation and basic usage — the commands you need on the CKS exam</div>
<pre>
  # Install kube-bench (one-liner for the exam)
  $ curl -L https://github.com/aquasecurity/kube-bench/releases/latest/download/\
    kube-bench_linux_amd64.tar.gz | tar xz
  $ sudo mv kube-bench /usr/local/bin/

  # Run ALL checks on the control plane (master node)
  $ kube-bench run --targets=master

  # Run ALL checks on worker nodes
  $ kube-bench run --targets=node

  # Run a SPECIFIC check (useful for verifying fixes)
  $ kube-bench run --check=1.2.1   # Only check API server anonymous auth

  # Run checks with JSON output (for automated processing)
  $ kube-bench run --json --targets=master > cis-report.json
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Reading kube-bench Output — How to Interpret Results</h4>
                <p>kube-bench output has three result types. Understanding each is essential because the exam expects you to know which ones demand action and which can be ignored:</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Action Required?</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong style="color:#4ade80;">[PASS]</strong></td><td>The configuration matches the CIS recommendation exactly. No action needed.</td><td>❌ No</td><td><code>[PASS] 1.2.1 Ensure --anonymous-auth=false</code> — your API server is correctly configured.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">[FAIL]</strong></td><td>The configuration does NOT meet the CIS recommendation. This is a security vulnerability that must be fixed.</td><td>✅ YES — fix immediately</td><td><code>[FAIL] 1.2.6 Ensure --authorization-mode=RBAC</code> — your authorization mode is set to AlwaysAllow. Fix it.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong style="color:#facc15;">[WARN]</strong></td><td>kube-bench cannot automatically determine if this applies to your environment. Manual review needed.</td><td>⚠️ Review — may apply</td><td><code>[WARN] 1.2.14 Ensure --audit-log-path is set</code> — kube-bench detected the flag but wasn't sure about the path value. Manually verify.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Real kube-bench Output — Annotated Example</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Actual kube-bench run on a default cluster — what you will see on exam day</div>
<pre>
  $ kube-bench run --check 1.2

  [INFO] 1 Master Node Security Configuration
  [INFO] 1.2 API Server
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  [PASS] 1.2.1  Ensure --anonymous-auth=false
         ✅ Good. No one can access the API without credentials.

  [FAIL] 1.2.2  Ensure --token-auth-file is not set
