                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKS Exam Reality: kube-bench Is Your Answer Key</h5>
                    <p>On the exam, a hardening task will typically say: "The cluster has misconfigurations. Harden it." The first thing you do is run kube-bench. The FAIL items in its output tell you <strong>exactly</strong> which flags to add or remove, and the remediation section tells you <strong>exactly</strong> which file to edit. Do not guess — let kube-bench guide you. The exam does not expect you to have memorized all 200+ CIS checks. It expects you to know how to use kube-bench to find and fix them.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>How kube-bench Works Under the Hood</h4>
                <p>Understanding kube-bench's internal architecture helps you troubleshoot when it behaves unexpectedly. kube-bench does not use the Kubernetes API at all — it reads configuration files directly from the node's filesystem and runs shell commands to inspect running processes. This is intentional: if the API server is misconfigured or down, kube-bench can still audit it because it reads the static pod manifest directly. Here is what kube-bench actually does for each check:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kube-bench Internal Architecture — How Each Check Is Performed</div>
<pre>
  For API SERVER checks (CIS 1.2.x):
  ─────────────────────────────────────
    1. Reads /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
    2. Parses the 'command' array for flags
    3. Checks: Is --anonymous-auth present? Is its value 'false'?
    4. If yes → [PASS]. If no → [FAIL]. If ambiguous → [WARN].

  For etcd checks (CIS 2.x):
  ───────────────────────────────────
    1. Reads /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
    2. Checks TLS flags, data directory permissions, cert file ownership
    3. Runs stat on cert files to verify 600 permissions

  For kubelet checks (CIS 4.2.x):
  ────────────────────────────────────
    1. Reads /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
    2. Runs ps aux | grep kubelet to check command-line flags
    3. Merges config file + CLI flags (CLI flags override config file)

  For OS-level checks (CIS 4.1.x):
  ──────────────────────────────────
    1. Runs shell commands: sysctl, iptables, systemctl
    2. Checks kernel parameters (net.ipv4.ip_forward, etc.)
    3. Verifies package versions for known-vulnerable software
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>kube-bench Run Targets — When to Use Each</h4>
                <p>kube-bench has multiple run targets that determine which set of checks is executed. Using the wrong target wastes time and produces irrelevant output. On the CKS exam, the task description tells you which component to harden — that determines the target:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>Checks Run</th><th>When to Use</th><th>Exam Context</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>--targets=master</code></td><td>API server, etcd, controller-manager, scheduler, control plane config</td><td>On control plane nodes only</td><td>"Harden the control plane" → use master</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--targets=node</code></td><td>kubelet, kube-proxy, container runtime, OS hardening</td><td>On worker nodes (and control plane nodes too — they also run kubelet)</td><td>"Harden the kubelet" → use node</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--targets=master,node</code></td><td>All checks (both sets)</td><td>Full cluster audit on a control plane node</td><td>"Audit and harden the cluster" → use both</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--targets=etcd</code></td><td>etcd-specific checks only</td><td>On etcd nodes (if etcd runs on separate nodes)</td><td>"Secure etcd" → use etcd</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--targets=policies</code></td><td>RBAC, NetworkPolicy, PSP/PSS, Secrets</td><td>Any node with kubectl access to the cluster</td><td>"Check RBAC configuration" → use policies</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Common kube-bench Pitfalls — What Can Go Wrong</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>😰 Pitfall 1: Running kube-bench from the Wrong Node</h5>
                    <p>kube-bench reads local files. If you run <code>--targets=master</code> on a worker node, it cannot find <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code> and reports everything as <code>[FAIL]</code> or <code>[WARN]</code>. Always run master-targeted checks on a control plane node. Use <code>kubectl get nodes -o wide</code> to identify which nodes have the <code>control-plane</code> role.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="scenario-box positive">
                    <h5>✅ Pitfall 2: Ignoring [WARN] Results</h5>
                    <p>A <code>[WARN]</code> means kube-bench could not automatically determine the check result. It does NOT mean the check passed. Always manually investigate <code>[WARN]</code> items — they may be hiding real vulnerabilities. On the exam, if a check returns <code>[WARN]</code>, run <code>kube-bench run --check=&lt;number&gt; -v</code> (verbose mode) to see exactly why it could not determine the result, then manually verify by inspecting the configuration file yourself.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>😰 Pitfall 3: Fixing a Check But kube-bench Still Shows [FAIL]</h5>
                    <p>This happens when you edit the wrong file or have a typo in the flag name. For API server checks, always verify your edit: <code>grep anonymous-auth /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. Wait 30 seconds for the kubelet to restart the static pod, then re-run kube-bench. For kubelet checks, remember that CLI flags in the systemd service file OVERRIDE settings in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>. Check both locations: <code>ps aux | grep kubelet | grep anonymous-auth</code> and <code>grep anonymous /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s4-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.3 Fixing Common CIS Failures — The API Server Manifest</h3>
                <p>The Kubernetes API server is configured through a <strong>static pod manifest</strong> located at <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. Unlike regular pods, static pods are managed directly by the kubelet — not by the API server or a Deployment controller. This means that editing the YAML file and saving it causes the kubelet to <strong>automatically restart the API server</strong> with the new configuration. There is no <code>kubectl apply</code> — there is no API server running yet to process it. You edit the file directly on the control plane node using <code>vi</code> or <code>nano</code>.</p>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 Critical: Editing Static Pod Manifests</h5>
                    <p>When you edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>, the kubelet detects the change within seconds and restarts the API server pod. During this restart (typically 10-30 seconds), <strong>kubectl commands will fail</strong> because the API is temporarily unavailable. Always make multiple flag changes in a SINGLE edit — do not edit, save, wait, edit, save, wait. Batch your changes. Also, make a backup before editing: <code>cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /root/kube-apiserver.yaml.backup</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Reconciling With Current Docs: Why the Backup Must Live OUTSIDE <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></h5>
                    <p>Every backup command in this chapter copies the manifest to <code>/root/</code> or <code>~</code> — never to a second file inside <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> itself. This isn't a style preference; official Kubernetes documentation on static pods explicitly warns why: the kubelet's static-pod directory watcher (the <strong>file source manager</strong> introduced in this chapter's Under the Hood section) scans <em>every filename that doesn't start with a dot</em> in that directory and tries to parse each one as a Pod manifest — it does <strong>not</strong> filter by file extension. A same-directory copy like <code>kube-apiserver.yaml.backup</code> is not recognized as "not a manifest" just because of its <code>.backup</code> suffix; the kubelet will attempt to parse it too, which the docs describe plainly as producing <strong>undefined behavior</strong> (most likely: a second, bogus static pod, or parse errors that interfere with the real one). The only filenames the kubelet skips outright are ones beginning with a literal dot (e.g. <code>.kube-apiserver.yaml.swp</code> from a crashed <code>vi</code> session is safely ignored — a real backup file is not). <strong>The fix is simple and is exactly what this chapter already does everywhere:</strong> always store backups in a directory the kubelet isn't watching — <code>/root/</code>, <code>/etc/kubernetes/backup/</code>, anywhere outside <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. As a bonus fact worth knowing: the watched directory itself isn't hardcoded — it's the kubelet's own <code>staticPodPath</code> field in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> (kubeadm sets it to <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests</code> by default), so on a non-kubeadm cluster always confirm the real path with <code>grep staticPodPath /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> rather than assuming the kubeadm default.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Critical API Server Flags — CIS Must-Pass Checks</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>CIS Check</th><th>Flag</th><th>Secure Value</th><th>What It Prevents</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.2.1</strong></td><td><code>--anonymous-auth</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Unauthenticated access. Without this, anyone on the network can query your API. Tesla breach enabler.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.2.2</strong></td><td><code>--token-auth-file</code></td><td>REMOVE entirely</td><td>Static token auth (legacy). Tokens in a file are never rotated and are trivial to steal.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.2.6</strong></td><td><code>--authorization-mode</code></td><td><code>Node,RBAC</code></td><td>AlwaysAllow mode. Only Node and RBAC should be present. AlwaysAllow grants every request.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.2.9</strong></td><td><code>--enable-admission-plugins</code></td><td>Include <code>NodeRestriction</code></td><td>Compromised kubelet modifying other nodes' resources. NodeRestriction limits kubelets to their own node.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.2.14</strong></td><td><code>--audit-log-path</code></td><td><code>/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</code></td><td>No forensic record of API activity. Without audit logs, you cannot investigate breaches.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.2.20</strong></td><td><code>--tls-cert-file</code> + <code>--tls-private-key-file</code></td><td>Valid paths to cert+key</td><td>Unencrypted API traffic. TLS is the most fundamental security control — mandatory.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.2.22</strong></td><td><code>--audit-policy-file</code></td><td>Path to audit policy YAML</td><td>Audit logging without a policy logs nothing useful. The policy defines WHAT to log.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.2.21</strong></td><td><code>--profiling</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Exposed <code>/debug/pprof/</code> endpoint. Leaks Go runtime internals (heap dumps, goroutine stacks, CPU profiles) that can expose in-memory secret material and is unnecessary attack surface in production.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.2.7</strong></td><td><code>--admission-control-config-file</code></td><td>Path to an AdmissionConfiguration YAML</td><td>Admission plugins that require external configuration (e.g. <code>EventRateLimit</code>, <code>ImagePolicyWebhook</code>) silently run with no limits/policy if this file is not supplied — not to be confused with <code>--audit-policy-file</code>, a different flag for a different purpose.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 "Node" Authorization Mode vs. "NodeRestriction" Admission Plugin — Two Different Controls With Confusingly Similar Names</h5>
                    <p>CIS 1.2.6 (<code>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</code>) and CIS 1.2.9 (<code>NodeRestriction</code> in <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code>) are frequently conflated as "the same fix" because their names overlap — they are not the same control, and official Kubernetes guidance is explicit that they are meant to be deployed <strong>together</strong>, not as alternatives:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>The <code>Node</code> authorization mode</strong> is an <em>authorizer</em> — it runs during the authorization phase, alongside RBAC, and grants each kubelet a narrow, scoped set of permissions (read its own Node object, read/write the Pods bound to it, read the Secrets/ConfigMaps/PVs those pods reference) based on its <code>system:node:&lt;nodename&gt;</code> identity. Without it, every kubelet would instead need broad RBAC role bindings to function at all.</li>
                        <li><strong>The <code>NodeRestriction</code> admission plugin</strong> is an <em>admission controller</em> — it runs after authorization has already granted access, and blocks a kubelet from writing to Node or Pod objects, or applying <code>node-restriction.kubernetes.io/</code>-namespaced labels, that don't belong to <strong>its own</strong> node — even though the Node authorizer already permits it to write to Node/Pod objects in general.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>The official Kubernetes hardening guide states this plainly: <em>"It is recommended that you use the Node and RBAC authorizers together, in combination with the NodeRestriction admission plugin."</em> Enabling only one leaves a real gap: Node authorization alone does not stop a compromised kubelet from tampering with resources belonging to a <em>different</em> node (that's NodeRestriction's job); NodeRestriction alone is meaningless if the authorizer layer never scoped the kubelet's access down from "everything" in the first place. On the exam, if you see either check FAIL in isolation, remember you may need to check the other too — they are a matched pair, not redundant checks of the same thing.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Reconciling With Current Docs: <code>--authorization-mode</code> Still Works, But It's No Longer the Only Way</h5>
                    <p>Everything in this chapter — CIS 1.2.6, kube-bench, and the entire <code>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</code> teaching above — is built around the classic <strong>flag-based</strong> authorization configuration, and that remains exactly right for the CKS exam and for the vast majority of clusters running today. But current upstream Kubernetes documentation describes a newer, complementary mechanism worth knowing: <strong>Structured Authorization Configuration</strong>, driven by a new <code>--authorization-config</code> flag pointing at an <code>AuthorizationConfiguration</code> YAML file, which reached <strong>stable (GA) in Kubernetes v1.32</strong>. Neither approach is "wrong" — they solve the same problem two different ways, much like the 4Cs vs. CNCF Lifecycle framings in Chapter 1's §1.4:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong><code>--authorization-mode</code> (the flag this chapter teaches):</strong> a fixed, comma-separated, ordered list chosen from <code>AlwaysAllow, AlwaysDeny, ABAC, Webhook, RBAC, Node</code>. Simple, and it's exactly what kube-bench's CIS 1.2.6 check inspects — because kube-bench reads the running process's command-line flags, and this is the flag it looks for.</li>
                        <li><strong><code>--authorization-config</code> (the modern alternative):</strong> a YAML file that lets you chain <em>multiple</em> authorizers — including several independent Webhook authorizers, each with its own timeout, failure policy, and CEL-based match conditions that decide which requests even reach that webhook. This is strictly more expressive than the flag (e.g. "send only <code>batch</code>-group requests to webhook A, everything else to webhook B") and can be reloaded without restarting the API server, unlike the flag-based approach.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>The one hard rule to remember: <strong>you cannot use both at once.</strong> Official docs are explicit — "setting both <code>--authorization-config</code> path and configuring an authorization webhook using the <code>--authorization-mode</code> and <code>--authorization-webhook-*</code> command line arguments is not allowed. If you try this, the API server reports an error message during startup, then exits immediately." On the exam, and on most clusters you'll administer day-to-day, you will see the flag-based form this chapter teaches — keep using it exactly as shown. But if you ever inherit a cluster where CIS 1.2.6 seems to not apply at all (no <code>--authorization-mode</code> flag present anywhere, API server still enforcing authorization correctly) — check for an <code>--authorization-config</code> flag before assuming the cluster is misconfigured; it may simply have adopted the newer mechanism, which kube-bench's CIS 1.2.6 check (written around the flag) may not be able to evaluate correctly yet.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Anatomy of a Secure kube-apiserver.yaml</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Key sections of the API server static pod manifest — annotated with CIS checks</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: v1
  kind: Pod
  metadata:
    name: kube-apiserver
    namespace: kube-system
    labels:
      component: kube-apiserver
  spec:
    containers:
    - name: kube-apiserver
      image: registry.k8s.io/kube-apiserver:v1.30.0
      command:
      - kube-apiserver
      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      # AUTHENTICATION FLAGS
      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      - --anonymous-auth=false                    # CIS 1.2.1 ✅
      # REMOVED: --token-auth-file                # CIS 1.2.2 ✅
      - --client-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt   # CA for verifying client certs (not the same check as --tls-cert-file below)

      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      # AUTHORIZATION FLAGS
      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      - --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC            # CIS 1.2.6 ✅

      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      # ADMISSION CONTROL FLAGS
      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      - --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity,AlwaysPullImages
                                                   # CIS 1.2.9 ✅

      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      # AUDIT LOGGING FLAGS
      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      - --audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml  # CIS 1.2.22 ✅
      - --audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log          # CIS 1.2.14 ✅
      - --audit-log-maxage=30                                    # Keep 30 days
      - --audit-log-maxbackup=10                                 # Keep 10 backups
      - --audit-log-maxsize=100                                  # Max 100MB each

      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      # TLS FLAGS
      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      - --tls-cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt    # CIS 1.2.20 ✅
      - --tls-private-key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.key

      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      # etcd TLS FLAGS
      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      - --etcd-cafile=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt
      - --etcd-certfile=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver-etcd-client.crt
      - --etcd-keyfile=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver-etcd-client.key
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 After Editing: Watch the Pod Restart</h5>
                    <p>After saving <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code>, wait a few seconds then verify the new pod is running: <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep apiserver</code>. The RESTARTS column should show a new restart count. Then immediately run kube-bench to verify your fix: <code>kube-bench run --check 1.2.1</code> should now show <code>[PASS]</code>. If it still shows <code>[FAIL]</code>, you edited the wrong flag or there is a typo — re-check the manifest.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What Actually Happens When You Edit a Static Pod Manifest</h4>
                <p>The kubelet on every control plane node runs a <strong>file watcher</strong> on <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. This is not a Kubernetes controller — it is a kubelet-internal goroutine that polls the directory every few seconds. Understanding the exact sequence helps you diagnose problems when the API server does not come back:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Static Pod Lifecycle — The Exact Sequence After Saving kube-apiserver.yaml</div>
<pre>
  T+0s:   You save kube-apiserver.yaml with new flags (:wq)
  T+2s:   kubelet's file watcher detects the modified inode
  T+3s:   kubelet compares the manifest hash with its cached hash
          → Hash differs → triggers a static pod update
  T+4s:   kubelet sends SIGTERM to the old API server container
  T+5s:   Old container stops (graceful period). Port 6443 goes dark.
  T+6s:   kubelet creates a NEW container with the updated command flags
  T+8s:   New API server container starts. Port 6443 begins listening.
  T+10s:  API server loads etcd data, initializes RBAC caches
  T+15s:  API server marks itself ready. kubectl commands work again.
  T+30s:  kube-bench re-run now reflects the new configuration.

  ⚠️  During T+5s to T+10s: ALL kubectl commands fail with
      "The connection to the server was refused". This is NORMAL.

  🔴 RECOVERY: If the API server does NOT come back after 60 seconds:
     1. Check the kubelet knows about the manifest:
        $ ls -la /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
     2. Check the kubelet logs for errors:
        $ journalctl -u kubelet -f | grep apiserver
     3. Check the new container's logs:
        $ crictl ps -a | grep apiserver
        $ crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;
     4. Most common cause: TYPO in a flag name. kube-apiserver refuses
        to start with an unrecognized flag. Check your YAML carefully.
     5. If all else fails: RESTORE FROM BACKUP.
        $ cp /root/kube-apiserver.yaml.backup /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
        → kubelet detects the change → restarts with known-good config
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Recovering from a Bad Manifest Edit — The Emergency Rollback</h4>
                <p>This is a scenario you MUST be prepared for on the CKS exam. You edit the API server manifest, save it, and suddenly kubectl stops working. The API server will not start because of an invalid flag. You cannot use kubectl to fix it because kubectl requires the API server. Here is the recovery procedure — memorize it:</p>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔴 EMERGENCY: API Server Not Starting — Recovery Steps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Step 1: Do NOT panic.</strong> Your cluster state (pods, deployments, PVs) is in etcd, not in the API server. etcd is still running. Your data is safe.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 2: SSH to the control plane node directly.</strong> You cannot use kubectl. Use SSH or the cloud console.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 3: Restore the backup.</strong> <code>cp /root/kube-apiserver.yaml.backup /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. This was created before your edit — right? (Always make a backup!)</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 4: Wait 30 seconds.</strong> kubelet detects the restored manifest and starts the API server with the known-good configuration.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 5: Verify.</strong> <code>kubectl get nodes</code> should work again.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 6: Debug what went wrong.</strong> <code>crictl logs &lt;old-container-id&gt;</code> to see the error message from the failed attempt. Fix the typo in your YAML, make a new backup, and try again.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-weight:700;margin-top:12px;">⚠️ CKS EXAM: Always make a backup before editing ANY manifest file. The exam scores you on whether the cluster works at the end, not just on whether you added the right flags. A cluster with a dead API server = FAIL.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s4-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.4 kubelet Hardening — CIS Node Checks</h3>
                <p>The kubelet runs on <strong>every node</strong> in the cluster. It is the agent responsible for starting and stopping pods, reporting node status, and managing container lifecycles. A compromised kubelet gives an attacker control over every pod on that node — including the ability to exec into containers, read pod logs, and access mounted Secrets. The kubelet is configured through <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> (and sometimes through command-line flags in the systemd service file at <code>/etc/systemd/system/kubelet.service.d/10-kubeadm.conf</code>).</p>

                <h4>kubelet Hardening — Before vs After</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="split-side split-bad">
                        <h5>❌ Insecure kubelet (Default)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>authentication.anonymous.enabled: true</strong> — Anyone can query the kubelet API without credentials</li>
                            <li><strong>authentication.webhook.enabled: false</strong> — No API server validation of kubelet requests</li>
                            <li><strong>authorization.mode: AlwaysAllow</strong> — Every authenticated request is permitted with no RBAC check</li>
                            <li><strong>readOnlyPort: 10255</strong> — Unauthenticated read-only port exposes pod list, container logs, and metrics to anyone</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-weight:700;">ATTACKER: curl http://node:10255/pods → Lists all pods. curl http://node:10255/stats/summary → All metrics. No credentials needed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="split-side split-good">
                        <h5>✅ Hardened kubelet (CKS)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>authentication.anonymous.enabled: false</strong> — All requests must be authenticated</li>
                            <li><strong>authentication.webhook.enabled: true</strong> — Every request is validated by the API server</li>
                            <li><strong>authorization.mode: Webhook</strong> — SubjectAccessReview checks every request against RBAC</li>
                            <li><strong>readOnlyPort: 0</strong> — Unauthenticated port is completely disabled</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="color:#4ade80;font-weight:700;">ATTACKER: curl http://node:10255/pods → Connection REFUSED. Anonymous access → 401 Unauthorized.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The kubelet Configuration File — Annotated</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml — the key hardening fields</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: kubelet.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
  kind: KubeletConfiguration

  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  # AUTHENTICATION (CIS 4.2.1)
  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  authentication:
    anonymous:
      enabled: false              # CIS 4.2.1 ✅ Block unauthenticated requests
    webhook:
      enabled: true               # Delegates authenticated-request validation to the API server
      cacheTTL: 2m0s              # (works together with authorization.mode below to satisfy CIS 4.2.2)

  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  # AUTHORIZATION (CIS 4.2.2)
  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  authorization:
    mode: Webhook                  # CIS 4.2.2 ✅ Every request → SubjectAccessReview

  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  # PORTS (CIS 4.2.3)
  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  readOnlyPort: 0                 # CIS 4.2.3 ✅ Disable insecure port entirely
  healthzPort: 10248              # Keep health check port (internal only)
  healthzBindAddress: 127.0.0.1   # Bind health check to localhost only

  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  # TLS (CIS 4.2.10)
  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  tlsCertFile: /var/lib/kubelet/pki/kubelet.crt
  tlsPrivateKeyFile: /var/lib/kubelet/pki/kubelet.key
  rotateCertificates: true        # Auto-rotate certs before expiry

  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  # PROTECT KERNEL DEFAULTS (CIS 4.2.6)
  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  protectKernelDefaults: true     # CIS 4.2.6 ✅ Kernel params cannot be overridden
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Reconciling With Current Docs: The Equivalent CLI Flags Are Deprecated, Not Removed</h5>
                    <p>Current upstream Kubernetes reference documentation marks nearly every security-relevant kubelet CLI flag — <code>--anonymous-auth</code>, <code>--authorization-mode</code>, <code>--read-only-port</code>, <code>--protect-kernel-defaults</code>, and others — with the same standing deprecation notice: <em>"This parameter should be set via the config file specified by the Kubelet's <code>--config</code> flag."</em> That is exactly the modern, recommended practice this section teaches — put every setting in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> and treat the CLI/systemd layer as off-limits for anything security-related. <strong>"Deprecated" does not mean "non-functional" or "ignored," though</strong> — every one of these flags still works today, and per the precedence chain covered later in this chapter's Under the Hood section, a CLI flag still WINS over the config file if both set the same field. That is precisely why the exam trap in Scenario &amp; Command Drills Q3 and Q7 (config.yaml edit silently overridden by a stale systemd drop-in) remains realistic rather than academic: knowing the flags are deprecated tells you where a fix <em>belongs</em> going forward; knowing they still take precedence tells you where to <em>look</em> when a "correct" config.yaml edit mysteriously has no effect.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Applying kubelet Changes</h4>
                <p>Unlike the API server (which auto-restarts via kubelet watching the manifest), the kubelet itself requires a manual restart after configuration changes. The kubelet is managed by systemd, not by a static pod manifest:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The kubelet restart procedure</div>
<pre>
  # Step 1: Edit the kubelet configuration
  $ vi /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml

  # Step 2: Restart kubelet (systemd-managed)
  $ systemctl daemon-reload
  $ systemctl restart kubelet

  # Step 3: Verify kubelet is running
  $ systemctl status kubelet
  ● kubelet.service - kubelet: The Kubernetes Node Agent
     Active: active (running)

  # Step 4: Verify the node is Ready
  $ kubectl get nodes
  NAME      STATUS   ROLES           AGE   VERSION
  node-1    Ready    control-plane   30d   v1.30.0

  # Step 5: Re-run kube-bench to verify the fix
  $ kube-bench run --targets=node --check=4.2.1
  [PASS] 4.2.1 Ensure --anonymous-auth=false ✅
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>⚠️ Restart Carefully — Node Goes NotReady Briefly</h5>
                    <p>When you restart the kubelet, the node briefly goes to <code>NotReady</code> status (typically 5-15 seconds). Pods on the node continue running during this period — the kubelet restart does not kill running containers. However, the scheduler will not place new pods on the node until it returns to <code>Ready</code>. On the CKS exam, always <strong>drain the node first</strong> if the question asks you to (<code>kubectl drain node-1 --ignore-daemonsets</code>), then restart kubelet, then uncordon (<code>kubectl uncordon node-1</code>). If the exam does not specify draining, a simple restart is fine.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>How kubelet Webhook Authentication Actually Works</h4>
                <p>When you set <code>authentication.webhook.enabled: true</code> and <code>authorization.mode: Webhook</code>, every request to the kubelet API (on port 10250) is validated by the API server — not by the kubelet itself. This is the most important security architecture detail for the CKS exam. Here is the exact flow:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubelet Webhook Auth Flow — Step by Step</div>
<pre>
  STEP 1: A request arrives at kubelet port 10250
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Example: kubectl logs easypj-pod → API server → kubelet:10250/logs

  STEP 2: kubelet checks: is the request authenticated?
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  With webhook enabled, kubelet does NOT trust its own certs.
  It extracts the bearer token from the request and sends it
  to the API server for validation:

  POST https://kube-apiserver:6443/apis/authentication.k8s.io/v1/tokenreview
  {
    "apiVersion": "authentication.k8s.io/v1",
    "kind": "TokenReview",
    "spec": {
      "token": "&lt;token-from-request&gt;"
    }
  }

  STEP 3: API server responds with the authenticated identity
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  {
    "status": {
      "authenticated": true,
      "user": {
        "username": "system:serviceaccount:kube-system:kubelet-api",
        "groups": ["system:serviceaccounts", "system:authenticated"]
      }
    }
  }

  STEP 4: kubelet checks authorization (with authorization.mode: Webhook)
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  POST https://kube-apiserver:6443/apis/authorization.k8s.io/v1/subjectaccessreview
  {
    "spec": {
      "user": "system:serviceaccount:kube-system:kubelet-api",
      "resourceAttributes": {
        "verb": "get",
        "resource": "pods",
        "subresource": "log",
        "name": "easypj-pod",
        "namespace": "easypj"
      }
    }
  }

  STEP 5: API server checks RBAC
  ──────────────────────────────
  Does SA "kube-system:kubelet-api" have a RoleBinding that allows
  "get" on "pods/log" in namespace "easypj"?

  If YES → HTTP 200. kubelet returns the logs.
  If NO  → HTTP 403. kubelet rejects the request.

  🔑 KEY INSIGHT: With webhook mode, the kubelet has ZERO authority
     to decide who can access its API. Every decision is made by the
     API server via RBAC. This means a compromised kubelet cannot
     grant access to unauthorized users — it MUST ask the API server.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>kubelet Certificate Rotation — Automated PKI Management</h4>
                <p>kubelet certificates expire after 1 year by default. Without rotation, your nodes eventually lose the ability to communicate with the API server — and your cluster breaks silently. The CKS exam expects you to know how to enable automatic certificate rotation so this never happens:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubelet Certificate Rotation Flow</div>
<pre>
  CONFIGURATION (in /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml):
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  rotateCertificates: true          # kubelet requests new certs
  serverTLSBootstrap: true          # kubelet uses CSR API for server cert

  THE AUTOMATIC ROTATION FLOW:
  ────────────────────────────
  1. kubelet monitors its certificate expiry date
  2. Sometime between 70% and 90% of the cert's lifetime elapsed —
     i.e. while 30% down to 10% of its total lifetime remains — the
     kubelet picks a RANDOM point in that window (jitter, to avoid every
     node in the fleet renewing at the same instant) and generates a new
     key pair + CertificateSigningRequest (CSR). For a 1-year cert this
     window is roughly month 8.4 through month 10.8 — NOT a fixed date.
  3. A controller (kube-controller-manager with
     --cluster-signing-cert-file and --cluster-signing-key-file)
     automatically approves the CSR and signs the new certificate —
     but ONLY for CLIENT certificate rotation (the kubelet's own identity).
  4. kubelet picks up the new cert and replaces the old one
  5. The node continues operating with NO interruption

  VERIFYING ROTATION:
  ───────────────────
  # Check if rotation is enabled
  $ grep rotateCertificates /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml

  # Check current cert expiry
  $ openssl x509 -in /var/lib/kubelet/pki/kubelet-client-current.pem \
      -noout -dates
  notBefore=Jan  1 00:00:00 2024 GMT
  notAfter=Dec 31 23:59:59 2024 GMT

  # List CSRs (pending rotation requests)
  $ kubectl get csr
  NAME        AGE   SIGNERNAME                     REQUESTOR          CONDITION
  csr-abc123  5m    kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-* system:node:node-1 Approved

  ⚠️  WITHOUT rotation: On day 366, the kubelet cert expires.
      kubelet disconnects from API server. Node becomes NotReady.
      Pods keep running but cannot be managed. Emergency cert renewal
      required — manually. Avoid this by enabling rotation NOW.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Reconciling With Current Docs: <code>rotateCertificates</code> and <code>serverTLSBootstrap</code> Are NOT Auto-Approved the Same Way</h5>
                    <p>The diagram above is accurate for <strong><code>rotateCertificates: true</code></strong> — kubelet <em>client</em> certificate renewals (proving the kubelet's own <code>system:node:&lt;name&gt;</code> identity to the API server) use the <code>kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client-kubelet</code> signer, and Kubernetes ships a built-in approving controller inside kube-controller-manager that auto-approves these — genuinely zero-touch. <strong><code>serverTLSBootstrap: true</code></strong> is a different beast: it requests a kubelet <em>serving</em> certificate (what clients like <code>kubectl logs</code>/<code>kubectl exec</code> validate when they connect TO the kubelet) using the <code>kubernetes.io/kubelet-serving</code> signer — and core Kubernetes <strong>deliberately does not</strong> auto-approve these. The reason is security, not an oversight: approving a serving cert safely requires verifying the requested IP addresses and DNS names actually belong to that node via some out-of-band mechanism, which the generic client-cert approver has no way to do. Left alone, <code>serverTLSBootstrap: true</code> without an approver just accumulates <code>Pending</code> CSRs forever (visible via <code>kubectl get csr</code>). In practice you need either your cloud provider's own CSR signer/approver, or a community controller such as the <strong>kubelet-csr-approver</strong> project, or manual <code>kubectl certificate approve &lt;name&gt;</code> per request. Don't assume enabling <code>serverTLSBootstrap</code> alone gives you the same "fully automatic" behavior as <code>rotateCertificates</code> — verify an approver is actually running, or your serving certs will silently never renew.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

