            <section class="section-block" id="cks-s4-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.5 Applying CIS Benchmark Fixes — Hands-On with easypj</h3>
                <p>Let's go through a realistic scenario: your easypj production cluster has just been audited and kube-bench reports 26 FAIL results — 14 on the control plane, 12 on the worker nodes (the exact numbers behind the CIS Compliance Scorecard at the end of this section). You don't have time to fix all 26 before the security review deadline — you need to prioritize. Here are the <strong>top 5 highest-impact fixes</strong>, the exact commands, and the kube-bench verification for each:</p>

                <h4>The CIS Fix Workflow — Your Repeatable Cycle</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The 5-step cycle for every CIS failure — memorize this</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  THE kube-bench → FIX → VERIFY CYCLE                         │
  │                                                              │
  │  1. RUN:    kube-bench run --targets=master,node             │
  │     → Note all [FAIL] items                                  │
  │                         │                                    │
  │                         ▼                                    │
  │  2. READ:   The remediation section tells you:               │
  │     • Which file to edit                                     │
  │     • Which flag to add/remove/change                        │
  │                         │                                    │
  │                         ▼                                    │
  │  3. EDIT:   vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml │
  │     (or /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml for kubelet)           │
  │     Batch ALL flag changes into ONE edit                     │
  │                         │                                    │
  │                         ▼                                    │
  │  4. WAIT:   API server: kubelet auto-restarts (10-30s)      │
  │     kubelet: systemctl restart kubelet                       │
  │                         │                                    │
  │                         ▼                                    │
  │  5. VERIFY: kube-bench run --check=&lt;check-number&gt;           │
  │     → [PASS] ✅ — confirmed. Next FAIL.                      │
  │     → [FAIL] ❌ — typo? wrong file? Re-check.                │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Top 5 Highest-Impact CIS Fixes for the easypj Cluster</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-1">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">1️⃣</div>
                        <h5>--anonymous-auth=false on API Server (CIS 1.2.1)</h5>
                        <p><strong>Impact:</strong> Prevents unauthenticated API access — the single most important CIS check. Without this, anyone on the network can query your API. <strong>File:</strong> <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. Add flag: <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>. <strong>Verify:</strong> <code>kube-bench run --check=1.2.1</code> → must show [PASS]. <strong>Test:</strong> <code>kubectl get pods --server=https://API_IP:6443 --insecure-skip-tls-verify</code> → should return "Unauthorized" (401).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">2️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Enable Audit Logging (CIS 1.2.14, 1.2.22)</h5>
                        <p><strong>Impact:</strong> Creates a forensic record of every API call — who did what and when. Essential for breach investigation and compliance. <strong>Files:</strong> Create <code>/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml</code>, then add flags to kube-apiserver.yaml: <code>--audit-policy-file</code>, <code>--audit-log-path</code>, <code>--audit-log-maxage=30</code>. <strong>Verify:</strong> <code>ls /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</code> — must exist and be non-empty after a kubectl command.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">3️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Disable kubelet Read-Only Port (CIS 4.2.3)</h5>
                        <p><strong>Impact:</strong> Closes port 10255, which exposes pod lists, container logs, and node metrics with NO authentication. An attacker on the same network can enumerate your entire workload from this port. <strong>File:</strong> <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>. Set: <code>readOnlyPort: 0</code>. <strong>Verify:</strong> <code>curl http://localhost:10255/pods</code> → "Connection refused". <strong>Restart:</strong> <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">4️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Enable NodeRestriction Admission Plugin (CIS 1.2.9)</h5>
                        <p><strong>Impact:</strong> Limits what a compromised kubelet can do. Without NodeRestriction, a kubelet can modify labels, taints, and resources belonging to OTHER nodes — enabling lateral movement from one compromised node to the entire cluster. <strong>File:</strong> kube-apiserver.yaml. Add <code>NodeRestriction</code> to <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> list. <strong>Verify:</strong> <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system kube-apiserver-* -o yaml | grep NodeRestriction</code> → must appear in the flag.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">5️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Enable Encryption at Rest for Secrets (CIS 1.2.31-34)</h5>
                        <p><strong>Impact:</strong> Secrets stored in etcd are encrypted with AES-256. Even with direct etcd access, Secrets are unreadable without the encryption key. <strong>Files:</strong> Create <code>/etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml</code> (EncryptionConfiguration resource), add <code>--encryption-provider-config</code> flag to API server. <strong>Verify:</strong> Create a test Secret, then check etcd directly — the value must be stored encrypted (<code>k8s:enc:aescbc:v1:</code> prefix). <strong>Note:</strong> This is covered in detail in Chapter 19 — here we establish that it is a CIS requirement.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Real-World Troubleshooting: When Your CIS Fix Does Not Work</h4>
                <p>On the exam — and in production — fixes do not always work on the first attempt. Here are the three most common failure patterns, their root causes, and the exact diagnostic commands you need:</p>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔴 Scenario 1: Edited kube-apiserver.yaml but kube-bench still shows [FAIL]</h5>
                    <p><strong>Root Cause:</strong> The API server has not restarted yet, or you edited the wrong manifest file. Kubeadm-based clusters store the manifest at <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. But if the cluster was installed with a different tool (kops, k3s, RKE), the path might differ.</p>
                    <p><strong>Diagnostic Commands:</strong></p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Verify the manifest file path is correct</span>
$ ps aux | grep kube-apiserver | grep -o 'manifest.*yaml'
→ Should show the actual manifest path being used

<span class="comment"># 2. Check if kubelet detected your change</span>
$ journalctl -u kubelet --since "2 minutes ago" | grep apiserver
→ Look for "static pod" and "kube-apiserver" messages

<span class="comment"># 3. Force the kubelet to re-sync</span>
$ systemctl restart kubelet
→ This forces the kubelet to re-read ALL manifests in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify the API server pod is running with your flags</span>
$ crictl inspect $(crictl ps --name kube-apiserver -q) | grep args</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box positive">
                    <h5>🟡 Scenario 2: Edited kubelet config.yaml but changes are ignored</h5>
                    <p><strong>Root Cause:</strong> Command-line flags passed to the kubelet via the systemd service file OVERRIDE settings in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>. If a flag like <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> appears in both the config file AND the systemd drop-in, the CLI value wins. This is a common exam trap.</p>
                    <p><strong>Diagnostic Commands:</strong></p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check the ACTUAL kubelet process flags (these override config.yaml)</span>
$ ps aux | grep kubelet | tr ' ' '\n' | grep anonymous
→ If --anonymous-auth=true appears here, config.yaml is IGNORED

<span class="comment"># 2. Find the systemd drop-in that sets the flag</span>
$ grep -r "anonymous-auth" /etc/systemd/system/kubelet.service.d/
→ Shows which file has the conflicting flag

<span class="comment"># 3. Fix: remove or change the flag in the systemd file, then restart</span>
$ systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart kubelet</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔴 Scenario 3: kube-bench says [WARN] when you expected [PASS]</h5>
                    <p><strong>Root Cause:</strong> kube-bench could not programmatically determine the check result. This often happens when a flag is present but its value is ambiguous, or when a configuration spans multiple files and kube-bench only checks one.</p>
                    <p><strong>Diagnostic Commands:</strong></p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Run kube-bench in verbose mode for the specific check</span>
$ kube-bench run --check=1.2.6 -v
→ This shows EXACTLY what kube-bench tested and why it returned [WARN]

<span class="comment"># 2. Manually verify the check</span>
$ grep authorization-mode /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
→ Confirm the flag value matches the CIS recommendation exactly

<span class="comment"># 3. If manually verified correct and kube-bench still says [WARN]:</span>
→ This is a known limitation. Document it. On the exam, [WARN] with
  manual verification may be acceptable if the configuration is correct.</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>kube-bench in CI/CD — Automating CIS Compliance</h4>
                <p>On the exam, you run kube-bench manually. In production, you automate it. This section is optional for the exam but essential for real-world operations. Understanding this pattern gives you context for WHY the manual steps exist:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kube-bench as a Kubernetes CronJob — Continuous Compliance</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: batch/v1
  kind: CronJob
  metadata:
    name: kube-bench-daily
    namespace: security
  spec:
    schedule: "0 3 * * *"           # Run every night at 3 AM
    jobTemplate:
      spec:
        template:
          spec:
            hostPID: true              # Access node's process namespace
            hostNetwork: true          # Access node's network
            nodeSelector:
              node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: ""
            containers:
            - name: kube-bench
              image: aquasec/kube-bench:latest
              command: ["kube-bench", "run", "--targets=master,node"]
              volumeMounts:
              - name: config
                mountPath: /etc/kubernetes
                readOnly: true
              - name: lib
                mountPath: /var/lib
                readOnly: true
            volumes:
            - name: config
              hostPath:
                path: /etc/kubernetes
            - name: lib
              hostPath:
                path: /var/lib
            restartPolicy: OnFailure

  # This CronJob runs kube-bench EVERY NIGHT on every control plane node.
  # It reads the node's filesystem to check configurations.
  # Results can be forwarded to Elasticsearch, Grafana, or AlertManager.
  # If a previously [PASS] check suddenly becomes [FAIL], an alert fires.
  # This is how you detect configuration drift automatically — no manual
  # audits needed.
</pre>
