                    <tr><td>5</td><td>1.2.18 ServiceAccount plugin</td><td>API Server</td><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code></td><td><code>--enable-admission-plugins=...,ServiceAccount,...</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>6</td><td>2.1 etcd cert-file/key-file</td><td>etcd</td><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code></td><td><code>--cert-file</code> and <code>--key-file</code> set</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>7</td><td>2.6 etcd client-cert-auth</td><td>etcd</td><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code></td><td><code>--client-cert-auth=true</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>8</td><td>4.2.1 kubelet anonymous-auth</td><td>Kubelet</td><td><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></td><td><code>authentication: anonymous: enabled: false</code> + restart</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>9</td><td>4.2.2 kubelet authorization</td><td>Kubelet</td><td><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></td><td><code>authorization: mode: Webhook</code> + restart</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>10</td><td>4.2.3 kubelet read-only-port</td><td>Kubelet</td><td><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></td><td><code>readOnlyPort: 0</code> + restart</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>11</td><td>1.2.21 profiling enabled</td><td>API Server</td><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code></td><td><code>--profiling=false</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>12</td><td>2.7 etcd data-dir perms/owner</td><td>etcd</td><td>filesystem (SSH to node)</td><td><code>chmod 700 /var/lib/etcd</code> + <code>chown etcd:etcd /var/lib/etcd</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>13</td><td>4.2.6 kubelet kernel defaults</td><td>Kubelet</td><td><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></td><td><code>protectKernelDefaults: true</code> + restart</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>14</td><td>1.2.14 / 1.2.22 no audit logging</td><td>API Server</td><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code></td><td><code>--audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</code> + <code>--audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml</code></td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Rows 11-14 Explained</h5>
                    <p><strong>Row 11 (profiling):</strong> the API server's <code>--profiling</code> flag (default <code>true</code>) exposes a <code>/debug/pprof/</code> HTTP endpoint used for Go runtime performance debugging — CPU profiles, heap dumps, goroutine stacks. In production this is pure attack surface: it can leak memory contents (potentially including secrets held in API server memory) and consumes CPU when queried. Fix: add <code>--profiling=false</code> to <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. There is no legitimate reason to leave it enabled outside active performance debugging sessions.</p>
                    <p><strong>Row 12 (etcd data-dir):</strong> etcd's data directory (default <code>/var/lib/etcd</code>) contains the entire cluster state, including every Secret in raw (or encrypted, if you did Row 3) form. If the directory or its files are world-readable, any local user on the etcd node can copy the database and, absent encryption at rest, read every Secret offline. Fix: <code>chmod 700 /var/lib/etcd && chown etcd:etcd /var/lib/etcd</code> (or <code>root:root</code> if etcd runs as root, which is the kubeadm default — check <code>ps -ef | grep etcd</code> to see which user actually owns the running process before choosing the owner).</p>
                    <p><strong>Row 13 (protectKernelDefaults):</strong> already covered in depth in §4.4 — included here so the fast-reference table has a single row for every check discussed anywhere in this chapter. See §4.4's annotated <code>config.yaml</code> diagram and Q6 for the full explanation.</p>
                    <p><strong>Row 14 (audit logging):</strong> this is one of the ten classic, most-tested CIS failures — "no audit logging" — and it is really <em>two</em> flags that must land together: <code>--audit-log-path</code> tells the API server WHERE to write the audit trail, and <code>--audit-policy-file</code> tells it WHAT to log. Neither one alone satisfies the check. Already covered in full in §4.3's flag table, §4.5's Top-5-Fix #2, and Lab Q&amp;A Q8 / Scenario Drill Q4 — included here so this fast-reference table has a row for every one of cks.txt's canonical top-10 failures, not just 9 of them.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 CKS Exam Pattern</h5><p>API server/etcd = edit manifest, auto-restart (~10-30s). Kubelet = edit config.yaml → <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code>. Always re-run kube-bench to verify.</p></div>

