                <h4>etcd Hardening Deep-Dive — The Checks Behind Rows 6, 7, and 12</h4>
                <p>etcd is the cluster's single source of truth — every Deployment, Secret, ConfigMap, and RBAC rule lives there. Yet it is easy to under-harden because the CKS curriculum spends more visible time on the API server and kubelet. Rows 6, 7, and 12 of the table above only give you the check numbers and one-line fixes; here is the full picture of etcd's own static pod manifest (<code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> on kubeadm clusters) so you understand exactly what each flag does and why kube-bench flags its absence.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Anatomy of a Secure etcd.yaml — Annotated with CIS Checks</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: v1
  kind: Pod
  metadata:
    name: etcd
    namespace: kube-system
  spec:
    containers:
    - name: etcd
      image: registry.k8s.io/etcd:3.5.9-0
      command:
      - etcd
      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      # CLIENT-FACING TLS (API server ↔ etcd)  — CIS 2.1
      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      - --cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt        # CIS 2.1 ✅
      - --key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key          # CIS 2.1 ✅
      - --client-cert-auth=true                                  # CIS 2.6 ✅ Require client certs
      - --trusted-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt

      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      # PEER TLS (etcd node ↔ etcd node)  — CIS 2.2-2.4
      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      - --peer-cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/peer.crt      # CIS 2.2 ✅
      - --peer-key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/peer.key        # CIS 2.2 ✅
      - --peer-client-cert-auth=true                             # CIS 2.4 ✅ Peers must present certs too
      - --peer-trusted-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt   # CIS 2.3 ✅

      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      # DISABLE SELF-SIGNED SHORTCUTS  — CIS 2.5
      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      # auto-tls / peer-auto-tls DEFAULT TO false in modern etcd, but if you
      # ever see them set to "true" in an exam cluster, that IS the FAIL:
      # REMOVED: --auto-tls=true         # Would auto-generate self-signed
      # REMOVED: --peer-auto-tls=true    #   certs with NO CA validation.
                                          #   Fix = remove the flag (or =false).

      - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 Why --auto-tls / --peer-auto-tls Are Dangerous</h5>
                    <p>When <code>--auto-tls=true</code>, etcd generates its own self-signed certificate at startup instead of using a certificate signed by your cluster's CA. This still encrypts traffic — but there is no chain of trust: <strong>any</strong> self-signed cert is accepted, so a man-in-the-middle attacker can present their own self-signed cert and etcd cannot tell the difference. It gives you the false confidence of "TLS is on" without the actual guarantee TLS is supposed to provide (verified identity). The CIS fix is simply to ensure the flag is absent or explicitly <code>false</code>, and that real CA-signed <code>--cert-file</code>/<code>--peer-cert-file</code> paths are configured instead.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>CIS Check</th><th>Flag / Setting</th><th>Secure Value</th><th>What It Prevents</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>2.1</strong></td><td><code>--cert-file</code> / <code>--key-file</code></td><td>Valid CA-signed cert paths</td><td>Plaintext client↔etcd traffic — every Secret would traverse the network unencrypted.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>2.2</strong></td><td><code>--peer-cert-file</code> / <code>--peer-key-file</code></td><td>Valid CA-signed cert paths</td><td>Plaintext etcd↔etcd replication traffic in multi-node etcd clusters.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>2.3</strong></td><td><code>--peer-trusted-ca-file</code></td><td>Path to cluster CA</td><td>Peers unable to validate each other's identity — any node could pretend to be an etcd peer.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>2.4</strong></td><td><code>--peer-client-cert-auth</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td>Unauthenticated peer connections — a rogue process could join the etcd cluster as a fake peer.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>2.5</strong></td><td><code>--auto-tls</code> / <code>--peer-auto-tls</code></td><td>Absent or <code>false</code></td><td>Self-signed certs with no CA validation — see the danger box above.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>2.6</strong></td><td><code>--client-cert-auth</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td>Unauthenticated clients reading/writing cluster state directly against etcd, bypassing the API server and RBAC entirely.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>2.7</strong></td><td>Data directory perms/owner</td><td><code>700</code>, owned by the etcd-running user</td><td>Local users on the node reading the raw database file off disk — a full offline copy of every Secret in the cluster.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Verify etcd data directory permissions and ownership (CIS 2.7)</span>
$ stat -c "%a %U:%G %n" /var/lib/etcd
750 etcd:etcd /var/lib/etcd          <span class="comment"># FAIL — group-readable (750), should be 700</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix it</span>
$ sudo chmod 700 /var/lib/etcd
$ sudo chown etcd:etcd /var/lib/etcd  <span class="comment"># or root:root — match the running process's user</span>

<span class="comment"># Confirm which user etcd actually runs as before choosing an owner</span>
$ ps -ef | grep [e]tcd | awk '{print $1}'
root                                   <span class="comment"># On kubeadm, etcd runs as root inside its static pod</span>

<span class="comment"># Re-verify</span>
$ stat -c "%a %U:%G %n" /var/lib/etcd
700 root:root /var/lib/etcd           <span class="comment"># PASS ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># etcd changes to the static pod manifest auto-restart just like the API server —</span>
<span class="comment"># but changing ONLY file permissions on /var/lib/etcd does NOT require a restart</span>
<span class="comment"># at all, since it's a filesystem-level fix, not a flag change.</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Master Node Configuration File Permissions — CIS 1.1.x, the Checks That Aren't About Flags At All</h4>
                <p>Every check discussed so far in this chapter — §4.3's API server table, §4.4's kubelet table, even the etcd flags immediately above — is about a <strong>flag value</strong>: is <code>--anonymous-auth</code> set correctly, is <code>--client-cert-auth</code> true. CIS section 1.1 ("Master Node Configuration Files") is a different animal entirely: it checks <strong>file permissions and ownership</strong> on the kubeconfig files kubeadm generates for each control-plane component, plus the PKI directory holding every certificate and private key in the cluster. kube-bench runs these checks by default under <code>--targets=master</code> right alongside the flag checks — they are easy to overlook because nothing in the FAIL output "looks like" a flag problem, yet a wrong permission here is often a more direct path to full cluster compromise than any single flag misconfiguration.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>File / Path</th><th>Correct Mode</th><th>Correct Owner</th><th>CIS Check</th><th>Why a Wrong Setting Is Dangerous</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/admin.conf</code></td><td>600</td><td>root:root</td><td>1.1.13 / 1.1.14</td><td>Contains a <strong>cluster-admin</strong> kubeconfig — the exact credential a cluster owner uses for <code>kubectl</code>. World- or group-readable means any local user on the control-plane node can copy it and run <code>kubectl --kubeconfig=admin.conf get secrets -A</code> as full cluster-admin — no RBAC check stands in the way, because this identity already holds the built-in <code>cluster-admin</code> binding.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/scheduler.conf</code></td><td>600</td><td>root:root</td><td>1.1.15 / 1.1.16</td><td>Contains the <code>system:kube-scheduler</code> identity's credentials. Leaked, it lets an attacker impersonate the scheduler — read every Pod spec and Node object cluster-wide.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/controller-manager.conf</code></td><td>600</td><td>root:root</td><td>1.1.17 / 1.1.18</td><td>Contains the <code>system:kube-controller-manager</code> identity's credentials. This controller has access to the <code>--service-account-private-key-file</code> path — the key that signs <strong>every</strong> ServiceAccount token in the cluster.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> (directory)</td><td>750 or more restrictive</td><td>root:root</td><td>1.1.19</td><td>If a non-root user owns this directory, they can replace the cluster's CA certificate/key with their own and mint valid-looking credentials for anything in the cluster — the single worst possible compromise short of root on every node.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/*.crt</code></td><td>600 or more restrictive (644 is acceptable — certs are public by design)</td><td>root:root</td><td>1.1.20</td><td>Certificates aren't secret by themselves, but loose cert permissions alongside a loose directory are a signal of broader neglect that kube-bench flags defensively.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/*.key</code></td><td>600</td><td>root:root</td><td>1.1.21</td><td>Private keys. World- or group-readable means any local user can extract the CA's private key (or the API server's, or etcd's) and impersonate that component to the rest of the cluster.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 admin.conf Is the Single Highest-Value File on the Entire Control Plane</h5>
                    <p>Of everything in the table above, <code>/etc/kubernetes/admin.conf</code> deserves special attention: unlike a service-account credential scoped to one component's narrow permissions, this is a full <code>cluster-admin</code>-bound identity — equivalent to root on the entire Kubernetes API. On the exam, if a task ever asks you to "audit control-plane node file permissions," check this file FIRST. In production, treat any process or account that can read this file as equivalent to a cluster administrator — which is exactly why kubeadm defaults it to <code>600</code>, and why loosening it, even "just for debugging," is never acceptable outside a fully isolated lab.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check all three kubeconfig files in one pass</span>
$ stat -c "%a %U:%G %n" /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf /etc/kubernetes/scheduler.conf /etc/kubernetes/controller-manager.conf
644 root:root /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf              <span class="comment"># FAIL — group/other readable, should be 600</span>
600 root:root /etc/kubernetes/scheduler.conf           <span class="comment"># PASS</span>
600 root:root /etc/kubernetes/controller-manager.conf  <span class="comment"># PASS</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix the FAIL</span>
$ sudo chmod 600 /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf
$ stat -c "%a %U:%G %n" /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf
600 root:root /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf               <span class="comment"># PASS ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># Check + fix the entire PKI directory in one pass</span>
$ stat -c "%a %U:%G %n" /etc/kubernetes/pki
750 root:root /etc/kubernetes/pki
$ sudo find /etc/kubernetes/pki -type f -name '*.key' -exec chmod 600 {} \;   <span class="comment"># private keys: 600</span>
$ sudo find /etc/kubernetes/pki -type f -name '*.crt' -exec chmod 644 {} \;   <span class="comment"># certs: 644 is fine, they're public by design</span>
$ sudo chown -R root:root /etc/kubernetes/pki

<span class="comment"># Verify with kube-bench</span>
$ kube-bench run --targets=master --check=1.1.13,1.1.14,1.1.15,1.1.16,1.1.17,1.1.18,1.1.19,1.1.20,1.1.21</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 No Restart Needed — This Is a Pure Filesystem Fix</h5>
                    <p>Unlike every flag-based fix elsewhere in this chapter, changing file permissions and ownership takes effect <strong>immediately</strong> and needs no restart of anything. The kubelet doesn't watch these files for content changes the way it watches manifests for spec changes — permissions are enforced by the OS kernel on every read attempt, not by any Kubernetes component. The instant <code>chmod</code> returns, the new permission is in force. This makes CIS 1.1.x checks some of the fastest, lowest-risk fixes available on the exam — no restart window, no brief kubectl outage, nothing to "wait for."</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key Takeaway for the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p>The CKS exam hardening workflow is <strong>always the same</strong>: (1) SSH to the node, (2) run kube-bench, (3) read the FAIL output, (4) edit the file it tells you to edit, (5) add/remove the flag it tells you to change, (6) wait for restart or restart manually, (7) re-run kube-bench to verify [PASS]. <strong>Do not improvise. Trust kube-bench. It is your answer key.</strong> The exam environment has kube-bench pre-installed. Use it on every hardening task — even if the question does not explicitly tell you to.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>How kube-bench Actually Works — The CIS Benchmark Engine Internals</h4>
                <p>kube-bench is not magic. It is a Go binary that reads the CIS Kubernetes Benchmark specification and executes a series of <strong>audit checks</strong> — each check is a small program that inspects a specific configuration file, runs a specific command, or queries a specific API endpoint. Understanding how it works internally helps you troubleshoot when a check that should pass fails, or when you need to write custom checks:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kube-bench Architecture — From CIS Spec to PASS/FAIL</div>
<pre>
  CIS KUBERNETES BENCHMARK (PDF from cisecurity.org)
  │  Contains ~200 checks organized by component:
  │  • 1.1 Master Node Configuration Files (~30 checks)
  │  • 1.2 API Server (~25 checks)
  │  • 1.3 Controller Manager (~10 checks)
  │  • 1.4 Scheduler (~5 checks)
  │  • 2.x etcd (~10 checks)
  │  • 3.x Control Plane Configuration (~20 checks)
  │  • 4.x Worker Node Configuration (~30 checks)
  │  • 5.x Kubernetes Policies (~60 checks)
  │
  ▼  (CIS spec is transcribed into YAML check definitions)
  │
  kube-bench/cfg/cis-1.XX/  (YAML check definitions bundled with kube-bench)
  │
  │  Example check for API server anonymous auth:
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  │  - id: 1.2.1                                            │
  │  │    text: "Ensure that the --anonymous-auth argument     │
  │  │           is set to false"                               │
  │  │    audit: "ps -ef | grep kube-apiserver | grep -v grep  │
  │  │            | grep anonymous-auth"                        │
  │  │    tests:                                                │
  │  │      test_items:                                         │
  │  │      - flag: "--anonymous-auth"                          │
  │  │        compare:                                          │
  │  │          op: eq                                          │
  │  │          value: false                                    │
  │  │    remediation: "Edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/         │
  │  │                   kube-apiserver.yaml and set            │
  │  │                   --anonymous-auth=false"                │
  │  │    scored: true                                          │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼  (kube-bench executes each check's audit command)
  │
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  CHECK EXECUTION ENGINE                                           │
  │  ────────────────────                                             │
  │  1. For each check, determine the node type (master/worker/etcd) │
  │     based on which components are detected.                       │
  │  2. Execute the audit command (ssh to node, run ps/grep/cat).   │
  │  3. Parse the output and compare against the expected value.     │
  │  4. Determine result:                                             │
  │     • PASS — expected value matched                               │
  │     • FAIL — expected value NOT matched                           │
  │     • WARN — check couldn't be fully verified                     │
  │     • INFO — informational only, not a pass/fail check           │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  OUTPUT: [PASS] 1.2.1 Ensure anonymous-auth is false
          [FAIL] 1.2.5 Ensure --kubelet-certificate-authority is set
          [WARN] 1.2.33 Ensure --encryption-provider-config is set
</pre>
                </div>
                <p><strong>The scored vs not-scored distinction:</strong> CIS assigns each check a "scored" flag. Scored checks count toward compliance scoring. Not-scored checks are best practices that may not apply to every environment. On the CKS exam, focus on scored FAIL items first — these are the ones that cost points. WARN items usually indicate that kube-bench couldn't definitively check the configuration (the file might be in a non-standard location, or the process might not be running on this node type).</p>

                <h4>Static Pod Manifests — Why Editing /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ Works Instantly</h4>
                <p>This is the most important Kubernetes internals concept for CKS hardening tasks — and the one that confuses candidates who come from a traditional systemd background. The API server, controller manager, scheduler, and etcd run as <strong>static pods</strong> — pods managed directly by the kubelet, not by the Kubernetes API server. Here's why that matters:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Static Pod Lifecycle — How Editing a Manifest File Restarts the Control Plane</div>
<pre>
  THE KUBELET WATCHES /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ EVERY FEW SECONDS
  │
  │  /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
  │  ├── kube-apiserver.yaml          ← kubelet watches this directory
  │  ├── kube-controller-manager.yaml
  │  ├── kube-scheduler.yaml
  │  └── etcd.yaml
  │
  ▼
  WHEN YOU EDIT A MANIFEST (e.g., add --anonymous-auth=false):
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  1. You save the file. kubelet detects the change (inotify or periodic poll).
  2. kubelet compares the new manifest to the running pod's hash.
  3. Hash changed → kubelet GRACEFULLY STOPS the running static pod.
  4. kubelet CREATES a new static pod from the updated manifest.
  5. The new pod starts with the NEW flags.
  6. kubelet registers the new pod with the API server.
  │
  ⚡ CRITICAL: If you make a SYNTAX ERROR in the YAML:
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  • kubelet cannot parse the manifest → does NOT stop the running pod.
  • The existing pod keeps running with the OLD configuration.
  • kubectl still works (the old API server is still running).
  • You can FIX the YAML and save again → kubelet will retry.
  │
  🔴 DANGER: If you make a VALID YAML change but with a BROKEN FLAG:
  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  • kubelet parses the YAML successfully → stops the OLD pod.
  • kubelet tries to start the NEW pod → the broken flag crashes the binary.
  • Crash → kubelet retries → crash → retry → CrashLoopBackOff.
  • kubectl STOPS WORKING (the API server is DOWN).
  • YOU CANNOT USE kubectl TO FIX IT.
  • RECOVERY: SSH to the control plane node → edit the manifest → FIX the flag →
    kubelet detects the change → starts the new pod with the FIXED flag.
  │
  ⚡ EXAM LESSON: ALWAYS BACKUP THE MANIFEST before editing:
  cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml ~/apiserver.bak
  If the API server doesn't come back, restore the backup via SSH.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

