            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch4-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The CIS Kubernetes Benchmark is 200+ binary, testable checks across 5 categories</strong> — control plane components, etcd, control-plane configuration, worker nodes, and Kubernetes policies. kube-bench automates auditing all of them and is pre-installed on the CKS exam.</li>
                    <li><strong>The workflow never changes:</strong> Run kube-bench → Read the FAIL/remediation output → Edit the correct file → Wait for auto-restart (API server) or restart manually (kubelet) → Re-run kube-bench to verify. Do not improvise — let kube-bench guide every fix.</li>
                    <li><strong>API server, etcd, controller-manager, and scheduler are static pods.</strong> Editing their manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> is auto-detected by the kubelet's file watcher and triggers an automatic restart in ~10-30 seconds — there is no <code>kubectl apply</code> step. Always back up the manifest before editing.</li>
                    <li><strong>The kubelet is a systemd service, not a static pod.</strong> Hardening <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> (disable anonymous auth, disable the read-only port, switch to Webhook authn/authz) requires <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code> — and CLI flags in the systemd drop-in file silently override the config file.</li>
                    <li><strong>FAIL demands an immediate fix; WARN demands investigation, not dismissal.</strong> WARN means kube-bench could not automatically determine the result — it is not a pass. On the easypj cluster, the top 5 highest-impact fixes alone raised the control-plane pass rate from 53% to 88%.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

