                    <div class="eq-question">You run kube-bench and see: [FAIL] 1.2.1 Ensure that the --anonymous-auth argument is set to false. Which file do you edit, what exact flag do you add, and how do you verify the fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. Add <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> to the command list. The kubelet auto-restarts the API server in ~30 seconds. Verify with both a positive test (authenticated access still works) and a negative test (anonymous access is now blocked):</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Edit the static pod manifest — add the missing flag
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
#   under command:  - --anonymous-auth=false

# 2. Wait for the kubelet's file watcher to auto-restart the API server (~10-30s)
watch sudo crictl ps --name kube-apiserver

# 3. POSITIVE test — authenticated access still works
kubectl get --raw=/healthz
# → ok

# 4. NEGATIVE test — anonymous access is now blocked
curl -k https://&lt;API_IP&gt;:6443/api
# → 401 Unauthorized  (before the fix, this would have returned 200 with API data)

# 5. Confirm the RUNNING process actually has the flag (not just the file)
ps aux | grep kube-apiserver | grep -o 'anonymous-auth=[a-z]*'
# → anonymous-auth=false

# 6. Final confirmation via kube-bench itself
kube-bench run --targets=master --check=1.2.1
# → [PASS] 1.2.1 Ensure --anonymous-auth=false ✅</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>CIS Benchmark 1.2.1 is the most commonly failed check on CKS. Anonymous auth is enabled by default. The fix requires editing the API server static pod manifest, which triggers an automatic restart. On the exam, you must verify BOTH that authenticated access still works AND that anonymous access is blocked. Use <code>ps aux | grep kube-apiserver | grep anonymous-auth</code> to confirm the running process has the flag.</p></div></details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">kube-bench reports [FAIL] 1.1.3: controller-manager pod spec permissions are not 600. What is the EXACT remediation command and why does this matter?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Fix the ownership and permissions, then verify ALL the manifests at once — CIS 1.1.3-1.1.8 check every one of them, not just the controller-manager:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># The exact remediation
sudo chmod 600 /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml
sudo chown root:root /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml

# Verify ALL control-plane manifests in one pass — don't stop at just this one
stat -c "%a %U:%G %n" /etc/kubernetes/manifests/*.yaml
# 600 root:root /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
# 600 root:root /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
# 600 root:root /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml   ✅ fixed
# 600 root:root /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml

kube-bench run --targets=master --check=1.1.3
# → [PASS] 1.1.3 Ensure controller manager pod specification file permissions ✅</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> If non-root users can read this file, they can see the controller-manager's configuration, including any flags like <code>--service-account-private-key-file</code> which points to the SA token signing key. This is a privilege escalation path.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Static pod manifests are YAML files on the control plane node. They must be owned by root:root with mode 600 because they contain sensitive configuration (TLS key paths, encryption keys, etc.). CIS checks 1.1.3-1.1.8 all verify file permissions on these manifests. On the exam, run <code>stat -c "%a %U:%G %n" /etc/kubernetes/manifests/*.yaml</code> to check all at once.</p></div></details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After editing kube-apiserver.yaml to fix a CIS failure, kubectl stops working. You can't run any commands. What happened and how do you recover?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>You made a syntax error in the manifest (wrong YAML indentation, invalid flag name, or missing value). The API server crashed and can't restart. <strong>Lesson:</strong> ALWAYS backup before editing static pod manifests. Recovery happens entirely via the node's own filesystem — never via <code>kubectl</code>, because the API server is the thing that's down:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. SSH directly to the control plane node — kubectl cannot help you here
ssh controlplane1

# 2. Confirm the API server container is actually crashing (not just slow)
sudo crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver
sudo crictl logs $(sudo crictl ps -aq --name kube-apiserver | head -1)
# → error message usually names the exact bad flag/indentation problem

# 3. Restore the pre-edit backup
sudo cp ~/apiserver.bak /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

# 4. Wait for the kubelet's file watcher to notice the restored manifest
watch sudo crictl ps --name kube-apiserver

# 5. Confirm recovery
kubectl get nodes
# → cluster responds again ✅</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the #1 exam disaster scenario. The kubelet watches <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and immediately tries to parse any saved YAML. Invalid YAML → kubelet can't start the container → API server is down → kubectl is dead. Recovery MUST happen via SSH to the node. On the exam, always run <code>cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml ~/apiserver.bak</code> BEFORE making any changes.</p></div></details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does CIS check 4.2.3 test, and what is the security risk if it FAILS?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>CIS 4.2.3 checks that the kubelet's <code>--read-only-port</code> is 0 (disabled). This port (default 10255) exposes pod information WITHOUT authentication. Fix: edit <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>, set <code>readOnlyPort: 0</code>, restart kubelet. Risk if enabled: anyone who can reach the node on port 10255 can list all running pods and their metadata without any credentials.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo vi /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
# Set: readOnlyPort: 0
sudo systemctl restart kubelet</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The kubelet read-only port has a genuinely confusing default that even upstream Kubernetes documentation has an open bug tracking (<code>kubernetes/kubernetes#94550</code>): the <code>--read-only-port</code> CLI flag's documented default is <code>10255</code> (enabled), but if you configure the kubelet purely via a <code>--config</code> file (as kubeadm does) and simply omit the field, the compiled-in struct default has been <code>0</code> (disabled) since Kubernetes 1.10 — the flag and the config-file field don't actually agree on what "default" means. Some managed platforms have since made this explicit at the platform level (for example, GKE disables the read-only port by default for clusters on its newer supported versions), but there is no universal Kubernetes-wide version where it flipped off for everyone. <strong>The practical lesson is identical either way: never trust any default — explicitly set <code>readOnlyPort: 0</code> and verify with kube-bench</strong>, because plenty of real clusters (kubeadm, kops, on-prem installs, and older managed clusters) still have it explicitly or implicitly open at 10255, which is exactly why CIS 4.2.3 exists as a check at all rather than being retired as "always fixed by default." It's an unauthenticated information disclosure vulnerability — anyone on the network can query <code>/pods</code>, <code>/stats/</code>, and other endpoints. On the CKS exam, this is a high-priority fix because it bypasses all authentication.</p></div></details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">kube-bench reports [FAIL] 2.1: "Ensure that the --cert-file and --key-file arguments are set as appropriate" for etcd. What files do these flags point to, and what happens if etcd runs without TLS?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The flags in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> should point to:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">--cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt     # etcd's server cert
--key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key      # etcd's private key
--peer-cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/peer.crt  # For etcd-to-etcd communication
--peer-key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/peer.key
--trusted-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt   # CA that signed the certs</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>Without TLS:</strong> All etcd traffic (including secrets!) flows in plaintext. Anyone with network access between the API server and etcd can sniff all cluster data. Etcd peer communication is also unencrypted.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Etcd TLS is a CIS control plane requirement. The etcd static pod manifest should have both <strong>client-to-server TLS</strong> (API server → etcd) and <strong>peer TLS</strong> (etcd → etcd). On the CKS exam, if you see etcd running with <code>--cert-file</code> but missing <code>--peer-cert-file</code>, that's a finding you must fix. Remember: etcd stores every Kubernetes secret — plaintext etcd traffic is a critical data exposure risk.</p></div></details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">CIS check 4.2.6 fails: "Ensure that the --protect-kernel-defaults argument is set to true" on the kubelet. What does this flag do, and what's the remediation?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><code>--protect-kernel-defaults=true</code> prevents the kubelet from modifying kernel parameters. If a pod requests kernel tunables that differ from the node's defaults, the kubelet will refuse to start the pod instead of modifying kernel settings.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Fix: Edit kubelet config
sudo vi /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
# Add: protectKernelDefaults: true
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

# Verify
kube-bench run --targets node --check 4.2.6</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Without <code>protectKernelDefaults</code>, a pod with sufficient privileges can modify kernel parameters via sysctl — potentially disabling security features. This flag locks the kernel configuration so pods can't tamper with node-level security. It's part of the <strong>System Hardening</strong> CKS domain (15% of exam). Note: after enabling, pods that legitimately need custom sysctls must use the <code>securityContext.sysctls</code> field with only safe sysctls allowed.</p></div></details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're on a control plane node and kubectl isn't configured on your workstation. How do you run kube-bench directly on the node? Provide the exact command, and contrast it with the Job-based approach for cases where you only have kubectl access.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Binary approach (direct on node — kube-bench is pre-installed on the exam nodes)
kube-bench run --targets master

# Job approach (useful when you only have kubectl access, no SSH)
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/aquasecurity/kube-bench/main/job.yaml
kubectl logs -f job/kube-bench
kubectl delete job kube-bench</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>Use the binary directly whenever you have SSH access</strong> — it's pre-installed on the exam nodes and is the fastest path. <strong>Use the Job approach</strong> when you only have kubectl access and no SSH to the node: (1) it doesn't require SSH, (2) it runs in the correct context automatically via the container image, and (3) it's a useful fallback for auditing a cluster you don't have direct node access to.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Kube-bench can run as a binary or as a Kubernetes Job. On the CKS exam the binary is pre-installed on the nodes, so direct use (SSH + <code>kube-bench run</code>) is the normal, fastest path. The Job method is a useful fallback when you only have kubectl access — it runs a container with the kube-bench binary, targets the correct node type (master/node), and outputs results to pod logs. Either way: identify FAILs, fix them, re-run to verify PASS.</p></div></details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">CIS check 1.2.14 fails: "Ensure that the --audit-log-path argument is set." You add the flag and restart the API server, but audit logs fill the disk within hours. How do you configure <strong>audit log rotation</strong> to prevent disk exhaustion?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Add audit log rotation flags alongside <code>--audit-log-path</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># In /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml, add:
--audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log
--audit-log-maxage=30        # Retain logs for 30 days
--audit-log-maxbackup=10     # Keep 10 rotated files
--audit-log-maxsize=100      # Rotate at 100 MB
--audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml</code></pre>
                    <p>Without log rotation, audit logs grow unbounded and can fill the control plane disk, crashing the API server and etcd. Combined with an Audit Policy that filters to only security-relevant events, this prevents disk exhaustion while maintaining the audit trail.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>CIS 1.2.14 requires audit logging, but the follow-up CIS checks (1.2.15-1.2.17) require log rotation, max age, and max backup. On the CKS exam, you'll typically need to configure ALL of these together. The gotcha: log rotation parameters only work with the <strong>file-based audit backend</strong>. If you use a webhook backend, rotation must be handled by the receiving system. Also, mount <code>/var/log/kubernetes/</code> as a hostPath volume in the API server manifest so logs persist across container restarts.</p></div></details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After fixing all kube-bench FAILs, you re-run and see [WARN] instead of [PASS]. What does WARN mean, and should you ignore it on the CKS exam?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>WARN</strong> means kube-bench cannot definitively determine the state. The check might be passing or failing — the tool couldn't gather enough information. Common causes: the configuration file was not found at the expected path, the flag is set but kube-bench can't verify the runtime value, or the check requires manual inspection.</p>
                    <p><strong>Do NOT ignore WARNs on the CKS exam.</strong> Investigate each WARN using the specific pattern that matches its cause:</p>
                    <div class="comparison-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>WARN Cause</th><th>Investigation Command</th><th>Exam Verdict</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Config file not found at kube-bench's expected path</td><td><code>ps aux | grep kubelet | grep -o 'config=[^ ]*'</code> — find the REAL path</td><td>Re-check the flag manually at the real path; likely still a FAIL if truly unset</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Flag present but value ambiguous to kube-bench</td><td><code>kube-bench run --check=&lt;id&gt; -v</code> — verbose mode shows the exact audit command it ran</td><td>Manually confirm the value is correct, then document as a verified false-positive WARN</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Check is <code>type: manual</code> — structurally can never auto-PASS</td><td><code>grep -A5 "id: &lt;check&gt;" /etc/kube-bench/cfg/*/master.yaml</code></td><td>Always WARN regardless of config — requires human judgment, not a tool fix</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>Some WARNs are unavoidable but you must demonstrate you investigated them:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Example exam-notes documentation for a verified false-positive WARN
# WARN 4.2.3: kubelet config at /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml — kube-bench
# checked /etc/kubernetes/kubelet.conf (wrong path for this distro).
# Manually verified: readOnlyPort: 0 is set correctly. Treating as PASS.</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The CKS exam scoring is nuanced. A FAIL needs to be fixed to PASS. A WARN that you investigate and confirm is actually passing IS acceptable — but only if you document it. Best practice: for every WARN, write a one-liner comment in your exam notes explaining why it's a false positive. Example: <code># WARN 4.2.3: kubelet config at /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml, kube-bench checked /etc/kubernetes/kubelet.conf — wrong path, manually verified</code>.</p></div></details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're hardening a worker node and need to fix these kubelet CIS failures: (a) anonymous-auth is true, (b) authorization-mode is AlwaysAllow, (c) readOnlyPort is 10255. Write the complete kubelet configuration YAML that fixes all three and the restart command.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
apiVersion: kubelet.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: KubeletConfiguration
authentication:
  anonymous:
    enabled: false          # Fixes (a) — no anonymous access
  webhook:
    enabled: true           # Enables API server to validate kubelet requests
  x509:
    clientCAFile: /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt
authorization:
  mode: Webhook             # Fixes (b) — delegates authz to API server
readOnlyPort: 0             # Fixes (c) — disables unauthenticated port
protectKernelDefaults: true # Bonus: prevent kernel tuning by pods
rotateCertificates: true    # Auto-rotate kubelet client certificates</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Restart kubelet to apply
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart kubelet
sudo systemctl status kubelet

# Verify the fixes
kube-bench run --targets node --check 4.2.1,4.2.2,4.2.3</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This question combines multiple CIS checks into one task — exactly how the CKS exam works. You're given a cluster with multiple findings and must fix them all. Key points: (a) <code>authorization-mode: AlwaysAllow</code> is the default and means the kubelet accepts ALL requests — this is catastrophic. (b) <code>Webhook</code> mode means the kubelet calls the API server's SubjectAccessReview API for every request. (c) The config file is at <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> on kubeadm clusters — but on the exam, check with <code>ps aux | grep kubelet | grep config</code> to find the actual path.</p></div></details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="cks-ch4-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 4 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (8)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — a broken cluster state, and you produce the fix with real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You SSH into a fresh worker node and run <code>kube-bench run --targets=master</code>. Almost every check reports <code>[FAIL]</code> or <code>[WARN]</code> — including ones you're SURE are configured correctly on the control plane. What went wrong, and what's the correct command?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>You ran a <strong>master-targeted</strong> audit on a <strong>worker node</strong>. kube-bench reads local files — <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code> does not exist on a worker, so every API-server, etcd, and controller-manager check fails to find its input and reports FAIL/WARN by default, regardless of the control plane's real configuration.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Confirm which role this node actually has
kubectl get nodes -o wide
# 2. Run the CORRECT target for THIS node
kube-bench run --targets=node          # worker-appropriate checks (kubelet, kube-proxy, OS)
# 3. Run master checks only on an actual control-plane node
ssh controlplane1
kube-bench run --targets=master</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is Pitfall 1 from §4.2 made real: kube-bench has no awareness of "the cluster" — it only inspects the filesystem and processes of the machine it runs on. Always confirm the node's role with <code>kubectl get nodes -o wide</code> (look at the <code>ROLES</code> column) before picking a <code>--targets</code> value, and run <code>master</code>/<code>etcd</code> checks only where those components actually live.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You add three new flags to <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code> to fix a batch of FAILs, save the file, and now every <code>kubectl</code> command hangs with "connection refused." You made a backup before editing. Walk through the exact recovery.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Confirm the apiserver container is actually crash-looping (not just slow)
sudo crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver
sudo crictl logs $(sudo crictl ps -aq --name kube-apiserver | head -1)
# → error usually names the exact bad flag, e.g. "unknown flag: --auditlog-path"

# 2. Restore your pre-edit backup
sudo cp /root/kube-apiserver.yaml.backup /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

# 3. Wait for the kubelet's SyncLoop to notice the restored file (poll or inotify)
watch sudo crictl ps --name kube-apiserver

# 4. Confirm recovery
kubectl get nodes

# 5. Fix your typo in a SCRATCH copy, re-verify the YAML, THEN reapply it —
#    never edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ directly a second time without
#    validating the flag names first
grep -A2 '\- --audit' /root/kube-apiserver.yaml.backup</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A single mistyped flag name (<code>--auditlog-path</code> instead of <code>--audit-log-path</code>) makes the kube-apiserver binary refuse to start entirely — it's not a "warning," it's a hard crash on every attempt, so the kubelet retries forever (CrashLoopBackOff). Because static pods are read from disk, not the API, recovery never touches <code>kubectl</code> — you always restore via the filesystem on the node itself. This is exactly why §4.3's backup step is non-negotiable.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You edit <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>, set <code>anonymous: enabled: false</code>, run <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code>, and re-run kube-bench — CIS 4.2.1 is still <code>[FAIL]</code>. The YAML syntax is valid and correctly indented. Diagnose and fix.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Check what flag the RUNNING kubelet process actually has (this always wins)
ps aux | grep kubelet | tr ' ' '\n' | grep anonymous-auth
# → --anonymous-auth=true    (a CLI flag is overriding your config.yaml edit)

# 2. Find which systemd drop-in sets it
grep -rl "anonymous-auth" /etc/systemd/system/kubelet.service.d/
# → /etc/systemd/system/kubelet.service.d/10-kubeadm.conf

# 3. Remove or correct the flag in THAT file, not config.yaml
sudo vi /etc/systemd/system/kubelet.service.d/10-kubeadm.conf
# delete or change:  --anonymous-auth=true

# 4. Reload systemd's unit cache AND restart (daemon-reload is required for drop-in changes)
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

# 5. Re-verify from the process table, then kube-bench
ps aux | grep kubelet | grep -o 'anonymous-auth=[a-z]*'
kube-bench run --targets node --check 4.2.1</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Command-line flags always outrank <code>--config</code> file settings for the SAME field (see this chapter's Under the Hood, deep-dive ③) — <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code> alone does nothing to fix this because you restarted the kubelet with the SAME winning CLI flag still in place. You must edit the systemd drop-in file itself, then <code>daemon-reload</code> (systemd needs to re-read the unit file) before <code>restart</code> picks up the change.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">kube-bench reports <code>[FAIL] 1.2.22 Ensure that the --audit-policy-file argument is set</code> even though you already added <code>--audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</code> and logs ARE being written. What's missing, and what happens if you skip this fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><code>--audit-log-path</code> only tells the API server WHERE to write logs — it does not tell it WHAT to log. Without <code>--audit-policy-file</code>, the API server uses no policy at all and audit logging is effectively a no-op destination with nothing routed to it (behavior varies by version, but the CIS check treats "no policy file" as a hard FAIL regardless).</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Create a minimal audit policy (log metadata for everything, more detail for Secrets)
sudo tee /etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml &gt;/dev/null &lt;&lt;'EOF'
apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1
kind: Policy
rules:
  - level: RequestResponse
    resources:
      - group: ""
        resources: ["secrets"]
  - level: Metadata
    resources:
      - group: ""
EOF

# 2. Mount it into the API server manifest and add the flag
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
#   volumeMounts: - name: audit-policy, mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml, readOnly: true
#   volumes:      - name: audit-policy, hostPath: {path: /etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml, type: File}
#   command:      - --audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml

# 3. Verify
kube-bench run --targets master --check 1.2.22</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--audit-log-path</code> and <code>--audit-policy-file</code> are a PAIR — the exam frequently tests only one half being present to see if you notice. A static pod manifest change also needs the matching <code>volumes</code>/<code>volumeMounts</code> entries or the container simply can't see the file path you referenced, producing a different failure (container CrashLoopBackOff with "no such file or directory") instead of the CIS check passing.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">CIS 1.2.9 fails: NodeRestriction is missing from <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code>. The current flag already reads <code>--enable-admission-plugins=NamespaceLifecycle,ServiceAccount,PodSecurity</code>. Add NodeRestriction WITHOUT breaking the existing plugins.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># WRONG — this REPLACES the whole list and disables everything else:
- --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction

# RIGHT — APPEND to the existing comma-separated list, don't overwrite it:
- --enable-admission-plugins=NamespaceLifecycle,ServiceAccount,PodSecurity,NodeRestriction

# Verify the full list is intact, not just NodeRestriction alone
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-apiserver -o yaml | grep enable-admission-plugins
kube-bench run --targets master --check 1.2.9</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> takes ONE comma-separated value, not repeatable flags — writing the flag a second time with just <code>NodeRestriction</code> silently REPLACES the first occurrence rather than merging with it (the last <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> in the command array wins, the others are simply ignored). This is a common self-inflicted regression: fixing check 1.2.9 while accidentally disabling ServiceAccount or PodSecurity admission that was already correctly enabled.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">kube-bench reports <code>[FAIL] 2.7 Ensure that the etcd data directory ownership is set correctly</code>. <code>stat</code> shows <code>750 root:etcd /var/lib/etcd</code>. Fix it — and explain why picking the wrong owner can make etcd fail to start.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. ALWAYS check which user the etcd PROCESS actually runs as first
ps -ef | grep [e]tcd | awk '{print $1}'
root                      # kubeadm's default: etcd runs as root inside its static pod

# 2. Fix ownership to MATCH that user, and lock permissions to 700
sudo chown root:root /var/lib/etcd
sudo chmod 700 /var/lib/etcd

# 3. Verify
stat -c "%a %U:%G %n" /var/lib/etcd     # 700 root:root /var/lib/etcd  ✅
kube-bench run --targets etcd --check 2.7</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>If you <code>chown</code> the data directory to a user OTHER than the one etcd actually runs as (e.g. blindly running <code>chown etcd:etcd</code> on a kubeadm cluster where etcd runs as root), the running process loses read/write access to its own data directory on the next restart and etcd fails to start — turning a compliance fix into a full cluster outage, since the API server cannot function without etcd. Always confirm the real running user with <code>ps -ef | grep etcd</code> before choosing an owner, exactly as §4.6 Row 12's explanation warns.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">kube-bench shows <code>[WARN] 1.2.6</code> for <code>--authorization-mode</code>. Running it verbose (<code>-v</code>) reveals the actual flag value is <code>Node,RBAC,AlwaysAllow</code>. Fix the flag, and explain why kube-bench reported WARN instead of a clean FAIL here.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Edit the API server manifest — remove ONLY the AlwaysAllow segment
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
# change:  --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC,AlwaysAllow
# to:      --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC

# Wait for the static pod auto-restart, then verify
watch sudo crictl ps --name kube-apiserver
kube-bench run --targets master --check 1.2.6</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This check often reports WARN rather than a hard FAIL because kube-bench's <code>has</code>/<code>nothave</code> comparison operators can only confirm "Node and RBAC are present" — evaluating whether an EXTRA, dangerous value like <code>AlwaysAllow</code> is ALSO present in the same comma-separated flag sometimes needs the verbose/manual read to be certain, which is exactly why §4.2 tells you never to treat WARN as "probably fine." <code>AlwaysAllow</code> anywhere in the chain grants every request unconditionally — removing it is not optional regardless of what the automated result said.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>[FAIL] 1.2.21 Ensure that the --profiling argument is set to false</code>. You add <code>--profiling=false</code> to the API server manifest, but you also want to confirm no OTHER debug-style endpoint is exposed on this cluster. What do you check, and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Fix the flagged check
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
# add:  --profiling=false

# 2. While you're in the manifest, also confirm the LEGACY insecure port is
#    truly gone (removed in modern K8s, but worth a defensive check on old clusters)
grep -E 'insecure-port|insecure-bind-address' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
# → should return NOTHING at all

# 3. Confirm profiling is closed on ALL control-plane components, not just the API server
grep profiling /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml
grep profiling /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml

# 4. Re-verify
kube-bench run --targets master --check 1.2.21</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--profiling</code> exists on the scheduler and controller-manager too (CIS 1.3.2 / 1.4.1) — fixing it on the API server alone leaves the same <code>/debug/pprof/</code> attack surface open on the other two components, which kube-bench checks as SEPARATE line items. "Fix the one check the exam mentioned" is a trap if you stop there; the underlying security principle (no unnecessary debug endpoints) applies to every control-plane binary, not just the one named in the FAIL message.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

