                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What flag MUST be removed from the Kubernetes Dashboard deployment to prevent unauthenticated access, and what attack does leaving it enabled allow?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><code>--enable-skip-login</code> must be removed (or set to false). When enabled, anyone who can reach the dashboard URL gets a "Skip" button that grants them the dashboard's own ServiceAccount privileges. If that SA has cluster-admin (which was the default in early versions), the attacker gets FULL cluster control with one click. This is how the Tesla cryptojacking attack began.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Detect it — read the running Deployment's actual args
kubectl get deploy kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard \
    -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].args}'
# → [...,"--enable-skip-login=true"]   ← this is the flag to remove

# Remove it via a JSON patch on the exact array index (see Drill Q3 for the full sequence)
kubectl patch deploy kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard --type=json \
    -p='[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/args/2"}]'
kubectl rollout status deploy/kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard</code></pre>
                    <div class="diagram-container">
                        <div class="diagram-title">The one-flag attack chain this single removal closes</div>
<pre>
  --enable-skip-login=true present on the Dashboard Deployment
        │
        ▼
  Attacker opens the Dashboard URL → clicks the "Skip" button
        │
        ▼
  Browser session assumes the Dashboard's OWN ServiceAccount identity
  — zero credentials presented, zero exploit used
        │
        ▼
  If that SA = cluster-admin (Tesla's default) → full cluster control, instantly
</pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The Tesla 2018 breach is the canonical example: attackers used Shodan to find exposed dashboards with <code>--enable-skip-login</code>, clicked "Skip", and deployed crypto-mining pods using the dashboard's cluster-admin ServiceAccount. On CKS, never deploy the dashboard with skip-login enabled. If you find it in an exam cluster, remove the flag immediately and restart the dashboard pod.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to give a developer read-only dashboard access to the easypj-prod namespace. Write the exact RBAC resources and ServiceAccount needed.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: dashboard-viewer
  namespace: easypj-prod
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: dashboard-readonly
  namespace: easypj-prod
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods", "services", "configmaps"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: dashboard-viewer-binding
  namespace: easypj-prod
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: dashboard-viewer
  namespace: easypj-prod
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: dashboard-readonly
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
# Get token: kubectl create token dashboard-viewer -n easypj-prod</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The dashboard authenticates via Bearer Token. Each user gets a unique ServiceAccount with scoped RBAC. The token is obtained via <code>kubectl create token &lt;sa&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> (K8s 1.24+). Never share tokens between users. The dashboard shows only what the ServiceAccount's RBAC allows — namespace-scoped Role for namespace-scoped access, ClusterRole for cluster-wide access. On the exam, always use Roles (not ClusterRoles) for dashboard access unless the task explicitly requires cluster-wide visibility.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How would you prevent external access to the dashboard while still allowing internal cluster administrators to use it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(1) Do NOT expose the dashboard via Ingress or LoadBalancer. Use <code>kubectl proxy</code> for local access: <code>kubectl proxy --port=8001</code> → access at <code>http://localhost:8001/api/v1/namespaces/kubernetes-dashboard/services/https:kubernetes-dashboard:/proxy/</code>. (2) If Ingress is required, add IP whitelist annotations and NetworkPolicy that only allows ingress from specific admin IPs. (3) Always require authentication — never use --enable-skip-login.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Local-only access — the CKS-preferred pattern, zero network exposure
kubectl proxy --port=8001
# → open: http://localhost:8001/api/v1/namespaces/kubernetes-dashboard/services/https:kubernetes-dashboard:/proxy/
# Binds to 127.0.0.1 only. Nothing outside THIS machine can ever reach it.</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># If remote admin access is genuinely unavoidable — restrict it to admin IPs, never leave it open
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: dashboard-admin-only
  namespace: kubernetes-dashboard
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      k8s-app: kubernetes-dashboard
  policyTypes: ["Ingress"]
  ingress:
  - from:
    - ipBlock: { cidr: 10.0.0.100/32 }    # admin workstation only
    ports:
    - port: 8443
  # Everything not explicitly listed above is denied — including any Ingress path.</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The safest way to use the dashboard is via <code>kubectl proxy</code> because: (a) it only listens on localhost (127.0.0.1), (b) it uses your kubeconfig credentials automatically (no token needed), and (c) it's ephemeral — it stops when you close the terminal. On the CKS exam, if asked to secure dashboard access, the answer is always <code>kubectl proxy</code> unless the task specifically requires remote access with OIDC.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the minimum RBAC permission a dashboard ServiceAccount needs to function, and why should you NEVER use cluster-admin?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Minimum: read-only access to pods, deployments, services, configmaps, and events in the specific namespaces the user needs to view. NEVER cluster-admin because: if the dashboard pod is compromised (e.g., via an XSS vulnerability in the dashboard UI), the attacker inherits the dashboard SA's permissions. cluster-admin = attacker gets full cluster control. Least privilege = attacker gets read-only view of one namespace.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role                        # namespace-scoped — prefer this over ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: dashboard-minimum
  namespace: easypj-prod
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods", "services", "configmaps", "events"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]        # read-only — no create/update/delete/patch
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
# NOTE: "secrets" is deliberately absent from every rule above.</code></pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>If Dashboard Pod Is Compromised (XSS / dependency CVE)</th><th>Attacker Inherits</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>SA bound to <code>cluster-admin</code></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">Every verb, every resource, every namespace — total cluster takeover</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>SA bound to <code>dashboard-minimum</code> above</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">Read-only view of one namespace's pods/services/configmaps/events/deployments — no Secrets, no writes, no other namespaces</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the principle of blast radius limitation. Every identity in Kubernetes — human or machine — gets the absolute minimum permissions. The dashboard is just another pod; if it's compromised, its ServiceAccount's RBAC is the attacker's ceiling. On the CKS exam, when configuring dashboard access, start with read-only verbs (get, list, watch) and only add write verbs (create, update, delete) if explicitly required by the task.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to configure the Kubernetes Dashboard with <strong>OIDC authentication</strong> so users log in via your corporate identity provider (e.g., Azure AD, Okta). What API server flags must be set, and how does the dashboard use them?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>API server OIDC flags in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">--oidc-issuer-url=https://login.microsoftonline.com/&lt;tenant-id&gt;/v2.0
--oidc-client-id=&lt;dashboard-client-id&gt;
--oidc-username-claim=email
--oidc-groups-claim=groups
--oidc-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/oidc-ca.crt</code></pre>
                    <p>The dashboard redirects users to the OIDC provider's login page. After authentication, the provider returns an ID token. The dashboard uses this token as the Bearer token for API server requests. The API server validates the token against the OIDC provider and extracts username/groups for RBAC. No static ServiceAccount tokens needed — every user is a real identity.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>OIDC is the recommended authentication method for production clusters because it integrates with existing identity systems. Each user gets their own identity (not shared ServiceAccounts), enabling proper RBAC and audit trails. On the CKS exam, you may need to verify OIDC flags are set correctly. Key point: the API server does NOT contact the OIDC provider on every request — it validates the JWT signature locally using the provider's public keys. It only contacts the provider during initial discovery.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You discover the Kubernetes Dashboard running in <code>kubernetes-dashboard</code> namespace with a Service of type LoadBalancer. What's the security risk, and what's the NetworkPolicy you'd apply to restrict dashboard access to only the corporate VPN IP range 10.0.0.0/8?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Risk:</strong> LoadBalancer exposes the dashboard to the public internet. Combined with weak/no authentication, this gives anyone worldwide a graphical UI to your cluster.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: dashboard-vpn-only
  namespace: kubernetes-dashboard
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      k8s-app: kubernetes-dashboard
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  ingress:
  - from:
    - ipBlock:
        cidr: 10.0.0.0/8       # Corporate VPN range
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 8443</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Even with the LoadBalancer still in place, this NetworkPolicy adds a critical layer: the CNI plugin drops ALL packets that don't match, before they even reach the dashboard pod. Defense in depth: the cloud firewall blocks external access, but NetworkPolicy provides a second enforcement point at the pod network level. On the CKS exam, always combine multiple controls — never rely on just one layer.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After deploying the dashboard, you notice it's running with <code>automountServiceAccountToken: true</code> (default). The dashboard pod does NOT need to make API calls on its own behalf — users provide their own tokens. What change should you make and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> on the dashboard pod spec or its ServiceAccount. The dashboard itself does not need a mounted token — users authenticate with their OWN tokens. A mounted token is an unnecessary attack surface: if an attacker compromises the dashboard pod (XSS, dependency vulnerability), they can steal the mounted token and use its RBAC permissions.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: kubernetes-dashboard
  namespace: kubernetes-dashboard
automountServiceAccountToken: false</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the "zero standing privileges" principle. The dashboard pod itself needs ZERO Kubernetes API access — it's a proxy that forwards user tokens. By disabling automount, even a complete dashboard compromise yields the attacker no Kubernetes credentials. On the CKS exam, always check whether a pod actually needs API access before allowing token mounting. If the pod only serves HTTP and doesn't call <code>kubectl</code> or the Kubernetes API, disable the token.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're auditing dashboard security and need to verify that NO ServiceAccount token has cluster-admin in the <code>kubernetes-dashboard</code> namespace. What commands do you run?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. List all ServiceAccounts in dashboard namespace
kubectl get sa -n kubernetes-dashboard

# 2. Check each SA's permissions
kubectl auth can-i --list -n kubernetes-dashboard \
    --as=system:serviceaccount:kubernetes-dashboard:kubernetes-dashboard

# 3. Find all ClusterRoleBindings referencing dashboard SAs
kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o json | jq '.items[] |
    select(.subjects[]?.namespace=="kubernetes-dashboard") |
    {binding: .metadata.name, role: .roleRef.name, subjects: .subjects}'

# 4. Find all RoleBindings in the dashboard namespace
kubectl get rolebindings -n kubernetes-dashboard -o wide

# 5. Check if dashboard SA can do anything dangerous
kubectl auth can-i create pods --as=system:serviceaccount:kubernetes-dashboard:kubernetes-dashboard
kubectl auth can-i delete secrets --as=system:serviceaccount:kubernetes-dashboard:kubernetes-dashboard</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This audit pattern is exactly what you'd do on the CKS exam. The key insight: ClusterRoleBindings are NOT namespace-scoped — even if the dashboard SA is in the <code>kubernetes-dashboard</code> namespace, a ClusterRoleBinding tied to that SA gives it cluster-wide power. Always check BOTH RoleBindings AND ClusterRoleBindings. The <code>kubectl auth can-i --as=</code> pattern is the fastest way to check what an attacker could do with a stolen token.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to enable TLS for the Kubernetes Dashboard. The dashboard provides its own self-signed certificate by default. What are the security implications, and how would you replace it with a proper certificate signed by the cluster's CA?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Self-signed cert risks:</strong> (1) Man-in-the-middle attacks — anyone can create a self-signed cert claiming to be the dashboard, (2) Browser warnings that train users to ignore security warnings, (3) No trust chain — you can't verify the dashboard's identity.</p>
                    <p><strong>Replace with cluster CA-signed cert:</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Generate a certificate signed by the cluster CA
openssl req -new -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes \
    -keyout dashboard.key -out dashboard.csr \
    -subj "/CN=kubernetes-dashboard.kube-system.svc"

openssl x509 -req -in dashboard.csr \
    -CA /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt \
    -CAkey /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.key \
    -CAcreateserial -out dashboard.crt -days 365

# Create TLS secret
kubectl create secret tls dashboard-tls \
    --cert=dashboard.crt --key=dashboard.key \
    -n kubernetes-dashboard

# Mount in dashboard deployment
# args: --tls-cert-file=/certs/dashboard.crt --tls-key-file=/certs/dashboard.key</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Using the cluster's CA to sign the dashboard certificate ensures that any client who trusts the cluster's CA (which includes all cluster components) will trust the dashboard. The CN should match the dashboard's Service DNS name: <code>&lt;service&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc</code>. On the CKS exam, TLS certificate management is tested — you should know how to generate, sign, and mount certificates as Kubernetes Secrets.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The CKS exam scenario: "A previous administrator deployed the Kubernetes Dashboard. You've been asked to secure it." Walk through your systematic approach — what do you check and in what order?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="process-steps vertical">
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Find it</h5><p><code>kubectl get pods,svc -A | grep dashboard</code> — locate the namespace, deployment, and service type.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Check exposure</h5><p><code>kubectl get svc -n kubernetes-dashboard</code> — if type=LoadBalancer or has external IP, it's exposed. Check Ingress: <code>kubectl get ingress -A</code>.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Check authentication</h5><p><code>kubectl get deploy kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard -o yaml | grep skip-login</code> — if present, REMOVE IT.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Check SA permissions</h5><p><code>kubectl auth can-i --list -n kubernetes-dashboard --as=system:serviceaccount:kubernetes-dashboard:kubernetes-dashboard</code></p></div></div>
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Apply NetworkPolicy</h5><p>Restrict ingress to dashboard pod from trusted sources only.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Verify</h5><p>Try accessing without auth → should fail. Access with valid token → works. Check audit logs for dashboard events.</p></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This systematic approach is how CKS exam tasks are structured. The exam doesn't just ask one question — it gives you a scenario and expects you to work through it methodically. The order matters: first find what you're securing, then check exposure (can attackers reach it?), then authentication (can attackers bypass it?), then authorization (what can attackers do with it?), then network controls (limit blast radius), then verify everything works.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 5 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (8)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — a broken cluster state, and you produce the fix with real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You inherit a cluster where <code>kubectl get svc -n kubernetes-dashboard</code> shows <code>TYPE=NodePort</code> with <code>31443</code> exposed on every node. Fix it to <code>ClusterIP</code> using a patch (not a full re-apply), and prove no node port remains reachable.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Patch the Service type in place
kubectl patch svc kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard \
    -p '{"spec":{"type":"ClusterIP"},"spec":{"ports":[{"port":443,"targetPort":8443}]}}'
# NOTE: patching type alone is usually enough — nodePort is dropped automatically
# once type is no longer NodePort/LoadBalancer.

# 2. Verify the Service
kubectl get svc kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard
# TYPE must now read ClusterIP, no PORT(S) showing a NodePort range value

# 3. Prove the old node port is dead — from OUTSIDE the cluster
curl -k --max-time 3 https://&lt;any-node-ip&gt;:31443
# → curl: (28) Connection timed out, or (7) Connection refused</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Changing <code>type</code> away from NodePort automatically deallocates the node port on every node — you don't need to unset it separately. On a cloud provider, changing away from LoadBalancer additionally triggers deletion of the cloud load balancer, which can take a minute to fully release the public IP; always re-verify with an external <code>curl</code> after the patch, not just <code>kubectl get svc</code>, since the Kubernetes object can show ClusterIP before the cloud side has actually finished tearing down the old endpoint.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The Dashboard's ServiceAccount is bound to <code>cluster-admin</code> via a ClusterRoleBinding you didn't create. Find it, remove it, and replace it with a least-privilege binding — using only <code>kubectl</code> commands.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Find every ClusterRoleBinding that references the dashboard SA
kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o json | jq -r '.items[] |
    select(.subjects[]?.namespace=="kubernetes-dashboard") |
    .metadata.name'
# → e.g. "kubernetes-dashboard-cluster-admin"

# 2. Confirm it really grants cluster-admin before deleting
kubectl get clusterrolebinding kubernetes-dashboard-cluster-admin -o jsonpath='{.roleRef.name}'
# → cluster-admin

# 3. Delete the dangerous binding
kubectl delete clusterrolebinding kubernetes-dashboard-cluster-admin

# 4. Create the least-privilege replacement
kubectl create clusterrole dashboard-readonly \
    --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods,services,configmaps,persistentvolumeclaims
kubectl create clusterrolebinding dashboard-reader-binding \
    --clusterrole=dashboard-readonly \
    --serviceaccount=kubernetes-dashboard:dashboard-reader

# 5. Prove the fix
kubectl auth can-i delete pods --as=system:serviceaccount:kubernetes-dashboard:dashboard-reader
# → no
kubectl auth can-i get secrets --as=system:serviceaccount:kubernetes-dashboard:dashboard-reader
# → no</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Never assume "the dashboard SA is namespaced, so its risk is namespaced" — a ClusterRoleBinding ignores namespaces entirely and applies cluster-wide regardless of which namespace the ServiceAccount lives in. Always search ClusterRoleBindings first when auditing any SA's real power; a RoleBinding-only search will miss exactly this kind of finding.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You find <code>--enable-skip-login=true</code> in the running Dashboard Deployment's container args. Patch it out in place (don't delete/recreate the Deployment), confirm the rollout completes, and confirm the "Skip" button is gone.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Inspect current args to get the exact index/value
kubectl get deploy kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard \
    -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].args}'
# → ["--auto-generate-certificates","--namespace=kubernetes-dashboard","--enable-skip-login=true"]

# 2. Replace the ENTIRE args array via strategic merge patch (safest for a list field)
kubectl patch deploy kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard --type=json -p='[
  {"op": "remove", "path": "/spec/template/spec/containers/0/args/2"}
]'
# (index 2 = the --enable-skip-login=true entry found in step 1)

# 3. Watch the rollout
kubectl rollout status deploy/kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard

# 4. Confirm the flag is gone from the NEW pod
kubectl get pod -n kubernetes-dashboard -l k8s-app=kubernetes-dashboard \
    -o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.containers[0].args}' | grep -c skip-login
# → 0

# 5. Confirm visually / functionally: open the Dashboard URL —
#    the "Skip" button must no longer be present on the login page.</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A JSON patch with <code>"op": "remove"</code> on an exact array index is the precise way to delete ONE flag from a Deployment's <code>args</code> list without rewriting the whole array by hand (which risks typos on a list you can't easily diff). Always re-read the args first — the index of the flag you want to remove shifts if someone has added or removed other args since the manifest was last edited.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The <code>dashboard-readonly</code> ClusterRole was created with <code>resources: ["pods","services","configmaps","secrets"]</code> — Secrets should never have been included. Fix the RBAC object directly and prove the SA can no longer read Secrets.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Confirm the over-broad rule
kubectl get clusterrole dashboard-readonly -o yaml | grep -A2 resources

# 2. Edit it out — either interactively...
kubectl edit clusterrole dashboard-readonly
#   remove "secrets" from the resources list, save & exit

# ...or non-interactively with a JSON patch (scriptable, exam-friendly):
kubectl patch clusterrole dashboard-readonly --type=json -p='[
  {"op":"replace","path":"/rules/0/resources","value":["pods","services","configmaps","persistentvolumeclaims"]}
]'

# 3. Verify — this MUST now return "no"
kubectl auth can-i get secrets \
    --as=system:serviceaccount:kubernetes-dashboard:dashboard-reader -A</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>RBAC edits take effect immediately — there is no restart, cache flush, or propagation delay to wait for, unlike static pod manifest changes. The moment the ClusterRole object is updated, the NEXT API request evaluated against it uses the new rule set. Always follow an RBAC edit with an immediate <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> check as living proof, rather than trusting the YAML alone.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">There is currently NO NetworkPolicy in the <code>kubernetes-dashboard</code> namespace — any pod in any namespace can reach the Dashboard Service directly. Write and apply a policy restricting ingress to only the admin CIDR <code>10.0.0.100/32</code> on port 8443, then verify from a pod in a different namespace that it now times out.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: dashboard-access
  namespace: kubernetes-dashboard
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      k8s-app: kubernetes-dashboard
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  ingress:
  - from:
    - ipBlock:
        cidr: 10.0.0.100/32
    ports:
    - port: 8443</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f dashboard-netpol.yaml

# Verify from an UNRELATED namespace/pod
kubectl run test --rm -it --image=busybox -n default -- \
    wget -T 3 -O- https://kubernetes-dashboard.kubernetes-dashboard:8443
# → wget: download timed out   (correctly blocked)</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Before this policy exists, Kubernetes' default network model is "allow all" between pods — a fresh cluster with no NetworkPolicy objects has zero pod-to-pod isolation. This is precisely CIS 5.3.2 from §5.3's audit checklist ("ensure that all Namespaces have Network Policies defined") — not to be confused with its sibling 5.3.1, which only verifies the CNI plugin is capable of enforcing NetworkPolicy at all. Order matters when writing a scoped-allow policy like this: it implicitly denies everything NOT explicitly listed under <code>ingress.from</code>, because any <code>ingress</code> policyType with at least one rule switches that pod selector from "allow all" to "deny all except what's listed."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The Dashboard's ServiceAccount currently has <code>automountServiceAccountToken: true</code> (the implicit default). Patch it to <code>false</code>, force a rollout, and confirm via <code>kubectl describe pod</code> that the new pod has no token volume mounted.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Patch the ServiceAccount
kubectl patch serviceaccount kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard \
    -p '{"automountServiceAccountToken": false}'

# 2. Force a rollout so a NEW pod picks up the change
#    (existing running pods keep their already-mounted token until replaced)
kubectl rollout restart deploy/kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard
kubectl rollout status deploy/kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard

# 3. Confirm no token volume on the NEW pod
kubectl describe pod -n kubernetes-dashboard -l k8s-app=kubernetes-dashboard | grep -A3 Mounts
# → no /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount mount listed</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Patching the ServiceAccount alone does NOT affect already-running pods — the projected token volume is injected at pod admission time and persists for the pod's lifetime. This is a common exam trap: you patch the SA, re-check the OLD pod, still see the token mounted, and wrongly conclude the fix didn't work. Always force a rollout (or wait for the natural next one) and check the NEW pod.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Exam-speed scenario: you are handed only a Dashboard URL and told "find how it's exposed and lock it down — you have 5 minutes." Give the exact triage command sequence, in order, before you touch anything.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Step 1 — find it and its exposure surface, in one breath
kubectl get pods,svc,ingress -A | grep -i dashboard

# Step 2 — check the Service type specifically (the single biggest risk factor)
kubectl get svc -n kubernetes-dashboard -o wide

# Step 3 — check for skip-login
kubectl get deploy -n kubernetes-dashboard -o yaml | grep -i skip-login

# Step 4 — check what its ServiceAccount can actually do
kubectl auth can-i --list -n kubernetes-dashboard \
    --as=system:serviceaccount:kubernetes-dashboard:$(kubectl get deploy kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.serviceAccountName}')

# Step 5 — check for an existing NetworkPolicy
kubectl get networkpolicy -n kubernetes-dashboard

# ONLY AFTER all 5 reads → start fixing, in the same order (exposure → auth → authz → network).</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Under exam time pressure, the temptation is to jump straight to fixing the first thing you notice (usually the Service type). The 5-minute triage sequence above front-loads ALL the reads before any writes, because a fix applied to the wrong root cause (e.g. tightening RBAC while the Service is still a public LoadBalancer) wastes exam minutes and can leave the actual highest-risk finding untouched. Read everything, THEN fix everything, in the same threat order this chapter teaches: exposure, authentication, authorization, network.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The Dashboard pod's <code>securityContext</code> has no <code>capabilities.drop</code> and no <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> — meaning the container can escalate privileges and retains its full default Linux capability set. Patch the Deployment's pod spec to lock this down, and confirm the new pod comes up healthy.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl patch deploy kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard --type=json -p='[
  {"op":"add","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/securityContext","value":{
      "runAsNonRoot": true,
      "runAsUser": 1001,
      "readOnlyRootFilesystem": true,
      "allowPrivilegeEscalation": false,
      "capabilities": {"drop": ["ALL"]}
  }}
]'

kubectl rollout status deploy/kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard
kubectl get pods -n kubernetes-dashboard -l k8s-app=kubernetes-dashboard
# → STATUS Running, 0 restarts</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>If the pod fails to start after this patch (CrashLoopBackOff, permission denied writing to disk), the most common cause is the missing <code>emptyDir</code> scratch volume for <code>/tmp</code> that pairs with <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> — see the hardened manifest example in §5.2. Always add the writable <code>tmp-volume</code> mount in the SAME patch, or the container-hardening fix will itself cause the outage it was meant to prevent.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-drill"></span>
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 5.1 — Sketch the "3 Independent Controls" from Memory</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 4 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>In <strong>4 minutes, from memory</strong>, name the 3 independent controls §5.2 requires for a secure Dashboard, write ONE concrete example of each, and explain what happens if exactly ONE of the three is missing while the other two remain in place.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> network isolation, authentication, authorization — and for each one, ask "if only THIS one is missing, can an attacker still get in?"</div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Concrete Example</th><th>If ONLY this one is missing</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Network isolation</td><td><code>type: ClusterIP</code> + NetworkPolicy restricting ingress to admin CIDR</td><td>An attacker on the same network segment (or a compromised pod anywhere, if NetworkPolicy is also absent) can reach the login page — but still needs a valid token/skip-login to get in</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Authentication</td><td>No <code>--enable-skip-login</code>; a valid Bearer token or OIDC login required</td><td>A skip-login button hands out full SA access with zero credentials to anyone who can reach the URL — this ALONE is what turned Tesla's exposure into a full breach</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Authorization (RBAC)</td><td>Dashboard SA scoped to read-only, no Secrets access, namespace-limited</td><td>Even with network isolation and real authentication, a compromised admin credential or session now yields cluster-admin instead of a narrow, contained blast radius</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the deepest insight is that these three are independent FAILURE MODES, not redundant layers of the same protection — losing network isolation still requires bypassing auth; losing auth still requires network reachability; losing RBAC scoping doesn't matter until one of the other two is ALSO broken. Tesla lost all three simultaneously, which is why the breach was instant and total.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 5.2 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 5.2 — Deploy, Break, and Re-Secure a Dashboard, Hands-On</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 12 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>On a disposable test cluster (kind/minikube — <strong>never production</strong>): install the official Dashboard manifest as-is, confirm it is insecure by default in the ways this chapter describes, then apply EVERY hardening control from §5.2 one at a time, re-verifying after each step, until the Security Verification Checklist from §5.2 passes completely.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> work through the checklist in §5.2 top to bottom — network exposure, external reachability, token scope, Secret access, NetworkPolicy, skip button — fixing and re-checking one at a time rather than applying everything at once and hoping.</div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 0. Install the stock manifest
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/dashboard/v2.7.0/aio/deploy/recommended.yaml

# 1. Baseline check — confirm it's ClusterIP by default on modern manifests
kubectl get svc -n kubernetes-dashboard
#   (older/custom manifests may already be NodePort — patch to ClusterIP if so)
kubectl patch svc kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard -p '{"spec":{"type":"ClusterIP"}}'

# 2. Create + apply the least-privilege SA (see §5.2's manifest)
kubectl apply -f dashboard-reader-rbac.yaml
kubectl patch deploy kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard --type=json \
    -p='[{"op":"replace","path":"/spec/template/spec/serviceAccountName","value":"dashboard-reader"}]'

# 3. Disable automount on the SA
kubectl patch serviceaccount dashboard-reader -n kubernetes-dashboard \
    -p '{"automountServiceAccountToken": false}'

# 4. Apply the NetworkPolicy restricting to your admin IP
kubectl apply -f dashboard-netpol.yaml

# 5. Re-run every check from §5.2's verification checklist:
kubectl get svc -n kubernetes-dashboard                                    # Check 1: ClusterIP
curl -k --max-time 3 https://$(minikube ip):$(kubectl get svc kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard -o jsonpath='{.spec.ports[0].nodePort}' 2>/dev/null || echo 1) # Check 2: refused/timeout
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:kubernetes-dashboard:dashboard-reader  # Check 3
kubectl auth can-i get secrets --as=system:serviceaccount:kubernetes-dashboard:dashboard-reader  # Check 4: no
kubectl run test --rm -it --image=busybox -- wget -T3 -O- https://kubernetes-dashboard.kubernetes-dashboard:8443  # Check 5: timeout
kubectl get deploy kubernetes-dashboard -n kubernetes-dashboard -o yaml | grep -i skip-login  # Check 6: empty</code></pre>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> if you fixed the checks IN ORDER and re-verified each one before moving to the next — rather than applying all four hardening YAMLs at once — you now have muscle memory for the exact incremental workflow the CKS exam rewards: prove a finding, apply ONE fix, prove the fix, move on. Applying everything at once and only checking at the end is how you fail to notice which specific YAML broke something.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 5.3 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 5.3 — Simulate the Tesla Attack Chain and Prove Each Control Blocks It</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 10 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>On the same disposable cluster: deliberately reconstruct Tesla's exact misconfiguration (NodePort Service, cluster-admin SA, no NetworkPolicy) and confirm you CAN reach it insecurely. Then add back the §5.4 breach-chain controls ONE LINK AT A TIME (in the same order as the chain diagram), and after each one, attempt the SAME attack step again to prove that specific link is now broken.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> follow the Tesla Breach Chain diagram from §5.4 top to bottom — recon/access, privilege, loot, pivot, impact, detection — adding exactly one control per step and re-attempting only that step's attack action.</div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Chain Step</th><th>Attack Action You Attempt</th><th>Control You Add</th><th>Result After Adding It</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1-2. Recon/Access</td><td><code>curl -k https://&lt;node-ip&gt;:&lt;nodeport&gt;</code> from outside the cluster</td><td>Patch Service to ClusterIP</td><td>Connection refused — no public endpoint left to find</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>3. Privilege</td><td>With a valid (still cluster-admin) token, run <code>kubectl auth can-i delete secrets --as=...</code></td><td>Rebind SA to <code>dashboard-readonly</code> ClusterRole</td><td>Returns "no" — token is now read-only</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>4. Loot</td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i get secrets --as=...</code></td><td>Remove <code>secrets</code> from the read-only ClusterRole's resources</td><td>Returns "no" — Secrets are unreachable even for a read-only identity</td></tr>
