            <section class="section-block" id="s6-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.1 The Default: All Pods Can Talk to All Pods</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes was designed with a <strong>flat network model</strong>: every pod gets a unique, cluster-routable IP address, and every pod can reach every other pod without NAT. This was a deliberate design choice to simplify pod-to-pod communication — no port mapping, no manual routing, no network segmentation. But it comes with a devastating security implication: <strong>there are zero network barriers between pods</strong>. One compromised pod means lateral movement to every other pod in the cluster.</p>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 The Flat Network Is the Default — Not a Bug</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes does not ship with any NetworkPolicies. There is no default-deny rule. There is no namespace isolation. Every pod, in every namespace, can initiate a TCP connection to every other pod, on every port. <strong>This is not a misconfiguration — it is the intended default.</strong> The CKS exam tests whether you know to override this default with explicit NetworkPolicies.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Flat Network vs Zero Trust — Side by Side</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="split-side split-bad">
                        <h5>❌ Flat Network (Default — No NetworkPolicies)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>easypj pod → postgres pod ✅</li>
                            <li>easypj pod → redis pod ✅</li>
                            <li>postgres pod → easypj pod ✅ (reverse!)</li>
                            <li>monitoring pod → any pod ✅</li>
                            <li>pod in other namespace → postgres ✅</li>
                            <li>attacker pod → ANY pod, ANY port ✅</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-weight:700;">RESULT: One compromised pod = access to EVERY pod in the cluster.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="split-side split-good">
                        <h5>✅ Zero Trust (With NetworkPolicies)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>easypj pod → postgres:5432 ✅</li>
                            <li>ingress → easypj:8000 ✅</li>
                            <li>ALL pods → DNS:53 ✅</li>
                            <li>postgres pod → easypj pod ❌</li>
                            <li>attacker pod → postgres ❌</li>
                            <li>pod in other namespace → anything ❌</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="color:#4ade80;font-weight:700;">RESULT: Compromised pod trapped in its own network bubble. Lateral movement blocked.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Why Kubernetes Chose a Flat Network — The Design Philosophy</h4>
                <p>The flat network is not an oversight — it is a deliberate architectural choice rooted in Kubernetes' origin at Google. The design principle is: <strong>every pod should be able to reach every other pod without NAT, without port mapping, and without manual route configuration.</strong> This simplifies development — a pod in namespace A can call a service in namespace B using its cluster DNS name, and it just works. The trade-off — security — was deferred to NetworkPolicies. The CKS exam tests whether you understand that this trade-off exists and that it is YOUR responsibility to apply the security layer that Kubernetes intentionally left open.</p>

                <h4>How Attackers Exploit the Flat Network — Lateral Movement in Practice</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Lateral Movement Without NetworkPolicies — The Attacker's Playbook</div>
<pre>
  ATTACKER compromises easypj pod via CVE in Pillow library
  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  $ id
  uid=0(root)  ← Container runs as root (default)

  STEP 1: DISCOVERY — What else is on this network?
  $ env | grep _SERVICE_HOST          # K8s injects service env vars
  POSTGRES_SERVICE_HOST=10.96.45.12   ← Found postgres!
  REDIS_SERVICE_HOST=10.96.78.34      ← Found redis!

  STEP 2: DATABASE ACCESS
  $ apt-get install -y postgresql-client
  $ psql -h $POSTGRES_SERVICE_HOST -U easypj_user -d jobpost
  → 45,000 user emails extracted. Zero network barriers.

  STEP 3: CACHE POISONING
  $ redis-cli -h $REDIS_SERVICE_HOST
  redis> KEYS *
  → All session tokens. All cached data available.

  STEP 4: CROSS-NAMESPACE ESCALATION
  $ for ip in 10.96.0.{1..254}; do curl -s --connect-timeout 1 http://$ip:9090; done
  → Found Prometheus and Elasticsearch in other namespaces.

  RESULT: One pod → database → cache → monitoring stack → complete cluster visibility.
  All because there were ZERO NetworkPolicies.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §6.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s6-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.2 NetworkPolicy Fundamentals</h3>
                <p>A NetworkPolicy is a <strong>namespaced Kubernetes resource</strong> that defines which network traffic is allowed to and from pods. It uses label selectors to identify target pods and rules to specify allowed traffic sources, destinations, and ports. Crucially, NetworkPolicies are <strong>additive</strong> — if multiple policies select the same pod, the effective rule set is the union of all allows. And once a pod is selected by ANY NetworkPolicy, <strong>all traffic is denied except what is explicitly allowed</strong>.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 CNI Dependency — Your CNI Plugin MUST Support NetworkPolicy</h5>
                    <p>NetworkPolicy resources are enforced by the CNI plugin, not by the API server. If your CNI does not support NetworkPolicy (Flannel, Kindnet), your policies will be <strong>silently ignored</strong> — you can apply them, kubectl will show them, but they will have ZERO effect on actual traffic. Always verify your CNI: <strong>Calico and Cilium</strong> fully support NetworkPolicy. Weave Net supports it. Flannel does not. On the CKS exam, the cluster has a compatible CNI pre-configured — you do not need to install one.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>NetworkPolicy Anatomy — Annotated Field by Field</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Every field of a NetworkPolicy, explained</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: NetworkPolicy
  metadata:
    name: postgres-netpol
    namespace: easypj-prod              # NetworkPolicy is NAMESPACED
  spec:
    podSelector:                         # WHICH PODS this policy applies to
      matchLabels:
        app: postgres                    # Selects pods with label app=postgres
    policyTypes:                         # WHAT DIRECTION of traffic
    - Ingress                            # Incoming traffic to the selected pods
    - Egress                             # Outgoing traffic FROM the selected pods
    ingress:                             # RULES for incoming traffic
    - from:                              # Traffic FROM which sources?
      - podSelector:                     # FROM pods with specific labels
          matchLabels:
            app: easypj                  # Only pods labeled app=easypj
      - namespaceSelector:              # FROM pods in specific namespaces
          matchLabels:
            kubernetes.io/metadata.name: easypj-prod
      - ipBlock:                        # FROM specific IP ranges
          cidr: 10.0.0.0/8
          except:                        # Exclude a subset
          - 10.0.0.0/24
      ports:                             # On WHICH ports?
      - protocol: TCP
        port: 5432                       # Only PostgreSQL port
    egress:                              # RULES for outgoing traffic
    - to:
      - podSelector:
          matchLabels:
            k8s-app: kube-dns            # Allow DNS queries
      ports:
      - protocol: UDP
        port: 53

  ⚠️  Empty ingress: [] = DENY ALL incoming traffic
  ⚠️  Empty egress: []  = DENY ALL outgoing traffic
  ⚠️  Missing policyTypes, ingress-only policy = only Ingress is affected
      (Egress stays open) — but an egress-only policy with NO policyTypes
      flips this: Ingress is ALWAYS implied too. See "The policyTypes Trap" below.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The <code>protocol</code> Field Accepts TCP, UDP, OR SCTP</h5>
                    <p>Every entry under <code>ports:</code> declares exactly one <code>protocol</code> — and per the official NetworkPolicy documentation, the accepted values are <strong><code>TCP</code>, <code>UDP</code>, and <code>SCTP</code></strong> (SCTP defaults to being assumed present in the API, but "you must be using a CNI plugin that supports SCTP protocol NetworkPolicies" — not every CNI enforces it). For the CKS exam this rarely goes beyond "know that SCTP exists as a third valid value" — but if a scenario mentions a telecom-style workload (SCTP is common in telco/5G signaling), don't assume <code>protocol</code> only accepts TCP or UDP.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>CNI Support for NetworkPolicy — Choose the Right Plugin</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>CNI Plugin</th><th>NetworkPolicy Support</th><th>eBPF</th><th>FQDN Egress</th><th>Production Ready</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Calico</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Full</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Cilium</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Full</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Weave Net</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Full</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ No</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Antrea</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Full</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ (uses OVS)</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ No</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Flannel</strong></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ None</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ No</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">⚠️ No NetPol support</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Kindnet</strong></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ None</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ No</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">⚠️ Testing only</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-weight:700;">⚠️ If your cluster uses Flannel or Kindnet: NetworkPolicy objects are created and visible via kubectl, but they are COMPLETELY IGNORED at the network level. All traffic still flows freely.</p>

                <h4>NetworkPolicy Decision Flowchart — How K8s Decides to Allow or Block</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The logic every CNI plugin follows for NetworkPolicy enforcement</div>
<pre>
  PACKET arrives at pod's network interface
       │
       ▼
  Q1: Is there ANY NetworkPolicy selecting this pod?
       ├── NO  ──▶ ✅ ALLOW (no policies = all traffic allowed)
       └── YES ──▶ Continue to Q2
                        │
                        ▼
  Q2: Is this packet EXPLICITLY allowed by at least ONE policy?
       ├── YES ──▶ ✅ ALLOW
       └── NO  ──▶ ❌ DENY  (implicit deny — the default when policies exist)

  ⚠️  CRITICAL RULE: NetworkPolicies are ADDITIVE (union of all allows).
      Policy A allows port 80 + Policy B allows port 443 = pod gets BOTH.
      No policy can override or subtract from another policy's allow rule.

  ⚠️  TRAP: Once a pod is selected by ANY NetworkPolicy, ALL traffic is
      DENIED by default except what is explicitly allowed by some policy.
      Pod with NO policies = wide open. Pod with ONE policy = locked down.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How CNI Plugins Enforce NetworkPolicies — iptables vs eBPF</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left">
                        <h5>iptables-based (Calico, Weave)</h5>
                        <p>Every NetworkPolicy rule is translated into iptables rules. Each allow rule becomes an ACCEPT entry; implicit deny becomes a DROP. Battle-tested but scales poorly — thousands of pods mean thousands of iptables rules, slowing packet processing.</p>
                        <p><strong>Debug:</strong> <code>iptables -L -n -v | grep cali</code> shows actual Calico rules.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel-right">
                        <h5>eBPF-based (Cilium)</h5>
                        <p>Uses eBPF programs attached before iptables. Each policy becomes an O(1) eBPF map lookup, scaling to tens of thousands of pods with no degradation. Also supports FQDN-based egress (e.g., "allow egress to *.github.com").</p>
                        <p><strong>Debug:</strong> <code>cilium policy get</code> and <code>cilium monitor</code> for real-time decisions.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The Additive Rule Behavior — Policies Cannot "Undo" Each Other</h4>
                <p>NetworkPolicies are <strong>purely additive</strong> — they can only ALLOW, never DENY. If Policy A allows monitoring pods to reach postgres, and Policy B allows easypj pods to reach postgres, the effective rule is the UNION: both can reach postgres. You cannot create Policy C to "block monitoring from postgres" — NetworkPolicies have no DENY rules. To remove access, you must DELETE the policy that granted it.</p>

                <h4>The policyTypes Trap — CKS Exam Pitfall</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>😰 policyTypes has an ASYMMETRIC default — Ingress is always implied, but Egress is implied only if the policy has egress rules. Omit it on an egress-only policy and you silently deny ingress you never meant to touch.</h5>
                    <p>You write a policy with egress rules only, intending to restrict just outbound traffic and leave ingress alone — but you forget <code>policyTypes</code>. Per the official default (§6.8 Rule 3), <strong>Ingress is implied unconditionally</strong> whether or not the policy has any ingress rules, and Egress is implied because egress rules are present. The result is <code>policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]</code> — and since the policy has ZERO ingress rules to allow anything in, every pod it selects loses ALL incoming traffic too, not just the egress you meant to lock down. (Your egress rules are NOT ignored — they take effect exactly as written; it's the ingress side that breaks silently. See Scenario &amp; Command Drill Q2 below for this exact bug caught live.) Always explicitly set <code>policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]</code> — or just <code>[Egress]</code> if ingress genuinely should stay untouched — so there is never a silent surprise.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §6.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s6-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.3 Common NetworkPolicy Patterns You Must Know</h3>
                <p>The CKS exam tests a specific set of NetworkPolicy patterns. You do not need to invent new ones — you need to recognize which pattern the question is asking for and apply it correctly. Here are the 7 essential patterns, with ready-to-use YAML and the exam scenario each one solves:</p>

                <h4>Pattern 1: Default-Deny All Ingress</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Blocks ALL incoming traffic to every pod in the namespace</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: NetworkPolicy
  metadata:
    name: default-deny-ingress
    namespace: easypj-prod
  spec:
    podSelector: {}          # {} = select ALL pods in this namespace
    policyTypes:
    - Ingress
    # No ingress rules specified → DENY ALL incoming traffic
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 2: Default-Deny All Egress</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Blocks ALL outgoing traffic from every pod — use with caution (DNS will break!)</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: NetworkPolicy
  metadata:
    name: default-deny-egress
    namespace: easypj-prod
  spec:
    podSelector: {}
    policyTypes:
    - Egress
    # No egress rules → DENY ALL outgoing. DNS breaks. Always add Pattern 7!
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 3: Allow Specific Pod to Reach a Specific Port (Database Access)</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Allow easypj pods → postgres on port 5432. Nothing else can reach postgres.</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: NetworkPolicy
  metadata:
    name: allow-easypj-to-postgres
    namespace: easypj-prod
  spec:
    podSelector:
      matchLabels:
        app: postgres            # This policy PROTECTS postgres pods
    policyTypes:
    - Ingress
    ingress:
    - from:
      - podSelector:
          matchLabels:
            app: easypj          # Only FROM easypj pods
      ports:
      - protocol: TCP
        port: 5432               # Only ON postgres port
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 4: Allow Traffic Within Same Namespace</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">All pods in the namespace can talk to each other — but nothing from outside</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: NetworkPolicy
  metadata:
    name: allow-same-namespace
    namespace: easypj-prod
  spec:
    podSelector: {}
    policyTypes:
    - Ingress
    ingress:
    - from:
      - podSelector: {}         # From ANY pod in this namespace
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 5: Allow Traffic from a Specific Namespace</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Allow ingress-controller in ingress-nginx namespace → easypj pods</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: NetworkPolicy
  metadata:
    name: allow-from-ingress-ns
    namespace: easypj-prod
  spec:
    podSelector:
      matchLabels:
        app: easypj
    policyTypes:
    - Ingress
    ingress:
    - from:
      - namespaceSelector:
          matchLabels:
            kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ingress-nginx
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 6: Allow Specific CIDR Block (External IPs)</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Allow traffic from a specific IP range — e.g., office VPN subnet</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: NetworkPolicy
  metadata:
    name: allow-from-office-vpn
    namespace: easypj-prod
  spec:
    podSelector:
      matchLabels:
        app: easypj
    policyTypes:
    - Ingress
    ingress:
    - from:
      - ipBlock:
          cidr: 10.100.0.0/16    # Office VPN subnet
      ports:
      - protocol: TCP
        port: 8000
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 7: Allow DNS Egress (Critical — DNS Breaks Without This)</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Allow all pods to reach CoreDNS on UDP 53. Always pair with default-deny-egress.</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: NetworkPolicy
  metadata:
    name: allow-dns-egress
    namespace: easypj-prod
  spec:
    podSelector: {}              # All pods need DNS
    policyTypes:
    - Egress
    egress:
    - to:
      - namespaceSelector:       # CoreDNS runs in kube-system
          matchLabels:
            kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
        podSelector:
          matchLabels:
            k8s-app: kube-dns    # The CoreDNS pods
      ports:
      - protocol: UDP
        port: 53                 # DNS port
      - protocol: TCP
        port: 53                 # DNS over TCP (for large responses)
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Bonus Pattern: Port Ranges with <code>endPort</code> (GA since Kubernetes v1.25)</h4>
                <p>A newer capability that real clusters use constantly but many CKS study guides skip: NetworkPolicy can allow a contiguous <strong>range</strong> of ports with a single rule, using <code>endPort</code> alongside <code>port</code>. Before this feature (and still, on any pre-1.25 cluster or CNI that hasn't implemented it), the only way to allow a range was to enumerate every single port as a separate list entry — for a 1000-port NodePort range that means 1000 lines of YAML. <code>endPort</code> replaces all of them with one rule.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Before vs after — allowing NodePort range 30000-30100 to a metrics-scraper pod</div>
<pre>
  ❌ WITHOUT endPort (pre-v1.25, or CNI lacks support) — one entry PER PORT
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  ports:                                                          │
  │  - protocol: TCP                                                 │
  │    port: 30000                                                   │
  │  - protocol: TCP                                                 │
  │    port: 30001                                                   │
  │  - protocol: TCP                                                 │
  │    port: 30002                                                   │
  │  ...                                          ← 101 entries total│
  │  - protocol: TCP                                                 │
  │    port: 30100                                                   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ✅ WITH endPort (v1.25+, NetworkPolicy API `networking.k8s.io/v1`)
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  ports:                                                          │
  │  - protocol: TCP                                                 │
  │    port: 30000       # start of range (inclusive)                │
  │    endPort: 30100    # end of range (inclusive) — ONE rule       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">allow-nodeport-range.yaml — monitoring can scrape the whole NodePort range</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-monitoring-nodeport-range
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          app: monitoring
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 30000       <span class="comment"># range start (inclusive)</span>
      endPort: 30100    <span class="comment"># range end (inclusive) — requires endPort support</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 endPort Has Requirements — It Is Not Universally Guaranteed</h5>
                    <p><code>endPort</code> requires BOTH the API server to be v1.25+ (where the field graduated to GA/stable and is always accepted) AND the CNI plugin to actually implement range enforcement — Calico, Cilium, and Antrea all support it, but a CNI that predates the feature will silently accept the field and then enforce only the single starting <code>port</code>, dropping everything above it. Always verify with a real probe at the top, middle, and bottom of the declared range — don't assume the whole range works just because the YAML applied without error. Per the official API validation rules: <code>endPort</code> can only be set if <code>port</code> is also set, <code>endPort</code> must be numeric (no named ports) and must be <strong>greater than or equal to</strong> <code>port</code> — and both ends of the range share the single <code>protocol</code> declared on that same <code>ports:</code> entry.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Essential Patterns — Quick Reference Table</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Use Case</th><th>Exam Frequency</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Default deny all ingress</td><td>Isolate a namespace</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">Very High</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Default deny all egress</td><td>Prevent data exfiltration</td><td style="color:#eab308;">High</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Allow same namespace</td><td>Team isolation</td><td style="color:#eab308;">High</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Allow specific port to pod</td><td>Database access control</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">Very High</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Allow DNS egress (53/UDP+TCP)</td><td>Apps need name resolution</td><td style="color:#facc15;">Medium</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Allow namespaceSelector</td><td>Cross-namespace allowed paths</td><td style="color:#eab308;">High</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Allow specific CIDR (ingress)</td><td>External trusted IPs only</td><td style="color:#facc15;">Medium</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern Combinations — Building Real-World Policies</h4>
                <p>The 7 patterns are building blocks. Real clusters combine them. The most common CKS task: Default-Deny Ingress (blocks all incoming) + Allow App→DB on specific port (creates one exception). Also common: Default-Deny Egress + Allow DNS (UDP+TCP 53) — without DNS, pods cannot resolve names and health checks fail. Always create DNS allow rules FIRST, then apply default-deny-egress to avoid breaking your pods during configuration.</p>

                <h4>Cluster-Wide Default-Deny — One-Liner for All Namespaces</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Apply default-deny to every namespace (skip kube-system and ingress)</div>
<pre>
  $ for ns in $(kubectl get ns -o name | grep -v -E 'kube-system|ingress-nginx'); do
      kubectl -n ${ns##*/} apply -f - &lt;&lt;EOF
      apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
      kind: NetworkPolicy
      metadata:
        name: default-deny-ingress
        namespace: ${ns##*/}
      spec:
        podSelector: {}
        policyTypes: [Ingress]
  EOF
    done
  ⚠️  Always exclude kube-system and ingress namespaces — they need cross-ns traffic.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §6.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s6-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.4 Testing and Verifying NetworkPolicy — Does It Actually Work?</h3>
                <p>NetworkPolicies are silent when they work correctly — blocked traffic simply times out with no error message. This makes testing essential. The standard approach is to use a <strong>busybox test pod</strong> to probe connections and verify which ones succeed (allowed) and which ones timeout (blocked).</p>

                <h4>The NetworkPolicy Testing Workflow</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Using busybox to verify NetworkPolicy enforcement</div>
<pre>
  # Step 1: Create a test pod in the SAME namespace
  $ kubectl run test-same --rm -it --image=busybox --restart=Never -n easypj-prod -- sh

  # Step 2: Test ALLOWED connections (should succeed)
  / # wget -q -O- --timeout=3 http://easypj-service:8000/jobs/
  → Returns HTML (allowed by Pattern 3)

  / # nc -zv postgres-service 5432
  → Connection to postgres-service 5432 succeeded! (allowed by Pattern 3)

  # Step 3: Test BLOCKED connections (should timeout)
  / # wget -q -O- --timeout=3 http://monitoring-service:9090/metrics
  → wget: download timed out (BLOCKED by default-deny)

  # Step 4: Create a test pod in a DIFFERENT namespace
  $ kubectl run test-other --rm -it --image=busybox --restart=Never -n default -- sh

  # Step 5: Test cross-namespace access (should be BLOCKED)
  / # wget -q -O- --timeout=3 http://easypj-service.easypj-prod:8000/
  → wget: download timed out (BLOCKED — cross-namespace denied)
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>easypj Connectivity Matrix — Expected Results</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>FROM (source)</th><th>TO (destination)</th><th>PORT</th><th>Expected Result</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>ingress-nginx</td><td>easypj pod</td><td>8000</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ ALLOWED</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>easypj pod</td><td>postgres pod</td><td>5432</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ ALLOWED</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>easypj pod</td><td>redis pod</td><td>6379</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ ALLOWED</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>ALL pods</td><td>kube-dns (DNS)</td><td>53</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ ALLOWED</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>monitoring pod</td><td>ALL pods</td><td>9090</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ ALLOWED</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>postgres pod</td><td>easypj pod</td><td>8000</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ BLOCKED</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>easypj pod</td><td>other namespace</td><td>ANY</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ BLOCKED</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>attacker-pod</td><td>postgres pod</td><td>5432</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ BLOCKED</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>easypj pod</td><td>internet IPs</td><td>ANY</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ BLOCKED</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>external IP (direct)</td><td>easypj pod</td><td>8000</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ BLOCKED (must go via ingress)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The 3-Second Timeout Rule</h5>
                    <p>When testing NetworkPolicies, use <code>--timeout=3</code> on wget or <code>-w 3</code> on nc. Blocked connections do not get a "Connection Refused" — they hang until the TCP SYN timeout (typically 30-75 seconds). The <code>--timeout</code> flag saves you from waiting. On the exam, if a connection takes more than 3 seconds, it is blocked. Move on.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Debugging When NetworkPolicies Do Not Work — Common Failure Modes</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔴 Failure 1: Policy applied but ALL traffic still flows</h5>
                    <p><strong>Root Cause:</strong> CNI does not support NetworkPolicy (Flannel), OR podSelector does not match any pods. If no pods are selected, the policy has zero effect. <strong>Diagnose:</strong> <code>kubectl get pods -n &lt;ns&gt; --show-labels</code> → verify labels match podSelector exactly (case-sensitive).</p>
                </div>
                <div class="scenario-box positive">
                    <h5>🟡 Failure 2: Egress rules ignored</h5>
                    <p><strong>Root Cause:</strong> policyTypes omitted — defaults to [Ingress]. Egress rules silently ignored. <strong>Diagnose:</strong> <code>kubectl get netpol &lt;name&gt; -o yaml | grep policyTypes</code> — must explicitly list Egress.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔴 Failure 3: DNS works but HTTP fails</h5>
                    <p><strong>Root Cause:</strong> You allowed UDP 53 (DNS) but your app needs TCP 53 for large DNS responses, OR your allow rule has the wrong port/protocol. <strong>Diagnose:</strong> <code>kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- nc -zv &lt;svc&gt; &lt;port&gt;</code> — verify TCP connectivity.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §6.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s6-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.5 Securing easypj with NetworkPolicies — Full Implementation</h3>
                <p>Let's apply everything to the easypj/jobpost application. We need 6 NetworkPolicies to achieve zero-trust networking while keeping the application functional:</p>

