                <h4>The 6 Policies for easypj Zero-Trust Networking</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-1">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">1️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Policy 1: Default-Deny All Ingress in easypj-prod</h5>
                        <p>Blocks ALL incoming traffic to every pod. Individual allow rules (Policies 2-6) create exceptions for specific flows. Without this policy, pods accept connections from anywhere.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">2️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Policy 2: Allow easypj → PostgreSQL (TCP 5432)</h5>
                        <p>podSelector: app=postgres. ingress from podSelector: app=easypj on port 5432. All other pods — including attacker pods — cannot reach the database.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">3️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Policy 3: Allow Ingress Controller → easypj (TCP 8000)</h5>
                        <p>podSelector: app=easypj. ingress from namespaceSelector: ingress-nginx on port 8000. Only the ingress controller can reach the Django app — direct internet access is blocked.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">4️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Policy 4: Allow easypj → Redis (TCP 6379)</h5>
                        <p>podSelector: app=redis. ingress from podSelector: app=easypj on port 6379. Django can cache job listings. No other pod can access the cache.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">5️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Policy 5: Allow ALL Pods → DNS (UDP+TCP 53)</h5>
                        <p>podSelector: {}. egress to namespaceSelector: kube-system, podSelector: k8s-app=kube-dns on port 53. CRITICAL: without this, name resolution fails and your app breaks.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">6️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Policy 6: Allow Monitoring → ALL Pods (TCP 9090)</h5>
                        <p>podSelector: {}. ingress from podSelector: app=monitoring on port 9090. Prometheus can scrape metrics from every pod. No other pod can access the metrics endpoint.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The Complete Manifest Set — Copy-Paste Ready for easypj</h4>
                <p>The cards above describe the six policies; here is the actual YAML you would apply. Save this as <code>easypj-netpols.yaml</code> and run <code>kubectl apply -f easypj-netpols.yaml</code> — six separate NetworkPolicy objects, applied together, produce the exact zero-trust topology drawn above.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-netpols.yaml — all 6 policies</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Policy 1: default-deny ALL ingress in the namespace (the baseline)</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: policy-1-default-deny-ingress
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  <span class="comment"># no ingress rules = deny all incoming traffic to every pod</span>
---
<span class="comment"># Policy 2: allow easypj -&gt; postgres on 5432 only</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: policy-2-allow-easypj-to-postgres
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          app: easypj
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 5432
---
<span class="comment"># Policy 3: allow the ingress controller -&gt; easypj on 8000 only</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: policy-3-allow-ingress-to-easypj
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  ingress:
  - from:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ingress-nginx
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 8000
---
<span class="comment"># Policy 4: allow easypj -&gt; redis on 6379 only</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: policy-4-allow-easypj-to-redis
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: redis
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          app: easypj
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 6379
---
<span class="comment"># Policy 5: allow ALL pods -&gt; kube-dns on 53 (UDP + TCP) — never skip this one</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: policy-5-allow-dns-egress
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes:
  - Egress
  egress:
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
      podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          k8s-app: kube-dns
    ports:
    - protocol: UDP
      port: 53
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 53
---
<span class="comment"># Policy 6: allow monitoring -&gt; ALL pods on 9090 (metrics scrape only)</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: policy-6-allow-monitoring-scrape
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          app: monitoring
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 9090</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why This Order Matters When Applying</h5>
                    <p>All 6 objects can be applied in a single <code>kubectl apply -f</code> call — Kubernetes does not care about apply order, only the final combined state. But if you are building this up interactively on the exam, apply <strong>Policy 5 (DNS) before Policy 1 (default-deny)</strong> — otherwise there is a brief window where your pods lose DNS resolution before the allow rule lands, which can make health checks flap. In production, always apply the full set atomically as one manifest to avoid that gap entirely.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §6.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s6-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.6 Restricting Cluster-Level Access — Protecting the API Server</h3>
                <p>The CKS curriculum requires <strong>Network security policies to restrict cluster level access</strong>. This means protecting the Kubernetes API server from unauthorized pod communication. Use NetworkPolicy egress rules + RBAC + disabled SA token automount for defense-in-depth.</p>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 The Attack Path This Section Kills</h5>
                    <p>NetworkPolicy is namespaced — it protects <strong>pods</strong>, it does not firewall the control plane itself. That distinction matters because of what every pod carries by default: a mounted ServiceAccount token that can authenticate to the API server at <code>https://kubernetes.default.svc</code> (which resolves to the real API server on <code>443</code>/<code>6443</code>). If a pod's egress is unrestricted, a single compromised container — one CVE in a Python dependency — can call the API server directly: list every Secret it has RBAC access to, create pods, or escalate through whatever permissions its ServiceAccount happens to hold. <strong>Most workloads never need to call the Kubernetes API at all.</strong> Postgres doesn't call the API server. Redis doesn't call the API server. Only controllers, operators, and CI/CD pods legitimately need that access — everything else should have it blocked by default. This is exactly what the CKS curriculum means by "use network security policies to restrict cluster level access," and it is the single control most candidates forget because NetworkPolicy discussions tend to focus on pod-to-pod traffic and skip pod-to-control-plane traffic entirely.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — Egress Policy Restricting Pod→API Server Access (deny by omission)</span></div><pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: restrict-api-access
  namespace: prod
spec:
  podSelector: {}          <span class="comment"># applies to EVERY ordinary workload pod (postgres, redis, easypj, ...)</span>
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress:
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector: {}
      podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          k8s-app: kube-dns
    ports:
    - protocol: UDP
      port: 53
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 53
  <span class="comment"># 10.96.0.1/32 (the API server's ClusterIP) is DELIBERATELY absent from</span>
  <span class="comment"># every rule above. NetworkPolicy has no explicit "deny" verb (§6.8 Rule 2) —</span>
  <span class="comment"># the API server becomes unreachable purely by OMISSION: once this policy</span>
  <span class="comment"># selects a pod for Egress, only what's listed here (DNS) is allowed, and</span>
  <span class="comment"># the API server was never listed, so it silently times out.</span>
---
<span class="comment"># The narrow exception: ONLY pods that legitimately call the API server</span>
<span class="comment"># (controllers, operators, CI/CD runners) get a SEPARATE policy re-opening it</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-controller-api-access
  namespace: prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      role: controller       <span class="comment"># label ONLY the pods that legitimately need API access</span>
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress:
  - to:
    - ipBlock:
        cidr: 10.96.0.1/32   <span class="comment"># API server ClusterIP — re-opened for this pod only</span>
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 443
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector: {}
      podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          k8s-app: kube-dns
    ports:
    - protocol: UDP
      port: 53
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 53</code></pre></div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 CKS Exam: Cluster-Level Access</h5><p>Restrict which pods reach the API server (NetworkPolicy egress) + restrict what they can do (RBAC) + remove their token (automountServiceAccountToken: false) = complete API isolation.</p></div>

                <h4>Verifying the Egress Lockdown — Prove the API Server Is Unreachable</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash — must TIMEOUT, not just fail fast</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># From inside a pod that should NOT be able to reach the API server:</span>
kubectl exec -n easypj-prod easypj-pod -- curl -m 3 -k https://kubernetes.default

<span class="comment"># Expected on a correctly-locked-down pod: no response, curl exits after 3s</span>
<span class="comment">#   curl: (28) Connection timed out after 3000 milliseconds</span>

<span class="comment"># If instead you get a 401/403 JSON body back, the TCP connection SUCCEEDED —</span>
<span class="comment"># the NetworkPolicy did NOT block it. RBAC/authn denied the request, but the</span>
<span class="comment"># attacker already proved network reachability and can keep probing with other</span>
<span class="comment"># tokens found on the filesystem. A timeout is the only proof the layer works.</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Layers That Guard Port 6443 — Defense-in-Depth for the API Server</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Five independent layers, five different components — an attacker must defeat ALL of them to reach the control plane</div>
<pre>
  Internet ──✗── cloud firewall / security group (admin CIDRs only)
  Nodes    ──✗── host firewall (ufw/iptables: 6443 reachable from LB only)
  Pods     ──✗── egress NetworkPolicy (no pod → 6443 unless explicitly allowed)
  Anyone   ──✗── --anonymous-auth=false + RBAC (no anonymous API calls survive)
  Kubelets ──✗── NodeRestriction admission (kubelet can only touch its own Node/Pods)

  Each ✗ is enforced by a DIFFERENT component in a DIFFERENT layer of the stack:
    cloud IAM  →  node OS firewall  →  CNI plugin  →  apiserver flags + RBAC  →  admission controller

  An attacker who defeats ONE layer still has to defeat the other four before
  reaching anything of value. This is defense-in-depth: no single control is
  ever a single point of failure for the control plane.
</pre>
                </div>
                <p>Notice that only <strong>one</strong> of these five layers is the NetworkPolicy from this chapter (the third). NetworkPolicy alone is necessary but never sufficient — both the CKS exam and real production clusters expect you to combine it with authentication hardening (<code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>, covered in Chapter 8), RBAC least-privilege (Chapter 9), and NodeRestriction (Chapter 11). Memorize this five-layer stack: if a scenario question asks "how do you restrict cluster-level access," the complete answer touches all five layers, not just the NetworkPolicy YAML you write in this chapter.</p>
                <p style="color:#8b949e;font-size:0.85em;">See also: <a href="#ch8">Chapter 8 — Restricting API Server Access</a> (<code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> and authentication hardening) · <a href="#ch9">Chapter 9 — RBAC</a> (least-privilege for whatever tokens remain valid) · <a href="#ch11">Chapter 11 — Node Restriction</a> (locking down what a compromised kubelet can touch).</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §6.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s6-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.7 NetworkPolicy Pattern Catalog — Exam-Ready Templates</h3>
                <p>Seven selector patterns cover essentially every CKS NetworkPolicy task. Drill them until you can write each one from memory in under three minutes — remember that policies are ADDITIVE (the effective rule is the union of all allows), that an empty <code>podSelector: {}</code> selects ALL pods in the namespace, and that <code>policyTypes</code> decides which direction is enforced even when the corresponding rule list is empty.</p>

                <h4>The 7 Patterns — Quick-Glance Table</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Skeleton / Trigger Phrase</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>1</td><td>Default deny ALL (ingress + egress)</td><td><code>podSelector: {}</code> + <code>policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]</code>, no rules</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>2</td><td>Deny ingress only</td><td><code>podSelector: {}</code> + <code>policyTypes: [Ingress]</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>3</td><td>Allow same-namespace</td><td><code>ingress.from: - podSelector: {}</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>4</td><td>Allow specific pod + port</td><td><code>from: podSelector matchLabels app=X</code>; <code>ports: [{port: N}]</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>5</td><td>Allow DNS egress</td><td>egress to <code>kube-system</code> + port 53 UDP/TCP — add to <strong>every</strong> egress-restricted pod!</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>6</td><td>Allow external CIDR</td><td><code>egress.to: - ipBlock: {cidr: x.x.x.x/nn}</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>7</td><td>Allow cross-namespace</td><td><code>from: - namespaceSelector matchLabels kubernetes.io/metadata.name=&lt;ns&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <p>These are the six card-based templates that cover 90% of CKS exam scenarios in ready-to-paste form. Each template needs only namespace names and labels customized:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div><h5>Deny All Ingress</h5><p><code>podSelector: {}<br>policyTypes: [Ingress]<br># No ingress rules</code></p><p style="color:#8b949e;font-size:0.78em;">Use: Starting point. Block everything, then add allows.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div><h5>Allow from Namespace</h5><p><code>ingress: [{from: [{namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {tier: frontend}}}]}]</code></p><p style="color:#8b949e;font-size:0.78em;">Use: Allow entire namespace. Label namespace first.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div><h5>Allow from Specific Pod</h5><p><code>ingress: [{from: [{podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: gateway}}}]}]</code></p><p style="color:#8b949e;font-size:0.78em;">Use: Allow single pod. Combine with namespaceSelector for cross-ns.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔌</div><h5>Allow Only Port</h5><p><code>ingress: [{ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 5432}]}]</code></p><p style="color:#8b949e;font-size:0.78em;">Use: Restrict to specific port. Database:5432, API:8080.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📡</div><h5>DNS Egress</h5><p><code>egress: [{to: [{namespaceSelector:{}, podSelector:{k8s-app: kube-dns}}], ports: [{protocol: UDP, port: 53}]}]</code></p><p style="color:#8b949e;font-size:0.78em;">Use: Required with deny-all egress. Always add DNS.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div><h5>API Server Egress</h5><p><code>egress: [{to: [{ipBlock: {cidr: 10.96.0.1/32}}], ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 443}]}]</code></p><p style="color:#8b949e;font-size:0.78em;">Use: Allow pods to reach API server. Combine with DNS.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 1 + 4 + 5 Combined — The easypj Production Trio</h4>
                <p>In practice, patterns are never used alone — a real namespace needs the baseline plus its exceptions. This is the minimum viable production posture for almost any namespace: <strong>block everything (Pattern 1), allow the one connection the app actually needs (Pattern 4), and never forget DNS (Pattern 5).</strong> Apply this trio to a fresh namespace before layering on any further exceptions.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">production-trio.yaml — patterns 1 + 4 + 5 combined</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Pattern 1: default-deny everything (the baseline every namespace should start with)</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: prod-trio-default-deny
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]
  <span class="comment"># no rules = deny everything as the baseline</span>
---
<span class="comment"># Pattern 4: allow easypj -&gt; postgres on 5432 only (the one exception the app needs)</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: prod-trio-allow-easypj-to-postgres
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels: {app: postgres}
  policyTypes: [Ingress]
  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: easypj}}
    ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 5432}]
---
<span class="comment"># Pattern 5: DNS egress — skip this and every pod loses name resolution</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: prod-trio-allow-dns-egress
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress:
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system}}
      podSelector: {matchLabels: {k8s-app: kube-dns}}
    ports:
    - {protocol: UDP, port: 53}
    - {protocol: TCP, port: 53}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key Takeaway for the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p>NetworkPolicy questions are the most common CKS tasks after RBAC. The exam pattern is always: (1) Create default-deny first, (2) Add specific allow rules for required communication, (3) Test with busybox, (4) Remember DNS — if you add egress deny, you MUST add a DNS allow rule or your pods will break silently. <strong>Always start with default-deny. Never forget DNS.</strong></p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §6.8 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s6-8">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.8 Official Semantics — the Rules the Exam Assumes You Already Know</h3>
                <p>Every NetworkPolicy pattern in this chapter rests on mechanics the CKS exam assumes you already understand cold: <strong>the five official rules governing how policies combine</strong>, <strong>who actually enforces the policy</strong>, and <strong>why DNS is the #1 thing that silently breaks</strong> when you lock a namespace down. Get these mechanics right and every NetworkPolicy task becomes mechanical; get them wrong and your YAML will look correct while traffic behaves unpredictably.</p>

                <h4>The 5 Official Rules — Straight From the Kubernetes Docs</h4>
                <p>The upstream NetworkPolicy documentation (<code>kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies</code>) states five behavioral rules that resolve almost every "why isn't my NetworkPolicy working" exam scenario. A correct-looking YAML manifest can still fail if you don't know these cold:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-1">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">1️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Isolation Is Per-Direction, Per-Pod</h5>
                        <p>A pod with NO NetworkPolicy selecting it is <strong>non-isolated</strong> (fully open) in that direction. The moment ANY policy's <code>podSelector</code> matches it AND lists that <code>policyType</code>, the pod becomes isolated in that direction — from then on, only explicitly matching rules are allowed. A pod can be isolated for Ingress but wide open for Egress (or vice versa), depending on which policies select it and which <code>policyTypes</code> they declare.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">2️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Multiple Policies Are Additive, Never Restrictive</h5>
                        <p>If pod X is selected by 3 policies, the allowed traffic is the <strong>UNION</strong> of all 3. You cannot write a second NetworkPolicy to further restrict what a first one already allowed — there is no "deny wins" or "first match wins" semantics like a firewall chain, and order of evaluation does not matter. To remove access, you must delete or edit the policy that granted it.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">3️⃣</div>
                        <h5>policyTypes Defaults If Omitted</h5>
                        <p><code>Ingress</code> is implied by default. <code>Egress</code> is implied ONLY if the policy object has <code>egress</code> rules present. This asymmetry is a trap — never rely on inference. <strong>Always write <code>policyTypes</code> explicitly.</strong> Relying on the default is one of the most common silent bugs in hand-written NetworkPolicy YAML.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">4️⃣</div>
                        <h5>A Connection Needs BOTH Sides to Agree</h5>
                        <p>The source pod's <strong>egress</strong> rules must allow the connection out, AND the destination pod's <strong>ingress</strong> rules must allow it in. If either side lacks a matching rule, the connection is blocked — even if the other side explicitly allows it. This is why true cross-namespace access (Lab Q&amp;A Q6 below) requires writing NetworkPolicies in BOTH namespaces.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">5️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Node Traffic Is Always Allowed</h5>
                        <p>Traffic to/from the node a pod runs on is <strong>never blocked</strong> by NetworkPolicy — this is an explicit exception carved out for <code>ipBlock</code> rules, and it holds <strong>regardless of the IP address of either the pod or the node</strong>. NetworkPolicy cannot fully isolate a pod from its own node — kubelet health checks (liveness/readiness probes originating from the kubelet itself) and node-level monitoring agents always get through. Don't rely on NetworkPolicy to stop traffic from a compromised kubelet or a <code>hostNetwork: true</code> pod.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>What NetworkPolicy Cannot Do — The Official API Limitations List</h4>
                <p>Everything in this chapter so far describes what NetworkPolicy <em>does</em>. Just as important — and a frequent source of over-confident exam answers and real production incidents — is what it explicitly does <strong>not</strong> do. The official Kubernetes documentation ships a dedicated "What you can't do with network policies (at least, not yet)" list; every item on it is a control the exam expects you to reach for a <em>different</em> tool to solve:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div><h5>No Explicit Deny Rule</h5><p>The model is deny-by-default with only ADD-an-allow semantics (§6.8 Rule 2). There is no "explicitly block this pod even though another policy allows it" primitive — to remove access you must edit or delete the policy that granted it.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏠</div><h5>No Loopback / Host-Traffic Blocking</h5><p>Pods cannot block localhost access, nor can they block traffic from their own resident node (§6.8 Rule 5). If you need to stop a compromised process from talking to itself over loopback, that's a Seccomp/AppArmor problem (Ch 14-15), not a NetworkPolicy one.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔐</div><h5>Nothing TLS-Related</h5><p>NetworkPolicy is pure L3/L4 (IP + port). It has no concept of certificates, mTLS, or encrypting traffic in transit. For identity-based, encrypted service-to-service traffic, you need a service mesh or Ingress controller — see <a href="#ch22">Ch 22: mTLS &amp; Istio</a>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📛</div><h5>No Targeting Services by Name</h5><p>You cannot write <code>to: service: postgres-svc</code>. You can only target by <strong>pod</strong> or <strong>namespace labels</strong> — which is usually a workable substitute, but it means renaming/relabeling a Service's backing pods can silently change what a policy matches.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🖥️</div><h5>No Node-Identity Targeting</h5><p>You can restrict traffic by IP/CIDR (<code>ipBlock</code>), but you cannot say "allow only from node X" by the node's Kubernetes identity — CIDR is a proxy for node IP, not a real identity check.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📊</div><h5>No Built-In Connection Logging</h5><p>NetworkPolicy cannot log which connections it blocked or allowed — there's no "show me every DROP from the last hour" API. For that visibility you need your CNI's own logging (e.g., Calico flow logs, Cilium Hubble) or a runtime tool like Falco.</p></div>
                </div>
                <p style="color:#8b949e;font-size:0.85em;">Also not supported: forcing traffic through a common gateway, third-party "policy request" workflows, and cluster-wide default policies applied automatically to every namespace (some managed distributions layer this on top of vanilla NetworkPolicy, but the upstream API itself has no such default — which is exactly why §6.3's "cluster-wide default-deny one-liner" has to loop over every namespace explicitly instead of relying on one global object).</p>

                <h4>How NetworkPolicy Is Enforced — The CNI Plugin Is the Firewall Engine</h4>
                <p>NetworkPolicy YAML is not enforced by Kubernetes itself. It is enforced by your <strong>CNI (Container Network Interface) plugin</strong> — Calico, Cilium, Weave, or another CNI that supports NetworkPolicy. This is the most important concept to understand because if your CNI doesn't support NetworkPolicy, your policies are <strong>silently ignored</strong> — you write YAML, kubectl accepts it, but nothing is actually enforced:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">NetworkPolicy Enforcement Architecture — From YAML to iptables/eBPF</div>
<pre>
  kubectl apply -f networkpolicy.yaml
  │
  ▼
  API SERVER: Validates the NetworkPolicy object → stores it in etcd.
  │  (This is ALL Kubernetes does. It does NOT enforce the policy.)
  │
  ▼
  CNI PLUGIN (e.g., Calico): Watches for NetworkPolicy objects via the K8s API.
  │
  │  CALICO'S ENFORCEMENT PATH:
  │  ───────────────────────────
  │  1. Calico controller detects new/modified NetworkPolicy.
  │  2. Translates the policy's podSelector + ingress/egress rules into
  │     iptables rules (or eBPF programs for Cilium).
  │  3. Programs these rules into EVERY node where matching pods run.
  │  4. iptables checks EVERY packet entering or leaving matching pods:
  │     • Source IP matches allowed CIDR? → ALLOW
  │     • Source pod label matches allowed podSelector? → ALLOW
  │     • Neither → DROP (silently — no ICMP response)
  │
  │  WHAT HAPPENS WITH A CNI THAT DOESN'T SUPPORT NETWORKPOLICY:
  │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  │  • Flannel (without NetworkPolicy extension): IGNORES the policy entirely.
  │  • All pods can still communicate. Your YAML did nothing.
  │  • kubectl describe networkpolicy shows the policy as "active" — but
  │    there is NO enforcement. This is a silent failure.
  │
  │  CNI PLUGIN NETWORKPOLICY SUPPORT:
  │  ───────────────────────────────────
  │  ✅ Calico      — Full support (iptables or eBPF)
  │  ✅ Cilium      — Full support (eBPF — most advanced)
  │  ✅ Weave Net   — Full support
  │  ⚠️  Flannel     — Only with Calico network policy engine
  │  ⚠️  Azure CNI   — Supports NetworkPolicy (Azure Network Policy Manager)
  │  ⚠️  AWS VPC CNI — Requires Calico for NetworkPolicy (AWS doesn't natively enforce)
  │  ❌ Docker bridge — NO support (development only, never production)
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  CKS EXAM REALITY:
  ─────────────────
  The CKS exam cluster uses a CNI that supports NetworkPolicy (usually Calico
  or Cilium). You do NOT need to install a CNI. Just write the YAML and test.
  But in real clusters, ALWAYS VERIFY your CNI supports NetworkPolicy before
  relying on it. Check: kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E 'calico|cilium|weave'
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The DNS Problem — Why Egress Deny Breaks Your Pods</h4>
                <p>This is the single most common NetworkPolicy mistake — and the one that CKS exam candidates trip on most. When you create a default-deny egress policy, your pods lose the ability to resolve DNS names. Why? Because DNS queries go to CoreDNS, which runs as a pod in <code>kube-system</code>. If your default-deny egress policy doesn't explicitly allow DNS, your pod can't even look up <code>postgres.easypj.svc.cluster.local</code>. Here's the exact mechanic:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">What Happens Without a DNS Egress Rule — The Silent Failure</div>
<pre>
  POD: easypj-django-7f8c9-abcde (namespace: easypj)
  │
  │  Application tries to connect: postgres.easypj.svc.cluster.local:5432
  │
  ▼
  STEP 1: The application calls getaddrinfo("postgres.easypj.svc.cluster.local")
  │  This triggers a DNS query.
  │
  ▼
  STEP 2: The container's /etc/resolv.conf points to CoreDNS:
  │  nameserver 10.96.0.10  (CoreDNS ClusterIP)
  │
  ▼
  STEP 3: The container sends a UDP packet to 10.96.0.10:53
  │  (DNS is UDP by default; falls back to TCP for large responses)
  │
  ▼
  STEP 4: NETWORKPOLICY CHECK — Is this egress allowed?
  │
  │  ┌─ NO DNS EGRESS RULE ──────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  │  • Policy says: egress to podSelector: app=postgres on 5432   │
  │  │  • But the DNS query is to podSelector: k8s-app=kube-dns on 53│
  │  │  • This does NOT match the postgres rule → DROP                │
  │  │  • DNS query NEVER reaches CoreDNS. No response.               │
  │  │  • Application hangs waiting for DNS resolution.               │
  │  │  • Eventually times out (30+ seconds). App appears broken.     │
  │  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  │  ┌─ WITH DNS EGRESS RULE ────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  │  • Policy ALSO has: egress to podSelector: k8s-app=kube-dns   │
  │  │    on port 53 (UDP + TCP)                                     │
  │  │  • DNS query MATCHES → ALLOWED                                 │
  │  │  • CoreDNS responds with: postgres.easypj.svc = 10.96.0.45    │
  │  │  • Application connects to 10.96.0.45:5432 → matches postgres │
  │  │    rule → ALLOWED → connection established ✅                  │
  │  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  THE DNS EGRESS RULE YOU MUST ADD:
  ─────────────────────────────────
  egress:
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
      podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          k8s-app: kube-dns
    ports:
    - protocol: UDP
      port: 53
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 53
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The #1 Exam Trap — podSelector + namespaceSelector: AND vs OR</h4>
                <p>This is the single most misread piece of NetworkPolicy YAML on the entire exam. Two selectors nested under the <strong>same</strong> list item combine with AND logic; two selectors written as <strong>separate</strong> list items combine with OR logic. The difference is one misplaced dash, and it silently changes a narrow, restrictive rule into a broad, permissive one:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">One misplaced dash changes the entire meaning of the rule</div>
<pre>
  SAME array element (nested under one "-") = AND (both must match)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ingress:
    - from:
      - namespaceSelector:
          matchLabels: {team: platform}
        podSelector:              ← SAME "-", no new dash
          matchLabels: {role: client}
    MEANS: pods labeled role=client, but ONLY inside namespaces
    labeled team=platform. Narrower, more restrictive.

  SEPARATE array elements (each its own "-") = OR (either matches)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ingress:
    - from:
      - namespaceSelector:
          matchLabels: {team: platform}
      - podSelector:              ← NEW "-" = separate rule
          matchLabels: {role: client}
    MEANS: ANY pod in a team=platform namespace, OR ANY pod (in the
    policy's own namespace) labeled role=client. Much broader.

  ⚠️  These look nearly identical in YAML — a single misplaced dash
      silently changes "AND" into "OR" and opens far more traffic
      than intended. Always verify with a connectivity test (§6.4).
</pre>
                </div>

                <p>The safest way to confirm which behavior you actually shipped is to read it back with <code>kubectl describe</code> — it renders the parsed rule structure, and AND vs OR shows up as one combined "From" block versus two separate ones:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Both variants applied to easypj — same labels, opposite blast radius</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># VARIANT A (AND) — restrictive: platform-team namespace AND role=client pod</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-and-variant
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels: {app: easypj}
  policyTypes: [Ingress]
  ingress:
  - from:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels: {team: platform}
      podSelector:
        matchLabels: {role: client}
---
<span class="comment"># VARIANT B (OR) — permissive: platform-team namespace OR role=client pod</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-or-variant
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels: {app: easypj}
  policyTypes: [Ingress]
  ingress:
  - from:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels: {team: platform}
    - podSelector:
        matchLabels: {role: client}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">bash — kubectl describe reveals which one you actually applied</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl describe netpol allow-and-variant -n easypj-prod
<span class="comment"># Allowing ingress traffic:
#   To Port: &lt;any&gt; (traffic allowed to all ports)
#   From:
#     NamespaceSelector: team=platform
#     PodSelector: role=client        ← printed on ONE combined "From" entry = AND
</span>
kubectl describe netpol allow-or-variant -n easypj-prod
<span class="comment"># Allowing ingress traffic:
#   To Port: &lt;any&gt; (traffic allowed to all ports)
#   From:
#     NamespaceSelector: team=platform
#   From:
#     PodSelector: role=client        ← printed as TWO SEPARATE "From" entries = OR
</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

