  │   4. PACKET ARRIVES AT POD INTERFACE                                       │
  │      → Kernel checks iptables/eBPF filter                                 │
  │      → Matches against pod labels, namespace labels, IP blocks           │
  │      → ALLOW or DROP                                                      │
  │                                                                           │
  │   🔑 NetworkPolicy without a supporting CNI = YAML stored in etcd but    │
  │      NOTHING enforces it. Always verify: kubectl get pods -n kube-system  │
  │      | grep -E 'calico|cilium|weave'                                       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    <p>The diagram above is the map — CNI watches, translates, programs, enforces. The three deep-dives below are the <strong>mechanics underneath</strong> each stop: exactly which kernel structures Calico actually programs and in what order (①), why Cilium's eBPF model is a fundamentally different enforcement primitive rather than just "iptables but faster" (②), and the single most common reason a newly-applied deny policy appears to do nothing to a connection that was already open (③). Understanding these three is what turns "I applied the YAML" into "I can explain, and diagnose, exactly what the kernel is doing with it."</p>

                    <h5>① Calico's iptables/ipset Translation Pipeline — From NetworkPolicy Object to Kernel Chains</h5>
                    <p>Calico's Felix agent (one per node) is the component that actually turns a <code>NetworkPolicy</code> object into kernel-enforced rules. Felix does NOT write one iptables rule per pod, per policy, per label match — that would not scale. Instead it uses a two-layer trick: <strong>ipsets</strong> (kernel-level, O(1)-lookup sets of IPs/CIDRs that can hold thousands of members) hold the dynamic membership (which pod IPs currently match a given selector), while a small, mostly-static set of <strong>iptables chains</strong> reference those ipsets by name. When a pod's labels change, or a new pod matching a selector is scheduled, Felix updates the ipset membership — it does NOT need to rewrite any iptables rule. This is exactly why Calico stays fast even in a namespace with thousands of churning pods: the expensive part (adding/removing a rule) rarely happens; the cheap part (updating set membership) happens constantly.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  FELIX'S TRANSLATION PIPELINE — NetworkPolicy object → running kernel state

  1. kubectl apply -f allow-easypj-to-postgres.yaml
       │
       ▼
  2. Felix (per-node agent) watches the Kubernetes API, sees the new/changed
     NetworkPolicy, and resolves its selectors against CURRENT cluster state:
       podSelector: {app: postgres}       → resolves to: [10.244.1.7, 10.244.2.9]
       ingress.from.podSelector: {app: easypj} → resolves to: [10.244.1.3, 10.244.3.5, ...]
       │
       ▼
  3. Felix programs an IPSET per selector (NOT per pod):
       ipset create cali40s:XyZ123 hash:ip
       ipset add    cali40s:XyZ123 10.244.1.3
       ipset add    cali40s:XyZ123 10.244.3.5
     (adding/removing a pod later = one `ipset add/del` — never touches iptables)
       │
       ▼
  4. Felix programs a SMALL, mostly-static set of iptables rules that
     REFERENCE the ipset by name, in a per-endpoint chain:
       -A cali-pi-_1a2b3c4d5e -m set --match-set cali40s:XyZ123 src \
          -p tcp --dport 5432 -j MARK --set-mark 0x10000/0x10000
       -A cali-pi-_1a2b3c4d5e -m mark --mark 0x10000/0x10000 -j RETURN
       (no match against the ipset by this point in the chain → falls through
        to the namespace's default policy chain → DROP, per §6.2's decision flow)
       │
       ▼
  5. Kernel netfilter evaluates the packet against this chain on every
     packet — but the EXPENSIVE selector-matching work was already done in
     step 3, ahead of time. The datapath itself only does cheap set lookups.
</pre>
