                        <thead><tr><th>What Changes</th><th>What Felix Actually Touches</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>A new pod matching <code>app: easypj</code> is scheduled</td><td>One <code>ipset add</code> to the existing set — no chain rewrite</td><td>Cheap — microseconds</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A pod's label changes so it no longer matches</td><td>One <code>ipset del</code> from the set</td><td>Cheap — microseconds</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>You apply a brand-new NetworkPolicy object</td><td>A new ipset created + a new per-endpoint iptables chain wired in</td><td>Moderate — happens once per policy, not per pod</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A pod is created/deleted (gets/loses its own veth)</td><td>Felix creates/removes that pod's OWN per-endpoint chain and its <code>cali-tw-*</code>/<code>cali-fw-*</code> hooks</td><td>Moderate — once per pod lifecycle event</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this matters for the exam and for production debugging:</strong> when you run <code>iptables -L -n -v | grep cali</code> (mentioned in §6.2) and see a wall of cryptic chain names like <code>cali-pi-_1a2b3c4d5e</code>, that hash is deterministic — it is derived from the policy's identity, not randomly generated per packet. If a policy "isn't working," check the ipset membership first (<code>ipset list cali40s:XyZ123</code>) before assuming the iptables rule itself is wrong — the single most common real-world Calico bug is a selector that resolves to an EMPTY ipset (a label typo), which silently behaves exactly like "no pods allowed" with zero error anywhere in <code>kubectl</code>.</div>

                    <h5>② Cilium's eBPF Identity-Based Enforcement — Why It's Not Just "iptables, but Faster"</h5>
                    <p>It's tempting to think of Cilium's eBPF enforcement as "the same idea as Calico, just implemented with a faster kernel primitive instead of iptables." That undersells what's actually different: Cilium does not primarily match on <strong>IP addresses</strong> at enforcement time at all — it assigns every pod a numeric <strong>security identity</strong> derived from its labels, and policy decisions are lookups keyed by <em>identity pairs</em>, not IP pairs. This is a fundamentally different data model, and it's why Cilium can enforce policy correctly across pod restarts, IP churn, and even multi-cluster meshes where the same IP range might be reused in different clusters.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  CILIUM'S IDENTITY MODEL — labels become a number BEFORE any packet is evaluated

  1. Pod scheduled with labels {app: easypj, tier: backend}
       │
       ▼
  2. Cilium's control plane computes a SECURITY IDENTITY — a numeric hash of
     the pod's security-relevant labels (NOT its IP, NOT its name):
       {app: easypj, tier: backend}  →  Identity 24601
     Every OTHER pod with the exact same label set shares Identity 24601,
     even across nodes, even after IP reassignment on restart.
       │
       ▼
  3. Cilium programs this mapping into an eBPF map keyed by IP → identity,
     attached at the pod's veth (via a `tc` clsact hook, not iptables):
       10.244.1.3 → Identity 24601
       10.244.3.5 → Identity 24601   (different IP, SAME identity)
       │
       ▼
  4. NetworkPolicy rules are compiled into a SEPARATE eBPF policy map keyed
     by (source identity, destination identity, port) → ALLOW/DENY:
       (24601, 8842, tcp/5432) → ALLOW      [easypj → postgres:5432]
       (*,      8842, tcp/5432) → DENY (implicit, once isolated)
       │
       ▼
  5. On EVERY packet: the eBPF program at the veth looks up the packet's
     source IP → identity (map lookup #1), destination IP → identity
     (map lookup #2), then (src-identity, dst-identity, port) → verdict
     (map lookup #3). All three are O(1) hash lookups — no linear scan
     through a rule list, unlike an iptables chain evaluated top-to-bottom.
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>iptables/ipset (Calico default)</th><th>eBPF Identity Model (Cilium)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>What a rule actually matches on</td><td>IP addresses (grouped via ipsets)</td><td>Numeric security identities (derived from labels)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Cost to evaluate one packet</td><td>Chain traversal — bounded by chain length, though ipset membership tests are O(1) each</td><td>Constant-time hash map lookups regardless of cluster size</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Behavior on pod IP reuse (fast restart)</td><td>Ipset must be updated with the new IP before enforcement is correct</td><td>Identity is recomputed from labels; correctness doesn't depend on IP timing at all</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Cross-node / cross-cluster consistency</td><td>Each node's iptables state is independently programmed by Felix</td><td>Identities are cluster-wide (and can be mesh-wide with Cluster Mesh) — same labels always same identity everywhere</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Where the code runs</td><td>Linux netfilter framework (kernel, but not sandboxed bytecode)</td><td>Verified eBPF bytecode, JIT-compiled, attached via <code>tc</code> at the veth — sandboxed by the kernel verifier</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The exam-relevant takeaway:</strong> when a Cilium-based cluster's NetworkPolicy "isn't working," <code>iptables -L</code> will show you almost nothing useful — the enforcement point is a different kernel subsystem entirely. Use <code>cilium policy get</code> to see the compiled identity-to-identity rules and <code>cilium monitor --type drop</code> to watch live DENY verdicts with the exact identity pair that was rejected. Never assume the Calico debugging commands from §6.2 apply unmodified to a Cilium cluster — always confirm which CNI is running (<code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E 'calico|cilium'</code>) before choosing your debugging toolkit.</div>

                    <h5>③ conntrack &amp; the "Already Established" Trap — Why a New Deny Policy Doesn't Always Cut an Existing Connection</h5>
                    <p>Here is a scenario that surprises even experienced administrators: you apply a NetworkPolicy that should immediately block an attacker's connection to postgres — but the attacker's <em>already-open</em> TCP session keeps working for several more minutes, or until the pod restarts. This is not a NetworkPolicy bug. It is <strong>conntrack</strong> (connection tracking) doing exactly what it was designed to do, and understanding it is the difference between "I applied the fix" and "I actually stopped the bleeding."</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  WHY AN EXISTING CONNECTION CAN SURVIVE A NEW DENY POLICY

  BEFORE the deny policy: attacker-pod ←──TCP session, ESTABLISHED──→ postgres:5432
  Linux's conntrack table already has an entry:
       SRC=10.244.9.9 DST=10.244.2.7 SPORT=51234 DPORT=5432 STATE=ESTABLISHED

  Both Calico (iptables) and Cilium (eBPF) commonly include a fast-path rule
  very close to the TOP of their evaluation, something conceptually like:
       -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
  This exists for a GOOD reason: without it, EVERY packet of EVERY connection
  would need full policy re-evaluation, which is wasteful — conntrack lets the
  datapath fast-path traffic it has ALREADY decided is legitimate.

  YOU APPLY A NEW DENY POLICY blocking attacker-pod → postgres:5432
       │
       ▼
  NEW connection attempts (SYN packets) → correctly hit the fresh policy →
  BLOCKED immediately. ✅
       │
  EXISTING connection's packets → conntrack recognizes them as
  ESTABLISHED,RELATED → fast-path ACCEPT fires BEFORE the new deny rule is
  even reached → the existing session KEEPS WORKING. ❌ (until it closes,
  times out, or the pod/node restarts and conntrack state is lost)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Does the New Deny Policy Stop It?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Attacker opens a brand-new connection AFTER the policy is applied</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes, immediately</td><td>SYN packet has no conntrack entry yet — full policy evaluation applies</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Attacker's connection was already ESTABLISHED before the policy was applied</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ No, not until it ends</td><td>conntrack fast-path bypasses full re-evaluation for known-good flows</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>The pod that owns the connection restarts</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes — new pod, new conntrack state</td><td>Conntrack entries are tied to the network namespace/veth, which restart destroys</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>You need the connection killed RIGHT NOW without a restart</td><td>Only via manual intervention</td><td><code>conntrack -D -s &lt;attacker-ip&gt; -d &lt;postgres-ip&gt; --dport 5432</code> forcibly deletes the tracked flow, forcing re-evaluation on the next packet</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The incident-response implication:</strong> if you are using NetworkPolicy as an emergency containment tool (as §30's incident-response playbook and this chapter's Connected Topics both reference), "I applied the deny-all policy" is NOT the same claim as "I terminated the attacker's active session." For true immediate containment, you must ALSO flush the relevant conntrack entries on every node the attacker's pod could be routed through — <code>conntrack -D</code> targeted at the specific flow, or <code>conntrack -F</code> to flush everything if you can tolerate briefly disrupting all connections cluster-wide. On the CKS exam this exact nuance rarely appears in a single question, but in a real production incident it is the single most common reason a security team believes an attacker is "cut off" while the attacker's shell is still very much alive.</div>
