            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch6-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The flat network is the deliberate default</strong> — Kubernetes ships with zero NetworkPolicies, zero namespace isolation, and every pod can reach every other pod on every port until you explicitly lock it down.</li>
                    <li><strong>NetworkPolicy is namespaced, additive, and CNI-enforced</strong> — the API server only stores the object; your CNI plugin (Calico, Cilium, Weave) is what actually translates it into iptables/eBPF packet filtering. Flannel silently ignores it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Always start with default-deny, then add allows</strong> — the 7 essential patterns (deny-ingress, deny-egress, allow-port, allow-namespace, allow-CIDR, allow-DNS, allow-same-namespace) cover nearly every CKS exam scenario.</li>
                    <li><strong>DNS is the #1 silent failure</strong> — a default-deny egress policy without an explicit UDP/TCP 53 allow rule to kube-dns breaks name resolution for every pod, with no error message beyond a timeout.</li>
                    <li><strong>NetworkPolicy is Layer 3/4 only</strong> — it controls IP/port traffic, not filesystem writes, syscalls, or application-layer attacks. Test every policy with a busybox/netshoot probe and a <code>--timeout=3</code> flag before trusting it.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch6-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>NetworkPolicy requires a CNI that supports it:</strong> Calico, Cilium, Weave, and Antrea support it. Flannel does NOT support NetworkPolicy natively. On the CKS exam, the cluster WILL have a supporting CNI — but verify with <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E 'calico|cilium|weave'</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>podSelector: {} means ALL pods:</strong> Empty selector matches every pod in the namespace. <code>podSelector: {}</code> with NO ingress rules = deny-all-ingress. With specific rules = those rules apply to all pods.</li>
                    <li><strong>Label selectors are AND, not OR:</strong> If you specify both <code>namespaceSelector</code> and <code>podSelector</code> in the same <code>from</code> entry, BOTH must match. For OR logic, use separate <code>from</code> entries.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch6-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
