            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch6-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 6 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a NetworkPolicy that blocks ALL ingress and egress traffic in the "secure-ns" namespace. Write the complete YAML.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-all
  namespace: secure-ns
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  - Egress
# No ingress or egress rules = DENY ALL</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>An empty <code>podSelector: {}</code> selects ALL pods in the namespace. When you declare <code>policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]</code> but provide NO rules, the default behavior is DENY ALL. This is the foundation of zero-trust networking: start with everything blocked, then add explicit allow rules. On the CKS exam, default-deny NetworkPolicy is the starting point for almost every network isolation task.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After applying default-deny-all, pods can't resolve DNS. Write the NetworkPolicy that fixes this WITHOUT opening any other traffic.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-dns
  namespace: secure-ns
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress:
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
      podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          k8s-app: kube-dns
    ports:
    - protocol: UDP
      port: 53</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the most common follow-up task after applying default-deny: pods need DNS to function. <code>podSelector</code> is nested under the SAME <code>from</code>/<code>to</code> list item as <code>namespaceSelector</code> (no separate dash) — per §6.8's AND-vs-OR trap, this is what makes the selectors use AND logic — BOTH <code>namespaceSelector</code> AND <code>podSelector</code> must match. DNS uses UDP port 53 (sometimes TCP 53 for large responses). On the exam, if you see "pods can't connect after applying NetworkPolicy," DNS is the #1 culprit. Always add a DNS allow rule immediately after default-deny.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You've applied a NetworkPolicy but traffic still flows. What's the most likely cause and how do you verify it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Step 1 — confirm which CNI is actually running (the #1 root cause)
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E 'calico|cilium|weave|flannel'
# → kube-flannel-ds-abc12   1/1   Running   ...   ← Flannel: NO NetworkPolicy support

# Step 2 — the object itself gives no warning even when nothing enforces it
kubectl get netpol -A
# → policy listed, no errors — API server only VALIDATES + STORES, never enforces

# Step 3 — prove it with a live probe rather than trusting `kubectl get`
kubectl run test --rm -it --image=busybox --restart=Never -- \
  wget -qO- --timeout=5 &lt;target-url&gt;
# → succeeds even though a deny policy is applied = enforcement confirmed OFF

# Fix: migrate to a NetworkPolicy-capable CNI (Calico or Cilium)</code></pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Root Cause</th><th>How To Confirm</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>CNI doesn't enforce NetworkPolicy</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E 'calico|cilium|weave'</code> returns nothing</td><td>Migrate to Calico or Cilium</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>podSelector matches zero pods</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n &lt;ns&gt; --show-labels</code> — compare against the policy's selector</td><td>Fix the label typo in the policy or on the pods</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Applied to the wrong namespace</td><td><code>kubectl get netpol -A -o wide</code></td><td>Re-apply with the correct <code>-n</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>NetworkPolicy is an API resource, but enforcement is done by the CNI plugin. Flannel has NO NetworkPolicy support — it silently ignores all NetworkPolicy resources. Calico, Cilium, and Weave Net all support it. On the CKS exam, if NetworkPolicy doesn't work, check the CNI first. Also check: are you in the right namespace? Does the podSelector actually match your pods? Use <code>kubectl describe networkpolicy</code> to see which pods are selected.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a NetworkPolicy that allows pods with label "app: frontend" to receive traffic ONLY from pods with label "app: api-gateway" on port 8080.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-from-gateway
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: frontend
  policyTypes: [Ingress]
  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          app: api-gateway
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 8080</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The <code>podSelector</code> in <code>spec</code> selects which pods this policy APPLIES TO (the target). The <code>podSelector</code> inside <code>ingress.from</code> selects which pods are ALLOWED to send traffic. This is a common point of confusion. This policy says: "pods labeled app=frontend can ONLY receive TCP/8080 traffic from pods labeled app=api-gateway." All other traffic (even from the same namespace) is denied to these pods.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to block pods in namespace "attack-surface" from reaching the metadata service at <code>169.254.169.254</code>. Write an egress NetworkPolicy that specifically blocks this IP while allowing all other egress traffic.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: block-metadata-ip
  namespace: attack-surface
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress:
  # Allow all egress EXCEPT to metadata IP
  - to:
    - ipBlock:
        cidr: 0.0.0.0/0
        except:
        - 169.254.169.254/32</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This uses the <code>ipBlock</code> with <code>except</code> pattern — allow 0.0.0.0/0 EXCEPT 169.254.169.254/32. This is more surgical than blocking all egress and whitelisting specific IPs. The metadata service (169.254.169.254) is the #1 cloud SSRF target — blocking it from pods prevents credential theft attacks like the Capital One breach. On the exam, you may also need to block metadata via iptables rules as a second layer (defense in depth).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You have two namespaces: "web" and "database". Pods in "web" with label <code>tier: frontend</code> need to reach pods in "database" with label <code>app: postgres</code> on port 5432. Write BOTH the ingress policy in "database" AND the egress policy in "web".</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># In namespace "database" — allow ingress from web pods
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-from-web
  namespace: database
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  policyTypes: [Ingress]
  ingress:
  - from:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          name: web
      podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          tier: frontend
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 5432
---
# In namespace "web" — allow egress to database pods
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-to-db
  namespace: web
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      tier: frontend
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress:
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          name: database
      podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          app: postgres
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 5432</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Cross-namespace NetworkPolicies require <strong>both sides</strong> for defense in depth. The database namespace should control who can send to it (ingress), and the web namespace should control where pods can send to (egress). The <code>namespaceSelector</code> relies on namespace labels — you must label namespaces: <code>kubectl label ns web name=web</code>. On the CKS exam, you may only be asked for ONE side, but understanding both shows deep knowledge.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You apply a NetworkPolicy with <code>podSelector: {}</code> but some pods in the namespace still accept traffic. What conditions could cause this?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Why NetworkPolicy Doesn't Apply</th><th>How To Confirm</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>hostNetwork: true</strong> pods</td><td>The pod uses the NODE's network namespace directly — it has no veth of its own for the CNI to attach policy to</td><td><code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.hostNetwork}'</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CNI not enforcing</strong> (e.g. Flannel)</td><td>The object is stored in etcd but nothing ever translates it into iptables/eBPF rules</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E 'calico|cilium|weave'</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Existing allow rule elsewhere</strong></td><td>Multiple NetworkPolicies are ADDITIVE (union) — if ANY policy selecting the pod allows the traffic, it's allowed</td><td><code>kubectl get netpol -n &lt;ns&gt; -o yaml</code> (review every policy that selects the pod)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Fast triage sequence for "podSelector: {} but traffic still gets through"
kubectl get pod &lt;pod&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.hostNetwork}{"\n"}'
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E 'calico|cilium|weave|flannel'
kubectl get netpol -n &lt;ns&gt; -o yaml | grep -E 'name:|podSelector|matchLabels'</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>NetworkPolicies are additive within a namespace. If you have 3 policies and ANY one of them allows a connection, it's allowed. This is why default-deny MUST be explicit — without it, pods accept all traffic by default. Also, <code>hostNetwork: true</code> pods use the host's network namespace, so NetworkPolicy doesn't touch them — use node-level firewalls (iptables/nftables) instead.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An attacker compromises a pod and tries to exfiltrate data by making an HTTPS request to <code>evil-server.com:443</code>. Write a NetworkPolicy that blocks ALL egress EXCEPT to specific internal CIDR ranges (10.0.0.0/8) and port 53 DNS. Include both TCP and UDP DNS.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: egress-lockdown
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress:
  - to:
    - ipBlock:
        cidr: 10.0.0.0/8
  - ports:
    - protocol: UDP
      port: 53
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 53
    to:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Egress lockdown is the most powerful NetworkPolicy pattern for preventing data exfiltration. This policy says: pods can only send traffic to 10.0.0.0/8 (your private network) and to CoreDNS for name resolution. No HTTPS to the internet, no DNS tunneling (DNS is restricted to your own CoreDNS pods, not external DNS servers). On the CKS exam, egress controls are often the answer to "prevent data exfiltration" scenario questions.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to allow ingress from a specific external IP range <code>203.0.113.0/24</code> (your office VPN) to pods labeled <code>app: dashboard</code> on port 8443. Write the NetworkPolicy. Why is <code>ipBlock</code> less secure than <code>podSelector</code>?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-vpn-dashboard
  namespace: kubernetes-dashboard
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: dashboard
  policyTypes: [Ingress]
  ingress:
  - from:
    - ipBlock:
        cidr: 203.0.113.0/24
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 8443</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>Why ipBlock is less secure:</strong> IP-based rules can be spoofed or bypassed if the attacker is on the same network segment. Pod-to-pod rules using <code>podSelector</code> are cryptographically enforced by the CNI (based on pod identity, not IP). IP-based rules are a necessary evil for external traffic but should never be your ONLY control.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>ipBlock</code> is for traffic from outside the cluster (or from pods using hostNetwork). Within the cluster, always prefer <code>podSelector</code> and <code>namespaceSelector</code> — they're identity-based, not IP-based. On the CKS exam, if you see <code>ipBlock</code> in a policy, ask: is this for external traffic? If the traffic is pod-to-pod, use selectors instead.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're given a cluster where an attacker compromised the "easypj" pod. The attacker tries 4 things: (a) connect to postgres:5432, (b) curl https://evil.com, (c) scan the internal network, (d) write to a mounted hostPath. Write the complete set of NetworkPolicies that would have prevented (a), (b), and (c). Which attack does NetworkPolicy NOT prevent?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>NetworkPolicy prevents (a), (b), (c):</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># Prevent (a): Only postgres pod can reach port 5432
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: db-only-from-postgres-pod
  namespace: database
spec:
  podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: postgres}}
  policyTypes: [Ingress]
  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: easypj-api}}
    ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 5432}]
---
# Prevent (b) + (c): Default-deny egress, allow only DNS + postgres
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: restrict-egress
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: easypj}}
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress:
  - to: [{podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: postgres}}}]
    ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 5432}]
  - to: [{namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {name: kube-system}}, podSelector: {matchLabels: {k8s-app: kube-dns}}}]
    ports: [{protocol: UDP, port: 53}]</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>NetworkPolicy does NOT prevent (d)</strong> — <code>hostPath</code> writes are a filesystem operation, not a network operation. This requires PodSecurity (Ch 17), <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code> (Ch 18), or AppArmor (Ch 14).</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This question tests boundary awareness: knowing what NetworkPolicy CAN and CANNOT protect. NetworkPolicy is Layer 3/4 (IP/port). It cannot stop: filesystem operations (hostPath), process execution (shell spawn), syscalls (nsenter), or application-layer attacks (SQL injection). On the CKS exam, always identify the attack vector first, then choose the correct defense layer — don't use NetworkPolicy for non-network problems.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch6-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 6 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (8)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — a broken cluster state, and you produce the fix with real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You inherit <code>easypj-prod</code> with <code>policy-1-default-deny-ingress</code> and a matching <code>default-deny-egress</code> already applied. Pods are now in <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> — their readiness probes call <code>postgres.easypj-prod.svc.cluster.local:5432</code> and it never resolves. No other egress may be opened. Fix it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Diagnose first — confirm it's DNS, not the postgres rule, that's missing
kubectl exec -n easypj-prod deploy/easypj -- nslookup postgres.easypj-prod.svc.cluster.local
# → "connection timed out; no servers could be reached"   (DNS itself is blocked, not just postgres)

kubectl get netpol -n easypj-prod
# → default-deny-egress exists; NO allow-dns-egress policy present

# Fix — add the DNS-only egress allow (Pattern 5 / Policy 5 from this chapter)
kubectl apply -f - &lt;&lt;EOF
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-dns-egress
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress:
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system}
      podSelector:
        matchLabels: {k8s-app: kube-dns}
    ports:
    - {protocol: UDP, port: 53}
    - {protocol: TCP, port: 53}
EOF

# Re-verify
kubectl exec -n easypj-prod deploy/easypj -- nslookup postgres.easypj-prod.svc.cluster.local
# → resolves; readiness probes recover within one probe interval</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is §6.3 Pattern 7 and §6.8's DNS mechanic in its most common real-world shape: default-deny-egress was applied without its mandatory DNS companion rule. The fix adds ONLY UDP/TCP 53 to kube-dns — it does not touch, weaken, or remove the existing deny-all posture for anything else. Always pair egress lockdown with a DNS allow rule in the SAME change, never as an afterthought.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate wrote this policy intending ONLY to restrict <strong>egress</strong> for <code>app: easypj</code> pods. After applying it, <code>ingress-nginx</code> can no longer reach the easypj pods on port 8000 either — something the policy never mentions. Explain why, and fix it without removing the egress restriction.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># THE BROKEN POLICY — no policyTypes field at all
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: easypj-egress-lockdown
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels: {app: easypj}
  egress:                      # egress rules ARE present...
  - to:
    - podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: postgres}}
    ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 5432}]
  # ...so per §6.8 Rule 3, policyTypes DEFAULTS to [Ingress, Egress] — NOT
  # [Egress] alone! Ingress is ALWAYS implied by default, and Egress is
  # ALSO implied because egress rules exist. With NO ingress rules block
  # at all, that implied [Ingress] now means "deny all ingress" too.</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># THE FIX — restore ingress explicitly, keep the intended egress restriction
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: easypj-egress-lockdown
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels: {app: easypj}
  policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]   # BOTH now explicit — no silent surprises
  ingress:
  - from:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ingress-nginx}
    ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 8000}]
  egress:
  - to:
    - podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: postgres}}
    ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 5432}]</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the mirror image of the trap most candidates expect (§6.8 Rule 3 usually gets quoted as "egress rules with no policyTypes silently do nothing"). The less-known half of the same rule is just as dangerous: <strong>Ingress is implied by default no matter what</strong>, so an "egress-only" policy with zero ingress rules and no explicit <code>policyTypes</code> still isolates the pod for ingress too — with no rules to allow anything in. Always write <code>policyTypes</code> explicitly and list every rule block you actually intend, even the ones you think are "obviously" untouched.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>policy-2-allow-easypj-to-postgres</code> is applied and <code>kubectl get netpol</code> shows it as active — but easypj still cannot reach postgres at all, not even on port 5432. Find the root cause and fix it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Step 1 — the policy LOOKS right; check what it actually selects
kubectl get netpol policy-2-allow-easypj-to-postgres -n easypj-prod -o yaml | grep -A2 podSelector
#   matchLabels:
#     app: postgre        ← TYPO. Missing the trailing "s".

# Step 2 — confirm zero pods actually carry that exact label
kubectl get pods -n easypj-prod -l app=postgre
# → No resources found                     (the selector matches NOTHING)
kubectl get pods -n easypj-prod --show-labels | grep postgres
# → app=postgres                            (the REAL label, correctly spelled)

# Step 3 — fix the typo
kubectl patch netpol policy-2-allow-easypj-to-postgres -n easypj-prod --type=json \
  -p='[{"op":"replace","path":"/spec/podSelector/matchLabels/app","value":"postgres"}]'

# Step 4 — re-verify with a busybox probe (per §6.4)
kubectl run t --rm -it --image=busybox --restart=Never -n easypj-prod -- \
  nc -zv -w3 postgres-service 5432
# → succeeded</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A <code>podSelector</code> that matches zero pods is syntactically valid YAML and applies without any error — it simply protects nothing and allows nothing, which LOOKS identical to "the policy isn't enforced" but is actually "the policy is enforced perfectly against an empty set." This is the single most common real-world NetworkPolicy bug (per this chapter's Under the Hood ①) and the first thing to check whenever a policy "does nothing": compare the selector's labels against <code>kubectl get pods --show-labels</code> character for character.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Security review flags this policy as unexpectedly permissive — pods in ANY namespace labeled <code>role: client</code> can reach the dashboard, not just clients inside the <code>team: platform</code> namespace as intended. Identify the exact bug and fix it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># THE BUG — two separate list items ("-") = OR, not AND
  ingress:
  - from:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels: {team: platform}
    - podSelector:                 # ← its OWN "-" makes this a SEPARATE rule
        matchLabels: {role: client}
  # Reads as: (any pod in a team=platform namespace) OR (any role=client
  # pod anywhere in the policy's own namespace) — far broader than intended.</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># THE FIX — merge under ONE list item so both conditions must hold (AND)
  ingress:
  - from:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels: {team: platform}
      podSelector:                 # ← SAME "-" as namespaceSelector above = AND
        matchLabels: {role: client}
  # Reads as: pods labeled role=client, ONLY inside namespaces labeled
  # team=platform. Exactly the narrow rule that was intended.</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is §6.8's "#1 exam trap" in its exact real-world form. The fix is a single space of indentation — moving <code>podSelector</code> to be a sibling key under the SAME <code>-</code> as <code>namespaceSelector</code> instead of its own new <code>-</code>. Always confirm which one you shipped with <code>kubectl describe netpol</code>: one combined "From" block means AND, two separate "From" blocks means OR.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A junior admin reports "I applied 6 NetworkPolicies and NOTHING is being blocked — every pod can still reach every other pod." <code>kubectl get netpol -A</code> shows all 6 as present with no errors. What do you check first, and what's the likely fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Check which CNI is actually running
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E 'calico|cilium|weave|flannel'
# → kube-flannel-ds-abc12   1/1   Running   ...     ← Flannel, NO NetworkPolicy support

# Confirm: the objects exist in etcd, but nothing enforces them
kubectl get netpol -A
# → all 6 policies listed, no errors — because Kubernetes' API server
#   only VALIDATES and STORES the object; it never enforces traffic itself

# Fix: migrate to a NetworkPolicy-capable CNI (commonly Calico, installed
# alongside or replacing Flannel; exact migration steps are cluster-specific
# and out of scope for a single command, but the diagnostic conclusion is
# definitive once the CNI check above returns Flannel).</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the CNI-dependency gotcha from §6.2 in its purest form: a completely correct set of NetworkPolicy YAML, applied without any errors, that has ZERO real-world effect because Flannel silently ignores every NetworkPolicy object. On the CKS exam the cluster's CNI is always pre-configured to support NetworkPolicy — but this exact failure mode is common in real self-managed clusters and is always the first thing to rule out before doubting your YAML.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Despite a namespace-wide default-deny-ingress policy, one pod — <code>legacy-metrics-exporter</code> — remains reachable from every other namespace on port 9100. <code>kubectl get netpol</code> shows no policy excludes it. Find the reason and the correct remediation.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Check the pod spec for hostNetwork
kubectl get pod legacy-metrics-exporter -n easypj-prod -o jsonpath='{.spec.hostNetwork}'
# → true

# This is the root cause: hostNetwork:true makes the pod use the NODE's
# network namespace directly instead of its own — NetworkPolicy, which is
# enforced per-pod-veth by the CNI, never sees this traffic at all.

# Remediation options, in order of preference:
# 1. Remove hostNetwork: true if it isn't actually required — most
#    exporters work fine with a normal pod network + a Service.
kubectl patch deploy legacy-metrics-exporter -n easypj-prod --type=json \
  -p='[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/template/spec/hostNetwork"}]'

# 2. If hostNetwork truly is required (e.g. it must see the node's real
#    interfaces), NetworkPolicy CANNOT help — restrict at the NODE level
#    instead (host firewall / iptables / a PSA policy blocking hostNetwork
#    pods outside an approved namespace).</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the same boundary-awareness principle Lab Q&amp;A Q7 and Q10 test: NetworkPolicy is enforced by the CNI at each pod's OWN network namespace (veth). A <code>hostNetwork: true</code> pod has no veth of its own — it IS the node's network namespace — so there is nothing for the CNI to attach policy to. On the exam, if a pod ignores every NetworkPolicy while other pods in the same namespace obey it, <code>hostNetwork</code> is the first field to check.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You wrote a correct ingress-allow policy in the <code>database</code> namespace permitting <code>web</code> pods to reach <code>postgres</code> on 5432 (as in Lab Q&amp;A Q6). Traffic is STILL blocked. <code>kubectl describe netpol</code> in <code>database</code> shows the rule exactly as intended. What's missing?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Check the SOURCE namespace's own policies — the destination side being
# correct is not sufficient (§6.8 Rule 4: BOTH sides must agree)
kubectl get netpol -n web
# → web-default-deny-egress    Egress    (no matching rule to "database" at all)

# The "web" namespace has its own default-deny-egress with no exception for
# reaching postgres in "database" — so the connection never leaves the
# source pod's egress chain, regardless of how permissive the destination
# namespace's ingress rule is.

# Fix — add the missing egress half in the SOURCE namespace
kubectl apply -f - &lt;&lt;EOF
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-to-db
  namespace: web
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels: {tier: frontend}
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress:
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: database}}
      podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: postgres}}
    ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 5432}]
EOF</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is §6.8 Rule 4 made concrete: a connection needs the source pod's egress rules AND the destination pod's ingress rules to BOTH allow it — checking only one side and assuming the other is fine is the most common cross-namespace NetworkPolicy debugging mistake. Always inspect NetworkPolicies in BOTH the source and destination namespaces before concluding a rule is "wrong."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A <code>namespaceSelector</code> rule using <code>kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ingress-nginx</code> matches nothing on an older cluster that was upgraded in place from Kubernetes 1.19. Ingress traffic that should be allowed is blocked. Diagnose and fix without upgrading the cluster.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># The kubernetes.io/metadata.name label is auto-applied by the API server
# to every namespace, but ONLY from Kubernetes v1.21 onward. A namespace
# that existed BEFORE the cluster was upgraded past 1.21 never received
# this label retroactively — the admission controller that sets it only
# runs on namespace CREATE, not on existing namespaces.

# Confirm the label is actually missing
kubectl get ns ingress-nginx --show-labels
# → NAME             STATUS   AGE   LABELS
#   ingress-nginx     Active   940d  &lt;none&gt;          ← no auto-label present

# Fix — apply the label manually (idempotent, safe to run any time)
kubectl label ns ingress-nginx kubernetes.io/metadata.name=ingress-nginx

# Re-verify
kubectl get ns ingress-nginx --show-labels
# → kubernetes.io/metadata.name=ingress-nginx
kubectl run t --rm -it --image=busybox --restart=Never -n ingress-nginx -- \
  wget -qO- --timeout=3 http://easypj-service.easypj-prod:8000/
# → succeeds now that the namespaceSelector actually resolves</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Every example in this chapter that uses <code>kubernetes.io/metadata.name</code> assumes the label is present — which is true for any namespace created on Kubernetes 1.21+, but NOT guaranteed for namespaces that predate an in-place upgrade. This is a real "it worked in my kind cluster but not in the customer's ancient production cluster" bug. The fix is always safe: manually labeling a namespace with its own name is idempotent and matches what the API server would have set automatically on a fresh cluster.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
