        <article class="chapter" id="ch7">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 7 · Ingress Security with TLS</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Every Kubernetes application that serves users over the internet must terminate TLS. Without TLS, every request — including passwords, session tokens, and personal data — travels over the network in <strong>plaintext</strong>, readable by anyone on any network hop between the user and your cluster. TLS is the most fundamental security control for web applications, and Kubernetes makes it straightforward: store the certificate in a Secret, reference it in an Ingress resource, and the Ingress controller handles the rest. This chapter teaches you how to create TLS certificates, automate their lifecycle with cert-manager and Let's Encrypt, and enforce HTTPS with redirects and security headers — all within the easypj cluster.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟢 Beginner</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🔐 TLS/SSL</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🌐 Ingress</span>
            </div>
            </div><!-- /chapter-intro -->

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Understand TLS termination — where encryption starts and ends in a K8s cluster</li>
                    <li>✅ Create TLS Secrets from existing certificates and reference them in Ingress resources</li>
                    <li>✅ Deploy cert-manager to automate certificate issuance and renewal from Let's Encrypt</li>
                    <li>✅ Configure ClusterIssuer vs Issuer — cluster-wide vs namespace-scoped certificate authorities</li>
                    <li>✅ Enforce HTTPS with SSL redirect, HSTS headers, and TLS version restrictions</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKS Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Setup <span class="cka-weight">10%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Minimize Microservice Vulns <span class="cka-weight">20%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🧠 The TLS Exam Pattern</h5>
                <p>CKS TLS tasks typically ask you to: (1) create a TLS Secret from provided certificate files, (2) configure an Ingress to use that Secret for TLS termination, (3) ensure HTTP traffic is redirected to HTTPS. The exam provides the certificate and key files — you do not need to generate them. Focus on the <code>kubectl create secret tls</code> command, the Ingress TLS spec, and the SSL redirect annotation.</p>
