            <!-- ─────────── §7.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s7-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">7.1 TLS Certificates in Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>TLS (Transport Layer Security) is the protocol that encrypts HTTP traffic — turning it into HTTPS. In Kubernetes, TLS termination typically happens at the <strong>Ingress controller</strong>: the Ingress controller holds the TLS certificate, decrypts incoming HTTPS traffic, and forwards the plain HTTP request to the backend pod inside the cluster. This is called <strong>TLS termination at the edge</strong> — the most common pattern for Kubernetes deployments.</p>

                <h4>TLS Termination — What It Means for easypj</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Three TLS deployment patterns — from none to end-to-end</div>
<pre>
  ❌ WITHOUT TLS:
  👤 User ──── HTTP (plaintext) ──────────────── easypj pod
  "password=secret" visible on every network hop.
  Anyone on the WiFi, ISP, or any router in between can read it.

  ✅ WITH TLS (termination at Ingress) — STANDARD PATTERN:
                         Ingress
  👤 User ──── HTTPS ──── [decrypt] ──── HTTP ─── easypj pod
      encrypted        TLS cert        internal plain
      on internet      stored here      (inside cluster)

  Traffic is encrypted from the user's browser to the Ingress controller.
  Inside the cluster, it is plain HTTP — but that is acceptable because
  the cluster network should be protected by NetworkPolicies (Chapter 6).

  🔐 WITH END-TO-END TLS (mTLS — Chapter 22):
  👤 User ──── HTTPS ──── Ingress ──── HTTPS ─── easypj pod
      encrypted        re-encrypts     pod cert verified
      both hops        to pod          (mTLS)
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Certificate Types — Which to Use When</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Certificate Type</th><th>Use Case</th><th>Browser Trust</th><th>Auto-Renewal</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Self-Signed</strong> (openssl req -x509)</td><td>Local dev, testing only</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">⚠️ "Not Secure" warning</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ Manual</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Let's Encrypt</strong> via cert-manager</td><td>Production public domains</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Trusted by all browsers</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Automatic (90-day certs)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Private CA</strong> (internal)</td><td>Internal services, air-gapped</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Only if CA cert is distributed</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Depends on CA setup</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Wildcard</strong> (*.easypj.com)</td><td>All subdomains with one cert</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ From public CA</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ One compromise = all subdomains</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Creating a TLS Secret — The CKS Exam Essential</h4>
                <p>The CKS exam expects you to create a TLS Secret from provided certificate files. This is the most common TLS task:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Creating a TLS Secret and referencing it in an Ingress</div>
<pre>
  # Step 1: Create TLS Secret from existing cert+key files
  $ kubectl create secret tls easypj-tls \
      --cert=/path/to/easypj.com.crt \
      --key=/path/to/easypj.com.key \
      -n easypj-prod

  # Step 2: Reference the Secret in an Ingress resource
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: Ingress
  metadata:
    name: easypj-ingress
    namespace: easypj-prod
  spec:
    tls:
    - hosts:
      - easypj.com
      - www.easypj.com
      secretName: easypj-tls          # ← The TLS Secret created above
    rules:
    - host: easypj.com
      http:
        paths:
        - path: /
          pathType: Prefix
          backend:
            service:
              name: easypj-service
              port:
                number: 8000

  # Step 3: Verify TLS is working
  $ curl -v https://easypj.com/jobs/
  → TLS handshake succeeds → HTTP 200 with job listings HTML

  # Step 4: Check the certificate details
  $ kubectl get secret easypj-tls -n easypj-prod -o yaml
  → data.tls.crt and data.tls.key are base64-encoded
  $ echo "&lt;base64-cert&gt;" | base64 -d | openssl x509 -noout -text
  → Shows subject, issuer, validity dates, SANs
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 kubectl create secret tls — The One Command to Remember</h5>
                    <p>The exam provides certificate files. Your job is to create the Secret and configure the Ingress. The command is <code>kubectl create secret tls &lt;name&gt; --cert=&lt;cert-file&gt; --key=&lt;key-file&gt;</code>. The Secret type must be <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code> — this type is automatically set when you use <code>kubectl create secret tls</code>. Do NOT use <code>kubectl create secret generic</code> for TLS certs — the Ingress controller will not recognize them.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Two Ordinary Ingress Fundamentals TLS Quietly Depends On</h4>
                <p>Every example in this chapter assumes two non-TLS-specific pieces of the <code>Ingress</code> object are already correct — <code>ingressClassName</code> and the relationship between <code>tls.hosts</code> and <code>rules[].host</code>. Neither is a TLS setting on its own, but a broken one produces symptoms that look exactly like a TLS bug, so a quick refresher belongs here before the rest of the chapter builds on top of it.</p>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>ingressClassName</code> — Which Controller Actually Answers This Ingress</h5>
                    <p>Every example Ingress in this chapter omits <code>spec.ingressClassName</code> for brevity, assuming the single ingress-nginx controller the CKS exam environment ships with. In a real (or multi-controller) cluster, the official Kubernetes docs make <code>ingressClassName</code> the recommended routing field: <code>spec.ingressClassName: nginx</code> tells Kubernetes exactly which installed <code>IngressClass</code> — and therefore which controller — should watch this object. If <code>ingressClassName</code> is omitted, Kubernetes falls back to whichever <code>IngressClass</code> carries the annotation <code>ingressclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: "true"</code>; if none is marked default (or more than one is), the Ingress can end up unserved by any controller with no error on the object itself. The older <code>kubernetes.io/ingress.class</code> annotation still works on many controllers for backward compatibility — recognize it in legacy manifests, but write <code>ingressClassName</code> in anything new.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ A Host Listed Only in <code>tls.hosts</code> Still Needs a Matching <code>rules[].host</code></h5>
                    <p>Several examples in this chapter (and Q10 in the Lab Q&amp;A below) list <code>www.easypj.com</code> in <code>spec.tls[0].hosts</code> so the certificate's SAN covers it, without necessarily adding a separate <code>spec.rules</code> entry for that exact host. That is enough for the TLS <em>handshake</em> to succeed — the Ingress controller matches the incoming SNI hostname against the <code>tls.hosts</code> list and presents the right certificate — but it is not enough for the <em>request</em> to go anywhere afterward: once decrypted, routing is decided purely by <code>rules[].host</code>, and a host with no matching rule falls through to the Ingress controller's default backend (typically a 404), not to whichever rule looks "closest." For a production Ingress meant to serve both names, add an explicit <code>rules</code> entry for <code>www.easypj.com</code> too (or redirect it to the apex, as this chapter's wildcard/apex scenario drill shows) — never rely on a shared <code>tls.hosts</code> entry to imply a route that doesn't actually exist.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What's Inside a TLS Certificate — And Why Each Field Matters</h4>
                <p>A TLS certificate is not just "a file that enables HTTPS." It is a structured document defined by the X.509 standard, containing specific fields that your browser and the Ingress controller validate before establishing a secure connection. Understanding these fields helps you debug TLS errors on the exam:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">X.509 Certificate Fields — What Your Browser Checks</div>
<pre>
  $ openssl x509 -in easypj.com.crt -noout -text

  Certificate:
      Data:
          Version: 3 (0x2)
          Serial Number: 04:2a:8f:...          ← Unique ID. Revoked certs listed by serial.
          Issuer: C=US, O=Let's Encrypt, CN=R3  ← Who SIGNED this cert. Browser must trust this CA.
          Validity:
              Not Before: Jan  1 00:00:00 2024 GMT  ← Cert is INVALID before this
              Not After : Apr  1 00:00:00 2024 GMT   ← Cert EXPIRES after this (90 days for LE)
          Subject: CN=easypj.com                 ← Who this cert identifies. Must match the URL.
          Subject Public Key Info:
              Public Key Algorithm: rsaEncryption
              RSA Public-Key: (2048 bit)         ← The public key. Private key stays in the Secret.
          X509v3 extensions:
              X509v3 Subject Alternative Name:   ← CRITICAL: browsers check this, not CN.
                  DNS:easypj.com, DNS:www.easypj.com

  🔑 KEY INSIGHT: Browsers validate the SAN (Subject Alternative Name) field,
     NOT the CN (Common Name). If easypj.com is in CN but not SAN, the browser
     will show a certificate error. Always include the domain in SAN.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How the TLS Handshake Works — What Happens in the First 300ms</h4>
                <p>Every HTTPS connection begins with a TLS handshake — a multi-step negotiation between the browser and the Ingress controller. Understanding this sequence helps you diagnose TLS errors when users report "connection not secure":</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">TLS 1.3 Handshake — Client Hello to Encrypted Data</div>
<pre>
  CLIENT (Browser)                           SERVER (Ingress Controller)
  ─────────────────                         ────────────────────────────
  1. ClientHello ─────────────────────▶
     • Supported TLS versions (1.2, 1.3)
     • Supported cipher suites
     • SNI: "easypj.com" (which site?)
                                          2. ServerHello ◀─────────────────
                                             • Chosen: TLS 1.3
                                             • Chosen cipher: AES-256-GCM
                                             • Server Certificate ▶
                                               (the easypj-tls Secret!)
  3. Verify certificate:
     • Signed by trusted CA? ✅
     • Not expired? ✅
     • SAN matches easypj.com? ✅
                                          4. Key Exchange (Diffie-Hellman)
  5. Symmetric encryption established
     All subsequent data is encrypted with AES-256-GCM

  ⏱️  Total time: ~300ms on a good connection.
  ❌ FAILURE POINTS:
     • Certificate expired → browser shows NET::ERR_CERT_DATE_INVALID
     • SAN mismatch → NET::ERR_CERT_COMMON_NAME_INVALID
     • Untrusted CA (self-signed) → NET::ERR_CERT_AUTHORITY_INVALID
     • TLS version too old (1.0/1.1 disabled by annotation) → handshake fails
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Why HTTP Inside the Cluster Is Acceptable — The Trust Boundary</h4>
                <p>After TLS termination at the Ingress controller, traffic to the backend pod is plain HTTP. This concerns many newcomers: "Isn't that insecure?" The answer is: <strong>it depends on your trust model.</strong> The Ingress controller and your application pods are inside the same cluster network, protected by NetworkPolicies (Chapter 6). An attacker who can sniff internal pod-to-pod traffic has already compromised a pod on that node — at which point they can read data from memory directly, making TLS between pods redundant for confidentiality (though not for authentication — that is where mTLS comes in, covered in Chapter 22). For the CKS exam, the standard pattern is TLS termination at the Ingress controller with HTTP to the backend. End-to-end TLS (mTLS) is an advanced topic tested separately.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Reconciling Note — The <code>Ingress</code> API Is Frozen; Gateway API Is Its Official Successor (Not Yet on the CKS Exam)</h5>
                    <p>Everything in this chapter is built on <code>networking.k8s.io/v1</code> <code>Ingress</code> — and that remains the correct choice for the CKS exam and for the overwhelming majority of clusters running today. But the official Kubernetes documentation is direct about where the API itself now stands: <code>Ingress</code> has been GA (stable) since Kubernetes v1.19, and per the docs it "is no longer being developed, and will have no further changes or updates made to it" — Kubernetes has no plans to remove it, but it is frozen in place. The project's own recommendation for new work is the <a href="https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/">Gateway API</a>, a separate, more expressive resource family (<code>Gateway</code>, <code>HTTPRoute</code>, <code>ReferenceGrant</code>) that models TLS through a <code>Gateway</code>'s <code>listeners[].tls.certificateRefs</code> instead of an Ingress's <code>spec.tls[].secretName</code> — same underlying idea (a Secret holding <code>tls.crt</code>/<code>tls.key</code>), a richer configuration surface layered on top.</p>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th></th><th><code>Ingress</code> (this chapter)</th><th>Gateway API (the official successor)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>API status</td><td>GA since v1.19 — frozen, no new features ever</td><td>Actively developed — the model the project recommends going forward</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>TLS config lives on</td><td><code>Ingress.spec.tls[].secretName</code></td><td><code>Gateway.spec.listeners[].tls.certificateRefs</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Controller selection</td><td><code>ingressClassName</code> → <code>IngressClass</code></td><td><code>gatewayClassName</code> → <code>GatewayClass</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>On the CKS exam today?</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes — this is what's tested</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Not yet part of the CKS curriculum</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p class="mb-0">Nothing in this chapter is obsolete — the exam, cert-manager, and the vast majority of production clusters today all still speak Ingress, and cert-manager's <code>Certificate</code> resource (§7.2) attaches identically to either API. Treat Gateway API the way §1.4 treats the CNCF Lifecycle Phases: a second, increasingly authoritative lens on the same underlying problem, worth recognizing by name well before it ever appears on an exam blueprint.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §7.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s7-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">7.2 cert-manager — Automated Certificate Management</h3>
                <p>Manually creating TLS certificates works, but it does not scale. Certificates expire. You forget to renew them. Your site goes down. <strong>cert-manager</strong> solves this by automating the entire certificate lifecycle: it watches Ingress resources, requests certificates from Let's Encrypt (or a private CA), stores them as Kubernetes Secrets, and automatically renews them 30 days before expiry. Once configured, you never think about certificate renewal again.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 cert-manager at a Glance</h5>
                    <p><strong>Home:</strong> cert-manager.io · <strong>License:</strong> Apache 2.0 · <strong>CKS Exam:</strong> ✅ Must know the concept. The exam may ask you to configure a ClusterIssuer and annotate an Ingress. <strong>How it works:</strong> cert-manager runs as a controller in your cluster. It watches for Certificate resources (or Ingress resources with annotations) and communicates with a Certificate Authority (CA) to fulfill them using the ACME protocol (for Let's Encrypt) or other protocols (for private CAs).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>cert-manager Certificate Lifecycle — Automated Flow for easypj</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">7-step automated certificate lifecycle — zero manual intervention</div>
<pre>
  1. You create an Ingress with annotation:
     cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: "letsencrypt-prod"
                        │
                        ▼
  2. cert-manager detects the Ingress → creates Certificate resource
                        │
                        ▼
  3. cert-manager creates a CertificateRequest
     → challenges Let's Encrypt via HTTP-01 challenge
     (Let's Encrypt makes an HTTP request to your domain to verify ownership)
                        │
                        ▼
  4. Let's Encrypt verifies you own easypj.com
     → issues a signed certificate (valid 90 days)
                        │
                        ▼
  5. cert-manager stores the certificate in a K8s Secret: easypj-tls
                        │
                        ▼
  6. Ingress controller reads the Secret → serves HTTPS to users
                        │
                        ▼
  7. 30 days before expiry → cert-manager AUTO-RENEWS! 🔄
     Zero manual intervention. The site never goes down due to expired certs.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>ClusterIssuer Configuration — Let's Encrypt</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ClusterIssuer YAML for Let's Encrypt production</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
  kind: ClusterIssuer                    # Cluster-wide (vs Issuer = namespace-scoped)
  metadata:
    name: letsencrypt-prod
  spec:
    acme:
      server: https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory  # Production CA
      email: admin@easypj.com            # Let's Encrypt sends expiry notifications here
      privateKeySecretRef:
        name: letsencrypt-prod-key       # cert-manager stores its ACME account key here
      solvers:
      - http01:                          # HTTP-01 challenge: Let's Encrypt checks your domain
          ingress:
            class: nginx                 # Which Ingress controller handles the challenge
  ---
  # Annotate your Ingress to use this ClusterIssuer:
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: Ingress
  metadata:
    name: easypj-ingress
    namespace: easypj-prod
    annotations:
      cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: "letsencrypt-prod"  # ← This triggers cert-manager
  spec:
    tls:
    - hosts:
      - easypj.com
      secretName: easypj-tls             # cert-manager will CREATE this Secret
    rules:
    - host: easypj.com
      http:
        paths:
        - path: /
          pathType: Prefix
          backend:
            service:
              name: easypj-service
              port:
                number: 8000
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>ClusterIssuer vs Issuer — When to Use Each</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Scope</th><th>Use Case</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>ClusterIssuer</strong></td><td>Cluster-wide — usable from any namespace</td><td>Shared Let's Encrypt config for the whole cluster. One ClusterIssuer serves all teams.</td><td><code>letsencrypt-prod</code> — every namespace can use it for their domain certs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Issuer</strong></td><td>Namespace-scoped</td><td>Team-specific CA config. Different namespaces use different CAs or different ACME accounts.</td><td><code>easypj-staging-issuer</code> — only the easypj namespace can use it</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Let's Encrypt Rate Limits — Use Staging First</h5>
                    <p>Let's Encrypt production has strict rate limits: 50 certificates per domain per week. If you are testing your cert-manager configuration, use the staging endpoint: <code>https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory</code>. Staging certificates are not trusted by browsers, but they let you verify that your DNS, Ingress, and cert-manager configuration are correct before you switch to production. On the CKS exam, you will use a provided CA endpoint — not the public Let's Encrypt.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>HTTP-01 vs DNS-01 Challenges — How Let's Encrypt Verifies Domain Ownership</h4>
                <p>Before Let's Encrypt issues a certificate, it must verify that you control the domain. It does this through <strong>challenges</strong> — cryptographic proofs of domain ownership. cert-manager supports two challenge types, and choosing the right one depends on your infrastructure:</p>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left">
                        <h5>HTTP-01 Challenge</h5>
                        <p>Let's Encrypt makes an HTTP request to <code>http://easypj.com/.well-known/acme-challenge/&lt;token&gt;</code>. cert-manager creates a temporary pod or configures the Ingress to serve the challenge response. This is the simplest method — it requires only that port 80 is reachable from the internet. <strong>Limitation:</strong> Cannot issue wildcard certificates. Does not work if your cluster is behind a firewall that blocks inbound HTTP.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel-right">
                        <h5>DNS-01 Challenge</h5>
                        <p>cert-manager creates a TXT record in your DNS zone (<code>_acme-challenge.easypj.com</code>). Let's Encrypt queries DNS to verify the record exists. This method works even if port 80 is blocked — it only requires DNS API access. <strong>Advantage:</strong> Supports wildcard certificates (<code>*.easypj.com</code>). <strong>Limitation:</strong> Requires cert-manager to have API credentials for your DNS provider (Route53, Cloudflare, Google Cloud DNS).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The Certificate Resource — Explicit Control Over Renewal Timing</h4>
                <p>Annotating an Ingress with <code>cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer</code> is the fastest path to a certificate, but cert-manager also lets you create a standalone <code>Certificate</code> resource directly. Production teams generally prefer this because it makes the exact set of covered domains and the renewal window explicit, reviewable, and version-controllable, instead of implicit inside an Ingress annotation:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml — cert-manager Certificate resource</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: Certificate
metadata:
  name: easypj-tls
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  secretName: easypj-tls              <span class="comment"># cert-manager writes tls.crt/tls.key here</span>
  duration: 2160h                     <span class="comment"># 90 days — total certificate lifetime</span>
  renewBefore: 720h                   <span class="comment"># 30 days — renew this far ahead of expiry</span>
  dnsNames:
    - easypj.com
    - www.easypj.com
  issuerRef:
    name: letsencrypt-prod
    kind: ClusterIssuer</code></pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Default If Omitted</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>duration</code></td><td>Total requested certificate lifetime</td><td>2160h (90 days) — Let's Encrypt caps it here regardless of what is requested</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>renewBefore</code></td><td>How long before expiry cert-manager starts renewal</td><td>Roughly 1/3 of the remaining lifetime (~30 days for a 90-day cert)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>secretName</code></td><td>Where the resulting <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code> Secret is written</td><td>Required — no default</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>Debugging cert-manager — When Certificates Do Not Issue</h4>
                <p>The most common cert-manager failure on the CKS exam is a Certificate resource stuck in a "Pending" or "Failed" state. Here is how to diagnose and fix it:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🔴 Certificate Stuck — Diagnostic Commands</div>
<pre>
  # 1. Check the Certificate resource status
  $ kubectl describe certificate easypj-tls -n easypj-prod
  → Look for "Ready: False" and the "Reason" field.
    Common reasons: "NotFound" (Secret doesn't exist yet — normal),
    "Pending" (waiting for challenge), "Failed" (challenge failed).

  # 2. Check the CertificateRequest
  $ kubectl get certificaterequest -n easypj-prod
  $ kubectl describe certificaterequest &lt;name&gt;
  → Shows the exact challenge status and any error messages.

  # 3. Check the Order (ACME order resource)
  $ kubectl get orders -n easypj-prod
  $ kubectl describe order &lt;name&gt;
  → Shows the ACME authorization URL and challenge status.

  # 4. Check the Challenge resource directly
  $ kubectl get challenges -n easypj-prod
  $ kubectl describe challenge &lt;name&gt;
  → "Reason: Waiting for HTTP-01 challenge propagation"
  → "Reason: DNS record not found" means DNS-01 failed.

  # 5. Check cert-manager logs
  $ kubectl logs -n cert-manager deploy/cert-manager
  → Look for ERROR lines about your domain.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The ACME Protocol Flow — What cert-manager Actually Does</h4>
                <p>cert-manager implements the ACME (Automated Certificate Management Environment) protocol — the same protocol used by Let's Encrypt. Understanding the ACME flow demystifies the cert-manager logs and helps you troubleshoot on the exam:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ACME Protocol Flow — cert-manager to Let's Encrypt</div>
<pre>
  cert-manager                              Let's Encrypt (ACME Server)
  ────────────                              ───────────────────────────
  1. Create ACME account
     POST /acme/new-acct ────────────────▶  Account created. Returns account URL.

  2. Submit certificate order
     POST /acme/new-order ───────────────▶  Order created. Returns:
       {                                      • Order URL
         "identifiers": [                     • Authorization URLs (one per domain)
           {"type":"dns","value":"easypj.com"}, • Finalize URL
           {"type":"dns","value":"www.easypj.com"}
         ]
       }

  3. Fetch authorizations
     POST /acme/authz/&lt;id&gt; ───────────────▶  Returns challenge details:
                                                • HTTP-01: token + expected response
                                                • DNS-01: TXT record value

  4. Complete challenges
     (HTTP-01: serve token at .well-known/)  Let's Encrypt verifies:
     (DNS-01: create TXT record)              • HTTP: GET http://easypj.com/.well-known/...
                                              • DNS: dig TXT _acme-challenge.easypj.com

  5. Finalize order
     POST /acme/finalize ─────────────────▶  Submit CSR (Certificate Signing Request)
       { "csr": "&lt;base64-encoded CSR&gt;" }

  6. Download certificate
     GET /acme/cert/&lt;id&gt; ─────────────────▶  Returns signed certificate chain
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §7.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s7-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">7.3 Enforcing HTTPS — Redirecting HTTP to HTTPS</h3>
                <p>Having a TLS certificate is not enough — you must also ensure that users always use HTTPS. A user who types <code>http://easypj.com</code> into their browser will connect over plain HTTP unless you explicitly redirect them to HTTPS. This is configured through Ingress annotations on the NGINX Ingress Controller — the most commonly used Ingress controller on the CKS exam.</p>

                <h4>Ingress Security Annotations — Complete Reference</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Annotation</th><th>Effect</th><th>Why It Matters</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "true"</code></td><td>Forces HTTP→HTTPS redirect (301)</td><td>Users who type http://easypj.com are automatically sent to https://easypj.com. Non-negotiable for production.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/configuration-snippet: &#124;</code><br/><code>  add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"</code></td><td>Adds HSTS header</td><td>Tells browsers: "always use HTTPS for this domain for the next year." Prevents SSL stripping attacks.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/configuration-snippet: &#124;</code><br/><code>  add_header X-Frame-Options "DENY"</code></td><td>Blocks clickjacking</td><td>Prevents your site from being embedded in an iframe on an attacker's page.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-protocols: "TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3"</code></td><td>Only allow modern TLS</td><td>Disables TLS 1.0 and 1.1 — both are broken and vulnerable to attacks like POODLE and BEAST.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/limit-rps: "10"</code></td><td>Rate limiting (10 req/sec per IP)</td><td>Prevents brute-force attacks and DoS by limiting how many requests a single IP can make.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-ciphers: "ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:..."</code></td><td>Restricts allowed cipher suites</td><td>Pairs with <code>ssl-protocols</code> — even on TLS 1.2+, weak CBC-mode ciphers are still vulnerable to padding-oracle attacks. Allow only modern AEAD (GCM) ciphers.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-passthrough: "true"</code></td><td>Disables TLS termination at the Ingress entirely</td><td>Routes purely on the SNI hostname; the backend pod terminates its own TLS. Requires the controller's <code>--enable-ssl-passthrough</code> flag — off by default.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/backend-protocol: "HTTPS"</code></td><td>Re-encrypts Ingress→pod traffic</td><td>The Ingress still terminates the client's TLS session, but opens a second TLS session to the backend pod instead of forwarding plain HTTP — closes the plaintext CNI-network gap.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/limit-whitelist: "10.0.0.0/8"</code></td><td>Exempts a CIDR range from rate limiting</td><td>Lets internal cluster/VPN traffic bypass <code>limit-rps</code> so health checks and internal callers are never throttled.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Complete Secure Ingress for easypj — All Controls Applied</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Production-ready Ingress YAML with TLS, HSTS, and security headers</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: Ingress
  metadata:
    name: easypj-secure-ingress
    namespace: easypj-prod
    annotations:
      # --- TLS &amp; HTTPS Enforcement ---
      cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: "letsencrypt-prod"
      nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "true"
      nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-protocols: "TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3"

      # --- Security Headers ---
      nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/configuration-snippet: |
        add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains" always;
        add_header X-Frame-Options "DENY" always;
        add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff" always;
        add_header X-XSS-Protection "1; mode=block" always;
        add_header Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin" always;
        add_header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'" always;

      # --- Rate Limiting ---
      nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/limit-rps: "20"
      nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/limit-whitelist: "10.0.0.0/8"  # Internal traffic unlimited

  spec:
    tls:
    - hosts:
      - easypj.com
      - www.easypj.com
      secretName: easypj-tls
    rules:
    - host: easypj.com
      http:
        paths:
        - path: /
          pathType: Prefix
          backend:
            service:
              name: easypj-service
              port:
                number: 8000
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How HSTS Works — Why It Is Critical</h4>
                <p>HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) solves a fundamental problem with HTTPS: the first request a user makes to your site may still be over HTTP. An attacker on the same network (coffee shop WiFi, airport, compromised router) can intercept that first HTTP request and redirect the user to a fake site — this is called an <strong>SSL stripping attack</strong>. HSTS tells the browser: "For the next year (31,536,000 seconds), always use HTTPS for this domain — do not even attempt HTTP." After the first successful HTTPS visit, the browser enforces this automatically. The <code>includeSubDomains</code> directive extends this protection to all subdomains (www.easypj.com, api.easypj.com, admin.easypj.com).</p>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>⚠️ HSTS Preloading — The Point of No Return</h5>
                    <p>You can submit your domain to the <strong>HSTS Preload List</strong> (hstspreload.org), which is hardcoded into Chrome, Firefox, and Safari. Once on this list, browsers will NEVER attempt HTTP for your domain — even on the very first visit. This is the strongest protection, but it is irreversible in practice. If you ever need to serve HTTP on any subdomain, you cannot be on the preload list. Do not submit unless you are 100% certain you will always serve HTTPS everywhere.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Testing Your TLS Configuration — curl and SSL Labs</h4>
                <p>After configuring TLS, always verify it works correctly before declaring the task complete. Here are the essential verification commands — use them on every TLS task:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">TLS Verification Commands</div>
<pre>
  # 1. Verify HTTPS is serving (no certificate error)
  $ curl -vI https://easypj.com 2>&1 | grep -E 'Server certificate|HTTP/'
  → HTTP/2 200
  → Server certificate: subject=CN=easypj.com, issuer=C=US,O=Let's Encrypt

  # 2. Verify HTTP→HTTPS redirect works
  $ curl -vI http://easypj.com 2>&1 | grep -E 'Location|HTTP/'
  → HTTP/1.1 308 Permanent Redirect
  → Location: https://easypj.com/

  # 3. Verify HSTS header is present
  $ curl -sI https://easypj.com | grep Strict-Transport
  → Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

  # 4. Verify TLS version (1.2+ only)
  $ openssl s_client -connect easypj.com:443 -tls1_2 < /dev/null 2>&1 | grep Verify
  → Verify return code: 0 (ok) — TLS 1.2 works
  $ openssl s_client -connect easypj.com:443 -tls1_1 < /dev/null 2>&1 | grep Verify
  → (should fail or return error — TLS 1.1 is blocked)

  # 5. Check certificate expiry date
  $ echo | openssl s_client -connect easypj.com:443 2>/dev/null | \
      openssl x509 -noout -dates
  → notBefore=Jan 1 00:00:00 2024 GMT
  → notAfter=Apr 1 00:00:00 2024 GMT
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Common TLS Misconfigurations and Their Symptoms</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Misconfiguration</th><th>Symptom</th><th>Root Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Wrong Secret type</td><td>Ingress serves default fake certificate</td><td>Used <code>kubectl create secret generic</code> instead of <code>tls</code></td><td>Delete and recreate with <code>kubectl create secret tls</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Certificate expired</td><td>Browser shows NET::ERR_CERT_DATE_INVALID</td><td>Cert past NotAfter date. cert-manager not installed or misconfigured.</td><td>Check cert-manager logs. Manually trigger renewal: <code>kubectl cert-manager renew easypj-tls</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Mismatched hostname</td><td>NET::ERR_CERT_COMMON_NAME_INVALID</td><td>Certificate SAN does not include the domain being accessed (e.g., cert for easypj.com but accessing www.easypj.com without www in SAN)</td><td>Request new cert with correct SANs. Include both root domain and www.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>TLS Secret in wrong namespace</td><td>Ingress uses default cert, not yours</td><td>Ingress and Secret must be in the same namespace</td><td>Recreate Secret in the same namespace as the Ingress</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>ssl-redirect not set</td><td>http:// still works, no redirect</td><td>Annotation missing or typo in annotation key</td><td>Add <code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "true"</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>TLS Attack Walkthrough — What Happens Without HTTPS</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Man-in-the-Middle Attack on Plain HTTP — The Real Risk</div>
<pre>
  SCENARIO: User on coffee shop WiFi accesses http://easypj.com/jobs/
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  STEP 1: Attacker on same WiFi runs ARP spoofing
  $ arpspoof -i wlan0 -t 192.168.1.50 192.168.1.1
  → Attacker positions between user and the router.

  STEP 2: User types http://easypj.com (NOT https)
  → Request goes through attacker's machine.
  → Attacker sees EVERYTHING in plaintext:
    GET /jobs/ HTTP/1.1
    Host: easypj.com
    Cookie: sessionid=abc123def456...    ← SESSION COOKIE STOLEN

  STEP 3: Attacker now has a valid session cookie
  $ curl https://easypj.com/admin/ -H "Cookie: sessionid=abc123..."
  → Attacker is LOGGED IN as the user. Can view profile, change settings,
    or if user is admin, access the Django admin panel.

  WITH TLS + HSTS enabled:
  ─────────────────────────
  User types http://easypj.com → Browser auto-redirects to https://
  (HSTS preload or previous visit). Even on first visit, if the site
  is on the HSTS preload list, browser NEVER attempts HTTP.
  Attacker sees only: CONNECT easypj.com:443 → ENCRYPTED DATA.
  Session cookie never exposed. Attack FAILS.

  🔑 KEY INSIGHT: TLS is not about hiding your content — it is about
     protecting your users' SESSION TOKENS from network-level attackers.
     One session token stolen = one user account compromised.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key Takeaway for the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p>TLS tasks are straightforward but detail-oriented. The exam pattern: (1) create a TLS Secret with <code>kubectl create secret tls --cert=... --key=...</code>, (2) add a <code>tls</code> section to the Ingress spec referencing the Secret, (3) add <code>ssl-redirect: true</code> annotation. <strong>Common mistake:</strong> using <code>kubectl create secret generic</code> instead of <code>kubectl create secret tls</code>. The Ingress controller will not recognize a generic Secret as a TLS certificate. The type must be <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>How TLS Termination Actually Works in Kubernetes Ingress</h4>
                <p>When you add a <code>tls</code> section to an Ingress resource, you are telling the Ingress controller (nginx-ingress, traefik, etc.) to handle TLS for you. But what actually happens to the traffic? Understanding this flow is critical for debugging "HTTPS not working" issues and for knowing where encryption boundaries lie:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">TLS Termination Flow — From Browser to Pod, Byte by Byte</div>
<pre>
  BROWSER: https://easypj.com/jobs/
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STEP 1: DNS Resolution                                                  │
  │  easypj.com → A record → 203.0.113.50 (Ingress Controller LoadBalancer IP)│
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STEP 2: TCP Connection to 203.0.113.50:443                             │
  │  The browser opens a TCP connection to the Ingress controller's IP on     │
  │  port 443 (HTTPS).                                                        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STEP 3: TLS HANDSHAKE (TLS 1.3)                                         │
  │  ────────────────────────────────                                         │
  │  Client → Server: ClientHello (supported ciphers, SNI: easypj.com)       │
  │  Server → Client: ServerHello + Certificate + ServerFinished             │
  │                    │                                                      │
  │                    │  The Ingress controller sends the TLS certificate    │
  │                    │  from the Secret referenced in the Ingress resource:  │
  │                    │  spec.tls[0].secretName: easypj-tls                  │
  │                    │  → Loads tls.crt and tls.key from the Secret        │
  │                    │                                                      │
  │  ⚡ WHAT THE BROWSER CHECKS:                                              │
  │  • Is the certificate signed by a CA the browser trusts? (Let's Encrypt) │
  │  • Does the certificate's CN or SAN include "easypj.com"? (SNI match)   │
  │  • Is the certificate NOT expired?                                       │
  │  • Is the certificate NOT revoked? (OCSP stapling check)                  │
  │  If ALL checks pass → 🔒 padlock icon appears in the browser.            │
  │  If ANY check fails → ⚠️ "Your connection is not private" warning.        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │  (TLS session established — encrypted tunnel between browser and Ingress)
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STEP 4: TLS TERMINATION — The Ingress Controller decrypts the request   │
  │  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────                      │
  │  • The encrypted HTTPS request is DECRYPTED at the Ingress controller.   │
  │  • The Ingress reads the HTTP Host header: "easypj.com".                 │
  │  • It matches this against Ingress rules to find the backend Service.    │
  │  • The decrypted (plain HTTP) request is forwarded to the backend pod.   │
  │  • ⚡ THIS IS WHERE ENCRYPTION ENDS (unless you use backend TLS).        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STEP 5: BACKEND CONNECTION (Ingress → easypj Pod, port 8000)            │
  │  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────                  │
  │  • The Ingress controller sends the HTTP request to the easypj Service's  │
  │    ClusterIP → kube-proxy routes to a pod → Django/Gunicorn handles it.  │
  │  • This connection is PLAIN HTTP (no TLS between Ingress and pod).       │
  │  • Traffic between Ingress and pod goes over the CNI network (overlay).   │
  │  • ⚡ SECURITY IMPLICATION: Anyone who can sniff the CNI network can      │
  │    see the plaintext HTTP traffic. Use mTLS (Ch22) or NetworkPolicy       │
  │    (Ch6) to protect this path.                                            │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  THE THREE TLS MODELS:
  ──────────────────────
  MODEL 1: TLS TERMINATION at Ingress (what most CKS tasks use)
  Browser ──TLS──▶ Ingress Controller ──HTTP──▶ Backend Pod
  (Encrypted to Ingress, plain to pod. Simplest. CKS default.)

  MODEL 2: TLS PASSTHROUGH (Ingress doesn't decrypt)
  Browser ────────TLS (end-to-end)──────────▶ Backend Pod
  (Pod handles TLS. Needs certs in pod. More secure, more complex.)

  MODEL 3: TLS RE-ENCRYPTION (Ingress decrypts, then re-encrypts to pod)
  Browser ──TLS──▶ Ingress ──TLS──▶ Backend Pod
  (Two separate TLS sessions. Requires certs on both Ingress AND pod.)
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Configuring TLS Passthrough — Model 2 in Practice</h4>
                <p>The three-model diagram above names TLS passthrough as an option, but it needs its own configuration — unlike termination and re-encryption, passthrough is <strong>not enabled by default</strong> on the ingress-nginx controller, and forgetting this is a common real-world (and exam) trap. With passthrough, the Ingress controller never decrypts anything: it inspects only the unencrypted <strong>SNI hostname</strong> in the ClientHello, routes the raw TCP stream to the matching backend Service based on that hostname alone, and the backend pod itself terminates TLS with its own certificate.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml — ssl-passthrough Ingress</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Step 0 — REQUIRED one-time controller flag (NOT on by default!):</span>
<span class="comment"># helm upgrade ingress-nginx ingress-nginx/ingress-nginx \</span>
<span class="comment">#   --set controller.extraArgs.enable-ssl-passthrough=true</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: easypj-passthrough-ingress
  namespace: easypj-prod
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-passthrough: "true"   <span class="comment"># ← needs the controller flag above too</span>
spec:
  tls:
  - hosts:
    - easypj.com                      <span class="comment"># used for SNI routing only — no Secret is read here</span>
  rules:
  - host: easypj.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: easypj-service
            port:
              number: 443              <span class="comment"># pod itself must listen on 443 with its own cert</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Passthrough's Hidden Cost — You Lose Layer 7 Routing</h5>
                    <p>Because the Ingress controller never decrypts the traffic, it cannot read the HTTP path, cannot apply <code>ssl-redirect</code>, HSTS headers, rate limiting, or path-based routing — all of those require reading plaintext HTTP, which passthrough never produces. Only SNI-based host routing survives. Use passthrough only when the backend legally or contractually must terminate its own TLS (a vendor appliance, a PCI segmentation boundary, or a workload that needs client-certificate mTLS the Ingress controller cannot broker). For the standard easypj web tier, Model 1 (termination at Ingress) remains the correct default.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Let's Encrypt and cert-manager — Automating Certificate Lifecycle</h4>
                <p>Manually creating TLS certificates every 90 days is unsustainable. cert-manager automates the entire certificate lifecycle — request, issuance, renewal, and Secret management. The CKS exam may ask you to configure cert-manager or understand the ACME protocol flow:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">cert-manager + Let's Encrypt — Automated TLS Lifecycle</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  CERT-MANAGER AUTOMATED LIFECYCLE                                       │
  │                                                                         │
  │  STEP 1: YOU CREATE A CERTIFICATE RESOURCE                              │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────                                │
  │  apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1                                         │
  │  kind: Certificate                                                       │
  │  metadata:                                                               │
  │    name: easypj-tls                                                      │
  │    namespace: easypj                                                     │
  │  spec:                                                                   │
  │    secretName: easypj-tls          ← TLS cert stored here               │
  │    dnsNames: [easypj.com, www.easypj.com]                                │
  │    issuerRef:                                                            │
  │      name: letsencrypt-prod         ← Which CA to use                   │
  │      kind: ClusterIssuer                                                 │
  │                                                                         │
  │  STEP 2: CERT-MANAGER DETECTS THE CERTIFICATE RESOURCE                  │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────                    │
  │  • cert-manager controller watches for Certificate resources.           │
  │  • Detects new "easypj-tls" Certificate → initiates issuance.           │
  │                                                                         │
  │  STEP 3: ACME CHALLENGE (prove you own the domain)                      │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────────────                        │
  │  • cert-manager creates a temporary Ingress or DNS record.              │
  │  • Let's Encrypt's servers verify: does easypj.com resolve to this IP?  │
  │  • HTTP-01 challenge: Let's Encrypt fetches                            │
  │    http://easypj.com/.well-known/acme-challenge/&lt;token&gt;                 │
  │  • DNS-01 challenge: Let's Encrypt checks a TXT record at              │
  │    _acme-challenge.easypj.com                                            │
  │                                                                         │
  │  STEP 4: CERTIFICATE ISSUED → STORED IN SECRET                          │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────────────                        │
  │  • Challenge passed → Let's Encrypt issues the certificate.             │
  │  • cert-manager stores tls.crt + tls.key in Secret "easypj-tls".       │
  │  • Updates the Certificate resource status to "Ready".                  │
  │                                                                         │
  │  STEP 5: INGRESS REFERENCES THE SECRET                                  │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────                                │
  │  spec:                                                                   │
  │    tls:                                                                  │
  │    - hosts: [easypj.com]                                                 │
  │      secretName: easypj-tls  ← Same Secret cert-manager created         │
  │                                                                         │
  │  STEP 6: AUTOMATIC RENEWAL (every 60 days, 30 days before expiry)       │
  │  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────         │
  │  • cert-manager monitors certificate expiry dates.                      │
  │  • 30 days before expiry → automatically re-runs ACME challenge.        │
  │  • New certificate issued → Secret updated → Ingress picks up new cert. │
  │  • ZERO manual intervention. Zero downtime.                             │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
