                    <p>The diagram above is the map — annotate, detect, challenge, issue, auto-renew. The three deep-dives below are the <strong>mechanics underneath</strong> each stop: exactly how the Ingress controller turns a changed Secret into a live certificate without ever restarting (①), the two-separate-keys cryptography the ACME protocol actually performs under that "sends ACME challenge" step (②), and the single most common reason a rotated or even revoked certificate keeps being honored by already-connected browsers for a while longer (③). Understanding these three is what turns "I applied the YAML" into "I can explain, and diagnose, exactly what the Ingress controller and the CA are doing with it."</p>

                    <h5>① The Dynamic SSL Certificate Store — How a Changed Secret Becomes Live HTTPS Without Restarting Anything</h5>
                    <p>A common misconception is that updating a TLS Secret makes the Ingress controller rewrite <code>nginx.conf</code> and reload the NGINX master process — the same heavyweight reload that happens when you add a brand-new <code>Ingress</code> host. That is <strong>not</strong> what happens for a pure certificate rotation on ingress-nginx (the controller most CKS tasks use). ingress-nginx embeds a Lua runtime (OpenResty) and keeps a <strong>dynamic, in-memory certificate store</strong> — a Lua shared-memory zone indexed by hostname. The controller's Go sync loop watches Secret objects through the standard Kubernetes informer/watch mechanism (the same client-go pattern every controller uses); when a Secret referenced by an Ingress's <code>tls.secretName</code> changes, the controller parses the new <code>tls.crt</code>/<code>tls.key</code> and pushes them into that Lua store — it does not touch <code>nginx.conf</code> at all.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  THE DYNAMIC CERT STORE PATH — Secret change → live HTTPS, zero reload

  1. kubectl apply / cert-manager updates Secret "easypj-tls" (new tls.crt/tls.key)
       │
       ▼
  2. ingress-nginx's controller process (Go) is watching this Secret via a
     Kubernetes informer (it was told to, because an Ingress's tls.secretName
     references it) — the watch fires within milliseconds of the API update
       │
       ▼
  3. Controller parses the PEM cert+key, validates they match, and calls into
     the embedded Lua VM to store them in an ngx.shared.DICT keyed by SNI host:
       lua_shared_dict[ "easypj.com" ] = { cert = <parsed cert>, key = <parsed key> }
     nginx.conf is NOT regenerated. NGINX workers are NOT reloaded.
       │
       ▼
  4. NEXT TLS HANDSHAKE for easypj.com arrives at any NGINX worker
       │
       ▼
  5. An `ssl_certificate_by_lua_block` callback fires DURING the handshake,
     BEFORE the server picks a certificate — it reads the SNI hostname from
     ClientHello, looks it up in the shared dict from step 3, and calls
     OpenSSL's low-level API (ssl.set_cert()/set_priv_key()) to serve THAT
     certificate for THIS connection only. Every worker process shares the
     same dict, so the new cert is live on ALL workers simultaneously.
</pre>
