                    <h5>② The ACME Protocol's Two-Key Cryptographic Model — Why cert-manager Creates an Account Key AND a Certificate Key</h5>
                    <p>The Chapter 7 roadmap's step 3 ("sends ACME challenge to Let's Encrypt") hides a detail that trips up even experienced administrators: cert-manager is quietly managing <strong>two entirely separate private keys</strong> for every certificate, each with a different cryptographic job, stored in different Secrets, and rotated on different schedules. Confusing the two — or accidentally deleting the wrong one — produces very different (and very confusing) failure modes.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  THE TWO KEYS — one proves WHO is asking, the other proves WHAT is being requested

  KEY #1 — THE ACME ACCOUNT KEY
  ─────────────────────────────
  • Created ONCE per ClusterIssuer/Issuer, stored in the Secret named by
    `spec.acme.privateKeySecretRef.name` (e.g. letsencrypt-prod-key)
  • Every single ACME protocol message (new-account, new-order, fetch
    authorization, respond to challenge, finalize) is wrapped in a
    JSON Web Signature (JWS) — an envelope SIGNED WITH THIS KEY:
       POST /acme/new-order
       { "protected": {"alg":"ES256","kid":"&lt;account-url&gt;","nonce":"..."},
         "payload": {"identifiers":[{"type":"dns","value":"easypj.com"}]},
         "signature": "&lt;computed using the ACCOUNT private key&gt;" }
  • Purpose: proves to Let's Encrypt "the same account that solved the
    challenge for easypj.com is the one requesting/renewing this cert."
  • Shared across EVERY certificate that ClusterIssuer ever issues.

  KEY #2 — THE CERTIFICATE'S OWN PRIVATE KEY
  ───────────────────────────────────────────
  • Created FRESH per Certificate resource (or reused, per `privateKey.rotationPolicy`),
    stored in the Secret named by `spec.secretName` (e.g. easypj-tls) alongside
    the issued certificate itself, as `tls.key`
  • Used ONLY to generate the CSR (Certificate Signing Request) embedded in
    the ACME "finalize" call — the CSR is a self-signed proof that says
    "this public key belongs to whoever holds its matching private key"
  • Signs NOTHING in the account-level protocol messages above — its ONLY
    cryptographic job is proof-of-possession for the certificate itself
  • Rotated at every renewal by default (`rotationPolicy: Always`) — a
    renewed cert normally gets a BRAND NEW key pair, not just a new
    expiry date on the old one
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>ACME Account Key</th><th>Certificate's Own Key</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Stored in</strong></td><td><code>privateKeySecretRef</code> on the Issuer/ClusterIssuer</td><td><code>spec.secretName</code> on the Certificate (same Secret as <code>tls.crt</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Signs</strong></td><td>Every ACME protocol JWS envelope</td><td>Nothing directly — used to build the CSR only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Scope</strong></td><td>One key shared by ALL certs from that Issuer</td><td>One key per Certificate resource</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Rotation</strong></td><td>Essentially never — created once, reused forever</td><td>Every renewal (default), or reused if <code>rotationPolicy: Never</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>If deleted</strong></td><td>cert-manager silently registers a brand-new ACME account on the next order — existing issued certs are unaffected (they're independent, already-signed objects)</td><td>cert-manager must issue a whole new cert+key pair — the old Secret's cert becomes orphaned/unusable once replaced</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this matters:</strong> deleting a ClusterIssuer's <code>privateKeySecretRef</code> Secret by mistake is far less destructive than it sounds — already-issued certificates remain perfectly valid until their own expiry, because Let's Encrypt already signed them and doesn't care which account key requested them after the fact. The real risk is repeating this mistake: each deletion forces a brand-new ACME account registration on the next certificate request, and Let's Encrypt rate-limits <em>account creation</em> separately from certificate issuance — enough repeated resets in a short window can get your requests throttled independently of your normal 50-certs-per-domain-per-week budget.</div>

                    <h5>③ Why Rotating (or Even Revoking) a Certificate Doesn't Immediately Cut Already-Connected Browsers — The Session Resumption Trap</h5>
                    <p>This is the TLS equivalent of the "conntrack keeps an old connection alive after a NetworkPolicy denies it" surprise from Chapter 6 — and it catches out administrators responding to a suspected private-key compromise just as often. A certificate ROTATION (new Secret pushed into the dynamic store from deep-dive ①) only changes what is served on the <strong>next fresh TLS handshake</strong>. It does nothing to sessions that already completed their handshake using the OLD certificate — and, because of TLS session resumption, some "new" connections aren't fresh handshakes either.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  WHY AN ALREADY-CONNECTED BROWSER KEEPS USING THE OLD CERTIFICATE'S SESSION

  BEFORE rotation: browser completed a full TLS 1.3 handshake using the OLD
  certificate. Both sides now hold SYMMETRIC session keys derived during that
  handshake (via the key exchange) — encryption from this point on uses those
  session keys directly. The certificate itself is never touched again for
  the lifetime of that TCP connection.

  YOU ROTATE THE CERTIFICATE (deep-dive ① updates the dynamic store)
       │
       ▼
  Existing, already-encrypted connections → keep flowing with the SAME
  session keys negotiated under the OLD cert. Rotating the cert changes
  NOTHING about data already in flight. ❌ (until the TCP connection closes)
       │
  BRAND-NEW connections (fresh TCP + fresh TLS handshake) → the Lua
  ssl_certificate_by_lua_block callback runs again → serves the NEW cert
  immediately. ✅
       │
  BUT: TLS 1.3 SESSION RESUMPTION (session tickets / PSK) lets a
  RECONNECTING client skip the full handshake entirely for a bounded ticket
  lifetime (commonly hours) — a resumed session reuses key material derived
  from the ORIGINAL (old-cert) handshake and never re-evaluates which
  certificate "would" be served. ⚠️ (until ticket keys themselves rotate,
  or the ticket's own lifetime expires)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Sees the New Certificate?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Brand-new client, fresh TCP + full TLS handshake, after rotation</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes, immediately</td><td>Lua callback fires on every fresh ClientHello — dynamic store is already updated</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Browser tab left open with an already-established HTTPS connection</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ No, not until it closes</td><td>Symmetric session keys were derived once at handshake time; the cert plays no further role in that connection</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Client reconnects using a TLS 1.3 session-resumption ticket</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Not necessarily</td><td>Resumption reuses key material from the ORIGINAL handshake — no certificate re-evaluation happens at all</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>You need every session using a compromised key cut RIGHT NOW</td><td>Only via extra steps</td><td>Rotate the cert AND revoke it with the CA (OCSP) AND force the Ingress controller to rotate its TLS session-ticket keys (or restart it) to invalidate outstanding resumption tickets</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The incident-response implication:</strong> if you rotate a TLS certificate in response to a suspected private-key leak, "I rotated it" is <strong>not</strong> the same claim as "I terminated every session that trusted the leaked key." For true immediate containment you must also revoke the old certificate with the issuing CA (so any NEW handshake that somehow still tries it fails hard, and OCSP-checking clients reject it), and force session-ticket-key rotation on the Ingress controller (a brief controller restart is the blunt way; some controllers expose a ticket-key-rotation interval instead) so that outstanding resumption tickets stop being honored. On the CKS exam this exact nuance rarely appears in a single question, but in a real production key-compromise incident it is the single most common reason a security team believes a leaked key has been "revoked" while a handful of long-lived, resumed sessions are still quietly using it.</div>
