            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch7-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>TLS terminates at the Ingress controller</strong> — HTTPS is decrypted there, then forwarded as plain HTTP to the backend pod; the pod-to-pod hop should be protected by NetworkPolicies (Ch6), not TLS, unless you need mTLS (Ch22).</li>
                    <li><strong>The exam essential is one command</strong> — <code>kubectl create secret tls &lt;name&gt; --cert=... --key=...</code> sets the correct <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code> Secret type automatically; <code>create secret generic</code> is never recognized by the Ingress controller.</li>
                    <li><strong>cert-manager automates the full lifecycle</strong> — detect Ingress annotation → ACME challenge (HTTP-01 or DNS-01) → certificate issued → stored in a Secret → auto-renewed 30 days before expiry, with zero manual intervention.</li>
                    <li><strong>A certificate alone is not enough</strong> — <code>ssl-redirect: "true"</code> forces HTTP→HTTPS, and an HSTS header (<code>max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains</code>) stops browsers from ever attempting plain HTTP again, closing the SSL-stripping window.</li>
                    <li><strong>Most TLS failures are naming/scoping bugs</strong> — wrong Secret type, a hostname missing from the certificate's SAN list, or the Secret living in the wrong namespace account for nearly every "Not Secure" symptom on the exam.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch7-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>tls.crt and tls.key — exact key names!</strong> The Secret created with <code>kubectl create secret tls</code> stores the cert as <code>tls.crt</code> and the key as <code>tls.key</code>. If you create the Secret manually from YAML, you MUST use these exact key names or the Ingress controller won't find them.</li>
                    <li><strong>SNI requires the host header:</strong> When testing TLS with curl, use <code>-H "Host: easypj.com"</code> or <code>--resolve easypj.com:443:&lt;INGRESS-IP&gt;</code>. Without it, the Ingress controller doesn't know which certificate to serve and returns the default (often a fake certificate).</li>
                    <li><strong>cert-manager CRDs must exist first:</strong> Installing cert-manager creates CRDs (ClusterIssuer, Issuer, Certificate). These take 30-60s to register. If you apply your Certificate resource immediately, it will fail with "no matches for kind Certificate" — wait a minute and retry.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

