                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a TLS Secret for an Ingress using a self-signed certificate. What openssl command generates the cert, and what kubectl command creates the Secret?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 365 -newkey rsa:2048 \
  -keyout tls.key -out tls.crt \
  -subj "/CN=jobs.easypj.com" \
  -addext "subjectAltName=DNS:jobs.easypj.com"
kubectl create secret tls easypj-tls \
  --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key \
  -n easypj-prod</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>kubectl create secret tls</code> automatically sets the Secret type to <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code>. The key names inside the Secret MUST be <code>tls.crt</code> and <code>tls.key</code>. On the exam, NEVER use <code>kubectl create secret generic</code> for TLS certificates. <strong>Never omit <code>-addext "subjectAltName=..."</code>:</strong> a certificate built from only <code>-subj "/CN=..."</code> has no SAN extension at all, and every modern browser (Chrome 58+, current Firefox/Safari) rejects it with <code>NET::ERR_CERT_COMMON_NAME_INVALID</code> even though the CN matches exactly — browsers validate the SAN field, never the CN, as covered in the X.509 certificate fields breakdown earlier in this chapter.</p></div>
                    </details>
