                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between HTTP-01 and DNS-01 ACME challenges, and when would you use each?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>HTTP-01:</strong> Let's Encrypt places a file at <code>/.well-known/acme-challenge/&lt;token&gt;</code>. Requires port 80 publicly accessible. Simpler but no Wildcard certs. <strong>DNS-01:</strong> Creates a TXT record in DNS. Works for internal services and supports Wildcard certs (<code>*.easypj.com</code>). Requires DNS provider API access. cert-manager automates both.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>HTTP-01</th><th>DNS-01</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Proves ownership via</strong></td><td>File served at <code>/.well-known/acme-challenge/&lt;token&gt;</code> on port 80</td><td>TXT record at <code>_acme-challenge.easypj.com</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Needs port 80 open?</strong></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">✅ Yes — required</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">❌ No — DNS API access only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Wildcard certs (<code>*.easypj.com</code>)?</strong></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ Not supported</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Supported</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Works behind a firewall/NetworkPolicy?</strong></td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Needs an explicit allow rule (see Scenario Drill Q3)</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ No inbound exception needed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Simple public single-host domains</td><td>Multi-tenant clusters, internal services, strict deny-all NetworkPolicies</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>DNS-01 is preferred for Kubernetes because pods are often not directly internet-accessible, Wildcard certs are essential for multi-tenant clusters, and DNS-01 works even when port 80 is firewalled. On the CKS exam, cert-manager with DNS-01 is the recommended pattern for production.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An Ingress is configured with TLS but browsers show "Not Secure." The Secret exists, the certificate is valid. What's likely wrong?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Most likely: the Ingress's <code>tls.hosts</code> field doesn't match the certificate's CN/SAN. Check: <code>openssl x509 -in tls.crt -text -noout | grep -A1 "Subject Alternative Name"</code>. The hostname must match exactly. Also check the Secret is in the SAME namespace as the Ingress.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. What hostname does the Ingress advertise for TLS?</span>
kubectl get ingress easypj-ingress -n easypj-prod -o jsonpath='{.spec.tls[0].hosts}'
<span class="comment"># → ["easypj.com"]</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. What hostname is actually inside the certificate's SAN?</span>
kubectl get secret easypj-tls -n easypj-prod -o jsonpath='{.data.tls\.crt}' \
  | base64 -d | openssl x509 -noout -text | grep -A1 "Subject Alternative Name"
<span class="comment"># → DNS:www.easypj.com          ← mismatch! Ingress says easypj.com, cert says www only

# 3. Confirm the Secret lives in the SAME namespace as the Ingress</span>
kubectl get secret easypj-tls -n easypj-prod
<span class="comment"># → NotFound here but found in "default" = classic cross-namespace mistake

# 4. Live-connection proof (bypasses any doubt about the YAML)</span>
openssl s_client -connect easypj.com:443 -servername easypj.com &lt; /dev/null 2&gt;/dev/null \
  | openssl x509 -noout -subject -ext subjectAltName
<span class="comment"># → subject=CN=www.easypj.com ; SAN: DNS:www.easypj.com   ← confirms the mismatch live</span></code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>TLS hostname mismatch is the #1 TLS debugging issue. The browser compares the URL hostname against the certificate's SAN list, not the CN. Wildcards like <code>*.easypj.com</code> match <code>jobs.easypj.com</code> but NOT <code>easypj.com</code> itself. Always verify with <code>openssl s_client -connect host:443 -servername host</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What Ingress annotations enable HSTS and automatic HTTP to HTTPS redirect? Write the complete annotation block.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">metadata:
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "true"
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/hsts: "true"
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/hsts-max-age: "31536000"
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/hsts-include-subdomains: "true"</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>HSTS tells browsers: "Always use HTTPS for this domain for the next year." It prevents SSL stripping attacks. <code>ssl-redirect</code> handles the immediate redirect; HSTS prevents the browser from even attempting HTTP in the future. Always include both when configuring TLS Ingress on the exam.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the cert-manager ClusterIssuer YAML for Let's Encrypt staging. Why use staging first?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: ClusterIssuer
metadata:
  name: letsencrypt-staging
spec:
  acme:
    server: https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
    email: security@easypj.com
    privateKeySecretRef:
      name: letsencrypt-staging-key
    solvers:
    - http01:
        ingress:
          class: nginx</code></pre>
                    <p>Production Let's Encrypt has rate limits (50 certs/week/domain). Staging has no limits — test first, then switch to <code>https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory</code>.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>cert-manager is the standard tool for automated TLS in Kubernetes. ClusterIssuer is cluster-scoped; Issuer is namespace-scoped. The privateKeySecretRef stores the ACME account key. Change the server URL from staging to production after verifying your configuration works.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Compare "TLS termination at Ingress" vs "mTLS end-to-end." Can a compromised pod in another namespace sniff traffic between Ingress and backend under each model?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>TLS Termination:</strong> YES, attacker CAN sniff. Traffic between Ingress and backend is plain HTTP. Any pod on the same CNI network can potentially capture it. <strong>mTLS End-to-End:</strong> NO, attacker CANNOT sniff. Every pod-to-pod connection is encrypted with mutually authenticated TLS. The attacker sees only encrypted bytes. Requires a service mesh (Istio/Linkerd) or manual mTLS.</p>
<pre>
  MODEL 1: TLS TERMINATION AT INGRESS (easypj standard pattern)
  👤 User ──HTTPS(encrypted)── Ingress ──HTTP(plaintext)── easypj pod
                                            ▲
                              🕵️ compromised pod on same CNI network
                                 CAN read this hop — sees full request/response

  MODEL 2: mTLS END-TO-END (Chapter 22 — Istio/Linkerd sidecar)
  👤 User ──HTTPS(encrypted)── Ingress ──mTLS(encrypted+authenticated)── easypj pod
                                            ▲
                              🕵️ compromised pod on same CNI network
                                 sees only opaque encrypted bytes — CANNOT read it
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Ingress↔Pod Traffic</th><th>Same-CNI Attacker Sees</th><th>Requires</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>TLS Termination</strong></td><td>Plain HTTP</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">Full plaintext request/response</td><td>NetworkPolicy (Ch6) as the only real protection for this hop</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>mTLS End-to-End</strong></td><td>Mutually-authenticated TLS</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">Encrypted bytes only</td><td>Service mesh sidecar (Istio/Linkerd) or manually-managed pod certs</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The TLS termination point is an encryption boundary. Everything behind it is plaintext. mTLS pushes encryption all the way to the backend pod. On the CKS exam, know when TLS termination is sufficient vs when mTLS is required (PCI/HIPAA, zero-trust). Chapter 22 covers service mesh mTLS.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your Ingress controller accepts TLS 1.0/1.1 connections. What's the risk and how do you restrict to TLS 1.2+ only?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>TLS 1.0/1.1 have known vulnerabilities (POODLE, BEAST). PCI DSS requires TLS 1.2+. Fix:</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">metadata:
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-protocols: "TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3"
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-ciphers: "ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384"</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>TLS protocol hardening is a CIS requirement. Only TLS 1.2+ should be enabled. The cipher list uses only modern AEAD ciphers (GCM mode). Older CBC-mode ciphers are vulnerable to padding oracle attacks. This falls under CKS domain "Minimize Microservice Vulnerabilities."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A TLS certificate expires in 7 days. How do you manually rotate it? What's cert-manager's auto-rotation approach?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Manual rotation
openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 365 -newkey rsa:2048 \
  -keyout tls-new.key -out tls-new.crt -subj "/CN=easypj.com"
kubectl delete secret easypj-tls -n easypj-prod
kubectl create secret tls easypj-tls --cert=tls-new.crt --key=tls-new.key -n easypj-prod
# Ingress controller auto-reloads the Secret — no restart needed</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>cert-manager auto-rotation:</strong> Set <code>renewBefore: 720h</code> (30 days) on the Certificate resource. cert-manager renews automatically and updates the Secret.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The Ingress controller watches Secrets and reloads automatically when they change. You can delete+recreate or update in place. For cert-manager, the <code>renewBefore</code> field controls how early renewal happens — 30 days is the recommended default to account for renewal failures and weekends.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Configure backend TLS so the Ingress connects to backend pods via HTTPS. What annotation and port changes are needed?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">metadata:
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/backend-protocol: "HTTPS"
spec:
  rules:
  - host: easypj.com
    http:
      paths:
      - backend:
          service:
            name: easypj-svc
            port:
              number: 443   # Backend listens on HTTPS</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Backend TLS (re-encryption) means the Ingress decrypts the client request, then re-encrypts for the backend. The backend pod needs its own TLS certificate. This provides encryption end-to-end — even if the CNI network is compromised, inter-pod traffic is encrypted.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>curl https://easypj.com</code> works but <code>curl https://www.easypj.com</code> fails with "SSL certificate problem." Both point to the same IP. What's the fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The certificate doesn't cover <code>www.easypj.com</code>. Fix: add it to <code>tls.hosts</code> AND ensure the cert's SAN includes it:</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  tls:
  - hosts:
    - easypj.com
    - www.easypj.com
    secretName: easypj-tls  # Cert must have both SANs</code></pre>
                    <p>Alternative: redirect www → non-www at the Ingress level, and only secure the non-www domain.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>SNI (Server Name Indication) lets a single IP:port serve multiple TLS certificates. The client sends the hostname during the TLS handshake. If no certificate matches, the Ingress serves a default fake cert that browsers reject. Always verify <code>tls.hosts</code> covers all domain variations and the cert's SAN list includes them all.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch7-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 7 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (7)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — a broken cluster state, and you produce the fix with real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An Ingress's <code>spec.tls[0].secretName</code> reads <code>easypj-tsl</code> (transposed letters). <code>kubectl get ingress</code> shows no errors and the Ingress "looks fine." Browsers show a self-signed default certificate instead of yours. Diagnose and fix.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Confirm the Ingress references a Secret that doesn't exist
kubectl get ingress easypj-ingress -n easypj-prod -o jsonpath='{.spec.tls[0].secretName}'
# → easypj-tsl

kubectl get secret easypj-tsl -n easypj-prod
# → Error from server (NotFound): secrets "easypj-tsl" not found

# 2. The REAL secret exists under the correct name
kubectl get secret easypj-tls -n easypj-prod
# → exists, type kubernetes.io/tls

# 3. Fix — patch the Ingress to the correct secretName
kubectl patch ingress easypj-ingress -n easypj-prod --type=json \
  -p='[{"op":"replace","path":"/spec/tls/0/secretName","value":"easypj-tls"}]'

# 4. Verify
curl -vI https://easypj.com 2>&1 | grep -E 'Server certificate|subject'</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Kubernetes never validates that <code>secretName</code> points at an object that actually exists — it's a plain string reference. When the named Secret is missing, ingress-nginx silently falls back to its own bootstrap self-signed "default certificate" for that host instead of erroring, which is exactly why <code>kubectl get ingress</code> reports nothing wrong. Always cross-check <code>tls.secretName</code> against <code>kubectl get secret</code> by exact string, not by eye — transposed letters are the single most common TLS "it looks configured but isn't" bug on the exam.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A cert-manager <code>Certificate</code> references <code>issuerRef: {name: letsencrypt-prod}</code> with no <code>kind</code> field. It has been stuck "Pending" for 20 minutes even though a <code>ClusterIssuer</code> named <code>letsencrypt-prod</code> is <code>Ready: True</code>. What's wrong?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl describe certificate easypj-tls -n easypj-prod
# → Reason: "Referenced issuer does not exist"

# The default for issuerRef.kind is "Issuer" (namespace-scoped), NOT
# "ClusterIssuer" — omitting kind makes cert-manager look for a
# namespace-scoped Issuer named letsencrypt-prod, which doesn't exist.</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># THE FIX — make the kind explicit
spec:
  secretName: easypj-tls
  issuerRef:
    name: letsencrypt-prod
    kind: ClusterIssuer   # ← was missing; defaults to "Issuer" otherwise</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is one of the most common real-world cert-manager misconfigurations, precisely because it produces no YAML validation error — <code>kind</code> is optional and silently defaults to the namespace-scoped <code>Issuer</code>. If your certificate authority is actually a <code>ClusterIssuer</code>, you must say so explicitly, every time. <code>kubectl describe certificate</code> is always the first diagnostic step for a stuck issuance — it reports the exact reason, unlike <code>kubectl get certificate</code> which only shows <code>READY: False</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Chapter 6's default-deny-ingress NetworkPolicy is applied cluster-wide in <code>easypj-prod</code>. A new cert-manager <code>Certificate</code> using an HTTP-01 solver has been stuck "Pending" ever since. No NetworkPolicy change was made when the Certificate was created. Explain the failure and fix it without weakening the deny-all posture.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Confirm it's the challenge, not DNS or the ClusterIssuer
kubectl describe challenge -n easypj-prod
# → "Waiting for HTTP-01 challenge propagation" — never resolves

# 2. Root cause: HTTP-01 requires Let's Encrypt to reach a temporary
# solver pod cert-manager creates on port 8089, routed through the
# Ingress on port 80. The chapter's default-deny-INGRESS policy has no
# exception for it — the external ACME validation request is dropped
# before it ever reaches the solver pod.

kubectl apply -f - <<EOF
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-acme-http01-solver
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels: {acme.cert-manager.io/http01-solver: "true"}
  policyTypes: [Ingress]
  ingress:
  - from: []            # solver pods must accept from ANY source — Let's
    ports:                # Encrypt's validation servers are not a fixed CIDR
    - {protocol: TCP, port: 8089}
EOF</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a direct collision between Chapter 6's zero-trust default and Chapter 7's HTTP-01 challenge: cert-manager's ephemeral solver pods are automatically labeled <code>acme.cert-manager.io/http01-solver: "true"</code>, but a blanket default-deny-ingress policy isolates them exactly like every other pod. The narrowest correct fix targets ONLY that label — it does not touch, weaken, or remove the deny-all posture for the real application pods. This is why production teams generally prefer DNS-01 (Chapter 7 §7.2's split-panel) for clusters with strict ingress NetworkPolicies: it needs no inbound exception at all.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A wildcard certificate for <code>*.easypj.com</code> is correctly installed. <code>https://jobs.easypj.com</code> works perfectly. <code>https://easypj.com</code> (the apex/root domain, no subdomain) fails with a certificate name mismatch. Why, and what are the two possible fixes?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>A wildcard SAN like <code>DNS:*.easypj.com</code> matches exactly one label to the left of the dot — <code>jobs.easypj.com</code> and <code>www.easypj.com</code> qualify, but <code>easypj.com</code> itself (zero labels before the wildcard position) does NOT match a wildcard SAN by the X.509 spec. Two fixes: (1) add <code>easypj.com</code> as an additional explicit SAN alongside the wildcard when requesting the cert (<code>dnsNames: [easypj.com, "*.easypj.com"]</code> in the cert-manager <code>Certificate</code> resource), or (2) redirect the apex domain to <code>www.easypj.com</code> at the DNS/Ingress level and only ever serve the wildcard-covered name directly.</p>
<pre>
  SAN: DNS:*.easypj.com   ← matches exactly ONE label left of the dot

  ✅ jobs.easypj.com        ("jobs" = one label)          → MATCHES
  ✅ www.easypj.com         ("www" = one label)            → MATCHES
  ❌ easypj.com             (zero labels — the apex itself) → NO MATCH
  ❌ a.b.easypj.com         (two labels — too deep)          → NO MATCH
</pre>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># FIX 1 — list the apex explicitly alongside the wildcard</span>
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: Certificate
metadata:
  name: easypj-tls
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  secretName: easypj-tls
  dnsNames:
    - easypj.com              <span class="comment"># ← apex added explicitly</span>
    - "*.easypj.com"           <span class="comment"># wildcard still covers every subdomain</span>
  issuerRef: {name: letsencrypt-prod, kind: ClusterIssuer}</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># FIX 2 — alternative: redirect the apex to the wildcard-covered name instead
# (only needed if you do NOT want to reissue the certificate)</span>
kubectl apply -f - &lt;&lt;EOF
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: easypj-apex-redirect
  namespace: easypj-prod
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/permanent-redirect: "https://www.easypj.com\$request_uri"
spec:
  rules:
  - host: easypj.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend: {service: {name: easypj-service, port: {number: 8000}}}
EOF</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the same SAN-matching mechanic tested throughout the chapter (§7.1's X.509 fields breakdown, §7.3's SNI mismatch table) applied to the specific case of wildcards. Candidates who only remember "wildcard = covers all subdomains" miss that the apex domain is not technically a subdomain of itself in SAN-matching terms — always list the apex explicitly if it must be served directly.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The Ingress has <code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "true"</code> and users still reach <code>easypj</code> over plain HTTP with no redirect. <code>curl -vI http://easypj.com</code> against the Ingress's IP correctly returns a 308 redirect. What else could explain users still landing on plain HTTP?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># The Ingress redirect logic is fine — the traffic never went through
# the Ingress in the first place. Check for a bypass path:
kubectl get svc easypj-service -n easypj-prod
# → TYPE: NodePort   (exposes port 80 directly on every node's IP!)

# Anyone hitting &lt;node-ip&gt;:&lt;nodePort&gt; reaches the Service directly —
# completely bypassing the Ingress controller, its TLS termination,
# AND its ssl-redirect annotation, none of which apply to NodePort traffic.

# Fix — remove the direct NodePort exposure; only the Ingress should
# be internet-reachable:
kubectl patch svc easypj-service -n easypj-prod -p '{"spec":{"type":"ClusterIP"}}'</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>ssl-redirect</code> is an Ingress-controller-level behavior — it only governs traffic that actually passes through the Ingress controller. A <code>NodePort</code> or <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service sitting alongside the Ingress creates a second, unprotected entry point that every TLS/redirect/HSTS annotation on the Ingress is completely blind to. On the exam and in production, always audit every Service's <code>type</code> for a security-sensitive app — <code>ClusterIP</code> should be the default, with only the Ingress controller itself exposed externally.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>openssl s_client -connect easypj.com:443 -tls1</code> unexpectedly SUCCEEDS even though the Ingress carries <code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-protocols: "TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3"</code>. Where should you look besides the Ingress annotation itself?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># The per-Ingress annotation can be overridden by the CONTROLLER-WIDE
# ConfigMap, which sets the default for hosts that DON'T set the
# annotation and can be misread as authoritative even when it isn't:
kubectl get configmap ingress-nginx-controller -n ingress-nginx -o yaml | grep ssl-protocols
# → ssl-protocols: "TLSv1.1 TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3"   (controller-wide default is too loose)

# Two valid fixes — pick based on whether OTHER Ingresses need TLS 1.1:
# (a) Tighten the controller-wide default (affects every Ingress that
#     doesn't override it):
kubectl patch configmap ingress-nginx-controller -n ingress-nginx --type=merge \
  -p '{"data":{"ssl-protocols":"TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3"}}'
# (b) Confirm the per-Ingress annotation's key is spelled correctly and
#     the Ingress actually uses the ingressClassName matching this
#     controller — a mismatched class means the annotation is simply
#     never read by the controller that is answering the connection.</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Per-Ingress annotations configure per-host NGINX directives, but the controller's own <code>ConfigMap</code> sets the baseline that applies whenever an annotation is absent or the request doesn't route through the Ingress you think it does. A passing annotation on the WRONG Ingress object (or an <code>ingressClassName</code>/controller mismatch in a multi-controller cluster) looks identical to "the annotation didn't work" from the outside — always verify with <code>openssl s_client</code> directly against the resulting live connection, not just by reading the YAML.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A response to <code>curl -sI https://easypj.com</code> shows two separate <code>Strict-Transport-Security</code> headers with different <code>max-age</code> values. What misconfiguration produces this, and which value actually takes effect in browsers?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># THE BROKEN ANNOTATION SET — two different mechanisms both adding HSTS
metadata:
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/hsts: "true"                 # built-in HSTS directive
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/hsts-max-age: "63072000"      # → 2 years
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/configuration-snippet: |
      add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains" always;
      # ← a SECOND, hand-written HSTS header — 1 year, and conflicting</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># THE FIX — pick exactly ONE mechanism, never both
metadata:
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/hsts: "true"
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/hsts-max-age: "31536000"
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/hsts-include-subdomains: "true"
    # configuration-snippet's manual Strict-Transport-Security line REMOVED —
    # let the built-in hsts-* annotations own this header exclusively</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The HTTP spec does not define how a client should merge two same-named response headers, so behavior is implementation-defined — most browsers use only the FIRST <code>Strict-Transport-Security</code> header and silently ignore the second, but this is not guaranteed across all clients, and relying on undefined behavior for a security control is itself the bug. The built-in <code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/hsts*</code> annotations and a hand-written <code>configuration-snippet</code> line are two independent code paths that both inject the same header — always own HSTS through exactly one of them, never mix them on the same Ingress.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch7-drill"></span>
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 7.1 — Sketch the Three TLS Models and the ACME Lifecycle From Memory</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 5 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
