                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 15 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>On a disposable <code>kind</code> cluster with ingress-nginx installed (<strong>never production</strong>): generate a self-signed cert for <code>jobs.easypj.test</code>, create the TLS Secret, wire up an Ingress with <code>ssl-redirect</code> and an HSTS header, and verify every one of §7.3's five verification commands against it. Then install cert-manager and a <strong>staging</strong> <code>ClusterIssuer</code> (never production — you'll hit rate limits), and watch a real <code>Certificate</code> resource go from <code>Pending</code> to <code>Ready</code>.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>--resolve jobs.easypj.test:443:&lt;ingress-ip&gt;</code> lets curl test a domain that isn't in real DNS. Self-signed certs need <code>curl -k</code> (or <code>--cacert</code> with your own CA) since the CA isn't publicly trusted.</div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Self-signed cert with a correct SAN (Q1's lesson from the Lab Q&A)
openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 365 -newkey rsa:2048 \
  -keyout tls.key -out tls.crt -subj "/CN=jobs.easypj.test" \
  -addext "subjectAltName=DNS:jobs.easypj.test"
kubectl create secret tls easypj-tls --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key

# 2. Ingress with enforcement annotations from §7.3
kubectl apply -f - <<EOF
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: easypj-ingress
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "true"
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/hsts: "true"
spec:
  tls: [{hosts: [jobs.easypj.test], secretName: easypj-tls}]
  rules:
  - host: jobs.easypj.test
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend: {service: {name: easypj-service, port: {number: 8000}}}
EOF

# 3. Run §7.3's five verification commands against the ingress-nginx IP
INGRESS_IP=$(kubectl get svc -n ingress-nginx ingress-nginx-controller -o jsonpath='{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].ip}')
curl -k -vI --resolve jobs.easypj.test:443:$INGRESS_IP https://jobs.easypj.test 2>&1 | grep -E 'HTTP/|Strict-Transport'
curl -vI --resolve jobs.easypj.test:80:$INGRESS_IP http://jobs.easypj.test 2>&1 | grep -E 'Location|HTTP/'

# 4. Now layer cert-manager (staging!) on top
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/cert-manager/cert-manager/releases/latest/download/cert-manager.yaml
kubectl apply -f - <<EOF
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: ClusterIssuer
metadata: {name: letsencrypt-staging}
spec:
  acme:
    server: https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
    email: test@easypj.test
    privateKeySecretRef: {name: letsencrypt-staging-key}
    solvers: [{http01: {ingress: {class: nginx}}}]
EOF
kubectl get certificate -w   # watch READY flip False → True</code></pre>
