            <section class="section-block" id="s8-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.1 The API Server is the Crown Jewel</h3>
                <p>Every operation in Kubernetes — whether initiated by a human with kubectl, a controller manager reconciling state, a kubelet reporting node status, or a pod reading a ConfigMap — flows through the <strong>kube-apiserver</strong>. It is the single component that reads from and writes to etcd. There is no back door. There is no direct etcd path (in a properly configured cluster). The API server is the gatekeeper, the authorizer, the auditor, and the admission controller — all in one process. If you compromise the API server, you bypass every security control in Kubernetes.</p>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 If the API Server Falls, Everything Falls</h5>
                    <p>A compromised API server can: read all Secrets in all namespaces, create cluster-admin ServiceAccounts, delete audit logs, modify RBAC rules to create permanent backdoors, deploy malicious pods on every node, and exfiltrate all data stored in etcd. <strong>Protecting the API server is not one security task among many — it is THE security task.</strong> Every other control (NetworkPolicy, Seccomp, AppArmor) assumes the API server is secure. If it is not, those controls are irrelevant.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Everything Flows Through the API Server</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The API server is the single entry point for ALL cluster operations</div>
<pre>
                        🌐 External Users (kubectl)
                        📦 Internal Pods (ServiceAccount tokens)
                        ⚙️  kubelet (node status, pod logs)
                        🔄 Controller Manager (reconciliation)
                        📊 Scheduler (pod placement)
                             │     │     │     │
                             ▼     ▼     ▼     ▼
                    ┌────────────────────────────────────┐
                    │        🧠 kube-apiserver           │
                    │        ┌─────────────────────┐     │
                    │        │ 1. AUTHENTICATION   │     │
                    │        │    Who are you?     │     │
                    │        ├─────────────────────┤     │
                    │        │ 2. AUTHORIZATION    │     │
                    │        │    What can you do? │     │
                    │        ├─────────────────────┤     │
                    │        │ 3. ADMISSION CTRL   │     │
                    │        │    Is this allowed? │     │
                    │        ├─────────────────────┤     │
                    │        │ 4. AUDIT LOGGING    │     │
                    │        │    Record everything│     │
                    │        └──────────┬──────────┘     │
                    └───────────────────┼────────────────┘
                                        │
                                        ▼
                              ┌─────────────────┐
                              │   🗄️  etcd      │
                              │   (persisted)   │
                              └─────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>What Attackers Can Learn from the API Server — Even Without Credentials</h4>
                <p>Even with anonymous auth enabled and minimal RBAC, the API server leaks valuable reconnaissance information to unauthenticated users. The <strong>discovery API</strong> (<code>/api</code>, <code>/apis</code>) returns the complete list of available API groups, resources, and versions — telling an attacker exactly which Kubernetes version you are running and which features are enabled. The <strong>version endpoint</strong> (<code>/version</code>) returns the exact Git commit and build date of your API server. An attacker can cross-reference this against the CVE database to identify unpatched vulnerabilities specific to your version. This is why <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> is not just about preventing data access — it is about denying attackers the reconnaissance they need to plan an attack.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">What an Anonymous Attacker Can Discover About Your Cluster</div>
<pre>
  $ curl -k https://10.0.0.1:6443/version
  {
    "major": "1",
    "minor": "30",
    "gitVersion": "v1.30.0",          ← Attacker knows your exact version
    "gitCommit": "7c48c2bd...",        ← Maps to specific CVE database entry
    "buildDate": "2024-04-17T...",     ← Knows when you last updated
    "platform": "linux/amd64"          ← Knows your architecture
  }
  → Attacker now searches: "Kubernetes v1.30.0 CVEs"
  → Finds CVE-2024-XXXX affecting your exact version

  $ curl -k https://10.0.0.1:6443/apis
  → Returns ALL available API groups:
    apps/v1, batch/v1, networking.k8s.io/v1, rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1,
    policy/v1 (PodSecurity is enabled!), cert-manager.io/v1 (you use cert-manager),
    monitoring.coreos.com/v1 (you use Prometheus operator)
  → Attacker now knows your entire software stack from ONE anonymous request.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>API Server Request Flow — From Network to etcd</h4>
