            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s8-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.2 Disabling Anonymous Authentication</h3>
                <p>By default, Kubernetes allows <strong>anonymous requests</strong> — requests that carry no authentication credentials. These requests are assigned the username <code>system:anonymous</code> and the group <code>system:unauthenticated</code>. While the default RBAC rules grant anonymous users almost no permissions, a misconfigured cluster may have accidentally granted access — and even without RBAC permissions, anonymous users can still query the API server's version, health endpoints, and discovery APIs, leaking valuable information about your cluster version and available resources.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 CIS 1.2.1 — The Most Fundamental Check</h5>
                    <p>The CIS Kubernetes Benchmark's very first API server check is: <strong>"Ensure that the --anonymous-auth argument is set to false."</strong> This is check #1.2.1 — and it is the most common FAIL on default clusters. Fixing it is the first thing you do on any hardening task.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Fixing Anonymous Authentication — Step by Step</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Edit the API server manifest to disable anonymous access</div>
<pre>
  # Step 1: Back up the manifest (ALWAYS do this first)
  $ cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml \
       /root/kube-apiserver.yaml.backup

  # Step 2: Edit the manifest
  $ vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

  # Find the command section and ensure this flag exists:
  spec:
    containers:
    - command:
      - kube-apiserver
      - --anonymous-auth=false          # ← ADD or CHANGE this to false

  # Step 3: Save and exit. kubelet detects the change and restarts
  # the API server automatically (10-30 seconds).

  # Step 4: Verify anonymous access is blocked
  $ curl -k https://localhost:6443/api/v1/namespaces
  {
    "kind": "Status",
    "status": "Failure",
    "message": "Unauthorized",          # ← 401 Unauthorized ✅
    "reason": "Unauthorized",
    "code": 401
  }

  # Step 5: Verify authenticated access still works
  $ kubectl get namespaces
  → Returns namespace list (using valid kubeconfig credentials) ✅
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ After Disabling Anonymous Auth — What Breaks?</h5>
                    <p>Most things keep working because they already use valid credentials: kubectl uses client certificates, controllers use ServiceAccount tokens, kubelets use bootstrap certificates. However, <strong>liveness and readiness probes configured with HTTP GET requests to the API server health endpoint will fail</strong> if they do not include authentication. The health endpoints (<code>/healthz</code>, <code>/livez</code>, <code>/readyz</code>) are exempt from authentication by default, but custom probes targeting other API paths will break. Always test after disabling anonymous auth.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What system:anonymous Can Actually Do — Testing Anonymous Permissions</h4>
                <p>Before disabling anonymous auth, it is valuable to audit what an anonymous user could have accessed. This tells you whether the cluster was already compromised through this vector:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Audit anonymous permissions before locking down</div>
<pre>
  # Check what anonymous can do RIGHT NOW
  $ kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:anonymous
  Resources   Non-Resource URLs   Resource Names   Verbs
  *.*         []                  []               []
  → Empty = no permissions (good!).

  # Check if anonymous was given any ClusterRoleBinding (BAD!)
  $ kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o yaml | grep -A5 system:anonymous
  → If ANY results appear, anonymous has been granted permissions.
    This is a critical finding — remove those bindings IMMEDIATELY.

  # Test anonymous access explicitly
  $ kubectl get pods --as=system:anonymous
  → Error from server (Forbidden): pods is forbidden
  → If this SUCCEEDS, your cluster is already compromised.

  # Test anonymous access to the version endpoint
  $ curl -k https://localhost:6443/version
  → If this returns JSON, anonymous can see your version.
  → After fix: should return 401 Unauthorized.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s8-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.3 Enabling Audit Logging on the API Server</h3>
                <p>Audit logging records <strong>every API request</strong> — who made it, what resource they accessed, what action they performed, and whether it succeeded or failed. Without audit logs, a security incident is a mystery: you have no way to determine which credentials were compromised, what data was accessed, or how long the attacker was in your cluster. With audit logs, you have a forensic timeline of every action. This is not optional for production — it is a requirement for PCI-DSS, SOC 2, HIPAA, and virtually every compliance framework.</p>

                <h4>Audit Policy Levels — How Much to Log</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>What It Logs</th><th>When to Use</th><th>Disk Impact</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>None</strong></td><td>Nothing — the event is not recorded at all</td><td>Noisy events (e.g., watch connections, health checks) that would flood the logs</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">Zero</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Metadata</strong></td><td>Who, what resource, what verb, when, source IP, response code — but NOT the request/response body</td><td>Most events. Sufficient for compliance and most investigations.</td><td style="color:#eab308;">Low</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Request</strong></td><td>Metadata + the full request body</td><td>Events where you need to see WHAT was submitted (e.g., pod specs, ConfigMap data)</td><td style="color:#f97316;">Medium</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>RequestResponse</strong></td><td>Metadata + request body + response body</td><td>Critical resources (Secrets access, RBAC changes) where you need the full picture</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">High — use sparingly</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Audit Policy YAML — Annotated Example</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Complete audit policy — what to log and at what level</div>
<pre>
  # /etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml
  apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1
  kind: Policy
  rules:
  # Don't log noisy events (saves disk space)
  - level: None
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["events"]
  - level: None
    userGroups: ["system:nodes"]
    verbs: ["get"]
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["nodes/status", "pods/status"]

  # Log Secret access metadata (who accessed which Secret, but NOT the values)
  - level: Metadata
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["secrets", "configmaps"]

  # Log full RBAC changes (who changed permissions)
  - level: RequestResponse
    resources:
    - group: "rbac.authorization.k8s.io"
      resources: ["*"]

  # Log pod exec with full request (captures the command executed)
  - level: RequestResponse
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["pods/exec"]

  # Default: log metadata for everything else
  - level: Metadata

  # API server flags to enable this policy:
  # --audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml
  # --audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log
  # --audit-log-maxage=30      (retain logs for 30 days)
  # --audit-log-maxbackup=10   (keep up to 10 rotated files)
  # --audit-log-maxsize=100    (rotate at 100 MB per file)
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Audit Best Practice: Log RBAC Changes at RequestResponse</h5>
                    <p>If an attacker modifies RBAC to create a backdoor, you need to see exactly what they changed. Always log <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code> resources at <code>RequestResponse</code> level. This captures both the RBAC manifest that was submitted AND the API server's confirmation response. Combined with <code>Metadata</code> logging for Secrets access, you have full forensic coverage of the most critical attack paths.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Reading Audit Logs — Understanding the JSON Format</h4>
                <p>Audit logs are written as JSON objects — one per line — to the file specified by <code>--audit-log-path</code>. Understanding the JSON structure lets you query logs with <code>jq</code> to answer forensic questions like "who deleted that pod?" or "which IP tried to access Secrets?":</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Anatomy of a Single Audit Log Entry</div>
<pre>
  {
    "kind": "Event",
    "apiVersion": "audit.k8s.io/v1",
    "level": "RequestResponse",          ← Which audit level captured this
    "auditID": "abc123-def456",          ← Unique ID to correlate request+response
    "stage": "ResponseComplete",         ← When in the request lifecycle
    "requestURI": "/api/v1/namespaces/easypj-prod/secrets/easypj-db",
    "verb": "get",                        ← What action (get, create, delete, list)
    "user": {
      "username": "system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:easypj-sa",  ← WHO
      "groups": ["system:serviceaccounts", "system:authenticated"]
    },
    "sourceIPs": ["10.0.1.23"],           ← WHERE from
    "userAgent": "easypj-app/v2.1.0",     ← WHAT client
    "objectRef": {
      "resource": "secrets",               ← WHAT resource
      "name": "easypj-db",
      "namespace": "easypj-prod"
    },
    "responseStatus": {
      "metadata": {},
      "code": 200                          ← SUCCESS (403 = denied, 401 = unauthenticated)
    },
    "requestReceivedTimestamp": "2024-06-15T14:23:07.123456Z",
    "stageTimestamp": "2024-06-15T14:23:07.234567Z"
  }

  # QUERY EXAMPLES with jq:
  # Find all Secret access events
  $ cat audit.log | jq 'select(.objectRef.resource=="secrets")'

  # Find all failed (403) requests
  $ cat audit.log | jq 'select(.responseStatus.code==403)'

  # Find requests from a specific IP
  $ cat audit.log | jq 'select(.sourceIPs[]=="10.0.1.23")'

  # Find all delete operations
  $ cat audit.log | jq 'select(.verb=="delete")'
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Audit Log Disk Management — Don't Fill the Control Plane Disk</h4>
                <p>Audit logs at RequestResponse level can generate gigabytes per day on a busy cluster. If the audit log fills the control plane disk, the API server cannot write logs and may stop accepting requests. This is a self-inflicted denial of service. The API server flags <code>--audit-log-maxage</code>, <code>--audit-log-maxbackup</code>, and <code>--audit-log-maxsize</code> control log rotation — but they only rotate the file, they do not enforce total disk usage. For production, always pair file-based audit logging with an <strong>audit webhook backend</strong> that streams logs to an external system (Elasticsearch, Splunk, or a cloud SIEM) where they can be properly stored, indexed, and queried without consuming control plane disk space.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s8-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.4 Restricting API Server to Specific IP Ranges</h3>
                <p>Authentication and authorization control WHO can access the API and WHAT they can do. But there is a prior question: <strong>who can even reach the API server in the first place?</strong> If the API server's port 6443 is exposed to the public internet, attackers can attempt authentication, probe for misconfigurations, and launch brute-force attacks — even if they never successfully authenticate. Network-layer restriction adds a critical first barrier: the attacker's packets never reach the API server at all.</p>

                <h4>API Server IP Restriction — The 4-Layer Approach</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Defense in depth for API server network access</div>
<pre>
  LAYER 1: Cloud Firewall / Security Group
  ─────────────────────────────────────────
  Rule: ALLOW TCP 6443 FROM 10.0.0.0/8 (internal network)
  Rule: DENY  TCP 6443 FROM 0.0.0.0/0  (internet)
  → Attacker on the internet cannot even establish a TCP connection.
  → Port 6443 is invisible to port scanners like Shodan and Masscan.

  LAYER 2: Kubernetes Authentication
  ───────────────────────────────────
  --anonymous-auth=false
  → Even if an attacker gets past the firewall (e.g., compromised internal host),
    they must present valid credentials to make any API call.

  LAYER 3: Kubernetes RBAC
  ─────────────────────────
  → Even if an attacker has valid credentials (e.g., a stolen kubeconfig),
    RBAC limits what those credentials can do to a specific namespace and verbs.

  LAYER 4: Audit Logging
  ───────────────────────
  → Every access attempt — successful or failed — is recorded with timestamp,
    source IP, username, and the exact API call. Forensic evidence for
    investigation and compliance.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>API Server Authentication Methods — Comparison</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>How It Works</th><th>Use Case</th><th>Security Level</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>X.509 Client Certificate</strong></td><td>kubectl uses cert from kubeconfig; API server verifies against cluster CA</td><td>Human administrators (kubeadm default)</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Strong (cert-based)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Bearer Token (ServiceAccount)</strong></td><td>SA token in Authorization header; signed by K8s token controller</td><td>Pods calling the API from inside the cluster</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Tokens can be long-lived</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>OIDC (OpenID Connect)</strong></td><td>External IdP (Google, GitHub, Azure AD) issues JWT; API server validates</td><td>Enterprise SSO for human users</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Strong (supports MFA)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Webhook Token Auth</strong></td><td>External server validates token via HTTP callback</td><td>Custom authentication systems</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Depends on webhook security</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Bootstrap Tokens</strong></td><td>Short-lived tokens for new nodes joining the cluster</td><td>kubeadm join — temporary use only</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Auto-expiring</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Static Token File</strong></td><td>Tokens stored in CSV file on control plane disk</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">❌ NEVER use in production</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">🔴 Tokens never rotate, stored in plaintext</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Cloud Firewall Integration — Putting Theory into Practice</h4>
                <p>The 4-layer model works, but implementing it requires knowing the exact cloud-specific commands. Here is how to restrict API server access on the three major cloud providers — and the equivalent for on-premises clusters using iptables:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Restricting Port 6443 — Cloud Provider Commands</div>
<pre>
  # AWS: Security Group Rule
  $ aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \
      --group-id sg-abc123 \
      --protocol tcp --port 6443 \
      --cidr 10.0.0.0/8
  # Also: REVOKE the 0.0.0.0/0 rule if it exists!
  $ aws ec2 revoke-security-group-ingress \
      --group-id sg-abc123 \
      --protocol tcp --port 6443 \
      --cidr 0.0.0.0/0

  # Azure: Network Security Group Rule
  $ az network nsg rule create \
      --nsg-name k8s-nsg \
      --name AllowApiServer \
      --priority 100 \
      --source-address-prefixes 10.0.0.0/8 \
      --destination-port-ranges 6443 \
      --access Allow

  # GCP: Firewall Rule
  $ gcloud compute firewall-rules create allow-api-server \
      --network k8s-vpc \
      --allow tcp:6443 \
      --source-ranges 10.0.0.0/8

  # On-Premises / Generic Linux: iptables
  $ iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 6443 -s 10.0.0.0/8 -j ACCEPT
  $ iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 6443 -j DROP    # Default deny
  $ iptables-save > /etc/iptables/rules.v4           # Persist
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s8-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.5 Insecure Port — Removing the Back Door</h3>
                <p>Older versions of Kubernetes (prior to 1.20) exposed an <strong>insecure API port on 8080</strong> that accepted requests with <strong>no TLS and no authentication</strong>. Anyone who could reach port 8080 could perform any API operation as cluster-admin — no credentials needed, no encryption, no audit log. This port was removed in Kubernetes 1.20, but some clusters running older versions or custom configurations may still have it enabled. The CKS exam expects you to verify it is disabled.</p>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 The Insecure Port — Complete Bypass of All Security</h5>
                    <p>Port 8080 bypasses authentication, authorization, admission control, and TLS. If it is enabled, every other security control in this chapter — anonymous auth disabled, audit logging, RBAC — is meaningless. An attacker who can reach port 8080 can do anything. <strong>This is not a vulnerability. This is a deliberate feature that was removed because it is inherently insecure.</strong></p>
                </div>

                <h4>API Server Ports — Which Are Safe, Which Are Dangerous</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Security Status</th><th>Action Required</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>6443</strong></td><td>Secure API (TLS + auth)</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Safe — this is the only port you should expose</td><td>Restrict with firewall to trusted IPs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>8080</strong></td><td>Insecure API (no TLS, no auth)</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">🔴 DANGER — removed in 1.20+</td><td>Verify <code>--insecure-port=0</code> or flag absent</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>10250</strong></td><td>kubelet API</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Must be locked with webhook auth</td><td>Anonymous auth disabled, webhook enabled</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>10255</strong></td><td>kubelet read-only (no auth!)</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">🔴 DANGER — disable</td><td><code>readOnlyPort: 0</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>2379</strong></td><td>etcd client</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ API server only</td><td>Firewall: allow only from control plane IPs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>2380</strong></td><td>etcd peer</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ etcd members only</td><td>Firewall: allow only between etcd nodes</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Verifying the Insecure Port Is Disabled</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Check and confirm port 8080 is gone</div>
<pre>
  # Step 1: Check the API server manifest for the insecure port flag
  $ grep insecure-port /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
  → Should show: --insecure-port=0
  → OR: the flag should be completely absent (removed in 1.20+)

  # Step 2: Verify no process is listening on 8080
  $ netstat -tlnp | grep 8080
  → Should return NOTHING (no process listening)

  # Step 3: Verify 8080 is not reachable
  $ curl http://localhost:8080/api/v1/namespaces
  → curl: (7) Failed to connect — Connection refused ✅

  # Step 4: Confirm the secure port works (6443)
  $ curl -k https://localhost:6443/healthz
  → ok ✅

  # If port 8080 IS listening:
  # 1. Edit kube-apiserver.yaml, add/change: --insecure-port=0
  # 2. Save and wait for kubelet to restart the static pod
  # 3. Re-run netstat -tlnp | grep 8080 → must be empty
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>API Server Before vs After — Security Posture Comparison</h4>
                <div class="stat-bar-group">
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">🔴 Default API Server (no hardening) — Security Score</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">12% — anonymous access, no audit, insecure port open</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill red" style="width: 12%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">🟡 With Anonymous Auth Disabled Only</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">35% — credentials required but still no audit trail</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill red" style="width: 35%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">🟡 With Auth + Audit Logging Enabled</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">65% — authenticated access + forensic record</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill red" style="width: 65%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">🟢 Fully Hardened (Auth + Audit + IP Restriction + No Insecure Port)</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">95% — multi-layer defense, CKS target state</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill red" style="width: 95%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>API Server Attack Walkthrough — What an Attacker Sees</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Scenario: API server on public internet with anonymous auth enabled</div>
<pre>
  ATTACKER scans the internet for open K8s API servers
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  $ shodan search 'port:6443 product:"Kubernetes"' --fields ip_str
  203.0.113.42   ← Found: easypj cluster API server

  $ curl -k https://203.0.113.42:6443/version
  { "major":"1", "minor":"30", "gitVersion":"v1.30.0", ... }
  → Attacker knows your EXACT version. Searches CVE database for v1.30.0.

  $ curl -k https://203.0.113.42:6443/api/v1/namespaces
  { "items": [ "easypj-prod", "easypj-dev", "kube-system", ... ] }
  → Anonymous access WORKED. Attacker can LIST namespaces with NO credentials!

  $ curl -k https://203.0.113.42:6443/api/v1/namespaces/easypj-prod/secrets
  → 403 Forbidden (anonymous can't read Secrets by default)
  → But if RBAC was misconfigured... 200 OK + ALL SECRETS returned.

  ATTACKER'S NEXT STEPS (even with just anonymous version + namespace access):
  1. Cross-reference v1.30.0 against CVE database → find exploitable CVEs
  2. Brute-force ServiceAccount tokens (if weak token generation)
  3. Look for exposed etcd (port 2379) on the same IP range
  4. Search GitHub for leaked kubeconfig files containing the same IP

  WITH --anonymous-auth=false:
  $ curl -k https://203.0.113.42:6443/version
  { "kind":"Status", "status":"Failure", "message":"Unauthorized", "code":401 }
  → Attacker learns NOTHING. No version. No namespace list. No CVEs to exploit.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s8-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.6 API Server Security Flag Quick Reference</h3>
                <p>The CKS curriculum requires <strong>restricting access to Kubernetes API</strong>. Below is every security-critical API server flag and its secure value:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Secure Value</th><th>What It Controls</th><th>CIS</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>--anonymous-auth</code></td><td><strong>false</strong></td><td>Blocks unauthenticated requests</td><td>1.2.1</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--authorization-mode</code></td><td><strong>Node,RBAC</strong></td><td>Authorizer chain. Never AlwaysAllow</td><td>1.2.7</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--enable-admission-plugins</code></td><td>Include <strong>NodeRestriction,ServiceAccount</strong></td><td>Admission plugins. Overrides defaults</td><td>1.2.18</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--kubelet-certificate-authority</code></td><td>Path to CA file</td><td>Verify kubelet TLS certificates</td><td>1.2.6</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--encryption-provider-config</code></td><td>Path to config YAML</td><td>Enable encryption at rest for Secrets</td><td>1.2.33</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-policy-file</code></td><td>Path to policy YAML</td><td>Enable audit logging</td><td>1.2.22</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-log-path</code></td><td>Path to log file</td><td>Where audit events are written</td><td>1.2.22</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--tls-cert-file</code> / <code>--tls-private-key-file</code></td><td>Paths to cert and key</td><td>API server TLS. Key permissions 600</td><td>1.2.3</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key Takeaway for the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p>API server hardening tasks always follow the same workflow: (1) <strong>Backup the manifest.</strong> (2) Edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. (3) Add/change the flag. (4) Wait for the kubelet to restart the static pod. (5) Verify with curl or kubectl. <strong>Never skip the backup.</strong> If you add a typo to a flag, the API server will not start — and kubectl will not work to fix it. The only recovery is to restore the backup via SSH to the control plane node.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Authentication → Authorization → Admission Pipeline — How the API Server Processes Every Request</h4>
                <p>The kube-apiserver is not a monolithic handler. It is a <strong>pipeline of independent stages</strong>, each with its own configuration flags and each separately testable. Understanding the exact order of these stages is critical because a misconfiguration in one stage can silently bypass later stages. Here is the exact pipeline:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kube-apiserver Request Pipeline — Every Stage, Every Flag, Every Chapter</div>
<pre>
  REQUEST: kubectl get pods -n easypj
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STAGE 1: TLS Termination &amp; Client Certificate Validation           │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────────────                    │
  │  Flags: --tls-cert-file, --tls-private-key-file, --client-ca-file   │
  │  • API server presents its TLS certificate to the client.           │
  │  • Client presents its certificate. API server verifies it against  │
  │    the CA in --client-ca-file.                                       │
  │  • If --client-ca-file is not set → client certs are NOT validated  │
  │    → anyone with any cert can connect (but still needs AuthN).      │
  │  ⚡ CKS: Chapter 8 — TLS mutual authentication                      │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STAGE 2: Authentication (Who are you?)                              │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────                             │
  │  Flags: --anonymous-auth, --oidc-issuer-url, --token-auth-file,      │
  │         --authentication-token-webhook-config-file                    │
  │  • The API server extracts identity from the request:                │
  │    - Client certificate CN = username, O = groups                    │
  │    - Bearer token → validated against OIDC, webhook, or static file  │
  │    - ServiceAccount JWT → validated by built-in SA authenticator     │
  │  • If NO identity is provided AND --anonymous-auth=true:              │
  │    → Authenticated as "system:anonymous" with group "system:unauthenticated"│
  │  • If --anonymous-auth=false: → 401 Unauthorized                     │
  │  ⚡ CKS: Chapter 8 — disable anonymous auth, Chapter 10 (SA tokens)  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STAGE 3: Authorization (What can you do?)                           │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────                            │
  │  Flags: --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC,ABAC,Webhook                  │
  │  • API server asks EACH authorizer in order:                         │
  │    - Node authorizer: is this a kubelet requesting its own pods?     │
  │    - RBAC authorizer: check RoleBindings/ClusterRoleBindings         │
  │    - ABAC: check static policy file (deprecated — don't use)         │
  │    - Webhook: ask external service                                    │
  │  • First authorizer that says DENY → request REJECTED (403)          │
  │  • First authorizer that says ALLOW → SHORT-CIRCUIT (next authorizers│
  │    are skipped).                                                      │
  │  • If NO authorizer says ALLOW → REJECTED (default deny).            │
  │  ⚡ CKS: Chapter 9 (RBAC), Chapter 11 (Node authorization)           │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STAGE 4: Admission Control (Mutating → Validating)                  │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────                    │
  │  Flags: --enable-admission-plugins, --admission-control-config-file   │
  │  • RUNS ONLY for mutating requests (create, update, delete, connect) │
  │  • SKIPPED for read requests (get, list, watch).                     │
  │  • TWO PHASES (in order):                                            │
  │    a) MUTATING plugins: can MODIFY the object (add defaults,         │
  │       inject sidecars, set securityContext).                          │
  │    b) VALIDATING plugins: can ACCEPT or REJECT the (possibly         │
  │       mutated) object. Cannot modify it.                              │
  │  • Key CKS plugins: NodeRestriction (Ch11), AlwaysPullImages,        │
  │    PodSecurity (Ch17).                                                │
  │  ⚡ CKS: Chapter 11 (NodeRestriction), Chapter 24 (webhooks)         │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STAGE 5: etcd Persistence                                           │
  │  ────────────────────────                                             │
  │  • Object (after mutation + validation) is serialized and stored.    │
  │  • If encryption is configured (Ch19), the object is encrypted       │
  │    BEFORE writing to etcd.                                            │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STAGE 6: Audit Logging                                              │
  │  ────────────────────────                                             │
  │  • AFTER the request is processed, the audit event is generated.     │
  │  • This is NOT on the critical path — audit logging failure does     │
  │    NOT block the request (by default).                                │
  │  ⚡ CKS: Chapter 28 (Audit Logging)                                   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  CKS MENTAL MODEL: Every exam task maps to one of these 6 stages.
  Stage 1 = TLS (Ch7-8). Stage 2 = AuthN (Ch8,10). Stage 3 = AuthZ (Ch9,11).
  Stage 4 = Admission (Ch11,24). Stage 5 = Encryption (Ch19). Stage 6 = Audit (Ch28).
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

