            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch8-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The API server is the single point through which everything flows</strong> — AuthN → AuthZ → Admission → etcd persistence → Audit; compromise it and every other control (NetworkPolicy, Seccomp, RBAC) becomes irrelevant.</li>
                    <li><strong>Anonymous auth is enabled by default</strong> — <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> (CIS 1.2.1) is the single most common CIS FAIL and the first fix on any hardening task; verify with a curl request that returns 401.</li>
                    <li><strong>Audit logging has 4 levels</strong> — None → Metadata → Request → RequestResponse; log RBAC changes and Secrets access at higher levels, everything else at Metadata, and ship logs off-disk via a webhook backend to avoid filling the control plane disk.</li>
                    <li><strong>Network restriction is the first line of defense</strong> — a cloud firewall or security group limiting port 6443 to trusted CIDRs means an attacker's packets never even reach the authentication layer.</li>
                    <li><strong>The insecure port (8080) is a complete bypass</strong> of TLS, AuthN, AuthZ, and audit logging — removed by default since 1.20, but always verify with <code>--insecure-port=0</code> and <code>netstat</code> that nothing is listening.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch8-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Changing authorization-mode while connected:</strong> If you change <code>--authorization-mode</code> from <code>AlwaysAllow</code> to <code>Node,RBAC</code>, your current kubectl session may stop working if your kubeconfig cert doesn't have proper RBAC. Use <code>kubectl auth can-i --list</code> BEFORE making changes to verify you'll retain access.</li>
                    <li><strong>anonymous-auth=false breaks kubelet:</strong> The kubelet communicates with the API server anonymously by default. If you disable anonymous auth without configuring kubelet TLS certificates, kubelets can't register nodes. Always check kubelet config when hardening the API server.</li>
                    <li><strong>Admission plugin order matters:</strong> <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> overrides the default set. If you only specify <code>NodeRestriction</code>, you'll disable ALL other default plugins (including critical ones like ServiceAccount, NamespaceLifecycle). Use <code>--enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,...</code> and ensure other defaults stay enabled.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch8-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>sed -i 's/--anonymous-auth=true/--anonymous-auth=false/' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code> — one-line fix, then wait for the static pod to restart.</li>
                    <li>Always check the full <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> list before editing it — if you only specify one plugin, it OVERRIDES the defaults, disabling all others.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

