            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch8-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 8 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Verify that anonymous access to the API server is disabled. What curl command proves it, and what flag did you set?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Flag: <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> in kube-apiserver.yaml. Verify: <code>curl -k https://api-server:6443/api</code> → 401 Unauthorized.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Anonymous auth is enabled by default. Disabling it means EVERY request must present credentials. Verify with curl without any auth headers — it must return 401. Also verify authenticated access still works with kubectl.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between --authorization-mode=RBAC and --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC, and why does order matter?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Node authorizer handles kubelet requests first — if a kubelet requests its own node or pods, ALLOW immediately. Everything else falls through to RBAC. Without Node, kubelets need broad RBAC permissions. Order: plugins are evaluated sequentially, first ALLOW wins.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The Node authorizer recognizes kubelets by <code>system:node:&lt;nodename&gt;</code> username and <code>system:nodes</code> group. It auto-allows access to: node's own Node object, pods scheduled to that node, secrets referenced by those pods, PVs attached to those pods. This is a security boundary — without it, a compromised kubelet could read any secret in the cluster.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What security risk does --profiling=true pose, and how do you verify it's disabled?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Profiling exposes /debug/pprof/ endpoints showing memory allocations, goroutine stacks, CPU profiles. Attackers use this for reconnaissance. Verify: <code>kubectl get --raw=/debug/pprof/</code> should return 403/404. Set <code>--profiling=false</code> in kube-apiserver.yaml.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>pprof endpoints are powerful debugging tools that reveal internal API server state. In production, they're an information disclosure risk. On the CKS exam, disabling profiling is a CIS benchmark requirement (check 1.1.21). Apply to all control plane components: kube-apiserver, kube-controller-manager, kube-scheduler.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Which admission plugin restricts kubelets to only modifying their own Node and Pod objects, and how do you enable it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>NodeRestriction</strong>. Add to <code>--enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,...</code> in kube-apiserver.yaml. Without it, a compromised kubelet could modify ANY node or pod. With it, kubelet on node01 only modifies the node01 Node object and pods scheduled there.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>NodeRestriction is a critical blast-radius limiter. It ensures that even if a kubelet is fully compromised, the attacker can only affect workloads on that specific node. This maps to the "least privilege" principle. The admission plugin also prevents kubelets from modifying node labels (preventing label-based scheduling attacks) and from modifying their own taints (preventing eviction bypass).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does the SecurityContextDeny admission plugin do, and why is it considered a coarse-grained control compared to PodSecurity Admission?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>SecurityContextDeny REJECTS any pod that sets ANY securityContext fields — even safe ones like <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>. It's an all-or-nothing control. PodSecurity Admission (PSA) is more granular: it allows <code>runAsNonRoot</code> but blocks <code>privileged: true</code>. PSA with "Restricted" profile is the modern replacement. SecurityContextDeny is deprecated in favor of PSA.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>On older CKS exam versions, SecurityContextDeny might still appear. Know that it exists but PSA is the recommended approach. If you see both enabled, SecurityContextDeny runs first and may reject pods before PSA evaluates them. The fix: remove SecurityContextDeny and rely on PSA with appropriate warn/audit/enforce labels.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to ensure every pod always pulls its image from the registry (never uses a cached image). Which admission plugin enforces this, and what's the security benefit?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>AlwaysPullImages</strong> admission plugin. It mutates every pod to set <code>imagePullPolicy: Always</code>, overriding any tag-based caching. Security benefit: if an attacker pushes a malicious image with the same tag to your registry, pods will pull the new (malicious) image on restart. However, AlwaysPullImages ensures you always get the LATEST version from the registry — combined with image signing (cosign), this prevents tag-mutation attacks.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Without AlwaysPullImages, a node that already has <code>easypj:v2.1</code> cached won't pull a new image even if the tag was updated in the registry. An attacker who compromises the registry can push a malicious image under the same tag, and existing nodes will keep running the cached (clean) version while new nodes get the malicious one — creating an inconsistent and dangerous state. AlwaysPullImages prevents this inconsistency.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you verify that the API server's TLS certificate is valid and trusted? What command shows the full certificate chain?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Check API server cert from outside
openssl s_client -connect api-server:6443 -showcerts &lt; /dev/null 2&gt;/dev/null | openssl x509 -text -noout | grep -E "Subject:|Issuer:|Not Before|Not After"
# Check kubeconfig's embedded cert
kubectl config view --raw -o jsonpath='{.users[0].user.client-certificate-data}' | base64 -d | openssl x509 -text -noout | grep -E "Subject:|Not After"
# Verify the cert is signed by the cluster CA
openssl verify -CAfile /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>TLS certificate validation is a common CKS exam task. Key checks: (1) Is the cert expired? (2) Does the CA chain verify? (3) Does the cert's CN/SAN match the API server's hostname? The API server cert is at <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt</code> on kubeadm clusters. The kubeconfig embeds the CA cert used to verify the API server — check it with <code>kubectl config view --raw</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The kubelet on worker-1 cannot register with the API server after you disabled anonymous auth. What configuration is missing, and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The kubelet needs TLS client certificates to authenticate to the API server. Fix in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">authentication:
  x509:
    clientCAFile: /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt
# Kubelet presents its own cert during TLS handshake
# Cert is auto-generated during kubeadm join, stored at:
# /var/lib/kubelet/pki/kubelet-client-current.pem</code></pre>
                    <p>Verify: <code>openssl x509 -in /var/lib/kubelet/pki/kubelet-client-current.pem -text -noout | grep Subject</code> should show <code>system:node:worker-1</code>.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Kubelets authenticate using TLS client certificates, not bearer tokens. The certificate's Subject CN is <code>system:node:&lt;nodename&gt;</code> and it belongs to the <code>system:nodes</code> group. This is how the Node authorizer identifies kubelets. On kubeadm clusters, TLS bootstrapping handles this automatically. On the exam, if kubelets can't connect after API server hardening, check their TLS certificate configuration.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're integrating Azure AD as an OIDC provider. What API server flags are required, and how does the API server validate tokens without calling Azure AD on every request?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">--oidc-issuer-url=https://login.microsoftonline.com/&lt;tenant-id&gt;/v2.0
--oidc-client-id=&lt;app-registration-id&gt;
--oidc-username-claim=email
--oidc-groups-claim=groups
--oidc-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/oidc-ca.crt</code></pre>
                    <p>The API server fetches the OIDC provider's public signing keys ONCE at startup (from <code>/.well-known/openid-configuration</code> → jwks_uri). Every subsequent JWT is validated locally by verifying the signature against these cached keys. No network call per request.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>OIDC integration is the recommended production authentication pattern. The API server only contacts the OIDC provider at startup and periodically to refresh keys. Token validation is local (JWT signature verification). This means OIDC works even during provider outages — as long as the API server has the cached keys. On the CKS exam, you may need to verify OIDC flags are set and that the CA file correctly validates the provider's TLS certificate.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to enable audit logging with a custom policy. What API server flags are required, and where must the audit policy file be mounted?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># API server flags in kube-apiserver.yaml
--audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml
--audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log
--audit-log-maxage=30
--audit-log-maxbackup=10
--audit-log-maxsize=100</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>Volume mounts required in kube-apiserver.yaml:</strong> Mount the audit policy file from hostPath: <code>/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml</code> → container: <code>/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml</code>. Mount the log directory: <code>/var/log/kubernetes/</code> → container: <code>/var/log/kubernetes/</code>.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a complete CKS exam task: enable audit logging end-to-end. The gotcha: you must add BOTH the flags AND the volume mounts to the API server manifest. Without volume mounts, the containerized API server can't access the host files. The audit policy file controls WHAT is logged; without it, all requests are logged at Metadata level which fills the disk. Always test: <code>kubectl get pods</code> then check <code>tail -f /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</code> for the event.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

