        <article class="chapter" id="ch9">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 9 · RBAC — Role-Based Access Control for Security</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">RBAC is the <strong>authorization engine</strong> of Kubernetes. After a user or ServiceAccount authenticates, RBAC determines what they can do — which resources they can access, which verbs they can perform, and in which namespaces. Misconfigured RBAC is the most common Kubernetes security vulnerability after exposed dashboards. A single wildcard <code>*</code> in a ClusterRole, a cluster-admin binding to the wrong subject, or an escalate verb granted to a non-admin — any of these gives an attacker full cluster control. This chapter teaches you to design, implement, and audit RBAC from a security-first perspective: least privilege by default, explicit allow rules only, and continuous verification that no subject has more permissions than they need.</p>
