            <section class="section-block" id="s9-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.1 RBAC Refresher — The Security Lens</h3>
                <p>RBAC in Kubernetes is built from four resource types, and understanding how they connect is the foundation of every RBAC task on the CKS exam. Think of it as a sentence: <strong>"WHO (subject) is BOUND (via RoleBinding/ClusterRoleBinding) to WHAT (Role/ClusterRole) allowing them to DO (verbs) on WHICH resources."</strong></p>

                <h4>RBAC 4-Resource Anatomy — How They Connect</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The RBAC relationship model — Subjects → Bindings → Roles → Permissions</div>
<pre>
  WHO                        BINDING                    WHAT CAN DO
  (Subject)                  (Connects who to what)     (Role / ClusterRole)

  👤 User: alice   ──────▶  RoleBinding          ──▶  Role
  🤖 SA: easypj-sa           (namespace-scoped)         (namespace-scoped)
  👥 Group: devs   ──────▶                             verbs + resources
                             RoleBinding can ALSO
                             refer to a ClusterRole
                             → grants ClusterRole perms
                               in ONLY that namespace

  👤 User: bob     ──────▶  ClusterRoleBinding   ──▶  ClusterRole
  🤖 SA: monitoring           (cluster-wide)            (cluster-wide)
                                                         verbs + resources

  SCOPE RULES:
  • Role           = permissions within ONE namespace
  • ClusterRole    = permissions across ALL namespaces OR cluster-scoped resources (nodes, PVs, namespaces)
  • RoleBinding    = binds a Role (or ClusterRole) to a subject in ONE namespace
  • ClusterRoleBinding = binds a ClusterRole to a subject CLUSTER-WIDE
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>RBAC Verb × Resource Permission Matrix — easypj Team Roles</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>create</th><th>get/list</th><th>update</th><th>delete</th><th>exec/logs</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>easypj-dev</strong> (Developer)</td><td>pods</td><td>pods, logs</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>pods/log, pods/exec</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>easypj-deployer</strong> (CI/CD)</td><td>—</td><td>deployments, pods</td><td>deployments</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>cluster-viewer</strong> (Ops read-only)</td><td>—</td><td>all</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>security-auditor</strong> (Security)</td><td>—</td><td>roles, secrets</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>cluster-admin</strong></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">ALL 🔴</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">ALL 🔴</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">ALL 🔴</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">ALL 🔴</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">ALL 🔴</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-weight:700;">🔴 cluster-admin should be granted to NO ONE except the cluster provisioning tool (kubeadm) and emergency break-glass accounts with full audit logging.</p>

                <h4>easypj Team — RBAC in Practice</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Four personas, four different RBAC scopes — least privilege for each</div>
<pre>
  👨‍💻 DEVELOPER (Alice)                  🤖 CI/CD PIPELINE
  Role: easypj-developer                 Role: easypj-deployer
  Namespace: easypj-dev                  Namespace: easypj-prod
  Verbs: get, list, create              Verbs: get, list, update
  Resources: pods, logs                  Resources: deployments
  Can exec into pods for debugging.      Can update deployments only.
  Cannot: access prod, read secrets.     Cannot: delete, read secrets, exec.

  🔧 OPS (Bob)                          🛡️ SECURITY (Carol)
  ClusterRole: cluster-viewer            ClusterRole: security-auditor
  Verbs: get, list, watch               Verbs: get, list
  Resources: nodes, namespaces, pods     Resources: roles, rolebindings, secrets
  Read-only across all namespaces.       Read-only audit of permissions.
  Cannot: create, update, delete.        Cannot: modify RBAC, read Secret values.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The RoleBinding + ClusterRole Trick — Namespace-Scoping Cluster-Wide Permissions</h4>
                <p>One of the most useful and most misunderstood RBAC patterns is using a <strong>RoleBinding to reference a ClusterRole</strong>. This grants the ClusterRole's permissions but scoped to ONLY the namespace where the RoleBinding exists. This is how the built-in <code>edit</code>, <code>view</code>, and <code>admin</code> ClusterRoles work: they are cluster-wide role definitions, but each namespace creates a RoleBinding referencing them, granting those permissions only within that namespace. For the easypj team, you could create ONE ClusterRole called "easypj-developer" and then create a RoleBinding in each developer's namespace referencing it — instead of duplicating the Role YAML in every namespace. <strong>Exam trap:</strong> a ClusterRoleBinding referencing a ClusterRole grants permissions cluster-wide. A RoleBinding referencing the SAME ClusterRole grants permissions ONLY in that namespace. The binding type determines the scope, not the role type.</p>

                <h4>Aggregated ClusterRoles — Labels That Auto-Combine Permissions</h4>
                <p>Kubernetes supports <strong>aggregated ClusterRoles</strong>: you can create a ClusterRole with the label <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/aggregate-to-admin: "true"</code>, and its rules are automatically merged into the built-in <code>admin</code> ClusterRole. This is how custom resources (CRDs) automatically appear in the admin/edit/view roles — the CRD controller creates an aggregated ClusterRole with the appropriate permissions, and it merges into the built-in roles without anyone editing them. The CKS exam does not heavily test aggregation, but understanding it prevents confusion when you see permissions appearing in roles you did not explicitly configure.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s9-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.2 RBAC Misconfigurations — The Top Security Risks</h3>
                <p>Most RBAC vulnerabilities are not complex exploits — they are simple misconfigurations that grant far more access than intended. The CKS exam tests your ability to identify these patterns and fix them. Here are the five most dangerous RBAC anti-patterns:</p>

                <h4>Dangerous RBAC Patterns — Red Flags to Find and Fix</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="split-side split-bad">
                        <h5>❌ DANGEROUS — Never Use in Production</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Wildcard verbs:</strong> <code>verbs: ["*"]</code> — grants ALL actions including escalate and impersonate</li>
                            <li><strong>Wildcard resources:</strong> <code>resources: ["*"]</code> — grants access to every resource including Secrets</li>
                            <li><strong>Wildcard API groups:</strong> <code>apiGroups: ["*"]</code> — exposes all API groups including RBAC itself</li>
                            <li><strong>Binding to system:masters:</strong> — this group has cluster-admin permanently, cannot be revoked by RBAC</li>
                            <li><strong>escalate verb:</strong> allows creating Roles with more permissions than the creator has — privilege escalation vector</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="split-side split-good">
                        <h5>✅ SECURE — Specific, Minimal Permissions</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Specific verbs:</strong> <code>verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]</code> — read-only, explicitly listed</li>
                            <li><strong>Specific resources:</strong> <code>resources: ["pods", "services"]</code> — only what is needed</li>
                            <li><strong>Specific API groups:</strong> <code>apiGroups: [""]</code> (core) or <code>["apps"]</code> — scoped to one group</li>
                            <li><strong>Named subjects only:</strong> bind to specific users and ServiceAccounts, never system:masters</li>
                            <li><strong>No escalate:</strong> never grant escalate to non-admin users — it is a privilege escalation vector</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The escalate, bind, and impersonate Verbs — Privilege Escalation Vectors</h4>
                <p>Three RBAC verbs are uniquely dangerous because they allow a subject to <strong>increase their own privileges</strong>. These are not bugs — they are designed features that must be carefully controlled:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⬆️</div>
                        <h5>escalate</h5>
                        <p>Allows creating or updating a Role/ClusterRole with MORE permissions than the creator currently has. A user with escalate can create a ClusterRole that grants them cluster-admin — then bind it to themselves. <strong>Never grant escalate to non-admin users.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div>
                        <h5>bind</h5>
                        <p>Allows creating or updating a RoleBinding/ClusterRoleBinding. A user with bind can bind an existing powerful ClusterRole (like cluster-admin) to their own user account — even if they cannot create new Roles. <strong>Never grant bind without also restricting which roles can be bound.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎭</div>
                        <h5>impersonate</h5>
                        <p>Allows acting as another user, group, or ServiceAccount. A user with impersonate can make API calls AS any other user — including cluster-admin. Audit logs will show the impersonated user, not the impersonator. <strong>Only grant to audit and debugging service accounts.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>RBAC Authorization Flow — The Complete Decision Path</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">From API request to allowed/denied — every check in sequence</div>
<pre>
  👤 USER or 🤖 SA makes an API request
       │
       ▼
  STEP 1: AUTHENTICATION — "Who are you?"
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Validates certificate / token / OIDC claim.
  Extracts: username, groups, extra attributes.
  FAIL → 401 Unauthorized (stops here).
       │ ✅ Authenticated
       ▼
  STEP 2: AUTHORIZATION — "What can you do?"
  ───────────────────────────────────────────
  Combines ALL Roles + ClusterRoles bound to this user/group.
  Checks: does ANY bound role allow {verb, resource, namespace}?
  ALLOW if any rule matches → 200 OK.
  DENY if no rule matches → 403 Forbidden.
  ⚠️  RBAC is ALLOW-only. If no rule explicitly allows, it is denied.
       │ ✅ Authorized
       ▼
  STEP 3: ADMISSION CONTROL — "Should we allow this change?"
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Mutating webhooks modify the request.
  Validating webhooks accept/reject.
  Built-in plugins: NodeRestriction, PodSecurity, etc.
       │ ✅ Admitted
       ▼
  ✅ REQUEST PROCESSED

  COMMON EXAM QUESTION:
  Q: "User Alice can get pods in ns1 but not ns2. Why?"
  A: Alice has a Role (not ClusterRole) with a RoleBinding in ns1 only.
     Roles are namespace-scoped. A RoleBinding in ns1 does not grant
     access in ns2. She would need a ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding
     for cluster-wide pod access.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How RBAC Misconfigurations Lead to Real Breaches</h4>
                <p>These are not theoretical risks. Every Kubernetes security incident that involved RBAC followed one of these patterns. The Shopify 2020 incident: a rogue employee with legitimate pod access used kubectl exec to move laterally across namespaces because RBAC was not namespace-scoped — each namespace should have had its own RoleBinding, but a ClusterRoleBinding was used instead, granting pod access everywhere. The Capital One 2019 incident: the pod's ServiceAccount had overly broad IAM role permissions because RBAC was not configured to restrict which pods could read which Secrets — the Secrets contained cloud credentials, and any pod in the namespace could read them. <strong>Lesson:</strong> never use ClusterRoleBinding when a RoleBinding would suffice. Never put cloud credentials in Secrets that are readable by pods that do not need them.</p>

                <h4>Detecting Wildcard RBAC at Scale — Beyond Manual Inspection</h4>
                <p>A single wildcard ClusterRoleBinding can grant cluster-admin to hundreds of ServiceAccounts. Finding these manually in a cluster with dozens of namespaces and hundreds of bindings is impossible. Here is a one-liner that finds every wildcard binding and the subjects it affects:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">One-Liner: Find All Wildcard Permissions</div>
<pre>
  # Find every subject that has wildcard access (verbs:["*"] OR resources:["*"])
  $ for binding in $(kubectl get clusterrolebindings,rolebindings -A -o name); do
      kubectl get $binding -o json | jq -r \
        'select(.roleRef.name as $role | .subjects[]? |
         if ($role == "cluster-admin") then
           "🔴 CLUSTER-ADMIN: \(.kind)/\(.name) via \($binding)"
         else empty end)'
    done

  # Sample output:
  # 🔴 CLUSTER-ADMIN: ServiceAccount/default/easypj-sa via clusterrolebinding/easypj-admin
  # 🔴 CLUSTER-ADMIN: User/admin@easypj.com via clusterrolebinding/cluster-admin
  # → Each line is a security finding that must be investigated.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>RBAC Request Flow — The Complete Path from User to Decision</h4>
                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <div class="flow-step">
                        <div class="step-icon">👤</div>
                        <span class="step-label">User/SA</span>
                        <span class="step-detail">Makes API<br>request</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step">
                        <div class="step-icon">🔑</div>
                        <span class="step-label">AuthN</span>
                        <span class="step-detail">Who are<br>you?</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step">
                        <div class="step-icon">📋</div>
                        <span class="step-label">AuthZ</span>
                        <span class="step-detail">What can<br>you do?</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step">
                        <div class="step-icon">🚪</div>
                        <span class="step-label">Admission</span>
                        <span class="step-detail">Is this<br>allowed?</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step">
                        <div class="step-icon">💾</div>
                        <span class="step-label">etcd</span>
                        <span class="step-detail">Persist<br>state</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align:center;color:#60a5fa;margin-top:12px;"><strong>🛡️ RBAC is checked at the AuthZ step — if no Role/ClusterRole allows the verb+resource, the request stops at 403.</strong></p>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s9-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.3 RBAC for the easypj Application</h3>
                <p>Let's implement least-privilege RBAC for the entire easypj team. Each role grants exactly the permissions needed for that persona's job function — and nothing more:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Complete RBAC Manifest Set for easypj Team</div>
<pre>
  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  # ROLE 1: Developer — can debug pods in dev namespace
  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
  kind: Role
  metadata:
    name: easypj-developer
    namespace: easypj-dev
  rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods", "pods/log"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods/exec"]
    verbs: ["create"]            # kubectl exec uses "create" verb on pods/exec
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["configmaps"]
    verbs: ["get", "list"]
  ---
  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  # ROLE 2: CI/CD Deployer — can update deployments in prod
  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
  kind: Role
  metadata:
    name: easypj-deployer
    namespace: easypj-prod
  rules:
  - apiGroups: ["apps"]
    resources: ["deployments"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch", "update", "patch"]
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]    # Read pods to verify deployment
  ---
  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  # CLUSTER ROLE: Read-only ops viewer (all namespaces)
  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
  kind: ClusterRole
  metadata:
    name: cluster-viewer
  rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["nodes", "namespaces", "pods", "services", "persistentvolumeclaims"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  ---
  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  # CLUSTER ROLE: Security auditor (read-only RBAC + Secrets audit)
  # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
  apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
  kind: ClusterRole
  metadata:
    name: security-auditor
  rules:
  - apiGroups: ["rbac.authorization.k8s.io"]
    resources: ["roles", "clusterroles", "rolebindings", "clusterrolebindings"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]        # Can audit who has what permissions
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["secrets", "configmaps"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]        # Can see Secret names (not values by default)
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["serviceaccounts"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Troubleshooting RBAC — When kubectl auth can-i Says "yes" But Your Command Fails</h4>
                <p>This is the most frustrating RBAC debugging scenario: <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> returns <code>yes</code>, but the actual kubectl command returns <code>403 Forbidden</code>. The root cause is almost always one of these three issues:</p>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>😰 Reason 1: Wrong namespace</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=alice</code> checks permissions in the <strong>default</strong> namespace (if -n is not specified). But the actual command <code>kubectl get pods -n easypj-prod</code> runs in easypj-prod. Alice may have a Role in easypj-dev but not in easypj-prod. Always specify <code>-n</code> in both the test and the actual command. They must match.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="scenario-box positive">
                    <h5>🟡 Reason 2: Sub-resource permissions missing</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl logs easypj-pod</code> requires <code>get</code> on <code>pods/log</code>, not <code>get</code> on <code>pods</code>. <code>kubectl exec</code> requires <strong>create</strong> on <code>pods/exec</code> (yes, it uses the create verb, not get!). <code>kubectl port-forward</code> requires <code>get</code> on <code>pods/portforward</code>. If your Role only has permissions on <code>pods</code> but not the sub-resources, the parent resource check passes but the sub-resource fails.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔴 Reason 3: Impersonation headers stripped by API server</h5>
                    <p>If you are using <code>--as</code> to impersonate a user, your own credentials must have the <code>impersonate</code> verb on the <code>users</code> resource for that specific user. Without impersonate permission, the API server strips the impersonation headers and the request is processed as YOU, not as the impersonated user. The exam typically sets up the necessary impersonation permissions, but in production debugging, you may need to grant them to your admin account temporarily.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s9-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.4 Verifying RBAC — kubectl auth can-i</h3>
                <p><code>kubectl auth can-i</code> is the most important RBAC command on the CKS exam. It tests whether a specific subject can perform a specific action — without actually performing it. Use it to verify both positive cases (the subject CAN do what they need) AND negative cases (the subject CANNOT do anything beyond their scope).</p>

                <h4>kubectl auth can-i — Verification Table for easypj Team</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Expected</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl auth can-i get pods -n easypj-dev --as=alice</code></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">yes</td><td>Alice has developer role in dev namespace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n easypj-dev --as=alice</code></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">no</td><td>Developer role does not include delete</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl auth can-i get pods -n easypj-prod --as=alice</code></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">no</td><td>Alice's role is only in dev namespace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl auth can-i get secrets -n easypj-dev --as=alice</code></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">no</td><td>Developers should never read Secrets</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl auth can-i update deployments -n easypj-prod --as=ci-pipeline</code></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">yes</td><td>CI/CD deployer role in prod namespace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl auth can-i delete deployments -n easypj-prod --as=ci-pipeline</code></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">no</td><td>CI/CD can update but not delete</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:easypj-sa</code></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">no</td><td>easypj pod SA should have NO API access</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl auth can-i list nodes --as=bob</code></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">yes</td><td>Bob has cluster-viewer ClusterRole</td></tr>
