            <section class="section-block" id="s9-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.6 Official RBAC Facts — Patterns the Exam Assumes You Already Know</h3>
                <p>Beyond building and testing RBAC, the CKS exam assumes a handful of facts about how the authorization engine actually behaves — the deny-by-default logic, the additive nature of permissions, and exactly which three verbs turn a limited Role into a privilege-escalation path. Know these cold; they explain every RBAC troubleshooting scenario you will face.</p>

                <h4>RBAC Deny-by-Default — How the Authorization Engine Thinks</h4>
                <p>Kubernetes RBAC is <strong>deny-by-default</strong>. If no RoleBinding or ClusterRoleBinding grants a specific permission, it is automatically denied. There is no "deny" rule in RBAC — you cannot explicitly deny a permission. You can only grant or not grant. This has profound implications for how you design and audit RBAC:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">RBAC Authorization Logic — How the API Server Decides ALLOW vs DENY</div>
<pre>
  REQUEST: User "alice" wants to DELETE pods in namespace "easypj-prod"
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STEP 1: FIND ALL BINDINGS for this user                             │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────                            │
  │  The RBAC authorizer searches ALL RoleBindings in "easypj-prod"      │
  │  AND ALL ClusterRoleBindings (cluster-wide).                          │
  │                                                                       │
  │  Found bindings for user "alice":                                    │
  │  • RoleBinding "pod-reader" in easypj-prod → Role "pod-reader"       │
  │    verbs: [get, list, watch]                                          │
  │    resources: [pods]                                                  │
  │  • ClusterRoleBinding "namespace-admin" → ClusterRole "admin"         │
  │    verbs: [get, list, watch, create, update, patch, delete]          │
  │    resources: [pods, deployments, services, ...]                      │
  │                                                                       │
  │  STEP 2: UNION all permissions from all bindings                     │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────                    │
  │  Combined permissions:                                                │
  │  pods: [get, list, watch] (from pod-reader)                           │
  │  pods: [get, list, watch, create, update, patch, delete] (from admin) │
  │  → Effective: pods: [get, list, watch, create, update, patch, delete] │
  │                                                                       │
  │  STEP 3: CHECK if the requested verb is in the effective set         │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────            │
  │  Requested: [delete] [pods] in [easypj-prod]                          │
  │  Effective set contains "delete" for "pods" → ALLOWED ✅              │
  │                                                                       │
  │  STEP 4: IF NOT FOUND → DENY (no explicit deny needed)               │
  │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────               │
  │  If alice had ONLY the "pod-reader" binding:                          │
  │  Effective set: pods: [get, list, watch]                              │
  │  Requested: [delete] → NOT in set → DENIED ❌                           │
  │  No "deny delete" rule was needed. The absence of permission = denial.│
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  CRITICAL RBAC PROPERTIES:
  ─────────────────────────
  1. Permissions are ADDITIVE. Two bindings = UNION of permissions.
  2. There is NO deny rule. You CANNOT say "alice can do everything EXCEPT delete pods."
     You must list exactly what alice CAN do. If you grant *, she gets delete.
  3. ClusterRoleBindings grant cluster-wide access. RoleBindings grant
     namespace-scoped access. Both apply simultaneously.
  4. The "*" wildcard in verbs means ALL verbs (including escalate, bind, impersonate).
     Granting "*" to a non-admin is effectively giving them cluster-admin.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The Three Most Dangerous RBAC Verbs — escalate, bind, and impersonate</h4>
                <p>These three verbs are special. They allow privilege escalation. Granting any of them to a non-admin user is equivalent to giving them cluster-admin — because they can use these verbs to give themselves cluster-admin:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⬆️</div>
                        <h5><code>escalate</code></h5>
                        <p>Allows creating/updating ClusterRoles even if you don't have the permissions those ClusterRoles grant. <strong>Attack:</strong> User creates a ClusterRole that grants themselves * on * → now they're cluster-admin. <strong>Never grant escalate to non-admins.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div>
                        <h5><code>bind</code></h5>
                        <p>Allows creating RoleBindings/ClusterRoleBindings even if you don't have the permissions in the role you're binding. <strong>Attack:</strong> User binds the existing cluster-admin ClusterRole to their own user account. <strong>Never grant bind to non-admins.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎭</div>
                        <h5><code>impersonate</code></h5>
                        <p>Allows acting as another user via <code>kubectl --as=other-user</code>. <strong>Attack:</strong> User impersonates system:admin or a ServiceAccount with cluster-admin. All audit logs show the impersonated user, not the attacker. <strong>Restrict impersonate tightly.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>🔧 Under the Hood — RBAC Evaluation Algorithm: How the API Server Matches Requests to Roles — Subject → RoleBinding → Role → Rules</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <h5>🗺️ RBAC Resolution — From kubectl Command to Allow/Deny Decision</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  kubectl get pods -n easypj-prod --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:dev-sa
       │
       ▼
  1. EXTRACT SUBJECT: "system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:dev-sa"
       │
       ▼
  2. FIND RoleBindings IN NAMESPACE "easypj-prod" WHERE subjects MATCH
     → RoleBinding: "dev-readonly-binding" references SA "dev-sa"
       │
       ▼
  3. RESOLVE Role: "dev-readonly"
     → rules: [{apiGroups:[""], resources:["pods"], verbs:["get","list"]}]
       │
       ▼
  4. MATCH request AGAINST rules:
     • apiGroup: "" (core) ✅ (request is for core API)
     • resource: "pods" ✅ (request is for pods)
     • verb: "get" in ["get","list"] ✅
     • resourceName: not specified → matches all pods ✅
       │
       ▼
  5. ALLOW → Return HTTP 200 with pod list

  IF NO MATCH → Also check ClusterRoleBindings (cluster-wide)
  IF STILL NO MATCH → DENY → HTTP 403 Forbidden

  🔑 RoleBindings are namespace-scoped. ClusterRoleBindings are cluster-wide.
     A RoleBinding can reference a ClusterRole for cross-namespace access.</pre>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Four resources, one sentence:</strong> WHO (subject) is BOUND (RoleBinding/ClusterRoleBinding) to WHAT (Role/ClusterRole) to DO (verbs) on WHICH resources — every RBAC task on the exam reduces to filling in that sentence.</li>
                    <li><strong>Scope comes from the binding, not the role:</strong> a RoleBinding referencing a ClusterRole grants permissions ONLY in that namespace; a ClusterRoleBinding referencing the same ClusterRole grants them cluster-wide.</li>
                    <li><strong>Five patterns cause almost every RBAC breach:</strong> wildcard verbs, wildcard resources, wildcard API groups, bindings to system:masters, and granting escalate/bind/impersonate to non-admins.</li>
                    <li><strong>kubectl auth can-i is the verification tool:</strong> always test the positive case (subject CAN do their job) and the negative case (subject CANNOT do anything beyond it) before submitting.</li>
                    <li><strong>RBAC is additive and deny-by-default:</strong> permissions from every binding UNION together, there is no explicit deny rule, and anything not explicitly granted is denied.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 9 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Role vs ClusterRole:</strong> Role is NAMESPACED — you MUST specify <code>-n &lt;ns&gt;</code>. ClusterRole is CLUSTER-WIDE. Using <code>kubectl create role</code> without <code>-n</code> creates it in default namespace, not cluster-wide.</li>
                    <li><strong>RBAC is deny-by-default:</strong> There is no "deny" rule in K8s RBAC. You cannot explicitly block a permission. If no Role grants the permission, it's denied. You can't create "allow-all-except-secrets" — list every allowed resource explicitly.</li>
                    <li><strong>aggregate-to-* labels:</strong> Default ClusterRoles like <code>view</code>, <code>edit</code>, <code>admin</code> use <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/aggregate-to-view: "true"</code> labels. Creating a ClusterRole with this label auto-merges its rules into the <code>view</code> role.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl auth can-i --list -n &lt;ns&gt; --as=&lt;subject&gt;</code> — shows ALL permissions at once.</li>
                    <li>Verbs: lowercase (get, list, create, delete, update, patch, watch). Resources: lowercase plural (pods, secrets).</li>
