                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What dangerous verb allows a user to act as another user, and how would you detect it being abused in audit logs?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The <strong>impersonate</strong> verb allows <code>kubectl --as=&lt;other-user&gt;</code>. Detection: audit logs show both the ORIGINAL user and the IMPERSONATED user. Query: <code>cat audit.log | jq 'select(.user.extra."authentication.k8s.io/original-username" != null)'</code>. This shows all requests where impersonation was used. The original username is preserved in the audit event annotations.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">RBAC is deny-by-default. A user complains they can't list pods. What command debugs this, and what's the most common mistake?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Debug: <code>kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=&lt;user&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code>. Most common mistakes: forgetting <code>-n</code> flag, using wrong namespace, confusing subresources (pods/log needs separate permission), or not waiting for RBAC propagation. Use <code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=&lt;sa&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> to see all permissions for an identity.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Kubernetes RBAC has no explicit "deny" rules — if no Role grants the permission, it's denied. The <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> command is the fastest debugging tool. Always test BOTH an allowed verb AND a denied verb when verifying RBAC on the exam.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You create a Role with <code>resources: ["*"]</code> and <code>verbs: ["*"]</code>. Is this equivalent to cluster-admin? What's the blast radius difference?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>No, it's NOT equivalent.</strong> A Role with wildcards is still NAMESPACE-SCOPED — the subject can do anything within that one namespace but cannot touch other namespaces or cluster-scoped resources (nodes, PVs, namespaces). cluster-admin is a ClusterRoleBinding to the built-in <code>cluster-admin</code> ClusterRole with <code>*/*/*</code> across everything. Blast radius: one namespace vs. entire cluster.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This distinction is critical for CKS. A namespace-admin role is dangerous but contained. cluster-admin is complete cluster takeover. On the exam: (1) never bind cluster-admin unless explicitly required, (2) never use wildcards even within a namespace, (3) a Role with wildcards can still delete all pods, secrets, and configmaps in that namespace.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain RBAC aggregation labels. How does the built-in "view" ClusterRole auto-expand when a new CustomResourceDefinition is created?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The built-in ClusterRoles <code>view</code>, <code>edit</code>, and <code>admin</code> use aggregation labels. When you create a ClusterRole with label <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/aggregate-to-view: "true"</code>, its rules are automatically merged into the <code>view</code> ClusterRole by the kube-controller-manager. CRD authors use this so users with "view" access automatically get read access to new custom resources without manually editing the view role.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Aggregation labels: <code>aggregate-to-view</code>, <code>aggregate-to-edit</code>, <code>aggregate-to-admin</code>. On the CKS exam, you may need to create a ClusterRole that aggregates into view so monitoring tools automatically get read access to your CRDs. The aggregation happens automatically — no need to modify the view role itself.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod's ServiceAccount has <code>get secrets</code> via RBAC, but <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>. Can the pod still read secrets via the API? Why or why not?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>No.</strong> <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> means the SA token is NOT mounted at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code>. Without a token, the pod cannot authenticate to the API server — RBAC permissions are irrelevant. The RBAC grant exists on paper but the pod has no way to present its identity. This is defense in depth: RBAC controls WHAT you can do; token mounting controls WHETHER you can try.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This tests the interaction between RBAC and SA tokens. On the CKS exam, use BOTH controls: (1) RBAC to limit permissions, AND (2) <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> on pods that don't need API access. Even if RBAC grants broad permissions, without a mounted token the pod can't use them.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A CI/CD pipeline needs to: update Deployments in "staging" AND create Secrets in "staging" (for TLS certs). It should read nothing else. Write the exact Role YAML.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: cicd-deployer
  namespace: staging
rules:
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch", "update", "patch"]
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["secrets"]
  verbs: ["create", "update", "patch"]
# No "get" or "list" on secrets — CI/CD creates but cannot READ existing secrets</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Least privilege means surgical verb selection. CI/CD needs UPDATE deployments but not DELETE. It needs CREATE secrets for new TLS certs but NOT GET (can't read existing database passwords). Every verb must be justified by a specific pipeline step. This granularity is exactly what the CKS exam tests.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An attacker compromises a pod and discovers its SA token. The SA has <code>get secrets</code> in "prod". What's the maximum damage, and what 3 RBAC-related fixes would have prevented it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Maximum damage:</strong> Attacker reads every Secret in "prod" — database passwords, API keys, TLS private keys, OAuth secrets. Can then pivot to databases, external APIs, and forge TLS certificates.</p>
                    <p><strong>3 fixes:</strong> (1) <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> — no token to steal. (2) Remove <code>get secrets</code> RBAC — the pod doesn't need API-level secret access; use env vars or mounted secrets instead. (3) External secret store (Vault) — secrets never exist in Kubernetes Secrets at all; the SA can only request its OWN secrets from Vault via a sidecar, not read cluster-wide secrets.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This scenario combines multiple CKS domains. Blast radius = what the SA can do (RBAC) × whether it has a token (automount) × what data exists in accessible resources (Secrets). On the exam, apply ALL three fixes for defense in depth — never rely on just one control.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

