            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 10 · Service Accounts — Securing Pod Identity</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Every pod in Kubernetes has an identity — a <strong>ServiceAccount</strong>. By default, every pod gets the <code>default</code> ServiceAccount in its namespace, and that ServiceAccount's JWT token is automatically mounted into the pod at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code>. Any process inside the pod can read this token and use it to authenticate to the Kubernetes API. If the ServiceAccount has RBAC permissions — even accidentally — every pod using it inherits those permissions. If the token is stolen (via a compromised container, a leaked log, or a misconfigured volume), the attacker has a permanent, non-expiring credential. This chapter teaches you to control pod identity: disable token auto-mounting for pods that do not need API access, create dedicated ServiceAccounts with minimal RBAC, and use bound tokens that automatically expire — eliminating the risk of stolen credentials granting persistent access.</p>
