            <section class="section-block" id="s10-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.1 How Pods Authenticate to the API Server</h3>
                <p>Every pod, when it starts, has a specific set of files mounted into it by the kubelet. These files are the pod's <strong>identity documents</strong> — and any process inside the container can read them:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">What the kubelet mounts into every pod — the identity files</div>
<pre>
  $ kubectl exec easypj-pod -- ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/
  ca.crt       ← Cluster CA certificate (to verify the API server's TLS cert)
  namespace    ← The pod's namespace (plain text: "easypj-prod")
  token        ← THE JWT TOKEN — this IS the pod's identity

  $ kubectl exec easypj-pod -- cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
  eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsImtpZCI6IiJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJrdWJlcm5ldGVzL3NlcnZpY2VhY2NvdW50Iiwia3ViZXJuZ
  XRlcy5pby9zZXJ2aWNlYWNjb3VudC9uYW1lc3BhY2UiOiJlYXN5cGotcHJvZCIsImt1YmVybmV0ZXMuaW8vc2Vydml
  jZWFjY291bnQvc2VydmljZS1hY2NvdW50Lm5hbWUiOiJkZWZhdWx0Iiwic3ViIjoic3lzdGVtOnNlcnZpY2VhY2Nvd
  W50OmVhc3lwai1wcm9kOmRlZmF1bHQifQ...

  # Decode the JWT payload (middle section between the dots):
  $ echo "eyJpc3Mi..." | cut -d'.' -f2 | base64 -d | python -m json.tool
  {
    "iss": "kubernetes/serviceaccount",
    "kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace": "easypj-prod",
    "kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/secret.name": "default-token-xxxxx",
    "kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/service-account.name": "default",
    "sub": "system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:default"
  }
  → This pod identifies as: system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:default
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How the Pod Uses Its Token — The Full Flow</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">From pod to API — authentication, authorization, response</div>
<pre>
  1. Pod starts → kubelet creates the token volume mount
     Mount path: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/
     Files: token (JWT), ca.crt (CA cert), namespace

  2. App inside pod reads the token file
     $ cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token

  3. App calls the K8s API with the token as a Bearer header
     $ curl https://kubernetes.default.svc/api/v1/namespaces/easypj-prod/pods \
         --cacert /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt \
         -H "Authorization: Bearer $(cat /var/run/.../token)"

  4. API server receives the request:
     a. Validates the JWT signature (signed by the SA token controller)
     b. Extracts: username = system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:default
     c. Looks up RBAC: does this SA have "list pods" in easypj-prod?
     d. If RBAC allows → returns pod list. If not → 403 Forbidden.

  IF automountServiceAccountToken: false:
     Step 1 never happens → /var/run/secrets/ is EMPTY
     → Pod CANNOT call the K8s API at all ✅
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How the ServiceAccount Token Controller Works — Behind the Scenes</h4>
                <p>Three Kubernetes components collaborate to make ServiceAccount tokens work. Understanding this chain helps you diagnose token issues and understand why bound tokens are fundamentally more secure:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The ServiceAccount Token Pipeline — 3 Components</div>
<pre>
  1. SERVICEACCOUNT CONTROLLER (kube-controller-manager)
     ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Watches: ServiceAccount creations
     Action: Creates a Secret (type: kubernetes.io/service-account-token)
             containing a signed JWT for the SA.
     ⚠️  This is LEGACY behavior — for STATIC tokens only.
         Bound tokens are NOT created by this controller.

  2. TOKEN CONTROLLER (kube-controller-manager)
     ─────────────────────────────────────────────
     Watches: ServiceAccount deletions
     Action: Deletes all Secrets associated with a deleted SA.
     Also: Signs the JWT using the cluster's ServiceAccount private key
           (--service-account-private-key-file on kube-controller-manager).

  3. KUBELET (on every node)
     ────────────────────────
     Action: Mounts the token into every pod's filesystem.
     For STATIC tokens: reads the Secret, writes the token file.
     For BOUND tokens: calls the TokenRequest API to get a fresh,
     time-limited token on every pod start AND on rotation.

  🔑 KEY INSIGHT: Bound tokens NEVER touch a Secret. The kubelet
     calls the TokenRequest API directly, gets a JWT with an expiry,
     and writes it to a tmpfs volume. No Secret. No persistence.
     When the token expires, the kubelet gets a new one automatically.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The TokenRequest API — How Bound Tokens Are Created</h4>
                <p>The TokenRequest API is the modern replacement for static ServiceAccount tokens. Instead of creating a permanent Secret, you request a temporary token with a specific audience and duration. This API is what the kubelet uses internally for projected tokens — and you can use it directly to generate short-lived tokens for CI/CD pipelines or debugging:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Using the TokenRequest API — Generate a Short-Lived Token</div>
<pre>
  # Create a 1-hour token for the easypj-sa ServiceAccount
  $ kubectl create token easypj-sa -n easypj-prod --duration=1h
  eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsImtpZCI6Ii...truncated...

  # Use it directly with curl
  $ TOKEN=$(kubectl create token easypj-sa -n easypj-prod --duration=1h)
  $ curl -k https://kubernetes.default.svc/api/v1/namespaces/easypj-prod/pods \
      -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"
  → Returns pods if SA has RBAC, 403 if not.

  # This token expires in 1 hour. No Secret exists. Nothing to clean up.
  # This is the recommended way to generate tokens for CI/CD, not static Secrets.

  ⚠️  kubectl create token requires K8s 1.24+. For older versions,
      use kubectl create token -- requested via the raw API.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s10-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.2 The Dangers of the Default Service Account</h3>
                <p>Every namespace has a <code>default</code> ServiceAccount — created automatically by the namespace controller. Unless a pod explicitly specifies <code>serviceAccountName</code>, it uses this default SA. The danger: <strong>every pod in the namespace shares the same identity.</strong> If you grant any RBAC permissions to the default SA, every pod inherits them. If one pod is compromised and its token is stolen, the attacker can impersonate every other pod in that namespace.</p>

                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="split-side split-bad">
                        <h5>❌ Default ServiceAccount — Shared Identity</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>All pods → "default" SA</li>
                            <li>Grant permissions to default → ALL pods get permissions</li>
                            <li>One compromised pod = all pods' identity stolen</li>
                            <li>Token never expires (old-style static token)</li>
                            <li>Default SA appears in every namespace</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-weight:700;">Compromised easypj pod → can read Secrets as default SA → can access API across namespace.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="split-side split-good">
                        <h5>✅ Dedicated ServiceAccounts — Isolated Identity</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>easypj pod → easypj-sa</li>
                            <li>postgres pod → postgres-sa</li>
                            <li>Each SA → minimal, specific RBAC permissions</li>
                            <li>Tokens can be time-limited (projected tokens)</li>
                            <li>Automount disabled for pods that don't need API</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="color:#4ade80;font-weight:700;">Compromised easypj pod → can only do what easypj-sa allows → nothing else.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 The Default SA Is Shared — Never Give It Permissions</h5>
                    <p>The <code>default</code> ServiceAccount should have <strong>zero RBAC permissions</strong> in every namespace. If you need a pod to access the API, create a dedicated ServiceAccount for that specific workload and grant permissions only to that SA. Never create a RoleBinding that references the default SA. If you see <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:default</code> in any RoleBinding or ClusterRoleBinding, that is a security finding that must be remediated immediately.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Auditing Default ServiceAccount Permissions — Find the Gaps</h4>
                <p>Use these commands to audit every namespace for default SA permissions. Any non-empty result is a security finding:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">One-Liner: Find All Permissions Granted to Default ServiceAccounts</div>
<pre>
  # Find every RoleBinding that references a default SA
  $ kubectl get rolebindings,clusterrolebindings -A -o json | jq -r \
      '.items[] | select(.subjects[]? | select(.kind=="ServiceAccount" and .name=="default")) |
       "\(.metadata.namespace)/\(.metadata.name) → default SA"'

  # Sample output (each line is a FINDING):
  # easypj-prod/easypj-admin-binding → default SA 🔴
  # kube-public/default-view → default SA 🟡 (intentional for kube-public)

  # Check what the default SA can actually do in a namespace
  $ kubectl auth can-i --list \
      --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:default -n easypj-prod
  → Must return NO permissions. If any appear, investigate immediately.

  # Find namespaces where the default SA has ANY RBAC binding
  $ for ns in $(kubectl get ns -o name | cut -d/ -f2); do
      count=$(kubectl get rolebindings -n $ns -o json | jq \
        '[.items[]? | select(.subjects[]? | select(.kind=="ServiceAccount" and .name=="default"))] | length')
      if [ "$count" -gt 0 ]; then
        echo "🔴 $ns: default SA has $count RoleBinding(s)"
      fi
    done
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Attack Scenario: What Happens When a ServiceAccount Token Is Stolen</h4>
                <p>Understanding the risk requires seeing the attack. Here is exactly what happens when an attacker compromises an easypj pod and extracts its ServiceAccount token — and how the outcome differs dramatically depending on whether the SA has permissions and whether the token is static or bound:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Scenario A: Pod with DEFAULT SA + Accidental RBAC Permissions</div>
<pre>
  ATTACKER compromises easypj pod via CVE → gains shell as root
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  $ whoami
  root     ← Container runs as root

  $ cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
  eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsImtpZCI6Ii...   ← THE POD'S IDENTITY

  $ curl -s https://kubernetes.default.svc/api/v1/namespaces/easypj-prod/secrets \
      --cacert /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt \
      -H "Authorization: Bearer $(cat /var/run/.../token)"
  {
    "items": [
      {"metadata":{"name":"easypj-db-credentials"}},
      {"metadata":{"name":"easypj-tls"}},
      {"metadata":{"name":"aws-s3-backup-key"}}
    ]
  }
  → Attacker can LIST all Secrets in the namespace!
    Because someone granted "get secrets" to the default SA.

  $ curl .../secrets/easypj-db-credentials ...
  → Returns DATABASE_URL with username and password.
  → Attacker now has production database credentials.

  IMPACT: Full database access. All user data. All job listings.
  CAUSE: Default SA + accidentally granted Secret read permission.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Scenario B: Pod with DEDICATED SA + Zero Permissions</div>
<pre>
  ATTACKER compromises easypj pod via CVE → gains shell as root
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  $ whoami
  root

  $ cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
  → FILE NOT FOUND (automountServiceAccountToken: false)

  $ ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/
  → DIRECTORY EMPTY

  $ curl https://kubernetes.default.svc/api/v1/namespaces/easypj-prod/secrets
  → curl: (77) SSL certificate problem: unable to get local issuer certificate
  → Cannot even verify the API server's TLS cert (no ca.crt mounted)

  IMPACT: Zero. No token. No API access. No lateral movement via K8s API.
  CAUSE: automountServiceAccountToken: false + dedicated SA with no RBAC.
  Attacker must find another path — and NetworkPolicies (Ch 6) block that too.
</pre>
