            <section class="section-block" id="s10-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.3 Bound Service Account Tokens (Projected Tokens)</h3>
                <p>Legacy ServiceAccount tokens (the kind stored as Kubernetes Secrets) are <strong>non-expiring, non-revocable, and valid for any API server audience</strong>. If an attacker steals one — from a compromised container, a leaked log file, or a misconfigured volume — they have permanent API access. <strong>Bound tokens</strong> (also called projected tokens) solve this: they are time-limited, audience-scoped, and automatically rotated by the kubelet. No Secret is created. No permanent credential exists.</p>

                <h4>Old vs New ServiceAccount Tokens</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Old (Static) Token</th><th>New (Bound) Token</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Stored as</strong></td><td>Kubernetes Secret (auto-created per SA)</td><td>Projected volume (tmpfs, never on disk)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Expiry</strong></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">Never expires 🔴</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">1 hour (default), configurable ✅</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Audience</strong></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">Any K8s API server 🔴</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">Specific audience (e.g., "vault") ✅</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Rotation</strong></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">Manual only 🔴</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">Automatic (kubelet rotates before expiry) ✅</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Revocable</strong></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">Only by deleting the Secret 🔴</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">Expires automatically ✅</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>If stolen</strong></td><td style="color:#ef4444;">Permanent access 🔴</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">Access expires within 1 hour ✅</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Available since</strong></td><td>Kubernetes 1.0</td><td>Kubernetes 1.21+ (enabled by default in 1.24+)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Configuring a Bound Token — Pod Spec</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pod YAML with a projected ServiceAccount token (1-hour expiry)</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: v1
  kind: Pod
  metadata:
    name: easypj-secure
  spec:
    serviceAccountName: easypj-sa         # Dedicated SA, not default
    automountServiceAccountToken: false    # Disable the default token mount
    containers:
    - name: easypj
      image: easypj:v2
      volumeMounts:
      - name: token
        mountPath: /var/run/secrets/tokens # Custom path, not the default
        readOnly: true
    volumes:
    - name: token
      projected:
        sources:
        - serviceAccountToken:
            path: token                    # The token file name
            expirationSeconds: 3600        # 1 hour expiry ← KEY FIELD
            audience: api                  # Audience claim in the JWT
  # ⚠️  The old default mount at /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/
  #     is EMPTY because automountServiceAccountToken: false.
  #     Only THIS projected token exists, and it expires in 1 hour.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>What Happens When a Bound Token Expires Mid-Request</h4>
                <p>A common concern with bound tokens: "What if my pod is in the middle of a long-running operation and the token expires?" The answer depends on your Kubernetes version and configuration. In K8s 1.22+, the kubelet automatically rotates the token BEFORE it expires — typically at 80% of its lifetime. The new token is written to the same file atomically, and any process that re-reads the file gets the new token. For HTTP clients that reuse connections (keep-alive), the existing connection continues to work — token validation only happens at connection establishment. For new connections, the pod reads the updated token file and presents the fresh token. <strong>Best practice:</strong> set <code>expirationSeconds</code> to at least 3600 (1 hour) to give the kubelet time to rotate. Avoid very short expirations (less than 600 seconds) unless your application explicitly handles token refresh.</p>

                <h4>Disabling Legacy Tokens Cluster-Wide</h4>
                <p>Even after you configure bound tokens for your pods, the old static token Secrets may still exist — and they never expire. The CKS exam may ask you to ensure that legacy tokens are not in use. The API server flag <code>--service-account-extend-token-expiration=false</code> (K8s 1.24+) prevents the legacy token controller from creating auto-mounted Secrets for new ServiceAccounts. Combined with <code>--service-account-issuer</code> and <code>--service-account-signing-key-file</code> (which configure the bound token issuer), you can ensure that all new tokens are bound tokens — no static Secrets created, no permanent credentials lingering in your cluster.</p>
            </section>

