            <section class="section-block" id="s10-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.5 Official ServiceAccount Facts — Precedence and Lifecycle Details</h3>
                <p>Beyond building and disabling ServiceAccounts, the CKS exam assumes a handful of exact facts: which setting wins when a pod and its ServiceAccount disagree about automounting, and the precise order the token lifecycle follows from creation to expiry. Know these cold — they explain every "why didn't this pod get a token" and "why did this pod's token stop working" troubleshooting scenario.</p>

                <h4>automountServiceAccountToken Precedence — Which Setting Wins</h4>
                <p>Both a <code>ServiceAccount</code> and a <code>Pod</code> can set <code>automountServiceAccountToken</code>. When they disagree, exactly one wins — and getting this backwards is the single most common ServiceAccount mistake on the exam:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Precedence Chain — Pod Beats ServiceAccount Beats Cluster Default</div>
<pre>
  PRECEDENCE (highest to lowest):
  ────────────────────────────────
  1. Pod spec's automountServiceAccountToken       ← WINS if set (true OR false)
  2. ServiceAccount's automountServiceAccountToken ← Used only if pod doesn't set it
  3. Cluster default                                ← true (token IS mounted) if
                                                        neither pod nor SA set it

  EXAMPLE — the trap the exam sets:
  ──────────────────────────────────
  ServiceAccount "easypj-sa":  automountServiceAccountToken: false
  Pod using easypj-sa:         automountServiceAccountToken: true   ← explicit override

  RESULT: Token IS mounted. The pod-level "true" overrides the SA-level "false".
  ⚠️  Setting automount: false ONLY on the SA is not a guarantee — any pod spec
      can still opt back in with an explicit "true". For a hard guarantee, verify
      at the pod level too, or admission-control against it (Ch 24).

  IF NEITHER SPECIFIES ANYTHING:
  Default SA, no automount field anywhere → automountServiceAccountToken defaults
  to true → token IS mounted. This is why "I didn't set anything" pods still get
  a token — the cluster default is permissive, not restrictive.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The Complete Token Lifecycle — 5 Stages, Exact Order</h4>
                <p>Every ServiceAccount token — legacy or bound — passes through the same five stages. The exam expects you to know which component performs each stage and what triggers the transition to the next:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What Happens</th><th>Performed By</th><th>Key Fact</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>1. Creation</strong></td><td>Legacy: Secret created at SA creation time. Bound: nothing created yet — deferred to pod start.</td><td>ServiceAccount/Token controller (legacy) or none (bound, on-demand)</td><td>Bound tokens have no creation-time artifact — nothing to find with <code>kubectl get secrets</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>2. Request</strong></td><td>Bound only: kubelet calls the TokenRequest API for a fresh JWT scoped to this pod.</td><td>kubelet, via TokenRequest API</td><td>Legacy tokens skip this stage entirely — they're read from the pre-created Secret.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>3. Mount</strong></td><td>Token written into a projected volume at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</code> (or a custom path for projected volumes).</td><td>kubelet</td><td>Same mount path for both token types — you cannot tell which type is mounted by path alone; check <code>exp</code> claim.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>4. Use</strong></td><td>Application reads the token file and sends it as <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>. API server validates signature, extracts <code>sub</code>, runs RBAC.</td><td>App process + API server</td><td>API server checks signature against <code>--service-account-key-file</code>; expired <code>exp</code> claim → 401, not 403.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>5. Rotation / Expiry</strong></td><td>Bound: kubelet rotates automatically at ~80% of TTL, atomically replacing the file. Legacy: never rotates — valid until the Secret is deleted.</td><td>kubelet (bound) / manual deletion (legacy)</td><td>Default bound TTL is 1 hour; minimum recommended <code>expirationSeconds</code> is 600s unless the app handles refresh itself.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Facts the Exam Assumes You Already Know</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🪪</div>
                        <h5>Every namespace gets a default SA automatically</h5>
                        <p>Created by the namespace controller the moment the namespace exists — you never create it yourself, and you can't delete it permanently (it's recreated).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗄️</div>
                        <h5>Legacy tokens ARE Secrets — with Secret-level RBAC</h5>
                        <p>Anyone who can <code>get secrets</code> in the namespace can read a legacy SA token and impersonate that SA. Bound tokens never create this exposure — there's no Secret to read.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5>RBAC changes do NOT revoke an issued legacy token</h5>
                        <p>A legacy token that already passed authentication keeps working under the OLD permission set's binding structure until the Secret itself is deleted — changing a RoleBinding doesn't invalidate a token already in an attacker's hands.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⏱️</div>
                        <h5>Revocation on pod deletion is NOT instant — ~60 seconds</h5>
                        <p>A bound/projected token is invalidated roughly <strong>60 seconds after</strong> <code>.metadata.deletionTimestamp</code> is set on its pod — not the instant <code>kubectl delete pod</code> is run. Don't assume a deleted pod's token stops working immediately; it has a short grace window.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5>serviceAccountName is IMMUTABLE after pod creation</h5>
                        <p>You cannot patch a running pod onto a different ServiceAccount — you must recreate it. This mirrors RBAC binding immutability (§9.6): both identity bindings are fixed at creation time, not editable in place.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎫</div>
                        <h5>kubectl create token is the modern way to mint one</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create token easypj-sa --duration=1h</code> mints a short-lived token for testing. Manually creating a long-lived <code>kubernetes.io/service-account-token</code> Secret still works but is the deprecated legacy path — prefer <code>create token</code> whenever you just need to verify SA behavior.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Automount Precedence — Official Reference Table</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>ServiceAccount Setting</th><th>Pod Spec Setting</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>automount: false</code></td><td>(not set)</td><td>NOT mounted</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>automount: false</code></td><td><code>automount: true</code></td><td>MOUNTED (Pod wins)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>automount: true</code> / not set</td><td><code>automount: false</code></td><td>NOT mounted (Pod wins)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>(not set → defaults true)</td><td>(not set)</td><td>MOUNTED (cluster default)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm" style="color:#8b949e;">Source: kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-service-account — the exact rules the exam expects you to already know.</p>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>🔧 Under the Hood — ServiceAccount Token Lifecycle: Token Creation → Projected Volume Mount → JWT Validation → Token Expiry</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <h5>🗺️ SA Token Flow — From Secret to API Call</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   SERVICE ACCOUNT TOKEN LIFECYCLE                                          │
  │                                                                           │
  │  1. TOKEN CREATION (by TokenController)                                   │
  │     • When a SA is created, TokenController generates a JWT               │
  │     • Claims: sub, iss, aud, exp, iat, kubernetes.io/namespace, sa/name  │
  │     • Signed with: --service-account-key-file (API server flag)           │
  │                                                                           │
  │  2. TOKEN MOUNTED (by kubelet, unless automount=false)                    │
  │     • Path: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/                │
  │     • Files: token (JWT), ca.crt (cluster CA), namespace (text)          │
  │     • Volume type: projected ServiceAccountTokenVolume                     │
  │     • Token is auto-rotated by kubelet when >80% of TTL elapsed          │
  │                                                                           │
  │  3. POD USES TOKEN (curl / python requests)                               │
  │     curl https://kubernetes.default.svc/api/v1/namespaces/default/pods \  │
  │       -H "Authorization: Bearer $(cat /var/run/secrets/.../token)"       │
  │       --cacert /var/run/secrets/.../ca.crt                                │
  │                                                                           │
  │  4. API SERVER VALIDATES TOKEN                                             │
  │     • Checks JWT signature against --service-account-key-file             │
  │     • Checks token hasn't expired (exp claim)                              │
  │     • Extracts username: system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:&lt;sa&gt;                 │
  │     • Proceeds to RBAC authorization                                       │
  │                                                                           │
  │  5. TOKEN EXPIRY                                                            │
  │     • Default TTL: 1 hour (configurable via --service-account-token-ttl)  │
  │     • kubelet auto-refreshes before expiry                                 │
  │     • Pod sees the new token automatically (projected volume)             │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Every pod authenticates via a mounted JWT:</strong> unless disabled, the kubelet mounts a ServiceAccount token at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</code> that any process inside the container can read and use to call the API.</li>
                    <li><strong>The default SA is dangerous because it's shared:</strong> never bind RBAC to <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:default</code> — audit for it with <code>kubectl get rolebindings,clusterrolebindings -A -o json | jq</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Bound tokens fix the static-token problem:</strong> time-limited (1hr default), audience-scoped, pod-bound, and auto-rotated by the kubelet at ~80% TTL — a stolen bound token expires on its own.</li>
                    <li><strong>Every task has two halves:</strong> <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> for pods that don't call the API, and a dedicated SA + minimal RBAC for pods that do — always verify both directions.</li>
                    <li><strong>Precedence is pod &gt; SA &gt; cluster default:</strong> a pod-level automount setting always overrides the SA's setting, and if neither is set, the cluster default is permissive (token mounted).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 10 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Default SA in every namespace:</strong> Every namespace has a <code>default</code> ServiceAccount that is automatically mounted unless you set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>. Forgetting this means every pod gets API access by default.</li>
                    <li><strong>SA token is a Secret:</strong> When you create an SA, K8s auto-creates a Secret containing the token. This Secret has RBAC protections just like any other Secret. If an attacker can read Secrets in the namespace, they can steal the SA token and impersonate the SA.</li>
                    <li><strong>disableServiceLinks is not security:</strong> <code>enableServiceLinks: false</code> only prevents environment variable injection of service IPs — it does NOT prevent the SA token from being mounted. For no API access, use <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-speed"></span>
