                <h4>🧠 Chapter 10 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ServiceAccount that has NO Kubernetes API access. Write the SA and the pod spec referencing it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: isolated-sa
  namespace: easypj-prod
automountServiceAccountToken: false
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: test-pod
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  serviceAccountName: isolated-sa
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx
# Verify: kubectl exec test-pod -- ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/
# Should return: No such file or directory</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's inside a mounted ServiceAccount token? Decode it and explain each claim.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl exec pod -- cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d | jq .
# {
#   "aud": ["https://kubernetes.default.svc"],  # Who this token is for
#   "exp": 1753600000,                            # Expiration (Unix timestamp)
#   "iat": 1753596400,                            # Issued at
#   "iss": "https://kubernetes.default.svc",      # Issuer (API server)
#   "kubernetes.io": {
#     "namespace": "easypj-prod",                  # Pod's namespace
#     "serviceaccount": {"name": "sa-name", ...}  # Which SA
#   },
#   "sub": "system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:sa-name"
# }</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens if a pod's SA token expires while the pod is running? Does the pod lose API access?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>No — the kubelet automatically rotates the token BEFORE it expires (when &gt;80% of TTL has elapsed). The new token is written to the same projected volume path, and applications reading the file see the updated token. This is transparent to the application. The default TTL is 1 hour, and rotation happens around the 48-minute mark.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you verify that a specific ServiceAccount can (or cannot) perform an action? Write the exact command.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl auth can-i get pods -n easypj-prod --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:easypj-sa
# Returns: yes or no

kubectl auth can-i --list -n easypj-prod --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:easypj-sa
# Lists ALL permissions for that SA</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between a ServiceAccount token (JWT) and a client certificate for authentication? Which is preferred for pods and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>SA Token (JWT):</strong> A signed JSON Web Token auto-mounted into pods. Contains claims (namespace, SA name, expiry). Automatically rotated by kubelet. Bound to a specific pod via projected tokens (K8s 1.21+). <strong>Client Certificate:</strong> An X.509 certificate used by kubectl and control plane components. Longer-lived, no automatic rotation. <strong>Pods use SA tokens</strong> because: (1) automatic provisioning — no manual cert generation, (2) automatic rotation — kubelet handles expiry, (3) pod-bound tokens prevent token reuse if the pod is deleted.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Kubernetes 1.21+ introduced bound tokens: each token is tied to a specific pod UID. If the pod is deleted, the token becomes invalid even if not yet expired. This is a critical security improvement over legacy tokens that were valid until expiry regardless of pod state. The CKS exam tests knowledge of token types and when to use each.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You have 50 pods in a namespace. Only 5 of them need API access. What's the most efficient way to disable token mounting for the other 45 pods?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> on the <strong>ServiceAccount</strong> itself. Every pod using that SA inherits the setting. For the 5 pods that need API access, create a separate SA with <code>automountServiceAccountToken: true</code> (the default). This is more efficient than setting it on each of the 45 individual pod specs and ensures new pods automatically get the secure default.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: no-api-sa
  namespace: prod
automountServiceAccountToken: false  # All pods using this SA get no token</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The CKS exam rewards efficiency. Setting <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> at the SA level is a single change that affects all pods using that SA. Setting it on each pod spec is error-prone — one forgotten pod is a security gap. Always think at the SA level for token mounting decisions.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a projected ServiceAccount token and how does it differ from the legacy token mounted at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</code>? Write a pod spec using projected tokens.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Legacy token:</strong> Mounted as a Secret, valid until the Secret is deleted, NOT pod-bound. <strong>Projected token:</strong> Created via TokenRequest API, bound to a specific pod UID, has configurable TTL, auto-rotated by kubelet.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: secure-pod
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx
    volumeMounts:
    - name: token
      mountPath: /var/run/secrets/tokens
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: token
    projected:
      sources:
      - serviceAccountToken:
          path: token
          expirationSeconds: 3600     # 1-hour TTL
          audience: api               # Bound to this audience</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Projected tokens (K8s 1.21+, GA in 1.24) are the modern standard. Key advantages: (1) pod-bound — token dies with the pod, (2) configurable TTL — set short lifetimes for sensitive pods, (3) audience-bound — token only works for specified audiences. On the CKS exam, use projected tokens with short expiration for pods that need temporary API access.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You discover that pods in namespace "legacy" are using ServiceAccounts that have <code>automountServiceAccountToken: true</code> but none of them need API access. Write the single command to find all such pods, and the fix.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Find pods with mounted tokens in namespace "legacy"
kubectl get pods -n legacy -o json | jq '.items[] | select(.spec.automountServiceAccountToken != false) | {name: .metadata.name, sa: .spec.serviceAccountName, automount: .spec.automountServiceAccountToken}'

# Fix: Patch the default ServiceAccount in that namespace
kubectl patch serviceaccount default -n legacy -p '{"automountServiceAccountToken": false}'

# Or create a dedicated SA for the namespace
kubectl create sa no-api -n legacy
kubectl patch sa no-api -n legacy -p '{"automountServiceAccountToken": false}'
# Then update deployments to use this SA</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a common CKS audit task. The jq query filters for pods that haven't explicitly disabled automount. The <code>default</code> SA in every namespace has automount enabled by default. Patching the default SA is the fastest fix but may break legitimate API access. A safer approach: create a dedicated no-api SA and gradually migrate pods to it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An attacker steals a pod's SA token file from <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> and exfiltrates it. They use it from an external server to query the Kubernetes API. What two Kubernetes features prevent this attack?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>1. Pod-bound tokens (projected tokens):</strong> The token is bound to the pod's UID. When the API server receives a request with this token, it verifies the pod still exists. If the original pod was deleted (even if you created a new one with the same name), the token is rejected. <strong>2. Token audience validation:</strong> The token's <code>aud</code> claim must match the API server's expected audience. An attacker can't reuse a token meant for the API server against a different service. <strong>3. Short TTL:</strong> Projected tokens can have expiration as low as 600 seconds — the stolen token expires quickly.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Legacy SA tokens (pre-1.21) had no pod binding — they were valid until the Secret was deleted, regardless of pod state. This was a significant security weakness. The CKS exam tests knowledge of projected tokens and their security properties. For production clusters, always use projected tokens with the shortest acceptable TTL.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to create a ServiceAccount for a monitoring agent that: can read pod metrics from the metrics API, cannot read Secrets, cannot create/modify anything, and has its token auto-rotated. Write the SA, ClusterRole, ClusterRoleBinding, and the pod spec with projected token.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: monitoring-agent
  namespace: monitoring
automountServiceAccountToken: false  # We use projected tokens instead
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: pod-metrics-reader
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: ["metrics.k8s.io"]
  resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: monitoring-metrics
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: monitoring-agent
  namespace: monitoring
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: pod-metrics-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: monitoring-agent
  namespace: monitoring
spec:
  serviceAccountName: monitoring-agent
  containers:
  - name: agent
    image: monitoring-agent:v1
    volumeMounts:
    - name: sa-token
      mountPath: /var/run/secrets/tokens
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: sa-token
    projected:
      sources:
      - serviceAccountToken:
          path: token
          expirationSeconds: 7200
          audience: https://kubernetes.default.svc</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the complete "least privilege monitoring agent" pattern tested on CKS. Key decisions: (1) SA has automount disabled because we use projected tokens. (2) ClusterRole is scoped to ONLY pods and metrics — no secrets, no configmaps, no write verbs. (3) ClusterRoleBinding is cluster-wide because the agent needs to see pods in all namespaces. (4) Projected token has 2-hour TTL — long enough for monitoring but short enough to limit stolen token risk.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

