                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> every pod's identity is a mounted JWT that any process inside the container can read — the danger is that the <code>default</code> ServiceAccount is shared by every pod in the namespace, so granting it any RBAC permission grants it to everything. The fix is two-fold: disable the mount entirely for pods that never call the API (<code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>), and give the pods that do call it a dedicated, minimally-scoped SA with a bound token that expires on its own.</p>
                    <p><strong>The takeaway:</strong> applied to easypj, that's four ServiceAccounts — two with zero API access and automount disabled, two with narrowly-scoped RBAC and bound tokens — decided by one question per pod: "does this workload call the Kubernetes API?" Precedence (pod beats SA) and the 5-stage token lifecycle are the exam-assumed facts that make every troubleshooting question in this chapter solvable.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>You are here:</strong> Step 10 — Cluster Hardening: Service Accounts. Next: Chapter 11 — Node Restriction &amp; Hardening Authorization.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
