            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 11 · Node Restriction &amp; Hardening Authorization</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Every Kubernetes node runs a <strong>kubelet</strong> — the agent that starts pods, reports node status, and manages container lifecycles. The kubelet communicates with the API server to register its node and update pod statuses. If a kubelet is compromised — through a container escape, a node-level exploit, or stolen credentials — it can wreak havoc: modify other nodes' labels, evict pods from healthy nodes, or impersonate other kubelets to redirect traffic. <strong>NodeRestriction</strong> is the admission plugin that limits each kubelet to only its own node and its own pods. Combined with the <strong>Node authorization mode</strong>, which restricts what kubelets can even request, these controls form a critical defense layer that the CKS exam tests heavily — and this chapter closes with the last gap in node security: the cloud metadata service at <code>169.254.169.254</code>, reachable from every pod by default and the single most common pivot point after a container escape or SSRF exploit.</p>
