            <section class="section-block" id="s11-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.1 The NodeRestriction Admission Plugin</h3>
                <p>NodeRestriction is an <strong>admission plugin</strong> — it intercepts API requests from kubelets and validates that each kubelet is only modifying resources it owns. Without NodeRestriction, a compromised kubelet on node-worker-2 can modify labels on node-worker-1 (impersonating another node), evict pods from node-worker-3, or modify pod labels to bypass scheduling constraints. With NodeRestriction, each kubelet is strictly limited to its own node object and the pods scheduled on it.</p>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 Without NodeRestriction — One Compromised Kubelet = Cluster-Wide Chaos</h5>
                    <p>A compromised kubelet can: modify ANY node's labels (redirecting workloads), evict pods from ANY node (causing denial of service), modify pod labels to bypass affinity rules, and update node statuses to disrupt scheduling. NodeRestriction prevents ALL of this by enforcing: "kubelet on node-X can only modify node-X and pods on node-X."</p>
                </div>

                <h4>NodeRestriction — What Each Kubelet Can and Cannot Touch</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="split-side split-bad">
                        <h5>❌ WITHOUT NodeRestriction</h5>
                        <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> kubelet on node-worker-2 is compromised.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>🔴 Modify labels on node-worker-1 (impersonate other nodes)</li>
                            <li>🔴 Evict pods from node-worker-3 (disrupt other nodes)</li>
                            <li>🔴 Modify pod labels it doesn't run (bypass scheduling)</li>
                            <li>🔴 Update any node's status to NotReady</li>
                            <li>🔴 This enables privilege escalation across the entire cluster</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="split-side split-good">
                        <h5>✅ WITH NodeRestriction</h5>
                        <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> kubelet on node-worker-2 is compromised.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>✅ Can read its own node object (node-worker-2) — only</li>
                            <li>✅ Can modify its own node status and labels — only</li>
                            <li>✅ Can read/write pods scheduled ON node-worker-2 — only</li>
                            <li>✅ Cannot touch node-worker-1 or node-worker-3 at all</li>
                            <li>✅ Blast radius contained to ONE node</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Enabling NodeRestriction — API Server Configuration</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Add NodeRestriction to the admission plugin list and verify</div>
<pre>
  # Step 1: Edit the API server manifest
  $ vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

  # Find --enable-admission-plugins and add NodeRestriction:
  - --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity,AlwaysPullImages,...

  # Step 2: Save → kubelet restarts API server (10-30 seconds)

  # Step 3: Verify NodeRestriction is active
  $ kubectl get pods -n kube-system kube-apiserver-* -o yaml | grep NodeRestriction
  → --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,...  ✅

  # Step 4: Test — a kubelet CANNOT modify another node's labels
  $ kubectl label node node-worker-1 test=value
  → (From the compromised kubelet on node-worker-2)
  → Error: node "node-worker-1" cannot be modified (NodeRestriction)
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How NodeRestriction Actually Works — The Internal Mechanism</h4>
                <p>NodeRestriction is not magic — it is a specific set of validation rules hardcoded into the API server's admission chain. Understanding exactly what it checks helps you diagnose when it blocks legitimate kubelet operations (rare, but possible during cluster upgrades or node replacements):</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">NodeRestriction Validation Rules — What Gets Checked</div>
<pre>
  RULE 1: kubelet identity is derived from the username "system:node:&lt;nodeName&gt;"
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  If a request comes from user "system:node:worker-2", NodeRestriction
  enforces that the kubelet can ONLY modify:
  • Node object: "worker-2" (its OWN node — not worker-1, not worker-3)
  • Pod objects: only pods where spec.nodeName == "worker-2"
  • Any attempt to modify worker-1 → REJECTED with "node 'worker-1' is not
    managed by node 'worker-2'"

  RULE 2: kubelet cannot modify taints on its own node with
          NoSchedule or NoExecute effects
  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  A compromised kubelet CANNOT taint its own node to attract or repel
  specific workloads. Only the API server (via scheduler) or cluster-admin
  can modify taints. This prevents a compromised kubelet from making its
  node a "preferred" target for sensitive pods.

  RULE 3: kubelet cannot set labels prefixed with
          "node-restriction.kubernetes.io/" on its own node
  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  This label prefix is RESERVED. Only the NodeRestriction admission
  plugin itself can set these labels. Kubelets cannot modify them.

  RULE 4: kubelet can only update its own node's status sub-resources
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Node status updates (Ready, MemoryPressure, DiskPressure) are
  restricted to the kubelet's own node. A kubelet on worker-2 cannot
  mark worker-3 as NotReady.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>What Happens When You Try to Bypass NodeRestriction</h4>
                <p>The CKS exam may present a scenario where NodeRestriction is disabled and ask you to enable it — or present a scenario where it IS enabled and ask you to diagnose why a kubelet operation is failing. Here is exactly what the API server returns when NodeRestriction blocks a request:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">NodeRestriction Blocks — Error Messages and What They Mean</div>
<pre>
  ATTEMPT 1: Kubelet on worker-2 tries to label worker-1
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  $ kubectl --as=system:node:worker-2 label node worker-1 test=value
  Error: nodes "worker-1" is forbidden: node "worker-2" is not allowed
  to modify node "worker-1"
  → FIX: Legitimate. The kubelet should NOT touch other nodes.

  ATTEMPT 2: Kubelet on worker-2 tries to evict a pod on worker-3
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  $ kubectl --as=system:node:worker-2 delete pod easypj-pod --grace-period=0
  Error: pods "easypj-pod" is forbidden: node "worker-2" is not allowed
  to modify pods not assigned to it
  → FIX: The pod is on worker-3, not worker-2. This is NodeRestriction working correctly.

  ATTEMPT 3: Kubelet tries to set taint with NoSchedule
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  $ kubectl --as=system:node:worker-2 taint node worker-2 dedicated=user:NoSchedule
  Error: node "worker-2" is forbidden: node "worker-2" is not allowed to
  modify taint "dedicated"
  → FIX: Only cluster-admin can modify taints. The kubelet cannot.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Kubelet Request Authorization Flow — Node Authorizer in Action</h4>
                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <div class="flow-step">
                        <div class="step-icon">⚙️</div>
                        <span class="step-label">Kubelet</span>
                        <span class="step-detail">system:node:<br>worker-2</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step">
                        <div class="step-icon">🔑</div>
                        <span class="step-label">Node AuthZ</span>
                        <span class="step-detail">Own node/<br>pods only?</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step">
                        <div class="step-icon">📋</div>
                        <span class="step-label">RBAC</span>
                        <span class="step-detail">Additional<br>checks</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step">
                        <div class="step-icon">🚪</div>
                        <span class="step-label">NodeRestriction</span>
                        <span class="step-detail">Admission<br>validation</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step">
                        <div class="step-icon">✅</div>
                        <span class="step-label">Allowed</span>
                        <span class="step-detail">Request<br>processed</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align:center;color:#60a5fa;margin-top:12px;"><strong>🔒 Three-layer defense: Node Authorizer (scope) → RBAC (permissions) → NodeRestriction (admission). All three must pass.</strong></p>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s11-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.2 Authorization Modes — Understanding the Chain</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes supports <strong>multiple authorization modes simultaneously</strong>, checked in order. When a request arrives, each mode is evaluated. The <strong>first mode that explicitly ALLOWS</strong> the request stops the chain — the request proceeds. If no mode ALLOWS, the request is DENIED. This means the order matters, and a poorly placed <code>AlwaysAllow</code> mode would grant every request before RBAC ever gets checked.</p>

                <h4>Authorization Mode Comparison</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>How It Works</th><th>Security Level</th><th>When to Use</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>AlwaysAllow</strong></td><td>Allow every request, no checks</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">🔴 NEVER IN PRODUCTION</td><td>Testing only — REMOVE before going live</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>AlwaysDeny</strong></td><td>Deny every request</td><td style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Testing only</td><td>Testing admission plugins — blocks everything</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>RBAC</strong></td><td>Roles + RoleBindings define permissions</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Standard</td><td>All production clusters — the primary mode</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Node</strong></td><td>Restricts kubelet API access by node</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Required</td><td>All production clusters — paired with RBAC</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Webhook</strong></td><td>External service decides allow/deny via HTTP</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Advanced</td><td>Custom authorization logic, OPA integration</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ABAC</strong></td><td>Attribute-based, policy file on disk</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">⚠️ Legacy — avoid</td><td>Legacy clusters — migrate to RBAC</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Secure Authorization Configuration</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The only authorization mode combination you should use</div>
<pre>
  # API server flag — secure configuration:
  --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC

  # What this does (checked in order):
  # 1. Node authorizer: kubelet requests checked first
  #    → Is this kubelet authorized to access this node/pod?
  #    → If ALLOW: request proceeds. If DENY: passes to RBAC.
  # 2. RBAC: all other requests checked against Roles/Bindings
  #    → Does this user/SA have a binding allowing this action?
  #    → If ALLOW: request proceeds. If no rule: DENIED.

  # ❌ NEVER DO THIS:
  --authorization-mode=AlwaysAllow,RBAC
  # → AlwaysAllow matches FIRST → ALL requests allowed → RBAC NEVER checked.
  # → This is equivalent to having NO authorization at all.

  # ✅ THE ORDER MATTERS:
  --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC
  # → Node checked first (kubelet-specific rules), then RBAC (everything else).
  # → If Node and RBAC both DENY, the request is denied.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>😰 Real-World Scenario: When AlwaysAllow Was Left Enabled</h5>
                    <p>A cluster was deployed with <code>--authorization-mode=AlwaysAllow,RBAC</code> during development for convenience. The cluster went to production without removing AlwaysAllow. Every API request — from every user, every ServiceAccount, even anonymous requests — was allowed. RBAC rules existed but were never consulted. The cluster ran for six months with effectively zero authorization. An attacker who gained a single pod's ServiceAccount token had full cluster-admin access — because AlwaysAllow granted it before RBAC could say no. <strong>Lesson: Always audit your authorization mode. One typo disables all authorization.</strong></p>
                </div>

                <h4>Authorization Mode Decision Tree</h4>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="decision-node question">⚙️ What does your --authorization-mode flag contain?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">AlwaysAllow present → 🔴 REMOVE IMMEDIATELY. This disables all authorization.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">No AlwaysAllow. Continue.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">📋 Does it include Node AND RBAC?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">❌ Missing Node → Add it. Kubelets need Node authorization. Missing RBAC → Add it. This is your primary authorization.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ Node,RBAC present → This is the CKS-recommended configuration.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">🔍 Is ABAC or static token file present?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">⚠️ ABAC/static tokens → Legacy. Plan migration to RBAC. Remove ABAC policy file and --token-auth-file flag.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">✅ Clean — only modern authorization modes. CKS target state.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>How the Node Authorizer Works — Internal Rules</h4>
                <p>The Node authorization mode is a special-purpose authorizer designed exclusively for kubelets. Unlike RBAC (which matches arbitrary rules to arbitrary subjects), the Node authorizer has a fixed set of rules based on the kubelet's identity. Understanding these rules helps you diagnose when the Node authorizer ALLOWS a request (and RBAC is never checked) vs when it passes the request to RBAC:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Node Authorizer — Built-in Permission Rules</div>
<pre>
  IDENTITY: A kubelet authenticates as "system:node:&lt;nodeName&gt;"
  GROUP: The kubelet is in the group "system:nodes"

  NODE AUTHORIZER ALLOWS (without consulting RBAC):
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  • Read services, endpoints, nodes (to discover cluster topology)
  • Read/write pods bound to its OWN node
  • Read/write node status for its OWN node
  • Read/write events for its OWN node's pods
  • Read ConfigMaps, Secrets referenced by pods on its node
  • Read PVs, PVCs referenced by pods on its node
  • CertificateSigningRequest: create/update for its own node cert

  NODE AUTHORIZER PASSES TO RBAC (no opinion):
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  • Any request NOT listed above → passed to the next authorizer (RBAC)
  • This means: if a kubelet tries to read ALL Secrets in a namespace,
    Node passes → RBAC checks → DENIED (unless RBAC explicitly allows)

  🔑 KEY INSIGHT: The Node authorizer is DEFENSE-IN-DEPTH for kubelets.
     It GRANTS the minimum permissions kubelets need to function, AND
     it RESTRICTS them from requesting anything beyond that.
     Even if RBAC somehow granted a kubelet cluster-admin (a catastrophic
     misconfiguration), the Node authorizer would still DENY requests
     to modify other nodes — because Node authorizer operates BEFORE RBAC.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s11-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.3 Protecting Node-Level Access</h3>
                <p>Node-level access — SSH, console, and kubelet API — represents the deepest level of compromise. If an attacker gains SSH access to a node, they can read all Secrets mounted on that node, access the container runtime directly, modify static pod manifests, and potentially compromise the entire control plane if they land on a control plane node. Node-level hardening is the last line of defense in the CKS model.</p>

                <h4>Node Access Hardening Checklist</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">SSH and Node-Level Security — Every Control</div>
<pre>
  □ SSH: Disable root login
      $ grep PermitRootLogin /etc/ssh/sshd_config
      → PermitRootLogin no

  □ SSH: Key authentication only — no passwords
      $ grep PasswordAuthentication /etc/ssh/sshd_config
      → PasswordAuthentication no

  □ SSH: Restrict to specific users/groups
      $ grep AllowUsers /etc/ssh/sshd_config
      → AllowUsers admin@10.0.0.0/8

  □ SSH: Audit all login attempts
      $ grep sshd /var/log/auth.log | grep Accepted
      → Review regularly for unauthorized access

  □ Kubelet API (10250): Anonymous auth disabled
      $ grep anonymous /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
      → enabled: false

  □ Kubelet API (10250): Webhook authorization
      $ grep authorization /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
      → mode: Webhook

  □ Kubelet read-only port (10255): DISABLED
      $ grep readOnlyPort /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
      → readOnlyPort: 0

  □ IAM roles (cloud): Minimal permissions for node instances
      AWS: Node IAM role should NOT have s3:* or iam:* permissions
      → Only ec2:DescribeInstances, ecr:GetAuthorizationToken, etc.

  □ Remove stale user accounts from all nodes
      $ awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 {print $1}' /etc/passwd
      → Audit: every human account must be justified.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Kubelet API Protection — The Last Network Barrier</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">How an attacker exploits an exposed kubelet API (port 10250)</div>
<pre>
  ATTACKER gains network access to node IP on port 10250
  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  WITHOUT kubelet hardening:
  $ curl -k https://node-worker-2:10250/pods
  → Returns ALL pods running on this node — full pod specs, labels, env vars
  $ curl -k https://node-worker-2:10250/stats/summary
  → Returns CPU, memory, network stats for every container
  $ curl -k https://node-worker-2:10250/run/.../easypj-pod/easypj?cmd=whoami
  → Executes "whoami" inside the easypj container → returns "root"

  WITH kubelet hardening (anonymous=false + webhook auth):
  $ curl -k https://node-worker-2:10250/pods
  → 401 Unauthorized — kubelet requires authentication
  $ curl -k -H "Authorization: Bearer &lt;stolen-SA-token&gt;" .../pods
  → 403 Forbidden — SA token not authorized for kubelet API (RBAC denies)
  $ curl -k -H "Authorization: Bearer &lt;valid-admin-token&gt;" .../pods
  → 200 OK + pod list — only authorized users can access kubelet API

  🔑 KEY INSIGHT: With webhook authorization, the kubelet sends EVERY request
     to the API server for a SubjectAccessReview. No local decision-making.
     The API server's RBAC controls who can access every kubelet.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Cloud IAM Role Minimization — Node Instances Should Have Zero Power</h4>
                <p>When Kubernetes nodes run on cloud providers, each node inherits the IAM role attached to its VM instance. A common security mistake is attaching broad IAM roles (like <code>AdministratorAccess</code>) to nodes "to make things easier." This means any pod on that node can access those cloud permissions via the instance metadata service. Here is what proper node IAM minimization looks like:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Cloud Provider</th><th>Minimum Required Permissions</th><th>NEVER Include</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>AWS</strong></td><td><code>ec2:DescribeInstances</code>, <code>ecr:GetAuthorizationToken</code>, <code>ecr:BatchGetImage</code></td><td style="color:#ef4444;"><code>s3:*</code>, <code>iam:*</code>, <code>sts:*</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Azure</strong></td><td><code>Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/read</code>, ACR pull permissions</td><td style="color:#ef4444;"><code>Contributor</code> role, Storage Account keys</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>GCP</strong></td><td><code>compute.instances.get</code>, <code>artifactregistry.reader</code></td><td style="color:#ef4444;"><code>Editor</code> role, Service Account Token Creator</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-weight:700;">🔴 CKS Rule: If a node IAM role includes <code>s3:*</code>, an attacker who compromises ANY pod on that node can read ALL S3 buckets in the account. This is exactly how Capital One was breached.</p>

                <h4>Node Compromise Attack Walkthrough — What Happens Step by Step</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Scenario: Attacker escapes container → gains node access → what now?</div>
<pre>
  ATTACKER has root on node-worker-2 (via container escape). Now what?
  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  STEP 1: Read all Secrets on this node
  $ for secret in $(ls /var/lib/kubelet/pods/*/volumes/kubernetes.io~secret/); do
      cat /var/lib/kubelet/pods/*/volumes/kubernetes.io~secret/$secret/* 2>/dev/null
    done
  → ALL Secrets from ALL pods on this node — DB passwords, API keys, TLS certs.

  STEP 2: Access kubelet credentials
  $ cat /var/lib/kubelet/pki/kubelet-client-current.pem
  → kubelet's client certificate — can authenticate to API server as "system:node:worker-2"

  STEP 3: Try to modify other nodes (WITHOUT NodeRestriction)
  $ curl -k https://kube-apiserver:6443/api/v1/nodes/worker-1 \
      --cert /var/lib/kubelet/pki/kubelet-client-current.pem \
      --key /var/lib/kubelet/pki/kubelet-client-key.pem \
      -X PATCH -H "Content-Type: application/merge-patch+json" \
      -d '{"metadata":{"labels":{"compromised":"true"}}}'
  → SUCCESS (without NodeRestriction) — attacker now controls worker-1 labels!

  STEP 4: WITH NodeRestriction enabled
  → Same request returns: "node worker-1 is not allowed to modify by node worker-2"
  → BLOCKED. Attacker trapped on worker-2.

  BLAST RADIUS COMPARISON:
  Without NodeRestriction: worker-2 compromise → ALL nodes compromised
  With NodeRestriction:    worker-2 compromise → ONLY worker-2 compromised
</pre>
                </div>

