                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key Takeaway for the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p>The CKS exam expects you to configure THREE things for node security: (1) <strong>NodeRestriction admission plugin</strong> on the API server, (2) <strong>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</strong> on the API server, and (3) <strong>kubelet hardening</strong> (anonymous=false, webhook auth, readOnlyPort=0). These three controls work together — missing any one leaves a gap. Always verify all three: <code>kubectl describe pod -n kube-system kube-apiserver-* | grep -E 'NodeRestriction|authorization-mode'</code> AND <code>grep -E 'anonymous|webhook|readOnlyPort' /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>How the Node Authorizer Works — The Allowlist That Protects Node Identity</h4>
                <p>The Node authorizer is a special-purpose authorizer that runs BEFORE RBAC in the authorization chain. It allows kubelets to perform the specific operations they need — but ONLY on resources belonging to their own node. This prevents a compromised kubelet on node-01 from accessing pods or secrets on node-02:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Node Authorizer Logic — What Each Kubelet Is Allowed to Do</div>
<pre>
  REQUEST: Kubelet on "worker-01" (authenticated as system:node:worker-01,
           group system:nodes) wants to UPDATE pod status for a pod
           running on "worker-02".

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  NODE AUTHORIZER CHECK (runs BEFORE RBAC)                                │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────                                 │
  │  Step 1: Is the requester in group "system:nodes"?                       │
  │  → YES (system:node:worker-01 is in system:nodes)                         │
  │                                                                           │
  │  Step 2: Is the requested operation in the kubelet allowlist?             │
  │  Kubelets are allowed to:                                                 │
  │  ✅ Read: services, endpoints, nodes (their own)                          │
  │  ✅ Write: node status (their own), pod status (their own pods),          │
  │     events (for their own pods)                                            │
  │  ✅ Auth: CertificateSigningRequests, TokenReviews                        │
  │  ❌ NOT allowed: Read/Write pods on OTHER nodes, secrets, configmaps,     │
  │     deployments, RBAC objects                                             │
  │  → Request is: UPDATE pod status for a pod on "worker-02"                 │
  │  → Node authorizer checks: Is this pod scheduled on "worker-01"?          │
  │  → NO — the pod is on worker-02, and worker-01's kubelet is asking.      │
  │  → DENY. (The kubelet cannot touch pods on other nodes.)                  │
  │                                                                           │
  │  CONTRAST: If the SAME kubelet requests pod status for a pod on           │
  │  worker-01 → the pod IS on worker-01 → ALLOW by Node authorizer.         │
  │  The request NEVER reaches RBAC (Node authorizer short-circuits).         │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  WHAT NODERESTRICTION ADDS ON TOP:
  ──────────────────────────────────
  Node authorizer = AUTHORIZATION (can the kubelet make this API call?)
  NodeRestriction = ADMISSION (even if authorized, can the kubelet modify
                    THIS specific field of THIS specific object?)

  NodeRestriction limits kubelets to ONLY modifying:
  • Their own Node object (node "worker-01" can only modify "worker-01")
  • Pods bound to their node (pod.spec.nodeName = "worker-01")
  • Labels prefixed with "node-restriction.kubernetes.io/"

  Without NodeRestriction: A compromised kubelet on worker-01 could change
  worker-02's labels (e.g., remove a taint), causing pods to schedule on a
  compromised node. WITH NodeRestriction: label modification is DENIED at
  admission time.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>NodeRestriction — What Each Kubelet CAN and CANNOT Touch</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Compromised Kubelet: Without vs With NodeRestriction</div>
<pre>
  SCENARIO: node-worker-2's kubelet is compromised

  WITHOUT NodeRestriction:
  Compromised kubelet on node-worker-2 CAN:
  ❌ Modify labels on node-worker-1 (impersonate another node)
  ❌ Evict pods from node-worker-3 (disrupt other nodes)
  ❌ Modify labels on pods it doesn't run (bypass scheduling)
  ❌ This enables privilege escalation across the entire cluster

  WITH NodeRestriction enabled:
  Compromised kubelet on node-worker-2 CAN ONLY:
  ✅ Read its own node object (node-worker-2)
  ✅ Modify its own node status and labels
  ✅ Read/write pods scheduled ON node-worker-2 only
  ✅ Cannot touch anything on node-worker-1 or node-worker-3

  🔑 CKS EXAM: CIS Benchmark 1.2.17 requires NodeRestriction.
     Flag: --enable-admission-plugins=...,NodeRestriction
     Verify: kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep apiserver
             → check the command line flags
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Authorization Mode Comparison — Complete Reference</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>How It Works</th><th>Security Level</th><th>CKS Notes</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>AlwaysAllow</strong></td><td>Allow everything, no checks</td><td><span style="color:#326ce5;">🔴 NEVER use</span></td><td>Exists only for bootstrapping. Disable immediately.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>AlwaysDeny</strong></td><td>Deny everything (testing only)</td><td><span style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Testing</span></td><td>Useful for testing RBAC policies in dry-run mode.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>RBAC</strong></td><td>Roles + RoleBindings + ClusterRoleBindings</td><td><span style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Use this</span></td><td>The primary authorization mode. Chapters 9, 33 cover this.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Node</strong></td><td>Kubelet access control — restricts kubelets to their own objects</td><td><span style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Required</span></td><td>Always pair with NodeRestriction admission plugin.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Webhook</strong></td><td>External authorization service (HTTP callback)</td><td><span style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Advanced</span></td><td>For integrating with external auth systems (OPA, custom).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ABAC</strong></td><td>Attribute-based, file-based policy</td><td><span style="color:#eab308;">⚠️ Legacy</span></td><td>Deprecated. Use RBAC instead. Appears in older CKS materials.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Recommended configuration:</strong> <code>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</code>. The order matters — Node is checked FIRST. If a kubelet request passes Node authorization, RBAC is never evaluated (efficiency). If Node denies it, RBAC is checked as a fallback (but should also deny it).</p>

                <h4>Node Access Hardening Checklist</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔑</div><h5>SSH Access</h5><p>Use SSO (not shared keys). Audit SSH keys monthly. Remove stale users. Disable root SSH login: <code>PermitRootLogin no</code> in <code>/etc/ssh/sshd_config</code>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div><h5>Kubelet API</h5><p>Never expose port 10250 to the internet. Bind to localhost only if possible. Use firewall rules: <code>iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 10250 -j DROP</code> (except from control plane).</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">☁️</div><h5>Cloud IAM</h5><p>Use node groups with minimal IAM roles. Cloud-managed nodes should have read-only access to container registry + logging. Never grant <code>ec2:*</code> or equivalent to node roles.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s11-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.4 Protecting Node Metadata &amp; Endpoints</h3>
                <p>The CKS curriculum's Cluster Setup domain names this explicitly: <strong>"protect node metadata and endpoints."</strong> Every cloud node exposes an <strong>instance metadata service (IMDS)</strong> at the link-local address <code>169.254.169.254</code> — reachable from every pod on that node by default, no NetworkPolicy required to reach it because it isn't a Kubernetes object at all. A single unauthenticated HTTP GET to the right path hands back the node's IAM role credentials. If your application has any request-forgery flaw, that GET can come from an attacker instead of your code.</p>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 This Is Exactly the Capital One Breach</h5>
                    <p>SSRF (server-side request forgery) in an application → the app is tricked into making an HTTP GET to <code>169.254.169.254</code> instead of the URL the user intended → the metadata service returns the node's IAM role credentials → the attacker uses those credentials directly against the cloud API, no Kubernetes RBAC involved at all. In the real 2019 breach, this path led to over 100 million customer records exposed from S3. <strong>NodeRestriction and RBAC (11.1-11.2) never even see this request</strong> — it never touches the Kubernetes API server.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Metadata SSRF Pivot — And the Three Cuts That Stop It</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">One vulnerable endpoint, three independent controls that each break the chain</div>
<pre>
  attacker → SSRF in easypj/jobpost → GET http://169.254.169.254/
             latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/node-role
                             │
          CUT 1 ─X─ NetworkPolicy egress: deny 169.254.169.254/32
                     (pods that truly need cloud identity use IRSA /
                      Workload Identity / Managed Identity instead)
                             │
          CUT 2 ─X─ IMDSv2 (AWS): session token required, PUT hop-limit=1
                     (a proxied SSRF GET can't complete the token
                      handshake or survive the extra network hop)
                     GCP/Azure: reject requests missing the required
                     header (Metadata-Flavor: Google / Metadata: true)
                             │
          CUT 3 ─X─ node IAM role = least privilege
                     (even a stolen credential is worth almost nothing —
                      no s3:*, no iam:*, no sts:*)
                             │
                 (if ALL THREE cuts fail) → cloud IAM credentials stolen
                             │
                 $ aws s3 ls --profile stolen-node-role
                 → full account S3 access (the Capital One outcome)
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Three Layers That Protect the Metadata Service</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5>1. NetworkPolicy Egress Deny</h5>
                        <p>Deny every pod's egress to <code>169.254.169.254/32</code> by default. Workloads that genuinely need cloud identity should use IRSA (AWS), Workload Identity (GCP), or Managed Identity (Azure) — not raw IMDS calls from inside the container.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔑</div>
                        <h5>2. IMDSv2 / Provider Headers</h5>
                        <p>AWS: <code>aws ec2 modify-instance-metadata-options --http-tokens required --http-put-response-hop-limit 1</code> — a simple proxied GET can no longer complete the token handshake. GCP/Azure: require <code>Metadata-Flavor: Google</code> / <code>Metadata: true</code> — a bare SSRF GET without that header is rejected.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>3. Least-Privilege Node IAM Role</h5>
                        <p>The node role gets only what the kubelet, CNI, and CSI actually need — <code>ec2:DescribeInstances</code>, <code>ecr:GetAuthorizationToken</code>. NEVER <code>s3:*</code>, <code>iam:*</code>, or <code>sts:*</code>. If the first two cuts fail, this is the last backstop.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Node Endpoints — Every Port and How to Harden It</h4>
                <p>"Endpoints" is the second half of the CKS objective — every port a node exposes, not just the metadata service, needs to be restricted to only the components/CIDRs that legitimately need it:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>Service</th><th>Hardening</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>10250</code></td><td>kubelet API (authenticated)</td><td>authn=Webhook, authz=Webhook — never expose to the internet</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>10255</code></td><td>kubelet read-only (legacy)</td><td style="color:#ef4444;"><code>readOnlyPort: 0</code> — turn it OFF entirely</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>10256</code></td><td>kube-proxy health check</td><td>localhost / node CIDR only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>6443</code></td><td>kube-apiserver</td><td>admin CIDR / VPN only — never <code>0.0.0.0/0</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>2379-2380</code></td><td>etcd client / peer</td><td>control-plane nodes only, mTLS required</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>10257</code></td><td>kube-controller-manager (secure)</td><td>localhost / control-plane CIDR only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>10259</code></td><td>kube-scheduler (secure)</td><td>localhost / control-plane CIDR only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>169.254.169.254</code></td><td>cloud metadata service (IMDS)</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">NetworkPolicy egress deny + IMDSv2</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Verifying Metadata &amp; Endpoint Protection on the Exam</h5>
                    <p>Three commands cover this section: <code>kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- curl -s -m 2 http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/</code> should time out or be refused once the NetworkPolicy is applied. <code>grep readOnlyPort /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> must show <code>0</code>. <code>aws ec2 describe-instances --query 'Reservations[].Instances[].MetadataOptions'</code> (or the GCP/Azure equivalent) must show tokens required and hop-limit 1.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

