            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>NodeRestriction (admission) contains a compromised kubelet to one node</strong> — <code>--enable-admission-plugins=...,NodeRestriction</code> limits it to its own Node object and its own pods only.</li>
                    <li><strong>Node authorizer + RBAC is the only safe authorization-mode combination</strong> — <code>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</code>, order matters, and a stray <code>AlwaysAllow</code> disables all authorization that follows it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Node-level access is the last network barrier</strong> — key-only SSH with root login disabled, kubelet API with anonymous auth off and webhook authorization on, and <code>readOnlyPort: 0</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Minimal cloud IAM roles on nodes limit the damage of any compromise</strong> — a node role with <code>s3:*</code> or <code>iam:*</code> turns a single pod compromise into a full account breach (Capital One).</li>
                    <li><strong>The cloud metadata service is reachable from every pod by default</strong> — deny egress to <code>169.254.169.254/32</code> with a NetworkPolicy, require IMDSv2 with a hop-limit of 1, and restrict every other node/control-plane port (10250, 10255, 10256, 6443, 2379-2380) to only the CIDRs that need it.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Node Authorizer ≠ NodeRestriction:</strong> Node Authorizer (authorization gate) lets kubelets READ their own resources. NodeRestriction (admission gate) limits kubelets to MODIFY only their own. Both are needed. Node Authorizer is a special authorizer in the authorization chain. NodeRestriction is an admission plugin.</li>
                    <li><strong>NodeRestriction without Node Authorizer:</strong> If you enable NodeRestriction but use <code>--authorization-mode=RBAC</code> (without Node), kubelets need explicit RBAC permissions to do anything. The cluster breaks. Always use <code>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</code> together with NodeRestriction.</li>
                    <li><strong>"NetworkPolicy already protects my metadata service" — not necessarily:</strong> a default-deny NetworkPolicy that only restricts pod-to-pod traffic does nothing for pod-to-169.254.169.254 traffic unless that CIDR is explicitly named in an egress deny rule — it's a link-local address, not a cluster pod IP.</li>
                    <li><strong>IMDSv1 is often still the default:</strong> many managed clusters ship with a hop-limit of 2 (needed for Docker's extra network hop), which is exactly enough for a containerized SSRF to reach the metadata service. You must explicitly set hop-limit to 1 and require tokens.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>Node Authorizer = Gate 2 (authorization). NodeRestriction = Gate 4 (admission). Both needed for complete protection.</li>
                    <li>Verify both with one command: <code>grep -E "authorization|NodeRestriction" /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>.</li>
                    <li>One NetworkPolicy line covers node metadata: <code>podSelector: {}</code> + an egress deny rule for <code>169.254.169.254/32</code>, applied cluster-wide as a baseline before allow-listing exceptions.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

