                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What SSH hardening commands should you run on every Kubernetes node? List at least 3.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Disable root SSH login
sudo sed -i 's/^PermitRootLogin.*/PermitRootLogin no/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config
# 2. Disable password authentication (keys only)
sudo sed -i 's/^PasswordAuthentication.*/PasswordAuthentication no/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config
# 3. Restart SSH to apply
sudo systemctl restart sshd
# 4. Remove inactive user accounts
sudo userdel -r old-admin  # Remove old admin account</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What ports must be open between Kubernetes components? List the essential ports and their purposes.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Control Plane:</strong> API server 6443 (kubectl, kubelets, controllers), etcd 2379-2380 (client+peer). <strong>Nodes:</strong> kubelet 10250 (API server→kubelet), NodePort 30000-32767 (external services), Calico BGP 179, Cilium VXLAN 8472. <strong>ALL other ports should be blocked</strong> by firewall rules. Verify: <code>sudo ss -tlnp</code> on each node.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between Node Authorizer and NodeRestriction? Can you have one without the other?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Node Authorizer</strong> (authorization): Allows kubelets to READ their own node, pods, secrets, and configmaps. Without it, kubelets need broad RBAC permissions. <strong>NodeRestriction</strong> (admission): Restricts kubelets to MODIFY only their OWN node and assigned pods. You CAN have Node Authorizer without NodeRestriction — kubelets can still modify other nodes' objects. You SHOULD have BOTH for complete security.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A kubelet on worker01 is compromised. With NodeRestriction enabled, what specific actions can the attacker NOT perform? List at least 4 blocked actions.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>NodeRestriction on worker01's kubelet blocks: (1) <strong>Modify node02's labels/taints</strong> — can't disrupt scheduling on other nodes. (2) <strong>Update pods on node02</strong> — can't tamper with other workloads. (3) <strong>Modify its OWN node labels to attract pods</strong> — can't hijack pods from other nodes via label manipulation. (4) <strong>Update Node object annotations</strong> — can't inject malicious metadata. (5) <strong>Modify any pod not assigned to worker01</strong> — lateral movement blocked.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>NodeRestriction enforces that a kubelet can ONLY modify: (a) the Node object for its own node, (b) Pod objects bound to its node. Everything else is rejected at admission time. This limits the blast radius of a kubelet compromise to a single node. Without NodeRestriction, a compromised kubelet becomes a cluster-wide attack vector.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What kernel hardening settings should be applied to Kubernetes nodes? List the sysctl parameters and their security purpose.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 10px 0;">
                        <thead><tr style="border-bottom: 2px solid #326ce5;"><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">sysctl Parameter</th><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Value</th><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;"><code>net.ipv4.ip_forward</code></td><td style="padding: 8px;">0 (on non-router nodes)</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Prevent node from routing packets — limits lateral movement</td></tr>
                            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;"><code>net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter</code></td><td style="padding: 8px;">1</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Reverse path filtering — drops spoofed packets</td></tr>
                            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;"><code>net.ipv4.tcp_syncookies</code></td><td style="padding: 8px;">1</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Prevent SYN flood DoS attacks</td></tr>
                            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;"><code>kernel.kptr_restrict</code></td><td style="padding: 8px;">2</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Hide kernel pointers — prevent kernel exploit development</td></tr>
                            <tr><td style="padding: 8px;"><code>kernel.dmesg_restrict</code></td><td style="padding: 8px;">1</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Restrict kernel log access to root only</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Node hardening is tested in the "System Hardening" CKS domain (15%). These kernel parameters are checked by CIS benchmarks. On the exam, you apply them via <code>/etc/sysctl.d/99-kubernetes.conf</code> and run <code>sysctl --system</code>. Combined with <code>--protect-kernel-defaults=true</code> on the kubelet, pods cannot override these settings.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to restrict which IP ranges can access the kubelet API (port 10250). Write the iptables rules and explain where they should be applied.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Allow control plane to reach kubelet (required for logs, exec, metrics)
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 10250 -s 10.0.0.0/24 -j ACCEPT
# Allow node's own pods (via CNI) — optional, CNI may bypass iptables
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 10250 -s 10.244.0.0/16 -j ACCEPT
# Drop everything else
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 10250 -j DROP
# Make persistent
iptables-save > /etc/iptables/rules.v4</code></pre>
                    <p>Apply on EVERY node. The kubelet API on port 10250 handles pod exec, logs, and metrics — it must be accessible from the control plane but NOT from other nodes or the internet.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Kubelet API security is a CIS benchmark requirement (CIS 4.2.x). The API server communicates with kubelets on port 10250. If this port is exposed, attackers can exec into pods, retrieve logs, and access metrics. iptables rules are the node-level firewall. Combined with <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> and <code>--authorization-mode=Webhook</code> on the kubelet, this provides defense in depth.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the KubeletConfiguration file, where is it located, and what security-critical fields does it contain?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The KubeletConfiguration file (default: <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>) controls ALL kubelet behavior. Security-critical fields:</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kubelet.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: KubeletConfiguration
authentication:
  anonymous:
    enabled: false          # No anonymous access
  webhook:
    enabled: true           # API server validates kubelet requests
authorization:
  mode: Webhook             # Delegate authz to API server
readOnlyPort: 0             # Disable unauthenticated read-only port
protectKernelDefaults: true # Prevent pod sysctl modifications
rotateCertificates: true    # Auto-rotate kubelet client certs
serverTLSBootstrap: true    # Auto-request serving certs</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The KubeletConfiguration is the single most important file for node security. On the CKS exam, when hardening a node, start here. The path varies by cluster setup — use <code>ps aux | grep kubelet | grep config</code> to find the actual config file path. After changes, run <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A node running Kubernetes also runs a legacy monitoring agent as root. What's the risk, and how would you isolate the monitoring agent from the Kubernetes components?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Risk:</strong> A compromised monitoring agent running as root can read kubelet credentials, static pod manifests (<code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>), PKI certificates, and audit logs — full node compromise.</p>
                    <p><strong>Isolation:</strong> (1) Run the agent as a non-root user with minimal Linux capabilities. (2) Use AppArmor/SELinux to confine the agent to its own directories. (3) Run it as a pod with restrictive securityContext instead of a systemd service. (4) Use <code>chmod 600</code> on all Kubernetes files and ensure they're owned by root:root so the non-root agent can't read them.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Shared nodes are a real-world scenario. When Kubernetes and other services share a node, the other services become part of your attack surface. The CKS approach: containerize everything possible, use filesystem permissions to protect Kubernetes files, and use Mandatory Access Control (AppArmor/SELinux) as a second layer.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code> to add NodeRestriction and change authorization-mode. After the restart, nodes show NotReady. What went wrong, and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Most likely:</strong> You set <code>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</code> but the Node authorizer can't identify kubelets because: (a) kubelet certificates don't have the correct organization (<code>system:nodes</code>), or (b) the kubelet username doesn't match <code>system:node:&lt;nodename&gt;</code> format.</p>
                    <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Check kubelet cert: <code>openssl x509 -in /var/lib/kubelet/pki/kubelet-client-current.pem -text -noout | grep Subject</code>. Must show <code>O=system:nodes, CN=system:node:&lt;nodename&gt;</code>. If using <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> on the API server, ensure kubelets have valid certificates — anonymous kubelet traffic is blocked.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the #1 disaster when hardening the API server. The Node authorizer requires kubelets to identify as <code>system:node:&lt;nodename&gt;</code> in the <code>system:nodes</code> group. If kubelet certificates don't match this pattern, ALL kubelets fail authorization, nodes go NotReady, and new pods can't be scheduled. Always verify kubelet certificate format before restarting the API server.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An audit finds the easypj/jobpost pod can successfully <code>curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/</code> and retrieve a valid IAM credential. Diagnose the three possible root causes and the fix for each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Cause 1 — No NetworkPolicy blocks it:</strong> egress to 169.254.169.254 was never denied because it's a link-local address, not a cluster pod IP, so a generic "deny all pod-to-pod" policy doesn't cover it. <strong>Fix:</strong> add an explicit egress-deny rule for <code>169.254.169.254/32</code>.</p>
                    <p><strong>Cause 2 — IMDSv1 still allowed:</strong> the instance metadata options were never set to require a session token. <strong>Fix (AWS):</strong> <code>aws ec2 modify-instance-metadata-options --instance-id &lt;id&gt; --http-tokens required --http-put-response-hop-limit 1</code>.</p>
                    <p><strong>Cause 3 — Node IAM role is over-broad:</strong> even if the request succeeds, the blast radius should be minimal. <strong>Fix:</strong> strip the node role down to only what the kubelet/CNI/CSI need; remove any <code>s3:*</code>/<code>iam:*</code>/<code>sts:*</code> grants.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the exact "protect node metadata and endpoints" CKS Cluster Setup objective in one scenario. The exam expects you to name and fix all three layers — a NetworkPolicy alone stops the SSRF pivot, but IMDSv2 and IAM minimization are the defense-in-depth backstops if the policy is ever missing or misconfigured on a namespace.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

