                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> a compromised kubelet is contained by two gates working together — the Node authorizer decides what it may read, NodeRestriction decides what it may modify — and both must be enabled, in the right order (<code>Node,RBAC</code>), or one silently does nothing. Node-level access (SSH, kubelet API) is the physical/network layer underneath both gates: if an attacker reaches it directly, admission plugins never get a say.</p>
                    <p><strong>The takeaway:</strong> the cloud metadata service closes the last gap — it answers requests from any pod without ever touching the Kubernetes authorization chain, so it needs its own independent controls (NetworkPolicy egress-deny, IMDSv2, minimal node IAM) rather than relying on NodeRestriction or RBAC to cover it. Applied to easypj, that's four independent layers — NodeRestriction, Node,RBAC, kubelet/SSH hardening, and metadata/endpoint lockdown — each one a CKS exam task in its own right.</p>
