            <section class="section-block" id="s12-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">12.1 Why Keeping Kubernetes Updated Is a Security Requirement</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes releases follow a strict support policy: the latest 3 minor versions (N, N-1, N-2) receive security patches. Versions older than N-2 are <strong>End of Life (EOL)</strong> — no patches, no CVEs fixed, no support. If your cluster runs v1.27 when v1.30 is the latest, any CVE discovered today for v1.27 will never be patched. Your only remediation is to <strong>upgrade to a supported version</strong>. Running an EOL version is not a configuration issue — it is a security incident waiting to happen.</p>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 EOL = No Security Patches — Ever</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes releases approximately every 4 months. Each minor version is supported for approximately 14 months. After that, the release branch is permanently closed. If CVE-2025-XXXX is discovered affecting v1.26 today, there will be NO patch for v1.26 because v1.26 is EOL. You MUST be on a supported version (v1.28, v1.29, or v1.30 as of mid-2025) to receive the fix.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Notable Kubernetes CVEs — Real Vulnerabilities, Real Consequences</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>CVE</th><th>Severity</th><th>Affected Versions</th><th>Impact</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>CVE-2018-1002105</strong> "The Big Bird"</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">CRITICAL 9.8</td><td>&lt; 1.10.11, &lt; 1.11.5, &lt; 1.12.3</td><td>Privilege escalation to cluster-admin via API server proxy — unauthenticated RCE</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CVE-2019-11247</strong></td><td style="color:#f97316;">HIGH 8.1</td><td>&lt; 1.13.9, &lt; 1.14.5</td><td>RBAC bypass: access cluster-scoped resources as if they were namespace-scoped</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CVE-2021-25741</strong></td><td style="color:#f97316;">HIGH 8.1</td><td>&lt; 1.19.15, &lt; 1.20.11, &lt; 1.21.5</td><td>Symlink attack: hostPath volume escapes its restricted directory</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CVE-2022-3294</strong></td><td style="color:#eab308;">HIGH 7.6</td><td>&lt; 1.25.4, &lt; 1.24.8</td><td>Node address type confusion → MITM attack between nodes</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-weight:700;">Every one of these CVEs was patched in a new Kubernetes version. Every unpatched cluster running an affected version was — and still is — vulnerable to these attacks. The fix is always the same: upgrade.</p>

                <h4>Kubernetes Support Window — Know When to Upgrade</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Version lifecycle — which versions get security patches</div>
<pre>
  If latest version is v1.30:

  v1.30 → ✅ Supported (latest) — receives all patches + new features
  v1.29 → ✅ Supported (N-1) — receives security patches only
  v1.28 → ✅ Supported (N-2) — receives security patches only
  v1.27 → ❌ EOL — NO patches. Vulnerable forever until upgraded.
  v1.26 → ❌ EOL — NO patches.
  v1.25 → ❌ EOL — NO patches.

  RULE: When v1.31 is released, v1.28 goes EOL. Always have an upgrade plan
        to stay within the N-2 window — that gives you ~14 months per upgrade.

  ⚠️  The CKS exam tests your awareness of this support window.
      If a question mentions a cluster on v1.25, the answer includes UPGRADE.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Attack Scenario: What Happens When You Run an EOL Version</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Scenario: Cluster on v1.26 (EOL). CVE-2024-XXXX disclosed with CVSS 9.8.</div>
<pre>
  DAY 0: CVE ANNOUNCED
  ────────────────────
  CVE-2024-XXXX: "Unauthenticated RCE via kube-apiserver proxy"
  CVSS: 9.8 (CRITICAL). Affects: Kubernetes &lt; 1.28.5, &lt; 1.29.2, &lt; 1.30.0
  Exploit published on GitHub same day. Mass scanners deploy within hours.

