            <section class="section-block" id="s12-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">12.2 Upgrade Strategy for a Running Cluster</h3>
                <p>Upgrading a Kubernetes cluster is a <strong>multi-phase operation</strong> that must follow a specific order: control plane first, then worker nodes. This order ensures the API server is always at a version equal to or higher than the kubelets — kubelets cannot be at a higher minor version than the API server. Breaking this rule causes the kubelet to be unable to communicate with the API server.</p>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ One Minor Version at a Time — No Skipping</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes only supports upgrading <strong>one minor version at a time</strong>. You cannot jump from v1.28 to v1.30 — you must go v1.28 → v1.29 → v1.30. The CKS exam tests this explicitly: if a cluster is on v1.27 and you are asked to upgrade to v1.29, you must upgrade to v1.28 first, THEN to v1.29. Attempting to skip a version will fail.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Safe Cluster Upgrade Sequence — Step by Step</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The complete kubeadm upgrade workflow</div>
<pre>
  PHASE 1: PREPARE
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  □ kubeadm upgrade plan → shows available versions
  □ Backup etcd: etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-pre-upgrade.db
  □ Check addon compatibility (Calico, Ingress, cert-manager)
  □ Read release notes for breaking changes and deprecations

  PHASE 2: UPGRADE CONTROL PLANE (one node at a time)
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  □ apt-get update && apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.29.x-00
  □ kubeadm upgrade apply v1.29.x
  □ apt-get install -y kubelet=1.29.x-00 kubectl=1.29.x-00
  □ systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart kubelet
  □ kubectl get nodes → control plane shows v1.29.x ✅

  PHASE 3: UPGRADE WORKER NODES (one at a time)
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  □ kubectl drain &lt;worker-node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
  □ SSH to worker: apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.29.x-00
  □ kubeadm upgrade node
  □ apt-get install -y kubelet=1.29.x-00 kubectl=1.29.x-00
  □ systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart kubelet
  □ kubectl uncordon &lt;worker-node&gt;
  □ Wait for node to become Ready → repeat for next worker

  PHASE 4: VERIFY
  ─────────────────
  □ kubectl get nodes → ALL nodes show v1.29.x
  □ kubectl version --short → client and server match
  □ Run kube-bench to verify no CIS regressions from upgrade
  □ Verify all easypj pods are running and healthy
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Critical Rules for Safe Upgrades</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⬆️</div>
                        <h5>One Minor at a Time</h5>
                        <p>Never skip versions. v1.28→v1.29→v1.30. Skipping causes API version incompatibilities that break the cluster.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div>
                        <h5>Always Backup etcd</h5>
                        <p>Before ANY upgrade: <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code>. If the upgrade corrupts etcd, the snapshot is your only recovery path.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div>
                        <h5>Drain Before Worker Upgrades</h5>
                        <p>Pods are rescheduled to other nodes during drain. Use <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> for DaemonSet pods that must run on every node.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

