                <h4>Version Compatibility — What Changes Between Minor Versions</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Version Constraint</th><th>What Happens If Mismatched</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>kube-apiserver → kubelet</strong></td><td>API server must be ≥ kubelet minor version</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">Kubelet cannot communicate — node goes NotReady</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubelet → kube-apiserver</strong></td><td>Kubelet can be up to THREE minor versions BEHIND API server (skew policy)</td><td style="color:#eab308;">Works, but upgrade kubelet soon</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubectl → kube-apiserver</strong></td><td>kubectl must be within ±1 minor version of API server</td><td style="color:#eab308;">May work, but some features unavailable</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CNI Plugin</strong></td><td>Must be compatible with target K8s version</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">NetworkPolicy enforcement may break</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td>Must be at a version supported by the API server</td><td style="color:#ef4444;">API server cannot connect to etcd — cluster down</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key Takeaway for the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p>The CKS exam version management questions are practical: you will be given a cluster on an outdated version and asked to upgrade it. The workflow is always: (1) <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code> to see available versions, (2) upgrade kubeadm binary first, (3) <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> on the control plane, (4) upgrade kubelet and kubectl, (5) drain worker nodes one at a time and upgrade them. <strong>Never skip versions. Always backup etcd before upgrading the control plane.</strong></p>
                </div>

                <h4>Kubernetes Version Skew Policy — Why You Can't Skip Versions</h4>
                <p>Kubernetes enforces a strict <strong>version skew policy</strong> that governs which versions can communicate with each other. This is not arbitrary — it exists because the API server, kubelet, and kubectl use versioned protobuf APIs that change between releases. Breaking the skew policy results in silent failures, API version mismatches, and corrupted etcd data:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kubernetes Version Skew Policy — What Can Talk to What</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  SKEW POLICY RULES (from kubernetes.io/docs/setup/version-skew-policy)   │
  │                                                                          │
  │  RULE 1: kubelet may be up to THREE minor versions OLDER than            │
  │          kube-apiserver (never newer — see Rule 2).                      │
  │  Example: API server at 1.30 → kubelet can be 1.30, 1.29, 1.28, or 1.27. │
  │           API server at 1.30 → kubelet CANNOT be 1.26 (4 versions old).  │
  │                                                                          │
  │  RULE 2: kubelet MUST NOT be NEWER than kube-apiserver.                  │
  │  Example: kubelet 1.30 + API server 1.29 → BROKEN.                       │
  │           Kubelet may use APIs that don't exist yet.                      │
  │                                                                          │
  │  RULE 3: kubectl MUST be within one minor version (newer OR older)       │
  │          of kube-apiserver.                                               │
  │  Example: kubectl 1.30 + API server 1.29 → OK.                           │
  │           kubectl 1.31 + API server 1.29 → BROKEN.                        │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ⚡ THE UPGRADE ORDER EXISTS BECAUSE OF THESE RULES:                     │
  │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────                       │
  │  1. Upgrade kubeadm FIRST (the upgrade tool itself).                     │
  │  2. kubeadm upgrade apply → upgrades API server, controller-manager,    │
  │     scheduler, and etcd (control plane components).                       │
  │  3. Upgrade kubelet and kubectl on control plane nodes.                  │
  │  4. Drain worker nodes one at a time. For each worker:                   │
  │     a) Upgrade kubeadm on worker.                                        │
  │     b) kubeadm upgrade node (updates kubelet config).                    │
  │     c) Upgrade kubelet + kubectl on worker.                              │
  │     d) Uncordon worker.                                                  │
  │                                                                          │
  │  WHY THIS ORDER:                                                         │
  │  • API server must be upgraded BEFORE kubelets (Rule 1).                 │
  │  • Kubelets must NOT be newer than API server (Rule 2).                  │
  │  • Worker components should be upgraded AFTER control plane.             │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How kubeadm upgrade apply Actually Works — Step by Step Inside the Control Plane</h4>
                <p>When you run <code>kubeadm upgrade apply v1.29.5</code>, kubeadm orchestrates a complex sequence across multiple components. Understanding this sequence helps you troubleshoot when upgrades fail — which they do, frequently, on the CKS exam:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Preflight Checks</h5>
                        <p>kubeadm verifies: cluster is healthy, all nodes are Ready, kubeadm version matches target, no pending certificate expirations, etcd is healthy. If any check fails, kubeadm REFUSES to proceed. Fix the issue before retrying.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>etcd Backup (if configured)</h5>
                        <p>kubeadm takes a snapshot of etcd before making changes. If the upgrade fails catastrophically, you can restore from this snapshot. The snapshot is stored on the control plane node's filesystem.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Renew Certificates (if needed)</h5>
                        <p>If certificates are near expiry, kubeadm renews them during the upgrade. This is automatic but can be controlled with flags.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Upgrade Static Pod Manifests</h5>
                        <p>kubeadm writes NEW manifests to /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ with updated image tags (e.g., v1.28.0 → v1.29.5). The kubelet detects the changes and restarts each static pod with the new image. Order: etcd → API server → controller-manager → scheduler.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Upgrade kubelet and kubectl Binaries</h5>
                        <p>kubeadm upgrade apply does NOT upgrade kubelet/kubectl binaries. You must do this separately with apt/yum. This is the #1 mistake on the CKS exam — candidates upgrade the control plane but forget to upgrade kubelet, and the version skew breaks node communication.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s12-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">12.4 Version Skew — Why Upgrade Order Is a Security Rule, Not a Suggestion</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes enforces a strict <strong>version skew policy</strong>: cluster components may only be a few minor versions apart from <code>kube-apiserver</code>. Violate it and you don't get a clean error — you get undefined behavior, silent RBAC/admission gaps, or a cluster that refuses to start. None of that is a bug report; it is a security incident, because a silently broken admission chain is a silently open door.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The Rule of Thumb</h5>
                    <p>The API server is always the <strong>newest</strong> component in the cluster. Everything else — controller-manager, scheduler, kubelet, kube-proxy, kubectl — must be the same version or OLDER, <strong>never newer</strong>. This single rule is why control-plane upgrade always comes before worker-node upgrade in the sequence you learned in §12.2: upgrading a kubelet past the API server version breaks the skew policy immediately.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Version Skew Policy — How Far Each Component May Lag the API Server</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Allowed Version Relative to kube-apiserver</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>kube-apiserver (HA)</strong></td><td>Within 1 minor version of each other</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kube-controller-manager / kube-scheduler</strong></td><td>Same minor OR up to 1 version OLDER (never newer)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubelet</strong></td><td>Same minor OR up to 3 versions OLDER (never newer)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kube-proxy</strong></td><td>Same as kubelet's rule</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubectl</strong></td><td>Within 1 minor version, older OR newer</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Upgrade Order — Why It Must Flow in Exactly This Direction</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Each step becomes the new ceiling the next step is measured against</div>
<pre>
  1. etcd            (back up first, upgrade if needed)
          │
          ▼
  2. kube-apiserver  ← becomes the NEW ceiling every other component is measured against
          │
          ▼
  3. controller-manager, scheduler   (same/older than apiserver)
          │
          ▼
  4. kubelet, kube-proxy on EACH worker node, one at a time, draining before and uncordoning after
          │
          ▼
  5. kubectl / CI tooling

  ❌ Upgrading a kubelet BEFORE the API server = violates skew policy
     → node may be marked NotReady, or worse, silently miss new
       admission/PSS behavior the newer API server expects
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 Never Upgrade a Kubelet Ahead of the API Server</h5>
                    <p>If a kubelet is upgraded before <code>kube-apiserver</code>, you have violated Rule 2 of the skew policy — a kubelet must never be newer than the API server it reports to. The failure isn't always loud: the node can be marked <code>NotReady</code> outright, or it can keep running while silently missing admission/PSS behavior that the (older) API server doesn't yet enforce the same way. Either outcome is a security gap, not just an availability blip. This is exactly why §12.2's upgrade order — control plane, then workers, one at a time — is a hard rule and not a convenience.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

