            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>An EOL Kubernetes version is a permanent, unpatchable vulnerability</strong> — the support window covers only N, N-1, N-2 (~14 months per version); once a version falls out of that window, no future CVE fix will ever reach it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Upgrades must flow control plane first, then workers, one minor version at a time</strong> — never skip a version (v1.28→v1.29→v1.30, not v1.28→v1.30), and always drain a worker before touching its kubelet, then uncordon after.</li>
                    <li><strong>Patch releases and minor releases demand different urgency</strong> — a patch (1.29.0→1.29.5) is a zero-risk security fix to apply immediately; a minor release (1.28→1.29) needs staging tests and a 6-8 month rollout window.</li>
                    <li><strong>The version skew policy is why the upgrade order is mandatory</strong> — the API server is always the version ceiling; controller-manager/scheduler/kubelet/kube-proxy/kubectl must be the same version or older, never newer, or the cluster silently breaks.</li>
                    <li><strong>Always back up etcd before any upgrade</strong> — <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> is the only recovery path if an upgrade corrupts cluster state; skipping it turns a 5-minute mistake into total data loss.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 12 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>kubeadm upgrade plan MUST succeed first:</strong> Run <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code> to verify the upgrade is valid BEFORE running <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code>. If plan fails, the apply will also fail — but with less helpful error messages.</li>
                    <li><strong>drain waits for PodDisruptionBudgets:</strong> If a PDB prevents eviction, <code>kubectl drain</code> hangs forever. Use <code>--disable-eviction</code> to force drain (pods are deleted, not evicted) or <code>--timeout=5m</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>kubelet and kubeadm versions must match:</strong> Upgrading kubeadm but forgetting to upgrade kubelet results in version skew. The kubelet must be ≤ kubeadm version. Always upgrade kubeadm first, then kubelet.</li>
                    <li><strong>"The upgrade finished, so I'm done" is false:</strong> <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> only upgrades control-plane static pods — kubelet and kubectl on every node are a SEPARATE, manual step. Forgetting them is the #1 CKS upgrade mistake and the exact failure §12.4's skew policy predicts.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl drain --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data &lt;node&gt;</code> — the standard exam drain, memorize it as one line.</li>
                    <li>Order, every time: kubeadm → <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> → drain → kubelet/kubectl → uncordon.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl version --short</code> shows both sides of the skew policy at once — client (kubectl) and server (API) versions, side by side.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

