            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 12 — Lab Q&amp;A (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">11 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After running kubeadm upgrade, worker nodes show NotReady. What did you forget to upgrade?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>You forgot to upgrade <strong>kubelet and kubectl</strong> on each node. <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> upgrades control plane components but does NOT upgrade kubelet or kubectl binaries. Fix: <code>apt-get install -y kubelet=1.30.x-* kubectl=1.30.x-*</code> on each node, then <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code>. This is the #1 CKS exam upgrade mistake.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the maximum allowed version difference between kube-apiserver and kubelet?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>kubelet can be up to <strong>3 minor versions behind</strong> the API server (v1.30 API server + v1.27 kubelet = OK, the oldest supported). kubelet must <strong>never be newer</strong> than the API server — not even by one minor version. The controller-manager and scheduler can be at most 1 minor version behind. kubectl can be 1 minor version ahead or behind. Exceeding these skews causes nodes to go NotReady and API calls to fail.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the correct upgrade order for Kubernetes components, and what command initiates the control plane upgrade?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Upgrade kubeadm itself
apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.30.x-*
# 2. Plan the upgrade (dry-run)
kubeadm upgrade plan
# 3. Apply the upgrade (upgrades control plane static pods)
kubeadm upgrade apply v1.30.x
# 4. Upgrade kubelet + kubectl on each node
apt-get install -y kubelet=1.30.x-* kubectl=1.30.x-*
systemctl restart kubelet
# 5. Verify
kubectl get nodes  # All should show Ready with new version</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do static pod upgrades work during a kubeadm upgrade? What happens to running pods during the process?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>kubeadm writes NEW static pod manifests to <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> with updated image tags (e.g., v1.29.5 → v1.30.0). The kubelet detects the changed files and restarts each static pod ONE AT A TIME in this order: etcd → API server → controller-manager → scheduler. During API server restart (~30 seconds), kubectl is temporarily unavailable — this is expected. Existing workload pods are NOT affected because they run on worker nodes, not as static pods.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Before any cluster upgrade, what's the SINGLE most critical prerequisite? Provide the exact command and explain why skipping it is catastrophic.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>etcd snapshot backup.</strong> If the upgrade corrupts etcd or the new version has a data-incompatible change, the snapshot is your ONLY recovery path. Without it, all cluster state (pods, services, secrets, RBAC, PVs) is permanently lost.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
  snapshot save /backup/etcd-pre-upgrade-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M).db
# Verify the snapshot
etcdctl snapshot status /backup/etcd-pre-upgrade-*.db --write-out=table</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the most important habit for any cluster operation, not just upgrades. On the CKS exam, you'll be asked to perform an upgrade — if you don't snapshot etcd first and something goes wrong, you fail the task. The snapshot takes seconds but saves hours of recovery. Always verify the snapshot with <code>snapshot status</code> before proceeding.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to upgrade a worker node without disrupting production traffic. Write the complete drain → upgrade → uncordon sequence with all required flags.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Cordon the node (no new pods scheduled)
kubectl cordon worker01
# 2. Drain with flags:
# --ignore-daemonsets: DaemonSet pods are OK, they'll stay
# --delete-emptydir-data: Accept data loss for emptydir volumes
# --force: Force delete pods not managed by a controller
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --force
# 3. Upgrade kubelet + kubectl
apt-get install -y kubelet=1.30.x-* kubectl=1.30.x-*
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl restart kubelet
# 4. Verify kubelet is running
systemctl status kubelet
# 5. Uncordon (allow new pods)
kubectl uncordon worker01
# 6. Verify node is Ready
kubectl get node worker01</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Drain → Upgrade → Uncordon is the standard node maintenance workflow. Key flags: <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> prevents drain from hanging (DaemonSet pods recreate immediately), <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code> acknowledges data loss risk, <code>--force</code> handles pods without controllers. On the CKS exam, always drain before upgrading a worker node.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After upgrading the control plane to v1.30, you discover that the <code>--insecure-port</code> flag was removed. Your monitoring tool relied on port 8080 for health checks. How do you adapt?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The insecure port was removed in Kubernetes 1.24. Use authenticated health endpoints instead:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># /healthz (authenticated) — check if API server is alive
kubectl get --raw /healthz
# /livez — more granular health check (individual components)
kubectl get --raw /livez?verbose
# /readyz — readiness check
kubectl get --raw /readyz

# For monitoring tools, use a ServiceAccount token:
curl -k -H "Authorization: Bearer $(cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token)" \
  https://kubernetes.default.svc/healthz</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>API server health endpoints were redesigned in K8s 1.24+. The insecure port (8080) is gone forever. All health checks now require authentication via the secure port (6443). On the CKS exam, if asked to set up monitoring after an upgrade, use <code>kubectl get --raw /healthz</code> (uses your kubeconfig) or create a dedicated SA for monitoring tools.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After upgrading, you re-run kube-bench and see new FAILs. Why might an upgrade introduce new CIS failures, and what's your remediation process?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Upgrades can introduce new CIS failures because: (1) New Kubernetes versions add new CIS checks that didn't exist before. (2) Default values for flags may change (e.g., a flag that defaulted to secure is now insecure). (3) Deprecated flags are removed, and your manifests may still reference them.</p>
                    <p><strong>Process:</strong> (1) Run <code>kube-bench run --targets master</code> after each upgrade. (2) Compare results to pre-upgrade baseline. (3) For each new FAIL, check the CIS benchmark changelog. (4) Apply the fix and re-test. (5) Document new false-positive WARNs.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>CIS benchmarks are version-specific — kube-bench v0.9.0 targets Kubernetes 1.30, while v0.7.0 targeted 1.25. Upgrading Kubernetes without upgrading kube-bench means you're checking against the wrong benchmark. Always use the kube-bench version that matches your Kubernetes version. On the CKS exam, post-upgrade kube-bench is a common task.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> fails midway with "etcd cluster is unhealthy." You have a pre-upgrade etcd snapshot. Walk through the recovery process.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Stop the API server (prevents writes during restore)
mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
# 2. Restore etcd from snapshot
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /backup/etcd-pre-upgrade.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored \
  --name=cp-node-1 \
  --initial-cluster=cp-node-1=https://10.0.0.1:2380 \
  --initial-advertise-peer-urls=https://10.0.0.1:2380
# 3. Point etcd to restored data
# Edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml:
#   Change --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd to --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored
# 4. Restart API server
mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
# 5. Verify cluster state
kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -A</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the worst-case upgrade scenario. The key insight: stop writes FIRST (move API server manifest), then restore etcd, then restart. If you restore etcd while the API server is writing, you'll have inconsistent state. On the CKS exam, etcd backup and restore is a core competency tested in multiple scenarios. Always snapshot before any destructive operation.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A critical CVE (e.g., CVE-2023-44487 — HTTP/2 Rapid Reset) is announced. Your cluster is vulnerable. Describe the complete patching process including verification.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>1. Assess:</strong> Check your version: <code>kubectl version --short</code>. Compare against the CVE advisory to confirm vulnerability. <strong>2. Plan:</strong> Check available patches — usually a patch release (v1.30.1 → v1.30.2). <strong>3. Backup:</strong> etcd snapshot. <strong>4. Upgrade control plane:</strong> <code>kubeadm upgrade apply v1.30.2</code>. <strong>5. Upgrade workers:</strong> Drain → upgrade kubelet → uncordon. <strong>6. Verify:</strong> (a) <code>kubectl get nodes</code> — all Ready with new version, (b) Check API server is no longer vulnerable: attempt the exploit or check the flag/behavior change, (c) Run kube-bench to ensure no new CIS failures. <strong>7. Document:</strong> Record the change in your compliance log.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Security patch management is a CKS exam competency. The process is identical to a regular upgrade but driven by CVE urgency. Key difference: security patches should be applied to the control plane FIRST (it's the most exposed), then workers. Always verify the vulnerability is actually fixed after patching — don't assume. The CKS exam may give you a scenario with a known CVE and ask you to remediate.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your control plane is on v1.30 and one worker node's kubelet is still on v1.26. Is this cluster in a supported state? Diagnose using the skew policy and give the fix.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>No.</strong> Per the version skew policy (§12.4), kubelet may lag <code>kube-apiserver</code> by at most 3 minor versions. v1.30 → v1.26 is a 4-minor-version gap — outside the supported skew. In practice this node is likely already <code>NotReady</code>, or is silently running with outdated admission/PSS behavior that the v1.30 API server no longer assumes.</p>
                    <p><strong>Fix:</strong> the kubelet must be walked up ONE minor version at a time to close the gap — it cannot jump straight to v1.30. Upgrade the node's kubelet/kubectl through v1.27 → v1.28 → v1.29 → v1.30, verifying <code>kubectl get nodes -o wide</code> shows Ready after each step, exactly like a control-plane upgrade would.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This scenario tests whether you can apply the skew policy's numbers, not just recite them — "one minor version at a time" from §12.2 applies to worker nodes that fell behind just as much as it applies to a live upgrade. The exam expects you to diagnose the gap size FIRST (compare <code>kubectl version --short</code> to <code>kubectl get nodes -o wide</code>'s kubelet column) before proposing a fix.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

