            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 13 · Reducing the OS Attack Surface — Node Hardening</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Kubernetes nodes run <strong>real Linux operating systems</strong> — Ubuntu, RHEL, Flatcar, or similar — and every package installed on those nodes, every service listening on a port, and every user account with SSH access is a potential entry point for an attacker. If a container escapes its sandbox (and container escapes <em>do</em> happen — CVE-2022-0185, CVE-2022-0492, CVE-2024-21626 all enabled kernel-level breakouts), the attacker lands directly on the <strong>node OS</strong>. What they find there determines whether the breach stops at one pod or escalates to every workload on every node in your cluster. This chapter teaches you the OS-level hardening techniques that form <strong>the last line of defense</strong> below Kubernetes — techniques that every CKS exam tests and every production cluster needs.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🖥️ System Hardening</span>
