            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🧠 OS Hardening in the CKS Exam — What They Actually Test</h5>
                <p>The CKS exam does not ask you to memorize every Linux service. It tests your ability to <strong>execute a hardening workflow</strong>:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>If the question says "reduce the attack surface of the worker node"</strong> → audit services with <code>systemctl list-units --type=service</code>, stop/disable unnecessary ones, and verify with <code>ss -tlnp</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>If the question says "harden SSH access to cluster nodes"</strong> → edit <code>/etc/ssh/sshd_config</code>: disable root login, disable password auth, restrict to key-only, add <code>AllowUsers</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>If the question says "restrict which processes can run on nodes"</strong> → remove unnecessary packages with <code>apt remove</code> / <code>yum remove</code> and verify with <code>dpkg -l</code> / <code>rpm -qa</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>The exam terminal gives you root SSH access to nodes. Use it to check EVERYTHING before submitting.</strong></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

