            <section class="section-block" id="s13-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.1 The Node OS Is Your Last Line of Defense</h3>
                <p>Picture this: an attacker exploits a remote code execution vulnerability in the easypj Django application. They gain a shell inside the Django container. They discover they're running as <code>root</code> (because the pod spec didn't set <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>). They find a kernel exploit — say, CVE-2022-0185, a heap overflow in the Linux kernel's <code>legacy_parse_param</code> function that allows an unprivileged user to escape containers and gain root on the host. They run the exploit. <strong>They are now on your Kubernetes node's operating system.</strong></p>

                <h4>Container Escape → What Happens Next Depends on OS Hardening</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left" style="flex:1;">
                        <div class="info-box danger">
                            <h5>🚨 UNHARDENED NODE — Full Cluster Compromise</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Attacker finds 200+ installed packages → picks unpatched services to exploit</li>
                                <li><code>ss -tlnp</code> shows 15+ open ports → finds vulnerable services to pivot through</li>
                                <li>Password SSH on port 22 → brute-forces from stolen /etc/shadow hashes</li>
                                <li><code>root</code> login allowed via SSH → lateral movement to every other node</li>
                                <li>iptables/ufw absent → unrestricted network access to all cluster nodes</li>
                                <li>Docker socket mounted → attacker spawns privileged containers on ANY node</li>
                                <li><strong>Result:</strong> Cluster-wide compromise in under 5 minutes</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel-right" style="flex:1;">
                        <div class="info-box tip">
                            <h5>🛡️ HARDENED NODE — Blast Radius Contained</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Minimal packages installed → no extra services to exploit</li>
                                <li><code>ss -tlnp</code> shows only kubelet(10250), CRI socket, and SSH — all firewalled</li>
                                <li>SSH key-only, AllowUsers restricted → brute-force impossible</li>
                                <li><code>PermitRootLogin no</code> → even with stolen key, root SSH blocked</li>
                                <li>iptables default-deny inbound → attacker's reverse shell can't phone home</li>
                                <li>No Docker socket, no unnecessary mounts → no container escape path to other nodes</li>
                                <li><strong>Result:</strong> Attacker trapped on one node, unable to pivot, detected by Falco in seconds</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The Container Escape Kill Chain — and Where OS Hardening Stops It</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Every stage of a container escape attack — and the OS-level defense at each point</div>
<pre>
  STAGE 1: INITIAL ACCESS — Attacker exploits app vulnerability (RCE in easypj Django)
  │
  ├─ 🛡️ OS DEFENSE: Minimal packages → fewer exploit targets. If the app runs as
  │    non-root (Chapter 18), most kernel exploits require privilege escalation first.
