                <h4>CIS Benchmarks for Linux — What Applies to Kubernetes Nodes</h4>
                <p>The <strong>CIS Distribution Independent Linux Benchmark</strong> complements the CIS Kubernetes Benchmark. While Chapter 4 focused on the K8s-specific benchmark (API server flags, etcd encryption, kubelet config), the Linux benchmark covers the OS layer beneath Kubernetes. Key sections that apply to K8s nodes:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>CIS §1: Filesystem</h5>
                        <p>Mount options (<code>nodev</code>, <code>nosuid</code>, <code>noexec</code> on <code>/tmp</code> and <code>/var/tmp</code>), sticky bit on world-writable directories, separate partitions for critical paths.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div>
                        <h5>CIS §2: Services</h5>
                        <p>Disable services not required for Kubernetes: autofs, avahi-daemon, cups, dhcpd, slapd, named, vsftpd, httpd, dovecot, smb, squid, snmpd, rsync, nis.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div>
                        <h5>CIS §3: Network</h5>
                        <p>Disable unused network protocols (DCCP, SCTP, RDS, TIPC). Enable firewall (iptables/ufw). Disable IP forwarding unless explicitly needed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div>
                        <h5>CIS §4: Logging</h5>
                        <p>Configure auditd to log critical system events. Ensure rsyslog or journald is collecting logs. Logs must be shipped off-node (Chapter 28).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">👤</div>
                        <h5>CIS §5: Access/Auth</h5>
                        <p>SSH hardening, password policy enforcement (<code>pam_pwquality</code>), restrict <code>su</code> to <code>wheel</code> group, lock inactive accounts, set <code>TMOUT</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛠️</div>
                        <h5>CIS §6: Maintenance</h5>
                        <p>Verify package integrity with <code>rpm -Va</code> / <code>dpkg --verify</code>. Check for unowned files. Verify file permissions against RPM/DEB database.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The 80/20 Rule of Node Hardening</h4>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚡ You Don't Need to Memorize 200 CIS Checks</h5>
                    <p>The CKS exam focuses on <strong>four high-impact categories</strong> that eliminate 80% of the OS attack surface:</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li><strong>Package minimization</strong> (Section 13.2) — Every removed package is a removed attack vector</li>
                        <li><strong>Port lockdown</strong> (Section 13.3) — Only the 5-8 ports Kubernetes needs should be open</li>
                        <li><strong>SSH hardening</strong> (Section 13.4) — Key-only auth, no root login, restricted users</li>
                        <li><strong>User/IAM restriction</strong> (Section 13.4) — Dedicated admin accounts, no shared root, sudo logging</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p>Master these four, and you can handle any CKS OS hardening task. The remaining CIS Linux checks are important for production but rarely tested directly on the exam.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Physics of a Container Escape — How Kernel Exploits Actually Work</h4>
                <p>To understand WHY OS hardening matters, you need to understand what happens at the kernel level when a container escape succeeds. A container is NOT a virtual machine — there is no hypervisor, no hardware virtualization, no separate kernel. <strong>Every container on a node shares the exact same Linux kernel.</strong> The only thing separating a container from the host is a collection of Linux namespace and cgroup constructs — and these are security <em>boundaries</em>, not impenetrable walls. A kernel exploit that breaks through a namespace boundary gives the attacker direct access to the host kernel — and from there, to every process, file, and network connection on the node.</p>

                <h5>Namespaces: The Invisible Walls That Containers Live Inside</h5>
                <p>Linux namespaces are the kernel feature that makes containers possible. When you run <code>docker run</code> or <code>crictl run</code>, the container runtime asks the kernel to create a set of namespaces for the new process:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Namespace Type</th><th>What It Isolates</th><th>Without It</th><th>Exploit Target?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Mount (mnt)</strong></td><td>Filesystem view — the container sees its own <code>/</code> root</td><td>Container sees host's entire filesystem including <code>/etc/shadow</code>, <code>/var/lib/kubelet</code>, <code>/var/lib/etcd</code></td><td style="color:#326ce5;">YES — CVE-2024-21626 exploited mount namespace via WORKDIR</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>PID</strong></td><td>Process tree — container only sees its own processes</td><td>Container sees EVERY process on the host — can <code>kill -9</code> kubelet, containerd, sshd</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">YES — PID namespace escape via /proc access</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Network (net)</strong></td><td>Network stack — container has its own interfaces, IP, routes</td><td>Container shares host's network namespace — can sniff ALL node traffic including etcd TLS</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">YES — hostNetwork: true bypasses this</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>UTS</strong></td><td>Hostname and domain name</td><td>Container can change node's hostname — potential for impersonation attacks</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">Partial</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>IPC</strong></td><td>Inter-process communication (shared memory, semaphores)</td><td>Container can read/write host processes' shared memory segments</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">YES — IPC namespace escape possible</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>User</strong></td><td>UID/GID mapping — root in container (UID 0) can be mapped to UID 65534 (nobody) on host</td><td>Root in container = root on host. This is how most escapes work.</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">YES — THE most common escape vector</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Cgroup</strong></td><td>Resource limits (CPU, memory) visibility</td><td>Container can see and modify other containers' limits — DoS attacks possible</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">Partial — CVE-2022-0492 exploited cgroup v1</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h5>How CVE-2022-0185 Actually Worked — A Step-by-Step Reconstruction</h5>
                <p>Let's walk through the most impactful container escape of 2022 — not in abstract terms, but in concrete technical steps. Understanding this one exploit will teach you why EVERY OS hardening measure in this chapter matters.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">CVE-2022-0185 — Linux Kernel Heap Overflow Escape, Step by Step</div>
<pre>
  THE SETUP:
  ──────────
  • Attacker has a shell inside a container (via compromised Django RCE in easypj)
  • Container runs as root (UID 0 inside container = UID 0 inside this user namespace)
  • Kernel version: 5.10.x (unpatched — K8s node hasn't been upgraded since 2022)
  • Seccomp: not configured (Chapter 15 — not yet applied in our story)
  • AppArmor: not configured (Chapter 14 — not yet applied)

  STEP 1: ATTACKER RECONNAISSANCE INSIDE THE CONTAINER
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  $ uname -r                    → 5.10.0-15-amd64
  $ cat /proc/self/status | grep -i seccomp
  Seccomp: 0                    → Seccomp is DISABLED. All 300+ syscalls available.
  $ cat /proc/self/attr/current → unconfined
  → AppArmor profile is "unconfined." No restrictions at all.

  FINDING: No seccomp, no AppArmor, old kernel. Perfect conditions.

  STEP 2: ATTACKER LOADS THE EXPLOIT
  ──────────────────────────────────
  The exploit for CVE-2022-0185 targets the legacy_parse_param() function
  in the Linux kernel's filesystem mounting code. This function is called
  when mounting certain filesystem types (like tmpfs) with specific options.

  key technical detail: The function allocates a 4096-byte (PAGE_SIZE) buffer
  with kmalloc(), then copies user-controlled data into it WITHOUT checking
  the size. Input > 4096 bytes overflows the heap buffer.

  The attacker crafts a payload that:
  a) Triggers the overflow by mounting a tmpfs with oversized options
  b) Overwrites a function pointer on the kernel heap with a controlled address
  c) Redirects execution to attacker-controlled kernel code
  d) That code calls commit_creds(prepare_kernel_cred(0)) → gives root

  STEP 3: EXECUTION — THE ESCAPE
  ──────────────────────────────
  $ unshare -U -m -p -f /bin/bash    # Create new user+mount+PID namespaces
  $ mount -t tmpfs -o "size=4096,...[OVERSIZED OPTIONS]..." none /tmp/escape
  → KERNEL PANICS... but the exploit has already:
    1. Overwritten the heap → redirected kernel code execution
    2. Called commit_creds(prepare_kernel_cred(0)) → attacker process now
       has root credentials (UID 0, GID 0) in the INITIAL (host) user namespace
    3. Escaped the container's namespaces → now sees the HOST filesystem

  STEP 4: ATTACKER NOW HAS ROOT ON THE HOST NODE
  ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  $ ls /                          → This is the HOST root filesystem
  /bin /boot /dev /etc /home /lib /media /mnt /opt /proc /root /run /sbin
  /srv /sys /tmp /usr /var

  $ cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
  → Now the attacker can read the API server manifest, find etcd certs,
    read every Secret in etcd, and pivot to other nodes.

  WHAT WOULD HAVE STOPPED THIS:
  ────────────────────────────
  ✅ PATCHED KERNEL (Ch12):       The vulnerability was fixed in 5.10.96.
                                  If kubeadm upgrade had been run, exploit fails.
  ✅ SECCOMP (Ch15):              The unshare() syscall would have been blocked
                                  by a restrictive seccomp profile → exploit
                                  cannot even begin.
  ✅ APPARMOR (Ch14):             The mount attempt would have been denied by
                                  an AppArmor profile that blocks mount() for
                                  the Django container.
  ✅ OS HARDENING (Ch13):         Even after escape, attacker finds minimal
                                  packages, firewalled ports, hardened SSH,
                                  and no compilers to build further exploits.

  LESSON: Defense in depth. Each layer ALONE might fail. ALL layers together
  make exploitation exponentially harder.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h5>Why Root in Container = Root on Host — The User Namespace Problem</h5>
                <p>This is the single most misunderstood concept in container security. When you run a container as root (UID 0) without user namespace remapping, that root user is <strong>the same UID 0 as the host's root user</strong>. The container's root can do anything that the host's root can do — IF it can escape the namespace boundaries. And if there's a kernel bug (like CVE-2022-0185), those boundaries are paper-thin.</p>
                <p>User namespace remapping (<code>userns-remap</code> in Docker, or <code>UserNamespacesSupport</code> in containerd) maps UID 0 inside the container to a high, unprivileged UID on the host (like 65534 = nobody). Even if the container escapes, the attacker lands as an unprivileged user on the host — not root. This is why Chapter 18 (Security Contexts) insists on <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> and why Chapter 21 covers user namespace remapping in depth.</p>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚡ The Root-in-Container Reality Check</h5>
                    <p>Run this command on ANY of your nodes right now:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods --all-namespaces -o json | jq '[.items[].spec.containers[] | select(.securityContext.runAsNonRoot != true)] | length'</code></pre>
                    <p>That number is how many containers in your cluster are running as root — and therefore have the potential to exploit a kernel escape vulnerability. Every one of those containers should have <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> set, unless there is an extremely specific reason why the application requires UID 0.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Filesystem Hardening — Mount Options and Why They're Critical</h4>
                <p>The CIS Distribution Independent Linux Benchmark §1 specifies mount options that should be applied to every partition on a Kubernetes node. These options are not just bureaucratic checkboxes — each one prevents a specific class of attack. Here's what they actually do and why they matter:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5><code>nodev</code></h5>
                        <p><strong>Blocks:</strong> Device files on this filesystem</p>
                        <p><strong>Attack prevented:</strong> Attacker creates a device node for <code>/dev/sda</code> on an unsecured partition, then reads/writes the raw disk — bypassing file permissions entirely.</p>
                        <p><strong>Where:</strong> <code>/tmp</code>, <code>/var/tmp</code>, <code>/home</code>, any user-writable partition</p>
                        <p><strong>CIS check:</strong> §1.1.2-1.1.5</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5><code>nosuid</code></h5>
                        <p><strong>Blocks:</strong> setuid/setgid bits on executables</p>
                        <p><strong>Attack prevented:</strong> Attacker uploads a setuid-root binary to <code>/tmp</code>, executes it, and immediately gains root. Without nosuid, the kernel ignores the setuid bit on this partition.</p>
                        <p><strong>Where:</strong> <code>/tmp</code>, <code>/var/tmp</code>, <code>/home</code></p>
                        <p><strong>CIS check:</strong> §1.1.3, 1.1.6-1.1.7</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5><code>noexec</code></h5>
                        <p><strong>Blocks:</strong> Execution of ANY binary on this filesystem</p>
                        <p><strong>Attack prevented:</strong> Attacker downloads a compiled exploit to <code>/tmp</code> and tries to run it. With noexec, the kernel refuses to execute anything on that mount — even with +x permissions.</p>
                        <p><strong>Where:</strong> <code>/tmp</code>, <code>/var/tmp</code>, <code>/dev/shm</code> (shared memory — commonly used for exploit staging)</p>
                        <p><strong>CIS check:</strong> §1.1.4-1.1.5, 1.1.8-1.1.15</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>The pattern is: <strong>any filesystem location that a non-root user (or a compromised container process) can write to should have nodev, nosuid, and noexec set.</strong> These three mount options alone have stopped more post-exploitation attacks than any other single hardening measure. Here's how to apply them:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Applying CIS-recommended mount options on K8s nodes</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Check current mount options for /tmp
mount | grep " /tmp "
# Example output: /dev/sda2 on /tmp type ext4 (rw,relatime)
# Missing: nodev, nosuid, noexec → VULNERABLE

# METHOD 1: Remount with options (immediate, non-persistent)
mount -o remount,nodev,nosuid,noexec /tmp
mount -o remount,nodev,nosuid,noexec /var/tmp
mount -o remount,nodev,nosuid,noexec /dev/shm

# METHOD 2: Add to /etc/fstab (persistent across reboots)
# Add these lines to /etc/fstab:
# /dev/sda2  /tmp       ext4  defaults,nodev,nosuid,noexec  0  0
# /dev/sda3  /var/tmp   ext4  defaults,nodev,nosuid,noexec  0  0
# tmpfs      /dev/shm   tmpfs defaults,nodev,nosuid,noexec  0  0

# METHOD 3: Use a separate partition for /tmp (prevents /tmp from filling root)
# This also satisfies CIS §1.1.2 — separate /tmp partition

# Verify after applying:
mount | grep -E '/tmp|/var/tmp|/dev/shm'
# Should show: (rw,nodev,nosuid,noexec,relatime)</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 13.2 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.2 Removing Unused Services and Packages</h3>
                <p>Every package installed on a Kubernetes node is a potential attack surface. A web server package like <code>nginx</code> or <code>apache2</code> has no business being on a K8s node — the pods run those services, not the node itself. Yet production clusters are routinely found with hundreds of unnecessary packages because nodes were provisioned from general-purpose OS images that included "kitchen sink" package sets. <strong>The rule is simple: if Kubernetes doesn't need it to run pods, it should not be installed on the node.</strong></p>

                <h4>The Audit Workflow — Find, Stop, Remove, Verify</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>FIND — List what's running</h5>
                        <p><code>systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running</code><br>Shows every active service on the node. Every line you don't recognize is a candidate for removal.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>STOP — Disable unnecessary services</h5>
                        <p><code>systemctl stop &lt;service&gt; && systemctl disable &lt;service&gt;</code><br>Stops the service immediately AND prevents it from restarting on reboot.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>REMOVE — Uninstall the package</h5>
                        <p><code>apt remove --purge &lt;package&gt;</code> (Debian) or <code>yum remove &lt;package&gt;</code> (RHEL). Purge removes config files too — leaving configs behind is a security risk.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>VERIFY — Confirm nothing's left</h5>
                        <p><code>systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running | wc -l</code> — count should decrease. <code>dpkg -l | grep &lt;package&gt;</code> — should return nothing.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Services to CHECK and REMOVE on Kubernetes Nodes</h4>
                <p>The table below lists the most common unnecessary services found on Kubernetes nodes. These are pulled from real-world node audits and the CIS Distribution Independent Linux Benchmark. <strong>Every</strong> service in the "Remove on K8s Node" column should be investigated — some may be required by your specific infrastructure, but most are not.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Service / Package</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Why It's on a Node</th><th>Action on K8s Node</th><th>Verification Command</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>apache2 / httpd / nginx</strong></td><td>Web server</td><td>Default in many OS images</td><td style="color:#326ce5; font-weight:600;">REMOVE — pods serve web traffic</td><td><code>dpkg -l | grep apache2</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>vsftpd / proftpd</strong></td><td>FTP server</td><td>Legacy provisioning</td><td style="color:#326ce5; font-weight:600;">REMOVE — use SCP/SFTP</td><td><code>systemctl status vsftpd</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>avahi-daemon</strong></td><td>mDNS / Zeroconf discovery</td><td>Desktop-oriented distros</td><td style="color:#326ce5; font-weight:600;">REMOVE — not needed on servers</td><td><code>systemctl status avahi-daemon</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>cups / cups-browsed</strong></td><td>Printing service</td><td>Desktop-oriented distros</td><td style="color:#326ce5; font-weight:600;">REMOVE — no printers on K8s nodes</td><td><code>systemctl status cups</code></td></tr>
