                <h4>How Attackers Weaponize Unnecessary Services — Three Real Attack Chains</h4>
                <p>It's easy to think "it's just a printing service, who cares?" But every service is a piece of software written by humans — and all software has bugs. Let's trace three real attack chains that started with "unnecessary" services on Kubernetes nodes:</p>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔴 ATTACK CHAIN 1: CUPS (Printing Service) → Privilege Escalation</h5>
                    <p><strong>The setup:</strong> CUPS runs as root (it needs to access printer devices) and listens on port 631. It was installed by default on the Ubuntu 20.04 image used for K8s nodes. Nobody thought to remove it — "it's just a printer service."</p>
                    <p><strong>The attack:</strong></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Attacker escapes a container to the host (kernel exploit, or misconfigured hostPath volume)</li>
                        <li>Runs <code>ss -tlnp</code> → finds CUPS listening on <code>127.0.0.1:631</code></li>
                        <li>CUPS has a history of RCE vulnerabilities (CVE-2024-47176, CVSS 9.1 — unauthenticated RCE via <code>cups-browsed</code>)</li>
                        <li>Attacker sends a malicious IPP packet to localhost:631 → gains code execution as <strong>root</strong> (CUPS runs as root)</li>
                        <li>Now has root on the node through a service that never should have been there</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p><strong>Prevention:</strong> <code>systemctl stop cups cups-browsed && apt remove --purge cups cups-browsed</code> — 10 seconds of work eliminates this entire attack surface forever.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔴 ATTACK CHAIN 2: Avahi-Daemon (mDNS) → Network Reconnaissance</h5>
                    <p><strong>The setup:</strong> avahi-daemon implements mDNS (Multicast DNS) and DNS-SD (Service Discovery) via the Zeroconf protocol. It broadcasts service information over the network and responds to queries. Installed by default on Ubuntu Server — yes, even server editions.</p>
                    <p><strong>The attack:</strong></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Attacker compromises any pod in the cluster (e.g., via a vulnerable npm package in a frontend pod)</li>
                        <li>From the pod, sends an mDNS query to <code>224.0.0.251:5353</code> (multicast address that avahi responds to)</li>
                        <li>avahi-daemon responds with: hostname, IP address, OS version, and a list of services running on the node</li>
                        <li>Attacker now has a <strong>complete inventory of the node</strong> — which K8s components are running, what version of Ubuntu, and network topology — without ever leaving their pod</li>
                        <li>This is pure reconnaissance. No exploit needed. avahi is designed to broadcast this information.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p><strong>Prevention:</strong> <code>systemctl stop avahi-daemon && systemctl mask avahi-daemon</code> — mDNS has zero legitimate use on a Kubernetes node. Containers handle their own service discovery via CoreDNS. avahi is pure attack surface.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔴 ATTACK CHAIN 3: Compilers (gcc/g++) → Building Exploits on the Node</h5>
                    <p><strong>The setup:</strong> gcc and g++ were installed as part of <code>build-essential</code> — a common meta-package that DevOps engineers install to compile kernel modules or troubleshoot. The node has full C/C++ compilation capability.</p>
                    <p><strong>The attack:</strong></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Attacker escapes to the node (any method — kernel exploit, misconfigured volume, stolen SSH key)</li>
                        <li>Discovers they are not root — they are uid 1000 (unprivileged user). Need privilege escalation.</li>
                        <li>Without gcc: attacker would need to find a pre-compiled exploit binary that works on this exact kernel version and architecture. This is slow and unreliable.</li>
                        <li>With gcc: attacker downloads exploit source code (publicly available on exploit-db.com), compiles it with <code>gcc -o exploit exploit.c -static</code>, and runs it. The whole process takes <strong>under 30 seconds.</strong></li>
                        <li>Compiles a local privilege escalation exploit (like Dirty Pipe — CVE-2022-0847) → gains root on the node</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p><strong>Prevention:</strong> <code>apt remove --purge build-essential gcc g++ make</code> — removing compilers forces attackers to use pre-compiled binaries that may not match your kernel/architecture, dramatically slowing their attack. Many modern container-optimized Linux distributions (Flatcar, Bottlerocket, Talos) ship with NO compilers at all.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>systemd Mask vs Disable — Understanding the Dependency Chain Deeply</h4>
                <p>When you run <code>systemctl disable &lt;service&gt;</code>, you are telling systemd "don't start this service automatically at boot." But the service can STILL be started — by another service that depends on it, by a socket activation trigger, by a D-Bus message, or by a manual <code>systemctl start</code>. A disabled service is dormant, not dead.</p>
                <p><code>systemctl mask &lt;service&gt;</code> goes further: it <strong>symlinks the service unit file to /dev/null</strong>. Now the service CANNOT be started under ANY circumstances — not manually, not by dependencies, not by triggers. The kernel literally cannot find the unit file to load.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">systemd Service States — The Lifecycle of a Service From Installed to Obliterated</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  systemd SERVICE STATES — weakest to strongest restriction              │
  │                                                                          │
  │  1. INSTALLED &amp; ENABLED (package just installed)                        │
  │     └→ systemctl status → "active (running)" or "inactive (dead)"       │
  │     └→ STARTS automatically at boot                                     │
  │     └→ CAN be started manually: systemctl start &lt;service&gt;               │
  │     └→ Dependencies CAN trigger it to start                             │
  │     └→ RISK: HIGH — service runs whenever it or its dependencies want   │
  │                                                                          │
  │  2. STOPPED but ENABLED (service stopped manually)                      │
  │     └→ systemctl stop &lt;service&gt;                                         │
  │     └→ WILL restart at next boot (because it's enabled)                 │
  │     └→ RISK: MEDIUM — stops now, comes back on reboot                   │
  │                                                                          │
  │  3. DISABLED (no automatic start, but CAN be started)  ⬅ EXAM FOCUS    │
  │     └→ systemctl disable &lt;service&gt;                                      │
  │     └→ Does NOT start at boot                                           │
  │     └→ CAN still be started manually: systemctl start &lt;service&gt;         │
  │     └→ Socket activation TRIGGERS it (if .socket unit exists)           │
  │     └→ Dependencies PULL it in: if "Wants=" or "Requires=" references   │
  │         this service, it WILL start when the dependent starts           │
  │     └→ RISK: MEDIUM — manual/dependency start still possible            │
  │                                                                          │
  │  4. MASKED (CANNOT be started by ANYTHING)  ⬅ STRONGEST                │
  │     └→ systemctl mask &lt;service&gt;                                         │
  │     └→ Creates symlink: /etc/systemd/system/&lt;service&gt;  →  /dev/null     │
  │     └→ Manual start FAILS: "Failed to start &lt;service&gt;: Unit is masked"  │
  │     └→ Dependencies IGNORE it — "Wants=masked-service" does nothing     │
  │     └→ Socket activation BLOCKED — even if socket gets traffic          │
  │     └→ RISK: ZERO — service is functionally deleted from systemd        │
  │                                                                          │
  │  5. PURGED (package removed from disk) ⬅ ULTIMATE                       │
  │     └→ apt remove --purge &lt;package&gt;                                     │
  │     └→ Binary, config files, unit files, man pages — ALL gone           │
  │     └→ Even if an attacker wants to start it, there's nothing to start  │
  │     └→ RISK: NEGATIVE — cannot be exploited because it doesn't exist    │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  CKS EXAM PATTERN:
  • "Disable the service" → systemctl disable (prevent boot start)
  • "Stop and disable the service" → systemctl stop && systemctl disable
  • "Ensure the service cannot be started" → systemctl mask
  • "Remove the package" → apt remove --purge (RHEL: yum remove)
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 13.3 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.3 Limiting Open Ports and Network Interfaces</h3>
                <p>Every open port on a Kubernetes node is a <strong>listening door</strong>. An attacker who escapes a container or compromises a node-level process can connect to any port that's bound to <code>0.0.0.0</code> (all interfaces). Worse, they can <em>open their own listening port</em> for a reverse shell, command-and-control channel, or cryptocurrency miner. A hardened Kubernetes node should have <strong>exactly 5-8 ports open</strong> — the minimum required for the control plane, worker components, and SSH. Everything else should be blocked at the firewall level.</p>

                <h4>Port Audit — What's Listening on Your Node?</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Auditing open ports on a Kubernetes node</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># METHOD 1: ss (socket statistics) — modern replacement for netstat
ss -tlnp
# -t = TCP, -l = listening, -n = numeric (no DNS resolution), -p = show process

# Sample output from a hardened K8s worker node:
# State  Recv-Q  Send-Q  Local Address:Port    Process
# LISTEN 0       128     127.0.0.1:10248       kubelet
# LISTEN 0       128     0.0.0.0:10250         kubelet
# LISTEN 0       128     127.0.0.1:10256       kube-proxy
# LISTEN 0       128     0.0.0.0:22            sshd

# METHOD 2: Check which INTERFACE each port is bound to
ss -tlnp | awk '{print $4}' | sort -u
# 127.0.0.1:XXXX  → localhost only (safe)
# 0.0.0.0:XXXX    → ALL interfaces (NEEDS FIREWALL)
# 192.168.x.x:XXXX → specific interface (better than 0.0.0.0)

# METHOD 3: Count open ports by process (spot anomalies)
ss -tlnp | awk '{print $NF}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
# Shows how many ports each process has open — any process with
# unexpected ports is a red flag</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Required Kubernetes Ports — What SHOULD Be Open</h4>
                <p>This is the authoritative port reference for Kubernetes nodes. <strong>Memorize these for the CKS exam.</strong> Every port NOT on this list should be investigated and firewalled.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Port(s)</th><th>Protocol</th><th>Bound To</th><th>Who Needs Access</th><th>Firewall: Allow From</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(50,108,229,0.05);"><td><strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td>6443</td><td>TCP</td><td>0.0.0.0</td><td>All cluster components, kubectl users</td><td>Admin IPs + cluster CIDR</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(50,108,229,0.05);"><td><strong>etcd client</strong></td><td>2379</td><td>TCP</td><td>0.0.0.0</td><td>API server only</td><td>Control plane nodes ONLY</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(50,108,229,0.05);"><td><strong>etcd peer</strong></td><td>2380</td><td>TCP</td><td>0.0.0.0</td><td>Other etcd members</td><td>Control plane nodes ONLY</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubelet API</strong></td><td>10250</td><td>TCP</td><td>0.0.0.0</td><td>API server, monitoring</td><td>Control plane + monitoring</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubelet healthz</strong></td><td>10248</td><td>TCP</td><td>127.0.0.1</td><td>Local health checks only</td><td>localhost (already safe)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kube-proxy health</strong></td><td>10256</td><td>TCP</td><td>127.0.0.1</td><td>Local health checks only</td><td>localhost (already safe)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kube-proxy metrics</strong></td><td>10249</td><td>TCP</td><td>127.0.0.1</td><td>Prometheus (optional)</td><td>localhost or monitoring IP</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td>10259</td><td>TCP</td><td>127.0.0.1</td><td>Local only</td><td>localhost (already safe)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kube-controller-manager</strong></td><td>10257</td><td>TCP</td><td>127.0.0.1</td><td>Local only</td><td>localhost (already safe)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>NodePort range</strong></td><td>30000-32767</td><td>TCP/UDP</td><td>0.0.0.0</td><td>External traffic to services</td><td>Restrict per-service</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Calico BGP</strong></td><td>179</td><td>TCP</td><td>0.0.0.0</td><td>Other Calico nodes</td><td>Cluster node IPs only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>SSH</strong></td><td>22</td><td>TCP</td><td>0.0.0.0</td><td>Administrators</td><td>Admin jump host IPs ONLY</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Firewall Configuration — Two Approaches for the CKS Exam</h4>
                <p>The CKS exam expects you to know both <strong>iptables</strong> (direct kernel firewall) and higher-level tools like <strong>ufw</strong> (Uncomplicated Firewall, Ubuntu) or <strong>firewalld</strong> (RHEL). iptables is the lowest common denominator — it works on EVERY Linux distribution and is always available. Use iptables when the exam explicitly asks for it; use ufw/firewalld when the exam gives you a choice.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — iptables firewall for a Kubernetes worker node (easypj worker-1)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# iptables HARDENING for K8s WORKER NODE
# This locks down a worker node to ONLY kubelet + SSH + CNI ports
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# STEP 1: SET DEFAULT POLICIES — DENY ALL INBOUND
iptables -P INPUT DROP
iptables -P FORWARD DROP
iptables -P OUTPUT ACCEPT  # Allow outbound (pods need internet access)

# STEP 2: ALLOW LOOPBACK (critical for local communication)
iptables -A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT

# STEP 3: ALLOW ESTABLISHED CONNECTIONS (responses to outbound traffic)
iptables -A INPUT -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT

# STEP 4: ALLOW SSH FROM ADMIN JUMP HOST ONLY (not the whole internet!)
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp -s 10.0.0.100 --dport 22 -j ACCEPT

# STEP 5: ALLOW KUBELET API — from control plane nodes only
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp -s 10.0.0.10 --dport 10250 -j ACCEPT  # cp-1
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp -s 10.0.0.11 --dport 10250 -j ACCEPT  # cp-2
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp -s 10.0.0.12 --dport 10250 -j ACCEPT  # cp-3

# STEP 6: ALLOW CNI (Calico BGP) from other cluster nodes
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp -s 10.0.0.0/24 --dport 179 -j ACCEPT

# STEP 7: ALLOW NODEPORT RANGE — only if needed
# iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 30000:32767 -j ACCEPT

# STEP 8: LOG AND DROP everything else
iptables -A INPUT -j LOG --log-prefix "IPTABLES-DENIED: " --log-level 4
iptables -A INPUT -j DROP

# STEP 9: SAVE RULES (persistent across reboots)
# Debian/Ubuntu:
apt install -y iptables-persistent
iptables-save > /etc/iptables/rules.v4
# RHEL/CentOS:
# iptables-save > /etc/sysconfig/iptables

# STEP 10: VERIFY RULES
iptables -L -v -n</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — ufw (Uncomplicated Firewall) for Ubuntu nodes — simpler syntax</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ufw is a friendlier frontend to iptables
# Default: DENY incoming, ALLOW outgoing
ufw default deny incoming
ufw default allow outgoing

# Allow SSH from admin subnet only
ufw allow from 10.0.0.0/28 to any port 22 proto tcp

# Allow kubelet API from control plane
ufw allow from 10.0.0.10 to any port 10250 proto tcp
ufw allow from 10.0.0.11 to any port 10250 proto tcp
ufw allow from 10.0.0.12 to any port 10250 proto tcp

# Allow Calico BGP from cluster nodes
ufw allow from 10.0.0.0/24 to any port 179 proto tcp

# Enable the firewall
ufw --force enable

# Verify
ufw status verbose</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Port Decision Flowchart — "Should This Port Be Open?"</h4>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="decision-node question">🔍 Is a Kubernetes component (kubelet, kube-proxy, CNI, etcd, apiserver) listening on this port?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ YES → Verify it's bound to localhost (127.0.0.1) if possible. If it MUST bind to 0.0.0.0, add an iptables rule restricting source IPs to cluster-internal ranges.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">❓ No. Continue.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">🔍 Is this SSH (port 22)?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ YES → Restrict to admin jump host IPs ONLY. Never expose SSH to 0.0.0.0/0. Use AllowUsers in sshd_config.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">🚫 NO and NOT a K8s component → STOP THE SERVICE and CLOSE THE PORT. If the process is essential, firewall the port to specific source IPs, then investigate why it's listening.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Disable Unused Network Protocols</h4>
                <p>The CIS benchmark also requires disabling unused kernel network protocols. Each protocol module loaded in the kernel is a potential attack surface. The commands below disable protocols that Kubernetes nodes should never need:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Disabling unused kernel network protocols</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Disable DCCP (Datagram Congestion Control Protocol)
echo "install dccp /bin/true" >> /etc/modprobe.d/CIS-dccp.conf

# Disable SCTP (Stream Control Transmission Protocol)
echo "install sctp /bin/true" >> /etc/modprobe.d/CIS-sctp.conf

# Disable RDS (Reliable Datagram Sockets)
echo "install rds /bin/true" >> /etc/modprobe.d/CIS-rds.conf

# Disable TIPC (Transparent Inter-Process Communication)
echo "install tipc /bin/true" >> /etc/modprobe.d/CIS-tipc.conf

# IP forwarding — REQUIRED for Kubernetes pod networking, do NOT disable
# (kubeadm prerequisites mandate net.ipv4.ip_forward=1 on every node; see §16.6)
echo "net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1" >> /etc/sysctl.d/99-cis.conf
echo "net.ipv6.conf.all.forwarding = 1" >> /etc/sysctl.d/99-cis.conf
sysctl -p /etc/sysctl.d/99-cis.conf

# Verify all four modules are blacklisted
lsmod | grep -E 'dccp|sctp|rds|tipc'
# Should return NOTHING</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>iptables Deep Dive — How Packets Flow Through Chains</h4>
                <p>Every network packet that arrives at or leaves a Linux node passes through the kernel's <strong>netfilter framework</strong>, which iptables configures. Understanding this flow is critical because a single misconfigured rule — a <code>DROP</code> where you need <code>ACCEPT</code> — can take down your entire cluster. Let's trace exactly what happens to a packet from the moment it hits the network interface:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">iptables Packet Flow — The Complete Path Through the Kernel</div>
<pre>
  INCOMING PACKET (e.g., kubectl command arriving at kube-apiserver:6443)
  │
  ▼
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  1. PREROUTING CHAIN (raw → mangle → nat)                          │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────                          │
  │  Purpose: DNAT (destination NAT) happens here.                      │
  │  • If the packet is for a NodePort service (30000-32767), DNAT      │
  │    rewrites the destination from the node IP to the pod IP.         │
  │  • If the packet is for a ClusterIP service, same thing.            │
  │  ⚡ CKS RELEVANCE: NodePort rules live in PREROUTING nat chain.     │
  │     If you DROP here, you break ALL NodePort services.              │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼  ROUTING DECISION: Is this packet for THIS node? Or should we forward it?
  │
  ├─ YES, for this node:
  │    │
  │    ▼
  │    ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │    │  2. INPUT CHAIN (mangle → filter)  ⬅⬅⬅ THIS IS WHERE YOUR RULES GO │
  │    │  ─────────────────────────────────                                   │
  │    │  Purpose: Filter packets DESTINED for local processes.               │
  │    │  • iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 6443 -s 10.0.0.0/24 -j ACCEPT  │
  │    │  • iptables -A INPUT -j DROP   ← default-deny everything else       │
  │    │  ⚡ CKS RELEVANCE: Every hardening rule you write goes in INPUT.     │
  │    │     The order matters: ACCEPT rules FIRST, then DROP at the end.     │
  │    │     If DROP comes first, EVERYTHING is dropped — including SSH.      │
  │    └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │    │
  │    ▼  Delivered to local process (e.g., kubelet on port 10250)
  │
  └─ NO, this node is a router:
       │
       ▼
       ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
       │  3. FORWARD CHAIN (mangle → filter)                                 │
       │  ─────────────────────────────────────                              │
       │  Purpose: Filter packets passing THROUGH this node (masquerade).    │
       │  • K8s workers forward traffic between pods on different nodes.     │
       │  • iptables -P FORWARD DROP is SAFE for workers (they don't route)  │
       │  • iptables -P FORWARD DROP on control plane is usually safe too    │
       │  ⚡ CKS RELEVANCE: If your node is a router/bridge, FORWARD must     │
       │     remain ACCEPT or pod-to-pod cross-node traffic will break.      │
       └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
       │
       ▼
       ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
       │  4. POSTROUTING CHAIN (mangle → nat)                                │
       │  ────────────────────────────────────────                           │
       │  Purpose: SNAT (source NAT) happens here — masquerading.            │
       │  • Pod traffic leaving the node gets SNAT'd to the node's IP.       │
       │  ⚡ CKS RELEVANCE: If you mess with POSTROUTING, pod egress breaks.  │
       └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  OUTGOING PACKET (locally generated):
  │
  ▼
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  5. OUTPUT CHAIN (raw → mangle → nat → filter)                      │
  │  ───────────────────────────────────────────────                     │
  │  Purpose: Filter packets GENERATED BY local processes.               │
  │  • iptables -P OUTPUT ACCEPT is the default and generally safe.     │
  │  • Restrictive OUTPUT rules can break pod egress, DNS, etc.         │
  │  ⚡ CKS RELEVANCE: Don't set OUTPUT to DROP on K8s nodes unless      │
  │     you have a VERY specific reason and tested pod networking.       │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  KEY TAKEAWAY FOR THE CKS EXAM:
  ─────────────────────────────────
  iptables -P INPUT DROP      → Blocks incoming traffic by default (GOOD)
  iptables -P FORWARD DROP    → Usually safe (nodes don't route)
  iptables -P OUTPUT ACCEPT   → Keep this ACCEPT (pods need to talk out)

  Then add ACCEPT rules in INPUT chain for: SSH, kubelet, CNI
  The ORDER is: ACCEPT rules first → LOG denied → DROP at the very end
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Why Binding to 127.0.0.1 vs 0.0.0.0 Matters — A Network-Level Analysis</h4>
                <p>When a process opens a socket with <code>bind()</code>, it specifies which IP address(es) to listen on. This is not just a configuration detail — it is a <strong>fundamental security boundary</strong>. The difference between binding to <code>127.0.0.1</code> and <code>0.0.0.0</code> is the difference between "only processes on this machine can reach this port" and "any machine on the network can reach this port."</p>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left" style="flex:1;">
                        <div class="info-box danger">
                            <h5>🔴 0.0.0.0 (INADDR_ANY) — All Interfaces</h5>
                            <p>The process binds to <strong>every network interface</strong> on the node:</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li><code>lo</code> (127.0.0.1) — localhost</li>
                                <li><code>eth0</code> (10.0.0.5) — primary network</li>
                                <li><code>caliXXX</code> (pod network) — CNI interfaces</li>
                                <li><code>tunl0</code> (tunnel) — overlay network</li>
                            </ul>
                            <p><strong>Attacker impact:</strong> Can reach this port from a pod on ANOTHER node (10.0.1.x → 10.0.0.5:10250), from the internet (if the node has a public IP), or from any compromised machine on the network.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel-right" style="flex:1;">
                        <div class="info-box tip">
                            <h5>🟢 127.0.0.1 — Localhost Only</h5>
                            <p>The process binds ONLY to the loopback interface:</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li><code>lo</code> (127.0.0.1) — localhost ONLY</li>
                            </ul>
                            <p><strong>Attacker impact:</strong> Even if they are on the same node (container escape), they can only reach this port from the host itself. An attacker on a different node or the internet cannot reach it at all — the kernel drops the packets at the network layer before the process ever sees them.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>Look at the Kubernetes port table above again — notice that <code>kube-scheduler</code> (10259) and <code>kube-controller-manager</code> (10257) bind to <strong>127.0.0.1</strong>. This is intentional: only the API server (running on the same control plane node) needs to talk to them. No one else — not even other pods on the same node — should be able to reach the scheduler or controller manager. If an attacker on a compromised pod could reach port 10259 and send a malicious scheduling request, they could influence where pods land — potentially placing privileged pods on compromised nodes.</p>

                <h4>Container Escape Scenario — Why Node Hardening Is Your Last Line</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header">
                        <span class="scenario-badge danger">ATTACK SCENARIO</span>
                        <span class="scenario-title">What Happens When a Container Escapes to an Unhardened vs Hardened Node</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Attacker escapes container via CVE in runc</strong><p>Using CVE-2024-21626, the attacker breaks out of the container namespace and lands on the host node as root (or a non-root user). They now have direct access to the node OS.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>On an UNHARDENED node:</strong><p>Attacker finds SSH running with password auth → brute-forces credentials. Finds <code>gcc</code> installed → compiles a kernel exploit. Finds <code>docker</code> still installed (not containerd) → accesses other containers directly. Finds FTP server running → exfiltrates data. The node has 200+ unnecessary packages installed — every one is a potential privilege escalation or persistence vector.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">🛡️</div><div class="step-content"><strong>On a HARDENED node (per this chapter):</strong><p>SSH is key-only, root login disabled → brute-force impossible. No compilers (gcc removed) → cannot compile exploits. Only containerd (no docker) → no direct container access. No FTP/printing/Bluetooth → no exfiltration channels. Only 12 essential packages → minimal attack surface. The attacker is trapped on a stripped-down OS with virtually no tools to escalate. <strong>Node hardening turned a catastrophic breach into a contained incident.</strong></p></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Automated Node Audit Script — Run This on Every Node Weekly</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Bash — Complete node security audit in one script</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
# node-audit.sh — Run weekly on every Kubernetes node
echo "=== NODE SECURITY AUDIT: $(hostname) — $(date) ==="

# 1. Check for unnecessary services running
echo "[1/8] Checking unnecessary services..."
BAD_SVCS="cups vsftpd apache2 nginx avahi-daemon bluetooth snapd"
for svc in $BAD_SVCS; do
  if systemctl is-active --quiet $svc 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "  ❌ $svc is RUNNING — should be removed"
  fi
done

# 2. Check open ports (only K8s-required ports should be listening)
echo "[2/8] Checking open ports..."
ALLOWED_PORTS="6443 2379 2380 10250 10256 179 4240"
ss -tlnp | while read line; do
  port=$(echo "$line" | grep -oP ':\d+' | tr -d ':' | head -1)
  if [ -n "$port" ] && ! echo "$ALLOWED_PORTS" | grep -qw "$port"; then
    echo "  ⚠️  Unexpected port $port is listening"
  fi
done

# 3. Check SSH configuration
echo "[3/8] Checking SSH hardening..."
grep -q "^PermitRootLogin no" /etc/ssh/sshd_config || echo "  ❌ Root login allowed"
grep -q "^PasswordAuthentication no" /etc/ssh/sshd_config || echo "  ❌ Password auth enabled"

# 4. Check for unnecessary users
echo "[4/8] Checking user accounts..."
for user in $(awk -F: '$3>=1000{print $1}' /etc/passwd); do
  if ! id -nG "$user" | grep -qw "k8s-admins"; then
    echo "  ⚠️  User $user exists but not in k8s-admins group"
  fi
done

# 5. Check kernel hardening
echo "[5/8] Checking kernel parameters..."
[ "$(sysctl -n net.ipv4.ip_forward)" = "1" ] || echo "  ❌ IP forwarding disabled — REQUIRED for pod networking, re-enable it"

# 6. Check for compilers
echo "[6/8] Checking for compilers (should be absent)..."
which gcc 2>/dev/null && echo "  ❌ gcc installed — remove with: apt remove gcc"
which make 2>/dev/null && echo "  ⚠️  make installed"

# 7. Check AppArmor
echo "[7/8] Checking AppArmor status..."
aa-status --enabled 2>/dev/null || echo "  ❌ AppArmor not enabled"

# 8. Summary
echo "[8/8] Audit complete. Review findings above."</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 13.4 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.4 User and IAM Hardening on Nodes</h3>
                <p>Who can SSH into your Kubernetes nodes? If the answer is "anyone with the root password" or "everyone in the DevOps team using the same key," your nodes are <strong>one stolen credential away from total compromise</strong>. Kubernetes ServiceAccounts control pod identity inside the cluster (Chapter 10), but <strong>Linux user accounts</strong> control who can access the node OS itself. These are entirely separate identity systems, and both must be hardened.</p>

                <h4>Kubernetes ServiceAccounts vs Linux User Accounts — Two Different Worlds</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left" style="flex:1;">
                        <div class="info-box">
                            <h5>🔑 ServiceAccounts (Chapter 10)</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Scope: Inside the Kubernetes cluster</li>
                                <li>Identity for: Pods, CI/CD pipelines, operators</li>
                                <li>Managed by: Kubernetes RBAC</li>
                                <li>Authentication: JWT tokens, bound tokens</li>
                                <li>Access: API server (kubectl, controllers)</li>
                                <li>Example: <code>system:serviceaccount:easypj:django-sa</code></li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel-right" style="flex:1;">
                        <div class="info-box">
                            <h5>🐧 Linux Users (This Chapter)</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Scope: The node operating system</li>
                                <li>Identity for: Human admins, automation agents</li>
                                <li>Managed by: <code>/etc/passwd</code>, <code>/etc/shadow</code>, PAM</li>
                                <li>Authentication: SSH keys, passwords, PAM modules</li>
                                <li>Access: SSH shell, file system, systemctl</li>
                                <li>Example: <code>k8s-admin</code> (uid 1001, in sudo group)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align:center; margin-top:12px; color:#8b949e; font-size:0.82rem;">A compromised ServiceAccount gives access to the Kubernetes API. A compromised Linux user gives access to <strong>the node OS itself</strong> — which is far more dangerous because it bypasses ALL Kubernetes security controls. This section focuses on preventing the latter.</p>

                <h4>The Node Access Hardening Checklist</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5>No Root SSH</h5>
                        <p>Set <code>PermitRootLogin no</code> in sshd_config. Root should only be accessible via <code>sudo</code> from a dedicated admin account.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔑</div>
                        <h5>Key-Only Auth</h5>
                        <p>Set <code>PasswordAuthentication no</code>. SSH keys are immune to brute-force. Passwords are not.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">👤</div>
                        <h5>AllowUsers Restriction</h5>
                        <p>Whitelist exactly which users can SSH. Every other user is denied before authentication even begins.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div>
                        <h5>Rotate Keys</h5>
                        <p>Rotate SSH keys quarterly. Remove keys for departed team members immediately. Audit <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code> monthly.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>SSHD Hardening — The Complete Configuration</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Hardening /etc/ssh/sshd_config for Kubernetes nodes</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# SSHD HARDENING for KUBERNETES NODES
# Run on every node (control plane AND worker)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# STEP 0: BACKUP THE ORIGINAL CONFIG
cp /etc/ssh/sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config.bak.$(date +%Y%m%d)

# STEP 1: APPLY HARDENED SETTINGS
cat >> /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/99-cks-hardening.conf << 'EOF'
# ── CKS Node Hardening ──

# AUTHENTICATION
PermitRootLogin no              # NEVER allow direct root SSH
PasswordAuthentication no       # Key-based auth ONLY
PubkeyAuthentication yes        # Enable SSH keys
ChallengeResponseAuthentication no  # No keyboard-interactive
KerberosAuthentication no       # No Kerberos
GSSAPIAuthentication no         # No GSSAPI

# ALLOW ONLY SPECIFIC USERS (whitelist approach)
AllowUsers k8s-admin ansible-deploy

# PROTOCOL AND CIPHERS
Protocol 2                      # SSHv2 ONLY (v1 is insecure)
Ciphers chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com,aes256-gcm@openssh.com
MACs hmac-sha2-512-etm@openssh.com,hmac-sha2-256-etm@openssh.com
KexAlgorithms curve25519-sha256,curve25519-sha256@libssh.org

# SESSION HARDENING
ClientAliveInterval 300         # Timeout idle sessions after 5 min
ClientAliveCountMax 2           # 2 x 300s = 10 min total before disconnect
MaxAuthTries 3                  # Only 3 auth attempts per connection
MaxSessions 5                   # Limit concurrent sessions per user
LoginGraceTime 30               # 30 seconds to authenticate after connecting
MaxStartups 10:30:60            # Rate-limit unauthenticated connections

# DISABLE UNNECESSARY FEATURES
X11Forwarding no                # No X11 on servers
AllowAgentForwarding no         # Prevent SSH agent hijacking
AllowTcpForwarding no           # Prevent SSH tunneling (bypasses firewalls)
PermitTunnel no                 # No SSH tunnels
PermitUserEnvironment no        # No user env vars via SSH
PrintMotd no                    # No message-of-the-day (info leak)
UsePAM yes                      # Keep PAM for session logging
EOF

# STEP 2: TEST THE CONFIGURATION (CRITICAL — don't skip this!)
sshd -t
# If this returns "Syntax OK", the config is valid.

# STEP 3: RELOAD SSHD (don't restart — reload keeps existing sessions alive)
systemctl reload sshd

# STEP 4: VERIFY FROM ANOTHER TERMINAL (before closing your current session!)
# Open a NEW terminal and try to SSH in. If it works, your config is valid.
# If it FAILS, you still have your original session to fix the config.

echo "✅ SSHD hardened. VERIFY by opening a new SSH session before closing this one."</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Creating a Dedicated Admin User — No More Shared Root</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Creating a hardened admin user on each K8s node</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# CREATE A HARDENED ADMIN USER (DO THIS ON EVERY NODE)
# Replaces shared root access with individual, auditable admin accounts
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# STEP 1: CREATE THE ADMIN USER
useradd -m -s /bin/bash k8s-admin
# -m = create home directory, -s = set shell

# STEP 2: SET A STRONG PASSWORD (used only as backup, SSH keys are primary)
passwd k8s-admin
# Enter a 16+ character random password

# STEP 3: ADD TO SUDO GROUP (with password requirement for sudo)
usermod -aG sudo k8s-admin       # Debian/Ubuntu
# usermod -aG wheel k8s-admin    # RHEL/CentOS

# STEP 4: SET UP SSH KEY AUTHENTICATION
mkdir -p /home/k8s-admin/.ssh
chmod 700 /home/k8s-admin/.ssh
# Copy the admin's PUBLIC key (NOT the private key!)
echo "ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAI..." > /home/k8s-admin/.ssh/authorized_keys
chmod 600 /home/k8s-admin/.ssh/authorized_keys
chown -R k8s-admin:k8s-admin /home/k8s-admin/.ssh

# STEP 5: CONFIGURE SUDO WITH AUDIT LOGGING
cat > /etc/sudoers.d/k8s-admin << 'EOF'
# k8s-admin — Kubernetes node administrator
# All sudo commands are logged to /var/log/sudo.log
Defaults:k8s-admin logfile=/var/log/sudo.log
Defaults:k8s-admin log_input,log_output
Defaults:k8s-admin !requiretty
k8s-admin ALL=(ALL) ALL
EOF
chmod 440 /etc/sudoers.d/k8s-admin

# STEP 6: SET PASSWORD POLICIES (via PAM)
# Enforce strong passwords for ANY local user
apt install -y libpam-pwquality
cat >> /etc/security/pwquality.conf << 'EOF'
minlen = 14
dcredit = -1          # At least 1 digit
ucredit = -1          # At least 1 uppercase
lcredit = -1          # At least 1 lowercase
ocredit = -1          # At least 1 special character
maxrepeat = 3         # Max 3 repeated characters
EOF

# STEP 7: LOCK INACTIVE ACCOUNTS (auto-lock after 30 days of inactivity)
useradd -D -f 30

# STEP 8: SET SESSION TIMEOUT (auto-logout after 15 min of inactivity)
echo "TMOUT=900" >> /etc/profile.d/cks-timeout.sh
echo "readonly TMOUT" >> /etc/profile.d/cks-timeout.sh
echo "export TMOUT" >> /etc/profile.d/cks-timeout.sh

echo "✅ Admin user 'k8s-admin' created. Use this account instead of root."</code></pre>
                </div>

