                <h4>The PAM Authentication Stack — How Linux Actually Authenticates Users</h4>
                <p>When you type <code>ssh k8s-admin@node-01</code>, you are not just hitting a password prompt. Behind the scenes, <strong>Pluggable Authentication Modules (PAM)</strong> orchestrates a chain of checks — each module in the chain must succeed for authentication to proceed. Understanding PAM is essential because misconfiguring it can lock you out of nodes permanently. Here's what happens when an SSH connection arrives:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">PAM Authentication Flow — What Happens When You SSH Into a Node</div>
<pre>
  SSH CONNECTION REQUEST: k8s-admin@node-01
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  1. TCP CONNECTION established (port 22)                        │
  │     └→ iptables ALLOWED this source IP  ✓                       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  2. SSHD checks AllowUsers / DenyUsers / AllowGroups            │
  │     └→ "k8s-admin" IS in AllowUsers → CONTINUE                 │
  │     └→ If NOT in AllowUsers → Connection CLOSED immediately     │
  │     ⚡ KEY INSIGHT: AllowUsers blocks BEFORE authentication even │
  │        begins. The attacker never sees a password prompt. This  │
  │        is why AllowUsers is more secure than just key-only auth. │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  3. PAM STACK: /etc/pam.d/sshd is consulted                     │
  │     ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │     │  pam_nologin.so     → Check if /etc/nologin exists       │ │
  │     │  pam_env.so         → Set environment variables          │ │
  │     │  pam_unix.so        → THE MAIN AUTH MODULE:              │ │
  │     │    • For PASSWORD auth: checks /etc/shadow hash          │ │
  │     │    • For KEY auth: checks ~/.ssh/authorized_keys         │ │
  │     │  pam_pwquality.so   → Enforce password complexity        │ │
  │     │  pam_tally2.so      → Count failed attempts (lockout)    │ │
  │     │  pam_faillock.so    → Alternative lockout module         │ │
  │     │  pam_limits.so      → Apply ulimits (max processes, etc) │ │
  │     │  pam_lastlog.so     → Update /var/log/lastlog            │ │
  │     │  pam_motd.so        → Print /etc/motd (if enabled)       │ │
  │     └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │                                                                  │
  │  THE CRITICAL PAM RULE: Each module returns one of:              │
  │  • success   → this module passed                                │
  │  • auth_err  → authentication failed (bad password/key)          │
  │  • acct_exp  → account expired / locked                          │
  │  • perm_denied → permission denied (user not in allowed group)   │
  │                                                                  │
  │  The STACK determines what happens:                              │
  │  • required   → module MUST succeed, but continue checking others│
  │  • requisite  → module MUST succeed, STOP on failure             │
  │  • sufficient → if this module succeeds, authentication PASSES   │
  │    (even if other modules fail)                                  │
  │  • optional   → result doesn't matter for success/failure        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  4. SESSION OPENED                                              │
  │     • pam_systemd.so → registers session with systemd-logind    │
  │     • pam_umask.so → sets file creation mask (default: 022)     │
  │     • Shell spawned: /bin/bash for k8s-admin                    │
  │     • TMOUT=900 → auto-logout after 15 minutes of inactivity    │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  CKS EXAM RELEVANCE:
  ────────────────────
  The exam may ask you to:
  • "Configure password complexity" → edit /etc/security/pwquality.conf
  • "Lock accounts after 5 failed attempts" → configure pam_tally2 or pam_faillock
  • "Set account expiration" → chage -E 2026-12-31 k8s-admin
  • "Restrict which users can use su" → edit /etc/pam.d/su, add pam_wheel.so
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The SSH Agent Forwarding Attack — A Deeper Explanation</h4>
                <p>When we set <code>AllowAgentForwarding no</code> in sshd_config, we are preventing a devastating attack that most engineers don't understand until it happens to them. SSH agent forwarding allows you to use your LOCAL SSH key on a REMOTE server — the remote server forwards authentication requests back to your local SSH agent. It's convenient. It's also a trap.</p>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔴 HOW SSH AGENT FORWARDING COMPROMISE WORKS</h5>
                    <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> Alice is a DevOps engineer. She connects to the K8s bastion host with agent forwarding enabled: <code>ssh -A alice@bastion.k8s.internal</code></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Alice's SSH agent socket (<code>/tmp/ssh-XXXXXX/agent.1234</code>) is forwarded to the bastion host at <code>/tmp/ssh-XXXXXX/agent.5678</code></li>
                        <li>An attacker who previously compromised the bastion host (or any intermediate jump host) can see this socket</li>
                        <li>The attacker runs: <code>SSH_AUTH_SOCK=/tmp/ssh-XXXXXX/agent.5678 ssh root@prod-db-01</code></li>
                        <li>SSH asks Alice's LOCAL agent to sign the authentication challenge with her private key</li>
                        <li>Alice's agent does it — it's designed to do exactly this</li>
                        <li>The attacker now has root access to prod-db-01, authenticated as Alice</li>
                        <li>Alice never sees a prompt, never knows her key was used</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p><strong>What makes this so dangerous:</strong> The attacker doesn't steal Alice's private key. They use it while it's still in Alice's possession, through the forwarded socket. Alice's key never leaves her machine — but the <em>ability to use it</em> is forwarded to a compromised host.</p>
                </div>
                <p>This is why <code>AllowAgentForwarding no</code> is on the hardening checklist. The alternative — using <code>ProxyJump</code> (<code>ssh -J bastion prod-node</code>) — achieves the same result without exposing the agent socket to intermediate hosts. ProxyJump tunnels the SSH connection through the jump host without forwarding the agent.</p>

                <h4>Why Shared Root Is Dangerous — The Audit Trail Problem</h4>
                <p>When five DevOps engineers all SSH into nodes as <code>root</code>, and one of them accidentally runs <code>kubectl delete namespace production</code> at 3 AM, who did it? With shared root, the answer is: <strong>nobody knows.</strong> The audit log shows:</p>
                <pre><code class="language-bash">Feb 14 03:17:42 node-01 sudo: root : TTY=pts/0 ; PWD=/root ; USER=root ; COMMAND=/usr/bin/kubectl delete namespace production</code></pre>
                <p>Every engineer uses the same account. Every command looks identical in logs. There is zero accountability. Now compare that to dedicated admin accounts with sudo logging:</p>
                <pre><code class="language-bash">Feb 14 03:17:42 node-01 sudo: k8s-alice : TTY=pts/0 ; PWD=/home/k8s-alice ; USER=root ; COMMAND=/usr/bin/kubectl delete namespace production
Feb 14 03:17:42 node-01 sudo: k8s-alice : COMMAND=/usr/bin/kubectl delete namespace production [INPUT: y\n]</code></pre>
                <p>Now you know <strong>exactly</strong> who ran the command, when they ran it, from which directory, and even what they typed as input. This is not just about security — it's about operational maturity. In regulated environments (PCI-DSS, SOC2, HIPAA), the ability to attribute every administrative action to a specific individual is a compliance <em>requirement</em>, not a nice-to-have.</p>
                <p>The <code>log_input</code> and <code>log_output</code> sudo options (which we configured in the admin user creation script) capture even more: every keystroke the user types into the sudo session and every byte of output. This creates a complete replayable record of every administrative session. Combined with shipping logs off-node (Chapter 28), this means even if an attacker compromises a node and deletes local logs, the audit trail survives on the central log server.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 13.5 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.5 Minimizing External Access to the Network</h3>
                <p>The CKS curriculum requires <strong>minimizing external access to the network</strong>. Protect every cluster endpoint — API server (6443), etcd (2379-2380), kubelet (10250), and node SSH (22) — from unauthorized external access. Use firewall rules, cloud security groups, and SSH hardening to restrict access to only authorized sources.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>Port</th><th>Restrict To</th><th>How</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td>API Server</td><td>6443</td><td>Control plane, kubelets, kubectl users</td><td>Cloud SG, iptables, NetworkPolicy</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>etcd</td><td>2379-2380</td><td>API server ONLY</td><td>Bind localhost or firewall</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Kubelet API</td><td>10250</td><td>API server ONLY</td><td>--authorization-mode=Webhook + firewall</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Kubelet Read-Only</td><td>10255</td><td>NO ONE — DISABLE</td><td>--read-only-port=0</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>SSH</td><td>22</td><td>Admin IPs only</td><td>Key-only auth, bastion, /etc/hosts.allow</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>
            </section>

