            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The node OS is Layer 2 of defense-in-depth</strong> — when a container escape (CVE-2022-0185, CVE-2024-21626) succeeds, what an attacker finds on the node OS determines whether the breach stays contained to one node or spreads across the entire cluster.</li>
                    <li><strong>Package minimization eliminates real attack chains</strong> — CUPS, avahi-daemon, and installed compilers (gcc/g++) are not theoretical risks; each has a documented path from "unnecessary service" to root on the node.</li>
                    <li><strong>A hardened node exposes only 5-8 ports</strong> — kubelet, the CNI, and SSH, all bound to the narrowest interface possible (127.0.0.1 where feasible) and firewalled with iptables default-deny INPUT.</li>
                    <li><strong>SSH and IAM hardening ends shared-root chaos</strong> — key-only auth, <code>PermitRootLogin no</code>, an <code>AllowUsers</code> whitelist, and a dedicated admin account with <code>log_input</code>/<code>log_output</code> sudo logging turn an unauditable free-for-all into an accountable, individually attributable trail.</li>
                    <li><strong>Always verify before submitting</strong> — the CKS exam grades on verification: re-check services, ports, SSH config, and users from a SECOND terminal before closing the one that got you in, or a lockout costs you the whole scenario.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 13 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Disabling kubelet breaks the node:</strong> Don't disable kubelet, containerd, or essential system services. Only remove services like FTP, NFS, SMTP, and other non-K8s services.</li>
                    <li><strong>SYS_ADMIN capability is the most dangerous:</strong> <code>SYS_ADMIN</code> combined with <code>privileged: true</code> gives near-root access to the host. Almost all kernel hardening is bypassed if a container has SYS_ADMIN.</li>
                    <li><strong>Kernel modules are persistent:</strong> Blacklisting a kernel module in <code>/etc/modprobe.d/blacklist.conf</code> prevents loading but doesn't unload currently loaded modules. Use <code>modprobe -r &lt;module&gt;</code> to unload.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

