            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 13 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">SSH into a worker node and list all running services. Which command shows services, and what non-Kubernetes services should typically be stopped?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><code>systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running</code>. Non-Kubernetes services to stop: cups (printing), avahi-daemon (mDNS), bluetooth, snapd, packagekitd, ModemManager. Only keep: sshd, kubelet, containerd/docker, kube-proxy (if not running as DaemonSet), and monitoring agents.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check which ports are open on a node, and what command closes an unnecessary port?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><code>ss -tlnp</code> lists all listening TCP ports with process names. To close: stop the service (<code>systemctl stop &lt;service&gt;</code>), disable it (<code>systemctl disable &lt;service&gt;</code>), or block with firewall (<code>iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport &lt;port&gt; -j DROP</code>). Kubernetes-required ports: 6443 (API), 2379-2380 (etcd), 10250 (kubelet), 10257 (controller-manager), 10259 (scheduler).</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What three SSH hardening changes should you make on every Kubernetes node? Write the exact commands.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo sed -i 's/^#PermitRootLogin.*/PermitRootLogin no/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config
sudo sed -i 's/^#PasswordAuthentication.*/PasswordAuthentication no/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config
sudo sed -i 's/^X11Forwarding.*/X11Forwarding no/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config
sudo systemctl restart sshd</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is AIDE and how do you use it to verify file integrity on a Kubernetes node?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>AIDE (Advanced Intrusion Detection Environment) creates a database of file checksums and detects changes. <code>aide --init</code> initializes the database. <code>aide --check</code> compares current files against the database and reports any modifications. Critical for detecting tampering with Kubernetes binaries, config files, and certificates. The CKS exam explicitly tests AIDE knowledge.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You run <code>ss -tulnp</code> on a worker node and see port 21 (FTP), port 25 (SMTP), and port 111 (portmapper) listening. What's the risk, and what's the exact remediation for each?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse;">
                        <thead><tr style="border-bottom: 2px solid #326ce5;"><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Port/Service</th><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Risk</th><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Remediation</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">21 (FTP)</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Plaintext file transfer; can exfiltrate sensitive files</td><td style="padding: 8px;"><code>systemctl stop vsftpd; systemctl disable vsftpd; apt remove vsftpd</code></td></tr>
                            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">25 (SMTP)</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Open relay; attacker sends spam/phishing from your node</td><td style="padding: 8px;"><code>systemctl stop postfix; systemctl disable postfix</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td style="padding: 8px;">111 (portmapper)</td><td style="padding: 8px;">NFS dependency; enables remote filesystem access</td><td style="padding: 8px;"><code>systemctl stop rpcbind; systemctl disable rpcbind</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Every open port is an attack surface. Kubernetes worker nodes should have the absolute minimum: kubelet (10250), containerd, and SSH (22). FTP on port 21 is a plaintext protocol — attackers can sniff credentials or exfiltrate etcd snapshots. SMTP allows spam relay. Portmapper enables NFS which can be exploited for container escape via mounts.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you disable IPv6 on a Kubernetes node, and why might you want to do this? What's the command and the persistent configuration?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Temporarily disable
sysctl -w net.ipv6.conf.all.disable_ipv6=1
sysctl -w net.ipv6.conf.default.disable_ipv6=1
# Persist across reboots
echo "net.ipv6.conf.all.disable_ipv6 = 1" >> /etc/sysctl.d/99-kubernetes.conf
echo "net.ipv6.conf.default.disable_ipv6 = 1" >> /etc/sysctl.d/99-kubernetes.conf
sysctl --system</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>Why:</strong> If your cluster uses IPv4-only networking, IPv6 is an unnecessary attack surface. Attackers can use IPv6 to bypass IPv4-only firewall rules or exploit dual-stack vulnerabilities. The CKS exam expects you to minimize attack surface — unused protocols should be disabled.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is part of OS hardening. The principle: if you're not using it, disable it. IPv6, Bluetooth, wireless, CUPS (printing), and Avahi (mDNS) are all unnecessary on server nodes. Each disabled protocol reduces your attack surface by one vector. The change must be persisted in <code>/etc/sysctl.d/</code> to survive reboots.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What Linux kernel modules are commonly blacklisted on Kubernetes nodes? Write the modprobe blacklist configuration and explain why each module is dangerous.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">cat << EOF > /etc/modprobe.d/kubernetes-blacklist.conf
# Network filesystems — container escape via mounts
blacklist nfs
blacklist nfsv3
blacklist nfsv4
# Obsolete/unnecessary filesystems
blacklist cramfs       # Compressed ROM — rarely needed
blacklist freevxfs     # Veritas — ancient
blacklist jffs2        # Flash filesystem — embedded devices only
blacklist hfs          # Apple — not on Linux servers
blacklist hfsplus
blacklist squashfs     # Read-only compressed — unless used for containers
blacklist udf          # Optical disc
# Unnecessary network protocols
blacklist dccp         # Datagram Congestion Control — rarely used
blacklist sctp         # Stream Control Transmission — unless explicitly needed
blacklist rds          # Reliable Datagram Sockets — Oracle RAC only
blacklist tipc         # Transparent Inter-Process Communication — cluster IPC
EOF</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>CIS benchmark Section 3 covers kernel module blacklisting. The CKS exam tests: (1) NFS modules must be blacklisted to prevent container escape via NFS mounts, (2) unused filesystems reduce the kernel attack surface (fewer parsers = fewer vulnerabilities), (3) unused network protocols eliminate entire protocol stacks from the attack surface. Verify with <code>lsmod | grep -E "nfs|cramfs|dccp"</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to enable Automatic Kernel Page Table Isolation (KPTI) and ASLR on a Kubernetes node. How do you verify they're active, and what do they protect against?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>KPTI (Meltdown mitigation):</strong> Isolates kernel and user page tables to prevent Meltdown attacks where a user-space process reads kernel memory.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Check if KPTI is enabled
dmesg | grep -i "Kernel/User page tables isolation"
# Or: cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/vulnerabilities/meltdown</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>ASLR (Address Space Layout Randomization):</strong> Randomizes memory addresses to make buffer overflow exploits harder.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Check ASLR value (0=none, 1=partial, 2=full)
sysctl kernel.randomize_va_space
# Expected: 2 (full randomization)</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>These kernel protections are last-line defenses. If a container escapes and gets code execution on the host, KPTI prevents reading kernel memory, and ASLR makes exploitation harder. On the CKS exam, CIS Section 3 covers kernel hardening. Always verify these are enabled on every node with <code>sysctl kernel.randomize_va_space</code> and <code>dmesg | grep isolation</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A node has unnecessary user accounts (bob, alice, monitoring) with shell access. How do you audit and lock these accounts? What's the security risk of leaving them active?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Find all users with shell access
cat /etc/passwd | grep -E "/bin/(bash|sh|zsh)" | grep -v root
# Lock unnecessary accounts
usermod -L bob
usermod -L alice
# Or remove the shell entirely
usermod -s /sbin/nologin bob
usermod -s /sbin/nologin alice
# Remove user if not needed
userdel -r monitoring</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>Risk:</strong> Each user account with a shell is a potential SSH entry point. Weak passwords, leaked private keys, or social engineering can give an attacker interactive access. Kubernetes nodes should have ONLY root and a single administrative user (with key-only SSH).</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>User account management is a CIS benchmark requirement. The CKS exam tests: (1) remove unnecessary users, (2) lock remaining non-root accounts, (3) ensure no users besides root have UID 0. On a Kubernetes node, only the cluster administrator needs SSH access — developers should never SSH to nodes.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You install AIDE on a Kubernetes node and initialize the database. Two weeks later, <code>aide --check</code> reports that <code>/usr/bin/kubelet</code> has changed. Walk through your incident response — what do you check and in what order?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="process-steps" style="flex-direction: column;">
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Verify it's expected</h5><p>Was there a recent upgrade? <code>apt list --installed | grep kubelet</code>. Compare the timestamp: <code>stat /usr/bin/kubelet</code>. If it matches an apt upgrade, this is a false positive.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Check file hash</h5><p><code>sha256sum /usr/bin/kubelet</code> and compare with the official release hash from <code>https://dl.k8s.io/v1.30.x/bin/linux/amd64/kubelet.sha256</code>.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Isolate the node</h5><p>If the hash doesn't match: <code>kubectl cordon &lt;node&gt;; kubectl drain &lt;node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --force</code>. Prevent the potentially-compromised kubelet from affecting workloads.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Forensic analysis</h5><p>Check: <code>ausearch -f /usr/bin/kubelet</code> (auditd), <code>journalctl -u kubelet --since "2 weeks ago"</code>, <code>last</code> (who logged in), <code>cat /var/log/auth.log | grep Accepted</code>.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Rebuild and rotate</h5><p>Replace kubelet binary from trusted source. Rotate all node certificates. Update AIDE database: <code>aide --update</code>.</p></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>AIDE alerts are high-priority because a changed kubelet binary could mean an attacker replaced it with a backdoored version that exfiltrates secrets or allows unauthorized pod access. The CKS exam tests incident response for file integrity alerts. Always isolate FIRST, then investigate — never assume it's a false positive until proven.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

