            <p class="chapter-lead">Imagine a security guard who stands outside every door in a building and checks your badge before letting you through. That's <strong>AppArmor</strong> — a Linux Security Module (LSM) that sits between every process and the operating system, checking every file access, every network connection, every capability request against a profile that says exactly what that process is allowed to do. Without AppArmor, a compromised container process can read <code>/etc/shadow</code>, write to <code>/proc</code>, open raw network sockets, and access <code>/dev</code> devices. With AppArmor, even a fully compromised Django process in the easypj container can only read its own application code, write to its media directory, and connect to the PostgreSQL and Redis ports — <strong>everything else is blocked at the kernel level before the process ever touches it.</strong> This chapter teaches you to write, test, and apply AppArmor profiles — the application-level sandbox that the CKS exam tests alongside Seccomp.</p>
