            <section class="section-block" id="s14-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.1 What Is AppArmor and Why Does It Matter for Containers?</h3>
                <p>AppArmor (Application Armor) is a <strong>Linux Security Module (LSM)</strong> — a kernel-level framework that enforces mandatory access control (MAC) on processes. Unlike discretionary access control (DAC), where the file owner sets permissions (chmod, chown), MAC is enforced by the kernel and <strong>cannot be overridden by the process owner</strong>. Even root cannot bypass an AppArmor profile in enforce mode. This makes AppArmor fundamentally different from and complementary to traditional Unix permissions.</p>

                <h4>The Linux Security Module Landscape — Where AppArmor Fits</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Security System</th><th>What It Controls</th><th>Level</th><th>Default On</th><th>Profile Location</th><th>CKS Chapter</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>DAC (Unix Permissions)</strong></td><td>File ownership and mode bits (rwx)</td><td>User-space</td><td>Always</td><td>File inode metadata</td><td>Ch 13</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(50,108,229,0.06);"><td><strong>AppArmor</strong></td><td>File paths, network, capabilities, mounts, signals</td><td>Kernel LSM</td><td>Ubuntu/Debian</td><td><code>/etc/apparmor.d/</code></td><td>Ch 14 (here)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>SELinux</strong></td><td>File labels, process contexts, type enforcement</td><td>Kernel LSM</td><td>RHEL/CentOS</td><td>Kernel policy store</td><td>Mentioned here</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Seccomp</strong></td><td>System calls (syscalls)</td><td>Kernel</td><td>Docker default</td><td>JSON in pod spec or node</td><td>Ch 15</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Kubernetes RBAC</strong></td><td>API server operations (get, create, delete pods, etc.)</td><td>K8s API</td><td>Configured per cluster</td><td>ClusterRole/RoleBinding YAML</td><td>Ch 9</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>AppArmor vs SELinux — The Two Linux MAC Systems</h4>
                <p>AppArmor and SELinux are the two major Mandatory Access Control systems in Linux. You need to understand both for the CKS exam because different cloud providers and distributions use different defaults:</p>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left" style="flex:1;">
                        <div class="info-box">
                            <h5>🛡️ AppArmor</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Default on:</strong> Ubuntu, Debian, OpenSUSE</li>
                                <li><strong>Model:</strong> Path-based — profiles reference filesystem paths (<code>/app/**</code>, <code>/etc/shadow</code>)</li>
                                <li><strong>Profiles:</strong> Simple text files in <code>/etc/apparmor.d/</code></li>
                                <li><strong>Learning curve:</strong> Low — paths are intuitive, auto-generation with aa-genprof</li>
                                <li><strong>Granularity:</strong> Per-application profiles attached to executables</li>
                                <li><strong>CKS exam:</strong> Uses AppArmor on Ubuntu nodes. You WILL encounter it.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel-right" style="flex:1;">
                        <div class="info-box">
                            <h5>🔒 SELinux</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Default on:</strong> RHEL, CentOS, Fedora, Amazon Linux</li>
                                <li><strong>Model:</strong> Label-based — every file, process, port, and socket gets a security context label</li>
                                <li><strong>Policies:</strong> Complex compiled policy modules — much harder to write manually</li>
                                <li><strong>Learning curve:</strong> High — labels are abstract, policy language is verbose</li>
                                <li><strong>Granularity:</strong> System-wide policy with type enforcement across all objects</li>
                                <li><strong>CKS exam:</strong> SELinux concepts may appear, but hands-on tasks use AppArmor on Ubuntu</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align:center; margin-top:12px; color:#8b949e; font-size:0.82rem;">The CKS exam uses <strong>Ubuntu nodes with AppArmor</strong>. If you see SELinux concepts in a question, the same principles apply — it's just a different syntax. Focus on AppArmor for the hands-on tasks.</p>

                <h4>AppArmor Concept — Before and After (The Security Badge Analogy)</h4>
                <p>Think of AppArmor like a <strong>security clearance badge</strong>. Without a badge, you can try to enter any room in the building. With a badge, the guard (the kernel) checks your badge against the room's access list before letting you through. Even if you're the CEO (root), if your badge doesn't list the nuclear reactor room, you don't get in.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">AppArmor — The Security Badge System at Kernel Level</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  📦 Container WITHOUT AppArmor (Unconfined)                              │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Django process (easypj) can access:                                     │
  │  ❌ /etc/shadow        → Read password hashes (credential theft)         │
  │  ❌ /proc/sysrq-trigger→ Trigger kernel emergency reboots                │
  │  ❌ /dev/sda           → Raw disk access (bypass all filesystem perms)   │
  │  ❌ /proc/1/ns/*       → Access init process namespaces (escape vector)  │
  │  ❌ Raw socket(AF_PACKET)→ Craft malicious network packets               │
  │  ❌ CAP_SYS_ADMIN      → Mount filesystems, load kernel modules          │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ANY exploit in Django/Python/postgres → attacker has FULL system access │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                   │
                    LOAD APPARMOR PROFILE: aa-enforce easypj-django
                                   │
                                   ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  📦 Container WITH AppArmor (Enforce) — Only what the badge allows       │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Django process (easypj) can ONLY:                                       │
  │  ✅ /app/** r          → Read its own application code (read-only!)      │
  │  ✅ /app/media/** rw   → Write uploaded files and generated static assets│
  │  ✅ /tmp/** rw         → Write temporary files (session data, uploads)   │
  │  ✅ network tcp        → Make TCP connections (postgres:5432, redis:6379)│
  │  ✅ /usr/bin/python3.* mr → Execute Python interpreter (mapped-read)     │
  │  🚫 EVERYTHING ELSE → DENIED + LOGGED by kernel BEFORE syscall completes │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Even a fully compromised Django process is TRAPPED in this sandbox.     │
  │  It cannot read /etc/shadow. It cannot touch /proc. It cannot escape.    │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How AppArmor Works at the Kernel Level — The Technical Flow</h4>
                <p>When a process inside a container tries to perform an operation — say, opening <code>/etc/shadow</code> for reading — the following chain of events occurs at kernel level. Understanding this flow is critical because it shows you exactly WHERE AppArmor blocks attacks and WHY it cannot be bypassed by the process:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kernel Access Check — What Happens When a Process Tries to Open a File</div>
<pre>
  PROCESS: open("/etc/shadow", O_RDONLY)
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  1. SYSCALL ENTRY — Process calls open() → triggers sys_open()   │
  │     in the Linux kernel. The CPU switches from user mode to      │
  │     kernel mode (ring 0).                                        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  2. DAC CHECK (Discretionary Access Control) — FIRST             │
  │     • Kernel checks the file's UID/GID against the process's     │
  │       effective UID/GID.                                         │
  │     • Checks the file's mode bits (rwx) for owner/group/other.   │
  │     • If the file is mode 000 and the process is not owner →     │
  │       EACCES (Permission denied) returned immediately.           │
  │     • /etc/shadow is mode 000, owned by root:shadow.             │
  │     • If the process is root (UID 0), DAC PASSES — root can      │
  │       read any file under DAC. ⚡ THIS IS WHY APPARMOR EXISTS.   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼  (DAC passed — process is root)
  │
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  3. LSM HOOK — The kernel calls the Linux Security Module hook   │
  │     • The kernel invokes security_file_open() — an LSM hook.     │
  │     • Every registered LSM gets a chance to veto the operation.  │
  │     • AppArmor checks: "Is this process confined by a profile?"  │
  │     • YES → AppArmor checks: "Does the profile allow reading     │
  │              /etc/shadow?"                                       │
  │     • The easypj-django profile has:                             │
  │       deny /etc/shadow r,     ← ⬅⬅⬅ THIS LINE BLOCKS IT         │
  │     • Result: EACCES (Permission denied)                         │
  │     • Kernel returns -EACCES to the process. The open() syscall  │
  │       fails. The file is never touched. The process gets a       │
  │       "Permission denied" error.                                 │
  │                                                                  │
  │  ⚡ KEY INSIGHT: AppArmor blocks at the LSM hook — AFTER DAC     │
  │     but BEFORE the file is actually opened. Root cannot bypass   │
  │     this because the LSM hook runs in kernel space (ring 0),     │
  │     and the process cannot disable or modify the profile from    │
  │     inside its own confinement.                                  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  4. AUDIT LOG — The denied access is logged                      │
  │     • kernel: audit: type=1400 audit(…): apparmor="DENIED"      │
  │       operation="open" profile="easypj-django"                   │
  │       name="/etc/shadow" pid=12345 comm="python"                 │
  │       requested_mask="r" denied_mask="r"                         │
  │     • This log entry appears in:                                 │
  │       - /var/log/syslog (Ubuntu)                                 │
  │       - /var/log/audit/audit.log (if auditd is running)          │
  │       - journalctl -k | grep apparmor                            │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  RESULT: The attacker's process gets "Permission denied."
          The kernel logged the attempt.
          The file was never read.
          The attack failed at step 3 of 4.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>What AppArmor CAN and CANNOT Protect Against</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🟢</div>
                        <h5>What AppArmor CAN Protect</h5>
                        <ul style="text-align:left; font-size:0.85rem;">
                            <li>✅ Unauthorized file reads (secrets, configs, /etc/shadow)</li>
                            <li>✅ Unauthorized file writes (backdoors, malware drops)</li>
                            <li>✅ Execution of unauthorized binaries (downloaded exploits)</li>
                            <li>✅ Network connections to unauthorized ports/hosts</li>
                            <li>✅ Linux capability abuse (CAP_SYS_ADMIN, CAP_NET_RAW)</li>
                            <li>✅ Mount operations (preventing container escape via mount)</li>
                            <li>✅ Signal sending (preventing process killing sprees)</li>
                            <li>✅ Ptrace attach (preventing memory scraping of other processes)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔴</div>
                        <h5>What AppArmor CANNOT Protect</h5>
                        <ul style="text-align:left; font-size:0.85rem;">
                            <li>❌ Kernel exploits that bypass LSMs (rare, high-severity CVEs)</li>
                            <li>❌ CPU/memory exhaustion (resource DoS — use cgroups instead)</li>
                            <li>❌ Network bandwidth abuse (use NetworkPolicy or CNI policy)</li>
                            <li>❌ Application logic flaws (SQL injection, XSS — AppArmor doesn't parse SQL)</li>
                            <li>❌ Data exfiltration via allowed channels (if the app can connect to postgres, it can exfiltrate data THROUGH postgres)</li>
                            <li>❌ Side-channel attacks (Spectre, Meltdown — these are hardware bugs)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p style="margin-top:16px;"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> AppArmor is a filesystem, capability, and network access control system. It is NOT a behavior analysis system. It won't detect that your Django app is suddenly SELECTing all rows from the users table and sending them to an unknown IP — if that IP is reachable via an allowed network rule. That detection is the job of runtime security tools like Falco (Chapter 29). AppArmor + Falco together provide defense-in-depth: AppArmor restricts what the process CAN do; Falco alerts on what it IS doing.</p>

                <h4>How AppArmor Confinement Survives Container Boundaries</h4>
                <p>One of the most powerful properties of AppArmor is that its profiles are <strong>namespace-agnostic</strong>. When a process is confined by an AppArmor profile, that confinement follows the process across namespace boundaries. This means that even if a process inside a container calls <code>unshare()</code> to create new namespaces, <code>setns()</code> to join existing ones, or <code>clone()</code> with the CLONE_NEWNS flag — the AppArmor profile remains attached. The kernel checks the profile at every LSM hook point regardless of which namespaces the process is in.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">AppArmor Profile Persistence Across Namespace Transitions</div>
<pre>
  PROCESS: easypj-django (PID 12345) confined by "easypj-django (enforce)"
  │
  ├─ In the container's MOUNT namespace: profile applies to /proc, /sys, /dev
  │  (these are the container's own mounts, not the host's)
  │
  ├─ Process calls unshare(CLONE_NEWNS) → creates a NEW mount namespace
  │  │  The process is NOW in a different mount namespace
  │  │  BUT: the AppArmor profile is still attached to PID 12345
  │  │  The kernel checks: "What profile confines PID 12345?"
  │  │  Answer: easypj-django (enforce) — SAME profile, SAME rules
  │  │
  │  └─ KEY INSIGHT: AppArmor is attached to the TASK STRUCT (kernel's
  │     representation of the process), not to any namespace. Namespaces
  │     can change, but the task struct's security context stays the same
  │     unless explicitly changed by a privileged operation.
  │
  ├─ Process calls setns() to join a different container's network namespace
  │  │  The process now sees different network interfaces
  │  │  BUT: profile still says "network tcp" only — can't create raw sockets
  │  │  even in the new namespace. Profile rules are namespace-transparent.
  │
  └─ ⚡ CONTRAST WITH SELINUX: SELinux labels are namespace-aware. A process
     in container A has a different SELinux context than container B. AppArmor
     is simpler: the profile is the profile, regardless of context.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>AppArmor vs SELinux — The Technical Architecture Difference That Matters</h4>
                <p>Many engineers treat AppArmor and SELinux as "the same thing, different distro." They are not. The fundamental architectural difference — path-based vs label-based — has profound implications for how you write, debug, and maintain security policies in production:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Architectural Aspect</th><th>AppArmor (Path-Based)</th><th>SELinux (Label-Based)</th><th>Why It Matters for K8s</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Identity Model</strong></td><td>"What executable are you?" Profiles match by filesystem path of the binary.</td><td>"What context label do you carry?" Every process, file, port has a type label.</td><td>In K8s containers, the executable path is known (/usr/bin/python). SELinux labels require RBAC integration — more complex.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>File Access Model</strong></td><td>"What path are you accessing?" Rules use filesystem paths.</td><td>"What label does the target have?" Rules match source type → target type.</td><td>AppArmor rule <code>/app/** r,</code> is immediately understandable. SELinux <code>allow httpd_t user_home_t:file read;</code> requires label knowledge.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Policy Development</strong></td><td>aa-genprof auto-generates from observed behavior. Edit text file, reload.</td><td>audit2allow converts denial logs into policy. Compile into module, load into kernel.</td><td>AppArmor: edit file → reload. SELinux: audit → convert → compile → semodule -i. AppArmor is faster for iteration.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Default Posture</strong></td><td>Allow what's listed, deny everything else (if in enforce mode). Unprofiled = unconfined.</td><td>Deny everything by default. Every interaction must be explicitly allowed.</td><td>SELinux is theoretically more secure (deny-by-default), but AppArmor's simplicity makes it more likely to be used correctly in practice.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>K8s State</strong></td><td>Ubuntu default. CKS exam focus. Annotation + securityContext.</td><td>RHEL default. Container-selinux policy. seLinuxOptions in securityContext.</td><td>CKS exam tests AppArmor hands-on. SELinux appears as theory or comparison. Know both, practice AppArmor.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p><strong>The path-based limitation you must understand:</strong> AppArmor identifies files by path. If an attacker creates a hard link from <code>/app/harmless.txt</code> to <code>/etc/shadow</code>, AppArmor evaluates the access against <code>/app/harmless.txt</code> — the path being opened — NOT <code>/etc/shadow</code> — the actual inode. However, the hard link can only be created if the profile allows writing to /app/ AND the filesystem is on the same partition, AND the attacker has permissions. This is why the profile has <code>/app/** r,</code> (read-only) — preventing writes that could be used to create such links. This is also why the explicit <code>deny /etc/shadow r,</code> rule exists: even if an attacker somehow opens /etc/shadow through an alias path, the explicit deny catches it.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 14.2 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.2 AppArmor Modes — Disabled, Complain, Enforce</h3>
                <p>AppArmor profiles can operate in <strong>three distinct modes</strong>, and choosing the wrong mode in the wrong context is the difference between "my application mysteriously broke" and "my application is secure." The CKS exam expects you to know exactly what each mode does, when to use it, and — most critically — how to <strong>switch between modes</strong> without disrupting running workloads.</p>

                <h4>The Three Modes — Visual Comparison</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Profile Loaded?</th><th>Violations Blocked?</th><th>Violations Logged?</th><th>Use Case</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(50,108,229,0.06);"><td><strong>Disabled / Unconfined</strong></td><td>❌ No profile</td><td>❌ Nothing blocked</td><td>❌ Nothing logged</td><td>Processes without a profile. The default for most containers.</td><td><code>aa-disable &lt;profile&gt;</code></td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(251,191,36,0.06);"><td><strong>Complain (Learning)</strong></td><td>✅ Profile loaded</td><td>❌ NOT blocked</td><td>✅ Everything logged</td><td>Profile development — let the app run normally, collect ALL access patterns, then refine the profile.</td><td><code>aa-complain &lt;profile&gt;</code></td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(76,175,80,0.06);"><td><strong>Enforce (Blocking)</strong></td><td>✅ Profile loaded</td><td>✅ ALL violations blocked</td><td>✅ Denials logged</td><td>Production — the profile blocks everything not explicitly allowed. This is the goal state.</td><td><code>aa-enforce &lt;profile&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>The AppArmor Profile Lifecycle — From Zero to Production</h4>
                <p>Creating an AppArmor profile is not a one-step process. It's a <strong>five-stage lifecycle</strong> that starts with automatic generation and ends with a production-hardened, enforce-mode profile applied to every pod. The CKS exam tests your understanding of this lifecycle — especially the transition from complain to enforce.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Complete AppArmor Profile Lifecycle — Zero to Production in 5 Stages</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                          │
  │  STAGE 1: GENERATE (auto-profile in complain mode)                      │
  │  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────                     │
  │  $ aa-genprof /usr/bin/python3                                          │
  │  → Starts profiling mode: launches the app, monitors ALL file,          │
  │    network, capability, and signal accesses the app makes.              │
  │  → As the app runs, aa-genprof records every access pattern.            │
  │  → When done (Ctrl+C or exit app), writes a draft profile to            │
  │    /etc/apparmor.d/usr.bin.python3 (auto-generated name).              │
  │  → The draft profile is LOADED in COMPLAIN mode automatically.          │
  │                                                                          │
  │  STAGE 2: TEST in COMPLAIN mode (log violations, don't block)           │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────              │
  │  $ aa-complain /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django                            │
  │  → Run the application through its NORMAL workflow: handle HTTP         │
  │    requests, connect to database, read/write files, serve static assets │
  │  → Meanwhile, monitor the logs for AppArmor events:                    │
  │    $ journalctl -k | grep apparmor | grep ALLOWED                       │
  │    (In complain mode, violations are tagged "ALLOWED" — meaning AppArmor│
  │     saw the access, would have blocked it in enforce mode, but let it   │
  │     through because we're in complain.)                                  │
  │  → These ALLOWED events are your TODO list: add them to the profile.    │
  │                                                                          │
  │  STAGE 3: REFINE (iterate on the profile based on logs)                 │
  │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────                     │
  │  Edit /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django and add:                            │
  │    • Paths that the app legitimately needs to access                    │
  │    • Network rules for database, cache, and API connections             │
  │    • Capabilities the app requires (rare — most apps need none)         │
  │  $ apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django  # Reload profile  │
  │  → Repeat STAGE 2-3 until no new ALLOWED events appear in logs.        │
  │                                                                          │
  │  STAGE 4: ENFORCE (block violations — production mode)                  │
  │  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────                │
  │  $ aa-enforce /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django                             │
  │  → Profile now blocks everything not explicitly allowed.                │
  │  → Monitor logs for DENIED events (these are actual blocks):           │
  │    $ journalctl -k | grep apparmor | grep DENIED                        │
  │  → If the app breaks, check DENIED events → the app needed something   │
  │    you didn't add to the profile. Go back to STAGE 2, add it.          │
  │                                                                          │
  │  STAGE 5: DEPLOY to Kubernetes (load on ALL nodes, apply to pods)      │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────       │
  │  • Copy profile to EVERY node: scp easypj-django node-0X:/etc/apparmor.d/│
  │  • Load on EVERY node: ssh node-0X apparmor_parser -a easypj-django     │
  │  • Apply to pod via annotation or securityContext (see 14.4)            │
  │  • Verify: kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- cat /proc/self/attr/current          │
  │    → Should show: easypj-django (enforce)                               │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Checking AppArmor Status — Essential Commands</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — AppArmor status, profile management, and log inspection</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── CHECK APPARMOR STATUS ──
aa-status
# Output shows:
#   apparmor module is loaded.
#   23 profiles are loaded.
#   18 profiles are in enforce mode.
#   5 profiles are in complain mode.
#   3 processes have profiles defined.

# ── LIST ALL LOADED PROFILES ──
aa-status | grep -E '^\s+\S+'
# Shows each profile name and its mode:
#   /usr/sbin/sshd (enforce)
#   easypj-django (complain)

# ── CHECK A SPECIFIC PROFILE ──
aa-status | grep easypj-django
# Output: easypj-django (enforce)

# ── LOAD A NEW PROFILE ──
apparmor_parser -a /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django
# -a = add/load the profile into the kernel

# ── RELOAD A MODIFIED PROFILE ──
apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django
# -r = replace the currently-loaded profile with the updated file

# ── UNLOAD A PROFILE ──
apparmor_parser -R /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django
# -R = remove the profile from kernel memory

# ── SWITCH A PROFILE TO COMPLAIN MODE ──
aa-complain /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django

# ── SWITCH A PROFILE TO ENFORCE MODE ──
aa-enforce /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django

# ── DISABLE A PROFILE (unconfine the process) ──
aa-disable /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django

# ── VIEW APPARMOR LOGS (violations) ──
journalctl -k | grep apparmor
# Look for:
#   apparmor="ALLOWED" → complain mode, would be blocked in enforce
#   apparmor="DENIED"  → enforce mode, actually blocked

# ── CHECK IF APPARMOR IS ENABLED IN THE KERNEL ──
cat /sys/module/apparmor/parameters/enabled
# Y = enabled, N = disabled

# ── CHECK A RUNNING PROCESS'S APPARMOR PROFILE ──
cat /proc/$(pgrep -f gunicorn)/attr/current
# Output: easypj-django (enforce)
# OR: unconfined (no profile)
</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The Complain-to-Enforce Transition — A Real-World Scenario</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🟡 SCENARIO: Deploying an AppArmor Profile for easypj Django</h5>
                    <p>You've generated a profile with aa-genprof. It's currently in complain mode. The Django app is running fine. The logs show 47 ALLOWED events over the past hour — all legitimate file accesses that Django, Gunicorn, or Python libraries need. You've added them all to the profile. Now it's time to switch to enforce mode.</p>
                    <p><strong>What you do:</strong></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li><code>aa-enforce /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django</code> — switch to blocking mode on one node first</li>
                        <li>Run <code>kubectl rollout restart deployment/easypj-django</code> — restart the pods on this node</li>
                        <li>Watch the logs: <code>journalctl -k -f | grep apparmor</code></li>
                        <li>If you see DENIED events → the app needed something you missed. Your options:
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>If it's a legitimate access:</strong> Add the path/capability to the profile, reload with <code>apparmor_parser -r</code>, switch back to enforce</li>
                                <li><strong>If it's NOT legitimate:</strong> The profile is working! The app should handle the Permission Denied error gracefully.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>If NO denied events after 30 minutes of normal traffic → profile is ready for production. Deploy to all nodes.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p><strong>Common mistake:</strong> Switching to enforce mode on ALL nodes at once. If you missed a required access, ALL pods break simultaneously. Always test on ONE node first.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What Actually Happens in the Kernel — Complain Mode vs Enforce Mode</h4>
                <p>The difference between complain and enforce is not just "log vs block." There is a specific kernel code path that diverges based on the profile mode. Understanding this divergence explains why complain mode is safe for testing and why enforce mode cannot be bypassed:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kernel Code Path — The Exact Difference Between Complain and Enforce</div>
<pre>
  PROCESS: open("/etc/shadow", O_RDONLY)
  │
  ▼
  DAC CHECK → passed (process is root)
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  LSM HOOK: security_file_open() — AppArmor's apparmor_file_open() runs  │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Step A: Look up the task's current profile from the task_struct        │
  │          → Found: "easypj-django" (profile pointer in kernel memory)    │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Step B: Walk the profile's file rules DFA (Deterministic Finite        │
  │          Automaton). AppArmor compiles path rules into a DFA at load     │
  │          time for O(1) path matching.                                    │
  │          → Match: "/etc/shadow" matches rule "deny /etc/shadow r,"      │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Step C: Check the PROFILE MODE (this is where it diverges):            │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌─ IF MODE == COMPLAIN ─────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │  • Profile says "deny /etc/shadow r," but mode is COMPLAIN.       │  │
  │  │  • AppArmor does NOT return -EACCES to the kernel.                │  │
  │  │  • Instead, it generates an audit message with "apparmor=ALLOWED" │  │
  │  │    (meaning: "I would have blocked this if I were in enforce").   │  │
  │  │  • Returns 0 (success) to the LSM hook.                           │  │
  │  │  • The kernel proceeds to actually open the file.                 │  │
  │  │  • RESULT: File opened SUCCESSFULLY. Log entry created.           │  │
  │  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌─ IF MODE == ENFORCE ──────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │  • Profile says "deny /etc/shadow r," and mode is ENFORCE.        │  │
  │  │  • AppArmor generates an audit message with "apparmor=DENIED"     │  │
  │  │    (meaning: "I blocked this access").                            │  │
  │  │  • Returns -EACCES (Permission denied) to the LSM hook.           │  │
  │  │  • The kernel sees the non-zero return and ABORTS the open().     │  │
  │  │  • The syscall returns -1 with errno = EACCES to the process.     │  │
  │  │  • RESULT: File NOT opened. Process gets "Permission denied."     │  │
  │  │  • The inode is NEVER touched. The file is NEVER read.            │  │
  │  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  CRITICAL INSIGHT FOR CKS:
  ─────────────────────────
  When you run aa-genprof or aa-complain, the profile is LOADED into the
  kernel with MODE=COMPLAIN. The DFA is built. The rule engine is active.
  But the return value from the LSM hook is always 0 (allow).

  When you run aa-enforce, the profile mode is TOGGLED to ENFORCE. The
  SAME DFA is used. The SAME rules are checked. The ONLY difference is
  that the return value is now -EACCES for denied operations.

  This is why the transition from complain to enforce is SAFE: the rules
  don't change. Only the enforcement behavior changes.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Profile Inheritance — How Child Processes Get Their Profiles</h4>
                <p>When a process with an AppArmor profile spawns a child process (via <code>fork()</code> + <code>exec()</code>), what profile does the child get? This is controlled by <strong>execute permissions</strong> in the parent's profile — specifically the difference between <code>ix</code>, <code>Px</code>, <code>Cx</code>, and <code>Ux</code>:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Execute Permission</th><th>Child Process Profile</th><th>When to Use</th><th>Risk Level</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>/usr/bin/python ix,</code></td><td><strong>Inherit</strong> — child gets the SAME profile as parent</td><td>Gunicorn spawning worker processes — all workers share the Django sandbox</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">🟢 Safe — same confinement</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>/usr/bin/postgres Px,</code></td><td><strong>Profile transition</strong> — child switches to its OWN profile (must exist)</td><td>Running a different application with different security needs</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">🟢 Safe — if profile exists</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>/usr/bin/helper Cx,</code></td><td><strong>Child profile</strong> — child gets a SUB-profile of the parent (parent//child naming)</td><td>Fine-grained sub-confinement for specific child processes</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">🟢 Safe — explicit sub-profile</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>/bin/bash Ux,</code></td><td><strong>Unconfined</strong> — child runs with NO profile at all</td><td>⚠️ Almost never appropriate for K8s containers</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">🔴 DANGEROUS — breaks containment</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p>For Kubernetes containers, the most common scenario is <code>ix</code> (inherit). Your Django container runs Gunicorn as PID 1. Gunicorn forks worker processes to handle HTTP requests. All workers inherit the same AppArmor profile — they're all confined identically. You don't need separate profiles for each Gunicorn worker. The <code>flags=(attach_disconnected)</code> setting in the profile header ensures that even if a process detaches from its parent (becomes a daemon), the AppArmor profile remains attached.</p>

                <h4>AppArmor Profile Workflow — From Zero to Enforce</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Complete AppArmor Profile Lifecycle — Generate → Test → Enforce → Verify</div>
<pre>
  STEP 1: GENERATE (auto-profile in complain mode)
  $ aa-genprof /usr/bin/python3
  → Runs app, records all file/network/cap accesses
  → Writes draft profile to /etc/apparmor.d/

  STEP 2: TEST in COMPLAIN mode (log violations, don't block)
  $ aa-complain /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django
  → Run easypj normally, check syslog for denied events
  $ journalctl -k | grep apparmor | grep ALLOWED
  → Add needed paths/permissions to the profile

  STEP 3: ENFORCE (block violations)
  $ aa-enforce /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django
  → Profile now active: violations → blocked + logged

  STEP 4: VERIFY profile loaded on all K8s nodes
  $ ssh node01 aa-status | grep easypj-django
  → Should show: easypj-django (enforce)

  STEP 5: APPLY to Kubernetes pod spec
  securityContext:
    appArmorProfile:
      type: Localhost
      localhostProfile: easypj-django

  🔑 CKS EXAM PATTERN: The exam tests whether you know the profile
     MUST exist on the node BEFORE the pod is created. If you create
     the pod first, it will be stuck in ContainerCreating because the
     container runtime cannot find the profile. Always: node first,
     then pod.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>AppArmor Modes Compared — When to Use Each</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Behaviour</th><th>Use Case</th><th>CKS Notes</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>disabled</strong></td><td>No AppArmor for this process. No restrictions whatsoever.</td><td>None — never use in production.</td><td>Some distributions ship with AppArmor disabled by default. Always check: <code>aa-status</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>complain</strong></td><td>Logs violations to syslog/journalctl. Does NOT block them.</td><td>Profile development: learn what your app actually accesses without breaking it.</td><td>Use <code>aa-genprof</code> to auto-generate, then test in complain mode for 24-48 hours.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>enforce</strong></td><td>Blocks ALL actions not explicitly allowed by the profile.</td><td>Production. Every pod should have an enforcing profile.</td><td>Once a profile passes complain testing, switch to enforce. Monitor for new denials after app updates.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 14.3 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.3 Writing AppArmor Profiles for easypj</h3>
                <p>An AppArmor profile is a plain text file that declares what a specific application is allowed to do. The syntax is straightforward — you specify <strong>file paths</strong> with permissions, <strong>network rules</strong> for protocols, and <strong>capability rules</strong> for Linux capabilities. Let's build profiles for the two critical containers in the easypj stack: the Django/Gunicorn application and the PostgreSQL database.</p>

                <h4>Profile Anatomy — Understanding Every Directive</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Directive</th><th>Syntax</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>#include</strong></td><td><code>#include &lt;file&gt;</code></td><td>Imports shared rules from another file (abstractions)</td><td><code>#include &lt;abstractions/base&gt;</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>File permission</strong></td><td><code>/path/** rwklm</code></td><td>Grants access to files matching the path glob</td><td><code>/app/** r,</code> (read-only for all files under /app)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Deny rule</strong></td><td><code>deny /path/** rw</code></td><td>Explicitly denies access — overrides broader allow rules</td><td><code>deny /etc/shadow r,</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Network rule</strong></td><td><code>network &lt;family&gt; &lt;type&gt;</code></td><td>Allows specific network protocol families</td><td><code>network tcp,</code> <code>network udp,</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Capability</strong></td><td><code>capability &lt;name&gt;,</code></td><td>Grants a specific Linux capability</td><td><code>capability net_bind_service,</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Mount rule</strong></td><td><code>mount,</code> or <code>deny mount,</code></td><td>Allows or denies filesystem mount operations</td><td><code>deny mount,</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Signal rule</strong></td><td><code>signal &lt;set&gt; &lt;sig&gt;,</code></td><td>Controls which signals a process can send</td><td><code>signal (receive) peer=unconfined,</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Ptrace rule</strong></td><td><code>ptrace,</code></td><td>Allows or denies process tracing (debugging)</td><td><code>deny ptrace,</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h5>File Permission Flags Explained</h5>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">r</div><h5>Read</h5><p>Allow reading file contents. Required for loading code, config files, libraries.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">w</div><h5>Write</h5><p>Allow writing/modifying files. Only grant to directories that actually need writes (media/, tmp/, logs/).</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">m</div><h5>Memory-Map</h5><p>Allow mmap() with PROT_EXEC. Required for loading shared libraries (.so files). Use <code>mr</code> for library dirs.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">k</div><h5>Lock</h5><p>Allow file locking (flock). Rarely needed for web apps. Usually safe to omit.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">l</div><h5>Link</h5><p>Allow creating hard links. Needed by package managers (pip, npm). Not needed at runtime.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">ix</div><h5>Inherit-Execute</h5><p>Execute the binary but keep the current profile. Use for child processes that should share the sandbox.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">Px</div><h5>Profile-Execute</h5><p>Execute the binary with its OWN profile. Use for child processes that need a different sandbox.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">Ux</div><h5>Unconfined-Execute</h5><p>Execute the binary with NO profile. ⚠️ Dangerous — avoid if possible.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>AppArmor Profile for easypj Django/Gunicorn</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">AppArmor Profile — /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# APPARMOR PROFILE: easypj-django
# Purpose: Sandbox the Django/Gunicorn application process
# Applies to: easypj Django container running Gunicorn
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

#include <tunables/global>

profile easypj-django flags=(attach_disconnected) {
  # ── INCLUDE SHARED ABSTRACTIONS ──
  #include <abstractions/base>
  #include <abstractions/python>
  #include <abstractions/ssl_certs>
  #include <abstractions/nameservice>     # DNS resolution

  # ── APPLICATION CODE (READ ONLY) ──
  /app/**                        r,      # Django source code, templates, static files
  /usr/lib/python3*/**           mr,     # Python standard library (need mmap for .so)
  /usr/local/lib/python3*/**     mr,     # pip-installed packages (Django, Gunicorn, etc.)

  # ── WRITABLE DIRECTORIES (only where app legitimately writes) ──
  /app/media/**                  rw,     # User-uploaded files (profile pics, documents)
  /app/staticfiles/**            rw,     # Collected static files (Django collectstatic)
  /tmp/**                        rw,     # Temporary files (session data, upload staging)

  # ── NETWORK (only the ports easypj actually uses) ──
  network tcp,                           # TCP for PostgreSQL, Redis, HTTP responses
  # Do NOT grant network udp unless the app uses UDP (Django doesn't)

  # ── EXPLICIT DENIALS (belt-and-suspenders) ──
  deny /etc/shadow               r,      # Password hashes — NEVER accessible
  deny /etc/kubernetes/**        r,      # K8s configs, kubeconfig, certs
  deny /proc/**                  rw,     # Kernel interfaces — escape vector
  deny /sys/**                   rw,     # Kernel sysfs — escape vector
  deny /dev/**                   rw,     # Devices — escape vector
  deny /root/**                  rw,     # Root home directory
  deny /var/log/**               w,      # Write to system logs (use stdout/stderr)
  deny mount,                            # Cannot mount filesystems (escape vector)
  deny ptrace,                           # Cannot attach debugger to other processes
  deny capability sys_admin,              # Cannot mount, swapon, etc.
  deny capability sys_ptrace,            # Cannot use ptrace at all
  deny capability net_raw,               # Cannot create raw sockets
  deny capability sys_module,            # Cannot load kernel modules
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>AppArmor Profile for PostgreSQL (easypj Database)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">AppArmor Profile — /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-postgres</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# APPARMOR PROFILE: easypj-postgres
# Purpose: Sandbox the PostgreSQL database process
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

#include <tunables/global>

profile easypj-postgres flags=(attach_disconnected) {
  #include <abstractions/base>
  #include <abstractions/nameservice>

  # ── POSTGRESQL BINARIES (execute only — read mapped) ──
  /usr/lib/postgresql/**/bin/postgres    mr,
  /usr/lib/postgresql/**/bin/*            mr,
  /usr/lib/postgresql/**/lib/**           mr,

  # ── DATA DIRECTORY (the only place postgres writes) ──
  /var/lib/postgresql/data/**            rw,
  /var/lib/postgresql/data/pg_wal/**     rw,
  /var/lib/postgresql/data/pg_log/**     rw,

  # ── TEMP AND CONFIG (minimal writes) ──
  /tmp/**                                 rw,
  /etc/postgresql/**                      r,
  /run/postgresql/**                      rw,

  # ── NETWORK (only PostgreSQL port) ──
  network tcp,                            # Listen on 5432, connect to replicas

  # ── REQUIRED CAPABILITIES ──
  capability ipc_lock,                    # Shared memory for connections
  capability sys_nice,                    # CPU priority adjustment

  # ── EXPLICIT DENIALS ──
  deny /etc/shadow               r,
  deny /etc/kubernetes/**        r,
  deny /proc/**                  rw,
  deny /sys/**                   rw,
  deny mount,
  deny ptrace,
  deny capability sys_admin,
  deny capability net_raw,
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Loading Profiles to Nodes and Applying to Pods</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Deploying AppArmor profiles to all nodes in the cluster</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# DEPLOY APPARMOR PROFILES to ALL K8s NODES
# Profiles MUST be on every node before pods can use them
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

PROFILES=("easypj-django" "easypj-postgres")
NODES=($(kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.addresses[?(@.type=="InternalIP")].address}'))

for NODE in "${NODES[@]}"; do
    echo "=== Deploying profiles to node: $NODE ==="

    # STEP 1: Copy profile files to the node
    for PROFILE in "${PROFILES[@]}"; do
        scp /path/to/profiles/${PROFILE} root@${NODE}:/etc/apparmor.d/${PROFILE}
    done

    # STEP 2: Load each profile into the kernel
    for PROFILE in "${PROFILES[@]}"; do
        ssh root@${NODE} "apparmor_parser -a /etc/apparmor.d/${PROFILE}"
    done

    # STEP 3: Set all profiles to enforce mode
    for PROFILE in "${PROFILES[@]}"; do
        ssh root@${NODE} "aa-enforce /etc/apparmor.d/${PROFILE}"
    done

    # STEP 4: Verify profiles are loaded and in enforce mode
    ssh root@${NODE} "aa-status | grep -E '${PROFILES[0]}|${PROFILES[1]}'"
done

echo "✅ All profiles deployed to all nodes"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Profile Abstractions — The Shared Building Blocks That Save You Hours</h4>
                <p>AppArmor <strong>abstractions</strong> are reusable, pre-written rule sets that you include in your profiles. Instead of writing 50 lines of rules to allow basic C library operations, DNS resolution, or SSL certificate access, you include one line like <code>#include &lt;abstractions/base&gt;</code>. The CKS exam expects you to know which abstractions exist and when to use them — you don't need to memorize their contents, but you should know what each one covers:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📚</div>
                        <h5><code>abstractions/base</code></h5>
                        <p>The foundation. Allows reading <code>/etc/ld.so.cache</code>, <code>/proc/filesystems</code>, <code>/proc/meminfo</code>, basic device access to <code>/dev/null</code>, <code>/dev/zero</code>, <code>/dev/random</code>, <code>/dev/urandom</code>, and terminal I/O. <strong>Every profile should include this.</strong> Without it, your app can't even read shared library caches.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🐍</div>
                        <h5><code>abstractions/python</code></h5>
                        <p>Allows Python to function: read <code>/usr/lib/python3*/**</code> with mr (mapped-read), execute <code>/usr/bin/python3*</code>, access Python bytecode cache directories, and use Python's ctypes FFI library. <strong>Always include for Django/Gunicorn/Flask containers.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5><code>abstractions/ssl_certs</code></h5>
                        <p>Allows reading TLS/SSL certificates from system trust stores: <code>/etc/ssl/certs/</code>, <code>/usr/share/ca-certificates/</code>, <code>/etc/ca-certificates.conf</code>. <strong>Include for any container that makes HTTPS connections</strong> (database TLS, external APIs, OAuth).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div>
                        <h5><code>abstractions/nameservice</code></h5>
                        <p>Enables DNS resolution: read <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code>, <code>/etc/nsswitch.conf</code>, <code>/etc/hosts</code>, connect to systemd-resolved or nscd sockets. <strong>Without this, your app cannot resolve DNS names.</strong> Pods that need to reach services by name MUST have this.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div>
                        <h5><code>abstractions/postgresql</code></h5>
                        <p>PostgreSQL-specific: access to data directory paths, shared memory, lock files. Use for PostgreSQL containers instead of writing all those rules manually.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div>
                        <h5>Custom Abstractions</h5>
                        <p>You can create your own abstractions in <code>/etc/apparmor.d/abstractions/</code>. Useful for organization-wide shared rules: "all our Django apps need these 10 paths" → one abstraction, included everywhere.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>To see what an abstraction actually contains: <code>cat /etc/apparmor.d/abstractions/base</code>. Each abstraction is just a text file with AppArmor rules — no magic. If an abstraction grants too much access for your security requirements, don't include it; write your own more restrictive rules instead.</p>

                <h4>Multi-Process Applications — How Gunicorn Workers, Celery Tasks, and Cron Jobs Stay Confined</h4>
                <p>Modern web applications are not single-process. Django under Gunicorn spawns multiple worker processes. Celery spawns task workers. Cron jobs spawn short-lived processes. Each of these must remain confined by the AppArmor profile. Here's how the mechanics work for each case:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Process Tree Confinement — Every Child Stays in the Sandbox</div>
<pre>
  PID 1: gunicorn (confined: easypj-django enforce)
  │
  ├─ PID 12: gunicorn worker (confined: easypj-django enforce) ── ix
  │    │  Gunicorn master forks workers with execve("/usr/bin/python", ...)
  │    │  Profile rule: /usr/bin/python3.* ix,  ← inherit profile
  │    │  Worker gets the SAME profile. Same restrictions apply.
  │    │
  │    ├─ HTTP request → opens /app/media/upload.jpg → ALLOWED (rule: /app/media/** rw)
  │    └─ HTTP request → opens /etc/shadow → DENIED (rule: deny /etc/shadow r)
  │
  ├─ PID 13: gunicorn worker (confined: easypj-django enforce) ── ix
  │
  ├─ PID 14: celery worker (confined: easypj-django enforce) ── ix
  │    │  Celery also runs under Python. Same binary, same path.
  │    │  Profile rule: /usr/bin/python3.* ix,  ← inherit profile
  │    │  KEY: If Celery needs to write to /var/run/celery/, that path MUST
  │    │  be in the profile or the worker will crash on startup.
  │
  └─ PID 15: cron (confined: easypj-django enforce) ── ix
       │  Cron runs manage.py commands. Same Python binary.
       │  BUT: cron jobs often need different permissions than web workers.
       │  Example: a cleanup cron job needs to delete old files from /app/media/
       │  If the profile only allows 'r' on /app/media/, the delete will fail.
       │
       │  SOLUTION: Use Cx (child profile) for processes that need different perms:
       │  /usr/bin/python3.* Cx -> easypj-django//cron
       │  profile easypj-django//cron { ... }  ← separate sub-profile for cron

  ⚡ THE attach_disconnected FLAG — CRITICAL FOR CONTAINERS:
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  In containers, PID 1 (the entrypoint) is the profile attachment point.
  When PID 1 forks a child that calls setsid() (becomes a session leader,
  common for daemonized processes), the child disconnects from the parent's
  process group. Without `flags=(attach_disconnected)`, AppArmor loses track
  of the child and it becomes UNCONFINED.

  With `flags=(attach_disconnected)`:
  • AppArmor follows the process through setsid() and daemon() calls
  • The kernel maintains the profile reference even after the process
    detaches from its parent's session
  • Every descendant of the original confined process stays confined

  This flag is ESSENTIAL for containers running Gunicorn (which daemonizes
  workers), Celery (which daemonizes task processors), or any multi-process
  Python application.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>AppArmor Troubleshooting — When Your Profile Breaks the App</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely Cause</th><th>Diagnosis Command</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Pod stuck in ContainerCreating</strong></td><td>AppArmor profile does not exist on the node the pod was scheduled to.</td><td><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code> → look for "cannot load profile" in Events. SSH to that node: <code>ls /etc/apparmor.d/</code> — is the profile file there?</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>App crashes with "Permission denied"</strong></td><td>Profile is in enforce mode and blocking a legitimate operation. The app needs more paths/permissions.</td><td>Switch to complain mode temporarily: <code>aa-complain /etc/apparmor.d/profile-name</code>. Check syslog: <code>journalctl -k | grep apparmor | grep DENIED</code>. Add the denied path to the profile.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>AppArmor not applied to pod</strong></td><td>Annotation typo or securityContext misconfiguration. The container runtime silently ignores unknown profiles.</td><td>Check pod annotations: <code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.metadata.annotations}'</code>. For securityContext: <code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].securityContext}'</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Profile works on node A but not node B</strong></td><td>Profile was only loaded on one node. AppArmor profiles must be present on EVERY node where the pod could run.</td><td>SSH to each worker: <code>aa-status | grep profile-name</code>. Use a DaemonSet to distribute profiles, or include them in your node provisioning (Terraform, Ansible).</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 14.4 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.4 Applying AppArmor in Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>Once your AppArmor profile is loaded on every node, you need to tell Kubernetes to <strong>attach that profile to your pod's containers</strong>. There are two ways to do this — the old annotation method (pre-1.30, still widely used and almost certainly on the CKS exam) and the new native securityContext method (1.30+). You must know BOTH.</p>

                <h4>Method Comparison — Annotation vs securityContext</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Annotation Method (pre-1.30)</th><th>securityContext Method (1.30+)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Where defined</strong></td><td><code>metadata.annotations</code> in pod spec</td><td><code>spec.containers[].securityContext</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Granularity</strong></td><td>Per container</td><td>Per container</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Annotation key</strong></td><td><code>container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/&lt;container-name&gt;</code></td><td>N/A — native field</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Value format</strong></td><td><code>localhost/&lt;profile-name&gt;</code> or <code>runtime/default</code> or <code>unconfined</code></td><td>Nested object: <code>type: Localhost</code>, <code>localhostProfile: &lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Status</strong></td><td>Beta (but stable in practice — on exam)</td><td>GA in 1.30</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CKS Exam</strong></td><td>✅ DEFINITELY tested — most clusters are pre-1.30</td><td>⚠️ May appear — know the syntax</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Method 1: Annotation-Based (Pre-1.30) — CKS Exam Primary</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — easypj Django deployment with AppArmor via annotation</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-django
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-django
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj-django
      annotations:
        # ⬅⬅⬅ APPARMOR VIA ANNOTATION (pre-1.30 method)
        # Format: container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/&lt;container-name&gt;: &lt;value&gt;
        # &lt;value&gt; can be:
        #   localhost/&lt;profile&gt;  → use a custom profile loaded on the node
        #   runtime/default      → use the container runtime's default AppArmor profile
        #   unconfined           → no AppArmor profile (INSECURE — avoid)
        container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/easypj: localhost/easypj-django
    spec:
      # ⚠️ IMPORTANT: The node must have the profile loaded at /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django
      # BEFORE this pod is scheduled. If the profile doesn't exist, the pod fails to start.
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: registry.easypj.com/easypj-django:v2.0.0
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000
        securityContext:
          runAsNonRoot: true
          runAsUser: 1000
          allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
          readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Method 2: Native securityContext (1.30+) — Future-Proof</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — easypj Django deployment with AppArmor via securityContext (1.30+)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-django
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-django
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj-django
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: registry.easypj.com/easypj-django:v2.0.0
        securityContext:
          runAsNonRoot: true
          runAsUser: 1000
          allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
          # ⬅⬅⬅ APPARMOR VIA securityContext (1.30+ native field)
          appArmorProfile:
            type: Localhost                # Localhost | RuntimeDefault | Unconfined
            localhostProfile: easypj-django # Profile name (must be loaded on node)
</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Verifying AppArmor Is Active in Running Pods</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Multiple methods to verify AppArmor profile is applied and working</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── METHOD 1: Check pod annotation (for annotation-based profiles) ──
kubectl get pod -n easypj -l app=easypj-django \
  -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.annotations}' | jq .
# Look for: "container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/easypj"
# Value should be: "localhost/easypj-django"

# ── METHOD 2: Check process's current AppArmor context INSIDE the container ──
kubectl exec -n easypj deploy/easypj-django -- cat /proc/self/attr/current
# Output: easypj-django (enforce)
# OR: unconfined (NO PROFILE — investigate immediately!)
# OR: easypj-django (complain) (profile loaded but NOT blocking)

# ── METHOD 3: Check from the NODE (more reliable) ──
NODE=$(kubectl get pod -n easypj -l app=easypj-django -o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.nodeName}')
ssh $NODE "aa-status | grep easypj-django"
# Output: easypj-django (enforce)

# ── METHOD 4: Check ALL pods' AppArmor status cluster-wide ──
kubectl get pods --all-namespaces -o json | jq -r '
  .items[] |
  select(.metadata.annotations."container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/" != null) |
  "\(.metadata.namespace)/\(.metadata.name): \(.metadata.annotations)"'

# ── METHOD 5: Verify AppArmor is actually BLOCKING violations ──
# On a node running the pod:
journalctl -k --since "5 minutes ago" | grep apparmor | grep DENIED
# If you see DENIED entries for easypj-django → profile is actively blocking
# If you see ALLOWED entries for easypj-django → profile is in COMPLAIN mode
# (violations logged but NOT blocked — switch to enforce!)
</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Common AppArmor Mistakes on the CKS Exam — And How to Avoid Them</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>Profile Not on All Nodes</h5>
                        <p>You loaded the profile on node-01, but the pod scheduled on node-02. <strong>Result:</strong> pod stays in ContainerCreating forever. <strong>Fix:</strong> Copy profile to ALL nodes before deploying the pod. Use a DaemonSet or node bootstrap script to ensure profiles are present on every node.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>Wrong Annotation Name</h5>
                        <p>You wrote <code>container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/easypj-django</code> but the container is named <code>easypj</code>. <strong>Result:</strong> AppArmor profile silently not applied. The container runs unconfined. <strong>Fix:</strong> The container name in the annotation must match <code>spec.containers[].name</code> exactly.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>Forgot to Switch to Enforce</h5>
                        <p>Profile loaded with <code>apparmor_parser -a</code>, but never ran <code>aa-enforce</code>. <strong>Result:</strong> Profile defaults to complain mode. Violations are logged but NOT blocked. You think you're protected, but you're not. <strong>Fix:</strong> Always check with <code>aa-status</code> — it shows the mode next to each profile name.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Troubleshooting AppArmor Denials — A Systematic Debugging Workflow</h4>
                <p>When you apply an AppArmor profile and your application breaks, don't panic. AppArmor denials are <strong>deterministic</strong> — the profile says exactly what's allowed, and the kernel enforces exactly that. There is no "sometimes it works." If an operation is denied, the profile doesn't allow it. The debugging workflow is systematic:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>CHECK THE MODE — Is the profile in enforce?</h5>
                        <p><code>aa-status | grep easypj-django</code><br>If it says <code>(complain)</code>, that's why denials aren't being logged as DENIED — switch to enforce. If it says <code>(enforce)</code>, continue to step 2.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>FIND THE DENIAL — What exactly was blocked?</h5>
                        <p><code>journalctl -k --since "5 min ago" | grep apparmor | grep DENIED</code><br>Each line shows: the profile name, the operation (open, mkdir, connect), the target path/port, and the PID. This is your exact TODO list.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>CLASSIFY THE DENIAL — Legitimate or malicious?</h5>
                        <p>Is the blocked path part of normal application behavior (e.g., Python reading a <code>.pyc</code> file) or is it suspicious (e.g., reading <code>/etc/shadow</code>)? Legitimate = add to profile. Suspicious = the profile is working — investigate the application.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>ADD THE RULE — Grant the needed access</h5>
                        <p>Edit <code>/etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django</code>, add the path/permission, reload: <code>apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django</code>. The reload is atomic — existing processes keep their current profile until their next exec(). Restart the pod to pick up the new profile.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>REPRODUCE AND VERIFY — The denial should disappear</h5>
                        <p>Trigger the same operation. Check <code>journalctl -k</code> again — the previously denied operation should now succeed silently (no audit entry = allowed). If it's still denied, check your path syntax — glob patterns can be tricky.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Common auditing gotcha:</strong> Not all AppArmor events go to <code>journalctl -k</code>. If <code>auditd</code> is installed and running, denials may go to <code>/var/log/audit/audit.log</code> instead. Check both. On systems without auditd, AppArmor uses the kernel's standard audit subsystem which routes through journald. The CKS exam environment typically uses journald — no auditd installed — so <code>journalctl -k</code> is your primary debugging tool.</p>

                <h4>What Happens When AppArmor Blocks Pod Startup — The ContainerCreating Nightmare</h4>
                <p>This is the most painful AppArmor failure mode — and the one the CKS exam loves to test. When you reference an AppArmor profile that doesn't exist on the node, or when the profile blocks the container's entrypoint from even starting, the pod gets stuck in <code>ContainerCreating</code> state. There is no obvious error message on <code>kubectl describe pod</code>. Here's exactly what happens and how to diagnose it:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">AppArmor Pod Startup Failure — The Complete Failure Chain</div>
<pre>
  kubectl apply -f easypj-deployment.yaml
  │
  ▼
  API SERVER: Pod created → assigned to node-02 → spec sent to kubelet
  │
  ▼
  KUBELET on node-02 receives the pod spec:
  │
  ├─ Reads annotation: container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/easypj:
  │  localhost/easypj-django
  │
  ├─ CHECKS: Does /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django exist on this node?
  │  │
  │  ├─ YES → Proceed to create container. Apply profile via runc/containerd.
  │  │
  │  └─ NO → ⚡ pod.status.phase = Pending
  │           pod.status.conditions[].reason = "AppArmorProfileNotFound"
  │           Event: "Warning  Failed  Cannot enforce AppArmor: profile
  │                   "easypj-django" is not loaded on the node"
  │           The kubelet RETRIES every few seconds. The pod stays in
  │           ContainerCreating FOREVER until you fix it.
  │
  ├─ ALTERNATIVE: Profile EXISTS on node and IS loaded, but entrypoint
  │  is BLOCKED by the profile.
  │  │
  │  └─ Container runtime (containerd/runc) calls execve() on entrypoint.
  │     AppArmor checks: is this execution allowed?
  │     If NOT allowed (missing Px/ix rule for the binary):
  │     → Container starts, entrypoint gets EACCES
  │     → Entrypoint crashes immediately (exit code 126 or 1)
  │     → Container enters CrashLoopBackOff
  │     → kubectl describe pod shows: "Error: container create failed:
  │       cannot enforce AppArmor profile"
  │     → kubectl logs shows: "Permission denied" on the entrypoint binary
  │
  └─ DIAGNOSIS CHECKLIST:
     1. kubectl describe pod → look for events mentioning "AppArmor"
     2. kubectl get events -n easypj --field-selector involvedObject.name=&lt;pod&gt;
     3. ssh to the node: aa-status | grep easypj-django  (profile loaded?)
     4. ssh to the node: cat /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django  (profile exists?)
     5. If profile loaded but pod failing: check the entrypoint binary path
        against the profile's execution rules.
</pre>
                </div>
                <p><strong>The exam trap:</strong> You'll be asked to apply an AppArmor profile to a pod. You create the pod, and it stays in ContainerCreating. You check everything — the YAML is correct, the annotation is correct. But you forgot to copy the profile to all nodes. The pod scheduled on node-03, and you only loaded the profile on node-01. The fix: copy the profile to ALL nodes, or use <code>nodeSelector</code> to pin the pod to the node that has the profile. For the exam, copying to all nodes is the expected answer.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 14.5 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.5 Official Node-Loading Procedure — the Steps Between "Write" and "Use"</h3>
                <p>Straight from the official Kubernetes documentation (<code>kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/security/apparmor</code>): <strong>the Kubernetes scheduler is NOT AppArmor-aware.</strong> Writing a profile and referencing it in a pod spec — everything covered in 14.3 and 14.4 — is only half the job. The profile must ALSO be loaded into the kernel on EVERY node the pod might land on, BEFORE the pod is scheduled there, or the kubelet rejects the pod with <code>apparmor profile not found</code>. This section is the missing link between "I wrote a profile" and "my pod is actually confined by it."</p>

                <h4>The Three Facts That Make or Break a Node-Loading Rollout</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛠️</div>
                        <h5>Load Method</h5>
                        <p><code>apparmor_parser</code> — not <code>kubectl</code>, not any Kubernetes API call. This is a <strong>node-level Linux operation</strong>, typically run via a DaemonSet or a configuration-management tool (Ansible, cloud-init), because it must execute on every single node.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div>
                        <h5>Persistent Storage Convention</h5>
                        <p><code>/etc/apparmor.d/</code> on the node filesystem. This is the same standard directory you already used in 14.3 — it's what makes the profile survive reboots and lets the node's AppArmor tooling find it automatically.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>Prerequisite Check</h5>
                        <p>The AppArmor kernel module must be enabled on the node. Most CKS exam clusters run Ubuntu, where it's on by default — but <strong>always verify</strong> rather than assume it's there.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The Full Node-Loading Sequence — Step 0 Through Step 5</h4>
                <p>This is the exact sequence the official docs describe, and the one the CKS exam expects you to execute without hesitation. Steps 0-2 happen ON THE NODE, before the pod ever exists. Steps 3-5 verify the chain end to end, from kernel to running container:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Official AppArmor Node-Loading Sequence — Write → Load → Verify → Use → Confirm</div>
<pre>
  STEP 0 — PREREQUISITE: Is AppArmor even enabled on this node?
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  $ cat /sys/module/apparmor/parameters/enabled
  → must print: Y
  ⚠️  If it prints N (or the file doesn't exist), STOP — nothing below will work.

  │
  ▼
  STEP 1 — WRITE the profile to /etc/apparmor.d/ (persists across reboots)
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django:
    #include &lt;tunables/global&gt;
    profile easypj-django flags=(attach_disconnected) {
      #include &lt;abstractions/base&gt;
      file,
      deny /etc/**       w,     ← block writes to /etc
      deny /bin/**       w,     ← block writes to /bin
    }

  │
  ▼
  STEP 2 — LOAD it into the kernel (run on EVERY node — DaemonSet/Ansible)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  $ sudo apparmor_parser -q /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django
  ⚠️  Must run on ALL nodes the pod could ever schedule to — not just one.

  │
  ▼
  STEP 3 — VERIFY it's loaded and active
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  $ cat /sys/kernel/security/apparmor/profiles | sort
  → should list: easypj-django (enforce)

  │
  ▼
  STEP 4 — NOW reference it in the pod spec (14.4) and schedule the pod
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  securityContext:
    appArmorProfile:
      type: Localhost
      localhostProfile: easypj-django

  │
  ▼
  STEP 5 — VERIFY the RUNNING container actually has it applied
  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  $ kubectl exec easypj -- cat /proc/1/attr/current
  → should print: easypj-django (enforce)
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The Profile File Referenced in Step 1</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">AppArmor Profile — /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django (official docs example)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-text">#include &lt;tunables/global&gt;

profile easypj-django flags=(attach_disconnected) {
  #include &lt;abstractions/base&gt;

  file,
  deny /etc/**       w,     # block writes to /etc
  deny /bin/**       w,     # block writes to /bin
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Verification Commands for Steps 0, 3, and 5</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — the exact verification commands from the official node-loading procedure</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># STEP 0 — Prerequisite: is the AppArmor kernel module enabled on this node?
cat /sys/module/apparmor/parameters/enabled
# Y = enabled, proceed. N = STOP, nothing below will work.

# STEP 2 — Load the profile into the kernel (run on EVERY node)
sudo apparmor_parser -q /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django

# STEP 3 — Verify the profile is loaded and active in the kernel
cat /sys/kernel/security/apparmor/profiles | sort
# Expect: easypj-django (enforce)

# STEP 5 — Verify the RUNNING container actually has the profile applied
kubectl exec easypj -- cat /proc/1/attr/current
# Expect: easypj-django (enforce)</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>⚠️ The #1 Real-World AppArmor Failure — Skipping Steps 0-2 on Even ONE Node</h5>
                    <p>Skipping steps 0-2 on even ONE node is the single most common real-world AppArmor failure. The pod schedules fine on nodes WITH the profile loaded — everything looks perfect in testing. Then the scheduler places a replica on a node that never had <code>apparmor_parser</code> run, and that pod gets stuck <code>Pending</code> or <code>CreateContainerError</code> the moment it lands there. There is no partial credit here: <strong>a profile that's loaded on 9 out of 10 nodes is a profile that WILL eventually fail</strong> — the day the scheduler picks that 10th node. Always load on ALL nodes. No exceptions, no "I'll get to it later."</p>
                </div>

                <p style="text-align:center; margin-top:12px; color:#8b949e; font-size:0.82rem;">This is exactly why 14.3's deployment script loops over <code>kubectl get nodes</code> to <code>scp</code> and <code>ssh apparmor_parser -a</code> on every single node — and why a DaemonSet is the production-grade answer to "how do I guarantee this runs on every node, including new ones that join later?"</p>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>🔧 Under the Hood — AppArmor Profile Syntax Deep Dive: File Rules, Capability Rules, Network Rules — Complete Annotated Reference</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                <h5>🗺️ Every Rule Type in an AppArmor Profile — Annotated</h5>
                <pre class="uth-diagram">
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   APPARMOR PROFILE ANATOMY — Every Rule Type Explained                     │
  │                                                                           │
  │  profile easypj-apparmor flags=(attach_disconnected,mediate_deleted) {   │
  │    #include &lt;abstractions/base&gt;    ← Basic filesystem + network access   │
  │                                                                           │
  │    # FILE RULES: r=read, w=write, m=memory-map, x=execute, ix=inherit    │
  │    /app/**              r,        ← Read all app files (recursive)        │
  │    /tmp/**              rw,       ← Read+Write temp files                 │
  │    /usr/bin/python3     rix,      ← Execute Python (inherit profile)      │
  │    deny /etc/shadow     r,        ← EXPLICITLY DENY reading shadow file   │
  │                                                                           │
  │    # CAPABILITY RULES: grant specific Linux capabilities                  │
  │    capability setuid,              ← Allow setuid syscall                 │
  │    capability dac_override,        ← Allow overriding file permissions    │
  │    # All OTHER capabilities are IMPLICITLY DENIED                          │
  │                                                                           │
  │    # NETWORK RULES: control protocol access                               │
  │    network inet tcp,               ← Allow TCP (HTTP, DB connections)     │
  │    network inet udp,               ← Allow UDP (DNS)                      │
  │    # All OTHER protocols are IMPLICITLY DENIED                             │
  │  }                                                                        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                </pre>
