            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>AppArmor is a kernel-level LSM, not a userspace convention</strong> — it enforces mandatory access control AFTER DAC passes, at the LSM hook, which is why even a compromised root process inside a container cannot bypass an enforce-mode profile (14.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Three modes form a lifecycle, not three unrelated settings</strong> — complain mode loads the SAME rule engine as enforce but always returns "allow" (safe for learning); enforce mode returns the real denial. Switching modes changes zero rules, only the enforcement behavior (14.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Profiles combine reusable abstractions with explicit deny rules</strong> — <code>#include &lt;abstractions/base|python|ssl_certs|nameservice&gt;</code> covers the common case, while explicit <code>deny /etc/shadow r,</code>-style rules are belt-and-suspenders even against path-alias tricks like hard links (14.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Kubernetes has two attachment mechanisms and one silent-failure trap</strong> — the pre-1.30 annotation and the 1.30+ <code>securityContext.appArmorProfile</code> field both require the container name to match <code>spec.containers[].name</code> exactly, or the profile silently never applies (14.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>The scheduler does not know or care about AppArmor</strong> — writing and referencing a profile is only half the job; <code>apparmor_parser</code> must load it into <code>/etc/apparmor.d/</code> on EVERY node BEFORE a pod can schedule there, or the kubelet rejects the pod outright (14.5).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 14 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Profile file must be loadable:</strong> AppArmor annotations reference profiles by name, but the profile must first be loaded into the kernel with <code>apparmor_parser</code>. If the profile isn't loaded, the pod fails to start with a "cannot enforce AppArmor" error.</li>
                    <li><strong>Path-based MAC means paths matter:</strong> AppArmor profiles specify file paths. If your container has a different filesystem layout than expected (e.g., the binary is at <code>/usr/local/bin/app</code> instead of <code>/usr/bin/app</code>), the profile won't match. Test in complain mode first.</li>
                    <li><strong>Not all nodes have AppArmor:</strong> AppArmor is default on Ubuntu/Debian. RHEL/CentOS use SELinux instead. On the CKS exam, nodes run Ubuntu — so AppArmor works. If the pod is scheduled to a node without AppArmor, it fails.</li>
                    <li><strong>The "works on 9/10 nodes" trap:</strong> loading a profile on some nodes but not others means the pod runs fine — right up until the scheduler picks the one node that never had <code>apparmor_parser</code> run. That replica gets stuck Pending/CreateContainerError. Deploy profiles via a DaemonSet or a loop over every node from <code>kubectl get nodes</code> — never by hand on "just the node I'm testing on" (14.5).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>aa-status | grep &lt;profile&gt;</code> — the fastest single command to check both "is it loaded" AND "which mode" at once.</li>
                    <li><code>aa-complain &lt;profile&gt;</code> to switch to log-only mode for testing; <code>aa-enforce &lt;profile&gt;</code> to switch to blocking. No reload needed — it's the same DFA, just a different return value.</li>
                    <li><code>journalctl -k | grep apparmor | grep DENIED</code> — your exact TODO list the moment enforce mode breaks something.</li>
                    <li>Before troubleshooting anything else on a "profile not applying" ticket, check <code>cat /sys/module/apparmor/parameters/enabled</code> on the node first (14.5) — a disabled kernel module wastes far more time than a wrong annotation.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 14)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">practice until automatic</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Prerequisite + load a profile onto a node (EXAM PATTERN — 14.5)</span>
cat /sys/module/apparmor/parameters/enabled  <span class="comment"># Y = AppArmor kernel module enabled</span>
sudo apparmor_parser -q /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django  <span class="comment"># Load into the kernel (run on EVERY node)</span>
cat /sys/kernel/security/apparmor/profiles | sort  <span class="comment"># Verify it's loaded + its mode</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Check status and switch modes</span>
aa-status | grep easypj-django  <span class="comment"># Loaded? Which mode?</span>
aa-complain /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django  <span class="comment"># Switch to log-only (testing)</span>
aa-enforce /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django   <span class="comment"># Switch to blocking (production)</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Apply AppArmor to a pod</span>
<span class="comment"># Annotation: container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/&lt;container&gt;: localhost/&lt;profile&gt;</span>
kubectl run test --image=nginx --annotations="container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/nginx=localhost/custom-profile"

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify the RUNNING container actually has the profile applied</span>
kubectl exec easypj -- cat /proc/1/attr/current  <span class="comment"># Expect: easypj-django (enforce)</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Test AppArmor by checking for a violation</span>
journalctl -k | grep apparmor | grep DENIED   <span class="comment"># Actual blocks (enforce mode)</span>
journalctl -k | grep apparmor | grep ALLOWED  <span class="comment"># Would-be blocks (complain mode)</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 14 — Lab Q&amp;A (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">11 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write an AppArmor profile that allows a container to read /app/**, write to /tmp/**, and use TCP networking. Deny everything else.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-text">#include &lt;tunables/global&gt;
profile easypj flags=(attach_disconnected,mediate_deleted) {
  #include &lt;abstractions/base&gt;
  /app/** r,
  /tmp/** rw,
  network inet tcp,
  /usr/bin/python3 rix,
  /opt/venv/bin/** rix,
  /** r,
}</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you apply an AppArmor profile to a running container? What's the difference between the annotation method and the securityContext method?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Annotation (older, deprecated in v1.30+):</strong> <code>container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/&lt;container&gt;: localhost/&lt;profile&gt;</code>. <strong>securityContext (newer, preferred):</strong> <code>securityContext.appArmorProfile.type: Localhost, localhostProfile: &lt;name&gt;</code>. The securityContext method is type-safe and validated by the API server — use this on the CKS exam if the cluster version supports it.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After loading an AppArmor profile, how do you verify it's actually enforced on a running pod?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- cat /proc/self/attr/current
# Should output: easypj-apparmor (enforce)

ssh node01 aa-status | grep easypj
# Shows: easypj-apparmor (enforce) — with process count</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between AppArmor complain mode and enforce mode? When would you use complain mode?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Enforce:</strong> Violations are BLOCKED and logged. <strong>Complain:</strong> Violations are only LOGGED (not blocked). Use complain mode when testing a new profile to see what would be blocked without actually breaking the application. Check logs: <code>dmesg | grep apparmor</code> or <code>aa-logprof</code>. Switch: <code>aa-complain &lt;profile&gt;</code> → <code>aa-enforce &lt;profile&gt;</code>.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write an AppArmor profile that denies a container access to <code>/etc/kubernetes/</code>, <code>/proc/sysrq-trigger</code>, and <code>/sys/kernel/</code>. Why are these paths dangerous?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-text">profile k8s-hardened flags=(attach_disconnected) {
  #include &lt;abstractions/base&gt;
  deny /etc/kubernetes/** rwxlkm,
  deny /proc/sysrq-trigger rw,
  deny /sys/kernel/** rw,
  /app/** r,
  /tmp/** rw,
}</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>Danger:</strong> <code>/etc/kubernetes/</code> contains PKI certs and kubeconfig — reading these enables API server impersonation. <code>/proc/sysrq-trigger</code> allows emergency reboot/shutdown commands. <code>/sys/kernel/</code> exposes kernel parameters that can disable security features.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>AppArmor denies take priority over allows. These <code>deny</code> rules form a safety net: even if the container somehow gains root and hostPath access to <code>/etc/kubernetes/</code>, AppArmor blocks the read. This is defense in depth — AppArmor is the last gate before the kernel.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod with an AppArmor profile in enforce mode is crashing. Logs show no AppArmor denials. What debugging steps do you take?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(1) Switch the profile to complain mode: <code>aa-complain &lt;profile&gt;</code>. (2) Recreate the pod and let it run. (3) Check AppArmor logs: <code>dmesg | grep -i apparmor | grep -i audit</code>. (4) Use <code>aa-logprof</code> to analyze logs and suggest profile additions. (5) If the pod still crashes with no AppArmor denials, the issue is NOT AppArmor — check pod events: <code>kubectl describe pod</code>, check if the profile is loaded: <code>aa-status | grep &lt;profile&gt;</code>, verify the annotation is correct.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Complain mode is the AppArmor debugging tool. It logs all would-be denials without blocking them, letting you see the complete picture of what the container needs. <code>aa-logprof</code> is interactive and will suggest new rules. Always test profiles in complain mode before enforcing them.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">AppArmor vs SELinux — what's the practical difference for CKS? Which one runs on the exam, and how do you check which MAC system is active?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>AppArmor:</strong> Path-based MAC. Profiles are simple text files referencing file paths. Default on Ubuntu/Debian. The CKS exam uses Ubuntu nodes → AppArmor is the tested MAC system. <strong>SELinux:</strong> Label-based MAC. Every file and process has a label. Default on RHEL/CentOS. More complex but more granular than AppArmor.</p>
                    <p><strong>Check active MAC:</strong> <code>aa-status</code> (AppArmor) or <code>getenforce</code> (SELinux). If <code>aa-status</code> returns "apparmor module is loaded" → AppArmor. If <code>getenforce</code> returns "Enforcing" → SELinux.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The CKS exam explicitly tests AppArmor, not SELinux, because the exam environment uses Ubuntu. Know how to: (1) write and load AppArmor profiles, (2) apply them to pods via annotation or securityContext, (3) debug with complain mode and aa-logprof. If you encounter a RHEL-based question, it will specify SELinux explicitly.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you make AppArmor profiles survive a node reboot? Where are profiles stored, and what command ensures they persist?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>AppArmor profiles are stored in <code>/etc/apparmor.d/</code>. They are automatically loaded at boot by the <code>apparmor</code> systemd service which reads all profiles from this directory. No additional persistence configuration is needed — simply place your profile file in <code>/etc/apparmor.d/</code> (e.g., <code>/etc/apparmor.d/k8s-easypj</code>) and it will be loaded on next boot.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># To load immediately without rebooting:
apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/k8s-easypj
# Verify persistence:
aa-status | grep k8s-easypj  # Shows loaded profiles
# After reboot, check again — profile should still be loaded</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>On the CKS exam, you may need to create a profile and ensure it survives a reboot. The key: profiles in <code>/etc/apparmor.d/</code> are auto-loaded. If you create a profile elsewhere and load it with <code>apparmor_parser</code>, it won't survive reboot unless you copy it to <code>/etc/apparmor.d/</code>. Always place production profiles in the standard directory.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the pod securityContext (not annotation) that applies an AppArmor profile named "easypj-restricted" to container "app". What Kubernetes version is required?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: secured-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx
    securityContext:
      appArmorProfile:
        type: Localhost
        localhostProfile: easypj-restricted</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>Requires Kubernetes 1.30+</strong> (GA in 1.30). Before 1.30, use the annotation method. The securityContext approach is preferred because: (1) it's validated by the API server, (2) it's part of the PodSecurity standards, (3) it's visible in <code>kubectl describe pod</code>.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The securityContext field for AppArmor graduated to GA in Kubernetes 1.30. On the CKS exam, if the cluster is 1.30+, use this method. For older clusters, use the annotation: <code>container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/app: localhost/easypj-restricted</code>. The exam may test both methods depending on the cluster version provided.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An attacker compromises a container and runs <code>cat /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt</code> via a hostPath mount. AppArmor is in complain mode. What do you see in the logs, and why is complain mode NOT sufficient for production?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>In complain mode, the access SUCCEEDS but is logged. You'd see in <code>dmesg</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text">audit: type=1400 apparmor="ALLOWED" operation="open" profile="k8s-easypj"
name="/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt" pid=12345 comm="cat"
requested_mask="r" denied_mask="r"</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>Why complain mode is insufficient:</strong> The attacker successfully reads the CA certificate. They can now use it to forge client certificates or validate stolen etcd certs. Complain mode LOGS attacks but doesn't STOP them. Production MUST use enforce mode. Complain mode is for development and testing ONLY.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This scenario illustrates the critical difference between detection and prevention. Complain mode is detection-only — useful for building profiles but useless for stopping attacks. The CKS exam expects you to: (1) use complain mode to develop and test profiles, (2) switch to enforce mode BEFORE considering a node production-ready. Never leave production profiles in complain mode.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You've written and loaded an AppArmor profile on node-01 only, then set it to enforce. A Deployment schedules 3 replicas across a 5-node cluster. What happens, and what's the correct fix per the official node-loading procedure (14.5)?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The replica(s) that land on node-01 start normally, fully confined by the profile. Any replica the scheduler places on one of the other 4 nodes — which never had <code>apparmor_parser</code> run — gets stuck <code>Pending</code>/<code>ContainerCreating</code>/<code>CreateContainerError</code>, because the kubelet checks locally for the profile and finds nothing. The Kubernetes scheduler has no concept of "which nodes have this AppArmor profile loaded" — it isn't AppArmor-aware at all, so it schedules pods anywhere.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Fix: run steps 0-2 on ALL 5 nodes, not just node-01
for NODE in $(kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.addresses[?(@.type=="InternalIP")].address}'); do
  ssh $NODE "cat /sys/module/apparmor/parameters/enabled"          # Step 0
  scp easypj-django root@$NODE:/etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django       # Step 1
  ssh $NODE "apparmor_parser -q /etc/apparmor.d/easypj-django"     # Step 2
  ssh $NODE "cat /sys/kernel/security/apparmor/profiles | sort"    # Step 3 verify
done</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the single most common AppArmor failure in real clusters — see the danger box in 14.5. Production-grade rollouts use a DaemonSet (so new nodes that join later are covered automatically) instead of a one-time loop, but for the exam, a loop over <code>kubectl get nodes</code> that copies and loads the profile on every node is the expected, exam-graded answer.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

