        <article class="chapter" id="ch15">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 15 · Seccomp — Filtering System Calls</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Every operation a container performs — reading a file, opening a network socket, spawning a child process — requires a <strong>system call (syscall)</strong> to the Linux kernel. There are over 300 syscalls in the Linux kernel. A typical Django application needs about 40-60 of them. The other 250+ — including <code>mount</code>, <code>ptrace</code>, <code>unshare</code>, <code>kexec_load</code> — are the syscalls that attackers use to escape containers, escalate privileges, and load kernel modules. <strong>Seccomp (Secure Computing Mode)</strong> is the Linux kernel feature that lets you filter syscalls — allowing only the ~60 your application needs and blocking the 250+ that are pure attack surface. Unlike AppArmor (which controls files, networks, and capabilities), Seccomp operates at the lowest possible level — the syscall boundary between user space and kernel space. This chapter teaches you to write, deploy, and verify Seccomp profiles that reduce the kernel attack surface of your easypj containers by over 80%.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🔍 Syscall Filtering</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🐧 Kernel Security</span>
            </div>
            </div><!-- /chapter-intro -->

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ What Seccomp is at the kernel level — how it differs from AppArmor, SELinux, and RBAC</li>
                    <li>✅ The three Seccomp profile types — RuntimeDefault, Localhost (custom), and Unconfined</li>
                    <li>✅ How to read and write Seccomp JSON profiles — the defaultAction and syscall action model</li>
                    <li>✅ How to create custom Seccomp profiles for the easypj Django and PostgreSQL containers</li>
                    <li>✅ How to deploy Seccomp profiles to nodes and apply them in pod securityContext</li>
                    <li>✅ How AppArmor and Seccomp differ — and when to use which (critical CKS exam knowledge)</li>
                    <li>✅ The exact on-disk seccomp path structure (including the often-missed <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory) and the precise <code>SCMP_ACT_</code> JSON syntax the runtime actually requires — plus the hard privileged/seccomp mutual-exclusivity constraint, straight from kubernetes.io</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKS Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">System Hardening <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></span>
