            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🧠 Seccomp in the CKS Exam — What They Actually Test</h5>
                <p>The CKS exam tests Seccomp in a focused, practical way. You need to know:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Apply RuntimeDefault to a pod:</strong> The simplest Seccomp task — add <code>seccompProfile: type: RuntimeDefault</code> to a pod's securityContext. Every pod should have this by default.</li>
                    <li><strong>Create and apply a custom Localhost profile:</strong> Write a JSON file, copy it to <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code> on every node, reference it in the pod spec.</li>
                    <li><strong>Verify the profile is active:</strong> Check <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/status</code> for <code>Seccomp: 2</code> (filter mode) or <code>0</code> (disabled).</li>
                    <li><strong>CRITICAL EXAM FACT:</strong> Custom Seccomp profiles must be in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code> on the node — NOT <code>/etc/seccomp/</code> or anywhere else. The CKS exam WILL test that you know the correct directory. (§15.5 has the precise on-disk layout, including a subdirectory this box simplifies away.)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP (lightweight) ─────────── -->
            <h4>🧭 Section Map — Chapter 15</h4>
            <p>This chapter moves from the concept (what Seccomp is and why it exists) to the mechanics (profile types, applying them in pod specs) to the comparison every exam question implicitly assumes you know (AppArmor vs Seccomp) to the exact, fact-checked details most guides simplify away — each section builds on the last.</p>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Answers</th><th>Takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s15-1"><strong>15.1</strong> What Is Seccomp and Why Does It Protect Containers?</a></td><td>What is Seccomp at the kernel level, and where does it sit relative to other controls?</td><td>Seccomp is a BPF-based syscall firewall — the last checkpoint before the kernel executes an operation, running after AppArmor, DAC, and capability checks.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s15-2"><strong>15.2</strong> Seccomp Profiles — RuntimeDefault and Custom</a></td><td>Which of the three profile types do I pick?</td><td>Unconfined blocks nothing, RuntimeDefault blocks ~44 syscalls (baseline), Localhost/custom can block 200+ (production hardening).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s15-3"><strong>15.3</strong> Applying Seccomp in Kubernetes Pod Specs</a></td><td>How do I actually wire a profile into a running pod, and verify it?</td><td><code>securityContext.seccompProfile</code>, deploy the JSON to every node BEFORE the pod schedules, verify with <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/status</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s15-4"><strong>15.4</strong> AppArmor vs Seccomp — Know the Difference</a></td><td>How is this different from AppArmor, and do I need both?</td><td>AppArmor controls paths/network/capabilities; Seccomp controls syscalls; AppArmor runs first — you need BOTH for defense-in-depth.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s15-5"><strong>15.5</strong> Official Seccomp Facts</a></td><td>What does kubernetes.io actually say, word for word?</td><td>The <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory, the full <code>SCMP_ACT_</code> prefix, and why <code>privileged: true</code> can never use a seccomp profile.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 15.1 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s15-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">15.1 What Is Seccomp and Why Does It Protect Containers?</h3>
                <p>Seccomp stands for <strong>Secure Computing Mode</strong>. It is a Linux kernel feature (available since kernel 2.6.12, 2005) that allows a process to restrict which system calls it can make. Think of it as a <strong>firewall for syscalls</strong> — every time a containerized process wants to talk to the kernel (to open a file, create a socket, spawn a thread), Seccomp checks: "Is this syscall on the allowed list?" If yes → proceed. If no → block (kill the process or return an error).</p>

                <h4>Where Seccomp Sits in the Security Stack — The Syscall Boundary</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Linux Security Layers — Where Seccomp Operates vs Other Controls</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  USER SPACE (Ring 3)                                                      │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  KUBERNETES RBAC (Ch9)                                            │   │
  │  │  Controls: Who can kubectl create/delete pods?                    │   │
  │  │  Level: Kubernetes API server                                     │   │
  │  ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤   │
  │  │  POD SECURITY STANDARDS (Ch17)                                    │   │
  │  │  Controls: Can this pod run as root? Privileged?                  │   │
  │  │  Level: Pod admission + runtime configuration                     │   │
  │  ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤   │
  │  │  APPARMOR (Ch14)                                                   │   │
  │  │  Controls: Can this process read /etc/shadow? Open TCP sockets?   │   │
  │  │  Level: File paths, network protocols, Linux capabilities         │   │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ═══════════════════════════ SYSCALL BOUNDARY ═══════════════════════════ │
  │  (CPU switches from Ring 3 → Ring 0. This is where Seccomp lives.)      │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  SECCOMP (Ch15) — THIS CHAPTER                                    │   │
  │  │  Controls: Which syscalls can this process make?                  │   │
  │  │  Level: The syscall interface itself — the LAST checkpoint        │   │
  │  │  before the kernel executes the requested operation.              │   │
  │  │                                                                    │   │
  │  │  ⚡ Seccomp runs AFTER AppArmor, AFTER DAC, AFTER capabilities.   │   │
  │  │  Even if ALL other security checks pass, Seccomp can still say NO.│   │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
  │                                                                          │
  │  KERNEL SPACE (Ring 0)                                                   │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  LINUX KERNEL                                                     │   │
