</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 15.2 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s15-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">15.2 Seccomp Profiles — RuntimeDefault and Custom</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes supports <strong>three Seccomp profile types</strong>, and the CKS exam expects you to know exactly when to use each one. The choice between them is a security tradeoff: RuntimeDefault is safe and easy but blocks only ~44 syscalls. Custom profiles can block 250+ syscalls but require careful testing. Unconfined blocks nothing — and should never be used in production.</p>

                <h4>The Three Profile Types — Comparated</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Profile Type</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Syscalls Blocked</th><th>Where Defined</th><th>When to Use</th><th>Security Level</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(50,108,229,0.06);"><td><strong>Unconfined</strong></td><td>No Seccomp filter. All 300+ syscalls available.</td><td>0 (none)</td><td>N/A — it's the absence of a profile</td><td>Debugging only. Never in production.</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">🔴 NONE</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(76,175,80,0.06);"><td><strong>RuntimeDefault</strong></td><td>Uses the container runtime's built-in default seccomp profile.</td><td>~44 syscalls</td><td>Built into containerd/CRI-O/Docker</td><td>Default for all pods. Minimum security baseline.</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">🟢 GOOD</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(76,175,80,0.06);"><td><strong>Localhost</strong></td><td>Custom JSON profile stored on the node's filesystem.</td><td>200+ (fully customizable)</td><td><code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code> on each node</td><td>Production hardening. Tailor to your app's exact syscall needs.</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">🟢 BEST</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>RuntimeDefault — What It Actually Blocks</h4>
                <p>The RuntimeDefault profile is NOT a Kubernetes construct — it is the <strong>Docker/containerd default seccomp profile</strong>, which has been the default for Docker since 2016. It blocks approximately 44 syscalls out of 300+, targeting the most commonly abused ones. Here are the key syscalls RuntimeDefault blocks:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">RuntimeDefault Blocked Syscalls — The 44 That Docker Decided Are Too Dangerous</div>
<pre>
  RUNTIMEDEFAULT BLOCKS THESE SYSCALLS (partial list of the ~44):
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  • mount, umount2        → Cannot mount/unmount filesystems
  • kexec_load, kexec_file_load → Cannot replace running kernel
  • init_module, finit_module, delete_module → Cannot load/unload kernel modules
  • ptrace                → Cannot attach debugger to other processes
  • personality           → Cannot change execution domain
  • setns, unshare        → Cannot join/create namespaces (ESCAPE VECTOR)
  • clone (with CLONE_NEWUSER, CLONE_NEWNET, etc.) → Blocked namespace-creation flags
  • bpf                   → Cannot load BPF programs
  • perf_event_open       → Cannot use performance monitoring (side-channel defense)
  • reboot                → Cannot reboot the system
  • swapon, swapoff       → Cannot enable/disable swap
  • clock_settime         → Cannot change system time
  • name_to_handle_at,    → Cannot resolve file handles (used for filesystem escapes)
    open_by_handle_at
  • kcmp                  → Cannot compare processes (used for ASLR bypass)
  • add_key, request_key, keyctl → Cannot manipulate kernel keyring
  • process_vm_readv,      → Cannot read/write another process's memory
    process_vm_writev
  • move_pages            → Cannot move memory pages (used for Rowhammer attacks)

  ⚡ KEY: RuntimeDefault blocks the syscalls that are MOST commonly used in
     container escape exploits. It is the MINIMUM every pod should have.
     But it leaves 250+ syscalls unblocked. For critical workloads, you
     need a custom profile that also blocks the other 200+.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Custom Seccomp Profile — JSON Format Explained</h4>
                <p>A custom Seccomp profile is a JSON file with a specific structure. The <code>defaultAction</code> sets the baseline — what happens to syscalls NOT in the list. The <code>syscalls</code> array overrides the default for specific syscalls:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">JSON — Anatomy of a Seccomp Profile</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "defaultAction": "SCMP_ACT_ERRNO",
  "defaultErrnoRet": 1,
  "architectures": [
    "SCMP_ARCH_X86_64",
    "SCMP_ARCH_AARCH64"
  ],
  "flags": [
    "SECCOMP_FILTER_FLAG_LOG"
  ],
  "syscalls": [
    {
      "names": ["read", "write", "open", "openat", "close"],
      "action": "SCMP_ACT_ALLOW"
    },
    {
      "names": ["mount", "umount2", "ptrace", "kexec_load"],
      "action": "SCMP_ACT_ERRNO"
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div><h5>defaultAction</h5><p>What happens to syscalls NOT in any list. <code>SCMP_ACT_ERRNO</code> (return error) is the standard. <code>SCMP_ACT_KILL</code> for maximum security.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div><h5>syscalls[].names</h5><p>Array of syscall names (strings). Use the Linux syscall name: "mount", not "__NR_mount" (165).</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div><h5>syscalls[].action</h5><p>ALLOW, ERRNO, KILL, LOG, or TRAP. Overrides defaultAction for these specific syscalls. <em>(Short forms here name the concept — the JSON field always spells out the full <code>SCMP_ACT_</code> prefix; see §15.5.)</em></p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏗️</div><h5>architectures</h5><p>Which CPU architectures this profile applies to. Must include your node's architecture (typically x86_64).</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Complete Custom Seccomp Profile for easypj Django</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">JSON — /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/easypj-django.json</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "defaultAction": "SCMP_ACT_ERRNO",
  "defaultErrnoRet": 1,
  "architectures": [
    "SCMP_ARCH_X86_64"
  ],
  "syscalls": [
    {
      "names": [
        "accept", "accept4", "access", "alarm", "bind",
        "brk", "capget", "chdir", "clock_gettime", "clone",
        "close", "connect", "dup", "dup2", "epoll_create1",
        "epoll_ctl", "epoll_pwait", "execve", "exit", "exit_group",
        "fchdir", "fcntl", "fdatasync", "flock", "fstat",
        "fstatfs", "fsync", "ftruncate", "futex",
        "getcwd", "getdents64", "getegid", "geteuid", "getgid",
        "getpeername", "getpid", "getppid", "getrandom", "getrlimit",
        "getsockname", "getsockopt", "gettid", "gettimeofday",
        "getuid", "ioctl", "kill", "listen", "lseek",
        "madvise", "mprotect", "mmap", "mremap", "msync",
        "munmap", "nanosleep", "open", "openat", "pause",
        "pipe", "pipe2", "poll", "ppoll", "prctl",
        "pread64", "pwrite64", "read", "readlink", "readv",
        "recvfrom", "recvmsg", "rename", "rt_sigaction",
        "rt_sigpending", "rt_sigprocmask", "rt_sigreturn",
        "rt_sigsuspend", "rt_sigtimedwait",
        "sched_getaffinity", "sched_getparam", "sched_getscheduler",
        "sched_yield", "select", "sendmsg", "sendto",
        "set_robust_list", "setsockopt", "shmat", "shmctl",
        "shmdt", "shmget", "shutdown", "sigaltstack", "socket",
        "socketpair", "stat", "statfs", "sysinfo", "tgkill",
