            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Seccomp is a kernel-level syscall firewall, not a userspace convention</strong> — a BPF program checks every syscall against the profile, running AFTER AppArmor, DAC, and capability checks, making it the last checkpoint before the kernel executes anything (15.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Three profile types trade off convenience against attack-surface reduction</strong> — Unconfined blocks nothing, RuntimeDefault blocks ~44 of the most dangerous syscalls out of the box, and a custom Localhost profile can block 250+, tailored exactly to what strace observed your app calling (15.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Applying Seccomp is a two-machine operation with one hard ordering rule</strong> — the JSON file must exist on the node BEFORE the pod schedules there (via <code>securityContext.seccompProfile</code>), or CreateContainer fails outright with no retry; verify with <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/status</code> → <code>Seccomp: 2</code> (15.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>AppArmor and Seccomp are complementary, not redundant</strong> — AppArmor blocks by PATH (what file/network/capability), Seccomp blocks by SYSCALL (what kernel operation); AppArmor runs first, and a defense-in-depth pod needs both plus a locked-down SecurityContext (15.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>The exam's favorite precision trap lives in §15.5</strong> — the real seccomp path includes a <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory the simplified examples omit, raw JSON requires the full <code>SCMP_ACT_</code> prefix (never the short "ALLOW"/"ERRNO" forms), and <code>privileged: true</code> silently forces Unconfined regardless of any <code>seccompProfile</code> setting — you cannot have privileged access and syscall filtering on the same container (15.5).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 15 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>RuntimeDefault ≠ no seccomp:</strong> <code>RuntimeDefault</code> uses the container runtime's built-in default seccomp profile (blocking ~44 dangerous syscalls). <code>Unconfined</code> disables seccomp entirely. Always use RuntimeDefault at minimum.</li>
                    <li><strong>localhostProfile path is relative — and has a subdirectory most examples skip:</strong> Profiles must be in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/</code> (see §15.5 — the <code>profiles/</code> part is easy to miss). If you reference <code>localhostProfile: custom.json</code>, the kubelet looks for <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/custom.json</code>. Wrong path = pod fails to start with "cannot load seccomp profile."</li>
                    <li><strong>Seccomp is NOT a per-pod default:</strong> Unlike PSS, Seccomp doesn't have a namespace-level default. Every pod must explicitly set it — or use <code>SeccompDefault</code> feature gate (K8s 1.25+) to apply RuntimeDefault cluster-wide.</li>
                    <li><strong>privileged: true silently defeats Seccomp:</strong> A privileged container always runs Unconfined no matter what <code>seccompProfile.type</code> says — the API server accepts the field, the kernel just never enforces it. If an exam task demands both privileged mode AND syscall filtering, that's a trap; the fix is to drop <code>privileged: true</code> and grant specific capabilities instead (15.5, Ch 18).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>The <code>SeccompDefault</code> feature gate (K8s 1.25+) applies RuntimeDefault cluster-wide automatically — no per-pod config needed if it's already enabled.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- cat /proc/1/status | grep Seccomp</code> is the single fastest verification command — <code>2</code> means active, <code>0</code> means disabled, don't bother parsing anything else first.</li>
                    <li>When a pod breaks right after you add a custom profile, don't reach for <code>SCMP_ACT_KILL</code> to debug — switch <code>defaultAction</code> to <code>SCMP_ACT_LOG</code> first, so you get a syscall list instead of a dead pod (15.5).</li>
                    <li>Muscle-memory the full path before the exam: <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/</code> — the missing <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory is the #1 reason a correctly-written profile still fails to load (15.5).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 15)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">practice until automatic</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check seccomp status on a pod</span>
kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- cat /proc/1/status | grep Seccomp  <span class="comment"># 0=disabled, 2=filter active</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Apply RuntimeDefault seccomp (simplest hardening — securityContext.seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault)</span>
kubectl run secured --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml | sed 's/containers:/securityContext:\n        seccompProfile:\n          type: RuntimeDefault\n      containers:/' | kubectl apply -f -

<span class="comment"># 3. Deploy + apply a custom seccomp profile (mkdir the profiles/ subdirectory FIRST — 15.5)</span>
ssh node01 "mkdir -p /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles"
scp custom.json root@node01:/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/custom.json
<span class="comment"># Pod spec: seccompProfile: {type: Localhost, localhostProfile: custom.json}</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Test syscall blocking</span>
kubectl exec secured-pod -- mount  <span class="comment"># Should fail with "permission denied" if mount is blocked</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Audit-mode debugging workflow (15.5): defaultAction SCMP_ACT_LOG first, SCMP_ACT_ERRNO once you know the syscall list</span>
journalctl -k | grep seccomp  <span class="comment"># or dmesg, depending on the node's logging setup</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 15 — Lab Q&amp;A (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">11 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a custom Seccomp profile that blocks the mount and ptrace syscalls but allows everything else. Apply it to a pod.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-json">{
  "defaultAction": "SCMP_ACT_ALLOW",
  "architectures": ["SCMP_ARCH_X86_64"],
  "syscalls": [
    {
      "names": ["mount", "umount2", "ptrace"],
      "action": "SCMP_ACT_ERRNO"
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What directory must custom Seccomp profiles be placed in on the node, and what happens if the file isn't there when the pod starts?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/</code> — note the <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory (§15.5). If the file isn't there when the kubelet tries to create the pod, the pod fails with <code>CreateContainerError: cannot load seccomp profile</code>. The kubelet reads the profile BEFORE starting the container — missing profile (or missing subdirectory) = pod never starts.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between SCMP_ACT_ERRNO, SCMP_ACT_KILL, and SCMP_ACT_ALLOW in a Seccomp profile?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>SCMP_ACT_ALLOW:</strong> Allow the syscall (whitelist mode). <strong>SCMP_ACT_ERRNO:</strong> Block the syscall and return an error code to the application (app can handle gracefully). <strong>SCMP_ACT_KILL:</strong> Block the syscall and KILL the process immediately (SIGSYS signal). Use ERRNO for most cases — it lets apps fail gracefully instead of crashing. All three action values must use the full <code>SCMP_ACT_</code> prefix in real JSON — "ALLOW" alone is not valid (§15.5).</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After applying Seccomp RuntimeDefault to a pod, how do you verify it's active at runtime?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- cat /proc/1/status | grep Seccomp
# Output: Seccomp: 2
# 0 = disabled, 1 = strict, 2 = filter (active)

kubectl get pod &lt;pod&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].securityContext.seccompProfile}'</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span><div class="eq-question">Write a custom Seccomp profile that blocks the <code>mount</code>, <code>unshare</code>, and <code>reboot</code> syscalls. What container escape techniques does this prevent?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-json">{
  "defaultAction": "SCMP_ACT_ALLOW",
  "architectures": ["SCMP_ARCH_X86_64"],
  "syscalls": [
    {
      "names": ["mount", "umount2", "unshare", "reboot", "kexec_load"],
      "action": "SCMP_ACT_ERRNO"
    }
  ]
}</code></pre><p><strong>Prevents:</strong> (1) <code>mount</code> — can't mount host filesystems, (2) <code>unshare</code> — can't create new namespaces (key to container escape), (3) <code>reboot</code> — can't DOS the node by rebooting, (4) <code>kexec_load</code> — can't load a new kernel (advanced persistence).</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Mount and unshare are the two most dangerous syscalls for container escape. Mount lets an attacker mount the host filesystem. Unshare lets an attacker leave the container's namespaces. Blocking these doesn't break normal applications (they don't need to mount filesystems or unshare namespaces). This is the CKS pattern: block dangerous syscalls that apps don't legitimately need.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span><div class="eq-question">What syscalls does RuntimeDefault Seccomp block? How is it different from Unconfined?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>Unconfined:</strong> Blocks NOTHING. Every syscall the kernel supports is available. This is the DEFAULT setting — if you don't specify a seccompProfile, your container is Unconfined. <strong>RuntimeDefault:</strong> Uses the container runtime's built-in default profile. Docker/containerd's default blocks ~44 syscalls including: <code>reboot</code>, <code>kexec_load</code>, <code>add_key</code>, <code>request_key</code>, <code>delete_module</code>, <code>init_module</code>, and several obsolete syscalls. It ALLOWS ~300 common syscalls needed by typical applications.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>RuntimeDefault is the "set it and forget it" Seccomp option. It provides immediate protection against a class of kernel exploits without breaking applications. On the CKS exam, if asked to harden a pod and you're short on time, set <code>seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault</code> — it's the single highest-impact, lowest-risk security setting.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span><div class="eq-question">Where are custom Seccomp profiles stored on a node, and how does the kubelet find them? What path must you use?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Custom profiles are stored in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/</code> (the kubelet's seccomp root plus the <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory — §15.5). The pod spec references them as <code>localhostProfile: &lt;filename&gt;</code> (without any path). Example: if the profile is at <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/custom.json</code>, the pod spec uses:</p><pre><code class="language-yaml">securityContext:
  seccompProfile:
    type: Localhost
    localhostProfile: custom.json  # Just the filename, resolved under profiles/</code></pre><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The kubelet automatically prepends <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/</code> to whatever <code>localhostProfile</code> value you give it. Profile files must be readable by the kubelet (usually root:root, 0644). On the CKS exam, if the kubelet can't find your profile, verify (1) it's in the exact <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory, not the parent directory, and (2) the filename matches exactly (case-sensitive).</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span><div class="eq-question">A pod with RuntimeDefault Seccomp is crashing with "operation not permitted" when trying to change file timestamps. Which syscall is being blocked, and how do you fix it without removing Seccomp entirely?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>The syscall is likely <code>utimensat</code> or <code>futimesat</code> — some Seccomp profiles block timestamp manipulation syscalls. Fix: create a custom profile that allows these specific syscalls while keeping all other RuntimeDefault restrictions:</p><pre><code class="language-json">{
  "defaultAction": "SCMP_ACT_ERRNO",
  "architectures": ["SCMP_ARCH_X86_64"],
  "syscalls": [
    {"names": ["utimensat", "futimesat", "write", "read", "openat", "close", "fstat", "mmap", "mprotect", "munmap", "brk", "rt_sigaction", "rt_sigprocmask", "rt_sigreturn", "ioctl", "access", "pipe2", "select", "sched_yield", "mremap", "msync", "mincore", "madvise", "shmget", "shmat", "shmctl", "dup2", "pause", "nanosleep", "getpid", "socket", "connect", "accept", "sendto", "recvfrom", "sendmsg", "recvmsg", "bind", "listen", "getsockname", "getpeername", "setsockopt", "getsockopt", "clone", "fork", "execve", "exit", "wait4", "kill", "uname", "fcntl", "flock", "fsync", "ftruncate", "getdents", "getcwd", "chdir", "fchdir", "rename", "mkdir", "rmdir", "creat", "link", "unlink", "symlink", "readlink", "chmod", "fchmod", "chown", "fchown", "lchown", "umask", "gettimeofday", "getrlimit", "getrusage", "sysinfo", "times", "ptrace", "getuid", "syslog", "getgid", "setuid", "setgid", "geteuid", "getegid", "setpgid", "getppid", "getpgid", "setsid", "setreuid", "setregid", "getgroups", "setgroups", "setresuid", "getresuid", "setresgid", "getresgid", "getpgid", "setfsuid", "setfsgid", "getsid", "capget", "capset", "rt_sigpending", "rt_sigtimedwait", "rt_sigqueueinfo", "sigaltstack", "utimensat", "futimesat"], "action": "SCMP_ACT_ALLOW"}
  ]
}</code></pre><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the Seccomp debugging workflow: (1) identify the blocked syscall from logs (<code>dmesg | grep seccomp</code>), (2) create a custom profile that whitelists the needed syscall, (3) apply and test. Never disable Seccomp entirely — always create a custom profile. The CKS exam tests your ability to write and debug custom Seccomp profiles.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span><div class="eq-question">What's the relationship between Seccomp, AppArmor, and PodSecurity? If all three are applied to a pod, in what order are they evaluated?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>They operate at different layers and are evaluated at different stages: <strong>PodSecurity (Admission):</strong> Evaluated BEFORE the pod is created. Checks if the pod spec meets the policy. If it fails, the pod is rejected. <strong>AppArmor (LSM):</strong> Evaluated at RUNTIME when the process accesses files. Operates at the Linux Security Module hook. <strong>Seccomp (Syscall Filter):</strong> Evaluated at RUNTIME when a syscall is made. Operates at the syscall entry point (before the kernel processes it).</p><p><strong>Order at runtime:</strong> Process makes syscall → Seccomp evaluates (if blocked, syscall fails) → AppArmor evaluates (if file access is blocked, operation fails). They are independent and complementary.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>These three controls form a layered defense: PodSecurity stops insecure pods from being created (Gate 4 — Admission). AppArmor limits what files a running container can access (Gate — LSM hooks). Seccomp limits what syscalls a container can make (Gate — syscall interface). On the CKS exam, use ALL three for defense in depth.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span><div class="eq-question">You discover that <code>/proc/sys/kernel/unprivileged_userns_clone</code> is set to 1 on all worker nodes. What's the container escape risk, and how do you fix it?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>Risk:</strong> When enabled, unprivileged users (including root inside a container) can create new user namespaces. A container escape technique uses <code>unshare -U</code> to create a user namespace where the container process becomes root on the HOST. Combined with other exploits, this leads to full node compromise.</p><pre><code class="language-bash"># Fix immediately
echo 0 > /proc/sys/kernel/unprivileged_userns_clone
# Persist
echo "kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone = 0" >> /etc/sysctl.d/99-security.conf
sysctl --system
# Verify
sysctl kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone  # Should be 0</code></pre><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Unprivileged user namespaces are a well-known container escape vector. They were the basis of CVE-2022-0492 (cgroups escape) and several others. Kubernetes 1.25+ sets this to 0 by default. On the CKS exam, check <code>sysctl kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone</code> on every node — if it's 1, it's a finding you must fix. Combine with Seccomp blocking <code>unshare</code> for defense in depth.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q11</span><div class="eq-question">A pod spec sets <code>securityContext.privileged: true</code> on its container AND sets <code>securityContext.seccompProfile: {type: Localhost, localhostProfile: easypj-audit.json}</code>. The engineer expects both privileged access and syscall filtering. What actually happens at runtime, and separately, where on disk must <code>easypj-audit.json</code> live for the reference to even resolve?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>Runtime behavior:</strong> The seccomp profile is silently ignored. A <code>privileged: true</code> container ALWAYS runs Unconfined, regardless of any <code>seccompProfile.type</code> value — the API server accepts the field, but the kernel never attaches a BPF filter to a privileged container. <strong>On-disk path:</strong> <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/easypj-audit.json</code> — note the <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory beneath the kubelet's seccomp root; a file placed directly in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code> without that subdirectory would never be found even for a non-privileged pod.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This question chains both §15.5 corrections into one scenario, which is exactly how the exam likes to combine facts. The correct remediation for the mutual-exclusivity trap is to remove <code>privileged: true</code> and grant only the specific capabilities the workload needs (Ch 18) — then the seccomp profile actually takes effect once the path is also fixed.</p></div></details></div>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-connected"></span>
