            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-cheatsheet"></span>
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">⚡ Ch 15 Visual Cheat Sheet — Seccomp</div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div><h5>Syscall Filtering</h5><p>Blocks dangerous syscalls at kernel level. JSON profiles. <code>seccompProfile.type</code>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div><h5>RuntimeDefault</h5><p>Container runtime's built-in profile. Blocks ~44 syscalls. Minimum baseline.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📁</div><h5>Custom Profiles</h5><p>Localhost type. File in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/</code>. Default ALLOW + specific DENY, or default ERRNO + explicit ALLOW list.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div><h5>Privileged Blocks Seccomp</h5><p><code>privileged: true</code> always runs Unconfined — mutually exclusive with syscall filtering (§15.5).</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p class="mb-0">Seccomp in one breath: a BPF-based syscall firewall, minimum RuntimeDefault (~44 blocked) or maximum custom Localhost profile (250+ blocked), applied via <code>securityContext.seccompProfile</code> and verified with <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/status</code>. The profile must exist on the node — at the correct <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory path — before the pod schedules, and it never applies at all to a <code>privileged: true</code> container.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📊 Chapter 15 Visual Summary — Seccomp at a Glance</div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔍</div>
                        <h5>Seccomp Basics (15.1)</h5>
                        <p><strong>What:</strong> Syscall firewall at the kernel boundary<br><strong>Blocks:</strong> 250+ unnecessary syscalls<br><strong>Uses:</strong> BPF programs in kernel<br><strong>Exam:</strong> RuntimeDefault + custom JSON profiles</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Profile Types (15.2)</h5>
                        <p><strong>Unconfined:</strong> No filtering — insecure<br><strong>RuntimeDefault:</strong> Blocks ~44 syscalls — minimum baseline<br><strong>Localhost:</strong> Custom JSON — maximum security</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚀</div>
                        <h5>Kubernetes (15.3)</h5>
                        <p><strong>Apply:</strong> securityContext.seccompProfile<br><strong>Type:</strong> RuntimeDefault | Localhost<br><strong>Path:</strong> /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/<br><strong>Verify:</strong> /proc/PID/status → Seccomp: 2</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚖️</div>
                        <h5>AppArmor vs Seccomp (15.4)</h5>
                        <p><strong>AppArmor:</strong> Files, network, capabilities<br><strong>Seccomp:</strong> Syscalls only<br><strong>Both:</strong> Needed for defense-in-depth<br><strong>Order:</strong> AppArmor FIRST, then Seccomp</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📐</div>
                        <h5>Official Facts (15.5)</h5>
                        <p><strong>Path:</strong> .../seccomp/profiles/&lt;file&gt;<br><strong>JSON:</strong> full SCMP_ACT_ prefix required<br><strong>Constraint:</strong> privileged=true → always Unconfined<br><strong>Debug:</strong> LOG mode first, then ERRNO</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Seccomp (§15.1) is a kernel-level BPF syscall firewall running after AppArmor and DAC — the last checkpoint before a syscall executes. Profile types (§15.2) trade convenience for coverage: Unconfined blocks nothing, RuntimeDefault blocks ~44, custom Localhost profiles can block 250+ once you've observed the real syscall list with <code>strace</code>. Applying Seccomp (§15.3) means getting the JSON onto every node BEFORE the pod schedules there — there's no retry if it's missing.</p>
                    <p><strong>The takeaway:</strong> AppArmor and Seccomp (§15.4) are complementary, not redundant — paths vs syscalls, and AppArmor runs first — which is why a defense-in-depth pod configures both plus a locked-down SecurityContext. §15.5's official facts are the exam's favorite precision trap: the real path has a <code>profiles/</code> subdirectory, real JSON needs the full <code>SCMP_ACT_</code> prefix, and <code>privileged: true</code> silently defeats seccomp entirely — no exceptions.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>You are here:</strong> Step 15 — System Hardening: Syscall-Level Sandboxing. Next: Chapter 16 — Open Ports, Packages &amp; IAM Minimization.</p>
