            <section class="section-block" id="s16-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.3 Limiting Kernel Modules — Blacklisting Dangerous Ones</h3>
                <p>Kernel modules are pieces of code that can be loaded into the running kernel to extend its functionality — filesystem drivers, hardware drivers, network protocol handlers. Every loaded module increases the kernel's attack surface. The CIS Benchmark mandates blacklisting specific modules that have no legitimate use on a Kubernetes node:</p>

                <h4>CIS-Mandated Kernel Module Blacklisting</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — /etc/modprobe.d/cis-blacklist.conf — CIS-required module blocks</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
# Create the CIS blacklist configuration
cat > /etc/modprobe.d/cis-blacklist.conf << 'EOF'
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# CIS KUBERNETES BENCHMARK — Mandated Kernel Module Blacklist
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── UNNECESSARY FILESYSTEM DRIVERS ──
install cramfs /bin/true       # Compressed ROM filesystem (embedded devices only)
install freevxfs /bin/true      # Veritas filesystem (legacy Unix)
install jffs2 /bin/true         # Journaling Flash filesystem (embedded devices)
install hfs /bin/true           # Apple HFS (Mac OS Classic — never on servers)
install hfsplus /bin/true       # Apple HFS+ (Mac OS Extended — never on servers)
install squashfs /bin/true      # SquashFS (compressed read-only — rare on servers)
install udf /bin/true           # Universal Disk Format (optical media — never on servers)

# ── DANGEROUS HARDWARE DRIVERS ──
install usb-storage /bin/true   # USB mass storage — physical attack vector
install firewire-core /bin/true # FireWire — DMA attack vector (direct memory access)

# ── DANGEROUS NETWORK PROTOCOLS ──
install dccp /bin/true          # Datagram Congestion Control Protocol
install sctp /bin/true          # Stream Control Transmission Protocol
install rds /bin/true           # Reliable Datagram Sockets
install tipc /bin/true          # Transparent Inter-Process Communication
EOF

# Verify the configuration
echo "=== Checking blacklisted modules ==="
for mod in cramfs freevxfs jffs2 hfs hfsplus squashfs udf usb-storage; do
    if lsmod | grep -q "^$mod "; then
        echo "⚠️  WARNING: $mod is currently loaded. Reboot to fully disable."
    else
        echo "✅ $mod is not loaded"
    fi
done

# Verify blacklist is preventing module load
echo ""
echo "=== Testing module load prevention ==="
modprobe --dry-run cramfs 2>&1
# Should show: modprobe: ERROR: could not insert 'cramfs': Operation not permitted</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 16.4 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s16-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.4 Cloud IAM for Kubernetes Nodes</h3>
                <p>In cloud environments, Kubernetes nodes run on cloud VMs that have their own identity — an AWS IAM role, a GCP Service Account, or an Azure Managed Identity. These cloud credentials are accessible to <strong>every pod running on that node</strong> via the instance metadata service (169.254.169.254). If the node's IAM role has excessive permissions, a compromised pod can steal those permissions and pivot to your entire cloud infrastructure. This was exactly how the Capital One breach worked.</p>

                <h4>Cloud IAM Least-Privilege — What Each Node REALLY Needs</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">AWS IAM Policy — Minimal permissions for an EKS worker node</div>
<pre>
  {
    "Version": "2012-10-17",
    "Statement": [
      {
        "Effect": "Allow",
        "Action": [
          "ec2:DescribeInstances",
          "ec2:DescribeNetworkInterfaces",
          "ec2:DescribeSecurityGroups",
          "ec2:DescribeSubnets",
          "ec2:DescribeVolumes"
        ],
        "Resource": "*"
      },
      {
        "Effect": "Allow",
        "Action": [
          "ecr:GetAuthorizationToken",
          "ecr:BatchCheckLayerAvailability",
          "ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer",
          "ecr:BatchGetImage"
        ],
        "Resource": "*"
      }
    ]
  }
  ❌ NEVER INCLUDE: s3:*, iam:*, sts:AssumeRole (allows full cloud pivot)
  ✅ THIS IS SUFFICIENT: Describe EC2 (for pod networking) + pull ECR images
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>IMDSv2 — Protecting the Node's Cloud Credentials from Pods</h4>
                <p>The Instance Metadata Service (IMDS) at 169.254.169.254 exposes the node's IAM credentials to every process on the node — including every pod. IMDSv2 adds a required session token, preventing simple <code>curl</code> from retrieving credentials. Combined with <code>hop limit = 1</code>, it prevents containers from accessing the metadata service at all:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Bash — Configure IMDSv2 on AWS (required for production EKS nodes)</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Enable IMDSv2 only (disable insecure IMDSv1)
aws ec2 modify-instance-metadata-options \
  --instance-id i-1234567890abcdef0 \
  --http-tokens required \
  --http-put-response-hop-limit 1

# Verify: from inside any pod, this should FAIL
kubectl run test-imds --rm -it --image=amazonlinux -- \
  curl -s http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/
# Expected: 401 Unauthorized (IMDSv1 disabled, token required)

# With hop limit = 1, even IMDSv2 requests from pods fail
# (pod → host network namespace = 2 hops)</code></pre>
                </div>

