            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 16.5 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s16-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.5 etcd Security — Restricting Who Can Read the Cluster Brain</h3>
                <p>etcd is the <strong>most sensitive component</strong> in your Kubernetes cluster. It stores every Secret, every ConfigMap, every pod spec, every RBAC rule — the entire state of your cluster. If an attacker reads etcd, they have everything. If they write to etcd, they own the cluster. etcd security is not optional — it is existential.</p>

                <h4>etcd Raft Consensus — Security Implications of the Protocol</h4>
                <p>etcd uses the <strong>Raft consensus algorithm</strong> to maintain consistency across multiple nodes. A write is committed only when a majority (quorum) of nodes acknowledge it. This has direct security implications that most K8s administrators never think about:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Raft Consensus Security — Why etcd Cluster Size Matters for Breach Scenarios</div>
<pre>
  etcd CLUSTER: 3 nodes (typical HA setup)
  ──────────────────────────────────────
  Quorum = floor(N/2) + 1 = floor(3/2) + 1 = 2 nodes

  SCENARIO A: Attacker compromises 1 etcd node
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  • Attacker can READ all data on the compromised node (full cluster state).
  • Attacker CANNOT modify data: writes to the compromised node will be
    rejected by the other 2 nodes (the compromised node can't form quorum alone).
  • RESULT: Data is READ but not CORRUPTED. Cluster continues operating normally.

  SCENARIO B: Attacker compromises 2 etcd nodes (quorum)
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  • Attacker controls QUORUM. They can:
    → Delete any key (including Secrets, pod specs, RBAC rules)
    → Modify any key (redirect traffic, change pod specs, escalate RBAC)
    → Add a new node to the cluster (persistence via new etcd member)
  • RESULT: Full cluster compromise. Attacker OWNS the cluster state.

  ⚡ SECURITY IMPLICATION:
  ───────────────────────
  • 3-node etcd cluster: tolerates 1 node failure/compromise.
  • 5-node etcd cluster: tolerates 2 node failures/compromises (quorum=3).
  • Why 5 nodes is more secure: An attacker must compromise 3 nodes to control
    etcd, not just 2. But 5 nodes adds more operational complexity.
  • ALWAYS use an ODD number of etcd nodes (3, 5, 7) to avoid split-brain.

  DEFENSE-IN-DEPTH FOR etcd:
  ──────────────────────────
  • Restrict port 2379 to API server IPs ONLY (firewall).
  • Use TLS with mutual authentication for ALL etcd communication.
  • Do NOT co-locate etcd with user workloads (dedicated control plane nodes).
  • Encrypt Secrets at rest so even a compromised etcd directory reveals nothing.
  • Monitor etcd logs for unauthorized access attempts.
  • Take regular etcd snapshots for disaster recovery.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>etcd Hardening Checklist — Five Critical Controls</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-1">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5>1. TLS Everywhere — Client and Peer Communication</h5>
                        <p>etcd must use TLS for ALL communication — both client (API server → etcd) and peer (etcd → etcd). The API server manifest must include: <code>--etcd-certfile</code>, <code>--etcd-keyfile</code>, <code>--etcd-cafile</code>. The etcd manifest must include: <code>--cert-file</code>, <code>--key-file</code>, <code>--peer-cert-file</code>, <code>--peer-key-file</code>, <code>--trusted-ca-file</code>. Without TLS, etcd traffic is plaintext — any network sniffing reveals every Secret in the cluster.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔥</div>
                        <h5>2. Firewall — Only the API Server Talks to etcd</h5>
                        <p>Port 2379 (etcd client) must be firewalled to allow ONLY the API server's IP address. No other component — not kubelet, not scheduler, not any pod — should be able to reach etcd. Use iptables: <code>iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 2379 -s &lt;API_SERVER_IP&gt; -j ACCEPT</code> followed by <code>iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 2379 -j DROP</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📁</div>
                        <h5>3. Data Directory Permissions — 700, Owned by etcd User</h5>
                        <p><code>chmod 700 /var/lib/etcd && chown etcd:etcd /var/lib/etcd</code>. Only the etcd process user should be able to read the data directory. The directory contains the entire cluster state in plaintext on disk — unless encryption at rest is configured (Chapter 19).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔐</div>
                        <h5>4. Peer Authentication — Mutual TLS Between etcd Members</h5>
                        <p>etcd peers (when running in a multi-node cluster) must mutually authenticate using TLS certificates. The <code>--peer-client-cert-auth=true</code> flag ensures that only nodes with valid etcd peer certificates can join the cluster. This prevents an attacker from adding a rogue etcd node to steal data.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔑</div>
                        <h5>5. Encryption at Rest — Secrets Must Be Unreadable on Disk</h5>
                        <p>By default, Secrets stored in etcd are base64-encoded — NOT encrypted. Anyone with disk access can decode them. EncryptionConfiguration (Chapter 19) encrypts Secrets before they are written to etcd, so even a compromised etcd data directory reveals nothing.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 16.6 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s16-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.6 Linux Kernel Hardening Parameters (sysctl)</h3>
                <p>The Linux kernel exposes hundreds of tunable parameters via <code>sysctl</code>. Many of these have direct security implications — they control IP forwarding, ICMP redirects, source routing, SYN flood protection, and address space layout randomization (ASLR). The CIS Benchmark specifies required values for production Kubernetes nodes:</p>

                <h4>CIS-Required Kernel Hardening via sysctl</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — /etc/sysctl.d/99-kubernetes-cis.conf — CIS kernel hardening</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
cat > /etc/sysctl.d/99-kubernetes-cis.conf << 'EOF'
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# CIS KUBERNETES BENCHMARK — Kernel Hardening Parameters
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── NETWORK HARDENING ──

# IP forwarding (REQUIRED for pod networking — DO NOT disable on K8s nodes)
net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1
net.ipv6.conf.all.forwarding = 1

# Disable ICMP redirects (prevent MITM redirect attacks)
# An attacker can send ICMP redirects to change routing tables
net.ipv4.conf.all.accept_redirects = 0
net.ipv4.conf.default.accept_redirects = 0
net.ipv6.conf.all.accept_redirects = 0

# Disable source packet routing (prevent IP spoofing)
# Attackers use source routing to bypass firewall rules
net.ipv4.conf.all.accept_source_route = 0
net.ipv4.conf.default.accept_source_route = 0

# Disable sending ICMP redirects (this node is not a router)
net.ipv4.conf.all.send_redirects = 0
net.ipv4.conf.default.send_redirects = 0

# Enable TCP SYN cookie protection (DDoS SYN flood mitigation)
# When SYN queue is full, use SYN cookies instead of dropping connections
net.ipv4.tcp_syncookies = 1

# Enable reverse path filtering (prevent IP spoofing from pods)
net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter = 1
net.ipv4.conf.default.rp_filter = 1

# ── MEMORY / ASLR HARDENING ──

# Maximum ASLR entropy (randomize memory addresses for all processes)
# 0 = no ASLR, 1 = partial, 2 = full (including heap and stack)
kernel.randomize_va_space = 2

# ── KERNEL POINTER / KEXEC RESTRICTIONS ──

# Restrict kernel pointer access in /proc (prevent info leaks)
kernel.kptr_restrict = 2

# Disable kexec (prevent replacing running kernel)
kernel.kexec_load_disabled = 1

# ── PROCESS / CORE DUMP RESTRICTIONS ──

# Restrict ptrace to direct child processes only
kernel.yama.ptrace_scope = 1

# Set core dump pattern (prevent core dumps from leaking memory contents)
kernel.core_pattern = |/bin/false
EOF

# Apply all sysctl changes
sysctl --system

# Verify key parameters
echo "=== Verifying CIS sysctl parameters ==="
sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward
sysctl net.ipv4.conf.all.accept_redirects
sysctl net.ipv4.tcp_syncookies
sysctl kernel.randomize_va_space
sysctl kernel.kptr_restrict
sysctl kernel.yama.ptrace_scope</code></pre>
                </div>
                <p><strong>What each parameter actually does:</strong> <code>accept_redirects=0</code> prevents an attacker on the same network from sending fake ICMP redirect messages that reroute your node's traffic through the attacker's machine. <code>tcp_syncookies=1</code> defends against SYN flood DDoS by using cryptographic cookies instead of allocating connection state for half-open connections. <code>randomize_va_space=2</code> randomizes the memory addresses of the stack, heap, and shared libraries — making it exponentially harder for attackers to exploit buffer overflow vulnerabilities because they cannot predict where their shellcode will land in memory.</p>

                <h4>How ASLR and kptr_restrict Stop Real Exploits — A Technical Deep Dive</h4>
                <p>Two of the most important sysctl parameters — <code>kernel.randomize_va_space</code> and <code>kernel.kptr_restrict</code> — work together to defeat memory corruption exploits. Understanding exactly HOW they work helps you appreciate why setting them correctly is non-negotiable:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ASLR + kptr_restrict — How They Defeat Exploits Together</div>
<pre>
  WITHOUT ASLR (randomize_va_space = 0):
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Process memory layout is PREDICTABLE (same addresses every run):
  ┌─────────────────┐ 0x7fffffffffff
  │  Stack          │ ← ALWAYS at 0x7ffffffde000
  │  (function      │    Attacker knows exactly where their shellcode
  │   return addrs) │    will land. They craft the exploit to jump here.
  ├─────────────────┤ 0x7ffff7e00000
  │  Shared Libs    │ ← ALWAYS at 0x7ffff7a00000
  │  (libc.so, etc) │    Attacker knows libc's address and can call
  │                 │    system("/bin/sh") by jumping to its known offset.
  ├─────────────────┤ 0x555555554000
  │  Heap           │ ← ALWAYS at 0x555555758000
  │  (malloc data)  │    Attacker can spray heap with shellcode at known addr.
  ├─────────────────┤ 0x400000
  │  Code (.text)   │ ← ALWAYS at 0x400000
  └─────────────────┘

  EXPLOIT DEVELOPMENT: Attacker writes: jump_to(0x7ffffffde000 + offset)
  one time. Exploit works on EVERY run, EVERY machine with same kernel.

  WITH ASLR (randomize_va_space = 2):
  ─────────────────────────────────────
  Process memory layout is RANDOMIZED (different on every execution):
  ┌─────────────────┐ varying address
  │  Stack          │ ← Randomized: could be anywhere in a ~32TB range
  │                 │    (x86-64 has 48-bit virtual address space)
  ├─────────────────┤ varying address
  │  Shared Libs    │ ← Randomized: different offset per process
  ├─────────────────┤ varying address
  │  Heap           │ ← Randomized: brk() starts at random address
  ├─────────────────┤ varying address
  │  Code (.text)   │ ← Position-Independent Executable (PIE): randomized
  └─────────────────┘

  EXPLOIT DEVELOPMENT: Attacker needs an INFORMATION LEAK to discover
  where anything is in memory. This is where kptr_restrict comes in:

  WITH kptr_restrict = 0:
  ────────────────────────
  cat /proc/kallsyms shows ALL kernel symbol addresses:
  ffffffff81000000 T _text
  ffffffff81234567 T sys_execve
  ffffffff81800000 D init_task
  → Attacker now knows kernel addresses. Can craft kernel exploits.

  WITH kptr_restrict = 2:
  ────────────────────────
  cat /proc/kallsyms shows: 0000000000000000 T _text
                             0000000000000000 T sys_execve
  → All addresses hidden. Attacker has no kernel address information.
  → Must find ANOTHER information leak — much harder.

  COMBINED DEFENSE:
  ─────────────────
  ASLR randomizes USER-SPACE addresses (stack, heap, libs, code).
  kptr_restrict hides KERNEL-SPACE addresses (syscall table, kernel functions).
  Both needed: user exploits need ASLR bypass. Kernel exploits need kptr leak.
  Without both defenses, exploits become dramatically easier to write.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 16.7: Platform Binaries &amp; Least-Privilege IAM ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s16-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.7 Verify Platform Binaries &amp; Least-Privilege Cloud IAM</h3>
                <p>The official CKS curriculum requires you to <strong>verify platform binaries before deploying</strong> and use <strong>least-privilege identity and access management</strong>. These two competencies work together: verified binaries ensure trusted components, least-privilege IAM ensures minimal cloud permissions.</p>

                <h4>Verifying Platform Binaries</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Bash — Verify Kubernetes Binaries</span></div><pre><code class="language-bash"># Verify K8s package integrity (Debian/Ubuntu)
dpkg -V kubelet kubeadm kubectl
# No output = all files match package manifest (CLEAN)
# ??5?????? /usr/bin/kubelet = md5 mismatch (TAMPERED)

# AIDE file integrity check
aide --check
# Compares monitored files against baseline. Reports added/removed/changed.

# Verify binary checksum against official release
curl -sL https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt | xargs -I{} sh -c 'curl -sL https://dl.k8s.io/{}/bin/linux/amd64/kubelet.sha256'
sha256sum /usr/bin/kubelet  # Compare with official checksum</code></pre></div>

                <h4>Least-Privilege Cloud IAM Patterns</h4>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ CKS Exam: Cloud IAM</h5><p>The exam tests cloud IAM minimization. Know: (1) AWS IRSA — pod gets its own IAM role via SA annotation, not the node's role. (2) GCP Workload Identity — maps K8s SA to GCP SA. (3) The Capital One breach happened because the NODE's IAM role had broad S3 access — every pod could exfiltrate data via the metadata service. Fix: per-pod IAM roles + IMDSv2 with hop limit 1.</p></div>
            </section>

