            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Minimization is a 15-point discipline, not a single action</strong> — package/service removal, SSH lockdown, AIDE file-integrity monitoring, compiler removal, kernel-module blacklisting, auto-updates, and cron/USB/log/audit/user/sudo hardening each close a distinct attack path (16.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Only a handful of ports are ever legitimately required</strong> — 6443/2379/2380/10250 on the control plane, 10250 (+ optionally 30000-32767) on workers, plus one CNI-specific port depending on plugin; everything else should be firewalled off (16.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Kernel modules are blocked with <code>install &lt;module&gt; /bin/true</code> in <code>/etc/modprobe.d/</code></strong> — CIS mandates blacklisting unused filesystem drivers, USB/FireWire, and risky network protocols like dccp and sctp (16.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Cloud IAM for nodes must be scoped to the bare minimum</strong> — describe/pull permissions only, never <code>s3:*</code>/<code>iam:*</code>; IMDSv2 with hop-limit 1 stops pods from stealing node credentials via the metadata service, which is exactly how Capital One was breached (16.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>etcd security is existential, not optional</strong> — Raft quorum math means compromising a minority of nodes only leaks reads, but a quorum grants full write control; TLS everywhere, firewalling port 2379 to the API server, 700 permissions, and encryption at rest are all non-negotiable (16.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>ASLR and kptr_restrict are a matched pair</strong> — ASLR randomizes user-space addresses, kptr_restrict hides kernel-space addresses in <code>/proc/kallsyms</code>, and an attacker needs both an ASLR bypass and a kernel pointer leak to build a reliable exploit chain (16.6).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 16 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>AIDE --init must run BEFORE --check:</strong> Running <code>aide --check</code> without first running <code>aide --init</code> produces meaningless results (no baseline to compare against). The exam will likely have AIDE already initialized — verify with <code>ls /var/lib/aide/aide.db</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Port 10250 is kubelet (keep open):</strong> Kubelet API serves on 10250. Don't block this port — it's required for <code>kubectl exec</code>, <code>kubectl logs</code>, and metrics. Do block ports 10255 (read-only, deprecated) and ports used by removed services.</li>
                    <li><strong>NodePort 30000-32767 are open by default:</strong> Even if you block these with iptables, Kubernetes can still allocate them. The correct fix is a NetworkPolicy, not a node firewall rule.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>dpkg -V kubelet kubeadm kubectl</code> — verify all K8s package file integrity in one command instead of checking each binary separately.</li>
                    <li><code>ss -tlnp</code> is the modern, faster replacement for <code>netstat -tulpn</code> — use it first when auditing listening ports under exam time pressure.</li>
                    <li><code>sysctl --system</code> applies every file under <code>/etc/sysctl.d/</code> in one pass — no need to <code>sysctl -p</code> each file individually after writing the CIS hardening config.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

