            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 16 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you list all loaded kernel modules and identify which ones are unnecessary for a Kubernetes node?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><code>lsmod</code> lists all loaded modules. Essential modules: overlay, br_netfilter, iptable_nat, ip_vs (if using IPVS mode). Unnecessary: bluetooth, btusb, snd_* (audio), joydev (joystick), uvcvideo (webcam), nouveau/radeon (GPU — unless doing GPU workloads). Remove: <code>modprobe -r &lt;module&gt;</code>. Blacklist: <code>echo "blacklist &lt;module&gt;" >> /etc/modprobe.d/blacklist.conf</code>.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the risk of leaving unnecessary kernel modules loaded? Give a real CVE example.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Every loaded kernel module adds to the attack surface. CVE-2022-0185 exploited the filesystem context functionality in the kernel — a feature most containers don't need but was available because the module was loaded. CVE-2016-0728 exploited the keyring facility. Both were exploitable from inside containers. Removing unused modules eliminates these attack paths entirely.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you permanently disable kernel module loading on a production node, and what's the risk?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><code>echo 1 > /proc/sys/kernel/modules_disabled</code> blocks ALL future module loading AND unloading. This is irreversible without a reboot. Risk: if a security update requires a new kernel module, you'll need to reboot the node. Only set this after verifying all required modules are loaded and tested. For production, set via sysctl: <code>kernel.modules_disabled=1</code> in <code>/etc/sysctl.d/99-security.conf</code>.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">AIDE reports that /usr/bin/kubelet has been modified. What does this mean and what should you do?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>AIDE detects file integrity violations. A modified kubelet binary means either: (a) a legitimate upgrade (verify with package manager: <code>dpkg -V kubelet</code>), or (b) an attacker replaced the binary with a backdoored version. If not a known upgrade: IMMEDIATELY cordon and drain the node, isolate it from the network, capture forensic evidence, and rebuild from a known-good image. This is a potential node compromise.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span><div class="eq-question">What Kubernetes-specific ports MUST be open on a worker node? List them with their default port numbers and which component uses each.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse;"><thead><tr style="border-bottom: 2px solid #326ce5;"><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Port</th><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Component</th><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">10250</td><td style="padding: 8px;">kubelet</td><td style="padding: 8px;">API server → kubelet (logs, exec, metrics)</td></tr><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">10255</td><td style="padding: 8px;">kubelet (read-only)</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Should be DISABLED (readOnlyPort: 0)</td></tr><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">30000-32767</td><td style="padding: 8px;">kube-proxy</td><td style="padding: 8px;">NodePort services</td></tr><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">8472</td><td style="padding: 8px;">CNI (Flannel)</td><td style="padding: 8px;">VXLAN overlay (varies by CNI)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding: 8px;">22</td><td style="padding: 8px;">SSH</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Administrative access</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Worker nodes need minimal open ports. Everything else should be firewalled. The kubelet read-only port (10255) exposes pod info without authentication — it must be disabled. NodePort range (30000-32767) is needed only if you use NodePort services. On the CKS exam, run <code>ss -tlnp</code> and verify only these ports are listening.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span><div class="eq-question">How do you verify the integrity of installed Kubernetes packages? Write the exact command and explain what output indicates tampering.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-bash"># Debian/Ubuntu
dpkg -V kubelet kubeadm kubectl
# Output: nothing = all good. If files are modified:
# ??5?????? c /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf  (config file changed — expected)
# ??5??????   /usr/bin/kubelet           (BINARY changed — INVESTIGATE!)
# RHEL/CentOS
rpm -V kubelet kubeadm kubectl
# Key: S=size, M=mode, 5=MD5, D=device, L=symlink, U=user, G=group, T=mtime</code></pre><p>Missing output = all files pass verification. Any output for binaries (<code>/usr/bin/*</code>) is suspicious. Config files (<code>/etc/*</code>) changing is expected after edits.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Package integrity verification is complementary to AIDE. <code>dpkg -V</code> checks files against the package manager's database (which should be trusted). AIDE checks against its own baseline (which could be compromised if the baseline was created after tampering). Use BOTH — package verification for known-good comparison, AIDE for ongoing monitoring.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span><div class="eq-question">A node has a GPU (NVIDIA) requiring a kernel module. How do you balance the need for the GPU module with the principle of minimizing kernel modules?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Keep the NVIDIA module but blacklist ALL other unnecessary modules. Create a whitelist approach: (1) Identify required modules: <code>lsmod | grep nvidia</code>. (2) Blacklist everything else: create <code>/etc/modprobe.d/blacklist-all.conf</code> with all non-essential modules. (3) Set <code>kernel.modules_disabled=1</code> AFTER loading NVIDIA (can't be undone without reboot, but prevents future module injection). (4) Document the exception with business justification for auditors.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Security is about risk management, not absolute rules. GPU workloads need kernel modules — that's a legitimate business requirement. The principle is: load ONLY what's required, blacklist everything else, and lock down future changes. On the CKS exam, don't blindly blacklist everything — read the scenario. If a pod needs GPU access, blacklisting NVIDIA breaks the workload and you fail the task.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span><div class="eq-question">How do you set up a cron job to run AIDE daily and email results? Write the crontab entry and explain the AIDE flags.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-bash"># Crontab entry (runs at 3 AM daily)
0 3 * * * /usr/bin/aide --check | mail -s "AIDE Report $(hostname) $(date +\%Y-\%m-\%d)" security@easypj.com
# Flags:
# --check: Compare current filesystem against database
# --update: Update database after legitimate changes
# --init: Initialize new database
# --compare: Compare two databases
# After legitimate changes (upgrades), update:
aide --update && cp /var/lib/aide/aide.db.new.gz /var/lib/aide/aide.db.gz</code></pre><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Automated AIDE checks are a CIS benchmark recommendation. The daily cron job provides continuous file integrity monitoring. After any legitimate system change (package upgrade, config edit), run <code>aide --update</code> to update the baseline — otherwise tomorrow's check will report false positives. On the CKS exam, know when to use <code>--check</code> (audit) vs <code>--update</code> (after changes).</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span><div class="eq-question">A node's cloud IAM role has <code>s3:*</code> and <code>ec2:*</code> permissions. What's the security risk for Kubernetes workloads, and how do you mitigate it without changing the node role?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>Risk:</strong> Any pod on this node can reach the cloud metadata service (169.254.169.254) and obtain these IAM credentials. A compromised pod can delete S3 buckets, terminate EC2 instances, or exfiltrate data.</p><p><strong>Mitigation without changing node role:</strong> (1) Block metadata service from pods: iptables rule to DROP 169.254.169.254, or NetworkPolicy egress block. (2) Use IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts) — pods use their own IAM roles, not the node's. (3) Set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> — pods can't authenticate to cloud APIs without explicit credentials. (4) AppArmor profile denying network access to 169.254.169.254.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the Capital One breach scenario. The cloud metadata service is the #1 cloud security risk for Kubernetes. Even if the node role is over-privileged, you can prevent pods from accessing it. On the CKS exam: (1) always block 169.254.169.254 from pods, (2) use IRSA for pod-level IAM, (3) never rely on node-level IAM for pod permissions.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span><div class="eq-question">After hardening a node by removing packages, blacklisting modules, and applying sysctl settings, how do you comprehensively verify the node is production-ready? List your verification checklist.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><div class="process-steps" style="flex-direction: column;"><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Service verification</h5><p><code>systemctl status kubelet containerd</code> — both must be active/running. <code>kubectl get node &lt;name&gt;</code> — must show Ready.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Port audit</h5><p><code>ss -tlnp</code> — only kubelet (10250), SSH (22), and containerd should listen.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Module audit</h5><p><code>lsmod | grep -v -E "overlay|br_netfilter|iptable|ip_vs|nf_|xt_"</code> — verify no unnecessary modules.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><div class="step-content"><h5>AIDE baseline</h5><p><code>aide --init && cp /var/lib/aide/aide.db.new.gz /var/lib/aide/aide.db.gz</code> — establish clean baseline.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Scheduling test</h5><p><code>kubectl run test --image=nginx -n default; kubectl get pod test -o wide</code> — must be Running on the hardened node.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><div class="step-content"><h5>kube-bench re-run</h5><p><code>kube-bench run --targets node</code> — all CIS checks for nodes should PASS.</p></div></div></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This verification checklist is exactly what the CKS exam expects after a hardening task. Don't just make changes — VERIFY them. The most common exam failure: hardening a node but breaking kubelet → node goes NotReady → no points awarded even if the hardening was correct. Always verify the node is still Ready and can schedule pods.</p></div></details></div>
            </div>

