            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 17 · Pod Security Standards — Admission-Level Enforcement</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Before Pod Security Standards (PSS), Kubernetes used PodSecurityPolicy (PSP) — a complex, confusing, and ultimately deprecated mechanism for controlling pod security. PSS, introduced in Kubernetes 1.22 and stable in 1.25, replaces PSP with a <strong>simple, namespace-scoped, label-based system</strong> with three predefined security levels: Privileged, Baseline, and Restricted. PSS is enforced at <strong>admission time</strong> — when a pod is created, the API server checks whether the pod spec violates the namespace's PSS level. If it does, the pod is rejected BEFORE it ever reaches a node. This chapter teaches you to apply the right PSS level to every namespace in your easypj cluster — ensuring that every pod in production runs with Restricted-level security, while system namespaces (kube-system) maintain the flexibility they need.</p>
