            <section class="section-block" id="s17-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">17.1 What Are Pod Security Standards (PSS)?</h3>
                <p>Pod Security Standards define three <strong>security levels</strong> that restrict what pods can do. These levels are cumulative — Baseline includes everything in Privileged plus restrictions, and Restricted includes everything in Baseline plus more. Importantly, PSS is enforced by a <strong>built-in admission controller</strong> (PodSecurity), not by an external webhook. This means it works out of the box on any Kubernetes cluster 1.23+ without installing anything extra.</p>

                <h4>The Three PSS Levels — What Each Allows and Blocks</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">PSS Security Levels — From Most Permissive to Most Restrictive</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  🔴 PRIVILEGED — No Restrictions (least secure)                          │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────                            │
  │  • Allows everything: hostNetwork, hostPID, hostIPC, privileged mode     │
  │  • Allows all Linux capabilities, including dangerous ones               │
  │  • Allows running as root (UID 0)                                        │
  │  • Allows hostPath volumes, /proc mount, /dev access                     │
  │  • Allows custom AppArmor/SELinux/seccomp profiles (or none)             │
  │  ⚡ USE CASE: kube-system pods (CNI, CSI drivers, node exporters)        │
  │  ⚡ NEVER USE: For user-facing workloads like easypj                     │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  🟡 BASELINE — Prevents Known Privilege Escalations (medium)             │
  │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────                   │
  │  • DISABLES: hostNetwork, hostPID, hostIPC (prevents container escape)   │
  │  • DISABLES: privileged containers                                       │
  │  • DISABLES: hostPath volumes (prevents host filesystem access)          │
  │  • DISABLES: CAP_SYS_ADMIN, CAP_NET_RAW, and other dangerous caps        │
  │  • DISABLES: seccompProfile.type: Unconfined (unset/RuntimeDefault OK)    │
  │  • ALLOWS: running as any user (root or non-root)                        │
  │  • ALLOWS: most Linux capabilities                                       │
  │  ⚡ USE CASE: Dev/staging namespaces, workloads being migrated to        │
  │     Restricted that still need flexibility                               │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  🟢 RESTRICTED — Maximum Security (strongly recommended for production)   │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────    │
  │  • ALL Baseline restrictions, PLUS:                                       │
  │  • REQUIRES: runAsNonRoot: true                                           │
  │  • seccomp Unconfined-block: already at BASELINE, not new (see §17.4)    │
  │  • REQUIRES: capabilities: drop: [ALL]                                    │
  │  • RESTRICTS: volume types to ConfigMap, Secret, EmptyDir, PVC, and      │
  │    projected volumes only (no hostPath, no flexVolume, no CSI ephemeral) │
  │  • RESTRICTS: /proc mount type to default only                            │
  │  • REQUIRES: readOnlyRootFilesystem: true (unless exception)              │
  │  ⚡ USE CASE: easypj-prod, any production namespace                       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Precision Correction — Seccomp Is Enforced Starting at BASELINE, Not Only Restricted</h5>
                    <p>The diagram above (and older PSS explainers in general) tend to list seccomp under "what Restricted requires," which is imprecise. Per the official k8s.io Pod Security Standards spec, blocking <code>seccompProfile.type: Unconfined</code> is already enforced at the <strong>Baseline</strong> level — it is NOT a new rule that Restricted adds. What Restricted actually adds on top of Baseline is a short, specific list of only <strong>four</strong> rules. See <a href="#s17-4">§17.4 — Official PSS Field-by-Field Reference</a> below for the exact, official field-by-field breakdown — memorize that table, not the "Restricted = stricter seccomp" shorthand.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>PSS vs PSP — Why the Switch Matters</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>PodSecurityPolicy (PSP - Deprecated)</th><th>Pod Security Standards (PSS - Current)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Status</strong></td><td>❌ Removed in K8s 1.25</td><td>✅ GA since 1.25</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Where applied</strong></td><td>Cluster-wide (RBAC-based)</td><td>Namespace-level (label-based)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Configuration</strong></td><td>Complex YAML with 30+ fields</td><td>Three predefined levels (Privileged/Baseline/Restricted)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Granularity</strong></td><td>Per-user/per-ServiceAccount</td><td>Per-namespace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Built-in</strong></td><td>Required enabling admission plugin</td><td>Built-in PodSecurity admission controller</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Extensibility</strong></td><td>Could create custom policies</td><td>Fixed predefined levels (use OPA/Kyverno for custom)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CKS Exam</strong></td><td>May appear as theory question</td><td>✅ Hands-on — apply labels, fix violations</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Side-by-Side — What Each Level Enforces on a Typical Pod Spec</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Pod Spec Field</th><th>Privileged</th><th>Baseline</th><th>Restricted</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>hostNetwork</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Allowed</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Blocked</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Blocked</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>hostPID / hostIPC</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Allowed</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Blocked</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Blocked</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>privileged: true</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Allowed</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Blocked</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Blocked</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>hostPath volumes</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Allowed</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Blocked</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Blocked</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>runAsNonRoot</strong></td><td style="color:#8b949e;">Not required</td><td style="color:#8b949e;">Not required</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ REQUIRED</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>seccompProfile</strong></td><td style="color:#8b949e;">Any value allowed (incl. Unconfined)</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">⚠️ Unconfined BLOCKED (unset/RuntimeDefault/Localhost OK)</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">⚠️ Same rule as Baseline — not a new addition (§17.4)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>capabilities.drop</strong></td><td style="color:#8b949e;">Not required</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">⚠️ Drops dangerous caps</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ REQUIRED (drop ALL)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>readOnlyRootFilesystem</strong></td><td style="color:#8b949e;">Not required</td><td style="color:#8b949e;">Not required</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ REQUIRED</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Volume types allowed</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">All types</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">⚠️ Most (no hostPath)</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">⚠️ Limited (no hostPath, flexVol, CSI ephemeral)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>How the PodSecurity Admission Controller Works Internally</h4>
                <p>When a pod is created, the PodSecurity admission controller runs as part of the API server's admission chain. It checks the pod spec against the namespace's PSS labels and returns ALLOW or DENY. Understanding this internal logic helps you troubleshoot when pods are unexpectedly rejected or when violations aren't caught:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">PodSecurity Admission Controller — Internal Validation Logic</div>
<pre>
  kubectl apply -f pod.yaml (namespace: easypj-prod)
  │
  ▼
  API SERVER receives the CREATE Pod request
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STEP 1: LOOK UP NAMESPACE LABELS                                        │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────                                        │
  │  The PodSecurity controller reads the namespace object for "easypj-prod":│
  │  labels:                                                                  │
  │    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted                         │
  │    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version: v1.29                      │
  │                                                                           │
  │  STEP 2: DETERMINE THE APPLICABLE POLICY                                  │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────                                │
  │  enforce=restricted → The controller loads the "restricted" policy        │
  │  definition for version v1.29 from its built-in policy library.           │
  │  The policy is a set of CHECKS, not a single rule. Each check validates   │
  │  one aspect of the pod spec.                                              │
  │                                                                           │
  │  STEP 3: RUN EACH CHECK AGAINST THE POD SPEC                              │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────                             │
  │  The controller iterates through ~15 checks for the Restricted level:     │
  │                                                                           │
  │  CHECK 1: runAsNonRoot                                                    │
  │  ─────────────────────                                                    │
  │  Reads: pod.spec.securityContext.runAsNonRoot                             │
  │  AND: container.securityContext.runAsNonRoot (for EACH container)         │
  │  Rule: At least one must be explicitly set to true.                        │
  │  If BOTH are unset → FAIL: "runAsNonRoot != true"                         │
  │                                                                           │
  │  CHECK 2: seccompProfile                                                  │
  │  ────────────────────────                                                  │
  │  Reads: pod.spec.securityContext.seccompProfile.type                      │
  │  AND: container.securityContext.seccompProfile.type                       │
  │  Rule: type must be "RuntimeDefault" or "Localhost".                      │
  │  If type is "Unconfined" or unset → FAIL                                 │
  │                                                                           │
  │  CHECK 3: capabilities                                                    │
  │  ──────────────────────                                                    │
  │  Reads: container.securityContext.capabilities.drop                       │
  │  Rule: drop must include ALL.                                              │
  │  If drop is missing or doesn't include ALL → FAIL                        │
  │                                                                           │
  │  CHECK 4: allowPrivilegeEscalation                                        │
  │  ──────────────────────────────────                                        │
  │  Reads: container.securityContext.allowPrivilegeEscalation                │
  │  Rule: must be explicitly set to false.                                    │
  │  If unset or true → FAIL                                                  │
  │                                                                           │
  │  CHECK 5-15: readOnlyRootFilesystem, volume types, /proc mount type,     │
  │  hostNetwork, hostPID, hostIPC, privileged, hostPath volumes,             │
  │  AppArmor profile, SELinux type, sysctls, procMount...                    │
  │                                                                           │
  │  STEP 4: AGGREGATE RESULTS                                                │
  │  ─────────────────────────                                                │
  │  ALL checks must pass → request is ALLOWED.                               │
  │  ANY check fails → ALL failures are aggregated into a single error        │
  │  message (so you see ALL violations at once, not one at a time).          │
  │                                                                           │
  │  STEP 5: RETURN DECISION                                                  │
  │  ────────────────────────                                                  │
  │  enforce mode: DENY → returns 403 Forbidden with violation details.       │
  │  audit mode: ALLOW → logs violation to audit log.                         │
  │  warn mode: ALLOW → returns warning to kubectl user.                      │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 17.2 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s17-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">17.2 Applying PSS to Namespaces</h3>
                <p>PSS is applied by adding <strong>labels to the Namespace object</strong>. Each label controls a specific enforcement mode for a specific security level. The label format is: <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/&lt;MODE&gt;: &lt;LEVEL&gt;</code>. You can apply multiple modes simultaneously — for example, enforce at Baseline but warn at Restricted to prepare for a future upgrade.</p>

                <h4>The Three Enforcement Modes</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5>enforce</h5>
                        <p>Violations are <strong>REJECTED</strong>. The pod is not created. The API server returns a 403 Forbidden error with details about which fields violated the policy. This is the mode you use in production.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div>
                        <h5>audit</h5>
                        <p>Violations are <strong>LOGGED</strong> to the audit log but the pod is ALLOWED. Use this to discover which pods would fail a stricter level without breaking anything. This is how you prepare to upgrade from Baseline to Restricted.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>warn</h5>
                        <p>Violations trigger a <strong>WARNING</strong> to the user who created the pod (visible in kubectl output), but the pod is ALLOWED. Use this to educate developers without blocking their work.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Namespace Label Examples — All Modes, All Levels</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Applying PSS labels to namespaces</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── PRODUCTION: Enforce Restricted (maximum security) ──
kubectl label namespace easypj-prod \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=v1.29

# ── STAGING: Enforce Baseline, Warn on Restricted ──
kubectl label namespace easypj-staging \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=baseline \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=v1.29

# ── DEV: Audit Restricted (log violations, don't block) ──
kubectl label namespace easypj-dev \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=baseline \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=v1.29

# ── KUBE-SYSTEM: Privileged (system pods need host access) ──
kubectl label namespace kube-system \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=privileged \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=v1.29

# ── VERIFY labels are applied ──
kubectl get namespace easypj-prod --show-labels
# Look for: pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted

# ── REMOVE PSS labels (revert to no enforcement) ──
kubectl label namespace easypj-dev \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce- \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn- \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit-</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Testing PSS — What Happens When a Pod Violates Restricted</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Test that PSS Restricted blocks a non-compliant pod</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Create a namespace with Restricted enforcement
kubectl create namespace pss-test
kubectl label namespace pss-test pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted

# Try to create a pod that runs as root (violates Restricted)
kubectl apply -n pss-test -f - << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: test-root
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:latest
    # ❌ MISSING: runAsNonRoot: true (required by Restricted)
    # ❌ MISSING: seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault (required by Restricted)
    # ❌ MISSING: capabilities: drop: [ALL] (required by Restricted)
EOF

# OUTPUT: Error from server (Forbidden): pods "test-root" is forbidden:
# violates PodSecurity "restricted:v1.29": unrestricted capabilities
# (container "nginx" must set securityContext.capabilities.drop=["ALL"]),
# runAsNonRoot != true (pod or container "nginx" must set
# securityContext.runAsNonRoot=true), seccompProfile (pod or container
# "nginx" must set securityContext.seccompProfile.type to "RuntimeDefault"
# or "Localhost")

# ✅ FIX the pod and try again
kubectl apply -n pss-test -f - << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: test-fixed
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:latest
    securityContext:
      runAsNonRoot: true
      runAsUser: 1000
      capabilities:
        drop: [ALL]
      seccompProfile:
        type: RuntimeDefault
EOF
# Pod created successfully ✅</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The Admission Plugin Chain — Where PSS Fits in the Request Pipeline</h4>
                <p>PSS is just one admission plugin among many. When a pod creation request reaches the API server, it passes through a CHAIN of admission plugins, each with a specific responsibility. Understanding the order helps you troubleshoot: if a pod is rejected, was it PSS? NodeRestriction? An OPA webhook? Here's the exact chain:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pod Creation Admission Chain — Every Plugin in Order</div>
<pre>
  CREATE POD REQUEST ENTERS ADMISSION CHAIN
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  MUTATING PHASE (plugins can MODIFY the pod spec)                        │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────                            │
  │  1. NamespaceLifecycle — Ensures namespace exists, is not terminating.   │
  │  2. MutatingAdmissionWebhook — External webhooks (Istio sidecar inject,  │
  │     Kyverno mutate rules). Can ADD containers, volumes, labels.          │
  │  3. PodSecurity (MUTATING) — Adds default seccompProfile if unset,       │
  │     adds default runAsNonRoot based on namespace policy (if configured). │
  │  ... (other mutating plugins)                                             │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  VALIDATING PHASE (plugins can ACCEPT or REJECT, but NOT modify)         │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────                │
  │  4. PodSecurity (VALIDATING) ← ⬅⬅⬅ PSS CHECKS RUN HERE                   │
  │     Checks the (possibly mutated) pod spec against namespace PSS labels. │
  │     If enforce=restricted → REJECTS violations.                           │
  │  5. NodeRestriction — Ensures kubelets only modify their own pods/nodes. │
  │  6. ValidatingAdmissionWebhook — External webhooks (OPA, Kyverno verify).│
  │  7. ResourceQuota — Ensures namespace resource limits not exceeded.      │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ⚡ KEY INSIGHT: PSS runs AFTER mutating webhooks. This means:
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  • If Istio injects a sidecar, PSS validates the sidecar too.
  • If Kyverno adds a default seccompProfile, PSS sees the default.
  • BUT: If the original pod spec violates Restricted and a mutating webhook
    is supposed to fix it, PSS might reject the pod BEFORE the webhook runs
    (because PSS runs in BOTH mutating and validating phases).
  • The mutating PSS plugin runs FIRST in the mutating phase and can add
    defaults, then the validating PSS plugin runs later to check compliance.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 17.3 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s17-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">17.3 PSS for the easypj Namespace</h3>
                <p>Now let's apply PSS to the easypj namespaces. The production namespace gets Restricted — every pod must run as non-root, with no capabilities, with RuntimeDefault Seccomp. The dev namespace gets Baseline (enforced) with Restricted warnings — developers can work freely but see warnings when their pods would fail production.</p>

                <h4>PSS Level Decision Flowchart — Which Level for Which Namespace?</h4>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="decision-node question">🔍 Does this namespace contain workloads that NEED host-level access? (CNI plugins, CSI drivers, node exporters, monitoring daemons)</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ YES → Use PRIVILEGED. But restrict with NetworkPolicy (Ch6) and RBAC (Ch9) to limit who can deploy to this namespace. Example: kube-system.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">❓ No — the pods are user workloads. Continue.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">🔍 Can ALL containers in this namespace run as non-root? (Dockerfiles have USER 1000 or equivalent)</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">❌ NO — some containers still need root → Use BASELINE (enforce). Add warn=restricted to nudge toward fixing. Example: easypj-dev.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ YES — all containers are non-root ready. Continue.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">🔍 Can ALL containers run with read-only root filesystem, drop ALL capabilities, and RuntimeDefault Seccomp?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">❌ NO → Use BASELINE (enforce) + audit=restricted. Review the audit logs to identify what needs fixing.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">🎉 YES → Use RESTRICTED! This is the maximum security level. All new pods will be validated against it. Example: easypj-prod.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Complete PSS Configuration for easypj Namespaces</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Configure PSS for all easypj namespaces</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
