                <h4>Migrating from Baseline to Restricted — A Real-World Scenario</h4>
                <p>Most organizations start with Baseline and gradually migrate to Restricted. This is not a one-day task — it requires updating Dockerfiles, fixing application code, and testing. Here's the systematic migration playbook:</p>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🟡 SCENARIO: Migrating easypj Namespaces from Baseline to Restricted</h5>
                    <p><strong>Current state:</strong> easypj-prod has enforce=baseline. Pods run as root (UID 0) because the Dockerfile doesn't specify a USER. Seccomp is not configured. Capabilities are not dropped.</p>
                    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> enforce=restricted without breaking production.</p>
                    <p><strong>Migration playbook — 4 phases over 2 sprints:</strong></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li><strong>PHASE 1: DISCOVERY (Sprint 1, Week 1)</strong>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Add <code>audit=restricted</code> and <code>warn=restricted</code> labels to easypj-prod (keep enforce=baseline).</li>
                                <li>For 1 week, collect audit logs: <code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-apiserver-* | grep "would violate PodSecurity"</code></li>
                                <li>Identify ALL pods that would violate Restricted. Document which fields need fixing for each.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li><strong>PHASE 2: FIX (Sprint 1, Week 2)</strong>
                            <ul>
                                <li>For each non-compliant pod, fix the root cause:
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>Dockerfile: Add <code>USER 1000</code> at the end</li>
                                        <li>Deployment YAML: Add <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>, <code>seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault</code>, <code>capabilities: drop: [ALL]</code></li>
                                        <li>Identify writable paths: Add emptyDir or PVC volumes for /tmp, /var/log, etc.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li>Deploy fixes to staging (easypj-staging) first. Verify pods start successfully with enforce=restricted.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li><strong>PHASE 3: ENFORCE (Sprint 2)</strong>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Change easypj-prod label: <code>enforce=restricted</code></li>
                                <li>Roll out fixed deployments one at a time (canary deployment).</li>
                                <li>Monitor: any pod that still violates Restricted will fail to create. Roll back and fix.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li><strong>PHASE 4: LOCK (Ongoing)</strong>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Add OPA/Kyverno policy (Ch24) to verify Dockerfiles have USER directives in CI/CD.</li>
                                <li>Add CI/CD check: every Deployment YAML must pass <code>kubectl apply --dry-run=server</code> against a Restricted namespace.</li>
                                <li>Goal: Prevent non-compliant pods from ever reaching the API server.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                    <p><strong>Common migration pitfalls:</strong></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Forgotten writable directories:</strong> App writes to /var/log/app.log but rootfs is read-only → crash. Fix: mount emptyDir at /var/log.</li>
                        <li><strong>Port binding below 1024:</strong> App binds to port 80 as non-root → EACCES. Fix: use port 8000 or add NET_BIND_SERVICE capability (but then you lose Restricted).</li>
                        <li><strong>Third-party images:</strong> The postgres:15 image runs as root by default. Fix: use postgres:15-alpine which runs as postgres user, or set securityContext explicitly.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 17.4 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s17-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">17.4 Official PSS Field-by-Field Reference — Exact Fields, Exact Values</h3>
                <p>Straight from <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards</a> — the complete, exact field list per level. The exam checks specific fields, not just "is it restricted": memorize this table, not just the concept.</p>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Precision Correction to §17.1</h5>
                    <p>Seccomp restriction (blocking <code>seccompProfile.type: Unconfined</code>) is enforced starting at <strong>BASELINE</strong>, not only at Restricted. What Restricted actually ADDS beyond Baseline is just <strong>four</strong> things: an allowed-volume-types whitelist, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation</code> must be <code>false</code>, <code>runAsNonRoot</code> must be <code>true</code>, and (since v1.23) <code>runAsUser</code> must not be <code>0</code>. Everything else — host namespaces, privileged mode, the capabilities <code>add</code> allowlist, hostPath, host ports, AppArmor, SELinux, procMount, seccomp, and sysctls — is already locked down at Baseline. <strong>Restricted = Baseline + those 4 extra rules.</strong> Nothing more.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Baseline — The Complete List of What It Restricts</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Allowed Value</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Host Namespaces</strong></td><td><code>hostNetwork</code> / <code>hostPID</code> / <code>hostIPC</code>: unset, <code>false</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Privileged Containers</strong></td><td><code>securityContext.privileged</code>: unset, <code>false</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Capabilities (add)</strong></td><td>unset, OR only from: <code>AUDIT_WRITE</code>, <code>CHOWN</code>, <code>DAC_OVERRIDE</code>, <code>FOWNER</code>, <code>FSETID</code>, <code>KILL</code>, <code>MKNOD</code>, <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code>, <code>SETFCAP</code>, <code>SETGID</code>, <code>SETPCAP</code>, <code>SETUID</code>, <code>SYS_CHROOT</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>HostPath Volumes</strong></td><td><code>spec.volumes[*].hostPath</code>: unset</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Host Ports</strong></td><td><code>containers[*].ports[*].hostPort</code>: unset, <code>0</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>AppArmor</strong></td><td>unset, <code>RuntimeDefault</code>, <code>Localhost</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>SELinux type</strong></td><td>unset, <code>container_t</code>, <code>container_init_t</code>, <code>container_kvm_t</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>/proc Mount Type</strong></td><td><code>procMount</code>: unset, <code>Default</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Seccomp</strong></td><td><code>seccompProfile.type</code>: unset, <code>RuntimeDefault</code>, <code>Localhost</code> — <strong>(<code>Unconfined</code> is BLOCKED here)</strong></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Sysctls</strong></td><td>unset, OR only from the kernel/net.ipv4 safe list</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Restricted — ONLY the 4 Rules It Adds On Top of Everything in Baseline</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Required Value</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Volume Types</strong></td><td>MUST be one of: <code>configMap</code>, <code>csi</code>, <code>downwardAPI</code>, <code>emptyDir</code>, <code>ephemeral</code>, <code>persistentVolumeClaim</code>, <code>projected</code>, <code>secret</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>allowPrivilegeEscalation</strong></td><td>MUST be <code>false</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>runAsNonRoot</strong></td><td>MUST be <code>true</code> (pod OR every container)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>runAsUser</strong> (v1.23+)</td><td>MUST NOT be <code>0</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Admission Label Syntax — Exact Format</h4>
                <p>Every PSS namespace label follows the same three-part pattern. Get the MODE/LEVEL vocabulary exact — the exam grades on exact label keys and values:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — PSS admission label syntax</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># Format:
#   pod-security.kubernetes.io/&lt;MODE&gt;: &lt;LEVEL&gt;
#
#   MODE  = enforce | audit | warn
#   LEVEL = privileged | baseline | restricted

apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: easypj-prod
  labels:
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 How to Use This Table on the Exam</h5>
                    <p>When you're handed a pod spec and told "make this pass Restricted," don't guess — walk the Restricted table's four rows first (volume types, allowPrivilegeEscalation, runAsNonRoot, runAsUser), then confirm the Baseline table's ten rows are already satisfied (they usually are, since most stock images only violate host namespaces, privileged mode, or seccomp — all Baseline-level fixes). Fixing "Restricted" is really fixing "Baseline + 4," not a mysterious extra-strict standard.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

