            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch17-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>PSS defines three cumulative levels — Privileged, Baseline, Restricted</strong> — enforced by the built-in PodSecurity admission controller at pod creation time; no external webhook or extra install is needed on Kubernetes 1.23+ (17.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>PSS is applied via labels on the Namespace object, never on the pod</strong> — <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/&lt;mode&gt;: &lt;level&gt;</code> with three independent modes (enforce blocks, audit logs, warn nudges) that can run simultaneously for a safe gradual rollout (17.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Real cluster mapping for easypj:</strong> easypj-prod → Restricted, easypj-dev → Baseline (enforce) + Restricted (warn/audit) while it's migrated, kube-system → Privileged with compensating NetworkPolicy/RBAC controls (17.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>PRECISION FACT (17.4):</strong> Restricted is NOT "Baseline plus stricter seccomp." Seccomp's Unconfined-block is already enforced at Baseline. Restricted's actual four additions are: an allowed-volume-types whitelist, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>, <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>, and (v1.23+) <code>runAsUser</code> ≠ 0 — memorize the official field table, not the vague "stricter" narrative.</li>
                    <li><strong>PSA only evaluates pods at creation/update time</strong> — raising a namespace's enforcement level does NOT kill already-running pods, but they'll be BLOCKED the next time they restart if still non-compliant. Always fix pods BEFORE flipping to enforce, using the audit → warn → enforce rollout pattern.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch17-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 17 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Restricted ≠ Baseline:</strong> Restricted adds the non-root requirement, the volume-type whitelist, allowPrivilegeEscalation=false, and runAsUser≠0. Most existing deployments that pass Baseline will FAIL Restricted — you MUST update pod specs before applying Restricted.</li>
                    <li><strong>PSS labels are per-namespace, not per-pod:</strong> You can't apply PSS to a single pod. It applies to ALL pods in the namespace. If you need per-pod granularity, use a ValidatingWebhook (OPA/Kyverno) instead of PSS.</li>
                    <li><strong>warn/audit don't block:</strong> <code>enforce</code> blocks, <code>warn</code> shows a warning in kubectl output, <code>audit</code> logs to audit log. Always start with warn+audit, verify no violations, THEN enforce.</li>
                    <li><strong>"Seccomp is Restricted-only" is a common exam misconception:</strong> Unconfined seccomp is already blocked at Baseline (§17.4). Don't waste exam time adding a seccompProfile fix when the real reason a pod fails Restricted is one of the four genuine Restricted-only rules (volume type, allowPrivilegeEscalation, runAsNonRoot, runAsUser).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch17-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
