            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch17-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 17 — Lab Q&amp;A (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">11 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What single kubectl command enforces PSS Restricted on namespace "prod-ns"? Write the exact command.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl label ns prod-ns pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted
kubectl label ns prod-ns pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=v1.30</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name three pod spec fields that will cause a pod to be rejected by PSS Restricted.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(1) Missing <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> — pod could run as root, (2) <code>hostPID: true</code> or <code>hostNetwork: true</code> — breaks pod isolation, (3) <code>privileged: true</code> — grants all capabilities + device access, (4) <code>capabilities.add</code> (any) — Restricted requires drop ALL, (5) <code>hostPath</code> volumes — direct node filesystem access.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're migrating from PSS Baseline to Restricted. What's the safest rollout strategy?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Phase 1: Add <code>audit=restricted</code> label — violations logged but pods still deploy. Phase 2: Add <code>warn=restricted</code> — developers see warnings. Phase 3: After all violating pods are fixed, add <code>enforce=restricted</code>. Never jump straight to enforce — existing non-compliant pods will be blocked from restarting.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between PSS Baseline and PSS Restricted? Give 2 examples of pods that would pass Baseline but fail Restricted.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Baseline prevents KNOWN privilege escalations (privileged containers, hostPath, hostNetwork, and — precisely — Unconfined seccomp). Restricted adds only four further rules on top: an allowed-volume-types whitelist, mandatory <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>, mandatory <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>, and (v1.23+) <code>runAsUser</code> ≠ 0. Pods that pass Baseline but fail Restricted: (1) A pod running as root (no <code>runAsNonRoot</code>) — Baseline allows root, Restricted doesn't. (2) A pod using a <code>hostPath</code>-adjacent volume type Restricted excludes, or missing <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> — both pass Baseline but are Restricted-only rules.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span><div class="eq-question">Write the PSS labels that audit, warn, AND enforce the Baseline standard on the "easypj-prod" namespace. What's the purpose of all three?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl label ns easypj-prod pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=baseline
kubectl label ns easypj-prod pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=baseline
kubectl label ns easypj-prod pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=baseline
kubectl label ns easypj-prod pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=v1.30</code></pre><p><strong>Purpose:</strong> enforce (blocks), audit (logs to audit log), warn (shows kubectl warning). All three together provide defense in depth: even if enforce is accidentally removed, audit and warn still provide visibility.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The three-label pattern is the CKS best practice. <code>enforce</code> provides the actual security. <code>audit</code> creates an audit trail for compliance. <code>warn</code> provides immediate feedback to developers. On the exam, always apply all three — it shows you understand defense in depth for policy enforcement.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span><div class="eq-question">A namespace has enforce=restricted. A pod with <code>runAsNonRoot: false</code> is submitted. What happens? What about a pod with <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> but no seccompProfile?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>runAsNonRoot: false → REJECTED.</strong> The pod is blocked at admission time with: <code>violates PodSecurity "restricted:v1.30": "runAsNonRoot != true"</code>.</p><p><strong>runAsNonRoot: true, no seccompProfile → REJECTED.</strong> The namespace's enforcement level is Restricted, but the seccomp rule being violated is actually the BASELINE rule (Unconfined-block applies from Baseline up) — an explicitly-unset seccompProfile does NOT trigger it (unset is allowed at both Baseline and Restricted); this rejection only fires if <code>seccompProfile.type</code> is explicitly set to <code>Unconfined</code>. If your pod is rejected purely on seccomp grounds under Restricted, re-check §17.4 — you may be looking at a different one of the four genuine Restricted-only rules instead.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a common CKS exam trap, and also the exact §17.1/§17.4 precision correction: don't assume every Restricted rejection is a "missing seccomp" problem. Walk the official Restricted table (§17.4: volume types, allowPrivilegeEscalation, runAsNonRoot, runAsUser) first, then the Baseline table, to find the real violated field.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span><div class="eq-question">What is the difference between PSS (Pod Security Standards) and PSA (Pod Security Admission)? Which is the policy definition and which is the enforcement mechanism?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>PSS (Pod Security Standards):</strong> The POLICY DEFINITIONS — Privileged, Baseline, Restricted. These are documents that define what's allowed at each level. <strong>PSA (Pod Security Admission):</strong> The ENFORCEMENT MECHANISM — a built-in admission controller that enforces PSS. PSA reads the namespace labels and rejects pods that violate the specified standard. Think of it as: PSS = the law, PSA = the police.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This distinction is tested on CKS. You can't "install PSS" — you enable PSA (the admission controller) and then apply PSS labels to namespaces. The admission controller <code>PodSecurity</code> must be in <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code>. If PSA isn't enabled, the PSS labels on namespaces have zero effect.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span><div class="eq-question">A namespace has enforce=restricted. A DaemonSet for node monitoring needs <code>hostNetwork: true</code> to collect node metrics. How do you handle this without removing Restricted from the namespace?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Option 1: Place the DaemonSet in a separate namespace (e.g., <code>node-monitoring</code>) with enforce=privileged but strong RBAC and NetworkPolicy. Option 2: Use <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=privileged</code> on the monitoring namespace but add compensating controls: AppArmor profile, custom Seccomp, readOnlyRootFilesystem. Option 3: Use a mutating admission webhook (Kyverno) to exempt specific pods from PSA enforcement. The key: you CANNOT make exceptions within a Restricted namespace — PSS is all-or-nothing per namespace.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>PSS is intentionally rigid — no exceptions within a namespace. This forces proper namespace segmentation. Privileged workloads go in privileged namespaces with compensating controls. On the CKS exam, the answer to "how to allow this privileged pod in a Restricted namespace" is always: "create a separate namespace with appropriate controls."</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span><div class="eq-question">Write the pod securityContext that satisfies ALL Restricted requirements. Include runAsNonRoot, seccompProfile, capabilities, and readOnlyRootFilesystem.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-yaml">securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000
  runAsGroup: 3000
  fsGroup: 2000
  seccompProfile:
    type: RuntimeDefault
containers:
- name: app
  image: nginx
  securityContext:
    allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
    capabilities:
      drop:
      - ALL
    readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code></pre><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the golden pod securityContext for CKS. Every field serves a purpose: <code>runAsNonRoot</code> prevents root, <code>seccompProfile</code> restricts syscalls, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> prevents setuid binaries, <code>capabilities.drop: [ALL]</code> strips all Linux capabilities, <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code> prevents writing to the container image. Memorize this block — it's used in multiple CKS tasks.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span><div class="eq-question">You notice that after applying enforce=baseline to the "default" namespace, existing pods still run but new pods that violate Baseline are rejected. Why are existing pods not affected?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>PSA only evaluates pods at <strong>creation time</strong> (CREATE and UPDATE operations). Existing pods were created before the label was applied and are grandfathered in. They continue to run even if they violate the policy. PSA does NOT enforce against already-running pods. This is why gradual rollout (audit → warn → enforce) is critical — you need time to fix existing pods before enforcement blocks their restarts.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the most important operational detail about PSA. On the CKS exam: (1) applying enforce=restricted does NOT kill running pods, (2) but if those pods restart (node failure, rolling update, eviction), they will be BLOCKED from restarting, (3) always fix existing pods BEFORE applying enforce. Use <code>kubectl get pods -n &lt;ns&gt; -o json | jq '.items[] | select(...violates...)'</code> to identify pods that need fixing.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q11</span><div class="eq-question">Per the official PSS field reference (§17.4), what EXACTLY does Restricted add on top of Baseline — and what's the most common exam misconception this corrects?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Restricted adds exactly four rules on top of everything already enforced at Baseline: (1) an allowed-volume-types whitelist (configMap, csi, downwardAPI, emptyDir, ephemeral, persistentVolumeClaim, projected, secret), (2) <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation</code> must be <code>false</code>, (3) <code>runAsNonRoot</code> must be <code>true</code>, (4) since v1.23, <code>runAsUser</code> must not be <code>0</code>. Everything else — host namespaces, privileged mode, the capabilities add-allowlist, hostPath, host ports, AppArmor, SELinux, procMount, <strong>seccomp</strong>, and sysctls — is already locked down at Baseline.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The most common exam misconception this corrects: assuming seccomp (blocking <code>Unconfined</code>) is a Restricted-only rule. It isn't — it's already enforced at Baseline. If a pod fails against a Restricted namespace, the field actually causing the failure is almost always one of the real four additions (most often missing <code>runAsNonRoot</code> or a disallowed volume type), not seccomp. Memorizing the official two-table field list (§17.4) instead of the informal "Restricted = stricter" narrative is what separates a fast, correct fix from wasted exam time.</p></div></details></div>
            </div>

