                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Privileged vs Baseline vs Restricted — What Each Allows</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────────┐ ┌───────────────────┐
  │  🔴 PRIVILEGED       │ │  🟡 BASELINE         │ │  🟢 RESTRICTED    │
  │  (Most permissive)   │ │  (Moderate security) │ │  (Maximum security)│
  ├─────────────────────┤ ├─────────────────────┤ ├───────────────────┤
  │                     │ │                     │ │                    │
  │  ✅ Allowed:        │ │  ❌ Blocked:        │ │  ❌ Blocked:       │
  │  • root user        │ │  • hostNetwork      │ │  • ALL in Baseline │
  │  • ALL caps         │ │  • hostPID          │ │  • runAsNonRoot=   │
  │  • hostNetwork      │ │  • hostIPC          │ │    false           │
  │  • hostPID          │ │  • privileged=true  │ │  • volume types:   │
  │  • hostIPC          │ │  • HostPath vols    │ │    HostPath, NFS,  │
  │  • ANY volume type  │ │  • SELinux options  │ │    etc.             │
  │  • ALL seccomp      │ │  • ProcMount=Unmask │ │  • allowPrivEsc=   │
  │  • ALL AppArmor     │ │  • AppArmor options │ │    false required  │
  │                     │ │                     │ │  • runAsUser       │
  │  Use for:           │ │  Use for:           │ │    != 0 (no root)  │
  │  • kube-system pods │ │  • dev namespaces   │ │                    │
  │  • CNI plugins      │ │  • trusted workloads│ │                    │
  │  • storage drivers  │ │                     │ │                    │
  │                     │ │                     │ │  Use for:           │
  │  ⚠️  NEVER use       │ │  ✅ Good starting   │ │  • production apps │
  │  for user apps      │ │  point for apps     │ │  • easypj-prod     │
  └─────────────────────┘ └─────────────────────┘ └───────────────────┘

  ⚠️  NOTE (§17.4 precision correction): Baseline's "❌ Blocked" list above
  also already includes Unconfined seccomp — it is not exclusive to
  Restricted. Restricted's real additions are the 4 rules in §17.4.

  🔒 ENFORCEMENT MODES (can combine all three):
  enforce = block the pod (hard stop)  │  audit = log violation only
  warn = show warning in API response  │  (use audit+warn first!)
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Pod Security Standards (§17.1) give you three cumulative security levels — Privileged (no limits, kube-system only), Baseline (blocks host access, privileged mode, and Unconfined seccomp), and Restricted (Baseline plus exactly four more rules) — all enforced automatically by the built-in PodSecurity admission controller the moment a pod is submitted. You turn a level on with namespace labels (§17.2): three independent modes — enforce, audit, warn — so you can see violations before you ever block anything.</p>
                    <p><strong>The takeaway:</strong> in a real cluster (§17.3) that mapping is concrete — easypj-prod runs Restricted, easypj-dev runs Baseline with Restricted warnings while it's migrated, and kube-system stays Privileged behind NetworkPolicy and RBAC. And the fact that closes the loop (§17.4): Restricted is not a vague "stricter" tier — it is Baseline plus exactly four named rules, because seccomp's Unconfined-block was already locked down at Baseline. Know the two official tables, and every "fix this pod for Restricted" task becomes a checklist, not a guess.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>You are here:</strong> Step 17 — Minimize Microservice Vulnerabilities: Pod Security Standards. Next: Chapter 18 — Security Contexts.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
