            <section class="section-block" id="s18-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.1 What Is a Security Context?</h3>
                <p>A SecurityContext is a section of the Kubernetes pod/container spec that defines <strong>privilege and access control settings</strong> for the pod and its containers. It can be set at two levels: <strong>pod-level</strong> (applies to all containers) and <strong>container-level</strong> (overrides pod-level for that specific container). The fields control everything from the Linux UID the process runs as, to which Linux capabilities are available, to whether the filesystem is writable:</p>

                <h4>SecurityContext Fields — Complete Annotated Reference</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Every SecurityContext Field — Pod-Level and Container-Level</div>
<pre>
  apiVersion: v1
  kind: Pod
  spec:
    # ── POD-LEVEL securityContext (applies to ALL containers) ──
    securityContext:
      runAsNonRoot: true          # ❌ Refuse to start if container runs as UID 0
      runAsUser: 1000             # 👤 Linux UID for ALL containers
      runAsGroup: 1000            # 👥 Linux GID for ALL containers
      fsGroup: 1000               # 📁 Filesystem group for volume ownership
      fsGroupChangePolicy: "OnRootMismatch"  # 🔄 When to chown volumes
      seccompProfile:             # 🔍 Syscall filter for ALL containers
        type: RuntimeDefault
      supplementalGroups: [2000]  # 👥 Additional Linux groups

    containers:
    - name: easypj
      # ── CONTAINER-LEVEL securityContext (overrides pod-level) ──
      securityContext:
        runAsNonRoot: true              # ❌ (container-specific)
        runAsUser: 1000                 # 👤 Override pod-level UID
        allowPrivilegeEscalation: false # ⬆️  Block setuid binaries
        capabilities:                   # 🧢 Linux capabilities
          drop: [ALL]                   #    Remove ALL (best practice)
          add: [NET_BIND_SERVICE]       #    Add back only what's needed
        readOnlyRootFilesystem: true    # 🧊 Make / read-only
        privileged: false               # 🚫 NOT a privileged container
        procMount: "Default"            # 📂 /proc mount type (Default or Unmasked)
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pod-Level vs Container-Level — Inheritance Rules</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Pod-Level securityContext</h5><p>Applies to ALL containers. Set common security settings here: runAsUser, fsGroup, seccompProfile. Every container inherits these unless it explicitly overrides them.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div><h5>Container-Level securityContext</h5><p>Overrides pod-level for that specific container. Use for container-specific settings: different UID, specific capabilities, readOnlyRootFilesystem (init containers may need write access).</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 18.2 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.2 Running as Non-Root — The Most Important Setting</h3>
                <p>If you take ONE thing from this entire guide, make it this: <strong>never run containers as root.</strong> Root in the container is the same UID 0 as root on the host. If a container escape occurs (kernel exploit, misconfigured volume, runtime bug), the attacker lands on the host as root — able to read every file, modify every process, and pivot to every other node. Running as non-root means the attacker lands as an unprivileged user — massively limiting what they can do even after a successful escape.</p>

                <h4>Root vs Non-Root — What Changes After a Container Escape</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left" style="flex:1;">
                        <div class="info-box danger">
                            <h5>🔴 ROOT CONTAINER (UID 0)</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Attacker escapes to host → lands as UID 0 (root)</li>
                                <li>Can read /etc/shadow, /var/lib/etcd, all Secrets</li>
                                <li>Can install kernel modules, rootkits, backdoors</li>
                                <li>Can modify system binaries (replace /usr/bin/kubectl)</li>
                                <li>Can access all host network interfaces</li>
                                <li>Can kill any process, including kubelet and containerd</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel-right" style="flex:1;">
                        <div class="info-box tip">
                            <h5>🟢 NON-ROOT CONTAINER (UID 1000)</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Attacker escapes to host → lands as UID 1000</li>
                                <li>Cannot read /etc/shadow (mode 000, root:shadow)</li>
                                <li>Cannot install kernel modules (needs CAP_SYS_MODULE)</li>
                                <li>Cannot modify system binaries (/usr/bin is root-owned)</li>
                                <li>Cannot open raw sockets (needs CAP_NET_RAW)</li>
                                <li>Must find a LOCAL PRIVILEGE ESCALATION exploit first</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Configuring Non-Root — The Pod Spec and Dockerfile Must Match</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML + Dockerfile — Non-root pod spec with matching Dockerfile USER</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ── POD SPEC ──
spec:
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true    # Kubernetes REFUSES to start if container is root
    runAsUser: 1000       # The Linux UID to run as
    runAsGroup: 1000      # The Linux GID to run as
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    image: registry.easypj.com/easypj-django:v2.0.0</code></pre>
                    <pre><code class="language-dockerfile"># ── DOCKERFILE (must match the UID in pod spec) ──
FROM python:3.12-slim

# Create a non-root user with the SAME UID as the pod spec
RUN groupadd -r -g 1000 easypj && useradd -r -u 1000 -g easypj easypj

# ... install dependencies, copy code ...

# ⬅⬅⬅ THIS IS CRITICAL: must be at the END of the Dockerfile
USER 1000

# ⚡ IMPORTANT: USER must come AFTER any commands that need root
# (apt-get, pip install --system, chown, mkdir in /etc)
# All COPY commands after USER will be owned by UID 1000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Why Root-in-Container = Root-on-Host — The UID Namespace Problem</h4>
                <p>This is the most misunderstood concept in container security. Linux UIDs are <strong>global within a user namespace</strong>. By default, a container shares the host's user namespace — meaning UID 0 inside the container IS UID 0 on the host. Docker/containerd can remap container UIDs to different host UIDs via <strong>user namespace remapping</strong>, but Kubernetes does not enable this by default. Until user namespace support becomes GA in Kubernetes (alpha in 1.25, beta in 1.27, GA targeted for 1.30), the ONLY protection is <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> + <code>runAsUser: &gt;0</code>:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">UID Mapping — With and Without User Namespaces</div>
<pre>
  WITHOUT USER NAMESPACES (Kubernetes default, all versions through 1.29):
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Container UID space = Host UID space (shared user namespace)

  Container: UID 0 (root) ───→ Host: UID 0 (root) — SAME USER
  Container: UID 1000       ───→ Host: UID 1000      — SAME USER

  ⚡ RESULT: If the container process escapes to the host, it keeps the
     SAME UID. Root in container = root on host. No translation occurs.

  WITH USER NAMESPACES (available in containerd, coming to Kubernetes):
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Container UID space is REMAPPED to a RANGE of high host UIDs:

  Container: UID 0 (root) ───→ Host: UID 65536 (unprivileged)
  Container: UID 1000       ───→ Host: UID 66536 (unprivileged)

  ⚡ RESULT: Even if the container runs as "root" (UID 0 inside the
     container), on the HOST it is UID 65536 — an unprivileged user.
     Container escape lands as an unprivileged user automatically.

  CURRENT STATE (Kubernetes 1.29):
  ────────────────────────────────
  • User namespaces in K8s are BETA (feature gate: UserNamespacesSupport)
  • Most production clusters run WITHOUT user namespace remapping
  • THEREFORE: runAsNonRoot: true + runAsUser: >0 is ESSENTIAL
  • When user namespaces become GA, runAsNonRoot is less critical —
    but still a best practice (defense in depth)
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 18.3 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.3 Linux Capabilities — Dropping What You Don't Need</h3>
                <p>Linux capabilities break the monolithic "root" privilege into ~40 granular permissions. Instead of "root can do anything," capabilities say "this process can bind to low ports, but cannot load kernel modules." By default, Docker/containerd grants containers a set of ~14 capabilities. PSS Restricted requires dropping ALL of them. A Django web application needs <strong>zero</strong> additional capabilities — it binds to port 8000 (above 1024, so no CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE needed), doesn't modify network settings, and doesn't touch kernel parameters.</p>

                <h4>Dangerous Capabilities — What They Allow, Why You Drop Them</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>What It Allows</th><th>Attack Scenario</th><th>Needed by easypj?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(50,108,229,0.06);"><td><strong>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</strong></td><td>Mount filesystems, swapon, setdomainname, and dozens of other privileged ops</td><td>Attacker mounts host filesystem into container, reads /etc/shadow</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ NEVER</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(50,108,229,0.06);"><td><strong>CAP_NET_RAW</strong></td><td>Create raw sockets (craft arbitrary packets)</td><td>Attacker runs ARP spoofing, TCP hijacking, or network scanning from inside container</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ NEVER</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(50,108,229,0.06);"><td><strong>CAP_SYS_PTRACE</strong></td><td>Trace arbitrary processes, read their memory</td><td>Attacker attaches to kubelet process, reads sensitive data from its memory</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ NEVER</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(50,108,229,0.06);"><td><strong>CAP_SYS_MODULE</strong></td><td>Load/unload kernel modules</td><td>Attacker loads a malicious kernel module that hides their processes</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ NEVER</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(50,108,229,0.06);"><td><strong>CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE</strong></td><td>Bypass file permission checks</td><td>Attacker reads /etc/shadow despite mode 000</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ NEVER</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(251,191,36,0.06);"><td><strong>CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE</strong></td><td>Bind to ports below 1024</td><td>Not dangerous by itself, but unnecessary if app uses port 8000+</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">⚠️ Only if port < 1024</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(76,175,80,0.06);"><td><strong>CAP_CHOWN</strong></td><td>Change file ownership</td><td>Minor — mostly needed for init containers that set up volume permissions</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">⚠️ Init containers only</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>The drop ALL + add-back Pattern</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Drop ALL capabilities, add back only what's needed</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">securityContext:
  capabilities:
    drop: [ALL]                    # Remove ALL 14 default capabilities
    add: []                        # easypj Django needs NONE
    # If the app needed NET_BIND_SERVICE:
    # add: [NET_BIND_SERVICE]      # Add back ONLY this one</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How the Kernel Checks Capabilities — The Internals That Make drop: [ALL] Work</h4>
                <p>When a containerized process attempts a privileged operation, the Linux kernel does NOT check "is this root?" It checks "does this process have the required capability in its capability bounding set?" Understanding this mechanism explains why <code>drop: [ALL]</code> is so effective — it strips every capability from every set, making even UID 0 processes unable to perform privileged operations:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Linux Capability Sets — What drop: [ALL] Actually Removes</div>
<pre>
  EVERY LINUX PROCESS HAS 5 CAPABILITY SETS:
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────

  1. PERMITTED SET (pP')
     • Capabilities the process is ALLOWED to use.
     • Can be added to the Effective set when needed.
     • drop: [ALL] clears this set completely.

  2. EFFECTIVE SET (pE)
     • Capabilities the process is USING RIGHT NOW.
     • The kernel checks this set on every privileged syscall.
     • If CAP_SYS_ADMIN is not in pE → mount() syscall returns EPERM.

  3. BOUNDING SET (pB)
     • Ultimate limit — no capability can ever be in pP' if not in pB.
     • drop: [ALL] clears this set. Once cleared, CANNOT be restored.
     • Even a setuid-root binary cannot regain capabilities outside pB.

  4. INHERITABLE SET (pI)
     • Capabilities passed to child processes across execve().
     • Cleared by drop: [ALL] — children get no capabilities.

  5. AMBIENT SET (pA)
     • Capabilities preserved across execve() of non-setuid binaries.
     • Allows unprivileged programs to retain specific capabilities.
     • Cleared by allowPrivilegeEscalation: false.

  WHAT HAPPENS WITH drop: [ALL]:
  ────────────────────────────
  Before: pP' = {CHOWN, DAC_OVERRIDE, NET_BIND_SERVICE, SYS_ADMIN, ...} (14 caps)
  After:  pP' = {} — ALL sets are empty
           pE = {}
           pB = {}
           pI = {}
           pA = {}

  Root (UID 0) with empty capability sets:
  ───────────────────────────────────────
  • Can read/write any file (DAC — UID 0 still bypasses file permissions)
  • Can send signals to any process
  • But CANNOT: mount filesystems, load kernel modules, change network
    settings, bind to ports < 1024, change system time, or any other
    operation that requires a specific capability.

  ⚡ THIS IS WHY drop: [ALL] + runAsNonRoot: true is the golden combination:
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  • Non-root: cannot bypass file permissions (DAC restricts access)
  • No capabilities: cannot perform ANY privileged kernel operation
  • Combined effect: The process is a normal Unix user with ZERO special powers
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Root vs Non-Root Container — Security Impact Comparison</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="split-side split-bad">
                        <h5>❌ ROOT CONTAINER</h5>
                        <p><code>securityContext: {}</code><br>→ runs as UID 0 (root)</p>
                        <p><strong>Container escape → root on node:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Can read ALL files on host</li>
                            <li>Can install rootkits</li>
                            <li>Can modify system files</li>
                            <li>Can access host network</li>
                            <li>Can load kernel modules</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="split-side split-good">
                        <h5>✅ NON-ROOT CONTAINER</h5>
                        <p><code>runAsNonRoot: true<br>runAsUser: 1000<br>runAsGroup: 1000</code></p>
                        <p><strong>Container escape → UID 1000 on node:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Very limited permissions</li>
                            <li>Cannot install rootkits</li>
                            <li>Cannot modify system files</li>
                            <li>Cannot access host network</li>
                            <li>Cannot load kernel modules</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 18.4 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.4 Privilege Escalation — The allowPrivilegeEscalation Flag</h3>
                <p><code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> prevents a process from gaining more privileges than its parent process through <strong>setuid binaries</strong> or other privilege escalation mechanisms. A setuid binary (like <code>/usr/bin/passwd</code>) runs with the privileges of the file owner (typically root), not the user who executed it. If an attacker finds a vulnerable setuid binary inside the container, they can exploit it to gain root — even if the container started as non-root. Setting this flag to false blocks ALL setuid execution.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Block privilege escalation in the container</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">securityContext:
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false  # Block setuid binaries
  # Also blocks: file capabilities, ambient capabilities
  # and any other mechanism that would increase privileges</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Precision Note — allowPrivilegeEscalation: false Is Not Always Honored</h5>
                    <p>Setting this field here only takes effect on a container that is NOT <code>privileged: true</code> and does NOT hold <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code>. If either condition is true, the effective value is silently forced back to <code>true</code> — no error, no admission warning, your YAML still reads <code>false</code>. This is why "drop ALL capabilities" and "allowPrivilegeEscalation: false" must always be applied TOGETHER, never either alone. See <a href="#s18-7">§18.7</a> for the exact rule straight from the official docs.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 18.5 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.5 Read-Only Root Filesystem — Immutability in Practice</h3>
                <p><code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> makes the container's root filesystem (/) <strong>read-only</strong>. The container cannot write to any path that is part of the image — no modifying binaries, no creating files in /etc, no writing logs to /var/log. This enforces the <strong>immutability principle</strong>: containers should not change after they start. If a container needs to write, it must use explicitly mounted volumes (emptyDir, PVC, ConfigMap) — which are visible and auditable.</p>

                <h4>Making easypj Django Work with Read-Only Root Filesystem</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Identify writable paths and mount volumes for each</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    securityContext:
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true  # / is READ-ONLY
    volumeMounts:
    - name: tmp
      mountPath: /tmp               # ✅ Writable: session files, upload staging
    - name: media
      mountPath: /app/media         # ✅ Writable: user-uploaded files
    - name: staticfiles
      mountPath: /app/staticfiles   # ✅ Writable: collected static files
    - name: cache
      mountPath: /var/cache/nginx   # ✅ Writable: HTTP cache
  volumes:
  - name: tmp
    emptyDir: {}                    # Ephemeral — cleared on pod restart
  - name: media
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: easypj-media       # Persistent — survives pod restarts
  - name: staticfiles
    emptyDir: {}
  - name: cache
    emptyDir:
      sizeLimit: 256Mi              # Limited size to prevent disk fill</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Container Escape & Defense Tree — 6 Attack Paths, 12 Defenses</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Every Escape Path Blocked by at Least Two Layers</div>
<pre>
  🔴 ATTACKER INSIDE CONTAINER — tries to escape to host node

  PATH 1: Exploit kernel vulnerability
  Attacker runs: exploit_linux_kernel.py
  → Needs dangerous syscalls (ptrace, mount, kexec_load)
  🛡️  STOPPED BY: Seccomp profile blocks those syscalls
  🛡️  STOPPED BY: gVisor/Kata sandbox (different kernel)

  PATH 2: Mount host filesystem
  Attacker runs: mount /dev/sda1 /mnt/host → reads /etc/shadow
  🛡️  STOPPED BY: cap drop ALL (no CAP_SYS_ADMIN)
  🛡️  STOPPED BY: privileged: false (no host mounts)

  PATH 3: Access host network
  Attacker runs: tcpdump on host interface → sniffs other pods
  🛡️  STOPPED BY: hostNetwork: false (pod has own namespace)
  🛡️  STOPPED BY: cap drop ALL (no CAP_NET_RAW)

  PATH 4: Write malicious binary
  Attacker writes: /usr/bin/backdoored-gunicorn
  🛡️  STOPPED BY: readOnlyRootFilesystem: true (write fails)
  🛡️  STOPPED BY: AppArmor blocks writes to /usr/bin/

  PATH 5: setuid privilege escalation
  Attacker runs: /bin/setuid-root-backdoor → child runs as root
  🛡️  STOPPED BY: allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
  🛡️  STOPPED BY: runAsNonRoot: true (pod starts non-root)

  PATH 6: Access Docker socket
  Attacker mounts: /var/run/docker.sock → creates privileged container
  🛡️  STOPPED BY: never mount docker.sock in user pods
  🛡️  STOPPED BY: PSS Restricted (no hostPath)

  🏁  Every escape path blocked by at least TWO layers.
      Defense-in-depth: even if one layer fails, another stops it.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 18.6 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.6 Complete Security Context for easypj — The Hardened Pod Spec</h3>
                <p>Here is the complete security-hardened pod spec for the easypj Django application. Every field is explained with its security purpose. This is the reference configuration that satisfies PSS Restricted and represents the gold standard for production pod security:</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Complete hardened pod spec for easypj Django</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-django
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  replicas: 3
  template:
    spec:
      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      # POD-LEVEL SECURITY CONTEXT (applies to all containers)
      # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      securityContext:
        runAsNonRoot: true            # ❌ Block root containers
        runAsUser: 1000               # 👤 Django runs as UID 1000
        runAsGroup: 1000              # 👥 GID 1000
        fsGroup: 1000                 # 📁 Volume files owned by GID 1000
        seccompProfile:               # 🔍 Block dangerous syscalls
          type: RuntimeDefault

      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: registry.easypj.com/easypj-django:v2.0.0
        # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        # CONTAINER-LEVEL SECURITY CONTEXT
        # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        securityContext:
          allowPrivilegeEscalation: false  # ⬆️  Block setuid binaries
          privileged: false                # 🚫 Not a privileged container
          capabilities:
            drop: [ALL]                    # 🧢 No Linux capabilities
          readOnlyRootFilesystem: true     # 🧊 / is read-only
        volumeMounts:
        - name: tmp
          mountPath: /tmp
        - name: media
          mountPath: /app/media
      volumes:
      - name: tmp
        emptyDir: {}
      - name: media
        persistentVolumeClaim:
          claimName: easypj-media</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 18.7 (sourced from cks.txt — NOT in cks_1.html) ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.7 Official SecurityContext Facts — Precedence, fsGroup, and Hidden Constraints</h3>
                <p>Straight from <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/security-context/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/security-context</a> — the exact precedence and constraint rules that explain <em>why</em> a well-built easypj security spec is laid out the way it is. Most fields exist at BOTH pod level (<code>spec.securityContext</code>) and container level (<code>container.securityContext</code>): <code>runAsUser</code>, <code>runAsGroup</code>, <code>runAsNonRoot</code>, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation</code>, <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code>, <code>capabilities</code>, <code>seccompProfile</code>, <code>seLinuxOptions</code>. When both are set, the <strong>CONTAINER-LEVEL value wins for that field only</strong> — every other pod-level field still applies untouched. A handful of fields — <code>fsGroup</code>, <code>fsGroupChangePolicy</code>, and <code>supplementalGroups</code> — exist ONLY at the pod level; there is no per-container override for them at all.</p>

                <h4>Precedence Map — Which Level Wins, Field by Field</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Pod Level?</th><th>Container Overrides?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>runAsUser / runAsGroup</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes — container wins</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>runAsNonRoot</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes — container wins</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>allowPrivilegeEscalation</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes — container wins</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>readOnlyRootFilesystem</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes — container wins</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>capabilities</strong></td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ No (container only)</td><td style="color:#8b949e;">n/a</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>seccompProfile</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes — container wins</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>appArmorProfile</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Yes — container wins</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(251,191,36,0.08);"><td><strong>fsGroup / fsGroupChangePolicy</strong></td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">⚠️ POD ONLY</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Cannot override</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(251,191,36,0.08);"><td><strong>supplementalGroups</strong></td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">⚠️ POD ONLY</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Cannot override</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p>Notice the last two rows: <code>fsGroup</code> and <code>supplementalGroups</code> control <em>volume</em> ownership, not process identity — and volumes are a pod-level concept (mounted once, shared by every container in the pod), so it makes structural sense that only the pod can set them.</p>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Precision Correction — "Capabilities" Has No Pod-Level Field At All</h5>
                    <p>Unlike runAsUser, seccompProfile, or readOnlyRootFilesystem, <code>capabilities</code> does NOT exist on <code>spec.securityContext</code> (pod level) at all — only on <code>container.securityContext</code>. There is nothing to "override" because there is no pod-level value to begin with. Earlier examples in this guide (and a common shorthand across CKS material) describe "pod-level drops ALL, container-level adds back" — treat that as teaching shorthand for two DIFFERENT containers in the same pod (one with <code>drop: [ALL]</code>, another with <code>drop: [ALL]</code> + <code>add: [...]</code>), not a literal pod-to-container inheritance chain. Every container's capabilities are set independently, from scratch, at the container level only.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 GOTCHA: runAsUser Without runAsGroup Defaults the Group to ROOT (GID 0)</h5>
                    <p>If you set <code>runAsUser</code> but omit <code>runAsGroup</code>, the container's <strong>primary group is NOT derived from runAsUser</strong> — it silently defaults to <strong>GID 0 (root)</strong>. A container running as UID 1000 with an unset <code>runAsGroup</code> is still, group-wise, running as root. Any file that's group-writable by root (common on shared volumes, package caches, or images built carelessly) becomes writable by this "non-root" container. Always set <code>runAsGroup</code> explicitly alongside <code>runAsUser</code> if you care about the group, not just the user.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Fix — Always Pair runAsUser with an Explicit runAsGroup</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — runAsUser set correctly, WITH runAsGroup alongside it</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000       # ❌ WRONG if runAsGroup is left unset below —
  runAsGroup: 1000       # ✅ primary group would silently default to GID 0 (root)!
                          #    Setting runAsGroup explicitly closes that gap.
  fsGroup: 1000           # 📁 also set fsGroup so mounted volumes match the same GID</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 TRAP: allowPrivilegeEscalation: false Is FORCED Back to true in Two Cases</h5>
                    <p><code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> is silently overridden — your <code>false</code> is IGNORED and the effective value becomes <code>true</code> — whenever either of these is also true on the same container:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>The container runs <code>privileged: true</code>, OR</li>
                        <li>The container has <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code> added via <code>capabilities.add</code></li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>There is no error, no admission rejection, no warning — the field just stops meaning anything. <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> only has real teeth on a <strong>non-privileged container that doesn't hold CAP_SYS_ADMIN</strong>. This is exactly why "drop ALL capabilities" (18.3) and "allowPrivilegeEscalation: false" (18.4) must be applied TOGETHER — either one alone leaves a gap the other closes.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>fsGroupChangePolicy — A Real Performance vs Consistency Tradeoff</h4>
                <p><code>fsGroupChangePolicy</code> (default: <code>OnRootMismatch</code>) controls how Kubernetes chowns volume files to match <code>fsGroup</code> when a volume is mounted. This is a genuine tradeoff, not a "pick the secure one" choice:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚡</div>
                        <h5>OnRootMismatch (default)</h5>
                        <p>Only chmods/chowns a volume if its ROOT directory doesn't already match <code>fsGroup</code>. Fast — skips the recursive walk entirely on subsequent mounts of the same PVC. Safe for reused PVCs that were already set up correctly. <strong>Best for:</strong> most production workloads, especially large volumes.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🐢</div>
                        <h5>Always</h5>
                        <p>Recursively re-chmods/chowns the ENTIRE volume tree on <strong>every single mount</strong>, regardless of current state. Slower — can add real startup latency on large volumes. Guarantees consistency, which matters when a volume is shared by multiple writers that may have left mismatched ownership behind. <strong>Best for:</strong> shared/multi-writer volumes where correctness must be guaranteed every time.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 How to Use This on the Exam</h5>
                    <p>If the exam gives you a pod spec with <code>runAsUser</code> set but a file-permission error in the container, check THREE things in order: (1) is <code>runAsGroup</code> missing — is the process running as GID 0 by accident? (2) is <code>fsGroup</code> set at all for the mounted volume? (3) is <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> actually taking effect, or is it being silently overridden by <code>privileged: true</code> / <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code> elsewhere in the same container? These three questions cover the large majority of "securityContext looks right but doesn't behave right" exam traps.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

