            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch18-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>SecurityContext exists at pod level (defaults for every container) and container level (overrides for one container)</strong> — most fields follow a per-field precedence rule: the container-level value wins ONLY for the fields it explicitly sets, every other pod-level field still applies (18.1, 18.7).</li>
                    <li><strong>Running as non-root is the single highest-leverage setting in this chapter:</strong> without user namespace remapping (still beta, not default, through Kubernetes 1.29), UID 0 in the container IS UID 0 on the host — <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> + <code>runAsUser</code> &gt; 0 is non-negotiable (18.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>capabilities.drop: [ALL] empties all 5 Linux capability sets</strong> (permitted, effective, bounding, inheritable, ambient) — combined with non-root, the process becomes an ordinary Unix user with zero special powers even after a successful container escape; capabilities exist ONLY at the container level, there is no pod-level field to inherit from (18.3, 18.7).</li>
                    <li><strong>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false blocks setuid escalation by setting the kernel's no_new_privs flag — but it is SILENTLY forced back to true</strong> if the container is <code>privileged: true</code> or holds <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code>, so it must always be paired with dropped capabilities and non-privileged mode, never relied on alone (18.4, 18.7). <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> then locks the image itself, with the few writable paths handled by explicit emptyDir/PVC mounts (18.5, 18.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>fsGroup, fsGroupChangePolicy, and supplementalGroups exist ONLY at the pod level</strong> — there is no per-container override — and the default <code>fsGroupChangePolicy: OnRootMismatch</code> trades a little consistency for real mount-time performance versus <code>Always</code>, which is safer only for shared multi-writer volumes (18.7).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch18-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 18 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Pod-level vs container-level win rule:</strong> Container-level securityContext OVERRIDES pod-level for runAsUser, readOnlyRootFilesystem, and every other field that exists at both levels — but ONLY for the fields it explicitly sets. If pod says <code>runAsUser: 1000</code> and container says <code>runAsUser: 2000</code>, the container runs as 2000, while every other pod-level field (fsGroup, seccompProfile, etc.) still applies untouched.</li>
                    <li><strong>runAsGroup is separate from runAsUser:</strong> If you set <code>runAsUser: 1000</code> but don't set <code>runAsGroup</code>, the container's primary group is 0 (root) — NOT derived from runAsUser. Always set both, or use <code>fsGroup</code> for mounted volumes (see §18.7).</li>
                    <li><strong>allowPrivilegeEscalation defaults to true:</strong> If you don't explicitly set <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>, a container can gain more privileges than its parent process via setuid binaries.</li>
                    <li><strong>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false can be a no-op:</strong> even when you DO set it to <code>false</code>, the effective value is silently forced back to <code>true</code> if the same container is <code>privileged: true</code> or holds <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code>. There's no warning — the exam expects you to drop ALL capabilities and set <code>privileged: false</code> alongside it, not rely on the flag in isolation (§18.7).</li>
                    <li><strong>fsGroup/supplementalGroups have no container-level override:</strong> if a task asks you to give one specific container a different volume group, the honest answer is "split it into a separate pod," not "add fsGroup under the container's securityContext" — that field doesn't exist there.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

