            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch18-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 18 — Lab Q&amp;A (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">11 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the complete securityContext that makes a pod CKS Restricted compliant: non-root, read-only FS, drop ALL capabilities, Seccomp RuntimeDefault, no privilege escalation.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000
  runAsGroup: 1000
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
  capabilities:
    drop: ["ALL"]
  seccompProfile:
    type: RuntimeDefault</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">If a pod-level securityContext drops ALL capabilities but a container-level securityContext adds NET_RAW, what capabilities does the container actually have?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The container gets ONLY NET_RAW. This question uses "pod-level drops ALL" as shorthand — per §18.7, <code>capabilities</code> is actually a container-only field, so in practice this means one container in the pod is configured with <code>drop: [ALL]</code> and another with <code>drop: [ALL]</code> + <code>add: [NET_RAW]</code>. Either way, capabilities never carry the original 14 default capabilities forward — a container only ever has what its OWN <code>drop</code>/<code>add</code> list produces.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens if you set runAsNonRoot: true but your Docker image defaults to root (USER not set)?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The container FAILS to start with <code>CreateContainerError: container has runAsNonRoot and image will run as root</code>. Kubernetes checks the image's default USER before starting the container. Fix: either set <code>runAsUser: 1000</code> in the pod spec (overrides image default), or rebuild the image with <code>USER 1000</code> in the Dockerfile.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between runAsUser in pod securityContext vs container securityContext?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Pod-level <code>runAsUser</code> sets the default UID for ALL containers that don't override it. Container-level <code>runAsUser</code> overrides the pod-level setting for that specific container. If both are set, the container-level wins. If neither is set, the container runs as the USER specified in the Docker image (usually root if not specified).</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> and what specific attack does it prevent? How does it interact with <code>no_new_privs</code> in the kernel?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> prevents a process from gaining more privileges than its parent. It sets the kernel's <code>no_new_privs</code> flag. This means: (1) setuid binaries are ignored — even if <code>/usr/bin/sudo</code> has setuid root, running it won't give root. (2) The process can never transition to a new AppArmor/SELinux label. (3) Seccomp cannot be relaxed — the process stays confined. (4) Capabilities cannot be added via <code>prctl(PR_CAPBSET_DROP)</code>.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is one of the most important securityContext fields for CKS. Even if an attacker finds a setuid binary or kernel exploit, <code>no_new_privs</code> prevents privilege escalation. It's a one-way door — once set, it can never be unset for the process lifetime. Always pair with <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> for complete privilege containment — and remember it only holds if the container is also not privileged and doesn't hold CAP_SYS_ADMIN (§18.7).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod needs to bind to port 80 (privileged port) but must run as non-root. What's the solution that doesn't violate CKS Restricted requirements?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Add <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code> capability to the container. This is a safe capability that specifically allows binding to ports below 1024 without granting any other network privileges:</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000
  capabilities:
    drop: ["ALL"]
    add: ["NET_BIND_SERVICE"]</code></pre>
                    <p>This works because Linux allows processes with <code>CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE</code> to bind to privileged ports regardless of UID. It's far safer than running as root.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the practical CKS approach: when a pod legitimately needs a Linux capability, add ONLY that specific capability after dropping ALL. NET_BIND_SERVICE is safe because it only enables port binding — it doesn't grant packet capture, interface modification, or firewall manipulation. This is the difference between "least capability" (good) and "running as root" (bad).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between <code>fsGroup</code>, <code>runAsGroup</code>, and <code>supplementalGroups</code>? When would you use each?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>runAsGroup:</strong> The primary GID the container process runs as. Set at pod or container level. <strong>fsGroup:</strong> The GID that owns ALL files in mounted volumes. Kubernetes recursively chowns volume files to this GID before starting the container (unless <code>fsGroupChangePolicy: OnRootMismatch</code> skips it because the root already matches — see §18.7). <strong>supplementalGroups:</strong> Additional GIDs the container process belongs to. Useful for accessing shared filesystems with group permissions. Both <code>fsGroup</code> and <code>supplementalGroups</code> are pod-level ONLY — no container can override them (§18.7).</p>
                    <p>Use case: container runs as UID 1000, needs write access to a shared NFS volume owned by GID 2000 → set <code>fsGroup: 2000</code> so the volume files are group-writable.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>These three fields control Linux group membership at different levels. <code>fsGroup</code> is unique to Kubernetes — it solves the problem of volume permissions when containers run as non-root. Without fsGroup, mounted volumes owned by root would be unwritable by non-root containers. On the CKS exam, if a non-root pod can't write to a volume, check fsGroup first.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the securityContext for a pod that needs to: read the host's timezone file, drop all capabilities except CHOWN for volume initialization, and prevent any privilege escalation. Include the volume mount.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
spec:
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true
    runAsUser: 1000
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: myapp:latest
    securityContext:
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
      capabilities:
        drop: ["ALL"]
        add: ["CHOWN"]
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
    volumeMounts:
    - name: timezone
      mountPath: /etc/localtime
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: timezone
    hostPath:
      path: /etc/localtime
      type: File</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a realistic CKS scenario: a pod needs minimal host access (timezone) and a limited capability (CHOWN for volume initialization). The security is maximized: non-root, no privilege escalation, read-only root FS, all capabilities dropped except CHOWN. The hostPath is limited to a single file (type: File) rather than a directory — this is more secure than a directory mount.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You set <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> on a pod and it crashes because the application needs to write to <code>/tmp</code> and <code>/var/cache</code>. How do you fix this without disabling readOnlyRootFilesystem?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Mount <code>emptyDir</code> volumes at the writable paths. The root filesystem stays read-only, but these specific directories are backed by writable tmpfs volumes:</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">containers:
- name: app
  securityContext:
    readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
  volumeMounts:
  - name: tmp
    mountPath: /tmp
  - name: cache
    mountPath: /var/cache
volumes:
- name: tmp
  emptyDir: {}
- name: cache
  emptyDir: {}</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the standard pattern for making applications work with readOnlyRootFilesystem. emptyDir volumes provide writable temporary storage that is ephemeral (cleared on pod restart). The key security benefit: the attacker can write to /tmp but can't modify the application binary at <code>/usr/bin/app</code> or system libraries at <code>/lib/</code>. On the CKS exam, if an app crashes with "read-only filesystem" errors, add emptyDir mounts for the writable paths.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You audit a cluster and find 80% of pods have NO securityContext at all. Write the Kyverno ClusterPolicy that would reject any pod without runAsNonRoot and readOnlyRootFilesystem.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-security-context
spec:
  validationFailureAction: Enforce
  rules:
  - name: check-run-as-non-root
    match:
      any:
      - resources:
          kinds: [Pod]
    validate:
      message: "runAsNonRoot must be true"
      pattern:
        spec:
          containers:
          - securityContext:
              runAsNonRoot: true
  - name: check-read-only-root
    match:
      any:
      - resources:
          kinds: [Pod]
    validate:
      message: "readOnlyRootFilesystem must be true"
      pattern:
        spec:
          containers:
          - securityContext:
              readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Policy-as-code (Kyverno/OPA) is the CKS approach to enforcing security at scale. Instead of manually verifying 80% of pods, you write a policy that rejects insecure pods automatically. Kyverno policies can also MUTATE pods to add defaults (e.g., auto-add RuntimeDefault seccomp). On the CKS exam, if asked to "enforce security across all namespaces," the answer involves admission controllers, not manual verification.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Two containers in the same pod are misbehaving. Container A sets <code>runAsUser: 2000</code> but omits <code>runAsGroup</code>. Container B sets <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> but also <code>privileged: true</code>. For EACH container, what is the actual effective runtime behavior, and how do you fix both?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Container A:</strong> it runs as UID 2000 but its primary GROUP is GID 0 (root) — <code>runAsGroup</code> is NOT derived from <code>runAsUser</code>, it defaults to root when left unset. Any file that's group-writable by root is writable by this container despite the non-root UID. <strong>Fix:</strong> add <code>runAsGroup: 2000</code> explicitly.</p>
                    <p><strong>Container B:</strong> <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> is silently IGNORED — because <code>privileged: true</code> is set on the same container, the effective value is forced back to <code>true</code> regardless of what the YAML says. There's no error or warning. <strong>Fix:</strong> the container cannot be privileged AND have privilege escalation blocked at the same time — remove <code>privileged: true</code> (and don't add <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code> either), or accept that this container has no privilege-escalation protection at all.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># Container A — fixed
securityContext:
  runAsUser: 2000
  runAsGroup: 2000       # explicit — no longer defaults to GID 0

# Container B — fixed
securityContext:
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
  privileged: false       # must be false for the flag above to mean anything
  capabilities:
    drop: ["ALL"]          # and must not re-add CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>These are the two hidden-constraint traps from §18.7, straight from the official kubernetes.io SecurityContext docs. Both fields exist at the container level and both LOOK correctly configured in isolation — the trap only becomes visible when you check the field's interaction with a sibling field (<code>runAsGroup</code> vs <code>runAsUser</code>; <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation</code> vs <code>privileged</code>/<code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code>). The exam rewards checking the WHOLE securityContext block for a container, not just the one field named in the task.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

