            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 19 · Secrets Management — Encryption at Rest</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Kubernetes Secrets are <strong>base64-encoded, not encrypted</strong>. This is the most dangerous default in Kubernetes — and the most commonly misunderstood. Anyone who can access etcd (the cluster's database) can read every Secret in plaintext by simply base64-decoding the values. If you have a database password stored as a Secret, and an attacker gains etcd access through a misconfigured RBAC rule, a compromised control plane node, or a stolen etcd backup — they have your database password instantly. This chapter teaches you to enable <strong>encryption at rest</strong> — encrypting Secrets with AES-GCM (or another provider) before they are written to etcd, so that even someone with direct etcd access sees only ciphertext. It also covers encryption provider selection, key rotation, best practices for the easypj application, and the official provider-ordering rule that governs every EncryptionConfiguration you will ever write.</p>
