            <section class="section-block" id="s19-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.1 The Problem: Secrets in etcd Are Stored in Plaintext (By Default)</h3>
                <p>The first time most engineers learn this, they don't believe it. <strong>Kubernetes Secrets are base64-encoded, not encrypted.</strong> Base64 is a character encoding — it transforms binary data into printable ASCII characters. It provides ZERO security. Anyone who can read the Secret can decode it instantly:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Base64 ≠ Encryption — The Dangerous Default</div>
<pre>
  kubectl create secret generic db-creds \
    --from-literal=password=supersecret123 \
    --from-literal=username=easypj-admin

  kubectl get secret db-creds -o yaml
  apiVersion: v1
  kind: Secret
  data:
    password: c3VwZXJzZWNyZXQxMjM=     ← "Looks encrypted?" ❌
    username: ZWFzeXBqLWFkbWlu         ← "Looks encrypted?" ❌

  ⚡ ONE COMMAND reveals the plaintext:
  ─────────────────────────────────────
  echo "c3VwZXJzZWNyZXQxMjM=" | base64 -d
  → supersecret123

  echo "ZWFzeXBqLWFkbWlu" | base64 -d
  → easypj-admin

  WHAT BASE64 ACTUALLY IS:
  ────────────────────────
  • Base64 is an ENCODING, not ENCRYPTION.
  • Purpose: safely transport binary data in text-only formats (JSON, YAML, email)
  • Security: ZERO. It's mathematically reversible without any key.
  • It's like writing your password in a slightly different alphabet — anyone
    who knows the alphabet (everyone) can read it.

  WITHOUT ENCRYPTION AT REST:
  ────────────────────────────
  etcd stores: c3VwZXJzZWNyZXQxMjM=  (plain base64)
  Attacker with etcd access: base64 -d → supersecret123
  Attacker with etcd backup: same thing
  Attacker with disk access: same thing

  WITH ENCRYPTION AT REST:
  ────────────────────────
  etcd stores: &lt;binary encrypted blob, unreadable&gt;
  Attacker with etcd access: sees random bytes, cannot decode
  Attacker with etcd backup: same — backup contains ciphertext
  Attacker with disk access: same — disk contains ciphertext
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Who Can Access etcd? — The Attack Vectors</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">👑</div><h5>Control Plane Compromise</h5><p>Anyone with SSH access to a control plane node can read <code>/var/lib/etcd/</code> directly. If the node is compromised, etcd data is exposed.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div><h5>etcd Backup Theft</h5><p>etcd snapshots are taken for disaster recovery. If a backup file is stored in an insecure location (S3 bucket, shared drive), all Secrets in it are exposed.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔐</div><h5>RBAC Misconfiguration</h5><p>A misconfigured RBAC rule granting <code>get secrets</code> to an untrusted user lets them read all Secrets through the API. Encryption doesn't prevent API access — it protects at the storage layer.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>How etcd Stores Secrets — The Write Path Without and With Encryption</h4>
                <p>Understanding exactly where encryption happens in the API server's write path helps you troubleshoot when it doesn't work. The encryption layer is a <strong>transparent wrapper</strong> around etcd — the API server encrypts data before sending it to etcd, and decrypts it when reading it back. Other components (kubectl, controllers) never see the encryption — they always work with plaintext Secrets:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Secret Write Path — Without Encryption vs With aesgcm Encryption</div>
<pre>
  WITHOUT ENCRYPTION (identity provider):
  ───────────────────────────────────────
  kubectl create secret → API server receives Secret → validates →
  base64-encodes values → writes raw base64 to etcd
  │
  etcd stores: {"password":"c3VwZXJzZWNyZXQxMjM="}   ← READABLE base64
  │
  Attacker with etcd access: base64 -d → supersecret123

  WITH ENCRYPTION (aesgcm provider):
  ───────────────────────────────────
  kubectl create secret → API server receives Secret → validates →
  base64-encodes values → 🔐 AES-GCM ENCRYPTS the entire Secret value →
  prepends "k8s:enc:aesgcm:v1:key1:" header → writes ciphertext to etcd
  │
  etcd stores: "k8s:enc:aesgcm:v1:key1:&lt;binary encrypted blob&gt;"
  │
  Attacker with etcd access: sees binary garbage. Cannot decode.
  │
  ON READ: API server reads from etcd → detects "k8s:enc:" prefix →
  looks up provider "aesgcm", key "key1" → decrypts → base64-decodes →
  returns plaintext Secret to kubectl

  ⚡ THE ENCRYPTION HEADER (stored in etcd with the encrypted data):
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  k8s:enc:aesgcm:v1:key1:
  │     │      │   │
  │     │      │   └── Key name (which key encrypted this value)
  │     │      └────── API version
  │     └───────────── Provider name (which algorithm to use for decryption)
  └─────────────────── Magic prefix (identifies encrypted data)

  This header means the API server ALWAYS knows how to decrypt a value,
  even if the EncryptionConfiguration changes. It stores the provider
  and key name with every encrypted Secret.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 19.2 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s19-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.2 Enabling Encryption at Rest</h3>
                <p>Encryption at rest is configured via an <strong>EncryptionConfiguration</strong> resource — a YAML file that tells the API server which resources to encrypt and which encryption provider to use. The file is passed to the API server via the <code>--encryption-provider-config</code> flag and must be mounted into the API server's static pod.</p>

                <h4>Complete Encryption Setup — 4 Steps</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash + YAML — Enable encryption at rest for Secrets</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># STEP 1: Generate a 32-byte encryption key
ENCRYPTION_KEY=$(head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64)
echo "Key: $ENCRYPTION_KEY"
# Example: aGVsbG93b3JsZHRoaXNpc2F0aGlydHl0d29ieXRla2V5Zm9yYWVz

# STEP 2: Create the EncryptionConfiguration file
cat > /etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml << EOF
apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
  - resources:
      - secrets
    providers:
      - aesgcm:                    # ← Encrypt with AES-GCM (recommended)
          keys:
            - name: key1
              secret: ${ENCRYPTION_KEY}
      - identity: {}               # ← Fallback: allow unencrypted reads
EOF</code></pre>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># STEP 3: Configure the API server to use the encryption config
# Edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml:
# 1. Add the flag:
#    --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml
# 2. Mount the file:
#    volumeMounts:
#    - name: encryption-config
#      mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml
#      readOnly: true
# 3. Add the volume:
#    volumes:
#    - name: encryption-config
#      hostPath:
#        path: /etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml
#        type: File
# 4. Wait for API server to restart (kubelet detects manifest change)

# STEP 4: Rotate ALL existing Secrets (critical!)
# Existing Secrets are NOT automatically encrypted — only NEW Secrets are.
kubectl get secrets --all-namespaces -o json | kubectl replace -f -
# This reads every Secret, decrypts with the old provider (identity/plaintext),
# and rewrites it encrypted with the new provider (aesgcm).</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Verifying Encryption Is Working</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Verify that Secrets are encrypted in etcd</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># METHOD 1: Check etcd directly (requires etcdctl and certs)
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/easypj-prod/db-creds \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
# If encrypted: output is binary garbage (k8s:enc:aesgcm:v1:key1:...)
# If NOT encrypted: output contains readable base64 (c3VwZXJzZWNyZXQxMjM=)

# METHOD 2: Check API server logs for encryption config
kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-apiserver-cp-1 | grep "encryption"
# Should show: "Loaded encryption provider" or similar

# METHOD 3: Create a new Secret and check
kubectl create secret generic test-encryption -n default \
  --from-literal=test=hello123
# Then verify via etcdctl that /registry/secrets/default/test-encryption
# contains k8s:enc:aesgcm:v1:key1: prefix</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 19.3 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s19-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.3 Encryption Providers — Which to Use</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes supports multiple encryption providers, each with different security and performance characteristics. The CKS exam expects you to know the differences:</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Algorithm</th><th>Key Size</th><th>Security</th><th>Performance</th><th>When to Use</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(50,108,229,0.06);"><td><strong>identity</strong></td><td>None (plaintext)</td><td>N/A</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ NONE</td><td>N/A</td><td>NEVER for production. Use only as fallback during migration.</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(76,175,80,0.06);"><td><strong>aesgcm</strong></td><td>AES-GCM (authenticated)</td><td>16/24/32 bytes</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Strong</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Fast</td><td><strong>Recommended.</strong> Authenticated encryption — detects tampering. Must rotate key every 200K writes.</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(251,191,36,0.06);"><td><strong>aescbc</strong></td><td>AES-CBC (unauthenticated)</td><td>32 bytes</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">⚠️ Good</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Fast</td><td>Use if aesgcm is not available. No tamper detection — prefer aesgcm.</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(76,175,80,0.06);"><td><strong>secretbox</strong></td><td>XSalsa20 + Poly1305</td><td>32 bytes</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Strong</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Very Fast</td><td>Good alternative to aesgcm. Faster on CPUs without AES-NI.</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:rgba(76,175,80,0.06);"><td><strong>kms</strong></td><td>External KMS (AWS KMS, GCP KMS, Azure Key Vault)</td><td>Managed by KMS</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Strongest</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">⚠️ Network latency</td><td>Enterprise best practice. Keys never touch the cluster. Envelope encryption.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>KMS Envelope Encryption — How External KMS Works</h4>
                <p>The KMS provider uses <strong>envelope encryption</strong> — a two-layer system where a Data Encryption Key (DEK) encrypts the actual data, and the KMS encrypts the DEK. This means the encryption key material never touches the Kubernetes cluster at all:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">KMS Envelope Encryption — Two-Layer Key System</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  WRITE PATH: kubectl create secret generic db-creds                       │
  │                                                                          │
  │  1. API server generates a random DEK (Data Encryption Key — 256-bit).   │
  │  2. DEK encrypts the Secret value using AES-GCM.                         │
  │  3. API server sends the DEK to AWS KMS / GCP KMS / Azure Key Vault:    │
  │     "Please encrypt this DEK with the customer-managed key"              │
  │  4. KMS encrypts the DEK and returns the encrypted DEK.                  │
  │  5. API server stores BOTH in etcd:                                      │
  │     {                                                                     │
  │       "encrypted_data": "&lt;AES-GCM ciphertext&gt;",                           │
  │       "encrypted_dek": "&lt;KMS-encrypted DEK&gt;"                              │
  │     }                                                                     │
  │                                                                          │
  │  READ PATH: kubectl get secret db-creds                                  │
  │                                                                          │
  │  1. API server reads encrypted blob + encrypted DEK from etcd.          │
  │  2. Sends encrypted DEK to KMS: "Please decrypt this DEK"               │
  │  3. KMS decrypts the DEK and returns it (only if the API server's       │
  │     IAM role has kms:Decrypt permission).                                │
  │  4. API server uses the DEK to decrypt the Secret value.                │
  │  5. Returns plaintext Secret to kubectl.                                 │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ⚡ ADVANTAGES:                                                           │
  │  ─────────────                                                            │
  │  • Key material NEVER stored in the cluster.                              │
  │  • Key rotation: rotate the KMS key → KMS re-encrypts all DEKs.         │
  │  • Audit trail: KMS logs every encrypt/decrypt operation.               │
  │  • Access control: IAM policies control who can decrypt (even with      │
  │    full etcd access, an attacker cannot decrypt without KMS access).    │
  │  • Revocation: disable the KMS key → ALL encrypted Secrets become       │
  │    permanently unreadable (emergency kill-switch for breaches).          │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Encryption Provider Comparison — Choose the Right Algorithm</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Algorithm</th><th>Key Size</th><th>Performance</th><th>CKS Recommendation</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>identity</strong></td><td>None — plaintext</td><td>N/A</td><td>Fastest</td><td>🔴 NEVER in production. Only useful during migration.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>aescbc</strong></td><td>AES-256-CBC + PKCS#7</td><td>32 bytes (256-bit)</td><td>✅ Fast</td><td>✅ Simple, no external dependencies, FIPS-compliant — but see 19.6: the official docs flag it as weaker.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>aesgcm</strong></td><td>AES-256-GCM</td><td>32 bytes</td><td>✅ Fast (slightly faster)</td><td>✅ Preferred by the official docs (19.6). Must rotate keys every 200K writes.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>secretbox</strong></td><td>XSalsa20 + Poly1305</td><td>32 bytes</td><td>🐢 Slower</td><td>⚠️ Only if AES hardware acceleration unavailable.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kms</strong></td><td>Delegates to cloud KMS</td><td>Cloud-managed</td><td>🐢 Slowest (API call)</td><td>✅ Production best practice. Envelope encryption: KMS encrypts DEK.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Secret Rotation — Automating the "Read + Rewrite" Pattern</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Bash — Rotate ALL secrets in all namespaces (re-encrypt with current key)</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># After changing the encryption key or adding a new provider:
# Step 1: Verify encryption is configured
kubectl get encryptionconfiguration -o yaml
# (this is a cluster-admin operation)

# Step 2: Rotate ALL secrets — read (decrypt) + write (encrypt)
kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -
# This reads every secret via the API server (which decrypts),
# then writes it back (the API server encrypts with the FIRST
# provider in the list before storing in etcd — the official
# provider-ordering rule, see §19.6).

# Step 3: Verify rotation status (check etcd for old key prefix)
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/ --prefix --keys-only | \
  while read key; do
    encrypted=$(etcdctl get "$key" --print-value-only | head -c 30)
    echo "$key → $encrypted"
  done
# All should show: k8s:enc:aescbc:v1:key2: (the NEW key)

# Step 4: Audit who can read secrets (find overly-permissive RBAC)
kubectl get clusterrole,role -A -o yaml | grep -B5 '"secrets"' | grep -E 'name:|namespace:'
# Every role with access to secrets is listed. Review each one.
kubectl get rolebindings,clusterrolebindings -A -o json | \
  jq '.items[] | select(.roleRef.name as $r | ["admin","edit","cluster-admin"] | index($r)) | {ns: .metadata.namespace, role: .roleRef.name, subjects: .subjects}'
# Shows every binding for admin/edit/cluster-admin roles — these
# roles ALL include secret read access by default!</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 19.4 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s19-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.4 Secrets Management in easypj — Best Practices</h3>
                <p>Beyond encryption at rest, the easypj Django application needs a comprehensive Secrets management strategy. Here are the best practices, applied to easypj's specific configuration needs:</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Complete secure Secret management for easypj</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ── DATABASE CREDENTIALS (used by Django settings.py) ──
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: db-creds
  namespace: easypj-prod
type: Opaque
stringData:
  DATABASE_URL: postgres://easypj:${DB_PASSWORD}@postgres:5432/easypj

# ── DJANGO SECRET_KEY (used for sessions, CSRF tokens) ──
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: django-secret
  namespace: easypj-prod
type: Opaque
stringData:
  SECRET_KEY: django-insecure-50chars-random-string-here

# ── MOUNT SECRETS AS ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES ──
spec:
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    envFrom:
    - secretRef:
        name: db-creds              # Injects DATABASE_URL as env var
    - secretRef:
        name: django-secret         # Injects SECRET_KEY as env var

# ⚡ NEVER store cloud credentials as Secrets — use IRSA/Workload Identity
# ⚡ NEVER commit Secret YAML to git — use sealed-secrets or external secrets
# ⚡ ALWAYS enable encryption at rest for the easypj-prod namespace</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Audit Who Can Read Secrets — The RBAC Check</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Audit which users and ServiceAccounts can read Secrets</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Find ALL RoleBindings/ClusterRoleBindings that grant secret access
kubectl get clusterrolebindings,rolebindings -A -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.roleRef.name | test(".*secret.*|.*admin.*|cluster-admin|edit")) | "\(.kind): \(.metadata.name) → \(.subjects)"'

# Check if a specific user can read secrets in easypj-prod
kubectl auth can-i get secrets -n easypj-prod --as=developer@easypj.com
# Must return: no

# Check if a ServiceAccount can read secrets across all namespaces
kubectl auth can-i list secrets --all-namespaces \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:ci-cd:deployer-sa
# Must return: no (CI/CD should NOT need to read Secrets — only create/update)</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Key Rotation — When and How to Rotate Encryption Keys</h4>
                <p>Encryption keys should be rotated regularly — not because the algorithm is weak, but because the longer a key is used, the more data is encrypted with it, and the greater the impact if the key is compromised. AES-GCM also has a safety limit: you should not encrypt more than ~2³² values with the same key (the birthday bound for GCM's nonce). For Kubernetes Secrets, this is unlikely, but key rotation is still a best practice. Notice that the new key always goes FIRST and the old key SECOND — that ordering is not a style choice, it's the official provider-ordering rule explained in full in §19.6:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Rotating encryption keys for Secrets</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Generate a NEW key
NEW_KEY=$(head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64)

# Update EncryptionConfiguration — ADD the new key FIRST, keep old key
cat > /etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml << EOF
apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
  - resources:
      - secrets
    providers:
      - aesgcm:
          keys:
            - name: key2           # ← NEW key FIRST (used for WRITES)
              secret: ${NEW_KEY}
            - name: key1           # ← OLD key SECOND (used for READS only)
              secret: &lt;old-key&gt;
      - identity: {}
EOF

# The API server will NOW:
# • Write NEW Secrets with key2 (first key in list)
# • Read OLD Secrets with key1 (still available for decryption)

# Re-encrypt ALL existing Secrets with the new key
kubectl get secrets --all-namespaces -o json | kubectl replace -f -
# This reads every Secret (decrypted with key1) and rewrites it
# (encrypted with key2).

# Once all Secrets are re-encrypted, you can REMOVE key1 from the config.
# Verify: check etcd — no values should have the old key name header.
# Then remove key1 and restart the API server.</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 19.5: Secret Rotation Patterns ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s19-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.5 Secret Rotation — Automated Patterns for the CKS Exam</h3>
                <p>The CKS curriculum requires you to <strong>manage Kubernetes secrets</strong>, and rotation is the most operationally critical aspect. Secrets that never rotate become increasingly vulnerable over time. Below are the three rotation patterns you need for the exam:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>When to Use</th><th>Command</th><th>Risk</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>1. Delete + Recreate</strong></td><td>ServiceAccount tokens, API keys</td><td><code>kubectl delete secret X && kubectl create secret generic X --from-literal=key=NEWVALUE</code></td><td>Pods using the secret must be restarted to pick up new value. Rolling restart required.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>2. In-Place Update</strong></td><td>Database passwords, TLS certs</td><td><code>kubectl create secret generic X --from-literal=key=NEWVALUE --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -</code></td><td>Pods with secret mounted as file auto-update (kubelet sync). Env vars do NOT auto-update.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>3. Encryption Key Rotation</strong></td><td>etcd encryption keys</td><td>Add new key first in providers list → <code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code> → remove old key</td><td>Removing old key before re-encryption = permanent data loss (§19.6).</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ CKS Exam: Secret Rotation</h5><p>Secrets mounted as <strong>files</strong> (via volumeMount) auto-update within ~60-90 seconds (kubelet sync interval). Secrets injected as <strong>environment variables</strong> NEVER auto-update — the pod must be restarted. This distinction is tested on the exam.</p></div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key Takeaway for the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p>Encryption at rest tasks on the CKS exam follow a <strong>five-step workflow</strong>: (1) Generate a random 32-byte key with openssl or /dev/urandom, (2) Create the EncryptionConfiguration YAML with the aesgcm provider, (3) Add <code>--encryption-provider-config</code> to the API server manifest AND mount the file, (4) Wait for the API server to restart, (5) Rotate ALL existing Secrets with <code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code>. <strong>Never skip step 5:</strong> existing Secrets are NOT auto-encrypted when you enable encryption. They remain in plaintext until rewritten. And remember: the identity provider in your EncryptionConfiguration should always be the LAST provider in the list — it's used as a fallback for READING old unencrypted data during migration, not for writing (the full rule is in §19.6).</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 19.6 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s19-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.6 Official EncryptionConfiguration Facts — the Provider-Ordering Rule</h3>
                <p>Straight from the official <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/encrypt-data/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kubernetes.io encryption-at-rest documentation</a>: ONE rule underlies everything you configured in 19.1-19.5, and it is exactly why key rotation (19.4, 19.5) puts the new key first while keeping the old key second.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 THE RULE</h5>
                    <p>The <strong>FIRST</strong> provider listed in <code>providers:</code> is used for <strong>ALL new writes</strong>. <strong>EVERY</strong> provider in the list is tried <strong>IN ORDER</strong> for reads until one succeeds. That single rule explains the entire providers-list ordering convention used throughout this chapter.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Provider List Order — the One Rule That Explains Everything</div>
<pre>
  providers:
    - aesgcm: {keys: [{name: key-new, secret: ...}]}  ← WRITES use
        this one, always, no matter how many providers follow
    - aescbc: {keys: [{name: key-old, secret: ...}]}  ← READ fallback
        only (old data still encrypted with this key stays readable)
    - identity: {}                                     ← LAST resort,
        treats anything undecryptable as already-plaintext

  ┌─────────────────────────────── READ ────────────────────────────────┐
  │  try provider 1 (aesgcm/key-new) → fail →                            │
  │  try provider 2 (aescbc/key-old) → fail →                            │
  │  try provider 3 (identity)  — ALWAYS "succeeds" (treats as plaintext) │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ┌─────────────────────────────── WRITE ───────────────────────────────┐
  │  ALWAYS provider 1 (aesgcm/key-new) — unconditionally, no matter     │
  │  what else is in the list or how many providers it contains          │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ⚡ THIS is why rotation (19.4, 19.5) adds the NEW key FIRST:
     new writes pick it up immediately, while data already encrypted
     with the OLD key (now in position 2) can still be read and
     re-encrypted before that key is ever removed.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Full Reference EncryptionConfiguration — Every Rule Applied</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Official provider-ordering pattern (kubernetes.io reference)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
  - resources:
      - secrets
      # Wildcard resource matching (v1.27+) — one entry can cover
      # a whole API group. More specific entries must be listed
      # BEFORE wildcards; overlapping wildcards are invalid.
      # - "*.apps"          # every resource in the apps API group
      # - "*.*"              # every resource in every API group
    providers:
      - aesgcm:                    # ← WRITES always use this provider —
          keys:                    #   it's FIRST, full stop.
            - name: key-new
              secret: &lt;base64-32-byte-key&gt;
      - aescbc:                    # ← READ fallback only. Old data
          keys:                    #   encrypted with key-old stays
            - name: key-old        #   readable until re-encrypted.
              secret: &lt;base64-32-byte-old-key&gt;
      - identity: {}               # ← LAST resort. NEVER put this first —
                                    #   see the danger box below.
  # Custom Resources (v1.26+) can be encrypted too, but ONLY if
  # explicitly listed here or matched by a wildcard — existing CR
  # data stays unencrypted until its next write (same rule as Secrets).
  - resources:
      - myresource.example.com
    providers:
      - aesgcm:
          keys:
            - name: key-new
              secret: &lt;base64-32-byte-key&gt;</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚫 NEVER Put identity FIRST</h5>
                    <p><code>identity</code> as the LAST fallback provider means "if nothing else can decrypt it, treat it as plaintext" — a reasonable escape hatch for reading old unencrypted data during a migration. But <strong>identity must NEVER be placed FIRST</strong>: because the first provider is always used for writes, that would <strong>disable encryption for every new write</strong> — silently, with no error. Be careful having <code>identity</code> present in a production config at all; it's an escape hatch, not a safety net.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>☠️ CRITICAL FAILURE MODE — Permanent Data Loss</h5>
                    <p>If you remove a key while data still encrypted with it exists in etcd, and <strong>no remaining provider can decrypt it</strong>, that data becomes <strong>PERMANENTLY INACCESSIBLE</strong> via the API — not a graceful error, requests for that object simply fail. This is exactly why any rotation sequence (19.4, 19.5) must ALWAYS re-encrypt (<code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code>) <strong>BEFORE</strong> removing the old key from the providers list. Verify re-encryption completed, THEN remove the key.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>aescbc vs aesgcm — What the Official Docs Actually Say</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Official Guidance</th><th>Use For</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>aescbc</strong></td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">⚠️ Explicitly flagged as weaker — vulnerable to a padding-oracle class of attack</td><td>Legacy compatibility only. Not recommended for new configurations.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>aesgcm</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Preferred for new local-key configurations</td><td>New EncryptionConfiguration files. Must still rotate before ~2³² writes with the same key.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kms</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Preferred overall — envelope encryption, key never touches the cluster</td><td>Production, enterprise, anywhere a cloud KMS is available.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 v1.26+ / v1.27+ Additions Worth Knowing</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Wildcard resource matching (v1.27+):</strong> <code>resources: ["*.apps"]</code> or <code>["*.*"]</code> lets one provider config cover a whole API group. More specific entries must be listed BEFORE wildcards, and overlapping wildcards are invalid configuration.</li>
                        <li><strong>Custom Resources (v1.26+):</strong> CRDs can be encrypted too — but only if explicitly listed or wildcard-matched. Existing CR data stays unencrypted until its next write, the exact same "only new writes are encrypted" rule that applies to plain Secrets (19.2).</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

