            <details class="uth">
                <summary>🔧 Under the Hood — Envelope Encryption: How the KMS Provider Works — DEK Encryption → DEK Encrypted by KMS → Stored Alongside Data</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                <pre class="uth-diagram">
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   ENCRYPTION AT REST — Provider Comparison                                 │
  │                                                                           │
  │   aescbc / aesgcm (LOCAL KEY):                                            │
  │   ─────────────────────────────                                            │
  │   Key stored in: EncryptionConfiguration YAML file on control plane       │
  │   Encryption: AES-256-CBC (aescbc) or AES-256-GCM (aesgcm)              │
  │   Key rotation: Add new key to providers list, re-write all Secrets       │
  │   Risk: YAML file on disk contains the encryption key                     │
  │   CKS Exam: Most commonly tested — simple, no external deps              │
  │                                                                           │
  │   kms (CLOUD KMS — Envelope Encryption):                                   │
  │   ──────────────────────────────────────                                    │
  │   1. API server generates a Data Encryption Key (DEK) for each write      │
  │   2. DEK encrypts the Secret data                                          │
  │   3. DEK is sent to cloud KMS → KMS encrypts DEK → returns encrypted DEK  │
  │   4. Encrypted DEK stored alongside encrypted data in etcd                │
  │   Key rotation: Rotate KMS key → old DEKs still readable via old key     │
  │   Risk: If cloud KMS is unreachable, API server cannot decrypt            │
  │   CKS Exam: Know the concept — envelope encryption, KMS dependency       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                </pre>
