            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Base64 is an encoding, not encryption</strong> — it provides zero security and is trivially reversible with one command; anyone with etcd access (control-plane compromise, stolen backup, RBAC misconfiguration) can read every Secret unless encryption at rest is explicitly enabled (19.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Encryption at rest is a 4-step setup that is NOT retroactive:</strong> generate a key, write an EncryptionConfiguration, add the API server flag AND mount the file, then re-encrypt every existing Secret with <code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code> — skipping the rotation step leaves old Secrets in plaintext indefinitely (19.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Provider choice matters:</strong> <code>identity</code> is plaintext (migration fallback only), <code>aesgcm</code>/<code>secretbox</code> are strong local-key options, and <code>kms</code> (envelope encryption — a DEK encrypts the data, the KMS encrypts the DEK) is the enterprise best practice because the key material never touches the cluster (19.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Rotation has three distinct patterns with three different blast radii</strong> — delete+recreate (needs a pod restart), in-place update (auto-picked-up for file-mounted Secrets, NOT for env vars), and encryption-key rotation (add the new key first, re-encrypt everything, only then remove the old key) — applied to easypj's DB credentials, Django SECRET_KEY, and RBAC-audited access (19.4, 19.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>One official rule explains all of it:</strong> the FIRST provider in the list is used for every write; EVERY provider is tried in order for reads until one succeeds. That's why rotation puts the new key first and keeps the old key second — and it's why <code>identity</code> must never be first (disables encryption for new writes) and why removing a key before re-encrypting causes PERMANENT data loss (19.6).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 19 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Encryption config is NOT mounted automatically:</strong> Adding <code>--encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml</code> to the API server manifest requires ALSO adding a volumeMount for the file AND a volume for the hostPath. Missing either = encryption silently disabled.</li>
                    <li><strong>First key in providers list encrypts NEW secrets:</strong> Only the FIRST provider in the list is used for encryption. All subsequent providers are for decryption only. To rotate keys, add a new first provider — old secrets remain decryptable by the old key.</li>
                    <li><strong>aescbc vs aesgcm vs secretbox:</strong> <code>aescbc</code> is simple and well-tested with no external dependencies, but the official docs flag it as weaker (padding-oracle class of attack) — prefer <code>aesgcm</code> or <code>kms</code> for new configurations (19.6). <code>secretbox</code> (XSalsa20+Poly1305) is a fast alternative if AES hardware acceleration is unavailable.</li>
                    <li><strong>identity FIRST silently disables encryption:</strong> because the first provider always wins for writes, an <code>identity</code> entry placed first means every new Secret is written in PLAINTEXT — with no warning. It must only ever appear LAST (19.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>Removing a key too early = permanent data loss:</strong> if you delete a key from the providers list while any Secret is still encrypted with it, and no remaining provider can decrypt it, that Secret becomes permanently unreadable via the API. Always re-encrypt ALL Secrets first, verify, THEN remove the old key (19.6).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>Prefer <code>aesgcm</code> or an external <code>kms</code> provider for new configurations — the official docs flag <code>aescbc</code> as weaker; only reach for aescbc when the exam task explicitly asks for it or for legacy compatibility.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get --raw /healthz/etcd</code> — the fastest sanity check that etcd is reachable before you start debugging "is encryption even working."</li>
