                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Enable encryption at rest for Secrets. Write the EncryptionConfiguration and the API server flag needed.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
- resources: ["secrets"]
  providers:
  - aescbc:
      keys:
      - name: key1
        secret: &lt;base64-32-byte-key&gt;
  - identity: {}  # LAST — fallback for reading old plaintext data
# API server flag: --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After enabling encryption, how do you verify that EXISTING secrets are encrypted? What command do you run?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>First, re-write all secrets: <code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code>. Then check etcd directly: <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/ns/name | hexdump -C</code>. Encrypted secrets show garbled binary with <code>k8s:enc:aescbc:v1:key1:</code> prefix. If you see plaintext (e.g., "django-secret-key"), encryption is not working for that specific secret.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why must the "identity" provider be LAST in the EncryptionConfiguration providers list?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The FIRST provider in the list is used for WRITES (19.6). If identity is first, all new secrets are written in plaintext. <code>identity: {}</code> means "no encryption" — it reads/writes plaintext data. It's included as the LAST provider for READING old data that was stored before encryption was enabled. Once all old secrets are rotated, you can remove the identity provider.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you rotate the encryption key? What happens if you remove an old key before all secrets are re-encrypted?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Add <code>key2</code> with a new secret to the providers list (FIRST position). Run <code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code> to re-encrypt all secrets with key2. Keep key1 in second position for reading old secrets. If you remove key1 before re-encryption, secrets still encrypted with key1 become PERMANENTLY UNREADABLE — the API server cannot decrypt them (the 19.6 critical failure mode). Always keep old keys until re-encryption is verified complete.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The EncryptionConfiguration YAML is created but the API server can't read it. What two things must you configure in the API server manifest BESIDES the flag?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>You need a <strong>volume</strong> (hostPath) and a <strong>volumeMount</strong> in the API server manifest:</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># In /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml:
spec:
  containers:
  - name: kube-apiserver
    command:
    - --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/encryption/encryption-config.yaml
    volumeMounts:
    - name: encryption-config
      mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/encryption
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: encryption-config
    hostPath:
      path: /etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml
      type: File</code></pre>
                    <p>The flag alone is not enough — the file must be accessible inside the API server container via a volume mount.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the #1 CKS exam trap for encryption configuration. Adding the flag without the volume mount results in "file not found" and the API server silently falls back to no encryption. Always verify with <code>kubectl get --raw /healthz/etcd</code> — if encryption is active, etcd health shows the encryption provider status.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What Kubernetes resources can be encrypted at rest? Beyond Secrets, what else should you consider encrypting?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>EncryptionConfiguration supports: <code>secrets</code>, <code>configmaps</code>, <code>serviceaccounts</code>, <code>tokenreviews</code>, <code>tokenrequests</code>, and custom resources (19.6 — CRDs require v1.26+ and must be explicitly listed or wildcard-matched). Beyond secrets, consider encrypting: (1) <strong>ConfigMaps</strong> — often contain semi-sensitive data like DB connection strings, feature flags, (2) <strong>ServiceAccount tokens</strong> — the token Secret contains a JWT that can be used to authenticate, (3) <strong>Custom resources</strong> — if you store sensitive app config in CRDs.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>While Secrets are the primary target, ConfigMaps often contain sensitive data that developers mistakenly put there (thinking "it's just config"). The CKS exam expects you to encrypt at minimum Secrets, but consider encrypting ConfigMaps if they contain sensitive values. Note: encrypting all resources increases CPU usage on the API server.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you verify that encryption at rest is working without accessing etcd directly? What API endpoint confirms encryption status?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Check encryption status via API server health endpoint
kubectl get --raw /healthz/etcd
# Output includes: "ok" if etcd is healthy

# Check encryption config
kubectl get --raw /livez/poststarthook/start-kube-apiserver-admission-initializer

# Verify a specific secret is encrypted (indirect method)
kubectl create secret generic verify-enc --from-literal=test=value
kubectl get secret verify-enc -o jsonpath='{.data.test}' | base64 -d
# Should decode to "value" — encryption is at the etcd level, not the API level</code></pre>
                    <p>There is no direct API endpoint that says "encryption is active." The verification method is to create a secret and check etcd directly. Or verify the API server has the flag: <code>ps aux | grep encryption-provider-config</code>.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Encryption is transparent to kubectl — you always see decrypted data. The only way to verify encryption is working is to: (1) check the API server has <code>--encryption-provider-config</code>, (2) check the config file exists and has valid keys, (3) read raw etcd data and confirm it's garbled. On the CKS exam, the task typically asks you to "verify encryption" — this means reading etcd directly.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You encrypt Secrets with aescbc. A developer complains that <code>kubectl get secret my-secret -o yaml</code> shows the data as base64-encoded plaintext. Is encryption broken?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>No, encryption is NOT broken.</strong> Encryption at rest protects data in etcd, not in transit via kubectl. When you run <code>kubectl get secret</code>, the API server reads the encrypted data from etcd, DECRYPTS it, and sends the plaintext over TLS to your kubectl. Base64 is encoding, not encryption — it's how Secrets are always represented in the API. The data IS encrypted in etcd, but kubectl always shows the decrypted value (base64-encoded for display).</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a common misunderstanding. Encryption at rest protects the data AT REST (on disk, in etcd). When accessed via the API server with proper authentication, the data is decrypted and served. The threat model: an attacker who steals the etcd disk or gains direct etcd access CANNOT read the data. An attacker with kubectl and RBAC access CAN read it (because they're authenticated).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to encrypt Secrets AND ConfigMaps with different encryption keys. Write the EncryptionConfiguration that uses key-secrets for Secrets and key-configmaps for ConfigMaps.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
- resources: ["secrets"]
  providers:
  - aescbc:
      keys:
      - name: key-secrets
        secret: &lt;base64-32-byte-key-1&gt;
  - identity: {}
- resources: ["configmaps"]
  providers:
  - aescbc:
      keys:
      - name: key-configmaps
        secret: &lt;base64-32-byte-key-2&gt;
  - identity: {}</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Each resource group in EncryptionConfiguration can have its own set of encryption providers and keys. This is defense in depth: if key-configmaps is compromised, Secrets are still protected by key-secrets. On the CKS exam, you may need to configure different encryption for different resource types — this is the pattern.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After a security incident, you suspect the encryption key was compromised. Walk through the complete key rotation and re-encryption process to ensure all secrets are protected with a new key.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="process-steps" style="flex-direction: column;"><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Generate new key</h5><p><code>head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64</code> — save as new-key.txt.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Update config</h5><p>Add new key as FIRST provider. Keep old (compromised) key as second provider for decryption of existing secrets.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Restart API server</h5><p>Wait for API server to restart with new config (30-60s).</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Re-encrypt ALL secrets</h5><p><code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code>. This reads each secret (decrypted with old key) and writes it back (encrypted with new key).</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Remove compromised key</h5><p>Delete the old key from providers list. Restart API server. Now only the new key can decrypt anything.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Revoke compromised key</h5><p>Securely delete the old key material. Revoke any cloud KMS grants.</p></div></div></div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Key rotation after a compromise follows the same process as routine rotation, but with urgency. The critical step is #4: re-encrypting ALL secrets. Until this completes, secrets encrypted with the compromised key are still vulnerable (if the attacker has etcd access). Steps 4 → 5 must never be reordered — that ordering IS the 19.6 critical failure mode guard. On the CKS exam, know the difference between routine rotation (keep old key for decryption) and post-compromise rotation (remove old key after all data is re-encrypted).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does every key-rotation example in this chapter put the NEW key FIRST in the providers list and the OLD key second, instead of just replacing the old key directly?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Because of the official provider-ordering rule (19.6): the <strong>first</strong> provider is used for ALL new writes, and <strong>every</strong> provider is tried in order for reads until one succeeds. Putting the new key first means every new write is immediately protected by it. Keeping the old key in second position means Secrets still encrypted with the old key remain readable — the API server falls through to provider 2 on read. If you replaced the old key directly (removed it instead of demoting it), any Secret not yet re-encrypted would become permanently undecryptable the instant the API server reads it — no provider left in the list could produce the plaintext.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This question is the "why" behind every rotation command in 19.2, 19.4, and 19.5 — they all follow the identical add-new-key-first, re-encrypt, then-remove-old-key sequence because that sequence is the only one consistent with how the API server actually resolves reads and writes against the providers list.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

