            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-cheatsheet"></span>
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">⚡ Ch 19 Visual Cheat Sheet — Secrets Encryption</div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔐</div><h5>Encryption at Rest</h5><p>Secrets encrypted in etcd. aesgcm/kms recommended (19.6). Enable via EncryptionConfiguration.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div><h5>Key Rotation</h5><p>Add new key first. Re-encrypt all secrets. Keep old keys for decryption until rotation is verified complete.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div><h5>Volume Mount Required</h5><p>Flag + volumeMount + volume. Missing any = encryption silently disabled.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🥇</div><h5>Provider Order (19.6)</h5><p>First provider = ALL writes. Every provider tried in order for reads. identity NEVER first.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p class="mb-0">Four things to remember cold: encryption protects data in etcd, not data returned by the API (kubectl always shows plaintext); enabling it is not retroactive — you must rotate every existing Secret; the API server flag needs a matching volume + volumeMount or it silently fails; and the entire providers-list ordering convention exists because of one rule — first provider writes, every provider is tried in order for reads (19.6).</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📊 Chapter 19 Visual Summary — Encryption at Rest at a Glance</div>
