</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Kubernetes Secrets are base64, not encrypted (19.1) — so the first job is turning on encryption at rest with an EncryptionConfiguration, the matching API server flag AND volume mount, and a full rotation of every existing Secret (19.2). Provider choice (19.3) then decides how strong that protection actually is: aesgcm/secretbox for solid local encryption, kms for envelope encryption where the key never touches the cluster, identity only as a migration fallback — never as the active provider.</p>
                    <p>Applied to easypj (19.4), that means separate Secrets per concern, injection via envFrom rather than hardcoding, and a standing RBAC audit of who can actually read them. Rotation (19.5) has three flavors — delete+recreate, in-place update, and encryption-key rotation — each with its own blast radius if mishandled.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> every command in 19.2-19.5 is really just an application of the ONE rule in 19.6 — the first provider always wins for writes, every provider is tried in order for reads, and that is exactly why rotation always adds the new key first and removes the old key only after re-encryption is verified complete.</p>
