            <section class="section-block" id="s20-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.1 Why Kubernetes Secrets Alone Are Not Enough for Enterprise</h3>
                <p>Chapter 19 solved the problem of encrypting Secrets in etcd. But encryption at rest is just one piece of the puzzle. Here are the gaps that Kubernetes Secrets alone cannot fill:</p>

                <h4>K8s Secrets vs External Secret Managers</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Kubernetes Secrets</th><th>HashiCorp Vault</th><th>AWS Secrets Manager</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Encryption at rest</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ With EncryptionConfiguration (Ch19)</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Always encrypted</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Always encrypted</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Automatic rotation</strong></td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Manual only</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Automatic with database plugins</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Built-in rotation schedules</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Dynamic secrets</strong></td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Static only</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Creates per-pod, auto-expiring creds</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Static only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Access audit</strong></td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Only via K8s audit logs (complex)</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Built-in audit device</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ CloudTrail integration</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Centralized mgmt</strong></td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ Per-cluster</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ One Vault for all clusters</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Regional service</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Multi-cloud</strong></td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ K8s only</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ K8s + VMs + serverless</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">⚠️ AWS only</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 20.2 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.2 HashiCorp Vault — Enterprise-Grade Secret Management</h3>
                <p>Vault is the gold standard for secret management. It stores, leases, and rotates secrets; generates dynamic database credentials on demand; and provides a complete audit trail. For Kubernetes, Vault integrates via the <strong>Kubernetes auth method</strong> — pods authenticate to Vault using their ServiceAccount JWT tokens, and Vault verifies the token with the Kubernetes API before granting access:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Vault Kubernetes Auth Flow — Pod → ServiceAccount → Vault → Dynamic Secret</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  VAULT + KUBERNETES INTEGRATION FLOW                                    │
  │                                                                         │
  │  STEP 1: POD AUTHENTICATES TO VAULT                                     │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────                                   │
  │  easypj pod has a ServiceAccount token mounted at:                      │
  │  /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token                    │
  │                                                                         │
  │  Vault Agent (sidecar or init container) reads this token and sends it  │
  │  to Vault: POST /v1/auth/kubernetes/login                               │
  │  {                                                                       │
  │    "role": "easypj-django",                                              │
  │    "jwt": "&lt;ServiceAccount JWT&gt;"                                       │
  │  }                                                                       │
  │                                                                         │
  │  STEP 2: VAULT VERIFIES THE TOKEN                                       │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────                                      │
  │  Vault calls the Kubernetes TokenReview API:                             │
  │  POST https://kube-apiserver:6443/apis/authentication.k8s.io/v1/tokenreviews│
  │                                                                         │
  │  K8s API verifies: ✅ Token is valid, signed by the cluster's CA        │
  │                    ✅ Token belongs to SA "easypj-sa" in ns "easypj-prod"│
  │                                                                         │
  │  Vault checks its role binding: "easypj-django" role allows SA          │
  │  "easypj-sa" in namespace "easypj-prod" → ✅ MATCH                       │
  │                                                                         │
  │  STEP 3: VAULT RETURNS A VAULT TOKEN + SECRETS                          │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────────                             │
  │  Vault issues a short-lived Vault token (TTL: 1 hour) and returns       │
  │  the secrets the easypj-django role is allowed to read:                 │
  │  {                                                                       │
  │    "data": {                                                             │
  │      "database-password": "xyz-dynamic-cred-2026",                       │
  │      "django-secret-key": "50-char-random-string",                       │
  │      "redis-password": "redis-secret-xyz"                                │
  │    },                                                                    │
  │    "lease_duration": 3600,                                               │
  │    "renewable": true                                                     │
  │  }                                                                       │
  │                                                                         │
  │  STEP 4: VAULT AGENT WRITES SECRETS TO POD FILESYSTEM                    │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────                    │
  │  Vault Agent sidecar writes secrets to a shared volume:                 │
  │  /vault/secrets/database-password → "xyz-dynamic-cred-2026"             │
  │  /vault/secrets/django-secret-key → "50-char-random-string"              │
  │                                                                         │
  │  The easypj app reads these files at startup (or watches for changes).  │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Dynamic Database Secrets — How Vault Generates Per-Pod Credentials</h4>
                <p>One of Vault's most powerful features is <strong>dynamic secrets</strong> for databases. Instead of storing a static database password that every pod shares (and that never changes), Vault generates a <strong>unique username and password for each pod</strong> that automatically expires after a configurable TTL. When the pod terminates (or the lease expires), Vault revokes the credential on the database. This means a compromised database password is worthless after the TTL — and each pod has a unique credential, so you can trace database activity to a specific pod:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Vault Dynamic Database Secrets — Per-Pod, Auto-Expiring Credentials</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  VAULT DYNAMIC DATABASE SECRET FLOW                                     │
  │                                                                         │
  │  SETUP (one-time):                                                      │
  │  ─────────────────                                                      │
  │  Vault admin configures the PostgreSQL database plugin:                 │
  │  vault write database/config/easypj-postgres \                          │
  │    plugin_name=postgresql-database-plugin \                              │
  │    allowed_roles="easypj-readonly" \                                      │
  │    connection_url="postgresql://{{username}}:{{password}}@postgres:5432/easypj"│
  │    username="vault-admin" \                                               │
  │    password="vault-admin-password"                                        │
  │                                                                         │
  │  Vault creates a role that defines the SQL to create credentials:        │
  │  vault write database/roles/easypj-readonly \                            │
  │    db_name=easypj-postgres \                                              │
  │    creation_statements="CREATE USER \"{{name}}\" WITH PASSWORD '{{password}}'│
  │                         VALID UNTIL '{{expiration}}'; \                    │
  │                         GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public     │
  │                         TO \"{{name}}\";" \                                  │
  │    default_ttl="1h" \                                                     │
  │    max_ttl="24h"                                                          │
  │                                                                         │
  │  RUNTIME (every pod startup):                                           │
  │  ────────────────────────────                                             │
  │  1. easypj pod starts → Vault Agent requests database credentials:       │
  │     vault read database/creds/easypj-readonly                            │
  │                                                                         │
  │  2. Vault connects to PostgreSQL AS vault-admin.                        │
  │  3. Vault executes the creation_statements SQL:                          │
  │     CREATE USER "v-easypj-abc123" WITH PASSWORD 'xyz-dynamic'            │
  │     VALID UNTIL '2026-06-14 15:00:00';                                    │
  │     GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO "v-easypj-abc123";    │
  │                                                                         │
  │  4. Vault returns the credentials to the pod:                            │
  │     username: v-easypj-abc123                                            │
  │     password: xyz-dynamic                                                │
  │     lease_id: database/creds/easypj-readonly/abc123                      │
  │     lease_duration: 1h                                                   │
  │                                                                         │
  │  5. Pod uses these credentials to connect to PostgreSQL.                │
  │                                                                         │
  │  AFTER 1 HOUR (TTL expiry):                                             │
  │  ────────────────────────────                                             │
  │  • Vault automatically connects to PostgreSQL and runs:                  │
  │    DROP USER "v-easypj-abc123"                                            │
  │  • The credential is REVOKED on the database.                            │
  │  • The pod needs to request NEW credentials (or its lease was renewed). │
  │                                                                         │
  │  ⚡ SECURITY BENEFITS:                                                   │
  │  ─────────────────────                                                    │
  │  • Stolen password → valid for MAX 1 hour (then auto-revoked)           │
  │  • Each pod has UNIQUE credentials → trace DB activity per pod          │
  │  • No static password in Secrets, ConfigMaps, or env vars               │
  │  • PostgreSQL never knows a long-lived password exists                  │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 20.3 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.3 External Secrets Operator (ESO)</h3>
                <p>The External Secrets Operator (ESO) takes a different approach from Vault Agent. Instead of injecting secrets directly into pods, ESO <strong>syncs secrets from external providers into Kubernetes Secrets</strong>. Your pods continue to use native K8s Secrets (envFrom, volume mounts) — they don't know the secrets came from AWS/GCP/Vault. This gives you external management with zero application changes:</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — ExternalSecret resource for easypj using AWS Secrets Manager</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1beta1
kind: ExternalSecret
metadata:
  name: easypj-db-creds
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  refreshInterval: 1h              # Sync every hour
  secretStoreRef:
    name: aws-secrets-manager      # Which backend to pull from
    kind: SecretStore
  target:
    name: db-creds                 # K8s Secret to create/update
    creationPolicy: Owner
  data:
  - secretKey: DATABASE_URL        # Key in the K8s Secret
    remoteRef:
      key: easypj/prod/database    # Key in AWS Secrets Manager
      property: url</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>ESO vs Vault Agent — Which Architecture to Choose</h4>
                <p>Both ESO and Vault Agent solve the external-secrets problem, but with fundamentally different architectures. The choice depends on whether you want zero application changes (ESO) or maximum security with dynamic secrets (Vault Agent):</p>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left" style="flex:1;">
                        <div class="info-box">
                            <h5>🔄 ESO (External Secrets Operator)</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>How:</strong> Syncs external secrets → K8s Secrets</li>
                                <li><strong>App changes:</strong> ZERO (uses native K8s Secret API)</li>
                                <li><strong>Refresh:</strong> Polling (1h interval typical)</li>
                                <li><strong>Dynamic secrets:</strong> Not supported</li>
                                <li><strong>Best for:</strong> AWS/GCP shops, lift-and-shift migrations</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel-right" style="flex:1;">
                        <div class="info-box">
                            <h5>🏦 Vault Agent Sidecar</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>How:</strong> Injects secrets directly into pod filesystem</li>
                                <li><strong>App changes:</strong> Read from /vault/secrets/ instead of env vars</li>
                                <li><strong>Refresh:</strong> Continuous (renews lease before expiry)</li>
                                <li><strong>Dynamic secrets:</strong> ✅ Fully supported</li>
                                <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Zero-trust, dynamic DB creds, multi-cloud</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Dynamic Secrets — The Killer Feature of Vault</h4>
                <p>Static secrets (like the ones in Kubernetes Secrets) are a "steal once, use forever" problem. <strong>Dynamic secrets</strong> are generated on-demand per request, automatically expire, and are unique to each consumer. Here's how Vault dynamic database credentials work for easypj:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Dynamic Database Credentials — Generated per Pod, Auto-Expiring</div>
<pre>
  1. easypj pod starts → Vault Agent sidecar reads SA token
  2. Sidecar authenticates to Vault: POST /v1/auth/kubernetes/login
     → Vault verifies token with K8s API → returns Vault token
  3. Sidecar requests database credentials:
     GET /v1/database/creds/easypj-readonly
     → Vault creates TEMPORARY postgres user with limited permissions
     → Returns: username (v-token-easypj-abc123), password (random),
        lease_duration: 1h, lease_id: database/creds/easypj-readonly/xyz
  4. Sidecar writes credentials to pod filesystem:
     /vault/secrets/db-username, /vault/secrets/db-password
  5. Django reads these files → connects to postgres
  6. After 1 hour: credentials EXPIRE → Vault automatically revokes
     the temporary postgres user → App must request new credentials

  🔑 KEY ADVANTAGES:
  • Stolen credentials are useless after 1 hour
  • Each pod gets UNIQUE credentials (blast radius isolation)
  • Postgres audit log shows WHICH pod accessed data (per-credential)
  • No manual rotation — Vault handles everything
  • If pod is compromised: credentials expire in &lt;1 hour automatically
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 20.4 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.4 Implementing Vault for easypj</h3>
                <p>Here is the complete Vault configuration for the easypj Django application. The Vault policy grants access to the secrets the Django app needs, the Kubernetes auth role binds the easypj ServiceAccount to that policy, and the Vault Agent sidecar injects secrets into the pod:</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">HCL — Vault policy for easypj Django application</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-hcl"># Vault policy: easypj-django-policy
path "secret/data/easypj-prod/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
path "database/creds/easypj-readonly" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}</code></pre>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Configure Kubernetes auth in Vault
vault auth enable kubernetes

vault write auth/kubernetes/config \
    token_reviewer_jwt="$(cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token)" \
    kubernetes_host="https://kube-apiserver:6443" \
    kubernetes_ca_cert="@/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt"

# Bind the easypj ServiceAccount to the policy
vault write auth/kubernetes/role/easypj-django \
    bound_service_account_names=easypj-sa \
    bound_service_account_namespaces=easypj-prod \
    policies=easypj-django-policy \
    ttl=1h</code></pre>
