            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Kubernetes Secrets alone leave four enterprise gaps open:</strong> no automatic rotation, no dynamic (auto-expiring) secrets, no built-in per-access audit trail, and no centralized management across clusters, VMs, and serverless — external managers like Vault exist specifically to close these gaps (20.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Vault's Kubernetes auth method trusts K8s identity, not a separate credential:</strong> a pod's ServiceAccount JWT is verified by Vault via the Kubernetes TokenReview API, and a matching role binding issues a short-lived Vault token scoped to specific policies (20.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Dynamic secrets are Vault's signature feature:</strong> per-pod database credentials are generated on demand, are unique per consumer, and are automatically revoked at TTL expiry — turning "stolen credential" into a bounded-time problem instead of a permanent one (20.2, 20.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>ESO and Vault Agent solve the same problem with opposite trade-offs:</strong> ESO syncs external secrets into ordinary K8s Secrets with zero application changes (but the data still lands in etcd); Vault Agent injects secrets directly into the pod filesystem, bypassing etcd entirely and supporting dynamic secrets, at the cost of the app needing to read from files instead of env vars (20.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>A working Vault integration is three pieces wired together:</strong> a least-privilege HCL policy scoped to exactly the paths easypj needs, a Kubernetes auth role binding the easypj-sa ServiceAccount in easypj-prod to that policy, and a Vault Agent sidecar (or CSI driver) that performs the login and writes secrets into the pod (20.4).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 20 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Vault token expiry:</strong> Vault tokens have a TTL. If the External Secrets Operator's Vault token expires, it stops syncing secrets — and existing Kubernetes Secrets remain with stale values. Always configure token renewal or use the Kubernetes auth method (which auto-renews).</li>
                    <li><strong>Secrets are still in etcd:</strong> External Secrets Operator creates regular Kubernetes Secret objects — which are stored in etcd. The external backend (Vault/AWS/GCP) is the SOURCE OF TRUTH, but the Secret data still exists in etcd. Use encryption at rest (Ch 19) for defense-in-depth.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

