            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 20 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the key difference between storing secrets in Kubernetes Secrets vs HashiCorp Vault?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Kubernetes Secrets: stored in etcd (encrypted at rest if configured), accessed via API server, no automatic rotation, no audit per-secret access. Vault: stored externally (never in etcd), injected at runtime via sidecar/CSI, supports dynamic secrets + automatic rotation, full audit trail per secret access, fine-grained access policies per secret path.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does a Kubernetes pod authenticate to HashiCorp Vault? What credential does it use?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The pod uses its mounted ServiceAccount JWT token. The Vault agent sends this token to Vault's Kubernetes auth endpoint. Vault calls the Kubernetes TokenReview API to validate the token and extract the SA's identity (namespace, name, UID). Vault then checks if this SA matches a configured Vault role and issues a Vault token with the role's policies.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the External Secrets Operator (ESO) and how does it differ from Vault Agent Sidecar?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>ESO:</strong> Syncs secrets from external providers (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager) into Kubernetes Secrets. The secret STILL ends up in etcd (but can be encrypted at rest). Apps read from K8s Secrets as usual. <strong>Vault Agent Sidecar:</strong> Injects secrets directly into the pod's filesystem as a shared volume. Secrets NEVER touch etcd. The app reads from a file, not a K8s Secret. ESO is simpler to adopt (no app changes), Vault Agent is more secure (secrets bypass etcd entirely).</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What Vault command stores a secret and what command reads it back? Write both.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">vault kv put secret/easypj/prod django-secret-key="value" db-password="pass"
vault kv get secret/easypj/prod
vault kv get -field=django-secret-key secret/easypj/prod  # Get single field</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span><div class="eq-question">How does Vault's dynamic secret engine for databases work? What security advantage does it provide over static secrets?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Vault connects to the database as a privileged user and creates a temporary, per-request database credential. Each pod gets a UNIQUE username/password that: (1) is valid for a short TTL (e.g., 1 hour), (2) is automatically revoked when the TTL expires, (3) has scoped permissions (e.g., read-only on specific tables). <strong>Advantage over static secrets:</strong> even if an attacker steals the credential, it expires quickly and can only access what's explicitly granted. No long-lived shared database password exists anywhere.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Dynamic secrets are Vault's killer feature for CKS. Instead of storing a shared PostgreSQL password that never changes, each pod instance gets its own temporary credential. On the exam, if asked about "credential rotation" or "preventing lateral movement after credential theft," dynamic secrets are the answer.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span><div class="eq-question">Compare Vault Agent Sidecar Injector vs Vault CSI Provider. Which one requires application changes, and which is transparent to the app?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>Vault Agent Sidecar:</strong> Injects a Vault agent container alongside your app. Secrets are written to a shared memory volume (<code>/vault/secrets/</code>). The app reads secrets from FILES. Requires some app awareness (knowing the file path). <strong>Vault CSI Provider:</strong> Uses the Kubernetes CSI (Container Storage Interface) to mount secrets as a volume. Secrets appear as files in the pod but NO sidecar container is needed. Completely transparent to the app. CSI is the newer, preferred approach on CKS because it's simpler and uses fewer resources.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Both methods keep secrets out of etcd. The difference is implementation: Sidecar = extra container + shared volume, CSI = kernel-level volume mount. On the CKS exam, CSI Provider is the modern recommended approach. But you should know both exist — older clusters may use the sidecar method.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span><div class="eq-question">Write the Vault policy that allows an easypj pod to read ONLY its own database password at <code>secret/easypj/prod</code> and nothing else.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-hcl">path "secret/data/easypj/prod" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
# Deny everything else
path "*" {
  capabilities = ["deny"]
}</code></pre><p>Bind this policy to a Vault role that maps to the <code>easypj-sa</code> ServiceAccount in the <code>prod</code> namespace. The pod can only read <code>secret/easypj/prod</code> — it cannot read <code>secret/other-app/prod</code> or write any secret.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Vault policies follow the least privilege principle — exactly like Kubernetes RBAC. Each application should have its own Vault policy scoped to its own secret paths. On the CKS exam, you may need to write a Vault policy or explain how Vault RBAC maps to Kubernetes RBAC. The key concept: Vault policies are path-based (file-system-like), not resource-based (like K8s RBAC).</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span><div class="eq-question">What is Vault's Kubernetes auth method, and what Kubernetes API does Vault call to validate a pod's identity?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Vault calls <strong>TokenReview</strong> API (<code>POST /apis/authentication.k8s.io/v1/tokenreviews</code>) to validate the pod's ServiceAccount JWT. The flow: (1) Pod sends its SA JWT to Vault. (2) Vault forwards the JWT to the Kubernetes API server's TokenReview endpoint. (3) API server validates the JWT signature and returns the SA identity (namespace, name, UID, groups). (4) Vault maps this identity to a Vault role. (5) Vault issues a Vault token scoped to that role's policies.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is Kubernetes-native authentication for external systems. Vault trusts the API server's token validation. The pod never needs a separate Vault credential — its Kubernetes identity IS its Vault identity. On the CKS exam, understand that Vault's Kubernetes auth method eliminates the need for static Vault tokens in pods.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span><div class="eq-question">An External Secrets Operator SecretStore fails to connect to AWS Secrets Manager with "AccessDenied." Where do you check the IAM permissions: on the node role, the pod's IRSA role, or the ESO controller's SA?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Check the <strong>ESO controller's ServiceAccount IRSA role</strong> (or the SecretStore's referenced credentials). ESO uses its own identity to fetch secrets from external providers. It does NOT use the node's IAM role or the application pod's SA. The flow: ESO controller → authenticates to AWS using its SA's IRSA → fetches secret from Secrets Manager → creates Kubernetes Secret → application pod reads the K8s Secret (no direct AWS access needed by the app pod).</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a common ESO debugging question. The SecretStore configuration determines which credentials ESO uses. In AWS, this is typically IRSA mapped to the ESO ServiceAccount. On the CKS exam: (1) ESO controller SA needs IAM permissions to read secrets, (2) application pods do NOT need IAM permissions, (3) node role is irrelevant for ESO.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span><div class="eq-question">You need to migrate 50 Kubernetes Secrets from plaintext etcd storage to Vault with zero application downtime. Describe the migration strategy using External Secrets Operator.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><ol><li><strong>Set up Vault + ESO:</strong> Deploy ESO, create SecretStore pointing to Vault.</li><li><strong>Import secrets to Vault:</strong> Export each K8s Secret → import to Vault: <code>kubectl get secret &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.data}' | while read k v; do vault kv put secret/app/$k $(echo $v | base64 -d); done</code>.</li><li><strong>Create ExternalSecrets:</strong> For each secret, create an ExternalSecret resource that maps Vault paths to K8s Secret keys. ESO syncs the secrets.</li><li><strong>Update Deployments:</strong> Change <code>secretKeyRef</code> references to point to the ESO-managed Secret names (same names, just now ESO-managed).</li><li><strong>Verify:</strong> Deploy to staging first. Confirm pods start and function. Rolling update to production.</li><li><strong>Cleanup:</strong> After verification, delete the original manually-created Secrets (ESO now owns them).</li></ol><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a real CKS scenario: migrating from native K8s Secrets to an external provider. The key design decision: ESO creates K8s Secrets that look identical to manually-created ones — same names, same keys. Applications don't need to change how they consume secrets (<code>secretKeyRef</code> still works). The only difference is the Secret is now synced from Vault instead of manually created.</p></div></details></div>
            </div>

