            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 21 · Container Runtime Sandboxing — gVisor and Kata Containers</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Every container on a Kubernetes node shares the <strong>same Linux kernel</strong>. This is the defining architectural property of containerization — and its greatest security weakness. A single kernel exploit (like CVE-2022-0185) in one container gives the attacker access to every other container AND the host. Seccomp (Ch15) and AppArmor (Ch14) reduce the attack surface by blocking syscalls and file access — but they cannot protect against a zero-day kernel vulnerability that bypasses those filters. <strong>Sandboxed runtimes</strong> — gVisor and Kata Containers — solve this by adding an additional isolation layer between the container and the host kernel. gVisor intercepts syscalls in userspace with a written-from-scratch kernel simulator (Sentry). Kata Containers runs each container in its own lightweight virtual machine with a dedicated kernel. This chapter teaches you how both work, how to select them via RuntimeClass, and when to use each for the easypj application.</p>
