            <section class="section-block" id="s21-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.1 The Container Isolation Problem</h3>
                <p>Standard containers (runc) use <strong>Linux namespaces and cgroups</strong> for isolation — software constructs in the shared kernel. There is no hardware boundary. When a process in one container exploits a kernel vulnerability, it breaks through the namespace boundaries and gains access to the shared kernel — and from there, to every process, file, and network socket on the node. Sandboxed runtimes add a barrier that even a kernel exploit cannot cross — either by intercepting syscalls before they reach the kernel (gVisor) or by giving each container its own kernel in a VM (Kata).</p>

                <h4>Three Levels of Container Isolation</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">runc (Default) vs gVisor vs Kata — Isolation Depth Compared</div>
<pre>
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  DEFAULT (runc) — Namespace Isolation Only                                  │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────                                      │
  │  ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐  ← All containers share            │
  │  │  Pod A   │ │  Pod B   │ │  Pod C   │     one kernel. Kernel             │
  │  │  Django  │ │  Nginx   │ │ Attacker │     exploit in Pod C                │
  │  └────┬─────┘ └────┬─────┘ └────┬─────┘     → root on host.                 │
  │       │            │            │                                           │
  │  ┌────┴────────────┴────────────┴─────┐                                      │
  │  │        SINGLE LINUX KERNEL         │  ← SHARED — one exploit = all pods  │
  │  └────────────────┬──────────────────┘                                      │
  │                   │                                                         │
  │  ┌────────────────┴──────────────────┐                                      │
  │  │           HARDWARE                │                                      │
  │  └───────────────────────────────────┘                                      │
  │                                                                            │
  │  Security: LOW. Kernel exploit → full node compromise.                     │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  gVisor (runsc) — User-Space Kernel Interception                            │
  │  ───────────────────────────────────────────                                │
  │  ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐                                    │
  │  │  Pod A   │ │  Pod B   │ │  Pod C   │  ← Applications see "Linux"        │
  │  │  Django  │ │  Nginx   │ │ Attacker │                                     │
  │  └────┬─────┘ └────┬─────┘ └────┬─────┘                                    │
  │       │ syscall     │ syscall    │ syscall                                  │
  │       ▼            ▼            ▼                                          │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐                                   │
  │  │   SENTRY (User-Space Kernel)         │  ← Written by Google. Handles     │
  │  │   • Implements ~300 Linux syscalls   │     syscalls WITHOUT the host     │
  │  │   • File system: Gofer proxy         │     kernel. Only ~20 minimal      │
  │  │   • Network: user-space TCP/IP stack │     syscalls reach host kernel.  │
  │  │   • Memory: isolated virtual memory  │                                   │
  │  └────────────────┬─────────────────────┘                                   │
  │                   │ ~20 minimal host syscalls (read, write, futex, etc.)    │
  │                   ▼                                                         │
  │  ┌────────────────┴──────────────────┐                                      │
  │  │        LINUX KERNEL (host)        │  ← Attack surface: 20 syscalls      │
  │  └───────────────────────────────────┘     instead of 300+.                  │
  │                                                                            │
  │  Security: HIGH. Kernel exploit in Pod C → trapped in Sentry.              │
  │  Sentry would need its OWN exploit to reach host kernel.                   │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  Kata Containers — Lightweight VM Per Pod                                   │
  │  ──────────────────────────────────────                                     │
  │  ┌─────────────┐ ┌─────────────┐ ┌─────────────┐                           │
  │  │   Pod A     │ │   Pod B     │ │   Pod C     │                           │
  │  │ ┌─────────┐ │ │ ┌─────────┐ │ │ ┌─────────┐ │                           │
  │  │ │Django   │ │ │ │Nginx    │ │ │ │Attacker │ │                           │
  │  │ └────┬────┘ │ │ └────┬────┘ │ │ └────┬────┘ │                           │
  │  │      │      │ │      │      │ │      │      │                           │
  │  │ ┌────┴────┐ │ │ ┌────┴────┐ │ │ ┌────┴────┐ │                           │
  │  │ │  KERNEL │ │ │ │  KERNEL │ │ │ │  KERNEL │ │  ← Dedicated kernel       │
  │  │ │ (Linux) │ │ │ │ (Linux) │ │ │ │ (Linux) │ │     per pod. HARDWARE     │
  │  │ └────┬────┘ │ │ └────┬────┘ │ │ └────┬────┘ │     isolated.             │
  │  └──────┼──────┘ └──────┼──────┘ └──────┼──────┘                           │
  │         │               │               │                                   │
  │  ┌──────┴───────────────┴───────────────┴──────┐                           │
  │  │          HYPERVISOR (QEMU / Firecracker)     │  ← Hardware-enforced      │
  │  └──────────────────────┬──────────────────────┘     isolation. Even a      │
  │                         │                            kernel exploit in Pod C │
  │  ┌──────────────────────┴──────────────────────┐    stays in Pod C's VM.   │
  │  │         LINUX KERNEL (host)                 │                           │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘                           │
  │                                                                            │
  │  Security: HIGHEST. Hardware-level isolation = same as separate servers.   │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>runc vs gVisor vs Kata — Comparison Table</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>runc (Default)</th><th>gVisor (runsc)</th><th>Kata Containers</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Isolation</strong></td><td>Namespaces only</td><td>User-space kernel (Sentry)</td><td>Lightweight VM per pod</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Kernel shared?</strong></td><td style="color:#326ce5;">Yes — shared by all pods</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">Partially — ~20 syscalls</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">No — dedicated per pod</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Performance</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">Native (no overhead)</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">~10% overhead</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">~20% overhead</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Startup time</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">Fastest</td><td style="color:#4ade80;">Fast</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">Slightly slower (boots VM)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Syscall support</strong></td><td>Full (~300+)</td><td>~300 implemented in Sentry</td><td>Full (has its own kernel)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Trusted workloads</td><td>Untrusted code, general workloads</td><td>Multi-tenant, maximum security</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>How gVisor Sentry Actually Works — The Syscall Interception Mechanism</h4>
                <p>gVisor's Sentry is not a full kernel — it's a <strong>user-space application</strong> written in Go that implements the Linux syscall interface. When a containerized application makes a syscall (e.g., <code>open("/etc/shadow")</code>), the normal path would be: CPU switches to kernel mode → kernel executes <code>sys_open()</code> → returns result. With gVisor, this path is intercepted at the <strong>platform layer</strong> before it ever reaches the host kernel:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">gVisor Architecture — How Syscalls Get from App to Host Kernel (Safely)</div>
<pre>
  APPLICATION: open("/app/config.py", O_RDONLY)
  │
  ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  SENTRY (User-Space Kernel) — Runs in the sandbox pod's process          │
  │  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────                       │
  │  1. App makes syscall → CPU switches to kernel mode → but gVisor uses    │
  │     ptrace/seccomp to INTERCEPT the syscall BEFORE the host kernel       │
  │     processes it.                                                         │
  │                                                                           │
  │  2. Sentry receives the syscall: open("/app/config.py", O_RDONLY)        │
  │                                                                           │
  │  3. Sentry has its OWN VFS (Virtual File System):                         │
  │     • Maintains a file descriptor table for the sandboxed process         │
  │     • Checks permissions against its OWN rules (not the host's)           │
  │     • If the file should be accessible → forwards to Gofer                │
  │     • If the file is NOT allowed → returns EACCES (never touches host)   │
  │                                                                           │
  │  4. Sentry translates the request into a 9P protocol message and sends   │
  │     it to the Gofer process (runs in a SEPARATE process, outside Sentry): │
  │     "Gofer: open /app/config.py, read 4096 bytes"                         │
  │                                                                           │
  │  5. Gofer makes the ACTUAL host syscall: open("/app/config.py") →         │
  │     Linux kernel processes it → returns file descriptor → Gofer reads    │
  │     4096 bytes → sends data back to Sentry via 9P → Sentry returns       │
  │     data to the application.                                               │
  │                                                                           │
  │  ⚡ WHY THIS IS SECURE:                                                    │
  │  ─────────────────────                                                     │
  │  • The application NEVER makes syscalls directly to the host kernel.     │
  │  • Sentry validates every syscall against its own rules BEFORE           │
  │    forwarding to Gofer.                                                    │
  │  • Gofer only performs a LIMITED set of host operations (file I/O,       │
  │    socket creation). No mounts, no ptrace, no module loading.            │
  │  • Sentry implements the syscall in USER SPACE — a memory bug in Sentry │
  │    corrupts Sentry's memory, NOT the host kernel.                         │
  │  • Kernel exploit in the application → the exploit runs inside Sentry,   │
  │    which is a user-space process. To reach the host kernel, the attacker │
  │    would need to exploit Sentry AND THEN exploit the host kernel —       │
  │    two completely independent vulnerabilities.                            │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 21.2 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s21-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.2 RuntimeClass — Selecting the Runtime in Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes uses <strong>RuntimeClass</strong> to let you specify which container runtime to use for each pod. You can run trusted pods with runc (fast, low overhead) and untrusted pods with gVisor or Kata (secure, isolated) — all in the same cluster:</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — RuntimeClass + Pod using gVisor</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ── RuntimeClass resource ──
apiVersion: node.k8s.io/v1
kind: RuntimeClass
metadata:
  name: gvisor
handler: runsc              # The CRI handler name for gVisor

# ── Pod using gVisor RuntimeClass ──
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: untrusted-workload
spec:
  runtimeClassName: gvisor   # ← Uses gVisor instead of default runc
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: untrusted-code-executor:v1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How RuntimeClass Maps to CRI Handlers in containerd</h4>
                <p>The <code>handler</code> field in RuntimeClass is not a magic string — it maps directly to a CRI handler registered in the container runtime's configuration. containerd uses a <strong>runtime configuration</strong> file that maps handler names to actual binary paths:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">TOML — containerd config.toml registering gVisor and Kata handlers</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-toml"># /etc/containerd/config.toml
version = 2

[plugins."io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri".containerd.runtimes.runc]
  runtime_type = "io.containerd.runc.v2"

[plugins."io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri".containerd.runtimes.runsc]
  runtime_type = "io.containerd.runsc.v1"

[plugins."io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri".containerd.runtimes.kata]
  runtime_type = "io.containerd.kata.v2"
  privileged_without_host_devices = true

# HOW THIS CONNECTS:
# RuntimeClass handler: "runsc" → containerd looks up [runtimes.runsc]
# → executes /usr/bin/runsc (gVisor) to create the container
# RuntimeClass handler: "kata" → containerd looks up [runtimes.kata]
# → executes /usr/bin/kata-runtime (Kata) to create the container</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Sandbox Performance Tradeoffs — When the Slowdown Is Worth It</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Workload Type</th><th>runc (Default)</th><th>gVisor</th><th>Kata Containers</th><th>Recommendation</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Web API (Django)</strong></td><td>100% baseline</td><td>~85% perf (syscall overhead)</td><td>~75% perf (VM overhead)</td><td>runc for easypj (trusted code, needs speed)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Database (Postgres)</strong></td><td>100% baseline</td><td>~70% perf (many syscalls)</td><td>~65% perf</td><td>runc (performance-critical)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>User upload handler</strong></td><td>100% baseline</td><td>~85% perf</td><td>~75% perf</td><td>gVisor (untrusted user input!)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CI/CD build jobs</strong></td><td>100% baseline</td><td>~80% perf</td><td>~70% perf</td><td>Kata (hardware isolation for untrusted builds)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Third-party microservice</strong></td><td>100% baseline</td><td>~85% perf</td><td>~75% perf</td><td>Kata or gVisor (untrusted vendor code)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Attack Scenario — What gVisor Prevents That runc Allows</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header">
                        <span class="scenario-badge danger">ATTACK SCENARIO</span>
                        <span class="scenario-title">CVE-2022-0185 — Linux Kernel Heap Overflow (CVSS 8.4)</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Attacker crafts malicious filesystem command</strong><p>Using the <code>unshare</code> syscall with <code>CLONE_NEWNS|CLONE_NEWUSER</code> flags, the attacker triggers a heap buffer overflow in the Linux kernel's <code>legacy_parse_param</code> function. This CVE affected ALL Linux kernels 5.1+ through early 2022.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>With runc: ATTACK SUCCEEDS</strong><p>runc containers share the host kernel. The attacker's exploit corrupts kernel memory → gains root privileges → escapes the container entirely. Full node compromise. <strong>Seccomp does NOT block unshare in RuntimeDefault!</strong></p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">🛡️</div><div class="step-content"><strong>With gVisor: ATTACK FAILS</strong><p>gVisor containers run on a USERSPACE kernel (Sentry). The attacker's exploit runs against Sentry, NOT the host Linux kernel. Sentry is written in Go (memory-safe language) — no heap overflow possible. <strong>Even if the attack "succeeds," the attacker only compromises Sentry, not the host.</strong> gVisor then applies a SECOND seccomp filter between Sentry and the host kernel for defense-in-depth.</p></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 21.3 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s21-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.3 When to Use Sandboxed Runtimes</h3>
                <p>Not every pod needs gVisor or Kata. The decision is a tradeoff between security and performance:</p>

                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="decision-node question">🔍 Is this pod running code from untrusted users? (User-uploaded code, third-party containers)</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ YES → Use gVisor or Kata. Example: easypj media processing container.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">❓ No — trusted internal code. Continue.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">🔍 Is this a multi-tenant cluster with different customers sharing nodes?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ YES → Use Kata Containers for hardware-level tenant isolation.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">❓ No — single tenant. Continue.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">🔍 Is this a system workload (CNI, CSI, monitoring) needing full kernel access?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div><div class="decision-node yes">✅ YES → Use runc (default). These pods need full syscall access.</div></div>
                        <div><div class="decision-node no">✅ Standard business workload (easypj Django) → runc + Seccomp + AppArmor is sufficient.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 21.4: Multi-Tenancy ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s21-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.4 Multi-Tenancy Isolation Patterns</h3>
                <p>The official CKS curriculum explicitly calls out <strong>multi-tenancy</strong> as a key isolation technique. Multi-tenancy means multiple teams, customers, or business units sharing the same Kubernetes cluster — each needing strong isolation guarantees that prevent one tenant from accessing another tenant's data, pods, or resources.</p>

                <h4>Multi-Tenancy Isolation Levels — From Soft to Hard</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Isolation Type</th><th>What It Separates</th><th>Bypass Risk</th><th>CKS Tool</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>1. Namespace</strong></td><td>Logical (Soft)</td><td>RBAC, ResourceQuota, NetworkPolicy per namespace</td><td>High — namespace is NOT a security boundary</td><td>Ch 9, Ch 6</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>2. Network</strong></td><td>Network (Medium)</td><td>NetworkPolicy, CiliumNetworkPolicy, Istio mTLS</td><td>Medium — misconfigured NP allows lateral</td><td>Ch 6, Ch 22</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>3. Pod Security</strong></td><td>Pod-Level (Medium)</td><td>PSS Restricted, securityContext, Seccomp, AppArmor</td><td>Medium — container escape may bypass</td><td>Ch 17, Ch 14, Ch 15</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>4. Runtime Sandbox</strong></td><td>Kernel (Hard)</td><td>gVisor user-space kernel per sandbox</td><td>Low — gVisor has its own syscall layer</td><td>Ch 21.1</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>5. Hardware VM</strong></td><td>Hardware (Hardest)</td><td>Kata Containers — dedicated lightweight VM per pod</td><td>Very Low — hardware-enforced isolation</td><td>Ch 21.2</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>6. Cluster-Level</strong></td><td>Physical (Maximum)</td><td>Separate clusters per tenant with federation</td><td>None — no shared kernel or network</td><td>Cluster API, vcluster</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>

                <h4>Multi-Tenancy Decision Framework</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-title">Which Isolation Level for Your Tenant?</div><pre>
  Q1: Do tenants share the same trust boundary?
      YES (same org) → Namespace + Network isolation (Level 1-2)
      NO (different customers) → Sandbox or Hardware VM (Level 4-5)

  Q2: Does any tenant run user-uploaded code?
      YES → Hardware VM isolation (Level 5-6)
      NO → Sandbox or Pod Security (Level 3-4)

  Q3: Is regulatory compliance required (PCI, HIPAA, SOC2)?
      YES → Hardware VM or separate cluster (Level 5-6)
      NO → Network + Pod Security sufficient (Level 2-3)
</pre></div>

                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 CKS Exam: Multi-Tenancy</h5><p>The exam may ask you to configure isolation for a specific tenant scenario. Key pattern: "Tenant A must not access Tenant B's pods" → NetworkPolicy with namespaceSelector. "Tenant A runs untrusted code" → gVisor RuntimeClass. "Tenant A needs PCI compliance" → Kata Containers or separate cluster.</p></div>
            </section>

