            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The shared kernel is the root weakness runc can't fix</strong> — every container on a node shares one Linux kernel, so a single kernel exploit reaches every pod. Sandboxed runtimes add a barrier a kernel exploit can't cross.</li>
                    <li><strong>gVisor and Kata solve it two different ways</strong> — gVisor's Sentry intercepts syscalls in userspace so the app never talks to the host kernel directly; Kata gives each pod its own dedicated kernel inside a lightweight VM (hardware-enforced isolation).</li>
                    <li><strong>RuntimeClass is the selector, not the runtime</strong> — <code>spec.runtimeClassName</code> points to a RuntimeClass whose <code>handler</code> must exactly match a runtime name registered in containerd's <code>config.toml</code>, or the pod sticks in <code>ContainerCreating</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Sandboxing is a tradeoff, not a default</strong> — ~10% overhead for gVisor, ~20% for Kata. Reserve it for untrusted code, multi-tenant nodes, and CI/CD build jobs; trusted, performance-critical workloads (databases, internal APIs) stay on runc + Seccomp + AppArmor.</li>
                    <li><strong>Multi-tenancy isolation is layered, not single-control</strong> — six levels from Namespace (soft) to a fully separate Cluster (hardest); RuntimeClass (Level 4-5) combines with NetworkPolicy, PSS, and RBAC for real tenant separation.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>RuntimeClass must be installed FIRST:</strong> The RuntimeClass resource AND the runtime binary (gVisor/Kata) must exist on the node BEFORE you create a pod referencing it. If the RuntimeClass exists but the runtime isn't installed on the target node, the pod gets stuck in <code>ContainerCreating</code> with a cryptic "failed to create shim task" error.</li>
                    <li><strong>Not all nodes support all runtimes:</strong> gVisor/Kata must be installed on EVERY node that might run sandboxed pods. Use nodeSelector or node affinity to ensure sandboxed pods only land on nodes with the runtime installed.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.runtimeClassName}'</code> — fastest way to confirm which runtime a running pod actually uses.</li>
                    <li>Pair every <code>runtimeClassName</code> with a matching <code>nodeSelector</code> — the scheduler does NOT verify the runtime is installed on the target node, only that the RuntimeClass resource exists.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- dmesg | grep -i gvisor</code> — quick sanity check that a pod is really sandboxed, not just referencing the RuntimeClass in its spec.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 21)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">practice until automatic</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check available RuntimeClasses</span>
kubectl get runtimeclass  <span class="comment"># Shows all installed sandboxed runtimes</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Apply a RuntimeClass to a pod (EXAM PATTERN)</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod spec: runtimeClassName: gvisor</span>
kubectl run sandboxed --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml | sed 's/containers:/runtimeClassName: gvisor\n  containers:/' | kubectl apply -f -

<span class="comment"># 3. Verify the runtime used by a running pod</span>
kubectl get pod sandboxed -o jsonpath='{.spec.runtimeClassName}'
kubectl exec sandboxed -- dmesg | grep -i gvisor  <span class="comment"># gVisor adds its own kernel messages</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Check if containerd supports the runtime</span>
cat /etc/containerd/config.toml | grep -A5 "plugins.*runtime"  <span class="comment"># Should list runc, gvisor, kata</span></code></pre>
