            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 21 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a RuntimeClass that uses gVisor and apply it to a pod. Write both YAML resources.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: node.k8s.io/v1
kind: RuntimeClass
metadata:
  name: gvisor
handler: runsc
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: sandboxed-pod
spec:
  runtimeClassName: gvisor
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the key architectural difference between gVisor and Kata Containers?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>gVisor implements a user-space kernel (Sentry) that intercepts syscalls and handles them in userspace — the application NEVER makes direct syscalls to the host kernel. Kata Containers runs each pod in a dedicated lightweight VM with its OWN Linux kernel — syscalls go to the guest kernel, not the host. gVisor = userspace kernel, Kata = hardware virtualization. gVisor is lighter weight; Kata provides stronger isolation.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">When would you use a sandboxed runtime instead of the default runc?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Use sandboxed runtimes for: (1) Untrusted workloads (user-uploaded code, multi-tenant environments), (2) Workloads processing sensitive data where container escape would be catastrophic, (3) CI/CD pipelines running arbitrary build code. The tradeoff is performance — gVisor has ~5-10% overhead, Kata has ~15-20% overhead vs native runc.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens if you specify <code>runtimeClassName: gvisor</code> but gVisor is not installed on the target node? How do you prevent scheduling to incompatible nodes?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The pod gets stuck in <code>ContainerCreating</code> with error: <code>failed to create shim task: runtime "runsc" not found</code>. Prevention: (1) Use <code>nodeSelector</code> with a label like <code>sandbox-runtime: gvisor</code> that only exists on nodes with gVisor installed. (2) Use <code>nodeAffinity</code> with <code>requiredDuringScheduling</code>. (3) Label nodes automatically at provisioning time: <code>kubectl label node &lt;name&gt; sandbox-runtime=gvisor</code>. (4) Verify the RuntimeClass handler name matches the containerd config.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the #1 operational issue with RuntimeClasses. The scheduler doesn't know which nodes have which runtimes — it only checks the RuntimeClass resource exists. You MUST use nodeSelector/affinity to ensure sandboxed pods land on compatible nodes. On the CKS exam, if a sandboxed pod won't start, check: (a) RuntimeClass handler name, (b) containerd config, (c) nodeSelector.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you configure containerd to support gVisor? Write the containerd config section and explain the handler name mapping.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-toml"># /etc/containerd/config.toml
version = 2
[plugins."io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri".containerd.runtimes.runsc]
  runtime_type = "io.containerd.runsc.v1"
[plugins."io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri".containerd.runtimes.runc]
  runtime_type = "io.containerd.runc.v2"
  [plugins."io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri".containerd.runtimes.runc.options]
    SystemdCgroup = true</code></pre>
                    <p>The RuntimeClass <code>handler: runsc</code> maps to the containerd runtime name <code>runsc</code> in the config. Kubernetes passes the handler name to containerd, containerd looks it up in its config, and launches the pod with the matching runtime binary (<code>/usr/bin/runsc</code> for gVisor).</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The handler name in RuntimeClass must match the runtime name in containerd config EXACTLY. Common mistake: naming the RuntimeClass handler "gvisor" but containerd has it as "runsc" — these must match. On the CKS exam, you may need to verify containerd config and RuntimeClass are consistent.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Can you apply Seccomp profiles to a gVisor-sandboxed pod? What's the interaction between gVisor's sentry kernel and Kubernetes Seccomp?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Kubernetes Seccomp is NOT applied inside gVisor.</strong> gVisor's sentry kernel implements its OWN syscall filtering that is separate from the host kernel's Seccomp. When you specify a Seccomp profile on a gVisor pod: (1) The host Seccomp filters gVisor's own limited syscalls (gVisor only makes ~50 safe syscalls). (2) The application's full set of syscalls is intercepted by gVisor's sentry kernel in userspace — Seccomp never sees them. In most cases, you don't need Seccomp on gVisor pods because gVisor itself provides stronger isolation than Seccomp.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>gVisor renders Seccomp unnecessary because the application never makes direct syscalls to the host. All syscalls are intercepted and handled by gVisor's user-space kernel, which implements its own security policies. On the CKS exam: if you're already using gVisor, you don't NEED to also apply Seccomp — it's redundant. But it doesn't hurt to have both (defense in depth).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What container escape techniques does gVisor protect against that runc does NOT? List at least 3 specific vulnerabilities that gVisor would have prevented.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>gVisor protects against kernel exploit-based container escapes because the application's syscalls never reach the host kernel. Specifically: (1) <strong>CVE-2019-5736 (runc escape):</strong> Overwrites runc binary via /proc/self/exe — gVisor has no /proc/self/exe to the host runc. (2) <strong>CVE-2022-0492 (cgroups escape):</strong> Release agent escape via cgroups — gVisor's sentry kernel doesn't expose cgroups to the container. (3) <strong>Dirty COW (CVE-2016-5195):</strong> Kernel race condition — gVisor runs in userspace, kernel exploits don't apply.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The fundamental difference: runc shares the host kernel with containers. A kernel exploit in a runc container can compromise the entire node. gVisor runs the application in a sandboxed userspace kernel — even if the application exploits "the kernel," it's only exploiting gVisor's sentry, not the host. This is the strongest container isolation available without hardware virtualization (Kata/VMs).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a Kyverno policy that mutates any pod in the "untrusted" namespace to use <code>runtimeClassName: gvisor</code> automatically.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: enforce-gvisor-untrusted
spec:
  rules:
  - name: set-gvisor-runtime
    match:
      any:
      - resources:
          kinds: [Pod]
          namespaces: [untrusted]
    mutate:
      patchStrategicMerge:
        spec:
          runtimeClassName: gvisor</code></pre>
                    <p>Any pod submitted to the "untrusted" namespace is automatically mutated to use gVisor. Developers don't need to remember to set it — the policy handles it transparently.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Policy automation is the CKS approach to security at scale. Instead of relying on developers to remember <code>runtimeClassName: gvisor</code>, a Kyverno policy enforces it automatically. This is a mutation (changing the pod spec) rather than validation (rejecting the pod). On the CKS exam, prefer mutation policies for security defaults — they reduce friction while maintaining security.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between gVisor's ptrace platform and kvm platform? Which provides better isolation?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>ptrace (default):</strong> gVisor's sentry uses Linux ptrace to intercept syscalls. Works everywhere but ~10% overhead. <strong>kvm:</strong> gVisor uses hardware virtualization (KVM) to create a sandboxed address space. Better isolation (ring-1 vs ring-0 attacker can't escape) but requires KVM support. kvm platform blocks speculative execution attacks (Spectre/Meltdown) that ptrace platform might be vulnerable to. For CKS: kvm platform is recommended for highest-security workloads.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is advanced gVisor configuration. On the CKS exam, you probably won't need to configure the platform, but you should know: (1) ptrace is the default and works everywhere, (2) kvm provides stronger isolation but requires hardware virtualization support on the node, (3) the platform is configured in containerd's config.toml under the gVisor runtime section.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A multi-tenant cluster runs workloads from different customers. What isolation strategy combines RuntimeClass with NetworkPolicy and PSS? Design the namespace configuration.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Namespace setup for a tenant "tenant-a"
kubectl create ns tenant-a
# 1. Pod Security: enforce Restricted
kubectl label ns tenant-a pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted
# 2. Network isolation: default-deny NetworkPolicy
kubectl apply -f - &lt;&lt;EOF
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata: {name: default-deny, namespace: tenant-a}
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]
EOF
# 3. Runtime isolation: auto-inject gVisor via Kyverno
# (policy from Q8 above — matches namespace tenant-a)
# 4. RBAC: tenant users only have access to their namespace
kubectl create role tenant-a-admin --verb=* --resource=* -n tenant-a
kubectl create rolebinding tenant-a-admin --role=tenant-a-admin --user=tenant-a-user -n tenant-a</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Multi-tenant isolation requires layered defenses: PSS (can't run privileged pods), NetworkPolicy (can't talk to other tenants), gVisor (can't escape container via kernel exploits), RBAC (can't access other namespaces). No single control is sufficient. On the CKS exam, when asked about multi-tenancy, always propose at least 3 layers of defense.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

