            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 22 · mTLS and Service Mesh Security</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">By default, pod-to-pod traffic inside a Kubernetes cluster is <strong>plaintext HTTP</strong>. Any process that can sniff the CNI network — a compromised pod, a malicious node exporter, a network administrator with tcpdump access — can read every request and response between your services. Database queries, API keys in headers, session tokens, user data — all visible. <strong>Mutual TLS (mTLS)</strong> solves this by encrypting pod-to-pod traffic AND authenticating both ends of every connection. A service mesh like Istio automates mTLS by injecting a sidecar proxy (Envoy) into every pod — handling certificate issuance, rotation, and enforcement without changing a single line of application code. This chapter teaches you how mTLS works, how Istio implements it, and how to secure every easypj service-to-service connection with automatic, transparent encryption.</p>
