            <section class="section-block" id="s22-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.1 Why Pod-to-Pod Traffic Is Insecure by Default</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes does not encrypt pod-to-pod traffic. NetworkPolicy (Ch 6) controls WHO can talk to whom, but does not encrypt the traffic itself. TLS at the Ingress (Ch 7) encrypts external-to-cluster traffic, but once inside the cluster, traffic between pods is plain HTTP. An attacker with any foothold in the cluster can sniff this traffic:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pod-to-Pod Traffic — Without mTLS vs With mTLS</div>
<pre>
  WITHOUT mTLS (plaintext — the Kubernetes default):
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  easypj pod ──── HTTP GET /jobs?search=engineer ────▶ postgres pod
              ◀──── SELECT * FROM jobs... ──────────
                    (database results in plaintext)

  Attacker pod on same node: tcpdump -i any port 5432
  → Captures: SELECT * FROM jobs WHERE title LIKE '%engineer%'
  → Captures: All job listing data in the response
  → Captures: Database credentials in the connection string
  → Captures: Session cookies, auth tokens in HTTP headers

  WITH mTLS (encrypted + authenticated — Istio default):
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  easypj pod ──TLS──▶ postgres pod
  (cert: easypj-sa)     (cert: postgres-sa)
  Both sides verify each other's certificate.
  Traffic is AES-GCM encrypted.
             ◀──TLS───

  Attacker pod: tcpdump → sees only encrypted bytes. Cannot decrypt.
  Attacker pod: tries to connect → has no valid cert → REFUSED.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s22-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.2 Mutual TLS (mTLS) — How It Works</h3>
                <p>Regular TLS (what HTTPS websites use) only authenticates the SERVER. The client (your browser) verifies the server's certificate, but the server doesn't verify the client. mTLS adds a second step: the client also presents a certificate, and the server verifies it. Both sides must prove their identity before any data is exchanged:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">mTLS Handshake — easypj → postgres, Step by Step</div>
<pre>
  easypj pod (client)                         postgres pod (server)
       │                                              │
       │ 1. ClientHello (supported ciphers, SNI)      │
       │ ──────────────────────────────────────────▶ │
       │                                              │
       │ 2. ServerHello + Server Certificate          │
       │    (cert contains: CN=postgres.easypj.svc)   │
       │ ◀────────────────────────────────────────── │
       │                                              │
       │ 3. easypj VERIFIES postgres cert:            │
       │    ✅ Signed by Istio cluster CA?            │
       │    ✅ CN matches postgres.easypj.svc?        │
       │    ✅ Not expired?                            │
       │    ✅ Not revoked?                            │
       │    All checks pass → continues               │
       │                                              │
       │ 4. Server: "Send me YOUR certificate"        │
       │ ◀──────── CertificateRequest ────────────── │
       │                                              │
       │ 5. easypj sends its OWN certificate          │
       │    (cert contains: CN=easypj-sa.easypj)     │
       │ ──────────── ClientCertificate ───────────▶ │
       │                                              │
       │ 6. postgres VERIFIES easypj cert:            │
       │    ✅ Signed by Istio cluster CA?            │
       │    ✅ CN matches allowed identity?           │
       │    ✅ Not expired?                            │
       │    All checks pass → continues               │
       │                                              │
       │ 7. ✅ BOTH sides authenticated!              │
       │    Encrypted TLS 1.3 tunnel established     │
       │ ◀═══════════ AES-GCM encrypted ═══════════▶ │

  WHAT HAPPENS IF AN ATTACKER POD TRIES TO CONNECT:
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Attacker pod → tries to connect to postgres:5432
  postgres: "Send me YOUR certificate"
  Attacker: has NO valid certificate (not signed by Istio CA)
  postgres: Connection REFUSED — TLS handshake fails
  Result: Attacker cannot even establish a TCP connection.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>TLS vs mTLS — The Key Difference</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left" style="flex:1;"><div class="info-box"><h5>🔒 Regular TLS (HTTPS)</h5><ul><li>Server proves identity</li><li>Client is ANONYMOUS</li><li>Any client can connect</li><li>Use: Public websites</li></ul></div></div>
                    <div class="panel-right" style="flex:1;"><div class="info-box"><h5>🔐 mTLS (Pod-to-Pod)</h5><ul><li>Server AND Client prove identity</li><li>Both authenticated</li><li>Only authorized clients connect</li><li>Use: Service-to-service communication</li></ul></div></div>
                </div>

                <h4>How Istio Automates Certificate Management — The Citadel (istiod) CA</h4>
                <p>Manual mTLS is a nightmare: generate a cert for every pod, distribute it, rotate it before expiry, revoke compromised certs. Istio's <strong>istiod</strong> (formerly Citadel) handles all of this automatically. Every Envoy sidecar gets a certificate from the Istio CA, rotated every 24 hours, without the application ever knowing:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Istio Certificate Lifecycle — Automatic Issuance and Rotation</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  ISTIO CERTIFICATE AUTOMATION                                            │
  │                                                                          │
  │  STEP 1: POD STARTS — SIDECAR REQUESTS A CERTIFICATE                    │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────────────                         │
  │  • Envoy sidecar starts alongside the application container.             │
  │  • Sidecar reads its ServiceAccount JWT token from the pod's filesystem. │
  │  • Sidecar sends a CSR (Certificate Signing Request) to istiod:         │
  │    POST /istiod/v1/certificate                                           │
  │    {                                                                       │
  │      "csr": "&lt;PEM-encoded CSR&gt;",                                          │
  │      "token": "&lt;ServiceAccount JWT&gt;"                                       │
  │    }                                                                       │
  │                                                                          │
  │  STEP 2: ISTIOD VALIDATES AND ISSUES                                     │
  │  ────────────────────────────────────────                                  │
  │  • istiod verifies the ServiceAccount JWT via Kubernetes TokenReview.   │
  │  • istiod extracts the pod's identity:                                    │
  │    spiffe://cluster.local/ns/easypj-prod/sa/easypj-sa                    │
  │  • istiod signs a certificate with the Istio root CA:                     │
  │    CN = easypj-sa.easypj-prod.svc.cluster.local                           │
  │    SAN = spiffe://cluster.local/ns/easypj-prod/sa/easypj-sa              │
  │    TTL = 24 hours                                                         │
  │  • Returns the signed cert + private key to the sidecar.                 │
  │                                                                          │
  │  STEP 3: AUTOMATIC ROTATION (every 12 hours)                             │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────                             │
  │  • The Envoy sidecar watches the certificate's expiry.                   │
  │  • At 50% of TTL (12 hours into a 24-hour cert), the sidecar            │
  │    automatically requests a NEW certificate from istiod.                 │
  │  • The new cert is loaded WITHOUT restarting the application.            │
  │  • The old cert remains valid until expiry (graceful overlap).           │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ⚡ SECURITY PROPERTIES:                                                  │
  │  ─────────────────────                                                    │
  │  • Short-lived certs (24h): stolen cert useless after 24 hours.          │
  │  • Per-pod identity: cert bound to ServiceAccount (SPIFFE ID).           │
  │  • Automatic rotation: zero human intervention.                           │
  │  • Compromise response: delete the pod → new pod gets new cert.          │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s22-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.3 Istio — Service Mesh for Automatic mTLS</h3>
                <p>Istio automates mTLS by injecting an <strong>Envoy proxy sidecar</strong> into every pod. Your application code sends plain HTTP to localhost — the Envoy sidecar intercepts it, encrypts it with mTLS, and sends it to the destination pod's Envoy sidecar, which decrypts it and forwards it to the destination app. Zero application code changes:</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — PeerAuthentication: enforce STRICT mTLS for easypj namespace</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: easypj-mtls
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  mtls:
    mode: STRICT     # ALL pods in this namespace MUST use mTLS

# STRICT: All connections must use mTLS. Plaintext REJECTED.
# PERMISSIVE: Accept both mTLS and plaintext (migration mode).
# DISABLE: No mTLS (insecure — don't use).</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How the Envoy Sidecar Intercepts Traffic — The iptables Redirect</h4>
                <p>How does the Envoy sidecar intercept traffic from the application without the application knowing? Istio configures <strong>iptables rules</strong> in the pod's network namespace that redirect ALL outbound TCP traffic through Envoy (localhost:15001). The app thinks it's connecting directly to postgres:5432 — iptables silently redirects to Envoy, which then handles mTLS:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Istio Sidecar Traffic Interception — iptables Redirect Architecture</div>
<pre>
  APPLICATION: connect("postgres.easypj.svc", 5432)
  │
  ▼
  POD'S iptables (injected by Istio init container):
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  iptables -t nat -A OUTPUT -p tcp -j REDIRECT --to-port 15001
  → ALL outbound TCP → redirected to localhost:15001 (Envoy)
  │
  ▼
  ENVOY SIDECAR (localhost:15001):
  ───────────────────────────────
  1. Receives redirected connection.
  2. Reads original destination (SO_ORIGINAL_DST): postgres:5432.
  3. Looks up in Istio service registry → selects a postgres endpoint.
  4. Establishes mTLS to postgres's Envoy sidecar.
  5. Both sides present certs → verify → encrypted tunnel.
  │
  ▼
  DESTINATION ENVOY → decrypts → forwards to localhost:5432 (postgres).
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Istio vs Cilium vs Linkerd — Which Service Mesh for mTLS?</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Istio</th><th>Cilium</th><th>Linkerd</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>mTLS</strong></td><td>✅ Auto (Citadel)</td><td>✅ Auto (Cilium identities)</td><td>✅ Auto (identity-based)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Proxy model</strong></td><td>Sidecar (Envoy)</td><td>Sidecar-free (eBPF in kernel)</td><td>Sidecar (linkerd-proxy, Rust)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Performance overhead</strong></td><td>~5-10ms latency</td><td>~1-2ms (near-native)</td><td>~1-3ms</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Complexity</strong></td><td>High (many CRDs)</td><td>Medium (K8s-native)</td><td>Low (simplest UX)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>NetworkPolicy + mTLS</strong></td><td>✅ Combined</td><td>✅ Combined (single CRD)</td><td>⚠️ Separate configs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CKS exam relevance</strong></td><td>⭐⭐ Most common</td><td>⭐ Growing</td><td>⭐ Simple alternative</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Verifying mTLS Is Actually Working</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Bash — Test that mTLS encrypts traffic and plaintext is rejected</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Verify STRICT mTLS is enabled in the namespace
kubectl get peerauthentication -n easypj-prod
# Should show: default  STRICT  (mode: STRICT)

# 2. Deploy a test pod WITHOUT an Istio sidecar
kubectl run test-no-sidecar --image=nginx -n easypj-prod \
  --overrides='{"metadata":{"annotations":{"sidecar.istio.io/inject":"false"}}}'

# 3. Try to curl the easypj service from the test pod
kubectl exec test-no-sidecar -n easypj-prod -- \
  curl -s http://easypj:8000
# Expected: curl: (56) Recv failure: Connection reset by peer
# ✅ PLAINTEXT REJECTED — mTLS requires encrypted traffic!

# 4. Try from a pod WITH a sidecar — should work
kubectl exec deploy/easypj -n easypj-prod -- \
  curl -s http://easypj:8000/healthz
# Expected: OK (200)
# ✅ SIDECAR-TO-SIDECAR TRAFFIC WORKS (mutual TLS)

# 5. Inspect the certificates in use
kubectl exec deploy/easypj -n easypj-prod -c istio-proxy -- \
  curl -s http://localhost:15000/certs | jq .
# Shows: certificate chain, expiry dates, SAN names

# 6. Check Istio's certificate authority
kubectl get secrets -n istio-system | grep cacerts
# These are the CA certs Istio uses to issue workload certificates</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s22-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.4 Securing easypj with Istio mTLS</h3>
                <p>Here is the complete Istio security configuration for the easypj application. It enforces STRICT mTLS for all pods in the production namespace and creates AuthorizationPolicies that only allow specific service-to-service communication:</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Complete Istio security for easypj</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ── Enforce mTLS for ALL pods in easypj-prod ──
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: default
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  mtls:
    mode: STRICT

# ── Only allow ingress-gateway → easypj (port 8000) ──
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
metadata:
  name: easypj-ingress
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-django
  action: ALLOW
  rules:
  - from:
    - source:
        principals: ["cluster.local/ns/istio-system/sa/ingress-gateway"]
    to:
    - operation:
        ports: ["8000"]

# ── Only allow easypj → postgres (port 5432) ──
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
metadata:
  name: postgres-access
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  action: ALLOW
  rules:
  - from:
    - source:
        principals: ["cluster.local/ns/easypj-prod/sa/easypj-sa"]
    to:
    - operation:
        ports: ["5432"]</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s22-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.5 Pod-to-Pod Encryption — Istio mTLS + Cilium WireGuard</h3>
                <p>The official CKS curriculum explicitly calls out <strong>Pod-to-Pod encryption (Cilium, Istio)</strong>. While Istio provides identity-based mTLS at L7 via sidecars, <strong>Cilium</strong> provides transparent L3 encryption using WireGuard — without sidecars or application changes. Both satisfy the CKS requirement at different layers:</p>

                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Istio mTLS (L7)</th><th>Cilium WireGuard (L3)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Encryption</strong></td><td>SPIFFE certs, sidecar proxies</td><td>WireGuard kernel module, no sidecars</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Identity</strong></td><td>SPIFFE (workload identity)</td><td>Node identity (IP-based)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Overhead</strong></td><td>~5-15% (sidecar proxy)</td><td>~1-3% (kernel WireGuard)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Requires sidecar</strong></td><td>Yes (istio-proxy)</td><td>No — transparent at kernel</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>

                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 CKS Exam: Pod-to-Pod Encryption</h5><p>If the exam asks for pod-to-pod encryption: (1) Istio PeerAuthentication STRICT mTLS (if Istio installed), or (2) Cilium with WireGuard enabled. Verify: <code>istioctl authn tls-check</code> for Istio, <code>cilium status | grep Encryption</code> for Cilium.</p></div>


                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key Takeaway for the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p>The CKS exam tests mTLS and service mesh concepts at a high level. Know: (1) Pod-to-pod traffic is plaintext by default — NetworkPolicy controls WHO but not encryption, (2) mTLS encrypts AND authenticates both ends, (3) Istio uses Envoy sidecars to automate mTLS without app changes, (4) PeerAuthentication sets mTLS mode (STRICT/PERMISSIVE/DISABLE) per namespace. The exam is unlikely to ask you to install Istio — focus on the conceptual understanding and YAML configuration of PeerAuthentication and AuthorizationPolicy.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

