            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Pod-to-pod traffic is plaintext HTTP by default</strong> — NetworkPolicy (Ch 6) controls WHO can connect, but never encrypts the connection itself; Ingress TLS (Ch 7) only protects traffic at the cluster edge.</li>
                    <li><strong>mTLS authenticates BOTH ends, not just the server</strong> — regular TLS only proves server identity; mTLS adds a client certificate too, so an attacker without a valid cert can't even complete the handshake.</li>
                    <li><strong>Istio makes mTLS transparent</strong> — an injected Envoy sidecar plus an iptables redirect intercepts all outbound traffic on localhost:15001, encrypts it, and the application never knows mTLS is happening.</li>
                    <li><strong>PeerAuthentication sets the mode; AuthorizationPolicy sets who's allowed</strong> — STRICT rejects plaintext outright, PERMISSIVE is a migration aid only; AuthorizationPolicy then scopes traffic by SPIFFE identity (ServiceAccount), not IP.</li>
                    <li><strong>The CKS curriculum names two valid answers for pod-to-pod encryption</strong> — Istio mTLS (L7, sidecar-based, ~5-15% overhead) or Cilium WireGuard (L3, kernel-based, ~1-3% overhead, no sidecars).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 22 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>PERMISSIVE ≠ disabled:</strong> PERMISSIVE mode accepts BOTH mTLS AND plaintext traffic. It's a migration tool, not a security control. For security, use STRICT. The exam will likely ask you to switch from PERMISSIVE to STRICT.</li>
                    <li><strong>mTLS requires sidecars:</strong> If a pod doesn't have the Istio sidecar injected (no <code>istio-proxy</code> container), mTLS policies don't apply to it. Check: <code>kubectl get pod -o json | jq '.spec.containers[].name'</code> — should include <code>istio-proxy</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>NetworkPolicy + mTLS = defense-in-depth:</strong> NetworkPolicy operates at L3/L4 (IP:port). mTLS operates at L5 (identity). An attacker who spoofs an IP bypasses NetworkPolicy but can't bypass mTLS because they don't have the SPIFFE certificate.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Check → Apply → Verify → Test, every time.</strong> <code>kubectl get peerauthentication -A</code> to see the current mode, apply STRICT, confirm every pod has an <code>istio-proxy</code> container, then <code>istioctl authn tls-check &lt;pod&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> to prove it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Enable sidecar injection with one label.</strong> <code>kubectl label namespace easypj-prod istio-injection=enabled</code> — but remember it only affects PODS CREATED AFTER the label; existing pods need a rollout restart.</li>
                    <li><strong>Prove mTLS with a negative test, not just a positive one.</strong> A plain <code>curl</code> from a pod WITHOUT a sidecar should fail with a connection reset — that failure IS the proof STRICT mode is working.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

