            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 22 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between TLS and mTLS? When would you use mTLS in Kubernetes?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>TLS: Server presents certificate, client verifies. mTLS: BOTH client AND server present certificates. Use mTLS for pod-to-pod communication when you need to verify the IDENTITY of both ends — not just encrypt traffic. In Kubernetes, pod-to-pod traffic is plaintext by default; mTLS via service mesh provides encryption + mutual authentication without application changes.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write an Istio PeerAuthentication that enforces STRICT mTLS in namespace "easypj-prod".</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: easypj-mtls
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  mtls:
    mode: STRICT  # PERMISSIVE allows plaintext fallback, STRICT requires mTLS</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is SPIFFE and how does Istio use it for pod identity in mTLS? What does a SPIFFE ID look like for a Kubernetes pod?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>SPIFFE (Secure Production Identity Framework For Everyone) is an identity standard. Istio issues each pod a SPIFFE ID in the format: <code>spiffe://cluster.local/ns/&lt;namespace&gt;/sa/&lt;serviceaccount&gt;</code>. Example: <code>spiffe://cluster.local/ns/easypj-prod/sa/easypj-api</code>. This ID is embedded in the X.509 certificate the sidecar proxy presents during mTLS. The receiving sidecar validates: (1) the cert is signed by the mesh CA, (2) the SPIFFE ID matches the expected identity, (3) any AuthorizationPolicy allows traffic from that identity.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>SPIFFE is the identity layer of zero-trust networking. Every pod gets a unique, cryptographically-verifiable identity based on its ServiceAccount. This enables RBAC-style authorization at the network level: "allow traffic from SA easypj-api in namespace prod to SA postgres in namespace database." On the CKS exam, understand that SPIFFE IDs replace IP-based trust with identity-based trust.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write an Istio AuthorizationPolicy that allows ONLY the <code>easypj-api</code> ServiceAccount in namespace <code>prod</code> to send traffic to <code>postgres</code> ServiceAccount in namespace <code>database</code> on port 5432.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
metadata:
  name: postgres-access
  namespace: database
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  action: ALLOW
  rules:
  - from:
    - source:
        principals:
        - cluster.local/ns/prod/sa/easypj-api
    to:
    - operation:
        ports: ["5432"]</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>AuthorizationPolicy is Istio's Layer 7 RBAC for network traffic. The <code>principals</code> field uses SPIFFE IDs to identify source workloads. This is much stronger than IP-based NetworkPolicy because it can't be spoofed — the identity is cryptographically verified via mTLS. On the CKS exam, combine NetworkPolicy (L3/4) with AuthorizationPolicy (L7) for defense in depth.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between Istio's PERMISSIVE and STRICT mTLS modes? When would you use PERMISSIVE mode in production?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>PERMISSIVE:</strong> Accepts BOTH mTLS and plaintext traffic. Used during migration when not all pods have sidecars yet. <strong>STRICT:</strong> REJECTS all non-mTLS traffic. Every pod must have a sidecar proxy. PERMISSIVE mode is a migration tool, not a production security posture. In production, use STRICT for all namespaces with sensitive data. The only valid use of PERMISSIVE is during a phased rollout where you're adding sidecars to existing workloads incrementally.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>PERMISSIVE is NOT a security control — it's a migration aid. If you leave namespaces in PERMISSIVE mode, an attacker who bypasses the sidecar (hostNetwork pod, compromised node) can send plaintext traffic that's accepted. On the CKS exam, if asked to "enable mTLS," the answer includes STRICT mode. PERMISSIVE is only acceptable during the rollout phase.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does Istio's sidecar proxy (Envoy) handle TLS certificate rotation? What happens if the cert expires during a connection?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Istio's Citadel (now istiod) issues short-lived certificates (default 24 hours) and the sidecar proxy automatically renews them BEFORE expiry. The flow: (1) Sidecar requests a new cert from istiod when &gt;50% of TTL remains. (2) istiod issues a new cert signed by the mesh CA. (3) Envoy hot-reloads the cert without dropping existing connections. (4) Existing TLS sessions continue with the old cert until natural termination. New connections use the new cert immediately. No connection drops during rotation.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Automatic certificate rotation is one of Istio's key security features. Unlike manual TLS where cert expiry causes outages, Istio handles rotation transparently. On the CKS exam, understand that mTLS in a service mesh eliminates the operational burden of certificate management — it's fully automated.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens to pod-to-pod traffic if istiod (control plane) goes down? Do existing mTLS connections keep working?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Existing connections: YES, they keep working.</strong> Envoy sidecars operate independently of the control plane for the data path. mTLS is performed sidecar-to-sidecar using already-cached certificates. <strong>New connections: YES, they still work</strong> as long as certificates haven't expired (24-hour default TTL). <strong>What fails:</strong> New sidecar injection (no istiod to mutate pods), certificate renewal (certs expire after 24h → new mTLS connections fail), configuration updates (new AuthorizationPolicies aren't propagated). The control plane is critical for management but not for the data path.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the "data plane survives control plane failure" design principle. It's a key architecture concept for CKS. The sidecar proxies form a peer-to-peer mesh that functions independently of the control plane. On the exam, this is important for high-availability planning: a control plane outage is a security degradation (no cert renewal, no policy updates) but not a service outage.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Compare service mesh mTLS (Istio/Linkerd) with manual mTLS using cert-manager. What are the tradeoffs?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse;"><thead><tr style="border-bottom: 2px solid #326ce5;"><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Aspect</th><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Service Mesh</th><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Manual mTLS (cert-manager)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">Setup</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Install mesh, inject sidecars</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Generate certs, mount Secrets, configure app</td></tr><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">Rotation</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Automatic, transparent</td><td style="padding: 8px;">cert-manager auto-rotates Secret, but app must reload</td></tr><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">App changes</td><td style="padding: 8px;">None (sidecar handles TLS)</td><td style="padding: 8px;">App must support mTLS (code changes)</td></tr><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">Granularity</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Per-pod identity (SPIFFE)</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Per-service identity (shared cert)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding: 8px;">Complexity</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Higher (sidecar overhead)</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Lower (no sidecar)</td></tr></tbody></table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Service mesh is the CKS-recommended approach for mTLS because it's transparent to applications and provides per-pod identity. Manual mTLS is simpler but requires application changes and provides coarser identity (per-service, not per-pod). On the exam, choose service mesh for greenfield deployments and cert-manager for lightweight mTLS where the mesh overhead isn't acceptable.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you enable automatic sidecar injection in a namespace? Write the command and explain when pods get the sidecar.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl label namespace easypj-prod istio-injection=enabled
# Or for revision-specific injection:
kubectl label namespace easypj-prod istio.io/rev=stable</code></pre>
                    <p>Pods get the sidecar at CREATION time. Existing pods are NOT retroactively injected. To inject existing pods, you must restart them: <code>kubectl rollout restart deployment -n easypj-prod</code>. The sidecar injection is performed by a mutating admission webhook that runs when the pod is created — it adds the <code>istio-proxy</code> container and <code>istio-init</code> init container to the pod spec.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Sidecar injection is a mutating admission webhook — it happens at Gate 4 (Admission). The namespace label tells Istio's webhook to modify pods in that namespace. On the CKS exam, you may need to: (1) enable injection on a namespace, (2) verify injection with <code>kubectl get pod -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].name}'</code> (should include <code>istio-proxy</code>), (3) troubleshoot failed injection by checking the webhook configuration.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod with an Istio sidecar can reach <code>postgres.database.svc.cluster.local:5432</code> but the connection fails with "TLS error: bad certificate." The destination pod also has a sidecar. What's the likely cause?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Most likely: <strong>mTLS mode mismatch.</strong> The source namespace has PERMISSIVE mode (allows plaintext) but the destination namespace has STRICT mode (requires mTLS). The source sidecar sends plaintext (because PERMISSIVE allows it), the destination sidecar expects mTLS → rejects. Fix: set both namespaces to STRICT, or at minimum set the destination to STRICT and the source will auto-negotiate mTLS if both have sidecars.</p>
                    <p>Other causes: (1) Istio CA certificates expired or not trusted, (2) clock skew between nodes (cert validity period check fails), (3) different trust domains (source and destination in different meshes with different CAs).</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>mTLS debugging is a common CKS scenario. The key insight: even in PERMISSIVE mode, if BOTH pods have sidecars, Istio will attempt mTLS. But explicit PERMISSIVE + STRICT mismatch across namespaces can cause confusion. On the exam, always check PeerAuthentication settings in BOTH namespaces when debugging mTLS issues. Use <code>istioctl authn tls-check &lt;pod&gt;</code> to see the actual TLS mode per connection.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

