            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-cheatsheet"></span>
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">⚡ Ch 22 Visual Cheat Sheet — mTLS &amp; Service Mesh</div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔐</div><h5>STRICT mTLS</h5><p>Both sides present certs. SPIFFE identity. PERMISSIVE = migration only.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🪪</div><h5>Sidecar Required</h5><p>istio-proxy container. Auto-injected via namespace label. No sidecar = no mTLS.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div><h5>L3/L4 + L5</h5><p>NetworkPolicy (IP:port) + mTLS (identity). Defense-in-depth.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> STRICT mTLS is the only real security posture — both sides present SPIFFE-based certificates and PERMISSIVE mode is a migration aid, not a control. mTLS only applies to pods carrying the <code>istio-proxy</code> sidecar, auto-injected via a namespace label but only for pods created after the label is set.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> mTLS (L5, identity) and NetworkPolicy (L3/L4, IP:port) are complementary, not redundant — stack them for defense-in-depth so a spoofed IP still can't produce a valid certificate.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📊 Chapter 22 Visual Summary — mTLS &amp; Service Mesh at a Glance</div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div><h5>Default (22.1)</h5><p><strong>Pod-to-pod = plaintext HTTP.</strong> Anyone on the network can sniff all inter-service traffic.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔐</div><h5>mTLS (22.2)</h5><p><strong>Both sides present certificates.</strong> Encrypted + authenticated. Unauthorized pods REFUSED.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div><h5>Istio (22.3-4)</h5><p><strong>Envoy sidecar automates mTLS.</strong> Zero app changes. STRICT mode for easypj-prod.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Kubernetes ships pod-to-pod traffic as plaintext HTTP by default (22.1) — mTLS closes that gap by making both client AND server present certificates, so an attacker who can sniff the network sees only encrypted bytes and cannot even complete a TCP handshake without a valid cert (22.2).</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> Istio automates the entire lifecycle — Envoy sidecars intercept traffic via iptables, istiod issues and rotates short-lived certificates, and a single PeerAuthentication resource flips a namespace from plaintext to STRICT mTLS with zero application code changes (22.3-22.5).</p>
                </div>
            </div>
