            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 23 · Image Security — Scanning and Minimizing Attack Surface</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">A container image is not just your application code — it's an <strong>entire Linux distribution frozen in time</strong>: an operating system, system libraries, a language runtime, package managers, shells, and hundreds of packages you never explicitly asked for. Every one of those packages is a potential <strong>CVE (Common Vulnerability and Exposure)</strong> — a publicly known security vulnerability with a CVSS score. A Docker Hub image left unrebuilt accumulates <strong>dozens of known vulnerabilities</strong> within months — the worked example in §23.1 below shows 25 CVEs in a Python image that's just 6 months old. Worse, images are <strong>immutable snapshots</strong> — the vulnerabilities baked in at build time stay there forever unless you rebuild. This chapter teaches you to minimize your image attack surface (distroless, multi-stage builds), scan every image with Trivy in CI/CD, pin production workloads to immutable digests, and enforce that only images from approved private registries can run in your cluster. <strong>You cannot secure what you cannot see — and you cannot see what's inside an image without scanning it.</strong></p>
