            <section class="section-block" id="s23-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.1 Why Container Images Are a Security Risk — The Full Picture</h3>
                <p>When you write <code>FROM python:3.12</code> in your Dockerfile, you are not just getting Python. You are pulling approximately <strong>1,000+ Debian packages</strong> into your application's runtime environment — curl, wget, bash, openssl, libc, systemd libraries, perl, and hundreds more. Many of these packages have <strong>known, publicly documented vulnerabilities</strong> with exploit code available online. The fundamental problem: <strong>images are static snapshots that rot over time.</strong> The day you build them, they may have zero known CVEs. Six months later, 23 new CVEs are discovered in packages you didn't even know were in the image — and your production pods are still running that old image.</p>

                <h4>The CVE Lifecycle — How a Vulnerability Ends Up in Your Container</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Discovery</strong><p>A security researcher or attacker finds a bug in curl 7.74.0 — a buffer overflow triggered by a malicious SOCKS5 proxy response.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>CVE Assignment</strong><p>MITRE assigns <strong>CVE-2023-38545</strong>. The vulnerability is now publicly tracked with a unique identifier. NVD (National Vulnerability Database) picks it up.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>CVSS Scoring</strong><p>NVD analysts assign a CVSS score: 9.8 (CRITICAL). Attack vector: Network, complexity: Low, privileges: None, user interaction: None. <strong>This is as bad as it gets.</strong></p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>Publication</strong><p>The CVE is published in NVD, MITRE, and security feeds. Trivy's vulnerability database ingests it within hours. Exploit code often appears on GitHub within days.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">5</span><strong>Distribution Lag</strong><p>Debian maintainers backport the fix to curl 7.74.0-1.3+deb11u7. But <strong>your image was built 6 months ago with curl 7.74.0-1.3+deb11u2</strong> — the vulnerable version. The fix exists, but YOUR image didn't get it because images don't auto-update.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">6</span><strong>Exploitation Window</strong><p>Your production pods running easypj:v1 are now vulnerable to CVE-2023-38545. Any network request your app makes could be intercepted. <strong>Trivy would catch this — but only if you scan.</strong></p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Core Insight: Images Are Immutable — Vulnerabilities Are Not</h5>
                    <p>Unlike a VM where you run <code>apt upgrade</code> and fix vulnerabilities, container images are <strong>immutable by design</strong>. The only way to fix a CVE in a running container is to <strong>rebuild the image</strong> with updated packages and redeploy. This means every image in your registry is a ticking time bomb — secure today, vulnerable tomorrow, without a single line of your code changing. This is why <strong>continuous scanning</strong> (not one-time scanning) is essential.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Image Layer Anatomy — Why Risk Accumulates in Layers</h4>
                <p>Every Dockerfile instruction creates a new filesystem layer. Each layer adds packages, libraries, and configuration files — and each layer can introduce vulnerabilities independently:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Docker Image Layer Stack — easypj:v1 (Debian-based, 6 layers)</div>
<pre>
  LAYER 6: COPY . /app                                    [your Django code — ~50MB]
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  LAYER 5: RUN pip install -r requirements.txt             [Python packages — ~200MB]
  │  Introduces: Django 4.2, gunicorn, psycopg2, celery, redis, ...
  │  Risk: Python package CVEs (e.g., CVE-2024-xxx in Django itself)
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  LAYER 4: RUN apt-get install gcc postgresql-client ...   [build tools — ~300MB]
  │  Introduces: gcc-10, libpq-dev, build-essential, make, git
  │  Risk: Compiler CVEs (CVE-2023-4039: gcc stack protector bypass)
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  LAYER 3: RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y curl   [utilities — ~50MB]
  │  Introduces: curl 7.74.0, libcurl4, ca-certificates
  │  Risk: CVE-2023-38545 — CRITICAL RCE via SOCKS5 (CVSS 9.8)
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  LAYER 2: RUN apt-get update && apt-get upgrade           [system updates — ~100MB]
  │  Introduces: openssl 1.1.1n, libc6 2.31, libssl1.1
  │  Risk: CVE-2023-44487 — HTTP/2 Rapid Reset DoS (CVSS 7.5)
  │  Risk: CVE-2023-4911 — glibc Looney Tunables LPE (CVSS 7.8)
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  LAYER 1: FROM python:3.12                                [base OS — ~350MB]
  │  Introduces: Debian 12 (bookworm), systemd libs, perl, bash, apt, dpkg
  │  Risk: Hundreds of OS-level packages, many with latent CVEs
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  🔴 TOTAL: ~1,050MB image. ~1,200 packages. 23 known CVEs after 6 months.
  🟢 GOAL:  ~20MB distroless image. ~5 packages. 0-1 CVEs.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>CVSS Scoring Explained — What Those Numbers Actually Mean</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>CVSS Range</th><th>Severity</th><th>What It Means</th><th>Action for CKS</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>9.0–10.0</strong></td><td style="color:#326ce5;font-weight:bold;">CRITICAL</td><td>Network-exploitable, no auth needed, no user interaction. Wormable. Example: Log4Shell (CVE-2021-44228, CVSS 10.0).</td><td>🚨 <strong>BLOCK DEPLOYMENT.</strong> Fix immediately. This is the "drop everything" severity.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>7.0–8.9</strong></td><td style="color:#f97316;font-weight:bold;">HIGH</td><td>Can lead to RCE, privilege escalation, or data breach. May require some conditions. Example: Looney Tunables (CVE-2023-4911, CVSS 7.8).</td><td>⚠️ <strong>Fix within SLA</strong> (e.g., 30 days). Document the risk acceptance if deferred.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>4.0–6.9</strong></td><td style="color:#fbbf24;font-weight:bold;">MEDIUM</td><td>Difficult to exploit or limited impact. Example: Information disclosure via timing attack. Often requires local access.</td><td>📋 <strong>Track and fix in next release cycle.</strong> Don't block deployment.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>0.1–3.9</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;font-weight:bold;">LOW</td><td>Minimal impact, theoretical exploit, or requires unrealistic conditions.</td><td>✅ <strong>Acknowledge, fix when convenient.</strong> Low priority.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>🔴 ATTACK SCENARIO: Exploiting CVE-2023-38545 (curl SOCKS5 RCE) in easypj:v1</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header">
                        <span class="scenario-badge danger">ATTACK SCENARIO</span>
                        <span class="scenario-title">How an Attacker Exploits a Known CVE in Your Container</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <div class="step-marker">1</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Reconnaissance</strong>
                                <p>Attacker scans your cluster's external endpoints. They discover your Django app at <code>jobs.easypj.com</code>. The HTTP response headers reveal: <code>Server: gunicorn/21.2.0</code>. They fingerprint the stack. They also find your public Docker image <code>easypj/easypj-django:v1</code> on Docker Hub. They pull it and scan it with Trivy themselves — discovering CVE-2023-38545 (curl heap buffer overflow, CVSS 9.8).</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <div class="step-marker">2</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Weaponization</strong>
                                <p>The attacker crafts a malicious SOCKS5 proxy server. When curl (inside your container) connects to this proxy, the proxy sends a specially crafted response that overflows curl's heap buffer — giving the attacker <strong>arbitrary code execution</strong> as the user running curl (UID 1000 in your container). The attacker hosts the malicious proxy at <code>evil-proxy.attacker.com:1080</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <div class="step-marker">3</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Delivery — How curl Gets Triggered</strong>
                                <p>Your Django app has a feature: "Import jobs from external URL." A user pastes a URL, the backend does <code>requests.get(user_url)</code> → which internally might use curl or libcurl if compiled with it. Or: your Celery task downloads a file from a user-supplied S3 URL using curl as a subprocess. The attacker submits a URL pointing to their malicious proxy: <code>socks5h://evil-proxy.attacker.com:1080</code>. Your container's curl connects to it.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <div class="step-marker">4</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Exploitation — Code Execution Inside the Container</strong>
                                <p>The malicious proxy sends the exploit payload. curl's heap buffer overflows. The attacker achieves <strong>Remote Code Execution (RCE)</strong> inside the easypj container — as UID 1000, inside the container's namespace. They now have a shell-equivalent inside your container. They can read <code>/app/easypj/settings.py</code> (with your Django SECRET_KEY and DATABASE_URL). They can access the PostgreSQL database at <code>postgres.easypj.svc.cluster.local:5432</code> because the container has network access to the DB service.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <div class="step-marker">5</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Lateral Movement</strong>
                                <p>From inside the container, the attacker reads the mounted ServiceAccount token at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code>. They use this token to list pods: <code>curl -k https://kubernetes.default.svc/api/v1/namespaces/easypj/pods</code>. If the ServiceAccount has excessive RBAC permissions (common mistake!), they can now deploy their own malicious pods, exfiltrate secrets, or access other namespaces.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <div class="step-marker">🛡️</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>How to Stop This Attack — Defense in Depth</strong>
                                <p>(1) <strong>Scan with Trivy BEFORE deploying</strong> — would have caught CVE-2023-38545 immediately. (2) <strong>Use distroless base image</strong> — no curl binary to exploit. (3) <strong>NetworkPolicy</strong> — block egress from app pods to internet (only allow DB and API egress). (4) <strong>Seccomp</strong> — block socket() syscalls to prevent outbound connections. (5) <strong>RBAC</strong> — least-privilege ServiceAccount with no pod listing permissions. (6) <strong>readOnlyRootFilesystem</strong> — prevents writing exploit tools to disk. <strong>Each layer alone is not enough. All six together make exploitation nearly impossible.</strong></p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

