            <section class="section-block" id="s23-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.2 Minimizing Image Attack Surface — The Distroless Revolution</h3>
                <p>Every package you remove is an <strong>attack vector eliminated forever</strong>. There is no patch, no CVE, and no exploit for a package that simply does not exist in your image. The goal is a <strong>minimal image</strong> that contains ONLY your application and its runtime dependencies — no shell, no package manager, no curl/wget/git/nc/perl. This is the philosophy behind <strong>Google's distroless images</strong>: "If you don't need it, it shouldn't be in the image."</p>

                <h4>Distroless Architecture — Why "No Shell" Is the Ultimate Security Win</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Distroless Image Anatomy — What's Present vs What's Absent</div>
<pre>
  DISTROLESS IMAGE (gcr.io/distroless/python3-debian12)
  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ✅ PRESENT (essentials only):
     • /usr/bin/python3.11          — Python interpreter
     • /usr/lib/python3.11/         — Python standard library
     • /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6  — glibc (Python needs it)
     • /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2  — dynamic linker
     • /etc/passwd, /etc/group      — user/group database (for USER directive)
     • /etc/ssl/certs/              — CA certificates (for TLS)
     • /tmp/                        — writable tmp (scratch space)
     • Your application files       — copied from builder stage

  ❌ ABSENT (attack vectors eliminated):
     • /bin/bash, /bin/sh           — NO SHELL. kubectl exec → fails.
     • /usr/bin/curl, /usr/bin/wget — NO download tools. Cannot pull malware.
     • /usr/bin/apt, /usr/bin/dpkg  — NO PACKAGE MANAGER. Cannot install tools.
     • /usr/bin/gcc, /usr/bin/make  — NO COMPILER. Cannot build exploits.
     • /usr/bin/git, /usr/bin/nc    — NO dev tools. Cannot clone repos.
     • /usr/bin/perl, /usr/bin/python2 — NO legacy runtimes with known CVEs.
     • /usr/bin/ssh, /usr/bin/scp   — NO remote access tools.
     • /usr/bin/cron, /usr/sbin/cron— NO job scheduler. Cannot persist tasks.
     • /bin/chmod, /bin/chown       — NO permission modifiers (in some distroless variants)
     • systemd, init scripts        — NO init system. PID 1 is your app.

  📊 RESULT:
     • Image size: ~20MB vs ~1,050MB full Debian image
     • Package count: ~5 vs ~1,200 packages
     • Attack surface: REDUCED BY ~99.6%
     • CVEs: 0-1 vs 23 in the full image
     • kubectl exec easypj -- /bin/bash → "exec: /bin/bash: no such file or directory"
     • This is a FEATURE, not a bug. Attackers cannot get a shell either.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The Distroless Mindset Shift</h5>
                    <p>Many developers resist distroless because they can't <code>kubectl exec</code> into the container to debug. This is the wrong instinct. In production, <strong>if you need a shell, your observability is broken.</strong> Fix your logging, metrics, and tracing instead. Use <code>kubectl logs</code>, Prometheus metrics, and distributed traces. If you absolutely need debugging access, use an <strong>ephemeral debug container</strong>: <code>kubectl debug -it easypj-pod --image=busybox --target=easypj</code>. This attaches a separate debugging container with a shell WITHOUT modifying the production image.</p>
