                <p style="text-align:center;font-size:0.85rem;color:#8b949e;margin-top:0.5rem;"><strong>Bottom line:</strong> In the full image scenario, the attacker gets a full Linux environment — bash, curl, apt, cron. In distroless, the attacker is confined to what Python's standard library can do. The difference in post-exploitation capability is <strong>massive.</strong></p>

                <h4>How to Migrate from Full Images to Distroless — Step by Step</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Identify shell/exec usage</strong><p>Find every place in your CI/CD, Helm charts, and ops runbooks that does <code>kubectl exec ... -- /bin/bash</code> or <code>kubectl exec ... -- python manage.py shell</code>. These will break with distroless.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>Replace exec-based tasks</strong><p>Management commands → run as Kubernetes Jobs or CronJobs. Debugging → use <code>kubectl debug</code> ephemeral containers. Health checks → use HTTP/TCP probes, not exec probes.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>Convert to multi-stage build</strong><p>Add a builder stage with <code>python:3.12-slim</code>. Move all <code>apt-get install</code>, <code>pip install</code>, and compilation to the builder stage. Use <code>COPY --from=builder</code> to bring artifacts into distroless.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>Test with Trivy</strong><p>Build the new image. Run <code>trivy image easypj:v2-distroless</code>. Compare CVE count with v1. Expect: 23→1 or 23→0. If you still see CVEs, check which packages remain.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">5</span><strong>Deploy to staging</strong><p>Deploy the distroless image to a staging namespace. Verify: app starts, responds to requests, logs appear (stdout/stderr still work!), health checks pass. Let it run for 24 hours.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">6</span><strong>Promote to production</strong><p>Roll out with canary deployment: 10% → 50% → 100%. Monitor error rates, latency, and crash loops. If everything is stable, you now have a <strong>99.6% smaller attack surface.</strong></p></div>
                </div>
