                <h4>The Fix Workflow — From Scan to Remediation</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Scan</strong><p><code>trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH easypj:v1</code> → 2 CRITICAL, 8 HIGH found.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>Triage</strong><p>For each CVE: Is the package actually used? Is it network-exploitable? Is there a public exploit? Decide: fix now vs. fix later vs. ignore with justification.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>Fix</strong><p>Update the base image in your Dockerfile: <code>FROM python:3.12-slim@sha256:abc123...</code> (pinning to digest ensures reproducibility). Or switch to a newer base: <code>FROM python:3.13-slim</code>.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>Rebuild</strong><p><code>docker build -t easypj:v2 .</code> — the new base image has updated packages. The CVEs should be resolved.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">5</span><strong>Rescan</strong><p><code>trivy image easypj:v2</code> — verify CRITICAL count dropped to 0, HIGH reduced significantly. <strong>Never assume a rebuild fixed everything.</strong></p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">6</span><strong>Deploy</strong><p>Push v2 to registry. Update Kubernetes deployments to use the new image. Monitor for regressions.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">🔄</span><strong>Repeat</strong><p><strong>Continuous scanning is not optional.</strong> Even after v2 is deployed, scan it weekly. New CVEs will be discovered. You need to know when your "secure" image becomes vulnerable again.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s23-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.4 Private Container Registries — Controlling Image Sources</h3>
                <p>Even the most secure, Trivy-scanned, distroless-based image is useless if an attacker can trick your cluster into pulling a <strong>malicious image</strong> from a public registry. The principle: <strong>your cluster should ONLY pull images from registries you control.</strong> This section covers the mechanics of private registries: authentication, imagePullSecrets, and admission controller enforcement.</p>

                <h4>The Authentication Flow — How Kubernetes Pulls Private Images</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Private Registry Authentication Flow — From Pod Creation to Image Pull</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  1. USER creates Pod with imagePullSecrets                           │
  │     ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │     │  spec:                                                    │    │
  │     │    imagePullSecrets:                                      │    │
  │     │    - name: ecr-credentials    ← references a Secret       │    │
  │     │    containers:                                            │    │
  │     │    - image: 12345.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/easypj │    │
  │     └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                         │                                             │
  │                         ▼                                             │
  │  2. KUBELET reads imagePullSecrets                                    │
  │     ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │     │  kubelet looks up Secret "ecr-credentials" in the pod's  │    │
  │     │  namespace. Secret contains:                              │    │
  │     │    .dockerconfigjson: base64({                            │    │
  │     │      "auths": {                                          │    │
  │     │        "12345.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com": {        │    │
  │     │          "username": "AWS",                              │    │
  │     │          "password": "eyJ...&lt;ECR auth token&gt;..."         │    │
  │     │        }                                                 │    │
  │     │      }                                                   │    │
  │     │    })                                                    │    │
  │     └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                         │                                             │
  │                         ▼                                             │
  │  3. KUBELET authenticates to registry                                 │
  │     ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │     │  kubelet → HTTPS → registry endpoint                      │    │
  │     │  Authorization: Basic base64(AWS:eyJ...)                  │    │
  │     │  GET /v2/easypj/manifests/v2.0.0                         │    │
  │     │  ← 200 OK (authenticated)                                 │    │
  │     └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                         │                                             │
  │                         ▼                                             │
  │  4. KUBELET pulls image layers                                        │
  │     ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │     │  kubelet downloads image layers from registry             │    │
  │     │  Stores in local container runtime cache                  │    │
  │     │  Starts container from the cached image                   │    │
  │     └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                                                                       │
  │  ⚠️  SECURITY NOTE: ECR tokens EXPIRE (typically 12 hours).          │
  │     You need a mechanism to refresh imagePullSecrets regularly.       │
  │     Solutions: kubelet ECR credential provider, Kyverno, or a CronJob.│
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Creating and Using imagePullSecrets</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash + YAML — Creating registry credentials and using them in a Pod</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Create the imagePullSecret ──
# Option A: From existing Docker credentials
kubectl create secret generic ecr-credentials \
  --from-file=.dockerconfigjson=$HOME/.docker/config.json \
  --type=kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson \
  -n easypj

# Option B: Directly provide credentials
kubectl create secret docker-registry ecr-credentials \
  --docker-server=12345.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com \
  --docker-username=AWS \
  --docker-password=$(aws ecr get-login-password) \
  --docker-email=ops@easypj.com \
  -n easypj

# ── Verify the secret ──
kubectl get secret ecr-credentials -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.data}' | jq .

# ── Patch default ServiceAccount to use the secret automatically ──
# Every pod in the namespace will use this secret without explicit reference
kubectl patch serviceaccount default -n easypj -p \
  '{"imagePullSecrets": [{"name": "ecr-credentials"}]}'</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Pod explicitly referencing imagePullSecrets</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-django
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  # ⬅⬅⬅ imagePullSecrets: Credentials for private registry
  imagePullSecrets:
  - name: ecr-credentials
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    # ⬅⬅⬅ Image MUST come from our private registry — NOT Docker Hub
    image: 12345.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/easypj-django:v2.0.0
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>🔴 ATTACK SCENARIO: Public Image Poisoning — When Docker Hub Images Are Not What They Seem</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header">
                        <span class="scenario-badge danger">ATTACK SCENARIO</span>
                        <span class="scenario-title">How Attackers Exploit Public Registries to Inject Malicious Images</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <div class="step-marker">1</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Typo-Squatting</strong>
                                <p>Your team uses <code>image: easypj/django-app:v2</code> in a Deployment. A developer accidentally types <code>image: easypj/djano-app:v2</code> (missing the 'g'). An attacker has already published <code>easypj/djano-app:v2</code> on Docker Hub — it looks identical to your app but contains a cryptominer binary that starts alongside gunicorn. The pod deploys. The cryptominer runs. Your cloud bill spikes. <strong>You never noticed because the app still works.</strong></p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <div class="step-marker">2</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Tag Mutability</strong>
                                <p>You deploy <code>image: python:3.12-slim</code> (using the floating tag). Today, <code>python:3.12-slim</code> points to a clean, official image. Tomorrow, a compromised Docker Hub account pushes a new image tagged <code>python:3.12-slim</code> that contains a backdoor. Your new pods pull the malicious image. <strong>The tag didn't change — the image it points to did.</strong></p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <div class="step-marker">3</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Abandoned Namespace Takeover</strong>
                                <p>You use a utility image from Docker Hub: <code>image: some-user/k8s-sidecar:v1.2</code>. The original author deletes their Docker Hub account. An attacker re-registers the username and pushes a new <code>some-user/k8s-sidecar:v1.2</code> with malware. Your cluster pulls it. The attacker now runs code in your cluster.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <div class="step-marker">🛡️</div>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Defense: Private Registry + Digest Pinning</strong>
                                <p>(1) <strong>Always use a private registry</strong> — proxy or cache all external images through a registry you control. Never pull directly from Docker Hub in production. (2) <strong>Pin to image digests, not tags:</strong> <code>image: registry.easypj.com/easypj@sha256:abc123...</code> — digests are immutable; tags are mutable (see 23.5). (3) <strong>Admission controller enforcement:</strong> use OPA/Gatekeeper or Kyverno to reject any pod that references an unapproved registry. (4) <strong>Regularly audit</strong> running images against approved registries.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Decision Tree: Private Registry Strategy for Your Organization</h4>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="tree-title">Choosing a Container Registry Architecture</div>
<pre>
  START: I need to control which images run in my cluster
  │
  ├─ Am I on a public cloud? (AWS, GCP, Azure)
  │  ├─ AWS → ECR (Elastic Container Registry)
  │  │  • IAM-based auth (no passwords to manage)
  │  │  • Image scanning built-in (ECR Scan / Inspector)
  │  │  • Immutable tags supported
  │  │  • Tip: Use ECR pull-through cache to proxy Docker Hub
  │  ├─ GCP → Artifact Registry
  │  │  • IAM-based auth
  │  │  • Vulnerability scanning built-in
  │  │  • Native integration with GKE
  │  └─ Azure → Azure Container Registry (ACR)
  │     • Azure AD auth
  │     • Defender for Containers scanning
  │     • Native integration with AKS
  │
  ├─ Am I self-hosted / on-prem?
  │  ├─ Harbor (CNCF graduated, full-featured)
  │  │  • Vulnerability scanning (Trivy built-in)
  │  │  • Image signing (Notary/Cosign)
  │  │  • RBAC, replication, garbage collection
  │  │  • Project-based image organization
  │  └─ Docker Registry (simpler, fewer features)
  │     • Basic pull/push only
  │     • No scanning, no signing
  │     • Good for dev environments only
  │
  └─ Do I need to proxy Docker Hub / public registries?
     └─ YES → Set up PULL-THROUGH CACHE
        • ECR Pull-Through Cache
        • Harbor Proxy Cache
        • Benefits: All images go through YOUR registry
        • You control vulnerability scanning on proxied images
        • You can block specific public images at the proxy level
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s23-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.5 Image Digest Pinning — Provenance You Can Trust</h3>
                <p>Trivy scanning (23.3) and private registries (23.4) both answer "is this image safe?" — but neither answers a subtler question: <strong>is the image running in production right now the SAME image you scanned?</strong> If you deploy by <strong>tag</strong>, the answer can silently become "no." A <strong>tag</strong> like <code>easypj:v1</code> is just a mutable pointer — anyone with push access to the repository can re-point it at a completely different set of bytes tomorrow, and your cluster will pull the new image next time a pod starts, without a single line of your Deployment YAML changing. A <strong>digest</strong> like <code>easypj@sha256:ab12...</code> is a cryptographic hash of the image's content — it is <strong>immutable by construction</strong>. Change one byte of the image and the digest changes. Pin to a digest and you have a mathematically verifiable guarantee that the image you scanned is the image that runs.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">TAG vs DIGEST — Why Pin to a Digest</div>
<pre>
  REFERENCE                    SECURITY PROPERTY
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  easypj:latest                ❌ mutable, ambiguous, pulls Always
  easypj:v1.2.3                ⚠️  mutable — tag can be re-pushed
  easypj@sha256:ab12...        ✅ immutable — exact bytes, verifiable
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Reference</th><th>Mutability</th><th>What Trivy Scanned Is What Runs?</th><th>CKS Recommendation</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>easypj:latest</code></td><td style="color:#f87171;font-weight:bold;">❌ Mutable</td><td>No guarantee — <code>:latest</code> can point anywhere by the time a pod starts.</td><td>🚨 <strong>Never in production.</strong> Ambiguous, defeats reproducibility.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>easypj:v1.2.3</code></td><td style="color:#fbbf24;font-weight:bold;">⚠️ Mutable</td><td>Usually — but a compromised or careless push can silently swap it.</td><td>⚠️ Acceptable for humans reading dashboards; not for enforcement.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>easypj@sha256:ab12...</code></td><td style="color:#4ade80;font-weight:bold;">✅ Immutable</td><td>Guaranteed — the hash IS the content.</td><td>🏆 <strong>Required for production workloads.</strong> Combine with admission enforcement.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Attack This Actually Stops</h5>
                    <p>You scan <code>easypj:v1.2.3</code> with Trivy, it comes back clean, and your pipeline pushes it to production. Two weeks later an attacker with compromised registry push access re-tags a backdoored image as <code>easypj:v1.2.3</code> — same tag, different bytes. The NEXT pod restart (a node drain, a HorizontalPodAutoscaler scale-up, a crash loop) pulls the new, malicious image under the OLD, already-approved tag. Nobody re-scans it because "we already scanned v1.2.3." Pinning by digest makes this attack structurally impossible: <code>easypj@sha256:ab12...</code> can only ever resolve to the exact bytes that hash produces.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>imagePullPolicy Semantics — Exam-Critical, Frequently Misread</h4>
                <p>Digest pinning only delivers its guarantee if paired with the right <code>imagePullPolicy</code>. The three values behave very differently, and the exam expects you to know the defaults cold:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>When It Pulls</th><th>Default For</th><th>CKS Notes</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>Always</code></td><td>Every single pod start, regardless of what's cached on the node.</td><td>Any image tagged <code>:latest</code> (implicit default).</td><td>Guarantees freshness but adds registry-auth dependency on every restart; combine with digest pinning for verifiable freshness.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>IfNotPresent</code></td><td>Only if the image is not already cached locally on the node.</td><td>Any tag OTHER than <code>:latest</code> (implicit default).</td><td>The right choice for digest-pinned production images — the digest is already immutable, so re-pulling on every start buys nothing.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>Never</code></td><td>Never pulls — the kubelet fails the pod if the image isn't already present on the node.</td><td>Nothing (must be set explicitly).</td><td>Used for air-gapped/edge nodes with pre-loaded images; a common exam trap when a pod is stuck in <code>ErrImageNeverPull</code>.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The Gotcha Hiding in Plain Sight</h5>
                    <p><code>:latest</code> silently defaults <code>imagePullPolicy</code> to <code>Always</code> — meaning every pod restart re-resolves the mutable <code>:latest</code> tag and re-pulls whatever it currently points to. This is precisely the opposite of what a production workload wants: you want the SAME verified bytes every time, not a fresh roll of the dice on every restart. Avoid <code>:latest</code> entirely in production; pin by digest and set <code>imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent</code> explicitly.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Resolving a Tag to Its Digest and Pinning It</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Resolve the current digest behind a tag before pinning it</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Option A: crane (no local pull required — queries the registry API directly) ──
crane digest registry.easypj.com/easypj-django:v1.2.3
# sha256:ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab

# ── Option B: docker (image must already be pulled locally) ──
docker pull registry.easypj.com/easypj-django:v1.2.3
docker inspect --format='{{index .RepoDigests 0}}' registry.easypj.com/easypj-django:v1.2.3
# registry.easypj.com/easypj-django@sha256:ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab

# ── Then scan the DIGEST, not the tag, so the scan result is bound to exact bytes ──
trivy image registry.easypj.com/easypj-django@sha256:ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Deployment pinned to an immutable digest, not a mutable tag</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-django
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-django
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj-django
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: easypj-django
        # ⬅⬅⬅ pinned to the exact digest resolved above — NOT a mutable tag
        image: registry.easypj.com/easypj-django@sha256:ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab12cd34ef56ab
        # digest is already immutable — no benefit to re-pulling on every restart
        imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Enforcing Digest-Only Images at Admission (Kyverno)</h4>
                <p>Scanning and pinning by convention only works until someone forgets. The reliable fix is to make non-digest images structurally impossible to deploy — reject them at admission, before they ever reach etcd (Ch 24, 26):</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Kyverno ClusterPolicy rejecting tag-based (non-digest) images</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-image-digest
spec:
  validationFailureAction: Enforce   # Block, don't just Audit/warn
  background: false                  # Only evaluate at admission time
  rules:
  - name: require-digest-not-tag
    match:
      any:
      - resources:
          kinds:
          - Pod
    validate:
      message: "Images must be pinned by digest (image@sha256:...), not a mutable tag. Resolve with `crane digest &lt;image&gt;:&lt;tag&gt;` and update the manifest."
      pattern:
        spec:
          containers:
          - image: "*@sha256:*"    # glob: the image string MUST contain a digest</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKS Exam: Digest Pinning</h5>
                    <p>Know the round trip cold: (1) resolve a tag to a digest with <code>crane digest</code> or <code>docker inspect --format='&#123;&#123;index .RepoDigests 0&#125;&#125;'</code>, (2) rewrite the manifest's <code>image:</code> field as <code>repo@sha256:...</code>, (3) set <code>imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent</code>, (4) optionally enforce it cluster-wide with a Kyverno/Gatekeeper policy so nobody can bypass it by hand-editing a Deployment.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s23-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.6 Permitted Registries — Whitelisting Trusted Image Sources</h3>
                <p>The official CKS curriculum requires you to <strong>secure your supply chain with permitted registries</strong>. Without registry allowlisting, any developer can pull <code>docker.io/random-user/backdoor:latest</code> — bypassing vulnerability scanning, image signing, and SBOM verification.</p>

                <h4>Registry Enforcement Methods</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>How It Works</th><th>CKS Relevance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>ImagePolicyWebhook</strong></td><td>API server calls external webhook on pod creation. Webhook checks image against allowlist.</td><td><strong>HIGH — Built-in admission plugin</strong></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>OPA/Gatekeeper</strong></td><td>Rego policy validates <code>input.review.object.spec.containers[_].image</code> against permitted registries.</td><td><strong>HIGH — Part of Ch 24</strong></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Kyverno</strong></td><td>ClusterPolicy with validate.deny checking image registry prefix.</td><td>Medium — Alternative to OPA</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>

                <h4>Gatekeeper Policy — Allow Only Private Registry</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Rego — Registry Allowlisting ConstraintTemplate</span></div><pre><code class="language-rego">package k8sallowedregistries
violation[{"msg": msg}] {
  container := input.review.object.spec.containers[_]
  not startswith(container.image, "ghcr.io/easypj/")
  not startswith(container.image, "easypj.azurecr.io/")
  msg := sprintf("container %v uses unapproved registry: %v", [container.name, container.image])
}</code></pre></div>

                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 CKS Exam: Permitted Registries</h5><p>Write a Gatekeeper Rego policy checking image prefix. Always allow <code>registry.k8s.io</code> for system images or your cluster will break. ImagePolicyWebhook requires external TLS service — Gatekeeper is faster for the exam.</p></div>
            </section>

