            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Images rot even when your code doesn't.</strong> A clean image today can carry 20+ CVEs in six months as new vulnerabilities are discovered in packages you never touched — continuous scanning, not one-time scanning, is the requirement (23.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Every removed package is an attack vector eliminated forever.</strong> Distroless + multi-stage builds strip shell, package manager, and compiler out of the final image — ~99.6% smaller attack surface and <code>kubectl exec</code> failing is a feature, not a bug (23.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Trivy is the scan gate, not the whole story.</strong> <code>trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --exit-code 1</code> blocks CI/CD, but the database must be refreshed before every scan or newly published CVEs are silently missed (23.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Private registries + imagePullSecrets stop the cluster from trusting the internet.</strong> Typo-squatting, tag mutability, and abandoned-namespace takeover are all real attack paths against public registries like Docker Hub (23.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Tags are mutable pointers; digests are the content itself.</strong> Pin production workloads to <code>image@sha256:...</code> with <code>imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent</code>, and enforce it with a Kyverno/Gatekeeper policy so a hand-edited Deployment can't bypass it (23.5) — then restrict which registries a digest is even allowed to come from (23.6).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 23 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>tag vs digest scanning:</strong> Scanning <code>nginx:latest</code> scans whatever image that tag currently points to. Scanning <code>nginx@sha256:abc123...</code> scans a specific, immutable image. Always use digests in production. On the exam, use whatever the question provides.</li>
                    <li><strong>:latest silently means imagePullPolicy: Always:</strong> if you don't set <code>imagePullPolicy</code> explicitly and the tag is <code>:latest</code>, Kubernetes defaults to <code>Always</code> — every pod restart re-resolves the mutable tag. Any other tag (or a digest) defaults to <code>IfNotPresent</code>. This default is a frequent source of "why did my pod pull a different image than last time?" confusion.</li>
                    <li><strong>--ignore-unfixed is a double-edged sword:</strong> It hides CVEs with no available fix, which reduces noise — but those CVEs are still present and exploitable. For a CKS exam hardening task, you may be expected to find ALL CVEs, including unfixed ones.</li>
                    <li><strong>Trivy DB must be updated:</strong> Trivy uses a local vulnerability database. If you haven't updated recently (<code>trivy image --update-db</code>), you may miss newly published CVEs. On the exam, the environment may or may not have internet access.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Scan → Triage → Fix → Rescan, every time.</strong> Never assume a rebuild fixed everything — <code>trivy image &lt;new-image&gt;</code> after every fix, not just before.</li>
                    <li><strong>Filter before you read.</strong> Start with <code>--severity CRITICAL,HIGH</code> to triage fast; drop to full output only when the exam explicitly asks for every finding.</li>
                    <li><strong>When pinning by digest, resolve once, paste everywhere.</strong> Run <code>crane digest &lt;image&gt;:&lt;tag&gt;</code> a single time and copy the exact <code>sha256:...</code> string into the manifest — don't retype it, a single wrong character silently produces a non-existent image reference.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 23)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">image scanning &amp; digest pinning — practice until automatic</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Scan an image for vulnerabilities (EXAM PATTERN)</span>
