                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Scan an image called "webapp:v1" for CRITICAL vulnerabilities and output the results as JSON. Write the exact command.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">trivy image --severity CRITICAL --format json -o trivy-report.json webapp:v1
# Filter for CRITICAL only, JSON output, save to file
# Alternative: trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH webapp:v1  (table format)</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create an imagePullSecret for a private registry at ghcr.io and reference it in a pod spec.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=ghcr.io \
  --docker-username=easypj \
  --docker-password=$GITHUB_TOKEN \
  -n easypj-prod
# Pod spec: spec.imagePullSecrets: [{name: regcred}]</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between Trivy scanning and image signing (cosign)? Why do you need BOTH?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Trivy finds KNOWN vulnerabilities (CVEs in public databases). It cannot detect: zero-day exploits, intentionally malicious code, or backdoors. Cosign verifies PROVENANCE — it proves the image was built by YOUR CI pipeline and hasn't been tampered with. Trivy = "is this image vulnerable?" Cosign = "is this image really ours?" You need both because scanning catches known bugs and signing catches supply chain attacks.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You scan <code>nginx:1.25</code> and find CVE-2023-44487 (HTTP/2 Rapid Reset) rated CRITICAL. What's the FIRST thing you should do, and how do you find the fixed version?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>First:</strong> Check if the CVE actually affects your deployment. Many CVEs are in libraries your app doesn't use. <strong>Find fix:</strong> <code>trivy image --format json nginx:1.25 | jq '.Results[].Vulnerabilities[] | select(.VulnerabilityID=="CVE-2023-44487") | .FixedVersion'</code>. Then check if a newer nginx tag includes the fix: <code>trivy image nginx:1.25.1 | grep CVE-2023-44487</code>. If a fix exists, update the image. <strong>If no fix:</strong> Mitigate via WAF, NetworkPolicy, or disabling HTTP/2 on the Ingress.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Vulnerability triage is a CKS competency. Not every CRITICAL CVE is exploitable in your context. Check: (1) is the vulnerable library actually loaded? (2) is the attack vector reachable? (3) is a fixed version available? The CKS exam expects you to demonstrate this triage process, not just report CVEs blindly.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you integrate Trivy into a CI/CD pipeline to BLOCK deployment of images with CRITICAL vulnerabilities? Write the pipeline step.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># In CI/CD pipeline (GitHub Actions example)
- name: Scan image for vulnerabilities
  run: |
    trivy image --severity CRITICAL --exit-code 1 --no-progress $IMAGE
    # --exit-code 1: Returns exit code 1 if CRITICAL CVEs found
    # Pipeline stops if exit code != 0
- name: Scan for HIGH vulnerabilities (warning only)
  run: |
    trivy image --severity HIGH --exit-code 0 --no-progress $IMAGE
    # --exit-code 0: Never fails the build, just reports</code></pre>
                    <p>Key flags: <code>--exit-code 1</code> makes Trivy fail the build if vulnerabilities are found. <code>--severity CRITICAL</code> only blocks on critical issues. <code>--ignore-unfixed</code> can be used to only block on fixable CVEs.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>CI/CD integration is where image scanning adds real security value — it prevents vulnerable images from ever reaching production. The CKS exam tests your understanding of the "shift left" principle: catch vulnerabilities at build time, not runtime. Use <code>--exit-code 1</code> for blocking gates and <code>--exit-code 0</code> for informational scans.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between Trivy Operator (cluster scanning) and trivy CLI (image scanning)? When would you use each?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Trivy CLI:</strong> Scans images at build time or on-demand. Use in CI/CD pipelines. Manual or scripted. <strong>Trivy Operator:</strong> Runs as a Kubernetes operator that continuously scans ALL running images in the cluster. Creates VulnerabilityReport CRDs per workload. Detects new CVEs in already-deployed images. Use for continuous runtime monitoring — a CVE discovered today in an image deployed last month will be detected. Both are needed: CLI for prevention (build time), Operator for detection (runtime).</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the prevention vs detection distinction. CLI scanning prevents vulnerable images from being deployed. Operator scanning detects vulnerabilities in images that are already running (because new CVEs are published daily). On the CKS exam, the answer to "how to continuously monitor for new CVEs" is Trivy Operator (or equivalent).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does Trivy detect vulnerabilities? What databases does it query, and how often should you update them?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Trivy compares the software bill of materials (SBOM) of an image against vulnerability databases: (1) <strong>OS packages:</strong> Compares against distribution-specific advisories (Alpine SecDB, Red Hat OVAL, Debian Security Tracker, Ubuntu CVE Tracker). (2) <strong>Language libraries:</strong> Compares against language-specific databases (GitHub Advisory Database for Go/Python/Node.js, Maven for Java). (3) <strong>Infrastructure as Code:</strong> Compares against misconfiguration policies. Trivy automatically downloads and caches databases. Update with <code>trivy image --update-db</code> or set <code>TRIVY_DB_REPOSITORY</code> for air-gapped environments. Update at least daily — new CVEs are published continuously.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The CKS exam tests this concept: scanning is only as good as the vulnerability database. An outdated Trivy DB misses new CVEs. On the exam, always run <code>--update-db</code> before scanning. For air-gapped environments, you may need to configure a local mirror of the Trivy database.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Scan a Kubernetes manifest file for misconfigurations before deploying. Write the Trivy command and explain what types of issues it detects.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Scan Kubernetes manifests for misconfigurations
trivy config ./k8s-manifests/
# Or scan a specific file
trivy config deployment.yaml
# Issues detected:
# - Privileged containers (should be false)
# - runAsNonRoot not set
# - readOnlyRootFilesystem not set
# - Capabilities not dropped
# - hostPath volumes
# - Containers running as root
# - Memory/CPU limits not set</code></pre>
                    <p>Trivy detects Kubernetes security misconfigurations based on built-in policies aligned with CIS benchmarks and Pod Security Standards.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>trivy config</code> extends Trivy beyond image scanning to infrastructure-as-code scanning. This is the "shift left" approach: catch security issues in your YAML before they reach the cluster. On the CKS exam, you may be asked to scan a directory of manifests and identify/fix security issues — this is the tool for that.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is running <code>nginx:1.21</code> (2 years old). Trivy Operator reports 45 CVEs. You update to <code>nginx:1.25</code> but still see 12 CVEs. What's the explanation, and what's your remediation plan?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> (1) Some CVEs are in shared base image layers (e.g., Debian packages) that are present in both versions. (2) Newer versions fix application CVEs but may still have OS-level CVEs. (3) Some CVEs are unfixed — no patch exists yet. <strong>Plan:</strong> (1) Switch to a minimal base image (<code>nginx:alpine</code> or <code>nginx:distroless</code>) — smaller attack surface = fewer CVEs. (2) For unfixed CVEs: assess exploitability, apply compensating controls (WAF, NetworkPolicy, AppArmor). (3) Monitor for the fix release and update when available.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>CVE management is about risk reduction, not perfection. Zero CVEs is an unrealistic goal. The CKS approach: (1) minimize base image size to reduce attack surface, (2) fix what you can, (3) add compensating controls for what you can't fix, (4) continuously monitor. On the exam, you may need to recommend a minimal base image and explain why it reduces CVE count.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to scan images in an air-gapped environment (no internet). How do you configure Trivy to use a local vulnerability database?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. On an internet-connected machine, download the DB
trivy image --download-db-only
# DB is cached at ~/.cache/trivy/db/

# 2. Copy the DB to the air-gapped environment
scp -r ~/.cache/trivy/db/ airgap-node:~/.cache/trivy/db/

# 3. Or host DB on an internal server
# Set environment variable:
export TRIVY_DB_REPOSITORY=https://internal-server/trivy-db

# 4. Run scan without updating (uses cached DB)
trivy image --skip-db-update nginx:1.25

# 5. For Java/Go dependency DBs, also cache:
trivy image --download-java-db-only</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Air-gapped scanning is a CKS exam scenario. The key flags: <code>--download-db-only</code> (download DB without scanning), <code>--skip-db-update</code> (scan with cached DB), <code>TRIVY_DB_REPOSITORY</code> (point to internal mirror). On the exam, if you can't reach the internet, check if the Trivy DB is pre-loaded in the environment or if you need to configure an internal repository.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why are production workloads pinned by digest instead of tag, and what two things must change in the manifest to do it correctly?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Tags are mutable pointers — anyone with registry push access can re-point <code>easypj:v1.2.3</code> at different bytes at any time, and the next pod restart silently pulls the new image under the OLD, already-scanned tag. A digest (<code>easypj@sha256:...</code>) is a hash of the content itself, so it can only ever resolve to those exact bytes. Two changes: (1) rewrite <code>image:</code> from <code>repo:tag</code> to <code>repo@sha256:&lt;hash&gt;</code> (resolved via <code>crane digest</code> or <code>docker inspect --format='&#123;&#123;index .RepoDigests 0&#125;&#125;'</code>), and (2) set <code>imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent</code> explicitly — a digest is already immutable, so there's no reason to force a re-pull, and you avoid ever landing on the <code>:latest</code>-implies-<code>Always</code> default.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Digest pinning is the provenance half of supply chain security — Trivy (23.3) answers "is this safe," digest pinning (23.5) answers "is this the SAME image I said was safe." On the exam, pair a digest-pinning task with a Kyverno/Gatekeeper policy requiring <code>image: "*@sha256:*"</code> so the control can't be bypassed by a hand edit.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

