                <h4>Security-Critical Built-in Admission Plugins — Deep Dive for CKS</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Plugin</th><th>Type</th><th>What It Does</th><th>CKS Exam Scenario</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>NodeRestriction</strong></td>
                                <td><span class="badge-validate">Validating</span></td>
                                <td>Limits a kubelet's API access to ONLY its own node's pods, secrets, and configmaps. A kubelet on node-01 cannot read secrets from pods on node-02. This is the primary defense against a compromised kubelet reading cross-node secrets.</td>
                                <td>"A compromised kubelet is reading secrets from other nodes. How do you prevent this?" → Enable NodeRestriction.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>PodSecurity</strong></td>
                                <td><span class="badge-validate">Validating</span></td>
                                <td>Enforces Pod Security Standards (PSS) at the namespace level. Three levels: privileged (no restrictions), baseline (minimal hardening — blocks hostPath, hostNetwork, privileged containers), restricted (maximum hardening — requires runAsNonRoot, seccomp, drops ALL capabilities). Replaces the deprecated PSP.</td>
                                <td>"Ensure all pods in namespace 'easypj' comply with the restricted PSS profile." → Label namespace with <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code>.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>LimitRanger</strong></td>
                                <td><span class="badge-mutate">Mutating</span> + <span class="badge-validate">Validating</span></td>
                                <td>Two-phase operation: (1) MUTATE: Sets default resource requests/limits for containers that don't specify them. (2) VALIDATE: Rejects containers that exceed min/max limits defined in the namespace's LimitRange object.</td>
                                <td>"Pods without resource limits are starving other workloads. How do you enforce defaults?" → Create a LimitRange object.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ResourceQuota</strong></td>
                                <td><span class="badge-validate">Validating</span></td>
                                <td>Enforces aggregate resource consumption caps per namespace: total CPU, memory, PVC count, Secrets count, ConfigMaps, Services. If creating a new pod would exceed the namespace quota, the request is denied BEFORE the pod is created.</td>
                                <td>"The 'easypj' namespace is consuming all cluster resources. Cap it at 10 CPUs and 20Gi memory." → Create a ResourceQuota.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ServiceAccount</strong></td>
                                <td><span class="badge-mutate">Mutating</span></td>
                                <td>Three automatic actions: (1) If pod.spec.serviceAccountName is empty → sets it to "default". (2) If automountServiceAccountToken is not false → adds a projected volume for the SA token. (3) Adds the token volume mount to each container. This is why pods get a default token even when you don't configure it.</td>
                                <td>Always enabled by default. Know that it exists. To disable: set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> on the pod or SA.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>MutatingAdmissionWebhook</strong></td>
                                <td><span class="badge-mutate">Mutating</span></td>
                                <td>Calls external HTTPS webhooks in sequence. Each webhook can modify the object using JSON Patch (RFC 6902). Used by Kyverno mutate rules, Istio sidecar injection, and custom defaulting logic. <strong>Reinvocation policy</strong> controls whether webhooks re-run after other mutations.</td>
                                <td>"How does Istio automatically inject the Envoy sidecar?" → MutatingAdmissionWebhook calls Istio sidecar injector.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ValidatingAdmissionWebhook</strong></td>
                                <td><span class="badge-validate">Validating</span></td>
                                <td>Calls external HTTPS webhooks that can accept/reject. All validating webhooks run in PARALLEL (not sequential). If ANY webhook returns DENY, the object is rejected. Used by OPA Gatekeeper and Kyverno validate rules.</td>
                                <td>"Block any pod that uses an image from Docker Hub." → Deploy OPA Gatekeeper with ValidatingWebhookConfiguration.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ImagePolicyWebhook</strong></td>
                                <td><span class="badge-validate">Validating</span></td>
                                <td>A specialized, simpler admission plugin that calls an external webhook specifically for IMAGE approval. Unlike ValidatingAdmissionWebhook (which gets the entire object), ImagePolicyWebhook sends ONLY image information. The backend decides: is this image allowed? Simpler to implement than full OPA/Kyverno but less flexible.</td>
                                <td>"Only allow images signed by our CI/CD pipeline, without deploying a full policy engine." → Configure ImagePolicyWebhook backend.</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Configuring Built-in Admission Plugins on the API Server</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — API server manifest with recommended admission plugins for CKS</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: kube-apiserver
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  containers:
  - name: kube-apiserver
    image: registry.k8s.io/kube-apiserver:v1.30.0
    command:
    - kube-apiserver
    - --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity,LimitRanger,ResourceQuota,ServiceAccount,MutatingAdmissionWebhook,ValidatingAdmissionWebhook
    - --disable-admission-plugins=DefaultStorageClass
    - --admission-control-config-file=/etc/kubernetes/admission-control.yaml
    volumeMounts:
    - name: admission-config
      mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/admission-control.yaml
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: admission-config
    hostPath:
      path: /etc/kubernetes/admission-control.yaml
      type: File</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How to Verify Which Admission Plugins ARE Active — Three Methods</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Verify admission plugins from the API server (no direct node access needed)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── METHOD 1: Check API server pod spec (requires node access) ──
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml \
  | grep enable-admission-plugins
# Or, from any machine with kubectl:
kubectl get pod -n kube-system -l component=kube-apiserver \
  -o json | jq '.items[0].spec.containers[0].command[] | select(contains("enable-admission"))'

# ── METHOD 2: kubectl api-versions (shows enabled API groups, not plugins) ──
# This shows which RESOURCE TYPES are available — admission plugins
# control HOW those types are processed, not which ones exist.

# ── METHOD 3: API server metrics (most reliable) ──
# If Prometheus metrics are enabled:
curl -sk https://localhost:6443/metrics | grep apiserver_admission_controller
# Shows latency and count per admission plugin per operation

# ── METHOD 4: Check logs at startup ──
kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt; | grep "Loaded"
# Shows: "Loaded 12 mutating admission controllers and 15 validating admission controllers"
# Then lists each one with its initialization status</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKS Exam Critical: Enabling Admission Plugins</h5>
                    <p>On the CKS exam, you may be asked to <strong>enable specific admission plugins</strong>. Remember: (1) The API server manifest is at <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. (2) Edit <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code>. (3) The API server pod restarts automatically (static pod). (4) <strong>Always include existing plugins when adding new ones</strong> — the flag is a COMPLETE LIST, not additive. If you replace <code>--enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction</code> with <code>--enable-admission-plugins=PodSecurity</code>, you LOST NodeRestriction and everything else. (5) Some plugins have dependencies: ServiceAccount plugin should run BEFORE MutatingAdmissionWebhook. (6) Verify with <code>kubectl describe pod kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt; -n kube-system</code> and check logs for "Loaded N mutating admission controllers."</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 24.2 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.2 Dynamic Admission Control — Webhooks Explained</h3>
                <p>Built-in admission plugins are powerful but limited — they can only enforce what the Kubernetes authors built into them. For <strong>custom organizational policies</strong>, you need <strong>dynamic admission control</strong>: webhooks that call an external HTTPS server. The API server sends the admission request to your webhook, and your webhook responds with "allow" or "deny." This is how OPA Gatekeeper, Kyverno, and custom policy engines integrate. Understanding the webhook protocol deeply — TLS, AdmissionReview, timeouts, failurePolicy — is essential for both the CKS exam and production operations.</p>

                <h4>Mutating vs Validating Webhooks — Two Different Purposes, Different Execution Models</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Mutating vs Validating Webhook — When and How They Fire</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                     ADMISSION WEBHOOK PHASES                              │
  │                                                                           │
  │  kubectl apply → API Server                                               │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ├── AuthN + AuthZ ────────────────────── ✅ Pass                    │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗    │
  │  ║  PHASE 1: MUTATING WEBHOOKS — Run SEQUENTIALLY                    ║    │
  │  ║  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  ║    │
  │  ║  Webhook A runs → modifies object → Webhook B sees MODIFIED obj  ║    │
  │  ║  Webhook B runs → modifies object → Webhook C sees both changes  ║    │
  │  ║                                                                   ║    │
  │  ║  Each webhook receives AdmissionReview with the current object:   ║    │
  │  ║  {                                                                 ║    │
  │  ║    "request": {                                                   ║    │
  │  ║      "uid": "abc123",                                            ║    │
  │  ║      "operation": "CREATE",                                      ║    │
  │  ║      "object": { ... },       ← Original or previously-mutated   ║    │
  │  ║      "oldObject": null        ← Only for UPDATE/DELETE ops       ║    │
  │  ║    }                                                               ║    │
  │  ║  }                                                                 ║    │
  │  ║                                                                   ║    │
  │  ║  Webhook responds with JSON Patch to modify the object:            ║    │
  │  ║  {                                                                  ║    │
  │  ║    "response": {                                                   ║    │
  │  ║      "uid": "abc123",                                             ║    │
  │  ║      "allowed": true,                                             ║    │
  │  ║      "patchType": "JSONPatch",                                    ║    │
  │  ║      "patch": "W3sib3AiOiJhZGQiLC..."  ← base64(JSON Patch)      ║    │
  │  ║    }                                                                ║    │
  │  ║  }                                                                  ║    │
  │  ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝    │
  │       │ Object has been MODIFIED                                          │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗    │
  │  ║  PHASE 2: VALIDATING WEBHOOKS — Run in PARALLEL                    ║    │
  │  ║  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  ║    │
  │  ║  ALL validating webhooks receive the SAME final object and run    ║    │
  │  ║  concurrently. If ANY returns DENY → request is rejected.         ║    │
  │  ║                                                                   ║    │
  │  ║  Webhook Response:                                                 ║    │
  │  ║  {                                                                 ║    │
  │  ║    "response": {                                                  ║    │
  │  ║      "uid": "abc123",                                            ║    │
  │  ║      "allowed": true,           ← false → 403 Forbidden          ║    │
  │  ║      "status": {                                                  ║    │
  │  ║        "code": 403,                                              ║    │
  │  ║        "message": "image not from approved registry"             ║    │
  │  ║      }                                                             ║    │
  │  ║    }                                                               ║    │
  │  ║  }                                                                 ║    │
  │  ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝    │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ├── ALL ALLOWED ✅ → Object persisted to etcd                      │
  │       └── ANY DENIED ❌ → 403 Forbidden                                  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  🔑 CRITICAL: MUTATING IS SEQUENTIAL, VALIDATING IS PARALLEL
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  • Mutating: Each webhook sees the output of the previous one.
    Order is determined by the MutatingWebhookConfiguration name.
  • Validating: All webhooks receive the identical final object.
    They run concurrently for maximum throughput.
  • If a mutating webhook TIMES OUT, the chain STOPS.
  • If a validating webhook TIMES OUT, it depends on failurePolicy.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Webhook TLS Certificate Management — The Most Common Production Problem</h4>
                <p>Every admission webhook requires <strong>mutual TLS</strong>: the API server must verify the webhook server's certificate, and the webhook server must verify the API server's client certificate. The <code>caBundle</code> field in the webhook configuration contains the CA certificate (base64-encoded PEM) that the API server uses to verify the webhook's TLS certificate. This is the single most common source of webhook failures in production:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Webhook TLS Handshake — What Actually Happens</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                     WEBHOOK TLS SETUP                                 │
  │                                                                       │
  │  STEP 1: Generate CA + certificates                                   │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  # Create a self-signed CA                                     │    │
  │  │  openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -days 365 -nodes \       │    │
  │  │    -keyout ca.key -out ca.crt \                               │    │
  │  │    -subj "/CN=admission-ca"                                    │    │
  │  │                                                                 │    │
  │  │  # Create webhook server cert (signed by CA)                   │    │
  │  │  openssl req -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout server.key \     │    │
  │  │    -out server.csr -subj "/CN=gatekeeper-webhook-service.\    │    │
  │  │    gatekeeper-system.svc"    ← MUST match Service DNS name     │    │
  │  │                                                                 │    │
  │  │  openssl x509 -req -in server.csr -CA ca.crt -CAkey ca.key \  │    │
  │  │    -CAcreateserial -out server.crt -days 365                   │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                                                                       │
  │  STEP 2: Store in Kubernetes Secret                                   │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  kubectl create secret tls gatekeeper-webhook-server-cert \   │    │
  │  │    --cert=server.crt --key=server.key \                       │    │
  │  │    -n gatekeeper-system                                        │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                                                                       │
  │  STEP 3: Configure ValidatingWebhookConfiguration                     │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  clientConfig:                                                 │    │
  │  │    service:                                                    │    │
  │  │      name: gatekeeper-webhook-service                          │    │
  │  │      namespace: gatekeeper-system                              │    │
  │  │      path: /v1/admit                                           │    │
  │  │    caBundle: <base64(ca.crt)>     ← API server uses this CA   │    │
  │  │                                      to VERIFY server cert    │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                                                                       │
  │  🔑 TLS VERIFICATION FLOW:                                            │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  1. API server connects to gatekeeper-webhook-service:443     │    │
  │  │  2. Webhook server presents server.crt                        │    │
  │  │  3. API server checks: Is server.crt signed by ca.crt?        │    │
  │  │  4. API server checks: Does CN/SAN match the service DNS?     │    │
  │  │     → CN must be: gatekeeper-webhook-service.gatekeeper-      │    │
  │  │       system.svc (or use SANs for wildcard: *.gatekeeper-     │    │
  │  │       system.svc)                                             │    │
  │  │  5. If EITHER check fails → TLS error → webhook call fails    │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🔴 The #1 Webhook Failure: CN/SAN Mismatch</h5>
                    <p>The most common webhook deployment error is a certificate CN that doesn't match the Kubernetes Service DNS name. The API server connects to the webhook using the service DNS name: <code>&lt;service-name&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc</code>. If the server certificate's CN (or SAN) doesn't match this EXACT name, TLS verification fails. Solution: always use the full service DNS name in the certificate's CN field, or add it as a Subject Alternative Name (SAN). With cert-manager, use a Certificate resource with the correct DNS name automatically.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Webhook Configuration — Every Field Explained</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — ValidatingWebhookConfiguration with ALL fields annotated</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingWebhookConfiguration
metadata:
  name: easypj-image-policy
webhooks:
- name: image-policy.easypj.com
  # ── RULES: Which API operations trigger this webhook? ──
  rules:
  - apiGroups:   [""]
    apiVersions: ["v1"]
    operations:  ["CREATE", "UPDATE"]   # Also: DELETE, CONNECT
    resources:   ["pods"]               # Also: "pods/status", "pods/ephemeralcontainers"
    scope:       "Namespaced"           # "Cluster" or "*" (both)

  # ── NAMESPACE SELECTOR: Filter by namespace labels ──
  namespaceSelector:
    matchLabels:
      admission.easypj.com/enabled: "true"
    # matchExpressions also supported for complex matching

  # ── OBJECT SELECTOR: Filter by object labels (K8s 1.15+) ──
  # More granular than namespaceSelector. Only sends objects
  # that match these labels to the webhook.
  objectSelector:
    matchLabels:
      security.easypj.com/scan: "required"

  # ── CLIENT CONFIG: Where is the webhook? ──
  clientConfig:
    # Option A: Kubernetes Service (production)
    service:
      name: gatekeeper-webhook-service
      namespace: gatekeeper-system
      path: /v1/admit
      port: 443
    # Option B: External URL (for external policy engines)
    # url: https://policy-engine.easypj.com/admit
    caBundle: LS0tLS1CRUdJTi...

  # ── WEBHOOK BEHAVIOR ──
  admissionReviewVersions: ["v1", "v1beta1"]  # v1 preferred, v1beta1 as fallback
  sideEffects: None               # None | NoneOnDryRun | Unknown
  # None: webhook has NO side effects (safe for dry-run)
  # NoneOnDryRun: webhook has side effects but NOT during dry-run
  # Unknown: must specify sideEffectClass for dry-run compatibility

  failurePolicy: Fail             # Fail | Ignore
  # Fail: if webhook UNREACHABLE → DENY request (secure, but risky)
  # Ignore: if webhook UNREACHABLE → ALLOW request (available, but insecure)

  timeoutSeconds: 10              # 1-30 seconds. Webhook must respond in this time.
  # If timeout is exceeded:
  #   failurePolicy=Fail → request DENIED
  #   failurePolicy=Ignore → request ALLOWED

  # ── MATCH POLICY: How rules are interpreted ──
  matchPolicy: Equivalent         # Equivalent | Exact
  # Equivalent: match resources even if API group/version differs
  # Exact: only match the EXACT apiGroups/apiVersions specified

  # ── REINVOCATION POLICY (MutatingWebhookConfiguration only) ──
  # reinvocationPolicy: IfNeeded  # Never | IfNeeded
  # Controls whether mutating webhooks re-run after other mutations</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>failurePolicy Deep Dive — The Most Important CKS Decision</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left">
                        <div class="panel-header" style="color:#326ce5;">failurePolicy: Fail (Secure — Fail CLOSED)</div>
<pre>
  SCENARIO: Policy engine pod crashes

  Developer: kubectl apply -f pod.yaml
  → API Server → AuthN/AuthZ ✅
  → Mutating phase ✅
  → Validating phase:
    → API Server tries POST /validate to Gatekeeper
    → Gatekeeper pod is DOWN → TCP connection refused
    → API Server retries (up to timeoutSeconds)
    → After 10s timeout: webhook call FAILED
    → failurePolicy: Fail → DENY request

  RESULT: 403 Forbidden
  "admission webhook 'image-policy.easypj.com'
   denied the request: webhook unreachable"

  IMPACT: NO pods can be created/updated cluster-wide
  MITIGATION: Run webhook with 3+ replicas, PDB,
              health checks, and alerting on webhook failures

  WHEN TO USE: Security-critical policies
  (image allowlist, PSS enforcement, RBAC validation)
</pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel-right">
                        <div class="panel-header" style="color:#4ade80;">failurePolicy: Ignore (Available — Fail OPEN)</div>
<pre>
  SCENARIO: Policy engine pod crashes

  Developer: kubectl apply -f pod.yaml
  → API Server → AuthN/AuthZ ✅
  → Mutating phase ✅
  → Validating phase:
    → API Server tries POST /validate to webhook
    → Webhook pod is DOWN → connection refused
    → After 10s timeout: webhook call FAILED
    → failurePolicy: Ignore → ALLOW request

  RESULT: Pod CREATED (even if it violates policy!)
  Developer sees: Pod created successfully
  Security team sees: Nothing (no alert, no log)

  IMPACT: All pods are deployed WITHOUT validation
  MITIGATION: Only use for non-security policies
              (default labels, annotations, formatting)

  WHEN TO USE: Non-critical policies
  (default labels, annotation injection, cosmetic checks)
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Debugging Webhook Failures — The CKS Troubleshooting Flow</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Check webhook pod status</strong><p><code>kubectl get pods -n gatekeeper-system</code> → Are all webhook pods Running? If CrashLoopBackOff → check logs.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>Check webhook Service endpoints</strong><p><code>kubectl get endpoints -n gatekeeper-system gatekeeper-webhook-service</code> → Are there ready endpoints? If empty → Service selector doesn't match pods.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>Check TLS certificate</strong><p><code>openssl s_client -connect gatekeeper-webhook-service.gatekeeper-system.svc:443</code> → Does the certificate CN match? Is it valid? Is it signed by the CA in caBundle?</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>Check API server logs</strong><p><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt; | grep webhook</code> → Look for "failed calling webhook" or "x509: certificate signed by unknown authority" errors.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">5</span><strong>Check webhook configuration</strong><p><code>kubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations</code> → Is failurePolicy: Fail set? Are there namespaceSelectors blocking your namespace? Is timeoutSeconds too low?</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">6</span><strong>Test webhook directly</strong><p>Use <code>curl</code> to send a test AdmissionReview to the webhook. If it returns 200 with allowed:true/false → webhook is working. If connection refused → TLS or network issue.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

