            <section class="section-block" id="s24-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.4 Kyverno — Simpler Kubernetes-Native Policies</h3>
                <p>OPA Gatekeeper is powerful but requires learning <strong>Rego</strong>, a functional logic programming language that many teams find challenging. <strong>Kyverno</strong> (Greek for "govern") takes a different approach: policies are written in <strong>pure YAML</strong> using Kubernetes-native patterns. No new language to learn. No separate template/constraint separation. Kyverno supports <strong>four policy types</strong>: Validate (deny non-compliant resources), Mutate (auto-fix missing fields), Generate (create additional resources like default NetworkPolicies), and VerifyImages (check image signatures with Cosign/Notary). For teams already comfortable with Kubernetes YAML, Kyverno offers a gentler learning curve.</p>

                <h4>Kyverno Architecture — How YAML Becomes Admission Control</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kyverno Internals — From ClusterPolicy to Admission Decision</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    KYVERNO ARCHITECTURE                                │
  │                                                                       │
  │  ┌─────────────────────┐     ┌──────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  Kyverno Controller  │     │  Kyverno Webhook Server           │   │
  │  │  (background loop)   │     │  (admission endpoint)             │   │
  │  │                      │     │                                   │   │
  │  │  • Watches CRDs:     │     │  • Receives AdmissionReview from  │   │
  │  │    - ClusterPolicy   │     │    API server                     │   │
  │  │    - Policy          │     │  • Evaluates policies against     │   │
  │  │  • Generates webhook │     │    the request object             │   │
  │  │    configurations    │     │  • Returns AdmissionResponse      │   │
  │  │  • Runs background   │     │  • Mutate: returns JSON Patch     │   │
  │  │    scans (audit)     │     │  • Validate: returns allow/deny   │   │
  │  │  • Generates Policy  │     │                                   │   │
  │  │    Reports           │     │                                   │   │
  │  └─────────────────────┘     └──────────────────────────────────┘   │
  │                                                                       │
  │  REQUEST FLOW:                                                        │
  │  ─────────────                                                        │
  │  API Server → POST /mutate → Kyverno mutating webhook → JSON Patch   │
  │            → POST /validate → Kyverno validating webhook → allow/deny│
  │                                                                       │
  │  POLICY EVALUATION ENGINE:                                            │
  │  ──────────────────────────                                          │
  │  Kyverno uses JMESPath (JSON Matching Expression Paths) for          │
  │  condition evaluation. JMESPath is a query language for JSON:        │
  │                                                                       │
  │  {{ request.object.spec.containers[].image }}                         │
  │  → ["registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2", "redis:7-alpine"]              │
  │                                                                       │
  │  Kyverno evaluates JMESPath expressions against the AdmissionReview  │
  │  request and determines if conditions match. This is the YAML-native │
  │  equivalent of Rego's input.review.object.spec.containers[_].        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Kyverno Policy Types — Four Actions You Can Take</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5>Validate</h5>
                        <p>Check resources and DENY non-compliant ones. Equivalent to OPA's violation rules. Uses <code>validate.deny.conditions</code> with JMESPath expressions.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div>
                        <h5>Mutate</h5>
                        <p>Automatically MODIFY resources via <code>patchStrategicMerge</code> or <code>patchesJson6902</code>. Auto-add securityContext, labels, annotations when developers forget.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✨</div>
                        <h5>Generate</h5>
                        <p>CREATE additional resources when a trigger fires. Auto-create NetworkPolicy for new namespaces. Clone secrets across namespaces. Sync ConfigMaps.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✍️</div>
                        <h5>VerifyImages</h5>
                        <p>Check container image SIGNATURES (Cosign, Notary, Sigstore). Only allow images signed by your CI/CD pipeline's key. Covered in Ch26.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Kyverno Validate Policy — Image Registry Allowlist</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Kyverno ClusterPolicy: Deny images from unapproved registries</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-approved-registry
spec:
  validationFailureAction: Enforce
  background: true
  rules:
  - name: check-image-registry
    match:
      any:
      - resources:
          kinds: [Pod, Deployment, StatefulSet, DaemonSet, Job, CronJob]
    exclude:
      any:
      - resources:
          namespaces: [kube-system, kyverno, gatekeeper-system]
    validate:
      message: >-
        Image {{ request.object.spec.containers[].image }} is not from
        an approved registry. Allowed: registry.easypj.com, gcr.io/easypj-project
      deny:
        conditions:
          any:
          - key: "{{ request.object.spec.initContainers[].image }}"
            operator: NotIn
            value: ["registry.easypj.com/*", "gcr.io/easypj-project/*"]
          - key: "{{ request.object.spec.containers[].image }}"
            operator: NotIn
            value: ["registry.easypj.com/*", "gcr.io/easypj-project/*"]
          - key: "{{ request.object.spec.ephemeralContainers[].image }}"
            operator: NotIn
            value: ["registry.easypj.com/*", "gcr.io/easypj-project/*"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Kyverno Mutate Policy — Auto-Add securityContext</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Kyverno ClusterPolicy: Auto-add securityContext if developer forgot it</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: add-default-securitycontext
spec:
  validationFailureAction: Enforce
  rules:
  - name: add-runAsNonRoot
    match:
      any:
      - resources:
          kinds: ["Pod"]
    mutate:
      patchStrategicMerge:
        spec:
          securityContext:
            runAsNonRoot: true
            runAsUser: 1000
            fsGroup: 1000
          containers:
          - (name): "*"
            securityContext:
              allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
              readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
              capabilities:
                drop: ["ALL"]
          initContainers:
          - (name): "*"
            securityContext:
              allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
              readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
              capabilities:
                drop: ["ALL"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Kyverno Generate Policy — Auto-Create NetworkPolicy on New Namespace</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Kyverno Generate Policy: Auto-create a default-deny NetworkPolicy for every new namespace</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: auto-create-default-deny-networkpolicy
spec:
  rules:
  - name: generate-default-deny
    match:
      any:
      - resources:
          kinds: ["Namespace"]
    # ── EXCLUDE system namespaces ──
    exclude:
      any:
      - resources:
          names: [kube-system, kube-public, kyverno]
    # ── GENERATE: Create a NetworkPolicy in the new namespace ──
    generate:
      apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
      kind: NetworkPolicy
      name: default-deny-all
      namespace: "{{ request.object.metadata.name }}"  # Target: the new namespace
      synchronize: true  # If someone deletes the NP, Kyverno recreates it
      data:
        spec:
          podSelector: {}        # Selects ALL pods in the namespace
          policyTypes:
          - Ingress
          - Egress
          # No ingress/egress rules = DENY ALL traffic by default
          # Individual apps must have their own NetworkPolicies to allow traffic</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Kyverno CLI Testing — Test Policies Before Deploying</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Kyverno CLI: Test policies locally before cluster deployment</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Test a policy against a resource (dry run) ──
kyverno apply /path/to/policy.yaml --resource /path/to/pod.yaml

# ── Test a policy against a Kubernetes resource in-cluster ──
