                <h4>OPA Gatekeeper vs Kyverno — Choosing the Right Policy Engine</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>OPA Gatekeeper</th><th>Kyverno</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Policy Language</strong></td><td><strong>Rego</strong> — Logic programming. Powerful, expressive, but steep learning curve.</td><td><strong>YAML + JMESPath</strong> — Kubernetes-native. Familiar syntax. Lower barrier to entry.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Policy Types</strong></td><td>Validate only (mutating needs separate webhook)</td><td><strong>Validate, Mutate, Generate, VerifyImages</strong> — all in one</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Template/Instance Split</strong></td><td>✅ ConstraintTemplate + Constraint (separation of concerns)</td><td>❌ Single ClusterPolicy/Policy resource (simpler but less reusable)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Image Verification</strong></td><td>Requires external Cosign webhook or custom solution</td><td><strong>Built-in VerifyImages</strong> with Cosign, Notary, Sigstore support</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Mutating</strong></td><td>❌ Not natively supported. Separate MutatingWebhook needed.</td><td>✅ Built-in mutate via patchStrategicMerge or patchesJson6902</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Generate</strong></td><td>❌ Not supported</td><td>✅ Auto-create resources on triggers (NetworkPolicies, secrets, ConfigMaps)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Policy Testing</strong></td><td>✅ <code>gator test</code> CLI with unit test framework</td><td>✅ <code>kyverno test</code> CLI with test manifests</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Audit & Reports</strong></td><td>✅ ConstraintStatus per constraint</td><td>✅ PolicyReport + ClusterPolicyReport CRDs with structured results</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Learning Curve</strong></td><td>🔴 Steep — new language paradigm (logic programming)</td><td>🟢 Gentle — YAML anyone can read; JMESPath adds complexity for advanced use</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Flexibility</strong></td><td>🟢 Extremely flexible — Rego is Turing-complete</td><td>🟡 Good for 95% of K8s use cases; complex logic hits JMESPath limits</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Ecosystem</strong></td><td>CNCF Graduated. Rego reusable beyond K8s (Terraform, Envoy, etc.)</td><td>CNCF Incubating. K8s-focused. Growing community.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CKS Exam</strong></td><td>✅ Know Rego basics, violation pattern, ConstraintTemplate/Constraint structure</td><td>✅ Know YAML structure, validate/mutate/generate rule types, JMESPath basics</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Best For</strong></td><td>Large orgs with platform teams, multi-domain policies, teams already using OPA</td><td>Teams wanting K8s-native experience, auto-mutation, built-in image verification</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Decision Tree: OPA Gatekeeper or Kyverno?</h4>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="tree-title">Policy Engine Selection Framework</div>
<pre>
  START: I need a Kubernetes admission policy engine
  │
  ├─ Does my team already know Rego or use OPA elsewhere? → OPA Gatekeeper
  ├─ Do I need MULTI-DOMAIN policies (K8s + Terraform + CI/CD)? → OPA (Rego is portable)
  ├─ Do I need auto-mutation of resources? → Kyverno (built-in mutate)
  ├─ Do I need image signature verification? → Kyverno (built-in VerifyImages)
  ├─ Do I need to auto-GENERATE resources on triggers? → Kyverno (Generate rules)
  ├─ Is my team YAML-native and resistant to learning Rego? → Kyverno
  └─ Need MAXIMUM flexibility for complex logic? → OPA Gatekeeper

  🟢 MANY ORGS USE BOTH: Kyverno for mutate/generate/verify, OPA for complex validation
</pre>
