            <section class="section-block" id="s24-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.5 Official Admission Controller Facts — Defaults and the Re-Validation Rule</h3>
                <p>Everything in 24.1–24.4 explains the mechanism and the tooling. This section is different: it's a small set of <strong>fact-checked ground truths straight from kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/admission-controllers</strong> — what's on BY DEFAULT on a standard cluster, the exact flag syntax, and one subtle mutation-order detail that explains "phantom" webhook re-calls that confuse even experienced admins. Get these wrong on the exam and you'll waste minutes fighting a plugin that was never off, or debugging a webhook that's "firing twice" for a perfectly good reason.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Verify First — Don't Fight a Plugin That's Already On</h5>
                    <p>MANY security-relevant plugins are <strong>ALREADY ENABLED by default</strong> on a standard cluster. A common exam mistake is running <code>--enable-admission-plugins=PodSecurity</code> as if it needs turning on — burning exam time re-declaring a default, and risking the "complete list, not additive" trap from 24.1 if you don't append correctly. Verify what's actually running BEFORE touching the flag:</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Bash — the first command to run before any admission-plugin task</span></div>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">ps aux | grep kube-apiserver | grep admission-plugins</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Default-Enabled Admission Controllers — The Security-Relevant Subset</h4>
                <p>The table below is the ground truth for what a stock kube-apiserver runs with no <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> flag at all. Everything marked ✅ is already protecting you; everything marked ❌ is a gap you must close yourself.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Plugin</th><th>Enabled by Default?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>NamespaceLifecycle</strong></td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>LimitRanger</strong></td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ServiceAccount</strong></td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>PodSecurity</strong></td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ResourceQuota</strong></td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>MutatingAdmissionWebhook</strong></td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ValidatingAdmissionWebhook</strong></td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</strong></td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:#2a0a0a80;border-left:3px solid #f85149;"><td><strong>NodeRestriction</strong></td><td>❌ NOT default — you must enable it</td></tr>
                            <tr style="background:#2a0a0a80;border-left:3px solid #f85149;"><td><strong>ImagePolicyWebhook</strong></td><td>❌ NOT default — you must enable it</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Two Most CKS-Relevant Plugins Are NOT On by Default</h5>
                    <p><strong>NodeRestriction</strong> and <strong>ImagePolicyWebhook</strong> — the two most CKS-relevant security plugins — are <strong>NOT</strong> on by default. Explicitly enabling <strong>NodeRestriction</strong> is one of the most common CKS exam tasks; never assume it (or ImagePolicyWebhook) is already protecting your cluster. This is exactly the correction applied to the 24.1 plugin catalog table — it originally mismarked NodeRestriction as default-enabled.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Flag Syntax — Comma-Separated Lists, Combinable in One Command</h4>
                <p><code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> and <code>--disable-admission-plugins</code> both take a <strong>comma-separated list</strong>, and the two flags can be used <strong>together in the same command</strong> — enable what you need, disable what you don't, in one edit:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Bash — enable and disable plugins together, on the static pod manifest</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml — command args
- --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,ImagePolicyWebhook
- --disable-admission-plugins=AlwaysAdmit</code></pre>
                </div>
                <p>Remember the 24.1 rule: <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> is a <strong>COMPLETE LIST</strong>, not an additive one. If the cluster already runs with a set of default plugins plus a few custom ones, and you overwrite the flag with only <code>NodeRestriction</code>, you silently DROP every other explicitly-enabled plugin — the defaults still apply (defaults are additive to whatever you specify), but any previously hand-added plugin is gone unless you list it again.</p>

                <h4>The Re-Validation Rule — Why Mutating Webhooks Can Re-Trigger Each Other</h4>
                <p>This is the subtle, exam-relevant detail that 24.2's "mutating runs first, then validating" model simplifies away. Admission actually runs in <strong>two passes within the mutating phase</strong>: the API server calls every mutating admission plugin/webhook in order once — but if <strong>ANY</strong> of them changed the object, the API server <strong>RE-RUNS every previously-called mutating webhook again</strong> against the newly-mutated object, so earlier webhooks get a chance to react to later webhooks' changes. Only once a full pass produces <strong>zero further changes</strong> does the API server move on to the validating phase.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Admission Re-Validation — Mutating Webhooks Re-Run After ANY Mutation</div>
<pre>
  MUTATING PHASE — SEQUENTIAL, WITH RE-VALIDATION
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Request arrives → mutating webhook chain begins (Webhooks A, B, C)

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  PASS 1 (initial order):                                             │
  │  Webhook A runs → does NOT modify the object                        │
  │  Webhook B runs → MODIFIES the object (adds a label)                 │
  │  Webhook C runs → sees B's change → does NOT modify further          │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                              │
                              │ 🔑 Object WAS mutated somewhere in Pass 1
                              ▼
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  PASS 2 (RE-RUN — because Pass 1 changed the object):                 │
  │  Webhook A runs AGAIN on the newly-mutated object                    │
  │    → this time A reacts to B's earlier change and mutates too        │
  │  Webhook B runs AGAIN → sees A's new change → no further change      │
  │  Webhook C runs AGAIN → no further change                            │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                              │
                              │ 🔑 NO mutation happened in Pass 2 → STABLE
                              ▼
  MUTATING PHASE COMPLETE → proceed to VALIDATING PHASE
  (all validating plugins/webhooks now run in PARALLEL against this
   final, fully-reconciled object)

  ⚠️  WHY THIS MATTERS: if Webhook A is NOT idempotent (e.g. it blindly
      APPENDS to a list instead of setting/checking a value), Pass 2
      produces a DIFFERENT result than Pass 1 would suggest — duplicate
      labels, doubled annotations, or worse. ALWAYS design mutating
      webhooks to be idempotent: safe to run more than once on the same
      object with no cumulative side effect. This re-run behavior is
      also why a chain of mutating webhooks can appear to "fire twice"
      in logs — it isn't a bug, it's the re-validation rule working as
      designed.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Rejection Is Immediate and Total — No Partial Admission</h5>
                    <p>Request rejection is immediate and total: if <strong>ANY</strong> single controller in either phase (mutating or validating) rejects the request, the <strong>WHOLE</strong> request fails right there — no partial admission, no "some fields applied." A pod either clears every mutating plugin, every re-run pass, and every validating plugin/webhook, or it is not created at all. There is no intermediate state written to etcd.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>🔧 Under the Hood — Admission Webhook Flow: MutatingWebhook → ValidatingWebhook → PSS → OPA → Kyverno — Execution Order and Interaction</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                <pre class="uth-diagram">
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   ADMISSION CONTROL EXECUTION ORDER                                        │
  │                                                                           │
  │   Pod creation request arrives                                             │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │   1. MUTATING ADMISSION (runs FIRST, can MODIFY object)                   │
  │      • Built-in mutating plugins (DefaultStorageClass, etc.)              │
  │      • MutatingWebhookConfigurations (Kyverno mutate, custom mutators)   │
  │      • If failurePolicy=Fail and webhook fails → REJECT pod              │
  │      • Output: modified pod object (mutations applied)                    │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │   2. OBJECT SCHEMA VALIDATION                                              │
  │      • API server validates the object against OpenAPI schema             │
  │      • Catches: wrong field names, invalid types, missing required fields │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │   3. VALIDATING ADMISSION (runs SECOND, can only ACCEPT/REJECT)           │
  │      • Built-in validating plugins (PodSecurity, NodeRestriction)         │
  │      • ValidatingWebhookConfigurations (Gatekeeper, Kyverno validate)    │
  │      • If failurePolicy=Fail and webhook fails → REJECT pod              │
  │      • If failurePolicy=Ignore and webhook fails → ACCEPT pod (risky!)   │
  │                                                                           │
  │   🔑 A mutating webhook can ADD securityContext fields. A validating     │
  │      webhook then checks if those fields meet policy. This is how         │
  │      Kyverno can both auto-fix AND validate in one ClusterPolicy.        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The admission chain is fixed at API server startup, not dynamic per request.</strong> Mutating plugins always run before validating plugins so that validators see the FINAL, fully-mutated object — never the original request (24.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Dynamic admission control is how you plug in custom logic the built-ins can't express.</strong> <code>failurePolicy</code> is the single highest-stakes field in any webhook config: <code>Fail</code> is secure but can block the whole cluster if your webhook crashes; <code>Ignore</code> keeps the cluster available but silently bypasses the policy (24.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>OPA Gatekeeper separates the CHECK from its APPLICATION.</strong> A ConstraintTemplate defines Rego logic once; a Constraint applies it with parameters to specific kinds/namespaces — platform teams write templates, app teams instantiate constraints (24.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Kyverno trades Rego's power for YAML-native simplicity</strong> and adds Mutate, Generate, and VerifyImages natively — capabilities Gatekeeper needs separate tooling to match (24.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Verify before you configure, and design for re-runs.</strong> ~8 security-relevant plugins (including PodSecurity and ValidatingAdmissionPolicy) are ALREADY on by default — only NodeRestriction and ImagePolicyWebhook require explicit enabling. And because the API server RE-RUNS every mutating webhook after any one of them changes the object, mutating webhooks must be idempotent — plus any single denial anywhere in the chain fails the WHOLE request, with no partial admission (24.5).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 24 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>CA bundle must be valid base64:</strong> The <code>caBundle</code> field in a webhook configuration must be valid base64-encoded PEM certificate. If the CA doesn't match the webhook server's TLS certificate, ALL admission requests fail with "x509: certificate signed by unknown authority."</li>
                    <li><strong>failurePolicy: Ignore creates a bypass:</strong> If the webhook server is down with <code>failurePolicy: Ignore</code>, all requests that the webhook was supposed to validate pass through unchecked. For security webhooks, always use <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>timeoutSeconds too low causes flakiness:</strong> Webhook timeout is 10s by default. If your webhook does complex validation (e.g., external API call), it may timeout, and with <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code>, legitimate requests get rejected. Test at scale.</li>
                    <li><strong>Don't re-enable a plugin that's already default:</strong> Running <code>--enable-admission-plugins=PodSecurity</code> wastes exam time on a plugin that's already on — and if you don't append the FULL existing list (it's a complete list, not additive), you can silently DROP a previously hand-enabled plugin like ImagePolicyWebhook. Always <code>ps aux | grep kube-apiserver | grep admission-plugins</code> first (24.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Non-idempotent mutating webhooks break under re-validation:</strong> the API server re-runs EVERY mutating webhook again if ANY of them changes the object (24.5). A webhook that blindly APPENDS a label or annotation instead of setting it will produce duplicates on the second pass — always design mutations to be safe to apply more than once.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Classify the mechanism before you touch anything:</strong> built-in plugin (edit a flag), webhook (edit a Configuration object), or policy engine (write Rego or a Kyverno ClusterPolicy) — each has a completely different fix, and misidentifying it wastes exam minutes.</li>
                    <li><strong>failurePolicy is a one-line decision with outsized consequences.</strong> Default to <code>Fail</code> for anything security-critical; reserve <code>Ignore</code> for cosmetic, non-blocking mutations only.</li>
                    <li><strong>Verify, don't assume, before every admission task:</strong> <code>ps aux | grep kube-apiserver | grep admission-plugins</code> before enabling anything, and <code>kubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations,mutatingwebhookconfigurations</code> before writing a new policy — you may already be halfway there.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 24)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">admission control — practice until automatic</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Verify what's actually enabled BEFORE touching any flag (do this FIRST — §24.5)</span>
