            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 24 — Lab Q&amp;A (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">11 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between a MutatingWebhookConfiguration and a ValidatingWebhookConfiguration?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>MutatingWebhooks run FIRST and can MODIFY the incoming object (e.g., inject sidecars, add default securityContext). ValidatingWebhooks run SECOND and can only ACCEPT or REJECT the request. Both are called via HTTP POST to external webhook servers with JSON payloads containing the admission review.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does failurePolicy: Fail vs failurePolicy: Ignore mean for an admission webhook, and which is more secure?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Fail:</strong> If the webhook server is unreachable, the admission request is REJECTED (fail-closed). More secure — prevents bypass by taking down the webhook. <strong>Ignore:</strong> If the webhook server is unreachable, the admission request proceeds as if the webhook doesn't exist (fail-open). Risky — an attacker could DoS the webhook server to bypass all its policies. Always use Fail for security-critical webhooks.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span><div class="eq-question">Write a ValidatingWebhookConfiguration that intercepts ALL pod CREATE/UPDATE operations and sends them to a webhook service <code>opa.default.svc</code> on <code>/v1/admit</code>.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingWebhookConfiguration
metadata: {name: opa-validator}
webhooks:
- name: opa.default.svc
  rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    apiVersions: ["v1"]
    operations: ["CREATE", "UPDATE"]
    resources: ["pods"]
    scope: Namespaced
  clientConfig:
    service:
      name: opa
      namespace: default
      path: /v1/admit
      port: 443
    caBundle: &lt;base64-encoded-CA-cert&gt;
  admissionReviewVersions: ["v1"]
  sideEffects: None
  failurePolicy: Fail</code></pre><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the baseline webhook configuration for CKS. Key fields: <code>rules</code> (what to intercept), <code>clientConfig</code> (where to send), <code>failurePolicy</code> (Fail = secure), <code>sideEffects: None</code> (required for dry-run support). The CA bundle must be the base64-encoded PEM certificate of the webhook server's TLS. On the exam, you may need to create this from scratch or debug a broken one.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span><div class="eq-question">What is the <code>sideEffects</code> field and why must it be set to <code>None</code> for most admission webhooks? What happens if it's set incorrectly?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><code>sideEffects</code> declares whether the webhook has side effects beyond the admission decision. <code>None</code> means the webhook only returns an admission decision — no external API calls, no database writes. If set to <code>None</code>, the API server can call the webhook during dry-run requests (<code>--dry-run=server</code>). If set incorrectly (e.g., "None" but the webhook actually writes to a database), dry-run requests will incorrectly trigger side effects. If the webhook HAS side effects, you MUST set <code>sideEffects: Some</code> or <code>sideEffects: NoneOnDryRun</code>. The API server won't call webhooks with side effects during dry-runs.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>sideEffects</code> is a safety mechanism. On the CKS exam, webhooks that only validate (like OPA) should have <code>sideEffects: None</code>. Webhooks that log to external systems should have <code>sideEffects: Some</code>. Incorrect declaration causes unexpected behavior with dry-run or causes the API server to reject the webhook configuration entirely.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span><div class="eq-question">What's the <code>namespaceSelector</code> in a webhook config, and how would you use it to only apply a validating webhook to namespaces labeled <code>env: prod</code>?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-yaml">webhooks:
- name: prod-validator.default.svc
  namespaceSelector:
    matchLabels:
      env: prod
  # OR exclude specific namespaces:
  namespaceSelector:
    matchExpressions:
    - key: kubernetes.io/metadata.name
      operator: NotIn
      values: [kube-system, kube-public]</code></pre><p><code>namespaceSelector</code> limits which namespaces the webhook applies to. Without it, the webhook applies to ALL namespaces. On the exam, use this to scope webhooks to production namespaces and exclude system namespaces.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Scoping is critical for production use. A webhook that validates all pods in all namespaces can break system components (kube-system pods may not meet your policies). Always exclude <code>kube-system</code> and <code>kube-public</code> from custom webhooks unless you specifically need to validate them.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span><div class="eq-question">Explain the order of execution: built-in admission plugins, mutating webhooks, and validating webhooks. What happens if a mutating webhook modifies a field that a validating webhook checks?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Order: (1) Built-in MUTATING admission plugins (e.g., NamespaceLifecycle, ServiceAccount), (2) External MUTATING webhooks (alphabetically by name), (3) Built-in VALIDATING admission plugins (e.g., PodSecurity), (4) External VALIDATING webhooks (alphabetically by name). If a mutating webhook modifies a field, the validating webhook sees the MODIFIED object. This is intentional: validating webhooks validate the final state, including mutations. If you don't want a mutation to affect validation, validate the field in a MUTATING webhook (which runs before validating).</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This ordering is critical for debugging. If your OPA policy is rejecting a pod that looks fine in the original YAML, check if a mutating webhook (like Istio sidecar injection) modified the pod before OPA saw it. Use <code>kubectl get pod -o yaml</code> to see the final mutated spec. On the CKS exam, understand that mutations happen before validations.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span><div class="eq-question">A webhook with <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> is causing ALL pod creations to fail because the webhook server is down. How do you recover without deleting the webhook permanently?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-bash"># Option 1: Patch failurePolicy to Ignore temporarily
kubectl patch validatingwebhookconfiguration &lt;name&gt; --type=json \
  -p='[{"op":"replace","path":"/webhooks/0/failurePolicy","value":"Ignore"}]'
# Fix the webhook server, then change back to Fail

# Option 2: Delete the webhook (save the YAML first)
kubectl get validatingwebhookconfiguration &lt;name&gt; -o yaml > webhook-backup.yaml
kubectl delete validatingwebhookconfiguration &lt;name&gt;
# Fix the server, then reapply: kubectl apply -f webhook-backup.yaml</code></pre><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a real production incident scenario. When a security webhook fails, it takes down the entire admission chain. Option 1 (patch to Ignore) is faster and preserves the webhook config. Option 2 (delete + reapply) is safer because you can verify the webhook config is intact before reapplying. On the CKS exam, you may face a cluster where "nothing can be created" — check webhook status first.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span><div class="eq-question">What is the ImagePolicyWebhook admission plugin and how does it differ from a ValidatingWebhookConfiguration for image validation?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>ImagePolicyWebhook:</strong> A BUILT-IN admission plugin specifically for image validation. Enabled via <code>--enable-admission-plugins=...,ImagePolicyWebhook</code> with <code>--admission-control-config-file</code> pointing to a config file. It sends image attributes (name, tag, digest) to an external webhook that approves/denies. <strong>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration:</strong> A general-purpose external webhook that can validate ANY resource, including pods and their images. More flexible but requires more setup. ImagePolicyWebhook is simpler if you ONLY need image validation; a ValidatingWebhookConfiguration is better if you need complex pod-level policies.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a CKS distinction. ImagePolicyWebhook is a single-purpose, built-in plugin for approving images — and per §24.5, it is NOT enabled by default, unlike most of the other security-relevant plugins. On the exam, you may be asked to configure either — know both exist and when to use each.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span><div class="eq-question">You have 3 validating webhooks and 2 mutating webhooks. A pod creation request arrives. In what order are they called, and what happens if webhook #2 rejects the pod after webhook #1 already mutated it?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Order: Mutating webhooks #1, #2 (alphabetically) → Validating webhooks #1, #2, #3 (alphabetically). If validating webhook #2 rejects the pod: (1) The pod is NOT created. (2) The mutation from mutating webhooks is discarded — it never reaches etcd. (3) The API server returns the rejection error to the client. (4) Other validating webhooks (#3) are NOT called (short-circuit on first rejection). (5) The client can fix the issue and resubmit — the mutating webhooks will run again on the new request. Mutations only persist if the request is ultimately accepted.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the complete admission flow. Key insight: mutations are "tentative" until the request is accepted. If a later validating webhook rejects, the mutations are rolled back. Also, validating webhooks short-circuit — the first rejection stops the chain. On the CKS exam, this ordering is tested when debugging why a pod modification isn't taking effect.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span><div class="eq-question">Write a MutatingWebhookConfiguration that injects a <code>seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault</code> into every pod in namespace "secure" that doesn't already have one. What's the advantage of mutation over validation for this use case?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: MutatingWebhookConfiguration
metadata: {name: seccomp-injector}
webhooks:
- name: seccomp.default.svc
  namespaceSelector:
    matchLabels:
      security: strict
  rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    operations: ["CREATE"]
    resources: ["pods"]
  clientConfig:
    service:
      name: seccomp-webhook
      namespace: default
    caBundle: &lt;ca-cert&gt;
  failurePolicy: Ignore  # Don't block pods if webhook is down
  sideEffects: None</code></pre><p><strong>Advantage of mutation:</strong> Developers don't need to remember to add Seccomp — the webhook adds it automatically. Validation would REJECT pods without Seccomp, creating friction. Mutation is transparent and ensures compliance without developer effort. Use mutation for defaults, validation for hard requirements.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Mutation vs validation is a key design choice. Mutate when you want to AUTO-APPLY a security setting. Validate when you want to ENFORCE that the developer explicitly sets it (or a specific value). On the CKS exam, use mutation for security defaults (Seccomp, securityContext) and validation for hard policies (no privileged pods, must use specific registry).</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q11</span><div class="eq-question">A stock cluster (no <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> flag set at all) already runs PodSecurity, ServiceAccount, and ValidatingAdmissionPolicy. (a) Do you need to explicitly enable any of these three? (b) Your Kyverno mutate rule adds a label to every pod, and an older custom mutating webhook reacts to that label by adding an annotation. Will the Kyverno rule ever get a chance to see that annotation and react to it?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>(a) No.</strong> All three are enabled by default on a stock kube-apiserver — verify first with <code>ps aux | grep kube-apiserver | grep admission-plugins</code> instead of wasting exam time re-declaring them (and risking dropping a hand-added plugin, since the flag is a complete list, not additive). <strong>(b) Yes.</strong> Per the re-validation rule (§24.5), if ANY mutating webhook changes the object, the API server RE-RUNS every previously-called mutating webhook again against the newly mutated object. So after the older webhook adds its annotation, Kyverno's mutate rule runs a second time and CAN react to that annotation.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This question combines both 24.5 facts: don't fight defaults, and design for re-invocation. Because mutating webhooks can be re-run more than once per request, every mutating webhook must be idempotent — setting/checking a value rather than blindly appending to it — or the second pass produces a different, often duplicated, result.</p></div></details></div>
            </div>

