                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏛️</div>
                        <h5>Kyverno (24.4)</h5>
                        <p><strong>Pure YAML. Validate/Mutate/Generate/VerifyImages.</strong> Auto-create NetworkPolicies for new namespaces. PolicyReport CRDs for compliance. CLI testing with kyverno test.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📖</div>
                        <h5>Official Facts (24.5)</h5>
                        <p><strong>Verify before you enable.</strong> ~8 security plugins already default-on; NodeRestriction &amp; ImagePolicyWebhook are not. Mutating webhooks RE-RUN after any mutation — design them idempotent. Any single denial fails the whole request.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> the CKS exam tests this chapter five distinct ways. (1) <strong>Enable admission plugins</strong> — edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>, modify <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> as a complete list, verify with <code>kubectl describe</code> or <code>ps aux</code>. (2) <strong>Configure a ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</strong> — know <code>clientConfig.service</code>, <code>caBundle</code>, <code>failurePolicy</code>, <code>namespaceSelector</code>, <code>objectSelector</code>. (3) <strong>Write an OPA ConstraintTemplate in Rego</strong> — the <code>violation[&#123;"msg": msg&#125;]</code> pattern, iterating with <code>containers[_]</code>, reading from <code>input.review.object</code>. (4) <strong>Write a Kyverno ClusterPolicy in YAML</strong> — <code>validate.deny.conditions</code>, JMESPath templating like <code>&#123;&#123; request.object.spec.containers[].image &#125;&#125;</code>, <code>mutate.patchStrategicMerge</code>. (5) <strong>Recite the official defaults and the re-validation rule</strong> — don't re-enable what's already on, and remember mutating webhooks can fire more than once per request.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> mutating always runs before validating, mutations are visible to every validator, and if a mutating webhook times out under <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code>, the request is denied before a single validator ever runs. Everything else in this chapter — Gatekeeper, Kyverno, the plugin catalog — is built on top of that one ordering guarantee.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
