            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 25 · Static Analysis — Scanning Manifests Before Deployment</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Admission controllers (Ch24) catch bad configurations at the <strong>API server gate</strong>. But by the time a manifest reaches the API server, developers have already written it, reviewed it, merged it, and deployed it. The best security is <strong>prevention at the source</strong>: catching misconfigurations the moment a developer writes them — in their IDE, in their pull request, before they ever reach a cluster. This is <strong>Shift Left Security</strong>: moving security checks as close to the developer as possible. Static analysis tools — <strong>kube-linter</strong>, <strong>kubesec</strong>, and <strong>conftest</strong> — scan Kubernetes YAML manifests for security issues without ever touching a live cluster. They find privileged containers, missing securityContexts, writable root filesystems, and images with floating tags before they become production incidents. <strong>The cheapest vulnerability to fix is the one that never reaches production.</strong></p>
