            <section class="section-block" id="s25-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.1 Why Static Analysis of YAML Matters — Shift Left Security</h3>
                <p>Every Kubernetes security incident begins with a YAML file. A developer writes a Deployment, sets <code>privileged: true</code> "just to make it work," commits it, and six months later that privileged container is the entry point for a cluster-wide breach. <strong>In February 2018, Tesla's Kubernetes cluster was breached</strong> because an unprotected dashboard allowed attackers to deploy crypto-mining pods — a misconfiguration that static analysis would have caught instantly. Static analysis catches these mistakes <strong>before they become incidents</strong>. Unlike admission controllers (which catch problems at deploy time) and runtime detection (which catches problems after they're exploited), static analysis works at the <strong>earliest possible moment</strong> — when the YAML is written.</p>

