                <h4>Static Analysis vs Admission Control — Complementary, Not Competing</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Static Analysis (Ch25)</th><th>Admission Control (Ch24)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>When</strong></td><td>Before commit, during CI/CD</td><td>At kubectl apply time</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Where</strong></td><td>Developer machine, CI runner</td><td>API server</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>What it sees</strong></td><td>YAML files on disk</td><td>Live API requests</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Can modify?</strong></td><td>No (analysis only)</td><td>Yes (mutating webhooks)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Cluster required?</strong></td><td>No (works offline)</td><td>Yes (must reach API server)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>False negatives?</strong></td><td>Possible (e.g., Helm template not rendered)</td><td>Very rare (sees final rendered object)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Developer feedback, CI/CD gates</td><td>Enforcement, default injection</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Strategy</strong></td><td><strong>Shift LEFT:</strong> Catch early</td><td><strong>Shift RIGHT:</strong> Enforce at gate</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align:center;font-size:0.85rem;color:#8b949e;margin-top:0.5rem;"><strong>BOTH are essential.</strong> Static analysis catches problems the developer can fix in seconds. Admission control catches problems that slip through CI/CD. Together they form a defense-in-depth strategy: <strong>catch early, enforce always.</strong></p>

