            <section class="section-block" id="s25-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.2 kube-linter — Kubernetes YAML Security Analysis</h3>
                <p><strong>kube-linter</strong> (by StackRox, now Red Hat) is a static analysis tool that checks Kubernetes YAML manifests against <strong>30+ built-in security checks</strong>. It's designed to be fast, opinionated, and CI/CD-friendly. Unlike kubesec (which gives a numeric score), kube-linter produces a simple <strong>pass/fail</strong> result for each check with actionable error messages.</p>

                <h4>How kube-linter Works Internally — Template Engine + Object Traversal</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kube-linter Architecture — From YAML File to Lint Result</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    KUBE-LINTER INTERNALS                               │
  │                                                                       │
  │  STEP 1: YAML PARSE + OBJECT DETECTION                                │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  kube-linter reads easypj-deployment.yaml                     │    │
  │  │  → Detects: Kind=Deployment, apiVersion=apps/v1              │    │
  │  │  → Parses the full object tree into memory                    │    │
  │  │  → Identifies: Pod template, containers, volumes, etc.       │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                         │                                             │
  │                         ▼                                             │
  │  STEP 2: CHECK REGISTRY — Which checks apply to this object?         │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  For Object: Deployment → applies to:                          │    │
  │  │  • Deployment-level checks (host-network, host-pid, etc.)     │    │
  │  │  • Pod template checks (container-level)                      │    │
  │  │  • Container-level checks (privileged, run-as-non-root, etc.) │    │
  │  │                                                                 │    │
  │  │  Each check is a Go template or Rego rule that traverses       │    │
  │  │  the object tree looking for specific patterns.                │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                         │                                             │
  │                         ▼                                             │
  │  STEP 3: CHECK EXECUTION — Run each matching check                    │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  check: privileged-container                                   │    │
  │  │    target: containers[].securityContext.privileged             │    │
  │  │    logic: if value == true → FAIL                              │    │
  │  │    result: container "easypj" has privileged=true → ❌         │    │
  │  │                                                                 │    │
  │  │  check: run-as-non-root                                         │    │
  │  │    target: containers[].securityContext.runAsNonRoot            │    │
  │  │    logic: if not set or false → FAIL                            │    │
  │  │    result: runAsNonRoot not set → ❌                             │    │
  │  │                                                                 │    │
  │  │  check: latest-tag                                              │    │
  │  │    target: containers[].image                                   │    │
  │  │    logic: if image ends with :latest or has no tag → FAIL       │    │
  │  │    result: image "easypj-django:latest" → ❌                    │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>kube-linter Built-in Checks — The Most Important Ones for CKS</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Check Name</th><th>What It Detects</th><th>Severity</th><th>Why It Matters</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>privileged-container</strong></td><td>Container has <code>securityContext.privileged: true</code></td><td style="color:#326ce5;">Critical</td><td>Privileged containers have ALL Linux capabilities and access to all host devices. Equivalent to root on the node.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>no-read-only-root-filesystem</strong></td><td><code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code> is not set or is false</td><td style="color:#f97316;">High</td><td>Writable root FS allows attackers to install tools, write scripts, and modify binaries after exploitation.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>run-as-non-root</strong></td><td><code>runAsNonRoot</code> is not set to true</td><td style="color:#f97316;">High</td><td>Containers running as root (UID 0) can escape the container via kernel exploits or misconfigured volumes.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>host-network</strong></td><td>Pod has <code>hostNetwork: true</code></td><td style="color:#f97316;">High</td><td>Host network access bypasses all NetworkPolicies and exposes pod to host interfaces.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>host-pid</strong></td><td>Pod has <code>hostPID: true</code></td><td style="color:#f97316;">High</td><td>Host PID namespace access allows listing and signaling all processes on the node.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>latest-tag</strong></td><td>Container image uses <code>:latest</code> tag</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">Medium</td><td>Floating tags are mutable — you don't know which image version is actually running.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>dangling-service</strong></td><td>Service selector matches no pods</td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">Medium</td><td>Indicates a misconfiguration — the Service has no backends.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Running kube-linter — Local, CI/CD, Pre-Commit, and Helm</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — kube-linter commands for all scanning scenarios</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Install ──
brew install kube-linter
# Or: curl -LO https://github.com/stackrox/kube-linter/releases/latest/download/kube-linter-linux

# ── Scan a single YAML file ──
kube-linter lint easypj-deployment.yaml

# ── Scan all YAML files in a directory ──
kube-linter lint ./k8s-manifests/

# ── Scan a Helm chart ──
kube-linter lint ./easypj-helm-chart/

# ── Scan with specific checks ──
kube-linter lint --include privileged-container,run-as-non-root ./manifests/
kube-linter lint --exclude latest-tag ./manifests/

# ── JSON output for CI/CD ──
kube-linter lint --format json ./manifests/ > kube-linter-report.json</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pre-Commit Hook — Catch Issues Before They Leave Your Machine</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — .pre-commit-config.yaml — Run kube-linter on every git commit</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># .pre-commit-config.yaml
repos:
- repo: local
  hooks:
  - id: kube-linter
    name: kube-linter
    entry: kube-linter lint
    language: system
    files: \.(yaml|yml)$
    pass_filenames: false    # Lint all k8s/ files, not just changed ones
    args: ['k8s/']

# Setup:
# pip install pre-commit
# pre-commit install
# Now EVERY git commit runs kube-linter.
# If it finds issues → commit is BLOCKED.</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>🔴 Example: What kube-linter Catches in an Insecure easypj Deployment</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left">
                        <div class="panel-header" style="color:#326ce5;">INSECURE MANIFEST — easypj-deployment.yaml</div>
<pre>
