            <section class="section-block" id="s25-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.3 kubesec — Security Risk Analysis for K8s Manifests</h3>
                <p>While kube-linter gives you pass/fail checks, <strong>kubesec</strong> (by ControlPlane) gives you a <strong>numeric risk score from 0 to 10</strong>. Each check contributes or subtracts points, and the final score represents the overall security posture of your manifest. This is invaluable for <strong>measuring security improvement over time</strong>: "Last month our deployment scored 3/10. After adding securityContexts, it's 7/10."</p>

                <h4>kubesec Scoring Algorithm — How Points Are Calculated</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubesec Scoring Breakdown — What Adds and Subtracts Points</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    KUBESEC SCORING MODEL (0-10)                            │
  │                                                                           │
  │  SCORE = SUM(points) / MAX_POSSIBLE_POINTS * 10                           │
  │                                                                           │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │  CHECK                          │  POINTS  │  CRITICALITY          │  │
  │  ├─────────────────────────────────┼──────────┼───────────────────────┤  │
  │  │  containers[].securityContext   │          │                        │  │
  │  │    .runAsNonRoot = true         │  +1      │  CRITICAL (blocks LPE) │  │
  │  │    .readOnlyRootFilesystem=true │  +1      │  HIGH (blocks writes)  │  │
  │  │    .allowPrivilegeEscalation=fls│  +1      │  HIGH (blocks setuid)  │  │
  │  │    .privileged = false          │  +1      │  CRITICAL              │  │
  │  │    .capabilities.drop = [ALL]   │  +1      │  CRITICAL              │  │
  │  │    .capabilities.add = []       │  +1      │  HIGH                  │  │
  │  │    .seccompProfile = set        │  +1      │  HIGH                  │  │
  │  │    .appArmorProfile = set       │  +1      │  MEDIUM                │  │
  │  ├─────────────────────────────────┼──────────┼───────────────────────┤  │
  │  │  DEDUCTIONS (negative points):  │          │                        │  │
  │  │    privileged = true            │  -5      │  IMMEDIATE FAIL        │  │
  │  │    allowPrivilegeEscalation=true│  -3      │  CRITICAL              │  │
  │  │    runAsNonRoot = false         │  -3      │  CRITICAL              │  │
  │  │    capabilities.add includes    │          │                        │  │
  │  │      SYS_ADMIN or NET_ADMIN     │  -3      │  HIGH                  │  │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  │                                                                           │
  │  SCORE INTERPRETATION:                                                    │
  │  ─────────────────────                                                    │
  │  0-3:  🔴 CRITICAL — Do not deploy. Missing essential controls.           │
  │  4-5:  🟠 HIGH — Only dev/staging. Needs improvement.                     │
  │  6-7:  🟡 MEDIUM — Acceptable for production with monitoring.             │
  │  8-9:  🟢 LOW — Good security posture.                                    │
  │  10:   ✅ IDEAL — All recommended controls. Rare.                         │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Running kubesec — CLI, HTTP Server, and CI/CD Integration</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — kubesec commands, HTTP server mode, and CI/CD score gating</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── CLI: Scan a manifest file ──
kubesec scan easypj-deployment.yaml

# ── CLI: Pipe from kubectl or helm ──
kubectl get deployment easypj-django -n easypj -o yaml | kubesec scan -
helm template ./easypj-chart | kubesec scan -

# ── HTTP SERVER MODE: Run kubesec as a service (for CI/CD) ──
kubesec server --port 8080 &
curl -sSX POST --data-binary @easypj-deployment.yaml \
  http://localhost:8080/scan | jq .

# ── CI/CD SCORE GATE ──
SCORE=$(kubesec scan easypj-deployment.yaml | jq -r '.[0].score')
if [ "$SCORE" -lt 4 ]; then
  echo "❌ Score $SCORE below threshold (≥4 required)"
  exit 1
fi

# ── TRACK SCORE OVER TIME: Save score as CI artifact ──
kubesec scan k8s/*.yaml | jq '[.[] | {object: .object, score: .score}]' \
  > kubesec-scores-$(date +%Y%m%d).json
# Archive this artifact — track score trends across builds</code></pre>
