            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Static analysis is the cheapest security check that exists</strong> — it runs before a cluster is ever involved, at $0 cost in the IDE vs $10,000+ once a breach is detected in production (25.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>kube-linter, kubesec, and conftest are complementary, not competing.</strong> kube-linter gives fast pass/fail checks with zero config; kubesec gives a trackable 0-10 score; conftest lets you enforce your own organization-specific Rego policies (25.2-25.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>kubesec's scoring is additive and asymmetric</strong> — most controls are worth +1, but <code>privileged: true</code> alone costs -5, and unsafe capabilities or a root user cost -3 each. Fixing the biggest deduction first gets you to a passing score fastest (25.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>conftest and OPA Gatekeeper (Ch 24) share Rego but not an input path</strong> — conftest reads the file on disk directly, Gatekeeper reads an AdmissionReview wrapping the object; the same policy logic needs a different <code>input</code> path in each (25.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Real pipelines chain four gates</strong> — YAML security (kube-linter + kubesec + conftest) → Dockerfile security (hadolint) → image scan (Trivy) → image signing (cosign) — and any single gate failing stops the manifest from ever reaching the cluster (25.5).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>conftest input path vs Gatekeeper input path:</strong> conftest evaluates YAML files directly, so the Rego input is the raw YAML structure: <code>input.spec.containers[_].securityContext</code> (or <code>input.spec.template.spec.containers[_]</code> for a Deployment). Gatekeeper receives the AdmissionReview object, so: <code>input.review.object.spec.containers[_].securityContext</code>. Same Rego language, different input paths.</li>
                    <li><strong>kubesec score is not absolute:</strong> A score of 7/10 might pass your threshold but still have dangerous configurations. Always check WHY points were deducted, not just the final score.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Run all three tools in one command chain during practice</strong> — <code>kube-linter lint f.yaml && kubesec scan f.yaml && conftest test f.yaml -p policies/</code> — so scanning becomes one muscle-memory action instead of three separate ones (25.2-25.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Fix the biggest kubesec deduction first, then rescan.</strong> Removing <code>privileged: true</code> alone can move a score more than every other fix combined — don't spend exam time chasing +1 checks before the -5 one is gone (25.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Keep a Rego snippet template memorized for both input shapes</strong> — <code>input.spec.template.spec.containers[_]</code> for a Deployment, <code>input.spec.containers[_]</code> for a bare Pod — so you don't lose time guessing which one conftest expects (25.4).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

