            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 25 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write an OPA ConstraintTemplate in Rego that blocks pods without CPU limits.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <pre><code class="language-rego">package k8srequirecpulimits
violation[{"msg": msg}] {
  container := input.review.object.spec.containers[_]
  not container.resources.limits.cpu
  msg := sprintf("container %v has no CPU limit", [container.name])
}</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between conftest and Gatekeeper? When would you use each?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Conftest</strong> runs Rego policies against YAML files in CI/CD (pre-deployment). <strong>Gatekeeper</strong> runs Rego policies as an admission controller inside Kubernetes (at deployment time). Use conftest to catch violations BEFORE they reach the cluster (shift-left). Use Gatekeeper to enforce policies at the admission gate (defense-in-depth). Both use the same Rego language. Conftest input path: <code>input.spec.template.spec...</code>. Gatekeeper input path: <code>input.review.object.spec...</code>.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a Gatekeeper ConstraintTemplate + Constraint that requires all pods to have <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>. Include both resources.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># ConstraintTemplate
apiVersion: templates.gatekeeper.sh/v1
kind: ConstraintTemplate
metadata: {name: k8srequirednonroot}
spec:
  crd:
    spec:
      names: {kind: K8sRequiredNonRoot}
  targets:
  - target: admission.k8s.gatekeeper.sh
    rego: |
      package k8srequirednonroot
      violation[{"msg": msg}] {
        container := input.review.object.spec.containers[_]
        not container.securityContext.runAsNonRoot == true
        msg := sprintf("container %v must set runAsNonRoot: true", [container.name])
      }
---
# Constraint
apiVersion: constraints.gatekeeper.sh/v1beta1
kind: K8sRequiredNonRoot
metadata: {name: require-run-as-non-root}
spec:
  match:
    kinds: [{apiGroups: [""], kinds: ["Pod"]}]</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Gatekeeper uses a two-resource model: ConstraintTemplate (defines the Rego policy logic) and Constraint (applies the policy with parameters). This separation allows reuse — one ConstraintTemplate can have multiple Constraints with different parameters. On the CKS exam, you create the template first, then the constraint.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's kubesec and what does a score of 7 mean? What specific checks does it perform on a pod spec?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>kubesec scores pod security from 0-10. A score of 7 falls in the 6-7 "Medium — acceptable for production with monitoring" band: good security, but room to improve. It checks: <code>runAsNonRoot</code>, <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code>, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation</code>, <code>capabilities.drop</code>/<code>add</code>, <code>seccompProfile</code>, <code>appArmorProfile</code>, and <code>privileged</code>. Each passing check is worth +1. Critical deductions: <code>privileged: true</code> is -5 (immediate critical fail), <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: true</code> is -3, <code>runAsNonRoot: false</code> is -3, and adding <code>SYS_ADMIN</code>/<code>NET_ADMIN</code> capabilities is -3. A score of 10 requires ALL recommended controls enabled — rare in practice.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>kubesec is a quick security scoring tool for CI/CD. On the CKS exam, you may be asked to scan a pod spec and identify what's causing a low score. The scoring breakdown (§25.3) tells you exactly which fields to add. A score of 0-3 is critical/do-not-deploy; 8 and above is a good security posture.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a Rego policy for conftest that blocks pods using the <code>:latest</code> tag.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-rego">package main
deny[msg] {
  container := input.spec.containers[_]
  endswith(container.image, ":latest")
  msg := sprintf("container %v uses :latest tag", [container.name])
}
deny[msg] {
  container := input.spec.containers[_]
  not contains(container.image, ":")
  msg := sprintf("container %v has no tag (defaults to :latest)", [container.name])
}</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>:latest tags are mutable — they can change without notice, bypassing image scanning and signing. The second rule catches images with NO tag, which also default to :latest. On the CKS exam, always enforce specific tags or digests. Use conftest in CI/CD to catch this before deployment.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's kube-linter and how does it differ from Trivy? Which one scans live clusters vs YAML files?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>kube-linter:</strong> Scans Kubernetes YAML manifests for security misconfigurations and best practices. Runs in CI/CD or against files. Checks: securityContext, resource limits, liveness probes, etc. <strong>Trivy:</strong> Scans container IMAGES for known vulnerabilities (CVEs) and also scans YAML configs. kube-linter is YAML-only; Trivy is image + YAML. kube-linter focuses on Kubernetes best practices; Trivy focuses on vulnerability databases. Use BOTH: kube-linter for config hygiene, Trivy for CVE detection.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>These tools are complementary. kube-linter catches "this pod runs as root" (config error). Trivy catches "nginx:1.25 has CVE-2024-XXXX" (vulnerability). On the CKS exam, you may use both — scan the manifest with kube-linter, scan the image with Trivy.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a Gatekeeper ConstraintTemplate that blocks pods from using any container image registry EXCEPT <code>easypj.azurecr.io</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: templates.gatekeeper.sh/v1
kind: ConstraintTemplate
metadata: {name: k8sallowedrepos}
spec:
  crd:
    spec:
      names: {kind: K8sAllowedRepos}
      validation:
        openAPIV3Schema:
          properties:
            repos:
              type: array
              items: {type: string}
  targets:
  - target: admission.k8s.gatekeeper.sh
    rego: |
      package k8sallowedrepos
      violation[{"msg": msg}] {
        container := input.review.object.spec.containers[_]
        not startswith(container.image, input.parameters.repos[_])
        msg := sprintf("container %v uses disallowed registry: %v", [container.name, container.image])
      }</code></pre>
                    <p>Constraint: <code>parameters: {repos: ["easypj.azurecr.io/"]}</code></p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Registry allowlisting is a supply chain security control. By restricting images to your private registry, you ensure: (1) all images have been scanned before being pushed to the registry, (2) no one can deploy images directly from Docker Hub, (3) every image is signed by your CI pipeline. On the CKS exam, this is a common Gatekeeper policy task.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You scan a pod YAML with kubesec and get 1/10. The deductions show: privileged:true (-5), runAsNonRoot:false (-3), capabilities.add includes SYS_ADMIN (-3). Write the corrected pod securityContext to achieve 9+/10.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
  capabilities:
    drop: ["ALL"]
  seccompProfile:
    type: RuntimeDefault
# Remove: privileged: true, and any added SYS_ADMIN/NET_ADMIN capabilities</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the CKS pattern: start with the kubesec deductions (§25.3), fix each one, rescan. <code>privileged: true</code> alone is the single biggest deduction (-5) and can take a score from a possible 10 down near 1. Removing it and the unsafe capability, then adding runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem, drop ALL, and a seccompProfile — each worth +1 — pushes the corrected manifest to 9+/10. On the exam, iteratively fix and rescan rather than guessing at the full fix in one pass.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does conftest integrate into a CI/CD pipeline? Write the pipeline step that fails the build if ANY Rego policy violation is found.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># In CI/CD (GitHub Actions / Jenkins)
conftest test deployment.yaml --policy policies/ --fail-on-warn
# --fail-on-warn: Fails if even a warning-level violation is found
# Exit code 1 = policy violation, pipeline stops

# Test multiple files
conftest test k8s-manifests/*.yaml --policy policies/

# Structured output for reporting
conftest test deployment.yaml --policy policies/ --output json | \
  jq '.[] | select(.failures | length > 0)'</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>CI/CD integration is the "shift-left" approach. conftest should run BEFORE any image is built or deployed. If policies fail, the pipeline stops. On the CKS exam, the supply chain security domain (20%) heavily tests CI/CD integration — know how to add security gates to pipelines.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Compare OPA Gatekeeper vs Kyverno for admission control. What are the key differences in architecture, language, and use case?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse;"><thead><tr style="border-bottom: 2px solid #326ce5;"><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Aspect</th><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">OPA Gatekeeper</th><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Kyverno</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">Language</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Rego (declarative policy language)</td><td style="padding: 8px;">YAML (Kubernetes-native)</td></tr><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">Architecture</td><td style="padding: 8px;">ConstraintTemplate + Constraint</td><td style="padding: 8px;">ClusterPolicy (single resource)</td></tr><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">Learning curve</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Higher (Rego syntax)</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Lower (YAML, K8s-like)</td></tr><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">Mutation</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Separate product (OPA, not Gatekeeper)</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Built-in (generate/mutate rules)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding: 8px;">CKS Exam</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Gatekeeper is more commonly referenced</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Growing in popularity</td></tr></tbody></table>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Both are CNCF projects and valid CKS answers. Gatekeeper is more mature with a larger ecosystem. Kyverno is simpler (no Rego required) and supports mutation natively. On the CKS exam: if the question mentions "Rego" or "ConstraintTemplate," the answer is Gatekeeper. If it mentions "ClusterPolicy" or "YAML-based policies," it's Kyverno. Know both architectures.</p></div>
