                <h4>How Image Signing Works — Cryptography in Plain English</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Generate Key Pair</strong><p><code>cosign generate-key-pair</code> creates an ECDSA-P256 private key (kept SECRET, used only in CI/CD) and a public key (shared with Kyverno for verification).</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>Sign the Image Digest</strong><p><code>cosign sign --key cosign.key registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2</code> resolves the tag to an immutable digest (sha256:abc...), hashes the manifest, signs with the private key, and stores the .sig OCI artifact.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>Verify at Admission</strong><p>Kyverno fetches the .sig from registry, verifies it against the configured public key. If the signature is cryptographically valid → ALLOW. If missing or invalid → DENY.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>Transparency (Keyless)</strong><p>With keyless signing, the signing event is also recorded in Rekor's public transparency log — providing an immutable, auditable record of who signed what and when.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>The in-toto Attestation Framework — Beyond Signatures</h4>
                <p>Image signatures answer "who signed this image?" But <strong>in-toto attestations</strong> answer deeper questions: "How was this built? From what source? Using which builder? With what dependencies?" Cosign supports in-toto attestations, allowing you to attach <strong>SLSA provenance</strong>, vulnerability scan results, and SBOM references as signed metadata alongside the image signature:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">in-toto Attestation Model — Predicates Signed by the Builder</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    IN-TOTO ATTESTATION FRAMEWORK                       │
  │                                                                       │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  ATTESTATION = Envelope {                                      │    │
  │  │    subject: [{                                                 │    │
  │  │      name: "registry.easypj.com/easypj",                      │    │
  │  │      digest: { sha256: "abc123..." }                          │    │
  │  │    }],                                                          │    │
  │  │    predicateType: "https://slsa.dev/provenance/v0.2",         │    │
  │  │    predicate: {                                                 │    │
  │  │      builder: { id: "https://github.com/easysj/easypj/..." },  │    │
  │  │      buildType: "https://github.com/actions/runner@v1",       │    │
  │  │      invocation: {                                              │    │
  │  │        configSource: { uri: "git+https://github.com/...",     │    │
  │  │          digest: { sha1: "def456..." },                        │    │
  │  │          entryPoint: ".github/workflows/build.yml" }           │    │
  │  │      },                                                         │    │
  │  │      materials: [{ uri: "git+https://github.com/easysj/easypj",│    │
  │  │        digest: { sha1: "def456..." } }]                         │    │
  │  │    }                                                            │    │
  │  │  }                                                              │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                                                                       │
  │  🔑 ATTESTATIONS PROVE:                                               │
  │  • WHO built it (builder identity)                                    │
  │  • HOW it was built (build type, entry point)                        │
  │  • FROM WHAT source (git repo + commit SHA)                          │
  │  • WITH WHAT materials (dependencies, base images)                   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>SBOM in Practice — The Log4Shell Wake-Up Call</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header"><span class="scenario-badge danger">REAL-WORLD EVENT</span><span class="scenario-title">Dec 2021 — Log4Shell (CVE-2021-44228, CVSS 10.0)</span></div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>CVE Published</strong><p>Remote code execution in Log4j 2.x via JNDI lookups. CVSS 10.0 — maximum severity.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Without SBOM</strong><p>WEEKS of manual auditing. Engineers grep Dockerfiles, Maven POMs, JAR files.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">3</div><div class="step-content"><strong>With SBOM — 30 Seconds</strong><p>SBOM generated in CI/CD. When Log4Shell hits: <code>grype sbom:image.sbom.json</code> → results in 30 seconds: "easypj:v2 — NOT affected."</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">4</div><div class="step-content"><strong>The Lesson</strong><p>SBOMs transform vulnerability response from weeks to seconds. US Executive Order 14028 now requires SBOMs for federal software. This is why cosign attests SBOMs alongside signatures — the SBOM reference above is one of the predicate types an in-toto attestation carries.</p></div></div>
                    </div>
