            <section class="section-block" id="s26-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.2 Cosign — Signing and Verifying Container Images</h3>
                <p><strong>Cosign</strong> is the flagship signing tool of the <strong>Sigstore</strong> project (CNCF-backed, open-source). It signs container images by creating digital signatures and storing them directly in the OCI registry alongside the image — no separate signature server needed. Cosign supports both key-based signing (traditional public/private key pairs) and keyless signing (OIDC identity + Rekor transparency log). It works with any OCI-compliant registry: Docker Hub, AWS ECR, GCP Artifact Registry, Azure ACR, Harbor, and more.</p>

                <h4>Cosign Key-Based Workflow — Generate, Sign, Verify</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Complete cosign key-based workflow with password protection</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# STEP 1: Generate a password-protected key pair
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
cosign generate-key-pair
# Prompts for a password — ALWAYS use one in production!
# Creates:
#   cosign.key   ← PRIVATE KEY (encrypted with password)
#   cosign.pub   ← PUBLIC KEY (give to Kyverno)

# To use the password in CI/CD non-interactively:
export COSIGN_PASSWORD="${{ secrets.COSIGN_KEY_PASSWORD }}"

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# STEP 2: Sign the image
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
cosign sign --key cosign.key registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.0.0
# What happens internally:
#   1. Resolves tag → digest (sha256:abc123...)
#   2. SHA256(manifest) → hash
#   3. ECDSA-Sign(private_key, hash) → signature
#   4. Pushes .sig as OCI artifact to registry

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# STEP 3: Verify the signature
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
cosign verify --key cosign.pub registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.0.0
# ✅ VALID: shows signature details + claims
# ❌ INVALID: "Error: no matching signatures found" (exit code 1)</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Cosign Key Rotation Strategy — Signing with Old + New Keys During Transition</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Key rotation: sign with both keys during transition period</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# KEY ROTATION STRATEGY (3-phase)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# PHASE 1: Generate new key pair (new key NOT yet trusted by Kyverno)
cosign generate-key-pair
# → cosign-v2.key, cosign-v2.pub

# PHASE 2: TRANSITION — Sign with BOTH old and new keys
cosign sign --key cosign-v1.key registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.0.1
cosign sign --key cosign-v2.key registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.0.1
# Image now has TWO valid signatures (old key + new key)

# PHASE 3: Update Kyverno to trust BOTH keys, then remove old key
# After all running pods use images signed with new key:
cosign sign --key cosign-v2.key registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.0.2
# (No longer sign with old key)
# → Remove old public key from Kyverno ClusterPolicy
# → Securely delete cosign-v1.key</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Cosign in CI/CD — GitHub Actions Integration</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — GitHub Actions: Build → Scan → Push → Sign easypj image</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">name: Build and Sign easypj
on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
jobs:
  build-sign:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      id-token: write
      contents: read
    steps:
    - uses: actions/checkout@v4
    - name: Build image
      run: docker build -t registry.easypj.com/easypj:${{ github.sha }} .
    - name: Trivy scan
      uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@master
      with:
        image-ref: registry.easypj.com/easypj:${{ github.sha }}
        exit-code: 1
        severity: CRITICAL,HIGH
    - name: Push to registry
      run: docker push registry.easypj.com/easypj:${{ github.sha }}
    - name: Install Cosign
      uses: sigstore/cosign-installer@v3
    - name: Sign image (key-based)
      run: |
        cosign sign --yes --key env://COSIGN_PRIVATE_KEY \
          registry.easypj.com/easypj:${{ github.sha }}
      env:
        COSIGN_PRIVATE_KEY: ${{ secrets.COSIGN_PRIVATE_KEY }}
        COSIGN_PASSWORD: ${{ secrets.COSIGN_KEY_PASSWORD }}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Multi-Signer Workflows — Release Manager + CI/CD Co-Signing</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Multiple signers: CI/CD key + release manager key</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># CI/CD pipeline signs the image with the CI key:
cosign sign --key ci-cosign.key registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.0.0

# Release manager manually verifies and co-signs with release key:
cosign verify --key ci-cosign.pub registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.0.0
cosign sign --key release-cosign.key registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.0.0

# Image now has TWO signatures: CI key + release key
# Kyverno policy requires BOTH signatures (count: 2):
# attestors:
# - count: 2
#   entries:
#   - keys: { publicKeys: ci-cosign.pub }
#   - keys: { publicKeys: release-cosign.pub }

# Verify both signatures exist:
cosign verify --key ci-cosign.pub registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.0.0
cosign verify --key release-cosign.pub registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.0.0</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How Cosign Stores Signatures — OCI Registry Internals</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Cosign Signature Storage — OCI Registry Layout</div>
<pre>
  registry.easypj.com/easypj
  ├── 📦 Manifest (tag: v2.0.0) → sha256:abc123def456...
  ├── sha256:abc123def456.sig    ← Signature (ECDSA over manifest hash)
  ├── sha256:abc123def456.att    ← Attestation (in-toto SLSA provenance)
  └── sha256:abc123def456.sbom   ← SBOM (SPDX/CycloneDX)

  🔑 Signatures reference digests (immutable), never tags (mutable).
     Multiple signers = multiple .sig objects, all for the same digest.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s26-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.3 Kyverno verifyImages — Enforcing Signed Images in Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>Signing images is only half the battle. The cluster must <strong>verify</strong> those signatures before running pods. Kyverno's <strong>verifyImages</strong> rule type does exactly this: at admission time, Kyverno retrieves the image's signature from the registry, verifies it against the configured public key(s), and either allows or denies the pod.</p>

                <h4>Kyverno verifyImages ClusterPolicy — Full Configuration with Key Rotation</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Kyverno ClusterPolicy with key rotation support</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-cosign-signature
spec:
  validationFailureAction: Enforce
  background: true
  rules:
  - name: check-cosign-signature
    match:
      any:
      - resources: { kinds: [Pod] }
    exclude:
      any:
      - resources: { namespaces: [kube-system, kyverno] }

    verifyImages:
    - imageReferences:
      - "registry.easypj.com/*"

      # ── Require at least 1 valid signature ──
      attestors:
      - count: 1
        entries:
        - keys:
            publicKeys: |-
              -----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----
              MFkwEwYHKoZIzj0CAQYIKoZIzj0DAQcDQgAE...
              -----END PUBLIC KEY-----
        # ── Key rotation: also accept OLD key during transition ──
        - keys:
            publicKeys: |-
              -----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----
              (previous key — remove after rotation complete)
              -----END PUBLIC KEY-----</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Verification Flow — What Kyverno Does When a Pod Is Created</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kyverno verifyImages Flow</div>
<pre>
  kubectl apply → API Server → Kyverno Webhook
  │
  ├─ STEP 1: Resolve tag → digest (sha256:abc123...)
  ├─ STEP 2: GET registry/.../sha256-abc123.sig
  │   ├── 404 → ❌ DENY: "image is not signed"
  │   └── 200 → Continue
  ├─ STEP 3: cosign verify --key easypj.pub
  │   ├── VALID ✅ → Pod allowed
  │   └── INVALID ❌ → 403: "signature validation failed"
  └─ STEP 4: (Optional) Check attestation predicates match policy
</pre>
                </div>

