            <section class="section-block" id="s26-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.4 Keyless Signing with Sigstore — The Future of Image Signing</h3>
                <p>Traditional key-based signing has a fundamental problem: <strong>private key management</strong>. You must generate, store, rotate, and protect the private key. <strong>Keyless signing</strong> (Sigstore's Fulcio + Rekor) eliminates long-lived private keys. The signer proves identity using <strong>OIDC</strong>, Fulcio issues a short-lived certificate, and Rekor records the event in an immutable transparency log.</p>

                <h4>Keyless Signing Architecture — Fulcio + Rekor + OIDC</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Keyless Signing — Ephemeral Keys + OIDC Identity + Transparency Log</div>
<pre>
  CI/CD (GitHub Actions)
  │
  ├─ 1. OIDC Token: "I am repo easysj/easypj on main branch"
  │     ↓
  ├─ 2. Fulcio CA: Verifies OIDC token → Issues 10-min X.509 cert
  │     Cert binds: identity → ephemeral public key
  │     ↓
  ├─ 3. Cosign signs image with ephemeral private key
  │     ↓
  ├─ 4. Certificate + signature → Rekor Transparency Log
  │     Immutable, public, auditable record
  │     ↓
  └─ 5. Signature (with Rekor reference) → OCI Registry

  🔑 KEYLESS = No long-lived secrets. Identity proven per-signature via OIDC.
     Compromise of CI/CD runner doesn't expose any persistent signing key.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Rekor Transparency Log — Querying and Auditing</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Querying Rekor for signature transparency records</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Search Rekor for entries related to an image ──
rekor-cli search --artifact registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.0.0

# ── Get details of a specific Rekor entry ──
rekor-cli get --uuid &lt;entry-uuid&gt;

# ── Verify the Rekor inclusion proof ──
cosign verify --key cosign.pub \
  --rekor-url https://rekor.sigstore.dev \
  registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.0.0

# ── Verify with identity + OIDC issuer (keyless) ──
cosign verify \
  --certificate-identity https://github.com/easysj/easypj/.github/workflows/build.yml@refs/heads/main \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
  registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.0.0</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Key-Based vs Keyless — Decision Framework</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Key-Based (cosign.key)</th><th>Keyless (OIDC + Fulcio + Rekor)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Key management</strong></td><td>🔴 Generate, store, rotate, revoke</td><td>🟢 No long-lived key to manage</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Key compromise</strong></td><td>🔴 Catastrophic — attacker signs anything</td><td>🟢 Minimal — no persistent key to steal</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Identity binding</strong></td><td>"Whoever has the key"</td><td>OIDC identity: repo + branch + workflow</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Transparency</strong></td><td>❌ None by default</td><td>✅ Rekor — immutable, public, auditable</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Offline/air-gapped</strong></td><td>✅ Works offline</td><td>🔴 Requires internet (Fulcio + Rekor)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CKS Exam</strong></td><td>✅ Primary — cosign sign --key</td><td>⚠️ Know concept + --keyless flag</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Air-gapped, simple, CKS exam</td><td>Production CI/CD, zero-trust, compliance</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Complete Supply Chain Attack — SolarWinds-Style Scenario</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header"><span class="scenario-badge danger">ATTACK SCENARIO</span><span class="scenario-title">How an Attacker Infiltrates the Build Pipeline Without Detection</span></div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Source Compromise</strong><p>Attacker gains access to the easypj GitHub repository via a compromised developer SSH key. They modify <code>settings.py</code> to add a secret backdoor that exfiltrates all Django SECRET_KEY values to a C2 server.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Build Poisoning</strong><p>The compromised code passes CI checks (Trivy scan, kube-linter pass). Docker build succeeds. <strong>Without signing:</strong> the poisoned image is pushed to the registry. <strong>With signing:</strong> the CI pipeline signs the image with cosign, but the SIGNATURE IS VALID because the build happened legitimately — the source was compromised, not the build.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">3</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Where Signing Helps (and Doesn't)</strong><p>Signing proves the image was BUILT by your CI pipeline — it does NOT prove the source code is clean. This is why SLSA Level 3 requires <strong>two-person review</strong> — someone must review the code before it's built. With SLSA L2+L3: (a) Provenance is signed → proves the build happened, (b) Two-person review required → prevents tampered code from being built, (c) SBOM attached → rapid CVE response when vulnerabilities are found in dependencies.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">4</div><div class="step-content"><strong>The Multi-Layer Defense</strong><p>① Code review (SLSA L3) catches malicious PR. ② If missed: cosign signature proves the build pipeline produced this image (not an attacker pushing directly). ③ If deployed: Kyverno verifyImages confirms the pod is running the signed image. ④ If exploited: SBOM enables rapid identification of affected images. ⑤ Falco detects the exfiltration at runtime. <strong>Five layers — the attacker must defeat ALL of them.</strong></p></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Cosign Internals — How Signing Works Under the Hood</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Cosign Architecture — Key-Based and Keyless Signing</div>
<pre>
  KEY-BASED SIGNING (traditional):
  ─────────────────────────────
  1. cosign generate-key-pair → cosign.key (private) + cosign.pub (public)
  2. cosign sign --key cosign.key IMAGE@sha256:abc...
     → Computes SHA-256 hash of image manifest
     → Signs hash with private key → creates .sig payload
     → Pushes .sig to registry as a separate OCI artifact
     → Tag: sha256-abc123.sig (stored alongside the image)
  3. cosign verify --key cosign.pub IMAGE@sha256:abc...
     → Downloads .sig from registry
     → Verifies signature against public key
     → Confirms: "This digest was signed by the holder of cosign.key"

  KEYLESS SIGNING (Sigstore):
  ──────────────────────────
  1. OIDC token from CI (proves: "I am repo X on branch Y")
  2. Fulcio CA issues short-lived cert (10 min) binding identity to public key
  3. Ephemeral private key signs the image digest
  4. Certificate + signature uploaded to Rekor transparency log
  5. cosign verify checks: identity matches, cert from Fulcio, entry in Rekor

  🔑 CKS EXAM: Know both methods. Key-based is simpler for the exam
     (generate key → sign → verify). Keyless is production best practice
     but requires OIDC infrastructure (GitHub Actions, GCP SA, etc.).
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

