            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Registry credentials prove push access, not authorship.</strong> Signing closes that gap — cryptographic signatures over immutable digests prove which private key produced the image, even if an attacker has valid push credentials (26.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Cosign's core workflow is three commands</strong> — <code>generate-key-pair</code> → <code>sign --key</code> → <code>verify --key</code>. Rotate keys in three phases (generate new → sign with BOTH old and new → retire the old key) rather than a hard cutover, so nothing already deployed suddenly fails verification (26.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Kyverno verifyImages turns verification from a manual step into an enforced admission gate.</strong> No .sig, or a signature that doesn't match a configured public key, means the pod is never created — not flagged after the fact (26.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Keyless signing trades a long-lived secret for a chain of trust:</strong> OIDC identity → Fulcio's 10-minute certificate → Rekor's immutable transparency log. Nothing persistent exists for an attacker to steal, even if the CI runner itself is compromised (26.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Signatures are always bound to the immutable digest, never the mutable tag.</strong> Even if an attacker repoints a tag to a malicious image after signing, that new digest has no valid signature — and signing alone still can't prove the SOURCE code was clean, only that YOUR pipeline built it.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>DIGEST, not tag:</strong> <code>cosign sign ghcr.io/easypj/api:latest</code> signs the tag, not the digest. If <code>:latest</code> changes, the signature becomes invalid. Always sign the digest: <code>cosign sign ghcr.io/easypj/api@sha256:abc...</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>cosign.key is your private key:</strong> If you lose it, you can't sign new images. If an attacker steals it, they can sign malicious images in your name. Protect it like a root CA key — use a KMS or Vault for production.</li>
                    <li><strong>OIDC token expiry:</strong> Keyless signing uses short-lived OIDC tokens. If your CI job takes too long, the token may expire before signing completes. The signature appears valid but verification fails with "token expired."</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Drill the exam task simulator until it's reflex</strong> — <code>generate-key-pair</code> → <code>sign --key</code> (digest!) → <code>verify --key</code> → keyless <code>sign</code> with no <code>--key</code> flag, letting cosign auto-detect CI OIDC (26.2, 26.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Failure mode #1 is signing a tag instead of a digest</strong> — always resolve to <code>@sha256:...</code> before signing, and never assume <code>:latest</code> or <code>:v2</code> stays pointed at the same content (26.1-26.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Failure mode #2 is losing or leaking cosign.key</strong> — treat the private key file like a root CA key: password-protect it, keep it in a secrets manager/KMS, and never commit it to Git (26.2).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

