            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 26 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Generate a cosign key pair and sign an image. Write the exact commands.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">cosign generate-key-pair  # Creates cosign.key + cosign.pub
cosign sign --key cosign.key ghcr.io/easypj/api@sha256:abc123...
cosign verify --key cosign.pub ghcr.io/easypj/api@sha256:abc123...
# Must use DIGEST (@sha256:...), NOT tag (:latest)</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between key-based and keyless cosign signing?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Key-based:</strong> You generate a long-lived key pair, store cosign.key securely, and distribute cosign.pub for verification. Simpler, works anywhere. <strong>Keyless:</strong> Uses OIDC token (from GitHub, GCP, etc.) + Fulcio CA for short-lived certificates. No key management. Requires OIDC provider. Cert appears in Rekor transparency log. Keyless is recommended for CI/CD pipelines.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the Rekor transparency log and why does cosign use it? How does it prevent supply chain attacks?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Rekor is an immutable, append-only transparency log that records every signing event. When you sign with cosign, the signature is recorded in Rekor. This provides: (1) <strong>Non-repudiation:</strong> an attacker who compromises your signing key can't remove old signatures from Rekor. (2) <strong>Auditability:</strong> you can verify when and by whom every image was signed. (3) <strong>Tamper detection:</strong> if a signature appears that's NOT in Rekor, it's suspicious. Rekor prevents the "stolen key" scenario where an attacker signs malicious images and you can't prove which signatures are legitimate.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Rekor is the "trust but verify" layer of sigstore. Even if your signing key is compromised, the attacker's signatures MUST appear in Rekor (if verification checks Rekor). This creates an audit trail you can use to identify the exact point of compromise. On the CKS exam, keyless signing automatically records in Rekor; key-based signing requires <code>--rekor-url</code> flag.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You sign <code>easypj:v2.1</code> with cosign. Later, a new image is pushed with the same tag. Can the attacker's image be verified with your signature?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>No.</strong> cosign signs the DIGEST of the image content, not the tag. If you signed <code>easypj@sha256:abc...</code> and the tag <code>:v2.1</code> now points to <code>sha256:xyz...</code>, your signature only verifies against <code>sha256:abc...</code>. The new image at <code>sha256:xyz...</code> has NO valid signature. However, if you signed using the TAG (<code>cosign sign easypj:v2.1</code>), the signature follows the content (cosign resolves the tag to a digest at signing time). The signature is ALWAYS tied to a digest, never a tag. This is why you should always reference images by digest in production.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The key insight: cosign signatures are cryptographically bound to image DIGESTS. Tags are just pointers. If you deploy using tags, you might accidentally deploy an unsigned image even though "the tag was signed previously." On the CKS exam, always use digests for deployment: <code>image: ghcr.io/easypj/api@sha256:abc...</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you verify a cosign signature in a Kubernetes admission controller? What component checks the signature before allowing the pod to deploy?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Use the <strong>Sigstore Policy Controller</strong> (or a custom ValidatingWebhook) that calls cosign verify. The controller intercepts pod creation, extracts the image reference, and runs <code>cosign verify</code> against it. If verification fails, the pod is rejected. Alternative: use Kyverno's <code>verifyImages</code> rule or Connaisseur. The key configuration is the public key (or Fulcio CA) that the controller trusts for verification.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Signing without enforcement is useless. The CKS supply chain domain expects you to close the loop: (1) sign images at build time (cosign), (2) verify signatures at deploy time (admission controller), (3) reject unsigned images. On the exam, you may be asked to configure the admission controller to enforce signature verification.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is Sigstore's Fulcio CA, and how does keyless signing work without you ever creating a private key?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Fulcio is a certificate authority that issues short-lived (10 minutes) code-signing certificates based on OIDC identity. Flow: (1) CI pipeline authenticates to OIDC provider (GitHub Actions → token). (2) OIDC token sent to Fulcio. (3) Fulcio validates the token and issues an ephemeral X.509 certificate with the OIDC identity embedded. (4) cosign uses this certificate to sign the image. (5) The certificate expires in 10 minutes — no long-lived private key exists. Verification uses Fulcio's root CA + the OIDC identity claim.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Keyless signing eliminates the #1 problem of code signing: key management. No private keys to protect, rotate, or lose. The "key" is your OIDC identity, which is tied to your CI platform's authentication. On the CKS exam, understand that keyless = no key management + identity-based verification.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the cosign command to verify a keyless-signed image from GitHub Actions, checking that the identity is <code>https://github.com/easypj/easypj/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/heads/main</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">cosign verify \
  --certificate-identity="https://github.com/easypj/easypj/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/heads/main" \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer="https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com" \
  ghcr.io/easypj/api@sha256:abc123...</code></pre>
                    <p>This verifies: (a) the signature is valid (cryptographic check), (b) the certificate was issued to the expected GitHub Actions workflow, (c) the OIDC issuer is GitHub (not a fake IdP). This ensures the image was built by YOUR pipeline, not impersonated.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Identity-based verification is the keyless verification pattern. Instead of checking "is this signed with the right key?" (key-based), you check "was this signed by the right CI workflow?" (identity-based). On the CKS exam, you may need to construct this verification command with the correct identity and issuer.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is an attestation in cosign/sigstore? How do you sign an SBOM attestation for an image?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>An attestation is a signed statement ABOUT an image (metadata, not the image itself). Common attestations: SBOM (software bill of materials), SLSA provenance (how the image was built), vulnerability scan results. Sign an SBOM attestation:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Generate and sign an SBOM attestation
cosign attest --predicate sbom.spdx.json --type spdx \
  ghcr.io/easypj/api@sha256:abc123...
# Verify the attestation
cosign verify-attestation --type spdx \
  --certificate-identity=... ghcr.io/easypj/api@sha256:abc123...</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Attestations extend signing beyond "is this our image?" to "what's in this image and how was it built?" SBOM attestations prove you know exactly what's in your image. SLSA provenance attestations prove the image was built through your CI pipeline (not a developer's laptop). On the CKS exam, attestations are tested in the supply chain security domain.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're deploying to production and the admission controller rejects your image because "cosign verification failed." What are the 3 most likely causes and how do you check each?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(1) <strong>Wrong digest:</strong> You specified the tag in the pod spec, the admission controller resolved it, but the tag now points to a different (unsigned) digest. Check: <code>cosign verify --key cosign.pub &lt;image&gt;@sha256:&lt;latest-digest&gt;</code>. (2) <strong>Wrong key:</strong> The admission controller has an outdated public key — the image was signed with a rotated key. Check: <code>cosign verify --key &lt;correct-key&gt;.pub &lt;image&gt;</code>. (3) <strong>Image not signed:</strong> The CI pipeline built and pushed the image but the signing step failed silently. Check: <code>cosign triangulate &lt;image&gt; --type=signature</code> to see if any signatures exist.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a real debugging scenario. On the CKS exam: (1) always check the digest first — tag-based deployment is the most common cause, (2) check key rotation — if you rotate keys, update the admission controller, (3) verify the signing step actually succeeded — CI pipelines can fail silently.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a complete CI pipeline step (GitHub Actions) that builds a Docker image, pushes it, generates an SBOM, and signs BOTH the image and the SBOM attestation with keyless signing.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">- name: Build and Sign
  run: |
    IMAGE=ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}:${{ github.sha }}
    # Build and push
    docker build -t $IMAGE .
    docker push $IMAGE
    DIGEST=$(docker inspect --format='{{index .RepoDigests 0}}' $IMAGE)
    # Generate SBOM
    syft $IMAGE -o spdx-json > sbom.json
    # Sign image (keyless via GitHub OIDC)
    cosign sign --yes $DIGEST
    # Attest SBOM
    cosign attest --predicate sbom.json --type spdx $DIGEST
  env:
    COSIGN_EXPERIMENTAL: "1"  # Enable keyless signing</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the complete supply chain security pipeline for CKS. Key points: (1) <code>${{ github.sha }}</code> ensures each commit gets a unique tag, (2) <code>--yes</code> skips the interactive prompt, (3) <code>COSIGN_EXPERIMENTAL=1</code> enables keyless signing before it's GA, (4) the DIGEST (not tag) is signed. On the exam, recognize each step and its security purpose.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

