                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">✍️</div><h5>Key-Based Signing</h5><p><code>cosign generate-key-pair</code>. Sign/verify with key files. Simpler, works anywhere.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔑</div><h5>Keyless (OIDC)</h5><p>Fulcio CA + Rekor log. Ephemeral keys. GitHub Actions/GCP OIDC.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📜</div><h5>Transparency Log</h5><p>Rekor records every signature. Immutable. Auditable. Verify with <code>cosign verify</code>.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> two ways to sign, one rule that never changes. Key-based is <code>generate-key-pair</code> + sign/verify with files you manage yourself — simplest, works offline, the primary CKS exam pattern. Keyless swaps that file for a chain of trust: your CI's OIDC identity gets a 10-minute Fulcio certificate, and every signature lands in Rekor's public, immutable log.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> whichever method you use, <code>cosign verify</code> is the single command that closes the loop — and it only ever trusts a digest, never a tag.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📊 Chapter 26 Visual Summary — Image Signing at a Glance</div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔐</div><h5>Why Sign (26.1)</h5><p><strong>Registry credentials ≠ trust.</strong> ECDSA signatures over immutable digests. in-toto attestations prove build provenance. Without signing, stolen credentials = instant compromise.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">✍️</div><h5>Cosign (26.2)</h5><p><strong>Sign with private key, verify with public.</strong> Password-protected keys. 3-phase key rotation. Multi-signer workflows for CI + release manager co-signing.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div><h5>Kyverno (26.3)</h5><p><strong>verifyImages at admission.</strong> Key rotation policy pattern. Troubleshooting guide for 5 common failures. Unsigned images → 403 Forbidden.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔑</div><h5>Keyless (26.4)</h5><p><strong>No long-lived keys.</strong> OIDC + Fulcio ephemeral cert + Rekor transparency log. Rekor querying for audit. Identity-based verification.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> registry credentials only prove you CAN push — they never prove what you pushed is what you built. Cosign (26.2) closes that gap with ECDSA signatures bound to immutable digests, and Kyverno's <code>verifyImages</code> (26.3) turns that signature into an enforced admission gate: no valid signature, no pod. Keyless signing (26.4) then removes the last weak point — the long-lived private key — by replacing it with an OIDC-issued, 10-minute Fulcio certificate logged immutably in Rekor.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> signing proves your pipeline built the image; it does NOT prove the source code was clean — that still needs code review and SLSA-level provenance (Ch 27). Signing is one layer in a multi-layer supply chain defense, not a silver bullet.</p>
