            <section class="section-block" id="s27-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">27.1 What Is an SBOM and Why Does It Matter?</h3>
                <p>An <strong>SBOM (Software Bill of Materials)</strong> is a complete, structured list of every component, library, and dependency in a software artifact — think of it as a <strong>nutrition label for software</strong>. For a container image like <code>easypj:v2</code>, the SBOM lists every OS package (curl, libssl, glibc), every Python package (Django, gunicorn, psycopg2), and every transitive dependency — each with its exact version. When a critical CVE like Log4Shell drops, you query your SBOM database instead of manually auditing hundreds of images. <strong>SBOM → query → "easypj:v2 does not use Log4j. Next." — in seconds, not weeks.</strong></p>

                <h4>The Log4Shell Incident — Why SBOMs Became Mandatory</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Log4Shell Response — Without SBOM vs With SBOM</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    DECEMBER 10, 2021 — LOG4SHELL DROPS                    │
  │                                                                           │
  │  WITHOUT SBOM (what actually happened):                                   │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────                                    │
  │  • CVE-2021-44228 published — CVSS 10.0, remote code execution           │
  │  • Security teams: "Are we affected?"                                     │
  │  • Manual audit begins: check every Java app, every container image      │
  │  • Weeks of work across hundreds of teams                                │
  │  • Many organizations didn't know they were affected for MONTHS          │
  │  • US government: "This cannot happen again"                              │
  │                                                                           │
  │  WITH SBOM (what should happen):                                          │
  │  ──────────────────────────────                                           │
  │  • CVE-2021-44228 published                                               │
  │  • Security team: grep SBOMs for "log4j"                                 │
  │  • Query returns: "easypj:v2 — log4j NOT FOUND"                          │
  │  • Query returns: "legacy-app:v1 — log4j-core 2.14.1 — AFFECTED!"      │
  │  • Response time: SECONDS, not weeks                                     │
  │  • Targeted fix: update ONLY the affected images                         │
  │                                                                           │
  │  🔑 SBOMs turn vulnerability response from a MANUAL AUDIT into           │
  │     a DATABASE QUERY. This is why the US Executive Order 14028           │
  │     (May 2021) requires SBOMs for all federal software.                  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>SBOM Formats — SPDX vs CycloneDX</h4>
