                <h4>VEX — Vulnerability Exploitability eXchange</h4>
                <p>An SBOM tells you <strong>what</strong> packages are in your software. A <strong>VEX</strong> document tells you <strong>whether a CVE in those packages is actually exploitable</strong> in YOUR specific context. For example: your easypj image contains libssl1.1 which has CVE-2023-44487 (HTTP/2 Rapid Reset DoS, CVSS 7.5). But your Django app runs behind an ALB that TERMINATES HTTP/2 — the vulnerable code path is never reached. A VEX document says: "CVE-2023-44487 in easypj:v2 → NOT AFFECTED (HTTP/2 terminated at ALB)." This prevents false-positive driven fire drills.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">SBOM + VEX = Complete Vulnerability Intelligence</div>
<pre>
  SBOM: "easypj:v2 contains libssl1.1 1.1.1n — has CVE-2023-44487 (CVSS 7.5)"
     │  ← This alone triggers PANIC: "We have a HIGH CVE!"
     │
     ▼
  VEX: "CVE-2023-44487 in easypj:v2 → NOT AFFECTED"
     │  ← VEX provides CONTEXT:
     │     • HTTP/2 is terminated at the ALB, not at the container
     │     • The vulnerable code path is never executed
     │     • No action needed — this is a FALSE POSITIVE in our context
     │
     ▼
  RESULT: Security team does NOT get paged. No emergency rebuild.
          VEX saved hours of investigation for a non-exploitable CVE.

  🔑 SBOM = "what's in the box" | VEX = "can it hurt us?"
     Together: actionable vulnerability intelligence, not just noise.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>SPDX vs CycloneDX — Full Feature Comparison</h4>
                <p>The table above covers the essentials; here's the deeper comparison for choosing a format deliberately rather than by tool default:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>SPDX 2.3</th><th>CycloneDX 1.6</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Origin</strong></td><td>Linux Foundation (2011)</td><td>OWASP (2017)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Primary focus</strong></td><td>License compliance + supply chain</td><td>Security + vulnerability management</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Fields covered</strong></td><td>Package, version, license, copyright, file hash</td><td>Component, version, purl, CPE, hash, vulnerabilities</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Vulnerability data</strong></td><td>Via VEX (Vulnerability Exploitability eXchange) extension</td><td>Built-in; can embed CVE IDs and severity directly</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ISO standard</strong></td><td>✅ ISO/IEC 5962:2021</td><td>ECMA-424 (not ISO)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Output formats</strong></td><td>JSON, YAML, tag-value, RDF/XML</td><td>JSON, XML</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Tool support</strong></td><td>syft, trivy, tern, FOSSA, ORT</td><td>syft, trivy, Dependency-Track, OWASP tools</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Kubernetes adoption</strong></td><td>Widely used; k8s SBOM WG recommends SPDX</td><td>Growing; used by Dependency-Track for continuous monitoring</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CKS preference</strong></td><td>✅ Both accepted — know either</td><td>✅ Both accepted — know either</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s27-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">27.2 Generating SBOMs for easypj Images</h3>
                <p>Three primary tools generate SBOMs for container images: <strong>Syft</strong> (by Anchore, most popular), <strong>Trivy</strong> (by Aqua, already used for CVE scanning), and <strong>Docker SBOM</strong> (built into Docker CLI). All three produce SPDX or CycloneDX output. Once generated, SBOMs should be <strong>attached to the image</strong> using Cosign and stored in the registry alongside the signature.</p>

                <h4>SBOM Generation Tools — Syft, Trivy, Docker</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Generate SBOMs with Syft, Trivy, and Docker</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# SYFT (Anchore) — Most feature-rich, recommended
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Install: curl -sSfL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/anchore/syft/main/install.sh | sh -s -- -b /usr/local/bin

# Generate SPDX JSON SBOM
syft registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2 -o spdx-json > easypj-v2.sbom.spdx.json

# Generate CycloneDX JSON SBOM
syft registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2 -o cyclonedx-json > easypj-v2.sbom.cdx.json

# Generate table output (human-readable)
syft registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2 -o table

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TRIVY — Already in your pipeline for CVE scanning
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Generate CycloneDX SBOM
trivy image --format cyclonedx --output easypj-v2.cdx.json registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2

# Generate SPDX SBOM
trivy image --format spdx-json --output easypj-v2.spdx.json registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# DOCKER — Built-in, no extra tools needed
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
docker sbom registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2
docker sbom --format spdx-json registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2 > easypj-v2.spdx.json</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Attaching SBOMs to Images with Cosign</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Generate SBOM and attach to image in CI/CD pipeline</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Complete CI/CD step: Build → SBOM → Sign → Attach
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

IMAGE=registry.easypj.com/easypj:${{ github.sha }}

# 1. Build and push
docker build -t $IMAGE .
docker push $IMAGE

# 2. Generate SBOM with Syft
syft $IMAGE -o spdx-json > easypj.sbom.spdx.json

# 3. Sign the image (Ch26)
cosign sign --key cosign.key $IMAGE

# 4. Attach SBOM to the image
cosign attach sbom --sbom easypj.sbom.spdx.json $IMAGE

# 5. Verify SBOM is attached
cosign verify-attestation --key cosign.pub --type spdxjson $IMAGE
# Output: verified + shows SBOM metadata

# 6. Download SBOM from registry (for CVE impact analysis)
cosign download sbom $IMAGE > easypj-downloaded.sbom.spdx.json</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>SBOM → CVE Impact Analysis — The Real Value</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
