                <h4>Grype — SBOM-Based Vulnerability Scanning</h4>
                <p>Once you have SBOMs, you can scan them for vulnerabilities without re-scanning the image. <strong>Grype</strong> (by Anchore, same creators as Syft) takes an SBOM file and matches every component against vulnerability databases. This is faster than Trivy image scanning because the SBOM has already done the package inventory work:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Scan SBOM for vulnerabilities with Grype</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Install Grype
curl -sSfL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/anchore/grype/main/install.sh | sh

# Scan an SBOM file (faster than image scan)
grype sbom:easypj-v2.sbom.spdx.json

# Scan with severity filter
grype sbom:easypj-v2.sbom.spdx.json --fail-on critical

# Output as JSON for pipeline processing
grype sbom:easypj-v2.sbom.spdx.json -o json > grype-results.json

# 🔑 KEY ADVANTAGE: Store SBOM once, scan many times
#    • Generate SBOM in CI/CD → attach to image
#    • When new CVE drops: download SBOM → grype scan
#    • No need to re-pull or re-scan the full image</code></pre>
