                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 SBOM Anti-Patterns to Avoid</h5>
                    <p><strong>DO NOT:</strong> Generate SBOM AFTER pushing the image to a public registry — that's too late. <strong>DO NOT:</strong> Store SBOM in the same repo as the source code without signing it — the SBOM itself can be tampered with. <strong>DO NOT:</strong> Use a single SBOM for all environments — dev dependencies differ from production. <strong>DO INSTEAD:</strong> Generate SBOM in CI, sign it with cosign, attach it to the image, and upload it to a central SBOM registry (Dependency-Track, Anchore, or graph-db). The SBOM is only valuable if it's (a) accurate, (b) signed, and (c) discoverable.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Distroless Images — Smaller SBOM, Smaller Attack Surface</h4>
                <p>An SBOM's value scales inversely with image size — every extra package is another line in the inventory and another potential CVE to track. This is the practical argument for minimal base images: fewer packages means a shorter, easier-to-audit SBOM.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Standard python:3.12</th><th>python:3.12-slim</th><th>distroless python</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Image size</strong></td><td>~1 GB</td><td>~120 MB</td><td>~50 MB</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Shell?</strong></td><td>✅ bash, sh</td><td>✅ sh</td><td>❌ No shell</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Package manager?</strong></td><td>✅ apt (full)</td><td>✅ apt (minimal)</td><td>❌ None</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Utilities (curl, wget)?</strong></td><td>✅ Many</td><td>⚠️ Few</td><td>❌ None</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CVEs (typical)</strong></td><td>30-50</td><td>5-10</td><td>0-2</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Attacker capability if compromised</strong></td><td>Full shell, download tools, script</td><td>Basic shell, limited tools</td><td>No tools — trapped!</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p><strong>The tradeoff:</strong> Distroless images are harder to debug. There's no <code>kubectl exec -- sh</code>. You must rely on logs (stdout/stderr) and health checks. For production workloads — where security outweighs developer convenience — this tradeoff is almost always worth it. For development environments, use the slim variant for a balance of security and debugability.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s27-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">27.3 SLSA — Supply Chain Levels for Software Artifacts</h3>
                <p><strong>SLSA</strong> (pronounced "salsa") is a security framework from Google and the OpenSSF that defines <strong>four levels of supply chain integrity</strong>. Each level builds on the previous one, providing increasing guarantees that your software hasn't been tampered with between source code and deployment. SLSA is to software supply chains what <strong>seatbelts → airbags → crumple zones</strong> are to car safety: progressive, layered protections.</p>

                <h4>The Four SLSA Levels — Progressive Supply Chain Integrity</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Name</th><th>Requirements</th><th>What It Prevents</th><th>easypj Status</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>0</strong></td>
                                <td>None</td>
                                <td>No guarantees. Build may be entirely undocumented.</td>
                                <td>Nothing. Any compromise goes undetected.</td>
                                <td style="color:#326ce5;">Starting point 🔴</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>1</strong></td>
                                <td>Provenance Exists</td>
                                <td>Build process produces provenance (who, what, how, when). Provenance is NOT signed, NOT verified.</td>
                                <td>Honest mistakes. Accidental misconfigurations.</td>
                                <td style="color:#fbbf24;">Syft SBOM generated 🟡</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>2</strong></td>
                                <td>Hosted Build Platform</td>
                                <td>Build runs on a hosted platform (GitHub Actions, GCB). Provenance is SIGNED and VERIFIABLE. Source + build are version-controlled.</td>
                                <td>Tampering after the build. Unsigned provenance modifications.</td>
                                <td style="color:#4ade80;">GitHub Actions + cosign attest ✅</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>3</strong></td>
                                <td>Hardened Builds</td>
                                <td>Isolated, ephemeral build environment. Builds are hermetic (no network, deterministic). Two-person review required.</td>
                                <td>Compromised build platform. Insider threats. Cache poisoning.</td>
                                <td style="color:#fbbf24;">Future goal 🟡</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

