            <section class="section-block" id="s27-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">27.4 Build Provenance &amp; in-toto Attestations</h3>
                <p><strong>Provenance</strong> answers a different question than an SBOM. An SBOM answers "WHAT is in this artifact?" — provenance answers <strong>"WHERE did this artifact come from, and HOW was it built?"</strong> Provenance is a signed, machine-readable record of the build: which commit, which builder, which steps, which inputs. An <strong>in-toto attestation</strong> is the format that record takes — a signed statement that cryptographically binds an image digest to metadata: source commit, builder identity, build steps, and materials. SLSA provenance (26.1, 27.3) is simply one <em>predicate type</em> expressed inside an in-toto attestation, and <strong>cosign</strong> — the same binary you used to sign images in Ch 26 — both attaches and verifies it: <code>cosign attest</code> to sign, <code>cosign verify-attestation</code> to check.</p>

                <h4>The Provenance Chain — From Commit to Admission</h4>
                <p>Put together, source control, CI, signing, and admission form one continuous, verifiable chain. Break any single link — an unsigned build, a forged predicate, a skipped admission check — and the guarantee collapses:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Provenance Chain — From Commit to Admission</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                 BUILD PROVENANCE CHAIN — easypj                            │
  │                                                                           │
  │  git commit (source)                                                      │
  │  github.com/easysj/easypj@abc123def                                       │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  CI builds image + records build steps                                    │
  │  (GitHub Actions: checkout → docker build → trivy scan → push)           │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  in-toto attestation (SLSA predicate)                                     │
  │  { subject: image digest, predicate: {builder, source, materials} }      │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  cosign attest  ──▶  SIGNS the attestation with the builder's key/OIDC   │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  registry stores image + signature + attestation                          │
  │  (all three travel together as OCI artifacts, same digest)                │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  admission (Kyverno verifyImages + cosign verify-attestation)             │
  │  • signature valid?  • attestation signed?  • builder identity trusted?   │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ├──▶ ALL CHECKS PASS  → pod is ADMITTED, running code traces        │
  │       │                        back to exactly this commit                │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       └──▶ ANY CHECK FAILS  → DENY — no unverified image ever runs        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>What an in-toto Attestation Actually Binds Together</h4>
                <p>An attestation is not just "a signature" — it is a signed <strong>envelope</strong> whose payload names a specific artifact (by digest, never by tag) and attaches a typed predicate describing it. For easypj, a SLSA-provenance attestation binds:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div>
                        <h5>Subject</h5>
                        <p>The exact image digest — <code>registry.easypj.com/easypj@sha256:abc...</code> — never a mutable tag (same rule as Ch 26 signing).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏗️</div>
                        <h5>Builder Identity</h5>
                        <p>WHO built it — e.g. <code>https://github.com/easysj/easypj/.github/workflows/build.yml</code>, not just "some CI server."</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Materials &amp; Source</h5>
                        <p>WHAT went in — the source commit SHA, the Dockerfile, the SBOM — everything the build consumed.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Generating and Verifying a SLSA Predicate for easypj — GitHub Actions</h4>
                <p>In practice, provenance generation is a CI step, not a manual task. Here is the easypj GitHub Actions workflow step that builds the predicate, signs it as an in-toto attestation, and verifies it before the job completes:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — GitHub Actions step: sign &amp; verify SLSA provenance for easypj</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">- name: Attest and verify build provenance
  env:
    IMAGE: registry.easypj.com/easypj@${{ steps.build.outputs.digest }}
    COSIGN_EXPERIMENTAL: "1"   # enable keyless (OIDC) signing
  run: |
    # 1. Build the in-toto/SLSA predicate — WHO, WHAT, HOW
    cat > provenance.json << EOF
    {
      "builder": { "id": "https://github.com/${{ github.repository }}/.github/workflows/build.yml@${{ github.ref }}" },
      "buildType": "https://github.com/actions/runner@v1",
      "invocation": {
        "configSource": {
          "uri": "git+https://github.com/${{ github.repository }}",
          "digest": { "sha1": "${{ github.sha }}" },
          "entryPoint": ".github/workflows/build.yml"
        }
      },
      "materials": [
        { "uri": "git+https://github.com/${{ github.repository }}",
          "digest": { "sha1": "${{ github.sha }}" } }
      ]
    }
    EOF

    # 2. Sign the predicate as an in-toto attestation (keyless — builder = GitHub OIDC)
    cosign attest --yes \
      --predicate provenance.json \
      --type slsaprovenance \
      $IMAGE

    # 3. Verify it right away — fail the CI job if the attestation is bad
    cosign verify-attestation \
      --type slsaprovenance \
      --certificate-identity-regexp "^https://github.com/${{ github.repository }}/" \
      --certificate-oidc-issuer "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com" \
      $IMAGE</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 27.4 ↔ Ch 26 — Same Binary, Different Subcommand</h5>
                    <p>Ch 26 taught <code>cosign sign</code> / <code>cosign verify</code> to prove <em>"this image was pushed by me."</em> This section's <code>cosign attest</code> / <code>cosign verify-attestation</code> prove something stronger: <em>"this image was built THIS way, from THIS commit, by THIS builder."</em> A signed image with no attestation still tells an attacker nothing about the build — pairing both closes the loop: <strong>identity (Ch 26) + history (27.4)</strong>.</p>
                </div>
