            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>An SBOM turns vulnerability response from a manual audit into a database query</strong> — Log4Shell took organizations weeks to triage without one; with SBOMs, "are we affected?" is a <code>grep</code> away (27.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Generation and attachment are two separate steps.</strong> Syft/Trivy/Docker produce the SBOM; <code>cosign attach sbom</code> or <code>cosign attest --type spdx</code> binds it to the image so it travels with the artifact and can be pulled back later with <code>cosign download sbom</code> (27.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>SLSA's four levels (0-3) are progressive, not a checklist to complete at once</strong> — 0 = no guarantees, 1 = provenance exists but unsigned, 2 = hosted + signed provenance (easypj's current target), 3 = hermetic + isolated + two-person-reviewed builds (27.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Provenance and signing answer different questions.</strong> Signing (Ch 26) proves WHO pushed the image; an in-toto attestation (27.4) proves WHERE it came from and HOW it was built — binding the image digest to its source commit, builder identity, and materials via <code>cosign attest</code> / <code>verify-attestation</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>None of this matters if the registry itself is weak.</strong> Tag immutability, pull-by-digest, and admission-time re-verification (27.5) are what turn "we signed it once" into "nothing unverified ever runs."</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>SPDX vs CycloneDX:</strong> Both are valid SBOM formats. SPDX is Linux Foundation standard (tag-value, JSON, YAML). CycloneDX is OWASP standard (JSON, XML). Tools have preferences — syft supports both. On the exam, use whichever the question specifies.</li>
                    <li><strong>SBOM is a snapshot, not a live inventory:</strong> An SBOM generated at build time reflects dependencies AT BUILD TIME. If a new CVE is discovered the next day, the SBOM won't know about it. SBOMs must be continuously scanned, not just generated once.</li>
                    <li><strong>SLSA levels are numbered 0-3, not 1-4.</strong> It's easy to misremember an off-by-one — Level 0 is "no guarantees," Level 3 (not 4) is the hardened, hermetic, two-person-reviewed top tier.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
