            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 27 — Lab Q&amp;A (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">11 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Generate an SBOM for a container image using syft. Write the exact command.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">syft ghcr.io/easypj/api:latest -o spdx-json
syft ghcr.io/easypj/api:latest -o cyclonedx-json
syft ghcr.io/easypj/api:latest --output sbom.spdx.json
# Common options: -o (format), --catalogers (which scanners to use)</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is SLSA Level 3? What additional guarantee does it provide over Level 2?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>SLSA Level 3 — Hardened Builds:</strong> the build is fully auditable — the build platform cryptographically attests to the recipe, source, and output artifact. Key difference from Level 2: Level 2 provides signed provenance but relies on the build platform being trusted. Level 3 adds <strong>hermetic builds</strong> (no network access during build) and <strong>isolated builds</strong> (each build in its own environment) plus two-person review. This means even if the build platform is compromised, the provenance log will show tampering. Level 3 requires hardened build platforms like Tekton Chains with TPM attestation.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span><div class="eq-question">What is an SBOM and why is it critical for supply chain security? What information does it contain?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>An SBOM (Software Bill of Materials) is a machine-readable inventory of every component in a software artifact. It contains: package names, versions, licenses, dependencies, checksums, and supplier information. Critical because: (1) when a CVE is published, you can immediately query SBOMs to find affected images, (2) you can verify images contain only expected components, (3) license compliance auditing becomes automated. Without SBOMs, you have no idea what's in your containers.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>SBOMs are the foundation of supply chain security. The CKS exam tests SBOM generation (syft), signing (cosign attest), and consumption (Trivy scanning). The key scenario: CVE announced → query SBOMs → find affected images → remediate in hours instead of weeks.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span><div class="eq-question">What are the four SLSA levels (0-3)? For each level, what guarantee does the consumer receive?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>L0 None:</strong> no guarantees — build may be entirely undocumented. <strong>L1 Provenance Exists:</strong> the build produces provenance (who/what/how/when), but it is NOT signed or verified. <strong>L2 Hosted Build Platform:</strong> the build runs on a hosted platform and provenance is SIGNED and verifiable — source and build are version-controlled. <strong>L3 Hardened Builds:</strong> isolated, ephemeral, hermetic build environment plus two-person review — even a compromised platform leaves detectable tampering. Most organizations target L2-L3.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>SLSA levels are the maturity model for supply chain security, numbered 0-3 (not 1-4 — that off-by-one is a common exam trap). L0 is no security, L1 is documentation only, L2 adds cryptographic signing, L3 adds build-environment hardening. On the CKS exam, know each level's consumer guarantee exactly as tabled in 27.3.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span><div class="eq-question">How do you attach an SBOM to a container image and retrieve it later? Write both commands.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-bash">cosign attach sbom --sbom sbom.spdx.json ghcr.io/easypj/api@sha256:abc123...
cosign download sbom ghcr.io/easypj/api@sha256:abc123... > retrieved-sbom.json
cosign attest --predicate sbom.spdx.json --type spdx ghcr.io/easypj/api@sha256:abc123...</code></pre><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>SBOMs can be stored as OCI artifacts (<code>attach</code>) or signed attestations (<code>attest</code>). Attesting is preferred because it cryptographically binds the SBOM to the image and records it in Rekor.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span><div class="eq-question">You discover an image contains log4j-core:2.14.0 vulnerable to Log4Shell. Using SBOM, how do you find ALL images with this vulnerable version?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-bash">for image in $(list-all-images); do
  cosign download sbom $image 2>/dev/null | \
    jq -e '.packages[] | select(.name=="log4j-core" and (.versionInfo | startswith("2.14")))' && \
    echo "VULNERABLE: $image"
done</code></pre><p>Without SBOMs, you'd rescan every image (hours/days). With SBOMs, you grep through inventory (seconds).</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the SBOM value proposition — turning vulnerability response from "scan everything" to "query your inventory." On the CKS exam, this scenario demonstrates why SBOMs are critical infrastructure.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span><div class="eq-question">What makes a build "hermetic" and "isolated" for SLSA Level 3? What build platform supports this?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>Hermetic:</strong> All inputs declared and versioned. No network access during build. Same inputs = same output. <strong>Isolated:</strong> Each build in its own ephemeral environment. No shared state. Previous builds cannot contaminate current builds. <strong>Platform:</strong> Tekton Pipelines with Tekton Chains, GitHub Actions with SLSA Generator, or Google Cloud Build. Tekton Chains provides SLSA L2 out of box and L3 with hermetic configuration.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Hermetic + isolated = the build is self-contained and tamper-evident. L3 is the threshold where you transition from "trust the platform" to "verify the platform." On the CKS exam, Tekton Chains is the Kubernetes-native SLSA implementation.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span><div class="eq-question">Compare SPDX and CycloneDX SBOM formats. Which does syft support?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>syft supports BOTH. <strong>SPDX</strong> (Linux Foundation): ISO standard (ISO/IEC 5962:2021). Richer metadata for license compliance. <strong>CycloneDX</strong> (OWASP): Focused on security use cases. More widely adopted in commercial tools. For CKS: use whichever is specified. If unspecified, SPDX-JSON is a safe default.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Both serve the same purpose. SPDX is stronger for license compliance; CycloneDX for vulnerability management. Know <code>syft -o spdx-json</code> and <code>syft -o cyclonedx-json</code>.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span><div class="eq-question">What is Tekton Chains and how does it implement SLSA for Kubernetes?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Tekton Chains watches Tekton PipelineRuns and automatically: (1) generates in-toto provenance (build recipe, source, builder identity), (2) signs the provenance with cosign, (3) stores the signature as an OCI artifact. Provides SLSA L2 out of box, L3 with hermetic builds. Chains automates supply chain attestation — no manual cosign steps needed after each build.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Tekton Chains is the Kubernetes-native bridge between CI/CD and supply chain security. On the CKS exam, if asked about "automated SLSA in Kubernetes," the answer is Tekton Chains.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span><div class="eq-question">A new zero-day is announced. You have SBOMs for 200 images. Walk through your triage process: find affected images, prioritize, remediate, and verify.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><div class="process-steps" style="flex-direction: column;"><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Query SBOMs</h5><p>Grep all SBOMs for the vulnerable package. Seconds, not hours.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Prioritize</h5><p>Production → staging → dev. Internet-facing before internal.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Patch and rebuild</h5><p>Update package, rebuild, scan with Trivy, sign with cosign.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Deploy</h5><p>Rolling update. Admission controller verifies new signature.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Verify</h5><p>Trivy Operator confirms zero affected images in production.</p></div></div></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This end-to-end workflow demonstrates SBOM value. Without SBOMs, step 1 takes days (rescanning). With SBOMs, step 1 takes seconds. On the CKS exam, this is the supply chain security incident response pattern.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q11</span><div class="eq-question">What does an in-toto attestation cryptographically bind together, and in what order does the provenance chain run from commit to a running pod?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>An in-toto attestation binds a specific <strong>subject</strong> (the image digest — never a mutable tag) to a typed <strong>predicate</strong> describing the build: builder identity, source commit, build steps, and materials. <strong>Chain order:</strong> (1) git commit in source control, (2) CI builds the image and records the build steps, (3) the build produces an in-toto attestation (SLSA predicate), (4) <code>cosign attest</code> signs that attestation, (5) the registry stores image + signature + attestation together, (6) at deploy time, admission (Kyverno + <code>cosign verify-attestation</code>) checks the signature and the attestation before allowing the pod to run.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The exam-relevant distinction: signing (Ch 26) proves identity — "I pushed this." An attestation (27.4) proves history — "this is how it was built, and from what." Breaking the chain at any link (unsigned build, forged predicate, or an admission controller that doesn't check attestations) means a compromised image can slip through even if the image itself is validly signed.</p></div></details></div>
            </div>

