                </div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> SBOM (27.1-27.2) answers "what's in it" — a queryable inventory that turns a Log4Shell-style fire drill into a database lookup. SLSA (27.3) grades HOW rigorously it was built, on a 0-3 scale. Build provenance and in-toto attestations (27.4) are the signed paperwork that makes a SLSA level provable, using the exact same <code>cosign</code> binary from Ch 26 — just <code>attest</code>/<code>verify-attestation</code> instead of <code>sign</code>/<code>verify</code>. None of it is real security until the artifact repository and the admission controller (27.5) actually enforce it on every pull.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> SBOM + SLSA together close the final supply chain gaps this guide covers — knowing exactly what's in your software, and proving exactly how it was built, checked automatically before a single line of it ever runs in your cluster.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
