            <section class="section-block" id="s28-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">28.1 What Is Audit Logging and Why Is It Non-Negotiable?</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes audit logging is a <strong>chronological record of every authenticated request</strong> made to the API server. Every <code>kubectl apply</code>, every <code>kubectl exec</code>, every controller reconciliation, every kubelet heartbeat — captured as a structured JSON event with timestamp, user identity, source IP, target resource, HTTP verb, and response code. Without audit logs, a security incident leaves no trace. <strong>With audit logs, you can replay the attack step by step.</strong></p>

                <h4>How Audit Logging Works Inside the API Server — The 4 Stages</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Audit Event Lifecycle — 4 Stages from Request Arrival to Completion</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    AUDIT STAGES IN THE API SERVER                          │
  │                                                                           │
  │  kubectl get secrets                                                     │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │
  │  │  STAGE 1: RequestReceived — Request arrives at API server       │     │
  │  │  • Timestamp: requestReceivedTimestamp                          │     │
  │  │  • Contains: user, verb, resource, sourceIP (NO response yet)  │     │
  │  │  • Policy level: at least Metadata needed to capture this      │     │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       │ AuthN → AuthZ → Admission                                         │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │
  │  │  STAGE 2: ResponseStarted — Response headers sent               │     │
  │  │  • Only generated for long-running requests (watch)            │     │
  │  │  • Timestamp: responseStartedTimestamp                          │     │
  │  │  • Useful for: detecting hung requests                          │     │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       │ Request processed → Response body generated                       │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │
  │  │  STAGE 3: ResponseComplete — Response fully sent                │     │
  │  │  • Most audit events captured HERE                              │     │
  │  │  • Contains: responseStatus.code, requestObject, responseObject  │     │
  │  │  • Timestamp: stageTimestamp                                    │     │
  │  │  • Policy level: RequestResponse needed for full body capture   │     │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       │ (Rare: panic during request handling)                             │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │
  │  │  STAGE 4: Panic — API server panicked during request            │     │
  │  │  • Generated when a goroutine panics during handling            │     │
  │  │  • Contains: panic stack trace                                  │     │
  │  │  • Always logged regardless of audit policy                     │     │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │
  │                                                                           │
  │  🔑 Use omitStages in the policy to suppress specific stages             │
  │     (e.g., omitStages: ["RequestReceived"] to only log final events)     │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>🔴 INCIDENT SCENARIO: How Audit Logs Reconstruct an Attack</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">3:14 AM Attack — Reconstructed from Audit Logs</div>
<pre>
  03:14:01  user=sa:easypj:deployer  verb=create  resource=pods
            response=201  sourceIP=203.0.113.42
            → Pod "crypto-miner" created with privileged=true 🚨
  03:14:05  user=sa:easypj:deployer  verb=create  resource=pods/exec
            response=101 (WebSocket upgrade)
            → Attacker exec'd into the pod 🚨
  03:14:12  user=sa:easypj:deployer  verb=get  resource=secrets
            name=easypj-db-creds  response=200
            → Database credentials read 🚨
  03:14:18  user=sa:easypj:deployer  verb=get  resource=configmaps
            name=django-settings  response=200
