            <section class="section-block" id="s28-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">28.4 Shipping Audit Logs to External Systems</h3>
                <p>Local files suffice for the CKS exam. In production, ship audit logs <strong>off-node</strong> to a SIEM for retention, alerting, and correlation. The API server supports a <strong>webhook backend</strong> that streams events via HTTPS in real time.</p>

                <h4>Webhook Backend Configuration — Streaming to Elasticsearch</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — /etc/kubernetes/audit-webhook.yaml — Webhook backend for SIEM streaming</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Config
clusters:
- name: elasticsearch
  cluster:
    server: https://elasticsearch.logging.svc:9200/_bulk
    certificate-authority: /etc/kubernetes/elasticsearch-ca.crt
users:
- name: audit-webhook
  user:
    client-certificate: /etc/kubernetes/audit-webhook-client.crt
    client-key: /etc/kubernetes/audit-webhook-client.key
contexts:
- name: default
  context:
    cluster: elasticsearch
    user: audit-webhook
current-context: default</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Batch vs Blocking Mode — Performance Trade-off</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left">
                        <div class="panel-header" style="color:#4ade80;">batch (async)</div>
<pre>
--audit-webhook-mode=batch

• Events buffered and sent in batches
• API requests NOT delayed by webhook
• If webhook is DOWN → events queued
• If buffer fills → events DROPPED ⚠️
• RECOMMENDED for production
• Trade: may lose events under extreme load
</pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel-right">
                        <div class="panel-header" style="color:#326ce5;">blocking (sync)</div>
<pre>
--audit-webhook-mode=blocking

• Each API request WAITS for webhook
• API latency = webhook latency
• If webhook is DOWN → API BLOCKED 🔴
• NO events lost (guaranteed delivery)
• RECOMMENDED for compliance (PCI-DSS)
• Trade: webhook outage = cluster outage
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Log Rotation and Retention — Production Best Practices</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Recommended Value</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-log-maxsize</code></td><td>100 (MB)</td><td>Small enough to rotate frequently, large enough to avoid excessive rotations</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-log-maxbackup</code></td><td>10-30</td><td>Retain enough history for incident investigation (10-30 days of logs)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-log-maxage</code></td><td>30 (days)</td><td>Matches SOC 2 and PCI-DSS retention requirements (30-90 days typical)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Disk space estimate</strong></td><td>~1-5 GB/day</td><td>Depends on cluster activity; budget accordingly for log volume</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>External shipping</strong></td><td>Webhook to SIEM</td><td>Off-node storage protects logs even if node is compromised</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s28-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">28.5 Detecting Suspicious Activity from Audit Logs</h3>
                <p>Raw audit logs produce thousands of events per minute. Security monitoring means identifying the <strong>signal in the noise</strong> — the patterns that indicate an active attack or policy violation.</p>

                <h4>Suspicious Audit Log Patterns — Red Flags with Detection Queries</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>🛡️ Red Flag</th><th>What It Looks Like</th><th>jq Detection</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Secret Access by Unexpected User</strong></td><td>Non-system SA reading secrets</td><td><code>select(.objectRef.resource=="secrets")</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Privilege Escalation</strong></td><td>Creating ClusterRoleBindings</td><td><code>select(.objectRef.resource=="clusterrolebindings")</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Unexpected Pod Exec</strong></td><td>kubectl exec by non-admin users</td><td><code>select(.objectRef.subresource=="exec")</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>HostNetwork/Privileged Pod</strong></td><td>Pod with dangerous specs created</td><td><code>select(.verb=="create" and .objectRef.resource=="pods")</code> + inspect requestObject</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>High Rate of Failed Auth</strong></td><td>Multiple 401/403 in short window</td><td><code>select(.responseStatus.code>=400)</code> + time window aggregation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Namespace-Crossing Access</strong></td><td>SA accessing resources outside its namespace</td><td><code>select(.user.username != .objectRef.namespace)</code> (SA pattern)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Webhook/Admission Changes</strong></td><td>Modifying ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</td><td><code>select(.objectRef.resource=="validatingwebhookconfigurations")</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Audit Policy Tampering</strong></td><td>Modifying API server manifest or audit policy</td><td>Monitor file access to /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ and /etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml</td></tr>
