            <section class="section-block" id="s28-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">28.6 Official Audit Facts — Levels vs Stages (the Exam's Favorite Confusion)</h3>
                <p>Straight from <code>kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/audit</code>: the exam frequently swaps "level" and "stage" in wrong-answer options — they control two completely different things. This section is the official, fact-checked disambiguation.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Concept: LEVEL and STAGE Are Two Independent Axes</h5>
                    <p><strong>LEVEL</strong> = how much detail is captured — defined PER RULE, in the policy YAML you write. <strong>STAGE</strong> = at which point in the request lifecycle an event fires — built INTO Kubernetes, not something you choose per rule to happen, though you CAN exclude specific stages globally (top-level <code>omitStages</code>) or per-rule (<code>omitStages</code> inside a rule). Every audit event has exactly one LEVEL and was generated at exactly one STAGE — the two values are independent and every event carries both.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>LEVEL — Exactly What Gets Captured, No More No Less</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>LEVEL</th><th>CAPTURES</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>None</strong></td><td>Nothing — event is dropped entirely</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Metadata</strong></td><td>User, timestamp, verb, resource, response code — NO request body, NO response body</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Request</strong></td><td>Metadata + the REQUEST body (e.g. the YAML you sent) — still no response body; N/A for non-resource URLs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>RequestResponse</strong></td><td>Metadata + REQUEST body + RESPONSE body — heaviest, N/A for non-resource URLs</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>STAGE — WHEN in the Request Lifecycle an Event Is Generated</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>STAGE</th><th>WHEN IT FIRES</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>RequestReceived</strong></td><td>As soon as the audit handler gets the request, before it's passed down the handler chain</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ResponseStarted</strong></td><td>Response headers sent, body not yet (only for long-running requests like watch)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ResponseComplete</strong></td><td>Response body fully sent — no more bytes coming</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Panic</strong></td><td>The handler chain panicked</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align:center;font-size:0.85rem;color:#8b949e;margin-top:0.5rem;"><strong>Memory hook:</strong> LEVEL answers "how much did we write down?" STAGE answers "when did we write it down?" A wrong-answer option that says something like "set the RequestResponse stage" or "the Metadata stage fires at ResponseComplete" is testing whether you've kept these separate.</p>

                <h4>Two Policy Facts the Exam Expects Verbatim</h4>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Fact 1: <code>rules: []</code> Is Illegal</h5>
                    <p>The audit Policy resource has exactly <strong>ONE mandatory field: <code>rules</code></strong>. A policy with <code>rules: []</code> (zero rules) is <strong>REJECTED as illegal</strong> by the API server at load time — the whole point of a policy is to have at least one rule. If you want to log nothing beyond a bare minimum, you still need at least a catch-all <code>- level: Metadata</code> (or <code>- level: None</code> to disable everything explicitly) — an empty list is not the same as "log nothing" and won't be accepted.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Fact 2: Rules Are Evaluated Top to Bottom, First Match Wins</h5>
                    <p>This is the same first-match-wins fact from 28.2, restated as an official rule: a narrow rule (e.g. "don't log <code>system:nodes</code>") must come <strong>FIRST</strong>, and the broad catch-all must come <strong>LAST</strong>. Reversing the order makes every rule after the catch-all <strong>unreachable</strong> — the API server never even evaluates them, and no error is raised to tell you.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Flag Precision — Which Flag Controls Which Backend</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-log-path</code></td><td>Enables the FILE backend. Omitting it disables file logging even if <code>--audit-policy-file</code> is set.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-log-maxage</code></td><td>Days to retain rotated log files</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-log-maxbackup</code></td><td>Maximum number of rotated files to keep</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-log-maxsize</code></td><td>Megabytes per file before rotation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-webhook-config-file</code></td><td>Enables the separate WEBHOOK backend (kubeconfig-style file)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Trap: File and Webhook Backends Default to OPPOSITE Modes</h5>
                    <p>The <strong>log (file) backend defaults to <code>blocking</code> mode</strong> — every request waits for the write. The separate <strong>webhook backend (<code>--audit-webhook-config-file</code>) defaults to <code>batch</code> mode</strong> — events are buffered and sent asynchronously. These are opposite defaults for two different backends, and it's an easy fact to mix up under exam pressure: don't assume both backends behave the same way just because they share the word "mode."</p>
                </div>

                <h4>omitStages in Practice — Halving Event Volume</h4>
                <p>Every request that reaches <code>ResponseComplete</code> was ALSO captured at <code>RequestReceived</code> — meaning a naive policy writes two events per request. Suppressing the early, less-informative stage is the standard exam pattern:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — omitStages: suppress the RequestReceived duplicate at the policy level</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1
kind: Policy
# Global omitStages — applies to every rule below unless a rule
# overrides it with its own omitStages
omitStages:
  - "RequestReceived"
rules:
  # Secrets: still fully logged at ResponseComplete, just not
  # duplicated at RequestReceived
  - level: RequestResponse
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["secrets"]

  # Per-rule omitStages also works — this rule keeps
  # RequestReceived events even though the global policy omits them
  - level: Metadata
    omitStages: []
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["pods"]

  # Default catch-all — MUST be last (Fact 2)
  - level: Metadata</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔧 Cross-Reference Correction — §28.1's Backend Table</h5>
                    <p>§28.1's "Audit Backend Types" table lists the Log Backend's <strong>Mode</strong> as "N/A (always async I/O)." Per the official facts above, that's imprecise: the log backend DOES have a configurable batch/blocking mode — it simply defaults to <strong>blocking</strong> (synchronous), which is the opposite default from the webhook backend's <strong>batch</strong> (asynchronous) default. Read that table's Mode row as "not commonly tuned on the exam," not as "no mode exists." Aside from that one row, 28.1 and 28.2's use of LEVEL and STAGE terminology is consistent with the official facts in this section — no other conflation found.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

