                <h5>🛡️ Detecting Audit Log Tampering — When Attackers Try to Cover Their Tracks</h5>
                <p>A sophisticated attacker who gains node access may try to <strong>delete or modify audit logs</strong> to hide their activity. Here's how to detect tampering and prevent it:</p>
                <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
  TAMPERING TECHNIQUES                         DETECTION COUNTERMEASURES
  ────────────────────                         ──────────────────────────

  🔴 Technique 1: Delete audit log files       ✅ Countermeasure 1: Ship off-node
  rm /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log               Webhook backend → SIEM in real-time.
                                                 Even if local files are deleted, the
                                                 SIEM has a complete copy.

  🔴 Technique 2: Truncate the audit log       ✅ Countermeasure 2: File integrity
  echo "" > /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log        Falco rule detects writes to
                                                 /var/log/kubernetes/. Inotify
                                                 watches on audit log directory.

  🔴 Technique 3: Modify individual events      ✅ Countermeasure 3: Immutable storage
  sed -i 's/"code":403/"code":200/' audit.log   Write audit logs to append-only
                                                 storage (S3 Object Lock, GCS
                                                 retention policy).

  🔴 Technique 4: Stop API server, remove       ✅ Countermeasure 4: CIS check
     --audit-log-path flag, restart              kube-bench CIS 1.2.22: "Ensure
                                                 --audit-log-path is set." Alert
                                                 if API server restarts without it.

  🔴 Technique 5: Flood audit logs with noise    ✅ Countermeasure 5: Rate monitoring
     (bury malicious events in noise)            Monitor audit log volume. Alert on
                                                 10x spike. Use log sampling for
                                                 high-volume events.

  🔑 DEFENSE IN DEPTH: Each countermeasure alone can be bypassed.
     All five together make tampering practically impossible:
     SIEM (off-node) + Falco (detection) + Immutable storage +
     CIS checks (prevention) + Volume monitoring.
</pre></div>

                <h5>🚨 CKS Exam Gotcha — Audit Logging Traps That Trip Up Candidates</h5>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Audit Logging Pitfalls on the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p><strong>1. Static pod manifest editing:</strong> When you edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>, the kubelet auto-restarts the API server. Your <code>kubectl</code> connection WILL drop. Wait 30-60 seconds and reconnect. <strong>2. Volume mounts are mandatory:</strong> Adding <code>--audit-policy-file</code> without adding the corresponding <code>volume</code> and <code>volumeMount</code> means the API server can't find the policy file — and audit logging silently fails. Always check ALL three: flag + volumeMount + volume. <strong>3. Wrong policy path:</strong> The policy file must be accessible INSIDE the API server container. If you mount it at <code>/etc/kubernetes/audit</code> but the flag says <code>--audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml</code>, the paths don't match. <strong>4. Forgetting omitStages:</strong> Without <code>omitStages: ["RequestReceived"]</code>, you get TWO audit events per request (RequestReceived + ResponseComplete). This doubles log volume with redundant data. <strong>5. Log rotation on exam:</strong> The CKS exam might give you a small disk. Set <code>--audit-log-maxsize=10</code> (small, 10MB) and <code>--audit-log-maxbackup=5</code> to avoid filling the disk during testing.</p>
                </div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch28-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Audit logs are the record that survives every other failure</strong> — when RBAC is bypassed, admission is circumvented, and runtime detection doesn't fire, the audit log is still there, because it's generated by the API server itself, not by a control you configured elsewhere (28.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Policy configuration has 3 mandatory pieces and one ordering rule.</strong> <code>--audit-policy-file</code> + its volumeMount + its volume must all match, and the catch-all rule must be LAST — first-match-wins means anything after it is unreachable (28.2, 28.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>jq is the exam's real audit-logging skill.</strong> <code>select(.objectRef.resource=="secrets")</code>, <code>select(.objectRef.subresource=="exec")</code>, and <code>select(.responseStatus.code >= 400)</code> answer nearly every "who did what" scenario (28.3, 28.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Local logs alone don't survive a compromised node.</strong> Webhook shipping to a SIEM is the off-node safety net — batch mode trades some events for speed, blocking mode trades cluster availability for zero loss (28.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>LEVEL and STAGE are not the same axis.</strong> LEVEL is a per-rule policy choice controlling how much is captured; STAGE is a built-in lifecycle point controlling when it's captured. A policy with <code>rules: []</code> is illegal — you always need at least a catch-all (28.6).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch28-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Audit policy requires mount + flag:</strong> Adding <code>--audit-policy-file</code> to the API server manifest without the corresponding volumeMount and volume means the file can't be found — and audit logging silently fails. Always check all three: flag, volumeMount, volume.</li>
                    <li><strong>omitStages saves disk but loses data:</strong> Omitting <code>RequestReceived</code> is standard (prevents duplicate events). Omitting <code>ResponseComplete</code> loses the response body. Omitting <code>Panic</code> means API server crashes aren't logged at all.</li>
                    <li><strong>"Level" and "stage" are NOT interchangeable words.</strong> A wrong-answer option that says "set the level to RequestReceived" or "the ResponseComplete level" is testing whether you've confused the two independent axes — level controls detail, stage controls timing (28.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>An audit policy with zero rules is not valid.</strong> <code>rules: []</code> is REJECTED by the API server, not silently treated as "audit nothing." You always need at least one rule, even if it's just a bare <code>- level: None</code> catch-all (28.6).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch28-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Type the 3-piece checklist before you touch YAML:</strong> flag, volumeMount, volume. If a task fails to produce log output, this is where 90% of exam mistakes live (28.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Write the catch-all rule FIRST in your head, LAST in the file.</strong> Draft specific-to-general mentally, then type general-to-specific in reverse so the broad <code>level: Metadata</code> lands at the bottom (28.2, 28.6).</li>
                    <li><strong>Keep one jq skeleton memorized:</strong> <code>cat audit.log | jq 'select(.FIELD=="VALUE")'</code> — swap in <code>.objectRef.resource</code>, <code>.objectRef.subresource</code>, <code>.user.username</code>, or <code>.responseStatus.code</code> depending on the question (28.3, 28.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Say "level = how much, stage = when" out loud</strong> before answering any question that uses both words — it's the fastest way to avoid the exam's favorite swap (28.6).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch28-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 28)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">audit logging — practice until automatic</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check if audit logging is enabled</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml | grep audit
