            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch28-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 28 — Lab Q&amp;A (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">11 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Enable audit logging on a kubeadm cluster. What files do you edit and what flags do you add?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code> (static pod). Add: <code>--audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml</code>, <code>--audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</code>, <code>--audit-log-maxage=30</code>, <code>--audit-log-maxbackup=10</code>, <code>--audit-log-maxsize=100</code>. Add volumeMount for <code>/etc/kubernetes</code> (read-only) and volume for <code>/var/log/kubernetes</code> (hostPath). Add volumeMount + volume for the policy file path. The kubelet auto-restarts the API server.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write an audit policy that logs all Secret operations at Metadata level and Pod changes at RequestResponse level.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1
kind: Policy
rules:
- level: Metadata
  resources:
  - group: ""
    resources: ["secrets"]
- level: RequestResponse
  resources:
  - group: ""
    resources: ["pods"]
- level: None
  omitStages: ["RequestReceived"]</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span><div class="eq-question">What are the four audit stages and at what point in request processing does each fire? Which should you typically omit?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>RequestReceived:</strong> Fires as soon as the API server receives the request. No authn/authz has occurred yet. <strong>ResponseStarted:</strong> Fires when response headers are sent. For long-running requests (watch), this fires before the response body. <strong>ResponseComplete:</strong> Fires when the full response (including body) has been sent. <strong>Panic:</strong> Fires if the API server panics (crashes) while processing the request. Typically omit <code>RequestReceived</code> because it duplicates <code>ResponseComplete</code> and doubles log volume without adding value.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Audit stages control WHEN events are written. The CKS exam tests this: <code>omitStages: ["RequestReceived"]</code> reduces log volume by ~50% without losing information. Never omit <code>ResponseComplete</code> — it contains the response code which is critical for security analysis.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span><div class="eq-question">How do you configure audit log webhook to ship events to an external SIEM? What additional resource is needed?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Add <code>--audit-webhook-config-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-webhook.yaml</code> to API server. The webhook config is a kubeconfig-style file pointing to an external audit backend:</p><pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Config
clusters:
- name: splunk
  cluster:
    server: https://splunk.easypj.com:8088/services/collector/event
    certificate-authority: /etc/kubernetes/pki/splunk-ca.crt
users:
- name: audit-webhook
  user:
    token: &lt;splunk-hec-token&gt;
current-context: audit
contexts:
- context:
    cluster: splunk
    user: audit-webhook
  name: audit</code></pre><p>Also configure the audit policy to use webhook backend. Both <code>--audit-log-path</code> (file) and <code>--audit-webhook-config-file</code> (webhook) can be used simultaneously for defense in depth.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Webhook audit backend is critical for production — it ships logs off-node in real-time. If an attacker compromises a node and deletes local audit logs, the SIEM still has the complete record. On the CKS exam, you may need to configure the webhook backend or explain why it's more secure than file-only logging.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span><div class="eq-question">How do you query audit logs to find: (a) all Secret reads by any user, (b) all failed authentication attempts, (c) all pod exec operations?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-bash"># (a) All Secret reads
cat audit.log | jq 'select(.objectRef.resource=="secrets" and .verb=="get")'
# (b) Failed authentication (HTTP 401)
cat audit.log | jq 'select(.responseStatus.code==401)'
# (c) Pod exec (subresource pods/exec)
cat audit.log | jq 'select(.objectRef.subresource=="exec")'
# Combined: Secret reads by a specific user in last 24h
cat audit.log | jq 'select(.objectRef.resource=="secrets" and .verb=="get" and .user.username=="system:serviceaccount:default:compromised-sa")'</code></pre><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Audit log querying is a CKS practical skill. The key fields: <code>.objectRef.resource</code> (what), <code>.verb</code> (action), <code>.user.username</code> (who), <code>.responseStatus.code</code> (result), <code>.stageTimestamp</code> (when). On the exam, you may be given a scenario ("find who read secrets at 3 AM") and need to construct the jq query.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span><div class="eq-question">What's the difference between audit log levels: None, Metadata, Request, and RequestResponse? When would you use each?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>None:</strong> Don't log. Use for high-volume resources you don't need to audit. <strong>Metadata:</strong> Log who, what, when, and response code — but NOT the request/response body. ~200 bytes per event. Use for high-volume reads. <strong>Request:</strong> Metadata + request body. ~2KB per event. Use when you need to see WHAT was sent. <strong>RequestResponse:</strong> Everything including response body. Can be very large. Use only for security-critical operations (Secrets access, RBAC changes, pod exec). The CKS pattern: Metadata for most resources, RequestResponse for Secrets and RBAC.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Audit levels are a storage/visibility tradeoff. On the CKS exam: (1) use <code>Metadata</code> for broad coverage, (2) use <code>RequestResponse</code> for security-sensitive resources, (3) use <code>None</code> for high-volume health checks that would flood the logs. Never use <code>RequestResponse</code> for all resources — your disk fills in minutes.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span><div class="eq-question">You add audit logging and the disk fills within hours. What audit policy rules would you add to reduce log volume while keeping security visibility?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-yaml">rules:
# Keep: Security-critical at RequestResponse
- level: RequestResponse
  resources: [{group: "", resources: ["secrets", "serviceaccounts"]}]
  verbs: ["create", "update", "patch", "delete"]
# Keep: RBAC changes
- level: RequestResponse
  resources: [{group: "rbac.authorization.k8s.io", resources: ["*"]}]
# Noise reduction: Health checks at None
- level: None
  nonResourceURLs: ["/healthz", "/readyz", "/livez"]
# Noise reduction: System component reads at None
- level: None
  users: ["system:kube-proxy", "system:kube-scheduler", "system:apiserver"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
# Default: Everything else at Metadata
- level: Metadata
  omitStages: ["RequestReceived"]</code></pre><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Audit log noise reduction is a production necessity. The CKS pattern: (1) keep security-critical resources at high verbosity, (2) explicitly suppress noisy system components, (3) default everything else to Metadata. Health checks (kubelet hitting /healthz every 10s) can generate 8,640 events/day per node — suppress them.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span><div class="eq-question">An attacker deleted audit log files from <code>/var/log/kubernetes/</code>. How would you detect this and what defense prevents it?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>Detection:</strong> Falco rule alerts on file deletion in <code>/var/log/kubernetes/</code>. AIDE detects missing/changed files. Cloud audit logs show "someone accessed the node." <strong>Prevention:</strong> (1) Ship logs off-node via webhook backend in real-time — the attacker can't delete what's already on the SIEM. (2) Set <code>chattr +a /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</code> (append-only) so even root can't delete without removing the attribute. (3) Mount <code>/var/log/kubernetes/</code> as a separate filesystem with restricted permissions. (4) Run auditd on the node to detect file deletion syscalls.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Log deletion is the "Repudiation" threat in STRIDE — the attacker destroys evidence. The CKS defense is: ship logs off-node in real-time (webhook), make local logs append-only (chattr), and detect tampering (Falco/AIDE). On the exam, webhook audit backend is the primary answer for preventing log tampering.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span><div class="eq-question">How do you verify that audit logging is actually working after configuration? What commands confirm events are being written?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Check API server has audit flags
ps aux | grep kube-apiserver | grep audit
# 2. Check audit log file exists and is growing
ls -la /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log
tail -f /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log
# 3. Create an event and verify it appears
kubectl create secret generic audit-test --from-literal=test=value
cat /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log | jq 'select(.objectRef.name=="audit-test")'
kubectl delete secret audit-test
# 4. Check audit policy is loaded
kubectl get --raw /healthz/poststarthook/audit-policy</code></pre><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Verification is critical on the CKS exam. After configuring audit logging, always: (1) create a test resource, (2) verify the event appears in the audit log, (3) delete the test resource. If the event doesn't appear, check: is the policy file mounted? Is the log directory writable? Is the API server running with the new flags?</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span><div class="eq-question">You need to forward audit logs from 50 clusters to a central SIEM. Design the architecture: what runs on each cluster, what's centralized, and how do you handle network partitions?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>Per-cluster:</strong> (1) Audit policy configured per cluster (may differ by sensitivity). (2) Two backends: file (local, for fallback) + webhook (remote, to SIEM). (3) Fluentd/Fluent Bit sidecar on API server node ships local logs as secondary path. <strong>Centralized:</strong> (1) SIEM (Splunk/ELK) receives and indexes all audit events. (2) Alerting rules: failed auth spikes, Secret reads by new SAs, RBAC changes. <strong>Network partition:</strong> Webhook backend buffers locally (file backend). When network is restored, Fluentd ships the backlog. No audit events are lost — the file backend is the safety net.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the enterprise audit architecture for CKS. The dual-backend pattern (webhook + file) is critical: webhook provides real-time SIEM integration, file provides resilience against network failures. On the exam, multi-cluster audit management tests your understanding of the 4Cs model: each cluster is independently configured but feeds into centralized monitoring.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q11</span><div class="eq-question">A scenario says: "Events for Secret reads should record who accessed them but the response body is unnecessary; separately, we want fewer duplicate events per request." Which two settings do you change, and which official term (level or stage) does each belong to?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>Setting 1 — LEVEL:</strong> Set Secret reads to <code>level: Metadata</code> (not <code>RequestResponse</code>) — Metadata captures who/what/when/response-code but never the response body, which is exactly "who accessed them, no body needed." <strong>Setting 2 — STAGE:</strong> Add <code>omitStages: ["RequestReceived"]</code> — this suppresses the early duplicate event generated at the RequestReceived stage, leaving only the ResponseComplete event per request.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This question is deliberately built to require BOTH axes in one scenario: reducing captured detail is a LEVEL change (per-rule, in the policy body), while reducing duplicate events per request is a STAGE change (omitStages, either global or per-rule). Conflating the two — e.g. thinking "omitStages" controls how much detail is captured, or that changing the level reduces duplicate events — is the exact trap 28.6 exists to prevent. Also remember: a policy can never have zero rules, and this rule must not be placed after the catch-all or it will never be evaluated.</p></div></details></div>
            </div>

