            <section class="section-block" id="s29-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">29.5 CKS Exam Tasks — Modifying Falco Rules</h3>
                <p>The CKS exam tests Falco rule modifications. Memorize these patterns — they cover 90% of exam scenarios.</p>

                <h4>Pattern 1: Add Exception with append</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Suppress "Terminal shell" alerts for health probes in kube-system</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># /etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml
- rule: Terminal shell in container
  append: true
  condition: >
    and not proc.cmdline contains "health-check"
    and not proc.cmdline contains "readiness"
    and not k8s.ns.name = "kube-system"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 2: Change Priority with override</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Escalate "Launch Privileged Container" from NOTICE to CRITICAL</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># /etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml
- rule: Launch Privileged Container
  override:
    condition: spawned_process and container and container.privileged=true
    priority: CRITICAL
    # Was NOTICE (informational), now CRITICAL (immediate response)
    # Use override when you want to COMPLETELY replace the rule</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 3: Custom Rule for Specific Behavior</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Detect any file write to /tmp/ (common malware dropper location)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># /etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml
- rule: File Write to /tmp in easypj
  desc: Detect writing files to /tmp (common malware dropper)
  condition: >
    evt.type=openat and evt.is_open_write=true and
    container.name=easypj and fd.name startswith /tmp/
  output: 📝 Write to /tmp: %fd.name by %proc.cmdline in pod %k8s.pod.name
  priority: WARNING
  tags: [easypj, filesystem, CKS]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>CKS Exam Troubleshooting — Common Issues</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Custom rule not firing</td><td>Rule file not loaded in falco.yaml</td><td>Add to <code>rules_file:</code> in /etc/falco/falco.yaml</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Rule syntax error</td><td>YAML indentation or missing colon</td><td>Run <code>falco --dry-run</code> to validate</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Override lost original protection</td><td>Used <code>override:</code> instead of <code>append: true</code></td><td>Use append unless you intend full replacement</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Container metadata missing</td><td>Falco not running with hostPID</td><td>Ensure <code>hostPID: true</code> in DaemonSet spec</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Decision Tree — append vs override: When to Use Which</h4>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="tree-title">Falco Rule Modification Strategy</div>
<pre>
  START: I need to modify a Falco rule
  │
  ├─ Am I adding an EXCEPTION to reduce noise?
  │  └─ YES → Use append: true
  │     Example: "Terminal shell fires on health probes → add exception"
  │
  ├─ Am I changing the rule's SCOPE? (different containers, namespaces)
  │  └─ YES → Use append: true (add more conditions)
  │     Example: "Only apply this rule to namespace 'easypj'"
  │
  ├─ Am I changing the PRIORITY?
  │  └─ YES → Use override: (replace rule entirely)
  │     WARNING: This replaces the ENTIRE rule — include the original condition!
  │
  ├─ Am I writing an entirely NEW detection?
  │  └─ YES → Write a new rule (don't touch existing rules)
  │     Example: "Detect curl/wget downloads in easypj containers"
  │
  └─ ⚠️ RULE OF THUMB:
     append = "add to existing" (safe)
     override = "replace entirely" (dangerous — must include original logic)
     new rule = "create from scratch" (safest — doesn't affect existing rules)
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s29-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">29.6 Behavioral Analytics — Detecting Anomalies Beyond Signature Rules</h3>
                <p>The official CKS curriculum requires you to <strong>perform behavioral analytics to detect malicious activities</strong>. Signature-based detection (Falco rules matching "shell spawned") catches known attacks. Behavioral analytics goes further — establishing a baseline of normal behavior and flagging deviations: a pod normally making 10 outbound connections/minute suddenly making 500 is anomalous even if each connection looks legitimate.</p>

                <h4>Signature-Based vs Behavioral Detection</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>How It Works</th><th>Catches</th><th>Misses</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Signature-Based</strong></td><td>Matches known patterns: "nc executed", "shell spawned"</td><td>Known attack tools</td><td>Zero-days, custom malware, slow exfil</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Behavioral (Metric)</strong></td><td>Learns normal pod behavior (CPU, net, syscalls/sec), alerts on deviations</td><td>Unknown attacks, data exfil, crypto mining</td><td>Attacks mimicking normal behavior</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Behavioral (Syscall)</strong></td><td>Profiles normal syscall sequences; alerts on new syscalls</td><td>Exploit attempts, container escape</td><td>Attacks using allowed syscalls</td></tr>
                </tbody></table></div>

                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 CKS Exam: Behavioral Analytics</h5><p>The exam may present Falco alerts + metrics and ask you to identify anomalous behavior. Pattern: normal pod = 0-2 alerts/day. Compromised pod = 50+ alerts in 10 minutes. Look for SPIKES in event frequency, unusual source IPs, or pods generating alerts that normally produce none.</p></div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>🔧 Under the Hood — How Falco Evaluates Rules Against Kernel Events: libsinsp State Machine → FilterCheck → Priority Bands → Alert Dispatch</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                <h5>🗺️ Inside the Falco Rule Engine — How a Syscall Becomes an Alert</h5>
                <p>When a container runs <code>cat /etc/shadow</code>, the kernel generates an <code>openat</code> syscall event. Falco's libscap captures this raw event, and libsinsp enriches it with container metadata. Then the rule engine evaluates it against every loaded rule. Here's exactly how that evaluation works:</p>
                <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   FALCO RULE EVALUATION — Single Syscall Through the Engine               │
  │                                                                           │
  │  SYSCALL: openat(AT_FDCWD, "/etc/shadow", O_RDONLY)                      │
  │  FROM:    container_id=abc123, pid=4567, uid=0                            │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │ 1. LIBSCAP: Raw event capture + enrichment                        │    │
  │  │    • Captures: syscall type (openat), args (fd, path, flags)     │    │
  │  │    • Enriches: container_id → container.name = "easypj"          │    │
  │  │               pid → process tree → proc.cmdline = "cat /etc..."  │    │
  │  │               uid → user.name = "root"                           │    │
  │  │               cgroup → k8s.ns.name, k8s.pod.name                 │    │
  │  │    • Output: structured event with ALL fields populated          │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │ 2. LIBSINSP: Stateful filtering & field resolution                │    │
  │  │    • Maintains state: which processes exist, which FDs are open  │    │
  │  │    • Resolves fields like fd.name (requires tracking open()      │    │
  │  │      syscalls and mapping fd numbers to paths)                   │    │
  │  │    • Resolves macros: shell_binaries → [bash, sh, zsh, ...]      │    │
  │  │    • Resolves lists: easypj_sensitive_files → [shadow, ...]      │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │ 3. RULE MATCHING: Sequential evaluation of all loaded rules       │    │
  │  │    Rule 1: "Terminal shell in container"                         │    │
  │  │      condition: spawned_process and container and shell           │    │
  │  │      → evt.type = "openat" (not a process event) → SKIP          │    │
  │  │    Rule 2: "Read sensitive file untrusted"                       │    │
  │  │      condition: open_read and container and                      │    │
  │  │                 fd.name startswith /etc/                         │    │
  │  │      → evt.type = "openat" ✅ (open_read macro matches)          │    │
  │  │      → container.name = "easypj" ✅                               │    │
  │  │      → fd.name = "/etc/shadow" ✅ (startswith /etc/)             │    │
  │  │      → MATCH! Rule fires.                                         │    │
  │  │    Rules 3-80: SKIPPED                                           │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │ 4. OUTPUT FORMATTING: Template substitution                       │    │
  │  │    Template: "Sensitive file opened (user=%user.name             │    │
  │  │              cmd=%proc.cmdline file=%fd.name)"                   │    │
  │  │    Resolved: "Sensitive file opened (user=root                   │    │
  │  │              cmd=cat /etc/shadow file=/etc/shadow)"              │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │       │                                                                    │
  │       ▼                                                                    │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │ 5. DISPATCH: Route alert based on priority + output config        │    │
  │  │    priority=CRITICAL → ALL channels (stdout + file + webhooks)   │    │
  │  │    priority=WARNING  → file + Slack (minimumpriority: WARNING)   │    │
  │  │    priority=DEBUG    → stdout only (development)                 │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                                                                           │
  │  🔑 Falco evaluates EVERY syscall against EVERY rule. With 80             │
  │     rules and 10,000 syscalls/sec → 800,000 evaluations/sec.              │
  │     Efficient conditions matter — check container.name first.             │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre></div>

                <h5>🎯 Falco Priority Decision Framework — When to Use Which Level</h5>
                <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
  PRIORITY LEVELS — From Loudest (EMERGENCY) to Quietest (DEBUG)
  (showing the 6 most CKS-relevant; Falco's full official set also has ALERT and ERROR)

  🔴 EMERGENCY  — Cluster-wide catastrophe
     Use when:  etcd data corruption, API server cert expired
     Action:    PAGE ON-CALL IMMEDIATELY (PagerDuty, phone call)

  🔴 CRITICAL   — Active attack or severe policy violation
     Use when:  Shell in prod container, sensitive file read,
                netcat backdoor, crypto miner, privilege escalation
     Action:    Page on-call within 5 minutes (PagerDuty)

  🟠 WARNING    — Suspicious but could be legitimate
     Use when:  Unexpected outbound connection, shell in dev,
                curl to K8s API from container, package manager use
     Action:    Slack notification, investigate within 1 hour

  🟡 NOTICE     — Informational, might indicate a problem
     Use when:  Privileged container launched, new namespace created
     Action:    Logged, reviewed in weekly security review

  🟢 INFO       — Routine operational data
     Use when:  Regular deployment, config change, cert renewal
     Action:    Logged for audit trail

  ⚪ DEBUG      — Development/troubleshooting only
     Use when:  Testing new rules, verbose event logging
     Action:    Never sent to production channels

  🔑 CKS EXAM: CRITICAL and WARNING are most commonly tested.
     CRITICAL = immediate response. WARNING = investigate.
     EMERGENCY is rarely used in exam scenarios.
</pre></div>

                <h5>🚨 CKS Exam Gotcha — Falco Rule Writing Pitfalls</h5>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Falco Rule Mistakes That Fail the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p><strong>1. Editing default rules file:</strong> Always edit <code>/etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml</code>, NEVER <code>/etc/falco/falco_rules.yaml</code>. The local file survives upgrades. <strong>2. Using override for exceptions:</strong> When the exam asks to "add an exception," use <code>append: true</code>. Override REPLACES the entire rule and removes existing protection. <strong>3. YAML indentation in conditions:</strong> The <code>&gt;</code> (folded block scalar) handles indentation differently than <code>|</code>. For multi-line conditions with <code>and</code>/<code>or</code>, use <code>&gt;</code> to fold into a single line. <strong>4. Mistyped rule names:</strong> Both <code>append: true</code> and <code>override:</code> reference an EXISTING rule by name. A typo creates a NEW rule instead — your changes silently do nothing. Verify with <code>falco --list | grep your-rule</code>. <strong>5. Not restarting Falco:</strong> Edit rules → restart Falco pod → verify with <code>falco --list</code>. Rule changes don't take effect until restart.</p>
                </div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Falco fills the kernel-level blind spot audit logs can't see</strong> — it monitors syscalls in real time via libsinsp/libscap (driver: kmod/eBPF/modern eBPF), deployed as a privileged DaemonSet with <code>hostPID: true</code> on every node (29.1, 29.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Rules are YAML — rule, desc, condition, output, priority, tags</strong> — reusable via macros and lists. <code>append: true</code> ADDS a condition safely; <code>override:</code> REPLACES the entire rule and can silently remove the original protection (29.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Always edit <code>/etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml</code>, never the default file.</strong> Validate with <code>falco --dry-run</code> and confirm with <code>falco --list | grep &lt;rule&gt;</code> before trusting any change (29.3, 29.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Know the top 10 of Falco's 80+ built-in rules</strong> — shell spawn, sensitive file read, netcat backdoor, privileged container, K8s API contact from a pod — and their default priorities (29.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Behavioral analytics goes beyond signatures.</strong> A baseline of normal syscall/network volume flags anomalies — like 500 outbound connections/min against a normal baseline of 10 — that signature rules alone would miss (29.6).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>append: true ADDS, doesn't replace:</strong> <code>append: true</code> appends <code>and ...</code> to the END of the existing condition. Without <code>append: true</code>, you're OVERRIDING the entire rule — which means you must re-specify the full condition. Appending is safer for exceptions.</li>
                    <li><strong>falco_rules.local.yaml is the only file you edit:</strong> The CKS exam will have a default <code>falco_rules.yaml</code>. DO NOT edit it. All custom rules, exceptions, and overrides go in <code>/etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml</code>. Editing the default file may get overwritten on Falco update.</li>
                    <li><strong>Rule names are case-sensitive:</strong> <code>rule: Terminal shell in container</code> must match exactly when using append/override. A typo creates a NEW rule instead of modifying the existing one.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Classify the task before you type YAML.</strong> "Add an exception" → <code>append: true</code>. "Change severity" → <code>override:</code> (and re-include the original condition!). "Brand-new detection" → write a new rule.</li>
                    <li><strong>Never touch the default file.</strong> All changes go in <code>/etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml</code> — the default <code>falco_rules.yaml</code> can be overwritten on upgrade.</li>
                    <li><strong>Validate before you trust it:</strong> <code>falco --dry-run</code> for syntax, <code>falco --list | grep &lt;rule&gt;</code> to confirm it loaded, then watch it fire with <code>tail -f /var/log/falco/alerts.log</code> or <code>journalctl -fu falco</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Memorize the 5-field rule skeleton</strong> — <code>rule</code>, <code>desc</code>, <code>condition</code>, <code>output</code>, <code>priority</code> (+ <code>tags</code>) — so you can write a custom rule from memory under time pressure.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

