            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 29 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a Falco rule that detects any execution of 'nc' (netcat) inside a container in the production namespace.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">- rule: Netcat Execution in Production
  desc: Detect nc/ncat execution in prod namespace
  condition: >
    spawned_process and
    proc.name in (nc, ncat, netcat) and
    k8s.ns.name = production
  output: "nc executed in production (user=%user.name pod=%k8s.pod.name cmd=%proc.cmdline)"
  priority: WARNING
  tags: [network, mitre_execution]</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you add an exception to an existing Falco rule without modifying the original rule file?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Create a rule in <code>/etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml</code> using <code>append: true</code> and add <code>and not ...</code> to the condition. Example: to exclude a specific container from the "Terminal shell in container" rule, write: <code>- rule: Terminal shell in container</code> with <code>append: true</code> and <code>condition: and not container.name = "allowed-debugger"</code>. This appends to the existing condition rather than replacing it. Always put custom rules in falco_rules.local.yaml — never edit the default falco_rules.yaml.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a Falco rule that detects any write to <code>/etc/</code> inside a container. Why is this dangerous?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">- rule: Write to etc directory
  desc: Detect any write to /etc inside container
  condition: >
    evt.type in (open,openat,creat) and
    evt.is_open_write=true and
    fd.name startswith /etc/ and
    container.id != host
  output: "File write to /etc/ detected (file=%fd.name user=%user.name)"
  priority: CRITICAL
  tags: [filesystem, mitre_persistence]</code></pre>
                    <p>Dangerous because: /etc contains cron jobs, init scripts, pam configs, and ssh keys. Writing to /etc is a persistence technique — attackers add backdoor users or scheduled tasks.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Detects both system file modification attempts inside containers.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What Falco condition field distinguishes events from containers vs the host? Why does this matter?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><code>container.id != host</code> ensures the rule only matches events inside containers, not on the host. Without this, Falco would alert on every host-level nc/wget/nmap execution (legitimate admin activity). <code>container.id = host</code> matches host-level events specifically. Always scope container-related rules with <code>container.id != host</code> to reduce noise.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Falco monitors both containers AND the host. Without proper scoping, you get false positives from system daemons and admin activity. On the CKS exam, always add <code>container.id != host</code> to container-specific rules.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Falco alerts on "Terminal shell in container" for a legitimate debugging pod. Write the exception rule WITHOUT modifying the default rule file.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">- rule: Terminal shell in container
  append: true
  condition: and not k8s.pod.name startswith "debug-"
  desc: Exclude debugging pods from terminal shell alerts</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>append: true is the key — it adds to the existing condition rather than replacing it. The pod name prefix pattern allows all "debug-*" pods to use shells without alerts. On the CKS exam, always use append: true for exceptions and place them in falco_rules.local.yaml.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are Falco's priority levels and what do they mean? Which tag maps to MITRE ATT&amp;CK?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Priorities: EMERGENCY (system-wide panic), ALERT (immediate action), CRITICAL (suspicious behavior — likely attack), ERROR (misconfiguration), WARNING (potentially suspicious), NOTICE (unusual but not suspicious), INFORMATIONAL (normal operations), DEBUG. MITRE mapping: use tags like <code>mitre_execution</code>, <code>mitre_persistence</code>, <code>mitre_credential_access</code>. These map Falco detections to MITRE ATT&amp;CK tactics for SOC integration. Most CKS rules use WARNING or CRITICAL.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Priority levels determine alert routing. CRITICAL and above should go to on-call. WARNING goes to the security dashboard. On the CKS exam, use CRITICAL for definite attacks (netcat, shell spawn, file write to /etc) and WARNING for potentially suspicious activity.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you validate Falco rules before applying them? What command checks syntax without starting Falco?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">falco --dry-run  # Validates all rules in default paths
falco -r /etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml --dry-run  # Validate specific file
falco --list  # List all loaded rules (validates + shows rules)
falco --list | grep -i "my-custom-rule"  # Check specific rule loaded</code></pre>
                    <p>Always run <code>--dry-run</code> before restarting Falco. A syntax error in falco_rules.local.yaml prevents Falco from starting, and you lose ALL runtime detection until fixed.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Rule validation is a CKS practical skill. A typo in a rule can take down Falco entirely, leaving the cluster with no runtime detection. On the exam, always validate with --dry-run before declaring the task complete.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a Falco rule that detects reading of <code>/etc/shadow</code> inside a container and maps to MITRE Credential Access (TA0006).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">- rule: Read sensitive file /etc/shadow
  desc: Detect attempts to read /etc/shadow
  condition: >
    evt.type in (open,openat) and
    fd.name = /etc/shadow and
    container.id != host
  output: "Sensitive file read: /etc/shadow (user=%user.name pod=%k8s.pod.name)"
  priority: CRITICAL
  tags: [filesystem, mitre_credential_access, T1003]</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>/etc/shadow contains password hashes — reading it is credential dumping. This rule maps to MITRE T1003 (OS Credential Dumping). On the CKS exam, including MITRE tags shows deep security knowledge and helps integrate Falco with SIEM correlation rules.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does Falco detect container escape attempts? What syscalls does it monitor?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Falco detects escapes by monitoring dangerous syscalls and namespace changes: <code>ptrace</code> (attach to host process), <code>nsenter</code> (enter host namespaces), <code>mount</code> (mount host filesystems), <code>unshare</code> (leave container namespaces), <code>setns</code> (join other namespaces). The built-in rule "Launch Sensitive Mount Container" detects privileged containers with dangerous volume mounts. "Container Drift Detected" detects new executables being created inside containers.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Falco uses kernel instrumentation (eBPF or kernel module) to watch every syscall. Unlike Seccomp which blocks syscalls, Falco reports them. This is detection, not prevention. On the CKS exam, Falco is your runtime DETECTION tool — use it alongside prevention tools (Seccomp, AppArmor).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Falco is generating 10,000 alerts/hour. You need to reduce noise while keeping security visibility. What strategies do you apply?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(1) <strong>Add exceptions:</strong> Use append:true to exclude known-good containers (monitoring agents, CI/CD runners). (2) <strong>Scope by namespace:</strong> Add <code>k8s.ns.name = production</code> to rules — only alert on prod. (3) <strong>Adjust priority:</strong> Downgrade noisy rules from CRITICAL to WARNING. (4) <strong>Rate limiting:</strong> Configure Falco's <code>rate</code> and <code>max-burst</code> in falco.yaml. (5) <strong>Output filtering:</strong> Use <code>program_output</code> to send only CRITICAL to on-call, WARNING to SIEM. Never reduce rule sensitivity — add exceptions for specific use cases instead of weakening the rule.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Alert fatigue is the #1 reason organizations disable Falco. The CKS approach: add targeted exceptions, not broad rule changes. On the exam, never suggest disabling a rule — always add specific exceptions or scope adjustments.</p></div>
