            <section class="section-block" id="s30-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">30.1 The Incident Response Lifecycle in Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes incident response follows a <strong>6-phase lifecycle</strong>: Detect → Contain → Investigate → Eradicate → Recover → Learn. Unlike traditional server IR, Kubernetes adds unique challenges: pods are ephemeral (delete them and evidence is gone), attackers can move laterally through the ServiceAccount token, and compromised pods can be replaced by Deployments before you investigate.</p>

                <h4>Incident Response Flowchart — Falco Alert to Resolution</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Complete IR Flow — 6 Phases from Detection to Learning</div>
<pre>
  🚨 ALERT: Falco detects shell in easypj pod at 03:14:05
       │
       ▼
  ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
  ║  PHASE 1: DETECT — Confirm the alert is real               ║
  ║  kubectl describe pod easypj-xxx                            ║
  ║  kubectl logs easypj-xxx --tail=50                          ║
  ║  → Check: is this a known maintenance task or an attack?   ║
  ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
       │ ❌ Confirmed: NOT legitimate
       ▼
  ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
  ║  PHASE 2: CONTAIN — Stop the bleeding                       ║
  ║  Remove pod from Service (kubectl label)                    ║
  ║  Apply emergency deny-all NetworkPolicy                     ║
  ║  → Pod still running but ISOLATED — can't reach/be reached  ║
  ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
       │
       ▼
  ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
  ║  PHASE 3: INVESTIGATE — Capture forensic evidence           ║
  ║  kubectl get pod -o yaml > forensic.yaml                    ║
  ║  kubectl exec -- env > env.txt                              ║
  ║  kubectl exec -- ps aux > processes.txt                     ║
  ║  Check audit logs for this SA's activity                    ║
  ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
       │
       ▼
  ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
  ║  PHASE 4: ERADICATE — Remove the threat                     ║
  ║  kubectl delete pod easypj-xxx --force                      ║
  ║  Revoke compromised ServiceAccount token                    ║
  ║  Rotate any secrets the pod had access to                   ║
  ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
       │
       ▼
  ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
  ║  PHASE 5: RECOVER — Restore normal operations               ║
  ║  Deployment creates clean replacement pod                    ║
  ║  Verify app is healthy and serving traffic                  ║
  ║  Monitor for 24 hours for recurrence                        ║
  ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
       │
       ▼
  ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
  ║  PHASE 6: LEARN — Prevent recurrence                        ║
  ║  Root cause analysis: how did attacker get in?              ║
  ║  Update: RBAC, admission policies, Falco rules               ║
  ║  Document: incident report, timeline, lessons learned       ║
  ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Kubernetes-Specific IR Challenges — Why Traditional IR Fails Here</h4>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Unique K8s IR Challenges That Break Traditional IR Playbooks</h5>
                    <p><strong>1. Ephemeral pods:</strong> Traditional IR says "preserve the server for forensics." In K8s, deleting a pod (even accidentally via Deployment rollback) destroys ALL evidence instantly. <strong>2. Self-healing Deployments:</strong> A compromised pod gets killed by a Deployment → a NEW pod spawns → the attacker is back. You must fix the DEPLOYMENT, not just the pod. <strong>3. Lateral movement via SA tokens:</strong> The compromised pod's ServiceAccount token can access the API server. An attacker can list pods, read secrets, and deploy new workloads from INSIDE the compromised container. <strong>4. Namespace isolation is weak:</strong> By default, pods can reach services in other namespaces. An attacker in namespace "easypj" can potentially access "kube-system" services. <strong>5. Evidence is distributed:</strong> Audit logs, pod logs, node logs, Falco alerts — all in different places. Correlating them manually is hard.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Decision Tree — Isolate vs Delete: Which Action When?</h4>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="tree-title">Incident Response Decision — Isolate or Delete?</div>
<pre>
  🚨 ALERT: Compromised pod detected
  │
  ├─ Is this a PRODUCTION pod serving live traffic?
  │  ├─ YES → Isolate (remove label) + let Deployment create clean replacement
  │  └─ NO (dev/staging) → Isolate for investigation at leisure
  │
  ├─ Do you NEED forensic evidence? (for compliance, legal, or RCA)
  │  ├─ YES → Isolate, capture forensics, THEN delete
  │  └─ NO (clear-cut crypto miner, no data access) → Delete immediately
  │
  ├─ Is the pod actively exfiltrating data RIGHT NOW?
  │  └─ YES → Isolate + apply egress-deny NetworkPolicy FIRST, then investigate
  │
  └─ 🟢 RULE OF THUMB:
     ALWAYS isolate first. You can always delete later.
     You can NEVER recover forensic evidence from a deleted pod.
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s30-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">30.2 Isolating a Compromised Pod Without Deleting It</h3>
                <p>The worst mistake in Kubernetes IR is <strong>deleting the pod before investigating</strong>. Once deleted, all forensic evidence — running processes, network connections, environment variables — is gone forever. Instead, <strong>isolate first</strong>: cut the pod's network, remove it from the Service, but keep it running for investigation.</p>

                <h4>Emergency Pod Isolation — Step-by-Step Command Sequence</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Isolate compromised pod while preserving forensic evidence</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># STEP 1: Identify the compromised pod
kubectl get pods -n easypj -o wide
# Note: pod name, IP, node, and container IDs

# STEP 2: Tag for quarantine, THEN remove from Service (stops traffic immediately!)
kubectl label pod easypj-django-abc123 -n easypj quarantine=true
kubectl label pod easypj-django-abc123 -n easypj app-
# "quarantine=true" gives the NetworkPolicy below a stable selector to
# target — it survives the next command. Removing the "app" label means
# Service selectors no longer match this pod; it stops receiving traffic
# INSTANTLY. (Order matters: if you selected on "app: easypj" instead,
# the policy would miss THIS pod and instead deny-all the healthy
# replicas that still carry that label.)

# STEP 3: Apply emergency deny-all NetworkPolicy (targets the quarantine label, not "app")
kubectl apply -f - << 'EOF'
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: emergency-isolate-compromised
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      quarantine: "true"
  policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]
  # No ingress/egress rules = DENY ALL traffic
EOF

# STEP 4: Verify isolation
kubectl exec easypj-legit-pod -n easypj -- \
  curl --connect-timeout 2 http://&lt;compromised-pod-IP&gt;:8000
# Should FAIL — NetworkPolicy blocks all traffic</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Why Not Just kubectl delete? — The Cost of Destroying Evidence</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left">
                        <div class="panel-header" style="color:#326ce5;">WRONG: Delete Immediately</div>
<pre>
kubectl delete pod compromised-pod --force

• Pod is GONE in milliseconds
• Deployment creates replacement
• Attacker's processes → GONE
• Attacker's files → GONE
• Network connections → GONE
• You know: nothing about the attack
• Attacker can repeat the same attack
  because root cause is NOT fixed
</pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel-right">
                        <div class="panel-header" style="color:#4ade80;">RIGHT: Isolate → Investigate → Delete</div>
<pre>
# 1. ISOLATE (Section 30.2)
kubectl label pod pwned app-
kubectl apply -f emergency-deny-all.yaml

# 2. INVESTIGATE (Section 30.3)
kubectl get pod pwned -o yaml > forensic.yaml
kubectl exec pwned -- ps aux > processes.txt
kubectl exec pwned -- env > env.txt

# 3. DELETE (after evidence captured)
kubectl delete pod pwned --force

• You know: what attacker ran, which
  files they touched, where they connected
• Root cause is identified and fixed
• New Falco rules detect this pattern
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s30-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">30.3 Forensic Investigation of a Kubernetes Pod</h3>
                <p>Before deleting the compromised pod, capture everything. This forensic snapshot is your evidence for root cause analysis, incident reports, and legal/compliance requirements.</p>

                <h4>Pod Forensics Checklist — Capture Before Deletion</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Complete forensic capture of a compromised pod</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">POD=easypj-django-abc123
NS=easypj
DIR=forensics-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)
mkdir -p $DIR

# 1. Full pod spec (with runtime state, node, IPs)
kubectl get pod $POD -n $NS -o yaml > $DIR/pod-spec.yaml

# 2. Environment variables (may contain leaked secrets!)
kubectl exec $POD -n $NS -- env > $DIR/env.txt

# 3. Running processes
kubectl exec $POD -n $NS -- ps aux > $DIR/processes.txt

# 4. Network connections
kubectl exec $POD -n $NS -- netstat -tunap > $DIR/netstat.txt 2>/dev/null
kubectl exec $POD -n $NS -- ss -tunap > $DIR/ss.txt 2>/dev/null

# 5. Filesystem anomalies (recently modified files, /tmp, /root)
kubectl exec $POD -n $NS -- find /tmp /root /home -type f -mmin -60 > $DIR/recent-files.txt 2>/dev/null
kubectl exec $POD -n $NS -- ls -laR /tmp > $DIR/tmp-listing.txt 2>/dev/null

# 6. Pod logs (may show attacker commands)
kubectl logs $POD -n $NS --tail=500 > $DIR/pod-logs.txt

# 7. Audit log events for this pod's namespace in the attack window
cat /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log | jq "select(.objectRef.namespace==\"$NS\" and .stageTimestamp >= \"03:00:00Z\")" > $DIR/audit-events.json

echo "✅ Forensic evidence saved to $DIR/"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Container Filesystem Snapshot — kubectl cp for Offline Analysis</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Copy suspicious files from pod to local machine for analysis</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Copy the entire /tmp directory (common malware dropper location)
kubectl cp easypj/easypj-django-abc123:/tmp $DIR/tmp-copy/

# Copy suspicious binaries for offline reverse engineering
kubectl cp easypj/easypj-django-abc123:/tmp/xmrig $DIR/suspicious-binary

# Copy modified system files (evidence of persistence)
kubectl cp easypj/easypj-django-abc123:/etc/crontab $DIR/crontab
kubectl cp easypj/easypj-django-abc123:/root/.bash_history $DIR/bash-history

# Copy the full container filesystem (if distroless, this is small)
kubectl cp easypj/easypj-django-abc123:/ $DIR/full-fs/ 2>/dev/null
# ⚠️ Only works if tar is available in the container</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s30-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">30.4 Common Attack Scenarios &amp; CKS Responses</h3>
                <p>Six attack scenarios appear repeatedly on the CKS exam and in real production incidents. Memorize the detection, containment, and eradication steps for each. <strong>This table IS your incident response cheat sheet.</strong></p>

                <h4>Attack → Response Playbook — 6 Common Scenarios (CKS Exam + Real World)</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Detection</th><th>Containment</th><th>Eradication</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>🪙 Crypto Miner</strong></td>
                                <td>Falco: "Unexpected outbound connection" to mining pool IPs. Node CPU at 100%. kubectl top pod shows abnormal usage.</td>
                                <td>Isolate pod (Section 30.2), check audit logs for who created it, verify image source</td>
                                <td>Delete pod, revoke creator's SA token, add admission rule blocking unknown images, block mining pool IPs via NetworkPolicy egress rules</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>🐚 Reverse Shell</strong></td>
                                <td>Falco: "Netcat remote code execution" (nc -e flag). Outbound connection to unusual port (4444, 1337, 31337).</td>
                                <td>Isolate pod immediately, apply egress-deny NetworkPolicy targeting attacker IP, kubectl label pod to remove from Service</td>
                                <td>Delete pod, rotate all secrets the pod could access, audit RBAC, scan image for backdoor</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>📤 Data Exfiltration</strong></td>
                                <td>Falco: sensitive file read + large outbound transfer (MBs to external IP). Audit log: secret/configmap reads by unexpected SA.</td>
                                <td>Isolate pod, block all egress via NetworkPolicy, check WHAT data was accessed (audit log objectRef), preserve pod for forensics</td>
                                <td>Delete pod, rotate ALL exfiltrated credentials, notify compliance team (GDPR/PCI), legal review if PII was involved</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>🔑 Privilege Escalation</strong></td>
                                <td>Audit log: ClusterRoleBinding created binding to cluster-admin. Falco: "Launch privileged container" or setuid binary execution.</td>
                                <td>Delete the malicious ClusterRoleBinding IMMEDIATELY, revoke creator's RBAC permissions, audit ALL existing bindings for anomalies</td>
                                <td>Enable NodeRestriction admission plugin, restrict ClusterRoleBinding creation to specific admin users only, enforce PSS Restricted</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>🔐 Secret Access by Unexpected SA</strong></td>
                                <td>Audit log: <code>jq 'select(.objectRef.resource=="secrets" and .verb=="get" or .verb=="list")'</code>. SA from dev namespace accessing prod secrets.</td>
                                <td>Immediately delete the SA token (kubectl delete secret &lt;sa-token&gt;), remove SA from any RoleBindings in the target namespace</td>
                                <td>Rotate all secrets in the accessed namespace, implement namespace-scoped RBAC (never cross-namespace access), audit all SA permissions</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>🔄 Lateral Movement (Pod→Pod)</strong></td>
                                <td>Falco: outbound connection from compromised pod to other pods on unusual ports. NetworkPolicy violation logs. kubectl exec from unexpected source IPs in audit logs.</td>
                                <td>Apply emergency default-deny NetworkPolicy in the namespace, isolate both source and target pods, check if SA token was used to list other pods via API</td>
                                <td>Implement strict default-deny NetworkPolicy (only allow known traffic), namespace isolation, mTLS via service mesh (Istio/Linkerd) for pod-to-pod auth</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
