            <section class="section-block" id="s30-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">30.6 Identifying Attack Phases — MITRE ATT&amp;CK for Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>The CKS curriculum requires you to <strong>"investigate and identify phases of attack and bad actors within the environment"</strong> — which means you must be able to place scattered evidence on a timeline, not just react to individual alerts. <strong>MITRE ATT&amp;CK for Containers</strong> is the shared vocabulary that makes this possible: every alert you see in Chapters 28-29 maps to a <strong>TACTIC</strong> — the attacker's goal at that moment. Naming the phase tells you what already happened <em>and</em> what is most likely to come next, so you can contain ahead of the attacker instead of chasing behind them.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The Investigative Loop</h5>
                    <p>Every phase-identification task, on the exam and in production, follows the same loop: <strong>(1) collect evidence</strong> — audit logs, Falco alerts, NetworkPolicy deny logs; <strong>(2) map each event to a tactic</strong> using the table below; <strong>(3) order the events</strong> on a timeline; <strong>(4) find the entry point and the current furthest phase reached</strong>; <strong>(5) contain forward of that phase</strong> — block what the attacker would need to do next, not just what they already did.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The MITRE ATT&amp;CK Kill Chain — Tactic → K8s Evidence → What's Next</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Reading Order: Each Tactic Is a Phase — Evidence on the Left, the Attacker's Likely Next Move on the Right</div>
<pre>
 🚪 Initial Access ──▶ ⚙️ Execution ──▶ 🔁 Persistence ──▶ ⬆️ Privilege Escalation
   anonymous API hit     Falco: shell/exec    new CronJob/          Falco: setuid,
   exposed dashboard     in pod               DaemonSet, added      hostPath, privileged
   exploited pod                              SA token              pod created
        │                                                                  │
        └──────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┘
                                         ▼
 🕶️ Defense Evasion ──▶ 🔑 Credential Access ──▶ 🔍 Discovery ──▶ ↔️ Lateral Movement
   audit log deleted      audit: get secrets,      audit: list       NetPol deny logs,
   Falco stopped,         read SA token,            pods/nodes/       pod→pod connections
   logs cleared           IMDS call                 secrets -A        to postgres
                                                                            │
                                                                            ▼
                                                    📤 Collection/Exfil ──▶ 💥 Impact
                                                      large egress, DB       crypto-miner CPU,
                                                      dump, outbound to      ransom, resource
                                                      unknown IP             deletion (goal reached)

 🔑 THE ONE RULE: whichever tactic your evidence reaches FURTHEST along this chain is
    where the attacker currently is. Contain the tactic that comes NEXT, not the one
    that already fired — Falco/audit only tell you the past, never the future.
</pre>
                    <div class="vs-prose">
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>In words:</strong> the chain runs Initial Access → Execution → Persistence → Privilege Escalation → Defense Evasion → Credential Access → Discovery → Lateral Movement → Collection/Exfiltration → Impact. It is not always strictly linear — an attacker can skip phases or loop back — but the K8s evidence source for each tactic is consistent enough to memorize: Falco owns Execution/Persistence/Privilege-Escalation signals, the audit log owns Credential-Access/Discovery signals, and NetworkPolicy/CNI logs own Lateral-Movement signals.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>MITRE ATT&amp;CK Tactics Reference — Signal to Phase</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Tactic (Phase)</th><th>K8s Evidence / Signal</th><th>Typical Next Phase</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>🚪 Initial Access</strong></td><td>Anonymous API hit, exposed dashboard, exploited pod</td><td>Execution</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>⚙️ Execution</strong></td><td>Falco: shell/exec in pod</td><td>Persistence / Privilege Escalation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🔁 Persistence</strong></td><td>New CronJob/DaemonSet, added SA token, modified image</td><td>Defense Evasion</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>⬆️ Privilege Escalation</strong></td><td>Falco: setuid, hostPath, privileged pod created</td><td>Credential Access</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🕶️ Defense Evasion</strong></td><td>Audit log deleted, Falco stopped, logs cleared</td><td>Credential Access</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🔑 Credential Access</strong></td><td>Audit: get secrets, read SA token, IMDS call</td><td>Discovery / Lateral Movement</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🔍 Discovery</strong></td><td>Audit: list pods/nodes/secrets <code>--all-namespaces</code></td><td>Lateral Movement</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>↔️ Lateral Movement</strong></td><td>NetPol deny logs, pod→pod connections to postgres</td><td>Collection</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>📤 Collection / Exfil</strong></td><td>Large egress, DB dump, outbound to unknown IP</td><td>Impact</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>💥 Impact</strong></td><td>Crypto-miner CPU, ransom, resource deletion</td><td>(goal reached)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>The Detection Surface — Which Tool Sees Which Threat Surface</h4>
                <p>Phase identification only works if you know which tool is watching which surface. Miss a surface, and that phase of the kill chain is invisible to you.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Threat Surface</th><th>What Watches It</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>🏗️ Infrastructure / Node</strong></td><td>kube-bench, node auditd, host IDS</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>📦 Applications / Pods</strong></td><td>Falco (syscalls), Trivy Operator (running CVEs)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🌐 Networks</strong></td><td>NetworkPolicy logs, CNI flow logs, service mesh</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🗄️ Data (Secrets / etcd)</strong></td><td>Audit log (secret access), encryption at rest</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>👤 Users / Identities</strong></td><td>Audit log (who did what), RBAC review</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>⚙️ Workloads</strong></td><td>Admission events, PSS violations, drift alerts</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>🔴 WORKED EXAMPLE: Correlating Three Signals Into One Phase Assessment</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header">
                        <span class="scenario-badge danger">ATTACK SCENARIO</span>
                        <span class="scenario-title">Falco + Audit + NetworkPolicy — Where Is This Attacker, Really?</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Signal 1 — Falco: "shell in pod"</strong><p>Falco fires "Terminal shell in container" for <code>easypj-django-abc123</code> at 03:14:05. Map to tactic: <strong>Execution</strong>. On its own, this only tells you the attacker got a foothold — not what they're after.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Signal 2 — Audit: "get secrets"</strong><p>7 seconds later, the audit log shows the pod's ServiceAccount issuing a <code>get</code> on <code>secrets</code> in the <code>easypj</code> namespace. Map to tactic: <strong>Credential Access</strong>. The attacker has moved past Execution — they now have (or are trying to get) credentials.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">3</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Signal 3 — NetPol deny: pod→postgres</strong><p>8 seconds after that, a NetworkPolicy deny log shows the same pod attempting a connection to the <code>postgres</code> pod on port 5432 — <strong>blocked</strong>. Map to tactic: <strong>Lateral Movement (attempted, not achieved)</strong>.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">🎯</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Assessment: mid-chain, pre-exfiltration</strong><p>Ordering the three signals — Execution → Credential Access → attempted Lateral Movement — the attacker's <strong>furthest reached phase is Credential Access</strong>, with a Lateral Movement attempt that your NetworkPolicy already stopped. That places them one phase before Collection/Exfiltration. <strong>Immediate action:</strong> isolate the pod NOW (30.2), treat every secret it could read as compromised and rotate immediately (30.5) — don't wait for confirmed exfiltration, because the NetPol block only bought you time, it didn't end the incident.</p></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Reconstructing an Attack Timeline — Correlating Audit + Falco Logs</h4>
                <p>Phase identification is only as good as your timeline. The two log sources use different formats and different clocks (audit uses <code>stageTimestamp</code>, Falco uses <code>time</code>) — merge them into one sorted stream before you try to map tactics.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Merge Falco alerts + audit log events into one MITRE-taggable timeline</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Extract Falco alerts for the incident window, tag source=falco
jq -c '{time: .time, source: "falco", rule: .rule, priority: .priority,
        pod: .output_fields."k8s.pod.name", user: .output_fields."user.name"}' \
    /var/log/falco/alerts.log > /tmp/falco-events.jsonl

# 2. Extract audit events for the same namespace, tag source=audit
jq -c 'select(.objectRef.namespace=="easypj") |
       {time: .stageTimestamp, source: "audit", verb: .verb,
        resource: .objectRef.resource, user: .user.username}' \
    /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log > /tmp/audit-events.jsonl

# 3. Merge + sort by timestamp into a single reconstructed timeline
cat /tmp/falco-events.jsonl /tmp/audit-events.jsonl | jq -s 'sort_by(.time)' > timeline.json

# 4. Walk timeline.json top to bottom, mapping each event to a MITRE tactic
#    (Falco "shell in pod" → Execution; audit "get secrets" → Credential Access; …)
#    The LAST tactic you can map is where the attacker currently is.
cat timeline.json | jq -r '.[] | "\(.time)  [\(.source)]  \(.rule // .verb)  \(.pod // .resource)"'</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKS Exam: Phase-Identification Tasks</h5>
                    <p>The exam may hand you 2-3 raw log lines (one Falco JSON alert, one or two audit log entries) and ask "what phase is this attack in, and what's the very next thing you should do?" Work the investigative loop: map each line to a tactic using the reference table, take the FURTHEST one as the attacker's current position, and answer with the containment action that blocks the NEXT phase — not a recap of what already happened.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch30-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>🔧 Under the Hood — Why Label Removal Isolates a Pod Instantly, and the Exam-Clock Breakdown of a Live Incident</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                <h5>🗺️ What Actually Happens When You Run <code>kubectl label pod app-</code></h5>
                <p>Section 30.2's first containment move — stripping the pod's label — feels almost too simple to be the fastest thing you can do. Here is why it works in well under a second, faster than any NetworkPolicy can propagate:</p>
                <pre class="uth-diagram">
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  LABEL REMOVAL — FROM kubectl TO ZERO TRAFFIC                          │
  │                                                                        │
  │  kubectl label pod easypj-django-abc123 -n easypj app-                 │
  │       │  (PATCH request to the API server; writes new Pod object      │
  │       │   to etcd with the "app" label deleted)                       │
  │       ▼                                                                │
  │  EndpointSlice controller — watches Pods via an informer, already     │
  │  has an open watch stream on Pod changes in the namespace              │
  │       │  (recomputes the EndpointSlice for the Service the instant    │
  │       │   the watch event arrives — no polling, no delay)             │
  │       ▼                                                                │
  │  Pod's IP is removed from the Service's EndpointSlice                  │
  │       │  (this is the same mechanism a rolling update uses to drain   │
  │       │   a pod before termination — nothing incident-specific)       │
  │       ▼                                                                │
  │  kube-proxy on every node — watches EndpointSlices, rewrites its       │
  │  iptables/IPVS rules to stop forwarding to that pod's IP               │
  │       │                                                                │
  │       ▼                                                                │
  │  NEW connections to the Service NEVER reach the pod again              │
  │  (typically &lt;1s end-to-end on a healthy control plane)               │
  │                                                                        │
  │  ⚠️ WHAT LABEL REMOVAL DOES NOT DO: it only stops traffic routed      │
  │  THROUGH the Service. Anything with the pod's IP directly, or an       │
  │  already-established TCP connection, is untouched — that's what the   │
  │  emergency NetworkPolicy in the SAME step sequence is for.             │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                </pre>
                <p>This is also why the two containment actions in 30.2 are paired, not alternatives: label removal kills new Service-routed traffic in under a second, while the deny-all NetworkPolicy is what actually blocks direct-IP connections and existing flows once the CNI re-evaluates them. Skip the NetworkPolicy and a reverse shell already holding a raw socket to the pod's IP keeps working straight through your label change.</p>

                <h5>🎯 Exam Task Simulator — Respond to a Compromised Pod</h5>
                <div class="process-steps"><div class="ps-step"><span class="ps-num">1</span><strong>CONTAIN (2 min):</strong> Remove pod labels (drop from Service), apply emergency deny-all NetworkPolicy.</div><div class="ps-step"><span class="ps-num">2</span><strong>FORENSICS (5 min):</strong> <code>kubectl get pod -o yaml</code>, <code>kubectl logs</code>, <code>kubectl exec -- ps aux</code>, <code>kubectl exec -- env</code>, audit log queries.</div><div class="ps-step"><span class="ps-num">3</span><strong>ERADICATE (3 min):</strong> Delete pod, revoke SA token, rotate secrets.</div><div class="ps-step"><span class="ps-num">4</span><strong>RECOVER (5 min):</strong> Deploy clean pod, verify functionality, document timeline.</div></div>
                <p class="mb-0">15 minutes, budgeted. Practice this exact sequence until the first 2 minutes (contain) are pure muscle memory — that's the phase where hesitation costs the most, because it's the only phase where the attacker is still active against a live target.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch30-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Never delete first.</strong> The 6-phase lifecycle is Detect → Contain → Investigate → Eradicate → Recover → Learn — isolate with a label removal + emergency deny-all NetworkPolicy, capture forensics, THEN delete. You can never recover evidence from a deleted pod (30.1, 30.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Forensic capture is a 7-point checklist</strong> — pod spec (<code>-o yaml</code>), env vars, running processes, network connections, filesystem changes, logs, and the audit events for that namespace — all captured BEFORE eradication (30.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Six attack scenarios cover most of the exam and most real incidents</strong> — crypto miner, reverse shell, data exfiltration, privilege escalation, unexpected secret access, lateral movement — each with its own detection/containment/eradication triple, plus a P1–P4 severity ladder for who gets notified and how fast (30.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Post-incident work is not optional:</strong> a timestamped timeline, a written root cause analysis, full credential rotation, a signed-and-encrypted chain of custody, and a filled-out incident report template close the loop (30.5).</li>
                    <li><strong>MITRE ATT&amp;CK gives every alert a place on a kill chain.</strong> Falco events map to Execution/Persistence/Privilege-Escalation, audit events map to Credential-Access/Discovery, NetworkPolicy logs map to Lateral Movement — correlate all three, find the FURTHEST phase reached, and contain the phase that comes next, not the one that already fired (30.6).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch30-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>NEVER delete first:</strong> Deleting the compromised pod destroys forensic evidence. Always: (1) isolate, (2) capture forensics, (3) THEN delete. If the exam asks you to "respond to an incident," the first command should NOT be <code>kubectl delete</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>deny-all NetworkPolicy also blocks DNS:</strong> If you apply a deny-all egress NetworkPolicy, pods can't reach CoreDNS. They become completely isolated (can't resolve anything). This is intentional for containment but may break legitimate sidecars.</li>
                    <li><strong>Label removal alone is not full containment:</strong> it stops new Service-routed traffic in under a second, but a reverse shell already holding the pod's raw IP keeps working until the NetworkPolicy lands — apply both, don't treat either as sufficient alone.</li>
                    <li><strong>"What phase is this?" wants the FURTHEST tactic, not the first one you noticed:</strong> given a Falco Execution alert and an audit Credential-Access event, the answer is Credential Access — naming Execution under-reports how far the attacker actually got.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch30-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Time-box the response like the exam does:</strong> ~2 min contain, ~5 min forensics, ~3 min eradicate, ~5 min recover. If you're still deciding whether to isolate past the 2-minute mark, isolate anyway — you can always stand down a false positive, but you can't undo destroyed evidence.</li>
                    <li><strong>Memorize the forensic capture one-liner set</strong> (<code>-o yaml</code>, <code>exec -- ps aux</code>, <code>exec -- env</code>, <code>logs --tail=500</code>) so you're not reconstructing it from memory under time pressure.</li>
                    <li><strong>Classify the attack type before you respond.</strong> The 6-scenario table in 30.4 tells you the containment command in advance — a crypto miner and a reverse shell do NOT get the same first move.</li>
                    <li><strong>For phase questions, map first, answer second:</strong> write "Falco shell = Execution, audit get-secrets = Credential Access" before you commit to a final answer — it's faster to get right than reasoning from the alert text alone.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

