                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A suspicious pod 'crypto-miner-abc' is running in namespace 'prod'. Walk through the incident response steps.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>1. Identify:</strong> <code>kubectl describe pod crypto-miner-abc -n prod</code> — check image, command, node. <strong>2. Isolate:</strong> <code>kubectl label pod crypto-miner-abc -n prod app-</code> (remove label). Apply deny-all NetworkPolicy in prod namespace. <strong>3. Forensics:</strong> <code>kubectl get pod crypto-miner-abc -n prod -o yaml > forensics.yaml</code>; <code>kubectl logs crypto-miner-abc -n prod > forensics.log</code>. <strong>4. Eradicate:</strong> <code>kubectl delete pod crypto-miner-abc -n prod</code>. <strong>5. Recover:</strong> Deploy clean replacement pod. <strong>6. Lessons:</strong> Review how the pod was created (RBAC audit), tighten ImagePolicyWebhook.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you create an emergency deny-all NetworkPolicy to isolate a namespace during an incident?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: emergency-deny-all
  namespace: prod
spec:
  podSelector: {}      # All pods
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  - Egress
  # No ingress/egress rules = deny all</code></pre>
                    <p>This blocks ALL ingress and egress traffic for all pods in the namespace. Apply with: <code>kubectl apply -f deny-all.yaml</code>. This is the fastest way to contain a compromised namespace.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between contain, eradicate, and recover in the NIST incident response framework as applied to Kubernetes?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Contain:</strong> Stop the bleeding WITHOUT destroying evidence. Apply NetworkPolicy, cordon node, revoke SA tokens. <strong>Eradicate:</strong> Remove the attacker's foothold. Delete compromised pods, SAs, backdoor RBAC. Rotate all secrets. <strong>Recover:</strong> Restore normal operations. Deploy clean replacements from signed images. Verify monitoring is active. On the CKS exam: cordon+drain+NetworkPolicy = contain. Delete pod + rotate secrets = eradicate. Redeploy = recover.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The order matters. Contain FIRST (stop damage), then forensics (gather evidence), then eradicate (remove threat), then recover (return to normal). On the CKS exam, always contain before doing anything else — isolated pods can't cause more damage while you investigate.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is compromised. What forensic evidence do you collect BEFORE deleting it? List the specific commands.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pod &lt;pod&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; -o yaml > forensic-pod.yaml
kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; > forensic-describe.txt
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; --all-containers --tail=1000 > forensic-logs.txt
kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; -- ps auxww > forensic-ps.txt
kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; -- netstat -tlnp > forensic-net.txt
kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; -- cat /proc/1/cgroup > forensic-cgroup.txt
# Check node for escaped processes
ssh &lt;node&gt; "ps aux | grep &lt;container-id&gt;"</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Forensics before eradication is exam-critical. Once you delete the pod, you lose the running state. Pod YAML gives the spec, logs show what happened, exec shows running processes and network connections. On the CKS exam, always collect forensics before deleting anything.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After containing a compromised pod, what RBAC changes do you make to prevent the attacker from creating new pods with the same compromised ServiceAccount?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl delete sa compromised-sa -n prod
kubectl delete rolebinding &lt;name&gt; -n prod
kubectl delete clusterrolebinding &lt;name&gt;
kubectl create sa replacement-sa -n prod  # New SA with new token
kubectl create token replacement-sa -n prod | kubectl create secret generic new-token --from-file=token=/dev/stdin -n prod</code></pre>
                    <p>Also check for backdoor ClusterRoleBindings: <code>kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o json | jq '.items[] | select(.subjects[]?.namespace=="prod")'</code>.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>ServiceAccount token rotation is critical after a compromise. Simply deleting the pod isn't enough — if the attacker copied the token, they can still authenticate. Deleting the SA invalidates all existing tokens. On the CKS exam, always delete compromised SAs and rotate secrets.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An attacker deployed a DaemonSet across all nodes. You need to remove it urgently. What's the fastest command sequence?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl delete daemonset &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; --force --grace-period=0
kubectl delete pods -n &lt;ns&gt; -l app=&lt;malicious-label&gt; --force --grace-period=0
# Verify cleanup
kubectl get pods -A | grep -i &lt;malicious-pattern&gt;</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>--force --grace-period=0 immediately deletes without waiting for graceful shutdown. For a DaemonSet, deleting the DaemonSet resource cascades to delete all its pods. On the CKS exam, speed matters in incident response — use force flags during containment but always collect forensics first.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What command shows you ALL recent activity in a compromised namespace? How do you check who created a malicious pod?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get events -n prod --sort-by='.lastTimestamp' | tail -20
kubectl audit-log query --namespace=prod --since=1h  # If audit logging is configured
cat /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log | jq 'select(.objectRef.namespace=="prod") | {time: .stageTimestamp, user: .user.username, verb, resource: .objectRef.resource, name: .objectRef.name}'</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Events show WHAT happened. Audit logs show WHO did it. On the CKS exam, if audit logging is enabled, always query it first. Events are ephemeral (1-hour retention by default) — audit logs are permanent (if configured).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between cordon and drain? When would you use each during incident response?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Cordon:</strong> Marks node as unschedulable — no NEW pods, but EXISTING pods keep running. Use when you want to preserve evidence (don't kill compromised pods yet). <strong>Drain:</strong> Evicts ALL pods from the node. Use after forensics are collected to move legitimate workloads off a compromised node. During IR: cordon first (preserve evidence), investigate, then drain (move good pods), then isolate the node for deep forensics.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Cordon is containment without destruction. Drain is relocation. On the CKS exam, never drain before collecting forensics — you'll destroy evidence. Cordon → investigate → drain → isolate node.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After an incident, you need to rotate ALL secrets in a namespace. What's the most efficient approach?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># List all secrets
kubectl get secrets -n prod
# For static secrets: delete and recreate
kubectl delete secret db-password api-key -n prod
kubectl create secret generic db-password --from-literal=password=&lt;new-value&gt; -n prod
# For TLS secrets: generate new certs
openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 365 -newkey rsa:2048 -keyout new.key -out new.crt -subj "/CN=new.easypj.com"
kubectl delete secret tls-cert -n prod
kubectl create secret tls tls-cert --cert=new.crt --key=new.key -n prod
# Restart pods to pick up new secrets
kubectl rollout restart deployment -n prod</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Post-incident secret rotation is mandatory. If the attacker exfiltrated secrets, they still have the old values. On the CKS exam, always include secret rotation in the recovery phase. For external secret stores (Vault), rotate at the source — the External Secrets Operator will sync the new values.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Design an incident response runbook for a "pod running cryptocurrency miner" alert from Falco. Include all phases with specific commands.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="process-steps" style="flex-direction: column;"><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Verify</h5><p><code>kubectl top pod &lt;pod&gt; -n prod</code> — crypto miners use 100% CPU. <code>kubectl describe pod</code> — check image.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Contain</h5><p><code>kubectl apply -f emergency-deny-all.yaml -n prod</code>. <code>kubectl cordon &lt;node&gt;</code> if all-node.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Forensics</h5><p>Save pod YAML, logs, ps output, env vars. Check audit logs for who created it.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Eradicate</h5><p>Delete pod, SA, RBAC bindings. Remove malicious image from registry if pushed.</p></div></div><div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><div class="step-content"><h5>Recover</h5><p>Deploy replacement, rotate secrets, verify monitoring, document lessons learned.</p></div></div></div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This runbook demonstrates the complete CKS IR workflow. The key: verify (confirm it's real), contain (stop damage), forensics (preserve evidence), eradicate (remove threat), recover (return to normal). On the exam, you'll be given one of these phases and expected to execute the correct commands.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're given three log lines: (1) Falco — "Terminal shell in container" for pod X at 10:02:00; (2) audit log — pod X's ServiceAccount performs <code>get secrets</code> at 10:02:09; (3) NetworkPolicy deny log — pod X blocked connecting to a database pod at 10:02:15. What MITRE ATT&amp;CK phase is the attacker in right now, and what's your immediate next action?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Mapping:</strong> (1) Falco shell spawn → <strong>Execution</strong>. (2) audit get secrets → <strong>Credential Access</strong>. (3) NetPol-blocked connection to the database → an attempted <strong>Lateral Movement</strong>, which did NOT succeed. <strong>Furthest confirmed phase: Credential Access</strong> (Lateral Movement was attempted but blocked, so treat it as "in progress," not "achieved"). <strong>Immediate action:</strong> isolate pod X now (label removal + deny-all NetworkPolicy), then rotate every secret that ServiceAccount could read — assume the credentials are already compromised even though exfiltration hasn't been confirmed. Do not wait for a Collection/Impact signal before acting.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the core 30.6 skill: correlate multi-source evidence (Falco + audit + NetworkPolicy), order it by timestamp, and answer with the phase that is FURTHEST along the chain — not the first or loudest alert. The correct response targets the NEXT phase (Collection/Exfiltration), because a blocked Lateral Movement attempt means the attacker is actively probing for a way to progress, and containment must get ahead of that, matching the worked example in 30.6.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

