        <article class="chapter" id="ch31">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 31 · Immutable Containers — Read-Only Filesystems</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">An attacker gets a shell inside your easypj container. What's the first thing they do? <strong>Write to disk.</strong> They download a crypto miner to <code>/tmp/</code>, modify <code>/etc/crontab</code> for persistence, replace <code>/usr/bin/python3</code> with a backdoored version. Every one of these attacks requires a <strong>writable filesystem</strong>. <strong>Immutable containers</strong> flip this: the root filesystem is <strong>read-only</strong>. Nothing can be written, modified, or deleted — not even by root. If an attacker gets in, they're trapped in a container where every write fails. The pod becomes a self-cleaning sandbox: restart it, and it's back to the original image. <strong>Immutability doesn't prevent the initial breach — it makes the breach irrelevant.</strong> But a field in YAML is only a promise; this chapter closes with the discipline that turns that promise into proof.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟢 Beginner-Friendly</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🧊 Immutability</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔍 Runtime Security</span>
                </div>
            </div><!-- /chapter-intro -->

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn — Core-Level Mastery</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Why immutability is a security property:</strong> How read-only filesystems neutralize post-exploitation attacks — malware installation, persistence, binary replacement</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Enforcing read-only filesystems:</strong> The <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> securityContext, handling writable directories with emptyDir and PVC volumes</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Pod Security Standards for immutability:</strong> How the Restricted PSS profile enforces read-only filesystems, and how to configure writable exceptions properly</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Verifying immutability at runtime:</strong> The three-step verify loop — inspect the applied spec, attempt a live write and confirm it fails, and monitor continuously for drift with Falco — because a <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code> field in YAML is a configuration, not proof it's enforced</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKS Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Monitoring, Logging &amp; Runtime Security <span class="cka-weight">20%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 The CKS Exam Grades the Running State, Not Your YAML's Intent</h5>
                <p>Immutability tasks look deceptively simple — one field, <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> — but the grader never checks your manifest's intent, only what the running pod actually enforces. Four things separate a passing answer from a pod that silently still accepts writes:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Anticipate the writable paths BEFORE you apply.</strong> Most application crashes after adding readOnlyRootFilesystem trace back to one missing emptyDir — check logs or strace for the failing path first (31.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Know when the field is redundant.</strong> If the namespace already enforces PSS Restricted, the pod is rejected without it — but you may still be asked to add it explicitly for defense in depth (31.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Never trust the field without testing it.</strong> A pod spec that LOOKS correct can still be writable if the mount didn't apply — always close with a live write-attempt test, not just <code>kubectl get</code> (31.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Falco is your backstop, not your primary control.</strong> If a task mentions "verify" or "detect," it wants the DETECT step of the loop — a Falco rule watching for write attempts — layered on top of, not instead of, the readOnlyRootFilesystem field itself (31.4).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

