            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 31 — Lab Q&amp;A (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">11 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Make an existing deployment immutable: add readOnlyRootFilesystem and mount emptyDir for /tmp. Write the YAML changes.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: app
        securityContext:
          readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
        volumeMounts:
        - name: tmp
          mountPath: /tmp
      volumes:
      - name: tmp
        emptyDir: {}</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between a container image being immutable vs. the container filesystem being immutable?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Immutable image:</strong> The image itself is never modified after build (tags should be digests, not :latest). Enforced by image signing (cosign) and ImagePolicyWebhook. <strong>Immutable filesystem:</strong> The container's root filesystem is mounted read-only (<code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code>). The container cannot write to the image layers. Enforced by securityContext. Both are complementary: immutable images prevent tampering in the registry, immutable filesystems prevent tampering at runtime.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why is immutability a security principle? How does it prevent attacker persistence?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Immutability means an attacker cannot persist changes inside a container. If they install a backdoor, add a user, or modify a binary, those changes disappear when the pod restarts. This forces attackers to either: (a) compromise the image build pipeline (harder, and detectable via signing), or (b) maintain continuous access (noisier, and detectable via Falco). Immutability turns container compromise from "persistent foothold" to "temporary incident."</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the core security value of immutability. Traditional servers are persistent — an attacker can install a rootkit that survives reboots. Immutable containers are ephemeral — every restart wipes the attacker's changes. On the CKS exam, immutability is both a configuration (<code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code>) and a cultural practice (never SSH to fix, always rebuild and redeploy).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An app needs to write to <code>/var/log/app</code>, <code>/tmp</code>, and <code>/var/run</code>. You want readOnlyRootFilesystem: true. Write the complete volume configuration.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">containers:
- name: app
  securityContext:
    readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
  volumeMounts:
  - name: app-logs
    mountPath: /var/log/app
  - name: tmp
    mountPath: /tmp
  - name: var-run
    mountPath: /var/run
volumes:
- name: app-logs
  emptyDir: {}
- name: tmp
  emptyDir: {}
- name: var-run
  emptyDir: {}</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>emptyDir volumes provide writable tmpfs storage that is ephemeral (cleared on pod restart). The root filesystem stays read-only. This is the standard pattern for making legacy apps work with immutable filesystems. On the CKS exam, identify which directories need writes and mount individual emptyDirs — don't mount / writable.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> interact with container image layers? Can the container still read from all layers?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Container images are composed of read-only layers by nature. <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> makes the writable layer (top layer) read-only too. The container CAN still read from all image layers — the binaries, libraries, and config files in the image are accessible. The container CANNOT write to ANY part of the filesystem except explicitly mounted emptyDir volumes. The image layers are never modified — writes would have gone to the now-read-only top layer.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This clarifies a common misunderstanding: readOnlyRootFilesystem doesn't make the container unable to read files — it prevents WRITES. The container functions normally, reading from image layers. Only write operations fail. On the CKS exam, know that readOnlyRootFilesystem is compatible with most applications that don't need runtime file writes.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What Kubernetes admission policies can enforce immutable containers at scale?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(1) PSS Restricted: requires <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> (among other settings). (2) Kyverno ClusterPolicy: mutate pods without readOnlyRootFilesystem to add it automatically, or validate and reject. (3) OPA Gatekeeper: ConstraintTemplate requiring readOnlyRootFilesystem in Rego. (4) kube-linter: CI/CD-time check. For scale: combine PSS Restricted (enforce) + Kyverno mutation (auto-add for namespaces not yet Restricted).</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Policy enforcement at scale is the CKS approach. You can't manually verify every pod. PSS Restricted provides base enforcement. Kyverno provides auto-remediation (mutation). On the CKS exam, prefer mutation over validation for security defaults — it reduces developer friction.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod with readOnlyRootFilesystem:true is crashing on startup. The app writes to a path you didn't anticipate. How do you discover what path needs to be writable?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; | grep -i "read-only\|permission denied\|EROFS"
kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- strace -e openat,write,mkdir -f /usr/bin/app 2>&1 | grep EROFS
# Then add emptyDir mount for the failing path</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Debugging immutable filesystem issues: the error message usually contains the path that failed. strace shows the exact syscall and path. On the CKS exam, if a pod crashes after adding readOnlyRootFilesystem, check logs for "read-only" or "EROFS" errors, then mount an emptyDir at that path.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the relationship between immutable containers and ephemeral containers for debugging?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Immutable containers don't have shells or debugging tools (by design — they're minimal images). Ephemeral containers (<code>kubectl debug</code>) allow attaching a temporary debugging container to a running pod WITHOUT modifying the pod spec. The debugging container can have tools (busybox, netshoot) while the main container stays immutable. This is the CKS debugging pattern: don't add tools to the image; use ephemeral containers on demand.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Ephemeral containers solve the "how do I debug an immutable container?" problem. They're non-persistent — they're removed when you exit. On the CKS exam, never suggest adding curl/vim/telnet to production images. Use ephemeral containers instead.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You deploy a pod from image <code>easypj:v2.1</code>. Later, <code>easypj:v2.1</code> is retagged to a malicious image. With immutability principles, how do you prevent this from affecting running pods?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Use DIGEST, not tag: <code>image: easypj@sha256:abc123...</code>. The digest is a cryptographic hash of the image content — it's immutable. Even if someone retags <code>:v2.1</code>, the digest reference still points to your original image. Running pods use the digest they were started with and won't change. For new deployments, the admission controller should enforce digest-only images (Kyverno/Gatekeeper policy). Additionally, <code>imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent</code> prevents re-pulling if the image already exists on the node.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Tags are mutable pointers. Digests are immutable content hashes. This is a fundamental immutability principle. On the CKS exam: (1) always deploy with digests, (2) enforce digests via admission policy, (3) use cosign to sign the digest (not the tag).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're auditing a cluster and find 200 pods. Write the command to find all pods without readOnlyRootFilesystem and explain your remediation plan.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq -r '.items[] |
  select(.spec.containers[].securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem != true) |
  "\(.metadata.namespace)/\(.metadata.name)"'</code></pre>
                    <p><strong>Remediation plan:</strong> (1) Prioritize production and publicly-facing pods. (2) For each pod, test if it works with readOnlyRootFilesystem. (3) Add emptyDir mounts for writable paths. (4) Deploy via rolling update. (5) Add Kyverno policy to prevent regression.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Audit → prioritize → fix → enforce. This is the CKS security improvement lifecycle. The jq query finds all non-immutable pods. On the exam, you may be asked to audit a cluster and report findings with a remediation plan — always include specific commands and a phased approach.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You applied readOnlyRootFilesystem: true to the easypj pod and <code>kubectl apply</code> succeeded with no errors. Is immutability now guaranteed? What proves it, and why does Falco still matter even if the write attempt fails?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>No. The API server accepting the manifest only proves the field was syntactically valid and admitted — not that the running container enforces it. Prove it with the three-step verify loop (31.4): <strong>(1) INSPECT</strong> — <code>kubectl get pod easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem}'</code> must print <code>true</code>. <strong>(2) ATTEMPT</strong> — <code>kubectl exec easypj -- touch /etc/malicious</code> must fail with "Read-only file system," while <code>kubectl exec easypj -- touch /tmp/ok-write-here</code> (the explicit mount) must succeed. <strong>(3) DETECT</strong> — Falco should log the write attempt regardless of whether it succeeded, because a blocked write is still a signal worth alerting on. If a kernel-level exploit ever finds a way around the mount flag, Falco's "Write below etc" / "Write below binary dir" rules are the only control left standing.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the core discipline of 31.4 — a readOnlyRootFilesystem: true field in a YAML manifest is a CONFIGURATION, not proof of enforcement. The CKS exam grades the running state of the cluster, not the intent expressed in your manifest, so every immutability task should end with the verify loop, not with <code>kubectl apply</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

