            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 "Continuous" Is the Word the Exam Is Testing</h5>
                <p>Every task in this chapter has a one-time version and a continuous version — the CKS exam almost always wants the continuous one. Four things separate a passing answer from a stale one-off check:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Don't confuse a scan with monitoring.</strong> A single <code>trivy image</code> run only proves the image was clean at that moment — Trivy Operator's 6-hour re-scan is what catches a CVE published the day after deployment (32.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Baseline before you can detect drift.</strong> kube-bench run once tells you today's state; kube-bench run on a schedule and DIFFED against yesterday's ConfigMap baseline is what actually catches configuration drift (32.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Audit mode is not enforcement — and that's the point.</strong> Gatekeeper's <code>dryrun</code>/<code>warn</code> modes report violations on EXISTING resources without breaking them; know the audit → remediate → enforce lifecycle, not just the deny path (32.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>A dashboard with no SLO is just numbers.</strong> "0 CRITICAL CVEs" is a target you can alert on; a CVE count with no threshold is trivia. Tie every metric to an SLO and an AlertManager rule (32.4).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

