                <h4>How Trivy Works Internally — The Scanning Engine Explained</h4>
                <p>Understanding <em>how</em> Trivy detects vulnerabilities is essential for troubleshooting and for the CKS exam. Trivy's scanning pipeline has three phases:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Image Acquisition</strong><p>Trivy pulls the image manifest and layer metadata <strong>without downloading the full image</strong>. It uses the OCI Distribution API to fetch only the filesystem layers it needs. This is why Trivy is fast — it doesn't need to <code>docker pull</code> the entire image.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>Package Enumeration</strong><p>Trivy walks each layer's filesystem, identifying installed packages. It supports 20+ package managers: <code>dpkg</code>/<code>apt</code> (Debian/Ubuntu), <code>rpm</code>/<code>yum</code>/<code>dnf</code> (RHEL/CentOS/Fedora), <code>apk</code> (Alpine), <code>pip</code>/<code>poetry</code>/<code>pipenv</code> (Python), <code>npm</code>/<code>yarn</code> (Node.js), <code>gem</code> (Ruby), <code>gomod</code> (Go), <code>jar</code>/<code>pom</code> (Java). It also reads SBOMs if present.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>Vulnerability Matching</strong><p>Each package (name + version) is looked up against Trivy's vulnerability databases. Databases are downloaded from: <strong>NVD</strong> (National Vulnerability Database), <strong>GitHub Advisory Database</strong> (GHSA), <strong>Red Hat OVAL</strong> v2, <strong>Debian Security Tracker</strong>, <strong>Alpine SecDB</strong>, <strong>Amazon Linux ALAS</strong>, and <strong>SUSE OVAL</strong>. The database is cached locally at <code>~/.cache/trivy/db/</code> and refreshed every 6 hours.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <div class="info-box-title">🔬 Why database freshness matters</div>
                    <p>Trivy's accuracy depends entirely on its vulnerability database being current. If the database is 7 days old, it won't detect CVEs published in the last week. The <code>--skip-db-update</code> flag skips the database refresh — useful in air-gapped environments where you manage the DB separately, but <strong>dangerous in production</strong> if you forget to update it.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Troubleshooting Common Trivy Operator Issues</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>No VulnerabilityReports created</strong></td><td>Trivy Operator pod is CrashLoopBackOff. Check with <code>kubectl logs -n trivy-system deploy/trivy-operator</code>. Common: OOMKilled due to insufficient memory.</td><td>Increase memory limits: <code>--set trivy.resources.limits.memory=4Gi</code>. The vulnerability DB alone is ~500MB compressed.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>VulnerabilityReport shows "Unknown" severity</strong></td><td>CVSS score is missing from the CVE entry in NVD. Some CVEs are published without scores initially.</td><td>Wait 24-48 hours for NVD to assign a score. Use <code>--severity CRITICAL,HIGH</code> to filter out unknowns.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Scans take too long (&gt;10 min per image)</strong></td><td>Large images (1GB+) with many layers. Trivy downloads each layer sequentially.</td><td>Use smaller base images (distroless, Alpine). Set <code>scanJobsConcurrentLimit</code> to control parallelism. Schedule scans during low-traffic hours.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Private registry images not scanned</strong></td><td>Trivy Operator doesn't have credentials to pull from private registries.</td><td>Create a Secret with registry credentials and reference it: <code>kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred --docker-server=myreg.io --docker-username=... --docker-password=...</code> then <code>helm upgrade trivy-operator aqua/trivy-operator --set trivy.privateRegistryScanSecretsNames={regcred}</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ClusterComplianceReport shows stale data</strong></td><td>Scans are scheduled but not completing. Check if scan jobs are pending or failing.</td><td><code>kubectl get jobs -n trivy-system</code> — look for failed/pending scan jobs. Increase <code>scanJobsConcurrentLimit</code> if jobs are queued.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

