            <section class="section-block" id="s32-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">32.2 Security Benchmarking in CI/CD — Detecting Configuration Drift</h3>
                <p><strong>kube-bench</strong> (by Aqua Security) checks your cluster against the <strong>CIS Kubernetes Benchmark</strong> — a comprehensive set of 100+ security configuration checks. Running it once after cluster setup is not enough: <strong>configuration drift</strong> happens when someone manually edits a kubelet flag, modifies API server arguments, or changes a file permission. Running kube-bench on a <strong>schedule</strong> (as a CronJob) ensures you detect drift within hours, not months.</p>

                <h4>kube-bench as a Kubernetes CronJob</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — kube-bench CronJob for nightly CIS benchmark checks</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: kube-bench-nightly
  namespace: security
spec:
  schedule: "0 3 * * *"           # 3:00 AM daily
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      template:
        spec:
          hostPID: true             # Needed to check node-level configs
          containers:
          - name: kube-bench
            image: aquasec/kube-bench:v0.8.0
            command:
            - kube-bench
            - --benchmark
            - cis-1.23               # CIS benchmark version
            - --targets
            - master,node            # Check both master and worker nodes
            - --json
            - --outputfile
            - /results/kube-bench-report.json
            volumeMounts:
            - name: results
              mountPath: /results
            - name: etc-kubernetes
              mountPath: /etc/kubernetes
              readOnly: true
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
          volumes:
          - name: results
            emptyDir: {}
          - name: etc-kubernetes
            hostPath:
              path: /etc/kubernetes
              type: Directory</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Processing kube-bench Results — Detecting Drift</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Compare today's kube-bench results with yesterday's baseline</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Extract FAIL count from latest kube-bench report
kubectl exec -n security job/kube-bench-nightly-$(date +%Y%m%d) \
  -- cat /results/kube-bench-report.json | \
  jq '[.Controls[].groups[].checks[] | select(.state=="FAIL")] | length'

# Compare with previous run (store in ConfigMap)
PREVIOUS=$(kubectl get configmap kube-bench-baseline -n security \
  -o jsonpath='{.data.fail_count}')
CURRENT=$(calculate_current_fails)

if [ "$CURRENT" -gt "$PREVIOUS" ]; then
  echo "ALERT: $((CURRENT - PREVIOUS)) NEW CIS benchmark failures detected!"
  echo "Configuration drift detected. Investigate immediately."

  # Find which checks newly failed
  diff <(kubectl get configmap kube-bench-baseline -n security \
    -o jsonpath='{.data.failed_checks}') <(get_current_failed_checks)
fi</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>CIS ID</th><th>What It Detects</th><th>Drift Scenario</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>API Server anonymous-auth</td><td>1.1.1</td><td><code>--anonymous-auth</code> must be false</td><td>Someone re-enabled for debugging, forgot to disable</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>etcd auto-TLS</td><td>2.4.1</td><td><code>--peer-auto-tls</code> must be false</td><td>New etcd node joined with insecure config</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>kubelet read-only port</td><td>4.2.1</td><td><code>--read-only-port</code> must be 0</td><td>kubelet restart lost custom flags, defaulted to port 10255</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>RBAC minimal</td><td>5.1.1</td><td>No overly-broad ClusterRoles</td><td>New operator installed with cluster-admin binding</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>PodSecurityPolicy</td><td>5.2.1</td><td>PSP or PSS must be enforced</td><td>Namespace label accidentally removed, PSS unenforced</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>The CIS Kubernetes Benchmark — What It Covers</h4>
                <p>The <strong>CIS (Center for Internet Security) Kubernetes Benchmark</strong> is the industry-standard security configuration guide. It organizes checks into <strong>5 sections</strong>:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔐</div><h5>Section 1: Control Plane</h5><p>API server, scheduler, controller-manager flags. Covers anonymous auth, insecure ports, TLS configuration, audit logging. <strong>~30 checks</strong> for master nodes only.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div><h5>Section 2: etcd</h5><p>etcd-specific security: peer/client TLS, auto-TLS disabled, certificate file permissions. <strong>~10 checks</strong> for etcd nodes.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🖥️</div><h5>Section 3: Control Plane Config</h5><p>File permissions and ownership for Kubernetes PKI certificates, config files, service account keys. <strong>~15 checks</strong> for master nodes.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">⚙️</div><h5>Section 4: Worker Nodes</h5><p>Kubelet configuration, kube-proxy settings, read-only port disabled, RotateKubeletServerCertificate enabled. <strong>~20 checks</strong> for all nodes.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📜</div><h5>Section 5: Policies</h5><p>RBAC, PodSecurityPolicies/Standards, NetworkPolicies, Secret management, admission control plugins. <strong>~30 checks</strong> — cluster-wide.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>kube-bench Output — Understanding the Report</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Text — Example kube-bench output (abbreviated)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-text">[INFO] 1 Master Node Security Configuration
[INFO] 1.1 Master Node Configuration Files
[PASS] 1.1.1 Ensure that the API server pod specification file
        permissions are set to 600 or more restrictive (Automated)
[PASS] 1.1.2 Ensure that the API server pod specification file
        ownership is set to root:root (Automated)
[FAIL] 1.1.3 Ensure that the controller manager pod specification
        file permissions are set to 600 or more restrictive (Automated)
[FAIL] 1.1.4 Ensure that the controller manager pod specification
        file ownership is set to root:root (Automated)

[INFO] 1.2 API Server
[PASS] 1.2.1 Ensure that the --anonymous-auth argument is set to false
[PASS] 1.2.2 Ensure that the --basic-auth-file argument is not set
[FAIL] 1.2.3 Ensure that the --token-auth-file parameter is not set

... (continued for ~100 checks)

