40 checks PASS
6 checks FAIL
2 checks WARN
1 checks INFO</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>kube-bench as a DaemonSet — Alternative to CronJob</h4>
                <p>A CronJob runs kube-bench on a schedule. A <strong>DaemonSet</strong> runs it on every node continuously. Use DaemonSet when you need per-node results and faster drift detection (every hour vs nightly):</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — kube-bench as a DaemonSet (runs on every node)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: kube-bench
  namespace: security
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: kube-bench
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: kube-bench
    spec:
      hostPID: true
      containers:
      - name: kube-bench
        image: aquasec/kube-bench:v0.8.0
        command:
        - /bin/sh
        - -c
        - |
          while true; do
            kube-bench --benchmark cis-1.23 --json \
              --outputfile /results/kube-bench-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).json
            sleep 3600   # Run every hour
          done
        volumeMounts:
        - name: results
          mountPath: /results
        - name: etc-kubernetes
          mountPath: /etc/kubernetes
          readOnly: true
        - name: var-lib
          mountPath: /var/lib
          readOnly: true
      volumes:
      - name: results
        emptyDir: {}
      - name: etc-kubernetes
        hostPath:
          path: /etc/kubernetes
          type: Directory
      - name: var-lib
        hostPath:
          path: /var/lib
          type: Directory</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Drift Detection Decision Tree</h4>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="decision-node"><strong>Did kube-bench fail count increase from baseline?</strong></div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div class="branch yes"><span class="branch-label">YES → Drift Detected</span><p>Investigate immediately. Check git log for cluster config changes, audit log for manual kubectl edits, node SSH access logs. Determine which CIS check newly failed and why.</p></div>
                        <div class="branch no"><span class="branch-label">NO → No Drift</span><p>Cluster configuration is stable. Record pass count as new baseline. Continue monitoring on schedule.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Common kube-bench Failures — Exact Remediation Commands</h4>
                <p>The CKS exam may ask you to fix specific CIS benchmark failures. Here are the most common ones and their exact fixes:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>CIS ID</th><th>Failure</th><th>Exact Remediation Command</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.1.1</strong></td><td>API server <code>--anonymous-auth</code> is true</td><td>Edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>, set <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>. The kubelet auto-restarts the API server.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.1.3</strong></td><td>Controller manager pod spec permissions not 600</td><td><code>chmod 600 /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.1.4</strong></td><td>Controller manager pod spec ownership not root:root</td><td><code>chown root:root /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.1.9</strong></td><td>API server <code>--authorization-mode</code> doesn't include Node,RBAC</td><td>Edit API server manifest, set <code>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</code>. This is critical — without Node authorization, kubelets can access any resource.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1.2.6</strong></td><td>API server <code>--profiling</code> is true (exposes debug endpoints)</td><td>Edit API server manifest, set <code>--profiling=false</code>. Profiling exposes memory/CPU data that aids reconnaissance.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>2.3.1</strong></td><td>etcd <code>--auto-tls</code> is true (allows self-signed certs)</td><td>Edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code>, remove <code>--auto-tls=true</code> or set <code>--auto-tls=false</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>4.1.1</strong></td><td>kubelet <code>--anonymous-auth</code> is true (unauthenticated kubelet access)</td><td>Edit kubelet config (<code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>), set <code>authentication.anonymous.enabled: false</code>, restart kubelet</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>4.2.1</strong></td><td>kubelet <code>--read-only-port</code> is non-zero (exposes unauthenticated metrics)</td><td>Edit kubelet config, set <code>readOnlyPort: 0</code>. This disables the legacy unauthenticated port 10255.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>5.1.5</strong></td><td>Default ServiceAccount has cluster-admin binding</td><td><code>kubectl delete clusterrolebinding default-cluster-admin</code> (if it exists). Default SA should have NO permissions.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>kube-bench in CI/CD — Shift-Left Security Benchmarking</h4>
                <p>Running kube-bench as a CronJob detects drift <em>after</em> it happens. Running it in CI/CD <strong>prevents</strong> drift before cluster provisioning. Integrate kube-bench into your Infrastructure-as-Code pipeline:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — GitHub Actions: Run kube-bench after Terraform provisions a new cluster</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># .github/workflows/cluster-security.yml
name: Cluster Security Validation
on:
  push:
    paths:
    - 'terraform/**'
    - 'ansible/**'
jobs:
  security-benchmark:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
    - name: Run kube-bench on newly-provisioned cluster
      run: |
        # Download kube-bench binary
        curl -L https://github.com/aquasecurity/kube-bench/releases/download/v0.8.0/kube-bench_0.8.0_linux_amd64.tar.gz | tar xz

        # Run against the cluster (uses current kubeconfig)
        ./kube-bench --benchmark cis-1.23 \
          --targets master,node \
          --json \
          --outputfile kube-bench-results.json

        # Parse results — fail pipeline if any FAIL
        FAIL_COUNT=$(jq '[.Controls[].groups[].checks[] | select(.state=="FAIL")] | length' kube-bench-results.json)

        if [ "$FAIL_COUNT" -gt 0 ]; then
          echo "❌ $FAIL_COUNT CIS benchmark checks FAILED"
          jq '.Controls[].groups[].checks[] | select(.state=="FAIL") | {id: .id, description: .text}' kube-bench-results.json
          exit 1
        fi
        echo "✅ All CIS checks passed"

    - name: Store baseline for drift detection
      run: |
        kubectl create configmap kube-bench-baseline \
          --from-file=kube-bench-results.json \
          -n security --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Automated Remediation — From Detection to Fix</h4>
                <p>Detecting drift is half the battle. <strong>Automated remediation</strong> closes the loop — when kube-bench finds a known fixable failure, fix it automatically instead of opening a ticket:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Auto-remediate common kube-bench failures (use with caution!)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
# Run kube-bench, parse results, auto-fix known-safe failures
# ⚠️ ONLY run in non-production environments first. Test thoroughly.

REPORT=$(kube-bench --benchmark cis-1.23 --json 2>/dev/null)

# Auto-fix: File permissions (1.1.3, 1.1.4, 1.1.5 — always safe to fix)
echo "$REPORT" | jq -r '.Controls[].groups[].checks[] |
  select(.state=="FAIL" and (.id | test("1.1.[3-5]"))) |
  .remediation' | while read -r cmd; do
  echo "Auto-fixing: $cmd"
  eval "$cmd"  # Execute the remediation command
done

# Auto-fix: Anonymous auth (1.1.1 — safe if you have RBAC)
if echo "$REPORT" | jq -e '.Controls[].groups[].checks[] |
  select(.id=="1.1.1" and .state=="FAIL")' > /dev/null 2>&1; then
  echo "Auto-fixing: Disabling anonymous auth on API server"
  sed -i 's/--anonymous-auth=true/--anonymous-auth=false/' \
    /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
fi

echo "✅ Automated remediation complete. Re-run kube-bench to verify."</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <div class="info-box-title">⚠️ Auto-Remediation Risks</div>
                    <p>Not all CIS failures are safe to auto-fix. <strong>Never auto-fix</strong> these in production without approval: etcd TLS changes (can break the cluster), RBAC changes (may break running workloads), kubelet flags (requires restart — may drain nodes). Always test auto-remediation in staging first, and implement as a <strong>suggestion</strong> (PR) rather than direct fix for production.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section-block" id="s32-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">32.3 Policy Compliance with OPA/Gatekeeper — Audit Mode</h3>
                <p>OPA Gatekeeper's <strong>audit mode</strong> checks <em>existing</em> resources against policies without blocking them. This is critical for brownfield clusters: you have 200+ running pods, and you can't simply enforce a restrictive policy that would kill half of them. Audit mode <strong>reports</strong> violations so you can <strong>remediate incrementally</strong>.</p>

                <h4>Gatekeeper Constraint — Require Image Digests (No <code>:latest</code> Tags)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Gatekeeper Constraint: require image digest instead of a mutable tag</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: constraints.gatekeeper.sh/v1beta1
kind: K8sRequireImageDigest     # Enforce image digests (no :latest)
metadata:
  name: require-image-digest
spec:
  match:
    kinds:
    - apiGroups: [""]
      kinds: [Pod]
    namespaces: ["easypj-prod"]
  parameters:
    exemptImages:
    - "registry.k8s.io/*"       # System images exempt
</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <div class="info-box-title">📋 Audit vs Enforcement — The Difference</div>
                    <p>Gatekeeper has three modes: <strong>dryrun</strong> (no blocking, no audit), <strong>warn</strong> (warnings in kubectl output), and <strong>deny</strong> (blocking). The <strong>audit</strong> functionality runs periodically (configurable, default 60s) and updates the constraint's <code>status.totalViolations</code> field — even for resources that existed before the constraint was created.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Querying Constraint Violations</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Get violation counts from Gatekeeper constraints</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># List all constraints and their violation counts
kubectl get constraints -A

# Detailed violation report for a specific constraint
kubectl get k8srequireimagedigest require-image-digest \
  -o jsonpath='{.status}' | jq .

# Output:
# {
#   "totalViolations": 3,
#   "violations": [
#     {"namespace": "easypj-prod", "name": "easypj-old-deploy-5d8f7c-abc"},
#     {"namespace": "easypj-dev", "name": "debug-pod"},
#     {"namespace": "default", "name": "nginx-test"}
#   ]
# }

# Find ALL pods violating ANY constraint (audit mode)
kubectl get constraints -A -o json | \
  jq '[.items[] | select(.status.totalViolations > 0) |
  {constraint: .metadata.name, violations: .status.totalViolations}]'</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Audit → Remediation Workflow</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Deploy constraint in audit/warn mode</strong><p>Gatekeeper audits existing pods for 7 days. No pods blocked. Violation reports accumulate.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>Review violation reports weekly</strong><p>Security team reviews <code>kubectl get constraints -A</code> output. Prioritize by namespace (prod first).</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>Remediate violating resources</strong><p>Fix manifests, rebuild images, update deployments. Verify violation count decreases.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>Switch to enforcement</strong><p>When violation count hits 0 for 2 consecutive weeks, switch constraint to <code>deny</code> mode. New violations blocked.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>OPA vs Gatekeeper — Understanding the Architecture</h4>
                <p>OPA (Open Policy Agent) is the <strong>policy engine</strong>. Gatekeeper is the <strong>Kubernetes admission controller</strong> built on top of OPA. Key distinction for the CKS exam:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>OPA (Standalone)</th><th>OPA Gatekeeper</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Architecture</strong></td><td>General-purpose policy engine (REST API). Can be used for any system — Terraform, Envoy, SSH, etc.</td><td>Kubernetes-native admission webhook. Uses OPA's Rego language under the hood.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Kubernetes integration</strong></td><td>Manual — you deploy OPA as a sidecar, write custom admission webhook config</td><td>Automatic — Gatekeeper registers as a ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Policy language</strong></td><td>Rego (OPA's custom declarative language)</td><td>Rego, but wrapped in CRDs (ConstraintTemplate + Constraint)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Audit capability</strong></td><td>Manual — you query OPA's REST API with data</td><td>Built-in — audit runs every 60s and updates <code>status.totalViolations</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Multi-system policy enforcement (beyond Kubernetes)</td><td>Kubernetes-only admission control + continuous audit</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Gatekeeper ConstraintTemplate — The Rego Policy Engine</h4>
                <p>The <strong>ConstraintTemplate</strong> defines WHAT to check. The <strong>Constraint</strong> defines HOW STRICTLY to check (parameters). This separation allows reusing the same Rego logic with different parameters across namespaces:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Custom ConstraintTemplate: Require all containers to have memory limits</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: templates.gatekeeper.sh/v1beta1
kind: ConstraintTemplate
metadata:
  name: k8srequirememorylimits
spec:
  crd:
    spec:
      names:
        kind: K8sRequireMemoryLimits
      validation:
        openAPIV3Schema:
          properties:
            minMemory:         # Parameter: minimum memory limit
              type: string
  targets:
  - target: admission.k8s.gatekeeper.sh
    rego: |
      package k8srequirememorylimits

      violation[{"msg": msg}] {
        container := input.review.object.spec.containers[_]
        not container.resources.limits.memory   # No limit set
        msg := sprintf("container <%v> has no memory limit", [container.name])
      }

      violation[{"msg": msg}] {
        container := input.review.object.spec.containers[_]
        min_mem := input.parameters.minMemory
        container.resources.limits.memory < min_mem
        msg := sprintf("container <%v> memory limit %v is below minimum %v",
              [container.name, container.resources.limits.memory, min_mem])
      }</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Instantiate the Constraint — Enforce or Audit</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Constraint: Enforce 256Mi minimum memory limit in production</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: constraints.gatekeeper.sh/v1beta1
kind: K8sRequireMemoryLimits    # Matches ConstraintTemplate
metadata:
  name: prod-memory-limits
spec:
  enforcementAction: deny       # "deny" = enforce, "dryrun" = audit only
  match:
    kinds:
    - apiGroups: [""]
      kinds: [Pod]
    namespaces: ["easypj-prod"]
  parameters:
    minMemory: "256Mi"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Violation Lifecycle — From Detection to Resolution</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Gatekeeper Audit Lifecycle: How Existing Violations Are Tracked</div>
<pre>
  TIME ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────►

  T0: Constraint created (enforcementAction: dryrun)
  │
  ├─ T+60s: First audit runs
  │   └── status.totalViolations: 12 (12 pods don't have memory limits)
  │
  ├─ T+7d: Weekly review. Security team sees 12 violations.
  │   └── Action: Jira tickets created for each violating namespace
  │
  ├─ T+10d: 8 pods fixed (new deployments with memory limits)
  │   └── Audit detects: status.totalViolations: 4 (auto-updated!)
  │
  ├─ T+14d: Remaining 4 fixed
  │   └── status.totalViolations: 0
  │
  ├─ T+21d: 2 weeks clean → Switch to enforcementAction: deny
  │   └── Any new pod without memory limit is REJECTED at admission
  │
  └─ T+30d: New dev pushes pod without limit → BLOCKED
      └── Error: admission webhook "validation.gatekeeper.sh" denied: ...
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Rego Language Deep Dive — The Core of OPA/Gatekeeper</h4>
                <p>Rego is a <strong>declarative</strong> policy language. You describe <em>what</em> a violation looks like, not <em>how</em> to find it. Understanding Rego is critical for writing custom Gatekeeper policies on the CKS exam:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Rego — Annotated Rego policy explained line by line</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-rego">package k8sdisallowhostports    # ① Package name = ConstraintTemplate name

# ② violation[] is a PARTIAL SET. Each iteration that reaches `msg :=`
#    adds one element to the set. Gatekeeper reports ALL elements as violations.
violation[{"msg": msg}] {

    # ③ input.review.object is the Kubernetes resource being evaluated.
    #    For a Pod admission review, it contains the full Pod spec.
    container := input.review.object.spec.containers[_]

    # ④ The underscore [_] is Rego's "iterate over all elements" operator.
    #    This line iterates over ALL containers in the pod.

    # ⑤ Check if ANY port has hostPort set. hostPort binds container port
    #    to host node port — a security risk (bypasses NetworkPolicy).
    container.ports[_].hostPort

    # ⑥ sprintf() formats the violation message shown to the user.
    msg := sprintf("container <%v> uses hostPort — this bypasses NetworkPolicy and is blocked by security policy", [container.name])
}

# ⑦ A second violation[] block checks initContainers too.
violation[{"msg": msg}] {
    container := input.review.object.spec.initContainers[_]
    container.ports[_].hostPort
    msg := sprintf("initContainer <%v> uses hostPort — this is blocked", [container.name])
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Rego Key Concepts — What You Need for CKS</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔍</div><h5>Iteration: <code>[_]</code></h5><p>The underscore-bracket syntax iterates over all elements of an array/dict. <code>containers[_]</code> means "for each container." <code>ports[_].hostPort</code> means "for each port, check if hostPort exists." This is Rego's most common pattern.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🧩</div><h5>Partial Sets: <code>violation[{}]</code></h5><p>Gatekeeper expects violations in a <strong>partial set</strong> named <code>violation</code>. Each element must be an object with a <code>msg</code> key. The body defines WHEN to add to the set. If the body evaluates to true, Gatekeeper reports a violation.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📥</div><h5><code>input.review</code></h5><p>The admission review object. <code>input.review.object</code> is the Kubernetes resource being evaluated. <code>input.parameters</code> holds the constraint's parameters (from the Constraint YAML). This is how you pass configuration to policies.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Gatekeeper Performance — Avoiding API Server Overload</h4>
                <p>Gatekeeper runs as an admission webhook. Every pod create/update passes through it. Poorly written Rego can slow down your entire cluster:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Performance Issue</th><th>Why It Matters</th><th>Solution</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Deeply nested iteration</strong></td><td><code>containers[_].env[_].valueFrom.secretKeyRef.name</code> — 4 levels of iteration. Each level multiplies the evaluation cost.</td><td>Flatten logic where possible. Use helper rules (<code>has_secret_env { ... }</code>) instead of inline iteration.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Too many constraints (50+)</strong></td><td>Gatekeeper evaluates ALL matching constraints for EVERY admission request. 50 constraints × 100 pods/day × 3 containers = 15,000 evaluations/day.</td><td>Scope constraints to specific namespaces. Use <code>match.namespaces</code> to limit evaluation scope. Merge similar checks into one constraint.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Large audit batches</strong></td><td>Gatekeeper audits all resources every 60 seconds. With 10,000 pods, this is 10,000 Rego evaluations per minute.</td><td>Increase <code>--audit-interval</code> to 300s (5 minutes). Use <code>--audit-chunk-size</code> to process in batches of 500.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Uncached external data</strong></td><td><code>http.send()</code> in Rego makes external HTTP calls. Each call blocks evaluation and can timeout.</td><td>Avoid <code>http.send</code> in admission-time policies. Use it only in audit policies with generous timeouts.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Testing Rego Policies — OPA Test Framework</h4>
                <p>Never deploy a Gatekeeper policy without testing it first. OPA ships with a built-in test framework:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Rego — Unit tests for the hostPort policy</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-rego">package k8sdisallowhostports

# Test: Pod WITHOUT hostPort should NOT violate
test_no_hostport_allowed {
    allowed := count(violation) == 0 with input as {
        "review": {
            "object": {
                "spec": {
                    "containers": [{
                        "name": "web",
                        "ports": [{"containerPort": 8080}]   # ✅ No hostPort
                    }]
                }
            }
        }
    }
    allowed == true
}

# Test: Pod WITH hostPort SHOULD violate
test_hostport_blocked {
    violations := violation with input as {
        "review": {
            "object": {
                "spec": {
                    "containers": [{
                        "name": "web",
                        "ports": [{"containerPort": 80, "hostPort": 80}]  # ❌ Has hostPort
                    }]
                }
            }
        }
    }
    count(violations) == 1
    violations[_].msg == "container <web> uses hostPort — this bypasses NetworkPolicy and is blocked by security policy"
}

# Run with: opa test . -v</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Complete Gatekeeper Policy Library — What to Deploy</h4>
                <p>Don't start from scratch. The CNCF maintains a community policy library. Here are the essential policies every CKS-secured cluster should have:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>What It Blocks</th><th>Severity</th><th>Audit First?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>K8sBlockNodePort</strong></td><td>Services of type NodePort (exposes nodes directly to internet)</td><td>HIGH</td><td>✅ Yes — existing NodePorts need migration to ClusterIP+Ingress</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>K8sDisallowedTags</strong></td><td>Images with <code>:latest</code> tag (unpinned, mutable)</td><td>HIGH</td><td>⚠️ Maybe — but most prod images should already be tagged</td></tr>
