                <h4>Security SLOs — Measuring "Am I Secure?"</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Security SLI</th><th>Measurement</th><th>SLO Target</th><th>Alert Threshold</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>CRITICAL CVEs in production images</td><td>Trivy Operator → Prometheus <code>trivy_image_critical_cves</code></td><td>0 CRITICAL CVEs</td><td>≥ 1 → P1 alert</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>CIS benchmark pass rate</td><td>kube-bench CronJob → pushgateway <code>cis_checks_passed_ratio</code></td><td>≥ 95% passing</td><td>&lt; 95% → P2 alert</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Gatekeeper constraint violations</td><td>Gatekeeper → Prometheus <code>gatekeeper_constraint_violations</code></td><td>0 violations in prod</td><td>≥ 1 in prod → P2 alert</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Falco alert rate</td><td>Falco Sidekick → Prometheus <code>falco_events_total</code></td><td>&lt; 20 events/hour</td><td>&gt; 50/hour → P3 alert</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pods without readOnlyRootFilesystem</td><td>Kyverno/OPA report → Prometheus</td><td>0 in prod</td><td>≥ 1 in prod → P2 alert</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Image scan freshness</td><td>Trivy Operator last scan timestamp</td><td>All images &lt; 24h old</td><td>&gt; 48h → P3 alert</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <div class="info-box-title">💡 Pro Tip: Security Burn Rate Alerts</div>
                    <p>Google SRE's "burn rate" concept applies to security too. If your SLO is "0 CRITICAL CVEs in production," any deviation is an immediate alert. But for "&lt; 5 HIGH CVEs," a burn rate alert fires when you consume 2% of your error budget in 1 hour (e.g., 2 HIGH CVEs appear in 1 hour = 40% of budget burned → alert). This catches rapid degradation before the SLO is exhausted.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>SLO/SLI/SLA — The Reliability Triad Applied to Security</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Definition</th><th>Security Example</th><th>Who Cares?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>SLI</strong> (Service Level Indicator)</td><td>The raw measurement. A number you can query.</td><td><code>trivy_image_critical_cves{namespace="easypj-prod"}</code> → 2</td><td>Engineers (what is the actual value?)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>SLO</strong> (Service Level Objective)</td><td>The target. A threshold the SLI must meet.</td><td>SLI &lt;= 0 (zero CRITICAL CVEs allowed)</td><td>Product + Security (what's acceptable?)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>SLA</strong> (Service Level Agreement)</td><td>The contract. What happens if SLO is breached.</td><td>If CVE count &gt; 0 for &gt;24h → $500 credit, breach notification to customers</td><td>Legal + Business (what are the consequences?)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
