                <h4>Building a Security Dashboard in Grafana — Step by Step</h4>
                <p>Instead of just describing the dashboard, here's exactly how to build each panel:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Stat Panel: CRITICAL CVEs (Big Number)</strong><p>Visualization: Stat. Query: <code>sum(trivy_image_critical_cves)</code>. Color: Green if 0, Red if &gt;0. Thresholds: 0=green, 1=red. Place this at the top-left — it's the most important number.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>Time Series: CVE Trend (Line Chart)</strong><p>Visualization: Time series. Query: <code>sum by (severity) (trivy_image_vulnerabilities_total)</code>. Legend: {{severity}}. Show last 30 days. Stack: off (lines). This shows if you're gaining or losing ground.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>Bar Gauge: CIS Pass Rate</strong><p>Visualization: Bar gauge. Query: <code>cis_checks_passed_ratio</code>. Max: 1.0. Thresholds: 0.95=green, 0.90=yellow, 0=red. Shows overall cluster hardening health.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>Table: Gatekeeper Violations</strong><p>Visualization: Table. Query: <code>gatekeeper_constraint_violations</code>. Columns: constraint name, namespace, violation count. Sort descending. Shows exactly which policies are violated and where.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">5</span><strong>Stat Panel: Falco Events/hr</strong><p>Visualization: Stat. Query: <code>rate(falco_events_total[1h]) * 3600</code>. Color thresholds: 0-20=green, 20-50=yellow, 50+=red. Spike detection for active attacks.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Managing Alert Fatigue — Don't Let Security Alerts Become Noise</h4>
                <p>The #1 failure mode of security monitoring is <strong>alert fatigue</strong>: so many alerts fire that engineers start ignoring ALL of them. Here's how to prevent it:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div><h5>1. Severity Triage</h5><p>P1 (page on-call) must be <strong>actionable and urgent</strong>. A P1 alert that fires daily for a known non-issue will be ignored within a week. Reserve P1 for: CRITICAL CVE in prod, etcd security breach, active attack detected.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">⏱️</div><h5>2. Debounce Periods</h5><p>Use <code>for: 15m</code> (not <code>for: 1m</code>) on P2/P3 alerts. A transient spike in Falco alerts during a deployment should NOT page anyone. Only alert if the condition persists. This eliminates 60-80% of false positives.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📊</div><h5>3. Alert Review Cadence</h5><p>Every 2 weeks, review ALL alerts that fired. Ask: Was it actionable? Did someone respond? If an alert fired 50 times and no one acted, <strong>delete or reclassify it</strong>. Alerts that aren't acted upon are just noise.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div><h5>4. Alert Dependencies</h5><p>Don't alert on symptoms AND causes separately. If "CRITICAL CVE in prod" fires, suppress "Image scan &gt; 48h old" for that image. AlertManager's <code>inhibit_rules</code> prevents cascading alerts from the same root cause.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div><h5>5. Runbooks for Every Alert</h5><p>Every alert MUST link to a runbook. The runbook answers: What does this alert mean? How do I verify it's real? What's the first step to fix it? Who do I escalate to if I can't fix it? Without runbooks, alerts cause panic instead of action.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📈</div><h5>6. Signal-to-Noise Dashboard</h5><p>Track <code>alerts_fired_total / alerts_acted_upon_total</code> over time. If this ratio exceeds 5:1, your alerting is broken. Target: &lt; 3:1. This single metric tells you if your security monitoring is useful or just noisy.</p></div>
                </div>
