            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>🔧 Under the Hood — How Trivy Matches Vulnerabilities: Package Enumeration → Database Lookup → CVE Correlation — The Complete Scanning Algorithm</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                <h5>🗺️ Trivy's Vulnerability Matching — The 3-Phase Pipeline</h5>
                <p>Trivy doesn't just "look up" CVEs — it performs a sophisticated correlation between installed packages, vulnerability databases, and OS-specific advisories. Understanding this algorithm helps you interpret scan results and troubleshoot false positives/negatives:</p>
                <pre class="uth-diagram">
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   TRIVY SCANNING PIPELINE — Complete Flow from Image to Report            │
  │                                                                           │
  │  PHASE 1: IMAGE ACQUISITION                                               │
  │  Input: easypj/api:v2.1.0                                                 │
  │  → Fetches image manifest from registry (OCI Distribution API)           │
  │  → Downloads compressed layer tarballs (NOT the full image)               │
  │  → Decompresses each layer into a temporary filesystem                    │
  │  → Walks the merged filesystem (all layers overlaid)                     │
  │                                                                           │
  │  PHASE 2: PACKAGE ENUMERATION (per OS type)                              │
  │  Debian/Ubuntu:  Walks /var/lib/dpkg/status (dpkg database)              │
  │                  → libcurl4=7.88.1-10+deb12u5                             │
  │  RHEL/CentOS:    Walks /var/lib/rpm/Packages (RPM database)              │
  │                  → curl-7.76.1-26.el9_3.3                                 │
  │  Alpine:         Parses /lib/apk/db/installed (APK database)             │
  │                  → curl-8.5.0-r0                                         │
  │  Python:         Walks site-packages/ for *.dist-info/METADATA           │
  │                  → Django==5.0.2, requests==2.31.0, Pillow==10.2.0       │
  │  Node.js:        Walks node_modules/*/package.json                       │
  │  Go/Java/Ruby:   Parses binary metadata / Gemfile.lock / pom.properties  │
  │                                                                           │
  │  PHASE 3: VULNERABILITY MATCHING                                          │
  │  For EACH package: (a) Detect OS version from /etc/os-release            │
  │  (b) Select advisory database (Debian Tracker, Red Hat OVAL, GHSA, NVD)  │
  │  (c) Compare version using distro-specific comparator                    │
  │  (d) Check if FIXED version exists in advisory                           │
  │  (e) Assign severity: NVD CVSS first, then distro advisory               │
  │                                                                           │
  │  OUTPUT: CVE-2023-38545 → libcurl4 7.88.1-10+deb12u5 (AFFECTED)         │
  │          Fixed in: 7.88.1-10+deb12u6 | Severity: HIGH (7.5)              │
  │                                                                           │
  │  🔑 Trivy enumerates PACKAGES and compares VERSIONS — it does NOT        │
  │     scan binaries. This misses: statically-linked vulns, custom-          │
  │     compiled software, and zero-days not yet in any database.             │
  │     Image signing (Ch26) + runtime detection (Ch29) fill these gaps.     │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>

                <h5>🔬 False Positives and False Negatives — Understanding Trivy's Limitations</h5>
                <pre class="uth-diagram">
  FALSE POSITIVE: Trivy reports CVE-2023-44487 (HTTP/2 Rapid Reset)
  in your Django/gunicorn container, but gunicorn only uses HTTP/1.1.
  WHY: nghttp2 library is installed in the base image, even though
  unused. Trivy flags ALL vulnerable packages regardless of usage.
  FIX: Remove unused packages (image minimization, Ch27).

  FALSE NEGATIVE: You COPY a statically-compiled Go binary into a
  scratch image. Trivy finds 0 packages → 0 CVEs → "clean".
  BUT: The binary was compiled with Go 1.21.0 which has CVE-2023-39325
  embedded. No package database = invisible to Trivy.
  FIX: Generate SBOM at BUILD time (Ch27) — syft scans the build
  environment, not just the final image.
</pre>
                </div>
            </details>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>CI/CD scanning and continuous scanning solve different problems</strong> — Trivy in CI/CD catches CVEs known at build time; Trivy Operator catches CVEs published AFTER deployment by re-scanning running images every 6 hours and producing VulnerabilityReport CRDs Prometheus can alert on (32.1).</li>
                    <li><strong>A one-time kube-bench run tells you today; a scheduled one tells you when something changed</strong> — run it as a CronJob or DaemonSet, store the pass/fail count as a baseline, and diff every new run against it to catch configuration drift within hours instead of the next manual audit (32.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Gatekeeper's audit mode makes brownfield policy rollout possible</strong> — <code>dryrun</code>/<code>warn</code> report violations on resources that already exist without blocking them; the lifecycle is always audit → remediate incrementally → switch to <code>deny</code> once violations hit 0 for a sustained period (32.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>A metric without an SLO is trivia, not monitoring</strong> — every security number (CRITICAL CVEs, CIS pass rate, Gatekeeper violations, Falco rate) needs a target and an AlertManager rule tied to it, routed by severity (P1 pages, P2 Slacks, P3 just annotates the dashboard) so alerts stay actionable instead of becoming noise (32.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Compliance is a snapshot; security is continuous</strong> — passing every CIS check and having zero Gatekeeper violations proves your configuration matches best practice at that instant, not that there's no active attack or undiscovered zero-day. This chapter is the machinery that keeps re-checking, forever.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Gatekeeper audit runs as a background loop, not a CronJob you create:</strong> the audit pod (part of the Gatekeeper controller-manager) must have read access to ALL resources it audits. If RBAC is too restrictive, the audit pod fails silently with "forbidden" errors — check both the controller-manager logs and the constraint's <code>status</code> field, not just <code>kubectl get constraints</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Compliance ≠ Security:</strong> Passing CIS benchmarks means your configuration matches best practices. It doesn't mean there are no active attacks or zero-day vulnerabilities. Compliance is a snapshot, security is continuous.</li>
                    <li><strong>A "clean" Trivy scan can still miss a vulnerable binary:</strong> Trivy enumerates packages via each OS's package database (dpkg/rpm/apk) — a statically-compiled binary COPYd into a scratch image has no package entry, so Trivy reports 0 CVEs even if the binary itself is vulnerable. Generate an SBOM at build time (syft) to catch what package-based scanning misses.</li>
                    <li><strong>Switching a Gatekeeper constraint to <code>deny</code> too early breaks production:</strong> the exam wants you to know the audit → remediate → enforce lifecycle — jumping straight to <code>enforcementAction: deny</code> on a brownfield cluster with existing violations will reject legitimate running workloads on their next update, not just new ones.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>If the task says "detect drift" or "catch new CVEs," reach for a schedule, not a one-off command.</strong> A single <code>trivy image</code> or <code>kube-bench run</code> only answers "right now" — the exam is testing whether you know to wire it into a CronJob/Operator (32.1, 32.2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Check <code>status.totalViolations</code> before writing any remediation script.</strong> <code>kubectl get constraints -A</code> is the fastest way to see which Gatekeeper policies already have a violation count — don't guess which namespace to start fixing (32.3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Every metric you're asked to expose needs a paired SLO in the same breath.</strong> If a task says "monitor CRITICAL CVEs," the complete answer is the PromQL query AND the AlertManager rule with a threshold — not just the query (32.4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Route by consequence, not by feeling.</strong> P1 = pages a human (CRITICAL CVE in prod), P2 = Slack (CIS drift, Gatekeeper violations), P3 = dashboard-only (scan freshness). Getting the routing tier right is often worth more points than the PromQL syntax itself (32.4).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

