            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 32 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your image passed the CI/CD Trivy scan with 0 CRITICAL CVEs two weeks ago and is still running in production. Is it still safe? What closes this gap?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Not necessarily. CI/CD scanning only proves the image was clean AT BUILD TIME — a CVE published the day after deployment leaves the running pod vulnerable with no CI trigger to catch it. Trivy Operator closes this gap: it re-scans every running image on a schedule (default every 6 hours) and on new pod creation, producing an updated <code>VulnerabilityReport</code> CRD the moment a new CVE matches an installed package.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the core distinction of 32.1 — shift-left (CI/CD) catches CVEs BEFORE deployment; shift-right (Trivy Operator) catches CVEs discovered AFTER deployment. The exam tests whether you reach for the in-cluster operator, not just a repeat CI scan.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the kubectl command to find every namespace with a pod carrying a CRITICAL CVE, using Trivy Operator's CRDs.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get vulnerabilityreports -A -o json | \
  jq '.items[] | select(.report.summary.criticalCount > 0) |
  {namespace: .metadata.namespace,
   name: .metadata.labels."trivy-operator.resource.name",
   critical: .report.summary.criticalCount}'</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>VulnerabilityReport is namespaced, one per workload. Querying with <code>-A</code> plus a jq filter on <code>report.summary.criticalCount</code> is the standard cluster-wide audit pattern — the same shape as the CIS/Gatekeeper audit queries in 32.2/32.3.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">kube-bench ran last night via CronJob and found 6 FAILs, same as the baseline stored yesterday. Two weeks later it finds 9 FAILs. What does this indicate, and what's your first investigative step?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Configuration drift</strong> — 3 new CIS checks started failing since the baseline was recorded. First step: diff the newly-failed check IDs against the baseline's failed-checks list (stored in the <code>kube-bench-baseline</code> ConfigMap), then check the audit log and node SSH access logs around the time drift likely started for manual <code>kubectl edit</code> or SSH-based config changes.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the drift-detection decision tree from 32.2: fail count increased from baseline → investigate immediately via git log / audit log / SSH logs, identify which check newly failed and why, then update the baseline once resolved.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to check both master and worker nodes against the CIS benchmark every hour instead of nightly, with per-node results. Which kube-bench deployment pattern fits, and why not the CronJob?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>A <strong>DaemonSet</strong> — it runs kube-bench continuously on every node (one pod per node, looping with <code>sleep 3600</code>), giving per-node results and hourly drift detection. A CronJob typically runs as a single Job against the cluster on a schedule (e.g. nightly) — coarser-grained and not inherently per-node without extra node-affinity configuration.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>32.2 draws this exact distinction: CronJob = scheduled, cluster-level; DaemonSet = continuous, per-node. Choose DaemonSet when the requirement explicitly calls for faster or per-node drift detection.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You have 200 running pods and want to require memory limits on every container without breaking anything today. Write the Gatekeeper rollout plan (not the full Rego), and name the enforcementAction to use FIRST.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>First: deploy the <code>K8sRequireMemoryLimits</code> Constraint with <code>enforcementAction: dryrun</code> (audit mode) — this reports violations via <code>status.totalViolations</code> without blocking any pod. Then: review violations weekly (<code>kubectl get constraints -A</code>), remediate incrementally (prioritize prod namespaces first), and only switch to <code>enforcementAction: deny</code> once violations hit 0 for 2 consecutive weeks.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the 4-step audit → remediation workflow from 32.3. Jumping straight to <code>deny</code> on a brownfield cluster is the classic exam trap — it would reject the very pods you're trying to bring into compliance.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a Rego violation rule (inside a ConstraintTemplate) that flags any container using the <code>:latest</code> image tag.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-rego">package k8sdisallowlatesttag

violation[{"msg": msg}] {
    container := input.review.object.spec.containers[_]
    endswith(container.image, ":latest")
    msg := sprintf("container <%v> uses the :latest tag — pin to a digest or fixed version", [container.name])
}</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Same shape as the hostPort example in 32.3: <code>containers[_]</code> iterates every container, the body checks a condition (here <code>endswith</code> instead of the hostPort field lookup), and <code>msg :=</code> only assigns — and therefore only adds to the violation set — when the condition is true.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your Gatekeeper policy is slowing down pod admission cluster-wide. You have 60 constraints, most unscoped. What are the two highest-impact fixes?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(1) <strong>Scope constraints with <code>match.namespaces</code></strong> instead of leaving them cluster-wide — 60 constraints × every pod in every namespace multiplies evaluation cost unnecessarily. (2) <strong>Flatten deeply nested Rego iteration</strong> (e.g. 4-level chains like <code>containers[_].env[_].valueFrom.secretKeyRef.name</code>) into helper rules — each nesting level multiplies evaluation cost per admission request.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>32.3's performance table lists these as the top two levers, alongside increasing <code>--audit-interval</code> and avoiding <code>http.send()</code> in admission-time policies — but namespace scoping and flattening iteration are the fixes with the biggest immediate payoff.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Design one PrometheusRule alert for "0 CRITICAL CVEs in production" and explain why it should route to PagerDuty, not Slack.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">- alert: CriticalCVEInProduction
  expr: trivy_image_critical_cves{namespace=~"easypj-prod|prod-.*"} > 0
  for: 5m
  labels: { severity: critical, priority: P1 }
  annotations:
    summary: "CRITICAL CVE in {{ $labels.namespace }}/{{ $labels.image }}"</code></pre>
                    <p>This routes to PagerDuty (P1) because the SLO target is exactly 0 — any nonzero value is a full SLO breach with no acceptable degradation window, unlike "&lt; 5 HIGH CVEs" which can tolerate some burn before paging. P1 alerts must stay rare and always-actionable, or they get ignored (32.4's alert fatigue section).</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The routing tier is determined by the SLO's tolerance for deviation, not by the tool that produced the metric. A hard-zero SLO = P1/page; a threshold SLO with room to degrade = P2/Slack; a freshness or informational metric = P3/dashboard-only.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Define SLI, SLO, and SLA using the "0 CRITICAL CVEs" example, and say which role (engineer/security/legal) cares about each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>SLI:</strong> the raw measurement — <code>trivy_image_critical_cves{namespace="easypj-prod"}</code> → e.g. 2. Engineers care ("what's the actual value?"). <strong>SLO:</strong> the target — SLI &lt;= 0. Product + Security care ("what's acceptable?"). <strong>SLA:</strong> the contract — if the CVE count stays &gt; 0 for more than 24 hours, a customer credit or breach notification is triggered. Legal + Business care ("what are the consequences of missing the SLO?").</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The SLI/SLO/SLA triad from 32.4 is a common exam framing question — know that SLI is the measured number, SLO is the internal target, and SLA is the external, often contractual, consequence.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A P2 "Falco alert rate high" alert has fired 50 times in two weeks and nobody has responded to any of them. What does 32.4 say to do, and what single ratio would have flagged this earlier?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Delete or reclassify the alert — an alert that fires repeatedly with zero response is pure noise, not a working control. The <code>alerts_fired_total / alerts_acted_upon_total</code> signal-to-noise ratio would have caught this: if it exceeds roughly 5:1, the alerting is broken; the target is under 3:1. A biweekly alert review cadence is exactly what's supposed to catch this pattern before it erodes trust in every other alert.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the alert-fatigue management framework from 32.4 — severity triage, debounce periods (<code>for: 15m</code>), review cadence, alert dependencies/inhibition, runbooks, and the signal-to-noise ratio are the six concrete levers, and this question exercises the review-cadence + ratio pair directly.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

