                <h4>Why Each Vulnerability Exists — Default Kubernetes Behavior Explained</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Vulnerability</th><th>Why It's the Default</th><th>Why It's Dangerous</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Pods run as root</strong></td><td>Kubernetes doesn't enforce <code>runAsNonRoot</code> by default — it's opt-in. Most base images (python, node, nginx) default to root.</td><td>Any container breakout = root on the host node. Attacker can read/write host filesystem, load kernel modules, access all processes.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>No NetworkPolicy</strong></td><td>Kubernetes is "flat network" by design — all pods can communicate. NetworkPolicy requires a CNI plugin that supports it (Calico, Cilium, Weave).</td><td>Compromised web pod can attack database, cache, and other services. Lateral movement is unrestricted.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Secrets in plaintext env</strong></td><td>It's the simplest way to pass config to apps. <code>kubectl create secret generic</code> is easy, but referencing secrets via <code>secretKeyRef</code> requires extra YAML.</td><td>Anyone who can <code>kubectl exec</code> or read pod specs can see secrets. Secrets are also plaintext in etcd by default.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>No Seccomp/AppArmor</strong></td><td>These are Linux kernel features that require OS-level setup. Kubernetes can't assume they're available on all nodes.</td><td>Container can call ANY syscall (~300+). Malware can use <code>ptrace</code> to inject into other processes, <code>mount</code> to access host filesystems.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Overpermissive RBAC</strong></td><td>The <code>default</code> ServiceAccount exists in every namespace. Many Helm charts and operators bind cluster-wide permissions without scoping them.</td><td>A compromised pod's SA token may have cluster-admin. Attacker can read all secrets, create privileged pods, delete resources.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

