                <h4>CI/CD Pipeline — Trivy Scan + Cosign Sign</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — GitHub Actions: Build, scan, sign easypj image</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">name: Build & Secure easypj Image
on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
    paths:
    - 'Dockerfile'
    - 'requirements.txt'
    - 'easypj/**'
    - 'jobpost/**'

jobs:
  build-scan-sign:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
    - uses: actions/checkout@v4

    # Step 1: Build the Docker image
    - name: Build image
      run: |
        docker build \
          -t ghcr.io/anihs-canvas/easypj:${{ github.sha }} \
          -t ghcr.io/anihs-canvas/easypj:latest \
          .

    # Step 2: Scan for vulnerabilities — BLOCK on CRITICAL
    - name: Trivy vulnerability scan
      uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@master
      with:
        image-ref: ghcr.io/anihs-canvas/easypj:${{ github.sha }}
        format: table
        exit-code: 1           # Fail pipeline on vulnerabilities
        severity: CRITICAL,HIGH
        vuln-type: os,library
      # ⚠️ Pipeline STOPS here if CRITICAL/HIGH CVEs found

    # Step 3: Generate SBOM (Software Bill of Materials)
    - name: Generate SBOM
      uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@master
      with:
        image-ref: ghcr.io/anihs-canvas/easypj:${{ github.sha }}
        format: cyclonedx
        output: easypj-sbom.json

    # Step 4: Sign the image with cosign (keyless, via OIDC)
    - name: Install Cosign
      uses: sigstore/cosign-installer@v3

    - name: Sign image
      run: |
        cosign sign \
          --yes \
          ghcr.io/anihs-canvas/easypj@${{ steps.build.outputs.digest }}
      env:
        COSIGN_EXPERIMENTAL: "true"  # Keyless signing via GitHub OIDC

    # Step 5: Push image + SBOM
    - name: Push to registry
      run: |
        docker push ghcr.io/anihs-canvas/easypj:${{ github.sha }}
        docker push ghcr.io/anihs-canvas/easypj:latest

    - name: Attach SBOM to image
      run: |
        cosign attach sbom --sbom easypj-sbom.json \
          ghcr.io/anihs-canvas/easypj:${{ github.sha }}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <div class="info-box-title">🔑 Why Multi-Stage Builds Matter for Security</div>
                    <p>The <strong>builder stage</strong> has <code>gcc</code>, <code>make</code>, and other build tools — these are attack vectors. The <strong>runtime stage</strong> only has the compiled application and runtime libraries. The final image is <strong>up to 82% smaller</strong> (see the size comparison table below) and has <strong>fewer CVEs</strong> because build tools and their vulnerabilities are left behind. This is the single highest-impact security improvement you can make to a Dockerfile.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Docker Layer Caching — Security Implications</h4>
                <p>Docker builds images in <strong>ordered layers</strong>. Each <code>RUN</code>, <code>COPY</code>, and <code>ADD</code> creates a new layer. Layers are cached: if a layer hasn't changed, Docker reuses it. This has critical security implications:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Docker Layer Ordering — Why It Matters for Security</div>
<pre>
  GOOD (secure):                BAD (insecure):
  ┌─────────────────────┐      ┌─────────────────────┐
  │ 1. FROM base        │      │ 1. FROM base        │
  │ 2. RUN apt install  │ ←    │ 2. COPY . .         │ ← Code changes
  │ 3. COPY req.txt     │   │  │ 3. RUN apt install  │   every time?
  │ 4. RUN pip install  │   │  │ 4. RUN pip install  │   │ Yes → cache
  │ 5. COPY . .         │   │  │ 5. COPY req.txt     │   │ MISS, all
  │                      │   │  │                      │   │ layers rebuild
  │ ✅ Layers 1-4 are   │   │  │ ❌ Layer 2 (code)   │
  │    CACHED unless    │   │  │    changes → ALL    │
  │    requirements.txt  │   │  │    subsequent      │
  │    changes. Fast!   │   │  │    layers rebuild!  │
  └─────────────────────┘      └─────────────────────┘

  🔑 SECURITY RULE: Copy dependencies FIRST (requirements.txt, package.json),
     THEN install them, THEN copy application code LAST.

  🛡️ BONUS: This also means the base image + dependencies layer is
     SCANNED ONCE by Trivy, not on every code change.</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Image Size Comparison — Before vs After Multi-Stage Build</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Single-Stage (python:3.12)</th><th>Multi-Stage (slim, builder→runtime)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Image size</strong></td><td>~1.02 GB</td><td>~180 MB (82% smaller)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Packages installed</strong></td><td>~450 (full Debian + Python + tools)</td><td>~120 (runtime only)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CRITICAL CVEs (typical)</strong></td><td>3-5 (gcc, git, curl, etc.)</td><td>0-1 (runtime only)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Build tools present</strong></td><td>✅ gcc, make, g++, autotools</td><td>❌ None — left in builder stage</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Attack surface</strong></td><td>Large — attacker can compile malware in-container</td><td>Minimal — no compilers, no dev headers</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Pull time (cold start)</strong></td><td>~45 seconds</td><td>~8 seconds</td></tr>
