                            <tr><td><strong>capabilities.drop</strong></td><td>2</td><td>❌ 0 points</td><td>✅ 2 points</td><td>Weighted: drop ALL→2, drop some→1, missing→0</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>allowPrivilegeEscalation</strong></td><td>1</td><td>❌ 0 points</td><td>✅ 1 point</td><td>Binary: set=false → 1, missing→0</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>resourceLimits.memory</strong></td><td>1</td><td>❌ 0 points</td><td>✅ 1 point</td><td>Binary: set → 1, missing → 0</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>resourceLimits.cpu</strong></td><td>1</td><td>❌ 0 points</td><td>✅ 1 point</td><td>Binary: set → 1, missing → 0</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>seccompProfile</strong></td><td>1</td><td>❌ 0 points</td><td>✅ 1 point</td><td>Binary: RuntimeDefault/Localhost→1, missing→0</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ServiceAccount (dedicated)</strong></td><td>1</td><td>❌ default→0</td><td>✅ dedicated→1</td><td>Deduction for using default SA</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Image tag</strong></td><td>1</td><td>❌ :latest→0</td><td>✅ digest→1</td><td>Deduction for mutable tags</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>TOTAL (this 9-check subset)</strong></td><td><strong>10</strong></td><td><strong>0</strong></td><td><strong>10</strong></td><td>Points 8-9 = CKS-ready. 10 = enterprise.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Reconciling with the headline score:</strong> this 9-check subset sums to 0/10 before and 10/10 after — but easypj's <em>actual reported</em> kubesec score is 2/10 → 8/10 (§33.1, §33.9 above). kubesec's real algorithm also weighs a handful of additional reliability/metadata checks not itemized in this table (and applies a small non-zero baseline), which accounts for the difference. Use this table to understand <em>which fields move the needle most</em> — <code>capabilities.drop: [ALL]</code> is the single biggest lever — not as a literal sum-to-total worksheet.</p>

                <h4>Continuous Improvement — Beyond Level 2 to Level 3</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">📋</span><strong>Week 1-2: Image signing + SBOM</strong><p>Add cosign signing to CI/CD. Generate SBOMs. Add Kyverno verifyImages policy. This takes you from 8→8.5.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">📋</span><strong>Week 3-4: OPA policies as code</strong><p>Move Gatekeeper constraints to Git. Add CI/CD pipeline for policy testing. Deploy via ArgoCD for GitOps. 8.5→9.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">📋</span><strong>Week 5-6: mTLS + service mesh</strong><p>Deploy Istio/Linkerd. Enable mTLS for all pod-to-pod traffic. Encrypt data in transit. 9→9.5.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">📋</span><strong>Week 7-8: Vault + external secrets</strong><p>Migrate all secrets to Vault. Use Vault CSI driver for auto-rotation. Remove Kubernetes Secrets entirely. 9.5→10.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <div class="info-box-title">💡 Key Takeaway for the CKS Exam</div>
                    <p>The CKS exam tests your ability to APPLY these controls, not just understand them. For each hardening step in this chapter, practice: (1) Apply the configuration (create/edit YAML), (2) Verify it works (run verification commands), (3) Test the negative case (confirm that violations are blocked). The exam is hands-on — you'll be in a terminal, not writing essays. <strong>Memorize the verification commands in this chapter.</strong></p>
