            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Hardening is a sequence, not a checklist</strong> — image supply chain (33.2) → pod SecurityContext (33.3) → namespace/admission control (33.4) → RBAC (33.5) → network isolation (33.6) → secrets encryption (33.7) → runtime monitoring (33.8). Order matters: PSS before pods exist, writable emptyDir before readOnlyRootFilesystem, RBAC before automount is disabled.</li>
                    <li><strong>Capabilities.drop: [ALL] is the single highest-leverage field</strong> in the entire chapter — it's the only kubesec check weighted double, and it closes off privilege escalation, kernel module loading, and network-level attacks in one line.</li>
                    <li><strong>Admission control is what makes hardening durable</strong> — a hand-written secure pod spec can be edited back to insecure by any developer with access; PSS Restricted, Kyverno, and Gatekeeper make the insecure state impossible to create in the first place.</li>
                    <li><strong>Prevention and detection are both required</strong> — SecurityContext, RBAC, and NetworkPolicy prevent; Falco and audit logging detect what prevention misses. A hardened cluster with no Falco is still blind to a novel exploit.</li>
                    <li><strong>Every control is verifiable, and the exam grades on verification</strong> — each of the 7 steps in this chapter ships with its own <code>kubectl exec</code>/<code>auth can-i</code>/etcd-inspection check. kubesec's 2/10 → 8/10 jump is the proof, not the goal.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>PSS FIRST, then everything else.</strong> Apply PSS Restricted to the namespace BEFORE deploying pods. If pods are already running without the required securityContext, PSS blocks their next update rather than the pods you already have — which hides the problem instead of surfacing it. Use <code>audit</code> or <code>warn</code> mode first if pods already exist, then flip to <code>enforce</code> once violations are fixed.</li>
                    <li><strong>readOnlyRootFilesystem breaks Django if you forget the writable paths.</strong> Django needs a writable <code>/tmp</code> (for uploaded files) and may need writable <code>/app/media</code>. Always mount an emptyDir or PVC for these paths <em>before</em> enabling readOnlyRootFilesystem, or the pod CrashLoopBackOffs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting the volumeMount for EncryptionConfiguration.</strong> Adding <code>--encryption-provider-config</code> to the API server without mounting the config file means encryption silently doesn't work — the flag points at a path the container can't see. Always add both the volume AND the volumeMount.</li>
                    <li><strong>Setting runAsNonRoot without creating a non-root user.</strong> If the Dockerfile doesn't create the UID referenced by <code>runAsUser</code>, the container fails with <code>CreateContainerError</code>. The fix lives in the Dockerfile (<code>useradd</code>), not the pod spec.</li>
                    <li><strong>NetworkPolicy without a CNI that supports it.</strong> NetworkPolicy is a Kubernetes API object, but ENFORCEMENT requires a CNI plugin (Calico, Cilium, Weave). Flannel accepts the YAML but silently ignores it — always verify with <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E 'calico|cilium|weave'</code> before trusting a policy.</li>
                    <li><strong>KMS encryption without key access.</strong> The <code>kms</code> encryption provider requires the API server to reach the cloud KMS at startup. If the KMS is unreachable (VPC misconfiguration, expired credentials), the API server WON'T START — test KMS connectivity before switching providers on a live cluster.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The 7-step sequence, memorized:</strong> 1) Image &amp; supply chain (Trivy + cosign + SBOM) → 2) securityContext → 3) PSS Restricted + admission → 4) RBAC → 5) NetworkPolicy → 6) Secrets encryption → 7) Falco + audit. Then verify with kubesec + kube-bench. Recite this order before touching a terminal — it's also the order that avoids the ordering gotchas above.</li>
                    <li><strong>PSS before pods, always.</strong> Labeling an existing namespace with running non-compliant pods only blocks their <em>next</em> update, not their current state — that gap hides broken hardening until it's too late to fix cleanly mid-exam.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pair every YAML edit with its verification command in the same breath</strong> — <code>kubectl exec ... whoami</code>, <code>kubectl auth can-i</code>, <code>kubectl get netpol</code>, <code>ps aux | grep encryption-provider-config</code>. The exam grades the proof, not the intent.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

