            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 33 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations, built around the easypj 7-step hardening walkthrough.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You run <code>kubesec scan</code> on an easypj deployment and get a score of 2/10. The scan flags: no <code>runAsNonRoot</code>, no <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code>, no <code>capabilities.drop</code>, and no resource limits. Write the <strong>minimum</strong> securityContext changes to bring the score to at least 7/10. Which single field gives the biggest score increase and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Add to the container spec: <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> (+1), <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> (+1), <code>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</code> (+2 — biggest increase because it's weighted double), <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> (+1), <code>seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault</code> (+1), <code>resources.limits.memory</code> and <code>resources.limits.cpu</code> (+2). The <strong>capabilities.drop: [ALL]</strong> gives the biggest single increase (2 points) because dropping all capabilities eliminates the container's ability to perform privileged operations.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You've labeled the <code>easypj-prod</code> namespace with <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code>. A developer tries to deploy a pod that runs as root. What error message will they see? Write the exact <code>kubectl</code> command that would confirm WHY the pod was rejected.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Error: <code>Error from server (Forbidden): pods "pod-name" is forbidden: violates PodSecurity "restricted:v1.30": runAsNonRoot != true (pod must set securityContext.runAsNonRoot=true)</code>. To confirm: <code>kubectl describe namespace easypj-prod</code> shows the PSS labels. <code>kubectl get events -n easypj-prod --field-selector reason=FailedCreate</code> shows the rejection event with the exact violation.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to configure encryption at rest for Secrets. Write the <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> YAML and the API server flag needed. After enabling encryption, how do you verify that EXISTING secrets are encrypted (not just new ones)?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>EncryptionConfiguration:</strong> <code>resources: [{resources: ["secrets"], providers: [{aescbc: {keys: [{name: "key1", secret: "&lt;base64-32-bytes&gt;"}]}}, {identity: {}}]}]</code>. <strong>API server flag:</strong> <code>--encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml</code>. <strong>Verify existing secrets:</strong> Run <code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code> to re-write all secrets (they'll be encrypted on write). Then check etcd directly: <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/easypj-prod/easypj-secrets | hexdump -C</code>. Encrypted data shows as garbled binary with <code>k8s:enc:aescbc:v1:key1:</code> prefix. Plaintext data shows readable strings.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your NetworkPolicy is set to default-deny-all in the <code>easypj-prod</code> namespace. You've added rules allowing ingress→easypj:8000, easypj→postgres:5432, and easypj→redis:6379. But pods still can't resolve service names. What rule is missing, and what's the exact YAML to fix it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Missing: <strong>DNS egress rule</strong>. Without it, pods can't resolve <code>postgres.easypj-prod.svc.cluster.local</code> to an IP. Fix:</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — allow-dns NetworkPolicy</span></div>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-dns
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress:
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
    - podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          k8s-app: kube-dns
    ports:
    - protocol: UDP
      port: 53</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An attacker gains shell access inside the easypj container. List <strong>exactly which</strong> of the Ch33 hardening controls block or limit each of these attacker actions: (a) writing a backdoor to <code>/etc/crontab</code>, (b) calling <code>mount</code> to access the host filesystem, (c) accessing the Kubernetes API with <code>curl https://kubernetes.default.svc</code>, (d) connecting to the production database.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(a) <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> → write to <code>/etc/crontab</code> returns EROFS. (b) <code>capabilities.drop: [ALL]</code> → no CAP_SYS_ADMIN, <code>mount</code> syscall blocked. Also <code>seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault</code> blocks <code>mount</code> at syscall level. (c) <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> → no token file at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code>. Even if a token existed, RBAC limits what the SA can do (Ch33.5 least privilege). (d) NetworkPolicy ALLOWS easypj→postgres:5432 (it's a legitimate connection). The attacker CAN connect — this is an intentional gap because the app needs DB access. Mitigation: Falco (Ch33.8) detects unusual query patterns.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After hardening easypj, you run kubesec and get 8/10. What are the remaining 2 points likely for, and why might you intentionally not get them?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The missing 2 points are typically for: (1) <strong>Resource limits (CPU/memory):</strong> kubesec deducts for missing resource requests/limits. Intentionally skipped because proper limits depend on load testing, not security. (2) <strong>Liveness/readiness probes:</strong> kubesec checks for probes but these are reliability, not security. A score of 8/10 on kubesec with all security controls in place is production-ready. Don't chase 10/10 if it means adding non-security features.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Security scoring tools have limitations. kubesec mixes security and reliability checks. On the CKS exam, prioritize the security-related deductions and understand which "failures" are acceptable. A secure pod at 8/10 is better than an insecure pod at 6/10 with resource limits.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the most important single change you made to easypj that provides the biggest security improvement? Justify your answer.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>PSS Restricted enforcement</strong> (Namespace labels: enforce=restricted). This single change blocks: root containers, privileged pods, hostPath mounts, hostNetwork, hostPID, containers without Seccomp, containers with added capabilities. It prevents 80% of the initial 6 attack vectors in one configuration change. Without PSS Restricted, every other control (RBAC, NetworkPolicy, encryption) can be bypassed by a privileged attacker who gains root on the node via container escape. PSS Restricted prevents the escape in the first place.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>On the CKS exam, when asked to prioritize, PSS Restricted is the #1 recommendation. It's a single change with massive blast radius reduction. Combine with SeccompDefault for automatic protection of all new pods.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to verify ALL Ch33 hardening controls are active on the running easypj deployment. Write the verification commands for each layer.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Bash — post-hardening verification checklist</span></div>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"># RBAC: Check SA can't list secrets
kubectl auth can-i list secrets --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj-prod:easypj-sa -n easypj-prod
# PSS: Check namespace labels
kubectl describe ns easypj-prod | grep pod-security
# NetworkPolicy: Check policies exist
kubectl get networkpolicy -n easypj-prod
# SecurityContext: Check pod spec
kubectl get pod -n easypj-prod -o json | jq '.items[0].spec.containers[0].securityContext'
# Encryption: Check API server flag
ps aux | grep kube-apiserver | grep encryption-provider-config
# Falco: Check service is running
systemctl status falco
# Image signing: Verify with cosign
cosign verify --key cosign.pub easypj@sha256:&lt;digest&gt;</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Verification is critical after hardening. On the CKS exam, every task ends with verification. Don't just make changes — prove they worked. Use these commands as your post-hardening checklist.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What would you do differently if easypj handled payment card data (PCI DSS compliance)? What additional controls beyond the Ch33 baseline are needed?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Additional PCI DSS controls: (1) <strong>mTLS everywhere:</strong> No plaintext pod-to-pod communication. Use Istio/Linkerd with STRICT mTLS. (2) <strong>gVisor runtime:</strong> Stronger kernel isolation for all pods handling card data. (3) <strong>FIPS 140-2 encryption:</strong> Use FIPS-validated cryptographic modules (not standard AES-CBC). (4) <strong>Network segmentation:</strong> CDE (Cardholder Data Environment) in a separate namespace with dedicated NetworkPolicy. (5) <strong>File integrity monitoring:</strong> AIDE on all nodes + Falco rules for config changes. (6) <strong>Audit log retention:</strong> 12 months minimum, immutable storage.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>PCI DSS raises the security bar. The CKS baseline (Ch33) is a good start, but compliance frameworks add specific requirements. On the exam, understand that different data classifications require different control levels — "one size fits all" security doesn't work.</p></div>
                    </details>
