                <h4>How CVE-2023-50447 Actually Works — The Technical Mechanics</h4>
                <p>Understanding <em>how</em> the exploit works is essential for preventing similar attacks. CVE-2023-50447 affects Pillow's EPS (Encapsulated PostScript) image parser:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Pillow receives an uploaded EPS file</strong><p>The Django view <code>upload_image(request)</code> calls <code>Image.open(uploaded_file)</code>. Pillow detects the EPS magic bytes (<code>%!PS-Adobe</code>) and routes to the EPS parser.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>EPS parser executes Ghostscript</strong><p>Pillow shells out to <code>gs</code> (Ghostscript) to rasterize the EPS into a bitmap. It passes the uploaded file as an argument WITHOUT proper sanitization.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>Ghostscript's <code>%pipe%</code> feature is exploited</strong><p>The attacker's EPS file contains: <code>(/bin/bash -c 'command') (r) file</code> — this uses Ghostscript's <code>%pipe%</code> operator to execute arbitrary shell commands. The command runs with the SAME privileges as the Python process.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>Command executes in container context</strong><p>The attacker's reverse shell Python script runs as UID 1000 (the easypj user). From here, the attacker has full access to the container's filesystem, network, and environment variables.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Why Container Escape Failed — Ch33 Hardening Defense-in-Depth</h4>
                <p>The attacker tried several container escape techniques. Here's why each FAILED due to our Chapter 33 hardening:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Escape Technique</th><th>What It Requires</th><th>Why It Failed</th><th>Ch33 Control</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Mount host filesystem</strong></td><td><code>mount /dev/sda1 /mnt/host</code> — needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN</td><td>❌ <code>capabilities.drop: [ALL]</code> — no CAP_SYS_ADMIN</td><td>33.3: Drop ALL capabilities</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Read host processes via /proc</strong></td><td><code>nsenter --target 1 --mount --uts --ipc --net --pid</code> — needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN + CAP_SYS_PTRACE</td><td>❌ Both capabilities dropped. Also blocked by Seccomp.</td><td>33.3: Seccomp RuntimeDefault</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Load kernel module</strong></td><td><code>insmod /tmp/evil.ko</code> — needs CAP_SYS_MODULE</td><td>❌ CAP_SYS_MODULE dropped</td><td>33.3: Drop ALL capabilities</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Exploit CVE in runc/containerd</strong></td><td>Requires vulnerable container runtime + specific syscalls</td><td>⚠️ Would work IF runtime was vulnerable. Seccomp blocks many dangerous syscalls.</td><td>33.3: Seccomp + readOnlyRootFilesystem</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Abuse hostPath volumes</strong></td><td>Requires pod to have hostPath volume mount</td><td>❌ easypj deployment uses emptyDir and PVC — NO hostPath</td><td>33.3: No hostPath in pod spec</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Kubernetes API exploit</strong></td><td>Requires mounted SA token + RBAC permissions</td><td>❌ <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> — no token</td><td>33.5: Disabled automount</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Network Forensics — Detecting the Reverse Shell from Outside the Container</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Detect reverse shells from the HOST node (not the container)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># From the HOST NODE where the compromised pod runs:

# 1. Find which node the pod is on
NODE=$(kubectl get pod easypj-7d9f8c5b6-xk4mw -n easypj-prod \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}')
echo "Pod is on node: $NODE"

# 2. SSH to that node and find the container's PID
