                <h4>How Falco Rules Actually Match — Under the Hood</h4>
                <p>Understanding Falco's rule engine helps you write better rules. Falco uses <strong>libsinsp</strong> (a kernel-level system call capture library) to monitor every syscall, then evaluates rules against a stream of events:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">⚙️</span><strong>Kernel → Ring Buffer → libsinsp</strong><p>Falco's kernel module (or eBPF probe) hooks into the kernel tracepoint infrastructure. Every <code>execve()</code>, <code>open()</code>, <code>connect()</code>, <code>bind()</code> syscall is captured with FULL context: PID, UID, GID, cgroup, command line, file paths, network addresses. This goes into a shared ring buffer between kernel and userspace.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">📋</span><strong>libsinsp enriches events</strong><p>libsinsp reads from the ring buffer and enriches each event with: container metadata (name, image, labels from container runtime), Kubernetes metadata (pod name, namespace, labels from K8s API), thread info (parent process, session). This is why Falco can filter by <code>container.name</code> and <code>k8s.ns.name</code>.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">🧠</span><strong>Rule engine evaluates conditions</strong><p>Falco iterates through ALL enabled rules for EACH event. A rule matches when its <code>condition</code> field evaluates to TRUE. Conditions can reference macros (like <code>spawned_process</code>), lists (like <code>shell_procs</code>), and field comparisons. The FIRST matching rule triggers an alert — multiple rules CAN fire for the same event.</p></div>
                </div>

