            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>🔧 Under the Hood — From Kernel Syscall to Falco Alert: How Detection and Enforcement Actually Fire During the easypj Incident</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <h5>🗺️ Two Parallel Kernel-Level Pipelines Firing During the Incident</h5>
                    <p>Falco (detection) and NetworkPolicy (enforcement) both operate below the YAML you write — but they work in fundamentally different ways. Understanding the difference explains exactly why Falco caught the shell in ~1 second while the over-permissive NetworkPolicy let the database dump sail straight through:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
  PIPELINE 1: DETECTION (Falco) — reactive, sees EVERYTHING after it happens
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    execve("/bin/bash") inside easypj container
         │
         ▼
    🐧 Kernel tracepoint hook (kernel module or eBPF probe)
         │   captures PID, UID, GID, cgroup, cmdline, file paths
         ▼
    🔄 Shared ring buffer (kernel → userspace)
         │
         ▼
    📋 libsinsp enrichment
         │   + container metadata (name, image, labels)
         │   + Kubernetes metadata (pod, namespace, labels)
         ▼
    🧠 Rule engine evaluates ALL enabled rules per event
         │   "Shell in easypj container" condition == TRUE
         ▼
    🚨 Alert fires → Slack + PagerDuty (T+0:01, ~1 second latency)

  PIPELINE 2: ENFORCEMENT (NetworkPolicy) — proactive, blocks BEFORE delivery
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    NetworkPolicy YAML applied via kubectl
         │
         ▼
    🌐 CNI plugin (Calico/Cilium) watches the API server
         │   translates podSelector + rules → iptables chain
         ▼
    🧱 cali-pi-easypj-prod chain installed on every node
         │   rule 1: MARK default-deny ingress
         │   rule 2-4: RETURN (allow) for ingress:8000, DNS:53, postgres:5432
         │   rule 5: DROP everything else — pkts counter increments per block
         ▼
    ⚡ Enforcement happens IN-KERNEL, per-packet — no userspace round-trip

  🔑 KEY INSIGHT: Falco is a WITNESS — it sees the syscall AFTER it already
     happened, and alerts fast but can't block. NetworkPolicy is a GATE — it
     blocks the packet before it's ever delivered, but only for traffic that
     was explicitly denied. An ALLOWED path, like easypj→postgres, sails
     straight through with ZERO visibility unless Falco or audit logging is
     also watching what happens ON that allowed connection. That's exactly
     the gap this incident exposed: the postgres connection was allowed by
     design, so only a Falco rule watching the PROCESS (not the network path)
     could have caught the Python-based data dump — which is precisely the
     rule that was missing (Section 34.2) and precisely the rule added in
     Section 34.4.</pre>
