            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The response order is fixed: Contain → Forensics → Eradicate → Recover → Learn.</strong> Deleting the pod first destroys the evidence you need for both the post-mortem and any compliance/legal follow-up — always isolate and capture before you clean up.</li>
                    <li><strong>Defense-in-depth passed AND failed in the same incident.</strong> Falco (3 detections) and readOnlyRootFilesystem (1 block) worked exactly as designed; an over-permissive NetworkPolicy, a Falco blind spot for Python-based DB access, and a 6-hour Trivy scan window were the 3 real gaps — and no single layer is expected to catch everything.</li>
                    <li><strong>emptyDir survives a container restart but not a pod deletion.</strong> This is why eradication means deleting the POD, not just restarting the container — restarting leaves the attacker's dropped malware sitting in /tmp untouched.</li>
                    <li><strong>secretKeyRef is not a secrets vault.</strong> It keeps credentials out of the pod spec, but it still injects the actual secret value into an environment variable — anyone with shell access can read it with <code>env | grep</code>. Vault (Ch 20) is the real fix for that gap.</li>
                    <li><strong>Post-incident work is what stops the repeat.</strong> All 3 identified gaps became concrete changes — a tighter egress NetworkPolicy, 3 new Falco rules, and a stricter CI/CD severity gate — plus a recurring weekly audit CronJob so the fixes don't silently regress.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 34 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Label removal is instant:</strong> Removing the <code>app</code> label from a pod immediately removes it from the Service endpoint list. New traffic stops flowing to the compromised pod within seconds — before you even apply the NetworkPolicy.</li>
                    <li><strong>Don't forget to rotate:</strong> Deleting the pod without rotating the SA token means the attacker's token (if exfiltrated) still works. Always delete and recreate the SA, and rotate any secrets the pod had access to.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>4-Phase Response, 15-minute target:</strong> <strong>CONTAIN (2m):</strong> remove labels, apply a deny-all NetworkPolicy. <strong>FORENSICS (5m):</strong> capture YAML, logs, ps, env, audit log. <strong>ERADICATE (3m):</strong> delete the pod, rotate the SA. <strong>RECOVER (5m):</strong> redeploy, verify, document.</li>
                    <li><strong>Isolate with a label removal before you even think about the NetworkPolicy</strong> — it's the fastest possible containment action and buys you time to write the quarantine policy correctly instead of rushing it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Capture forensics in a fixed order</strong> (pod spec → env → processes → network → logs → audit) so you never forget a step under pressure — script it once, run it every time.</li>
