                <h4>🧠 Chapter 34 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations, built around the easypj Pillow-CVE incident.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Falco fires: "Terminal shell in container easypj." You SSH to the node and find the pod's PID. What <strong>three <code>nsenter</code> commands</strong> would you run to capture: (a) running processes, (b) network connections, and (c) the filesystem state inside the compromised container?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(a) <code>nsenter -t $PID -m -u -i -n -p -- ps aux</code> — enters all namespaces and lists processes. (b) <code>nsenter -t $PID -n -- ss -tunap</code> — enters network namespace only, shows TCP/UDP connections. (c) <code>nsenter -t $PID -m -- ls -laR /tmp /etc /root</code> — enters mount namespace, lists suspicious directories. The key insight: <code>-m</code> gives you the container's filesystem view, <code>-n</code> gives network, <code>-p</code> gives processes.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An attacker got shell access via CVE-2023-50447 in Pillow. They try to persist by writing to <code>/etc/crontab</code>. The write fails with "Read-only file system." The attacker then writes malware to <code>/tmp/backdoor</code> (which succeeds). Will the malware survive a pod restart? Will it survive a pod deletion? Why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Pod restart:</strong> YES, malware survives. <code>/tmp</code> is an emptyDir volume — it persists across container restarts within the same pod. The container stops and starts, but <code>/tmp</code> remains. <strong>Pod deletion:</strong> NO, malware is destroyed. emptyDir is deleted when the POD is deleted (not just the container). The Deployment creates a NEW pod with a fresh emptyDir. This is why incident response must DELETE the pod, not just restart the container.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">During the Pillow CVE attack, the attacker connects to the postgres database via <code>python3 -c "import psycopg2; psycopg2.connect(...)"</code>. Why does this succeed despite the default-deny NetworkPolicy? Should this be blocked? If so, how?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>It succeeds because the NetworkPolicy <strong>explicitly allows</strong> easypj→postgres:5432 (the app needs DB access). You can't block this without breaking the application. Instead, defense-in-depth applies: (1) Falco detects unusual query patterns (SELECT * FROM auth_user), (2) Database audit logging records the query, (3) NetworkPolicy is a LAYER — it restricts lateral movement but can't block legitimate-appearing traffic. The real fix is preventing the shell access entirely (update Pillow, apply seccomp, read-only FS for persistence prevention).</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to capture forensic evidence before deleting the compromised pod. Write the exact <code>kubectl</code> command sequence to capture: pod spec, environment variables, running processes, network connections, pod logs, and the /tmp directory contents.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">POD=easypj-7d9f8c5b6-xk4mw; NS=easypj-prod; DIR=forensics-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)
mkdir -p $DIR
kubectl get pod $POD -n $NS -o yaml > $DIR/pod-spec.yaml
kubectl exec $POD -n $NS -- env > $DIR/env.txt
kubectl exec $POD -n $NS -- ps aux > $DIR/processes.txt
kubectl exec $POD -n $NS -- ss -tunap > $DIR/netstat.txt 2>/dev/null
kubectl logs $POD -n $NS --tail=500 > $DIR/pod-logs.txt
kubectl cp $NS/$POD:/tmp $DIR/tmp-copy/</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">During the Pillow CVE incident, what 3 detection gaps allowed the attacker to progress? How would you close each?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(1) <strong>NetworkPolicy allowed external egress:</strong> The attacker downloaded a reverse shell payload. Fix: restrict egress to only required endpoints (DB, DNS, monitoring). (2) <strong>Falco didn't detect database exfiltration:</strong> No rule for unusual query patterns. Fix: add Falco rule detecting SELECT * from sensitive tables. (3) <strong>Trivy scanning latency:</strong> CVE was known but image wasn't rescanned after deployment. Fix: Trivy Operator for continuous scanning of running images.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Post-incident gap analysis is a CKS competency. The pattern: identify what SHOULD have caught the attack, explain why it didn't, implement the fix. On the exam, always propose specific, testable improvements — not vague "improve security" statements.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After the Pillow incident, you implement tighter NetworkPolicy. Write the policy that restricts easypj pod egress to ONLY: postgres (5432), DNS (53), and monitoring (9090). Everything else blocked.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata: {name: easypj-restricted-egress, namespace: easypj-prod}
spec:
  podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: easypj}}
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress:
  - to: [{podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: postgres}}}]
    ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 5432}]
  - to: [{namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {name: kube-system}}, podSelector: {matchLabels: {k8s-app: kube-dns}}}]
    ports: [{protocol: UDP, port: 53}, {protocol: TCP, port: 53}]
  - to: [{podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: prometheus}}}]
    ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 9090}]</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Post-incident hardening: the egress lockdown prevents attackers from downloading tools or exfiltrating data. Every connection must be explicitly justified. On the CKS exam, this is the standard post-incident fix for any scenario involving external communication.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between incident response for a Kubernetes pod vs a traditional VM? What makes K8s IR faster?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>K8s advantages:</strong> (1) Pods are ephemeral — delete and redeploy in seconds (VM rebuild takes hours). (2) Declarative state — you know exactly what SHOULD be running (GitOps). (3) Namespace isolation — contain blast radius by namespace, not by rack. (4) Immutable images — redeployed pod is guaranteed clean (signed + scanned). (5) Audit logging per API call — every action is recorded. <strong>K8s challenges:</strong> (1) Pods share kernel — container escape affects all pods on node. (2) Dynamic IPs — harder to trace network flows. (3) Many layers — need to check pod, namespace, node, and cloud.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Kubernetes IR is fundamentally faster because of immutability and declarative state. On the CKS exam, emphasize speed: contain in seconds (NetworkPolicy), eradicate in minutes (delete + redeploy), recover in minutes (rolling update). Traditional IR takes hours/days.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After eradicating the compromised pod and rotating secrets, how do you verify the cluster is clean? Write the verification checklist.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. No unexpected pods
kubectl get pods -A | grep -v -E "kube-system|easypj-prod|monitoring"
# 2. No unexpected RBAC
kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o json | jq '.items[] | select(.subjects[]?.name=="compromised-sa")'
# 3. No unexpected network policies
kubectl get networkpolicy -A
# 4. Falco is running
systemctl status falco
# 5. Audit log shows no more access from compromised SA
cat audit.log | jq 'select(.user.username=="system:serviceaccount:prod:compromised-sa")'
# 6. kube-bench passes
kube-bench run --targets master,node</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Verification is the final IR phase. Don't assume the attacker only compromised one pod — check for lateral movement artifacts. On the CKS exam, post-incident verification is as important as the incident response itself.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Design a weekly automated security audit CronJob that checks for: pods running as root, pods without readOnlyRootFilesystem, expired certificates, and new CVEs. What tools/commands would it run?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata: {name: weekly-security-audit, namespace: security}
spec:
  schedule: "0 2 * * 0"  # Sunday 2 AM
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      template:
        spec:
          containers:
          - name: audit
            image: security-toolkit:latest
            command:
            - /bin/bash
            - -c
            - |
              kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq '.items[] | select(.spec.containers[].securityContext.runAsNonRoot != true)' > /reports/root-pods.txt
              kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq '.items[] | select(.spec.containers[].securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem != true)' > /reports/writable-pods.txt
              trivy k8s --report summary cluster > /reports/trivy-scan.txt
              kube-bench run --targets master,node > /reports/cis-bench.txt
          restartPolicy: OnFailure</code></pre>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Automated auditing prevents regression. The CKS exam tests your ability to automate security checks. A CronJob that runs kube-bench + Trivy + custom jq queries provides continuous compliance monitoring.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After the incident, management asks: "Could this have been prevented?" Provide a prioritized list of the top 3 preventive controls with cost estimates.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse;"><thead><tr style="border-bottom: 2px solid #326ce5;"><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">#</th><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Control</th><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Prevents</th><th style="text-align:left; padding: 8px; color: #93c5fd;">Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">1</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Trivy Operator (continuous scanning)</td><td style="padding: 8px;">CVE detection within hours of disclosure</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Free (OSS)</td></tr><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1e3a5f;"><td style="padding: 8px;">2</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Egress NetworkPolicy lockdown</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Reverse shell download blocked</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Free (CNI-dependent)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding: 8px;">3</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Image signing + admission enforcement</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Only CI-built images can deploy</td><td style="padding: 8px;">Free (cosign + Kyverno)</td></tr></tbody></table>
