                <h4>How ArgoCD Reconciliation Works — Under the Hood</h4>
                <p>ArgoCD's <strong>reconciliation loop</strong> is the engine that makes GitOps work. Understanding it helps you debug sync issues and optimize performance:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Git Poll (every 3 minutes)</strong><p>ArgoCD's <code>argocd-application-controller</code> polls the Git repository at the configured interval (default: 3 min). It compares the <code>targetRevision</code> (HEAD of main branch) with the last-known commit SHA. If they differ, a new sync is triggered.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>Manifest Generation</strong><p>ArgoCD renders the manifests using the configured tool: plain YAML directory (most common for security policies), Helm chart, Kustomize, or Jsonnet. The output is a flat list of Kubernetes resource objects.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>Diff Calculation</strong><p>ArgoCD compares the desired state (from Git) with the live state (from cluster API). It uses a three-way merge: (1) previous desired state, (2) current desired state, (3) live state. This detects: new resources, deleted resources, modified resources.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>Sync Execution</strong><p>If <code>syncPolicy.automated.prune=true</code>, ArgoCD DELETES resources that exist in the cluster but NOT in Git. If <code>selfHeal=true</code>, it CORRECTS resources that were modified outside Git. Sync order respects <code>argocd.argoproj.io/sync-wave</code> annotations.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">5</span><strong>Health Assessment</strong><p>After sync, ArgoCD checks resource health: Deployment progressing? Pod running? NetworkPolicy applied? Health status is reported in the ArgoCD UI: Healthy, Progressing, Degraded, or Missing.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>ArgoCD RBAC — Who Can Sync Security Policies?</h4>
                <p>ArgoCD itself needs RBAC. You don't want any developer to be able to manually sync or override security policies:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — ArgoCD RBAC ConfigMap: Restrict sync permissions to security team</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: argocd-rbac-cm
  namespace: argocd
data:
  policy.csv: |
    # Role definitions
    p, role:security-admin, applications, sync, easypj-security-policies, allow
    p, role:security-admin, applications, get, easypj-security-policies, allow
    p, role:security-admin, applications, override, easypj-security-policies, allow

    # Read-only for all other applications (developers can see but not touch)
    p, role:developer, applications, get, *, allow

    # Group mappings (via OIDC groups from SSO)
    g, security-team, role:security-admin
    g, dev-team, role:developer

  policy.default: role:readonly     # Everyone else = read-only

  # Scopes: Restrict what each role can do
  scopes: '[groups, email]'</code></pre>
