                <h4>How ArgoCD Syncs ConfigMaps — Falco Rule Deployment in Detail</h4>
                <p>When you update a Falco rule in Git and ArgoCD syncs the ConfigMap, it does NOT automatically reload Falco. The DaemonSet must detect the ConfigMap change and restart. Here's the full chain:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Git change pushed</strong><p>You edit <code>falco/falco-rules-easypj.yaml</code> in Git. Push to feature branch, open PR, CI passes, merge to main.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>ArgoCD detects (≤3 min)</strong><p>ArgoCD polls Git, detects new commit, diffs the ConfigMap. The <code>data</code> field has changed. ArgoCD applies the updated ConfigMap to the cluster.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>ConfigMap updated in K8s</strong><p>The ConfigMap <code>falco-custom-rules</code> in namespace <code>falco</code> now contains the new rules. But Falco pods are still running with the OLD rules!</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>Kubelet syncs ConfigMap to node (≤1 min)</strong><p>The kubelet on each node detects the ConfigMap change and updates the mounted file at <code>/etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml</code>. But Falco reads rules ONCE at startup — it won't see the update.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">5</span><strong>Falco must reload or restart</strong><p>Option A: <code>kubectl rollout restart ds/falco -n falco</code> (manual, causes brief gap). Option B: Use a sidecar that watches the ConfigMap and sends SIGHUP to Falco. Option C: Configure ArgoCD to trigger a Job that restarts Falco after ConfigMap sync.</p></div>
                </div>

