                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your production cluster is destroyed. You have a Git repository at <code>github.com/Anihs-Canvas/easypj-security</code> with all security manifests and an ArgoCD Application definition. List the <strong>exact steps</strong> to restore the full security posture in under 15 minutes.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Provision new cluster (Terraform/EKS/GKE)
terraform apply   # ~10 min
# 2. Install ArgoCD
kubectl create ns argocd
kubectl apply -n argocd -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml
# 3. Deploy the bootstrap Application
kubectl apply -f argocd/application.yaml
# 4. Verify sync
argocd app sync easypj-security-policies
argocd app get easypj-security-policies
# ✅ RBAC, NetworkPolicies, PSS labels, Gatekeeper, Kyverno,
#    Falco rules, audit policy, kube-bench CronJob — all restored.</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You've stored Falco rules, Gatekeeper ConstraintTemplates, and the Kubernetes audit policy in Git. ArgoCD deploys them. A team member suggests "just run <code>kubectl apply</code> from my laptop — it's faster." Give <strong>three concrete reasons</strong> why this is a security anti-pattern.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(1) <strong>No audit trail:</strong> <code>kubectl apply</code> from a laptop leaves no record of WHO changed WHAT. Git commits provide author, timestamp, diff, and PR review chain. (2) <strong>No review:</strong> Direct <code>kubectl apply</code> bypasses PR review. A malicious or mistaken change is applied instantly with no second pair of eyes. GitOps requires approval before merge. (3) <strong>No drift detection:</strong> If the same person later edits the policy directly on the cluster again, there's no automatic correction. ArgoCD with <code>selfHeal: true</code> detects and reverts unauthorized changes within 3 minutes.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between ArgoCD selfHeal and automated sync? When would you use each for security policies?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Automated sync:</strong> ArgoCD automatically applies changes from Git when it detects a new commit. It does NOT revert manual cluster edits. <strong>Self-heal (selfHeal: true):</strong> ArgoCD continuously monitors the cluster and reverts ANY change that doesn't match Git — including manual edits, accidental deletions, and unauthorized modifications. For security policies, ALWAYS use selfHeal: true. Without it, an attacker who gains kubectl access can edit NetworkPolicies/rules and the changes persist until the next Git commit (which may never come).</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>SelfHeal is the security-critical setting. It turns GitOps from "deploy from Git" into "enforce Git as the single source of truth." On the CKS exam, selfHeal: true is the answer to "how to prevent unauthorized changes to security policies."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the ArgoCD Application that deploys all CKS security policies with selfHeal enabled. What critical annotation prevents ArgoCD from being used as an attack vector?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Application
metadata: {name: security-policies, namespace: argocd}
spec:
  project: default
  source:
    repoURL: https://github.com/Anihs-Canvas/easypj-security
    targetRevision: main
    path: policies/
  destination:
    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
    namespace: argocd
  syncPolicy:
    automated:
      prune: true
      selfHeal: true
    syncOptions:
    - Validate=true
    - CreateNamespace=true</code></pre>
                    <p>The <code>syncPolicy.automated.selfHeal: true</code> is critical. Also: restrict which repos ArgoCD can sync — never allow arbitrary public repos. Use private repos with branch protection and required reviews.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>ArgoCD itself must be secured. If an attacker compromises your Git repo, ArgoCD will happily sync malicious policies. Mitigation: branch protection rules, required PR reviews, signed commits, and restricting ArgoCD to specific repos/projects.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does GitOps for security policies help with compliance audits? What evidence can you provide to auditors?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>GitOps provides: (1) <strong>Complete change history:</strong> git log shows every policy change with author, timestamp, and diff. (2) <strong>Approval evidence:</strong> PR reviews prove changes were reviewed before deployment. (3) <strong>Deployment evidence:</strong> ArgoCD sync history shows when each change was applied to the cluster. (4) <strong>Drift evidence:</strong> ArgoCD logs show if/when manual changes were detected and auto-reverted. (5) <strong>Rollback capability:</strong> git revert proves you can restore previous state. For auditors: "show me who changed this firewall rule and who approved it" — GitOps has both answers in seconds.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Compliance is a key GitOps benefit. Traditional approaches require manual evidence gathering. GitOps makes audit evidence inherent to the process. On the CKS exam, GitOps is the recommended approach for managing security policies at scale.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A CI/CD pipeline runs 8 security tools. One of them (kubesec) is flaky and sometimes fails for legitimate configs. How do you handle this without removing kubesec or allowing insecure code through?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>(1) <strong>Advisory mode:</strong> Run flaky tools as "advisory" — they report results but don't block the pipeline. Example: <code>kubesec scan --exit-code 0</code>. (2) <strong>Threshold-based blocking:</strong> Only block if score is critically low (&lt; 3) but warn if 3-7. (3) <strong>Dashboard review:</strong> Publish advisory results to a dashboard; SecOps reviews weekly. (4) <strong>Fix the root cause:</strong> Investigate why kubesec is flaky — update rules, fix false positives. Never completely disable a security tool — reduce its blocking power but keep it in the pipeline for visibility.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Security tooling must balance rigor with developer productivity. The pattern: block on critical/high-confidence findings, warn on medium, ignore low. On the CKS exam, never suggest removing a security tool from the pipeline — adjust its failure threshold instead.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you prevent secrets from being committed to Git in a GitOps workflow? What tools scan for secrets in CI?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Pre-commit:</strong> (1) <strong>detect-secrets</strong> or <strong>git-secrets</strong> — scans staged files for API keys, tokens, passwords BEFORE commit. (2) <strong>pre-commit hooks</strong> — configured in <code>.pre-commit-config.yaml</code>. <strong>CI pipeline:</strong> (3) <strong>truffleHog</strong> or <strong>Gitleaks</strong> — scans all commits in the PR for secrets. (4) <strong>git-secrets</strong> — scans commit history. <strong>Post-commit:</strong> If a secret is detected after commit: (a) rotate the secret IMMEDIATELY, (b) use <code>git filter-branch</code> or <code>BFG Repo-Cleaner</code> to remove from history, (c) force-push to overwrite. Never assume "it was only there for a minute" — consider it compromised.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Secrets in Git are the #1 GitOps security failure. The CKS approach: prevent (pre-commit hooks), detect (CI scanning), respond (rotate + purge). On the exam, always recommend pre-commit hooks as the first line of defense.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Design the complete GitOps security pipeline: from developer branch to production deployment. What security checks happen at each stage, and what tools enforce them?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="process-steps" style="flex-direction: column;">
                        <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Pre-commit (Developer Laptop)</strong><p>detect-secrets, yamllint, kube-linter (optional). Block commit if secrets found.</p></div>
                        <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>PR Checks (CI Pipeline)</strong><p>yamllint → kube-linter → kubesec → conftest → Trivy → Gitleaks. Block merge if CRITICAL findings.</p></div>
                        <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>Merge (CI Pipeline)</strong><p>cosign sign + attest SBOM. Push signed image. SBOM uploaded to registry.</p></div>
                        <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>ArgoCD Sync (Cluster)</strong><p>ArgoCD applies manifests. selfHeal: true immediately reverts unauthorized changes.</p></div>
                        <div class="step"><span class="step-number">5</span><strong>Admission (Cluster)</strong><p>Kyverno verifyImages (cosign). Gatekeeper validating policies. PSS Restricted enforcement.</p></div>
                        <div class="step"><span class="step-number">6</span><strong>Runtime (Cluster)</strong><p>Falco monitoring. Trivy Operator continuous scanning. kube-bench CronJob weekly audit.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the complete end-to-end CKS security pipeline. Every stage has specific tools and failure criteria. On the exam, you may be asked to identify which stage is missing or what tool should be added at a specific point. Know this flow.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

