                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🖥️</div><h5>Night Before</h5><p>Test PSI browser compatibility. Charge laptop. Prepare ID. Clear desk of all papers. Test webcam + microphone. Set up in a quiet room with a closed door. Tell family/roommates you cannot be disturbed for 2.5 hours.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">⏰</div><h5>Morning Of</h5><p>Wake up 2+ hours before. Eat a proper meal. Drink water (but not too much — no bathroom breaks!). Do a 10-minute warmup: <code>kubectl run test --image=nginx</code>, write a quick NetworkPolicy, scan an image with Trivy. Get your brain into terminal mode.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div><h5>During Exam</h5><p>Read every question twice. Always run <code>kubectl config use-context</code> FIRST. Use the notepad feature to track completed tasks. If stuck for &gt;3 minutes, SKIP and return later. Save verification commands as proof.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>How CKS Scoring Works — Points, Weighting, and Partial Credit</h4>
                <p>The CKS scoring system is <strong>not disclosed in detail</strong> by the Linux Foundation, but years of candidate experience reveal consistent patterns:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>What We Know</th><th>Implication for You</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Task weighting</strong></td><td>Tasks are weighted by difficulty. A simple RBAC task may be worth 4%, while a complex encryption-at-rest task may be worth 8-12%.</td><td>Don't skip hard tasks assuming they're worth the same as easy ones. But don't spend 30 min on an 8% task either.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Partial credit</strong></td><td>YES — many tasks award partial credit. If you create the EncryptionConfiguration correctly but forget to mount it, you might get 60-70% of the points.</td><td>Always submit SOMETHING. Even a partial solution is better than a blank task. The <code>kubectl</code> command that creates the resource is often worth points by itself.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Verification is scored</strong></td><td>Some tasks explicitly ask you to VERIFY your work (e.g., "Verify encryption is working"). The verification step IS part of the score.</td><td>Read the entire task. The last sentence often says "Verify that..." — that's scorable. Run <code>kubectl get</code> or <code>kubectl describe</code> as your verification.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Idempotency</strong></td><td>The scoring script runs multiple times. If your solution isn't idempotent (fails on re-run), you may lose points.</td><td>Use <code>kubectl apply</code> not <code>kubectl create</code>. Write manifests, don't run imperative commands. The scoring system re-applies your YAML.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>No negative scoring</strong></td><td>Wrong answers don't subtract from correct ones. A wrong answer = 0 points for that task, not -X.</td><td>ALWAYS attempt every task. There's no penalty for being wrong. A lucky guess or partial solution earns points.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>How to Use the Kubernetes Documentation Effectively</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">🔍</span><strong>Search, Don't Browse</strong><p>Type <code>kubernetes.io/docs</code> into the address bar, then use the search box. Don't click through the navigation tree. Search for exact resource names: "NetworkPolicy", "PodSecurityPolicy", "EncryptionConfiguration". The search is fast and accurate.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">📋</span><strong>Copy the YAML Example</strong><p>Every concept page has a YAML example. COPY IT. Paste into <code>vi</code> and modify only the fields you need. This is 3× faster than writing YAML from memory and avoids indentation errors.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">🏷️</span><strong>Use Task References</strong><p>The CKS exam references specific Kubernetes versions (e.g., v1.30). Use the version selector at the top of the docs page to match. API fields change between versions — using the wrong version's example may fail.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">⚡</span><strong>Pre-Bookmark Critical Pages</strong><p>Before the exam, practice searching for: Falco Rules, NetworkPolicy, Pod Security Standards, EncryptionConfiguration, Audit Policy, RBAC, Seccomp, AppArmor. Know exactly which search term finds the right page in &lt;3 seconds.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

