            <section class="section-block" id="s36-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">36.3 Context Switching — The Most Important CKS Exam Habit</h3>
                <p>This single mistake causes more CKS failures than any other: <strong>forgetting to switch cluster contexts between tasks.</strong> The exam provides multiple clusters (typically 2-4). Each task specifies which cluster to use. If you solve Task 3 on Cluster A when it asked for Cluster B, you get zero points — even if your solution is perfect.</p>

                <h4>The Context Switch Ritual — Do This Every Single Time</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — The EXACT workflow for every CKS exam task</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# STEP 0: BEFORE TOUCHING ANYTHING — SWITCH CONTEXT FIRST
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# 1. COPY the context command from the task description
#    Task says: "Context: mk8s-cluster-2"
kubectl config use-context mk8s-cluster-2

# 2. VERIFY you're on the right cluster
kubectl config current-context
# Expected output: mk8s-cluster-2

# 3. VERIFY you can see the expected resources
kubectl get nodes
# If you see 0 nodes or "not found" → WRONG CLUSTER!

# 4. CHECK which namespace the task expects
# Task may say: "Namespace: security" or imply it
kubectl get ns
# If the task says "Create in namespace 'prod'" and you don't see it:
kubectl create ns prod    # Create it! Don't assume it exists.

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE RITUAL (repeat for EVERY task):
#   ☐ 1. Read the context name
#   ☐ 2. kubectl config use-context <name>
#   ☐ 3. kubectl config current-context (verify)
#   ☐ 4. kubectl get nodes (sanity check)
#   ☐ 5. NOW solve the task
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Common Context Switching Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>Consequence</th><th>Prevention</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Typing context name wrong</strong></td><td><code>kubectl config use-context mk8s-cluster2</code> (missing hyphen) → silently fails, you're still on the previous cluster</td><td>Always run <code>kubectl config current-context</code> AFTER switching. If it doesn't match, you typed it wrong.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Assuming the namespace exists</strong></td><td>You create all resources in the default namespace. Task asked for namespace "web". Zero points.</td><td>Read the task carefully. If a namespace is specified, CREATE it first: <code>kubectl create ns &lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Forgetting between tasks</strong></td><td>You solve Task 4 on the context from Task 3. Task 4's solution is on the wrong cluster. Zero points.</td><td>Make the ritual automatic. Train yourself: every time you read a new task, your fingers type <code>kubectl config use-context</code> automatically.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>SSH context confusion</strong></td><td>Task says "SSH to node01". You run commands on the wrong node. Zero points.</td><td>After SSH: <code>hostname</code> to verify. Some tasks use <code>ssh node01</code>, others use <code>ssh mk8s-node-1</code>. Pay attention to the exact hostname.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

