            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch36-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The context switch ritual is non-negotiable:</strong> <code>kubectl config use-context</code> → <code>current-context</code> → <code>get nodes</code> before touching any task. This single habit prevents the #1 cause of CKS failure — a perfect solution scored zero because it landed on the wrong cluster.</li>
                    <li><strong>The 3-pass strategy turns 120 minutes into a plan, not a panic:</strong> Pass 1 easy (0-45min, target 5-8 tasks) → Pass 2 medium (45-105min, target 4-7 tasks) → Pass 3 hard + verify (105-120min). Never work hard tasks first.</li>
                    <li><strong>Documentation is your notes, not a fallback:</strong> search kubernetes.io for the exact resource name, copy the working YAML example, modify only what's needed — 3× faster and less error-prone than writing from memory.</li>
                    <li><strong>Partial credit means always submit something:</strong> a task solved on the wrong context scores zero even if perfect; a partial EncryptionConfiguration missing one volume mount can still score 60-70%. A blank task always scores 0%.</li>
                    <li><strong>15 memorized file paths under 3 parent directories replace documentation searches entirely:</strong> knowing them cold before the exam starts saves 5-10 minutes per task across the whole exam — often the difference between finishing Pass 3 or not.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch36-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Gotchas — Ch 36 Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Don't spend 20 min on a 5-min task:</strong> Every task is weighted. A 4% task isn't worth 20 minutes. If you're stuck at the 5-minute mark, flag it and move on. Come back in Pass 3 if you have time.</li>
                    <li><strong>Read the ENTIRE task before typing:</strong> CKS exam tasks often have a twist in the last sentence. "Create a NetworkPolicy... oh, and also ensure DNS traffic is allowed." Read fully, then plan, then type.</li>
                    <li><strong>Verify, don't assume:</strong> After completing a task, run a verification command. Don't just check that kubectl apply succeeded — check that the policy actually BLOCKS traffic.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch36-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Alias and autocomplete before task 1:</strong> <code>alias k=kubectl</code>, <code>export do="--dry-run=client -o yaml"</code>, and enable <code>kubectl completion bash</code> in the first 30 seconds — worth several minutes across the whole exam.</li>
                    <li><strong>Search, don't recall:</strong> type the resource name into the kubernetes.io/docs search box and copy the example YAML instead of writing from memory — it's faster and eliminates indentation mistakes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Verify with one line, not a wall of output:</strong> prefer targeted checks (<code>kubectl auth can-i</code>, <code>kubectl get &lt;resource&gt; -o yaml | grep</code>) over scrolling through full <code>describe</code> output under time pressure.</li>
                    <li><strong>When in doubt, submit something:</strong> a wrong answer costs nothing, a blank task guarantees zero — always leave a partial attempt behind before moving on.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

