            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 36 — Lab Q&amp;A (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">10 CKS practice questions with full answers and explanations.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're 35 minutes into the exam. You've completed 4 tasks. Task 5 asks you to configure encryption at rest — you know it requires editing the API server manifest, creating an EncryptionConfiguration, and verifying with etcdctl. You estimate it'll take 12 minutes. What should you do?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>SKIP Task 5 and mark it for Pass 3. Encryption at rest is a HARD task (8-15 min). At T+35, you're still in Pass 1 (45 min). You should have 5-8 easy tasks done by now. Spending 12 minutes on one hard task in Pass 1 would put you behind schedule. Mark it in your notepad: "Task 5: SKIP — Encryption at rest — Return Pass 3." Move to Task 6. You'll return to it in the final 15 minutes when you've banked all the easy/medium points.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Task triage only works if you actually follow it — being 4 tasks in at T+35 with a hard task in front of you is exactly the trap the 3-pass strategy exists to prevent. Discipline here is what separates a pass from a near-miss.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Task says: "Context: cluster2-prod". You type <code>kubectl config use-context cluster2prod</code> (missing the hyphen). <code>kubectl config current-context</code> still shows <code>cluster1-dev</code>. You don't notice and solve the task. What's the result?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Zero points. You solved the task on <code>cluster1-dev</code> when it asked for <code>cluster2-prod</code>. The scoring script checks resources on the CORRECT cluster — it won't find your solution. This is the #1 cause of CKS failures. Prevention: ALWAYS run <code>kubectl config current-context</code> AFTER switching and verify it matches the task's context name EXACTLY. If the output doesn't match what the task says, you typed it wrong.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A mistyped context name doesn't error loudly — <code>use-context</code> on a non-existent name fails, but a typo that happens to be close can silently leave you on the previous context with no visible warning. That's exactly why step 2 of the ritual (verify with <code>current-context</code>) is mandatory, not optional.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You have 8 minutes left. You've attempted 14 of 17 tasks but are unsure about Tasks 9, 12, and 16. You haven't verified any of your earlier answers. What's the best use of your remaining 8 minutes?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>VERIFY your Pass 1 answers (the easy tasks you solved confidently). A wrong context switch on an easy task invalidates it completely. Spend 5 minutes verifying: check <code>kubectl config current-context</code>, confirm resources exist with <code>kubectl get</code>, run verification commands from 36.3. Then spend the last 3 minutes attempting ONE of the uncertain tasks — pick the one you're closest to solving. A partial solution earns partial credit. A verified easy task = guaranteed points. A desperate hard task = possible points.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Late in the exam, verification has a better points-per-minute ratio than attempting new hard tasks — you're protecting points you likely already earned rather than gambling for new ones.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to create a NetworkPolicy but can't remember the exact YAML structure. What's your fastest path to a working solution — writing from memory, searching the Kubernetes docs, or using <code>kubectl explain</code>?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Search the Kubernetes docs</strong> (fastest). Type <code>kubernetes.io/docs</code> in the browser, search "NetworkPolicy", scroll to the example YAML, copy it, paste into <code>vi</code>, modify. This takes ~60 seconds and guarantees correct syntax. <strong>kubectl explain</strong> (useful for field paths): <code>kubectl explain networkpolicy.spec</code> shows the structure but NOT a working example. <strong>Writing from memory</strong> (risky): You might forget <code>policyTypes</code> or misindent <code>ingress.from</code>. Time cost of fixing YAML errors &gt; time saved by not searching docs. The allowed browser tabs ARE your external memory.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The exam explicitly permits the Kubernetes docs precisely because writing complex nested YAML from memory under time pressure is where most syntax errors happen — use the resource the exam gives you.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span><div class="eq-question">What's the 3-pass strategy for the CKS exam? Describe what you do in each pass and roughly how long each takes.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>Pass 1 (0-45 min): Speed Run.</strong> Solve ONLY easy tasks (2-4 min each). Skip anything that looks hard or unclear. Goal: 5-8 tasks done, ~50% of exam. <strong>Pass 2 (45-105 min): Medium Tasks.</strong> Tackle medium-difficulty tasks (5-8 min each). These need more steps but you've practiced them. Goal: another 4-7 tasks done, ~85% of exam. <strong>Pass 3 (105-120 min): Hard Tasks + Verify.</strong> (a) Attempt hard tasks (encryption, Falco rules, AppArmor) — up to 10-15 min. (b) Verify Pass 1 answers — context switches, resource existence. (c) Last few minutes: submit.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the proven CKS time management strategy. Pass 1 banks easy points and builds confidence. Pass 2 tackles what you expected. Pass 3 is for stretch goals and verification. Never do hard tasks first — you'll burn time and panic.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span><div class="eq-question">What are the 5 most common CKS exam mistakes that cause point loss? List each and the prevention technique.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>(1) <strong>Wrong cluster context</strong> — forgetting <code>kubectl config use-context</code>. Prevent: run <code>kubectl config current-context</code> after EVERY switch. (2) <strong>Not saving YAML files</strong> — editing in vim and closing without <code>:wq</code>. Prevent: always <code>cat</code> the file after editing to confirm. (3) <strong>Not waiting for API server restart</strong> — editing manifests then immediately testing. Prevent: wait 30-60s, check <code>kubectl get nodes</code>. (4) <strong>Missing volume mounts</strong> — adding flags to API server manifest without the corresponding volume/volumeMount. Prevent: always check all three (flag, volumeMount, volume). (5) <strong>Not verifying solutions</strong> — assuming it works. Prevent: run the verification command after every task.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>These 5 mistakes account for ~80% of point loss on CKS. Memorize them and practice their preventions. On the exam, check for these before submitting each task.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span><div class="eq-question">You have 20 tasks and 120 minutes. What's the rough time allocation per task? Which tasks should you plan to spend 10+ minutes on?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Average: 6 minutes per task (120 min ÷ 20 tasks). But allocate unevenly: <strong>Easy (2-4 min):</strong> RBAC roles, NetworkPolicy, SA creation, PSS labels, kube-bench scan. <strong>Medium (5-8 min):</strong> Audit policy, Falco rules, Seccomp profiles, TLS Secrets, upgrade. <strong>Hard (10-15 min):</strong> EncryptionConfiguration setup, AppArmor profile creation + loading + pod annotation, complete pod securityContext hardening, etcd backup/restore. Plan to spend 10+ on: EncryptionConfiguration (requires API server manifest edit + volume mounts + verification), AppArmor (requires profile creation + parser load + pod annotation + test), and any "from scratch" security configuration.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Task triage is essential. Going into the exam, you should know which topics are hard and budget time accordingly. On the exam, don't spend 15 minutes on an "easy" task (you're overthinking it) and don't spend 3 minutes on a "hard" task (you're not solving it properly).</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span><div class="eq-question">What <code>kubectl</code> commands should you ALWAYS alias before starting the exam? What's the benefit?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-bash">alias k=kubectl
export do="--dry-run=client -o yaml"
alias kn="kubectl config set-context --current --namespace"
# Usage: k get pods, k run nginx --image=nginx $do, kn default
# Time saved: ~2 seconds per command × 100+ commands = 3+ minutes saved</code></pre><p>Also enable kubectl autocomplete: <code>source &lt;(kubectl completion bash)</code>. Combined with <code>k</code> alias, this saves 5-10 minutes over the exam.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Aliases and autocomplete are the easiest time-savers. Set them up in the first 30 seconds of the exam. On the CKS exam, every second counts — 3 minutes saved is one more verification pass or one more attempted task.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span><div class="eq-question">The CKS exam allows 1 browser tab for kubernetes.io/docs. What's the most efficient way to find what you need? Give specific URL patterns for common tasks.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-bash"># Quick URL patterns for common CKS topics:
kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/encrypt-data/  # Encryption config
kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/policy/pod-security-policy/     # PSS
kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/security-context/  # securityContext
kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/   # NetworkPolicy
kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/apparmor/  # AppArmor
kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/audit/                 # Audit policy
kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/       # RBAC</code></pre><p>Click the "Example" heading — the doc page has working YAML you can copy. Don't read the full page; scroll to examples.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The allowed browser is your external memory. Memorize URL patterns, not YAML syntax. On the exam: type the URL fragment in the search bar → click the page → Ctrl+F "Example" → copy → paste → modify. 60 seconds to a working solution.</p></div></details></div>
