                <h4>Task A: Run kube-bench on Control Plane, Fix Top 3 FAIL Items</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header"><span class="scenario-badge info">TASK</span><span class="scenario-title">Context: mk8s-cluster-1 | Time: 8 min | Points: 5</span></div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">📋</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Objective:</strong> Run kube-bench against the control plane. Identify the top 3 FAIL items and fix them. Verify each fix.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">✅ Solution</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Step 1: Run kube-bench on the control plane node
ssh control-plane-1
kube-bench run --targets master --benchmark cis-1.23

# Step 2: Identify failures (example: 1.1.1, 1.1.3, 1.2.6)
# Fix 1: 1.1.1 — API server anonymous auth enabled
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
# Add/change: - --anonymous-auth=false
# Wait ~30 seconds for the API server to restart automatically

# Fix 2: 1.1.3 — Controller manager pod spec permissions not 600
sudo chmod 600 /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml
sudo chown root:root /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml

# Fix 3: 1.2.6 — API server profiling enabled
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
# Change: - --profiling=true  →  - --profiling=false

# Step 3: Re-run kube-bench to verify
kube-bench run --targets master --benchmark cis-1.23
# Verify: The 3 FAIL items are now PASS</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Task B: Apply NetworkPolicy Set to Isolate a Namespace</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header"><span class="scenario-badge info">TASK</span><span class="scenario-title">Context: mk8s-cluster-1 | Time: 10 min | Points: 7</span></div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">📋</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Objective:</strong> Create NetworkPolicies in namespace "isolated" that block all ingress/egress, then allow DNS and a specific pod-to-pod connection.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">✅ Solution</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Step 1: Create namespace
kubectl create ns isolated

# Step 2: Default deny all ingress + egress
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-all
  namespace: isolated
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]
EOF

# Step 3: Allow DNS
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-dns
  namespace: isolated
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: [Egress]
  egress:
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system}}
      podSelector: {matchLabels: {k8s-app: kube-dns}}
    ports: [{protocol: UDP, port: 53}, {protocol: TCP, port: 53}]
EOF

# Step 4: Test
kubectl run test --rm -it --image=busybox --restart=Never -n isolated -- nslookup kubernetes.default
# ✅ Should WORK (DNS allowed)
kubectl run test --rm -it --image=busybox --restart=Never -n isolated -- wget -qO- --timeout=3 google.com
# ❌ Should FAIL (external blocked)</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Task C: Configure Ingress with TLS for a Test Service</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header"><span class="scenario-badge info">TASK</span><span class="scenario-title">Context: mk8s-cluster-1 | Time: 10 min | Points: 6</span></div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">📋</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Objective:</strong> Deploy a test web app, create a TLS Secret, and configure an Ingress to serve it over HTTPS. Verify with curl.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">✅ Solution</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Step 1: Deploy a test app + service
kubectl create ns web-test
kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx -n web-test
kubectl expose deployment nginx --port=80 -n web-test

# Step 2: Generate a self-signed TLS certificate
openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 365 -newkey rsa:2048 \
  -keyout tls.key -out tls.crt \
  -subj "/CN=test.example.com/O=test"

# Step 3: Create TLS Secret
kubectl create secret tls web-tls --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key -n web-test

# Step 4: Create Ingress with TLS
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: nginx-tls
  namespace: web-test
spec:
  tls:
  - hosts:
    - test.example.com
    secretName: web-tls
  rules:
  - host: test.example.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: nginx
            port:
              number: 80
EOF

# Step 5: Verify
curl -k https://test.example.com
# Should return nginx welcome page (use -k to skip CA verification for self-signed)</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Task D: Secure Kubernetes Dashboard Access with Token + NetworkPolicy</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header"><span class="scenario-badge info">TASK</span><span class="scenario-title">Context: mk8s-cluster-1 | Time: 10 min | Points: 6</span></div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">📋</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Objective:</strong> Deploy Kubernetes Dashboard, create a ServiceAccount with cluster-admin access, obtain its token, and apply a NetworkPolicy restricting access to a specific IP range.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">✅ Solution</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Step 1: Deploy Dashboard
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/dashboard/v2.7.0/aio/deploy/recommended.yaml

# Step 2: Create admin ServiceAccount + ClusterRoleBinding
kubectl create sa dashboard-admin -n kubernetes-dashboard
kubectl create clusterrolebinding dashboard-admin \
  --clusterrole=cluster-admin \
  --serviceaccount=kubernetes-dashboard:dashboard-admin

# Step 3: Create long-lived token (K8s v1.24+)
kubectl create token dashboard-admin -n kubernetes-dashboard --duration=24h

# Step 4: Restrict access with NetworkPolicy
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: restrict-dashboard
  namespace: kubernetes-dashboard
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      k8s-app: kubernetes-dashboard
  policyTypes: [Ingress]
  ingress:
  - from:
    - ipBlock:
        cidr: 192.168.1.0/24   # Only office network
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 8443
EOF

# Step 5: Access dashboard
kubectl proxy &
# Open: http://localhost:8001/api/v1/namespaces/kubernetes-dashboard/services/https:kubernetes-dashboard:/proxy/
# Login with the token from Step 3</code></pre>
