                <h4>Task B: Write Falco Rule to Detect Shell in Specific Container</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header"><span class="scenario-badge info">TASK</span><span class="scenario-title">Context: mk8s-cluster-6 | Time: 8 min | Points: 7</span></div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">📋</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Objective:</strong> Write a custom Falco rule that detects any shell spawned in a container named "web". Place the rule in /etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml. Test by creating a pod named "web" and running a shell inside it.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">✅ Solution</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Step 1: Write custom Falco rule
sudo tee /etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml &lt;&lt;EOF
- rule: Shell in web container
  desc: Alert on any shell spawned in the web container
  condition: >
    spawned_process and container.name = "web"
    and proc.name in (bash, sh, zsh, dash)
  output: >
    🚨 Shell detected in web container! (user=%user.name
    pod=%pod.name cmdline=%proc.cmdline)
  priority: CRITICAL
  tags: [web, shell, practice]
EOF

# Step 2: Validate rule syntax
sudo falco --validate /etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml

# Step 3: Restart Falco (or send SIGHUP)
sudo systemctl restart falco
# Or: sudo kill -1 $(pidof falco)

# Step 4: Test — create web pod and exec a shell
kubectl run web --image=nginx
kubectl exec web -- sh -c "echo 'test shell'"

# Step 5: Check Falco logs for the alert
sudo journalctl -u falco --since "1 min ago" | grep "Shell in web"
# Or: sudo cat /var/log/falco.log | grep "Shell in web"</code></pre>
