                <h4>Task C: Investigate a Falco Alert — Identify the Suspicious Process</h4>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header"><span class="scenario-badge info">TASK</span><span class="scenario-title">Context: mk8s-cluster-6 | Time: 8 min | Points: 7</span></div>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step"><div class="step-marker">📋</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Objective:</strong> A Falco alert fired: "Unexpected outbound connection". Investigate: find the pod that triggered it, identify the suspicious process, capture the process details, and determine if it's malicious.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">✅ Solution</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Step 1: Read the Falco alert
# Alert output: "🌐 EXTERNAL connection! pod=test-pod container=app dest=198.51.100.23:4444 cmdline=sh"

# Step 2: Find the pod
kubectl get pods -A | grep test-pod
# Identified: pod test-pod in namespace default

# Step 3: Inspect the pod
kubectl describe pod test-pod -n default
# Check: which image? when created? what node?

# Step 4: Check running processes in the pod
kubectl exec test-pod -n default -- ps auxf
# Look for: sh process, or the process that made the connection

# Step 5: Check network connections
kubectl exec test-pod -n default -- ss -tunap
# Look for: connection to 198.51.100.23:4444

# Step 6: Capture forensic evidence
kubectl logs test-pod -n default --tail=500 > /tmp/incident-logs.txt
kubectl get pod test-pod -n default -o yaml > /tmp/incident-pod.yaml

# Step 7: Assessment
# The IP 198.51.100.23 is not in our allowed range → likely C2 server
# Action: isolate pod immediately (see Ch34, Ch30 for incident response)</code></pre>
