                <h4>Command Pattern Recognition — Groups to Memorize</h4>
                <p>Instead of memorizing 40 individual commands, group them into <strong>6 patterns</strong>. Each pattern has a consistent structure:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔀</div><h5>Context Pattern</h5><p><code>kubectl config use-context NAME</code><br><code>kubectl config current-context</code><br><code>kubectl get nodes</code><br><code>kubectl get ns</code></p><p>ALWAYS run these first. Every single task.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div><h5>Create Pattern</h5><p><code>kubectl create ns/role/rolebinding/sa/secret/configmap</code><br>All follow: <code>kubectl create TYPE NAME [flags]</code><br>Use <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> to preview.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔍</div><h5>Inspect Pattern</h5><p><code>kubectl get/describe/logs/exec/top</code><br><code>get</code> = what exists<br><code>describe</code> = detailed state<br><code>logs</code> = what happened<br><code>exec</code> = go inside</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔐</div><h5>Auth Pattern</h5><p><code>kubectl auth can-i VERB RESOURCE --as=SA</code><br>ALWAYS test both positive (allowed) and negative (denied). <br>If the task creates RBAC, the verification is <code>auth can-i</code>.</p></div>
