            <section class="section-block" id="s38-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">38.3 Security Context Quick Reference</h3>
                <p>The pod <code>securityContext</code> appears in 50% of CKS tasks. This is the 7-field pattern to memorize:</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — The CKS Golden SecurityContext Template (copy-paste, then customize)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># Pod-level securityContext (applies to all containers)
spec:
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true              # ① MUST be true for PSS Restricted
    runAsUser: 1000                 # ② Specific UID (not 0)
    runAsGroup: 1000                #    Companion GID (paired with runAsUser, not separately counted)
    fsGroup: 1000                   # ③ Volume ownership
    seccompProfile:                 # ④ Kernel syscall filter
      type: RuntimeDefault          #    Required by PSS Restricted

  containers:
  - name: app
    image: myimage@sha256:abc...
    securityContext:
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false  # ⑤ Block setuid binaries
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true     # ⑥ Block writes to root FS
      capabilities:
        drop:
        - ALL                          # ⑦ DROP ALL capabilities
      # ADD back only what's needed:
      # add: ["NET_BIND_SERVICE"]     # For binding to ports <1024

    # Writable volumes (required with readOnlyRootFilesystem)
    volumeMounts:
    - name: tmp
      mountPath: /tmp
    - name: cache
      mountPath: /var/cache
  volumes:
  - name: tmp
    emptyDir: {}
  - name: cache
    emptyDir: {}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>SecurityContext Verification Commands</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>What to Check</th><th>Command</th><th>Expected Output</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Running as root?</td><td><code>kubectl exec POD -- whoami</code></td><td>NOT "root"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Filesystem writable?</td><td><code>kubectl exec POD -- touch /test</code></td><td>"Read-only file system" error</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Capabilities dropped?</td><td><code>kubectl exec POD -- cat /proc/1/status | grep CapEff</code></td><td>0000000000000000</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Seccomp active?</td><td><code>kubectl exec POD -- cat /proc/1/status | grep Seccomp</code></td><td>2 (filter mode)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Privilege escalation blocked?</td><td><code>kubectl exec POD -- cat /proc/1/status | grep NoNewPrivs</code></td><td>1</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>PSS compliance?</td><td><code>kubectl label ns --dry-run=server POD --overrides='{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"securityContext":{"runAsNonRoot":false}}}}}'</code></td><td>Error: violates PodSecurity</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>SecurityContext Decision Tree — Which Fields When?</h4>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="decision-node"><strong>What does the CKS task ask for?</strong></div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div class="branch yes"><span class="branch-label">"Apply PSS Restricted" or "Harden the pod"</span><p>→ Use ALL 7 fields: runAsNonRoot, runAsUser, allowPrivilegeEscalation, readOnlyRootFilesystem, capabilities.drop:ALL, seccompProfile:RuntimeDefault, fsGroup. This is the maximum-security configuration.</p></div>
                        <div class="branch no"><span class="branch-label">"Prevent privilege escalation" or "Non-root only"</span><p>→ Use: runAsNonRoot:true + allowPrivilegeEscalation:false + capabilities.drop:[ALL]. This is the minimum-viable security configuration.</p></div>
                        <div class="branch no"><span class="branch-label">"Read-only filesystem" or "Immutable container"</span><p>→ Use: readOnlyRootFilesystem:true + add emptyDir volumes for /tmp, /var/cache, /var/run. ALWAYS add volumes when setting readOnlyRootFilesystem — the app WILL break without them.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION 38.4 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s38-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">38.4 NetworkPolicy Quick Reference</h3>
                <p>The CKS exam ALWAYS tests NetworkPolicies. Memorize this 3-policy pattern:</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — The CKS NetworkPolicy Golden Trio (default-deny + DNS + app)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">---
# POLICY 1: Default deny all (ALWAYS start here)
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-all
  namespace: TARGET-NS
spec:
  podSelector: {}               # Applies to ALL pods
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress                      # Block ALL incoming
  - Egress                       # Block ALL outgoing
---
# POLICY 2: Allow DNS (required for service discovery)
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-dns
  namespace: TARGET-NS
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes:
  - Egress
  egress:
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
      podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          k8s-app: kube-dns
    ports:
    - protocol: UDP
      port: 53
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 53
---
# POLICY 3: Allow specific traffic (customize for task)
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-app
  namespace: TARGET-NS
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: myapp               # Target pods
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  ingress:
  - from:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          name: source-ns      # Source namespace
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 8080</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>NetworkPolicy Test Patterns — Verify Your Policies Work</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div><h5>Test DNS (should work)</h5><p><code>kubectl run test --rm -it --image=busybox -n NS -- nslookup kubernetes.default</code><br>If this FAILS → you forgot the DNS egress rule. This is the #1 NetworkPolicy mistake.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div><h5>Test allowed (should work)</h5><p><code>kubectl run test --rm -it --image=busybox -n NS -- wget -qO- --timeout=3 http://SVC.NS:PORT</code><br>This tests the ingress rule allowing source→target traffic.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">❌</div><h5>Test blocked (should fail)</h5><p><code>kubectl run test --rm -it --image=nginx -n OTHER-NS -- curl http://SVC.TARGET-NS:PORT</code><br>Must FAIL. If it succeeds → your default-deny isn't working or has a gap.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

