            <section class="section-block" id="s38-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">38.5 Falco Rule Quick Reference</h3>
                <p>Falco custom rules appear in nearly every CKS exam. This 5-line template adapts to any task:</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Falco Rule Template (memorize this pattern)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># /etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml — NEVER edit falco_rules.yaml!
- rule: RULE_NAME                           # ① Unique rule name
  desc: DESCRIPTION                          # ② What it detects
  condition: >                               # ③ The detection logic
    spawned_process                          #    Most common macro
    and container.name = "NAME"              #    Filter by container
    and proc.name in (bash, sh)              #    Filter by process
  output: >                                  # ④ Alert message
    ALERT: pod=%pod.name cmd=%proc.cmdline
  priority: CRITICAL                         # ⑤ CRITICAL|WARNING|NOTICE
  tags: [TAG1, TAG2]                         #    Optional metadata</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Common Falco Condition Macros</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Macro</th><th>What It Detects</th><th>Example Use</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>spawned_process</code></td><td>Any new process creation</td><td>Base for most rules</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>shell_procs</code></td><td>Shell processes (bash, sh, zsh)</td><td>Detect interactive shells</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>outbound</code></td><td>Outbound network connections</td><td>Detect C2 communication</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>inbound</code></td><td>Inbound network connections</td><td>Detect unexpected services</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>open_write</code></td><td>File opened for writing</td><td>Detect file modifications</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>open_read</code></td><td>File opened for reading</td><td>Detect sensitive file access</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Falco Lifecycle Commands</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">Bash — Falco management commands (memorize these 5)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># 1. Validate rule syntax
falco --validate /etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml

# 2. Graceful reload (no event loss)
kill -1 $(pidof falco)

# 3. Follow Falco logs
kubectl logs -n falco -l app=falco -f

# 4. Restart Falco DaemonSet (after ConfigMap update)
kubectl rollout restart ds/falco -n falco

# 5. Check Falco status
systemctl status falco    # If running as systemd service</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Falco Rule Writing Checklist — Don't Miss These Steps</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Write the rule in the RIGHT file</strong><p>Always edit <code>/etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml</code>. NEVER edit <code>/etc/falco/falco_rules.yaml</code> — it gets overwritten on Falco upgrades, wiping your custom rules.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>Validate syntax</strong><p>Run <code>falco --validate /etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml</code>. If there's a syntax error, Falco silently IGNORES the entire file. No warnings at runtime — your rules just don't work.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>Test the rule</strong><p>Trigger the condition manually: <code>kubectl exec POD -- sh -c "command"</code>. Check Falco logs for your alert. If you don't see it, the rule didn't match.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>Reload or restart</strong><p>Falco reads rules at STARTUP. After editing: <code>kill -1 $(pidof falco)</code> for graceful reload, or <code>kubectl rollout restart ds/falco -n falco</code> if running as DaemonSet.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Memorization Mnemonic — Falco Rule Fields</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Field</th><th>What to Write</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>C</strong></td><td>condition</td><td><code>spawned_process and container.name = "X" and proc.name in (Y)</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>O</strong></td><td>output</td><td><code>"ALERT: pod=%pod.name cmd=%proc.cmdline"</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>D</strong></td><td>desc</td><td>One sentence describing what the rule detects</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>P</strong></td><td>priority</td><td><code>CRITICAL</code> | <code>WARNING</code> | <code>NOTICE</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>R</strong></td><td>rule</td><td>Unique rule name (hyphenated-lowercase)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align:center;"><strong>Mnemonic: "CODPR" — Condition, Output, Desc, Priority, Rule</strong></p>
            </section>

