                <div class="uth-body">
                <h5>🛡️ Template 1: The SecurityContext (7 Fields) — Why Each One Matters</h5>
                <pre class="uth-diagram">
  securityContext:                        ← Container-level (can also be pod-level)
    runAsNonRoot: true                    ← ① REJECTS if image runs as root (UID 0)
                                               If your Dockerfile uses USER 1000,
                                               this is safe. If no USER set → crash.
    runAsUser: 1000                       ← ② Specific UID (must match Dockerfile USER)
    fsGroup: 1000                         ← ③ Volume mount ownership (GID)
    allowPrivilegeEscalation: false       ← ④ BLOCKS setuid binaries (chmod +s).
                                               Even if binary has SUID bit, kernel
                                               ignores it. Prevents privilege gain.
    readOnlyRootFilesystem: true          ← ⑤ MOUNTS root FS with MS_RDONLY flag.
                                               Any write() → EROFS (errno 30).
                                               Requires emptyDir at /tmp for apps.
    capabilities:
      drop: ["ALL"]                       ← ⑥ REMOVES all 14 Linux capabilities.
                                               No CAP_SYS_ADMIN → can't mount().
                                               No CAP_NET_RAW → can't sniff.
    seccompProfile:
      type: RuntimeDefault                ← ⑦ BLOCKS ~300 dangerous syscalls.
                                               mount(), ptrace(), reboot() — all
                                               blocked. Only ~60 safe syscalls allowed.

  🔑 MEMORIZE: r-r-f-a-r-c-s — runAsNonRoot, runAsUser, fsGroup, allowPrivilegeEscalation,
     readOnlyRootFilesystem, capabilities, seccomp. 7 fields, 30 seconds to type.
</pre>

                <h5>🌐 Template 2: The NetworkPolicy Trio — Default-Deny + DNS + App-Allow</h5>
                <pre class="uth-diagram">
  POLICY 0: default-deny-all               ← BLOCKS everything first
    podSelector: {}                         ← Applies to ALL pods in namespace
    policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]          ← MUST include both for full isolation
    # No ingress/egress rules = deny all

  POLICY 1: allow-dns                       ← CRITICAL: without this, nothing works
    podSelector: {}
    policyTypes: [Egress]
    egress:
    - to:
      - namespaceSelector:                  ← kube-system namespace
          matchLabels:
            kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
      - podSelector:                        ← kube-dns pods ONLY
          matchLabels:
            k8s-app: kube-dns
      ports:
      - protocol: UDP
        port: 53                            ← DNS uses UDP 53

  POLICY 2: allow-app-traffic               ← Allow specific app communication
    podSelector:
      matchLabels:
        app: easypj                         ← Target: easypj pods
    policyTypes: [Ingress]
    ingress:
    - from:
      - namespaceSelector:
          matchLabels:
            name: ingress-nginx             ← Source: ingress controller
      ports:
      - protocol: TCP
        port: 8000                          ← Only this port

  🔑 ADD egress rules for: database (TCP 5432), cache (TCP 6379), monitoring (TCP 9090).
     NEVER forget DNS — it's the #1 cause of "my NetworkPolicy broke everything."
</pre>

                <h5>🦅 Template 3: The Falco Rule — 5 Lines You'll Adapt for Every Custom Rule</h5>
                <pre class="uth-diagram">
  - macro: app_container                    ← ① Reusable condition: which container?
    condition: container.name = "easypj"

  - list: sensitive_binaries                ← ② Reusable list: what to watch for?
    items: [bash, sh, nc, curl, wget]

  - rule: Suspicious Activity in App        ← ③ Rule name + description
    desc: Detect dangerous commands in app container
    condition: >                            ← ④ The condition: what triggers this?
      spawned_process and                   ←    Must be a new process
      app_container and                     ←    In our specific container
      proc.name in (sensitive_binaries)     ←    Running a watched binary
    output: >                               ← ⑤ The alert message (template vars)
      Suspicious %proc.name! (user=%user.name
      cmd=%proc.cmdline pod=%k8s.pod.name
      ns=%k8s.ns.name)
    priority: WARNING                       ← CRITICAL|WARNING|NOTICE|INFO|DEBUG
    tags: [easypj, suspicious, CKS]

  🔑 Write this ONCE in your notepad at exam start. Adapt for each Falco task.
     Change: macro name, list items, rule name, condition field, output message.
</pre>
