  seccompProfile:
    type: RuntimeDefault</code></pre><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Memorize this block. It appears in ~50% of CKS tasks. Write it from memory in under 60 seconds. If you can't remember a field, search kubernetes.io/docs for "security context".</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span><div class="eq-question">From memory: what's the default-deny NetworkPolicy YAML? What's the DNS allow rule that must follow it?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>Default-deny:</strong> empty podSelector + empty ingress/egress rules + policyTypes [Ingress, Egress]. <strong>DNS allow:</strong> egress to kube-system namespace + kube-dns pod + UDP port 53. Together they block all traffic except DNS. Master these two YAMLs — they're the foundation of every CKS NetworkPolicy task.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>These two policies are the most frequently written YAML on the CKS exam. Memorize them. Default-deny first, then DNS allow, then specific application rules. Always in that order.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span><div class="eq-question">What are the 3 critical file paths you must know for the CKS exam? List them and what each contains.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p><strong>1. /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</strong> — Static pod manifests for API server, etcd, controller-manager, scheduler. Edit these to add security flags. Kubelet auto-restarts on change. <strong>2. /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</strong> — Kubelet config: anonymous auth, authorization mode, readOnlyPort, protectKernelDefaults. <strong>3. /etc/kubernetes/pki/</strong> — All PKI certificates: CA, API server, etcd, front-proxy. Also etcd subdirectory for etcd-specific certs.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>These three paths cover ~80% of file-based CKS tasks. Memorize them. When in doubt: manifests for API server config, config.yaml for kubelet config, pki for certificates.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span><div class="eq-question">What alias and autocomplete setup should you run in the first 30 seconds of the CKS exam? Write the exact commands.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-bash">alias k=kubectl
export do="--dry-run=client -o yaml"
source &lt;(kubectl completion bash)
complete -F __start_kubectl k  # Enable completion for alias 'k'
alias kn="kubectl config set-context --current --namespace"
# Verify
k version --short
kubectl config current-context</code></pre><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>First 30 seconds: aliases + completion + context check. This saves 5-10 minutes over the exam. Do it while reading the first task — muscle memory, no thinking required.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span><div class="eq-question">What's the single most important verification command after editing the API server manifest? What do you check?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><pre><code class="language-bash"># Wait 30-60s for restart, then:
kubectl get nodes  # Must show Ready
# Check the new flag is active:
ps aux | grep kube-apiserver | grep &lt;your-flag&gt;
# Verify API server is healthy:
kubectl get --raw /healthz  # Must return "ok"</code></pre><p>If <code>kubectl get nodes</code> hangs or fails, the API server didn't restart — check the manifest for YAML errors. Recovery: restore from backup.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the #1 post-edit verification. If the API server doesn't come back, nothing else works. Always backup the manifest before editing. Always verify after editing.</p></div></details></div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span><div class="eq-question">You have 5 minutes before the exam starts. What 3 things do you write on your scratch paper/notepad?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>(1) <strong>7-field securityContext:</strong> runAsNonRoot:true, runAsUser:1000, fsGroup:1000, allowPrivilegeEscalation:false, readOnlyRootFilesystem:true, capabilities.drop:[ALL], seccompProfile.type:RuntimeDefault. (2) <strong>3 critical paths:</strong> /etc/kubernetes/manifests/, /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml, /etc/kubernetes/pki/. (3) <strong>Default-deny NP + DNS NP:</strong> empty podSelector, policyTypes [Ingress,Egress] + egress rule for kube-system/kube-dns:UDP:53. Also write: <code>alias k=kubectl; export do="--dry-run=client -o yaml"</code>. This brain dump saves you from searching docs for the most common patterns.</p><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The brain dump technique. In the 5 minutes before the exam starts (while reading instructions), write down the patterns you're most likely to forget under pressure. When you need them at minute 105, they're right there on your scratch paper.</p></div></details></div>
