            <section class="section-block" id="sb-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.1 NetworkPolicy: Default Deny All (Ingress + Egress)</h3>
                <p>The <strong>foundation of network microsegmentation</strong>. Apply this FIRST to any namespace, then add specific allow rules. Without this, all pods in the namespace can communicate freely.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — networkpolicy-default-deny-all.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-all
  namespace: &lt;your-namespace&gt;    # ← CHANGE ME
spec:
  podSelector: {}                  # Applies to ALL pods in the namespace
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress                        # Block all INCOMING traffic
  - Egress                         # Block all OUTGOING traffic
  # No ingress/egress rules = DENY ALL traffic by default
  # Pods can still communicate via localhost (same pod, different containers)</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.2 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.2 NetworkPolicy: Allow Specific Pod-to-Pod Traffic</h3>
                <p>Allow ingress from pods with a specific label, on a specific port. Stack multiple ingress rules for different sources.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — networkpolicy-allow-app.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-from-ingress
  namespace: &lt;your-namespace&gt;
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: &lt;target-app&gt;              # ← Target: protect THESE pods
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  ingress:
  - from:
    - namespaceSelector:              # Allow from ingress controller namespace
        matchLabels:
          kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ingress-nginx
    - podSelector:                    # Allow from pods with this label
        matchLabels:
          app: &lt;source-app&gt;
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: &lt;target-port&gt;            # ← Only this port is allowed</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.3 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.3 NetworkPolicy: Allow DNS Egress</h3>
                <p><strong>CRITICAL: If you use default-deny-all, you MUST add this rule.</strong> Without DNS egress, pods cannot resolve service names and everything breaks.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — networkpolicy-allow-dns.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-dns-egress
  namespace: &lt;your-namespace&gt;
spec:
  podSelector: {}                    # All pods need DNS
  policyTypes:
  - Egress
  egress:
  - to:                              # Allow traffic TO CoreDNS
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
      podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          k8s-app: kube-dns
    ports:
    - protocol: UDP
      port: 53                       # DNS uses UDP/53
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 53                       # DNS can also use TCP/53 for large responses</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.4 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.4 Pod Security Context: Full Restricted Profile</h3>
                <p>The <strong>gold standard CKS pod security context</strong>. Copy this block into any pod spec for maximum hardening. Every field is annotated with WHY it matters.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — Full CKS Restricted securityContext block</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  containers:
  - name: &lt;app&gt;
    image: &lt;registry&gt;/&lt;image&gt;@sha256:&lt;digest&gt;  # ← Digest, NOT :latest
    securityContext:
      # ① BLOCK root: container MUST run as a non-root user
      runAsNonRoot: true
      # ② Specific UID — must match USER in Dockerfile
      runAsUser: 1000
      # ③ Specific GID for file access
      runAsGroup: 1000
      # ④ BLOCK setuid binaries — kernel ignores SUID bit
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
      # ⑤ MOUNT root FS with MS_RDONLY — all writes return EROFS
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
      # ⑥ REMOVE all 14 Linux capabilities (CAP_SYS_ADMIN, etc.)
      capabilities:
        drop:
        - ALL
      # ⑦ KERNEL syscall filter — blocks ~300 dangerous syscalls
      seccompProfile:
        type: RuntimeDefault           # Use "Localhost" for custom profiles
    volumeMounts:
    - name: tmp                        # emptyDir for writable /tmp
      mountPath: /tmp
    - name: runtime                    # emptyDir for /var/run (socket/PID files)
      mountPath: /var/run
  volumes:
  - name: tmp
    emptyDir: {}
  - name: runtime
    emptyDir: {}</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.5 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.5 Role + RoleBinding: Read-Only Namespace Access</h3>
                <p>Grants <strong>read-only access</strong> to pods, services, and configmaps in a single namespace. The most common RBAC pattern on the CKS exam.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — rbac-readonly.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: readonly
  namespace: &lt;your-namespace&gt;
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]                     # Core API group
  resources: ["pods", "services", "configmaps", "endpoints"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]     # Read-only — no create/update/delete
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments", "replicasets"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: readonly-binding
  namespace: &lt;your-namespace&gt;
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: &lt;service-account-name&gt;
  namespace: &lt;sa-namespace&gt;          # Can be DIFFERENT from Role's namespace
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: readonly
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.6 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.6 ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding: Cross-Namespace Viewer</h3>
                <p>Grant read-only access to pods <strong>across ALL namespaces</strong>. Use ClusterRole (not Role) for cluster-scoped or cross-namespace access.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — clusterrole-viewer.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: cross-ns-viewer                # ClusterRole — no namespace field!
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods", "services", "nodes", "namespaces"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: ["networking.k8s.io"]
  resources: ["networkpolicies"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: cross-ns-viewer-binding
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: &lt;service-account-name&gt;
  namespace: &lt;sa-namespace&gt;
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: cross-ns-viewer
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.7 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.7 ServiceAccount with Disabled Automount</h3>
                <p>Every pod that doesn't need Kubernetes API access should use this pattern. <strong>This single change prevents lateral movement via stolen SA tokens.</strong></p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — serviceaccount-no-automount.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: &lt;app-sa&gt;
  namespace: &lt;your-namespace&gt;
automountServiceAccountToken: false    # ← THE KEY FIELD: no token mounted
---
# In your Pod/Deployment spec, reference this SA:
spec:
  serviceAccountName: &lt;app-sa&gt;         # Uses our dedicated SA (NOT default)
  automountServiceAccountToken: false   # Double-guarantee: also at pod level
  containers:
  - name: &lt;app&gt;
    # No /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ directory exists
    # Attacker cannot curl the Kubernetes API even with shell access</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.8 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-8">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.8 Audit Policy: Medium Coverage (CKS Exam Ready)</h3>
                <p>A balanced audit policy that logs security-critical events without flooding the disk. <strong>This is the policy you'll create on the CKS exam.</strong></p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — audit-policy.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1
kind: Policy
omitStages: ["RequestReceived"]        # Only log final response — avoid duplicates
rules:
  # SUPPRESS: System component noise
  - level: None
    users: ["system:kube-proxy", "system:nodes"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["endpoints", "services", "nodes", "nodes/status"]

  # CRITICAL: Log ALL secret access at Metadata level (who, when, response code)
  - level: RequestResponse
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["secrets"]

  # CRITICAL: Log ALL RBAC changes (privilege escalation detection)
  - level: RequestResponse
    resources:
    - group: "rbac.authorization.k8s.io"
      resources: ["clusterroles", "clusterrolebindings", "roles", "rolebindings"]

  # CRITICAL: Log ALL pod exec/portforward/attach events
  - level: RequestResponse
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["pods/exec", "pods/portforward", "pods/attach"]

  # IMPORTANT: Log pod mutations (create, update, delete)
  - level: Request
    resources:
    - group: ""
      resources: ["pods"]
    verbs: ["create", "update", "patch", "delete"]

  # DEFAULT: Log everything else at Metadata level
  - level: Metadata</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.9 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-9">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.9 EncryptionConfiguration: AES-CBC for Secrets</h3>
                <p>Enable encryption at rest for Secrets in etcd. <strong>Remember: you must also mount this file to the API server and add the --encryption-provider-config flag.</strong></p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — encryption-config.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
- resources:
  - secrets                            # Encrypt Secret resources
  providers:
  - aescbc:                            # AES-256-CBC with PKCS#7 padding
      keys:
      - name: key1
        secret: &lt;base64-encoded-32-byte-key&gt;  # ← Generate: head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64
  - identity: {}                       # Fallback: plaintext (required for key rotation)

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# API SERVER CONFIGURATION (in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml):
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Add flag:
#   - --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml
# Add volume:
#   - name: encryption-config
#     hostPath:
#       path: /etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml
#       type: File
# Add volumeMount:
#   - name: encryption-config
#     mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml
#     readOnly: true</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.10 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-10">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.10 Falco Custom Rule Template</h3>
                <p>The <strong>5-line template</strong> you'll adapt for every Falco exam task. Change the macro name, list items, condition, and output — everything else stays the same.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — /etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ① Macro: reusable container filter
- macro: &lt;app&gt;_container
  condition: container.name = "&lt;container-name&gt;"

# ② List: reusable value set
- list: &lt;app&gt;_sensitive_binaries
  items: [bash, sh, nc, curl, wget, python3]

# ③ Rule: the detection logic
- rule: Suspicious Activity in &lt;App&gt;
  desc: Detect dangerous commands inside the &lt;app&gt; container
  condition: &gt;
    spawned_process and                 # Must be a NEW process (not existing)
    &lt;app&gt;_container and                # In our specific container
    proc.name in (&lt;app&gt;_sensitive_binaries)  # Running a watched binary
  output: &gt;
    🚨 Suspicious %proc.name in &lt;app&gt;!
    (user=%user.name cmd=%proc.cmdline
    pod=%k8s.pod.name ns=%k8s.ns.name)
  priority: WARNING                    # CRITICAL|WARNING|NOTICE|INFO|DEBUG
  tags: [&lt;app&gt;, suspicious, CKS]</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.11 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-11">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.11 AppArmor Profile Template</h3>
                <p>Restrict what a container can do at the kernel level. <strong>Load on every node that runs the pod.</strong> Annotated with what each section controls.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">Plaintext — /etc/apparmor.d/&lt;profile-name&gt;</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-text">#include &lt;tunables/global&gt;

profile &lt;app&gt;-apparmor flags=(attach_disconnected,mediate_deleted) {
  #include &lt;abstractions/base&gt;        # Basic filesystem + network access

  # ── FILESYSTEM RULES ──
  /app/            r,                  # Read application code
  /app/**          r,                  # Read all subdirectories
  /tmp/            rw,                 # Read+Write /tmp (emptyDir)
  /tmp/**          rw,                 # Read+Write all temp files
  /opt/venv/       r,                  # Read Python virtual environment
  /opt/venv/**     r,

  # ── NETWORK RULES ──
  network inet tcp,                    # Allow TCP (HTTP, database)
  network inet udp,                    # Allow UDP (DNS)

  # ── PROCESS RULES ──
  /usr/bin/python3 rix,               # Execute Python3
  /opt/venv/bin/** rix,               # Execute venv binaries
  capability       setuid,             # Allow setuid (needed by some runtimes)
  capability       setgid,
  capability       dac_override,       # Allow file permission overrides

  # ── DENY EVERYTHING ELSE ──
  /**              r,                  # Read-only for everything else
}

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# LOAD THE PROFILE (on each node):
#   apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/&lt;app&gt;-apparmor
#
# APPLY TO POD (annotation in pod metadata):
#   container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/&lt;container&gt;: localhost/&lt;app&gt;-apparmor</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.12 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-12">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.12 OPA ConstraintTemplate: Approved Registry Policy</h3>
                <p>Gatekeeper policy that <strong>only allows images from approved registries</strong>. Blocks Docker Hub and other public registries unless explicitly allowed.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — gatekeeper-constrainttemplate-approved-registries.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: templates.gatekeeper.sh/v1beta1
kind: ConstraintTemplate
metadata:
  name: k8sallowedrepos
spec:
  crd:
    spec:
      names:
        kind: K8sAllowedRepos
      validation:
        openAPIV3Schema:
          properties:
            repos:
              type: array
              items:
                type: string
  targets:
  - target: admission.k8s.gatekeeper.sh
    rego: |
      package k8sallowedrepos
      violation[{"msg": msg}] {
        container := input.review.object.spec.containers[_]
        not allowed_repo(container.image)
        msg := sprintf("image %v is from an unapproved registry", [container.image])
      }
      violation[{"msg": msg}] {
        container := input.review.object.spec.initContainers[_]
        not allowed_repo(container.image)
        msg := sprintf("initContainer image %v is from an unapproved registry", [container.image])
      }
      allowed_repo(image) {
        repos := input.parameters.repos
        repo := repos[_]
        startswith(image, repo)
      }
---
apiVersion: constraints.gatekeeper.sh/v1beta1
kind: K8sAllowedRepos
metadata:
  name: approved-registries-only
spec:
  enforcementAction: deny
  match:
    kinds:
    - apiGroups: [""]
      kinds: [Pod]
    namespaces: [&lt;your-namespace&gt;]
  parameters:
    repos:
    - "ghcr.io/&lt;your-org&gt;/"          # Your private registry
    - "registry.k8s.io/"                # Kubernetes system images
    # ❌ Docker Hub NOT in this list → BLOCKED</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.13 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-13">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.13 Kyverno ClusterPolicy: Require Non-Root</h3>
                <p>Kyverno policy that <strong>mutates pods</strong> to enforce non-root and adds readOnlyRootFilesystem if missing. Simpler YAML syntax than Gatekeeper's Rego.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — kyverno-require-nonroot.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-nonroot-readonly
spec:
  validationFailureAction: Enforce      # "Enforce" = block | "Audit" = report only
  background: true                      # Also check existing pods
  rules:
  - name: check-nonroot-readonly
    match:
      any:
      - resources:
          kinds: [Pod]
    validate:
      message: "Pod must run as non-root with read-only root filesystem"
      pattern:
        spec:
          containers:
          - securityContext:
              runAsNonRoot: true
              readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
          # ^^ If these are missing from ANY container → pod REJECTED
    # ── OPTIONAL: Auto-fix (mutate) if you want Kyverno to ADD these ──
    # mutate:
    #   patchStrategicMerge:
    #     spec:
    #       containers:
    #       - (name): "*"
    #         securityContext:
    #           runAsNonRoot: true
    #           readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.14 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-14">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.14 Kyverno ClusterPolicy: verifyImages with cosign</h3>
                <p>Kyverno policy that <strong>verifies cosign signatures</strong> before allowing pods to run. Ensures only images signed by your CI pipeline are deployed.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — kyverno-verify-images.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: verify-cosign-signatures
spec:
  validationFailureAction: Enforce
  background: true
  rules:
  - name: check-image-signature
    match:
      any:
      - resources:
          kinds: [Pod]
          namespaces: [&lt;your-namespace&gt;]
    verifyImages:
    - imageReferences:
      - "ghcr.io/&lt;your-org&gt;/*"         # Only check images from YOUR registry
      attestors:
      - count: 1
        entries:
        - keyless:                       # Verify via OIDC (GitHub Actions)
            subject: "https://github.com/&lt;org&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;/.github/workflows/build.yml@refs/heads/main"
            issuer: "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
            rekor:
              url: https://rekor.sigstore.dev
      # ── OR use public key verification ──
      # attestors:
      # - count: 1
      #   entries:
      #   - keys:
      #       publicKeys: |-
      #         -----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----
      #         &lt;your-cosign-public-key&gt;
      #         -----END PUBLIC KEY-----</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══════════ SECTION B.15 ═══════════ -->
            <section class="section-block" id="sb-15">
                <h3 class="section-h">B.15 PSS Labels: Namespace (all three modes)</h3>
                <p>Apply Pod Security Standards to a namespace — <strong>enforce</strong> (block), <strong>warn</strong> (notify), and <strong>audit</strong> (log). Always apply all three for defense in depth.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML — namespace-pss-labels.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: &lt;your-namespace&gt;
  labels:
    # ── ENFORCE: Reject non-compliant pods (the security gate) ──
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version: v1.30

    # ── WARN: Show warning for non-compliant pods (developer feedback) ──
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn-version: v1.30

    # ── AUDIT: Log violations without blocking (compliance trail) ──
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit-version: v1.30

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# PSS LEVELS:
#   privileged  = no restrictions (not recommended)
#   baseline    = prevents known privilege escalations
#   restricted  = maximum pod hardening (CKS standard)
#
# ROLLOUT STRATEGY:
#   1. Apply 'audit' only → measure violations → fix pods
#   2. Add 'warn' → developers see warnings → fix manifests
#   3. Add 'enforce' → non-compliant pods BLOCKED
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>
        </article>
