            <div class="section-block">
                <!-- ═══════════ MAIN ERROR TABLE ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">Error → Cause → Fix — Complete Reference Table</h3>
                <p>Every error you're likely to encounter on the CKS exam, organized by domain. <strong>Match the error message to the table row, execute the fix.</strong></p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:8%;">Domain</th><th style="width:28%;">Error / Symptom</th><th style="width:28%;">Root Cause</th><th style="width:36%;">Exact Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#4ade80;">PSS</span></td>
                                <td><code>Error from server (Forbidden): pods "X" is forbidden: violates PodSecurity "restricted:v1.30"</code></td>
                                <td>Namespace has <code>pod-security.../enforce=restricted</code> but your pod doesn't comply</td>
                                <td>Add to pod spec: <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>, <code>seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault</code>, <code>capabilities.drop: [ALL]</code>, remove privileged/hostPath/hostNetwork</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#f97316;">AppArmor</span></td>
                                <td><code>Cannot enforce AppArmor: profile "&lt;name&gt;" is not loaded</code></td>
                                <td>AppArmor profile not loaded on the node where the pod is scheduled</td>
                                <td>SSH to the node → <code>apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/&lt;profile&gt;</code> → <code>aa-status | grep &lt;profile&gt;</code> to verify</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#60a5fa;">NetworkPolicy</span></td>
                                <td>NetworkPolicy applied but traffic still flows — pods can still communicate despite default-deny</td>
                                <td>CNI plugin does not support NetworkPolicy (e.g., Flannel)</td>
                                <td>Verify: <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E 'calico|cilium|weave'</code>. If none found, switch CNI to Calico or Cilium</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#60a5fa;">NetworkPolicy</span></td>
                                <td>Pods can't resolve DNS after applying NetworkPolicy — <code>nslookup</code> times out</td>
                                <td>Default-deny blocks DNS egress (UDP/53 to kube-dns)</td>
                                <td>Add DNS egress rule: allow UDP/53 + TCP/53 to <code>kube-system</code> namespace, <code>k8s-app: kube-dns</code> pod selector</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#326ce5;">Falco</span></td>
                                <td>Falco not detecting expected events — no alerts firing even when suspicious activity occurs</td>
                                <td>Rule condition incorrect or Falco not restarted after rule change</td>
                                <td>Validate: <code>falco --dry-run</code>. Check loaded rules: <code>falco --list | grep &lt;rule-name&gt;</code>. Restart: <code>kubectl rollout restart ds falco -n falco</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#326ce5;">Falco</span></td>
                                <td><code>Error loading rules file: YAML syntax error</code></td>
                                <td>Indentation error in <code>/etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml</code> or missing colon</td>
                                <td>Use <code>falco --dry-run</code> to validate syntax. Check YAML indentation (must be 2 spaces). Ensure condition uses <code>&gt;</code> (folded block scalar) for multi-line</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#eab308;">Encryption</span></td>
                                <td><code>etcdctl get</code> returns plaintext despite EncryptionConfiguration being configured</td>
                                <td>EncryptionConfiguration providers ordered incorrectly — <code>identity: {}</code> is first</td>
                                <td>Move your encryption provider (aescbc/aesgcm) ABOVE <code>identity: {}</code> in the providers list. The FIRST provider is used for writes. Restart API server</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#eab308;">Encryption</span></td>
                                <td>Existing Secrets still in plaintext after enabling encryption</td>
                                <td>Only NEW secrets are encrypted. Existing secrets must be re-written to trigger encryption</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code> — reads all secrets (decrypted) and writes them back (encrypted with new key)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#a78bfa;">Image Signing</span></td>
                                <td><code>cosign verify</code> fails: <code>Error: no matching signatures</code></td>
                                <td>Wrong public key used, or image was signed with a different key</td>
                                <td>Verify you're using the correct <code>cosign.pub</code> that matches <code>cosign.key</code> used for signing. Check: <code>cosign verify --key cosign.pub &lt;image&gt;@sha256:&lt;digest&gt;</code> — must use digest, not tag</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#fbbf24;">RBAC</span></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl exec</code> denied: <code>Error from server (Forbidden): pods "X" is forbidden: User cannot create resource "pods/exec"</code></td>
                                <td>RBAC Role is missing the <code>pods/exec</code> subresource verb</td>
                                <td>Add to Role: <code>resources: ["pods/exec"], verbs: ["create"]</code>. Note: <code>pods/exec</code> is a SUBRESOURCE, not the same as <code>pods</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#4ade80;">Seccomp</span></td>
                                <td>Pod fails to start: <code>CreateContainerError</code> or app crashes immediately with "Operation not permitted"</td>
                                <td>RuntimeDefault Seccomp profile blocks a syscall your app needs (e.g., <code>clone</code>, <code>unshare</code>)</td>
                                <td>Create a custom Seccomp profile that allows the specific syscall. Place in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code>. Reference as <code>type: Localhost, localhostProfile: &lt;profile&gt;</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#f97316;">API Server</span></td>
                                <td>Anonymous auth still works after setting <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> — <code>curl -k https://api:6443</code> still returns data</td>
                                <td>API server manifest not updated correctly or didn't restart</td>
                                <td>Check: <code>crictl ps | grep apiserver</code> shows new container. Verify flag is present: <code>cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml | grep anonymous-auth</code>. Wait 30-60 seconds for auto-restart</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#fbbf24;">ServiceAccount</span></td>
                                <td>SA token still mounted after setting <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code></td>
                                <td>Old pod still running (pre-change). Only NEW pods get the setting</td>
                                <td>Delete the pod (or rollout restart Deployment): <code>kubectl delete pod &lt;name&gt;</code>. New pod will have no token mounted. Verify: <code>kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/</code> → "No such file"</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#eab308;">Audit</span></td>
                                <td>Audit log file not created at <code>/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</code> after enabling audit logging</td>
                                <td>Log path not writable by API server, or volume mount missing from manifest</td>
                                <td>Check: <code>ls -la /var/log/kubernetes/</code> — directory must exist and be writable. Verify API server manifest has BOTH the <code>--audit-log-path</code> flag AND the volume mount for <code>/var/log/kubernetes</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#a78bfa;">OPA/Gatekeeper</span></td>
                                <td>Constraint applied but not enforcing — pods with unapproved images still deploy</td>
                                <td>Constraint <code>enforcementAction</code> set to <code>dryrun</code> or <code>warn</code> instead of <code>deny</code></td>
                                <td>Set <code>enforcementAction: deny</code> in Constraint spec. Check: <code>kubectl get constraints -A</code> shows violation count but doesn't block until enforcementAction=deny</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#a78bfa;">ImagePolicyWebhook</span></td>
                                <td>ImagePolicyWebhook blocks ALL images — even approved ones. Cluster is unusable</td>
                                <td>Webhook server unreachable or returning errors for all requests (fail-closed behavior)</td>
                                <td>Check webhook server is running: <code>kubectl get pods -n &lt;webhook-ns&gt;</code>. Check certs: <code>kubectl describe validatingwebhookconfiguration</code>. If webhook is down and you need emergency access, delete the ValidatingWebhookConfiguration temporarily</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><span style="color:#60a5fa;">Trivy</span></td>
                                <td>Trivy reports too many false positives — CVEs that don't affect your app</td>
                                <td>Old vulnerability database; missing vendor-specific advisories</td>
                                <td>Update DB: <code>trivy image --update-db &lt;image&gt;</code>. Filter by severity: <code>--severity CRITICAL,HIGH</code>. Check CVE is actually fixed in your distro: <code>trivy image --ignore-unfixed &lt;image&gt;</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ DIAGNOSTIC PATTERNS ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">Diagnostic Patterns — How to Approach Any Security Error</h3>
                <p>When you encounter an error that isn't in the table above, follow this systematic diagnostic flow:</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔍</div>
                        <h5>1. Identify the Layer</h5>
                        <p>Which security layer is failing? <strong>Admission?</strong> (Pod rejected before creation) → Check PSS labels, Gatekeeper constraints, Kyverno policies. <strong>Runtime?</strong> (Pod created but crashes) → Check securityContext, Seccomp, AppArmor, capabilities. <strong>Network?</strong> (Can't connect) → Check NetworkPolicy, CNI plugin, DNS egress. <strong>API?</strong> (kubectl denied) → Check RBAC, SA token, context.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>2. Check the Events</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> — look at the Events section at the bottom. It often says EXACTLY what's wrong: "violates PodSecurity", "AppArmor profile not found", "failed to pull image". <code>kubectl get events -n &lt;ns&gt; --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'</code> shows the most recent events across the namespace.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔬</div>
                        <h5>3. Inspect the Pod Spec</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; -o yaml | grep -A20 securityContext</code> — check what security settings are actually applied. <code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].securityContext}' | jq .</code> — pretty-print the full security context.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🖥️</div>
                        <h5>4. Check the Node</h5>
                        <p>For Seccomp/AppArmor issues: SSH to the node and verify profiles exist. <code>ls /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code>, <code>aa-status | grep &lt;profile&gt;</code>. For kubelet issues: <code>journalctl -u kubelet --since "5 min ago"</code>. For API server: <code>crictl logs &lt;apiserver-container-id&gt;</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div>
                        <h5>5. Restart and Retry</h5>
                        <p>Many security changes require a RESTART to take effect: API server flags → kubelet auto-restarts (~30s). Falco rules → <code>systemctl restart falco</code> or restart DaemonSet. Kubelet config → <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code>. Pod security context changes → delete and recreate the pod (not just restart). After restart, VERIFY the change took effect.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📖</div>
                        <h5>6. Search the Docs</h5>
                        <p>On the CKS exam, <code>kubernetes.io/docs</code> is your external memory. Search for the exact error message or the resource name. The docs page often includes a "Troubleshooting" section. Copy the example, modify it, apply it. Don't guess — the docs have the correct YAML structure.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ QUICK DECISION TREE ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">Error Type → Diagnostic Path — Quick Decision Tree</h3>
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="tree-title">What kind of error are you seeing?</div>
<pre>
  ERROR MESSAGE APPEARS
  │
  ├─ "Forbidden" or "violates PodSecurity" or "denied"
  │  └─ ADMISSION ERROR → Check PSS labels, Gatekeeper constraints, Kyverno
  │     → kubectl describe ns &lt;ns&gt; | grep pod-security
  │     → kubectl get constraints -A
  │     → kubectl get clusterpolicies
  │
  ├─ "Read-only file system" or "Operation not permitted"
  │  └─ RUNTIME ERROR → Check securityContext (readOnlyRootFilesystem, Seccomp)
  │     → kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- touch /test-write
  │     → kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- cat /proc/1/status | grep Seccomp
  │
  ├─ "Failed to connect" or "Connection timed out"
  │  └─ NETWORK ERROR → Check NetworkPolicy, CNI, DNS
  │     → kubectl get netpol -n &lt;ns&gt;
  │     → kubectl run test --rm -it --image=busybox -- wget -qO- --timeout=5 &lt;url&gt;
  │
  ├─ "cannot create resource" or "User cannot"
  │  └─ RBAC ERROR → Check Role, RoleBinding, SA
  │     → kubectl auth can-i &lt;verb&gt; &lt;resource&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; --as=&lt;sa&gt;
  │
  ├─ "CrashLoopBackOff" or "CreateContainerError"
  │  └─ STARTUP ERROR → Check container logs, events, Seccomp/AppArmor
  │     → kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous
  │     → kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt; | tail -20
  │
  └─ "No such file or directory" or "not found"
     └─ CONFIG ERROR → Check file paths, mounts, volume existence
        → ls -la &lt;path&gt; (from node)
        → kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt; | grep -A10 Mounts
</pre>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ EXAM-SPECIFIC GOTCHAS ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">🚨 CKS Exam-Specific Gotchas — Errors That Are Easy to Miss</h3>
                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>Wrong Cluster Context</h5>
                        <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> You create all resources correctly but the scoring script gives 0 points. <strong>Cause:</strong> You solved the task on the wrong cluster context. <strong>Fix:</strong> Always run <code>kubectl config use-context &lt;name&gt;</code> FIRST, then <code>kubectl config current-context</code> to verify.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>Missing Volume Mount</h5>
                        <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> API server flag is set but config file is not found. <strong>Cause:</strong> You added <code>--audit-policy-file</code> or <code>--encryption-provider-config</code> but forgot the volume mount. <strong>Fix:</strong> Always add BOTH the flag AND the volume + volumeMount in kube-apiserver.yaml.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>Wrong Namespace</h5>
                        <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> Resources created but grader doesn't find them. <strong>Cause:</strong> You created resources in the wrong namespace (e.g., "default" instead of "easypj-prod"). <strong>Fix:</strong> Read the task carefully. If a namespace is specified, create it first: <code>kubectl create ns &lt;name&gt;</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
