        <article class="chapter" id="appendix-d">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Appendix D · Security Terminology Glossary</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">A complete glossary of <strong>Kubernetes security terminology</strong>. Every term is defined in the context of how it appears on the CKS exam and in real-world cluster security. Terms marked with 🎓 are heavily tested on the CKS exam. <strong>Use this glossary to decode exam questions quickly — if a term is unfamiliar, look it up here first.</strong></p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 Glossary</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">62 Terms</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">A-Z Organized</span>
            </div>
            </div><!-- /chapter-intro -->

            <div class="section-block">
                <!-- ═══════════ A ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">A</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Admission Controller</strong></td><td>A plugin that intercepts authenticated API requests before they are persisted to etcd. Can <strong>mutate</strong> (modify) or <strong>validate</strong> (accept/reject) requests. Examples: PodSecurity, ImagePolicyWebhook, Gatekeeper, Kyverno. The CKS exam tests MutatingWebhookConfiguration and ValidatingWebhookConfiguration creation.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 AppArmor</strong></td><td>A Linux Security Module (LSM) that restricts program capabilities via per-program profiles. In Kubernetes, applied via pod annotation: <code>container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/&lt;container&gt;: localhost/&lt;profile&gt;</code>. Profiles are loaded on each node from <code>/etc/apparmor.d/</code>. Enforces mandatory access control (MAC) at the kernel level.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Attack Surface</strong></td><td>The sum of all possible points (interfaces, APIs, ports, services) where an attacker can attempt to enter or extract data from a system. In Kubernetes: exposed LoadBalancers, public Ingress endpoints, mounted ServiceAccount tokens, writable container filesystems, unauthenticated kubelet ports. Minimizing attack surface is the goal of cluster hardening.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Audit Log</strong></td><td>A chronological, structured (JSON) record of every request made to the Kubernetes API server. Records: who (user), what (verb, resource), when (timestamp), from where (source IP), result (response code). Configured via audit policy YAML and enabled with <code>--audit-policy-file</code> and <code>--audit-log-path</code> API server flags. Essential for PCI-DSS, SOC 2, HIPAA compliance.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Authentication (AuthN)</strong></td><td>Verifying the identity of a user or service — "who are you?" In Kubernetes: client certificates, bearer tokens (ServiceAccount JWT), OIDC tokens, webhook token authentication. Failed AuthN returns HTTP 401. After AuthN, the user's identity is available for Authorization (AuthZ).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Authorization (AuthZ)</strong></td><td>Determining what an authenticated entity is allowed to do — "what can you do?" In Kubernetes: RBAC (Role-Based Access Control), Node authorizer, ABAC (legacy), webhook authorization. Failed AuthZ returns HTTP 403. Configured via Roles, ClusterRoles, RoleBindings, ClusterRoleBindings.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>automountServiceAccountToken</strong></td><td>A field on ServiceAccount and Pod specs that controls whether the Kubernetes API token is automatically mounted at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code>. Setting to <code>false</code> prevents pods from accessing the Kubernetes API — a critical defense against lateral movement after container compromise.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ B ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">B</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Base Image</strong></td><td>The foundational image layer your Dockerfile builds upon (e.g., <code>python:3.12-slim</code>, <code>alpine:3.19</code>). Security implications: base images carry their own CVEs. Choosing minimal base images (distroless, Alpine, slim variants) reduces attack surface. The CKS exam expects you to know image layering and minimization.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Baseline (PSS)</strong></td><td>The middle Pod Security Standard level. Prevents known privilege escalations (hostPath volumes, hostNetwork, privileged containers) but allows some flexibility (any Seccomp/AppArmor profile, non-root not strictly required). Suitable for development namespaces. The CKS exam tests applying Baseline vs Restricted labels.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ C ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">C</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 CA (Certificate Authority)</strong></td><td>An entity that issues and signs digital certificates. In Kubernetes: the cluster CA at <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt</code> signs all internal certificates (API server, kubelet, etcd). The CA certificate is distributed to all components for TLS verification. Compromising the CA key compromises the entire cluster PKI.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Capabilities (Linux)</strong></td><td>Fine-grained privileges split from the traditional all-powerful root user. Instead of UID 0 having all powers, capabilities grant specific permissions: <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code> (mount, swapon), <code>CAP_NET_RAW</code> (raw sockets), <code>CAP_SYS_PTRACE</code> (trace processes). Kubernetes: <code>capabilities.drop: [ALL]</code> removes all 14 default capabilities. <code>capabilities.add: [NET_BIND_SERVICE]</code> grants only what's needed.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 CIS Benchmark</strong></td><td>Center for Internet Security configuration security standards. The CIS Kubernetes Benchmark defines 100+ security checks across 5 sections: control plane, etcd, control plane config, worker nodes, policies. kube-bench automates CIS benchmark checking. The CKS exam tests fixing specific CIS failures (e.g., 1.1.1: disable anonymous auth).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 ClusterRole</strong></td><td>An RBAC role that grants permissions across ALL namespaces or to cluster-scoped resources (nodes, namespaces, PersistentVolumes). Unlike a Role (namespaced), a ClusterRole has no namespace field. Used with ClusterRoleBinding for cluster-wide access, or with RoleBinding for cross-namespace access.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Container Escape</strong></td><td>Exploiting a vulnerability to break out of a container's isolation and access the host node. Techniques: exploiting runc CVEs, abusing <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code> to mount host filesystems, using <code>nsenter</code> with host PID namespace access. Defense: drop ALL capabilities, Seccomp RuntimeDefault, read-only root FS, no hostPath, no privileged containers.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 cosign</strong></td><td>A CNCF-backed tool (part of Sigstore) for signing and verifying container images. <code>cosign sign --key cosign.key &lt;image&gt;</code> creates a digital signature stored as an OCI artifact. <code>cosign verify --key cosign.pub &lt;image&gt;</code> verifies the signature. Supports keyless signing via OIDC (GitHub Actions, GCP) with Fulcio CA + Rekor transparency log.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 CVE</strong></td><td>Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures — a public database of known security flaws in software. Each CVE has an ID (e.g., CVE-2024-21626), CVSS severity score (0-10), and affected version ranges. Trivy scans container images against CVE databases (NVD, GitHub Advisory, distro-specific trackers). The CKS exam expects you to scan images and report CRITICAL CVEs.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CycloneDX</strong></td><td>An OWASP standard for Software Bill of Materials (SBOM). XML/JSON format focused on security and vulnerability management. Natively supports embedding CVE data within the SBOM. Used by Trivy and Dependency-Track. Alternative to SPDX format. Both are accepted on the CKS exam.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ D ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">D</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Defense in Depth</strong></td><td>A security strategy using multiple, independent layers of protection so that no single failure results in a complete breach. In Kubernetes: RBAC (who can do what) → NetworkPolicy (what can talk to what) → PSS (pod hardening) → Seccomp/AppArmor (kernel restrictions) → readOnlyRootFilesystem (no persistence) → Falco (detection) → Audit logs (forensics). Each layer assumes the previous one might fail.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>distroless</strong></td><td>Minimal container images containing ONLY the application and its runtime dependencies — no shell, no package manager, no utilities. Examples: <code>gcr.io/distroless/python3</code>, <code>gcr.io/distroless/static</code>. Benefits: tiny attack surface, near-zero CVEs, attacker trapped with no tools. Tradeoff: no <code>kubectl exec -- sh</code> for debugging — rely on logs and metrics.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ E ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">E</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 eBPF</strong></td><td>Extended Berkeley Packet Filter — a Linux kernel technology that allows sandboxed programs to run in the kernel without changing kernel source code or loading modules. Used by: Falco (syscall capture without kernel module), Cilium (NetworkPolicy enforcement at kernel level), Tetragon (runtime security). Safer than kernel modules — eBPF programs pass through a verifier before loading.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Encryption at Rest</strong></td><td>Protecting data stored on disk by encrypting it before writing. In Kubernetes: <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> enables AES-CBC or AES-GCM encryption of Secrets in etcd. The API server encrypts on write and decrypts on read — transparent to users. Without it, Secrets are stored as plaintext base64 in etcd, readable by anyone with etcd access.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>emptyDir</strong></td><td>A Kubernetes volume type that provides an ephemeral directory tied to a pod's lifecycle. Data is deleted when the pod is removed. Security note: emptyDir is writable even when <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code>, making it a potential malware persistence location until pod deletion. Use <code>sizeLimit</code> to prevent disk exhaustion attacks.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 etcd</strong></td><td>A distributed, consistent key-value store that is Kubernetes' "source of truth." All cluster state (pods, services, secrets, RBAC, configmaps) is stored in etcd. Security: must be TLS-encrypted (peer + client certs), accessible only from control plane nodes, and have Secrets encrypted at rest. Access requires client certificates at <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>etcdctl</strong></td><td>The command-line client for interacting with etcd directly. Used on the CKS exam for: reading Secrets from etcd to verify encryption (<code>etcdctl get /registry/secrets/...</code>), creating snapshots (<code>snapshot save</code>), restoring from backups (<code>snapshot restore</code>), and checking cluster health (<code>endpoint health</code>). Requires TLS certificates for authentication.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ F ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">F</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Falco</strong></td><td>A CNCF-graduated runtime security tool that monitors Linux kernel system calls in real time. Detects: shell spawns in containers, sensitive file reads, unexpected network connections, privilege escalations. Architecture: kernel driver (kmod/eBPF) → libscap event capture → libsinsp engine → rules evaluation → alert output. Rules are YAML files at <code>/etc/falco/falco_rules.yaml</code> (default) and <code>/etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml</code> (custom — edit this one).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>falcosidekick</strong></td><td>A companion daemon that receives Falco alerts via gRPC and forwards them to 30+ destinations simultaneously: Slack, PagerDuty, Elasticsearch, Loki, S3, Kafka, Teams, Discord, Opsgenie, Datadog, etc. Enables production-grade alert routing without modifying Falco itself. Configured via YAML file with per-destination minimum priority thresholds.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ G ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">G</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Gatekeeper (OPA)</strong></td><td>An admission controller built on the Open Policy Agent (OPA) engine. Uses the Rego declarative language to define policies as ConstraintTemplates (the logic) and Constraints (the parameters). Enforces policies at pod creation time. Has audit mode to find existing violations without blocking. The CKS exam tests writing ConstraintTemplates with Rego.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 GitOps</strong></td><td>A deployment methodology where Git repositories are the single source of truth for desired state. An operator (ArgoCD, Flux) continuously reconciles the cluster state with Git. Security benefit: every change has a commit history (who, what, when), PR review requirement, and automated drift correction. <code>selfHeal: true</code> auto-corrects manual <code>kubectl edit</code> changes within 3 minutes.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ H-I ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">H — I</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>hostPath</strong></td><td>A volume type that mounts a file or directory from the HOST node's filesystem into a pod. Extremely dangerous — a compromised pod with hostPath can read/write ANY file on the node, including <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> (control plane config), <code>/var/lib/kubelet/</code> (Seccomp profiles), and <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> (TLS certs). Blocked by PSS Restricted. Only for system DaemonSets (CNI, CSI).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Image Pull Policy</strong></td><td>Controls when Kubernetes pulls a container image. <code>Always</code> (pull every time — safest), <code>IfNotPresent</code> (use cached if available), <code>Never</code> (only use local images). Security: <code>Always</code> with digest references prevents running stale images after a compromised registry fix. <code>:latest</code> tag is mutable and should never be used in production.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 imagePullSecrets</strong></td><td>A Kubernetes Secret of type <code>kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson</code> containing credentials for private container registries. Referenced in pod spec to authenticate image pulls. Must be in the SAME namespace as the pod. Created with: <code>kubectl create secret docker-registry &lt;name&gt; --docker-server=... --docker-username=... --docker-password=...</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Immutable Container</strong></td><td>A container whose root filesystem is read-only (<code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code>). Once running, nothing can be written, modified, or deleted — not even by root. Changes must go through CI/CD pipeline and image rebuild. Prevents: malware installation, binary replacement, cron job persistence. Writable directories (/tmp, /var/run) are provided via emptyDir or PVC volumes.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ K ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">K</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 kube-apiserver</strong></td><td>The front door to Kubernetes. ALL operations (kubectl, controllers, kubelets) go through the API server. It handles authentication, authorization, admission control, and audit logging. Runs as a static pod from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. Security flags: <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>, <code>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</code>, <code>--audit-policy-file=...</code>, <code>--encryption-provider-config=...</code>, <code>--profiling=false</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 kube-bench</strong></td><td>An Aqua Security tool that checks Kubernetes clusters against the CIS Kubernetes Benchmark. Run with: <code>kube-bench run --targets master</code> (control plane) or <code>kube-bench run --targets node</code> (worker nodes). The CKS exam may ask you to run kube-bench and fix the top FAIL items. See Appendix A.6 for commands.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 kubelet</strong></td><td>The node agent that runs on every worker (and control plane) node. Registers the node with the API server and ensures containers are running. Security config at <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>: disable anonymous auth (<code>authentication.anonymous.enabled: false</code>), enable webhook authorization (<code>authorization.mode: Webhook</code>), disable read-only port (<code>readOnlyPort: 0</code>).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubesec</strong></td><td>A security scoring tool for Kubernetes pod specs. Rates deployments from 0 (insecure) to 10 (fully hardened). Checks: runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem, capabilities, resource limits, Seccomp, ServiceAccount. Use: <code>kubectl get deploy &lt;name&gt; -o yaml | kubesec-scan -</code>. The CKS standard is a score of 7-8/10.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Kyverno</strong></td><td>A Kubernetes-native policy engine that uses YAML (not Rego) for policy definitions. Can: validate (reject non-compliant pods), mutate (auto-add securityContext fields), generate (create default NetworkPolicies), and verifyImages (check cosign signatures). The CKS exam may test Kyverno ClusterPolicy creation for image verification and security context enforcement.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ L-M ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">L — M</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Least Privilege</strong></td><td>A security principle: every entity (user, ServiceAccount, pod) should have ONLY the minimum permissions needed to perform its function — and no more. Applied via: minimal RBAC roles (just the verbs and resources needed), disabled automount for ServiceAccounts, dropped Linux capabilities, restricted NetworkPolicy egress rules.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 mTLS (Mutual TLS)</strong></td><td>TLS where BOTH client and server present certificates to authenticate each other. In Kubernetes: etcd peer communication uses mTLS, service mesh (Istio/Linkerd) can enforce mTLS for all pod-to-pod traffic. Provides: encryption in transit + mutual authentication. Different from regular TLS where only the server is authenticated.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ N ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">N</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 NetworkPolicy</strong></td><td>A Kubernetes resource that controls pod-to-pod communication at the network level (L3/L4). Defines ingress (incoming) and egress (outgoing) rules based on pod selectors, namespace selectors, and IP blocks. Uses pod labels for targeting. <strong>Requires a CNI plugin that supports it</strong> (Calico, Cilium, Weave — NOT Flannel). Default behavior without NetworkPolicy: all traffic allowed.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>NodeRestriction</strong></td><td>An admission plugin that restricts kubelet permissions. A kubelet can only modify its OWN node object and pods bound to its node. Prevents a compromised kubelet from modifying other nodes or reading all cluster secrets. Enabled with: <code>--enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction</code> on the API server.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ O-P ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">O — P</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>OCI (Open Container Initiative)</strong></td><td>Standards for container formats and runtimes. Defines: image spec (how images are structured as layers), runtime spec (how containers are executed), distribution spec (how registries store and serve images). Cosign stores signatures as OCI artifacts alongside images. SBOMs can be attached as OCI artifacts via <code>cosign attach sbom</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 OPA (Open Policy Agent)</strong></td><td>A general-purpose policy engine that evaluates Rego policies against structured data (JSON/YAML). Gatekeeper is OPA packaged as a Kubernetes admission controller. OPA itself can be used for non-K8s policy enforcement: Terraform plans, Envoy authorization, SSH access control. The CKS exam focuses on OPA via Gatekeeper for admission control.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Pod Security Admission (PSA)</strong></td><td>The built-in Kubernetes admission controller (since v1.25) that enforces Pod Security Standards. Replaces the deprecated PodSecurityPolicy (PSP). Controlled entirely by namespace labels — no YAML manifests needed. Three levels: privileged, baseline, restricted. Three modes: enforce (block), warn (notify), audit (log).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Privileged Container</strong></td><td>A container that runs with ALL Linux capabilities and access to ALL host devices. Equivalent to running as root on the host. Enabled by: <code>securityContext.privileged: true</code>. BLOCKED by PSS Baseline and Restricted. Should be avoided entirely — if a tool needs privileged, question whether it should run in Kubernetes at all.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ R ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">R</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)</strong></td><td>Kubernetes' primary authorization mechanism. Defines WHO (subjects: users, groups, ServiceAccounts) can do WHAT (verbs: get, list, create, update, delete, watch) on WHICH resources (pods, secrets, deployments, etc.) in WHICH scope (namespace via Role, cluster via ClusterRole). The CKS exam heavily tests Role + RoleBinding creation and verification with <code>kubectl auth can-i</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 readOnlyRootFilesystem</strong></td><td>A container securityContext field that mounts the root filesystem with the <code>MS_RDONLY</code> flag. Any <code>write()</code> syscall returns <code>EROFS</code> (errno 30). Enforced at the KERNEL level — even root (UID 0) cannot bypass. Requires emptyDir or PVC volumes for paths that need writes (/tmp, /var/run, /app/media). Part of the CKS Restricted pod profile.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Rego</strong></td><td>The declarative policy language used by OPA and Gatekeeper. Describes WHAT a violation looks like (not HOW to find it). Uses <code>violation[{msg}]</code> partial sets to report policy failures. Key syntax: <code>[_]</code> iteration operator, <code>input.review.object</code> for the Kubernetes resource being evaluated, <code>input.parameters</code> for constraint configuration.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Rekor</strong></td><td>A transparency log service (part of Sigstore) that records signed metadata for software artifacts. Cosign can upload signatures to Rekor, providing an immutable, publicly-auditable record. The CKS exam: know that Rekor = transparency log, Fulcio = certificate authority for keyless signing.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>RuntimeClass</strong></td><td>A Kubernetes resource that selects a container runtime configuration. Used to run untrusted workloads with sandboxed runtimes: gVisor (user-space kernel), Kata Containers (lightweight VM). Applied via <code>spec.runtimeClassName: &lt;name&gt;</code> in pod spec. Requires the runtime handler to be installed on nodes.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ S ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">S</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 SBOM</strong></td><td>Software Bill of Materials — a machine-readable inventory of every component, library, and dependency in a software artifact. Formats: SPDX (Linux Foundation, license-focused) and CycloneDX (OWASP, security-focused). Generated with syft or Trivy. Attached to images via <code>cosign attach sbom</code>. Critical for supply chain: when a new CVE drops, query the SBOM to identify affected images instantly.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Seccomp (Secure Computing Mode)</strong></td><td>A Linux kernel feature that filters system calls. A Seccomp profile defines WHICH syscalls a process can make — all others are blocked. <code>RuntimeDefault</code> blocks ~300 dangerous syscalls (mount, ptrace, reboot) while allowing ~60 safe ones. Custom profiles are JSON files placed in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code>. Applied via <code>securityContext.seccompProfile.type</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 SecurityContext</strong></td><td>A pod/container specification section that controls security settings: runAsNonRoot, runAsUser, allowPrivilegeEscalation, readOnlyRootFilesystem, capabilities (add/drop), seccompProfile, appArmorProfile. Can be set at both pod level (applies to all containers) and container level (overrides pod level). The CKS exam tests writing complete security contexts.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 ServiceAccount (SA)</strong></td><td>A Kubernetes identity for pods (not users). Every pod runs with exactly one ServiceAccount. The SA's token is mounted at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> unless <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>. RBAC permissions are bound to SAs via RoleBindings. The CKS exam tests creating dedicated SAs, disabling automount, and verifying permissions.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 SLSA</strong></td><td>Supply Chain Levels for Software Artifacts — a security framework (from Google/OpenSSF) defining four levels of build integrity: L0 (none), L1 (provenance exists), L2 (hosted + signed), L3 (hardened + hermetic). Provides increasing guarantees that software hasn't been tampered with between source and deployment. Pronounced "salsa."</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>SPDX</strong></td><td>Software Package Data Exchange — an ISO/IEC 5962:2021 standard for SBOM. JSON, YAML, tag-value, and RDF/XML formats. Focused on license compliance AND supply chain security. Used by the Kubernetes SBOM Working Group. Alternative to CycloneDX. Both are accepted on the CKS exam.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Static Pod</strong></td><td>A pod managed directly by the kubelet on a specific node, not by the API server. Control plane components (API server, controller-manager, scheduler, etcd) run as static pods. Manifests are at <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — editing these files causes the kubelet to automatically restart the pod. The CKS exam involves editing these manifests for security configuration.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>STRIDE</strong></td><td>A threat modeling framework: Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information Disclosure, Denial of Service, Elevation of Privilege. Used on the CKS exam to categorize threats: an "attacker reading secrets" question → Information Disclosure → encryption/Vault. "Attacker impersonating another user" → Spoofing → authentication hardening.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- ═══════════ T-V ═══════════ -->
                <h3 class="section-h">T — V</h3>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%;">Term</th><th style="width:78%;">Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Trivy</strong></td><td>An open-source vulnerability scanner by Aqua Security. Scans: container images, filesystems, git repositories, Kubernetes manifests. Detects: CVEs (OS packages + language libraries), misconfigurations, exposed secrets, SBOM generation. The CKS exam: <code>trivy image &lt;image&gt;</code> scans for vulnerabilities, <code>--severity CRITICAL,HIGH</code> filters, <code>--exit-code 1</code> fails CI/CD on findings.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Trivy Operator</strong></td><td>A Kubernetes operator that runs Trivy continuously inside the cluster. Creates VulnerabilityReport CRDs for all running images. Re-scans when new CVEs are published. Complements CI/CD scanning by catching vulnerabilities discovered AFTER deployment. Installed via Helm from Aqua Security charts.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>🎓 Vault (HashiCorp)</strong></td><td>An external secret management platform that stores, rotates, and dynamically generates secrets outside Kubernetes. Pods authenticate to Vault via Kubernetes auth method, receive time-limited credentials. Secrets never touch etcd. Integration: Vault Sidecar Injector or CSI driver. The CKS exam: know the concept and basic integration pattern — Vault is for production, EncryptionConfiguration is for the exam.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</strong></td><td>An admission webhook that VALIDATES (accepts or rejects) API requests. Called AFTER mutating webhooks. Examples: Gatekeeper constraints, Kyverno validate rules, ImagePolicyWebhook. If the webhook server is unreachable, the API server's failure policy determines whether to accept (fail-open) or reject (fail-closed) the request.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
