                <h4>🔄 Retake Strategy — What to Do If You Don't Pass</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">1</span><strong>Review Your Score Report</strong><p>The Linux Foundation provides a domain-by-domain breakdown of your performance. Identify which domains you scored lowest in. Focus your retake preparation on those domains specifically.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">2</span><strong>Analyze What Went Wrong</strong><p>Did you run out of time? (Practice speed drills.) Did you forget context switching? (Build the ritual habit.) Did you not know the commands? (Review Appendix A and practice labs.) Were you anxious? (Practice under timed conditions more.)</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">3</span><strong>Retake Within 2-4 Weeks</strong><p>Your free retake is valid for 12 months. Don't wait too long — the knowledge fades. Schedule your retake 2-4 weeks after the failed attempt. Focus study on weak domains, not re-reading everything.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">4</span><strong>What's Different on Retake</strong><p>The retake exam has different tasks but the same format and domains. The environment is identical. Your previous exam experience is an advantage — you know exactly what to expect.</p></div>
