                <h4>Weekly Study Routine — How to Structure Each Week</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">📖</span><strong>Days 1-2: Read (2-3 hours)</strong><p>Read the assigned chapters. Don't just skim — understand every code block and diagram. Write down questions. Highlight commands you'll need to memorize. Focus on the "Why" behind each security control, not just the "What."</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">💻</span><strong>Days 3-5: Lab (4-6 hours)</strong><p>Do the hands-on lab for the week. Use a real cluster (minikube, kind, or cloud sandbox). Type every command — don't copy-paste. If something doesn't work, debug it. The bugs you fix during practice are the bugs you'll fix FAST on the exam.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">🔄</span><strong>Day 6: Review (1-2 hours)</strong><p>Re-read the chapters with fresh eyes after the lab. The concepts will click differently after hands-on experience. Review any commands that were difficult in the lab. Update your personal cheat sheet.</p></div>
                    <div class="step"><span class="step-number">🧠</span><strong>Day 7: Flashcards (1 hour)</strong><p>Create flashcards for the week's key commands and file paths. Quiz yourself. The goal is RECALL, not recognition — can you type the command from memory? Practice until the commands flow from muscle memory.</p></div>
                </div>

